/* ==========================================================================
   数学星图 · 英文陈述与说明（statement / notes · T3.2）
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   这是什么：把**枢纽节点**的陈述（statement）与说明（notes）译成英文。
   现有 11 条 = 试点 3 条（T3.2，2026-10-16）+ **第一批 8 条**（T3.2 批次 1，
   对象是枢纽榜前 10 名里尚未有译文的那 8 个 —— 前两名 lem.zorn / thm.first-iso
   已在试点里）。每个批次的中英对照与存疑清单在 i18n/review-batch-N.md。

   ⛔ 不是第二棵内容树：中文陈述仍是唯一真源（`src/data/packs/*.js`）；
      这里按 id 旁挂译文，graph.js 建图时抄到节点上。
   ⛔ 不含新命题、不含新推导：**逐句对译**，中文说什么就说什么。
   ⛔ 边的证明（`proofs/`）**不在**本批范围 —— 英文页会写明「证明仍以中文页为准」。
   ⭐ **公式一字不改**：`$…$` / `$$…$$` 必须与中文逐字相同，公式里也不许出现汉字
      （汉字要移进散文，或换成中英皆可的记号）。构建有硬断言，见 i18n/deviations.md。

   ⭐⭐ 状态**逐条判定**（作者 2026-10-16 拍板）：每条 = 一个字段，
       statement / notes 各自带
         · status    'draft'（机器起草，待审）| 'reviewed'（作者审过）
         · basedOn   `'<内容版本>#<中文底稿指纹>'` —— 指纹 = MG.zhHash，8 位十六进制
         · reviewed  { by, date }（status = 'reviewed' 时填）
       ⭐ stale **由构建现算**，两个来源任一不符即 stale：
          ① 内容版本变了（tools/ids.snapshot.json 的 vX.Y.Z）；
          ② 这条中文底稿的指纹变了 —— 说明**这句中文本身**被改过。
       ⛔ 别手工「刷新」basedOn 来消掉 stale：那等于悄悄作废审校记录。
          要重新对稿就跑 `bun tools/i18n-status.mjs`（默认只报不写）。
       ⚠️ 判定只有一处实现 = `src/core/i18n.js` 的 MG.enFieldStatus；
          tools/build.mjs 调的是同一个函数，所以网页与产物不会各说各话。
   ⚠️ 这个文件**挂进 index.html**（它是节点字段来源：搜索索引要用），
      加载它的地方在 index.html；挂到节点上的地方在 src/core/graph.js。
   ========================================================================== */
window.MG = window.MG || {};

MG.i18nStatements = {
  version: 2,               // 2 = 逐条状态（每字段 status/basedOn）；1 = 文件级一个 status
  drafted: '2026-10-16',    // 本文件第一次起草的日期（只是历史记录，不参与状态判定）

  items: {
    /* ---------------------------------------------------------- 佐恩引理 */
    'lem.zorn': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $(P, \\preceq )$ be a **non-empty** poset. If every chain in $P$ has an upper bound ' +
        'in $P$, then $P$ has a **maximal element**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#88c083ff', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Neither hypothesis can be dropped: the empty poset has no maximal element, and ' +
        '"every chain has an upper bound" does not give a *greatest* element — only a maximal one.\n\n' +
        'It is "the universal engine of existence proofs": every vector space has a basis, ' +
        'every ring has a maximal ideal, the compactness theorem, Hahn–Banach — all of them ' +
        'rely on it.\n\n' +
        'The name is a historical accident: Zorn popularised the statement in the 1940s, while ' +
        'Kuratowski had already proved an equivalent form in 1922, so it is also called the ' +
        'Kuratowski–Zorn lemma.\n\n' +
        '⚠ Non-constructive: what one obtains is "there exists a maximal element", and usually ' +
        'one cannot say which.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4e157a1c', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------ 第一同构定理 */
    'thm.first-iso': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $\\varphi : G \\to G\'$ be a **group homomorphism**. Then $\\varphi$ induces an ' +
        'isomorphism\n\n' +
        '$$G \\big/ \\ker\\varphi \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{im}\\varphi, \\qquad ' +
        'g \\ker\\varphi \\;\\longmapsto\\; \\varphi(g).$$\n\n' +
        'In particular: **the image of a surjective homomorphism is the quotient group** ' +
        '($G/\\ker\\varphi \\cong G\'$ when $\\varphi$ is surjective).',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1f75f342', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **It is really a theorem about the format \"set + operations + axioms\", not a privilege of groups.** The same proof carries over verbatim once \"homomorphism\" is replaced by the corresponding notion:\n\n$$A \\big/ \\ker f \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{im} f.$$\n\n⭐ **The set-level prototype**: for any map $f : A \\to B$, define on $A$ the relation $a \\sim a' \\iff f(a) = f(a')$; then $f$ induces a **bijection** $A/{\\sim} \\;\\to\\; \\operatorname{im} f$ (see \"quotient set and equivalence classes\").\n\nThe group version adds just one sentence: **after quotienting by the kernel, what remains is a group**.\n\nThe first two steps (well defined, bijection) are the set-level content; only the third (homomorphism) is new.\n\n**What question does it answer?** \"Where does a homomorphism send a group?\" has a two-part answer: the **kernel** (what is collapsed) and the **image** (what survives), locked together by one isomorphism. So studying homomorphisms is studying normal subgroups — which is why group theory becomes the study of the subgroup lattice.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8f22f54f'
      },
    },

    /* -------------------------------------------------------- 距离空间 */
    'def.metric-space': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'A **metric space** is a pair (X, d): $X$ is a set and ' +
        '$d : X \\times X \\to [0, +\\infty )$ is a **metric** on $X$, satisfying\n\n' +
        '- **non-degeneracy**: $d(x, y) = 0 \\iff x = y$\n' +
        '- **symmetry**: $d(x, y) = d(y, x)$\n' +
        '- **triangle inequality**: $d(x, z) \\le d(x, y) + d(y, z)$\n\n' +
        'From $d$ one defines the **open balls** $B(x, r) = \\{ y \\in X : d(x, y) < r \\}$, and ' +
        'declares $U \\subseteq X$ to be open $\\iff U$ is a union of open balls. This puts a ' +
        'topology on $X$.\n\n' +
        'Given $\\varepsilon > 0$, a set $D \\subseteq X$ is called an **$\\varepsilon$-net** if ' +
        '$\\forall x \\in X, \\exists y \\in D : d(x, y) < \\varepsilon$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#31649b03', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'The open balls form a **base** for this topology. Every metric space is first ' +
        'countable and normal (hence Hausdorff).\n\n' +
        'The properties that follow fall into two classes: **compactness / closedness** depend ' +
        'only on the open sets and are **topological**; **completeness / total boundedness** ' +
        'use $d$ and are **metric**. This dividing line is the point of the whole cluster.\n\n' +
        '⚠ Completeness is **not** a topological property: $\\mathbb{R}$ and the open interval ' +
        '(0, 1) are homeomorphic, yet only the first is complete.' +
        '**Only one of the three axioms is "hard"** (they are usually listed side by side, but they carry very different weight):\n\n- **Non-degeneracy** $d(x,y)=0 \\iff x=y$: **read it as two halves.** "$x=y \\implies d(x,y)=0$" is nearly free (often built into how $d$ is given); "$d(x,y)=0 \\implies x=y$" is the half with content (weakening it gives a **pseudometric**).\n- **Symmetry**: **can be dropped** — what is left is a **quasi-metric** (e.g. a "one-way shortest path"); the topology is still definable, but balls are asymmetric (the ball for $d$ and the ball for the reversed quasi-metric differ).\n- **Triangle inequality**: **the only indispensable one.** Without it the open balls no longer form a base for a topology — the concrete failure is that an intersection stops containing a ball, which is precisely what the definition of "open" uses.\n\n⚠ **"$d$ takes values in $[0,+\\infty)$ rather than $[0,+\\infty]$"**: allowing $+\\infty$ wrecks the notion of a connected component (the discrete metric $d \\equiv +\\infty$ misbehaves), so the standard definition takes finite values; if a map between metric spaces needs $+\\infty$, say so explicitly.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8cd35552', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ======================================================================
       T3.2 批次 1（2026-10-16）：枢纽榜前 10 名里还没有译文的 8 个
       （前两名 lem.zorn / thm.first-iso 在试点里）。中英对照 + 存疑见
        i18n/review-batch-1.md。
       ====================================================================== */

    /* -------------------------------------------- 3. Cauchy 列与零列 */
    'def.cauchy-null': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $(x_n)$ be a **sequence of rational numbers** (that is, a function ' +
        '$\\omega \\to \\mathbb{Q}$), let $|x|$ be the absolute value on $\\mathbb{Q}$, and let ' +
        '$\\varepsilon$ always range over **positive rationals** (no real numbers are needed yet).\n\n' +
        '$(x_n)$ is a **Cauchy sequence**:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall m, n \\ge N : ' +
        '|x_m - x_n| < \\varepsilon$$\n\n' +
        '$(x_n)$ is a **null sequence**:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall n \\ge N : |x_n| < \\varepsilon$$\n\n' +
        'Two Cauchy sequences are **equivalent**:\n\n' +
        'Two Cauchy sequences are **equivalent** (written $(x_n) \\sim (y_n)$) if and only if ' +
        '$(x_n - y_n)$ is a null sequence.\n\n' +
        '($\\sim$ is an equivalence relation: reflexivity and symmetry are immediate, ' +
        'transitivity follows from the triangle inequality.)',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ba3632c', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '⭐ **Why divide by the null sequences**: the same real number has many approximating ' +
        'sequences — for instance $(1, 1, 1, \\ldots)$ and $(1.1,\\ 1.01,\\ 1.001,\\ \\ldots)$ ' +
        'should both represent $1$, and their difference is a null sequence. Equivalence classes ' +
        'glue "different approximations of one and the same number" into a single object.\n\n' +
        '⚠ Here we use **only the order and the absolute value of $\\mathbb{Q}$** ($\\varepsilon$ ' +
        'ranges over positive rationals, and the absolute value is the one on $\\mathbb{Q}$), so ' +
        'there is **no circularity**: this step makes perfect sense before $\\mathbb{R}$ is ' +
        'constructed. The language of "distance, Cauchy sequence, completeness" in metric spaces ' +
        'is exactly what was abstracted out of this construction.\n\n' +
        'Three facts needed later:\n' +
        '- a Cauchy sequence is **bounded**;\n' +
        '- **sum, difference and product of Cauchy sequences are again Cauchy**;\n' +
        '- **a Cauchy sequence that is not a null sequence satisfies $|x_n| \\ge \\delta$ from ' +
        'some index on** (for some positive rational $\\delta$) — hence its reciprocal, after ' +
        'discarding finitely many terms, is again Cauchy. The third fact is the entire basis ' +
        'for "multiplication has inverses".',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bf2a2534', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------ 4. 表示函子 */
    'def.representable': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ be a functor.\n\n' +
        '**(i) Universal element.** An object $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$ together with an element ' +
        '$s \\in F(X)$ is called **universal** for $F$ if\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall Y \\in \\mathcal{C},\\ \\forall t \\in F(Y),\\ \\exists! \\, f : X \\to Y,' +
        '\\qquad F(f)(s) = t$$\n\n' +
        'One then also says that $F$ is **represented** by $(X, s)$.\n\n' +
        '**(ii) Representable.** If $F \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, -)$, one says ' +
        'that $F$ is **represented** by $X$, and $X$ is called a **representing object** of $F$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#befbd619', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'In one sentence: **all the data of $F$ is generated by one element $s$ of $X$**. Given ' +
        'any other $t$, there is a unique $f$ pushing $s$ across to it — so elements elsewhere ' +
        'do not come from nowhere; they are pushed out of $s$.\n\n' +
        'An equivalent phrasing: $(X, s)$ is universal $\\iff$ $(X, s)$ is an initial object of ' +
        'the category of elements $\\operatorname{el}(F)$ — the word "universal" is used in the ' +
        'same way on this level and on the level below.\n\n' +
        'Examples:\n\n' +
        '- Let $k \\in \\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathbf{CRng})$ and let ' +
        '$F : k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Alg} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ be the forgetful functor. Then ' +
        '$(k[t],\\ t)$ represents $F$: for $A \\in k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Alg}$ and $a \\in F(A)$, ' +
        'sending $t \\mapsto a$ induces a unique $k$-algebra homomorphism $k[t] \\to A$.\n' +
        '- Let $F : \\mathbf{Ring} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ be the constant functor at the one-point ' +
        'set. Then $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents $F$: every ring has a unique $\\mathbb{Z} \\to R$.\n' +
        '- Fix a topological space $X$ and a subspace $Y \\subseteq X$, and take the ' +
        'contravariant functor\n\n' +
        '  $$F : \\mathbf{Top}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathbf{Set}, \\qquad Z \\mapsto ' +
        '\\{\\, f \\in \\mathcal{C}(Z, X) \\ \\mid\\ f(Z) \\subseteq Y \\,\\}$$\n\n' +
        '  (here $\\mathcal{C}(Z, X)$ denotes the set of continuous maps $Z \\to X$). ' +
        'It is represented by the natural element $i : Y \\rightarrowtail X$ of $F(Y)$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c93883dc', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------ 5. 加法 / 阿贝尔范畴 */
    'def.abelian-category': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'An **abelian category** is a **preabelian category** satisfying one of the following ' +
        '**equivalent** conditions (for preadditive, additive and preabelian categories see ' +
        '`def.pre-additive-category`, `def.additive-category`, `def.pre-abelian-category`):\n\n' +
        '1. every monomorphism is regular (dually, every epimorphism is regular);\n' +
        '2. if $i : N \\rightarrowtail M$ is a monomorphism, then ' +
        '$N \\cong \\ker\\bigl(M \\twoheadrightarrow \\operatorname{coker}(i)\\bigr)$ (and ' +
        'dually);\n' +
        '3. every morphism factors (uniquely up to isomorphism) as an epimorphism followed by a ' +
        'monomorphism;\n' +
        '4. for every $f : M \\to N$, ' +
        '$\\ker\\bigl(N \\to \\operatorname{coker} f\\bigr) \\cong ' +
        '\\operatorname{coker}\\bigl(\\ker f \\to M\\bigr)$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#70512d43', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'In an abelian category one says **injective / surjective** rather than monomorphism / ' +
        'epimorphism — because condition 1 guarantees that "regular" and "cancellable" are the ' +
        'same thing.\n\n' +
        'Examples: $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ is abelian; so is $\\mathbf{Ab}(\\mathcal{T})$ for a ' +
        'topos; so is $\\mathbf{AbCHaus}$. But $\\mathbf{AbTop}$ is **not** (the ' +
        'identity map $\\mathbb{Z}^{\\mathrm{disc}} \\to \\mathbb{Z}^{\\mathrm{coarse}}$ is not ' +
        'strict), and neither is $\\mathbf{AbHaus}$.\n\n' +
        'An abelian category is the **working environment** of homological algebra as a whole: ' +
        'with kernels and cokernels one can even state "exact sequence"; an additive category ' +
        'only supplies the additive skeleton (zero object and direct sums).' +
        '**Which of the four is the real axiom, and which are its consequences** (they are usually listed side by side, but condition 4 is the weakest and behaves most like an axiom, while the first three each cost something):\n\n- (1) $\\implies$ (2): **one kernel-plus-cokernel diagram chase.** $i : N \\rightarrowtail M$ identifies $N$ with $\\ker(M \\to \\operatorname{coker} i)$; this uses only the preabelian fact that a monomorphism is its own kernel — **no assumption beyond the additive structure**.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (1): **almost free in reverse** ($N$ being isomorphic to the kernel of a cokernel simply says $i$ is a kernel, i.e. regular).\n- (1) $\\iff$ (3): **this step needs kernels and cokernels** (i.e. preabelian). Factor $f = M \\to N$ as $M \\twoheadrightarrow \\operatorname{im} f \\rightarrowtail N$; "the epimorphism half is regular" is exactly (1). ⚠ Uniqueness holds **up to isomorphism** — the word matters: the factorisation is not strict, since one image can have different representatives.\n- (1) $\\iff$ (4): **the most "algebraic" one** (Abel\'s original form): $\\ker(N \\to \\operatorname{coker} f) \\cong \\operatorname{coker}(\\ker f \\to M)$. It is equivalent to "the first isomorphism theorem holds in the category", and needs **kernels and cokernels to exist** plus **regularity of mono/epi**.\n\n⚠ **Preabelian $\\ne$ abelian — the point**: preabelian asks only that kernels and cokernels exist; any one of (1)–(4) can **fail**. The standard counterexamples are $\\mathbf{PreAb}$ (preabelian groups) or filtered vector spaces, where an epimorphism need not be a cokernel and a monomorphism need not be a kernel. So **abelian = preabelian + (any one of the equivalent conditions)**, and that last clause cannot be dropped.\n\n⚠ **Two more traps**: in the definition of "additive category", "all direct sums" and "all finite products" agree only **given preadditivity** (in a general category finite products and finite coproducts need not coincide); and "all finite limits and colimits" is the phrasing attached to (2), which does **not** imply abelianness ($\\mathbf{Set}$ has finite limits and coproducts yet is far from abelian) — the difference lies in disjointness, universality and effectiveness.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e667995c', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------- 6. 乘积测度 */
    'def.product-measure': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$ and $(Y, \\mathcal{N}, \\nu )$ be measure spaces.\n\n' +
        '**① Rectangles**: sets of the form $A \\times B$ (with $A \\in \\mathcal{M}$, ' +
        '$B \\in \\mathcal{N}$) are called (measurable) rectangles. All rectangles **generate** ' +
        '$\\mathcal{M} \\otimes  \\mathcal{N}$.\n\n' +
        '**② Premeasure on rectangles**: let $\\mathcal{A}$ be the collection of all **finite ' +
        'disjoint unions of rectangles**. For\n\n' +
        '$$E = \\bigsqcup_{j=1}^{n} (A_j \\times B_j)$$\n\n' +
        'define\n\n' +
        '$$\\mu_{0} (E) := \\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\mu(A_j) \\nu(B_j)$$\n\n' +
        'Then $\\mu_{0}$ is a **premeasure** on the algebra $\\mathcal{A}$.\n\n' +
        '**③ Extension**: $\\mu_{0}$ induces an outer measure on $X \\times Y$, and restricting ' +
        'it to $\\mathcal{M} \\otimes  \\mathcal{N}$ gives a measure, written\n\n' +
        '$$\\mu \\times \\nu$$\n\n' +
        'that is, the **product measure**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#174abecd', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Well-definedness of $\\mu_{0} $ has to be checked (the same $E$ can be split into ' +
        'different disjoint unions of rectangles) — this is exactly why "rectangles" are ' +
        'replaced by "**disjoint** unions of rectangles": under disjoint unions, additivity is ' +
        'easy to verify.\n\n' +
        '**Everything goes through when the measures are $\\sigma$-finite**: if $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ ' +
        'are both $\\sigma$-finite, then $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is $\\sigma$-finite as well, and by ' +
        'Carathéodory uniqueness the measure constructed this way is unique.\n\n' +
        '⚠ The whole machine of this step (premeasure $\\to$ outer measure $\\to$ Carathéodory ' +
        'extension $\\to$ uniqueness) is exactly the off-the-shelf quartet from the ' +
        '"constructing measures" cluster.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#15d8cb14', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------- 7. 紧生成空间是余反射子范畴 */
    'prop.cg-coreflective': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'The category $k\\mathbf{Top}$ of compactly generated spaces is a **coreflective ' +
        'subcategory** of $\\mathbf{Top}$: the inclusion functor ' +
        '$i : k\\mathbf{Top} \\hookrightarrow \\mathbf{Top}$ has a **right** adjoint\n\n' +
        '$$k : \\mathbf{Top} \\longrightarrow k\\mathbf{Top}, \\qquad X \\mapsto kX$$\n\n' +
        'that is, $i \\dashv k$. The space $kX$ has the same underlying set as $X$, with the ' +
        'topology replaced by the $k$-topology.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0d2ec83c', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '**What has to be shown**: for every compactly generated space $X$ and every topological ' +
        'space $Y$,\n\n' +
        '$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Top}}(X,\\ Y) \\;\\cong\\; ' +
        '\\operatorname{Hom}_{k\\mathbf{Top}}\\bigl(X,\\ kY\\bigr).$$\n\n' +
        '**Proof.** The counit is $\\varepsilon_{Y} : kY \\to Y$ (the identity map, topology from ' +
        'finer to coarser, continuous).\n\n' +
        '- First, $kY$ really is compactly generated: compose every continuous map $S \\to Y$ ' +
        '($S$ compact Hausdorff) with $\\varepsilon_{Y}$ to lift it to $S \\to kY$, then use the ' +
        'definition of $kY$ (how $k$-open sets are taken) to verify that "the topology is ' +
        'determined by these maps".\n' +
        '- So whenever $f : X \\to Y$ is continuous (with $X$ compactly generated), ' +
        '$f = \\varepsilon_{Y} \\circ f\': X \\to kY$ yields a continuous map $f\'$ into $kY$: ' +
        'let $U \\subseteq kY$ be open; we must show that $f\'^{-1}(U)$ is open in $X$. Since ' +
        '$X$ is compactly generated, it suffices to check for every compact $L \\subseteq X$ ' +
        'that $f\'^{-1}(U) \\cap L$ is open in $L$. But $f|_{L} : L \\to Y$ is continuous and ' +
        '$L$ is compact, so $f(L)$ is compact; $U \\cap f(L)$ is open in $f(L)$, hence ' +
        '$(f|_{L})^{-1}\\bigl(U \\cap f(L)\\bigr) = f\'^{-1}(U) \\cap L$ is open in $L$. ∎\n' +
        '- The converse direction follows directly from continuity of $\\varepsilon_{Y}$. The ' +
        'two directions are mutually inverse, which is the isomorphism.\n\n' +
        '⭐ Keep this side by side with compact Hausdorff: $\\mathbf{CHaus}$ is a **reflective** ' +
        'subcategory of $\\mathbf{Top}$ (the inclusion has a **left** adjoint $\\beta$), whereas ' +
        '$k\\mathbf{Top}$ is **coreflective** (the inclusion has a **right** adjoint $k$). One ' +
        '"tightens" a space, the other "loosens" it — hence the former is closed under limits ' +
        'and the latter under **colimits**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b6c3c8a9', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------- 8. 拓扑斯上的阿贝尔群是 Grothendieck 范畴 */
    'thm.topos-abelian-grothendieck': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'If $\\mathcal{T}$ is a topos, then the category $\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{Ab})$ of abelian ' +
        'sheaves on $\\mathcal{T}$ is a **Grothendieck category**: it has a small set of ' +
        'generators, has all colimits, and filtered colimits are exact.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f13b85f8', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en: "One more remark: $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot X$ is a **generator** and is **flat**, but it is **not** projective — $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot X, -) \\cong \\Gamma(X, -)$ (global sections) is only left exact, so a nonsplit short exact sequence of sheaves $0 \\to \\mathcal{F} \\to \\mathcal{G} \\to \\mathcal{H} \\to 0$ shows that it is not right exact. Projectivity holds only in the presheaf category (where $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot h_{X}, -) \\cong (-)(X)$ is exact, see `prop.presheaf-free-projective`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d3472a73'
      },
    },

    /* --------------------------- 9. NBV 函数的导数与测度的关系 */
    'prop.nbv-derivative': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $F \\in NBV$. Then $F\' \\in L^1(m)$, and\n\n' +
        '$$\\mu_F \\perp  m \\iff F\' = 0\\quad  \\text{a.e.}$$\n' +
        '$$\\mu_F \\ll  m \\iff F(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{x} F\'(t) dt$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2fff7b16', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Proof: write $d\\mu_F = d\\lambda + f\\cdot dm$ (Lebesgue decomposition); by the ' +
        'corresponding theorem for measures, $f = F\'$ a.e. Hence\n\n' +
        '$\\cdot$ $\\mu_F \\perp  m$ means the absolutely continuous part vanishes, that is ' +
        '$f = 0 \\implies F\' = 0 \\text{a.e.}$;\n\n' +
        '$\\cdot$ $\\mu_F \\ll  m$ means $\\lambda = 0$, so ' +
        '$F(x) = \\mu_F((-\\infty,x]) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f\\cdot dm = \\int_{-\\infty}^x F\'\\cdot dt$.\n\n' +
        '⭐ This is the measure-theoretic version of the fundamental theorem of calculus: ' +
        '**whether a function can be integrated back depends on whether $\\mu _F$ is absolutely ' +
        'continuous**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e730d43c', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* --------------------------------------- 10. 环 → σ-环 的判据 */
    'thm.ring-monotone-sigma': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $\\mathfrak{A}$ be a **ring**. Then the following three conditions are equivalent:\n\n' +
        '1. $\\mathfrak{A}$ is a $\\sigma$-ring;\n' +
        '2. $\\mathfrak{A}$ is a monotone class;\n' +
        '3. $\\mathfrak{A}$ is closed under countable disjoint unions.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5d3aa74a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Note that the hypothesis "One more remark: $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot X$ is a **generator** and is **flat**, but it is **not** projective — $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot X, -) \\cong \\Gamma(X, -)$ (global sections) is only left exact, so a nonsplit short exact sequence of sheaves $0 \\to \\mathcal{F} \\to \\mathcal{G} \\to \\mathcal{H} \\to 0$ shows that it is not right exact. Projectivity holds only in the presheaf category (where $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot h_{X}, -) \\cong (-)(X)$ is exact, see `prop.presheaf-free-projective`)." cannot be dropped — a monotone ' +
        'class is in general not closed under finite unions, so being a monotone class by ' +
        'itself does not imply being a $\\sigma$-ring.\n\n' +
        'This theorem is the technical core of the monotone class theorem: it is precisely what ' +
        'lets one reduce "true for all $\\sigma$-algebras" to "first verify it on a ring, then ' +
        'pass to monotone limits".' +
        '**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (all three conditions are purely about set operations, but the ring hypothesis cannot be dropped):\n\n- (1) $\\implies$ (3): a countable disjoint union is a special case of a countable union — free.\n- (1) $\\implies$ (2): increasing unions are immediate from the definition; for decreasing intersections take the first term minus the successive differences as a countable union (the whole set need not belong to the family).\n- (2) $\\implies$ (1): the ring gives finite unions, and the monotone class keeps their increasing limit inside the family.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (1): set $B_n = A_n \\setminus \\bigcup_{j<n} A_j$; the ring guarantees every $B_n$ lies in the family, and closure under disjoint unions finishes.\n- The ring hypothesis cannot be dropped: on a two-point set, the family of the two singletons is a monotone class, yet it does not contain their union.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a3966daf', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ================================================ 批次 2a（枢纽榜第 11–20 名里的短条）
       ⚠️ 这几条**还没审**（status: 'draft'）—— 作者审完再翻，翻法见 tools/i18n-review.mjs。 */

    /* ------------------------------------------------ Hausdorff 极大原理 */
    'thm.hausdorff': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $(P, \\preceq )$ be a poset and $C_{0} \\subseteq P$ a chain. Then there is a ' +
        '**maximal chain** $M$ of $P$ with $C_{0} \\subseteq M$.\n\n' +
        'Here "maximal chain" means a chain that is maximal under inclusion $\\subseteq$: if ' +
        '$M \\subseteq M\' \\subseteq P$ and $M\'$ is again a chain, then $M\' = M$.\n\n' +
        'Taking $C_{0} = \\emptyset$ gives: **every non-empty poset has a maximal chain.**',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#317a7f66', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'In form it predates Zorn\'s lemma (Hausdorff, 1914), and it is equivalent to it.\n\n' +
        'The existence of a maximal chain is exactly the rigorous version of the intuition ' +
        '"keep adding elements from below until you can add no more".',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#53809b5a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ 截口 */
    'def.section': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $E \\subseteq X \\times Y$. For $x \\in X$ and $y \\in Y$ define\n\n' +
        '$$E_x := \\{y \\in Y : (x, y) \\in E\\}$$\n\n' +
        '$$E^y := \\{x \\in X : (x, y) \\in E\\}$$\n\n' +
        'called respectively the **vertical section** of $E$ at $x$ and the **horizontal ' +
        'section** at $y$.\n\n' +
        'Similarly, for a function $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb{C}$ define\n\n' +
        '$$f_x(y) := f^y(x) := f(x, y)$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ad479275', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Intuition: cut a plane figure with a vertical (or horizontal) line; the segment you ' +
        'cut out is the section.\n\n' +
        'Sections are the language of Fubini\'s theorem: the double integral ' +
        '$\\iint f d(\\mu \\times \\nu)$ is "integrate along the sections first, then integrate ' +
        'the resulting function of the other variable".',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8645e773', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* -------------------------------------------------------- 良序定理 */
    'thm.wellordering': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Every set can be given a well-ordering: for any set $X$ there is a well-ordering ' +
        'relation $\\preceq$ on $X$.\n\n' +
        'In other words: every set $X$ is equinumerous with some ordinal.\n\n' +
        'In particular, every set has a cardinality, and the real line admits a well-ordering ' +
        '(though this well-ordering cannot be written down explicitly).',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f0e20930', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Zermelo introduced the axiom of choice explicitly in 1904 in order to prove that ' +
        'every set can be well-ordered; this is where AC first appears.\n\n' +
        'The well-ordering theorem immediately yields the **comparability theorem for ' +
        'cardinals**: any two sets have comparable cardinalities.\n\n' +
        'It cannot be proved in ZF; but ZF together with it proves AC, so the two are ' +
        'equivalent.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bf8c5d7a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ---------------------------------------------------- 基数可比定理 */
    'thm.cardinal-comparable': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Under the **axiom of choice**: for any two sets $A$ and $B$ we have ' +
        '$\\#A \\le \\#B$ or $\\#B \\le \\#A$.\n\n' +
        'That is, any two cardinals are comparable.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ffeadee2', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '⭐ This is the licence to *order* cardinals: without it, even the sentence ' +
        '"$A$ is bigger than $B$" can be meaningless.\n\n' +
        'Route of the proof (see the edge): the **well-ordering theorem** well-orders both ' +
        '$A$ and $B$, the corresponding ordinals are comparable, and hence one direction ' +
        'admits an injection. So this theorem, AC and the well-ordering theorem are three ' +
        'ways of saying the same thing.\n\n' +
        '⚠ Without AC there really are incomparable cardinals (Jech, *The Axiom of Choice*).',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#90e06db8', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------ 全忠实与单位 */
    'prop.adjoint-full-faithful': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $F \\dashv G$ with unit $\\eta$ and counit $\\varepsilon$. Then $F$ is ' +
        '**fully faithful** $\\iff$ $\\eta$ is an isomorphism; $G$ is **fully faithful** ' +
        '$\\iff$ $\\varepsilon$ is an isomorphism.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#315d7da7', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'So "a left adjoint is fully faithful" reduces to checking that every component of a ' +
        'single natural transformation is invertible — far easier than checking Hom-sets ' +
        'pair by pair.\n\n' +
        'This is also the dividing line between **reflective** and **coreflective**: ' +
        'reflective means "the inclusion functor has a left adjoint", and an inclusion ' +
        'functor is always fully faithful (full subcategory), so for a reflection the counit ' +
        'is always an isomorphism.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9b807211', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ================================================ 批次 2b（枢纽榜第 11–20 名剩下的 5 条） */

    /* -------------------------------------------------------- 单调类定理 */
    'thm.monotone-class': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $\\mathcal{E} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$ with $\\mathcal{E} \\ne \\emptyset$. Then\n\n' +
        '$$\\mathcal{M}(\\mathcal{E}) = \\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E}) \\iff ' +
        '\\forall A \\in \\mathcal{E} : A^c \\in \\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E})\\ \\wedge\\ ' +
        '\\forall A, B \\in \\mathcal{E} : A \\cup B \\in \\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E})$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#48f20867', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '**This is the combined "standard + strengthened" version.**\n\n' +
        '**Standard version** (Halmos §6 / Folland 2.37): if $\\mathcal{A}$ is an **algebra** ' +
        'then $\\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{A}) = \\mathcal{M}(\\mathcal{A})$.\n\n' +
        'What is stated above is the **strengthened version**: "$\\mathcal{E}$ is an algebra" ' +
        'is replaced by two weaker conditions — the **complements** and the **unions** of ' +
        'sets in $\\mathcal{E}$ only have to land in $\\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E})$. The proof ' +
        'is still the usual three steps of the monotone class theorem:\n\n' +
        '**①** Let $\\mathcal{K}_{1} = \\{A \\in \\mathfrak{m} : A^{c} \\in \\mathfrak{m}\\}$. ' +
        'It contains $\\mathcal{E}$ (first condition), and is itself a monotone class ' +
        '($\\mathfrak{m}$ is monotone $\\implies$ limits stay in $\\mathfrak{m}$; complements ' +
        'use one decreasing limit) $\\implies \\mathcal{K}_{1} = \\mathfrak{m}$, i.e. ' +
        '$\\mathfrak{m}$ is closed under **complements**.\n\n' +
        '**②** Fix $A \\in \\mathcal{E}$ and let ' +
        '$\\mathcal{K}_{2} = \\{B \\in \\mathfrak{m} : A \\cup B \\in \\mathfrak{m}\\}$. By the ' +
        'second condition $\\mathcal{K}_{2} \\supseteq \\mathcal{E} \\implies ' +
        '\\mathcal{K}_{2} = \\mathfrak{m}$.\n\n' +
        '**③** Fix $B \\in \\mathfrak{m}$ and let ' +
        '$\\mathcal{K}_{3} = \\{A \\in \\mathfrak{m} : A \\cup B \\in \\mathfrak{m}\\}$. By ②, ' +
        '$\\mathcal{K}_{3} \\supseteq \\mathcal{E} \\implies \\mathcal{K}_{3} = \\mathfrak{m}$, ' +
        'i.e. $\\mathfrak{m}$ is closed under **unions**.\n\n' +
        'Hence $\\mathfrak{m}$ is closed under $\\cup$ and complements, and is a monotone ' +
        'class $\\implies$ $\\mathfrak{m}$ is a $\\sigma$-algebra $\\implies ' +
        '\\mathcal{M}(\\mathcal{E}) \\subseteq \\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E})$; the reverse ' +
        'inclusion always holds, so the two sides are equal. (One needs ' +
        '$\\mathcal{E} \\ne \\emptyset$, otherwise ' +
        '$\\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E}) = \\{\\emptyset \\}$.)\n\n' +
        '⚠ Replacing $\\cup$ by $\\cap$ or $\\setminus$ gives the same conclusion — once ' +
        'complements are available, the various closure properties imply each other, so the ' +
        'symbol used here does not affect whether the theorem is true.\n\n' +
        '**How it is used** (the same for either version): to prove that a property holds ' +
        'for all sets in $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathcal{E})$, it suffices to ($i$) prove it for ' +
        '$\\mathcal{E}$ and (ii) prove that "it holds" is preserved by increasing unions and ' +
        'decreasing intersections — then the sets for which it holds form a monotone class ' +
        'containing $\\mathcal{E}$, hence one containing ' +
        '$\\mathfrak{m}(\\mathcal{E}) = \\mathcal{M}(\\mathcal{E})$. Uniqueness of the measure ' +
        'extension, the section formula for product measures and the approximation of ' +
        'measurable functions all follow this pattern.\n\n' +
        '⚠ It is a sister result of "the criterion for a ring to be a $\\sigma$-ring": that ' +
        'one says ring $+$ monotone $\\implies$ $\\sigma$-ring, this one says algebra ' +
        '$\\implies$ monotone class and $\\sigma$-algebra coincide.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#42f670ae', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------- 截口可测 */
    'prop.section-measurable': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**(a)** If $E \\in \\mathcal{M} \\otimes  \\mathcal{N}$, then for every ' +
        '$x \\in X$ we have $E_x \\in \\mathcal{N}$, and for every $y \\in Y$ we have ' +
        '$E^y \\in \\mathcal{M}$.\n\n' +
        '**(b)** If $f$ is $\\mathcal{M} \\otimes  \\mathcal{N}$-measurable, then for every ' +
        '$x$ the function $f_x$ is $\\mathcal{N}$-measurable, and for every $y$ the function ' +
        '$f^y$ is $\\mathcal{M}$-measurable.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2db4532b', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'The proof is the standard pattern "rectangles first, then $\\sigma$-algebras". Let\n\n' +
        '$$\\mathcal{R} = \\{E \\subseteq X \\times Y : E_x \\in \\mathcal{N} \\ \\wedge\\ ' +
        'E^y \\in \\mathcal{M}\\quad \\forall(x, y)\\}$$\n\n' +
        'Then $\\mathcal{R}$ contains all rectangles, and **$\\mathcal{R}$ is a ' +
        '$\\sigma$-algebra** (the section operations preserve unions, intersections and ' +
        'complements). Hence $\\mathcal{R} \\supseteq \\mathcal{M} \\otimes \\mathcal{N}$.\n\n' +
        '(b) follows from (a) and two identities:\n\n' +
        '$$f_x^{-1}(B) = (f^{-1}(B))_x, \\quad  f^{y-1}(B) = (f^{-1}(B))^y$$\n\n' +
        '⚠ Note that (b) says "**every** $x$ works", whereas Fubini\'s theorem can only ' +
        'guarantee "almost every" — because Fubini also needs integrability.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bd705c8a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------ 商映射与局部紧空间作积 */
    'thm.quotient-product': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $q : X \\to Y$ be a **quotient map** and $Z$ a **locally compact Hausdorff** ' +
        'space. Then\n\n' +
        '$$q \\times \\mathrm{id}_{Z} : X \\times Z \\longrightarrow Y \\times Z$$\n\n' +
        'is again a quotient map.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aca983a4', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '⭐ **This is the engine behind "compactly generated spaces are closed under ' +
        'products"**, and it is where a search for "quotient map" or "locally compact" ' +
        'should land.\n\n' +
        '**How the proof goes (continuity of the inverse).** Write ' +
        '$W := (q \\times \\mathrm{id}_{Z})^{-1}(U)$ and show that $W$ is open. Take a point ' +
        '$(x, z) \\in W$ and put $y = q(x)$. Since $U$ is open in $Y \\times Z$, one can pick ' +
        'a basic open set\n\n' +
        '$$V \\times K \\;\\subseteq\\; U, \\qquad y \\in V,\\;\\; z \\in K,$$\n\n' +
        'where $V$ is open and $K$ is a **compact neighbourhood** of $z$ (a locally compact ' +
        'Hausdorff space has a base of compact neighbourhoods at every point, and $K$ may be ' +
        'required to lie inside any prescribed neighbourhood). Since $q$ is a quotient map, ' +
        '$q^{-1}(V)$ is open in $X$, so\n\n' +
        '$$q^{-1}(V) \\times K \\;\\subseteq\\; W$$\n\n' +
        'is an open neighbourhood of $(x, z)$ — hence $(x, z)$ is an interior point of $W$, ' +
        'so $W$ is open. ∎\n\n' +
        '⚠ **The locally compact hypothesis cannot be dropped.** In general "a quotient map ' +
        'times the identity" is not a quotient map: take $q$ to be some badly behaved ' +
        'quotient and $Z$ a space that is neither locally compact nor compactly generated; ' +
        'the product topology is then strictly finer than the topology the quotient space ' +
        'should have.\n\n' +
        'Terminology: some write this as "$Z$ locally compact Hausdorff $\\implies$ the ' +
        'functor $- \\times Z$ preserves quotient maps".',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#34fccb9f', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ 整数 ℤ */
    'def.int': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'On $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ define\n\n' +
        '$$(a, b) \\sim (c, d) :\\iff a + d = b + c$$\n\n' +
        '(intuition: $(a, b)$ stands for $a - b$; this equivalence relation says precisely ' +
        'that the differences are equal). It really is an equivalence relation. Put\n\n' +
        '$$\\mathbb{Z} := (\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N}) / \\sim$$\n\n' +
        'Its elements are written $[a, b]$ (that is, $a - b$). The **operations and order** ' +
        'on $\\mathbb{Z}$ (both must first be checked to be independent of the choice of ' +
        'representatives):\n\n' +
        '$$[a, b] + [c, d] := [a + c,\\ b + d]$$\n\n' +
        '$$[a, b] \\cdot [c, d] := [ac + bd,\\ ad + bc]$$\n\n' +
        '$$[a, b] \\le [c, d] :\\iff a + d \\le b + c$$\n\n' +
        'Here addition and order come from those of $\\mathbb{N}$, and the formula for ' +
        'multiplication is copied from $(a - b)(c - d) = ac + bd - (ad + bc)$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5c26a908', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '⭐ The motivation in one sentence: **subtraction does not work inside ' +
        '$\\mathbb{N}$** ($3 - 5$ does not exist), yet the *pair* "$3 - 5$" always makes ' +
        'sense — so one takes the difference itself as a new object.\n\n' +
        'By-product: $\\mathbb{Z}$ has an additive zero $[0, 0] = 0$ and additive inverses ' +
        '$-[a, b] = [b, a]$ — precisely what $\\mathbb{N}$ lacks.\n\n' +
        '⚠ Inside $\\mathbb{Z}$, the copy of $\\mathbb{N}$ is **embedded** via ' +
        '$a \\mapsto [a, 0]$ (injective, and preserving the operations and the order), so ' +
        'writing $3 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ uses this embedding — that step cannot be skipped.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe258a30', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* --------------------------------------------- Kan 延拓的两个例子 */
    'ex.kan-extension': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**(1)** A diagram $D : I \\to \\mathcal{C}$ has a colimit $X$ $\\iff$ the left Kan ' +
        'extension $p_{!}D : \\mathbf{1} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ along the projection ' +
        '$p : I \\to \\mathbf{1}$ exists, and its value is\n\n' +
        '$$(p_{!}D)(\\ast) = \\varinjlim_{i \\in I} D(i) = X$$\n\n' +
        '**(2)** A functor $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ has a right adjoint $G$ ' +
        '$\\iff$ the left Kan extension of the identity functor $1_{\\mathcal{C}}$ along ' +
        '$F$ exists, and then\n\n' +
        '$$G = F_{!}1_{\\mathcal{C}}, \\qquad \\operatorname{Hom}(F_{!}1_{\\mathcal{C}},\\ ' +
        'G) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}(1_{\\mathcal{C}},\\ G \\circ F)$$\n\n' +
        'The map $1_{\\mathcal{C}} \\implies G \\circ F$ corresponding to the right-hand ' +
        'side is exactly the **unit** $\\eta$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#15b421bb', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Read the two together: **colimits and right adjoints are special cases of Kan ' +
        'extensions**. The Kan extension is the unifying exit of this whole cluster — the ' +
        'definitions scattered earlier (limit, adjunction, reflection) each fall into place ' +
        'inside it.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#77203f35', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ================================================ 批次 3a（枢纽榜第 21–30 名，前四条） */

    /* -------------------------------------------------- 紧的三个等价刻画 */
    'thm.metric-compact-equiv': {
      statement: {
        en:
        "Let $X$ be a metric space with metric $d$. Then the following three conditions are **equivalent**:\n\n" +
        "- (a) $X$ is **compact**: every open cover has a finite subcover;\n" +
        "- (b) $X$ is **sequentially compact**: every sequence has a subsequence converging in $X$;\n" +
        "- (c) $X$ is **complete and totally bounded**.",
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c4151627', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The equivalence of compactness, sequential compactness, and completeness plus total boundedness is proved on the edge `imp.metric-compact-equiv-proof`: compact $\\implies$ sequentially compact uses the contradiction that a neighbourhood of every point meets only finitely many terms; sequentially compact $\\implies$ complete/totally bounded uses Cauchy subsequences and $\\varepsilon$-nets; totally bounded $\\implies$ compact uses Lebesgue numbers.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#302a8a85'
      },
    },

    /* -------------------------------------------------------- 伴随函子定理 */
    'thm.saft': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $\\mathcal{D}$ be a **small** and **complete** category. Then a functor ' +
        '$G : \\mathcal{D} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ has a left adjoint $\\iff$ $G$ preserves all ' +
        'limits.\n\n' +
        'The left adjoint is given by a limit construction:\n\n' +
        '$$F(X) \\;=\\; \\varprojlim_{(Y,\\, f) \\in (X \\downarrow G)} Y$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f6d84124', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Preservation of limits is the only obstruction.** Together with \"right adjoints preserve limits\" this is a complete criterion: preserving limits is necessary, and on complete small categories it is also sufficient.\n\nThe meaning of the construction: all ways of mapping $X$ into the image of $G$ form a category $X \\downarrow G$; a limit in it — $F(X)$ — is the object of $\\mathcal{D}$ \"closest\" to $X$. Every $X \\to G(Y)$ can be organised into $X \\to G(F(X)) \\to G(Y)$.\n\nThe verification is term by term from the universal property of representing objects.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#373b3299'
      },
    },

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------- 米田嵌入 */
    'prop.yoneda-embedding': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'The correspondence\n\n' +
        '$$\\delta : \\mathcal{C} \\longrightarrow \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}, \\qquad ' +
        'X \\mapsto h_{X} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(-, X)$$\n\n' +
        'is a **fully faithful** functor, and it **preserves all limits**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f759aee2', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Fully faithful means ' +
        '$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}}(h_{X}, h_{Y}) \\cong ' +
        '\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, Y)$ — the arrows among the $h_{X}$ reproduce ' +
        'the arrows among the $X$ exactly, no more and no less.\n\n' +
        'Once $\\mathcal{C}$ is embedded in $\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}$, questions inside ' +
        '$\\mathcal{C}$ can be solved in a category that **has all limits and colimits** — ' +
        'this is the standard way of "completing" a category.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#94b15f1c', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ 可缩族 */
    'def.shrinks-nicely': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'A family $\\{E_r\\}_{r > 0}$ of Borel subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is said to ' +
        '**shrink nicely to $x$** iff\n\n' +
        '$\\cdot$ $E_r \\subseteq B(x, r)$ holds for every $r$;\n' +
        '$\\cdot$ there is $\\alpha > 0$ with ' +
        '$m(E_r) > \\alpha\\cdot m(B(x, r))$ for every $r$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#def659f2', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'What the two conditions say: **it fits inside** ($E_r$ lies in the ball) and **it ' +
        'is not too flat** (its volume is at least $\\alpha$ times that of the ball).\n\n' +
        'Typical examples: take $E_r = B(x, r)$ itself ($\\alpha = 1$); or take $E_r$ to be ' +
        'a fixed shape inside the ball (a cube, say), rescaled proportionally.\n\n' +
        'Counterexample: let $E_r$ be a long thin sliver inside the ball — the volume ratio ' +
        'can tend to 0, and such a family does not shrink nicely to $x$.\n\n' +
        '⭐ The notion was introduced so that differentiation theorems **do not depend on ' +
        'the shape of the ball**: as long as it is not flat, the conclusion stands.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cd8d3dd5', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ================================================ 批次 3b（枢纽榜第 21–30 名，最后五条） */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ 外测度 */
    'def.outer-measure': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $X$ be a non-empty set. A function ' +
        '$\\mu^* : \\mathcal{P}(X) \\to [0, +\\infty]$ is an **outer measure** iff\n\n' +
        '- $\\mu^*(\\emptyset) = 0$\n' +
        '- **Monotone**: $A \\subseteq B \\implies \\mu^*(A) \\le \\mu^*(B)$\n' +
        '- **Countably subadditive**: ' +
        '$\\mu^*( \\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\infty} A_j ) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty} \\mu^*(A_j)$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#24d3359e', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'An outer measure is defined on **all** subsets ($\\mathcal{P}(X)$, not on some ' +
        '$\\sigma$-algebra); the price is that it only has **sub**additivity.\n\n' +
        'So an outer measure is not a measure — it is too large even to be additive on ' +
        '$\\mathcal{P}(X)$. Carathéodory\'s idea is to single out the sets that "behave ' +
        'well" (the next definition); on those the outer measure does become a genuine ' +
        'measure.\n\n' +
        'What Lebesgue first constructed was an outer measure: ' +
        '$\\mu^{*}(A) = \\inf\\{ \\sum (b_{n} - a_{n}) : A \\subseteq \\bigcup (a_{n}, b_{n}) \\}$.' +
        '**Which of the three conditions is "hard"** (the three are asymmetric — one is normalisation, one is almost free, one is a real condition):\n\n- $\\mu^{*}(\\emptyset) = 0$: **normalisation**, there only so that $0 \\le \\mu^{*}$ below is meaningful (otherwise the constant $+\\infty$ would satisfy the other two).\n- **Monotonicity**: **almost free**, but deriving it uses countable subadditivity: $B = A \\cup (B \\setminus A)$ $\\implies$ $\\mu^{*}(B) \\le \\mu^{*}(A) + \\mu^{*}(B\\setminus A)$; monotonicity is usually listed as an axiom to save that step, and in a concrete construction (Carathéodory) it is verified directly.\n- **Countable subadditivity**: **this is the real condition**, the only one with content — it ties "the size of one set" to "countable operations". ⚠ Note that it is an **inequality**: an outer measure is **not** additive (additivity on disjoint unions is what makes it a measure, and that is an extra conclusion on Carathéodory-measurable sets).\n\n**Two distinctions that must stay separate**:\n\n- **An outer measure is defined on $\\mathcal{P}(X)$** (all subsets), whereas a measure (`def.measure`) is defined only on a $\\sigma$-algebra — that is the whole point of an outer measure as a **construction device**: first put a crude "size" on all subsets, then single out the subsets on which it is additive.\n- **Monotone plus countably subadditive does not say "the value on a countable union is the limit"**: on increasing unions an outer measure only guarantees $\\mu^{*}(\\bigcup A_{n}) \\le \\lim \\mu^{*}(A_{n})$; equality needs continuity from below, hence measurability — exactly what the Carathéodory condition supplies.\n\n⚠ Notation: the star in $\\mu^{*}$ means "defined on $\\mathcal{P}(X)$", not "dual of something". Some books write $m^{*}$ or $\\nu^{*}$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#63a085ee', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------- ℝ 不可数 */
    'thm.real-uncountable': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**The real line $\\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable**: there is no surjection from ' +
        '$\\mathbb{N}$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$, that is, $\\mathbb{R}$ cannot be arranged as a ' +
        'sequence.\n\n' +
        'A stronger conclusion:\n\n' +
        '$$\\#\\mathbb{R} = \\#\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) = 2^{\\aleph_0} > \\aleph_0$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#75028b5a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '⭐ The proof goes by **nested closed intervals + diagonalisation** (see the edge): ' +
        'assume $\\mathbb{R}$ can be arranged as $x_1, x_2, \\ldots$, then bisect to build a ' +
        'nested sequence of closed intervals, ruling out the $x_n$ one by one, and use ' +
        '**completeness** to extract their unique common point — which differs from every ' +
        '$x_n$.\n\n' +
        '⚠ The whole proof uses **only the completeness of $\\mathbb{R}$** (the ' +
        'least-upper-bound principle); cardinal theory is not needed. The half saying ' +
        '$\\#\\mathbb{R} = 2^{\\aleph_0}$ matches binary expansions against ' +
        '$\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ and then quotes Cantor\'s theorem.\n\n' +
        '**The most counter-intuitive point of this theorem**: $\\mathbb{Q}$ and ' +
        '$\\mathbb{R}$ are both dense and each can be approximated by the other, yet ' +
        '$\\mathbb{Q}$ is countable (it can be arranged as a sequence) while ' +
        '$\\mathbb{R}$ is not. **"Dense" and "equally many" are two different things** — ' +
        'and this is precisely why analysis has to be done over the reals rather than over ' +
        'the rationals.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f1e86a60', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ---------------------------------------------------- 紧 Haus 是反射子范畴 */
    'prop.chaus-reflective': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '$\\mathbf{CHaus}$ is a **reflective subcategory** of $\\mathbf{Top}$; the ' +
        'reflection is called the **Stone–Čech compactification**:\n\n' +
        '$$\\beta : \\mathbf{Top} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{CHaus}, \\qquad ' +
        'X \\mapsto \\beta X$$\n\n' +
        'that is, for every $X \\in \\mathbf{Top}$ and every compact Hausdorff space $K$,\n\n' +
        '$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{CHaus}}\\bigl(\\beta X,\\ K\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; ' +
        '\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Top}}\\bigl(X,\\ K\\bigr)$$\n\n' +
        'and the inclusion $X \\to \\beta X$ is an isomorphism whenever $X$ itself is ' +
        'compact Hausdorff.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b01d3a4', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'Construction: send $X$ into the Tychonoff cube\n\n' +
        '$$e_{X} : X \\longrightarrow [0,1]^{C(X,[0,1])}, \\qquad x \\mapsto (f(x))_{f}$$\n\n' +
        'and then take the closure $\\beta X := \\overline{e_{X}(X)}$. The cube is compact ' +
        'Hausdorff, hence so is every closed subset of it.\n\n' +
        '⭐ **$e_{X}$ is injective $\\iff$ $X$ is a Tychonoff ($T_{3.5}$) space** — that ' +
        'is, there are enough continuous functions to separate distinct points. So for a ' +
        'general topological space, $\\beta X$ is the compactification obtained after ' +
        'collecting all the information that continuous functions can see.\n\n' +
        'Corollary: **any limit of compact Hausdorff spaces is again compact Hausdorff**. ' +
        'This is what the general statement "a reflective subcategory is closed under ' +
        'limits" looks like here — from now on, taking limits in $\\mathbf{CHaus}$ no ' +
        'longer requires re-checking compactness and Hausdorffness.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5e7e6161', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ 复测度 */
    'def.complex-measure': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'A **complex measure** on a measurable space $(X, \\mathcal{M})$ is a map\n\n' +
        '$$\\nu : \\mathcal{M} \\to \\mathbb{C}$$\n\n' +
        'such that\n\n' +
        '$$\\nu(\\emptyset) = 0, $$\n\n' +
        'and for pairwise disjoint $\\{E_j\\} \\subseteq \\mathcal{M}$:\n\n' +
        '$$\\nu( \\bigsqcup_j E_j ) = \\sum_j \\nu(E_j)$$\n\n' +
        '(the series on the right converges absolutely).',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bde1a964', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        '⚠ Compared with a signed measure, a complex measure is **automatically finite** ' +
        '(its values lie in $\\mathbb{C}$, no $\\pm \\infty$ is involved), and the series in ' +
        'countable additivity **converges absolutely automatically**.\n\n' +
        'So in the world of complex measures the headaches of "finiteness" and ' +
        '"rearrangement" disappear entirely — at the cost of no longer having examples ' +
        'with "infinite mass".',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e5f47458', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ------------------------------------------------------ 本性上界与 L^∞ */
    'def.essential-sup': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Definition\n\n' +
        '$$\\|f\\|_\\infty := \\inf \\{\\, a \\ge 0 : \\mu(\\{\\, |f| > a \\,\\}) = 0 \\,\\}$$\n\n' +
        '(After ignoring completely the values of $f$ on null sets, this is its "true" ' +
        'upper bound.) This infimum **is attained**: for $a = \\|f\\|_\\infty$ itself we ' +
        'have $\\mu(\\{|f| > a\\}) = 0$.\n\n' +
        'For a **measurable** function $f : X \\to \\mathbb{C}$ (measurability is defined ' +
        'at "measurable function") put\n\n' +
        '$$\nL^\\infty(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu) := \\{\\, f : X \\to \\mathbb{C} : ' +
        '\\|f\\|_\\infty < \\infty \\,\\}\n$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fd63d442', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
      notes: {
        en:
        'An equivalent form: $\\|f\\|_\\infty \\le M \\iff |f(x)| \\le M$ holds for a.e. ' +
        '$x$.\n\n' +
        'What the word "essential" means: **bad values on null sets simply do not count**. ' +
        'For instance, if $f = 0$ a.e. but $f$ takes the value $+\\infty$ on some null set, ' +
        'we still have $\\|f\\|_\\infty = 0$.\n\n' +
        '⚠ $\\|f\\|_\\infty$ differs from "$\\sup |f|$": the latter can become large ' +
        'because of a single point on a null set.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#667e877a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-10-16' },
      },
    },

    /* ================================================ 2026-11-01 · 微分几何补洞那 7 个新节点
       ⭐ 逐句对译中文（`src/data/packs/150-diffgeom-fiber.js`），**公式一字不改**：
          中文里是块级 $$…$$ 的，这里也必须块级，且 tex 逐字相同（构建有公式不变量硬断言）。
       ⚠️ status 一律 'draft'（我没有审校权）；basedOn 的指纹见 tools/i18n-status.mjs。
       📌 只译了 statement；notes 留待下一批。 */

    'def.local-trivialization': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it does**: this is the whole reason a bundle is called a bundle — locally it can ' +
        'always be flattened into a product. The clause "every point has a neighbourhood such ' +
        'that…" in the definitions of fiber bundles, vector bundles, principal bundles and ' +
        'covering spaces is exactly this statement.\n\n' +
        'Let $\\pi : E\\to B$ be a continuous surjection, $F$ a topological space and ' +
        '$U\\subseteq B$ an open set. A continuous map\n\n' +
        '$$\\Phi : \\pi^{-1}(U)\\longrightarrow U\\times F$$\n\n' +
        'is called a **local trivialization** of $\\pi$ over $U$ if $\\Phi$ is a homeomorphism ' +
        'and commutes with the projection:\n\n' +
        '$$\\operatorname{pr}_{1}\\circ\\Phi = \\pi .$$\n\n' +
        'Thus $\\Phi$ flattens the piece of the bundle over $U$ into the **trivial product** ' +
        '$U\\times F$: each fiber $E_{b} := \\pi^{-1}(b)$ is sent to $\\{b\\}\\times F$, and is ' +
        'therefore homeomorphic to $F$. If every point of $B$ has such an open neighbourhood ' +
        'with a local trivialization, $\\pi$ is called **locally trivial**.\n\n' +
        '**Smooth case**: when $E, B, F$ are smooth manifolds and $\\pi$ is smooth, one replaces ' +
        '"homeomorphism" by "**diffeomorphism**" in the definition; that is the local ' +
        'trivialization of a smooth bundle — without it even the tangent bundle would not be a ' +
        'smooth bundle.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0cf9dc1d', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.cotangent-bundle': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it does**: the tangent space carries "where can I go", the cotangent space carries ' +
        '"how do I measure". Stacking the dual spaces over all points gives the home of ' +
        '1-forms — a differential form is not a formula out of nowhere, it is a section of this ' +
        'bundle.\n\n' +
        'Let $M$ be a smooth $n$-manifold. The **cotangent space** at $p$ is the dual space\n\n' +
        '$$T_{p}^{\\ast}M := (T_{p}M)^{\\ast} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{R}}\\bigl(T_{p}M,\\mathbb{R}\\bigr),$$\n\n' +
        'whose elements are called **covectors**, or **1-forms at $p$** (also Pfaffian forms). ' +
        'The **cotangent bundle** stacks them over the base points:\n\n' +
        '$$T^{\\ast}M := \\bigsqcup_{p\\in M}T_{p}^{\\ast}M,$$\n\n' +
        'with projection $\\pi : T^{\\ast}M\\to M$ sending $\\alpha\\in T_{p}^{\\ast}M$ to ' +
        '$\\pi(\\alpha) := p$.\n\n' +
        '**Smooth structure**: on a chart $(U,\\varphi)$ the differentials ' +
        '$\\mathrm{d}x^{i}\\big|_{p}$ of the coordinate functions $x^{1},\\ldots,x^{n}$ form a ' +
        'basis of $T_{p}^{\\ast}M$ (dual to $\\partial/\\partial x^{i}\\big|_{p}$), so\n\n' +
        '$$\\Phi : \\pi^{-1}(U)\\longrightarrow\\varphi(U)\\times\\mathbb{R}^{n}$$\n\n' +
        '$$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{i}\\big|_{p}\\longmapsto\\bigl(\\varphi(p),\\,a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\bigr),$$\n\n' +
        'is a bijection. Taking it as a chart makes $T^{\\ast}M$ a smooth $2n$-manifold, and ' +
        '$\\pi$ a **smooth vector bundle of rank $n$** over $M$: the local trivializations are ' +
        'the maps $\\Phi$ above, and the vector-space structure on the fibers comes from the dual ' +
        'space.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b81febc', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.fiber-bundle': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it says**: a vector bundle only requires the fiber to be a vector space. Replacing ' +
        'the vector space by an **arbitrary manifold $F$** gives a fiber bundle — covering ' +
        'spaces, principal bundles and vector bundles all fall under this one definition.\n\n' +
        'Let $E, B, F$ be smooth manifolds and $\\pi : E\\to B$ a smooth surjection. One calls ' +
        '$\\pi$ (together with $E$) a **fiber bundle with fiber $F$ and base $B$** if every point ' +
        '$b\\in B$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ and a diffeomorphism\n\n' +
        '$$\\Phi : \\pi^{-1}(U)\\longrightarrow U\\times F,\\qquad \\operatorname{pr}_{1}\\circ\\Phi = \\pi ,$$\n\n' +
        'that is, $\\Phi$ commutes with the projection. Such a $\\Phi$ is called a **local ' +
        'trivialization**, $E_{b} := \\pi^{-1}(b)$ the **fiber** over $b$, and $E$ the **total ' +
        'space**.\n\n' +
        '**Transition functions**: two trivializations over the same open cover necessarily ' +
        'differ over their overlap by a fiberwise self-homeomorphism\n\n' +
        '$$\\Phi_{\\alpha}\\circ\\Phi_{\\beta}^{-1}\\,(b, y) = \\bigl(b,\\ g_{\\alpha\\beta}(b)\\,y\\bigr),$$\n\n' +
        'where $g_{\\alpha\\beta} : U_{\\alpha}\\cap U_{\\beta}\\to\\operatorname{Diff}(F)$ is the ' +
        '**transition function**. They satisfy the cocycle conditions ' +
        '$g_{\\alpha\\alpha} = \\operatorname{id}$ and ' +
        '$g_{\\alpha\\beta}\\,g_{\\beta\\gamma}\\,g_{\\gamma\\alpha} = \\operatorname{id}$ (on ' +
        'overlaps), and they measure how twisted the bundle is.\n\n' +
        'If a Lie group $G$ acts on $F$ and the trivializations can always be chosen so that all ' +
        '$g_{\\alpha\\beta}$ take values in $G$, one says the bundle has **structure group** $G$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#32b532b4', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.principal-bundle': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it says**: the most self-referential class of fiber bundles — the fiber is not an ' +
        'arbitrary manifold but the structure group $G$ itself, and $G$ acts freely on the bundle ' +
        'from the right. Every vector bundle can be built from a principal bundle (by taking an ' +
        'associated bundle).\n\n' +
        'Let $G$ be a Lie group, $P, B$ smooth manifolds and $\\pi : P\\to B$ smooth. A ' +
        '**principal $G$-bundle** over $P$ is the fiber bundle $\\pi$ together with a **right ' +
        'action** of $G$ on $P$\n\n' +
        '$$P\\times G\\longrightarrow P,\\qquad (u,g)\\longmapsto u\\cdot g ,$$\n\n' +
        'such that\n\n' +
        '1. **fibers are preserved**: $\\pi(u\\cdot g) = \\pi(u)$;\n' +
        '2. **the action is free and transitive on each fiber**: $u\\cdot g = u \\iff g = e$, and ' +
        'any two points of the same fiber $P_{b}$ differ by a right multiplication (so $P_{b}$ ' +
        'is homeomorphic to $G$);\n' +
        '3. **it is locally trivial and $G$-equivariant**: every point has a neighbourhood $U$ ' +
        'and a diffeomorphism $\\Phi : \\pi^{-1}(U)\\to U\\times G$, written ' +
        '$\\Phi(u) = \\bigl(\\pi(u),\\varphi(u)\\bigr)$, satisfying\n\n' +
        '$$\\Phi(u\\cdot g) = \\bigl(\\pi(u),\\ \\varphi(u)\\,g\\bigr).$$\n\n' +
        'The fiber is then $G$ (right multiplication $g\\mapsto hg$ carries it into itself), and ' +
        'so is the structure group $G$. The **frame bundle** gives the most used examples: for a ' +
        'vector bundle $E\\to B$ of rank $k$, the bundle of frames is a principal ' +
        '$\\operatorname{GL}(k,\\mathbb{R})$-bundle — this is the bridge between vector bundles ' +
        'and principal bundles.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2fbdf8a3', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.form-on-manifold': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it does**: it replaces "the $k$-form written in coordinates" by a **chart-free** ' +
        'description. Once that is done, a form really is an object on the manifold, and not ' +
        '"an expression inside some chart".\n\n' +
        'Let $M$ be a smooth $n$-manifold. A **differential $k$-form** $\\omega$ on $M$ assigns ' +
        'to each point $p\\in M$ an **alternating $k$-linear form**\n\n' +
        '$$\\omega_{p} : T_{p}M\\times\\cdots\\times T_{p}M\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{R},$$\n\n' +
        'and it **depends smoothly**: for every chart $(U,x)$ the function ' +
        '$p\\mapsto\\omega_{p}\\bigl(\\partial_{i_{1}}\\big|_{p},\\ldots,\\partial_{i_{k}}\\big|_{p}\\bigr)$ ' +
        'is smooth on $U$. The set of all $k$-forms on $M$ is denoted $\\Omega^{k}(M)$.\n\n' +
        '**Smoothness is equivalent to smooth coefficients**: on $(U,x)$ the form $\\omega$ is ' +
        'uniquely written as\n\n' +
        '$$\\omega\\big|_{U} = \\sum_{I}a_{I}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{I},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x^{I} := \\mathrm{d}x^{i_{1}}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{i_{k}},$$\n\n' +
        'where $I$ runs over all $i_{1}<\\cdots<i_{k}$ and the coefficients are given by ' +
        '$a_{I} := \\omega\\bigl(\\partial_{i_{1}},\\ldots,\\partial_{i_{k}}\\bigr)$. On the overlap ' +
        'of two charts the two sets of coefficients are converted into one another by the ' +
        '$k$-minors of the Jacobian matrix — and minors are smooth, so "the coefficients are ' +
        'smooth" does not depend on the chart chosen. This step is what makes the definition ' +
        'work at all: **it welds a local expression into a global object**.\n\n' +
        '**Conventions**: $\\Omega^{0}(M) := C^{\\infty}(M)$; $\\Omega^{1}(M)$ consists of the ' +
        '1-forms; and $\\Omega^{k}(M) = 0$ for $k > n$ (there is no nonzero alternating ' +
        '$k$-linear form on an $n$-dimensional space). The direct sum ' +
        '$\\Omega^{\\bullet}(M) = \\bigoplus_{k\\ge 0}\\Omega^{k}(M)$, with the wedge product, is ' +
        'a graded algebra.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5038a87a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'prop.exterior-derivative-manifold': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it does**: the operator $\\mathrm{d}$ on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is written by a ' +
        'coordinate formula; carried to a manifold it must answer one question — is ' +
        '$\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ computed in one chart the same object as in another? This ' +
        'proposition settles it, and then $\\mathrm{d}$ can be written globally on a manifold.\n\n' +
        'Let $M$ be a smooth manifold. There is a **unique** family of operators ' +
        '$\\mathrm{d} : \\Omega^{k}(M)\\to\\Omega^{k+1}(M)$ for $k\\ge 0$ such that\n\n' +
        '1. on functions $\\mathrm{d}f$ is the differential of $f$: ' +
        '$\\mathrm{d}f_{p}(v) = v(f)$ for $v\\in T_{p}M$;\n' +
        '2. $\\mathrm{d}$ is $\\mathbb{R}$-linear;\n' +
        '3. **anti-derivation**: for a $k$-form $\\omega$, ' +
        '$\\mathrm{d}(\\omega\\wedge\\tau) = \\mathrm{d}\\omega\\wedge\\tau + (-1)^{k}\\omega\\wedge\\mathrm{d}\\tau$;\n' +
        '4. $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$.\n\n' +
        'Moreover, in any chart $(U,x)$ it equals the coordinate formula\n\n' +
        '$$\\mathrm{d}\\Bigl(\\sum_{I}a_{I}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{I}\\Bigr) = \\sum_{I}\\mathrm{d}a_{I}\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{I},$$\n\n' +
        'where $\\mathrm{d}x^{I} := \\mathrm{d}x^{i_{1}}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{i_{k}}$ ' +
        'and $\\mathrm{d}a_{I}$ is the differential of the function $a_{I}$. Hence ' +
        '"$\\mathrm{d}$ does not depend on the chart" is not an extra assumption, but the ' +
        'conclusion of this proposition.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3a0efaf9', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'prop.pullback-form-manifold': {
      statement: {
        en:
        '**What it does**: it carries a $k$-form back along a smooth map **between manifolds**. ' +
        'The version for open subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is only its local appearance.\n\n' +
        'Let $F : M\\to N$ be a smooth map and $\\omega\\in\\Omega^{k}(N)$. The **pullback** ' +
        '$F^{\\ast}\\omega\\in\\Omega^{k}(M)$ is defined pointwise by\n\n' +
        '$$(F^{\\ast}\\omega)_{p}(v_{1},\\ldots,v_{k}) := \\omega_{F(p)}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}F_{p}v_{1},\\ldots,\\mathrm{d}F_{p}v_{k}\\bigr)$$\n\n' +
        'for $v_{i}\\in T_{p}M$. Then $F^{\\ast}\\omega$ is smooth, and\n\n' +
        '1. $F^{\\ast}$ is $\\mathbb{R}$-linear; on functions $F^{\\ast}f = f\\circ F$;\n' +
        '2. **it preserves the wedge product**: ' +
        '$F^{\\ast}(\\omega\\wedge\\eta) = F^{\\ast}\\omega\\wedge F^{\\ast}\\eta$;\n' +
        '3. **it commutes with $\\mathrm{d}$**: ' +
        '$\\mathrm{d}(F^{\\ast}\\omega) = F^{\\ast}(\\mathrm{d}\\omega)$;\n' +
        '4. **it preserves composition**: $(G\\circ F)^{\\ast} = F^{\\ast}\\circ G^{\\ast}$ and ' +
        '$(\\operatorname{id})^{\\ast} = \\operatorname{id}$.\n\n' +
        'Thus $F^{\\ast} : \\Omega^{\\bullet}(N)\\to\\Omega^{\\bullet}(M)$ is a homomorphism of ' +
        '**differential graded algebras**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c76f8521', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    /* ================================================ 2026-11-01 · ZFC 九条公理（图的入口层）
       ⭐ 逐句对译中文，公式**一字不改**；status 直接 'reviewed'
          （作者 2026-11-01 授权：「draft 都通过，以后不用给我审校，直接提交就好」）。
       📌 只译 statement；notes 留待下一批。 */

    'ax.ext': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'A set is determined uniquely by its elements:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall A \\forall B [ \\forall x ( x \\in A \\leftrightarrow  x \\in B ) \\to A = B ]$$\n\n' +
        'The converse direction ($A = B \\to$ the elements are exactly the same) holds automatically ' +
        'in first-order logic with equality, which is why the axiom is often written with ' +
        '$\\leftrightarrow$. It guarantees that describing a set by a condition determines it ' +
        'uniquely, so a notation such as $\\{ x \\in A : \\varphi (x) \\}$ is never ambiguous.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#db41f727', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.sep': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'For every formula $\\varphi (x, p)$ (in which $B$ does not occur), the following is an ' +
        'axiom:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall p \\forall A \\exists B \\forall x [ x \\in B \\leftrightarrow  ( x \\in A \\wedge  \\varphi(x, p) ) ]$$\n\n' +
        'By extensionality such a $B$ is unique, and one writes ' +
        '$B = \\{ x \\in A : \\varphi (x, p) \\}$. It is commonly called the "subset axiom".',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e310c0cf', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.pair': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Given any two sets, one may pack them into "the set whose only elements are these two":\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall a \\forall b \\exists A \\forall x [ x \\in A \\leftrightarrow  ( x = a \\vee  x = b ) ]$$\n\n' +
        'By extensionality $A$ is unique; it is written { a, b } (the unordered pair). Taking ' +
        '$a = b$ gives the singleton { a }.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9aad67c7', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.union': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'The elements of all members of a family of sets again form a set:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall F \\exists A \\forall x [ x \\in A \\leftrightarrow  \\exists Y ( Y \\in F \\wedge  x \\in Y ) ]$$\n\n' +
        'It is written $\\bigcup F$. Together with the pairing axiom it yields the binary union ' +
        '$a \\cup b = \\bigcup \\{ a, b \\}$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ae9c0db1', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.power': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'The subsets of a set form a set:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall A \\exists P \\forall x [ x \\in P \\leftrightarrow  x \\subseteq A ]$$\n\n' +
        'It is written $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ (or P(A)). It turns "all subsets of $A$" itself into a set ' +
        '— the power set is the basic device for building larger sets.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9417a12a', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.inf': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'There exists an "inductive set" — a set containing $\\emptyset$ and closed under the ' +
        'successor operation:\n\n' +
        '$$\\exists I [ \\emptyset \\in I \\wedge  \\forall x ( x \\in I \\to x \\cup \\{ x \\} \\in I ) ]$$\n\n' +
        'The clause $\\emptyset \\in I$ **cannot be dropped**: $I = \\omega \\setminus \\{0\\}$ is non-empty ' +
        'and closed under successor ($x \\ne 0 \\implies S(x) \\ne 0$), yet it does not contain ' +
        '$\\emptyset$ — "non-empty + closed under successor" does not force $\\emptyset \\in I$. ' +
        '($\\emptyset$ is a primitive object, see "the empty set"). This axiom asserts ' +
        'the existence of an infinite set, and is the only source ' +
        'from which the set of natural numbers $\\omega$ is built.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ccdf2115', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.repl': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'If a formula defines a "functional relation", the image of any set is again a set. For ' +
        'every formula $\\varphi (x, y, p)$ (in which $B$ does not occur):\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall p [ \\forall x\\forall y\\forall z ( \\varphi(x,y,p) \\wedge  \\varphi(x,z,p) \\to y = z ) \\to \\forall A \\exists B \\forall y ( y \\in B \\leftrightarrow  \\exists x \\in A, \\varphi(x,y,p) ) ]$$\n\n' +
        'It guarantees that "replacement" never leaves the set-theoretic universe: the image $B$ ' +
        'is a set too.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0835fe0f', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.found': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Every non-empty set has an "$\\in$-minimal" element:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall A [ A \\ne \\emptyset \\to \\exists x ( x \\in A \\wedge  x \\cap A = \\emptyset ) ]$$\n\n' +
        'An equivalent form: $\\in$ is a well-founded relation, so there is no infinite descending ' +
        'chain\n\n' +
        '$$x_0 \\ni  x_1 \\ni  x_2 \\ni  \\cdots$$',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ccfc3b6b', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'ax.choice': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'From any family of non-empty sets one can pick one element from each, "all at once". ' +
        'Written in terms of a choice function:\n\n' +
        '$\\operatorname{dom}$ is defined only for functions, so the assertion says: "there is a function $f$ with $\\operatorname{dom} f = F$, and $f(X) \\in X$ for every $X \\in F$\":\n\n' +
        '$\\forall F [ ( \\forall X \\in F, X \\ne \\emptyset ) \\to \\exists f ( \\operatorname{dom} f = F \\wedge  \\forall X \\in F, f(X) \\in X ) ]$\n' +
        'Another common form (pairwise disjoint form): if $F$ is a family of pairwise disjoint ' +
        'non-empty sets, then there is a set $C$ with $\\#(C \\cap X) = 1$ for every $X \\in F$.\n\n' +
        'The choice function $f$ is the device for "picking all at once"; AC asserts that such a ' +
        'simultaneous choice is always possible.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#96318197', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },


    /* ================================================ 2026-11-01 · 入口层：子集 / 有序对 / 关系 / 映射 / 函数
       ⭐ 逐句对译；公式一字不改。status 直接 'reviewed'（作者 2026-11-01 授权）。 */

    'def.subset': {
      statement: {
        en: `Let $A$ and $B$ be sets.

- **$A$ is a subset of $B$** (written $A \\subseteq B$): $\\forall x ( x \\in A \\to x \\in B )$
- **$A$ is a proper subset of $B$** (written $A \\subset B$): $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\ne B$.

Basic properties: $\\subseteq$ is reflexive and transitive; and by the axiom of extensionality, two sets are equal exactly when they contain each other:

$$A = B \\iff A \\subseteq B \\wedge  B \\subseteq A$$

This is the most common way to prove two sets equal: **containment both ways**.`,
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6fb94ac5', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.pair': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Kuratowski\'s definition:\n\n' +
        '$$(a, b) := \\{ \\{a\\}, \\{a, b\\} \\}$$\n\n' +
        'The pairing and power-set axioms guarantee that this is a set.\n\n' +
        '**Characteristic property** (this is all that "ordered" means):\n\n' +
        '$$(a, b) = (c, d) \\iff a = c \\wedge  b = d$$\n\n' +
        'An $n$-tuple is defined recursively: ' +
        '$(a_{1}$ …$a_{n}) = ( (a_{1}$ …$a_{n-1}), a_{n} )$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a830b9b2', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.rel': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $A$ and $B$ be sets.\n\n' +
        'A **relation** $R$ consists of ordered pairs; $R \\subseteq A \\times B$ means that $R$ ' +
        'is a relation from $A$ to $B$.\n\n' +
        '- **domain** $\\operatorname{dom} R = \\{ a : \\exists b (a, b) \\in R \\}$;\n' +
        '- **range** $\\operatorname{ran} R = \\{ b : \\exists a (a, b) \\in R \\}$;\n' +
        '- **inverse** $R^{-1} = \\{ (b, a) : (a, b) \\in R \\}$.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ebf411a5', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.function': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $f$ be a relation from $A$ to $B$. Then $f$ is a **map** (written $f : A \\to B$) ' +
        'if and only if every input has exactly one output:\n\n' +
        '$$\\forall a \\in A, \\exists! b \\in B, (a, b) \\in f$$\n\n' +
        'Uniqueness is what makes the notation $f(a) = b$ unambiguous; in that case ' +
        '$\\operatorname{dom} f = A$, and the **image** of $f$ is ' +
        '$f(A) = \\{ f(a) : a \\in A \\}$.\n\n' +
        'Injective / surjective / bijective are separate nodes; a map whose values lie in a set ' +
        'of numbers has its own name, a **function**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2c875827', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'def.number-function': {
      statement: {
        en:
        'Let $X$ be a set and $f : X \\to Y$ a **map**. If its values lie in a **set of numbers** — that is, ' +
        '$Y \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ or $Y \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}$ — then $f$ is called a ' +
        '**function**.',
        status: 'reviewed', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0f3927eb', reviewed: { by: 'bingyingzizz', date: '2026-11-01' },
      },
    },

    'thm.noself': {
      statement: {
        en: "$$\\forall A ( A \\notin A )$$\n\nMore generally, the Axiom of Foundation rules out every finite $\\in$-cycle:\n\n$$A_0 \\ni  A_1 \\ni  \\cdots \\ni  A_n = A_0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#add1d770',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This theorem shows that the set $R = \\{ x : x \\notin x \\}$ from Russell's paradox cannot exist — otherwise $R \\in R$ and $R \\notin R$ would both hold.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a5d74868',
      },
    },

    'thm.empty': {
      statement: {
        en: "There exists a set with no elements:\n\n$$\\exists B \\forall x ( x \\notin B )$$\n\nBy the Axiom of Extensionality such a $B$ is unique; it is written $\\emptyset$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2debfc32',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$\\emptyset$ is the least element for $\\in$ and also for $\\subseteq$: $\\emptyset \\subseteq A$ holds for every $A$.\n\nThe point of this theorem is that **$\\emptyset$ need not be taken as a primitive object**: the Axiom Scheme of Separation already produces it (from any set $A$, take $\\{x \\in A : x \\ne x\\}$). So in ZF the empty set is not an extra assumption.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#acd90126',
      },
    },

    'thm.product': {
      statement: {
        en: "Given any two sets, the collection of all ordered pairs is again a set:\n\n$$\\forall A \\forall B \\exists C \\forall z [ z \\in C \\leftrightarrow  \\exists a \\in A, \\exists b \\in B, z = (a, b) ]$$\n\nIt is written $A \\times B = \\{ (a, b) : a \\in A, b \\in B \\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f405d7a0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "It is the prerequisite for defining relations and functions as sets: without $A \\times B$ there is no such thing as $R \\subseteq A \\times B$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a6658787',
      },
    },

    'def.empty': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **empty set** is the set with no elements, written $\\emptyset$:\n\n$$\\emptyset := \\{ x : x \\ne x \\}$$\n\n\"$x \\ne x$\" fails for every $x$, so this set has no elements at all. It is a **primitive object of the language**, on the same level as the Axiom of Extensionality and first-order logic.\n\nHence for every set $A$: $\\emptyset \\subseteq A$ (vacuously true), and $\\emptyset$ is the least element under $\\subseteq$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e1460b5c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Conditions such as \"$\\emptyset \\in \\mathcal{A}$\" and \"$\\emptyset \\notin F$\" are everywhere in measure theory (the Axiom of Choice requires the family to contain no empty set; $\\mu(\\emptyset) = 0$ is the first axiom of a measure).\n\nThe Axiom of Extensionality supplies **at most** one such set;\n\nthe half that says one **exists** is a separate result — see \"the empty set exists\".\n\nDistinguish $\\emptyset$ from $\\{\\emptyset\\}$: the latter has one element (namely the empty set), so $\\{\\emptyset\\} \\ne \\emptyset$.\n\n\n$\\emptyset \\subseteq A$ is **vacuously true**: there is no $x \\in \\emptyset$ to check.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9a3aa702',
      },
    },

    'def.power-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a set. The **power set** of $X$ is the set whose elements are all subsets of $X$:\n\n$$\\mathcal{P}(X) := \\{ A : A \\subseteq X \\}$$\n\nThus $A \\in \\mathcal{P}(X) \\iff A \\subseteq X$, and every element of $\\mathcal{P}(X)$ is itself a set.\n\nBy the **Axiom of Power Set**, $\\mathcal{P}(X)$ is indeed a set; by Extensionality it is unique.\n\nIn the finite case the number of elements is given by $\\#X = n \\implies \\#\\mathcal{P}(X) = 2^{n}$ (each element is either taken or not).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#46639831',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The power set is the step that **turns a property into an object**: to speak about \"all subsets of $X$\" as a thing in itself (rather than one subset at a time) one needs the power set.\n\n$\\mathcal{P}(X)$ is also the stage for several important constructions:\n- Outer measures are defined on **all subsets**: $\\mu^* : \\mathcal{P}(X) \\to [0, +\\infty]$;\n\n\n- Rings, $\\sigma$-algebras and monotone classes are each a family of subsets of $X$, i.e. a subfamily of $\\mathcal{P}(X)$;\n\n\n- The generated $\\sigma$-algebra is the intersection of all $\\sigma$-algebras containing $\\mathcal{E}$, and $\\mathcal{P}(X)$ guarantees that this intersection is non-empty.\n\n$\\emptyset \\in \\mathcal{P}(X)$ and $X \\in \\mathcal{P}(X)$ for every $X$ — a power set is never empty.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e4c969dd',
      },
    },

    'def.ordinal': {
      statement: {
        en: "A set $\\alpha$ is an **ordinal** (a von Neumann ordinal) iff:\n\n1. **$\\alpha$ is transitive**: $\\forall x \\in \\alpha,\\; x \\subseteq \\alpha$ (an element of an element is again an element);\n2. **$\\in$ well-orders $\\alpha$**: restricted to $\\alpha$, $\\in$ makes $\\alpha$ a **well-ordered set** — that is, for any $x, y \\in \\alpha$ exactly one of $x \\in y$, $x = y$, $y \\in x$ holds, and every non-empty subset of $\\alpha$ has an $\\in$-least element.\n\nBy transitivity, **every element of $\\alpha$ is itself an ordinal**. Conventions: $0 := \\emptyset$, the **successor ordinal** $\\alpha + 1 := \\alpha \\cup \\{ \\alpha \\}$; a non-zero ordinal with no greatest element is a **limit ordinal**, and $\\omega$ is the least limit ordinal. The class of all ordinals is written $\\mathrm{On}$.\n\nThe first few are $\\emptyset,\\ \\{ \\emptyset \\},\\ \\{ \\emptyset, \\{ \\emptyset \\} \\},\\ \\ldots$, that is $0, 1, 2, \\ldots$;\n\nthen come $\\omega,\\ \\omega + 1,\\ \\omega + 2,\\ \\ldots,\\ \\omega + \\omega,\\ \\ldots$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#42c21521',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The format is still \"set + relation + axioms\"**: ordinals are not objects out of nowhere — a **transitive set** together with the **ready-made** relation $\\in$ determines an ordinal. To decide whether a set is an ordinal, check these two clauses.\n\n**Why not take order types as the objects** — this is the most important step in this pack: an order type is an isomorphism class, and there are more order types of well-orders than fit into any set, so they cannot be collected (see \"the ordinals do not form a set\"). Ordinals avoid this: each order type is given a **concrete representative**, namely a transitive well-ordered set. Comparing order types then becomes comparing $\\in$, which can be done outright.\n\n**$\\alpha \\in \\beta$ and $\\alpha < \\beta$ are the same thing**: on ordinals, $\\in$ is the order. Writing $\\alpha < \\beta$ really uses $\\in$ underneath.\n\nOrdinals are not just \"a generalization of $\\mathbb{N}$\". The ordinals after $\\omega$ get longer and longer, but their **size** (cardinality) can stay at $\\aleph_0$ — for ordinals, \"longer\" and \"larger\" are two different things.\n\n\n\n**Which assumptions each direction uses** (the definition of an ordinal needs no Choice; the existence of a representative for each order type is a separate construction):\n- being a von Neumann ordinal $\\iff$ transitive and well-ordered by membership: both directions are unfolding the definition, with no compactness, no Choice and no metric; the convention $\\alpha<\\beta\\iff\\alpha\\in\\beta$ is also a notational definition.\n- elements of an ordinal are ordinals: inherit the well-order and use transitivity of membership on the ambient set to show the element is transitive; a general transitive set does not by itself have transitive elements.\n- well-ordered set $\\implies$ existence of a unique ordinal of the same order type: build it by well-founded recursion and collect the image with Replacement; this is not an unfolding of the definition, and it does not need the Choice that well-orders an arbitrary set.\n- transitivity cannot be dropped: the members of $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ are well-ordered by membership as a single point, yet the set is not transitive; the well-order condition cannot be dropped either: $\\{0,1,\\{1\\}\\}$ is transitive, but $0$ and $\\{1\\}$ are incomparable under membership.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b7e8d4cc',
      },
    },

    'thm.ordinal-trichotomy': {
      statement: {
        en: "For any two ordinals $\\alpha, \\beta$, **exactly one** of the following holds:\n\n$$\\alpha \\in \\beta, \\qquad \\alpha = \\beta, \\qquad \\beta \\in \\alpha$$\n\nIn particular, $\\in$ is a **well-order** on the class of all ordinals: every non-empty family of ordinals has a least element.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#191c1080',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Trichotomy lets ordinals serve as **coordinates**: the order types of any two **well-ordered sets** are comparable, and **no Choice is needed**.\n\nThis is a completely different statement from \"any two **cardinals** are comparable\", which does need AC (see \"the comparability theorem for cardinals\"). The difference is that an ordinal **carries** a well-order — namely $\\in$ — whereas a general set has no a priori well-order, and finding one for it is exactly the content of the well-ordering theorem ($\\iff$ the Axiom of Choice).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4396eafd',
      },
    },

    'thm.burali-forti': {
      statement: {
        en: "The class $\\mathrm{On}$ of all ordinals **is not a set**: there is no set whose elements are exactly all the ordinals.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e28f5e4e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is one line: if $\\mathrm{On}$ were a set, it would be **transitive** (elements of ordinals are ordinals) and $\\in$ would **well-order** it (trichotomy gives comparability), so $\\mathrm{On}$ would itself be an ordinal, hence $\\mathrm{On} \\in \\mathrm{On}$ — contradicting the fact that no ordinal is an element of itself ($\\in$ is a well-order on ordinals, which forbids $\\in$-cycles).\n\nThis is exactly the other face of **Hartogs' theorem**: there are too many ordinals to fit into any set. \"There is an ordinal that does not inject into $X$\" and \"the ordinals do not form a set\" say the same thing.\n\nRelation to Russell's paradox: both contradictions come from treating a certain \"totality\" as a set. In ZFC they are resolved the same way — that totality is not a set, so the Axiom Scheme of Separation cannot even be applied to it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cf126a32',
      },
    },

    'def.equinumerous': {
      statement: {
        en: "Sets $A$ and $B$ are **equinumerous** (written $\\#A = \\#B$ or $A \\approx B$) iff there is a **bijection**\n\n$$f : A \\to B$$\n\nEquinumerosity is an equivalence relation on sets: reflexive (identity map), symmetric (inverse map), transitive (composition).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8c01f4ed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the **right way to compare sizes**: instead of counting (an infinite set cannot be counted out), ask whether the two sets can be paired off one-to-one.\n\nClassical results:\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is equinumerous with $\\mathbb{Q}$ and with $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ (both are countable);\n\n\n- $(0, 1)$ is equinumerous with $\\mathbb{R}$ (via something like $x \\mapsto \\tan \\pi(x - 1/2)$);\n\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ are **not** equinumerous (Cantor's theorem).\n\n$A \\subseteq B$ has nothing to do with equinumerosity: $\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Q}$ yet the two are equinumerous — this is precisely the dividing line between infinite and finite sets (Dedekind's characterization: equinumerous with a proper subset $\\iff$ infinite).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e9fe1f5a',
      },
    },

    'thm.cantor': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every set $A$:\n\n$$\\#A < \\#\\mathcal{P}(A)$$\n\nThat is, there is **no** surjection from $A$ onto $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ (and hence no bijection either), while $a \\mapsto \\{a\\}$ is an injection.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#195c290e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Corollary: **there is no largest cardinal** — from any set, taking the power set gives a larger one.\n\nHence\n\n$\\aleph_0 < 2^{\\aleph_0} < 2^{2^{\\aleph_0}} < \\cdots$\n\nTaking $A = \\mathbb{N}$ gives \"$\\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable\" ($\\#\\mathbb{R} = 2^{\\aleph_0}$).\n\nThe proof uses the **diagonal method** (see the proof on the edge): the \"diagonal set\" $D = \\{ a \\in A : a \\notin f(a) \\}$ is not in the image of $f$.\n\nThis is the most theorem-producing construction in the history of mathematics; Cantor–Bernstein, Gödel's incompleteness theorem and the halting problem all use it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c411105f',
      },
    },

    'thm.schroder-bernstein': {
      statement: {
        en: "If there are injections $f : A \\to B$ and $g : B \\to A$, then there is a **bijection** $A \\to B$; that is,\n\n$$\\#A \\le \\#B \\ \\wedge \\ \\#B \\le \\#A \\implies \\#A = \\#B$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c2d4190e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Its value is **convenience**: to prove that two sets are equinumerous it suffices to build one injection in each direction; the bijection itself need not be written down. (Compare the other route to Cantor's theorem — an injection but no surjection — which is the dual statement.)\n\nIt **does not need** the Axiom of Choice: the proof is constructive (see the proof on the edge), which is what makes it more usable than \"any two cardinals are comparable\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2c2323bf',
      },
    },

    'def.choicefn': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F$ be a family of sets with $\\emptyset \\notin F$.\n\nA function $f$ is a **choice function** for $F$ iff **both** of the following hold:\n\n(i) $f$ is a function and $\\operatorname{dom} f = F$;\n(ii) $\\forall X \\in F$, $f(X) \\in X$.\n\nThat is, $f$ picks one element from each member of $F$.\n\nThe Axiom of Choice can therefore be stated concisely as: **every family $F$ with $\\emptyset \\notin F$ has a choice function**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f5549253',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "If $F$ is finite, one can pick the elements one by one, and no AC is needed. The difficulty lies only with infinite families, especially uncountable ones.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6d9a6efe',
      },
    },

    'lem.tukey': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{A}$ be a **non-empty family of sets with finite character**. Then $(\\mathcal{A}, \\subseteq )$ has a maximal element.\n\nThat is, there is $A \\in \\mathcal{A}$ such that no $B \\in \\mathcal{A}$ satisfies $A \\subset B$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0fcde180',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The advantage of Tukey's lemma is that it **saves verification**: as soon as the family in question has finite character, the tedious check that \"every chain has an upper bound\" can be skipped.\n\nExample: the family of linearly independent sets has finite character ($X$ is linearly independent $\\iff$ every finite subset of $X$ is linearly independent), so \"every linearly independent set extends to a maximal one\" holds automatically.\n\nHistory: Teichmüller (1939) and Tukey (1940) stated it independently.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#89317279',
      },
    },

    'thm.compactness': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\Gamma$ be a set of first-order formulas. If **every finite subset** of $\\Gamma$ has a model, then $\\Gamma$ itself has a model.\n\nEquivalently: if $\\Gamma \\vDash \\varphi$, then there is a **finite** $\\Gamma_{0} \\subseteq \\Gamma$ with $\\Gamma_{0} \\vDash \\varphi$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#39f5f6d3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "It is a **one-sentence corollary**: by the completeness theorem, having a model $\\iff$ being consistent; and inconsistency is witnessed by a **finite** proof, which uses only finitely many formulas of $\\Gamma$. So inconsistency is always finitely visible, and taking the contrapositive gives compactness.\n\n**Second proof** (avoiding completeness): use ultraproducts / Łoś's theorem — take the directed family of finite subsets as the index set, choose a model of each finite subset, and the ultraproduct is a model of the whole theory. Ultraproducts are not developed here, so this is only a remark.\n\nIt is **the reason first-order logic cannot express finiteness**: let $\\Gamma$ consist of infinitely many sentences saying \"field + characteristic 0 + finite\". Every finite subset is satisfiable in some finite field, so the whole of $\\Gamma$ has a model — an **infinite** \"finite field\". Likewise the standard model of the natural numbers cannot be characterised by first-order axioms: non-standard analysis is a direct product of this theorem.\n\n**The resemblance to topological compactness (`def.compact`) is only in the name**: that one says \"every open cover has a finite subcover\", this one says \"finitely satisfiable $\\implies$ satisfiable\". The two are connected only by an **analogy edge**, not by a dependence of theorems.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#86ad03b3',
      },
    },

    'def.topology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a set and $\\mathcal{T} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$ a family of subsets of $X$. Then $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ is a **topological space** and $\\mathcal{T}$ is a **topology** on $X$ iff:\n\n1. **Both ends**: $\\emptyset \\in \\mathcal{T}$ and $X \\in \\mathcal{T}$;\n2. **Closed under arbitrary unions**: $\\{ U_i \\}_{i \\in I} \\subseteq \\mathcal{T} \\implies \\bigcup_{i \\in I} U_i \\in \\mathcal{T}$ ($I$ is arbitrary, possibly uncountable);\n3. **Closed under finite intersections**: $U, V \\in \\mathcal{T} \\implies U \\cap V \\in \\mathcal{T}$.\n\nThe members of $\\mathcal{T}$ are called **open sets**, and their complements **closed sets**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b6563e95',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The three axioms abstract the properties of open intervals on the real line: a union of arbitrarily many open sets is open, but only **finitely** many open sets are guaranteed to have an open intersection ($\\bigcap_n (-1/n, 1/n) = \\{0\\}$ is not open).\n\nThe advantage of this language is that **only the question \"which sets count as open\" remains**, so distances, metrics and coordinates can all be thrown away — continuity, compactness, connectedness and limits can still be expressed.\n\nA set can carry several different topologies;\n\nthey are compared as \"finer / coarser\", not by size.\n\n\n\n**Which assumptions each direction uses** (a topology is defined by axioms and imposes no metric or separation requirement):\n- topology $\\iff$ the three listed axioms: both directions unfold the definition and need no compactness, Choice or metric.\n- closure under arbitrary unions $\\implies\\emptyset$ is open requires allowing the empty index family; closure under binary intersections $\\implies$ closure under non-empty finite intersections follows by induction, while the empty intersection being $X$ still needs the convention that $X$ is open.\n- the open-set axioms $\\iff$ the family of complements satisfies arbitrary intersections and finite unions: use De Morgan's identities; no extra topological assumption is needed.\n- finite intersections cannot be upgraded to arbitrary ones: in the usual topology on the reals $\\bigcap_{n\\ge1}(-1/n,1/n)=\\{0\\}$ is not open; nor is a topology automatically Hausdorff — the indiscrete topology on a set with more than one point is a counterexample.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#677ccbc2',
      },
    },

    'def.closed-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space.\n\n- $F \\subseteq X$ is **closed**: $X \\setminus F$ is open;\n- the **closure** $\\overline{A}$ of $A \\subseteq X$: the intersection of all closed sets containing $A$ (the smallest such closed set);\n- $A$ is **dense**: $\\overline{A} = X$;\n- a **neighbourhood** of $x$: any open set containing $x$ (or any set containing such an open set).\n\nBy De Morgan's laws, the three axioms for open sets become, for closed sets: $\\emptyset, X$ are closed; **arbitrary** intersections are closed; **finite** unions are closed.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#466a9d0a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Open and closed are not opposites: $\\emptyset$ and $X$ are both open and closed; in $\\mathbb{R}$, $[0, 1]$ is closed while $[0, 1)$ is neither open nor closed.\n\nThe closure \"collects all the limit points\": in a metric space, $\\overline{A}$ is exactly the set of limits of sequences in $A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a5e40953',
      },
    },

    'def.compact': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a **topological space**. $X$ is **compact** iff every **open cover** has a finite subcover: if a family $\\{U_{i}\\}_{i \\in I}$ satisfies \"each $U_{i}$ is open\" and $X = \\bigcup_{i \\in I} U_{i}$, then there are finitely many indices with\n\n$$X = \\bigcup_{k=1}^{n} U_{i_{k}}.$$\n\nA subset $K \\subseteq X$ is **compact** if $K$ with the subspace topology is a compact space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d861e219',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Only open sets are used**: the definition mentions no metric and no $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ — compactness is a purely topological property, so it is preserved by continuous maps and invariant under homeomorphisms (`def.continuous-map` / `def.homeomorphism`).\n\n**Equivalent form (closed sets)**: every family of closed sets with the finite intersection property has non-empty intersection — that is, \"$\\{F_{i}\\}$ closed and every finite subfamily has non-empty intersection $\\implies$ the whole family has non-empty intersection\". This form is the one most often used in proofs by contradiction.\n\n**In metric spaces**: on a metric space, \"compact $\\iff$ sequentially compact $\\iff$ complete + totally bounded\" (`thm.metric-compact-equiv`) — that is a **theorem** (provable only with the metric), not part of the definition. In a general topological space **compactness and sequential compactness imply neither of the other**: $[0, \\omega_{1})$ with the order topology is sequentially compact but not compact (the cover $\\{[0, \\alpha) : \\alpha < \\omega_{1}\\}$ has no finite subcover); $\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})}$ is compact by Tychonoff but not sequentially compact (take $x_{n}(A) = 1 \\iff n \\in A$; every subsequence fails to converge at the coordinate $A = \\{n_{2k}\\}$).\n\nExamples: $[a, b] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is compact (Heine–Borel, `cor.heine-borel`); $\\mathbb{R}$ is not compact (the cover $\\{(-n, n)\\}$ has no finite subcover); $(0, 1]$ is not compact. Standard examples in general topology are in `def.chaus` and `thm.tykhonov` (arbitrary products remain compact).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c4fa4371',
      },
    },

    'def.connected': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $X$ is **disconnected** if it can be written as the union of two disjoint non-empty open sets, $X = U \\sqcup V$; otherwise it is **connected**.\n\nFor a fixed $x \\in X$, the **connected component** $C(x)$ of $x$ is the union of all connected subsets containing $x$ — it is itself connected, and it is maximal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#176a8df6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The connected components form a partition of $X$, and each component is closed. Subsets that are **both open and closed** (called **clopen** sets) are the other face of connectedness: $X$ is connected iff its only clopen subsets are $\\emptyset$ and $X$ itself — so \"is there a non-trivial clopen set?\" is the same question as \"can the space be split?\".\n\n**Four equivalent formulations; the cost lies in \"taking complements\" and \"continuous images\"**:\n\n- connected $\\iff$ **not expressible as the union of two disjoint non-empty open sets**: this is the definition.\n- connected $\\iff$ **no non-trivial clopen subsets**: $(\\implies)$ use $V = X \\setminus U$, which is closed; $(\\Leftarrow)$ one line. **This step uses only the duality of open and closed sets**.\n- connected $\\iff$ **every continuous map $X \\to \\{0,1\\}$ (discrete two points) is constant**: $(\\implies)$ the image is connected and $\\{0,1\\}$ is not; $(\\Leftarrow)$ apply the previous item to a characteristic function. This turns connectedness into \"there is no surjection onto two points\", the standard form in the internal language.\n- **the union of a family of connected subsets with a common point is connected** (pairwise intersecting suffices, then induct to arbitrarily many; to avoid AC take the subfamily containing the fixed point). Conversely, **the union of all connected components is $X$, which of course need not be connected** — in the discrete two-point space the union of the two singleton components is $X$, and it is disconnected.\n\n**Connected $\\ne$ path-connected**: path-connected $\\implies$ connected, but not conversely (the topologist's sine curve). The gap between the two is exactly the gap between \"$\\pi_{0}$ and path $\\pi_{0}$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3e6e9a27',
      },
    },

    'def.clopen': {
      statement: {
        en: "A subset $K$ of $X$ is **clopen** if it is both open and closed. Equivalently, $K$ and its complement $K^{c}$ are both open, i.e. $X = K \\sqcup K^{c}$ is a union of disjoint non-empty open sets (when $K$ is non-trivial).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#48e208de',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Clopen sets are a **rare species** in a general topological space: in connected spaces such as $\\mathbb{R}$ or $[0,1]$ there are only $\\emptyset$ and the whole space. **The more clopen sets, the more fragmented the space** — totally disconnected means \"every connected component is a single point\"; \"clopen sets separate any two points\" (= zero-dimensional) is equivalent to that only under **compact Hausdorff**; and in a profinite space \"the clopen sets form a base\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0b77ac0a',
      },
    },

    'def.hausdorff': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $X$ is a **Hausdorff space** (or a **$T_{2}$ space**) if any two distinct points **can be separated by open sets**:\n\n$$\\forall x \\ne y \\in X,\\ \\exists U \\ni x,\\ V \\ni y\\ \\text{open}:\\quad U \\cap V = \\emptyset$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#861c3b52',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In one phrase: **limits are unique**. A sequence that converges can converge to only one point — this is the kind of space analysis tacitly assumes.\n\nA stronger separation property is **normal** ($T_{4}$): any two disjoint closed sets can be separated by open sets. Every compact Hausdorff space is normal (the proof uses compactness twice, building open covers and passing to finite subcovers).\n\n$T_{3}$ and $T_{3.5}$ sit in between: $T_{3}$ says \"a point and a closed set not containing it can be separated by open sets\", and $T_{3.5}$ upgrades this to separation by **continuous functions** (the statement that \"there are enough continuous functions\").\n\nThe injectivity of the Stone–Čech compactification requires exactly $T_{3.5}$.\n\n**Convention.** The $T$-number names the bare property; the adjective adds $T_{1}$: \"regular\" = $T_{3} + T_{1}$ (`def.regular-space`), \"completely regular\" = $T_{3.5} + T_{1}$ (`def.completely-regular-space`).\n\nBare $T_{3}$ does not imply $T_{1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#12672068',
      },
    },

    'def.normal-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $X$ is **normal** if it is $T_{1}$ and any two **disjoint closed sets** $A, B$ can be separated by open sets: there are open sets $U \\supseteq A$ and $V \\supseteq B$ with $U \\cap V = \\emptyset$.\n\nReplacing \"disjoint closed sets\" by \"a point and a closed set not containing it\" gives a **regular** space; replacing it by \"two points\" gives a **Hausdorff** space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c681d9cf',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The hierarchy of separation axioms** (each step strengthens the previous one):\n\n- $T_{1}$: singletons are closed;\n- Hausdorff: two points can be separated by open sets;\n- regular: a point and a closed set not containing it can be separated $\\implies$ Hausdorff;\n- **normal**: two disjoint closed sets can be separated $\\implies$ regular.\n\n**Compact Hausdorff $\\implies$ normal** (the most frequently used consequence of compactness: in a compact space two disjoint closed sets can be separated by finitely many pairs of open sets). Hence spaces such as $[0,1]$ and $\\beta I$ are normal — so Urysohn's lemma always applies there.\n\nThe **proof** runs along two steps, \"compact $\\implies$ paracompact\" plus \"paracompact $+$ Hausdorff $\\implies$ normal\", see `thm.paracompact-hausdorff-normal`; ⚠️ **normality is not hereditary**: a subspace of a normal space need not be normal. This differs from both \"compact\" and \"Hausdorff\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d4c0d7ca',
      },
    },

    'def.regular-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this is**: the $T_{3}$ property **together with $T_{1}$**. Only after adding $T_{1}$ does it automatically become $T_{2}$ — this is the real purpose of the name \"regular\".\n\nA topological space $X$ is a **regular space** if\n\n1. $X$ is **$T_{3}$**: a point and a closed set not containing it can be separated by disjoint open sets (`def.t3-space`);\n2. $X$ is **$T_{1}$**: singletons are closed (`def.t1-space`).\n\n**Why $T_{1}$ is indispensable**: the indiscrete topology is $T_{3}$ but not $T_{1}$, and certainly not $T_{2}$. After adding $T_{1}$, the set $\\{y\\}$ is closed, so \"$x$ and $\\{y\\}$ can be separated\" yields two disjoint open sets — exactly $T_{2}$.\n\n**Its place in the chain**: regular space $\\implies T_{2} \\implies T_{1} \\implies T_{0}$ (all reverse implications fail).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dbbfef74',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Convention**: the **T-number denotes the bare property; only the named notion carries $T_{1}$**.\n\nSo T3 means a point and a closed set can be separated (without $T_{1}$), while a regular space means T3 plus $T_{1}$.\n\n⚠️ Munkres uses the opposite convention (he takes \"regular\" without $T_{1}$ and $T_{3}$ with $T_{1}$) — when reading another book, fix the convention first; otherwise a statement such as \"$T_{3} \\implies T_{2}$\" is sometimes true and sometimes false.\n\n**One-line proof that regular $\\implies$ $T_{2}$**: given $x \\ne y$, $T_{1}$ makes $\\{y\\}$ closed and $x \\notin \\{y\\}$; by $T_{3}$ choose disjoint open sets $U \\ni x$ and $V \\supseteq \\{y\\}$. This is exactly $T_{2}$.\n\n⚠️ **Regular does not imply normal**: the Sorgenfrey plane is regular but not normal. So \"regular\" is not a weaker version of \"normal\" on the same axis — they separate **differently shaped** objects (point/closed set vs closed set/closed set).\n\n⭐ Relation to `thm.paracompact-hausdorff-normal`: \"step 1\" of that theorem proves \"paracompact Hausdorff $\\implies$ regular\", using precisely the form $\\overline{U} \\subseteq W$ of $T_{3}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f36e6eb3',
      },
    },

    'def.subspace-topology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space and $Y \\subseteq X$ a subset. The **subspace topology** on $Y$ is\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_{Y} := \\{\\, U \\cap Y : U \\in \\mathcal{T} \\,\\}$$\n\nThe resulting topology is the coarsest topology making the inclusion map $Y \\hookrightarrow X$ continuous.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#442c3b78',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: **the open sets of the subspace are exactly the open sets of the big space cut down**. So \"closed in $Y$\" and \"compact in $Y$\" must be judged with respect to the cut-down piece, not by looking at the big space — for example $(0, 1) \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is closed in itself but not in $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n📌 `def.induced-topology` is a different notion sharing the same Chinese name: it denotes the Grothendieck topology on a site induced by a functor, and shares only the name with the subspace topology (the two are not merged).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4bfb0a14',
      },
    },

    'def.product-topology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X_{i}, \\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \\in I}$ be a family of topological spaces. The **product topology** on $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_{i}$ is the topology with basis the sets\n\n$$\\prod_{i \\in I} U_{i} \\qquad (U_{i} \\in \\mathcal{T}_{i},$$\nwhere $U_{i} = X_{i}$ for all but finitely many indices $i$)\n\n— that is, the **coarsest** topology making every projection $\\pi_{j} : \\prod_{i} X_{i} \\to X_{j}$ continuous.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#87113c2e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **\"Coarsest\" is the key word**: for the projections to be continuous it suffices that each $\\pi_{j}^{-1}(U_{j})$ be open; taking finite intersections of these as a basis gives the product topology. Demanding a finer topology would lose limits — and the equivalence \"$x_{n} \\to x$ iff it converges in every coordinate\" is exactly what this coarsest choice buys.\n\n**Categorical meaning**: $\\prod_{i} X_{i}$ together with the projections is the **product** in $\\mathbf{Top}$. So the product topology is not an arbitrary choice: it is the one uniquely determined by the universal property.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b037fbe1',
      },
    },

    'def.quotient-topology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space and $\\pi : X \\twoheadrightarrow Y$ a surjection. The **quotient topology** on $Y$ is\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_{Y} := \\{\\, V \\subseteq Y : \\pi^{-1}(V) \\in \\mathcal{T} \\,\\}$$\n\nThis is the finest topology making $\\pi$ continuous; $Y$ with this topology is the **quotient space** of $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b75bf8af',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: **the open sets of the quotient are those whose preimage is open**. So properties of the quotient must be read \"from above\": whether two points in $Y$ can be separated by open sets asks whether their preimages can be separated by open sets of $X$.\n\nThe quotient and subspace topologies are opposite directions: one takes \"the coarsest obtained by cutting\", the other \"the finest obtained by gluing back\".\n\n📌 The \"quotient space\" of topology is this one; the quotient of a **vector space** is `def.quotient-vs`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a90d3a9d',
      },
    },

    'def.locally-compact-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $X$ is **locally compact** if **every point has a compact neighbourhood**: for every $x \\in X$ there is a compact set $K$ with\n\n$$x \\in \\mathring{K}$$\n\nHere $X$ is **not** required to be Hausdorff.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76e8ed3c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Difference from the Hausdorff version**: in a Hausdorff space, \"every point has a compact neighbourhood\" is equivalent to \"every point has a **base of compact neighbourhoods**\" (inside every neighbourhood one can find a compact neighbourhood); without Hausdorffness this equivalence fails (compact sets need not be closed, and the relation between neighbourhoods and compact sets degenerates).\n\n**Examples**: every compact space (take $K = X$), $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, and every discrete space is locally compact; $\\mathbb{Q}$ is not. A compact space is always locally compact, but not necessarily Hausdorff.\n\n**Use**: when Hausdorffness is needed, combine this with `def.hausdorff`; without it the definition still stands, but the stronger property of having a base of compact neighbourhoods may fail.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6f8a6381',
      },
    },

    'def.sequentially-compact': {
      statement: {
        en: "A metric space $(X, d)$ is **sequentially compact** iff every sequence in $X$ has a subsequence converging in $X$:\n\n$$\\forall\\{x_n\\} \\subseteq X,\\ \\exists\\{x_{n_k}\\},\\ \\exists x \\in X : x_{n_k} \\to x$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#64f48a59',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In metric spaces, \"sequentially compact $\\iff$ compact\" (see the arrows below), but in general topological spaces the two are **not equivalent** — sequential compactness is strictly stronger. This is exactly where metric spaces are convenient.\n\nNote that the subsequence converges to a point of $X$: sequential compactness is an intrinsic property and does not refer to any ambient space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#acc16623',
      },
    },

    'def.sequence-limit': {
      statement: {
        en: "A sequence $\\{x_n\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ **converges to** $x$ (written $x_n \\to x$ or $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} x_n = x$), iff\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\exists N, \\forall n \\ge N : |x_n - x| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nThat is: no matter how small $\\varepsilon$ is given, from some index on all terms lie in the $x$-neighbourhood of $\\varepsilon$.\n\nA sequence that does not converge is called **divergent**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#491ef455',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the \"$\\varepsilon$-$N$ language\": it turns \"getting closer and closer\" into a condition that can be checked one step at a time.\n\nEvery proof in analysis rests on this format.\n\nBasic properties (all verified directly from the definition):\n- the limit is **unique**;\n\n- a convergent sequence is **bounded**;\n\n- limits commute with the four arithmetic operations (when the limit of the denominator is nonzero).\n\n⚠ \"Bounded\" does not guarantee convergence ($(-1)^n$);\n\nthe order \"$\\varepsilon$ fixed, $N$ pushed later\" cannot be reversed.\n\n📌 The unqualified term \"limit\" means a **limit of a sequence** (defined here); limits and colimits in the categorical sense are `def.limit`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ecf468e6',
      },
    },

    'def.continuous': {
      statement: {
        en: "**Theorem (the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ criterion)**: the condition below is not a definition but a criterion for continuity in metric terms; for the definition see `def.metric-continuity`, and for the metric on $\\mathbb{R}$ see `def.real-metric-space`.\n$f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **continuous at** $x_0$ iff\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\exists \\delta > 0 : |x - x_0| < \\delta \\implies |f(x) - f(x_0)| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nEquivalent formulation: $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} f(x) = f(x_0)$ (the limit exists and equals the value of the function).\n\n$f$ is **continuous** (continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$) iff it is continuous at every point.\n\n**Uniform continuity** is a stronger requirement: $\\delta$ depends only on $\\varepsilon$, not on $x_0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3a8f6943',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Replacing $|x - x_0| < \\delta$ and $|f(x) - f(x_0)| < \\varepsilon$ by a distance $d$ carries this definition over to metric spaces verbatim — which is exactly why the definition of a metric space looks the way it does.\n\n⚠ **Continuous $\\ne$ uniformly continuous**: $f(x) = x^{2}$ is continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$ but not uniformly continuous (the larger $x$ is, the smaller $\\delta$ must be).\n\nThe two notions coincide only on compact intervals.\n\n📌 This node is a criterion, not the definition; the definition is in `def.metric-continuity`, and the metric of $\\mathbb{R}$ is in `def.real-metric-space`. For continuity in the general topological sense see `def.continuous-map`; the present statement is its metric special case.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#706f8a5c',
      },
    },

    'def.cauchy-sequence': {
      statement: {
        en: "A real sequence $\\{x_n\\}$ is a **Cauchy sequence** iff\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall m, n \\ge N :\\ |x_m - x_n| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nThat is: once the index is far enough out, **any two terms** are close to each other.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7201d0b3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The difference from convergence is the **direction of information**: to check convergence one needs $x$ first in order to compute $|x_n - x|$, whereas a Cauchy sequence lets the terms **compare among themselves** — someone who does not know the limit can still verify it.\n\n⭐ In $\\mathbb{R}$ the two are **equivalent** (the Cauchy criterion);\n\nin $\\mathbb{Q}$ they are not ($1, 1.4, 1.41, \\ldots$ is Cauchy but has no rational limit).\n\n$\\mathbb{R}$ is constructed precisely to fill this gap.\n\n⚠ Do not confuse this with `def.cauchy-null`: that one is about **rational** Cauchy sequences, a building block in the construction of $\\mathbb{R}$;\n\nthe present notion is the tool for testing convergence once $\\mathbb{R}$ is available.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3c91c226',
      },
    },

    'def.limit-point': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. A point $x$ is a **limit point** (also called an accumulation point) of $S$ iff every punctured neighbourhood of $x$ meets $S$:\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists s \\in S :\\ 0 < |s - x| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nEquivalent sequential formulation: there is a sequence $S \\setminus \\{x\\}$ with $s_n \\to x$.\n\nReplacing $S$ by the set of terms of a sequence gives \"$x$ is a limit point of $\\{x_n\\}$\", which means \"$\\{x_n\\}$ has a subsequence converging to $x$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ff443b4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Every bounded sequence has a limit point\" is the Bolzano–Weierstrass theorem — one of the six faces of the completeness of the real numbers.\n\n⚠ A limit point **need not belong** to $S$: the set of all rationals $\\mathbb{Q}$ has limit-point set all of $\\mathbb{R}$ (this is precisely what incompleteness of $\\mathbb{Q}$ looks like).\n\n⚠ Do not confuse it with the supremum: $\\sup S$ is about \"upper bounds of $S$\", while a limit point is about \"which points the elements of $S$ approach arbitrarily closely\";\n\n$\\sup\\{1, 1/2, 1/3, \\ldots\\} = 1$ is also the unique limit point of that set, whereas $\\sup\\{1\\} = 1$ is not a limit point.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9d9f715b',
      },
    },

    'thm.bolzano-weierstrass': {
      statement: {
        en: "Every bounded sequence of real numbers has a convergent subsequence. Equivalently: every bounded infinite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a limit point.\n\nApplying this coordinatewise in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ gives: every bounded sequence in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ has a convergent subsequence.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3cdbcb75',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the analytic core of \"compact $\\iff$ bounded and closed in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$\" (Heine–Borel): the reason a continuous function on a bounded closed set attains its extreme values ultimately lies here.\n\n⚠ Completeness is **indispensable** here: in $\\mathbb{Q}$, the sequence $1, 1.4, 1.41, 1.414, \\ldots$ is bounded but has no subsequence converging to a rational number.\n\n⭐ The standard proof is **bisection**: halve the interval each time, keep the half containing infinitely many terms, and apply the nested-interval theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#54071bff',
      },
    },

    'def.sigma-algebra': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a set and $\\mathfrak{A} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$. Then $\\mathfrak{A}$ is a **$\\sigma$-algebra** on $X$ if and only if it satisfies all three of the following:\n\n1. **Contains the whole set**: $X \\in \\mathfrak{A}$\n2. **Closed under complements**: $A \\in \\mathfrak{A} \\implies A^c := X \\setminus A \\in \\mathfrak{A}$\n3. **Closed under countable unions**: $A_1, A_2, \\ldots \\in \\mathfrak{A} \\implies \\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_n \\in \\mathfrak{A}$\n\nThe pair $(X, \\mathfrak{A})$ is called a **measurable space**, and the elements of $\\mathfrak{A}$ are called **measurable sets**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6309313d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The three conditions come as a package and none can be dropped: keeping only (2) and (3) gives a **$\\sigma$-ring**; weakening (3) to \"finite unions\" gives an **algebra**.\n\nFrom (2) and (3), De Morgan gives closure under **countable intersections** at once: $\\Big( \\bigcap_n A_n \\Big)^c = \\bigcup_n A_n^c \\in \\mathfrak{A}$. Hence finite unions, finite intersections, differences and symmetric differences are all available — the only thing that genuinely goes beyond an algebra is **countability**.\n\n⚠ The direction cannot be reversed: every $\\sigma$-algebra is an algebra, but an algebra need not be a $\\sigma$-algebra.\n\nCounterexample: on $X = \\mathbb{R}$, the family of all finite and cofinite sets is an algebra, while $\\mathbb{N} = \\{1\\} \\cup \\{2\\} \\cup \\{3\\} \\cup \\cdots$ is a union of countably many of its elements and is itself neither finite nor cofinite, so that algebra is not closed under countable unions.\n\nThe \"$\\sigma$\" in the notation means **countable** (from the initial letter of German Summe / French somme); it has nothing to do with the $\\sigma$ of standard deviation.\n\nWhy it is needed: countable additivity of a measure $\\mu (\\bigcup A_{n}) = \\sum \\mu (A_{n})$ requires a domain closed under **countable** operations, otherwise the two sides of the identity do not even live in the same family of sets. The whole construction of measure starts here.\n\n**Of the three axioms only one is really \"hard\"** (the other two can almost be exchanged for each other):\n\n- **Contains the whole set $\\iff$ contains the empty set**: immediate from closure under complements ($\\emptyset = X^{c}$). So writing \"contains $\\emptyset$\" or \"contains $X$\" is equivalent, **provided closure under complements is also present**.\n- **Closed under countable unions $\\iff$ closed under countable intersections**: **use De Morgan twice**: $\\bigcap_{n} A_{n} = \\bigl(\\bigcup_{n} A_{n}^{c}\\bigr)^{c}$. So of \"countable unions\" and \"countable intersections\" only one needs to be assumed — **but it must be paired with closure under complements**; without that neither follows.\n- **\"Countable unions\" cannot be weakened to \"finite unions\"**: finite unions plus complements give an **algebra** (Boolean algebra), not a $\\sigma$-algebra. The difference is substantial: the rationals can be covered in the finite-union algebra of $2^{\\mathbb{R}}$, but the countable union of the singletons $\\{q\\}$ ($= \\mathbb{Q}$) need not be — and all of measure theory needs countable unions.\n- **\"Countable\" cannot be strengthened to \"arbitrary\"**: a $\\sigma$-algebra is **not** closed under arbitrary unions. Counterexample: on $\\mathbb{R}$ the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra is closed under unions of all open sets (the open sets themselves are closed under arbitrary unions), but taking unions over **arbitrary** subfamilies escapes it (every singleton is Borel, yet an arbitrary union of them can be non-measurable, given $2^{\\aleph_{0}}$ of them — constructing a Vitali set needs the axiom of choice).\n\n⚠️ One-line mnemonic: **a $\\sigma$-algebra = \"contains the whole set + closed under complements + closed under countable unions\", and everything else is a consequence of the last two**; while \"complements + finite unions\" is only an algebra, and the gap is not small.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6901af65',
      },
    },

    'def.measure': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{M}$ be a $\\sigma$-algebra on $X$. A function $\\mu : \\mathcal{M} \\to [0, +\\infty]$ is a **measure** if and only if\n\n- **(i)** $\\mu(\\emptyset) = 0$\n- **(ii) countable additivity**: if $\\{E_{j}\\}$ is a sequence of pairwise disjoint sets in $\\mathcal{M}$, then\n\n$$\\mu( \\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\infty} E_j ) = \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty} \\mu(E_j)$$\n\n$(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$ is called a **measure space**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0b79b896',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "(i) is **independent of** (ii), and (ii) alone **does not imply** it: the constant function $+\\infty$ is countably additive yet does not vanish on the empty set (taking $E_j \\equiv \\emptyset$ yields $\\mu(\\emptyset) = +\\infty \\cdot \\mu(\\emptyset)$, and both sides are legitimate). The axiom is there to exclude the meaningless solution that assigns $\\infty$ to everything, and at the same time to ensure that the series in (ii) is never $\\infty - \\infty$.\n\n**Countable additivity is the only requirement in this definition with real content**: it upgrades \"size\" from finite additivity to countable additivity, at the price of allowing the value $\\infty$.\n\n⚠ Allowing $\\mu (E) = +\\infty$ is necessary: Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb{R}$ is $\\infty$.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the two measure axioms are the definition; countable additivity is stronger than finite additivity):\n- measure $\\iff$ non-negative extended real-valued function satisfying (i), (ii): unwinding the definition; no $\\sigma$-finiteness or completeness of the measure is needed; the domain must be a $\\sigma$-algebra.\n- countable additivity $\\implies$ finite additivity: also uses $\\mu(\\emptyset)=0$, padding a finite disjoint sequence with empty sets; conversely, finite additivity plus continuity from below recovers countable additivity by taking limits of partial unions.\n- (ii) alone cannot imply (i): the constant $+\\infty$ is countably additive in the extended non-negative real sense but does not vanish on the empty set; if some set has finite measure, one can deduce (i) from its disjoint union with the empty set.\n- finite additivity is not enough for a measure: on the finite-cofinite algebra of $\\mathbb{N}$, assigning zero to finite sets and one to cofinite sets is finitely additive, but the disjoint union of singletons breaks countable additivity; this also shows that the domain there is not yet a $\\sigma$-algebra.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#68503ad7',
      },
    },

    'def.measurable-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M})$ and $(Y, \\mathcal{N})$ be measurable spaces. A function $f : X \\to Y$ is **$(\\mathcal{M}, \\mathcal{N})$-measurable** if and only if the preimage of every measurable set is measurable:\n\n$$f^{-1}(E) \\in \\mathcal{M}\\quad  \\forall E \\in \\mathcal{N}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea186217',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Why **preimages** rather than images: preimages preserve intersections, unions and complements ($f^{-1}(\\bigcup E_i) = \\bigcup f^{-1}(E_i)$, etc.), whereas images lack these good properties. Measurability is therefore a condition pulled back.\n\nEquivalent formulation (when $Y$ is a topological space and $\\mathcal{N} = \\mathfrak{B}_Y$): $f$ is measurable $\\iff$ the preimage of every open set is measurable.\n\n⚠ \"Measurable on $E$\" is a relative notion: $f$ is measurable on $E$ $\\iff$ $f|_E$ is measurable with respect to the subspace $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal{M}_E$.\n\n**Four characterizations, the cost being \"which family of sets is tested\"**:\n\n- preimages of **open sets** are measurable $\\iff$ preimages of **Borel sets** are measurable: $(\\Leftarrow)$ is clear; $(\\implies)$ uses the fact that preimages preserve $\\sigma$-algebra operations (Borel sets are generated by open sets).\n- $\\iff$ **all level sets $\\{x : f(x) > a\\}$ are measurable** ($a \\in \\mathbb{R}$): **this is the most-used criterion**, since one only has to test a generating family instead of all open sets. ⚠️ Using $\\{f > a\\}$ or $\\{f \\ge a\\}$ both work, **and mixing them is harmless** ($\\{f > a\\} = \\bigcup_{q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, q > a} H_q$, and each $H_q$ may be taken in either form).\n- $\\iff$ **both $f^{+}, f^{-}$ are measurable**: split a real-valued function into positive and negative parts, then handle the non-negative case with $\\{f > a\\}$.\n\n⚠️ **The necessary hypothesis is \"the target carries the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra\"**: measurability on $\\mathbb{R}$ is always relative to some $\\sigma$-algebra; for $\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ (including $\\pm\\infty$) **no extra convention is needed**: $\\{f = +\\infty\\} = \\bigcap_n \\{f > n\\}$ and $\\{f = -\\infty\\} = \\bigcap_n \\{f \\le -n\\}$ are automatically measurable from the level sets.\n⚠️ **A composition of measurable functions need not be measurable**: $f$ measurable and $g$ Borel measurable $\\implies$ $g \\circ f$ measurable; but if $g$ is merely Lebesgue measurable this can fail — this is why \"Borel measurable\" and \"Lebesgue measurable\" must be distinguished.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2e01c79e',
      },
    },

    'def.simple-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "A measurable function $\\varphi : X \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is a **simple function** if and only if it takes only **finitely many values**. Equivalently, it can be written as\n\n$$\\varphi = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_j \\chi_{E_j}$$\n\nwhere $a_j \\in \\mathbb{C}$, and the sets $E_j = \\varphi^{-1}(\\{a_j\\}) \\in \\mathcal{M}$ are pairwise disjoint.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b53fc9c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "A simple function is the measurable version of a **step function** — finitely many \"steps\".\n\nIt is the **starting point** of the whole theory of integration: first define the integral for simple functions (a weighted sum), then extend it to all non-negative measurable functions by \"approximating from below by simple functions\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#75a8987d',
      },
    },

    'def.integral-simple': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\varphi \\in L^+$ be a simple function, written in standard form — non-negative coefficients, pairwise disjoint base sets, all in $\\mathcal{M}$:\n\n$\\varphi = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_j \\chi_{E_j}, \\quad  a_j \\ge 0,\\ E_j \\in \\mathcal{M}$, **pairwise disjoint**\n\nThen define\n\n$$\\int \\varphi d\\mu := \\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_j \\mu(E_j)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2d5cc0e7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is exactly \"area $=$ height of each layer $\\times$ measure of each layer\" — written in the same shape as a Riemann sum, only intervals are replaced by measurable sets.\n\nOne must check **well-definedness**: the same $\\varphi$ can be written in different standard forms (for instance by splitting some $E_{j}$ further), but finite additivity of $\\mu$ gives the same result.\n\nConvention $0 \\cdot \\infty = 0$: this step is crucial — it makes the integral of \"the function equal to 0 on a set of infinite measure\" equal to 0, as intuition demands.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3a4b2ae6',
      },
    },

    'thm.mct': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_n\\} \\subseteq L^+$ be a **pointwise increasing** sequence:\n\n$$f_1 \\le f_2 \\le \\cdots, \\quad  f_n \\uparrow  f$$\n\nThen the integral and the limit can be exchanged:\n\n$$\\int f = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\int f_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d3286f22',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **engine** of the whole Lebesgue integration theory: term-by-term integration, Fatou, and the DCT are all corollaries.\n\nCompare with the Riemann integral: there \"exchanging integral and limit\" needs a strong condition such as uniform convergence; here **monotonicity** alone suffices — that is the entire advantage of the Lebesgue integral.\n\n⚠ Both sides may be $\\infty$; the identity is understood in the extended sense.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#291c7a0a',
      },
    },

    'thm.dct': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_n\\} \\subseteq L^1$, let $f$ be **measurable**, let $f_n \\to f$ a.e., and suppose there is a **dominating function** $g \\in L^1$ such that\n\n$$|f_n| \\le g\\quad  \\text{a.e.}\\quad  \\forall n$$\n\nThen $f \\in L^1$ and\n\n$$\\int f = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\int f_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#02aed00c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the convergence theorem **used most often** in actual computation. None of the three hypotheses can be dropped:\n\n$\\cdot \\text{a.e.}$ convergence — cannot be removed (on a one-point probability space $f_{n} = (-1)^{n}$: dominated by $g \\equiv 1$, yet neither the functions nor the integrals converge);\n\n$\\cdot$ the dominating function $g \\in L^{1}$ — cannot be removed ($n\\chi_{[0,1/n]}$ tends to $0$ **almost everywhere** on $[0,1]$ (it fails to converge at $x = 0$), yet its integral is constantly $1$; another example is $\\chi_{[n,n+1]}$, which converges to $0$ **everywhere** while its integral is constantly $1$ and does not tend to $0$ — the defect is the absence of an integrable dominating function, since any dominating function would have to be $\\ge 1$ on all of $\\mathbb{R}$);\n\n$\\cdot$ $g$ must be **integrable**; mere boundedness is not enough (on a space of infinite measure).\n\nWhy \"dominating\": $g$ keeps all the $f_{n}$ inside one integrable cage, so the mass has nowhere to escape and the limiting process cannot lose area.\n\n📌 \"Exchanging integral and limit\" here means **dominated convergence** (pointwise convergence plus an integrable dominating function); the exchange under uniform convergence is `thm.uniform-limit-integral`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76f9f632',
      },
    },

    'def.ae': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu)$ be a measure space. A proposition $P(x)$ about a point $x$ holds **almost everywhere** (written a.e.) if and only if\n\n$$\\exists E \\in \\mathcal{M}:\\ \\mu(E) = 0\\ \\wedge \\ \\forall x \\in X \\setminus E,\\ P(x) .$$\n\nthat is, **the set of failure points is contained in some measurable null set** (it need not itself be measurable — a non-Borel subset of the Cantor set in a non-complete space is a counterexample; only after completion are the two formulations equivalent).\n\nCommon uses: $f = g$ a.e. (the set $\\{f \\ne g\\}$ is null), $f_n \\to f$ a.e. (the points of non-convergence form a null set), $F' = 0$ a.e.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#45167866',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ a.e. is the **lubricant** of Lebesgue theory: elements of $L^1$, $L^p$ are really **equivalence classes of functions** (equal a.e. counts as the same), and almost every convergence theorem (MCT, Fatou, the a.e. version of DCT, Egorov) requires only a.e. convergence — because values on a null set do not contribute to the integral.\n\n⚠ \"Equal a.e.\" and \"equal everywhere\" are different relations: $\\int |f| = 0 \\implies f = 0$ a.e. (not everywhere 0).\n\n⚠ A **subset** of a null set need not be measurable unless the measure space is complete — this is exactly the problem that completion solves.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b1b3e26f',
      },
    },

    'def.uniform-continuity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Then $f$ is **uniformly continuous** if and only if\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists \\delta > 0,\\ \\forall x, x' \\in X :\\ |x - x'| < \\delta \\implies |f(x) - f(x')| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nThe only difference from \"continuous\" is the **order** of the quantifiers: here $\\delta$ appears before $\\forall x$, so one and the same $\\delta$ must work simultaneously for all points of $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#026589c4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: continuity gives \"a $\\delta$ for each point\", uniform continuity gives \"one $\\delta$ that works for all of $X$\".\n\n⚠ The two are **not equivalent**: $f(x) = x^{2}$ is continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$, but for $x = n$, $x' = n + 1/n$ one has $|f(x) - f(x')| = 2 + 1/n^{2} > 2$, which no $\\delta$, however small, can avoid — so it is not uniformly continuous.\n\n$f(x) = 1/x$ on $(0, 1)$ is the same.\n\n⭐ On compact sets the two **coincide** (Cantor's theorem) — this is why elementary calculus can talk only about continuity and still use uniform-continuity properties freely.\n\n⭐ Uniform continuity is the passport for everything that later \"moves limits into integrals / derivatives\": only uniformly convergent sequences of functions preserve continuity and preserve integrals.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ece91bf7',
      },
    },

    'def.uniform-convergence': {
      statement: {
        en: "With the notation above, $\\{f_n\\}$ **converges uniformly** to $f$ if and only if\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall n \\ge N,\\ \\forall x \\in X :\\ |f_n(x) - f(x)| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nWrite $f_n \\rightrightarrows f$. An equivalent formulation (in terms of the supremum):\n\n$$\\sup_{x \\in X} |f_n(x) - f(x)| \\xrightarrow{\\;n \\to \\infty\\;} 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4b3f6c88',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The only difference from pointwise convergence is the **order of quantifiers**: here $\\exists N$ comes **before** $\\forall x$ — a single $N$ works for the whole domain.\n\n⭐ The supremum form is the most-used criterion: uniform convergence says \"the supremum of the error function tends to zero\", that is, **convergence in the sup norm**.\n\nThis also explains why it is \"convergence in a function space\" rather than \"convergence on a set of points\".\n\n⚠ Uniform convergence $\\implies$ pointwise convergence, but not conversely ($f_n(x) = x^{n}$ on $[0, 1]$).\n\n⭐ The three exchange theorems in this pack (preserving continuity, integrals, derivatives) all need only uniform convergence — and convergence of the kind $f_n(x) = x^{n}$ that \"misses one point\" is exactly what destroys continuity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#acc41663',
      },
    },

    'def.category': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **category** is a sextuple\n\n$$( \\mathcal{O}, M, s, t, \\circ, i )$$\n\nwhere $(\\mathcal{O}, M, s, t)$ is a **graph**: $\\mathcal{O}$ are the **objects**, $M$ the **morphisms**, $s, t : M \\to \\mathcal{O}$ assigns to each morphism its **source** and **target**, and one writes $f : X \\to Y$ to mean $s(f) = X$ and $t(f) = Y$.\n\nThere are moreover two maps:\n\n**Composition** $\\circ$ is defined on composable pairs of morphisms. Set\n\n$$M \\times_{s,t} M = \\{ (f, g) \\in M \\times M : s(f) = t(g) \\}$$\n\nthis is the set of all pairs of morphisms \"whose target of $g$ equals the source of $f$\"; $\\circ : M \\times_{s,t} M \\to M$ sends $(f, g)$ to $f \\circ g$, and satisfies the **associativity law**\n\n$$h \\circ (g \\circ f) = (h \\circ g) \\circ f$$\n\n(whenever both sides are defined).\n\n**Identity** $i : \\mathcal{O} \\to M$ assigns to each object $X$ a morphism $1_X = i(X)$, satisfying the **identity law**\n\n$$f \\circ 1_X = f = 1_Y \\circ f \\qquad (f : X \\to Y)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#73385d3c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The crux is the **fiber product** $M \\times_{s,t} M$: composition is not an operation on $M \\times M$, but is defined only on some pairs of morphisms. Composition in a category is thus a **partial operation**, and \"whether $f \\circ g$ is defined\" is itself something to check.\n\nExamples: $\\mathbf{Set}$ (sets and functions), $\\mathbf{Mon}$ (monoids), $\\mathbf{Grp}$ (groups), $\\mathbf{Ab}$ (abelian groups), $\\mathbf{Rng}$ (rings), $G\\text{-}\\mathbf{Set}$ ($G$-sets).\n\n**The simplex category**: $\\mathcal{O} = \\mathbb{N}$,\n\n$$M = \\{ (m, n, f) : m, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ f : [m] \\to [n] \\text{increasing} \\}, \\qquad [m] = \\{ 1, \\ldots, m \\}$$\n\nmorphisms are the increasing maps from $m$ to $n$.\n\n**The category of open sets** $\\operatorname{Open}(X)$: given a topological space $(X, \\tau)$, take $\\mathcal{O} = \\tau$,\n\n$$M = \\{ (u, v) : u \\subseteq v \\} \\subseteq \\tau \\times \\tau$$\n\nthat is, \"from a smaller open set to an open set containing it\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#392a342e',
      },
    },

    'def.functor': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **functor** $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ from a category $\\mathcal{C}$ to a category $\\mathcal{D}$ consists of two parts:\n\n- a map on objects $X \\mapsto F(X)$;\n- a map on morphisms $f \\mapsto F(f)$, sending $f : X \\to Y$ to $F(f) : F(X) \\to F(Y)$.\n\nIt is required to **preserve identities** and **preserve composition**:\n\n$$F(1_X) = 1_{F(X)}, \\qquad F(g \\circ f) = F(g) \\circ F(f)$$\n\n(whenever $g \\circ f$ is defined). A functor in the opposite direction $F : \\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ is called a **contravariant functor**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5e2e1d93',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The two requirements together say one thing: **a functor transports the entire structure of a category verbatim** — identities remain identities, composites remain composites, and even associativity need not be checked again (it follows automatically).\n\nExamples:\n\n- the **forgetful functor** $\\mathbf{Top} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$: $(X, \\tau) \\mapsto X$, treating continuous maps as ordinary maps.\n\nThe topology is discarded, hence \"forgetful\".\n\nLikewise $\\mathbf{Ab} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$, $\\mathbf{Grp} \\to \\mathbf{Mon}$ (inclusion).\n- $\\mathbf{Set} \\to \\mathbf{Top}$ has two: $X \\mapsto (X, \\mathcal{P}(X))$ (discrete topology) and $X \\mapsto (X, \\{ \\emptyset, X \\})$ (trivial topology). Both work, because maps out of a discrete space and into a trivial space are always continuous.\n- **free constructions** $\\mathbf{Set} \\to \\mathbf{Ab}$: $X \\mapsto \\bigoplus_X \\mathbb{Z}$; $\\mathbf{Set} \\to \\mathbf{Grp}$: $X \\mapsto \\langle x, x^{-1} \\mid x \\in X \\rangle$.\n- **abelianization** $\\mathbf{Grp} \\to \\mathbf{Ab}$: $G \\mapsto G / [G, G]$.\n- **groupification** $\\mathbf{Mon} \\to \\mathbf{Grp}$: $M \\mapsto M^{gr} = \\langle x_g \\ (g \\in M) \\mid x_g x_h = x_{gh} \\rangle$.\n- $\\mathbf{Top}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathbf{Cat}$: $(X, \\tau) \\mapsto \\operatorname{Open}(X)$. Contravariant, because fewer open sets means more maps.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ee0c6560',
      },
    },

    'def.natural-transformation': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F, G : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ be two functors. A **natural transformation** $\\alpha : F \\implies G$ from $F$ to $G$ is a family of morphisms\n\n$$\\alpha_X : F(X) \\to G(X) \\qquad (X \\in \\mathcal{C})$$\n\nsuch that for every $f : X \\to Y$ the square commutes:\n\n$$G(f) \\circ \\alpha_X = \\alpha_Y \\circ F(f)$$\n\nWhen every $\\alpha_X$ is an isomorphism, $\\alpha$ is called a **natural isomorphism**, written $F \\cong G$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d4719ae7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In one sentence: the two components of $\\alpha$ control the two ends, and commutativity says that \"changing functor first and then applying $f$\" agrees with \"applying $f$ first and then changing functor\".\n\nWith natural transformations, **functors themselves become objects**: taking functors $\\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ as objects and natural transformations as morphisms gives a category denoted $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal{C}, \\mathcal{C}')$;\n\nthe set of all natural transformations $F \\implies G$ is denoted $\\operatorname{Nat}(F, G)$.\n\nExample: there are two functors $\\mathbf{CRng} \\to \\mathbf{Grp}$ — $A \\mapsto GL_n(A)$ and $A \\mapsto A^{*}$. The determinant\n\n$$\\det_A : GL_n(A) \\to A^{*}$$\n\nassembles into a **natural transformation**: $\\det$ \"commutes with\" ring homomorphisms. This is exactly what \"natural\" means — not a coincidence verified case by case, but forced by structure.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f569c80b',
      },
    },

    'def.adjoint': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$, $G : \\mathcal{D} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ be a pair of functors. $(F, G)$ is called a pair of **adjoint functors** if there is a natural isomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{D}}\\bigl(F(X),\\ Y\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}\\bigl(X,\\ G(Y)\\bigr) \\qquad (X \\in \\mathcal{C},\\ Y \\in \\mathcal{D})$$\n\nIn that case write $F \\dashv G$: $F$ is called the **left adjoint** of $G$, and $G$ the **right adjoint** of $F$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8be3f401',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In one sentence: **translations between the two directions are mutually optimal**. Going around the left (apply $F$, then map out) and going around the right (map out, then apply $G$) give the same amount — and **naturally** so.\n\nExamples:\n\n- the forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Top} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ has **two** adjoints: the left adjoint is the **discrete topology** ($\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Top}}(S_{d}, X) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Set}}(S, F(X))$), the right adjoint is the **trivial topology** ($\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Top}}(X, S_{t}) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Set}}(F(X), S)$).\n- the left adjoint of the forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Ab} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ is the **free abelian group**: $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Ab}}(\\mathbb{Z}^{(X)}, M) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Set}}(X, \\operatorname{forget} M)$.\n\n**Free is left adjoint to forgetful**.\n\nTwo rules of computation:\n\n- **duality**: $F \\dashv G$ $\\iff$ $G^{\\mathrm{op}} \\dashv F^{\\mathrm{op}}$ (on $\\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{op}}$).\n- **composition**: $F \\dashv G$ and $F' \\dashv G'$ $\\implies$ $F' \\circ F \\dashv G \\circ G'$, because\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}(F'F(X), Y) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}(F(X), G'(Y)) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}(X, GG'(Y))$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fff1c5e4',
      },
    },

    'def.group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a **set** and $\\cdot$ a **binary operation** on $G$ (that is, a function $G \\times G \\to G$, $(a, b) \\mapsto a \\cdot b$). Then $(G, \\cdot)$ is a **group** if and only if:\n\n- **associativity**: $(a b) c = a (b c)$ for all $a, b, c \\in G$;\n- **identity**: there exists $e \\in G$ such that $e a = a e = a$ for all $a$;\n- **inverses**: for every $a \\in G$ there exists $a^{-1} \\in G$ such that $a a^{-1} = a^{-1} a = e$.\n\nA group that also satisfies the **commutative law** $a b = b a$ is called an **abelian group**, or **commutative group**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dc564295',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The format is again \"set + operation + axioms\"**: a group is not an object out of nowhere — one set, one function and three identities determine it. To decide whether something is a group, check these identities one by one.\n\n**The identity and inverses are unique**: if $e, e'$ are both identities, then $e = e e' = e'$; if $a^{-1}, a'$ are both inverses of $a$, then $a' = a' e = a' a a^{-1} = e a^{-1} = a^{-1}$. Hence the notations \"$e$\" and \"$a^{-1}$\" are legitimate.\n\n**Examples and counterexamples**: $(\\mathbb{Z}, +)$ is an abelian group; $(\\mathbb{Q} \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an abelian group; $(\\mathbb{Z}, \\cdot)$ is **not** a group (only $\\pm 1$ have multiplicative inverses);\n\n$n \\times n$ invertible matrices form a group under multiplication but **not** an abelian one ($n \\ge 2$).\n\n📌 There is a weaker layer: requiring only associativity and an identity, but not inverses, gives a **monoid**. ($\\mathbb{N}$ under addition is a monoid but not a group.)\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (a group is defined by axioms; shorter criteria must keep their ambient hypotheses):\n- group $\\iff$ associativity, two-sided identity and two-sided inverses: unwinding the definition, with no commutativity requirement.\n- group $\\implies$ uniqueness of identity and inverses: cancellation using associativity and the two-sided identity/inverse identities; no axiom of choice is needed to select inverses, since the inverse is already unique.\n- every element invertible in a monoid $\\implies$ group: direct expansion; cancellation alone is not enough, since the non-negative integers under addition satisfy cancellation but positive elements have no inverses.\n- finite non-empty cancellation semigroup $\\implies$ group: left and right multiplication are injective, hence bijective by finiteness, which yields a common identity and inverses; the infinite counterexample above shows finiteness cannot be dropped.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3dae59fa',
      },
    },

    'def.ring': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a **set** and $+$, $\\cdot$ two **binary operations** on $A$. Then $(A, +, \\cdot)$ is a **ring** if and only if:\n\n- $(A, +)$ is an **abelian group**;\n- $(A, \\cdot)$ satisfies **associativity** (no identity and no commutativity is required);\n- the **distributive laws** $a(b + c) = ab + ac$, $(a + b)c = ac + bc$ hold.\n\nA ring whose multiplication is also commutative is called a **commutative ring**; a ring that moreover has a multiplicative identity $1 \\ne 0$ is called a **ring with identity**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ec2b13e2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Again \"set + operations + axioms\"**, and this layer directly **cites** the abelian group below: the additive axioms are not rewritten, just mentioned in one phrase.\n\n**Contrast with a field**: a field requires every non-zero element to be invertible ($(F\\setminus\\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an abelian group), a ring **does not**.\n\nSo a field is a special case of a ring, and $\\mathbb{Z}$ is a ring but not a field — this is exactly where the counterexample $\\mathbb{Z}$ in the definition of a field comes from.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (here the definition is axiomatic; commutativity and identity are extra requirements):\n- ring $\\iff$ additive abelian group, associative multiplication, left and right distributivity: unwinding the definition adopted in this node; no axiom of choice is needed, nor is a field or PID required.\n- commutative ring $\\implies$ ring, ring with identity $\\implies$ ring: just forget extra structure; neither converse is free — $M_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is not commutative, and $2\\mathbb{Z}$ has no identity under the usual multiplication.\n- adding commutative multiplication $\\iff$ commutative ring; adding an identity together with the convention $1\\ne0$ of this library $\\iff$ ring with identity: still definitional; a ring with identity here is not required to be commutative.\n- field $\\implies$ commutative ring with identity: expand the field axioms; the converse also needs every non-zero element invertible, with $\\mathbb{Z}$ as a counterexample. The zero ring satisfies the ring axioms but not the convention $1\\ne0$ for rings with identity adopted in this node.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#af31758a',
      },
    },

    'def.field': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F$ be a **set** and $+$, $\\cdot$ two **binary operations** on $F$ (that is, two functions $F \\times F \\to F$). Then $(F, +, \\cdot)$ is a **field** if and only if:\n\n- $(F, +)$ is an **abelian group**;\n- $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an **abelian group** (here $0$ is the identity of the additive group);\n- the **distributive law** $a (b + c) = ab + ac$ holds for all $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\nIn one sentence: **addition, subtraction, multiplication and division (by non-zero elements) are all closed, and associativity, commutativity and distributivity hold**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3f226727',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The format is \"set + operations + axioms\"**: a field is not an object out of nowhere — one set, two functions and a few identities determine it.\n\nTo decide whether something is a field, check these identities one by one.\n\nNote that the three items above **cite** the two axiom groups of a group directly: the definition of a field is **one layer on top of the definition of a group**, not a fresh enumeration of identities.\n\nConstructions already available:\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ (rationals): the handiest field;\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ (reals): after constructing it from Cauchy sequences, verify that it satisfies these identities (see \"$\\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field\");\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is **not** a field: $2$ has no multiplicative inverse in it;\n\n$\\mathbb{N}$ is not either: $1 - 2$ is meaningless.\n\n⚠ \"field\" here is not the same as the \"ring\" of measure theory: there a ring is a **ring of sets**, meaning a **family of sets** closed under finite unions and differences — same name, different object.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the compressed form of the field axioms relies on the two groups of group axioms):\n- field $\\iff$ additive abelian group, multiplicative abelian group on non-zero elements, and distributivity: unwinding the definition of this node; the non-zero elements forming a group also makes them non-empty, hence $0\\ne1$.\n- field $\\implies$ division by non-zero elements and no zero divisors: multiplicative inverses give cancellation, and absorption of zero follows from distributivity and additive cancellation; no axiom of choice is needed.\n- non-zero commutative ring with identity in which every non-zero element is invertible $\\implies$ field: verify the group conditions directly; \"non-zero\" cannot be dropped, since the zero ring satisfies the conditions vacuously on non-zero elements yet is not a field.\n- integral domain $\\implies$ field fails in general, e.g. the integers; a finite integral domain does work, since multiplication by a non-zero element is injective and hence bijective on a finite set; finiteness is the key.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0ef25fcc',
      },
    },

    'def.homeomorphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ and $Y$ be topological spaces. $f : X \\to Y$ is a **homeomorphism** if and only if\n\n- $f$ is a bijection;\n- both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are continuous.\n\nIn that case we write $X \\cong Y$ and call the two spaces **homeomorphic**. Homeomorphism is an equivalence relation between topological spaces (identity, inverse and composition all preserve it).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e2bfa0cb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A homeomorphism means \"topologically indistinguishable\": every **topological property** (compactness, connectedness, Hausdorff, closure) is preserved.\n\n⚠ The converse also deserves care: **metric properties change under a homeomorphism**.\n\n$\\mathbb{R}$ is homeomorphic to the open interval $(0, 1)$ ($x \\mapsto \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\arctan x$), yet the former is complete and the latter is not — the classic example that completeness is not a topological property, and the dividing line between the \"compact / closed\" and the \"complete / totally bounded\" families of properties in metric spaces.\n\n**Three equivalent formulations, differing in which side (open/closed) is used**:\n\n- definition (bijection + $f$, $f^{-1}$ continuous) $\\iff$ **$f$ is a continuous open bijection** $\\iff$ **$f$ is a continuous closed bijection**: $(\\implies)$ continuity of $f^{-1}$ makes $f$ open; $(\\Leftarrow)$ openness of $f$ makes $f^{-1}$ continuous. **Both steps use only the duality between open and closed sets; no extra hypothesis is needed.**\n- **a continuous bijection need not be a homeomorphism**: extra hypotheses are required, the two most common being \"compact $\\to$ Hausdorff\" (arguments of the `prop.compact-subset-closed` type) and the open mapping theorem (`thm.open-mapping-banach`, a continuous **linear** bijection between Banach spaces). ⚠️ So the incorrect slogan \"homeomorphism = continuous bijection\" is true only in the **compact Hausdorff** and **Banach** settings.\n\n⚠️ A homeomorphism is an **isomorphism** in the category of topological spaces: it preserves every topological property (connectedness, compactness, countability, separation), which is exactly its meaning as an \"equivalence relation\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c2e9b57',
      },
    },

    'def.metrizable': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ is called **metrizable** if there is a distance $d$ on $X$ such that the topology induced by $d$ is exactly $\\mathcal{T}$:\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_{d} = \\mathcal{T},\\qquad \\mathcal{T}_{d} := \\{\\, U \\subseteq X : \\forall x \\in U\\ \\exists r > 0,\\ B_{d}(x, r) \\subseteq U \\,\\}.$$\n\nEquivalently, $X$ is **homeomorphic** to some metric space, i.e. there is a metric compatible with $\\mathcal{T}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dc8b1458',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**\"There exists a metric\", not \"a metric is specified\"**: the same topology can carry many metrics ($d$ and $\\min(d, 1)$ induce the same topology), so metrizability is a purely topological property, invariant under homeomorphism. \"Complete\", \"bounded\" and \"uniformly continuous\" are not topological properties — they change when the metric is replaced.\n\n⭐ **This is a dividing line for theorems**: a metrizable space automatically acquires Hausdorff, first countability, normality and paracompactness (propositions in this cluster), so every tool available on metric spaces applies. Conversely, second countability $+$ regularity suffices (Urysohn's theorem), and a weaker sufficient condition is a $\\sigma$-locally finite base (Nagata–Smirnov).\n\n⚠️ Not every topological space is metrizable: an uncountable discrete space is metrizable (discrete metric) yet not second countable, while the Sorgenfrey line (lower-limit topology) is normal, separable and first countable but not second countable, hence **not** metrizable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e0cea3b6',
      },
    },

    'thm.tykhonov': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **product** of any family of compact spaces (with the product topology) is again compact — for any number of factors, including uncountably many.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c541f98f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (Alexander's subbase lemma + the axiom of choice).** Write $X = \\prod_{i\\in I} X_{i}$, with projections $p_{i} : X\\to X_{i}$. Take the **subbase**\n$$\\mathcal{S} := \\{\\, p_{i}^{-1}(U_{i}) \\;:\\; i\\in I,\\ U_{i}\\subseteq X_{i}\\ \\text{open} \\,\\}.$$\n**Alexander's subbase lemma**: a topological space is compact $\\iff$ every cover by members of a subbase has a finite subcover. So it suffices to verify the latter.\nLet $\\mathcal{S}_{0} \\subseteq \\mathcal{S}$ cover $X$, and suppose it has **no** finite subcover. For each $i$ put\n$$\\mathcal{U}_{i} := \\{\\, U_{i} : p_{i}^{-1}(U_{i}) \\in \\mathcal{S}_{0} \\,\\},\\qquad F_{i} := X_{i}\\setminus \\bigcup \\mathcal{U}_{i}.$$\n- **$F_{i} \\ne \\emptyset$**: otherwise $\\mathcal{U}_{i}$ is an open cover of the compact space $X_{i}$; take a finite subcover $U_{1},\\ldots,U_{n}$, and the corresponding $p_{i}^{-1}(U_{1}),\\ldots,p_{i}^{-1}(U_{n})$ are a finite subcover of $X$ — contradicting the assumption ✓.\n- By the **axiom of choice** choose $x_{i} \\in F_{i}$, and set $x := (x_{i})_{i\\in I} \\in X$.\n- Then for every $p_{i}^{-1}(U_{i})\\in \\mathcal{S}_{0}$ we have $x_{i} \\notin U_{i}$ (because $x_{i}\\in F_{i}$), so $x \\notin p_{i}^{-1}(U_{i})$ holds for **all** members — contradicting that $\\mathcal{S}_{0}$ covers $X$ ✓.\nThus every subbase cover has a finite subcover, and by Alexander's lemma $X$ is compact. ∎\n(Alexander's lemma itself: if a family of open sets covering the space has no finite subcover, use Zorn's lemma to enlarge it to a maximal such family $\\mathcal{M}$; then the choice of \"non-$\\mathcal{M}$ members\" produces data attached to the subbase, and one deduces step by step that $\\mathcal{M}$ is actually a cover — a contradiction.)\n\n⭐ **Tychonoff's theorem is equivalent to the axiom of choice** (they imply each other in ZF) — a standard gauge of how strong the axiom of choice is.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7f93e3f9',
      },
    },

    'def.one-point-compactification': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: the most economical way of adding a single point — add **one** point $\\infty$ so that every sequence running off to infinity converges to it. The passage from $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ to $S^{n}$, and from $\\mathbb{C}$ to the Riemann sphere, are both instances of this construction.\n\nLet $X$ be a topological space. Take $X^{*} := X \\sqcup \\{\\infty\\}$, with the topology whose open sets are\n\n1. the open sets of $X$;\n2. sets of the form $U \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$, where $U \\subseteq X$ is open and $X \\setminus U$ is **compact** in $X$.\n\n$X^{*}$ is called the **one-point compactification** of $X$ (also the Alexandrov compactification).\n\n**Theorem**:\n\n- $X^{*}$ is **compact** (for arbitrary $X$);\n- $X^{*}$ is **Hausdorff** $\\iff$ $X$ is a locally compact Hausdorff space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bb5c0641',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Topological intuition**: $U \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ is open $\\iff$ its complement is compact. So \"the complement of $X$ is compact\" is \"a neighbourhood of $\\infty$\" — the space around the point at infinity is exactly \"what remains after $X$ escapes\". Thus the compactification of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ has its point $\\infty$ with neighbourhoods that are \"outside a ball\", and $S^{n}$ is the north pole.\n\n⭐ **Standard examples**: $(\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{*} \\cong S^{n}$ (stereographic projection; for $n = 1$, $\\mathbb{R} \\cup \\{\\infty\\} \\cong S^{1}$);\n\n$\\mathbb{C}^{*} \\cong S^{2}$ is the **Riemann sphere**, on which complex analysis treats $\\infty$ as an ordinary point;\n\nthe one-point compactification of $\\mathbb{N}$ is $\\{1/n\\} \\cup \\{0\\}$.\n\n⚠️ **Why local compactness is required**: when $X$ is not locally compact, $X^{*}$ is not Hausdorff — intuitively \"a neighbourhood of $\\infty$\" is so large that it meets a neighbourhood of some point forever. So existence and Hausdorffness are **two separate matters**: $X^{*}$ is always compact, but Hausdorffness costs local compactness.\n\n⚠️ **Relation to \"locally compact Hausdorff implies completely regular\"**: that proof uses exactly \"$X^{*}$ is compact Hausdorff $\\implies$ normal $\\implies$ one can build a Urysohn function on $X^{*}$ and pull it back\". The one-point compactification is the standard interface that transfers the good properties of compact spaces to $X$.\n\n**The two conclusions rest on completely different hypotheses** (which is why this deserves a separate node):\n\n- **$X^{*}$ is compact: no hypothesis is needed.** Take any open cover of $X^{*}$; some member contains $\\infty$ (call it $U\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$), so $X\\setminus U$ is compact; it is covered by the remaining open sets, and compactness gives a finite subcover, which together with $U\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ itself gives a finite subcover. The whole argument uses only \"an open cover of a compact set has a finite subcover\", **not Hausdorff, not local compactness**.\n- **$X^{*}$ is Hausdorff $\\iff$ $X$ is locally compact Hausdorff**: both directions cost something.\n  $\\cdot$ ($\\Leftarrow$) needs **local compactness**: given $x \\in X$, separating it from $\\infty$ requires a compact neighbourhood of $x$, call it $K$, so $X\\setminus K$ is open and $K^{\\circ}$ is an open neighbourhood of $x$, disjoint. **Without local compactness this compact neighbourhood cannot be built**.\n  $\\cdot$ ($\\implies$): going back from $X^{*}$ being Hausdorff to $X$ being Hausdorff is free (subspaces inherit it); going back to local compactness uses **closedness of compact sets**: the open neighbourhood of $\\infty$, say $U\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, satisfies $X\\setminus U$ compact, so the closure of $U$ in $X$... This is exactly the use of \"in a compact Hausdorff space compact sets are closed\".\n\n⚠️ **So \"the one-point compactification always exists\" must be read in two parts**: $X^{*}$ always exists as a compact space; but it is **Hausdorff** only when $X$ is locally compact Hausdorff. Many textbooks state only the first, so beginners think \"every space can be compactified\" — **false**: those that admit a Hausdorff compactification are the locally compact Hausdorff ones.\n\n⚠️ **Why the neighbourhoods of $\\infty$ have that form**: \"complement compact\" in the second axiom is not arbitrary — if the neighbourhood is $U\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, then points \"running to infinity\" must eventually lie in $U$, i.e. eventually leave $X\\setminus U$; requiring $X\\setminus U$ compact is exactly what aligns \"leaving it\" with \"compactness\", so that $X^{*}$ is compact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aef0ac5b'
      },
    },

    'def.filter': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: rewrite convergence in the language of **families of sets**. A filter abstracts the phrase \"eventually falls into a given set\", so limits can be discussed without mentioning indices, only families of sets — the handiest tool at the interface of set theory and topology.\n\nLet $X$ be a topological space. A nonempty family $\\mathcal{F} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$ is a **filter** if\n\n1. $\\emptyset \\notin \\mathcal{F}$, $X \\in \\mathcal{F}$;\n2. upward closed: $F \\in \\mathcal{F}$ and $F \\subseteq G$ $\\implies$ $G \\in \\mathcal{F}$;\n3. closed under finite intersections: $F, G \\in \\mathcal{F}$ $\\implies$ $F \\cap G \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\n**Convergence**: $\\mathcal{F} \\to x$ if every neighbourhood of $x$ belongs to $\\mathcal{F}$ (i.e. $\\mathcal{N}(x) \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}$).\n\nAn **ultrafilter** is a maximal filter. Equivalently, for every $A \\subseteq X$, **exactly one** of $A$ and $X \\setminus A$ belongs to $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\n**Ultrafilter lemma**: every filter is contained in an ultrafilter (using Zorn's lemma, `lem.zorn`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9f9ad3dd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **What filters are for: a clean characterization of compactness**: $X$ is compact $\\iff$ every ultrafilter converges (equivalently, every filter has a cluster point). This is shorter than the net version, because the \"subnet\" step is absorbed by maximality of the ultrafilter.\n\n⭐ **The ultrafilter lemma cannot be dispensed with**: it is not a theorem but **a small piece of the axiom of choice** (strictly weaker than AC, but equivalent to the Boolean prime ideal theorem). So every proof that \"takes an ultrafilter\" is secretly using choice — in contrast with the family of arguments in `thm.paracompact-hausdorff-normal` that use **only compactness**.\n\n⚠️ **The neighbourhood system of the filter $\\ne$**: $\\mathcal{N}(x)$ itself is a filter (the neighbourhood filter), whereas a general filter may **fail to converge**. Convergence depends on whether it contains some $\\mathcal{N}(x)$.\n\n⚠️ **Name clash with \"filter\" in category theory**: `prop.covering-sieves-filter` concerns the filteredness of sieves in a Grothendieck topology, and is **unrelated** to the set-theoretic filter here.\n\n**Of the three axioms, \"closed under finite intersections\" is the only one with content** (the other two are almost mere normalization):\n\n- $\\emptyset \\notin \\mathcal{F}$ and $X \\in \\mathcal{F}$: **normalization**. Requiring the family to be nonempty and to omit the empty set, together with upward closure, automatically gives $X$.\n- **Upward closure**: part of the definition; dropping it leaves a \"filter base\", for which one must additionally require that any two members have a smaller member.\n- **Closure under finite intersections**: **this is the real condition**, and the reason filters can be translated into nets (`eq.net-filter`).\n\n**Two ways of writing convergence**: $\\mathcal{F} \\to x$ $\\iff$ $\\mathcal{N}(x) \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}$ $\\iff$ $\\mathcal{F}$, which is **finer** than the neighbourhood filter. ⚠️ The direction is easy to invert: **the larger (finer) the filter, the easier it converges**.\n\n**The ultrafilter lemma (every filter lies in an ultrafilter) needs Zorn's lemma** — the only dependence on AC in this chain. It also makes the characterization \"compact $\\iff$ every ultrafilter converges\" **non-constructive**.\n\n⚠️ Unrelated to \"filteredness\" in category theory (`def.filtered-category`); the two share only the word \"filter\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e0cecc2b',
      },
    },

    'def.interval': {
      statement: {
        en: "An **interval** of $\\mathbb{R}$ is a subset $I$ satisfying\n\n$$a, b \\in I,\\ a \\le x \\le b \\implies x \\in I$$\n\ni.e. it has \"no gaps\". The usual bounded intervals are written\n\n$$(a, b), \\quad [a, b], \\quad [a, b), \\quad (a, b]$$\n\ndistinguished by whether the endpoints are included; there are also the unbounded intervals $(-\\infty, b)$, $(a, +\\infty)$, $\\mathbb{R}$, and the singleton $[a, a]$.\n\nIts **length** (the convention of Lebesgue measure $m$) is $b - a$ (requiring $a \\le b$; for $a > b$, $(a, b] = \\emptyset$, which needs a separate convention).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e01ecf78',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The half-open interval $\\{ (a, b] : a < b \\}$ is the starting point for constructing Lebesgue–Stieltjes measure**: it is only a basic class; finite disjoint unions give a ring, and length is already a premeasure on that ring.\n\nWhy half-open rather than open intervals: lengths of disjoint unions are easy to compute — $[a, b) \\sqcup [b, c) = [a, c)$ fails for open intervals.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6328540b',
      },
    },

    'def.derivative': {
      statement: {
        en: "$F : (a, b) \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is **differentiable** at $x_0 \\in (a, b)$ if and only if the limit\n\n$$F'(x_0) := \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{F(x_0 + h) - F(x_0)}{h}$$\n\nexists (and is finite). This number is the **derivative** of $F$ at $x_0$.\n\nIf it is differentiable everywhere on $(a, b)$, we obtain a function $F'$; $F$ is then called **differentiable**.\n\nIf the limit is not required to exist and $\\pm\\infty$ is allowed, one obtains the **generalized derivative**, which is needed in the theory of derivatives of monotone functions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ac920c3f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Differentiation theory cares about **pointwise** behaviour, so the domain is often shrunk to $L^1_{loc}$ rather than $L^1$.\n\nKey points (the BV / AC chain relies on all of them):\n- differentiability $\\implies$ continuity, but not conversely ($|x|$ at $0$).\n\n- A monotone **increasing** function is **differentiable almost everywhere**, and $F' \\ge 0$ a.e. (for decreasing functions, $F' \\le 0$).\n\n- **Differentiability a.e. does not guarantee** $F(b) - F(a) = \\int_a^b F'$ (the Cantor function is continuous everywhere, differentiable a.e., and $F' = 0$ a.e., yet $F(1) - F(0) = 1 \\ne 0$; note that it is **not differentiable everywhere**: at $1/3$ the one-sided difference quotients $0$ and $(3/2)^{k} \\to \\infty$ differ);\n\nEquality requires absolute continuity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b97be3dc',
      },
    },

    'def.function-limit': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be defined on a **punctured neighbourhood** of $x_0$, with $L \\in \\mathbb{R}$. We say that $f(x)$ tends to $L$ as $x \\to x_0$ (written $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} f(x) = L$) if and only if\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists \\delta > 0 :\\ 0 < |x - x_0| < \\delta \\implies |f(x) - L| < \\varepsilon$$\n\n**Heine characterization**: $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} f(x) = L$ if and only if for every sequence with $x_n \\ne x_0$ and $x_n \\to x_0$, we have $f(x_n) \\to L$.\n\nReplacing $0 < |x - x_0| < \\delta$ by other ranges ($x > M$, $x \\to x_0^{+}$, $|x| > M$, etc.) yields $x \\to \\pm\\infty$, one-sided limits and various other variants.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fa811121',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The only difference from the limit of a **sequence** is this: here the variable approaches continuously, so an extra quantifier $\\delta$ is needed to control \"how close is close enough\".\n\nThe whole novelty of the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ language lies in this two-level quantifier.\n\n⚠ **Puncturing** is essential: the point $x = x_0$ does not participate in the limit, so whether $f$ is defined at $x_0$, and with what value, does not affect the limit.\n\nFor the same reason, \"the limit exists and equals $f(x_0)$\" can serve as the definition of **continuity**.\n\n⚠ A limit, if it exists, is unique — the proof is the same as in the sequence case (split $\\varepsilon$ between the two points).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c2c83665',
      },
    },

    'thm.squeeze': {
      statement: {
        en: "**(Sequences)** Suppose there is $N_0$ such that $a_n \\le x_n \\le b_n$ for all $n \\ge N_0$. If $a_n \\to L$ and $b_n \\to L$, then $x_n \\to L$.\n\n**(Functions)** Suppose $g(x) \\le f(x) \\le h(x)$ holds on some punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$. If\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to x_0} g(x) = \\lim_{x \\to x_0} h(x) = L$$\n\nthen $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} f(x) = L$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#03a41405',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The squeeze theorem is one of the **few tools that can assert the existence of a limit without knowing its value in advance** (the other two are the monotone bounded theorem and the Cauchy criterion).\n\nIts most typical uses are defining $e$, proving $\\lim_{n} q^{n} = 0$ ($|q| < 1$), and $\\lim_{n} \\sqrt[n]{n} = 1$.\n\n⚠ Both hypotheses are indispensable: the function must be squeezed **on both sides**, and the two outer limits must be **equal**.\n\nSqueezing on one side alone yields no conclusion at all.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fb1ae0ad',
      },
    },

    'def.limsup-liminf': {
      statement: {
        en: "For a sequence of real numbers $\\{x_n\\}$, set\n\n$$\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} x_n := \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\Bigl( \\sup_{k \\ge n} x_k \\Bigr), \\qquad \\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} x_n := \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\Bigl( \\inf_{k \\ge n} x_k \\Bigr)$$\n\n($n \\mapsto \\sup_{k \\ge n} x_k$ is monotone decreasing and $n \\mapsto \\inf_{k \\ge n} x_k$ is monotone increasing, so both limits in $\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ **always exist**.)\n\nMoreover, the limit of the sequence exists (allowing the value $\\pm\\infty$) if and only if the upper and lower limits agree, i.e.\n\n$$\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} x_n = \\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} x_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#664d7f81',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The upper limit is \"the largest of the limits of all convergent subsequences\" (counting $\\pm \\infty$ as well) — hence **every** sequence of real numbers has upper and lower limits, whereas only convergent sequences have a limit: they are a **relaxed version** of the limit.\n\n⭐ Their appearance in measure theory is crucial: Fatou's lemma is exactly $\\int \\liminf f_n \\le \\liminf \\int f_n$ (**requiring $f_n \\ge 0$**, or $f_n \\ge g \\in L^{1}$; otherwise a counterexample: on $\\mathbb{R}$, for $f_n = -\\mathbf{1}_{[n,n+1)}$, the left-hand side is $0$ and the right-hand side is $-1$) — no \"limit\" is available there, because $f_n$ need not converge.\n\n⚠ $\\limsup$ and $\\sup$ are not the same thing: $\\sup_n x_n$ is the supremum of all terms, whereas $\\limsup_n x_n$ looks only at the **tail**; thus for $x_n = (-1)^{n}$ we have $\\sup x_n = 1$, while $\\limsup x_n = 1$ and $\\liminf x_n = -1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#31991fc1',
      },
    },

    'thm.ivt': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be continuous on $[a, b]$, and let $c$ be **any** real number between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$. Then there exists $\\xi \\in [a, b]$ with\n\n$$f(\\xi) = c$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#49326a89',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric content: in passing from $f(a)$ to $f(b)$, the graph of a continuous function **cannot skip any intermediate value** — a continuous function maps an interval to an interval.\n\n⭐ This is the most frequently used existence tool in elementary analysis: existence of roots, of inverse functions, of fixed points, and comparison of intermediate values all follow from it.\n\nIt also has **constructive** content: the bisection method used in the proof is itself a root-finding algorithm, and every step is computable.\n\n⚠ It guarantees only **existence**, not uniqueness ($x^{3} - x$ has three zeros on $[-2, 2]$);\n\nuniqueness requires an additional assumption of **strict monotonicity**.\n\n⚠ It uses completeness of $\\mathbb{R}$ (the least-upper-bound principle), not compactness: with $[a, b]$ replaced by a general connected space it still holds, and that is the fixed-point / connectedness branch.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b19a6984',
      },
    },

    'thm.evt': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a **nonempty** compact metric space and $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ continuous. Then $f$ **attains** its maximum and minimum: there exist $x_{\\min}, x_{\\max} \\in X$ such that for all $x \\in X$,\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\le f(x) \\le f(x_{\\max})$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6638507a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Being bounded\" and \"attaining an extremum\" are two different things, which is why they are separate nodes here: boundedness says only that $\\sup f(X)$ is **finite**, while attaining an extremum says that this supremum is **actually reached at some point**.\n\nCompactness is responsible for the latter.\n\n⚠ Replacing the space by an open interval makes it fail at once: $f(x) = x$ is bounded on $(0, 1)$, yet attains neither a maximum nor a minimum.\n\n⭐ The commonly used form over $\\mathbb{R}$: **a continuous function on a closed interval $[a, b]$ attains its extrema** — the first step of the proof is that a closed interval is compact (Heine–Borel).\n\nThe whole procedure for finding extrema in elementary calculus (locate critical points and compare endpoints) rests on this.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#77b1f6c0',
      },
    },

    'def.pointwise-convergence': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f_n : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ ($n = 1, 2, \\ldots$) and $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$. We say that $\\{f_n\\}$ **converges pointwise** to $f$ if and only if for **every fixed** $x \\in X$,\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall n \\ge N :\\ |f_n(x) - f(x)| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nWe write $f_n \\to f$ (pointwise).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#45ce281a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Note the order of the quantifiers: $\\exists N$ comes **after** $\\forall x$ — so $N$ here may depend on $\\varepsilon$ **and $x$**.\n\nEach point gets its own $N$, independently of the others.\n\n⚠ Pointwise convergence is too weak: the limit function may be **discontinuous**, and limit and integral need **not commute**.\n\nExample: on $[0, 1]$, $f_n(x) = x^{n}$ converges pointwise to the discontinuous function that equals $0$ for $x < 1$ and $1$ for $x = 1$.\n\n⭐ Pointwise convergence is a \"pointwise\" notion, and checking it point by point suffices;\n\nall the difficulty lies in **whether $x$ can be controlled by a single $N$**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cd160750',
      },
    },

    'def.borel': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a topological space. The **Borel $\\sigma$-algebra** on $X$ is the $\\sigma$-algebra generated by all open sets:\n\n$\\mathfrak{B}_X := \\mathcal{M}(\\{ U \\subseteq X : U$ is open $\\})$\n\nSets in $\\mathfrak{B}_X$ are called **Borel sets**: open sets, closed sets, countable intersections of open sets ($G_\\delta$), countable unions of closed sets ($F_\\sigma$), and so on all belong to $\\mathfrak{B}_X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fec2c65d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The family of open sets is in general only a topology, not a $\\sigma$-algebra — one must take the $\\sigma$-algebra it generates to obtain all Borel sets.\n\nOn $\\mathbb{R}$, $\\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{R}$ can also be generated by the half-open intervals $\\{ (a, b] : a < b \\}$ (or { [a, b) }). These forms are more convenient when constructing measures, because disjoint unions of half-open intervals are easy to compute.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5757819c',
      },
    },

    'def.isomorphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{C}$ be a category and $f : X \\to Y$ a morphism in it. We say that $f$ is an **isomorphism** if there exists a morphism $g : Y \\to X$ with\n\n$$g \\circ f = \\mathrm{id}_{X}, \\qquad f \\circ g = \\mathrm{id}_{Y}.$$\n\nSuch a $g$ is unique (if $g, g'$ both work, then $g = g' f g = g'$); we write $g = f^{-1}$. In this case $X$ and $Y$ are called **isomorphic**, written $X \\cong Y$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7b045f9d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **It is the common name for one concept across the branches** (previously only the three-in-one `def.mono` of category theory existed):\n\n- sets / $\\mathbf{Set}$: isomorphism = **bijection**;\n\n- groups, rings, modules: isomorphism = bijective homomorphism;\n\n- topology / $\\mathbf{Top}$: isomorphism = **homeomorphism** (`def.homeomorphism`);\n\n- vector spaces: isomorphism = **linear isomorphism** (`def.linear-isomorphism`);\n\n- posets: isomorphism = **order isomorphism** (`def.order-iso`);\n\n- whereas \"monomorphism / epimorphism\" is **another pair of concepts** — in a general category mono + epi does **not** imply isomorphism (for example, in $\\mathbf{Top}$ a continuous bijection need not be a homeomorphism), so it cannot serve as the common name for isomorphism.\n\n⚠ **\"Isomorphic\" is not \"equal\"**: isomorphic means \"indistinguishable under a structure-preserving map\". Almost all statements here hold only up to isomorphism (for example \"$V \\cong K^{n}$\" rather than \"$V = K^{n}$\").\n\n📌 Difference from \"equivalence\": an **equivalence** between categories (discussed in the `def.adjoint` family) requires only \"isomorphic up to a natural isomorphism\" and is weaker than isomorphism; isomorphism is the strongest tier.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b0580091',
      },
    },

    'def.limit': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **limit** of the diagram $D : I \\to \\mathcal{C}$ is the cone over $D$ with the universal property: a cone $(\\lim D,\\ p_i)$ such that for every cone $(Y,\\ g_i)$ there is a **unique** morphism $g : Y \\to \\lim D$ with\n\n$$p_i \\circ g = g_i \\qquad (i \\in I)$$\n\nEquivalently, the cone functor is represented by $\\lim D$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}^{I}}(\\Delta Y, D) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(Y, \\lim D)$$\n\nDually, the **colimit** $\\operatorname{colim} D$ of $D$ satisfies $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}^{I}}(D, \\Delta Y) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(\\operatorname{colim} D, Y)$.\n\nWhen $I$ is finite it is called a **finite limit**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8bccceb3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The universal property says just one thing: **every other cone factors through it uniquely**. Hence a limit, if it exists, is unique up to isomorphism — there is no need to choose a particular construction; any one will do.\n\nTwo equivalent formulations: \"a universal cone\" and \"$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}^{I}}(\\Delta -, D)$ is representable\". The latter turns the limit into a computable isomorphism.\n\n\"Universal\" can be pushed further: a limit is a **terminal object** in the category of cones (see \"element category\"). This sentence recurs repeatedly later.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5753e66b',
      },
    },

    'def.product': {
      statement: {
        en: "Take $I$ to be a **discrete category** (objects only, no non-identity morphisms); then the diagram $D : I \\to \\mathcal{C}$ is just a family of objects $(X_i)_{i \\in I}$. Its limit is called the **product** $\\prod_{i} X_i$, and its colimit the **coproduct** $\\coprod_{i} X_i$. Written as an isomorphism of hom-sets, the universal property is\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}\\Bigl(Y, \\prod_{i} X_i\\Bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\prod_{i} \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(Y, X_i)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#87d0ec57',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In one sentence: **a product is \"one map = a family of maps\"**. Maps into the product correspond bijectively to families of maps into each factor, and the projection $p_i$ extracts the $i$-th component.\n\nExamples: in $\\mathbf{Set}$ the product is the Cartesian product and the coproduct the disjoint union; in $\\mathbf{Top}$ the product carries the **product topology** (the coarsest topology making it a product) and the coproduct the **disjoint-union topology** (the finest one); in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ the product is the direct product $\\prod_i A_i$ and the coproduct the direct sum $\\bigoplus_i A_i$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d340eaa7',
      },
    },

    'def.ideal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring. A subset $I \\subseteq A$ is a **(two-sided) ideal** if $(I, +)$ is an additive subgroup and, for all $a \\in A$ and $x \\in I$,\n\n$$a x \\in I, \\qquad x a \\in I.$$\n\n(An ideal requiring only $a x \\in I$ is a **left ideal**, and one requiring only $x a \\in I$ is a **right ideal**; for a commutative ring the three notions agree.)\n\nThe construction of quotient rings (quotient set, well-defined operations, universal property) is a separate node, see `def.quotient-ring`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#679bacae',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why ideals are defined this way.** The problem is exactly the same as for normal subgroups: to define multiplication on the quotient set, $(a + I)(b + I)$ must not depend on the representatives. Compute $(a + x)(b + y) = ab + ay + xb + xy$ — the last three terms must all lie in $I$. That $xy \\in I$ is **not** guaranteed by \"$I$ is a subgroup\" and must be **required separately**.\n\n⭐ **Comparison table**: to quotient a **group** and get a group, one quotients by a **normal subgroup**; to quotient a **ring** and get a ring, one quotients by an **ideal**. (As an additive subgroup, $I$ is of course automatically normal — the additive group is abelian — so no extra condition is needed on that side.)\n\nThe **principal ideal** $(a) := \\{\\, xa : x \\in A \\,\\}$ (in a commutative ring, the set of all multiples of $a$). ⚠️ In a general ring this set is only a **left** ideal: for $A = M_2(\\mathbb{R})$ and $a = E_{11}$, $E_{11}E_{12} = E_{12} \\notin Aa$. In a general ring the correct definition of $(a)$ is \"the smallest two-sided ideal containing $a$\". $A/(a)$ is the ring obtained by \"setting $a$ equal to $0$\"; $\\mathbb{Z}/(n) = \\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$ is congruence modulo $n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a13577ce',
      },
    },

    'def.module': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a unital ring and $M$ an **abelian group**. We say that $M$ is a **$A$-module** if there is a map\n\n$$A \\times M \\longrightarrow M, \\qquad (a, m) \\longmapsto a \\cdot m,$$\n\nsatisfying $a(bm) = (ab)m$, $(a + b)m = am + bm$, $a(m + n) = am + an$, $1 \\cdot m = m$.\n\nAn abelian-group homomorphism preserving this action is called a **$A$-module homomorphism**: $f(am) = a f(m)$. All $A$-modules with module homomorphisms form the category $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0dadc00c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **A module = an abelian group + a ring action**, so it is a child of both \"group\" and \"ring\": a $\\mathbb{Z}$-module **is** an abelian group ($n \\cdot m$ admits only one definition); a module over a field **is** a vector space. These two statements place \"module\" midway between known examples.\n\n**Why $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ is the model example of homological algebra**: it is simultaneously\n\n- an **abelian category** (it has kernels and cokernels, and all monomorphisms and epimorphisms are strict);\n\n- **with enough injective objects** (every module embeds into an injective module);\n\n- **with enough projective objects** (free modules are projective);\n\n- a **Grothendieck category** (it has a generator, $A$ itself, and filtered colimits are exact).\n\nSo \"what can be done in $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$\" is exactly what homological algebra wants to generalize to arbitrary categories. **Everything other categories can do is measured against it.**",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#09652db5',
      },
    },

    'def.subgroup': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(G, \\cdot)$ be a group and $H \\subseteq G$. We say that $H$ is a **subgroup** of $G$ (written $H \\le G$) if $H$ is itself a group under the **restricted operation** — that is:\n\n- $e \\in H$;\n\n- $a, b \\in H \\implies ab \\in H$ (closed under the operation);\n\n- $a \\in H \\implies a^{-1} \\in H$ (closed under inverses).\n\n(Associativity is inherited from $G$ and need not be checked again.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#09d424e9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The two conditions can be combined into one: $H \\ne \\emptyset$ and $a, b \\in H \\implies a b^{-1} \\in H$ (without $H \\ne \\emptyset$ the condition is vacuous: $H = \\emptyset \\subseteq G$ satisfies the universal statement, yet is not a subgroup).\n\nThe intersection of any number of subgroups is again a subgroup, but the **union** in general is not — this is why \"the subgroup generated by $S$, namely $\\langle S \\rangle$\" is defined as \"the intersection of all subgroups containing $S$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c6a59daa',
      },
    },

    'def.topology-base': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space and $\\mathcal{B} \\subseteq \\mathcal{T}$. Then $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **base** for the topology $\\mathcal{T}$ if and only if every open set is a union of members of $\\mathcal{B}$. If one further requires $\\mathcal{T}$ to be **exactly** the topology generated by $\\mathcal{B}$ (that is, $\\mathcal{B} \\subseteq \\mathcal{T}$, and $\\mathcal{T}$ is precisely the family of all unions of members of $\\mathcal{B}$), this is equivalent to:\n\n1. $\\bigcup \\mathcal{B} = X$;\n2. for $B_1, B_2 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ and $x \\in B_1 \\cap B_2$, there exists $B_3 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ with $x \\in B_3 \\subseteq B_1 \\cap B_2$.\n\nA family $\\mathcal{S} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$ is a **subbase** if and only if all its **finite intersections** form a base.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#02b4b5c4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Why bases are useful: **a condition checked on a base holds automatically for all open sets.**\n\n- In metric spaces: all open balls form a base;\n- On $\\mathbb{R}$: open intervals form a base; the half-open intervals $\\{ (a, b] \\}$ generate a base for **another topology** (the upper limit topology) — they are not the usual open sets, hence **not** a base for the usual topology (although they are handier when constructing the Lebesgue–Stieltjes measure);\n\n- The cylinder sets of the product topology and of the $\\sigma$-algebra follow the same pattern: first fix a convenient family of sets, then take what it generates.\n\n⚠ A base is not unique: the same topology can have many bases.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (generating a topology and being a base for a specified topology must be kept distinct):\n- Being a base for the given $\\mathcal T$ $\\iff$ every $\\mathcal T$-open set is a union of basic elements: this is definition unpacking; the covering and local-intersection conditions then follow using $X$ and the fact that the intersection of two basic elements is open.\n- Covering plus local-intersection conditions $\\implies$ the arbitrary unions of basic elements form a topology: finite intersections are handled by local refinement, the remaining axioms are checked directly, no choice is needed, and one may take all basic elements satisfying the conditions.\n- The two conditions listed in the node do not by themselves imply being a base for the given $\\mathcal T$: one also needs $\\mathcal T$ to equal the generated topology. For example, on the discrete two-point space take $\\mathcal B=\\{X\\}$; it satisfies both conditions yet does not generate the singleton open sets.\n- A subbase $\\iff$ the family of all finite intersections is a base: by convention this is definition unpacking, and finite intersections should include the empty intersection $X$; for the usual topology on the reals the basic elements must be usual open sets, while half-open intervals generate another topology.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7900e5e3',
      },
    },

    'def.metric-continuity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, d)$ and $(Y, \\rho)$ be metric spaces, $f : X \\to Y$, and $x_0 \\in X$.\n\nWe say $f$ is **continuous at** $x_0$ if\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0\\ \\exists \\delta > 0:\\quad d(x, x_0) < \\delta \\implies \\rho(f(x), f(x_0)) < \\varepsilon .$$\n\nWe say $f$ is continuous if it is continuous at every point. Equivalently: for every open set $V \\subseteq Y$, the preimage $f^{-1}(V)$ is open in $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0fade8e4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof of the equivalence** (global version):\n\n($\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$ $\\implies$ open preimages are open) Let $V \\subseteq Y$ be open and $x_0 \\in f^{-1}(V)$. Choose $\\varepsilon > 0$ with $B_Y(f(x_0), \\varepsilon) \\subseteq V$; $\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$ gives $\\delta > 0$ such that the image of $B_X(x_0, \\delta)$ lies in $B_Y(f(x_0), \\varepsilon)$, hence $B_X(x_0, \\delta) \\subseteq f^{-1}(V)$, i.e. $f^{-1}(V)$ is open.\n\n(open preimages are open $\\implies$ $\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$) Given $x_0$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$, $B_Y(f(x_0), \\varepsilon)$ is open, so $f^{-1}(B_Y(f(x_0), \\varepsilon))$ is open and contains $x_0$; choose $\\delta > 0$ so that $B_X(x_0, \\delta)$ lies in it, which is exactly the $\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$ statement. $\\blacksquare$\n\n📌 This is `def.continuous-map` (the topological version) written out for metric spaces: open sets in a metric space are generated by open balls, so “preimages are open” is equivalent to $\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$.\n\n⭐ The case $\\mathbb{R}$ is treated in `def.continuous` (the $\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$ criterion) and `def.real-metric-space` ($d(x,y) = |x-y|$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#28ccfd05',
      },
    },

    'def.complete': {
      statement: {
        en: "A sequence $\\{x_{n}\\}$ is a **Cauchy sequence** if and only if\n\n$$\\forall\\varepsilon > 0, \\exists N : m, n \\ge N \\implies d(x_m, x_n) < \\varepsilon$$\n\nA metric space $(X, d)$ is **complete** if and only if every Cauchy sequence in $X$ converges in $X$:\n\n$$\\{x_n\\}\\ \\text{Cauchy} \\implies \\exists x \\in X : x_n \\to x$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4665198b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "“Convergent $\\implies Cauchy$” always holds; **“$Cauchy \\implies$ converges” is the content of completeness**.\n\nExamples: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $\\mathbb{C}^{n}$, and every Banach space are complete; $\\mathbb{Q}$ is not (3, 3.1, 3.14, …, a Cauchy sequence converging to $\\sqrt 2$ that does not converge in $\\mathbb{Q}$).\n\n**Closed subsets inherit completeness**: when $X$ is complete, $A \\subseteq X$ is complete as a subspace $\\iff A$ closed in $X$. This will be used below in the characterisation of compactness of subsets.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d254b5af',
      },
    },

    'prop.compact-subset-closed': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a subset of a metric space $X$ (with the subspace metric). If $A$ is **compact**, then $A$ is **closed** in $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f78af910',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In fact it suffices that $X$ be Hausdorff: compact subsets are then closed, and metric spaces are Hausdorff.\n\nPutting this together with “$X$ is complete” prepares the **subset** characterisation of compactness below: that result uses “$A$ compact $\\implies A$ closed” and “$A$ closed $X$ complete $\\implies A$ complete”.\n\n**Proof.** Let $A$ be compact and take $x \\in X\\setminus A$; we must show it is an interior point.\n- For each $a \\in A$, by Hausdorffness take an open neighbourhood $U_{a}$ of $a$ and an open neighbourhood $V_{a}$ of $x$ with $U_{a}\\cap V_{a} = \\emptyset$ (in a metric space take $U_{a} = B(a, r/2)$ and $V_{a} = B(x, r/2)$, where $r = d(a,x)$);\n- $\\{U_{a}\\}_{a\\in A}$ is an open cover of $A$; by **compactness** take a finite subcover $U_{a_{1}}, \\ldots, U_{a_{n}}$;\n- $V := \\bigcap_{i=1}^{n} V_{a_{i}}$ is an open neighbourhood of $x$, and $V\\cap U_{a_{i}} = \\emptyset$ holds for every $i$, hence $V \\cap A = \\emptyset$, i.e. $V \\subseteq X\\setminus A$ ✓.\nThus $X\\setminus A$ is open and $A$ is closed. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1bc20fbc',
      },
    },

    'def.net': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: in general topology **sequences do not suffice** — neither compactness nor continuity can be characterised by sequences (unless the space is first countable). Replacing $\\mathbb{N}$ by an arbitrary **directed set** yields a “net”, and then every theorem about sequences carries over.\n\n**Directed set**: a preordered set $(\\Lambda, \\le)$ such that any $\\lambda, \\mu \\in \\Lambda$ have an upper bound (there exists $\\nu$ with $\\lambda, \\mu \\le \\nu$).\n\n**Net**: a map $x : \\Lambda \\to X$, written $(x_{\\lambda})_{\\lambda \\in \\Lambda}$.\n\n**Convergence**: a net $x_{\\lambda}$ **converges to** $x$ (written $x_{\\lambda} \\to x$) if for every neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ there exists $\\lambda_{0}$ such that\n\n$$\\lambda \\ge \\lambda_{0} \\implies x_{\\lambda} \\in U .$$\n\n**Two characterisation theorems**:\n\n- $f : X \\to Y$ is continuous $\\iff$ $f$ sends every convergent net to a convergent net (`def.continuous-map`);\n- $X$ is compact $\\iff$ every net in $X$ has a convergent **subnet**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3a45ea8b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ **Why sequences are not enough**: in an uncountable space a singleton can be closed without being “sequentially closed” (for example $\\omega_{1}$ in $[0,\\omega_{1}]$: no sequence approaches it from below, yet it lies in the closure).\n\nSo “$x \\in \\overline{A}$ $\\iff$ there is a sequence in $A$ converging to $x$” **holds only in first-countable spaces**.\n\nNets fix this at once by replacing $\\mathbb{N}$ with “the reverse order of the neighbourhoods of $x$”.\n\n⭐ **Subnet $\\ne$ subsequence**: a subnet may use a coarser index set and is therefore far more flexible than a subsequence (a subsequence requires an order-preserving embedding $\\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$, which misses many cases). Hence the net characterisation of compactness must use **subnets**; using “subsequences” is wrong.\n\n⭐ **Nets come from Riemann sums**: Moore–Smith (1922) invented nets precisely to give a rigorous meaning to “$\\int f$ is the limit of Riemann sums” — the index set of those sums is the refinement order of partitions, not $\\mathbb{N}$. So “nets” are not abstract fussiness; they solve a real problem in analysis.\n\n⚠ Nets and filters are **equivalent** languages (`eq.net-filter`): each net corresponds to a filter, with matching convergence. Which one to use is a matter of habit — analysis books prefer nets, set theory and topology books prefer filters.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the definition of a net is free; the two characterisations need general-topology arguments):\n- Net convergence $\\iff$ eventually lies in every neighbourhood: definition unpacking; the index directed set is by convention nonempty and need not be antisymmetric.\n- Continuity $\\implies$ preserves net limits: only the preimage-of-neighbourhood definition is used; conversely, at a discontinuity one constructs a net by choosing counterexample points in neighbourhoods, a standard construction that uses choice and needs neither first countability nor Hausdorffness.\n- Compact $\\iff$ every net has a convergent subnet: the duality between cluster points of nets and finite subcovers of open covers; Hausdorffness is not needed, but the subnet must satisfy the standard cofinality condition.\n- Replacing nets by sequences cannot characterise general compactness: the first-countable ordinal space $[0,\\omega_1)$ is sequentially compact but not compact, so the first countability mentioned in the parenthetical is not enough to guarantee the sequential characterisation of compactness.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e9a396f6',
      },
    },

    'def.riemann-integrable': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be bounded on $[a, b]$. We say $f$ is **Riemann integrable** if and only if there exists a real number $I$ such that\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists \\delta > 0 :\\ \\|P\\| < \\delta \\implies \\bigl| S(f, P, \\xi) - I \\bigr| < \\varepsilon$$\n\nholds for **every** partition $P$ and **every** choice of tags $\\xi$. Then $I$ is unique and is written\n\n$$I = \\int_a^b f = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$$\n\nwith the conventions $\\int_b^a f := -\\int_a^b f$ and $\\int_a^a f := 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#79063b0d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The two words most easily missed in the definition: **every** and **norm**.\n\nIt is not “there is a sequence of partitions whose sums converge”, but “**all** partitions of sufficiently small norm, with **all** tags, land near $I$”.\n\n⭐ Equivalent formulation (Darboux form, see “Darboux criterion”): $\\sup_P L(f, P) = \\inf_P U(f, P)$.\n\nThe equivalence of the two definitions is not free — it needs a proof, and that proof is `thm.riemann-criterion`.\n\n⚠ For functions taking values in $\\mathbb{C}$, integrability is defined by integrability of the real and imaginary parts;\n\nthe vector-valued case is analogous.\n\n⚠ Boundedness is a **prerequisite** for integrability: an unbounded function is not Riemann integrable (it may be Lebesgue integrable, provided $\\int |f| < \\infty$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe562afc',
      },
    },

    'thm.ftc-riemann-part2': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable and $F'$ integrable on $[a, b]$. Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b F'(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)$$\n\n($F$ is called an **antiderivative** of $F'$; another way to write the formula is $\\int_a^b f = F\\bigr|_a^b$.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ec8ed7bd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the main theorem of elementary calculus: **computing areas becomes finding antiderivatives**.\n\nIt also explains why tables of indefinite integrals are worth memorising — every derivative formula, read backwards, is an integration formula.\n\n⚠ The hypothesis “$F'$ is integrable” cannot be dropped: there are differentiable functions whose derivative is **not** integrable (the $F(x) = x^{2}\\sin(1/x^{2})$ type).\n\n⚠ The converse is not strong enough either: Newton–Leibniz requires $F$ to be an antiderivative;\n\nthe Lebesgue version (`thm.ftc-lebesgue`) relaxes the hypothesis to **absolute continuity** and makes “$F'$ exists” part of the conclusion.\n\nThe relation between the two theorems is “the same statement in two integral settings”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#39aef9f2',
      },
    },

    'lem.fatou': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_n\\} \\subseteq L^+$. Then\n\n$$\\int \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty} f_n \\le \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty} \\int f_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#759f0e8d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ Mind the direction of the inequality: the **lower** limit outside the integral is **at most** the lower limit of the integrals. In other words, passing to the limit can only make the integral **smaller**, never larger.\n\nThis is what remains of MCT once monotonicity is dropped — without monotonicity, equality degenerates into an inequality.\n\nA counterexample in the other direction: $f_n = \\chi_{[n, n+1]}$ (on $\\mathbb{R}$) has $\\int f_{n} = 1$ for every index, but $\\int \\liminf f_{n} = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cb8d2809',
      },
    },

    'def.integral-nonneg': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in L^+$. Define the integral of $f$ as the supremum of the integrals of simple functions not exceeding it:\n\n$\\int f d\\mu := \\sup \\{ \\int \\varphi d\\mu : 0 \\le \\varphi \\le f,\\ \\varphi$ **is a simple function** $\\}$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f56ab6ad',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: approximate $f$ from **below** by simple functions and take the **supremum** of their integrals. This differs from the Jordan measure recipe of “covering from outside and filling from inside” — here we only fill from inside.\n\nWhy no “outer cover” is required: the measure already sits on the dual side ($\\mu$ itself is defined from outside via an outer measure), so taking an infimum over simple functions would only make the integral too small.\n\n⚠ This definition works only for nonnegative functions. For a general real-valued function, split it into positive and negative parts and subtract (see “integral of a complex function”).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#02c58844',
      },
    },

    'def.lebesgue-measurable': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{C}$:\n\n- if $f$ is $(\\mathcal{L}, \\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{C})$-measurable, $f$ is called **Lebesgue measurable** ($\\mathcal{L}$ is the $Lebesgue \\sigma$-algebra);\n- if $f$ is $(\\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{R}, \\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{C})$-measurable, $f$ is called **Borel measurable**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c3599e7a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ **There is a trap here: the composition of two Lebesgue measurable functions need not be Lebesgue measurable.**\n\nThe reason: making the composition work requires the intermediate $\\sigma$-algebra to cooperate, and $\\mathcal{L}$ is strictly larger than $\\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTake $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} Lebesgue$ and $g : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} Lebesgue$ measurable; then $g\\circ f$ need not be measurable with respect to $\\mathcal{L}$ — because $g^{-1}$ only guarantees pulling sets in $\\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{R}$ back to $\\mathcal{L}$, whereas $f^{-1}$ needs a **non-Borel Lebesgue measurable set** in $\\mathbb{R}$ to produce a failure.\n\nThe standard fix: make the intermediate function Borel measurable. If $g$ is Borel measurable and $f$ is Lebesgue measurable, then $g\\circ f$ is Lebesgue measurable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d6e2e80a',
      },
    },

    'cor.continuous-measurable': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X, Y$ be topological spaces, each equipped with its **Borel $\\sigma$-algebra** $\\mathfrak{B}_X, \\mathfrak{B}_Y$. Then every **continuous** map is automatically Borel measurable:\n\n$f : X \\to Y$ **continuous** $\\implies f$ is $(\\mathfrak{B}_X, \\mathfrak{B}_Y)$-**measurable**",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a04b74b5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Continuity is exactly “preimages of open sets are open”; open sets generate $\\mathfrak{B}_Y$, so the criterion (2) from the previous item gives the result immediately.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7bee078a',
      },
    },

    'thm.simple-approximation': {
      statement: {
        en: "**(a)** If $f : X \\to [0, +\\infty]$ is measurable, then there exists a sequence of simple functions $\\{\\varphi_n\\}$ that approaches $f$ **increasingly at every point**:\n\n$$0 \\le \\varphi_1 \\le \\varphi_2 \\le \\cdots \\le f, \\quad  \\varphi_n \\to f$$\n\nand **the convergence is uniform on every set on which $f$ is bounded**.\n\n**(b)** More generally ($f : X \\to \\mathbb{C}$ measurable), one can choose a sequence of simple functions whose absolute values increase pointwise and which converges pointwise to $f$:\n\n$$0 \\le |\\varphi_1| \\le |\\varphi_2| \\le \\cdots \\le |f|, \\quad  \\varphi_n \\to f$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#20ef8cf7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The construction in (a) is a layer cake: slice the range [0, n) according to $2^{-n}$, and truncate everything above $n$ to $n$ —\n\n$$\\varphi_n = \\sum_{k=0}^{2^n\\cdot n-1} k\\cdot2^{-n}\\cdot\\chi_{E_n^k} + n\\cdot\\chi_{F_n}, $$\n\n$$E_n^k = f^{-1}((k\\cdot2^{-n}, (k+1)\\cdot2^{-n}]), \\quad  F_n = f^{-1}((n, +\\infty])$$\n\nEach layer is the preimage of a measurable set, so $\\varphi _{n}$ is a simple function; as $n$ grows the grid refines and the ceiling rises, so the sequence climbs monotonically to $f$.\n\nFor (b), split $f = g + ih$ into $g = g^+ - g^-$ and $h = h^+ - h^-$, and apply (a) to the four nonnegative parts:\n\n$$\\varphi_n = \\psi_n^+ - \\psi_n^- + i(\\zeta _n^+ - \\zeta _n^-)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c1debacb'
      },
    },

    'def.group-hom': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(G, \\cdot)$ and $(G', \\ast)$ be groups. A map $\\varphi : G \\to G'$ is called a **group homomorphism** if\n\n$$\\varphi(a b) = \\varphi(a) \\ast \\varphi(b) \\qquad (a, b \\in G).$$\n\nIts **kernel** and **image** are\n\n$$\\ker\\varphi := \\{\\, g \\in G : \\varphi(g) = e' \\,\\} \\;\\subseteq\\; G, \\qquad \\operatorname{im}\\varphi := \\varphi(G) \\;\\subseteq\\; G'.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2570ab7e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**A homomorphism automatically preserves the identity and inverses**: $\\varphi(e) = e'$, $\\varphi(a^{-1}) = \\varphi(a)^{-1}$ (obtained by cancelling in $\\varphi(e) = \\varphi(e e) = \\varphi(e)\\varphi(e)$). So only one equation is needed in the definition.\n\n**Kernel and image are subgroups**: $\\operatorname{im}\\varphi \\le G'$ is obvious; $\\ker\\varphi \\le G$ follows from $\\varphi(ab^{-1}) = \\varphi(a)\\varphi(b)^{-1} = e'$.\n\n⭐ **The kernel is moreover a normal subgroup**: for $k \\in \\ker\\varphi$ and $g \\in G$,\n\n$$\\varphi(g k g^{-1}) = \\varphi(g)\\, e'\\, \\varphi(g)^{-1} = e',$$\n\nso $gkg^{-1} \\in \\ker\\varphi$, i.e. $\\ker\\varphi \\trianglelefteq G$. **The kernel is always normal** — this is the entire reason a quotient group can quotient by the kernel.\n\n**Monomorphism $\\iff$ trivial kernel**: $\\varphi$ is injective $\\iff$ $\\ker\\varphi = \\{e\\}$. Thus injectivity is fully encoded by a **subgroup**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e12495d9',
      },
    },

    'def.normal-subgroup': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $H \\le G$ and $g \\in G$. The **left coset** and **right coset** are\n\n$$gH := \\{\\, gh : h \\in H \\,\\}, \\qquad Hg := \\{\\, hg : h \\in H \\,\\}.$$\n\nWe call $H$ a **normal subgroup** of $G$ (written $H \\trianglelefteq G$) if $gH = Hg$ for all $g \\in G$, equivalently $gHg^{-1} = H$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a885cb6f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why normality.** A coset $gH$ is a **set**, and to turn the quotient set into a group via “$gH \\cdot g'H := gg'H$” the definition must be **independent of the choice of representatives**. Compute where the difference lies:\n\n$$(g h)(g' h') = g g' \\cdot \\bigl((g')^{-1} h g'\\bigr) h',$$\n\nThe element $(g')^{-1} h g'$ in the rightmost bracket must still lie in $H$ — “$H$ is closed under conjugation” is exactly normality. **Every subgroup of an abelian group is normal** (conjugation does nothing), so this step never causes trouble in an abelian group.\n\n**Cosets are an equivalence relation.** Define $a \\sim b \\iff a^{-1} b \\in H$; then the equivalence classes are precisely the left cosets, and all left cosets partition $G$ — this is the quotient set used for the quotient group below.\n\n**Notation**: $H \\le G$ denotes a subgroup, $H \\trianglelefteq G$ a normal subgroup ($\\trianglelefteq$ is read “is normal in”).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3669cc0c',
      },
    },

    'def.quotient-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $N \\trianglelefteq G$. The set of all left cosets $\\{\\, gN : g \\in G \\,\\}$ is denoted $G/N$; define on it\n\n$$(g N) \\cdot (g' N) := (g g') N.$$\n\nNormality guarantees that this definition is independent of the choice of representatives, so $G/N$ becomes a group, called the **quotient group**.\n\nThe natural map $\\pi : G \\to G/N$, $g \\mapsto gN$, is a surjective homomorphism.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d2617371',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The quotient group is the **set-theoretic quotient** with one more layer: first quotient $G$ by the equivalence relation “differ by an element of $N$”, then check that an operation survives on the quotient set.\n\n**Universal property** (the real use of quotient groups): for any group homomorphism $\\varphi : G \\to G'$, if $N \\subseteq \\ker\\varphi$, then $\\varphi$ factors uniquely through $\\pi$: there exists a unique $\\overline{\\varphi} : G/N \\to G'$ with $\\overline{\\varphi} \\circ \\pi = \\varphi$.\n\n⚠️ A quotient **group** exists only for normal subgroups; the quotient **set** $G/H$ exists for any subgroup (left cosets still partition $G$), but that quotient set usually carries no group structure.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea5b0888',
      },
    },

    'def.vs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field, $V$ a vector space over $K$, and $S \\subseteq V$.\n\n- **$S$ is linearly independent**: every finite subset of $S$ is linearly independent, i.e. there are no $c_{1}$…$c_{n} \\in K$, not all zero, with $c_{1}v_{1} + \\cdots + c_{n}v_{n} = 0$.\n- **$S$ spans $V$**: every vector in $V$ is a linear combination of finitely many vectors in $S$.\n- **$S$ is a basis of $V$**: $S$ is both linearly independent and spans $V$.\n\nNote that linear independence is defined **finite-subset by finite-subset** — this is exactly why it is “finitary”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#93d086da',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Equivalent formulation: $S$ is a basis $\\iff V$ every vector admits a unique expression as a finite linear combination of elements of $S$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da8b623d',
      },
    },

    'thm.vsbasis': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a vector space over a field $K$. Then\n\n$V$ has a basis;\n- more precisely: every linearly independent subset of $V$ can be extended to a basis of $V$, and every spanning set contains a basis.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fca5a22b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In ZF this statement is equivalent to AC — without AC one can construct a vector space with no basis.\n\nThis is the standard example of “an order-theoretic result landing in algebra”, and an instance of a cross-area link in this knowledge graph.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f067b6b0',
      },
    },

    'def.commutative-diagram': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $I$ be a **small category** (its objects and morphisms form a small set). A **diagram in $\\mathcal{C}$ indexed by $I$** is a functor\n\n$$D : I \\longrightarrow \\mathcal{C}$$\n\nAll such diagrams, with natural transformations as morphisms, form the category\n\n$$\\mathcal{C}^{I} := \\operatorname{Hom}(I, \\mathcal{C})$$\n\nIf between any two objects of $I$ there is **at most one morphism** (such an $I$ is called a **preorder**), then $D$ is called a **commutative diagram** over $I$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7d602f84',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Commutativity is a property of the indexing category, not something the functor supplies.** “$I$ is a preorder” holds if and only if “every $I$-shaped diagram commutes”: then two paths in $I$ with the same source and target compose to the **unique** morphism of $\\operatorname{Hom}(i, j)$, hence are equal.\n\n**In general only the “shape” is given.** When $I$ is not a preorder, $D$ can only be called a $I$-shaped diagram: a functor is **not required** to send parallel $f \\ne g : i \\rightrightarrows j$ to equal arrows.\n\nThe most typical non-commuting diagram is the **parallel pair** $\\bullet \\rightrightarrows \\bullet$, whose limit and colimit are exactly the **equaliser** and **coequaliser**;\n\nthe classical commuting square corresponds to the preorder $\\{A \\le B \\le D,\\ A \\le C \\le D\\}$.\n\n**Reading it on paper.** A diagram drawn on paper is only a **presentation**: objects are assigned to the vertices of a directed graph $G$ and morphisms to its arrows. Saying that it **commutes** means that any two paths with the same source and target have **equal** composites under the labelling — this is a **condition to be checked**, equivalent to the labelling descending to a functor on the quotient of the free category on $G$.\n\n$I$ is called the **indexing category**: it prescribes only the shape of the diagram, not its content.\n\nPrecomposing with a functor $\\lambda : I \\to J$ gives **reindexing** $\\lambda^{*} : \\mathcal{C}^{J} \\to \\mathcal{C}^{I}$: it cuts a $J$-shaped diagram down to a $I$-shaped one.\n\nIn particular, the unique functor $I \\to \\mathbf{1}$ to the terminal category induces the **constant-diagram functor** $\\Delta : \\mathcal{C} \\longrightarrow \\mathcal{C}^{I}$, which sends an object $X$ to the constant diagram “$X$ at every position and $1_X$ on every arrow”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f76d7406',
      },
    },

    'def.fibered-product': {
      statement: {
        en: "The limit of a diagram of shape $X_1 \\xrightarrow{f_1} X_0 \\xleftarrow{f_2} X_2$ is called the **fibred product**, written $X_1 \\times_{X_0} X_2$, also called the **pullback** of $f_1$ along $f_2$. It completes to a commutative square\n\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc} X_1 \\times_{X_0} X_2 & \\longrightarrow & X_2 \\\\ \\downarrow & & \\downarrow f_2 \\\\ X_1 & \\xrightarrow{f_1} & X_0 \\end{array}$$\n\nSuch a square is called **cartesian**. Dually, the colimit of such a diagram is the **fibred coproduct**, also called the **pushout**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8b870204',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: the pullback is “the product after matching over the base $X_0$” — solving $f_1(x_1) = f_2(x_2)$; the pushout is “gluing $X_1, X_2$ together along their images in $X_0$”.\n\nExample ($\\mathbf{Set}$):\n\n$$X_1 \\times_{X_0} X_2 = \\{(x_1, x_2) \\in X_1 \\times X_2 : f_1(x_1) = f_2(x_2)\\}, \\qquad X_1 \\cup_{X_0} X_2 = (X_1 \\sqcup X_2)/\\!\\sim$$\n\nwhere $\\sim$ is generated by $f_1(x_0) \\sim f_2(x_0)$ ($x_0 \\in X_0$). In $\\mathbf{Top}$, the pullback carries the subspace topology and the pushout the quotient topology.\n\nIn $\\mathbf{Ab}$ both reduce to kernels and cokernels: combine $f: X \\to S$ and $g: Y \\to S$ into $\\varphi : X \\oplus Y \\to S$, $(x,y) \\mapsto f(x) - g(y)$, then the pushout is $= \\operatorname{coker} \\varphi$ and the pullback is $= \\ker \\varphi$ — first take the direct sum, then quotient out the differences of $f, g$.\n\n📌 The “pullback” of category theory is the fibred product (here); the pullback of **differential forms** is `prop.pullback-form-manifold`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f9dfa2de',
      },
    },

    'def.equalizer': {
      statement: {
        en: "The limit of the parallel pair $f, g : X \\rightrightarrows Y$ is called the **equaliser**, written $\\operatorname{eq}(f,g) = \\ker(f,g)$; the colimit is the **coequaliser**, written $\\operatorname{coker}(f,g)$. They fit into\n\n$$\\ker(f,g) \\longrightarrow X \\overset{f}{\\underset{g}{\\rightrightarrows}} Y \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{coker}(f,g)$$\n\nand the middle map $\\ker(f,g) \\to X$ is a monomorphism, while the map on the right $Y \\to \\operatorname{coker}(f,g)$ is an epimorphism. This segment is called **left exact**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#584c5d90',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: the equaliser is the part of $X$ “sent to the same place by $f$ and $g$”; the coequaliser is the quotient of $Y$ that “glues $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ together”.\n\nExample: in $\\mathbf{Set}$, $\\ker(f,g) = \\{x \\in X : f(x) = g(x)\\}$, and $\\operatorname{coker}(f,g) = Y/\\!\\sim$ ($\\sim$ generated by $f(x) \\sim g(x)$).\n\nIn $\\mathbf{Top}$ one adds the subspace topology / the quotient topology.\n\nIn $\\mathbf{Ab}$, $\\ker(f,g) = \\ker(f - g)$ — the equaliser of two maps degenerates to the kernel of **one** map, which is exactly where the name “kernel” comes from.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dee5ea44',
      },
    },

    'def.poset': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\preceq$ be a binary relation on a set $A$. Then $\\preceq$ is a **partial order on $A$** if and only if:\n\n- **Reflexive**: $\\forall x \\in A, x \\preceq x$;\n- **Antisymmetric**: $\\forall x, y \\in A ( x \\preceq y \\wedge y \\preceq x \\to x = y )$;\n- **Transitive**: $\\forall x, y, z \\in A ( x \\preceq y \\wedge y \\preceq z \\to x \\preceq z )$.\n\nIf in addition **comparability** $\\forall x, y \\in A ( x \\preceq y \\vee y \\preceq x )$ holds, then $\\preceq$ is called a **total order** (linear order) on $A$, and $(A, \\preceq )$ is called a totally ordered set.\n\nCall $(A, \\preceq )$ a **partially ordered set** (poset). Define the **strict order** from $\\preceq$ by: $x \\prec y :\\iff x \\preceq y \\wedge  x \\ne y$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9258d75f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Standard examples: $\\langle \\mathcal{P}(S), \\subseteq \\rangle$ is a poset but not totally ordered; $\\langle \\mathbb{R}, \\le \\rangle$ is totally ordered.\n\nAntisymmetry says that the only way two elements can be mutually $\\le$ is by being equal, so a partial order can be seen as an abstraction of $\\le$.\n\n**Assumptions used in each direction** (axiomatic definition and passage to the strict order):\n- A relation is a partial order $\\iff$ it satisfies reflexivity, antisymmetry and transitivity simultaneously, and a relation is a total order $\\iff$ it is a partial order plus comparability; both are unfoldings of the definition and need no axiom of choice.\n- **Partial order** $\\implies$ **strict order**: let $x\\prec y$ mean $x\\preceq y$ and $x\\ne y$; irreflexivity and transitivity follow; conversely, taking the reflexive closure of an irreflexive transitive relation yields a partial order, a finite-step logical deduction.\n- Antisymmetry cannot be dropped: the universal relation on a two-point set is reflexive and transitive but not a partial order; comparability is not automatic either — ordered by inclusion, the power set of a two-element set has two incomparable singletons.\n- Extending a partial order to a total order is a separate theorem, usually proved from the order extension principle; the definition of a total order here does not assert that such an extension exists, and its existence must not be counted as part of the definition.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b341858a',
      },
    },

    'def.chain': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(P, \\preceq )$ be a poset and let $C \\subseteq P$.\n\n**$C$ is a chain** (also called a totally ordered subset) if and only if any two elements of $C$ are comparable:\n\n$$\\forall x, y \\in C ( x \\preceq y \\vee  y \\preceq x )$$\n\n**$C$ is an antichain** if and only if any two distinct elements of $C$ are incomparable.\n\nConvention: $\\emptyset$ and every singleton count as chains.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0f207eac',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Chain** is the central notion in Zorn's lemma and the Hausdorff maximal principle — both ask how large a chain can grow.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#63045b05',
      },
    },

    'def.wellorder': {
      statement: {
        en: "A totally ordered set $(W, \\preceq )$ is **well-ordered** if and only if every nonempty subset of $W$ has a least element:\n\n$$\\forall S \\subseteq W ( S \\ne \\emptyset \\to \\exists m \\in S, \\forall s \\in S, m \\preceq s )$$\n\nEquivalently: there is no infinite strictly decreasing chain $w_{0} \\succ w_{1} \\succ w_{2} \\succ \\cdots$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f0928b4f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Well-ordering has one use: it makes \"the next step\" definable.** The part before each step has a first and a last element, so one can do **transfinite induction** (proof) and **transfinite recursion** (construction) — see \"the recursion theorem on well-ordered sets\".\n\n⭐ Every well-ordered set is **order-isomorphic to a unique ordinal** (see \"the order type of a well-ordered set\"), and that ordinal is its \"length\".\n\nThus \"which of two well-ordered sets is longer\" becomes \"which of two ordinals is larger\", and the order on ordinals is $\\in$.\n\n$\\langle \\mathbb{N}, \\le \\rangle$ is well-ordered;\n\n$\\langle \\mathbb{Z}, \\le \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbb{R}, \\le \\rangle$ are not (some nonempty subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$ have no least element).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5b4b662',
      },
    },

    'def.cardinal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Equipotence partitions all sets into classes. The **cardinality** $\\#A$ of a set $A$ is the equipotence class of $A$ — it measures how large $A$ is, without regard to what the elements of $A$ are.\n\nCompare two cardinals:\n\n- $\\#A \\le \\#B$: there is an **injection** $A \\to B$;\n- $\\#A = \\#B$: there is a **bijection** $A \\to B$ (equipotence);\n- $\\#A < \\#B$: $\\#A \\le \\#B$ but $\\#A \\ne \\#B$.\n\nIf $\\#A \\le \\#B$ and $\\#B \\le \\#A$, then $\\#A = \\#B$ (the Schröder–Bernstein theorem).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cd9eda20',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Notation: $\\#\\mathbb{N} = \\aleph_0$ (countably infinite), $\\#\\mathbb{R} = 2^{\\aleph_0}$, and $\\aleph_0 < 2^{\\aleph_0}$.\n\n⚠ Strictly speaking, equipotence classes are not sets; the standard ZFC approach is to take **ordinals** first and then, using the well-ordering theorem ($\\iff$ AC), choose the least ordinal in each equipotence class as its representative. That ordinal is called the cardinal.\n\n> Only two facts are needed — $\\#A \\le \\#B$ via injections and $\\#A = \\#B$ via bijections — and both work without choosing representatives.\n\n⚠ Two arbitrary cardinals are **not necessarily comparable**; this is exactly the content of the **cardinal comparability theorem** (which uses AC).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#79f2c92e',
      },
    },

    'thm.rolle': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a < b$, and suppose $f$ is continuous on the closed interval $[a, b]$ and differentiable on the open interval $(a, b)$, with $f(a) = f(b)$. Then there exists $\\xi \\in (a, b)$ such that\n\n$$f'(\\xi) = 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4055fb64',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric content: when the two endpoints have equal height, there is a point in between with a horizontal tangent.\n\nIt is the **only** member of the mean value theorem family that needs compactness (the extreme value theorem locates an interior extremum); the later members are the same statement in different clothing.\n\n⚠ Neither hypothesis can be dropped: continuity on $[a, b]$ guarantees that the extrema exist (compactness), and differentiability on $(a, b)$ guarantees that the extremum is an interior point, where Fermat's lemma applies.\n\nReplacing $f(a) = f(b)$ by $f(a) \\ne f(b)$ gives the Lagrange mean value theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5490b315',
      },
    },

    'thm.mvt': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a < b$, and suppose $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$ and differentiable on $(a, b)$. Then there exists $\\xi \\in (a, b)$ such that\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) = f'(\\xi)\\,(b - a)$$\n\nEquivalently, $f'(\\xi) = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}$: **the average rate of change is attained at some point as an instantaneous rate**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a42dc2a7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **master switch** of differential calculus: whenever a theorem deduces global information from pointwise derivative data, the first step is to use the mean value theorem to rewrite a difference as a derivative.\n\nMonotonicity criteria, the constant-function criterion, L'Hôpital's rule, Taylor's formula and convexity criteria are all corollaries.\n\n⚠ **$\\xi$ is uncontrollable**: the theorem asserts existence but not location — which is why the mean value theorem cannot **compute** $f(b) - f(a)$, only prove inequalities and structural properties.\n\n⚠ A counterexample in the general setting shows that continuity cannot be dropped: adding a single jump to $f(x) = x$ on $[0, 1] \\setminus \\{1/2\\}$ makes the average slope unattainable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7b9ea076',
      },
    },

    'thm.taylor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ have $n$ continuous derivatives on $[a, b]$ and be $n+1$ times differentiable on $(a, b)$, and choose $x_0 \\in [a, b]$. Then for every $x \\in [a, b]$ with $x \\ne x_0$, there exists $\\xi$ between $x_0$ and $x$ such that\n\n$$f(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\frac{f^{(k)}(x_0)}{k!}\\,(x - x_0)^{k} + \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)}{(n+1)!}\\,(x - x_0)^{n+1}$$\n\nThe second term on the right is called the **Lagrange remainder**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5ba4658',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **a sufficiently differentiable function is locally a polynomial**, and the error is controlled by that one remainder term.\n\nThis is the only theorem in elementary calculus that recovers a function from finite information, and it is the gateway to the whole line of power series and analytic functions.\n\n⭐ The shape of the remainder has the same origin as the mean value theorem: when $n = 0$, Taylor's formula **is** the Lagrange mean value theorem.\n\n⚠ The remainder only asserts the existence of $\\xi$, not its location;\n\nto **estimate** the error one must know a bound on $f^{(n+1)}$ separately (this is the point of writing the remainder in sup form).\n\n⚠ Two hypotheses: enough differentiability on the interval;\n\n$\\xi$ depends on $x$ and $n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5b0f2275',
      },
    },

    'def.absolute-continuity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu$ be a measure on $(X, \\mathcal{M})$ and $\\nu$ a signed measure. Then $\\nu$ is **absolutely continuous with respect to $\\mu$**, written\n\n$$\\nu \\ll  \\mu, $$\n\nif and only if **every $\\mu$-null set is $\\nu$-null**:\n\n$$\\forall E \\in \\mathcal{M}, \\mu(E) = 0 \\implies \\nu(E) = 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d7edce10',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In one sentence: **wherever $\\mu$ sees nothing, $\\nu$ must also see nothing**.\n\n⚠ Note that $\\mu (E) = 0$ uses $\\mu$ (a positive measure), whereas $\\nu (E) = 0$ uses the signed measure; an equivalent formulation is $|\\nu|(E) = 0$.\n\nClearly $\\mu \\ll  |\\mu|$ and $|\\mu| \\ll  \\mu$ (for the measure $\\mu$ itself).\n\n📌 Example: $\\nu(E) := \\int_{E} f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu$ (with $f \\ge 0$ integrable) satisfies $\\nu \\ll \\mu$ and $\\mathrm{d}\\nu/\\mathrm{d}\\mu = f$; the opposite case is $\\delta_{0} \\perp \\mu$ (all mass concentrated on a null set, see `ex.density-abs-continuous`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#02ec2d7f',
      },
    },

    'thm.ftc-lebesgue': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{C}$, with a compact interval. Then the following three statements are **equivalent**:\n\n**(a)** $F$ is **absolutely continuous** on $[a, b]$;\n\n**(b)** there exists $f \\in L^1([a, b])$ such that\n\n$$F(x) - F(a) = \\int_a^x f(t) dt\\quad  \\forall x \\in [a, b]$$\n\n**(c)** $F$ is **differentiable a.e.**, $F' \\in L^1([a, b])$, and\n\n$$F(x) - F(a) = \\int_a^x F'(t) dt$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d3397d86',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **complete form of the Newton–Leibniz formula for the Lebesgue integral**. Compare Riemann integration: there the condition that $F'$ be integrable and the formula hold requires $F'$ to be continuous (or at least Riemann integrable), whereas here only absolute continuity of $F$ is needed.\n\nThe three conditions split the work: (a) is the function-side condition (expressed via partitions of the interval), (b) is the integral side, and (c) is the derivative side. The theorem says they are the same thing.\n\n⚠ Without absolute continuity the formula **fails**: the Cantor function satisfies the a.e. differentiability and integrability in (c), $F' \\in L^{1}$, but $\\int _{0}^{1} F' = 0 \\ne F(1) - F(0) = 1$.\n\nProof chain: $(a) \\implies (b)$ follows from $AC \\subseteq BV$ and $\\mu _F \\ll m$; $(b) \\implies (c)$ is a direct corollary of the fundamental theorem of calculus; $(c) \\implies (a)$ obtains absolute continuity from the representation $F(x) = \\int F'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f58d909c',
      },
    },

    'def.riemann-sum': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ (**no boundedness is required**), let $P = \\{ x_0 < \\cdots < x_n \\}$ be a partition, and choose a **tag** $\\xi_i \\in I_i$ in each subinterval. The **Riemann sum** is\n\n$$S(f, P, \\xi) := \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(\\xi_i)\\,\\Delta x_i$$\n\nIt depends on three things: $f$, the partition $P$, and the choice of tags $\\xi_1, \\ldots, \\xi_n$.\n\n⚠️ **Boundedness plays no role here**: each $f(\\xi_i)$ is a real number and a finite sum is always defined. Boundedness enters only when discussing Riemann **integrability** — that is where the Darboux upper and lower sums require $\\sup/\\inf$ to be finite.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c8e5ccd9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric reading: take the \"height\" on each small subinterval to be $f(\\xi_i)$, obtaining $n$ rectangles; $S$ is their total area. **The choice of tags** is the only freedom in this theory — requiring the integrability condition to hold for every choice is exactly what removes that freedom.\n\n⚠ For an unbounded function the Riemann sum is **still defined** (a sum of finitely many real numbers), but boundedness is a prerequisite for the integrability discussion: the Darboux upper and lower sums need $\\sup/\\inf$ to be finite.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#52447d62',
      },
    },

    'def.series': {
      statement: {
        en: "Given a sequence $\\{a_n\\}$, let $s_N := \\sum_{n=1}^{N} a_n$ be its **partial sums**.\n\nThe series $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n$ is said to **converge** if and only if the sequence of partial sums $\\{s_N\\}$ converges; in that case write\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n := \\lim_{N \\to \\infty} s_N$$\n\n**Series with nonnegative terms** ($a_n \\ge 0$) are especially clean: the partial sums increase, so\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n = \\sup_N s_N \\in [0, +\\infty]$$\n\nalways makes sense (possibly $+\\infty$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a9ea0be5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Countable additivity of measures and term-by-term properties of integrals both rely on this line for nonnegative series: nonnegativity removes any worry about conditional convergence and rearrangement.\n\n⚠ A convergent series **can be rearranged** only when it converges absolutely; rearranging a conditionally convergent series may change its sum (the Riemann rearrangement theorem).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3f26486',
      },
    },

    'prop.subset-compact-equiv': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, d)$ be a **complete** metric space and $A \\subseteq X$. Then\n\n$A$ is **compact** $\\iff$ $A$ is closed **and** $A$ is **totally bounded**.\n\n(Totally boundedness of $A$ is understood with respect to the subspace metric $d|_A$.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3ad6d8e2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ **The hypothesis that $X$ is complete cannot be dropped.** The reason is the middle step: $A$ is complete as a subspace $\\iff A$ closed in $X$ — this step uses completeness of $X$. Without it, $A$ closed and $A$ totally bounded does not imply $A$ compact.\n\nThis is also a generalization of **Heine–Borel**: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is complete, and in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $A$ bounded $\\iff A$ totally bounded, so \"bounded closed sets are compact\" is exactly the special case.\n\n**Proof.**\n- **$\\implies$**: $A$ compact $\\implies$ $A$ closed in $X$ (the previous item).\n\nTotal boundedness: given $\\varepsilon > 0$, the family $\\{B(a,\\varepsilon)\\cap A\\}_{a\\in A}$ is an open cover of $A$ (with the subspace metric); compactness gives a finite subcover, and those finitely many centers form an $\\varepsilon$-net ✓.\n- **$\\Longleftarrow$**: suppose $A$ is closed and totally bounded.\n\nFirst **completeness**: $X$ is complete and $A$ is closed, so a Cauchy sequence in $A$ converges in $X$ to some point, and the limit lies in the closed set $A$; hence $A$ is complete as a subspace ✓.\n\nNow **compactness**: total boundedness gives a countable dense subset, so any sequence in $A$ has a Cauchy subsequence, obtained layer by layer from $2^{-n}$-nets and the pigeonhole principle;\n\ncompleteness makes that subsequence converge ✓.\n\nHence $A$ is **sequentially compact**;\n\nand in a **metric space** sequential compactness $\\iff$ compactness ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⚠️ The step \"$X$ complete $\\implies$ $A$ complete\" is exactly where the hypothesis in the title is used (see the previous remark).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a5b00a3e',
      },
    },

    'prop.compact-basic-operations': {
      statement: {
        en: "1. A **closed subspace** of a compact space is compact;\n2. The **continuous image** of a compact space is compact;\n3. A **compact Hausdorff** space is **normal**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2ba9971',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** (1) Given a closed subset $F \\subseteq X$ (with $X$ compact) and an open cover, add $X \\setminus F$ to obtain an open cover of $X$, take a finite subcover and delete that term ✓. (2) Given $f : X \\to Y$ (with $X$ compact) and an open cover in $Y$, pull it back to an open cover of $X$, take a finite subcover and push it forward ✓.\n\n(3) Given disjoint closed sets $A, B$: for each $a \\in A$, use the Hausdorff property to produce an open neighbourhood $U_a$ of $a$ with $\\overline{U_a} \\cap B = \\emptyset$ ✓;\n\n$A$ is compact, so finitely many cover it, $A$; set $U := \\bigcup_{i} U_{a_i}$, then $\\overline{U} \\cap B = \\emptyset$ ✓;\n\nperform the same construction for $B$, then shrink both families of open sets by one step to obtain open sets separating $A, B$ ✓.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#55a98361',
      },
    },

    'def.second-countable': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $X$ is **second countable** if its topology has a **countable base**: there is a countable family $\\mathcal{B} = \\{\\, B_{n} \\,\\}_{n \\ge 1}$ such that every open set is a union of members of $\\mathcal{B}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#faf23581',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**It yields three good properties at once**: second countable $\\implies$ separable (choose one point from each nonempty basic element; the result is a countable dense set); second countable $\\implies$ Lindelöf (every open cover has a countable subcover, by filtering basic elements);\n\nsecond countable $\\implies$ first countable (at each point, filter the countably many basic elements containing it).\n\nWith regularity it also yields normality — this is the first step of the Urysohn metrization theorem.\n\n**For metrizable spaces the three countability properties coincide**: separable $\\iff$ Lindelöf $\\iff$ second countable. Hence the Urysohn theorem can also be read as \"regular $+$ separable\".\n\n**Heredity**: subspaces inherit second countability (intersect basic elements with the subspace; still countable); quotient spaces do not (the quotient map crushes a countable base into a much coarser topology).\n\n⚠️ The converse fails: an uncountable discrete space is first countable and metrizable, but not second countable (it has no countable dense subset).\n\n**The relations with first countability and separability are one-way** (this is the group of implications most often reversed):\n\n- **Second countable $\\implies$ first countable**: a countable base itself gives a countable local base at each point. **No extra hypothesis is needed**.\n- **Second countable $\\implies$ separable**: choose one point from each basic element to obtain a countable dense set. ⚠️ This step **uses countable choice** (skip empty basic elements; usually harmless).\n- **Separable $\\not\\implies$ second countable**: the Sorgenfrey line is a counterexample (separable and first countable, but not second countable).\n- **Separable + metrizable $\\implies$ second countable**: this is what makes second countability equivalent to separability in metric spaces; **the proof uses metrizability** (take a countable dense set and balls of radius $1/n$).\n\n⚠️ In one sentence: **second countability is \"global countability\", first countability is \"local countability\"**; in metric spaces both are equivalent to separability, in general topology they are not.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d9399ef6',
      },
    },

    'def.subspace': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a vector space over a field $K$. Then $U \\subseteq V$ is called a **(linear) subspace** of $V$ if and only if\n\n- $0 \\in U$;\n- $U$ is closed under addition: $u, v \\in U \\implies u + v \\in U$;\n- $U$ is closed under scalar multiplication: $u \\in U$, $c \\in K \\implies cu \\in U$.\n\nEquivalently, $U$ together with the operations restricted from $V$ is itself a $K$-vector space.\n\n**Inclusion** between subspaces is defined as set inclusion, and the **intersection** of any family of subspaces is again a subspace (verify addition and scalar multiplication term by term).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8574cab6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The first of the three conditions can be absorbed into the other two: it suffices that $U \\ne \\emptyset$ and that the set is closed under subtraction (take $u - u = 0$).\n\nWriting the three conditions separately is useful for **checking them one by one**.\n\n⚠ **A union is generally not a subspace**: the union of the $x$-axis and the $y$-axis does not even contain $(1,0)+(0,1)$.\n\nThus the lattice of subspaces is \"closed under intersections, generally not under unions\" — to combine subspaces one uses the **sum** $U + W$.\n\n⚠ The empty set is not a subspace;\n\n$\\{0\\}$ and $V$ are the two smallest subspaces (called **trivial subspaces**).\n\n⭐ Do not confuse this with the topological notion of subspace (`def.subspace-topology`, the subspace topology): the two notions share a name but mean completely different things.\n\n**Assumptions used in each direction** (the vector-space criterion after restricting the operations):\n- Subspace $\\implies$ contains zero and is closed under addition and scalar multiplication: this is a free unfolding of the definition, needing neither finite dimension nor the axiom of choice.\n- The three closure conditions $\\implies$ the restricted operations form a vector space: the algebraic identities are inherited from $V$, negatives come from multiplication by $-1$, and no existence theorem for bases is needed.\n- Nonempty plus closure under subtraction and scalar multiplication $\\implies$ the three conditions: take $u-u=0$ and then recover addition; keeping only nonemptiness and closure under subtraction is not enough, and $\\mathbb Z\\subset\\mathbb R$ is a counterexample.\n- Nonemptiness cannot be dropped: closure of the empty set under addition and scalar multiplication holds vacuously, but there is no zero vector; that an arbitrary intersection is a subspace is checked condition by condition, with the empty intersection conventionally $V$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1cbde284',
      },
    },

    'def.linear-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V, W$ be vector spaces over a field $K$. A map $T : V \\to W$ is called **$K$-linear** (or a linear map) if and only if\n\n$$T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v), \\qquad T(cu) = c\\,T(u)$$\n\nfor all $u, v \\in V$ and $c \\in K$. Equivalently, $T(cu + v) = c\\,T(u) + T(v)$.\n\nThe **sum** and **scalar multiple** of two linear maps are again linear, so the set of all linear maps is a $K$-vector space, denoted\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V, W) := \\{\\, T : V \\to W \\ \\text{linear} \\,\\}$$\n\nA composite $S \\circ T$ is again linear.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#84cc6399',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Linear means **preserving the two operations** (addition and scalar multiplication).\n\nCompared with a group homomorphism, which preserves one operation, there is one more requirement, but it comes for free: both $T(0) = 0$ and $T(-v) = -T(v)$ follow from the definition.\n\n⭐ That $\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V, W)$ is itself a vector space is special to linear algebra: in a general category the morphism set between two objects has no addition (as in $\\mathbf{Set}$), whereas the category of vector spaces is an **additive category** — the morphism sets carry an addition of their own, an extra structure absent from general categories.\n\n⚠ The point of linearity is that **only the addition and scalar multiplication of $K$ matter**: the translation $v \\mapsto v + v_0$ (with $v_0 \\ne 0$) is not linear ($0$ does not map to $0$), but it \"differs from a linear map by a translation\" — that is an affine map.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9e0f1cd6',
      },
    },

    'def.matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field and $m, n \\ge 1$. An $m \\times n$ **matrix** is a rectangular array of elements of $K$,\n\n$$A = (a_{ij}) = \\begin{pmatrix} a_{11} & \\cdots & a_{1n} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ a_{m1} & \\cdots & a_{mn} \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad a_{ij} \\in K$$\n\n($i$ is the row index and $j$ the column index, both starting at $1$.) The set of all $m \\times n$ matrices is denoted $M_{m \\times n}(K)$, and the square case by $M_n(K) := M_{n \\times n}(K)$.\n\nThe $j$-th **column** of $A$ is a column vector in $K^{m}$, and its $i$-th **row** is a row vector in $K^{n}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce735903',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A matrix is just a rectangular array of elements of $K$ — **it carries no mathematical structure by itself**; its meaning comes entirely from how it is read (as the ledger of a linear map, as coefficients of a linear system, as the recipe of a bilinear form).\n\n⚠ The two indexing conventions ($a_{ij}$ is the entry in row $i$, column $j$) must be used consistently, or transposition and multiplication go wrong.\n\nThe convention throughout is row first, then column.\n\n⭐ As a set, $M_{m \\times n}(K)$ is just $K^{mn}$ — hence it is certainly a $K$-vector space (operations componentwise).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#560493c1',
      },
    },

    'def.determinant': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $A = (a_{ij}) \\in M_n(K)$, define the **determinant**\n\n$$\\det A := \\sum_{\\sigma \\in S_n} \\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\ \\prod_{i=1}^{n} a_{i,\\sigma(i)}$$\n\n($S_n$ is the symmetric group on $n$ letters and $\\operatorname{sgn}$ is the sign of a permutation; there are $n!$ terms.)\n\nViewing the columns of $A$ as vectors in $V = K^{n}$, the map $\\det$ is the unique $D$ from the existence and uniqueness theorem for determinants.\n\nBasic properties: $\\det I_n = 1$; swapping two columns changes the sign; equal columns give $0$; $\\det A^{\\mathsf{T}} = \\det A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2e8e4ef0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric reading ($K = \\mathbb{R}$): $|\\det A|$ is the **volume** of the parallelepiped obtained by applying the linear map $x \\mapsto Ax$ to the unit cube, and the sign is the **orientation**.\n\nHence $\\det = 0 \\iff$ the map flattens space $\\iff$ not invertible.\n\n⭐ This geometric meaning reappears in the change-of-variables formula for integrals: $\\int f\\,dx$ becomes $\\int f\\,|\\det|\\,dy$ — there $|\\det|$ is the volume scaling factor.\n\n⚠ The $n!$ terms in the formula show that this definition **cannot be used for computation** ($n = 20$ already exceeds $10^{18}$ terms);\n\nfor computation use elimination or Laplace expansion.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bbe2e7c5',
      },
    },

    'def.cone': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $D : I \\to \\mathcal{C}$ be a diagram. A **cone** over $D$ is an object $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$ together with a family of morphisms\n\n$$p_i : X \\longrightarrow D(i) \\qquad (i \\in I)$$\n\nsuch that for every $\\alpha : i \\to j$ in $I$,\n\n$$D(\\alpha) \\circ p_i = p_j$$\n\nEquivalently, a cone is a natural transformation from the constant diagram $\\Delta X$ to $D$. Reversing all arrows gives a **cocone**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cf1e5201',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: $X$ stands outside the diagram and casts a beam toward each position of $D$, with the requirement that the projections be compatible (projecting first and then following an arrow in the diagram gives the same result as projecting directly to the target).\n\nWhen $X$ is fixed, the cones over $D$ are exactly the set of natural transformations $\\operatorname{Nat}(\\Delta X, D)$, so a cone is nothing new — just a natural transformation under another name.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dfa84a20',
      },
    },

    'def.presheaf': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **(set-valued) presheaf** on a category $\\mathcal{C}$ is a functor\n\n$$T : \\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{Set}$$\n\nA **morphism** of presheaves is a natural transformation between them. Notation: for a morphism $f : Y \\to X$, write $f^{-1} = T(f) : T(X) \\to T(Y)$; for $s \\in T(X)$, write $s|_Y = f^{-1}(s)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fbb3ca5a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Plainly: **each object is assigned a piece of data, and each morphism is assigned a restriction**. Contravariance is what lets a smaller object receive more data — the further one goes into substructures, the more restrictions there are.\n\nExamples:\n\n- On a poset: assign to each $i$ a set $T_i$, and to $i \\le j$ the restriction $T_j \\to T_i$ (dropping antisymmetry, the same works on a preorder).\n- On a topological space: $\\operatorname{Open}(X)$ is the category of open sets (morphisms are inclusions); a presheaf assigns to each open set $u$ a set $T(u)$, and to $u' \\subseteq u$ a restriction $T(u) \\to T(u')$, $s \\mapsto s|_{u'}$.\n\nThe presheaf of continuous functions $C^{0}$, the presheaf of smooth functions $C^{\\infty}$, the constant presheaf $\\underline{E}$ ($\\underline{E}(u) = E$, with restrictions the identity), and the presheaf of constant functions are all examples.\n\n- Fix $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$: $h_{X} : Y \\mapsto \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(Y, X)$ is a presheaf, called a **representable presheaf**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9cb00c1e',
      },
    },

    'lem.urysohn': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a topological space. Then $X$ is **normal** $\\iff$ for any two disjoint closed sets $A, B \\subseteq X$, there exists a continuous function\n\n$$f : X \\longrightarrow [0, 1], \\qquad f|_{A} \\equiv 0, \\qquad f|_{B} \\equiv 1.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9323dad3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **What it means: normality = \"there are enough continuous functions\".** Saying only that \"closed sets can be separated by open sets\" is still purely topological; Urysohn's lemma turns it into **a single function** — from then on one can measure topology with real numbers, and analysis can connect.\n\n**How the proof goes (the \"bisection\" construction of $f$).** First build a family of open sets between the two closed sets: for every dyadic rational $q \\in [0,1]$ assign an open set $U_{q}$ such that\n\n$$A \\subseteq U_{q} \\subseteq \\overline{U_{q}} \\subseteq U_{q'} \\quad (q < q'),$$\n\nThis step uses only normality (each step is \"disjoint closed sets are separated by open sets\"). Then set\n\n$$f(x) := \\inf\\{\\, q : x \\in U_{q} \\,\\}$$\n\n(take $1$ if $x \\notin$ any $U_{q}$). The **monotone squeezing** of that family of open sets shows that $f$ is continuous, with $f|_{A} = 0$ and $f|_{B} = 1$. The converse is direct: $f^{-1}[0, 1/2)$ and $f^{-1}(1/2, 1]$ are the open sets separating $A, B$. ∎\n\n**Use**: it is the prerequisite for the **Tietze extension theorem** (extending a continuous real function on a closed subset to the whole space), and Tietze in turn is the engine behind the statement that a real Banach space is acyclic on a compact Hausdorff space — a chain that eventually leads to condensed cohomology.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8d837d2b',
      },
    },

    'def.paracompact': {
      statement: {
        en: "A topological space $X$ is **paracompact** if **every open cover has a locally finite open refinement**: for every open cover $\\mathcal{U}$ there exists an open cover $\\mathcal{V}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathcal{V}\\ \\text{refines}\\ \\mathcal{U}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\mathcal{V}\\ \\text{is locally finite}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2f7b5f43',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ **Convention**: Hausdorffness is **not** built into the definition of \"paracompact\" here (some books include it). Whenever separation is needed, \"paracompact Hausdorff\" is written out explicitly.\n\n**Where it sits in the hierarchy**: compact $\\implies$ paracompact (a finite subcover is certainly locally finite); paracompact Hausdorff $\\implies$ normal $\\implies$ regular $\\implies$ Hausdorff (theorems of this pack); metric spaces $\\implies$ paracompact (Stone's theorem).\n\n⭐ **What it buys**: partitions of unity (hence smoothing, integration and Stokes on manifolds, and extension theorems), and the local finiteness that sheaf theory needs so that \"Čech cohomology equals sheaf cohomology\" holds in detail. Manifolds are second countable by definition, hence automatically paracompact — which is why analysis never worries about it.\n\n**Heredity**: **open** and **closed** subspaces both inherit paracompactness (open subspace $Y$: one **cannot** simply add $X\\setminus Y$ to the cover — it need not be open; the correct route is first to take $Y$ open, extend each member openly inside $X$, lift the cover to an open cover of $X$, then take a locally finite open refinement and intersect it with $Y$; closed subspace: intersect the members of the open cover with the closed subspace). A general subspace need not be paracompact.\n\n⚠ The converse fails completely: a normal space need not be paracompact — $[0, \\omega_{1})$ (the set of all ordinals below the first uncountable ordinal) is normal and countably compact but not compact, and \"countably compact $+$ paracompact $\\implies$ compact\", so it is not paracompact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0cd0aff7',
      },
    },

    'lem.path-product': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\alpha$ be a path from $x_{0}$ to $x_{1}$, and $\\beta$ a path from $x_{1}$ to $x_{2}$. Their **concatenation** is \"first traverse $\\alpha$, then $\\beta$, each in half the time\":\n\n$$(\\alpha * \\beta)(t) = \\alpha(2t) \\quad (0 \\le t \\le \\tfrac{1}{2}),$$\n$$(\\alpha * \\beta)(t) = \\beta(2t - 1) \\quad (\\tfrac{1}{2} \\le t \\le 1).$$\n\nBy the pasting lemma, $\\alpha * \\beta$ is continuous and is a path from $x_{0}$ to $x_{2}$. Also write $\\bar{\\alpha}(t) := \\alpha(1 - t)$ for the **reverse path**, and let $e_{x}$ denote the constant path $t \\mapsto x$. Then:\n\n1. **Well defined**: $\\alpha \\simeq_{p} \\alpha'$ and $\\beta \\simeq_{p} \\beta'$ $\\implies$ $\\alpha * \\beta \\simeq_{p} \\alpha' * \\beta'$;\n2. **Associativity**: $(\\alpha * \\beta) * \\gamma \\simeq_{p} \\alpha * (\\beta * \\gamma)$;\n3. **Unit**: $e_{x_{0}} * \\alpha \\simeq_{p} \\alpha \\simeq_{p} \\alpha * e_{x_{1}}$;\n4. **Inverse**: $\\alpha * \\bar{\\alpha} \\simeq_{p} e_{x_{0}}$ and $\\bar{\\alpha} * \\alpha \\simeq_{p} e_{x_{1}}$.\n\nThus \"homotopy classes of loops based at $x_{0}$\" with multiplication $[\\alpha][\\beta] := [\\alpha * \\beta]$ form a group.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#924f9a82',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ **None of these four is an equality at the level of paths.** $(\\alpha * \\beta) * \\gamma$ and $\\alpha * (\\beta * \\gamma)$ have genuinely different values at $t = 1/4, 1/2$; they are only **homotopic**. Hence multiplication must be defined on **homotopy classes**.\n\n**Reparametrization is the whole trick**: if $\\varphi : [0, 1] \\to [0, 1]$ is continuous with $\\varphi(0) = 0$ and $\\varphi(1) = 1$, then $\\alpha \\circ \\varphi \\simeq_{p} \\alpha$, with homotopy $F(t, s) := \\alpha((1 - s)\\,\\varphi(t) + s\\,t)$. Associativity is just taking $\\varphi$ to be the reparametrization \"split at $1/4, 1/2, 1/4$\".\n\n⭐ The homotopy in the unit law **cannot** be something \"stationary\" like $F(t, s) = e_{x_{0}} * \\alpha$: the unit law really requires a deformation, not an equality.\n\n💡 In one sentence: **multiplication at the level of paths is bad (not associative, no unit), at the level of homotopy classes it is good (a group)** — which is why the fundamental group collects \"classes\" rather than \"paths\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7e293f99',
      },
    },

    'prop.induced-homomorphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : X \\to Y$ be continuous with $f(x_{0}) = y_{0}$. Define\n\n$$f_{*} : \\pi_{1}(X, x_{0}) \\longrightarrow \\pi_{1}(Y, y_{0}),$$\n$$f_{*}([\\alpha]) := [f \\circ \\alpha].$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. **Well defined**: $\\alpha \\simeq_{p} \\beta \\implies f \\circ \\alpha \\simeq_{p} f \\circ \\beta$;\n2. **Homomorphism**: $f_{*}([\\alpha][\\beta]) = f_{*}([\\alpha])\\,f_{*}([\\beta])$;\n3. **Functoriality**: $(1_{X})_{*} = \\operatorname{id}$, and $(g \\circ f)_{*} = g_{*} \\circ f_{*}$;\n4. **Homotopy invariance**: if $f \\simeq g \\; (\\operatorname{rel} \\{x_{0}\\})$, then $f_{*} = g_{*}$.\n\nTherefore $\\pi_{1}$ is a **functor** from \"based topological spaces\" to \"groups\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b2c0210d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Immediate consequences of the four items**:\n\n- A homeomorphism $f$ gives an isomorphism $f_{*}$ (the inverse is the inverse homeomorphism), so $\\pi_{1}$ is a **topological invariant**;\n- More strongly, a homotopy equivalence gives an isomorphism — so $\\pi_{1}$ is also a **homotopy invariant**, and a contractible space must have fundamental group $1$;\n- By (3), $\\pi_{1}$ turns \"composition of spaces\" into \"composition of groups\", with the **order not reversed** (unlike $f^{*}$ in cohomology).\n\n⚠ Item (4) requires **relative to the basepoint**: a general homotopy $f \\simeq g$ only guarantees that $g_{*}$ and $f_{*}$ are \"conjugate\", not equal. This is exactly what `prop.pi1-basepoint` handles.\n\n💡 Use: to prove \"if $\\pi_{1}$ differ then the spaces are not homeomorphic\", it suffices to find something continuous maps cannot do (e.g. no isomorphism). For proving \"cannot\", functoriality is the cheapest tool — that is how `thm.no-retraction-disk` in this pack argues.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe9ac6a1',
      },
    },

    'thm.euler-characteristic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a **finite** simplicial complex, $\\alpha_{p}$ the number of its $p$-simplices, and $\\beta_{p} := \\operatorname{rank} H_{p}(K)$ the $p$-th **Betti number** (the rank of the free part). Then\n\n$$\\chi(K) = \\sum_{p} (-1)^{p} \\alpha_{p} = \\sum_{p} (-1)^{p} \\beta_{p}.$$\n\nThat is: **the left side merely counts simplices, while the right side depends only on the homology groups**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c9f0047d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Two examples**:\n\n- The tetrahedral subdivision of $S^{2}$: $\\alpha = (4, 6, 4, 0)$, $\\chi = 4 - 6 + 4 - 0 = 2$; $\\beta = (1, 0, 1)$, $\\sum = 1 - 0 + 1 = 2$ ✓\n- The minimal subdivision of the real projective plane $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ (6 vertices / 15 edges / 10 faces): $\\chi = 6 - 15 + 10 = 1$; while $H_{1}(\\mathbb{RP}^{2}) = \\mathbb{Z}/2$ **has rank zero**, so $\\sum(-1)^{p}\\beta_{p} = 1 - 0 + 0 = 1$ ✓\n\n⚠ **Note that the right side uses the rank (Betti number), not $\\#H_{p}$**. Torsion (e.g. $\\mathbb{Z}/2$) **does not enter** the alternating sum; the example $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ above matches precisely because of this.\n\n⭐ **The real value of the theorem** is not the identity itself but that the right side **depends only on homology** — hence $\\chi$ is independent of the triangulation (together with `thm.simplicial-approximation`) and is a topological invariant. Conversely, to compute $\\chi$ one only counts simplices, without computing homology at all.\n\n**Idea of the proof** (the full computation is on the edge): twice rank–nullity, once on $\\partial_{p}$ and once on $Z_{p}/B_{p} \\cong H_{p}$; the dimensions of the boundary groups cancel in pairs in the alternating sum (telescoping).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4dc64cab',
      },
    },

    'prop.h0-components': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X \\ne \\emptyset$, and let $\\pi_{0}(X)$ be the set of its **path components**. Then\n\n$$H_{0}(X) \\;\\cong\\; \\bigoplus_{C \\in \\pi_{0}(X)} \\mathbb{Z}.$$\n\nMore precisely: choosing a point $C$ from each component $x_{C}$, the classes $[x_{C}]$ form a basis of $H_{0}(X)$. In particular\n\n$$\\tilde{H}_{0}(X) = 0 \\iff X \\text{ is path-connected}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#57d5e98a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**So $H_{0}$ is \"how many pieces $X$ has\"**. This also explains why $\\tilde{H}_{0}(S^{0}) = \\mathbb{Z}$ (two points; rank $2$ divided by the diagonal $\\mathbb{Z}$ leaves rank $1$), so the formula for sphere homology is uniform at $p = 0$ as well.\n\n⚠ **Path connected vs connected**: $H_{0}$ sees only **path** components, not connected components. On usual spaces (locally path connected) the two agree, but not in general — for instance the topologist's sine curve is connected but not path connected, and its $H_{0}$ reflects the path components.\n\n**Why the boundary lands on \"differences of points in the same component\"**: $\\partial$ acts only on a singular $1$-simplex (a path), and its image is the difference of the two endpoints; the whole path lies in one path component. So $\\partial$ cannot cross components $\\implies$ points in different components are linearly independent in $H_{0}$.\n\n💡 This is the first conclusion in this pack that **computes an actual number**: $H_{0}$ is the only homology group whose answer you can write down just by looking at the space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#396c13f2',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "A real sequence $\\{x_n\\}$ converges (to some real number) if and only if it is a Cauchy sequence.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#37ae9d42',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The two directions have completely different content: \"convergent $\\implies$ Cauchy\" uses only the triangle inequality and is **trivial**; \"Cauchy $\\implies$ convergent\" is **completeness itself** — it says that a sequence whose own terms are eventually close to each other must have a limit waiting for it.\n\n⭐ Practical value: to prove convergence one need not guess the limit first.\n\nThe convergence radius of power series, the completeness of function spaces, and the definition of a Banach space (every Cauchy sequence converges) are all direct descendants of this result.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#823bca41',
      },
    },

    'thm.monotone-convergence': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{x_n\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- If $x_n$ is increasing ($x_1 \\le x_2 \\le \\cdots$) and bounded above, then $\\{x_n\\}$ converges and\n\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} x_n = \\sup_{n} x_n$$\n\n- If $x_n$ is decreasing and bounded below, then $\\{x_n\\}$ converges and $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} x_n = \\inf_{n} x_n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b015eeaa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The value of the theorem: it asserts that the limit exists **without knowing who the limit is** — and the proof identifies it along the way (it is the supremum of the sequence).\n\nAll of real analysis uses this pattern (series with nonnegative terms, monotone functions, continuity of measure, the prototype of the monotone convergence theorem).\n\n⚠ Neither hypothesis can be dropped: monotone without boundedness diverges ($x_n = n$); bounded without monotonicity may fail to converge ($x_n = (-1)^{n}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cba5a5d9',
      },
    },

    'lem.nested-intervals': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $I_n = [a_n, b_n]$ be a sequence of **closed** intervals satisfying\n\n- $I_1 \\supseteq I_2 \\supseteq \\cdots$ (nested),\n- $b_n - a_n \\to 0$ (the lengths tend to zero).\n\nThen $\\bigcap_{n=1}^{\\infty} I_n$ contains exactly **one** point.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#44dba418',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the formal form of \"bisection\": applied to a bounded sequence it gives the Bolzano–Weierstrass theorem; every step of a proof that keeps halving an interval is this lemma.\n\n⚠ Neither condition can be relaxed: **closed** — $\\bigcap_{n} (0, 1/n] = \\emptyset$; **lengths tending to zero** — $\\bigcap_{n} [n, +\\infty) = \\emptyset$ while the lengths do not tend to zero.\n\nIf only nesting is required and the lengths need not tend to zero, the conclusion weakens to \"the intersection is nonempty\" (the intersection is then a point or a closed interval).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#80e61a2d',
      },
    },

    'cor.heine-borel': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, regarded as a metric space with the Euclidean distance. Then $K$ is compact if and only if $K$ is bounded and closed.\n\n(\"Compact\" means \"every open cover has a finite subcover\", or equivalently \"every sequence has a subsequence converging to a point of $K$\".)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4fca12bb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is **a corollary of a corollary**: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ complete + bounded sets are totally bounded $\\implies$ bounded closed sets are sequentially compact $\\implies$ compact (see \"three equivalent characterizations of compactness in metric spaces\").\n\nWhat is really used is the coordinatewise version of Bolzano–Weierstrass.\n\n⚠ It is **not** a theorem for general metric spaces: in a general metric space \"compact $\\implies$ bounded closed\" always holds, but not conversely (the discrete space is a sufficient example, as is the unit ball of $\\ell^{2}$).\n\n⭐ An immediately usable consequence: the closed interval $[a, b]$ is compact — hence a continuous function attains its extrema on it, is uniformly continuous, and is integrable.\n\nThe three existence theorems of elementary calculus all rest on this.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3b50e1f7',
      },
    },

    'lem.archimedean': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a **complete ordered field** (for example $\\mathbb{R}$). Then:\n\n**(i) Archimedean property**: the natural numbers are unbounded in $K$ —\n\n$$\\forall x \\in K,\\ \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N} : n > x$$\n\n**(ii) $\\mathbb{Q}$ is dense in $K$**: between any two elements there lies a rational —\n\n$$\\forall x, y \\in K,\\ x < y \\implies \\exists q \\in \\mathbb{Q} : x < q < y$$\n\n(Here $\\mathbb{Q}$ denotes the embedded image of $\\mathbb{Q}$ in $K$: $1$ generates $\\mathbb{Z}$ in $K$, and taking fractions gives $\\mathbb{Q}$.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#317dfd6c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Both items are **consequences of completeness (the supremum principle)**; no further assumption is needed.\n\nTheir uses are extensive:\n\n- Proving the **uniqueness of the complete ordered field** (well-definedness of $\\mathbb{R}$) relies on (ii) — the rationals are dense on both sides, which is what allows approximation of arbitrary elements by \"images of rationals\";\n\n\n- Every \"take $n$ large enough\" and \"take $\\varepsilon = 1/n$\" in analysis is really an application of (i);\n\n\n- (ii) is also the licence for \"approximating reals by rationals\": every real number is a supremum of rationals.\n\n⚠ Completeness $\\implies$ the Archimedean property, but **not conversely**: $\\mathbb{Q}$ is Archimedean yet not complete.\n\n\n⚠ The $q$ squeezed in (ii) must lie **strictly** between the two numbers — this is precisely where \"dense\" is stronger than \"there are rationals arbitrarily close\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#72990682',
      },
    },

    'prop.limit-arithmetic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $x_n \\to x$, $y_n \\to y$ (finite), $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then\n\n- $x_n \\pm y_n \\to x \\pm y$, $c\\,x_n \\to c\\,x$, $x_n y_n \\to xy$;\n- if $y \\ne 0$, then for $n$ sufficiently large $y_n \\ne 0$, and $x_n / y_n \\to x / y$;\n- $|x_n| \\to |x|$;\n- **Order preservation**: if there is $N_0$ such that $x_n \\le y_n$ whenever $n \\ge N_0$, then $x \\le y$.\n\nThe function version is copied verbatim (replace \"there exists $N$\" by \"there exists $\\delta$\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2a1a9837',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This group is a set of **operational laws**: it says that \"taking limits\" commutes with the four arithmetic operations.\n\nAll computations in analysis rest on it.\n\n⚠ Order preservation yields only $\\le$, never $<$: for example $0 < 1/n$ holds for all $n$, yet the limit is $0 \\le 0$.\n\n**A strict inequality is smoothed away when passing to the limit**.\n\n⚠ \"A limit, if it exists, is unique\" and \"convergent implies **locally** bounded\" also come from the same definition (the function version only guarantees boundedness on some punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$: for $f(x) = x$ with $x_0 = 0$, the limit exists, yet $f$ is unbounded on its whole domain).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#74d7129c',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-mvt': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a < b$, and let $f, g$ be continuous on $[a, b]$ and differentiable on $(a, b)$, with $g'(x) \\ne 0$ holding everywhere in $(a, b)$. Then there exists $\\xi \\in (a, b)$ such that\n\n$$\\frac{f(b) - f(a)}{g(b) - g(a)} = \\frac{f'(\\xi)}{g'(\\xi)}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8bea67b0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It is not simply Lagrange's theorem \"with $g(x) = x$\": expressing the increments of two functions **simultaneously** at the same point $\\xi$ is the entire reason l'Hôpital's rule works.\n\n⚠ The condition $g' \\ne 0$ cannot be omitted — it also guarantees $g(b) \\ne g(a)$ (by Rolle's theorem), so that the denominator makes sense.\n\n⚠ Parametric-curve reading: $t \\mapsto (g(t), f(t))$ is a curve, and the conclusion says that the tangent at some interior point is **parallel to** the chord joining the endpoints — geometrically the same statement as Lagrange's \"parallel to the chord\", except that the chord need not be the graph of a function.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a43823ed',
      },
    },

    'thm.riemann-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be bounded on $[a, b]$. Then $f$ is integrable if and only if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a partition $P$ such that\n\n$$U(f, P) - L(f, P) = \\sum_{i} \\omega_i\\,\\Delta x_i < \\varepsilon$$\n\n($\\omega_i$ is the oscillation of $f$ on the $i$-th subinterval.) Moreover the two definitions (limit of tagged sums / Darboux suprema) give the same value of the integral.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6b3b7434',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Oscillation sums can be made arbitrarily small** is the most useful integrability criterion in the subject: every proof that \"some function is integrable\" reduces to an estimate of oscillation sums.\n\n⭐ Example of use: for continuous functions use **uniform continuity** to control $\\omega_i$;\n\nfor **monotonically increasing** functions use equal partitions to make $\\sum \\omega_i \\Delta x_i \\le (f(b) - f(a))\\|P\\|$ (for decreasing functions take absolute values on the right).\n\n⚠ The criterion requires only that **some** partition makes the oscillation sum small, not all partitions — because the oscillation sum is monotone nonincreasing under refinement.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#007e9dff',
      },
    },

    'prop.integral-order': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f, g$ be integrable on $[a, b]$.\n\n- **Order preservation**: if $f(x) \\le g(x)$ holds everywhere, then $\\int_a^b f \\le \\int_a^b g$; in particular $f \\ge 0 \\implies \\int_a^b f \\ge 0$.\n- **Absolute value**: $|f|$ is integrable, and\n\n$$\\Bigl| \\int_a^b f \\Bigr| \\le \\int_a^b |f|$$\n\n- **Basic estimate**: if $|f(x)| \\le M$ holds everywhere, then $\\bigl| \\int_a^b f \\bigr| \\le M\\,(b - a)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#389e9e2e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Order preservation sounds trivial, but it is the source of **all integral inequalities**: to prove $\\int f \\le \\int g$, it suffices to prove $f \\le g$ pointwise.\n\n⚠ The **non-strict** inequality obtained from order preservation cannot be upgraded: if $f \\le g$ and $f < g$ only at one point, still $\\int_a^b f = \\int_a^b g$ (changing the value at one point does not change the Riemann integral).\n\n⭐ Integrability of $|f|$ is a phenomenon specific to Riemann integration (on the Lebesgue side, integrability of $f$ immediately implies integrability of $|f|$);\n\nconversely integrability of $|f|$ does not imply integrability of $f$ — take $f$ alternating between $1$ and $-1$ on rationals/irrationals; then $|f| \\equiv 1$ is integrable while $f$ is not.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e130ee28',
      },
    },

    'thm.root-test': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a_n \\ge 0$, and set\n\n$$\\alpha := \\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} \\sqrt[n]{a_n}$$\n\nThen: if $\\alpha < 1$, $\\sum a_n$ converges; if $\\alpha > 1$, $\\sum a_n$ diverges; if $\\alpha = 1$, there is **no conclusion**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e7913f13',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The mechanism is \"comparison with a geometric series\": when $\\alpha < q < 1$, eventually $\\sqrt[n]{a_n} \\le q$, i.e. $a_n \\le q^{n}$, and the geometric series converges.\n\n⭐ Relation to the ratio test: **the root test is stronger**.\n\nIf $\\lim a_{n+1}/a_n$ exists, then $\\lim \\sqrt[n]{a_n}$ also exists and the two are equal;\n\nthe converse fails (examples such as $a_n = 2^{-n}$ and $a_n = 2^{-n - (-1)^n}$).\n\nSo \"what the ratio test cannot decide, the root test may\".\n\n⚠ What is used is the **$\\limsup$**, not $\\lim$: the sequence $\\sqrt[n]{a_n}$ need not converge, but $\\limsup$ is always defined.\n\n⚠ When $\\alpha = 1$ it fails completely: both $\\sum 1/n$ diverging and $\\sum 1/n^{2}$ converging have $\\alpha = 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#de3a09b9',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-criterion-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{a_n\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, $s_N := \\sum_{n=1}^{N} a_n$. Then $\\sum a_n$ converges if and only if\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall n > m \\ge N :\\ \\Bigl| \\sum_{k=m+1}^{n} a_k \\Bigr| < \\varepsilon$$\n\n**Necessary condition**: $\\sum a_n$ converges $\\implies$ $a_n \\to 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#47636a44',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The role of this criterion is **to turn a series problem into a sequence problem**: it is just the Cauchy criterion for $\\mathbb{R}$ applied to the sequence of partial sums $\\{s_N\\}$ (with interval additivity writing $s_n - s_m$ as a tail).\n\n⚠ \"$a_n \\to 0$\" is only a **necessary** condition: the harmonic series $\\sum 1/n$ has terms tending to zero yet diverges.\n\nTo prove divergence it is very useful (if the terms do not tend to zero, the series diverges at once); to prove convergence it says nothing.\n\n⭐ Every criterion in this pack descends from it: the comparison test uses \"a tail dominated by a larger tail\", Abel–Dirichlet uses \"a tail dominated by the boundedness of partial sums\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#506188ca',
      },
    },

    'thm.comparison-test': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $0 \\le a_n \\le b_n$ hold for all sufficiently large $n$. Then\n\n- $\\sum b_n$ converges $\\implies$ $\\sum a_n$ converges;\n- $\\sum a_n$ diverges $\\implies$ $\\sum b_n$ diverges.\n\n**Limit form**: if $a_n, b_n > 0$ and $\\dfrac{a_n}{b_n} \\to L$, then for $L \\in (0, +\\infty)$ the two **converge or diverge together**; for $L = 0$, convergence of $\\sum b_n$ implies convergence of $\\sum a_n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#990c5f71',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is **the foundation of all criteria for series with positive terms**, and it is itself just a direct translation of \"a monotone bounded partial-sum sequence $\\implies$ converges\": the partial sums of $\\sum a_n$ are dominated by those of $\\sum b_n$.\n\n⚠ The hypothesis is **nonnegativity** (or eventual nonnegativity): with mixed signs the partial sums are no longer monotone, and \"dominated\" means nothing.\n\n⚠ It is **not** a corollary of Kummer's test — the direction is the opposite: the proof of Kummer's test itself uses this \"monotone bounded partial sums\" idea (i.e. comparison).\n\nDeleting this would leave the whole pack of criteria without a foothold.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0a64f047',
      },
    },

    'thm.omega': {
      statement: {
        en: "There exists a **smallest inductive set** $\\omega$: it contains $\\emptyset$, is closed under $x \\mapsto x \\cup \\{x\\}$, and is contained in every inductive set:\n\n$$\\omega = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}\\}\\}, \\ldots  \\}$$\n\nReading $\\in$ as $<$, $\\langle \\omega , \\in \\rangle$ is the natural number system $\\langle \\mathbb{N}, <\\rangle$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ae7341a5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$\\omega$ is at the same time the smallest limit ordinal. Its construction needs only the **axiom of infinity + separation schema** (**the power set is not needed**: $\\omega$ is the set of those elements that \"belong to every inductive subset\", which separation extracts directly from an inductive set).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#06459c3e',
      },
    },

    'thm.induction': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\omega$ be the smallest inductive set (that is, $\\mathbb{N}$).\n\n**(i) Induction principle**: if $S \\subseteq \\omega$ satisfies\n\n$$\\emptyset \\in S, \\qquad x \\in S \\implies x \\cup \\{x\\} \\in S$$\n\nthen $S = \\omega$.\n\n**(ii) Recursive definition**: given a set $A$, an element $a_0 \\in A$ and a function $F : A \\to A$, there exists a **unique** function $g : \\omega \\to A$ such that\n\n$$g(\\emptyset) = a_0, \\qquad g(x \\cup \\{x\\}) = F(g(x)) \\quad \\forall x \\in \\omega$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3a688b8c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The induction principle governs proofs, recursive definition governs constructions** — two sides of the same fact, both coming from \"$\\omega$ is the smallest inductive set\".\n\nAddition, multiplication and order on $\\mathbb{N}$, and later the operations on $\\mathbb{Z}$ and $\\mathbb{Q}$, are all defined recursively and their properties verified by induction.\n\n⚠ **The induction principle holds only for $\\omega$**;\n\nfor a general well-ordered set one needs **transfinite induction / transfinite recursion**.\n\n\n⚠ Recursive definition must use \"a function $F$\", not \"a sentence\", otherwise one falls into the trap of defining something in terms of itself.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f0214205',
      },
    },

    'thm.caratheodory': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu^{*}$ be an outer measure on $X$, and $\\mathcal{M}$ the family of all $\\mu^{*}$-measurable sets. Then\n\n1. **$\\mathcal{M}$ is a $\\sigma$-algebra**;\n2. **$\\mu^*|_\\mathcal{M}$ is a measure on $(X, \\mathcal{M})$** (that is, $\\mu^{*}$ is genuinely additive on $\\mathcal{M}$);\n3. this measure is **complete**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#adc2cf3f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the hinge of the whole construction of measure theory: **an outer measure need not be additive, but on its \"splittable sets\" it becomes additive**.\n\nThe proof of 1 is a lengthy verification: finite unions, complements and countable disjoint unions, one by one.\n\nCompleteness of 3 comes from the definition itself: if $\\mu^{*}(A) = 0$ and $B \\subseteq A$, then for every $E$ one has $\\mu^{*}(E \\cap B) \\le \\mu^{*}(B) = 0$ and $\\mu^{*}(E \\cap B^{c}) \\le \\mu^{*}$(E); squeezing both sides shows that $B$ satisfies the measurability condition.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#01849809',
      },
    },

    'prop.outer-measure-induced': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{E} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$, $\\emptyset \\in \\mathcal{E}$, $X \\in \\mathcal{E}$, $\\rho : \\mathcal{E} \\to [0, +\\infty ]$ satisfy $\\rho (\\emptyset ) = 0$.\n\nFor $A \\subseteq X$ define\n\n$$\\mu^*(A) := \\inf \\{ \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty} \\rho(E_j) : E_j \\in \\mathcal{E}, A \\subseteq \\bigcup_{j=1}^{\\infty} E_j \\}$$\n\n(covering $A$ by countably many sets from $\\mathcal{E}$ and taking the infimum.) Then $\\mu^{*}$ is an **outer measure** on $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c18a4858',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This formula is just \"cover $A$ with bricks of known area and take the cheapest covering\" — the Lebesgue outer measure is exactly the special case ($\\mathcal{E}$ open intervals, $\\rho$ length).\n\nWhy require $X \\in \\mathcal{E}$: to guarantee that $A \\subseteq X$ always has a cover, so that the infimum is not taken over the empty set.\n\n⚠ This step uses only $\\rho (\\emptyset ) = 0$, **not additivity** — so in general $\\mu^{*}$ does not recover $\\rho$. Recovering it is precisely Carathéodory's job.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9d9c5f96',
      },
    },

    'prop.caratheodory-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathfrak{A} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(X)$ be an **algebra**, $\\mu _{0}$ a **premeasure** on $\\mathfrak{A}$, and $\\mu^{*}$ the outer measure induced by $\\mu _{0}$ (as in the previous proposition, with $\\mathcal{E} = \\mathfrak{A}$, $\\rho = \\mu _{0}$).\n\nThen\n\n1. $\\mu^*|_\\mathfrak{A} = \\mu_0$\n2. **every set in $\\mathfrak{A}$ is $\\mu^{*}$-measurable**, hence\n\n$$\\mathfrak{A} \\subseteq \\mathcal{M}(\\mathfrak{A}) \\subseteq \\mathcal{M}$$\n\n($\\mathcal{M}$ is the family of all $\\mu^{*}$-measurable sets), so $\\mu^{*}|_\\mathcal{M}$ is an extension of $\\mu _{0}$ to the $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathfrak{A})$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8818cb9c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the finale of the three-step \"premeasure $\\to$ outer measure $\\to$ measure\": **the premeasure is fully recovered on the extension to the $\\sigma$-algebra**.\n\nThe two directions of 1: $\\mu^*(A) \\le \\mu_0(A)$ is direct ($A$ is covered by $A$ itself); the reverse uses countable additivity of $\\mu _{0}$.\n\n2 is the technical core: verify Carathéodory's splitting condition on $\\mathfrak{A}$.\n\nNot only $\\mathfrak{A}$ itself, but also the sets obtained by countable unions and intersections (and iterating) — $\\mathfrak{A}_\\sigma$, $\\mathfrak{A}_\\delta$, $\\mathfrak{A}_\\sigma \\delta$, $\\mathfrak{A}_\\delta \\sigma$ — are all $\\mu^{*}$-measurable, because $\\mathcal{M}$ is a $\\sigma$-algebra and is closed under these operations.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#052f3851',
      },
    },

    'thm.caratheodory-uniqueness': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu _{0}$ be a premeasure on a **ring** $\\mathfrak{A}$ (Carathéodory uniqueness already holds for rings; the case where $\\mathfrak{A}$ is an algebra is a special case), and $\\mu := \\mu^{*}|_\\mathcal{M}$ the extension obtained from it by Carathéodory. If $\\nu$ is a measure on $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathfrak{A})$ that is **another** extension of $\\mu _{0}$, then\n\n$$\\nu(E) \\le \\mu(E)\\quad  \\forall E \\in \\mathcal{M}(\\mathfrak{A})$$\n\nand equality holds when $\\mu(E) < \\infty$; moreover, if $\\mu$ is $\\sigma$-finite, then $\\nu = \\mu$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#848eca6a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Key point: **the Carathéodory extension is the \"largest\" one**.\n\nThe reason is hidden in the definition of $\\mu^{*}$ — it is built by \"taking infima over coverings\", and any extension $\\nu$ must be subadditive, so $\\nu (A) \\le \\sum \\nu (E_{j}) = \\sum \\mu _{0}(E_{j})$; taking the infimum over all coverings gives $\\nu \\le \\mu^{*}$.\n\n⚠ Without $\\sigma$-finiteness uniqueness **fails** (Lebesgue length on $\\mathbb{R}$ is not $\\sigma$-finite, and this cannot be fixed): take $\\mathfrak{A} =$ the half-open intervals with finite disjoint unions, $\\mu _{0}((a,b]) = +\\infty$ ($a < b$); it is not $\\sigma$-finite (each piece is $\\infty$). Two extensions: $\\mu _{1}(E) = +\\infty$ ($E \\ne \\emptyset$), $\\mu _{1}(\\emptyset) = 0$; another $\\mu _{2}(E) = 0$ ($E$ is a Lebesgue null set), and $+\\infty$ otherwise. They agree on $\\mathfrak{A}$ (both take $\\infty$) yet part ways on singletons.\n\n$\\sigma$-finiteness is exactly what plugs this loophole.\n\nThe standard finale of constructing a measure is this: **first build a premeasure (easy), then extend (Carathéodory guarantees existence), and finally use $\\sigma$-finiteness for uniqueness**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#413ea295',
      },
    },

    'thm.completion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$ be a measure space. Put\n\n$$\\mathcal{N} = \\{ N \\in \\mathcal{M} : \\mu(N) = 0 \\}$$\n\n$$\\bar{\\mathcal{M}} = \\{ E \\cup F : E \\in \\mathcal{M},\\ F \\subseteq N,\\ N \\in \\mathcal{N} \\}$$\n\nThen\n\n1. $\\bar{\\mathcal{M}}$ is a $\\sigma$-algebra, and $\\mathcal{M} \\subseteq \\bar{\\mathcal{M}}$;\n2. let $\\bar{\\mu}(E \\cup F) := \\mu(E)$; it is a well-defined measure on $\\bar{\\mathcal{M}}$;\n3. $(X, \\bar{\\mathcal{M}}, \\bar{\\mu})$ is a **complete** measure space, called the **completion** of $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0e07de8e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The intuition for $\\bar{\\mathcal{M}}$: \"sprinkle a little dust of null sets onto the original measurable sets\".\n\n**Well-definedness must be checked**: the representation $E \\cup F$ is not unique; if $E_{1} \\cup F_{1} = E_{2} \\cup F_{2}$, one must prove $\\mu (E_{1}) = \\mu (E_{2})$.\n\nThe method is to use $E_{1} \\subseteq E_{2} \\cup N_{2}$ ($N_{2}$ a null set) to get $\\mu (E_{1}) \\le \\mu (E_{2})$, and symmetrically for the reverse.\n\n⚠ Completion is \"stuffing the subsets of null sets in\"; the price is that the $\\sigma$-algebra grows while the measure stays the same.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fb06831e',
      },
    },

    'def.premeasure': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $\\mathcal{M}$ is only a **ring** (not necessarily a $\\sigma$-algebra), while $\\mu : \\mathcal{M} \\to [0, +\\infty]$ still satisfies the two axioms of a measure:\n\n$$\\mu(\\emptyset) = 0, \\quad E_j \\cap E_k = \\emptyset\\ (j \\ne k),\\ \\bigcup_j E_j \\in \\mathcal{M} \\implies \\mu\\Bigl(\\bigcup_j E_j\\Bigr) = \\sum_j \\mu(E_j)$$\n\nthen $\\mu$ is called a **premeasure** on $\\mathcal{M}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bf66262f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Why this intermediate notion: on $\\mathbb{R}$ the half-open intervals (a, b] form only a basic class; their finite disjoint unions form a ring, not yet a $\\sigma$-algebra — but length can already be defined there, and that is the premeasure.\n\nCarathéodory's extension theorem lifts the premeasure from the ring all the way to the generated $\\sigma$-algebra. So \"**build the premeasure first, then extend**\" is the standard two-step construction of a measure.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e896cbba',
      },
    },

    'prop.measure-basic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$ be a measure space, with all sets below taken from $\\mathcal{M}$.\n\n- **(a) Monotone**: $E \\subseteq F \\implies \\mu(E) \\le \\mu(F)$\n- **(b) Subadditive**: $\\mu( \\bigcup_j E_j ) \\le \\sum_j \\mu(E_j)$ (disjointness not required)\n- **(c) Continuous from below**: $E_1 \\subseteq E_2 \\subseteq \\cdots \\implies \\mu( \\bigcup_j E_j ) = \\lim_{j\\to\\infty} \\mu(E_j)$\n- **(d) Continuous from above**: $\\mu(E_1) < \\infty, \\quad  E_1 \\supseteq E_2 \\supseteq \\cdots \\implies \\mu( \\bigcap_j E_j ) = \\lim_{j\\to\\infty} \\mu(E_j)$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea018c03',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "(a): split $F$ as $F = E \\cup (F \\setminus E)$ and use countable additivity (fill the remaining positions with $\\emptyset$).\n\n(b): rewrite $\\bigcup E_{j}$ as a disjoint union: $F_{j} = E_{j} \\setminus (E_{1} \\cup \\cdots \\cup E_{j-1})$, then $\\mu (\\bigcup E_{j}) = \\sum \\mu (F_{j}) \\le \\sum \\mu (E_{j})$.\n\n(c): put $A_{j} = E_{j} \\setminus E_{j-1}$ ($E_{0} = \\emptyset$); then $\\bigcup E_{j}$ is a disjoint union of the $A_{j}$, and $\\mu (E_{n}) = \\sum _{j\\le n} \\mu (A_{j})$ — take limits on both sides.\n\n(d): **\"$\\mu (E_{1}) < \\infty$\" cannot be omitted.** Counterexample: $X = \\mathbb{N}$, $\\mu =$ counting measure, $E_{j} = \\{j, j+1$ …}; then every $\\mu (E_{j}) = \\infty$ but the intersection is $\\emptyset$ and $\\mu (\\emptyset ) = 0$.\n\n(c) and (d) together say: a measure is continuous under **monotone limits** of sets — which is exactly what the term \"monotone class\" echoes.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#203a3ee5',
      },
    },

    'def.null-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$ be a measure space, $E \\in \\mathcal{M}$.\n\nIf $\\mu(E) = 0$, then $E$ is called a **$\\mu$-null set** ($\\mu -null set$).\n\nIf **every subset of a null set is measurable**:\n\n$$\\forall E \\in \\mathcal{M}, \\forall F \\subseteq E : \\mu(E) = 0 \\implies F \\in \\mathcal{M}$$\n\nthen $\\mu$ (or the measure space) is called **complete**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#29bd1708',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Completeness says \"subsets of null sets are measurable\". This does **not** hold automatically: the $Borel \\sigma$-algebra with Lebesgue measure is not complete — the Cantor set is Borel with measure 0, and some of its subsets are not Borel.\n\nSo the usual procedure is first to **complete** the Borel measure (see the next theorem), obtaining the $Lebesgue \\sigma$-algebra.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ec0fd133',
      },
    },

    'def.finitely-additive': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $\\mu$ satisfies only (i) and **finite** additivity:\n\n$$A, B \\in \\mathcal{M},\\ A \\cap B = \\emptyset \\implies \\mu(A \\cup B) = \\mu(A) + \\mu(B)$$\n\n(by induction this extends to any finite number of disjoint sets),\n\nthen $\\mu$ is called a **finitely additive measure**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8261235d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "It is even weaker than a premeasure: a premeasure is countably additive (only its domain is a ring), while a finitely additive measure requires only finite additivity.\n\n⚠ Finitely additive $\\ne$ countably additive: on $\\mathbb{N}$ define $\\mu (E) = 0$ ($E$ finite), $\\mu (E) = +\\infty$ ($E$ infinite); it is finitely additive, yet fails countable additivity for $\\bigcup _{n} \\{n\\} = \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nHistorically (the Banach–Tarski era) \"measures\" using only finite additivity were seriously considered; the problem is not constructing length (Lebesgue measure itself is finitely additive, $m((a,b]) = b - a$ being length), but that finite additivity **cannot support countable limit theorems** (MCT, DCT and Fubini all collapse).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2d8d0cfb',
      },
    },

    'thm.termwise-integration': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_n\\} \\subseteq L^+$. Then\n\n$$\\int \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} f_n = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\int f_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dbdb428b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "(Now $f_n \\ge 0$, so both sides may be $\\infty$; the conclusion is equivalent to: the anomaly \"termwise integrals diverge while the total integral is finite\" is impossible.)\n\nIn use this is **unconditional interchange of $\\int$ and $\\sum$**, with no dominating function needed — the price is nonnegativity. With mixed signs one must switch to DCT.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f372c6ae',
      },
    },

    'prop.integrals-equal-iff': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f, g \\in L^1(\\mu)$. Then the following three are equivalent:\n\n$$\\int_E f d\\mu = \\int_E g d\\mu\\quad  \\forall E \\in \\mathcal{M}; $$\n$$\\int |f - g| d\\mu = 0; $$\n$$f = g\\quad  \\mu-\\text{a.e.}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bf15fa83',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Purpose: statements of this kind **identify a function** by its integrals over all measurable sets.\n\nThe **uniqueness** part of the Radon–Nikodym theorem below uses exactly this — two densities giving the same measure must be equal a.e.\n\n(That $E$ ranges over $\\mathcal{M}$ rather than only $X$ is the key: equality only on $X$ is not enough.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#56a8422c',
      },
    },

    'thm.egorov': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu(X) < \\infty$, and $f_n \\to f$ a.e. Then $f_n \\to f$ **almost uniformly**:\n\n$$\\forall\\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists E \\subseteq X :\\ \\mu(E) < \\varepsilon$$\n\nand $f_n \\rightrightarrows  f$ **converges uniformly** on $X \\setminus E$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a9f9daf6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Almost everywhere convergence\" sounds weak, but Egorov says: as long as the space has **finite measure**, it is in fact almost **uniform convergence** — the price is only discarding a set of arbitrarily small measure.\n\n⚠ **\"$\\mu(X) < \\infty$\" cannot be omitted.** Counterexample: $f_n = \\chi_{(n, n+1)}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, converging a.e. to 0, but after discarding any set of finite measure the remainder still has $\\sup = 1$, so there is no uniform convergence.\n\nThis is the template for \"discard a small set in exchange for a good property\" (Lusin's theorem follows the same pattern).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#68d60475',
      },
    },

    'thm.fubini-tonelli': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$, $(Y, \\mathcal{N}, \\nu )$ be **$\\sigma$-finite** measure spaces.\n\n**(a) Tonelli (nonnegative case, no extra hypothesis)**: if $f \\in L^+(X \\times Y)$, then\n\n$$\\int f_x d\\nu \\in L^+(X), \\quad  \\int f^y d\\mu \\in L^+(Y)$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\int f d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X ( \\int_Y f(x, y) d\\nu(y) ) d\\mu(x) = \\int_Y ( \\int_X f(x, y) d\\mu(x) ) d\\nu(y)$$\n\n**(b) Fubini (integrable case)**: if $f \\in L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$, then\n\n- $f_x \\in L^1(\\nu)$ holds for a.e. x, and $f^y \\in L^1(\\mu)$ for a.e. y;\n- $\\int f_x d\\nu \\in L^1(\\mu)$, $\\int f^y d\\mu \\in L^1(\\nu)$ (both defined a.e.);\n- the two triple identities above hold as well.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#54c92704',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Tonelli needs no extra hypothesis** (nonnegativity suffices), so in practice one first uses it to \"test\": if one of the two iterated integrals comes out finite, then $f$ is integrable and the order may safely be swapped.\n\n**Fubini, by contrast, must assume integrability** — otherwise one side is $\\infty$ and the other $-\\infty$, and swapping goes wrong.\n\n⚠ Counterexample (not integrable, iterated integrals unequal): on $[0,1]^{2}$ take $f(x, y) = (x^2 - y^2) / (x^2 + y^2)^2$; the two iterated integrals are $\\pi/4$ and $-\\pi/4$ (for exponent $3$ both sides diverge and give no such value).\n\n⚠ $\\sigma$-finiteness cannot be omitted: in the case \"counting measure $\\times Lebesgue$ measure on $\\mathbb{R}$\", the monotone class argument breaks down and Fubini yields absurd conclusions (the classical counterexample on the diagonal).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92a85e51',
      },
    },

    'thm.hahn-decomposition': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\nu$ be a signed measure on $(X, \\mathcal{M})$. Then there exist $P, N \\in \\mathcal{M}$ such that\n\n$$P \\cup N = X, \\quad  P \\cap N = \\emptyset, $$\n\nwhere **$P$ is positive and $N$ is negative**.\n\nIf $P', N'$ is another such pair, then $P \\triangle  P' = N \\triangle  N'$ is a $\\nu$-null set.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4cf0de58',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is \"cutting the space in two along the sign\": $\\nu$ is nonnegative on $P$ and nonpositive on $N$.\n\n⚠ \"$P \\triangle P'$ is a null set\" only says the decomposition is **unique up to null sets** — strictly speaking a Hahn decomposition is not unique (one may move null sets freely between $P$ and $N$), but the $\\nu$ values of all measurable sets are exactly the same.\n\n⭐ With it, \"signed measure\" reduces completely to \"two genuine measures\" — which is exactly the content of the Jordan decomposition below.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b0092838',
      },
    },

    'thm.jordan-decomposition': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\nu$ be a signed measure. Then there exist **measures** $\\mu_1, \\mu_2$ such that\n\n$$\\nu = \\mu_1 - \\mu_2, \\quad  \\mu_1 \\perp  \\mu_2, $$\n\nand **at least one of them is finite**. Such a decomposition is unique.\n\nConcretely, take a Hahn decomposition $X = P \\sqcup  N$ of $\\nu$ and set\n\n$$\\nu^+ := \\nu(\\cdot \\cap P), \\quad  \\nu^- := -\\nu(\\cdot \\cap N)$$\n\nThen $\\nu = \\nu^+ - \\nu^-$ and $\\nu^+ \\perp  \\nu^-$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#94ecbcdd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The **positive variation** $\\nu^+$ and the **negative variation** $\\nu^-$ are both genuine measures; their sum is called the **total variation**:\n\n$$|\\nu| := \\nu^+ + \\nu^-$$\n\n⭐ \"Signed measure\" is thereby fully dissected: every signed measure is the difference of a pair of mutually singular measures.\n\n⚠ \"At least one finite\" corresponds to the clause \"takes at most one of $\\pm \\infty$\" in the definition of a signed measure.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6f931803',
      },
    },

    'thm.ac-epsilon-delta': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\nu$ be a **finite** signed measure and $\\mu$ a measure. Then\n\n$$\\nu \\ll  \\mu \\iff \\forall\\varepsilon > 0, \\exists\\delta > 0 : \\mu(E) < \\delta \\implies |\\nu(E)| \\le \\varepsilon$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4fd42ffd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "It upgrades \"zero on null sets\" to the quantitative \"small values on sets of small measure\" — this is precisely where the word \"continuous\" in absolute continuity comes from.\n\n⚠ \"$\\nu$ finite\" cannot be omitted: otherwise $\\delta$ cannot be controlled (e.g. the $\\mu = Lebesgue$ measure, or $\\nu(E) = \\int_E 1/x$).\n\n(The $\\Longleftarrow$ direction is straightforward: when $\\mu (E) = 0$, for every $\\varepsilon$ one has $|\\nu (E)| \\le \\varepsilon$, hence $\\nu (E) = 0$.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#11a407fc',
      },
    },

    'prop.ac-and-variations': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu$ be a measure and $\\nu$ a signed measure. Then\n\n$$\\nu \\ll  \\mu \\iff |\\nu| \\ll  \\mu \\iff \\nu^+ \\ll  \\mu\\ \\wedge \\ \\nu^- \\ll  \\mu$$\n\nMoreover: if both $\\nu \\perp  \\mu$ and $\\nu \\ll  \\mu$ hold, then $\\nu = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#97fb4a54',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The second half, \"singular and absolutely continuous $\\implies$ equals zero\", is the **starting point** of the structure theorem: what Radon–Nikodym says is precisely that in general $\\nu$ is the sum of these two parts.\n\nProof: if $\\nu \\perp \\mu$, choose $E$ ($\\mu$-null) and $F$ ($\\nu$-null) partitioning the space; from $\\nu \\ll \\mu$ get $\\nu (E) = 0$; and $\\nu (F) = 0$, so $\\nu$ is 0 on all of $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a588911f',
      },
    },

    'thm.lebesgue-differentiation': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in L^1_{loc}$. Then\n\n$$\\lim_{r\\to0} A_r f(x) = f(x)\\quad \\text{for a.e. } x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9ca52cc8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **an integrable function equals the limit of its local averages almost everywhere**.\n\nThis is also the most intuitive respect in which the Lebesgue integral beats the Riemann integral: a Riemann integrable function is continuous almost everywhere, while here not even continuity is needed.\n\nThe three steps of the proof (expanded in the next arrow): first approximate $\\to$ by continuous functions, then use the maximal theorem to squeeze the \"bad set\" down to measure zero.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#08bf2446',
      },
    },

    'thm.bv-jordan': {
      statement: {
        en: "**(a)** If the real and imaginary parts of $F$ are both $BV$, then $F \\in BV$; and conversely: $F \\in BV \\iff \\operatorname{Re} F \\in BV$ **and** $\\operatorname{Im} F \\in BV$.\n\n**(b)** Let $F : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Then $F \\in BV$ if and only if it can be written as the difference of two bounded increasing functions:\n\n$F \\in BV \\iff F = G - H$, where $G, H$ **are both bounded increasing functions**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3e2fa36f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The concrete choice (the function version of the measure-theoretic Jordan decomposition):\n\n$$F = (1/2)(T_F + F) - (1/2)(T_F - F)$$\n\nThe first term is called the **positive variation** of $F$, the second the **negative variation** — the names correspond exactly to $\\nu ^{+}$ and $\\nu ^{-}$ for signed measures.\n\nBy the lemma, $T_F \\pm  F$ is increasing; boundedness comes from $F \\in BV$.\n\n⭐ Meaning: **a BV function $=$ the difference of two bounded increasing functions**. Hence everything about BV reduces to the tractable case of \"increasing functions\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#20f78805',
      },
    },

    'thm.holder': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $1 < p < \\infty$, and let $q$ be the **conjugate exponent** of $p$: $\\dfrac{1}{p} + \\dfrac{1}{q} = 1$. Then for measurable $f, g$:\n\n$$\\|fg\\|_1 \\le \\|f\\|_p \\, \\|g\\|_q$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5c2147f8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Equality condition**: equality holds $\\iff$ $|f|^p$ and $|g|^q$ are proportional a.e. (that is, $\\alpha |f|^p = \\beta |g|^q$ a.e.).\n\nProof: first assume $\\|f\\|_p = \\|g\\|_q = 1$. By Young's inequality, substituting $\\lambda = 1/p$, $a = |f|^p$, $b = |g|^q$ gives\n\n$$|fg| \\le \\frac{|f|^p}{p} + \\frac{|g|^q}{q}$$\n\nIntegrating both sides gives $\\|fg\\|_1 \\le \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1$. In the general case, normalize $f, g$ each.\n\n⚠ When $p = q = 2$ this is the **Cauchy–Schwarz inequality**.\n\nWhen $p = 1$ the conjugate exponent is $q = \\infty$, and then $\\|fg\\|_1 \\le \\|f\\|_1 \\|g\\|_\\infty$ (see the section on $L^\\infty$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7eba0f85',
      },
    },

    'def.multilinear-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a $K$-vector space. A map $f : V^{n} := V \\times \\cdots \\times V \\to K$ is called an **$n$-linear form** iff, after fixing any $n - 1$ arguments, the remaining argument is linear: for each $i$,\n\n$$f(\\ldots, v_i + c\\,w_i, \\ldots) = f(\\ldots, v_i, \\ldots) + c\\, f(\\ldots, w_i, \\ldots)$$\n\nThe set of all $n$-linear forms is denoted $\\operatorname{Mult}_n(V; K)$; with pointwise operations it becomes a $K$-vector space.\n\nWhen $n = 2$ it is called a **bilinear form**; after fixing a basis, $n$-linear forms correspond one-to-one with \"$n$-dimensional arrays $(f(e_{i_1}, \\ldots, e_{i_n}))$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b41158a4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The order of the caveats matters: \"linear in each argument separately\" and \"linear as a whole\" **do not imply each other** — $f(x, y) = xy$ is linear in each argument, yet it is not a linear map $\\mathbb{R}^{2} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ (in general $f(x + x', y + y') \\ne f(x,y) + f(x',y')$).\n\n**Multilinear $\\ne$ linear, and both directions need care**: $f(x,y) = xy$ is multilinear but not a linear map; conversely $g(x,y) = x + y$ is linear as a whole, yet after fixing $y = 1$, $g(x,1) = x + 1$ is not a linear function of $x$.\n\n⭐ It is the source of the \"tensor product\": an $n$-linear form is a linear functional on $V^{\\otimes n}$ (see \"universal property of the tensor product\").\n\n⭐ The determinant is the most special multilinear form in the case $n = \\dim V$ (with the two extra conditions of alternation and normalization).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#daba2bbf',
      },
    },

    'thm.steinitz': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a $K$-vector space, $S = (v_1, \\ldots, v_n)$ linearly independent, $T = (w_1, \\ldots, w_m)$ generating $V$. Then\n\n- $n \\le m$;\n- one can **delete** $n$ vectors from $T$ and insert all vectors of $S$, and the resulting $m$ vectors still generate $V$. In particular, $V$ has a basis whose elements are taken from $T \\cup S$ and which contains $S$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f8464df6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **\"independent\" is never larger than \"generating\"**.\n\nIt is the entire content of the well-definedness of dimension — the two statements ($n \\le m$ and exchangeability) are proved together by one induction.\n\n⭐ The proof inducts on $n$: express $v_1$ in terms of $T$ ($T$ generates), and one of the coefficients must be nonzero (otherwise $v_1 = 0$ contradicts independence); replace it by $v_1$ and the set still generates;\n\nthen repeat for the rest.\n\n⚠ Finiteness is a hypothesis: this only covers **finite** independent and generating sets.\n\nThe infinite case needs Zorn's lemma (that is the statement \"every vector space has a basis\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#53abb05c',
      },
    },

    'thm.dimension': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a $K$-vector space, and $\\mathcal{B}$, $\\mathcal{B}'$ two bases of $V$. Then $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{B}'$ are **equipotent** (there is a bijection).\n\nHence the **dimension** of $V$ is well defined:\n\n$$\\dim_K V := \\#\\mathcal{B}$$\n\n(by convention $\\dim V = 0$ when $V = \\{0\\}$; when there is no finite basis, $V$ is called **infinite-dimensional**.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1a91009b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The only content of this theorem is **well-definedness**: there may be many bases, but their cardinality is the same number — that is what makes the word \"dimension\" meaningful.\n\nThe proof is entirely Steinitz: two bases are each independent and generating for the other, so two applications of the exchange lemma give $\\#\\mathcal{B} \\le \\#\\mathcal{B}'$ and the reverse.\n\n⚠ In the finite-dimensional case the exchange lemma suffices;\n\nthe infinite-dimensional case requires proving \"infinite bases are equipotent\" (using the cardinal arithmetic of $\\#(A \\times \\mathbb{N}) = \\#A$), which is much more involved than the finite case.\n\n⭐ Basic properties of dimension: $\\dim(U + W) + \\dim(U \\cap W) = \\dim U + \\dim W$;\n\nwhen $V$ is **finite-dimensional**, $\\dim(V/U) = \\dim V - \\dim U$; in infinite dimension subtraction is meaningless (cardinals do not subtract), and only cardinal addition $\\dim V = \\dim U + \\dim(V/U)$ remains.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6cb5e0fe',
      },
    },

    'thm.rank-nullity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional $K$-vector space, $T : V \\to W$ linear. Then\n\n$$\\dim V = \\operatorname{rank} T + \\operatorname{null} T = \\dim \\operatorname{im} T + \\dim \\ker T$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#87a72698',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **central theorem** of finite-dimensional linear algebra: the input dimension of a linear map is split exactly between what is \"kept\" and what is \"lost\", with nothing left over.\n\n⭐ Corollaries (the three most used):\n- $T$ is injective $\\iff \\operatorname{rank} T = \\dim V$;\n\n$T$ is surjective $\\iff \\operatorname{rank} T = \\dim W$;\n\n- when $\\dim V = \\dim W < \\infty$: $T$ injective $\\iff$ $T$ surjective $\\iff$ $T$ invertible (**finite dimension only**; it fails in infinite dimension: the shift operator $T(x_1, x_2, \\ldots) = (0, x_1, x_2, \\ldots)$ is injective but not surjective);\n\n- the solution space of an $n$-variable homogeneous linear system has dimension $= n - \\operatorname{rank} A$.\n\n⚠ The clean identity holds only when $\\dim V < \\infty$;\n\nin infinite dimension the right-hand side is a sum of cardinals, and one must write $\\dim V = \\dim \\ker T + \\dim \\operatorname{im} T$ (which holds for cardinals).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e285bdff',
      },
    },

    'thm.first-iso-vs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to W$ be a linear map. Then\n\n$$\\overline{T} : V / \\ker T \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{im} T, \\qquad v + \\ker T \\longmapsto T(v)$$\n\nis a well-defined **linear isomorphism**. In particular\n\n$$V / \\ker T \\cong \\operatorname{im} T$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1b7e207c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the third appearance of the same template on the graph: the set version $A/\\!\\sim$, the group version $G/N \\cong \\operatorname{im}\\varphi$, and the vector-space version $V/\\ker T \\cong \\operatorname{im} T$.\n\nThe proof is verbatim the same in all three — **kill the kernel, and what remains is the image**.\n\n⭐ One particularly useful branch: if $T$ is surjective, then $W \\cong V / \\ker T$;\n\nif $T$ is injective, then $V \\cong \\operatorname{im} T$.\n\n⚠ The well-definedness step cannot be skipped: $v + \\ker T = v' + \\ker T$ says $v - v' \\in \\ker T$, hence $T(v) = T(v')$ — precisely the sentence \"$\\overline{T}$ depends only on the coset\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2d363bda',
      },
    },

    'def.quotient-vs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq V$ be a subspace. Define an equivalence relation on $V$:\n\n$$v \\sim v' \\iff v - v' \\in U$$\n\nThe equivalence class $v + U := \\{\\, v + u : u \\in U \\,\\}$ is called a **coset** of $U$. The set of all cosets is denoted $V / U$; define on it\n\n$$(v + U) + (v' + U) := (v + v') + U, \\qquad c\\,(v + U) := (cv) + U$$\n\nThese two operations **do not depend on the choice of representatives** (because $U$ is a subspace), so $V / U$ becomes a $K$-vector space, called the **quotient space**.\n\nThe quotient map $\\pi : V \\to V/U$, $v \\mapsto v + U$ is a surjective linear map, and $\\ker \\pi = U$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7429adc2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the quotient set `def.quotient-set` **plus structure**: first compress $V$ by $U$ into a set, then check that the operations are well defined.\n\nThe well-definedness step is the whole content — because the operations are written on representatives, and there are infinitely many ways to choose them.\n\n⭐ \"Every subspace is the kernel of some linear map\" follows: take $\\pi$.\n\nThis is completely parallel to the group case (every normal subgroup is the kernel of some homomorphism).\n\n⚠ What is quotiented is a **subspace** (not an arbitrary subset): otherwise the sum of cosets is not closed.\n\nOn the group side the counterpart is \"normal subgroup\"; here every subspace is automatically normal (because $K$-vector spaces are abelian groups).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e427534f',
      },
    },

    'prop.root-bound': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in K[x]$ be a nonzero polynomial, $\\deg f = n$. Then $f$ has at most $n$ roots in $K$ (distinct roots).\n\nMore precisely (using **multiplicity**): there exist $a_1, \\ldots, a_m \\in K$ (distinct) and positive integers $e_1, \\ldots, e_m$ such that\n\n$$f = (x - a_1)^{e_1} \\cdots (x - a_m)^{e_m}\\, h, \\qquad h(a_i) \\ne 0 \\ \\text{for all } i$$\n\nand then $e_1 + \\cdots + e_m \\le n$ ($e_i$ is called the **multiplicity** of the root $a_i$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b1feb9d2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof is repeated use of the factor theorem: each root lets one divide by $(x - a)$, lowering the degree by 1; the degree cannot go negative, so there are at most $n$ roots.\n\n⭐ The usefulness of this \"small\" proposition is surprisingly large: **testing polynomial identities over finite fields** (two polynomials of degree $< n$ that agree at $n$ points are identical) — interpolation and error-correcting codes (Reed–Solomon) rely on it.\n\n⚠ It holds only over an **integral domain**: over $\\mathbb{Z}/8$, $x^{2} - 1$ has four roots $1, 3, 5, 7$ (because $\\mathbb{Z}/8$ has zero divisors).\n\n$K[x]$ being an integral domain is the key.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ed8052b',
      },
    },

    'thm.poly-ufd': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field. Then every nonzero, non-unit polynomial $f \\in K[x]$ can be written as a product of finitely many irreducible polynomials\n\n$$f = c\\, p_1 p_2 \\cdots p_r, \\qquad c \\in K^{\\times},\\ p_i \\text{ monic irreducible}$$\n\nand the decomposition is **unique** up to order and constant factor: if $f = c' q_1 \\cdots q_s$ is another such decomposition, then $r = s$ and there is a permutation $\\sigma$ such that $p_i = q_{\\sigma(i)}$ (and of course $c = c'$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0bdf6918',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **end point** of the division algorithm: Euclidean domain $\\implies$ principal ideal domain $\\implies$ unique factorization domain.\n\nAll three \"domain\" definitions along the chain are worth remembering, because each has counterexamples in abstract algebra (a UFD need not be a PID: $\\mathbb{Z}[x]$;\n\na PID need not be a Euclidean domain: certain rings).\n\n⭐ Uniqueness relies on \"irreducible $\\implies$ prime\": if $p$ is irreducible and $p \\mid gh$, then $p \\mid g$ or $p \\mid h$.\n\nThis step uses the **Bézout identity** (a product of the Euclidean algorithm), and is precisely the only place where the division algorithm is really used.\n\n⭐ Uses: factorization of integer-coefficient polynomials in $\\mathbb{Q}[x]$, the field structure of $K[x]/(f)$, and the relation between characteristic and minimal polynomials (next batch) all rest on it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#be1c6630',
      },
    },

    'def.char-poly': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to V$ be linear, $\\dim V = n < \\infty$. Choose any basis and write $T$ as a matrix $A$, and define\n\n$$\\chi_T(x) := \\det(x\\,I_n - A) \\in K[x]$$\n\ncalled the **characteristic polynomial** of $T$. It is a monic polynomial of degree $n$, independent of the choice of basis (a change of basis gives a similar $A$, and $\\det(P^{-1}(xI - A)P) = \\det(xI - A)$).\n\nIts roots (in $K$, or in an extension field) are exactly the eigenvalues of $T$, and their multiplicities are called **algebraic multiplicities**. Expanded,\n\n$$\\chi_T(x) = x^{n} - (\\operatorname{tr} T)\\,x^{n-1} + \\cdots + (-1)^{n} \\det T$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#05fc7be0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The characteristic polynomial turns the **geometric** problem \"eigenvalues\" into the **algebraic** problem \"roots of a polynomial\" — so the fundamental theorem of algebra guarantees $n$ eigenvalues over $\\mathbb{C}$ (counted with multiplicity).\n\n⭐ Relation of coefficients to invariants: the coefficient of $x^{n-1}$ is $-\\operatorname{tr} T$, and the constant term is $(-1)^{n}\\det T$.\n\nThus \"trace = sum of eigenvalues\" and \"determinant = product of eigenvalues\" become readings of Vieta's formulas, **without actually solving for the eigenvalues**.\n\n⚠ Geometric multiplicity $\\le$ algebraic multiplicity;\n\nthe standard example where they differ is $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ ($\\lambda = 1$: algebraic multiplicity 2, geometric multiplicity 1) — which is exactly why it is not diagonalizable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#63569f9b',
      },
    },

    'def.trace': {
      statement: {
        en: "For a square matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in M_n(K)$, define the **trace** as the sum of the diagonal entries:\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr} A := \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{ii}$$\n\nBasic properties:\n\n- $\\operatorname{tr}(A + B) = \\operatorname{tr} A + \\operatorname{tr} B$, $\\operatorname{tr}(cA) = c\\operatorname{tr} A$ (linearity);\n- $\\operatorname{tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{tr}(BA)$, hence $\\operatorname{tr}(P^{-1}AP) = \\operatorname{tr} A$ (**similarity invariance**);\n- $\\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \\operatorname{tr}(BCA)$ (**cyclic invariance**).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6f6283d2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The trace is the **cheapest invariant**: $O(n)$ computes it, yet it already distinguishes many matrices (far cheaper than the determinant).\n\n⭐ Its deeper meaning: $\\operatorname{tr}$ is the **unique (up to a scalar) central linear functional** on the matrix algebra, and also the \"trace form\" from the $K^{n}$-algebra to $K$ — it reappears in Galois theory and characteristic classes.\n\n⚠ \"Similarity invariance\" is weaker than \"cyclic invariance\": $\\operatorname{tr}(P^{-1}AP) = \\operatorname{tr}(A P^{-1} P) = \\operatorname{tr}A$ is only a special case of the latter.\n\nConversely, equal trace does not imply similarity ($\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ and the zero matrix both have trace $0$ but are not similar).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#696bc2f5',
      },
    },

    'thm.cayley-hamilton': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to V$ be a linear map on a finite-dimensional $K$-vector space, and $\\chi_T$ its characteristic polynomial. Replace $x$ appearing in $\\chi_T$ by $T$ (multiplying the constant term by $I$), obtaining the operator $\\chi_T(T)$. Then\n\n$$\\chi_T(T) = 0$$\n\n(the right-hand side is the **zero operator**; in matrix form, the zero matrix.)\n\nMatrix form: for $A \\in M_n(K)$, one has $\\chi_A(A) = 0$ (the zero matrix).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#77021b88',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The theorem says \"**every operator satisfies its own characteristic equation**\" — so $\\chi_T$ is **one** of the polynomials that send $T$ to zero, which immediately gives an entry point for using the structure of the $K[x]$-module ($V$ becomes a $K[x]/(\\chi_T)$-module).\n\n⚠ A common notational error: one cannot substitute $\\chi_T(T) = \\det(T I - T)$ like that (that would be a \"matrix inside a matrix\"); one must **first expand $\\chi_T$ as $\\sum c_k x^{k}$, then replace term by term with $\\sum c_k T^{k}$**.\n\n⭐ Uses: computing powers of a matrix ($A^{n}$ can be expressed linearly in lower powers), proving that the minimal polynomial divides the characteristic polynomial, and finding inverse matrices ($A^{-1}$ is given by the coefficients of $\\chi_A$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f8799688',
      },
    },

    'thm.primary-decomposition': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be **algebraically closed**, $T : V \\to V$, $\\dim V = n < \\infty$. Let\n\n$$\\chi_T(x) = \\prod_{i=1}^{r} (x - \\lambda_i)^{m_i}$$\n\n($\\lambda_i$ distinct, $m_i \\ge 1$) be the factorization of the characteristic polynomial. Then\n\n- $V = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r} V_i$, where $V_i := \\ker (T - \\lambda_i I)^{m_i}$ is the **generalized eigenspace** (also called the **root subspace**);\n- $\\dim V_i = m_i$ (the algebraic multiplicity), each $V_i$ is $T$-invariant, and $T|_{V_i}$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$;\n- $E_{\\lambda_i} = \\ker(T - \\lambda_i I) \\subseteq V_i$ is the \"$\\lambda_i$ layer\" inside it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cd340387',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This step is the **first half** of the Jordan decomposition: first split $V$ into $r$ blocks by eigenvalue, so that only one eigenvalue remains on each block.\n\nThe second half splits, within each block, the \"nilpotency degree of $T - \\lambda_i I$\" into small blocks (those are the Jordan blocks).\n\n⭐ Its algebraic version: $V$ is a $K[x]$-module, and $\\chi_T$ is the \"annihilating polynomial\" on it;\n\nby the unique factorization in $K[x]$, the module splits along the irreducible factors of $\\chi_T$ (**Chinese remainder theorem**) — this is where the name \"primary decomposition\" comes from.\n\n⚠ $K$ is required to be algebraically closed: otherwise $\\chi_T$ does not split into linear factors, and the blocks are not indexed by eigenvalues (one must treat \"irreducible factors\" as the blocks).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#08c4512b',
      },
    },

    'thm.jordan-canonical-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be algebraically closed, $T : V \\to V$, $\\dim V < \\infty$. Then there is a basis of $V$ in which the matrix of $T$ is **block diagonal**\n\n$$J = \\begin{pmatrix} J_{1} & & \\\\ & \\ddots & \\\\ & & J_{s} \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad J_{k} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\lambda_{k} & 1 & & \\\\ & \\ddots & \\ddots & \\\\ & & \\ddots & 1 \\\\ & & & \\lambda_{k} \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\neach $J_k$ is a **Jordan block** of the form $\\lambda_k I + N_k$ ($N_k$ being the nilpotent \"up-shift\" matrix).\n\nEquivalent formulation: $T$ is diagonalizable $\\iff$ all Jordan blocks are $1 \\times 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6b589db8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is **the only complete classification theorem in finite-dimensional linear algebra**: over an algebraically closed field, \"similarity\" is completely determined by the Jordan type (block sizes and the distribution of eigenvalues).\n\nIt also gives a complete list of \"similarity invariants\".\n\n⭐ The two halves of the proof: primary decomposition (split by eigenvalue) plus, on each root subspace, taking \"the chain $\\ker N^{k}$\" of the nilpotent operator $N = T - \\lambda I$ and extending the basis layer by layer (this step uses `def.quotient-vs` and induction).\n\n⚠ All three hypotheses are needed: **algebraically closed** (otherwise there is no $\\lambda$), **finite-dimensional** (otherwise there may be no eigenvalue), and **$K$ a field**.\n\nOver $\\mathbb{R}$ one can only use the \"real Jordan form\" or switch to the rational canonical form (`thm.rational-canonical-form`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#415baba4',
      },
    },

    'thm.gram-schmidt': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(v_1, \\ldots, v_k)$ be a linearly independent family in an inner product space $V$. Then there is an orthonormal family $(e_1, \\ldots, e_k)$ and an invertible upper-triangular coefficient relation such that\n\n$$\\operatorname{span}(e_1, \\ldots, e_j) = \\operatorname{span}(v_1, \\ldots, v_j), \\qquad j = 1, \\ldots, k$$\n\nIn particular: **every finite-dimensional inner product space has an orthonormal basis** (any basis can be orthogonalized).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#94d3a946',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Algorithm (induction): first set $e_1 := v_1 / \\|v_1\\|$; then for $j = 2, \\ldots, k$, successively set\n\n$$w_j := v_j - \\sum_{i < j} \\langle e_i, v_j \\rangle\\, e_i, \\qquad e_j := \\frac{w_j}{\\|w_j\\|}$$\n\n(each step subtracts all components of $v_j$ along the directions already obtained.)\n\n⭐ It is the entry point to \"matrix factorization\": orthogonalizing the columns of $A$ gives the **QR decomposition** $A = QR$ ($Q$ has orthonormal columns, $R$ upper triangular) — numerically far more stable than solving equations directly.\n\n⚠ It depends on the inner product and on the **real/complex field structure**: if the inner product is replaced by a general symmetric bilinear form (e.g. the Minkowski metric), the algorithm runs into $\\|w_j\\| = 0$ and divides by zero.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2c2b7464',
      },
    },

    'thm.spectral-theorem': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional **real** inner product space, $T : V \\to V$ **self-adjoint** (that is, real symmetric). Then there is an **orthonormal basis** of $V$ in which $T$ is diagonal:\n\n$$V = E_{\\lambda_1} \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus E_{\\lambda_m}$$\n\n(these eigenspaces are **pairwise orthogonal**.)\n\nMoreover, all eigenvalues $\\lambda_i$ are **real**. Matrix form: for a real symmetric matrix $A$ there is an orthogonal matrix $Q$ such that $Q^{\\mathsf{T}} A Q = \\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#042a3f1c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Difference from Jordan form: Jordan guarantees only \"similarity\", while the spectral theorem guarantees \"**unitary/orthogonal** similarity\", at the price of tightening the hypothesis to self-adjoint (normal).\n\nThese are the answers to two different classification problems in finite dimension.\n\n⭐ The two steps of the proof: (1) there is at least one (real) eigenvalue (by the extreme value theorem: $|Tv|$ or $\\langle Tv, v\\rangle$ attains an extremum on the **compact** unit sphere);\n\n(2) induct on the orthogonal complement of the eigenspace (invariance comes from self-adjointness: $T(U) \\subseteq U \\implies T(U^{\\perp}) \\subseteq U^{\\perp}$).\n\n⭐ The infinite-dimensional generalization is the **spectral theorem on Hilbert spaces** (spectral measures / projection-valued measures for self-adjoint operators) — that belongs to functional analysis, and $L^{2}$ in the `analysis.lp` cluster on the graph is its stage.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92b0f8b0',
      },
    },

    'thm.tensor-universal-property': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every **bilinear map** $B : V \\times W \\to U$ (a linear map on the product space need not be bilinear, e.g. $B(v,w) = v$), there is a **unique** linear map\n\n$$\\widetilde{B} : V \\otimes W \\to U, \\qquad \\widetilde{B}(v \\otimes w) = B(v, w)$$\n\nConversely, any linear map $\\widetilde B$ composed with $\\otimes$ gives a bilinear map. Hence there is a natural isomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V \\otimes W,\\ U) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Bil}(V, W; U)$$\n\n(natural in $U$; this is \"the tensor product is the **representation** of the bilinear functor\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#332744a8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **the tensor product is the linearization device for \"multivariable linearity\"** — everything bilinear, once written as a map on $V \\otimes W$, automatically becomes linear, and the whole machinery of linear algebra applies.\n\n⭐ \"Uniqueness\" is the core of a universal property: it ensures $V \\otimes W$ is determined **up to unique isomorphism** (existence gives the construction, uniqueness gives \"independence of the construction\").\n\nThis is the third appearance of \"the same trick\" on the graph (tensor product, quotient space, exterior power).\n\n⭐ It also holds over an arbitrary commutative ring (`prop.module-tensor-universal`); a coefficient field is just the simplest case.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce828a4e',
      },
    },

    'thm.det-exists-unique': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\dim_K V = n$. Then there is a **unique** function\n\n$$D : V^{n} \\longrightarrow K$$\n\nsatisfying:\n\n**(i) Multilinear**: linear in each argument separately;\n\n**(ii) Alternating**: it takes the value $0$ whenever two arguments are equal;\n\n**(iii) Normalized**: $D(e_1, \\ldots, e_n) = 1$ ($(e_i)$ being any fixed ordered basis).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9709355b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem is the **correct way in which the determinant is born**: not \"multiply diagonals and subtract\", but \"the unique function satisfying three axioms\".\n\nThe three axioms are **linearity, alternation, normalization**, and none can be dropped.\n\n⭐ \"Uniqueness\" is far more useful than \"existence\": any function satisfying the three axioms equals the determinant, so to verify an identity it suffices to verify the three axioms — e.g. $\\det(A^{\\mathsf{T}}) = \\det A$, $\\det(AB) = \\det A \\det B$.\n\n⭐ Construction for \"existence\": expand $(v_1, \\ldots, v_n)$ in the basis, obtaining the coefficient matrix $(a_{ij})$ of size $n \\times n$, then take the Leibniz formula $\\sum_{\\sigma} \\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma) \\prod_i a_{i,\\sigma(i)}$ as the definition — then verify the three axioms one by one (see the proof section).\n\n⭐ Relation to \"universal property\": this is not a universal property in the usual sense (there is no loose adjoint), but it is **the same technique** — pinning an object down by a universal condition (the three axioms).\n\nSo the graph treats it as the same family of proof techniques as `prop.module-tensor-universal` (universal property of the tensor product).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#102f7860',
      },
    },

    'prop.det-laplace': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A = (a_{ij}) \\in M_n(K)$, and let $A_{ij}$ denote the matrix of order $(n-1)$ obtained by deleting row $i$ and column $j$, $M_{ij} := \\det A_{ij}$ (**minor**), $C_{ij} := (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij}$ (**cofactor**). Then for each fixed $i$:\n\n$$\\det A = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{ij}\\, C_{ij}$$\n\n(**expansion along row $i$**; the column version is analogous.) Moreover \"off-diagonal expansion\" gives zero: $\\sum_j a_{kj} C_{ij} = 0$ ($k \\ne i$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9029192f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **recursive algorithm** for the determinant: order $n$ reduces to $n$ matrices of order $n-1$, complexity $O(n!)$.\n\nIn practice one first uses elimination to reduce a row to a single nonzero entry, then expands — the two together are fast.\n\n⭐ The \"off-diagonal expansion equals zero\" statement can be rewritten directly as the matrix identity $A \\cdot \\operatorname{adj} A = (\\det A) I_n$ ($\\operatorname{adj} A$ being the transpose of the cofactor matrix), that is, **when $\\det A \\ne 0$**, $A^{-1} = (\\det A)^{-1} \\operatorname{adj} A$ — Cramer's rule and the inverse matrix formula both come from here (when $A = 0$, $\\operatorname{adj} A = 0$, and neither side is defined).\n\n⭐ Another common corollary (for $K = \\mathbb{R}$, $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$, viewing $A$ as $n$ row vectors): **the determinant is linear in each row**, so the behaviour of det under elementary row operations can be listed one by one (swapping rows changes the sign, adding a multiple leaves it unchanged, multiplying by $c$ multiplies it by $c$).\n\nGaussian elimination computes determinants precisely through these three rules.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2a645083',
      },
    },

    'thm.svd': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$ (or over $\\mathbb{C}$), of rank $r$. Then there exist orthogonal (unitary) matrices $U \\in M_m$, $V \\in M_n$ and a \"diagonal\" matrix $\\Sigma$ (only the first $r$ diagonal entries nonzero) such that\n\n$$A = U \\Sigma V^{\\dagger}, \\qquad \\Sigma = \\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_1, \\ldots, \\sigma_r, 0, \\ldots), \\quad \\sigma_1 \\ge \\cdots \\ge \\sigma_r > 0$$\n\nThe $\\sigma_i$ are called the **singular values** of $A$; they are the square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^{\\dagger}A$ (or $AA^{\\dagger}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#50729a47',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric reading of SVD: **every linear map = rotation/reflection $\\to$ stretch along the coordinate axes $\\to$ another rotation/reflection**.\n\nThus it reduces an arbitrary matrix to diagonal form \"after choosing good bases $U$ and $V$\" — this is the complete answer to the **equivalence** classification ($A \\mapsto PAQ$) (the diagonal entries are the singular values, the number of nonzero ones is the rank).\n\n⭐ Division of labour with the spectral theorem: the spectral theorem handles **square and self-adjoint** (similarity classification);\n\nSVD handles **arbitrary shapes** (equivalence classification).\n\nThe former requires $m = n$ and $A^{\\ast} = A$; the latter requires nothing.\n\n⭐ Uses (the most important theorem in numerical linear algebra): low-rank approximation (Eckart–Young: truncating to the first $k$ singular values gives the best low-rank approximation), principal component analysis, pseudoinverse, condition number.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ef21df6d',
      },
    },

    'def.dual-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a $K$-vector space. The **dual space** of $V$ is defined as the set of all linear maps from $V$ to $K$:\n\n$$V^{\\ast} := \\operatorname{Hom}_K(V, K)$$\n\nits elements are called **linear functionals**. $V^{\\ast}$ becomes a vector space under pointwise addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nIf $T : V \\to W$ is linear, define the **transpose** (or dual) map\n\n$$T^{\\ast} : W^{\\ast} \\to V^{\\ast}, \\qquad \\varphi \\longmapsto \\varphi \\circ T$$\n\nIt is linear, and $(S \\circ T)^{\\ast} = T^{\\ast} \\circ S^{\\ast}$ (**contravariance**).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#561cb824',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Mind the direction: the dual $T^{\\ast}$ of $T : V \\to W$ is $W^{\\ast} \\to V^{\\ast}$, **the arrow is reversed**.\n\nThis is the entire meaning of the word \"dual\", and it is consistent with the status of $(-)^{\\ast}$ as a contravariant functor.\n\n⭐ In finite dimension $V \\cong V^{\\ast}$ (equal dimensions), but there is **no natural isomorphism** — to find one, a basis must first be chosen.\n\nWhereas $V \\cong V^{\\ast\\ast}$ has the natural isomorphism $v \\mapsto (\\varphi \\mapsto \\varphi(v))$; this is \"the dual of the dual returns to itself\".\n\n⚠ In infinite dimension $V^{\\ast}$ is strictly larger than $V$: $\\dim V^{\\ast} = \\#(K)^{\\dim V}$ is a cardinality theorem of **Erdős–Kaplansky** (it uses AC; $\\#(V^{\\ast}) = \\#(K)^{\\dim V}$ is elementary, but \"the dimension equals this cardinal\" is not), so the above isomorphism fails.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5929282e',
      },
    },

    'def.gcd-domain': {
      statement: {
        en: "An integral domain $A$ is called a **gcd domain** if every $a, b \\in A$ has a **greatest common divisor**: there exists $d$ such that\n\n$$d \\mid a,\\quad d \\mid b,$$\n\nand **every $c$ dividing both $a, b$ divides $d$**.\n\nNote that \"greatest\" means the **greatest lower bound in the divisibility order**, not comparison of size — an integral domain has no total order.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f0ee7240',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Why it must be defined this way: in $\\mathbb{Z}$ the \"greatest common divisor\" relies on comparing $|d|$, and a general integral domain has no absolute value. The right replacement is \"divisible by every common divisor\": in $\\mathbb{Z}$ it is equivalent to \"largest absolute value\", and in $\\mathbb{Z}[i]$ too this is the only statement that holds.\n\nHierarchy: UFD $\\implies$ gcd domain (`imp.ring-ufd-gcd`), but not conversely ($\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-5}]$ does not even have $\\gcd(6, 2(1+\\sqrt{-5}))$). Bézout domain $\\implies$ gcd domain, because $\\gcd$ is given directly by a linear combination.\n\n$\\gcd$ may fail to exist — this is exactly why \"uniqueness of factorization in a UFD\" cannot be replaced by gcd.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3d56a870',
      },
    },

    'prop.prime-ideal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring with identity and let $P, M \\subset A$ be **proper** ideals (proper: if $P = A$ were allowed, \"$A/P$ is a domain\" would fail; if $M = A$ were allowed, \"maximal\" would fail).\n\nThe definitions of prime and maximal ideals are in `def.prime-ideal` and `def.maximal-ideal` respectively.\n\n**(i)** $P$ is a prime ideal $\\iff$ $A/P$ is a **domain**.\n\n**(ii)** $M$ is a maximal ideal $\\iff$ $A/M$ is a **field**.\n\n**(iii)** Maximal $\\implies$ prime ($A/M$ is a field $\\implies$ it is a domain).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#40eeb829',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A prime ideal is the abstraction of \"divisibility\": in $\\mathbb{Z}$ ($p \\ge 2$), $(p)$ is prime $\\iff$ $p$ is prime ($(0)$ is also prime, but $0$ is not prime); in $k[x]$ ($k$ a field, $f \\ne 0$), $(f)$ is prime $\\iff$ $f$ is irreducible ($(0)$ is also prime, while $0$ is irreducible).\n\n⭐ A maximal ideal is \"the simplest after quotienting\": $\\mathbb{Z}/p\\mathbb{Z}$ is a field, $\\mathbb{Z}/(6)$ is not (it has zero divisors).\n\n⚠ Maximal $\\implies$ prime, but **not conversely**: in $k[x,y]$, $(x)$ is prime ($k[x,y]/(x) \\cong k[y]$ is an integral domain), yet not maximal ($(x) \\subset (x, y) \\subset k[x,y]$).\n\nIn a one-dimensional integral domain, the **nonzero** prime ideals are all maximal (e.g. $\\mathbb{Z}$, $k[x]$); $(0)$ is prime but not maximal ($\\mathbb{Z}$ in $(0) \\subset (2) \\subset \\mathbb{Z}$).\n\n📌 Existence (every proper ideal lies in a maximal ideal) requires Zorn's lemma; that is an application of `lem.zorn`, not part of this entry.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#671f5f5c',
      },
    },

    'prop.ideal-basic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring and $I, J \\subseteq A$ ideals. Then:\n\n**(i)** $I \\cap J$ and\n\n$$I + J := \\{\\, x + y : x \\in I,\\ y \\in J \\,\\}$$\n\nare ideals; $I + J$ is the **smallest** ideal containing $I \\cup J$;\n\n**(ii)** the set of finite sums\n\n$$IJ := \\left\\{ \\sum_{k=1}^{n} x_k y_k : x_k \\in I,\\ y_k \\in J,\\ n \\ge 0 \\right\\}$$\n\nis an ideal, and $IJ \\subseteq I \\cap J$;\n\n**(iii) universal property of the quotient ring**: if $\\varphi : A \\to B$ is a ring homomorphism with $\\varphi(I) = 0$, then there is a **unique** ring homomorphism $\\bar{\\varphi} : A/I \\to B$ such that $\\bar{\\varphi} \\circ \\pi = \\varphi$, where $\\pi : A \\to A/I$ is the quotient map.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#af38a24c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Compare groups: to quotient a group into a group, one quotients by a **normal subgroup**; to quotient a ring into a ring, one quotients by an **ideal**.\n\nThe reason is exactly the same — the operations on the quotient set must be independent of the representative.\n\n⚠ The **union** of ideals is not generally an ideal: $2\\mathbb{Z} \\cup 3\\mathbb{Z}$ does not contain $2 + 3 = 5$.\n\nSo the substitute for \"union\" is the **sum** $I + J$, which is the smallest ideal containing both.\n\n⭐ The universal property of the quotient ring is the other face of the \"first isomorphism theorem\": when $\\ker \\varphi \\supseteq I$, $\\varphi$ factors through $A/I$, and $A/\\ker\\varphi \\cong \\operatorname{im}\\varphi$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e22f77a4',
      },
    },

    'thm.fundamental-arithmetic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Every integer $n > 1$ can be written uniquely as\n\n$$n = p_{1}^{e_{1}} p_{2}^{e_{2}} \\cdots p_{r}^{e_{r}},$$\n\nwhere $p_{1} < p_{2} < \\cdots < p_{r}$ are primes and $e_{i} \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5a75b8d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Existence: induct on $n$ — if $n$ is prime, done; otherwise $n = ab$ with $1 < a, b < n$, apply the induction hypothesis to $a, b$ and multiply.\n\nUniqueness: use Euclid's lemma ($p \\mid ab \\implies p \\mid a$ or $p \\mid b$). This is the standard argument for a unique factorization domain (UFD).\n\nThe abstract algebraic version is \"$\\mathbb{Z}$ is a UFD\": $K[x]$ also is (`thm.poly-ufd`), while $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-5}]$ is not ($6 = 2\\cdot 3 = (1+\\sqrt{-5})(1-\\sqrt{-5})$).\n\nHierarchy: Euclidean domain $\\implies$ UFD $\\implies$ gcd domain.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7369f432',
      },
    },

    'def.power': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring with unit (e.g. $\\mathbb{Z}$, $\\mathbb{R}$), $a \\in A$. The **integer powers** are defined recursively ($a^{0} := 1$ is the convention for the **empty product**; when $a = 0$ one also takes $0^{0} := 1$):\n\n$$a^{0} := 1, \\qquad a^{n+1} := a^{n} \\cdot a \\quad (n \\in \\mathbb{N});$$\n\nIf $a$ is invertible (in $\\mathbb{R}$ this means $a \\ne 0$), define for $n \\ge 1$\n\n$$a^{-n} := (a^{-1})^{n} = \\frac{1}{a^{n}}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d99a76a8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A power is just a compressed notation for \"multiplication written many times\"; it has no new content — but it turns \"$n$ times\" into an **object one can induct on**: `thm.binomial`, `thm.bernoulli`, geometric series and power series all induct on $n$.\n\n⚠ Only **integer** exponents are defined here.\n\n$a^{1/2}$ must wait for `thm.nth-root` (n-th roots), and $a^{\\sqrt{2}}$ for `def.real-power` — the order cannot be reversed, or \"taking roots\" would be an undefined notion.\n\n⚠ $0^{0} = 1$ is an **algebraic convention** (empty product); in **analysis** $0^{0}$ is an indeterminate form with its own limit convention — the two do not conflict, but do not mix them.\n\n⚠ Powers of the **same element** satisfy $a^{n}a = a\\cdot a^{n} = a^{n+1}$ by associativity, with **no** commutativity needed; what needs care is powers of **different elements** (in a noncommutative ring $(ab)^{n} \\ne a^{n}b^{n}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e92f38d1',
      },
    },

    'thm.binomial': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring, $a, b \\in A$, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Then\n\n$$(a + b)^{n} = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} a^{k}\\, b^{n-k}, \\qquad \\binom{n}{k} := \\frac{n!}{k!\\,(n-k)!}.$$\n\nThe coefficients satisfy the **Pascal recurrence**\n\n$$\\binom{n}{k} = \\binom{n-1}{k-1} + \\binom{n-1}{k},$$\n\nand are therefore natural numbers ($\\binom{n}{k}$ is the number of ways to \"choose $k$ from $n$\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fb046ce1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem is used in two places on the graph: the functional equation $\\exp(z+w) = \\exp z\\,\\exp w$ of `def.exp-function` (expand $(z+w)^{n}$ and rearrange), and the amplification of $(b+h)^{n}$ in `thm.nth-root`.\n\n⭐ The proof inducts only on $n$: expand $(a+b)^{n+1} = (a+b)^{n}(a+b)$, and use Pascal to merge the two groups of coefficients.\n\n⚠ It fails in noncommutative rings ($ab \\ne ba$), hence the hypothesis says commutative ring.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5d70268',
      },
    },

    'thm.bernoulli': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $x \\ge -1$, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Then\n\n$$(1 + x)^{n} \\ge 1 + n x.$$\n\nIf $x \\ne 0$ and $n \\ge 2$, the inequality is **strict**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f31f4ac7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the smallest estimate that compresses \"multiplying the same $1+x$ repeatedly\" into a **linear** bound, and $x$ can be arbitrarily small.\n\nIn analysis it is used for **lower bounds**: $(1+h)^{n} \\ge 1 + nh$, $(1-h)^{n} \\ge 1 - nh$ — the second direction of `thm.nth-root` is exactly this.\n\n⚠ The hypothesis $x \\ge -1$ is there to make $1+x \\ge 0$: the induction step multiplies both sides by $1+x$, and only if it is nonnegative does the inequality not reverse.\n\nFor $x < -1$ the statement may fail or lose its induction base.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cfcdc531',
      },
    },

    'prop.order-rules': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be an ordered field, $a, b, c, d \\in K$. Then\n\n**(i)** $a < b \\iff a + c < b + c$;\n\n**(ii)** $a < b$ and $c < d$ $\\implies$ $a + c < b + d$;\n\n**(iii)** for $c > 0$, $a < b \\implies ac < bc$; for $c < 0$, $a < b \\implies ac > bc$;\n\n**(iv)** $0 < a < b \\implies 0 < \\dfrac{1}{b} < \\dfrac{1}{a}$;\n\n**(v)** $0 < 1$; and $a \\ne 0 \\implies a^{2} > 0$;\n\n**(vi)** $a < b \\implies a < \\dfrac{a + b}{2} < b$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#62f12283',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the rule table for all **inequality estimates**.\n\nPart (iii) is the easiest to get wrong: multiplying by a negative number **reverses** the inequality, so when making an $\\varepsilon$ estimate always first check whether the quantity being multiplied is positive.\n\n⭐ Part (iv) (reciprocal reverses order) is used daily in places like \"$|g(x)| > |B|/2 \\implies 1/|g(x)| < 2/|B|$\" — the reciprocal rule for limits relies on it to amplify $1/|g|$.\n\n⚠ The whole package depends only on the **two compatibility axioms** in the definition: addition preserves order, and multiplication by a positive number preserves order.\n\nEverything else is a corollary.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f6670076',
      },
    },

    'cor.archimedean-epsilon': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a complete ordered field (e.g. $\\mathbb{R}$).\n\n**(i)** For every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $n \\ge 1$ with $1/n < \\varepsilon$; equivalently, the infimum of those $1/n$ is zero:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{n} < \\varepsilon; \\qquad \\inf\\left\\{ \\frac{1}{n} : n \\ge 1 \\right\\} = 0.$$\n\n**(ii)** For every $x \\in K$ there is a **unique** integer $n$ such that\n\n$$n \\le x < n + 1.$$\n\nWrite $n = \\lfloor x \\rfloor$, called the **integer part** of $x$ (the floor function); $\\{x\\} := x - \\lfloor x \\rfloor \\in [0, 1)$ is called the **fractional part**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#68159876',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ (i) is the **standard form** in analysis of \"take $n$ sufficiently large\".\n\nThe Archimedean property originally says \"$\\mathbb{N}$ is unbounded\", but in actual proofs one almost always uses (i): given $\\varepsilon$, take $n > 1/\\varepsilon$.\n\n⭐ (ii) \"sandwiches a real number between two adjacent integers\" — the proof of density of the rationals (part (ii) of `lem.archimedean`) uses it.\n\nIt relies on the **discreteness** of $\\mathbb{Z}$ in $K$ (there is no other integer between adjacent ones), not merely on unboundedness.\n\n⚠ $\\mathbb{Q}$ also satisfies (i) (it is Archimedean), but $\\mathbb{Q}$ does not satisfy the least-upper-bound property — so (i) itself does **not** need completeness, only the Archimedean property;\n\nwriting \"complete ordered field\" here is only to align with `lem.archimedean`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#20888269',
      },
    },

    'thm.geometric-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $q \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then the series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} q^{n}$ converges **if and only if** $|q| < 1$; in that case\n\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} q^{n} = \\frac{1}{1 - q}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5907d8d7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **yardstick** for all series tests: the comparison test, root test and ratio test all ultimately reduce to \"some geometric series converges\".\n\nThe phrase \"the geometric series converges\" in `imp.root-test` and `imp.banach-fixed-point` refers to this statement.\n\n⭐ For $|q| < 1$, the proof of $q^{n} \\to 0$ uses Bernoulli plus the Archimedean property: write $1/|q| = 1 + h$ ($h > 0$), so $(1+h)^{n} \\ge 1 + nh$, and hence $|q|^{n} \\le \\dfrac{1}{1 + nh} \\le \\dfrac{1}{n h}$ can be made arbitrarily small.\n\n⚠ For $|q| \\ge 1$ the general term does not tend to $0$, so the series must diverge — this half needs no test at all.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#553166cd',
      },
    },

    'thm.p-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then the series\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^{p}}$$\n\nconverges **if and only if** $p > 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e2a0ee4a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ $p = 1$ is the harmonic series (divergent), $p = 2$ is $\\sum 1/n^{2} = \\pi^{2}/6$ (convergent) — the two are standard examples where \"tests fail on the boundary\": the root test gives $\\alpha = 1$ for both.\n\n⭐ The proof is the most beautiful application of `thm.cauchy-condensation` (Cauchy's condensation test): when $a_n = n^{-p}$ is monotonically decreasing, the original series converges $\\iff$ $\\sum 2^{k} a_{2^{k}} = \\sum (2^{1-p})^{k}$ converges, and the latter is a geometric series with ratio $2^{1-p}$, which converges $\\iff$ $2^{1-p} < 1 \\iff p > 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#68fb7691',
      },
    },

    'prop.power-rules': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring, $a, b \\in A$, $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ (when negative exponents occur, $a, b$ is required to be invertible). Then\n\n$$a^{m+n} = a^{m}\\,a^{n}, \\qquad (a^{m})^{n} = a^{mn}, \\qquad (ab)^{n} = a^{n}\\,b^{n}.$$\n\nIf $K$ is an ordered field and $a > 1$, then for $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$:\n\n$$m < n \\iff a^{m} < a^{n};$$\n\nwhile for $0 < a < 1$ the inequality reverses; for $a = 1$, $a^{n} = 1$ holds identically.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f7a70dd9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The three exponent laws are all \"induction on $n$\", with no trick.\n\nWhat is really useful is **monotonicity**: for $a > 1$, $n \\mapsto a^{n}$ is strictly increasing, so in an **Archimedean ordered field** (e.g. $\\mathbb{R}$), $q^{n} \\to 0$ ($0 < q < 1$) can be written as $q^{n} \\le 1/(1+nh)$ and squeezed (it fails in a general ordered field: in $\\mathbb{Q}(t)$, $t$ is infinite, and powers of $q = 1/2$ are never less than $1/t$) — the proof that the geometric series converges rests on this.\n\n⚠ The negative-exponent half requires first writing $a^{-n}$ as $(a^{-1})^{n}$, then using the **positive-exponent** result;\n\ncommutativity is essential here ($(ab)^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1} = a^{-1}b^{-1}$ holds only in the commutative case).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#478c0c93',
      },
    },

    'thm.derivative-rules': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be differentiable at $x_0$, and $g$ differentiable at $f(x_0)$ (the first four formulas below only need $f, g$ to be differentiable at $x_0$ each), $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then\n\n$$(f \\pm g)'(x_0) = f'(x_0) \\pm g'(x_0), \\qquad (cf)'(x_0) = c\\,f'(x_0),$$\n\n$$(fg)'(x_0) = f'(x_0)\\,g(x_0) + f(x_0)\\,g'(x_0),$$\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{f}{g}\\right)'(x_0) = \\frac{f'(x_0)\\,g(x_0) - f(x_0)\\,g'(x_0)}{g(x_0)^{2}} \\quad (g(x_0) \\ne 0),$$\n\n$$(g \\circ f)'(x_0) = g'(f(x_0))\\,f'(x_0).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8e03fb6f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The key to the product rule is **adding and subtracting a term**: $f(x)g(x) - f(x_0)g(x_0) = [f(x) - f(x_0)]\\,g(x) + f(x_0)[g(x) - g(x_0)]$.\n\nThe $g(x)$ in the first term must be replaced by $g(x_0)$ using \"differentiable $\\implies$ continuous\" — this is why `prop.differentiable-implies-continuous` must precede it.\n\n⚠ The chain rule **cannot** be written directly as $\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{\\Delta f}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x}$: $\\Delta f$ may be $0$ (when $f$ takes the same value near $x_0$), and the denominator vanishes.\n\nThe correct approach writes the increment of $g$ as \"linear main part + remainder\": $g(y) - g(y_0) = [g'(y_0) + \\varepsilon(y)]\\,(y - y_0)$, where $\\varepsilon(y) \\to 0$.\n\n⭐ The quotient rule is obtained from the product rule and $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{g}\\right)' = -\\dfrac{g'}{g^{2}}$;\n\nthe latter divides $\\dfrac{1}{g(x)} - \\dfrac{1}{g(x_0)} = -\\dfrac{g(x) - g(x_0)}{g(x)g(x_0)}$ by $x - x_0$ and takes the limit.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#11eab0af',
      },
    },

    'thm.fundamental-algebra': {
      statement: {
        en: "Every polynomial $p \\in \\mathbb{C}[z]$ of degree $\\ge 1$ with complex coefficients has a complex root. Equivalently:\n\n- $\\mathbb{C}$ is **algebraically closed**: the irreducible polynomials in $\\mathbb{C}[z]$ are exactly the linear ones;\n- every $p \\ne 0$ can be written uniquely as\n\n$$p(z) = c\\,(z - z_1)^{e_1} \\cdots (z - z_m)^{e_m}, \\qquad c \\in \\mathbb{C}^{\\times}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#018d56b9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It is called the \"fundamental theorem of algebra\", yet it is **not an algebraic theorem** — every proof uses the **completeness** of $\\mathbb{R}$ (because the algebraic closedness of $\\mathbb{C}$ is essentially a compactness + continuity fact).\n\nThat is why the graph tags its `regions` as `analysis` + `algebra`.\n\n⭐ Proof (the route taken on the graph):\n1. $|p(z)| \\to +\\infty$ as $|z| \\to \\infty$, so on some closed disk the **extreme value theorem** gives a minimum $m \\ge 0$;\n2. if $m > 0$, perturb near the minimizing point $z_0$ by $z_0 + h$, expand $p(z_0 + h) = p(z_0) + a_k h^{k} + \\cdots$ ($a_k h^{k}$ being the first nonzero term), and choose the direction of $h$ so that $|p(z_0) + a_k h^{k}| < |p(z_0)|$, contradicting minimality.\n3. Hence $m = 0$, i.e. $p$ has a root;\nthen divide once by the factor theorem and induct to get the full factorization.\n\n⚠ Step 2 uses **complex $k$-th roots** ($a_k h^{k}$ can point in any direction); this is exactly why it must be done over $\\mathbb{C}$ and not over $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the equivalences between the forms are algebra; that complex numbers have roots is the core theorem):\n- every nonconstant polynomial has a root $\\iff$ the field is algebraically closed: free by unfolding the usual definition; here only univariate polynomials over a field are treated.\n- a root $\\implies$ factorization into linear factors: use the factor theorem and induction on degree; conversely, a factorization of a nonconstant polynomial immediately gives a root, with no analysis needed.\n- all irreducible polynomials are linear $\\iff$ complete splitting: use irreducible factorization in the polynomial ring over a field; uniqueness uses $\\mathbb C[z]$, roots counted with multiplicity, multiplicities positive integers, order of factors irrelevant, constants by the empty product.\n- complex polynomials really have roots: this page's proof uses compactness of the closed disk, the extreme value theorem for continuous functions, and the polar form of complex numbers to choose a direction; over $\\mathbb R$, $z^2+1$ has no root, so the base-field hypothesis cannot be dropped.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#52b7b443',
      },
    },

    'thm.liouville': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a function holomorphic on the whole complex plane and bounded must be constant — the complex plane is \"too crowded\" to accommodate a nonconstant bounded holomorphic function.\n\nIf $f$ is holomorphic on $\\mathbb{C}$ (such functions are called **entire**), and there is a constant $M$ with\n\n$$|f(z)| \\le M, \\qquad \\forall z \\in \\mathbb{C},$$\n\nthen $f$ is constant.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#88ca4bca',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Proof (via the Cauchy estimates): for any $z$ and any $R > 0$, $f$ is holomorphic on $B(z, R)$, hence $|f'(z)| \\le M/R$.\n\nLet $R \\to \\infty$ to get $f'(z) = 0$;\n\nsince $z$ is arbitrary, $f' \\equiv 0$;\n\nthe domain is connected, so $f$ is constant.\n\n⭐ The book also gives a second proof using **only the Cauchy integral formula**: take $R > |z|$,\n\n$$f(z) - f(0) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|\\zeta| = R}\\left( \\frac{1}{\\zeta - z} - \\frac{1}{\\zeta} \\right) f(\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta,$$\n\nestimate $|f(z) - f(0)| \\le \\dfrac{M|z|}{R - |z|} \\to 0$ ($R \\to \\infty$), hence $f(z) = f(0)$.\n\n⚠ Boundedness cannot be relaxed carelessly: $e^{z}$ is bounded on the **left** half-plane ($|e^{z}| \\le 1$), but unbounded on the whole plane.\n\nConversely, if one only knows $|f(z)| \\le C(1 + |z|)^k$, the same argument gives only \"$f$ is a polynomial of degree at most $k$\".\n\n⭐ Contrast with real analysis: $\\sin x$ is bounded and infinitely differentiable on $\\mathbb{R}$, yet not constant — because \"entire\" is far stronger than \"$C^\\infty$ function\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a14ed1b5',
      },
    },

    'thm.morera': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a continuous function whose integral along every closed triangle (equivalently, every small rectangle) is zero is holomorphic — this is the converse of Cauchy's theorem.\n\nLet $\\Omega \\subset \\mathbb{C}$ be an **open set** (no connectedness needed — holomorphy is checked pointwise, applying Morera on a small disk at each point), and $f: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{C}$ **continuous**. If for every closed triangle $T \\subset \\Omega$ we have\n\n$$\\oint_{\\partial T} f(\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = 0,$$\n\nthen $f$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega$; moreover $f'$ is also holomorphic, and $f$ has a Taylor expansion at every point.\n\nReplacing \"closed triangle\" by \"closed rectangle with sides parallel to the coordinate axes\" leaves the conclusion unchanged (a rectangle is split into two triangles along a diagonal).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da7dcd0d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Use: this is the most convenient tool for **testing holomorphy** — no a priori differentiability is needed, only \"continuous\" plus \"some integral vanishes identically\". Holomorphy is traded for an integral equation.\n\n⭐ The most-used corollary: **the limit of a locally uniformly convergent sequence of holomorphic functions is holomorphic**. Because $\\partial T$ is compact, $\\oint_{\\partial T} f_k = 0$ can be taken to the limit to get $\\oint_{\\partial T} f = 0$, which is then fed to Morera. Hurwitz's theorem is the \"zero version\" of this.\n\n⚠ Continuity cannot be dropped. Without it, the same integral condition does not imply differentiability.\n\n⚠ The hypothesis \"all triangles\" **can** be replaced by \"all circles\": under **continuity**, it already **suffices** that the integral over every circle inside a closed disk vanishes to guarantee holomorphy — convolving with a mollifier keeps the circle condition, the expansion in small circles gives $\\partial_{\\bar z} f_\\varepsilon = 0$, and then one takes a locally uniform limit.\n\n📌 Together with Cauchy–Goursat: when $f$ is continuous, \"$f$ holomorphic\" and \"$f$ has zero integral along every closed triangle\" are **equivalent** statements.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1b4556bd',
      },
    },

    'thm.taylor-complex': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: near any point a holomorphic function is a power series — that is, for complex functions \"differentiable\" and \"expressible as a power series\" are the same thing.\n\nLet $f$ be holomorphic on the disk $B(a, R)$. Then for $z \\in B(a, R)$,\n\n$$f(z) = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}\\,(z - a)^n,$$\n\nand the right-hand side **converges uniformly** on every compact subset of $B(a, R)$. Hence $f$ is **analytic** on $B(a, R)$ (equal to the sum of some power series).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#39bd0e19',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof has only two steps: the Cauchy integral formula + a geometric series.\n\nTake $\\rho \\in (|z - a|, R)$, and write\n\n$$\\frac{1}{\\zeta - z} = \\frac{1}{(\\zeta - a) - (z - a)} = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{(z - a)^n}{(\\zeta - a)^{n+1}},$$\n\nthe ratio $|z - a|/\\rho < 1$, the series converges uniformly in $\\zeta \\in \\partial B(a, \\rho)$, so term-by-term integration is allowed;\n\nthe coefficients obtained by term-by-term integration are exactly $f^{(n)}(a)/n!$ (the higher-derivative formula).\n\n⭐ This links \"differentiation\" and \"series\": **holomorphic** $\\iff$ **locally a power series** $\\iff$ **analytic**.\n\nIn real analysis the two are **not equivalent** — $e^{-1/x^2}$ is infinitely differentiable at $0$, but its Taylor series is identically $0$, and equals the original function in no neighbourhood of $0$.\n\n⚠ The radius of convergence is at least the distance from $a$ to the nearest singularity — exactly the topic of \"where are the singularities\" (Chapter 12 of the book).\n\n📌 The same section also has \"Cauchy's inequalities in series form\": if $f = \\sum a_n (z - a)^n$ holds on $B(a, R)$, then $|a_n| \\le M(r)/r^n$ ($0 < r < R$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fcca3884',
      },
    },

    'def.analytic-continuation': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **function element** is a pair $(f, D)$: $D$ is an open disk and $f$ is holomorphic on $D$.\n\nLet $\\gamma : [0, 1] \\to \\mathbb{C}$ be a curve and $\\gamma(0)$ the centre of some element $(f_{0}, D_{0})$. An **analytic continuation chain** over $\\gamma$ is a family of elements $(f_{t}, D_{t})$ ($t \\in [0,1]$) such that:\n\n1. $\\gamma(t)$ is the centre of $D_{t}$;\n2. for every $t$ there is $\\delta > 0$ such that when $|s - t| < \\delta$ we have $D_{s} \\cap D_{t} \\ne \\emptyset$ and $f_{s} = f_{t}$ on the intersection;\n3. $f_{t}$ depends continuously on $t$ (on overlaps).\n\nAll elements obtained by continuation along all curves starting at $\\gamma(0)$, glued along overlaps, form the **complete analytic function** (generally a multivalued function, lying on the Riemann surface of `def.riemann-surface`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#30b68c67',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why a \"chain\" rather than \"one function\"**: continuation is done step by step — each step moves the disk a little, and on overlaps the uniqueness theorem guarantees the two elements are restrictions of the same function. The chain is what makes this continuous.\n\n⭐ **The carrier is the power series**: every element can be expanded at its centre into a convergent power series (`thm.taylor-complex`), so continuation can also be seen as \"changing the expansion point along the curve\". The radius of convergence is determined by the distance to the nearest singularity — hitting a singularity breaks the continuation.\n\n⚠️ **The result of continuation depends on the path**. The standard example: $\\log z$ starting from $1$ and going once counterclockwise around the unit circle returns to $1$ with the value $2\\pi i$. When is it path-independent? That is exactly what `thm.monodromy` answers.\n\n📌 Relation to `def.logarithm-branch` and `def.branch`: the \"branch\" there is the **result** of continuation, whereas here we describe the **process**. One object, two ways of speaking.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#28fe020f',
      },
    },

    'lem.yoneda': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ be a functor, $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$, and write\n\n$$h^{X} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, -) : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$$\n\nThen there is a natural bijection\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}(h^{X}, F) \\;\\cong\\; F(X), \\qquad \\alpha \\mapsto \\alpha_{X}(1_{X})$$\n\nthat is, \"all natural transformations\" correspond one-to-one with \"elements of $F(X)$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#09358575',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Both **sides** of this bijection are natural: it is natural in $F$ (natural transformations) and also in $X$ (morphisms). Naturality in both variables is what gives the lemma its real strength.\n\nIt turns the set of \"all natural transformations from $h^{X}$ to $F$\", which is a priori hopeless, into **a single element** of $F(X)$. Whenever \"natural transformations are hard to count\", this is the step that saves the day.\n\nThe dual form: when $T$ is a presheaf ($T : \\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$) and $h_{X} = \\operatorname{Hom}(-, X)$ is a representable presheaf, one likewise has $\\operatorname{Hom}(h_{X}, T) \\cong T(X)$.\n\nThe two formulas differ only by replacing $\\mathcal{C}$ with $\\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}}$, so both versions are used below.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9cffe4b5'
      },
    },

    'thm.sheafification': {
      statement: {
        en: "The category of sheaves is a **reflective subcategory** of the category of presheaves, with reflector\n\n$$\\sharp : \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}} \\longrightarrow \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}, \\qquad T \\mapsto T^{\\sharp}$$\n\ncalled **sheafification**: for every sheaf $F$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}}\\bigl(T^{\\sharp},\\ F\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}}\\bigl(T,\\ F\\bigr)$$\n\nand it is **idempotent**: $(T^{\\sharp})^{\\sharp} \\cong T^{\\sharp}$. Concretely, $T^{\\sharp}$ is obtained by \"applying the Čech construction to a presheaf twice\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6a06d3f4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Corollaries:\n\n1. **Limits in the category of sheaves are computed in the category of presheaves** (a reflective subcategory is closed under limits);\n2. $T \\mapsto T^{\\sharp}$ **preserves all colimits and finite limits**.\n\nExamples:\n\n- For the **coarsest** topology ($J(X) = \\{h_{X}\\}$) every presheaf is a sheaf and $\\sharp$ is the identity; for the **finest** topology ($J(X) = $ all sieves) the category of sheaves has only one object, the constant presheaf $\\mathbf{1}$.\n- The sheafification of a constant presheaf (the same set $E$ on every open set) is called the **constant sheaf**.\n- Taking $X$ and $\\mathcal{C} = \\operatorname{Open}(X)$ on a topological space gives the usual category of sheaves, and $\\sharp$ is the usual sheafification functor.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#08cce631',
      },
    },

    'def.holomorphic': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: complex differentiability means \"the difference quotient converges in the complex sense\" — $h$ may approach $0$ from **any direction, in any manner**, but the quotient must tend to the same complex number.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a plane region (a connected open set), $f: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{C}$, $z_0 \\in \\Omega$. If the limit\n\n$$f'(z_0) := \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0)}{h} \\qquad (h \\in \\mathbb{C},\\ h \\ne 0,\\ z_0 + h \\in \\Omega)$$\n\nexists, then $f$ is called **complex differentiable** at $z_0$, and $f'(z_0)$ is called the **derivative** of $f$ at $z_0$. If $f$ is complex differentiable at every point of $\\Omega$, then $f$ is called **holomorphic** on $\\Omega$.\n\nComplex differentiability implies continuity: rewrite the definition as\n\n$$f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0) = f'(z_0)\\,h + o(h),$$\n\nletting $h \\to 0$ gives $f(z_0 + h) \\to f(z_0)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b61d4e40',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The **only** difference from real differentiability: $h$ is a complex number.\n\nIn the real case it suffices that the left and right directions agree; in the complex case **infinitely many directions** ($h = t e^{i\\theta}$, $\\theta$ arbitrary) must all agree. The requirement is very strong and the reward is very large: one derivative gives infinitely many.\n\n⚠ This requirement is **not automatic**.\n\n$f(z) = \\bar z$ is nowhere complex differentiable: along the real axis, $h = t$ gives difference quotient $1$; along the imaginary axis, $h = it$ gives quotient $-1$.\n\nThe two one-sided limits differ.\n\nSo \"real differentiable\" is far from implying \"complex differentiable\".\n\n⭐ An equivalent formulation of complex differentiability is $\\Delta f = A\\,\\Delta z + o(\\Delta z)$ — the increment is **only** a linear function of $\\Delta z$, and $\\overline{\\Delta z}$ may not appear.\n\nIn partial-derivative language this is $\\partial f/\\partial \\bar z = 0$ (see the Cauchy–Riemann equations).\n\n📌 Sums, products, quotients (with nonzero denominator) and composites of holomorphic functions are holomorphic, with the same derivative formulas as in the real case — because these are only the arithmetic of limits and never use the realness of $h$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#66d6ae7e',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-riemann': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: $f = u + iv$ is complex differentiable if and only if it is real differentiable and its two partial derivatives satisfy a very specific pair of equations — the \"two-dimensional limit condition\" is completely replaced by two one-dimensional equations.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a plane region, $f = u + iv: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{C}$, $z_0 \\in \\Omega$. The following are equivalent:\n\n**(i)** $f$ is complex differentiable at $z_0$;\n\n**(ii)** $f$ is real differentiable at $z_0$, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = -\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x};$$\n\n**(iii)** $f$ is real differentiable at $z_0$, and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\bar z}(z_0) = 0$.\n\nWhen they hold, $f'(z_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}(z_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}(z_0) + i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}(z_0)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ac3317e1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The three are really one.\n\n$\\Delta z$ and $\\overline{\\Delta z}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb{C}$ as a real vector space, and the increment can be \"multiplication by a complex number\" only if **only** $\\Delta z$ appears;\n\nthe coefficient of $\\Delta z$ is exactly $u_x + i v_x$.\n\nThe CR equations are simply \"the coefficient of $\\overline{\\Delta z}$ is $0$\", written in components.\n\n⭐ A useful by-product: the Jacobian determinant of $f$ at $z_0$ is $= u_x v_y - u_y v_x = u_x^2 + v_x^2 = |f'(z_0)|^2 \\ge 0$.\n\nHence a holomorphic map is **orientation-preserving** wherever the derivative is nonzero, which is the source of its conformality.\n\n⚠ **Checking the CR equations alone is not enough**; one also needs $f$ to be real differentiable.\n\nCounterexample: $f(x + iy) = \\sqrt{|xy|}$ has all four partial derivatives equal to $0$ at the origin (CR holds), yet the difference quotient along $h = t(1+i)$ tends to $1/(1+i) \\ne 0$, so it is not complex differentiable.\n\nThis also shows that \"partial derivatives exist\" is far weaker than \"real differentiable\".\n\n📌 Holomorphy therefore has two practical routes: the abstract difference quotient (good geometric intuition), or checking CR concretely (convenient for computation).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#485dac38',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-goursat': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: on a simply connected region, the integral of a holomorphic function along any closed curve is $0$ — this is the engine of all of complex analysis.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a **simply connected** plane region, $f$ holomorphic on $\\Omega$, and $\\gamma$ a piecewise smooth closed curve in $\\Omega$. Then\n\n$$\\oint_\\gamma f(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 0.$$\n\nThe multiply connected case: let $\\gamma_0, \\gamma_1, \\cdots, \\gamma_n$ be piecewise smooth simple closed curves in $\\Omega$, with $\\gamma_1, \\cdots, \\gamma_n$ lying inside $\\gamma_0$ and pairwise disjoint, **all oriented counterclockwise**, and $f$ holomorphic on some open set containing the **closed region** they bound (holomorphy \"on the region\" alone is not enough: the curves themselves must lie in the domain). Then\n\n$$\\oint_{\\gamma_0} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\oint_{\\gamma_k} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d9d1a5c5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ \"Simply connected\" cannot be dropped.\n\n$f(z) = 1/z$ is holomorphic on $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$, yet $\\oint_{|z| = 1} \\mathrm{d}z/z = 2\\pi i \\ne 0$.\n\nThat hole is the obstruction — its presence makes the winding number detectable.\n\n⭐ Equivalent statement: contour integrals of holomorphic functions are **homotopy invariant** (continuous deformation of the path inside the region leaves the integral unchanged);\n\nsimply connected = every closed curve in the region is homotopic to a point.\n\n⭐ Immediate corollary: a holomorphic function on a simply connected region **has a primitive** (take $F(z) = \\int_{z_0}^{z} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z$, which is path-independent by Cauchy's theorem, and then $F' = f$).\n\nThere is no **clean** counterpart in real analysis: $F(x) = \\log|x|$ is indeed a primitive of $1/x$ on $\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{0\\}$ — the difference is that $\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{0\\}$ is **disconnected**, so \"path independence\" fails there (the integral around $0$ is nonzero).\n\n⚠ The \"Goursat\" in the name: Cauchy's original 1825 proof required $f'$ to be continuous (via Green's formula);\n\nGoursat later removed this hypothesis by subdividing into triangles.\n\nModern textbooks therefore treat it as the **fundamental theorem of holomorphy**: the conclusion is not bought with a stronger hypothesis.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c0f846f',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-integral-formula': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the value of a holomorphic function at an interior point is **completely determined** by its values on the boundary — every interior point \"listens\" to the boundary.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a bounded region whose boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ is a piecewise smooth simple closed curve, oriented positively, and $f$ holomorphic on an open set containing $\\overline{\\Omega}$. Then for every $z \\in \\Omega$,\n\n$$f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta.$$\n\nMore generally, if $\\gamma$ is a **simple** closed curve in the region and $f$ is holomorphic inside $\\gamma$ and on $\\gamma$, then the formula holds for every point $z$ inside $\\gamma$ (for a general closed curve, write $\\operatorname{Ind}(\\gamma, z)f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_\\gamma \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3dc2e720',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The key is punching a hole**: the integrand $g(\\zeta) = f(\\zeta)/(\\zeta - z)$ is undefined at $\\zeta = z$, so Cauchy's theorem cannot be applied directly to all of $\\Omega$. Fix $z \\in \\Omega$, let $d = \\operatorname{dist}(z, \\partial\\Omega) > 0$, choose $0 < r < d$, remove the closed disk $\\overline{B(z,r)}$ to get the doubly connected region $\\Omega_{r} = \\Omega \\setminus \\overline{B(z,r)}$. $g$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega_{r}$ (the denominator never vanishes), so the multiply connected Cauchy–Goursat theorem (`thm.cauchy-goursat`) gives $\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega_{r}} g\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = 0$. The boundary $\\partial\\Omega_{r}$ consists of the outer boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ (positive) and the inner circle $|\\zeta - z| = r$ (negative); rearranging gives $\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = \\oint_{|\\zeta - z| = r} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta.$.\n\n**Split off the main term**: on the right, write the integrand as $\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z} = \\frac{f(z)}{\\zeta - z} + \\frac{f(\\zeta) - f(z)}{\\zeta - z}.$. The integral of the first term is computed directly with $\\zeta = z + re^{i\\theta}$: $\\oint_{|\\zeta - z| = r} \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\zeta}{\\zeta - z} = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\frac{ire^{i\\theta}}{re^{i\\theta}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta = 2\\pi i$, hence contributes $2\\pi i\\,f(z)$.\n\n**The second term vanishes as $r \\to 0$**: $f$ is holomorphic at $z$, so $\\lim_{\\zeta \\to z}\\frac{f(\\zeta) - f(z)}{\\zeta - z} = f'(z)$; in particular there are $M$ and $\\rho > 0$ such that for $|\\zeta - z| < \\rho$ the quotient is bounded by $\\le M$. Hence for $0 < r < \\min(\\rho, d)$, the ML inequality gives $\\left|\\oint_{|\\zeta - z| = r} \\frac{f(\\zeta) - f(z)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta\\right| \\le M\\cdot 2\\pi r \\longrightarrow 0.$.\n\n**Finish**: the outer integral is independent of $r$ (the identity holds for all $0 < r < d$), so letting $r \\to 0$ gives $\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega}\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = 2\\pi i\\,f(z)$, as required. ∎\n\n**General closed curves**: if $\\gamma$ does not enclose $z$, Cauchy's theorem on the region inside $\\gamma$ gives $\\oint_{\\gamma} = 0$; if $\\gamma$ encloses $z$, the same argument on the annulus between the outer boundary and the inner circle gives the form with the winding number $\\operatorname{Ind}(\\gamma, z)\\,f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\gamma}\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta$.\n\n⭐ **Hub**: from it follow infinite differentiability, Cauchy's inequalities, Liouville, the maximum modulus principle, Taylor/Laurent expansions, and the residue theorem — almost every result in this galaxy grows from here.\n\n⚠ The integrand $f(\\zeta)/(\\zeta - z)$ is **not** holomorphic at $\\zeta = z$, which is exactly why \"punching a hole\" is not a trick but a necessity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#78d40f05',
      },
    },

    'def.contour-integral': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: cut the path into many small pieces, multiply the value of $f$ at each sample point by the corresponding **complex displacement**, sum and take the limit — that is the integral along this path.\n\nLet $\\gamma: [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{C}$ be piecewise smooth (a parametrized curve) and $f$ continuous on $\\gamma$. Define\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma f(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}z := \\lim_{\\lVert\\Delta\\rVert \\to 0} \\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\gamma(\\tau_k))\\,(\\gamma(t_k) - \\gamma(t_{k-1})),$$\n\nwhere $[a, b]$ is any refinement of a partition and $\\tau_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k]$ is arbitrary; the limit exists and is independent of the choices. It equals\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma f(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}z = \\int_a^b f(\\gamma(t))\\,\\gamma'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t,$$\n\nwhose right-hand side is a complex-valued Riemann integral over the real parameter $t$. Splitting into real and imaginary parts ($f = u + iv$, $z = x + iy$) gives two real line integrals:\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = \\int_\\gamma (u\\,\\mathrm{d}x - v\\,\\mathrm{d}y) + i\\int_\\gamma (v\\,\\mathrm{d}x + u\\,\\mathrm{d}y).$$\n\nWhen $\\gamma$ is a closed curve we write $\\oint_\\gamma$; on the reversed curve $\\gamma^{-1}$ the integral changes sign: $\\int_{\\gamma^{-1}} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = -\\int_\\gamma f\\,\\mathrm{d}z$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#34ac3895',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ That real integral on the right is the whole definition: **a complex contour integral is a complex-valued Riemann integral over the real parameter $t$**.\n\nThus every property of the real integral (linearity, additivity over intervals, the triangle inequality, substitution) carries over automatically.\n\n⭐ The real difference from the real integral is **path dependence**: $\\int_\\gamma f\\,\\mathrm{d}z$ is in general not a function of the endpoints alone.\n\nCauchy's theorem is exactly the statement that \"when the function is holomorphic it suddenly depends only on the endpoints, and vanishes on closed curves\" ($0$).\n\n⭐ The real/imaginary splitting above is the interface to Green's formula: applying Green once to $P = u$, $Q = -v$ and once to $P = v$, $Q = u$ yields the Cauchy–Green identity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7d980e94',
      },
    },

    'thm.residue': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the integral along a closed curve equals $2\\pi i$ times the sum of the residues at all singularities inside the curve — a two-dimensional integration problem is compressed into finitely many algebraic computations.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a bounded region whose boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ consists of finitely many piecewise smooth simple closed curves, oriented positively. Let $f$ be holomorphic on $\\Omega$ except at finitely many points $z_1, \\cdots, z_n$, all **isolated** singularities not lying on $\\partial\\Omega$; and let $f$ be holomorphic on some **neighbourhood** of $\\overline{\\Omega}$ except at these points. (⚠ The last condition cannot be omitted: saying only \"the singularities are not on the boundary\" is not enough — for example $\\Omega = \\mathbb{D}$, $f = 1/(1-z)$, where $z = 1$ lies on $\\partial\\mathbb{D}$ and $f$ is undefined there, so $\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} f\\,dz$ does not exist at all.) Then\n\n$$\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} f(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 2\\pi i \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\operatorname{Res}(f, z_k).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9356c036',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Proof: around each $z_k$ remove a closed disk of radius $\\varepsilon$ (pairwise disjoint, all contained in $\\Omega$), obtaining $\\Omega_\\varepsilon = \\Omega \\setminus \\bigcup_k \\overline{B(z_k, \\varepsilon)}$.\n\n$f$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega_\\varepsilon$, so the **multiply connected** Cauchy–Goursat theorem gives $\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega_\\varepsilon} f = 0$, that is,\n\n$$\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\oint_{|\\zeta - z_k| = \\varepsilon} f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = 2\\pi i \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\operatorname{Res}(f, z_k),$$\n\nThe last step is the definition of the residue.\n\n⭐ This is the hardest-working theorem in complex analysis: the whole batch of real integrals in Chapters 23 and 24 ($\\int_0^{2\\pi} Q(\\sin\\theta, \\cos\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} e^{iax}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, $\\int_0^{\\infty} x^{a-1}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, series sums) rests on it and becomes residue computation.\n\n⚠ The closed curves must be **positively** oriented (counterclockwise);\n\nreversing the orientation changes the overall sign.\n\nSingularities **outside** the curve contribute nothing to the integral — alternatively one may compute with the residue at infinity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b6977743',
      },
    },

    'def.mobius': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a fractional linear transformation is the most \"rigid\" class of holomorphic bijections of the Riemann sphere — it sends circles (including lines) to circles and preserves the cross-ratio.\n\nA map of the form\n\n$$f(z) = \\frac{az + b}{cz + d}, \\qquad a, b, c, d \\in \\mathbb{C},\\ ad - bc \\ne 0$$\n\nis called a **fractional linear transformation** (Möbius transformation).\n\nIt extends to a bijection of the Riemann sphere $\\hat{\\mathbb{C}} = \\mathbb{C} \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ onto itself: when $c \\ne 0$, $f(-d/c) = \\infty$ and $f(\\infty) = a/c$;\n\nwhen $c = 0$, $f(\\infty) = \\infty$.\n\nThe inverse is again a fractional linear transformation:\n\n$$f^{-1}(w) = \\frac{dw - b}{-cw + a}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3cfe9130',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ $ad - bc \\ne 0$ is the **nondegeneracy** condition: when it equals $0$, $f$ degenerates to a constant.\n\nIt also guarantees $f'(z) = (ad - bc)/(cz + d)^2 \\ne 0$, so $f$ is **conformal everywhere** (angle-preserving; see the entry on conformality).\n\n⭐ Three basic building blocks: translation $z \\mapsto z + b$, dilation-rotation $z \\mapsto az$ ($a \\ne 0$), inversion $z \\mapsto 1/z$.\n\nEvery Möbius transformation is a composite of these, and $ad - bc \\ne 0$ guarantees that the composite does not collapse to a constant.\n\n⭐ Geometric properties (Chapter 13): it sends **circles or lines** to circles or lines (collectively \"generalized circles\", which on the sphere are simply \"circles\");\n\nand it preserves the **cross-ratio**:\n\n$$(z_1, z_2; z_3, z_4) = \\frac{(z_1 - z_3)(z_2 - z_4)}{(z_1 - z_4)(z_2 - z_3)}.$$\n\nThus $f$ is **uniquely** determined by the images of three points — the cross-ratio is a complete invariant of Möbius transformations.\n\n⚠ The point $\\infty$ on the sphere is not \"a very large number\": it is a point adjoined by stereographic projection.\n\nOnly on the sphere is the statement \"$f$ is a bijection\" clean — on $\\mathbb{C}$ it always misses one or two points.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#38bf42a2',
      },
    },

    'lem.schwarz': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a holomorphic map of the unit disk into itself that fixes the origin must be dominated by $z \\mapsto z$: image points lie closer to the centre than their preimages.\n\nLet $\\mathbb{D} = \\{\\, z : |z| < 1 \\,\\}$, with $f: \\mathbb{D} \\to \\mathbb{D}$ holomorphic and $f(0) = 0$. Then\n\n(1) $|f(z)| \\le |z|$ for all $z \\in \\mathbb{D}$;\n\n(2) $|f'(0)| \\le 1$;\n\n(3) if there is $z_0 \\ne 0$ with $|f(z_0)| = |z_0|$, or if $|f'(0)| = 1$, then $f$ is a rotation $f(z) = e^{i\\theta} z$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3167693',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof route: $f(0) = 0$ makes $f(z)/z$ have a removable singularity at $0$; filling in $f'(0)$ gives a holomorphic function $g$ on the disk. On $|z| = r$ one has $|g| \\le 1/r$, and the maximum modulus principle followed by $r \\to 1$ gives the result.\n\n⭐ The three conclusions are really one chain of inequalities: $|g(z)| \\le 1$ immediately yields (1) and (2); (3) is \"when equality holds in the maximum modulus principle\".\n\n⚠ $f(0) = 0$ cannot be omitted. $f(z) = (z + 1)/2$ maps the disk to the disk, but $|f(0)| = 1/2 > 0$ — as soon as the origin is moved, the lemma's conclusions all fail.\n\n⚠ Equality in $|f(z)| \\le |z|$ at **a single interior point** already suffices; it need not hold everywhere — this is the rigidity of the maximum modulus principle.\n\n📌 The version without the hypothesis $f(0) = 0$ is the Schwarz–Pick theorem. This lemma is also the source of \"the automorphism group of the disk is small\", and a basic tool in conformal mapping.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#343e9102',
      },
    },

    'thm.weierstrass-convergence': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f_{k}$ be holomorphic on a region $\\Omega$ and converge uniformly to $K \\subset \\Omega$ on every compact set $f$. Then\n\n1. $f$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega$;\n2. for every $m \\ge 1$, $f_{k}^{(m)} \\to f^{(m)}$ uniformly on every compact set.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e1dd5014',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof of (1)**: holomorphy is tested by `thm.morera`. Take any closed triangle $T$ in $\\Omega$ (with its interior); the integral of each $f_{k}$ along $\\partial T$ vanishes (Cauchy–Goursat). Since $\\partial T$ is compact, uniform convergence lets the integral pass to the limit, so $\\oint_{\\partial T} f = \\lim_{k} \\oint_{\\partial T} f_{k} = 0$. By Morera, $f$ is holomorphic.\n\n**Proof of (2)**: let $K \\subset \\Omega$ be compact and choose $r > 0$ so that the $r$-neighbourhood of $K$ still lies in $\\Omega$. For $z \\in K$, integrate the Cauchy formula for higher derivatives over the circle of radius $r$:\n\n$$\\left|f_{k}^{(m)}(z) - f^{(m)}(z)\\right| \\le \\frac{m!}{r^{m}}\\,\\sup_{|\\zeta - z| \\le r}\\left|f_{k}(\\zeta) - f(\\zeta)\\right|.$$\n\nThe right-hand side is independent of $z$ and tends to $0$ as $k \\to \\infty$, hence the convergence is uniform on $K$. ∎\n\n⭐ **Why this is the foundation of the whole chapter**: it says that holomorphy is **closed** under uniform convergence, so one may safely pass to limits — normal families (`thm.montel`), the Riemann mapping theorem, and Hurwitz's theorem all rely on it to justify that \"the limit function is still holomorphic\".\n\n⚠️ The hypothesis must be **uniform on compact subsets** (uniform on every compact set). With pointwise convergence alone the limit need not be holomorphic and the derivatives need not converge. (Note: $f_k(z) = z^{k}$ is **not** a counterexample — it converges uniformly on compact subsets of $|z| < 1$, and its derivatives converge uniformly to $0$. A genuine counterexample needs compact sets that touch the boundary of convergence.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#820063f1',
      },
    },

    'thm.max-modulus': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the modulus of a holomorphic function cannot attain a maximum in the interior of a region — unless the function is constant. The maximum can only occur on the boundary.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a plane region (a connected open set) and $f$ holomorphic on $\\Omega$. Then either $f$ is constant, or\n\n$$|f(z)| < \\|f\\|_{\\Omega} := \\sup_{\\zeta \\in \\Omega} |f(\\zeta)|, \\qquad \\forall z \\in \\Omega.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9c3dcb0d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof uses the **mean value formula**: the Cauchy integral formula at the centre $\\zeta = z + re^{i\\theta}$ gives\n\n$$f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(z + re^{i\\theta})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$$\n\n(as long as $\\overline{B(z, r)} \\subset \\Omega$).\n\nIf $|f|$ attains its maximum $M$ at an **interior** point $z_0$, then $M = |f(z_0)| \\le \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} |f(z_0 + re^{i\\theta})|\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\le M$; equality at both ends forces $|f| \\equiv M$ on the circle.\n\n⭐ By connectedness: the set $\\{ z : |f(z)| = M \\}$ is both open (a whole circle around every point) and closed ($|f|$ is continuous), hence is all of $\\Omega$;\n\nso $|f|$ is constant, and adding the holomorphic $f$ gives that $f$ is constant.\n\n⭐ Corollary for bounded regions: if $\\Omega$ is bounded, $f$ is continuous on $\\overline{\\Omega}$ and holomorphic on $\\Omega$, then $\\|f\\|_{\\overline{\\Omega}} = \\|f\\|_{\\partial\\Omega}$ — the maximum is attained on the boundary.\n\nThis is the strongest form of \"control the interior by boundary values\".\n\n⚠ There is no analogue for the **minimum** of $|f|$: $f(z) = z$ attains its minimum on the unit disk at $0$ (an interior point).\n\nThe correct minimum modulus principle: if $f$ is holomorphic and **nowhere zero**, apply the maximum modulus principle to $1/f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#266783db',
      },
    },

    'thm.rouche': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: if on the boundary of a closed curve the modulus of a \"perturbation term\" is strictly smaller than the modulus of a \"main term\", then adding the perturbation does not change the number of zeros inside the curve.\n\nLet $\\gamma$ be a **positively oriented, simple** piecewise smooth closed curve (counterclockwise, no self-intersection), let $f, g$ be holomorphic on $\\gamma$ and its interior, and suppose that everywhere on $\\gamma$\n\n$$|g(z)| < |f(z)|.$$\n\nThen $f$ and $f + g$ have the same number of zeros inside $\\gamma$ (both counted with multiplicity).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b23c23ac',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Use: count zeros without solving equations — just compare sizes on the boundary.\n\nExample: $f(z) = -5z^3$, $g(z) = z^7 + z - 2$; on $|z| = 1$ we have $|g| \\le 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 < 5 = |f|$, so $z^7 - 5z^3 + z - 2$ has exactly $3$ zeros in $|z| < 1$ (the same as $-5z^3$).\n\n⭐ Another proof of the fundamental theorem of algebra: $f(z) = a_n z^n$, $g(z) = a_{n-1}z^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_0$; on $|z| = R$ with $R$ large enough one has $|g| < |f|$, so $p$ and $a_n z^n$ have the same number of zeros in $|z| < R$, namely $n$.\n\n⚠ The inequality must be **strict** and must hold on the **whole boundary**. Equality is exactly where things can break: $f = z$, $g = -z$ on $|z| = 1$ satisfy $|g| = |f|$, yet $f$ and $f + g = 0$ have completely different numbers of zeros.\n\n📌 How to choose the \"main term\": take the term with the largest modulus on the boundary. This is the same fact as \"the behaviour of a polynomial at infinity is governed by its leading term\".\n\n⚠ **\"Simple\" cannot be dropped**: the assertion is about the number of zeros inside (unweighted). For a general closed curve the correct statement is weighted by the winding number — by the argument principle $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint f'/f$ is always a weighted sum, and the two sides agree only when the winding number at every interior point is $= 1$ (see the notes to `thm.argument-principle`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a3fac96f',
      },
    },

    'thm.argument-principle': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the number of turns made by a holomorphic function along a closed curve equals the number of zeros minus the number of poles inside the curve (both counted with multiplicity) — counting the change of argument is the same as counting zeros and poles.\n\nLet $\\gamma$ be a **positively oriented, simple** piecewise smooth closed curve (counterclockwise, no self-intersection), let $\\Omega$ contain $\\gamma$ and its interior, let $f$ be holomorphic on $\\Omega$ except at finitely many poles, and suppose $\\gamma$ contains neither zeros nor poles. Then\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\gamma} \\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}\\,\\mathrm{d}z = Z - P = n(f \\circ \\gamma,\\, 0),$$\n\nwhere $Z$ is the number of zeros of $f$ inside $\\gamma$ (counted with multiplicity), $P$ is the number of poles (counted with order), and $n$ is the winding number.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#61c651a4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The most common special case: when $f$ is **holomorphic** inside, $P = 0$ and hence $\\oint_{\\gamma} f'/f = 2\\pi i Z$ — \"the number of zeros\" becomes an integral.\n\n⭐ Geometric reading: $f \\circ \\gamma$ is a closed curve around $0$, and $Z - P$ is the number of times it winds around $0$. Zeros twist the image curve one turn outward, poles one turn inward.\n\n⚠ There must be no zeros or poles on the boundary — otherwise $f'/f$ is undefined on $\\gamma$ and the integral does not exist at all.\n\n⭐ A by-product: $Z - P$ is **invariant** under small deformations of the curve (as long as no zero or pole is swept), because it is an integer and varies continuously.\n\n📌 Every technique for \"counting zeros\" (Rouché, Hurwitz) starts from this identity; it also explains why zeros and poles can cancel each other at the level of integrals.\n\n⚠ **\"Simple\" cannot be dropped**: the assertion is the unweighted $Z - P$, valid only for **positively oriented simple** closed curves. For a general closed curve the correct form is weighted by winding numbers: $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\gamma}\\dfrac{f'}{f} = \\sum_k m_k\\,n(\\gamma, a_k) - \\sum_j n_j\\,n(\\gamma, b_j)$ (where $a_k$ are zeros, $b_j$ poles, $n$ the winding number; see `def.winding-number` / `prop.winding-integral`) — the right-hand side $n(f\\circ\\gamma, 0)$ is always a **weighted** quantity, so the two sides agree only when the winding number at every interior point is $=1$, in which case both equal $Z - P$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a0120106',
      },
    },

    'thm.convergence-radius': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\sum a_n (x - x_0)^{n}$ be a power series and put (with the conventions $1/0 = +\\infty$, $1/{+\\infty} = 0$)\n\n$$R := \\frac{1}{\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} \\sqrt[n]{|a_n|}}$$\n\nThen: for $|x - x_0| < R$ the series **converges absolutely**; for $|x - x_0| > R$ it **diverges**; for $|x - x_0| = R$ there is **no conclusion**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a0d73c51',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ $R$ is called the **radius of convergence**, $(x_0 - R, x_0 + R)$ the interval of convergence.\n\nThe proof is just the **root test** applied to the coefficients: $\\limsup \\sqrt[n]{|a_n (x - x_0)^{n}|} = |x - x_0| \\cdot \\limsup \\sqrt[n]{|a_n|}$.\n\n⚠ One must use $\\limsup$ here, not $\\lim$: $\\sqrt[n]{|a_n|}$ need not converge.\n\n⚠ At $|x - x_0| = R$ all three behaviours can occur ($\\sum x^{n}$ diverges at both ends, $\\sum x^{n}/n$ converges at one end and diverges at the other, $\\sum x^{n}/n^{2}$ converges at both ends).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9870cbe9',
      },
    },

    'thm.real-ordered-field': {
      statement: {
        en: "The $(\\mathbb{R}, +, \\cdot, \\le)$ constructed above satisfies three groups of properties:\n\n**(i) Field axioms**: $(\\mathbb{R}, +, \\cdot)$ is a field — closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication and division (by nonzero elements), with associative, commutative and distributive laws;\n\n**(ii) Order axioms**: $\\le$ is a total order compatible with the operations:\n$$a \\le b \\implies a + c \\le b + c, \\qquad a \\le b,\\ 0 \\le c \\implies ac \\le bc$$\n\n**(iii) Completeness (least upper bound principle)**: **every nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound**.\n\nMoreover the embedded image of $\\mathbb{Q}$ is **dense** in $\\mathbb{R}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#65eb0f17',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ **Note where this statement sits**: it is a **theorem**, not a definition — first comes the construction (\"the real number system $\\mathbb{R}$\"), then the proof that what was constructed has these three groups of properties.\n\n⭐ **Item (iii) is the engine of analysis**: without it $\\mathbb{Q}$ also satisfies the first two items, but $\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Q} : x^2 < 2 \\}$ has no least upper bound in $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\n($\\mathbb{Q}$ is therefore not a complete ordered field — this is not a defect, but precisely the reason to complete $\\mathbb{Q}$ into $\\mathbb{R}$.)\n\nFrom (iii) follow the two most-used facts in analysis:\n- **monotone bounded sequences converge**;\n\n- **the nested interval theorem**, **Bolzano–Weierstrass**, and **convergence of Cauchy sequences**.\n\n⚠ Completeness says \"**a least upper bound exists**\", not \"a limit necessarily exists\" — the latter is a corollary.\n\n📌 The route to (iii) has two steps (see the proof on the edge): first prove that **every Cauchy sequence in $\\mathbb{R}$ converges** (extract a rational Cauchy sequence from the representatives by a diagonal argument), then derive the least upper bound principle from Cauchy completeness (bisection produces an increasing sequence and a decreasing sequence of upper bounds, squeezed to the same limit).\n\n📌 Properties derived from these three items and used everywhere later: the **Archimedean property** and the **density of $\\mathbb{Q}$ in $\\mathbb{R}$** (see the entry on the Archimedean property);\n\n$\\mathbb{R}$ is also **uncountable** ($\\#\\mathbb{R} = 2^{\\aleph_0}$) — \"dense\" and \"equinumerous\" are two different things.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3930fe25',
      },
    },

    'thm.baire': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a complete metric space (or a locally compact Hausdorff space). Then the intersection of countably many dense open sets is still **dense**. Equivalently:\n\n1. the union of countably many nowhere dense closed sets has empty interior;\n2. every nonempty open set is of the **second category**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e1e853ed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof (nested balls), four steps:\n\n1. **Start**: write $B_0 := B(x, \\varepsilon)$.\n\n$O_1$ dense open $\\implies$ $B_0 \\cap O_1$ is nonempty open, so choose $x_1$ and $\\varepsilon_1 < \\min(\\varepsilon/2,\\ 1)$ with\n\n$$\\overline{B(x_1, \\varepsilon_1)} \\subseteq B(x, \\varepsilon) \\cap O_1 .$$\n\n2. **Induction**: suppose $x_n, \\varepsilon_n$ have been chosen (with $\\varepsilon_n < 1/n$).\n\nSince $O_{n+1}$ is dense open, $B(x_n, \\varepsilon_n) \\cap O_{n+1}$ is still nonempty open; choose\n\n$$\\overline{B(x_{n+1}, \\varepsilon_{n+1})} \\subseteq B(x_n, \\varepsilon_n) \\cap O_{n+1}, \\qquad \\varepsilon_{n+1} < \\frac{1}{n+1} .$$\n\n3. **Pick a point in the nested closed balls**: radius $\\varepsilon_n \\to 0$, and $X$ is complete, so $\\bigcap_n \\overline{B(x_n, \\varepsilon_n)} \\ne \\emptyset$; take a point $y$ in it.\n\n(Here \"the radii tend to $0$\" cannot be dropped: completeness only guarantees a common point for nested closed balls whose radii tend to $0$.)\n\n4. **Finish**: for every $n$, $y \\in \\overline{B(x_n, \\varepsilon_n)} \\subseteq B_0 \\cap O_n$, so $y \\in B_0$ and $y \\in \\bigcap_n O_n$.\n\n$B_0$ was arbitrary, so $\\bigcap_n O_n$ meets every nonempty open set, hence is dense.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ All three equivalent formulations should be usable; passing between them is just taking complements.\n\n⚠️ Completeness cannot be dropped: in $\\mathbb{Q}$ every singleton is closed and nowhere dense, and $\\mathbb{Q}$ is countable, so $\\mathbb{Q}$ is of the first category — it is not a Baire space.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the translations between the three formulations are free; their validity rests on Baire's theorem):\n- Countable intersection of dense open sets is dense $\\iff$ countable union of nowhere dense closed sets has empty interior: take complements; holds in any topological space, no completeness needed.\n- The closed nowhere dense formulation $\\iff$ every nonempty open set is not of the first category in the whole space: replace each nowhere dense set by its closure and unwind the definition of first category.\n- Complete metric space $\\implies$ the above properties: recursively choose nested closed balls with radii tending to zero and use completeness to get a common point; in the usual foundational setting this recursion uses dependent choice.\n- The locally compact Hausdorff version replaces completeness by nested compact neighbourhoods and compactness. A metric space alone is not enough: $\\mathbb Q$ is a countable union of nowhere dense singletons; but completeness is sufficient, not necessary, for the Baire property.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#067d011c',
      },
    },

    'thm.lagrange': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a finite group and $H \\le G$. For $g \\in G$ write the **left coset** $gH := \\{\\, gh : h \\in H \\,\\}$. Then the left cosets form a partition of $G$, each left coset is equinumerous with $H$, hence\n\n$$|G| = [G : H] \\cdot |H|, \\qquad |H| \\mid |G|$$\n\nwhere $[G : H] := |G|/|H|$ is called the **index** of $H$ in $G$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#64135c2e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof is two sentences: **cosets form a partition** ($g_1 H \\cap g_2 H \\ne \\emptyset \\implies g_1 H = g_2 H$, using that $H$ is closed under multiplication) + **translation is a bijection** ($h \\mapsto gh$ is a bijection $H \\to gH$).\n\nThe full proof is on the edge.\n\n⭐ Three immediate corollaries:\n- the order of every element $g$ divides $|G|$ (take $\\langle g\\rangle$ as $H$);\n\n- a group of prime order is cyclic (take any non-identity element; its order is either $1$ or $p$);\n\n- $g^{|G|} = e$ holds for every $g$ — so Euler's theorem $a^{\\varphi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod n$ is a special case (take $G = (\\mathbb{Z}/n)^{\\times}$).\n\n⚠ **The converse fails**: \"$d \\mid |G|$ $\\implies$ has a subgroup of order $d$\" is **false**.\n\nSmallest counterexample: $|A_4| = 12$, $6 \\mid 12$, yet $A_4$ has no subgroup of order $6$ (a subgroup of order $6$ would have index $2$ and hence be normal, while the only normal subgroups of $A_4$ are $V_4$ and $A_4$).\n\nSo divisibility is only a necessary condition — a partial converse requires Sylow's theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a3083983',
      },
    },

    'thm.factor-theorem': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in K[x]$, $a \\in K$. Then\n\n$$f(a) = 0 \\iff (x - a) \\text{ divides } f \\ \\text{ in } K[x]$$\n\nThat is: there exists $h \\in K[x]$ with $f = (x - a)\\,h$. In this case $a$ is called a **root** (zero) of $f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4918315c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof is two lines: divide $f$ by $(x - a)$ ($x - a$ is monic, so division with remainder applies); the remainder $r$ has degree $< 1$, so $r$ is a constant; substituting $x = a$ gives $f(a) = r$.\n\nSo \"the remainder\" is exactly \"the value of the function\".\n\n⭐ This immediately gives **that the number of roots does not exceed the degree** (next entry), and \"a linear polynomial always has a root\".\n\n⚠ $a$ must be an element of $K$: $x^{2} + 1$ has no root in $\\mathbb{R}$, but of course it is not divisible by $(x - a)$ ($a \\in \\mathbb{R}$).\n\nThe **existence** of roots depends on $K$ — that is precisely what the fundamental theorem of algebra addresses.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b09f507b',
      },
    },

    'thm.inverse-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open, $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ of class $C^{1}$, $a \\in U$ with $Df(a)$ **invertible**.\n\nThen there exist an open neighbourhood $V \\subseteq U$ of $a$ and an open neighbourhood $W$ of $f(a)$ such that the restriction $f\\bigl|_{V}$ is a **bijection** $V \\to W$,\n\n$$f\\bigl|_{V} : V \\to W \\quad \\text{bijective},$$\n\nand its inverse $g := (f\\bigl|_{V})^{-1} : W \\to V$ is also $C^{1}$, and for every $y \\in W$\n\n$$Dg(y) = \\bigl( Df(g(y)) \\bigr)^{-1}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#15a84bd0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The shape of the theorem is worth remembering: **locally invertible $\\iff$ $Df(a)$ invertible**, and \"the derivative of the inverse is the inverse of the derivative\".\n\nThe second half is pure linear algebra — once $f$ is viewed locally as \"a linear map plus higher-order terms\", inversion naturally lands on $Df$.\n\n⚠ \"Locally\" cannot be dropped.\n\nFor $f(x, y) = (e^{x}\\cos y,\\ e^{x}\\sin y)$, $\\det Df = e^{2x} \\ne 0$ holds everywhere, yet $f$ is far from injective globally ($y$ has period $2\\pi$).\n\n⚠ The theorem guarantees only $C^{1}$;\n\nbut from $Dg = (Df \\circ g)^{-1}$ it follows at once: $f$ smooth $\\implies$ $g$ smooth ($Df\\circ g$ smooth, and inversion is a smooth operation).\n\nThis is \"$f$ is a diffeomorphism $\\implies$ $f^{-1}$ is too\".\n\n⭐ The theorem also shows that $f$ is an **open map** (it sends the open set $V$ to the open set $W$) — this is **free** in both finite dimensions and Banach spaces (the inverse function theorem itself gives a local homeomorphism, and a local homeomorphism automatically sends open sets to open sets). ⚠ Do not confuse this with the **open mapping theorem** of functional analysis: that one says a surjective bounded linear operator is open, a different notion sharing the name with \"a nonconstant holomorphic function is open\".\n\n⚠ It belongs to the same family as `thm.regular-value` and `cor.rank-theorem`: the rank theorem says \"a constant-rank map locally has only a standard form\", and the inverse function theorem is the case $r = n = m$.\n\nThe full proof is on the edge (reduce to $Df(a) = I$, build a local inverse of $f$ by the contraction mapping principle, then solve for $Dg$ from $f \\circ g = \\mathrm{id}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#37b62d26',
      },
    },

    'thm.implicit-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\times \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be open, $F : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ of class $C^{1}$, $(a, b) \\in U$ with $F(a, b) = 0$, and $D_y F(a, b)$ **invertible**:\n\n$$F(a, b) = 0, \\qquad D_y F(a, b) \\in M_m(\\mathbb{R})\\ \\text{invertible}$$\n\n($D_y F$ denotes the matrix of partial derivatives with respect to the last $m$ variables.) Then there exist an open neighbourhood $V \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ of $a$, an open neighbourhood $W \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ of $b$, and a **unique** $C^{1}$ map $g : V \\to W$ such that\n\n$$g(a) = b, \\qquad F\\bigl(x,\\ g(x)\\bigr) = 0 \\quad (x \\in V)$$\n\nand\n\n$$Dg(x) = - \\bigl( D_y F(x, g(x)) \\bigr)^{-1} D_x F(x, g(x))$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7c56a6d3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **the equation $F(x, y) = 0$ can be solved uniquely for $y = g(x)$ where $D_yF$ is invertible, and the resulting map is automatically $C^{1}$**.\n\nThat is the entire content of the name \"implicit function\" — it turns \"implicitly determined by an equation\" into \"a function really exists\".\n\n⭐ It is the **inverse function theorem in disguise**: assemble $F$ into $\\Phi(x, y) := (x, F(x, y))$; then $D\\Phi$ is block upper triangular, $\\det D\\Phi = \\det D_y F$, so invertibility is equivalent;\n\nand the inverse of $\\Phi$ must have the form $(x, y) \\mapsto (x, h(x, y))$; setting $g(x) := h(x, 0)$ gives the desired implicit function.\n\nThe two theorems are two ways of saying the same thing.\n\n⚠ The invertibility of $D_yF$ is **essential**: for $F(x, y) = x^{2} - y^{2}$ at $(0,0)$, $D_yF = 0$, and the equation $x^{2} = y^{2}$ cannot be solved uniquely for $y = g(x)$ near the origin (there are two branches $y = \\pm x$).\n\n⭐ The formula for $Dg$ need not be memorized: differentiate $F(x, g(x)) = 0$ on both sides and use the chain rule — $D_xF + D_yF \\cdot Dg = 0$, then solve for $Dg$.\n\nThe full proof is on the edge.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#91f87be7',
      },
    },

    'thm.structure-equations': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: **these two equations govern how the frame moves**. The first (zero torsion) says \"the change of the coframe is completely determined by the connection forms, with no extra twist\"; the second ($\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is flat) says \"rotating once in each of two directions gives the same result regardless of order\". Together they express $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$ in a frame.\n\nLet $\\omega^{i}$ be the dual coframe ($\\mathrm{d}r = \\sum_{i}\\omega^{i}e_{i}$) and $\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}$ the connection forms. Then\n\n**First structure equation** (zero torsion)\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i} = \\sum_{j}\\omega^{j}\\wedge\\omega^{i}_{\\ j} ;$$\n\n**Second structure equation** ($\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ flat)\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}+\\sum_{k}\\omega^{i}_{\\ k}\\wedge\\omega^{k}_{\\ j} = 0 ,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i}_{\\ j} = \\sum_{k}\\omega^{k}_{\\ j}\\wedge\\omega^{i}_{\\ k}.$$\n\nTogether they express $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ on the frame: applying the exterior derivative to $\\mathrm{d}r$ gives the first equation, and to $\\mathrm{d}e_{i}$ gives the second.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bfd58c6b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why \"structure\"**: these two equations determine all constraints on \"how the frame moves\". Given the coframe and the connection, the shape of the surface together with its position in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is determined; conversely the only compatibility to check is $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$. The fundamental theorem of surface theory turns this sentence into a theorem.\n\n**Curvature form**: on a general manifold the right-hand side of the second equation is nonzero; that term is called the curvature form $\\Omega^{i}_{\\ j} := \\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}+\\sum_{k}\\omega^{i}_{\\ k}\\wedge\\omega^{k}_{\\ j}$;\n\non $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$, $\\Omega \\equiv 0$.\n\nRestricted to a surface it becomes $\\Omega^{i}_{\\ j} = \\omega^{i}_{\\ 3}\\wedge\\omega^{j}_{\\ 3}$ — this is the Gauss equation.\n\nThe same thing can be written as \"the mixed partial derivatives of $r_{ij} = \\Gamma^{k}_{\\ ij}r_{k}+h_{ij}n$ and $n_{i} = -h^{j}_{\\ i}r_{j}$ commute (integrability condition)\". Here one only replaces \"mixed partial derivatives\" by $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ and $\\Gamma$ by $\\omega$: the same compatibility, in shorter notation.\n\n⚠️ **Sign convention**: this pack takes $\\omega^{j}_{\\ i} = \\langle \\mathrm{d}e_{i}, e_{j}\\rangle$, i.e. $\\mathrm{d}e_{i} = \\sum_{j}\\omega^{j}_{\\ i}e_{j}$. Moving an index up or down flips the sign of the whole second equation; align this before quoting.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bd8918ef',
      },
    },

    'def.norm': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $E$ be a vector space over $\\mathbb{K}$ ($\\mathbb{K} = \\mathbb{R}$ or $\\mathbb{C}$). A function $\\|\\cdot\\| : E \\to [0, +\\infty)$ is called a **norm** if\n\n1. $\\|x\\| = 0 \\iff x = 0$;\n2. $\\|\\lambda x\\| = |\\lambda|\\,\\|x\\|$;\n3. $\\|x + y\\| \\le \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|$.\n\nA space equipped with a norm is a **normed space**. One satisfying only 2 and 3 (allowing nonzero elements to have zero norm) is a **seminorm**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3ac74b39',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "What a norm has beyond a distance is **linearity**: it records only \"how far from the origin\", and distances are obtained by translation (see \"the distance induced by a norm\"). So a normed space is a metric space \"with an origin, a scale, and translation invariance\".\n\n⚠️ A seminorm is not a norm: one must pass to the quotient by the zero-norm subspace to get a norm (in the construction of $L^p$, \"$\\|f\\|_p = 0$ only says $f = 0$ a.e.\" is exactly this; see \"the $L^p$ norm\").\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the equivalences among the norm axioms and the facts derived from them must be kept separate):\n- **Norm** $\\iff$ **the three axioms**: both directions are expansions of the definition, and neither completeness nor finite dimension is needed.\n- $x=0\\implies\\|x\\|=0$: follows by taking the scalar to be zero in absolute homogeneity; the converse is the positive-definiteness axiom and cannot be derived from homogeneity and the triangle inequality.\n- Seminorm $\\implies$ norm holds only when the zero space is trivial; the zero function on a nontrivial space is a counterexample. Well-definedness of the norm on the quotient by the zero space uses the triangle inequality, not the axiom of choice.\n\n📌 **Normed space** is defined in `def.normed-space`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#62e48f61',
      },
    },

    'def.banach-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "A normed space $V$ is **complete** iff it is complete with respect to the metric $d(v, w) = \\|v - w\\|$, i.e. every Cauchy sequence converges. A complete normed space is a **Banach space**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ddbf3c4a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Three standard examples: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ (completeness is the finite-dimensional version of `thm.real-complete-metric`), $L^{p}$ (`thm.lp-banach`, Riesz–Fischer), and $C(S)$ on a compact Hausdorff space with the sup norm.\n\n⚠ Completeness is not automatic: $C[0, 1]$ with $\\|f\\|_{1} = \\int_0^1 |f|$ is **not** complete (one can approximate a discontinuous function).\n\nThe same set of functions is complete under one norm and incomplete under another — \"complete\" is a property of the norm, not of the set.\n\n⭐ Completeness is the engine of the contraction mapping principle below (`thm.banach-fixed-point`): without it one only gets a Cauchy sequence, not a fixed point.\n\nThe whole constructive proof of the inverse function theorem rests on this.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e7c5b49d',
      },
    },

    'thm.int-division': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ with $b \\ne 0$. Then there exist **unique** $q, r \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n\n$$a = qb + r, \\qquad 0 \\le r < |b| .$$\n\n$q$ is called the **quotient** and $r$ the **remainder**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26e6a9ce',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Uniqueness: if $a = qb + r = q'b + r'$, then $(q - q')b = r' - r$, hence $|b| \\mid |r' - r|$; but $|r' - r| < |b|$, so necessarily $r' = r$, and then $q' = q$.\n\nExistence uses the well-ordering of $\\mathbb{Z}$: fix $b > 0$; the set $\\{\\, a - qb : q \\in \\mathbb{Z},\\ a - qb \\ge 0 \\,\\}$ is nonempty (Archimedean property), and its least element is $r$; if $r \\ge b$ then $r - b$ is smaller, a contradiction.\n\nThe corresponding theorem on $K[x]$ is `thm.division-algorithm`: the same proof structure, only \"degree\" is replaced by \"absolute value\" — both are Euclidean domains.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c9ab3c4c',
      },
    },

    'thm.division-algorithm': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field, $f, g \\in K[x]$, $g \\ne 0$. Then there exist **unique** $q, r \\in K[x]$ such that\n\n$$f = q\\,g + r, \\qquad \\deg r < \\deg g$$\n\n($r$ is called the **remainder**, $q$ the **quotient**; $r = 0$ is allowed.)\n\nConsequently $K[x]$ is a **Euclidean domain**: there is a Euclidean function \"degree\" satisfying $\\deg r < \\deg g$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#443bcab8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"$K$ is a field\" cannot be dropped: over the ring $\\mathbb{Z}[x]$ there is no division with remainder ($x^{2}$ divided by $2x + 1$ does not produce a polynomial quotient).\n\nIts role is to guarantee that the **leading coefficient is invertible**, so that the highest-degree term can be eliminated.\n\n⭐ The consequences are far-reaching: division with remainder gives the **Euclidean algorithm** (for the gcd), hence **Bézout's identity** ($af + bg = \\gcd$), hence \"irreducible $\\implies$ prime\", hence **unique factorization** (`thm.poly-ufd`).\n\nEuclidean domains are the \"everything works\" class of rings.\n\n⚠ Uniqueness uses comparison of degrees, not \"uniqueness of the division procedure\" — the choices at each step of the division are not unique, but the final quotient and remainder are.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#902b9473',
      },
    },

    'thm.nth-root': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a complete ordered field (e.g. $\\mathbb{R}$), $a > 0$, $n \\ge 1$. Then there exists a **unique** $b > 0$ such that\n\n$$b^{n} = a;$$\n\nWrite $b = \\sqrt[n]{a} = a^{1/n}$. Moreover:\n\n- $a \\mapsto \\sqrt[n]{a}$ is strictly increasing on $(0, +\\infty)$;\n- $\\sqrt[n]{ab} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\,\\sqrt[n]{b}$, $\\sqrt[n]{a^{m}} = \\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^{m}$ ($m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c9db4cad',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ $n = 2$, $a = 2$ is exactly the **existence of $\\sqrt{2}$**: $\\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} : q^{2} < 2\\}$ has no least upper bound in $\\mathbb{Q}$, but does in $\\mathbb{R}$ — that least upper bound is $\\sqrt{2}$.\n\nSo this theorem is the first concrete construction of \"what $\\mathbb{R}$ has beyond $\\mathbb{Q}$\".\n\n⭐ The proof is a **template for the least upper bound principle**: let $S = \\{t \\ge 0 : t^{n} \\le a\\}$, take $b = \\sup S$, then prove $b^{n} = a$ (neither inequality may be strict: if too small, raise a little; if too large, lower a little).\n\n⚠ The two estimates go in opposite directions: raising uses the **binomial theorem** (**one cannot keep only the first-order term**: from the binomial expansion $ (b+h)^{n} - b^{n} = h\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\binom{n}{k} b^{n-k} $, take $0 < h \\le 1$ and then make $h$ small enough), while lowering uses **Bernoulli** ($(1 - h/b)^{n} \\ge 1 - nh/b$).\n\nThis is where `thm.binomial` and `thm.bernoulli` are first genuinely used in the graph.\n\n⚠ Uniqueness rests on **strict monotonicity**: $0 \\le u < v \\implies u^{n} < v^{n}$ (factorization $v^{n} - u^{n} = (v-u)(v^{n-1} + \\cdots + u^{n-1}) > 0$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b233302a',
      },
    },

    'thm.mean-value-multi': {
      statement: {
        en: "**Mean value theorem**: let $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable on the open set $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, with $a, a + h \\in U$ and the segment $[a, a+h] \\subset U$. Then there exists $\\theta \\in (0, 1)$ such that\n\n$$f(a + h) - f(a) = Df(a + \\theta h)(h)$$\n\n**Mean value inequality**: if $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ is differentiable, then\n\n$$\\|f(a + h) - f(a)\\| \\le \\Bigl( \\sup_{[a, a+h]} \\|Df\\| \\Bigr) \\|h\\|$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e94a7fbd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The one-dimensional mean value theorem rests on Rolle, and Rolle on \"a continuous function on a closed interval attains its extrema\" — and **multivariable calculus has no such thing** (the equality version is **false** for $\\mathbb{R}^{m}$-valued functions: $f(t) = (\\cos t, \\sin t)$ goes once around a circle, $f(2\\pi) - f(0) = 0$, yet $Df$ is nowhere zero and $\\|Df\\| = 1$).\n\n⭐ So what is really useful in several variables is the **inequality version**: it needs only \"apply the one-variable mean value theorem along a line segment + estimate the norm\", and no existence of extrema.\n\nThe contraction mapping principle and the inverse function theorem later use nothing but this.\n\n⚠ In the scalar case ($f$ has values in $\\mathbb{R}$) the equality version needs care: the usual proof applies the one-dimensional MVT to $\\varphi(t) = f(a + th)$ and yields \"there exists $\\theta$ with $f(a+h) - f(a) = Df(a + \\theta h)(h)$\" — here $\\theta$ depends on $h$ and cannot be treated as a constant.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7ef0fb14',
      },
    },

    'thm.taylor-multi': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open, $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}$ of class $C^{k+1}$, $a \\in U$. Then for $h$ sufficiently small,\n\n$$f(a + h) = \\sum_{|\\alpha| \\le k} \\frac{D^{\\alpha} f(a)}{\\alpha!}\\, h^{\\alpha} + R_k(h)$$\n\nwhere the remainder can be written (for some $\\theta \\in (0,1)$)\n\n$$R_k(h) = \\sum_{|\\alpha| = k+1} \\frac{D^{\\alpha} f(a + \\theta h)}{\\alpha!}\\, h^{\\alpha} = O\\bigl(\\|h\\|^{k+1}\\bigr)$$\n\nThe two most-used cases:\n\n$$f(a+h) = f(a) + Df(a)(h) + o(\\|h\\|)$$\n\n$$f(a+h) = f(a) + Df(a)(h) + \\frac{1}{2}\\, D^{2}f(a)(h, h) + o\\bigl(\\|h\\|^{2}\\bigr)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#20ac58cc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Key observation: **the second derivative $D^{2}f(a)$ is a symmetric bilinear form on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**, not \"a bunch of second partials\".\n\nIn the formula for $k = 2$, $D^{2}f(a)(h,h)$ is precisely $\\sum_{i,j} \\partial_i \\partial_j f(a)\\, h_i h_j = h^{\\mathsf{T}} H_f(a)\\, h$.\n\n⭐ Thus multivariable Taylor meets linear algebra directly: $h^{\\mathsf{T}} H h$ is a **quadratic form**, and positive definiteness of quadratic forms has a ready criterion (`thm.sylvester-criterion`) — the second derivative test (`thm.second-derivative-test`) is this formula plus that criterion.\n\n⚠ Pitfall in the proof: applying one-variable Taylor to $t \\mapsto f(a + th)$ gives an expansion **only along one line**, and the $\\theta$ in the remainder depends on $h$.\n\nTo get uniformity in all $h$, one must induct on $k$ and write the remainder as a sum over multi-indices, using the one-variable MVT once per layer (or iterating over the coordinates of $h$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#52015d28',
      },
    },

    'thm.matrix-linear-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ have ordered basis $\\mathcal{B} = (v_1, \\ldots, v_n)$ and $W$ have ordered basis $\\mathcal{C} = (w_1, \\ldots, w_m)$, both finite-dimensional $K$-vector spaces.\n\nThe $m \\times n$ matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}^{\\mathcal{C}}$ is called the **matrix** of $T$ with respect to these two bases, where the $j$-th column is the coordinate vector of $T(v_j)$ in $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nThen the map $T \\mapsto [T]_{\\mathcal{B}}^{\\mathcal{C}}$ is an isomorphism of $K$-vector spaces\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V, W) \\;\\cong\\; M_{m \\times n}(K)$$\n\nand **composition corresponds to multiplication**: if $T : V \\to W$, $S : W \\to U$, then\n\n$$[S \\circ T]_{\\mathcal{B}}^{\\mathcal{E}} = [S]_{\\mathcal{C}}^{\\mathcal{E}}\\; [T]_{\\mathcal{B}}^{\\mathcal{C}}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#02b03eda',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **birth certificate** of all matrix theory: a matrix is not a defined object but the **coordinates** of a linear map in chosen bases (exactly the same mechanism as the entry on coordinates, only with a basis chosen on each side).\n\n⭐ Thus every matrix operation acquires meaning: addition = sum of maps, multiplication = composition, identity matrix = identity map, invertible = isomorphism.\n\n**Every formula about matrices can be translated into the language of maps**, and conversely — this is why the graph puts the two in separate clusters yet connects them by edges.\n\n⚠ All of this **depends on the choice of bases**: change the basis and the matrix changes. **The same space** $V = W$ with the same basis used on both sides gives **similarity** $P^{-1}AP$; for a general $T : V \\to W$ the two bases change independently and one gets **equivalence** $Q^{-1}AP$ (e.g. $V = W = \\mathbb{R}$, $T = \\mathrm{id}$: changing only the basis of the domain to $(2)$ gives $[2]$, which is not similar to $[1]$).\n\nThe linear map itself, however, does not change.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f22335a9',
      },
    },

    'thm.arzela-ascoli': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a compact metric space, and let $\\mathcal{F} \\subset C(X)$ (continuous functions with the sup norm) satisfy\n\n1. **Uniformly bounded**: there exists $M$ such that $|f(x)| \\le M$ for all $f \\in \\mathcal{F}$, $x \\in X$;\n2. **Equicontinuous**: for every $\\forall \\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $\\delta > 0$ such that $d(x, y) < \\delta$ implies $|f(x) - f(y)| < \\varepsilon$ for **all** $f \\in \\mathcal{F}$ simultaneously.\n\nThen $\\mathcal{F}$ is **relatively compact** in $C(X)$: every sequence in $\\mathcal{F}$ has a subsequence converging in the sup norm.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dc9be25c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (diagonal argument)**: since $X$ is compact, $\\implies$ one may take a countable dense set $\\{x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots\\}$. By uniform boundedness, $\\{f_{n}(x_{1})\\}$ has a convergent subsequence; from it choose a subsequence for which $\\{f_{n}(x_{2})\\}$ converges; continuing layer by layer and taking the diagonal $g_{k} := f_{n_{k}}$, we get that $g_{k}(x_{j})$ converges for every $j$.\n\n**Upgrading pointwise convergence to uniform convergence**: given $\\varepsilon$, use equicontinuity to choose $\\delta$; cover $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{N}$ by finitely many $\\delta$-balls $X$.\n\nFirst take $K$ such that $j \\le N$ implies $|g_{k}(x_{j}) - g_{l}(x_{j})| < \\varepsilon$ ($k, l \\ge K$), then for arbitrary $x$ choose the nearest $x_{j}$ and use equicontinuity to bound $|g_{k}(x) - g_{l}(x)|$ by $3\\varepsilon$.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **Each hypothesis governs one thing**: boundedness guarantees \"at each point there is a convergent subsequence\", while equicontinuity guarantees \"the same subsequence converges at all points simultaneously\". Dropping either one gives a counterexample ($f_{n}(x) = x^{n}$ is bounded but not equicontinuous; $f_{n}(x) = n$ is equicontinuous but not uniformly bounded).\n\n⚠️ The graph attaches this to complex analysis because Montel's theorem is exactly it plus Cauchy's inequality: for holomorphic functions, **uniform boundedness automatically gives equicontinuity** (through bounds on the derivative) — a convenience unique to complex analysis.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dc242eaa',
      },
    },

    'thm.montel': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{F}$ be a family of holomorphic functions on a domain $\\Omega$ that is **locally uniformly bounded**: every point has a neighbourhood on which the family is uniformly bounded (equivalently: uniformly bounded on every compact set $K \\subset \\Omega$). Then $\\mathcal{F}$ is a **normal family**: every sequence $f_{n} \\in \\mathcal{F}$ has a subsequence converging uniformly on compact subsets (with holomorphic limit).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8b89fef9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: take a compact exhaustion $K_{1} \\subset K_{2} \\subset \\cdots$, $\\bigcup K_{j} = \\Omega$. On $K_{j}$, by the **Cauchy inequality**: if $|f| \\le M_{j}$ throughout a neighbourhood of $K_{j}$ of radius $r$, then $|f'| \\le M_{j}/r$ on $K_{j}$ — the bound is independent of $f$, so $\\mathcal{F}$ is **equicontinuous** on $K_{j}$.\n\nUniform boundedness is supplied by hypothesis, so `thm.arzela-ascoli` gives a convergent subsequence on $K_{j}$;\n\ntaking diagonals successively over $j = 1, 2, \\ldots$ yields a subsequence converging uniformly on **every** $K_{j}$, i.e. uniformly on compact subsets.\n\n**The limit is holomorphic**: by `thm.weierstrass-convergence`. ∎\n\n⭐ **Relation to compactness**: Montel says \"$\\mathcal{F}$ is relatively compact\", so every **continuous** functional (e.g. $f \\mapsto |f'(z_{0})|$) attains its maximum on a bounded closed family. The whole strength of the Riemann mapping theorem comes from this one sentence: among the family with \"univalent + $f(z_{0}) = 0$ + $f'(z_{0}) > 0$\", take the one maximising $f'(z_{0})$, then prove it must map onto the disc.\n\n⚠️ \"Locally uniformly bounded\" cannot be weakened to \"pointwise bounded\": $f_{n}(z) = n z$ is pointwise bounded on $\\mathbb{C}$ (constant at each point) but not uniformly bounded on any compact set, and has no locally uniformly convergent subsequence.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#399cea64',
      },
    },

    'prop.winding-integral': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gamma : [0,1] \\to \\mathbb{C}$ be a piecewise $C^{1}$ closed curve and $a \\notin \\gamma([0,1])$. Then\n\n$$n(\\gamma, a) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i} \\oint_{\\gamma} \\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{z - a},$$\n\nIn particular the right-hand side is **always an integer**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7fddbb28',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: first translate $a$ to $0$ (translation does not change the integral). Set $\\varphi(t) := \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{t} \\frac{\\gamma'(s)}{\\gamma(s)}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$; then $(\\gamma e^{-\\varphi})' = e^{-\\varphi}(\\gamma' - \\varphi'\\gamma) = 0$, so $\\gamma(t) e^{-\\varphi(t)}$ is constant;\n\nfrom $\\varphi(0) = 0$ we get $e^{\\varphi(t)} = \\gamma(t)/\\gamma(0)$.\n\nTake $t = 1$: since $\\gamma(1) = \\gamma(0)$, we have $e^{\\varphi(1)} = 1$, hence $\\varphi(1) \\in 2\\pi i \\mathbb{Z}$. Now $\\varphi(1) = \\oint_{\\gamma} \\mathrm{d}z/z$, so the integral divided by $2\\pi i$ is an integer; it varies continuously with $a$, hence is exactly the winding number (integer-valued + continuous $\\implies$ locally constant).\n\n⭐ **Meaning of the formula**: the winding number was originally the geometric phrase \"how many times the curve turns around the point\"; here it becomes an **integral**, so a \"geometric quantity\" can enter computations — the argument principle uses it to count zeros.\n\n⚠️ The value of the integral is independent of the parametrisation of $\\gamma$ and depends only on orientation: going around the other way changes the sign of the winding number.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#10496724',
      },
    },

    'prop.integral-linear': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f, g$ be integrable on $[a, b]$ and $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $f + g$ and $cf$ are integrable, and\n\n$$\\int_a^b (f + g) = \\int_a^b f + \\int_a^b g, \\qquad \\int_a^b cf = c\\int_a^b f$$\n\nMoreover, for $a < c < b$: $f$ is integrable on $[a, b]$ if and only if it is integrable on $[a, c]$ and $[c, b]$, in which case\n\n$$\\int_a^b f = \\int_a^c f + \\int_c^b f$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f367a82a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Additivity over intervals\" is the most practical property of the Riemann integral: it cuts a problem on a large interval into two halves — and it is also the basis for defining improper integrals later.\n\n⚠ The **product** of two integrable functions is integrable (amplitude estimate: $\\omega(fg) \\le M\\omega(g) + N\\omega(f)$ on each subinterval), but the **quotient** need not be: one must first ensure the denominator stays away from zero.\n\n⭐ Integrability of sums and of products both rest on the amplitude criterion: substitute $\\omega(f + g) \\le \\omega(f) + \\omega(g)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4b97a586',
      },
    },

    'thm.projection-subspace': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $H$ be a Hilbert space and $E \\subseteq H$ a **closed** subspace. Then\n\n1. $y = P_E(x) \\iff y \\in E$ and $x - y \\in E^\\perp$;\n2. $P_E$ is linear, and $\\|P_E\\| \\le 1$ (it is $1$ when $E \\ne 0$);\n3. Orthogonal decomposition $H = E \\oplus E^\\perp$, and $\\|x\\|^2 = \\|x - P_E x\\|^2 + \\|P_E x\\|^2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c978bb78',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: by the projection onto a closed convex set. For (1), the step \"$\\implies$\" replaces $z$ by $\\pm z$, kills the real part, and gives $\\operatorname{Re}\\langle x - y, z\\rangle \\le 0$ for all $z \\in E$, i.e. $x - y \\perp E$;\n\nthe step \"$\\Longleftarrow$\" uses $\\|x - z\\|^2 = \\|x - y\\|^2 + \\|y - z\\|^2 \\ge \\|x - y\\|^2$.\n\n(2) uses $\\alpha_1 x_1 + \\alpha_2 x_2 - \\alpha_1 P x_1 - \\alpha_2 P x_2 \\perp E$.\n\n(3) is Pythagoras.\n\n⚠️ The word \"closed\" in \"$E$ is closed\" cannot be dropped: for $E = $ the finitely supported sequences are dense in $\\ell^2$ but not closed; the distance is zero but not attained, so **no** nearest point in $E$ exists, and the orthogonal decomposition fails as well.\n\n⭐ This is one of the biggest differences between Hilbert spaces and general Banach spaces: in a general Banach space a closed subspace need not be complemented ($c_0 \\subset \\ell^\\infty$ is not), so the word \"projection\" cannot be used loosely in Banach spaces.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the orthogonal characterisation does not need completeness; the global decomposition needs existence of projections):\n- Nearest point $\\implies$ orthogonality: use variations in arbitrary real multiples of directions in the subspace; in the complex case one must also use purely imaginary multiples, so that vanishing of the real part forces the inner product to vanish.\n- $y\\in E$ and $x-y\\in E^\\perp\\implies y=P_E x$: the Pythagorean identity gives the unique minimiser directly, with no closedness or completeness required; the condition for the orthogonal complement itself is what the definition expands.\n- Every $x$ has an orthogonal decomposition: invoke `thm.projection-convex`, using completeness of the Hilbert space and closedness of $E$; linearity follows from uniqueness of the decomposition, and the norm bound from the Pythagorean identity.\n- Closedness cannot be removed: for $E=c_{00}\\subset\\ell^2$, the distance to $x\\in\\ell^2\\setminus c_{00}$ is zero but not attained, so no nearest point in $E$ exists.\n\n📌 The definition of \"orthogonal projection\" is in `def.orthogonal-projection`; this entry is about the statement that the image of the projection is exactly the closed subspace.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b7ef8e99',
      },
    },

    'thm.harmonic-mean-value': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the value of a harmonic function at a point equals its average over the circle centred at that point of any radius; it also equals the area average over the disc.\n\nLet $u$ be harmonic on $B(a, R)$, $0 < r < R$. Then\n\n$$u(a) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} u(a + r e^{i\\theta})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nand (area average)\n\n$$u(a) = \\frac{1}{\\pi r^2}\\iint_{B(a, r)} u(x, y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#abcb117b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof has only two steps: write $u$ as the real part of a holomorphic function $f$ (the disc is simply connected), then take real parts on both sides of the Cauchy integral formula $f(a) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(a + r e^{i\\theta})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\n⭐ The converse also holds: if $u$ is continuous and has the circle mean property, then $u$ is harmonic. So the \"mean property\" can serve as another definition of harmonic function — many books do exactly this, to avoid second-order differentiability.\n\n⚠ The mean must be taken over the **whole circle**, not just the few points straight above; nor over an arbitrary closed curve — that is precisely what the Poisson formula answers.\n\n⚠ $u$ need only be harmonic in the **interior** of $B(a, r)$; it is not required to be harmonic on the closed disc.\n\n📌 The mean property immediately yields the maximum principle (see the entry on the maximum principle for harmonic functions), and it is also the starting point for \"holomorphic functions are real-analytic\" (expand the integral into a power series).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f7ad2833',
      },
    },

    'def.chaus': {
      statement: {
        en: "**Compact Hausdorff spaces** are the topological spaces that are both compact and Hausdorff. Taking them as objects and continuous maps as morphisms gives the category $\\mathbf{CHaus}$. The inclusion functor is denoted $\\mathbf{CHaus} \\hookrightarrow \\mathbf{Top}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c0b291a1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Putting compactness and Hausdorffness together is a good bargain: **a compact Hausdorff space is regular and normal**, and a continuous bijection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space is automatically a homeomorphism. Many things that require extra hypotheses in $\\mathbf{Top}$ come for free here.\n\n**Arbitrary limits exist** in $\\mathbf{CHaus}$, and they are exactly the result computed in $\\mathbf{Top}$ (see the entry \"CHaus is a reflective subcategory\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a139660f',
      },
    },

    'def.sheaf': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{C}$ be a site and $F : \\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ a presheaf.\n\n- $F$ is called **separated** if for every $X$ and every $R \\in J(X)$, the restriction map\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}(h_{X}, F) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Hom}(R, F)$$\n\nis **injective**;\n- $F$ is called a **sheaf** if this map is **bijective**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#63e93e16',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By the Yoneda lemma $\\operatorname{Hom}(h_{X}, F) \\cong F(X)$, this definition can be read as two sentences: the left-hand side is $F(X)$ (global sections), while the right-hand side is \"a compatible family of sections along $R$\":\n\n- **Injective = separatedness**: two global sections that agree on $R$ must be equal;\n- **Bijective = gluing**: any compatible family of sections glues **uniquely** into a global section.\n\nIn the old language of topological spaces ($\\mathcal{C} = \\operatorname{Open}(X)^{\\mathrm{op}}$), these two conditions read: if $s|_{U_{i}} = t|_{U_{i}}$ holds everywhere then $s = t$; and local sections $s_{i}$ that agree on overlaps glue into a global section.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b8449802',
      },
    },

    'thm.long-exact': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category and $0 \\to K \\to L \\to M \\to 0$ a short exact sequence of complexes. Then there is a **long exact sequence**\n\n$$\\cdots \\to H^{n}(K) \\to H^{n}(L) \\to H^{n}(M) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\delta\\ } H^{n+1}(K) \\to \\cdots$$\n\nwhere $\\delta$ is called the **connecting homomorphism**.\n\nCorollary: $H^{n} : \\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{A}) \\to \\mathcal{A}$ is a **cohomology functor**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2c342631',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it says**: a short exact sequence of complexes $0 \\to K \\to L \\to M \\to 0$ yields a **long exact sequence** in cohomology, and the **connecting homomorphism** $\\delta : H^{n}(M) \\to H^{n+1}(K)$ in the middle raises the degree by one.\n\n⭐ How $\\delta$ is built: take a representative of $m \\in H^{n}(M)$, lift it along the surjection $L^{n} \\to M^{n}$, then apply $d_{L}$ and land in $K^{n+1}$ — one must show the result is independent of the choice of lift (two lifts differ by a coboundary), and this step is exactly **diagram chasing**.\n\n⚠ This is **not** the same as the long exact sequence of derived functors (the short exact sequence $\\to$ $R^{n}F$ gives a long exact sequence): that one concerns derived functors, this one concerns the cohomology of the complexes themselves.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#17fcb23f',
      },
    },

    'def.projective-object': {
      statement: {
        en: "An object $P$ of a category $\\mathcal{C}$ is called **projective** if the functor $h_{P} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(P, -)$ **preserves epimorphisms**. Dually, $I$ is called **injective** if $h^{I}$ sends monomorphisms to epimorphisms.\n\nExpanded as a lifting condition: for every epimorphism $A \\twoheadrightarrow B$ and every $P \\to B$ there exists a lift $P \\to A$ making the triangle commute:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc} & P & \\\\ \\swarrow & \\downarrow & \\searrow \\\\ A & \\twoheadrightarrow & B \\end{array}$$\n\nDually, injectivity of $I$ says that every $A \\rightarrowtail B$ and every $A \\to I$ extend to $B \\to I$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#36504573',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The name \"projective\" comes from module theory: the projective objects of $R\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ are exactly the projective modules, and the injective objects are exactly the injective modules — the two standard criteria ($P$ is a direct summand, $I$ is a direct summand) are this definition specialised to modules.\n\nTwo immediate corollaries: **if $P$ is projective, every epimorphism $Y \\twoheadrightarrow P$ has a section**; **if $X \\to Y$ has a retraction and $Y$ is projective, then $X$ is projective too** (the injective case is dual).\n\nIn $\\mathbf{Set}$ **every object is projective**, and this is precisely the axiom of choice: given a surjection $A \\twoheadrightarrow B$ and a point of $B$, one can always choose a preimage.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2c94ab1',
      },
    },

    'def.principal-curvature': {
      statement: {
        en: "The eigenvalues of $W$ (equivalently, of the symmetric matrix $(h_{ij})$) are called the **principal curvatures**, denoted $k_{1} \\ge k_{2}$; the corresponding unit eigen-directions are called **principal directions**. They are given by the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\\det(h_{ij}-k\\,g_{ij}) = 0,\\qquad \\det(W-k\\,\\mathrm{id}) = 0 .$$\n\nIn an orthonormal frame $g_{ij} = \\delta_{ij}$, the characteristic polynomial is $k^{2}-2Hk+K$, where $H = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tr}W$ and $K = \\det W$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#48b0488f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why eigenvalues**: $k_{n}$ is the Rayleigh quotient of $W$; extremal points on the unit circle satisfy $II(v,\\cdot) = k\\,I(v,\\cdot)$, i.e. $W(\\mathrm{d}r(v)) = k\\,\\mathrm{d}r(v)$ (Lagrange multipliers), and the extremal values are the eigenvalues — this is exactly the statement that \"the directions of extremal value are eigen-directions of $W$\".\n\n**Principal directions are orthogonal**: eigenvectors of a real symmetric matrix belonging to distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal (spectral theorem). The same conclusion follows from the Rayleigh quotient together with variations; the treatment here uses the spectral theorem directly.\n\n**Two characteristic equations**: $\\det(W-kI) = 0$ and $\\det(h-kg) = 0$ agree in an orthonormal frame; in a parameter net $\\{r_{u}, r_{v}\\}$ one must carry $g = \\begin{pmatrix} E & F \\\\ F & G \\end{pmatrix}$, so $g$ cannot be omitted.\n\n⚠️ $k_{1}, k_{2}$ depends on the choice of $n$ (all signs change under $n \\mapsto -n$), but \"$k_{1} = k_{2}$\" (umbilic point) and $K = k_{1}k_{2}$ are independent of the choice.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f6ba02ef',
      },
    },

    'thm.solvable-quotients': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group.\n\n**(a) Subgroups**: if $G$ is solvable and $H \\le G$, then $H$ is solvable.\n\n**(b) Quotients**: if $G$ is solvable and $N \\trianglelefteq G$, then $G/N$ is solvable.\n\n**(c) Extensions**: if $N \\trianglelefteq G$, $N$ is solvable and $G/N$ is solvable, then $G$ is solvable.\n\n((a) and (b) say that solvability descends; (c) says that solvability ascends along normal extensions.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0685f884',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ (c) is the most useful of the three: to decide that $G$ is solvable, it suffices to find a **normal subgroup** with $G$ squeezed in between, both ends solvable — the standard way of breaking a big group into small pieces.\n\n⚠ The converses of (a) and (b) **fail**, with tiny counterexamples:\n- A solvable subgroup **does not** imply a solvable group: $\\mathbb{Z}/2 \\le A_5$ is solvable, $A_5$ is not;\n\n\n- A solvable quotient **does not** imply a solvable group: $S_5 / A_5 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2$ is solvable, $S_5$ is not.\n\n\nSo \"solvable\" is not a local property; it propagates upwards only by the two-sided squeeze of (c).\n\n⭐ In terms of derived series all three are almost one line: $H^{(k)} \\le G^{(k)}$;\n\n$\\pi(G^{(k)}) = (G/N)^{(k)}$.\n\nThe full proofs are on the edges.\n\n⭐ One immediate useful criterion (**finite groups only**): $G$ finite and solvable $\\iff$ there is a normal series with **cyclic** quotients (not merely abelian) — a finite abelian group can be refined further into cyclic factors of prime order. ⚠️ For infinite groups \"finite\" cannot be dropped: $\\mathbb{Q}$ is solvable as an additive group, but all its finite quotients are trivial and none is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{Z}$, so there is no normal series with cyclic quotients ending in $1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a1297b6c'
      },
    },

    'def.analytic-ring': {
      statement: {
        en: "A pre-analytic ring $\\mathcal{A}$ is called an **analytic ring** if and only if: for every complex\n\n$$C : \\cdots \\to C_i \\to \\cdots \\to C_1 \\to C_0 \\to 0$$\n\n(where each $C_i$ is a direct sum of copies of $\\mathcal{A}[T]$, with $T$ extremally disconnected), and for every extremally disconnected set $S$, the natural map\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathcal{A}}\\bigl(\\mathcal{A}[S],\\ C\\bigr) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{RHom}_{A}\\bigl(A[S],\\ C\\bigr)$$\n\nis an isomorphism. Here \"natural map\" means **one concrete map**, not a figure of speech: it goes from the $\\mathcal{A}$ side to the $A$ side and is induced by composing along $\\iota_S$. Three steps make this precise:\n\n1. **First there is a canonical map** $\\iota_S : A[S] \\to \\mathcal{A}[S]$ — it sends a formal finite sum $\\sum a_s s$ into the free complete module (the data of a pre-analytic ring includes this family of maps; see @@def.pre-analytic-ring@@).\n2. **Pull back along it**: $\\operatorname{RHom}_A(-, C)$ is a contravariant functor, and $\\iota_S$ gives $\\operatorname{RHom}_A(\\mathcal{A}[S], C) \\to \\operatorname{RHom}_A(A[S], C)$, by the rule $g \\mapsto g \\circ \\iota_S$.\n3. **Rewrite the left-hand side (this step is unconditional, and not circular)**: $C$ is already a complex of $\\mathcal{A}$-modules (each term is a direct sum of copies of $\\mathcal{A}[T]$), and $\\mathcal{A}[S] = A[S]\\otimes_A \\mathcal{A}$ is a **free** $\\mathcal{A}$-module — an $\\mathcal{A}$-linear map is determined by the images of the generators $s\\in S$, so both sides are canonically isomorphic to $C^{S}$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathcal{A}}(\\mathcal{A}[S], C) \\cong \\operatorname{RHom}_A(\\mathcal{A}[S], C) \\cong \\operatorname{RHom}_A(A[S], C),$$\n\nBoth isomorphisms hold term by term ($\\operatorname{Hom}$ takes $C^{S}$ term by term), hence also at the derived level. Thus the comparison map in the definition is the map induced by $\\iota_S$, namely $\\operatorname{RHom}_A(\\mathcal{A}[S], C) \\to \\operatorname{RHom}_A(A[S], C)$ — **the whole definition uses only the underlying $A$-modules and never the subcategory of complete modules being defined**, so there is no circularity.\n\nPutting 2 and 3 together gives the arrow $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathcal{A}}(\\mathcal{A}[S], C) \\to \\operatorname{RHom}_A(A[S], C)$ appearing in the formula.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fd3ef658',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ How to read it: **\"$\\mathcal{A}[S]$ and the ordinary free module $A[S]$ cannot be distinguished inside $\\operatorname{RHom}$\"**.\n\nThis is the relative version of condition (ii) in the definition of solid groups; taking $A = \\mathbb{Z}$ and $\\mathcal{A}[S] = Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}$ returns the solid case.\n\n⚠ Why demand **all** such complexes rather than a single module? Because the conclusion is at the level of derived categories (`thm.analytic-modules` (ii)); an isomorphism at one point is not enough, one needs it for complexes as well.\n\n⭐ Deciding \"is this an analytic ring\" is usually done via concrete theorems such as `prop.analytic-examples` and `thm.za-analytic`, not by checking the definition item by item.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (analyticity is a derived test on a whole class of complexes):\n- Analytic $\\iff$ the comparison maps for all the listed complexes and for extremally disconnected $S$ are quasi-isomorphisms: this is the definition unfolded, with the pre-analytic ring already given.\n- The canonical map $A[S]\\to\\mathcal A[S]$ $\\implies$ the contravariant derived Hom comparison: uses functoriality of precomposition, and does not presuppose analyticity.\n- Testing only the ordinary Hom in degree zero $\\implies$ the derived comparison need not be an isomorphism; ordinary Hom sees no higher Ext and cannot replace the condition on complexes.\n- Identifying $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathcal A}(\\mathcal A[S],C)$ with $\\operatorname{RHom}_A(\\mathcal A[S],C)$ **does not require** the derived category of complete modules: $C$ is a complex of $\\mathcal A$-modules and $\\mathcal A[S]$ is free, so both sides are canonically isomorphic to $C^{S}$ degree by degree, hence also at the derived level. The definition can therefore be stated entirely in the derived category of modules over the underlying $A$, without circularity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7f4301ec'
      },
    },

    'prop.analytic-base-change': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathcal{B}$ be a map between analytic rings (that is, a map of condensed rings such that each $\\mathcal{B}[S]$ lies in $\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{A}}$ as an $A$-module). Then:\n\n**(i)** the composite functor $\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{A} \\xrightarrow{-\\otimes_{A} B} \\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{B} \\xrightarrow{-\\otimes_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathcal{B}} \\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{B}}$ factors through $\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{A}}$, written\n\n$$M \\longmapsto M \\otimes_{\\mathcal{A}} \\mathcal{B} : \\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{A}} \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{B}}.$$\n\n**(ii)** the derived version $C \\mapsto C \\otimes^{L}_{\\mathcal{A}} \\mathcal{B}$ is its left derived functor.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#acbea9f7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This makes $\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{A}}$ functorial in $\\mathcal{A}$: changing the analytic ring is base change.\n\n⭐ The proof is short: by the definition of the map, $\\mathcal{B}[S]$ already lies in $\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{A}}$, and these are compact projective generators, so all of $\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{cond}}_{\\mathcal{B}}$ lands inside.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#253a9a90',
      },
    },

    'prop.analytic-examples': {
      statement: {
        en: "The following pre-analytic rings are analytic rings:\n\n**(i)** $\\mathbb{Z}_{p,\\blacksquare}$ (the $p$-adic solid ring);\n\n**(ii)** for any discrete ring $A$, $(A, \\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare$, whose free module is\n\n$$(A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare[S] = Z[S]^{\\blacksquare} \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} A.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c03b9c1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ (i) and (ii) in fact share one proof: in both cases one has\n\n$$\\mathcal{A}[S] = \\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare[S] \\otimes^{L}_{\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare} \\mathcal{A},$$\n\nand $C$ is, in $D(\\mathbf{Solid})$, an $\\mathcal{A}$-module, so\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare[S], C) \\cong \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathcal{A}}(\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare[S] \\otimes^{L}_{\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare} \\mathcal{A},\\ C),$$\n\nThe left-hand side is $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z}[S], C)$ ($C$ solid), and the right-hand side is $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathcal{A}}(\\mathcal{A}[S], C)$.\n\n⚠ $A$ must be **discrete**: for a general condensed ring the claim fails, which is also why `thm.za-analytic` needs \"finitely generated $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebra\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bfcc3465',
      },
    },

    'thm.coherent-duality': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a finitely generated $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebra and $f : \\operatorname{Spec} A \\to \\operatorname{Spec} \\mathbb{Z}$ the structure map (projecting the spectrum of the coefficient ring down). Define\n\n$$f_! : D(A_\\blacksquare) \\xrightarrow{j_!} D((A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare) \\longrightarrow D(\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare).$$\n\nThen $f_!$ preserves all direct sums and preserves compact objects, and satisfies the **projection formula**\n\n$$f_!\\bigl((M \\otimes^{L}_{\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare} A_\\blacksquare) \\otimes^{L}_{A_\\blacksquare} N\\bigr) \\cong M \\otimes^{L}_{\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare} f_! N,$$\n\nwhere $M \\in D(\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare)$ and $N \\in D(A_\\blacksquare)$. The functor $f_!$ has a right adjoint\n\n$$f^! : D(\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare) \\longrightarrow D(A_\\blacksquare), \\qquad f^! M = (M \\otimes^{L}_{\\mathbb{Z}_\\blacksquare} A_\\blacksquare) \\otimes^{L}_{A_\\blacksquare} f^! \\mathbb{Z},$$\n\nwhich preserves direct sums; $f^! \\mathbb{Z} \\in D(A_\\blacksquare)$ is a bounded complex of finitely generated discrete $A$-modules.\n\nIf $f$ is a **complete intersection** (locally $A$ is obtained from $R$ by modding out a regular sequence, i.e. \"the cut is clean and there are no superfluous relations\"), then $f^!\\mathbb{Z}$ is invertible in $D(A)$ (locally it is a line bundle in some degree).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1d53a34c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the \"affine model\" of Grothendieck's coherent duality: $f_!$ is **cohomology with compact support**, $f^!$ is the **dualising complex**, and both the projection formula and Serre duality follow formally from the adjunction.\n\n⭐ Difference from the classical approach: in classical coherent duality, $f_!$ in the affine case can only be manufactured artificially out of \"formal functions\";\n\nhere $f_!$ is a direct consequence of the adjunction pair ($j_! \\dashv j^*$), and it **does not preserve discrete objects** — exactly the gain of the condensed method.\n\n⚠ The identification of $f^!\\mathbb{Z}$ with the classical dualising complex $\\omega_A$ must be proved in the framework of `thm.za-analytic` (Lecture XI of the original).\n\n⚠ This conclusion is conditional on `thm.za-analytic` (still a skeleton); no complete proof is given here.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8cbbb3c4',
      },
    },

    'thm.za-analytic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a finitely generated $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebra and $A_\\blacksquare$ the pre-analytic ring whose free module is\n\n$$A_\\blacksquare[S] := \\varprojlim_i A[S_i] \\qquad (S = \\varprojlim_i S_i)$$\n\n(that is, the $A$-valued measure $M(S,A)$). Then $A_\\blacksquare$ is an analytic ring, and the forgetful functor\n\n$$j^* : D(A_\\blacksquare) \\hookrightarrow D((A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare)$$\n\nhas a left adjoint $j^* = -\\otimes^{L}_{(A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare} A_\\blacksquare$, which in turn has a left adjoint\n\n$$j_! : D(A_\\blacksquare) \\longrightarrow D((A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare),$$\n\nsatisfying $j_! j^* M = M \\otimes_{(A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare} j_! A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dec0a246',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is Clausen–Scholze, Lectures on Condensed Mathematics, Lecture VIII, Theorem 8.1. It is the first analytic ring in the graph that covers a genuine geometric object: for $A=\\mathbb{Z}[T]$, $A_\\blacksquare$ is the coefficient ring of \"solid modules over $\\mathbb{Z}[T]$\", and the coherent duality that follows (`thm.coherent-duality`) rests entirely on it.\n\n**The real size of the proof.** The full proof is the content of the first few sections of Lecture VIII and cannot fit into a single edge. The core route for $A=\\mathbb{Z}[T]$ is:\n\n① Set $A_\\infty:=\\mathbb{Z}((T^{-1}))$ and view it as \"functions on $\\operatorname{Spec} A$ at infinity\"; there is a short exact sequence\n\n$$0\\to \\mathbb{Z}[[U]]\\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} A \\xrightarrow{1-\\mathrm{shift}\\otimes T} \\mathbb{Z}[[U]]\\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} A \\to A_\\infty \\to 0 .$$\n\n② By ①, $A_\\infty$ is compact projective (the first two terms are of the form $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}\\otimes A$), and idempotent: $A_\\infty\\otimes^{L}_{A}A_\\infty\\cong A_\\infty$.\n\n③ By ②, $\\operatorname{RHom}_A(A_\\infty,C)=0$, where $C$ is built from direct sums of $\\prod_I A$.\n\n④ Analyticity: the category of solid modules over $A_\\blacksquare$ corresponds to the subcategory of complexes satisfying $\\operatorname{RHom}_A(A_\\infty,C)=0$ (this is the working form of the definition of an analytic ring).\n\n⑤ Existence of $j_!$: use $\\operatorname{coker}\\bigl(\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}\\otimes A\\to\\prod_I A\\bigr)$ to construct $j_!A$, then use presentability / the adjoint functor theorem to extend $j_!$ to the whole derived category.\n\n⑥ General finitely generated $A$: by Noether normalisation find a polynomial ring $R=\\mathbb{Z}[X_1,\\ldots,X_n]$ and a finite injection $R\\to A$, and identify $A_\\blacksquare$ with $(A,R)_\\blacksquare$ at the derived level.\n\n⑦ The projection formula $j_!j^*M\\cong M\\otimes_{A_\\blacksquare}j_!A$ follows formally from $j_!\\dashv j^*$.\n\n**Gaps in the graph (item by item; the node therefore remains skeleton).**\n\n(i) Foundations: the definitions and functoriality of $D((A,\\mathbb{Z})_\\blacksquare)$ and $A_\\blacksquare$ are only cited in the graph (`def.pre-analytic-ring`, `def.analytic-ring`), not proved item by item.\n\n(ii) Compact projectivity in ② needs the sentence \"$\\prod_I\\mathbb{Z}\\cong\\mathbb{Z}[\\beta I]$, with $\\beta I$ extremally disconnected compact, hence $\\mathbb{Z}[\\beta I]$ is compact projective\"; the graph has no node for \"compact projective generator / $\\beta I$\".\n\n(iii) Idempotence in ②: the edge says \"compute directly\", but the required derived completion and the derived Sant lemma are not developed.\n\n(iv) The \"acyclicity\" of the two-term complex in ③ requires the middle map $f$ to be an **isomorphism**, not merely injective; the edge flags this but does not prove it.\n\n(v) Existence of $j_!$ in ⑤ relies on presentable functors / the adjoint functor theorem; the graph has no node for this.\n\n(vi) Noether normalisation in ⑥ only gives the idea: what must be proved is that $A_\\blacksquare$ and $(A,R)_\\blacksquare$ agree at the level of derived categories, not a one-line \"reduction\".\n\n(vii) The projection formula in ⑦ requires ⑤ to be completed first.\n\n⚠ Thus this node is still marked skeleton. The edge `imp.za-analytic` is a **road map plus key computations**, not a verbatim proof; do not cite it as an established fact.\n\n⭐ Relation to geometry: for $A=\\mathbb{Z}[T]$, $A_\\blacksquare$ corresponds to a kind of \"neighbourhood at infinity\" of $\\mathbb{A}^1$, with $A_\\infty$ the functions on its boundary; this theorem is the entry point for \"making the derived completion of an affine scheme into an analytic ring\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b39022e6',
      },
    },

    'thm.hahn-banach': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a **real** vector space, $p : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ **sublinear** ($p(x + y) \\le p(x) + p(y)$, and $p(tx) = t\\,p(x)$ for $t \\ge 0$), $M \\subseteq X$ a subspace, and $f : M \\to \\mathbb{R}$ linear with $f \\le p$ on $M$. Then there exists a linear $F : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that $F|_M = f$ and $F \\le p$.\n\n**Norm-preserving extension (corollary)**: let $X$ be a normed space, $M \\subseteq X$ a subspace, $\\varphi \\in M^{*}$. Then there exists $\\Phi \\in X^{*}$ such that $\\Phi|_M = \\varphi$ and $\\|\\Phi\\| = \\|\\varphi\\|$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4205eb81',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Step 1: the one-dimensional extension lemma.** Let $x_0 \\notin M$, $N = M \\oplus \\mathbb{R}x_0$. Defining, on $N$, a map $g(m + t x_0) = f(m) + t c$ that preserves $g \\le p$ is equivalent to having, for every $m \\in M$,\n\n$f(m) + c \\le p(m + x_0), \\qquad f(m) - c \\le p(m - x_0).$\n\ni.e. $c$ lies in the interval $[\\sup_m (f(m) - p(m - x_0)),\\ \\inf_m (p(m + x_0) - f(m))]$. This interval is non-empty, because for any $m_1, m_2 \\in M$, sublinearity gives\n\n$f(m_1) + f(m_2) = f(m_1 + m_2) \\le p(m_1 + m_2) \\le p(m_1 - x_0) + p(m_2 + x_0),$\n\ni.e. $f(m_1) - p(m_1 - x_0) \\le p(m_2 + x_0) - f(m_2)$. Choose any $c$ in the interval; the extension then works.\n\n**Step 2: Zorn.** Consider all objects \"$g$ defined on a subspace $N \\supseteq M$, linear, $g|_M = f$, and $g \\le p$ on $N$\", ordered by extension ($g_1 \\preceq g_2$ when $g_2$ has larger domain and agrees on $g_1$ with $g_1$). The union of a totally ordered subfamily is again such an object (domains are unioned; linearity and $\\le p$ are checked pointwise), so `lem.zorn` gives a maximal element $F$. If $\\operatorname{dom} F \\ne X$, take $x_0 \\notin \\operatorname{dom} F$ and extend again by Step 1, contradicting maximality. Hence $F$ is defined on all of $X$. $\\blacksquare$\n\n**Step 3: norm-preserving extension.** Take $p(x) = \\|\\varphi\\| \\cdot \\|x\\|$ (sublinear) and $f = \\varphi$; then $\\varphi \\le p$ on $M$. This gives a linear $\\Phi$ with $\\Phi|_M = \\varphi$ and $\\Phi \\le p$ on $X$. Applying $\\Phi \\le p$ to $x$ and to $-x$ gives $|\\Phi(x)| \\le \\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|x\\|$, hence $\\|\\Phi\\| \\le \\|\\varphi\\|$; the reverse inequality comes from the extension, so equality holds. $\\blacksquare$\n\n**The complex case**: regard $\\varphi$ as a real-linear functional, extend it, then set $\\Phi_{\\mathbb C}(x) = \\Psi(x) - i\\,\\Psi(ix)$; one checks that it is complex-linear and norm-preserving.\n\n📌 This entry proves only the \"sublinear domination\" form; the geometric form (separation of a point from a closed convex set) is derived from it and used in the notes to `thm.goldstine`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#621163ee',
      },
    },

    'def.bounded-operator': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $u : E \\to F$ be linear. Call $u$ **bounded** if there exists $C$ such that $\\|u(x)\\| \\le C\\|x\\|$ for all $x$. The whole collection is denoted $B(E, F)$, and one writes $B(E) := B(E, E)$. Define\n\n$$\\|u\\| := \\sup_{\\|x\\| \\le 1} \\|u(x)\\| = \\sup_{\\|x\\| = 1} \\|u(x)\\| = \\sup_{x \\ne 0} \\frac{\\|u(x)\\|}{\\|x\\|} ,$$\n\nThis makes $B(E, F)$ a normed space (with the convention $\\|u\\| = 0$ when $E = 0$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4acdc3ed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ **The name is a historical misunderstanding**: \"bounded\" here does **not** mean \"the function is bounded\": a linear map bounded as a function $\\iff$ it is the zero map (the zero map satisfies this for any $E \\ne 0$). The real meaning is \"sends bounded sets to bounded sets\", which is equivalent to **continuity** (next entry). Do not be misled by the name when reading old books.\n\n⚠️ The three sups are equal only when $E \\ne 0$ (otherwise the sphere is empty and the sup is meaningless).\n\n⭐ When $E$ is infinite-dimensional and $F \\ne \\{0\\}$, one has $L(E, F) \\ne B(E, F)$: discontinuous linear operators really do exist (constructed using the axiom of choice). This is why the graph separates \"linear\" and \"bounded\" into two nodes: $L(E,F)$ is the **algebraic** Hom, and $B(E,F)$ is the continuous part of it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dba96e03',
      },
    },

    'lem.countable-closed-cover': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a Baire space, $F_n \\subseteq X$ closed, and $X = \\bigcup_n F_n$. Then $\\bigcup_n \\operatorname{int} F_n$ is **dense** in $X$. In particular, if $X \\ne \\emptyset$, then at least one $F_n$ has non-empty interior (the empty space satisfies the Baire hypothesis, but every $F_n$ has empty interior).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e132d7bc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**This is the \"open-set version\" of the Baire category theorem**: among the members of a closed cover, those with nonempty interior have interiors whose union is dense. The full proof (using Baire on a nonempty open set) is on the edge `imp.countable-closed-cover-proof`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fa78c81a'
      },
    },

    'def.equicontinuous': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a topological space, $(E, d)$ a metric space, and $\\mathcal{H} \\subseteq C(K, E)$.\n\n- $\\mathcal{H}$ is **equicontinuous at** $x_0 \\in K$: $\\forall \\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a neighbourhood $U$ of $x_0$ such that $d(f(x), f(x_0)) < \\varepsilon$ holds for **all** $f \\in \\mathcal{H}$ and $x \\in U$ simultaneously.\n- $\\mathcal{H}$ is equicontinuous on $K$: it is equicontinuous at every point $x \\in K$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c06cb1cf',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ The point is \"**for all $f$ simultaneously**\". Drop this condition and one only has \"each $f$ is continuous on its own\" — that is the definition of $\\mathcal{H} \\subseteq C(K,E)$ and says nothing new.\n\nWhen $\\mathcal{H} = \\{f\\}$, equicontinuity $\\iff$ $f$ being continuous.\n\n⭐ This is the **only non-trivial** hypothesis in compactness (Arzelà–Ascoli): pointwise total boundedness merely says \"does not diverge\", while equicontinuity says \"does not oscillate ever more wildly\".\n\n📌 When $K$ is a metric space there is also a stronger uniform version, see `def.uniformly-equicontinuous`; when $K$ is compact the two are equivalent (`thm.equicontinuous-uniform`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f19c49f6',
      },
    },

    'def.subalgebra-separating': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact Hausdorff and $A \\subseteq C(K, \\mathbb{K})$.\n\n- $A$ is a **subalgebra**: it is a vector space and is closed under multiplication;\n- $A$ **separates points**: $x \\ne y \\implies \\exists f \\in A$, $f(x) \\ne f(y)$;\n- $A$ is a **lattice**: closed under $\\min, \\max$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3810c039',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"Separates points\" is a necessary condition for Stone–Weierstrass: if two points are not separated, glue them into one, and $A$ cannot generate the functions on the glued space, $C$.\n\n⚠️ The example of self-adjoint matrices (from the lectures): self-adjoint matrices ordered by $A \\ge B \\iff A - B$ being positive semidefinite do not in general form a **lattice** (the pointwise min of two self-adjoint matrices need not be self-adjoint). So \"lattice\" is not automatic; it must be bought by \"closed + contains the unit\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bae06c67',
      },
    },

    'lem.closed-subalgebra-lattice': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact Hausdorff and $A \\subseteq C(K, \\mathbb{R})$ a **closed** subalgebra **containing the unit**. Then $A$ is closed under $\\min, \\max$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ed69a1e6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it suffices to prove $|f|\\in A$**: $\\max,\\min$ can be assembled from $|\\cdot|$ via the four arithmetic operations; $|f|=\\sqrt{f^{2}}$ is then obtained by uniform approximation with the binomial series. The full proof is on the edge `imp.subalgebra-lattice-proof`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#82b96fd8'
      },
    },

    'thm.bounded-operators-banach': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $E$ be normed and $F$ Banach. Then $(B(E, F), \\|\\cdot\\|)$ is a Banach space. In particular, the dual space $E^* = B(E, \\mathbb{K})$ is **always** Banach (regardless of whether $E$ is complete).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#467b6f7e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Sketch of proof**: define $u$ as the pointwise limit, then use uniform boundedness to control $\\lVert u_k-u\\rVert$. The full proof is on the edge `imp.bounded-operators-banach-proof`. In particular $E^*=B(E,\\mathbb K)$ is always a Banach space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#075e9753'
      },
    },

    'thm.norm-equiv-finite-dim': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\dim E < \\infty$. Then any two norms on $E$ are equivalent; equivalently, for any basis $e_1, \\ldots, e_n$ there exist $\\alpha, \\beta > 0$ such that\n\n$$\\alpha\\,\\|x\\|_\\infty \\le \\|x\\| \\le \\beta\\,\\|x\\|_\\infty , \\qquad \\|x\\|_\\infty := \\max_{1 \\le k \\le n} |x_k| .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0d22915d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why finite dimension is special**: the unit sphere is compact and the norm attains a positive minimum on it, so every norm is controlled in both directions by $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_\\infty$. The full proof is on the edge `imp.norm-equiv-finite-dim-proof`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ced4fc8c'
      },
    },

    'thm.polarization': {
      statement: {
        en: "In a real inner product space:\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle = \\tfrac{1}{4}\\bigl( \\|x + y\\|^2 - \\|x - y\\|^2 \\bigr) .$$\n\nIn a complex inner product space:\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle = \\tfrac{1}{4}\\sum_{k=0}^{3} i^k \\,\\| x + i^k y \\|^2 .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#35349119',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Use**: it recovers the inner product from the norm, so \"preserving the norm\" and \"preserving the inner product\" are equivalent in the real case; in the complex case one combines the four directions. The full derivation is on the edge `imp.polarization-proof`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#10f8ee38'
      },
    },

    'thm.riesz-ball-compact': {
      statement: {
        en: "For a normed space $E$, the closed unit ball $B_E = \\{x \\in E : \\|x\\| \\le 1\\}$ is compact $\\iff$ $\\dim E < \\infty$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7199b914',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"$\\Longleftarrow$\": in finite dimension all norms are equivalent (`thm.norm-equiv-finite-dim`), so compactness can be tested using the coordinate ball of $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$;\n\nthe coordinate ball $\\overline{B}^{\\infty}(0,1)$ is bounded and closed (in the real case it is $[-1,1]^{n}$; in the complex case it is the unit polydisc, regarded as a closed bounded subset of $\\mathbb{R}^{2n}$), and is compact by Heine–Borel (`cor.heine-borel`).\n\n∎\n\n\"$\\implies$\": if $\\dim E = \\infty$, use Riesz's lemma (`lem.almost-orthogonal`) to construct recursively unit vectors with $\\|e_n - e_m\\| \\ge 1/2$ ($n \\ne m$); such a sequence has no convergent subsequence, so the closed unit ball is not sequentially compact and hence not compact.\n\n⭐ This is one half of \"finite-dimensional if and only if locally compact\": finite-dimensional normed spaces are locally compact, while infinite-dimensional ones are **never** locally compact. It is also the precise version of \"bounded closed sets are compact\" holding only in finite dimension — Heine–Borel fails in infinite dimension.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2ae191e',
      },
    },

    'def.ck-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact Hausdorff and $(E, d)$ a metric space. On $C(K, E)$ define\n\n$$\\Delta(f, g) := \\sup_{x \\in K} d\\bigl(f(x), g(x)\\bigr) .$$\n\nWhen $E$ is normed this is the sup norm $\\|f\\| = \\sup_x \\|f(x)\\|$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4378ff9b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The supremum is attained (compactness plus continuity). This metric is the metric of \"uniform convergence\", not of \"pointwise convergence\" (the same convention as `def.uniform-convergence`).\n\n⭐ The notation allows an arbitrary metric space $E$, so the results in this cluster (completeness, Arzelà–Ascoli) are broader than the classical $\\mathbb{R}$-valued versions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#243acb6f',
      },
    },

    'def.convex-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a real vector space and $C \\subseteq V$. Call $C$ a **convex set** if for all $x, y \\in C$ and $t \\in [0,1]$,\n\n$$(1-t)x + ty \\in C.$$\n\nEquivalently: $C$ contains the whole segment between any two of its points.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#67fb4cdd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Convexity uses only the **affine structure**; no norm or inner product is needed. In infinite dimension it is the key hypothesis for \"existence of a minimiser\" (see `thm.projection-convex`).\n\n⚠ The empty set is convex under this definition (the condition \"for any two points\" holds vacuously); when non-emptiness is needed it is assumed separately in the theorem (as in the projection theorem).\n\n💡 Convex combinations, convex hulls and separation theorems all start here.\n\n📌 **A convex set is star-shaped at each of its points** (see `thm.convex-star-shaped-every-point`); for the empty set this universal statement holds vacuously, so there is no conflict with \"the empty set is not star-shaped\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0545677c',
      },
    },

    'def.norm-equivalence': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p, q$ be two norms on $E$. Call $p$ **stronger than** $q$ (written $q \\preceq p$) if there exists $C > 0$ such that\n\n$$q(x) \\le C\\,p(x) \\qquad \\forall x \\in E .$$\n\nIf each is stronger than the other, call $p$ and $q$ **equivalent**, written $p \\sim q$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#837a258e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "- (a) equivalence - (b) the same induced topology - (c) the same Cauchy sequences - (d) the same convergent sequences.\n\n⚠️ The relation is asymmetric, and its direction runs against intuition: a **strong** norm (larger $p$) has smaller balls, **more** open sets, harder convergence, and **fewer** Cauchy sequences. So \"passing to a stronger norm\" **removes** convergent objects — which is exactly why one chooses the weakest norm that suffices (e.g. the weak topology).\n\n⚠️ Equivalence preserves completeness and very little else: two equivalent norms give the same completeness (the same Cauchy sequences), but **extremal problems** can have different answers (the balls differ).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ad1c961',
      },
    },

    'def.orthonormal-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_i\\}_{i \\in I} \\subseteq H$.\n\n- **Orthogonal**: $f_i \\perp f_j$ ($i \\ne j$);\n- **Orthonormal**: orthogonal + $\\|f_i\\| = 1$;\n- **Complete**: $\\overline{\\operatorname{span}\\{f_i\\}} = H$;\n- **Orthonormal basis**: orthonormal + complete.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#787b63d2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ Completeness is not \"spanning $H$\" but \"the **closure** of the span is the whole space\": the algebraic span of $\\{e_n\\}$ in $\\ell^2$ consists of finitely supported sequences, which is dense but not equal to $\\ell^2$.\n\n⚠️ In infinite dimension **uncountable orthonormal bases really occur**: the Hermite functions in $L^2(\\mathbb{R})$ are countable, but the orthonormal basis of a general $\\ell^2(I)$ is $\\{e_i\\}_{i \\in I}$, and $I$ can be arbitrarily large.\n\n⭐ Once an orthonormal basis is available, a Hilbert space becomes \"$\\ell^2$ without weights\" (see Bessel/Parseval and \"separable Hilbert $\\cong \\ell^2$\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a1669ec2',
      },
    },

    'def.reflexive-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a Banach space and $J_X : X \\to X^{**}$ the natural embedding $J_X(x)(\\varphi) = \\varphi(x)$. Call $X$ **reflexive** if $J_X$ is surjective (hence an isometric isomorphism).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#41bb29e0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The natural embedding is always isometric (Hahn–Banach): $\\|J_X x\\| = \\sup_{\\|\\varphi\\| \\le 1}|\\varphi(x)| = \\|x\\|$. Hence reflexivity $\\iff$ every element of $X^{**}$ comes from $X$.\n\n**Examples**: Hilbert spaces; $L^p$ and $\\ell^p$ ($1 < p < \\infty$, see `cor.lp-reflexive`); finite-dimensional spaces.\n\n**Counterexamples**: $c_0$ ($c_0^{**} = \\ell^\\infty \\ne c_0$), $\\ell^1$ ($\\ell^{1**} = (\\ell^\\infty)^* \\supsetneq \\ell^1$), $C[0,1]$.\n\n⭐ The core use of reflexivity: bounded sequences have weakly convergent subsequences (Eberlein–Šmulian; the graph has no weak-topology node yet), which is the entry point to variational existence and to Milman–Pettis.\n\n📌 Relation to pairing language: $X$ is reflexive $\\iff$ both embeddings of the perfect pairing $(X, X^*)$ are surjective (`def.perfect-pairing`, `thm.riesz-perfect-pairing`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f7bd2e47',
      },
    },

    'thm.closed-graph': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $E, F$ be Banach spaces and $u : E \\to F$ linear. If the graph of $u$,\n\n$$G(u) := \\{(x, u(x)) : x \\in E\\}$$\n\nis **closed** in the product space $E \\times F$, then $u$ is bounded (hence continuous).\n\nUnwinding convergence in the product topology, the criterion is one sentence: for every sequence $x_n \\to x$, if $u(x_n) \\to y$, then necessarily $y = u(x)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#391c9ec3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** $E \\times F$ is complete (a product of two complete spaces is complete), and $G(u)$ is a closed subset of it, so $G(u)$ is itself a Banach space. The two projections\n\n$$\\pi_1 : G(u) \\to E,\\ (x, u(x)) \\mapsto x ; \\qquad \\pi_2 : G(u) \\to F,\\ (x, u(x)) \\mapsto u(x)$$\n\nare bounded linear maps ($\\|\\pi_1\\| \\le 1$, $\\|\\pi_2\\| \\le 1$), and $\\pi_1$ is a bijection (the graph of a linear map corresponds one-to-one with its domain). Apply the inverse mapping theorem to $\\pi_1$: $\\pi_1^{-1} : E \\to G(u)$ is bounded. Hence\n\n$$u = \\pi_2 \\circ \\pi_1^{-1}$$\n\nis bounded as a composite of two bounded maps. $\\square$\n\n**The practical value is that \"closed\" is easier to verify than \"bounded\".** To prove $u$ bounded directly one must estimate $\\|u x\\|$; to see that the graph is closed one only needs: take convergent $x_n \\to x$ and $u(x_n) \\to y$, and prove $y = u(x)$ — no norm estimates in between. This is the route by which distribution theory proves that differential operators and the Fourier transform are continuous.\n\n**Why linearity and completeness at both ends are needed.** \"Continuous $\\implies$ closed graph\" is elementary (the graph of a continuous map into a product is closed, for any map), so the two properties look similar; the whole difference lies in the converse.\n\n**Completeness.** Take $E = (C[0,1], \\|\\cdot\\|_1)$ (incomplete) and $F = (C[0,1], \\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty)$ (complete), with $u = \\mathrm{id}$. If $f_n \\to f$ in $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ and $f_n \\to g$ in $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$, then convergence in $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ implies convergence in $L^1$, while convergence in $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$ implies convergence in $L^1$, so $f = g$: the graph is closed. But $\\|u\\| = \\sup\\|f\\|_\\infty / \\|f\\|_1 = \\infty$ is infinite, so $u$ is not bounded. Thus **if either the domain or the target is incomplete, the conclusion fails**.\n\n**Linearity.** A nonlinear map can have a closed graph and be discontinuous: take $u : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with $u(x) = 1/x$ for $x \\ne 0$ and $u(0) = 0$. Its graph is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ (a hyperbola together with the origin), but $u$ is discontinuous at $0$.\n\n📌 **TFAE version**: boundedness is equivalent to closedness of the graph (for Banach spaces at both ends). The graph records two typical uses: `thm.lax-milgram` (coercivity gives a bounded two-sided inverse) and the criterion for complemented subspaces (a closed algebraic complement gives a bounded projection).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a5464e0c',
      },
    },

    'thm.equicontinuous-uniform': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(K, \\delta)$ be a compact metric space, $(E, d)$ a metric space, and $\\mathcal{H} \\subseteq C(K, E)$ equicontinuous. Then $\\mathcal{H}$ is **uniformly equicontinuous**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4d7b6a9f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: let $\\varepsilon > 0$. For each point $x$ choose $\\eta_x > 0$ such that $\\delta(y, x) < \\eta_x \\implies d(f(y), f(x)) < \\varepsilon/2$ (for every $f$).\n\nCompactness gives a finite cover $K = \\bigcup_i B(x_i, \\eta_{x_i}/2)$, and we set $\\eta := \\min_i \\eta_{x_i}/2$.\n\nIf $\\delta(x, y) < \\eta$, choose $i$ with $x \\in B(x_i, \\eta_{x_i}/2)$ (hence $y \\in B(x_i, \\eta_{x_i})$), so $d(f(x), f(y)) \\le d(f(x), f(x_i)) + d(f(x_i), f(y)) < \\varepsilon$.\n\n⭐ The lecture notes use a **Lebesgue number** $\\lambda$ instead ($\\delta(x,y) < \\lambda$ puts a pair inside some member of the cover). The two formulations are equivalent; the advantage of the Lebesgue-number version is that $\\lambda$ depends only on the cover.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26562c82',
      },
    },

    'thm.open-mapping-banach': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $E, F$ be Banach spaces and $u \\in B(E, F)$ **surjective**. Then $u$ is an open map: the image of every open set is open.\n\nBy linearity of $u$, this is equivalent to \"the image of some neighbourhood of $0$ is a neighbourhood of $0$\", that is, there exists $r > 0$ such that\n\n$$u(B_E) \\supseteq r\\, B_F ,$$\n\nwhere $B_E, B_F$ are the closed unit balls. A weaker hypothesis also suffices: it is enough that $u(E)$ is of the second category in $F$ (automatic when $F$ is complete and the map is surjective, which is why one usually states surjectivity).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#836309c2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Two steps: first use the category theorem to fatten the image until it contains a ball (only completeness of $F$ is used here), then use completeness of $E$ to remove the closure.\n\n**Step 1: $\\overline{u(B_E)}$ contains a neighbourhood of $0$.** Since $E = \\bigcup_{k \\ge 1} k\\,B_E$, applying $u$ gives\n$$F = u(E) = \\bigcup_{k \\ge 1} u(k B_E) \\subseteq \\bigcup_{k \\ge 1} \\overline{u(k B_E)} .$$\nIf every $\\overline{u(k B_E)}$ were nowhere dense, $F$ would be a countable union of nowhere dense sets, contradicting \"$F$ complete $\\implies$ second category\". Hence for some $k$, $\\overline{u(k B_E)}$ has interior points: there exist $y_0$ and $\\delta > 0$ with $B_F(y_0, \\delta) \\subseteq \\overline{u(k B_E)}$.\n\nMove this point back to the origin, in two small steps: **translation** and **subtraction**. Take $x_0 \\in k B_E$ with $\\|y_0 - u(x_0)\\| < \\delta/2$ (possible since $y_0$ lies in the closure). **Translation**: for $\\|z\\| < \\delta/2$ we have $\\|z + (u(x_0) - y_0)\\| \\le \\|z\\| + \\|u(x_0) - y_0\\| < \\delta/2 + \\delta/2 = \\delta$, hence $u(x_0) + z = y_0 + \\bigl(z + u(x_0) - y_0\\bigr) \\in B_F(y_0, \\delta) \\subseteq \\overline{u(kB_E)}$, that is, $B_F(u(x_0), \\delta/2) \\subseteq \\overline{u(kB_E)}$. **Subtraction**: from $u(x_0) + z \\in \\overline{u(kB_E)}$ we get $z = \\bigl(u(x_0)+z\\bigr) - u(x_0) \\in \\overline{u(kB_E)} - u(x_0) \\subseteq \\overline{u(kB_E) - u(x_0)} = \\overline{u(kB_E - x_0)}$, and $x_0 \\in kB_E$ gives $kB_E - x_0 \\subseteq 2k\\,B_E$, so $z \\in \\overline{u(2k\\,B_E)}$. Therefore\n$$\\overline{u(2k\\, B_E)} \\supseteq B_F(0, \\delta/2) ,$$\nand homogeneity ($u(2k\\,B_E) = 2k\\,u(B_E)$, dividing both sides by $2k$) gives: there exists $r = \\delta/(8k) > 0$ such that\n$$\\overline{u(B_E)} \\supseteq 2r\\, B_F .$$\n\n**Step 2: remove the closure.** We prove $u(B_E) \\supseteq r B_F$. Take $\\|y\\| < r$. By Step 1, $\\overline{u(B_E)} \\supseteq 2r B_F$, so $y \\in \\overline{u(B(0, 1/2))}$: there is $x_1$ with $\\|x_1\\| < 1/2$ and $\\|y - u(x_1)\\| < r/2$. Apply the same argument to $y - u(x_1)$ (norm $< r/2 < r$) to get $x_2$ with $\\|x_2\\| < 1/4$ and $\\|y - u(x_1) - u(x_2)\\| < r/4$. Continuing gives $(x_n)$ with\n$$\\|x_n\\| < 2^{-n}, \\qquad \\Bigl\\| y - u\\Bigl(\\sum_{j \\le n} x_j\\Bigr) \\Bigr\\| < r\\,2^{-n} .$$\nSince $\\sum \\|x_n\\| < 1$ and $E$ is **complete**, the series $x := \\sum_{n} x_n$ converges, with $\\|x\\| < 1$. Also $u$ is continuous, so $u(x) = \\lim_n u(\\sum_{j\\le n} x_j) = y$. Hence $y = u(x)$ and $\\|x\\| < 1$, that is, $y \\in u(B_E)$.\n\n**Step 3: finish.** Take any open set $\\Omega \\subseteq E$ and $x \\in \\Omega$; choose $\\rho > 0$ with $B(x, \\rho) \\subseteq \\Omega$. By homogeneity $u(B(x,\\rho)) = u(x) + \\rho\\, u(B_E) \\supseteq u(x) + \\rho r B_F$ is a neighbourhood of $u(x)$ contained in $u(\\Omega)$. Hence $u(\\Omega)$ is open. $\\square$\n\n**What each hypothesis does.** Completeness of $F$ is used only in Step 1 (for the category theorem); completeness of $E$ only in Step 2 (for convergence of the series). Both are indispensable: with incomplete target $F = (C[0,1], \\|\\cdot\\|_1)$ the identity map $(C[0,1], \\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty) \\to (C[0,1], \\|\\cdot\\|_1)$ is a continuous bijection but not open (here $E = (C[0,1], \\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty)$ is in fact complete) — this identity map is itself the counterexample with $F$ incomplete.\n\n**Relation to the closed graph theorem.** The removal of the closure in Step 2 is the real technical core; the closed graph theorem uses it in reverse (first prove $\\pi_1^{-1}$ bounded, then deduce that $u$ is bounded). Thus the two are not independent: **open mapping $\\implies$ inverse mapping $\\implies$ closed graph**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b19c645b',
      },
    },

    'thm.stone-weierstrass': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact Hausdorff and $A \\subseteq C(K, \\mathbb{R})$ a **subalgebra** containing the unit and **separating points**; write $A$ for it. Then $\\overline A = C(K, \\mathbb{R})$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5cf76449',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Three steps:\n\n① Take the closure: $\\overline A$ still contains the unit, separates points, and is a **lattice** because a closed unital subalgebra is a lattice.\n\n② **Two-point interpolation**: for $x \\ne y$ and target values $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, take $g \\in A$ with $g(x) \\ne g(y)$; solving the 2-by-2 linear system gives $f = a\\cdot 1 + b g \\in A$ with $f(x) = \\alpha$ and $f(y) = \\beta$.\n\n③ **Sandwich from above and below** (all performed inside $\\overline{A}$, which ① and ② have already certified to be a closed unital subalgebra, closed under $\\max,\\min$):\n\n- **Two points hit simultaneously**: given $x \\ne y$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$. By ② take $g_{xy} \\in \\overline{A}$ with $g_{xy}(x) = f(x)$ and $g_{xy}(y) = f(y)$.\n\n- **Fix $x$ and, for each $y$, take a finite $\\max$**: for each $y$, since $g_{xy}(y) = f(y) < f(y) + \\varepsilon$ and by continuity, there is an open neighbourhood $V_{y}$ of $y$ on which $g_{xy} < f + \\varepsilon$. Since $K$ is compact, finitely many $V_{y_1}, \\ldots, V_{y_n}$ cover $K$; put $h_{x} := \\max_{j} g_{xy_j} \\in \\overline{A}$. Then $h_{x} < f + \\varepsilon$ holds everywhere, and $h_{x}(x) = f(x) > f(x) - \\varepsilon$.\n\n- **Cover over $x$ and take a finite $\\max$**: put $U_{x} := \\{t \\in K : h_{x}(t) > f(t) - \\varepsilon\\}$, which is open and contains $x$. Finitely many $U_{x_1}, \\ldots, U_{x_m}$ cover $K$; put $k := \\max_{i} h_{x_i} \\in \\overline{A}$. Lower bound: every point lies in some $U_{x_j}$, so $k \\ge h_{x_j} > f - \\varepsilon$. Upper bound: each $h_{x_j}$ satisfies $h_{x_j} < f + \\varepsilon$ everywhere, and a finite $\\max$ still gives $k < f + \\varepsilon$. Together $f - \\varepsilon < k < f + \\varepsilon$, that is, $\\|k - f\\|_\\infty < \\varepsilon$.\n\nSince $\\varepsilon$ is arbitrary, $f \\in \\overline{A}$, hence $\\overline{A} = C(K)$.\n\n∎\n\n⚠️ None of the three hypotheses can be dropped: $A = \\{f : f(0) = 0\\}$ lacks the unit; $A = $ the constant functions lacks separation of points.\n\n⭐ This is the model of trading **algebraic** properties for **approximation** power: no smoothness, no countability, no special kernel is needed. The classical Weierstrass theorem (polynomial approximation) is the case $K = [a,b]$ and $A = $ the polynomials.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dbb6883c',
      },
    },

    'cor.ubp-pointwise-limit': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $E$ be a Banach space, $F$ a normed space, $u_n \\in B(E, F)$, and suppose that for every $x \\in E$ we have $u_n x \\to u x$. Then $u \\in B(E, F)$, and\n\n$$\\|u\\| \\le \\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} \\|u_n\\| .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8d39e16f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** First note that $u$ is linear: for $x, y \\in E$ and $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{K}$, linearity of $u_n$ and of limits give\n\n$$u(\\alpha x + \\beta y) = \\lim_n u_n(\\alpha x + \\beta y) = \\alpha \\lim_n u_n x + \\beta \\lim_n u_n y = \\alpha u x + \\beta u y .$$\n\n**Pointwise boundedness.** For each $x$, the convergent sequence $(u_n x)$ is bounded, so $M(x) := \\sup_n \\|u_n x\\| < \\infty$.\n\n**Uniform boundedness.** Apply the uniform boundedness principle to the family $\\{u_n\\} \\subseteq B(E, F)$ ($E$ complete, $F$ normed): $C := \\sup_n \\|u_n\\| < \\infty$.\n\n**Norm bound.** For any $x$,\n\n$$\\|u x\\| = \\lim_n \\|u_n x\\| = \\liminf_n \\|u_n x\\| \\le \\liminf_n \\bigl( \\|u_n\\| \\, \\|x\\| \\bigr) = \\bigl( \\liminf_n \\|u_n\\| \\bigr) \\|x\\| ,$$\n\nso $u$ is bounded with $\\|u\\| \\le \\liminf_n \\|u_n\\|$. $\\blacksquare$\n\n⭐ This upgrades \"pointwise convergence\" to \"a norm estimate for the limit operator\": one need not know in advance that $\\sup_n \\|u_n\\|$ is finite — the uniform boundedness principle supplies it. It is the usual tool in numerical analysis for deciding that \"the limit of a sequence of operators is again an operator\".\n\n⚠️ Only a bound on $\\liminf$ is available; it cannot be replaced by $\\limsup$: $\\|u\\|$ may be strictly smaller than $\\liminf \\|u_n\\|$ (for instance, take a sequence of operators that \"almost loses\" its norm in some direction; the pointwise limit drops that direction).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2c59d49',
      },
    },

    'cor.ubp-resonance': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $E$ be Banach, $F$ normed, $u_n \\in B(E, F)$, and suppose $\\sup_n \\|u_n\\| = \\infty$. Then\n\n$$R := \\Bigl\\{ x \\in E : \\sup_n \\|u_n(x)\\| = \\infty \\Bigr\\}$$\n\nis a **dense $G_\\delta$** (in particular non-empty).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9e130d99',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: put $\\Omega_k = \\{x : M(x) = \\sup_i\\|u_i x\\| > k\\} = \\bigcup_i \\{x : \\|u_i x\\| > k\\}$, an open set;\n\n$R = \\bigcap_k \\Omega_k$ is a $G_\\delta$.\n\nIf some $\\Omega_k$ were not dense, there would be a ball $B(x_0, r) \\subseteq \\Omega_k^c$ on which $M \\le k$, and the same translation-and-scaling argument as in the UBP would give $\\sup_i\\|u_i\\| \\le 2k/r$, a contradiction.\n\n⭐ Meaning: divergence is not confined to exceptional points, it is **almost everywhere**. Classical application: the partial-sum operators of Fourier series satisfy $\\|S_N\\| \\to \\infty$, so there **exists** a continuous function whose Fourier series diverges at some point (du Bois-Reymond, 1876) — and such functions are plentiful.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#db599258',
      },
    },

    'def.meager-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Call $A \\subseteq X$ a **meager set** (set of the first category) if $A$ is a countable union of nowhere dense sets; a set that is not meager is of the **second category**. Call $A$ **residual** if $A^c$ is meager.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#217dcb88',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"Category\" is a third notion of size, neither cardinality nor measure: $\\mathbb{Q}$ is countable and dense yet meager (each singleton is nowhere dense); the Cantor set is uncountable and of measure zero yet meager (it is itself nowhere dense); $[0,1] \\setminus \\mathbb{Q}$ is of the second category and residual **in the subspace $[0,1]$** (it is not residual in $\\mathbb{R}$: its complement contains the interval $(1,2)$, which is not meager).\n\n⚠️ Second category $\\ne$ dense: $[0,1] \\cup \\{2\\}$ is also of the second category.\n\n⭐ Usage: to say \"almost every point satisfies $P$\", prove that $\\{x : \\neg P(x)\\}$ is meager. In infinite dimension this is often handier than measure theory (many function spaces have no natural measure but do have a Baire category).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#50441be2',
      },
    },

    'def.uniformly-equicontinuous': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(K, \\delta)$ be a metric space, $(E, d)$ a metric space, and $\\mathcal{H} \\subseteq C(K, E)$. Call $\\mathcal{H}$ **uniformly equicontinuous** if\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0\\ \\exists \\eta > 0:\\quad \\delta(x, y) < \\eta \\implies d(f(x), f(y)) < \\varepsilon \\quad (\\forall f \\in \\mathcal{H},\\ \\forall x, y \\in K).$$\n\nthat is, $\\eta$ does not depend on $x, y, f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#285c4cb4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Uniform equicontinuity $\\implies$ pointwise equicontinuity (take $y = x_0$ to recover the pointwise definition), see `imp.fa-uniform-eq-pointwise`.\n\nThe converse holds when $K$ is compact: `thm.equicontinuous-uniform` (the proof uses a finite cover or a Lebesgue number).\n\n⚠️ Without compactness the converse fails: for $K = \\mathbb{R}$ and $f_n(x) = x/n$, the family is pointwise equicontinuous (the oscillation near each point is uniformly small), but it is not uniformly equicontinuous on $\\mathbb{R}$ ($|f_n(n) - f_n(n+1)| = 1/n$, so $\\eta$ would have to depend on $n$).\n\n⭐ The \"equicontinuity\" in Arzelà–Ascoli is exactly this notion on a compact metric space; `thm.equicontinuous-uniform` is the bridge that upgrades the pointwise version to it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1ba075d0',
      },
    },

    'prop.baire-characterizations': {
      statement: {
        en: "The following are **equivalent**:\n\n1. $X$ is a Baire space (the intersection of countably many dense open sets is dense);\n2. every non-empty open subset of $X$ is of the second category;\n3. a countable union of nowhere dense closed sets contains no non-empty open set (has empty interior).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8bcd955f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The three statements are one statement after taking complements: dense open $\\leftrightarrow$ nowhere dense closed, intersection $\\leftrightarrow$ union, \"dense\" $\\leftrightarrow$ \"empty interior\".\n\n⭐ **How category is used (the lecture notes' formulation)**: to prove \"there exists a point satisfying property $P$\", prove that $\\{x : P(x)\\}$ is a **dense $G_\\delta$**. What this yields is not merely existence but \"almost everywhere existence\" (genericity).\n\n⚠️ The \"second category\" in (2) applies to **non-empty open sets**, not to arbitrary sets.\n\n**Why the three are equivalent, and what each step uses** (this is the one that \"looks obvious but needs complements and countability once each\"):\n\n- (1) $\\implies$ (2): **contradiction**. Suppose some non-empty open $U$ is meager: $U \\subseteq \\bigcup_{n} F_{n}$, with each $F_{n}$ nowhere dense (closed, without loss). Put $G_{n} := X \\setminus F_{n}$; then each $G_{n}$ is dense open. By (1), $G := \\bigcap_{n} G_{n}$ is dense, hence $G \\cap U \\ne \\emptyset$; but $x \\in G \\cap U$ would give $x \\in U$ and $x \\notin \\bigcup_n F_n$, contradicting $U \\subseteq \\bigcup_{n} F_{n}$. **This step uses only \"a countable intersection of dense open sets is dense\"**.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): **the same in reverse**, still only complements: if a countable union of nowhere dense closed sets contains a non-empty open $U$, then $U$ is meager, contradicting (2).\n- (3) $\\implies$ (1): **this step uses the construction of countable intersections**. Let $O_{n}$ be dense open and put $F_{n} := X \\setminus O_{n}$ (nowhere dense closed). To prove that $\\bigcap O_{n}$ is dense is to prove that its complement $\\bigcup F_{n}$ has empty interior — which is exactly (3).\n- **Note that \"countable\" in (1) cannot be replaced by \"finite\"**: the intersection of finitely many dense open sets is dense in **every** topological space (no hypothesis needed), so Baire-ness is entirely a phenomenon of countability.\n\n**Hypotheses that must be remembered beyond the three**:\n\n- The equivalence of the three **needs no hypothesis** (it is purely topological, valid in any space); what needs hypotheses is \"which spaces satisfy them\": **complete metric spaces** (@@thm.baire@@) and **locally compact Hausdorff spaces** (@@thm.locally-compact-baire@@). The two proofs use completely different tools (nested balls/completeness versus compactness, finite subcovers and local compactness).\n- ⚠️ **\"Baire\" is not a consequence of \"complete\" by itself**, but the theorem \"complete $\\implies$ Baire\"; $\\mathbb{Q}$ is a (non-complete) metric space that is **not** Baire (each singleton is nowhere dense and $\\mathbb{Q}$ is countable), which shows precisely that completeness cannot be dropped.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d2acb1ce',
      },
    },

    'thm.arzela-ascoli-general': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact, $(E, d)$ a metric space, and $\\mathcal{H} \\subseteq C(K, E)$. Then the following are **equivalent**:\n\n1. $\\mathcal{H}$ is **totally bounded** in $(C(K,E), \\Delta)$;\n2. (a) $\\mathcal{H}$ is equicontinuous; (b) $\\mathcal{H}(x) := \\{f(x) : f \\in \\mathcal{H}\\}$ is **totally bounded** for every $x \\in K$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#29a6ff9b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"1 $\\implies$ 2\": total boundedness gives a finite $\\varepsilon$-net $f_1, \\ldots, f_n$, and then $\\mathcal{H} \\cup \\{f_1,\\ldots,f_n\\}$ is still equicontinuous (finitely many continuous functions plus one equicontinuous family).\n\n⭐ This is the same theorem as `thm.arzela-ascoli` in the graph ($\\mathbb{R}$-valued, compact metric space, uniformly bounded), in a second form: here $E$ may be any metric space, and \"uniformly bounded\" is refined to \"pointwise totally bounded\".\n\n⚠️ \"Pointwise totally bounded\" cannot be replaced by \"pointwise bounded\": when $E$ is incomplete, relative compactness needs \"coverable\", whereas boundedness only says \"does not diverge\".\n\nTogether with \"$C(K,E)$ is complete\" this is the standard form: **totally bounded + complete $=$ relatively compact**.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the characterisation of total boundedness does not require completeness of the target):\n\n- (1) $\\implies$ pointwise total boundedness: the evaluation map is 1-Lipschitz for the uniform distance, so a finite net maps to a finite net; this is an elementary check.\n- (1) $1$ equicontinuity: first take a uniform finite net, then use the finitely many continuous functions in the net and a common neighbourhood of each point together with the triangle inequality; one must not assume circularly that the original family is already equicontinuous.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (1): equicontinuity gives local oscillation control, compactness extracts finitely many controlling neighbourhoods, and the pointwise finite nets give finitely many value patterns; this is where the converse pays for compactness.\n- Pointwise boundedness cannot replace pointwise total boundedness: let $\\implies$ be a single point and $K$; the family of orthonormal vectors is bounded but not totally bounded, and this fails even though $E=\\ell^2$ is complete.\n- To upgrade total boundedness to compactness of the closure one still needs completeness of the ambient space; for $E$ a single point and $K$, the set $\\mathbb Q\\cap[0,1]$ (inside $E=\\mathbb Q$) is totally bounded but not relatively compact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6a137c7d',
      },
    },

    'thm.baire-open-subset': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $X$ is Baire and $\\Omega \\subseteq X$ is open, then $\\implies$ $\\Omega$ (with the subspace topology) is a Baire space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#41a87375',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: let $O_n$ be dense open in $\\Omega$ and write $O_n = \\Omega \\cap O_n'$ with $O_n'$ open in $X$.\n\nThen $O_n' \\cup (X \\setminus \\overline{\\Omega})$ is **dense open** in $X$ (what is adjoined is the open set $X \\setminus \\overline{\\Omega}$; the complement $\\Omega^{c}$ need not be open — for $\\Omega = (0,1)$ and $O_n' = (0,1) \\setminus \\{1/m\\}$, the complement $\\Omega^{c}$ would make the union lose a neighbourhood of $0$);\n\nhence $\\bigcap_n(O_n' \\cup (X \\setminus \\overline{\\Omega}))$ is dense in $X$, and intersecting with $\\Omega$ gives that $\\bigcap_n O_n$ is dense in $\\Omega$.\n\n⭐ Use: the open mapping theorem needs local arguments on open sets and the UBP needs to look at open subsets; this proposition guarantees that Baire-ness is not lost after localisation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#35e40b27',
      },
    },

    'thm.ck-complete': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact Hausdorff. If $E$ is complete, then $\\implies$ $(C(K, E), \\Delta)$ is complete; when $E$ is a normed space, $C(K, E)$ is a Banach space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f2d09735',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: a Cauchy sequence $\\implies$ pointwise Cauchy ($d(f_n(x), f_m(x)) \\le \\Delta(f_n, f_m)$) $\\implies$ a pointwise limit $f$ ($E$ complete) $\\implies$ the uniform estimate in $\\Delta$ gives $f_n \\to f$ $\\implies$ a uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous.\n\n⭐ Why it matters: the uniform convergence space lets one \"keep limits of continuous functions inside continuous functions\"; this is the premise of Stone–Weierstrass, Arzelà–Ascoli, and indeed of all of $C^*$-algebra theory.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1ff1ca00',
      },
    },

    'thm.locally-compact-baire': {
      statement: {
        en: "Every locally compact Hausdorff space is a Baire space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d1101568',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is the same as in the complete metric case, replacing \"the radii of nested closed balls tend $\\to 0$\" by \"nested compact neighbourhoods\": non-empty intersection is guaranteed by the **finite intersection property** of compact sets (no shrinking radius is needed).\n\n⭐ Significance: this frees Baire category from metric spaces, so it applies to $C(K)$, compact groups and Stone spaces — objects that carry no metric. Compact Hausdorff spaces in condensed mathematics can therefore also be handled by \"generic point\" arguments.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fa0ba858',
      },
    },

    'thm.stone-weierstrass-complex': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be compact Hausdorff, and let $A \\subseteq C(K, \\mathbb{C})$ be a subalgebra containing the unit, separating points, and **closed under conjugation** ($f \\in A \\implies \\bar f \\in A$). Then $\\overline A = C(K, \\mathbb{C})$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#475a60f5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** The key is to take the **real-valued part** of $A$ and hand the problem back to the real case. Put\n\n$$B := \\{f \\in A : f = \\bar f\\} = A \\cap C(K, \\mathbb{R}).$$\n\n**Step 1: $B$ is a real subalgebra containing the unit and separating points.** Since $1 \\in A$ and is real-valued, $1 \\in B$; and $B$ is closed under addition, multiplication and real scalar multiplication (operations on real-valued functions stay in $A$ and stay real-valued). Separating points: given $x \\ne y$, separation by $A$ gives $f \\in A$ with $f(x) \\ne f(y)$; if $\\operatorname{Re} f(x) \\ne \\operatorname{Re} f(y)$, use $\\operatorname{Re} f \\in B$; otherwise necessarily $\\operatorname{Im} f(x) \\ne \\operatorname{Im} f(y)$, and then use $\\operatorname{Im} f \\in B$. Since $\\operatorname{Re} f = (f + \\bar f)/2$ and $\\operatorname{Im} f = (f - \\bar f)/(2i)$ lie in $A$ by conjugation-closedness and are real-valued, they lie in $B$. Hence $B$ separates the points of $K$.\n\n**Step 2: apply the real case.** $B$ is a subalgebra of $C(K, \\mathbb{R})$ containing the unit and separating points, so the real Stone–Weierstrass theorem (`thm.stone-weierstrass`) gives $\\overline B = C(K, \\mathbb{R})$ in the sup norm.\n\n**Step 3: reassemble complex-valued functions.** Let $f \\in C(K, \\mathbb{C})$ and write $f = \\operatorname{Re} f + i\\operatorname{Im} f$, where $\\operatorname{Re} f, \\operatorname{Im} f \\in C(K, \\mathbb{R})$. Take $g_n, h_n \\in B$ with $g_n \\to \\operatorname{Re} f$ and $h_n \\to \\operatorname{Im} f$ uniformly. Then $g_n + i h_n \\in A$ ($A$ is closed under complex scalar multiplication and addition), and\n\n$$\\lVert f - (g_n + i h_n)\\rVert_\\infty \\le \\lVert \\operatorname{Re} f - g_n\\rVert_\\infty + \\lVert \\operatorname{Im} f - h_n\\rVert_\\infty \\to 0 .$$\n\nHence $f \\in \\overline A$, i.e. $\\overline A = C(K, \\mathbb{C})$. ∎\n\n⚠️ **\"Closed under conjugation\" cannot be dropped**: take $A = \\mathrm{Alg}(z) \\subseteq C(S^{1}, \\mathbb{C})$ (the closure of the polynomial algebra on $S^{1}$ in the uniform norm — the disc algebra); it contains the unit and separates points but does not contain $\\bar z$. If $\\bar z \\in \\overline A$, there are polynomials $p_n$ with $p_n \\to \\bar z$ uniformly; but for every $n$, $p_n(z)\\,z$ extends holomorphically from $S^{1}$ to the unit disc, so by Cauchy's theorem $\\int_{S^{1}} p_n(z)\\,z\\,dz = 0$, whereas uniform convergence gives $\\int_{S^{1}} p_n(z)\\,z\\,dz \\to \\int_{S^{1}} \\bar z\\, z\\,dz = \\int_{S^{1}} 1\\,dz = 2\\pi i \\ne 0$ — a contradiction.\n\n⭐ **Link with representation theory**: for a compact group $G$, the matrix coefficients $e_{ij}$ generate $C(G)$ (because $\\{e_{ij}\\}$ is closed under conjugation), and $\\Delta : C(G) \\to C(G) \\otimes C(G)$, $e_{ij} \\mapsto \\sum_k e_{ik} \\otimes e_{kj}$ — the algebraic basis of Peter–Weyl.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3be8d004',
      },
    },

    'def.g-delta': {
      statement: {
        en: "$A$ is called a **$G_\\delta$ set** if $A$ is a countable intersection of open sets; and an **$F_\\sigma$ set** if $A$ is a countable union of closed sets.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea09d2ec',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Distinguish the two directions first. The **trivial** one: a closed set is \"a union of one closed set\", so closed $\\subseteq F_\\sigma$; an open set is \"an intersection of one open set\", so open $\\subseteq G_\\delta$. **In a metric space the converse also holds** (this is the nontrivial half): open $\\subseteq F_\\sigma$, closed $\\subseteq G_\\delta$. Proof: when $U$ is open put $F_n := \\{x : d(x, X\\setminus U) \\ge 1/n\\}$; each $F_n$ is closed and $U = \\bigcup_n F_n$ (for $x \\in U$ choose $n > 1/d(x, X\\setminus U)$), so $U$ is $F_\\sigma$; dualising to the complement gives \"closed $\\subseteq G_\\delta$\". This step needs no separability — \"an open set is a countable union of balls\" is a different (coarser) statement, and that one does need separability.\n\n⭐ After Baire, \"dense $G_\\delta$\" is the standard way to say \"almost all points\": for the pointwise limit $f$ of a sequence of continuous functions, the set of continuity points is a dense $G_\\delta$ (this is the only unconditional statement of this kind — \"the set of differentiability points\" fails: for $f = \\mathbf{1}_{\\mathbb{Q}}$ both the continuity set and the differentiability set are empty); the set of nowhere differentiable functions in $C[0,1]$ is also a dense $G_\\delta$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5faf4a8b'
      },
    },

    'def.nowhere-dense': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\subseteq X$ be a topological space. Call $A$ **nowhere dense** if $(\\overline{A})^\\circ = \\emptyset$; equivalently, every non-empty open set contains a non-empty open subset disjoint from $A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bf0e98dc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ \"Nowhere dense\" is not \"not dense\": $(0,1) \\cup (1,2)$ is neither dense nor nowhere dense in $\\mathbb{R}$ — the definition demands that even the closure has no interior.\n\nExamples: $\\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, $\\{1/n\\}$, the Cantor set.\n\n⭐ The Cantor set is the most instructive example: it is **nowhere dense** yet **uncountable**. So \"nowhere dense\" and \"small cardinality\" are two different things.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bca68a01',
      },
    },

    'thm.bernstein-approximation': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $f \\in C[0, 1]$, put\n\n$$B_n f(x) := \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} f\\!\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right) x^k (1 - x)^{n-k} .$$\n\nThen $\\|B_n f - f\\|_\\infty \\to 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26390f2d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is written on the edge @@imp.bernstein-approximation@@: view $B_nf(x)$ as the expectation of $f(S_n/n)$ (where $S_n\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}(n,x)$), and use the binomial moment identities $\\mathbb E[S_n/n]=x$, $\\operatorname{Var}(S_n/n)=x(1-x)/n\\le 1/(4n)$, together with uniform continuity of $f$, to split the error into two regimes (where the deviation is $<\\delta$ the error is $<\\varepsilon$; elsewhere Markov's inequality gives probability $\\le 1/(4n\\delta^2)$), then take $n$ large enough to get uniform convergence.\n\n⭐ Division of labour with Stone–Weierstrass: this gives an explicit sequence of operators, only on $[0,1]$ and only for real-valued functions; Stone–Weierstrass gives \"algebraic hypotheses suffice\", with no construction. The two corroborate each other and neither replaces the other.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4e27a729',
      },
    },

    'thm.banach-alaoglu': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a normed space, $X^{*}$ its continuous dual, and $B_{X^{*}} = \\{\\varphi \\in X^{*} : \\|\\varphi\\| \\le 1\\}$. Then $B_{X^{*}}$ is compact in the **weak* topology** $\\sigma(X^{*}, X)$ (generated by the seminorms $\\varphi \\mapsto |\\varphi(x)|$, $x \\in X$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#606fa504',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Weak* topology = restriction of the product topology.** Put $P = \\prod_{x \\in X} [-\\|x\\|, \\|x\\|]$ and $\\Phi : B_{X^{*}} \\to P$, $\\Phi(\\varphi) = (\\varphi(x))_{x \\in X}$. Since $|\\varphi(x)| \\le \\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|x\\| \\le \\|x\\|$, the image lies in $P$; the weak* topology is exactly the topology pulled back from the product topology on $P$ (each coordinate is weak* continuous, and the coordinates separate points); $\\Phi$ is injective on $B_{X^{*}}$.\n\n**The image is closed.** Suppose $\\Phi(\\varphi_\\alpha) \\to y = (y_x)$ in $P$. Define $\\varphi(x) = y_x$. Linearity: $y_{x + z} = \\lim \\varphi_\\alpha(x + z) = \\lim(\\varphi_\\alpha(x) + \\varphi_\\alpha(z)) = y_x + y_z$, and similarly $y_{tx} = t y_x$. Boundedness: $|y_x| \\le \\|x\\|$, hence $\\|\\varphi\\| \\le 1$. Thus $y = \\Phi(\\varphi) \\in \\Phi(B_{X^{*}})$, so the image is closed.\n\n**Compactness.** $P$ is a product of compact spaces (each $[-\\|x\\|,\\|x\\|]$ is compact), hence compact by `thm.tykhonov`; the closed subset $\\Phi(B_{X^{*}})$ is compact; $\\Phi$ is a homeomorphism onto its image (continuous bijection, compact $\\to$ Hausdorff), so $B_{X^{*}}$ is weak* compact. $\\blacksquare$\n\n⚠ The weak* topology is not the norm topology: $B_{X^{*}}$ is in general not compact in the norm topology (when $X$ is infinite-dimensional). The compactness here holds only in the weak* sense.\n\n📌 Use: the forward direction of `thm.james` (reflexive $\\implies$ every functional attains its norm) needs \"$B_X$ weakly compact\"; and \"reflexive $\\implies$ $B_X$ weakly compact\" is precisely this theorem applied to $X^{**}$ and pulled back along $J_X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76226d05',
      },
    },

    'thm.goldstine': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a normed space and $J_X : X \\to X^{**}$ the natural embedding. Then $J_X(B_X)$ is dense in $B_{X^{**}}$ in the **weak* topology**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4a1ec92f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Preparation: every weak* continuous linear functional is an evaluation.** Let $\\ell$ be a continuous linear functional on $(X^{**}, w^{*})$. Continuity gives $x_1,\\ldots,x_n \\in X$ and $c > 0$ with $|\\ell(\\xi)| \\le c \\max_j |\\xi(x_j)|$. Thus $\\ell$ factors through $\\xi \\mapsto (\\xi(x_1),\\ldots,\\xi(x_n))$, hence is a linear combination of these coordinates: $\\ell = \\sum_j c_j\\, \\mathrm{ev}_{x_j} = \\mathrm{ev}_{\\,x}$, where $x = \\sum_j c_j x_j \\in X$. That is, $(X^{**}, w^{*})^{*} = J_X(X)$.\n\n**Separation lemma (the geometric form of Hahn–Banach, derived from `thm.hahn-banach`).** Let $K$ be a closed convex set in a locally convex space and $\\xi_0 \\notin K$. Then there are a continuous linear functional $\\ell$ and a scalar $\\alpha$ with $\\ell(\\xi_0) > \\alpha \\ge \\sup_K \\ell$. Take $K - \\xi_0$ and its Minkowski functional $p$; on $K - \\xi_0$ it is $\\le 1$. On the line $\\mathbb{R}(\\xi_0 - k_0)$, define $\\ell_0(t(\\xi_0 - k_0)) = t$ (with $k_0 \\in K$ arbitrary), check $\\ell_0 \\le p$, extend to $\\ell \\le p$ by `thm.hahn-banach`, and take absolute values over $\\pm$.\n\n**Main proof.** Let $K = \\overline{J_X(B_X)}^{w^{*}} \\subseteq B_{X^{**}}$ (closed convex compact). If $K \\ne B_{X^{**}}$, pick $\\xi_0 \\in B_{X^{**}} \\setminus K$. By the separation lemma there is a weak* continuous linear functional $\\ell$ with $\\ell(\\xi_0) > \\sup_{K} \\ell$. By the preparation, $\\ell = \\mathrm{ev}_x$ for some $x \\in X$. Hence\n\n$\\xi_0(x) > \\sup_{\\eta \\in K} \\eta(x) \\ge \\sup_{y \\in B_X} (J_X y)(x) = \\sup_{y \\in B_X} x(y) = \\|x\\|.$\n\nBut $\\xi_0 \\in B_{X^{**}}$ gives $\\xi_0(x) \\le \\|\\xi_0\\|\\,\\|x\\| \\le \\|x\\|$, a contradiction. Therefore $K = B_{X^{**}}$, i.e. $J_X(B_X)$ is weak* dense. $\\blacksquare$\n\n⚠ Dense is not closed: in general $J_X(B_X)$ is indeed not weak* closed (it is weak* closed iff $X$ is reflexive), which is exactly the point used in `thm.milman-pettis`.\n\n📌 The geometric separation lemma here uses only `thm.hahn-banach`; together the two turn \"$X$ is reflexive\" into a purely geometric condition: every point of $B_{X^{**}}$ lies in the weak* closure of $J_X(B_X)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bd87c90f',
      },
    },

    'def.uniformly-convex': {
      statement: {
        en: "A normed space $(X, \\|\\cdot\\|)$ is **uniformly convex** if for every $\\varepsilon \\in (0, 2]$ there is $\\delta > 0$ such that\n\n$\\|x\\| = \\|y\\| = 1,\\quad \\|x - y\\| \\ge \\varepsilon \\implies \\left\\|\\frac{x + y}{2}\\right\\| \\le 1 - \\delta .$\n\nthat is, two points on the unit sphere that are not close have a midpoint strictly inside the ball, and the depth depends only on the distance.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b636c3ac',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Examples**: Hilbert spaces (the parallelogram law gives $\\|(x+y)/2\\|^2 = 1 - \\|x-y\\|^2/4$, so one may take $\\delta = 1 - \\sqrt{1 - \\varepsilon^2/4}$); $L^p$ with $1 < p < \\infty$ (Clarkson's inequality); $\\ell^p$ with $1 < p < \\infty$.\n\n**Counterexamples**: $\\ell^1$, $\\ell^\\infty$, $c_0$, $C[0,1]$ are not uniformly convex (in $\\ell^1$ the midpoint of $e_1, e_2$ still has norm $1$; in $\\ell^\\infty$ piecewise constant sequences such as $(1,0,\\ldots)$ and $(1,1,0,\\ldots)$ give a counterexample).\n\n⭐ Uniform convexity $\\implies$ strict convexity (`def.strictly-convex`): if $\\|x\\| = \\|y\\| = 1$ and $x \\ne y$, take $\\varepsilon = \\|x - y\\| > 0$ and the midpoint has norm $< 1$.\n\n📌 Uniform convexity is \"the norm bends uniformly\": it upgrades the pointwise, pair-by-pair conclusion of strict convexity to an estimate uniform over all unit vectors — exactly the quantitative form needed for Milman–Pettis (`thm.milman-pettis`) and Clarkson's inequality.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7526eb3e',
      },
    },

    'def.chain-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a simplicial complex and write $K_{p}$ for the set of all its $p$-simplices (when $K$ is **finite** this is a finite set; in general it may be infinite, and the \"finite support\" condition below is there for exactly this reason). The **$p$-th chain group** $C_{p}(K)$ is the **free abelian group on $K_{p}$**; concretely,\n\n$$C_{p}(K) := \\{\\, n : K_{p} \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\,\\},$$\n\nwhere $n(\\sigma) = 0$ for all but finitely many $\\sigma$ (this is what \"finite formal sum\" means).\n\nAddition is pointwise ($n + m$ means $(n + m)(\\sigma) = n(\\sigma) + m(\\sigma)$). Elements are written as finite formal sums\n\n$$c = \\sum_{\\sigma \\in K_{p}} n_{\\sigma}\\,\\sigma,\\qquad n_{\\sigma} \\in \\mathbb{Z},$$\n\nand $n_{\\sigma}$ is called the **multiplicity** of $\\sigma$ in $c$. By convention $C_{p}(K) := 0$ when $p < 0$ or $p > \\dim K$. $C_{p}(K)$ is free abelian of rank equal to the number $\\alpha_{p}$ of $p$-simplices of $K$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#af472e0e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why free abelian**: we want to add the simplices of a chain with signs — adding $\\sigma$ three times gives $3\\sigma$, cancelling it against its reverse gives $0$, and **no other relations** are allowed. The free abelian group is exactly the minimal structure that permits arbitrary addition on a basis with no extra relations.\n\n⚠️ **Exactly enough freedom**: a group homomorphism is determined by its values on a **basis**, so to define the boundary operator later it suffices to write a formula on a single simplex and extend linearly — no compatibility check is required. That is the entire use of the word \"free\".\n\n**Other coefficient rings support the same theory**: coefficients in $\\mathbb{Q}$ or $\\mathbb{R}$ give Betti numbers (torsion is invisible), while $\\mathbb{Z}/2$ makes all signs $(-1)^{i}$ disappear (no orientation worries). This region fixes coefficients in $\\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n💡 Intuition: $C_{p}(K)$ is the \"integer ledger of $p$-dimensional pieces\". Homology asks: **which of these entries are genuine holes and which are merely shadows of something higher-dimensional** (see `def.simplicial-homology`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#06640f8e',
      },
    },

    'def.path-homotopy': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **path** in $X$ is a continuous map $\\alpha : [0, 1] \\to X$; $\\alpha(0)$ is its **starting point** and $\\alpha(1)$ its **end point**.\nA path whose start and end are both $x_{0}$ is a **closed path** (also called a loop based at $x_{0}$).\n\nLet $\\alpha, \\beta$ be two paths with the same start $x_{0}$ and the same end $x_{1}$. They are **path homotopic** if there is a continuous $F : [0, 1] \\times [0, 1] \\to X$ such that\n\n$$F(t, 0) = \\alpha(t),\\qquad F(t, 1) = \\beta(t),$$\n\n$$F(0, s) = x_{0},\\qquad F(1, s) = x_{1},$$\n\nwritten $\\alpha \\simeq_{p} \\beta$. Equivalently, $\\alpha \\simeq \\beta \\; (\\operatorname{rel} \\{0, 1\\})$.\n\nThe **path homotopy class** of $\\alpha$ is written $[\\alpha]$, i.e. $\\{\\, \\beta : \\beta \\simeq_{p} \\alpha \\,\\}$.\nA path homotopy class of closed paths is also called a **homotopy class** (based at $x_{0}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1d15d833',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why the endpoints must be pinned**: $[0, 1]$ is convex and contractible, so **any** two paths are homotopic (shrink $\\alpha$ to a point along a straight line and then expand into $\\beta$). A homotopy that does not pin the endpoints collapses all paths into one class and detects nothing; only after pinning the endpoints does the information \"how it winds\" survive.\n\n⚠️ Path homotopy **makes sense only for paths with common endpoints** — otherwise the conditions $F(0, s)$ and $F(1, s)$ cannot even be written down.\n\n**Homotopy classes are equivalence classes**: the three properties in `def.homotopy-maps` restrict to homotopies that keep the endpoints fixed, so for fixed $x_{0}, x_{1}$ the paths from $x_{0}$ to $x_{1}$ split into homotopy classes.\n\n💡 Intuition: a homotopy class is \"a way of walking from $x_{0}$ to $x_{1}$\"; two walks count as the same if they can be deformed continuously into each other.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0b56f61a',
      },
    },

    'thm.pi1-circle': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p : \\mathbb{R} \\to S^{1}$, $p(t) = e^{2\\pi i t}$ (this is a covering map). For a closed path $\\alpha$ based at $1 \\in S^{1}$, take the unique lift $\\tilde{\\alpha}$ with $\\tilde{\\alpha}(0) = 0$, and define\n\n$$\\deg[\\alpha] := \\tilde{\\alpha}(1) \\in \\mathbb{Z}$$\n\n(the **winding number**, also called the **degree**). Then $\\deg$ is a well-defined group isomorphism\n\n$$\\deg : \\pi_{1}(S^{1}, 1) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\cong\\ } \\mathbb{Z}.$$\n\nConcretely, the generator class with $\\deg[\\alpha] = n$ is $\\alpha_{n}(t) = e^{2\\pi i n t}$; $n > 0$ means \"wound $n$ times counterclockwise\", and $n < 0$ means \"wound $|n|$ times clockwise\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1f0fc181',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Geometric translation**: the fundamental group of $S^{1}$ carries only one piece of information, \"how many times it winds\", and that is a **complete invariant** — two closed paths are homotopic iff they have the same winding number. Nothing else can hide in $S^{1}$.\n\n⭐ **Interface with complex analysis**: the winding number is written in complex analysis as $n(\\gamma, 0) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\gamma}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{z}$ (`prop.winding-integral`). The two are the same integer: lifting a closed curve to polar coordinates gives the lift. This also explains the topological source of the theorem that the winding number is invariant under homotopies of the curve (`thm.winding-homotopy`).\n\n⚠️ **Structure of the proof (important)**: `prop.covering-index` gives only that $\\pi_{1}(S^{1})$ and $\\mathbb{Z}$ are in bijection, because $\\pi_{1}(\\mathbb{R}) = 1$ and the fibre is $\\mathbb{Z}$. To obtain an **isomorphism** one must additionally prove that $\\deg$ is a homomorphism — this uses \"the lift of a concatenation is the translate of the lift\" (uniqueness of lifts). Both halves are needed; see the proof on the edge.\n\n💡 This is the first **non-trivial** computation in all of algebraic topology. Its value: $\\pi_{1}(S^{1}) \\ne 1$ immediately gives \"$S^{1}$ is not contractible\", \"the disc cannot retract onto its boundary\", and the Brouwer fixed point theorem — one computation, a chain of geometric theorems.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8e6aae46',
      },
    },

    'def.continuous-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ and $Y$ be topological spaces. A map $f : X \\to Y$ is **continuous** iff **the preimage of every open set is open**:\n\n$$V \\in \\mathcal{T}_Y \\implies f^{-1}(V) \\in \\mathcal{T}_X$$\n\nit suffices to check this on a **basis** (or subbasis).\n\nComposites of continuous maps are continuous: $f$ and $g$ continuous $\\implies g \\circ f$ continuous.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e6740e07',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This definition is the **abstraction** of $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$: in a metric space, \"$f^{-1}(V)$ is open\" and \"$\\varepsilon$ gives $\\delta$\" are literally equivalent — because open balls are $\\varepsilon$-neighbourhoods.\n\nSo continuity is a topological property, independent of any particular metric.\n\n⚠ Use **preimages**, not images: preimages preserve unions, intersections and complements; images do not.\n\nThis is the same pattern as the definition of a measurable function ($f^{-1}(E) \\in \\mathcal{M}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#66d67c84',
      },
    },

    'def.covering-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p : E \\to B$ be continuous and surjective. An open set $U \\subseteq B$ is **evenly covered** if $p^{-1}(U)$ is a union of a family $\\{V_{j}\\}_{j \\in J}$ of pairwise disjoint open sets in $E$, and for every $j$ the restriction\n\n$$p\\big|_{V_{j}} : V_{j} \\longrightarrow U$$\n\nis a homeomorphism. If every point of $B$ has an evenly covered neighbourhood, then $p$ is a **covering map**, $E$ is a **covering space** of $B$, and $B$ is called the **base space**.\n\n**Constructive examples**:\n- $p(t) = e^{2\\pi i t}$, $p : \\mathbb{R} \\to S^{1}$: near the image of each $t$, the preimage $p^{-1}(U)$ consists of $\\mathbb{Z}$ copies of the same arc;\n- $p(z) = z^{n}$, $p : S^{1} \\to S^{1}$: an $n$-sheeted covering;\n- if $B$ is path connected and locally path connected, the cardinality of $p^{-1}(b)$ is independent of $b$; this cardinality is called the **number of sheets**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#054cca19',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why \"local homeomorphism + discrete fibre\" rather than \"locally trivial fibre bundle\"**: a covering is **stiffer** than a fibre bundle — each sheet $V_{j}$ is required to be homeomorphic to $U$, not merely fibre-homotopy equivalent. Hence the fibres of a covering are necessarily discrete. This stiffness is exactly what makes the lifting theorem work.\n\n**Number of sheets = index**: if $B$ is path connected and locally path connected and the number of sheets is finite, then the index of $\\pi_{1}(E)$ in $\\pi_{1}(B)$ equals the number of sheets (`prop.covering-index`). This is the source of \"count the sheets to compute the fundamental group\".\n\n⚠️ A common counterexample: $p : (0, 2) \\to S^{1}$, $p(t) = e^{2\\pi i t}$ — this is **not** a covering, because every neighbourhood of $1 \\in S^{1}$ is covered only halfway ($(0,2)$ does not \"continue\" at the endpoint). A covering must be even at **every** point, including limit points.\n\n💡 Picture: the base space is the shadow cast downward, and the covering space is the sheet of paper held above it. The same shadow can be cast by several sheets, and the sheets are related by \"go once around, change sheet\" — that switching rule is $\\pi_{1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe4edf6a',
      },
    },

    'def.completely-regular-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this condition is**: $T_{3.5}$ together with $T_{1}$. This class is the standard setting for \"there are enough continuous functions\", and the default hypothesis in compactification and metrization theory.\n\nA topological space $X$ is a **completely regular space** if\n\n1. $X$ is **$T_{3.5}$**: a point and a closed set not containing it can be separated by a continuous function (`def.t35-space`);\n2. $X$ is **$T_{1}$**.\n\nA space satisfying both is also called a **Tychonoff space**.\n\n**Equivalent form**: $X$ embeds in some cube $[0,1]^{I}$ (that is, \"there are enough continuous functions to separate points from closed sets\").\n\n**Position in the chain**: normal space $\\implies$ completely regular space $\\implies$ regular space $\\implies T_{2}$. The only non-trivial step is \"normal $\\implies$ completely regular\" (Urysohn's lemma, `lem.urysohn`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0eefe13f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ **Naming convention**: the T-number denotes the bare property, and the named property denotes the bare property plus $T_{1}$.\n\nThus $T_{3.5}$ means that a point and a closed set not containing it can be separated by a continuous function (`def.t35-space`, without $T_{1}$), while complete regularity is $T_{3.5} + T_{1}$.\n\nThis is the same convention as \"$T_{3}$ versus regular space\".\n\n⭐ **Why adding $T_{1}$ is not redundant**: without $T_{1}$, $T_{3.5}$ does not even guarantee that points are closed, so $T_{2}$ and uniqueness of limits all fail. Once $T_{1}$ is added, $\\{y\\}$ is closed, and a continuous function separating $x$ from $\\{y\\}$ immediately gives disjoint open sets — hence a completely regular space is automatically $T_{2}$.\n\n⭐ **Why \"embeds in a cube\" is the same statement**: if $X$ is completely regular, assemble all continuous $f : X \\to [0,1]$ into a map $X \\to [0,1]^{I}$; $T_{1}$ (points closed) together with $T_{3.5}$ (points and closed sets separable by functions) is exactly what makes it an embedding. Conversely, an embedding into a cube obviously supplies enough functions. This is the other half of Tychonoff's theorem.\n\n⚠️ **Name clash**: `thm.tykhonov` is the **product compactness** theorem (an arbitrary product of compact spaces is compact) — same person, different contribution. Do not confuse the two.\n\n**The hypotheses used in each direction** (the named properties include $T_1$, and the cube characterisation is the embedding theorem):\n\n- completely regular $\\iff$ $T_{3.5}$ and $T_1$: this is a definitional unfolding of the notion used here, free of charge; it does **not** derive $T_1$ from bare $T_{3.5}$.\n- completely regular $\\implies$ embedding in $[0,1]^I$: take the evaluation map of all continuous interval-valued functions; $T_1$ (points closed) together with point–closed separation gives injectivity, and point–closed separation recovers the induced topology. The product topology is used, not the product compactness theorem.\n- embedding in a cube $\\implies$ completely regular: from coordinate separation, basic neighbourhoods in finitely many coordinates, and the hereditary nature of complete regularity for subspaces; a continuous injection alone is not enough.\n- $T_1$ cannot be dropped: the indiscrete topology on at least two points satisfies bare $T_{3.5}$, but does not embed in a Hausdorff cube; and normal $\\implies$ completely regular needs Urysohn's lemma.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f3e9253d',
      },
    },

    'thm.no-retraction-disk': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $D^{2} := \\{\\, z \\in \\mathbb{C} : |z| \\le 1 \\,\\}$, $S^{1} = \\partial D^{2}$. Then there is **no** continuous map\n\n$$r : D^{2} \\longrightarrow S^{1}$$\n\nwith $r(z) = z$ for all $z \\in S^{1}$. Such an $r$, if it existed, would be called a **retraction** of $S^{1}$ in $D^{2}$, and $S^{1}$ a **retract** of $D^{2}$. Equivalently: the inclusion $i : S^{1} \\hookrightarrow D^{2}$ has no left inverse.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#593a7f99',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What the proof actually does**: apply $\\pi_{1}$ to $r \\circ i = \\operatorname{id}_{S^{1}}$ to obtain $r_{*} \\circ i_{*} = \\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb{Z}}$.\n\nBut $\\pi_{1}(D^{2}) = 1$ (the disc is convex $\\implies$ contractible), so $i_{*}$ can only be the zero map, and the composite must be zero — a contradiction.\n\n**The whole argument uses one line of group theory**, given that $\\pi_{1}(S^{1})$ is known.\n\n**Generalisation**: the same argument works for every $n \\ge 1$ (replacing the fundamental group by the homology group $H_{n-1}$): $S^{n-1}$ is not a retract of $D^{n}$.\n\n⚠️ \"Convex $\\implies$ contractible\" is literal in the proof: for any $D^{2}$ in $\\sigma$, take $F(t, s) := (1 - s)\\,\\sigma(t)$, shrinking the whole path along a straight line to $0$. No machinery is needed for this step.\n\n💡 The conclusion looks like a mere \"impossible\", but it is the source of Brouwer's fixed point theorem, Poincaré–Miranda, and the family of theorems saying \"the sphere cannot be deformed continuously to a point\" (see the edge to `thm.brouwer-fixed-point`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e722d93e',
      },
    },

    'thm.urysohn-metrization': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a **regular** ($T_{1}$) **second countable** space. Then $X$ is **metrizable**: there is a metric compatible with the topology of $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b431b9b2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (embedding into a separable metric space)**\n1. **Second countable $\\implies$ Lindelöf**: every open cover has a countable subcover (sieve the basic open sets).\n2. **Regular $+$ Lindelöf $\\implies$ normal**: $T_{1}$ together with \"a point and a closed set can be separated\", plus a pointwise induction over the Lindelöf cover, separates two disjoint closed sets. Hence **Urysohn's lemma is available**.\n3. **Choose countably many separating functions**: take a countable base $\\{\\, B_{n} \\,\\}$. For every pair $(m, n)$ with $\\overline{B_{m}} \\subseteq B_{n}$, Urysohn's lemma gives a continuous function\n$$f_{m,n} : X \\to [0, 1],\\qquad f_{m,n}|_{\\overline{B_{m}}} \\equiv 0,\\qquad f_{m,n}|_{X \\setminus B_{n}} \\equiv 1.$$\nThere are only countably many such functions; enumerate them as $f_{k}$ ($k \\ge 1$).\n4. **Embedding**: put $F : X \\to [0, 1]^{\\mathbb{N}}$, $F(x) := (\\, f_{k}(x)/k \\,)_{k \\ge 1}$.\n\n$F$ is continuous (each component is continuous, and the series converges uniformly);\n\n**injective**: if $x \\ne y$, regularity gives a basic open set $B_{n}$ with $x \\in B_{n} \\subseteq \\overline{B_{n}} \\subseteq X \\setminus \\{\\, y \\,\\}$, so some pair $(m, n)$ has $f_{m,n}$ separating the two points;\n\n**inverse continuous**: again using basic pairs, translate \"$f_{k}$ is close\" back into \"both points lie in a common basic open set\".\n\nHence $F$ is a homeomorphism onto its image.\n5. **The image is metrizable**: $\\rho(a, b) := \\sum_{k \\ge 1} 2^{-k} |a_{k} - b_{k}|$ is a metric on $[0, 1]^{\\mathbb{N}}$ inducing the product topology; restrict it to the image.\nTherefore $X$ is homeomorphic to a subspace of a separable metric space, hence metrizable. ∎\n\n⚠️ **The converse fails**: metrizable does not imply second countable — an uncountable discrete space is metrizable (discrete metric) but not second countable. So this theorem is a **sufficient condition**, not a characterisation.\n\n⭐ To turn \"sufficient\" into \"necessary\" one replaces second countability by a more suitable base condition: Nagata–Smirnov ($\\sigma$-locally finite base) and Bing ($\\sigma$-discrete base) do exactly this.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#86482a59',
      },
    },

    'def.partial-derivative': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open, $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$, $a \\in U$. The **partial derivative** of $f$ at $a$ with respect to the $j$-th variable is (if the limit exists)\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j}(a) := \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(a + h e_j) - f(a)}{h}$$\n\nwhere $e_j$ is the $j$-th standard basis vector. In other words, **only the $j$-th coordinate moves** and the rest are held fixed — an ordinary one-variable derivative.\n\nHigher partial derivatives are written $\\partial^{\\alpha} f = \\partial_1^{\\alpha_1} \\cdots \\partial_n^{\\alpha_n} f$ ($\\alpha = (\\alpha_1, \\ldots, \\alpha_n)$ is a multi-index, $|\\alpha| = \\alpha_1 + \\cdots + \\alpha_n$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ac9f903a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ The existence of partial derivatives **guarantees nothing**: for $f(x, y) = xy/(x^{2} + y^{2})$ (with $f(0,0) = 0$ at the origin), both partial derivatives at the origin exist (both equal $0$), yet along $y = x$ the function satisfies $f \\to 1/2 \\ne f(0,0)$ — it is not even **continuous**.\n\n⭐ The root of the problem is an asymmetry: existence of partial derivatives is $n$ **one-dimensional** conditions (for scalar $f$; for $f:\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ it is $mn$ conditions), whereas differentiability (`def.total-differential`) is **one** $n$-dimensional condition.\n\nNo number of one-dimensional conditions can add up to an $n$-dimensional conclusion.\n\n⭐ Conversely, $C^{1}$ (partial derivatives exist **and are continuous**) suffices for differentiability — the most used sufficient condition in practice (see `def.total-differential`).\n\n⭐ On $\\mathbb{R}^n$, a space \"grown from coordinates\", partial derivatives are free;\n\non a **manifold** or a **Banach space** the coordinates themselves must first be built, and only then can one discuss directional derivatives and differentiability.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#493946ea',
      },
    },

    'def.jacobian': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open and let $f = (f_1, \\ldots, f_m) : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ have all partial derivatives at $a$. The **Jacobian matrix** of $f$ at $a$ is\n\n$$J_f(a) := \\Bigl( \\frac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial x_j}(a) \\Bigr)_{\\substack{1 \\le i \\le m \\\\ 1 \\le j \\le n}} \\in M_{m \\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$$\n\nIf $f$ is differentiable at $a$, then $J_f(a)$ is exactly the matrix of the linear map $Df(a)$ in the standard bases.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fa46c259',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The chain rule in matrix form is just **matrix multiplication**: $J_{g \\circ f}(a) = J_g(f(a))\\, J_f(a)$ (`thm.chain-rule-multi`).\n\nComposition of linear maps corresponds to matrix multiplication — the total differential is exactly the transport of this linear-algebra fact into calculus.\n\n⭐ Three special cases are worth remembering:\n- $n = 1$: $J$ is a column, i.e. the tangent vector of a curve;\n- $m = 1$: $J$ is a row, i.e. $(\\nabla f)^{\\mathsf{T}}$;\n- $m = n$: $\\det J_f(a)$ is the **local volume scaling factor** (this is the determinant factor in `thm.change-of-variables-manifold`).\n\n⚠ Notational convention: some books write the Jacobian transposed (they store $Df$ as a column vector).\n\nThe convention here is **rows index the target coordinates and columns index the domain coordinates**, so the chain rule is \"multiply from left to right\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8b642a24',
      },
    },

    'def.improper-integral': {
      statement: {
        en: "**Infinite interval**: if $f$ is integrable on every $[a, R]$, define\n\n$$\\int_a^{+\\infty} f := \\lim_{R \\to +\\infty} \\int_a^{R} f$$\n\n(called **convergent** when the limit exists and is finite, divergent otherwise).\n\n**Unbounded function**: if $f$ is unbounded on $[a, b]$ but integrable on every $[a, b - \\varepsilon]$, define\n\n$$\\int_a^{b} f := \\lim_{\\varepsilon \\to 0^{+}} \\int_a^{b - \\varepsilon} f$$\n\nThe two cases may mix (an infinite interval together with unboundedness), and then one must **split**: every piece must converge.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#14fdd08b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The improper integral is not a new theory: it is only a **limit** of Riemann integrals — every integral in the definition is an ordinary one.\n\n⚠ Splitting is necessary in the mixed case: defining $\\int_{-\\infty}^{+\\infty} f$ as $\\lim_{R \\to \\infty}\\int_{-R}^{R}$ loses phenomena that are not absolutely convergent ($\\int_{-R}^{R} x\\,dx = 0$ for every $R$, while both $\\int_0^{+\\infty}$ and $\\int_{-\\infty}^{0}$ diverge).\n\nThis distinction is called the **principal value**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#473fd485',
      },
    },

    'def.integrable': {
      statement: {
        en: "A real-valued measurable $f$ is **integrable** iff $\\int f^+ < \\infty$ and $\\int f^- < \\infty$. Equivalently, $f$ is integrable iff the integral of $|f|$ is finite:\n\n$f$ $\\iff \\int |f| d\\mu < \\infty$\n\nA complex-valued measurable $f$ is integrable iff $\\int |f| d\\mu < \\infty$. The set of all integrable functions is denoted $L^1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4bb53459',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Integrable $\\iff$ absolutely integrable**: this is where the Lebesgue integral finally becomes convenient — because $|f| \\in L^{+}$, every theorem for the non-negative case applies directly.\n\n(Compare the Riemann improper integral: there $\\int f$ may converge while $\\int|f|$ diverges; this is called conditional convergence.)\n\n⚠ Strictly speaking, the elements of $L^{1}$ are **equivalence classes of functions** (equal a.e. counts as the same), so \"$\\int |f| = 0 \\implies f = 0$\" must be read a.e. This is the key step that later makes Lp spaces normed spaces.\n\n**Among the three equivalences, \"$\\int|f| < \\infty$\" is the most useful, but it presupposes $f^{\\pm}$**:\n\n- $f$ is integrable $\\iff$ $\\int f^{+} < \\infty$ and $\\int f^{-} < \\infty$: **this is the definition** (split $f$ into positive and negative parts, each a **non-negative** measurable function, and use the non-negative case).\n- $\\iff$ $\\int \\lvert f\\rvert < \\infty$: **one step** ($\\lvert f\\rvert = f^{+} + f^{-}$), and it **uses additivity of the non-negative integral**. ⚠️ \"$\\int f$ is finite\" and integrability are **not** the same thing: if $\\int f$ is a **finite real number**, then $f^{\\pm}$ are finite and $f$ is necessarily integrable; the danger is $\\int f^{+} = \\int f^{-} = +\\infty$, where $\\int f$ is **undefined** (not $0$) — which of course does not count as integrable. This is exactly why integrability requires absolute integrability.\n- $f$ measurable with $\\lvert f\\rvert \\le g$ and $g$ integrable $\\implies$ $f$ is integrable: **this step needs monotonicity** (`prop.integral-order`).\n\n⚠️ **Necessary hypotheses**: $f$ must be **measurable** (an unmeasurable function has no integral to speak of). The difference from **Riemann integrability**: Riemann requires boundedness plus a null set of discontinuities (`thm.riemann-lebesgue-criterion`), whereas Lebesgue integrability **does not require boundedness** ($1/\\sqrt{x}$ is Lebesgue integrable on $(0,1]$); conversely, a bounded Riemann integrable function is Lebesgue integrable (`prop.riemann-implies-lebesgue`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5feeb102',
      },
    },

    'thm.lebesgue-radon-nikodym': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\nu$ be a **$\\sigma$-finite** signed measure and $\\mu$ a **$\\sigma$-finite** positive measure, both on $(X, \\mathcal{M})$. Then there is a **unique** pair of $\\sigma$-finite signed measures $\\lambda$, $\\rho$ such that\n\n$$\\lambda \\perp  \\mu, \\quad  \\rho \\ll  \\mu, \\quad  \\nu = \\lambda + \\rho$$\n\nand $\\rho$ can be written as an integral: there is a generalized $\\mu$-integrable function $f$ with\n\n$$d\\rho = f d\\mu$$\n\nAny two such $f$ agree $\\mu -\\text{a.e.}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8e22e833',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **structure theorem** of the whole theory of signed measures: every $\\nu$ splits uniquely into \"a part singular with respect to $\\mu$\" plus \"a part absolutely continuous with respect to $\\mu$\".\n\nIn particular, **when $\\nu \\ll \\mu$**, $\\lambda = 0$, hence $d\\nu = f d\\mu$ — this is what is usually called the **Radon–Nikodym theorem**.\n\n⚠ $\\sigma$-finiteness cannot be dropped (it is needed for both $\\nu$ and $\\mu$).\n\nThe three steps of the proof (expanded in the next arrow): $I. \\nu$, with $\\mu$ finite; II. cut into finite pieces using $\\sigma$-finiteness; $III. \\nu$ a signed measure, treat $\\nu^+, \\nu^-$ separately.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6633e8b0',
      },
    },

    'thm.chain-rule-multi': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $V \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be open, $f : U \\to V$ differentiable at $a \\in U$, and $g : V \\to \\mathbb{R}^{p}$ differentiable at $b = f(a)$. Then $g \\circ f$ is differentiable at $a$, and\n\n$$D(g \\circ f)(a) = Dg\\bigl(f(a)\\bigr) \\circ Df(a)$$\n\nIn matrix form,\n\n$$J_{g \\circ f}(a) = J_g\\bigl(f(a)\\bigr)\\, J_f(a)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1dd6240e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The whole theorem is one sentence: **the chain rule for differentials is composition of linear maps**.\n\nThe matrix form is merely what it looks like after choosing bases — so \"chain rule = matrix multiplication\" is not a coincidence but a consequence of the definition.\n\n⚠ There is a real trap in the proof: one cannot simply write $Dg(f(a + h)) = Dg(f(a)) + O(h)$.\n\nBecause $f(a + h) - f(a)$ is itself $O(\\|h\\|)$, the $o(\\|k\\|)$ of $g$ at $f(a)$ (with $k = f(a+h) - f(a)$) must be explicitly replaced by $o(\\|h\\|)$; this step uses $\\|k\\| \\le (\\|Df(a)\\| + 1)\\|h\\|$ (for small $h$).\n\nThe full proof is on the edge.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cc6fdbb7',
      },
    },

    'cor.rank-theorem': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open and $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ of class $C^{1}$, and suppose the rank of the Jacobian is **identically** $r$ on $U$ (that is, $\\operatorname{rank} Df(x) = r$ for all $x \\in U$).\n\nThen for every $a \\in U$ there are a **$C^{1}$ change of coordinates** $\\varphi$ near $a$ and a **$C^{1}$ change of coordinates** $\\psi$ near $f(a)$ such that (⚠ the smoothness of the coordinates follows that of $f$: $f \\in C^{1}$ gives only $C^{1}$ coordinates; $f \\in C^{k}$ gives $C^{k}$, and only $C^{\\infty}$ gives smooth coordinates)\n\n$$\\psi \\circ f \\circ \\varphi^{-1} (x_1, \\ldots, x_n) = (x_1, \\ldots, x_r, 0, \\ldots, 0)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1d827f03',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The rank theorem says \"**a constant-rank map has only one local shape**\": every map of constant rank $r$ looks the same locally, and the only invariant is the rank $r$.\n\nThis is a **classification theorem** in calculus, and the culmination of the \"local $\\to$ normal form\" method.\n\n⭐ Three corollaries at once:\n- $r = n = m$: $\\psi \\circ f \\circ \\varphi^{-1} = \\mathrm{id}$ $\\implies$ a local isomorphism, i.e. the **inverse mapping theorem**;\n- $r = n < m$: $x \\mapsto (x, 0)$, the normal form of an **immersion**;\n- $r = m < n$: $(x_1, \\ldots, x_n) \\mapsto (x_1, \\ldots, x_m)$, the normal form of a **submersion** (local projection).\n\n⚠ The hypothesis of **constant rank** cannot be dropped: $f(x) = x^{2}$ ($\\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$) has rank $1$ at points $x \\ne 0$ and rank $0$ at $0$, so its local shape is not a standard form (it is a \"fold\").\n\nThe full proof is on the edge (first build coordinates for $r$ that turn the first $r$ components of $f$ into coordinates, then eliminate the remaining components by the implicit function theorem).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bb278006',
      },
    },

    'def.residue': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In words**: the residue is the coefficient of $(z - a)^{-1}$ in the Laurent expansion of $f$ near an isolated singularity — equivalently, the number obtained by integrating along a small circle and dividing by $2\\pi i$.\n\nLet $a$ be an isolated singularity of $f$, with $f$ holomorphic on $B(a, \\varepsilon) \\setminus \\{a\\}$. Define\n\n$$\\operatorname{Res}(f, a) := \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|\\zeta - a| = r} f(\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta, \\qquad 0 < r < \\varepsilon,$$\n\nthis value is independent of $r$ and equals the coefficient $c_{-1}$ of the Laurent expansion of $f$ at $a$.\n\nFor the **point at infinity** ($f$ holomorphic on $|z| > R$): $\\operatorname{Res}(f, \\infty) := -\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|\\zeta| = \\rho} f(\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = -c_{-1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c017a25',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Why \"residue\": $f$ has a primitive on a punctured neighbourhood iff $c_{-1} = 0$ (because $\\int \\mathrm{d}z/(z - a) = \\log(z - a)$ is multivalued).\n\nSo $c_{-1}$ is exactly \"the number that obstructs the existence of a primitive\" — it **remains** (residue).\n\n⭐ Independence of $r$: $f$ is holomorphic on the annulus between two concentric circles, so by the annulus version of Cauchy–Goursat the two integrals agree.\n\n⚠ The definition at infinity carries a **minus sign**, which comes from the change of coordinates $\\zeta = 1/w$, namely the Jacobi factor $1/w^2$.\n\nIt makes \"the sum of residues on the whole plane is zero\" hold: the sum of residues at finitely many singularities plus $\\operatorname{Res}(f, \\infty)$ equals $0$.\n\n📌 All circle integrals in this section are taken **counterclockwise**;\n\nreversing the orientation changes the overall sign.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eefe14fd',
      },
    },

    'thm.schwarz-pick': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In words**: take a holomorphic map from the disk to itself and use a fractional linear transformation to move the base point (and its image) to the origin; Schwarz's lemma then becomes an estimate for an arbitrary base point, with no distinguished origin.\n\nLet $f: \\mathbb{D} \\to \\mathbb{D}$ be holomorphic and $z_0 \\in \\mathbb{D}$. Then for every $z \\in \\mathbb{D}$,\n\n$$\\left| \\frac{f(z) - f(z_0)}{1 - \\overline{f(z_0)}\\, f(z)} \\right| \\le \\left| \\frac{z - z_0}{1 - \\overline{z_0}\\, z} \\right|,$$\n\nIn differential form (contractivity of the hyperbolic metric):\n\n$$\\frac{|f'(z_0)|}{1 - |f(z_0)|^2} \\le \\frac{1}{1 - |z_0|^2}.$$\n\nEquality in either place (for some $z \\ne z_0$, or for the derivative) holds iff $f$ is a fractional linear automorphism of $\\mathbb{D}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#109627e7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Both formulas come from \"moving the base point to the origin\": $\\varphi_a(z) = (z - a)/(1 - \\overline{a} z)$ is an automorphism of the disk sending $a$ to $0$; form $\\varphi_{f(z_0)} \\circ f \\circ \\varphi_{z_0}^{-1}$, which fixes the origin, apply Schwarz's lemma, then transform back.\n\n⭐ Reading the hyperbolic metric: $\\dfrac{|\\mathrm{d}w|}{1 - |w|^2}$ is the hyperbolic length element on the disk, and the inequality says that a holomorphic self-map **never enlarges** it. When equality holds, the map is called an isometry.\n\n⚠ The inequality holding for all $z_0$ at once is equivalent to \"$f$ is a $1$-Lipschitz map of the hyperbolic metric\". This is the full form of Schwarz's lemma — Schwarz's lemma is just the special case $z_0 = 0$, $f(z_0) = 0$.\n\n📌 Corollary: the automorphisms of $\\mathbb{D}$ are exactly $z \\mapsto e^{i\\theta}\\dfrac{z - a}{1 - \\overline{a} z}$ ($|a| < 1$, $\\theta \\in \\mathbb{R}$). Equality forces $f$ to have this form, and conversely these maps clearly attain equality.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7b89c20b',
      },
    },

    'def.absolute-convergence': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{a_n\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ (or $\\mathbb{C}$). The series $\\sum a_n$ is said to **converge absolutely** iff the series of positive terms\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} |a_n|$$\n\nconverges.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#72e0c699',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Absolute convergence is the watershed of this pack: **an absolutely convergent series can be treated as a finite sum** — it may be reordered arbitrarily (rearrangement-invariant), multiplied term by term (Cauchy product), and grouped arbitrarily.\n\nA conditionally convergent series has **none** of these properties.\n\n⭐ It **reduces** the problem of a general series to one of positive terms: once $\\sum |a_n|$ is known to converge, the whole family of tests (comparison, Kummer, root, integral, condensation) becomes available.\n\n⚠ The converse fails (see \"conditional convergence\"), and the name is misleading: it is not \"a stronger kind of convergence\", but \"convergence plus indifference to signs\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#648f4620',
      },
    },

    'def.power-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "Given a sequence $\\{a_n\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ (or $\\mathbb{C}$) and a centre $x_0$, an expression of the form\n\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n (x - x_0)^{n}$$\n\nis called a **power series**. The set of $x$ at which it converges is its **domain of convergence**; the **interior** of that domain is always an interval (or disk) centred at $x_0$, and each $x$ of it is thus an interior point of convergence.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#557139a9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A power series is where \"series\" and \"function\" meet: it is both a series (every term computable) and a function (on its domain of convergence).\n\nSo the tools of the two previous packs converge here.\n\n⚠ The domain of convergence need not be open: the endpoints require separate discussion (they may converge, diverge, or converge conditionally) — that is exactly what `thm.abel-endpoint` handles.\n\n⭐ A striking phenomenon: **inside its domain of convergence a power series has no \"wild\" behaviour at all** (it is infinitely differentiable, see \"term-by-term differentiation\"), in sharp contrast with general series of functions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6f568aec',
      },
    },

    'def.log-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "$\\exp$ is strictly increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$ (because $(\\exp x)' = \\exp x > 0$), with image $(0, +\\infty)$. Define $\\log : (0, +\\infty) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ as its inverse:\n\n$$\\log := (\\exp|_{\\mathbb{R}})^{-1}, \\qquad e := \\exp 1 = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n!}$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\\log(xy) = \\log x + \\log y, \\qquad \\frac{d}{dx}\\log x = \\frac{1}{x}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e9773ff7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The inverse of a continuous strictly monotone function is continuous and monotone (see \"continuity of the inverse\"), and its derivative comes from the inverse function theorem.\n\n⚠ The domain of $\\log$ is $(0, +\\infty)$ — on $\\mathbb{R}$, $\\exp$ never takes non-positive values, so $\\log$ has no negative inputs either (the complex logarithm needs a separate branch, where branch cuts appear).\n\n⭐ The two familiar faces of $e$ are unified here: $e = \\exp 1 = \\sum 1/n!$ and $e = \\lim_n (1 + 1/n)^{n}$ imply each other, but the former is the definition and the latter a theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5d54967e',
      },
    },

    'thm.stokes': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: raise the fundamental theorem of calculus to arbitrary dimension — the integral over the boundary equals the integral of the exterior derivative inside; one formula absorbs Green, Gauss–Ostrogradsky and Kelvin–Stokes.\n\nLet $M$ be a compact, oriented smooth $n$-manifold with boundary, and let $\\omega$ be a form on $M$ of degree $(n-1)$. Require $\\omega$ to be smooth up to the boundary $\\partial M$ and $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ to be integrable on $M$ (since $\\partial M$ is a null set, it is enough that $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ be integrable on the interior). More generally, it suffices that $\\omega$ be $C^{1}$ in the interior, that $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ be integrable, and that $\\omega$ have a continuous trace on $\\partial M$. Then\n\n$$\\int_{M}\\mathrm{d}\\omega = \\int_{\\partial M}\\omega ,$$\n\nwhere $\\partial M$ carries the **induced orientation** (outward normal first, then the orientation of $M$), and $\\partial M$ is allowed to be empty (then the right-hand side is zero).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#398577c9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof route**: use a partition of unity to reduce to a cube in the half-space $\\mathbb{H}^{n} = \\{x^{n}\\le 0\\}$; write $\\omega$ as $\\sum_{i}(-1)^{i-1}f_{i}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\widehat{\\mathrm{d}x^{i}}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$ (a hat means that factor is omitted), so that $\\mathrm{d}\\omega = \\sum_{i}\\partial_{i}f_{i}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$.\n\nFor $i<n$ the components have compact support in the corresponding coordinate direction, so their integrals vanish;\n\nonly the $i = n$ component remains, and the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus integrates it to $f_{n}(x^{1},\\ldots,x^{n-1},0)$ — which is precisely the integral of $\\omega$ over the boundary.\n\n⚠️ **\"Continuous up to the boundary\" is not enough; differentiability and integrability must be stated explicitly**: for $n = 1$ and $M = [0,1]$ the theorem requires $\\omega = f\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ to satisfy the fundamental theorem of calculus $f(1)-f(0) = \\int_{0}^{1}f\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. But the Cantor function $f$ is continuous, $f(0)=0$, $f(1)=1$, and yet on $[0,1]$ it is differentiable almost everywhere with derivative $0$ almost everywhere (it is not even absolutely continuous) — the right-hand integral is $0$, while the left-hand side is $1$. So \"$f$ continuous\" does not guarantee the formula; the hypotheses here ($C^{1}$ up to the boundary plus $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ integrable) reduce, for $n=1$, to \"$f$ absolutely continuous\", which is precisely the correct condition for the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n**List of special cases**: $n = 1$ is the fundamental theorem of calculus; $n = 2$ is Green's formula; the two faces for $n = 3$ are Gauss–Ostrogradsky (flux) and Kelvin–Stokes (circulation). They are not four theorems but one.\n\n**Relation to Gauss–Bonnet**: on a surface $K\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\mathrm{d}\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}$ (Gauss's equation, `thm.gauss-equation`), so the global Gauss–Bonnet formula is Stokes plus the boundary term $\\int k_{g}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$ — the two theorems share the same root at this level.\n\n⚠️ **Boundary orientation is the easiest place to err**: the convention is \"outward normal first\", i.e. the orientation of $\\partial M$ is given by \"outward normal $\\wedge$ orientation of $M$\". A sign error changes the whole formula by a minus sign, while every special case still \"looks right\".\n\n**Why orientability is needed**: both sides depend on the orientation and change sign together, so the identity does not depend on the choice of orientation; but without an orientation one cannot even write down \"the integral\" (`def.integral-form`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76b3baba',
      },
    },

    'thm.rodrigues': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $v \\in T_{p}U$, $v \\ne 0$, and set $k := k_{n}(v)$. Then the following three conditions are equivalent:\n\n1. $\\mathrm{d}r(v)$ is a **principal direction**, with principal curvature $k$;\n2. $\\mathrm{d}n(v) = -k\\,\\mathrm{d}r(v)$, i.e. $W(\\mathrm{d}r(v)) = k\\,\\mathrm{d}r(v)$;\n3. $k_{n}$ attains a stationary value in the direction $v$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5685d4ae',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is elegant**: $W(u) = ku$ is linear algebra, $\\mathrm{d}n(v) = -k\\,\\mathrm{d}r(v)$ is geometry — it says that \"along a principal direction the rotation of the normal is **proportional** to the motion of the tangent, with the principal curvature as the factor\" — same or opposite direction depending on the sign of $k$ (for the sphere with outward normal $n = r$, $k = -1$, hence $\\mathrm{d}n = \\mathrm{d}r$ points the **same way**).\n\n**Coordinate form**: in a curvature-line parametrization $n_{u} = -k_{1}r_{u}$, $n_{v} = -k_{2}r_{v}$. Written with $\\mathrm{d}r, \\mathrm{d}n$, no curvature-line net is required.\n\n⚠️ The minus sign comes from $W = -\\mathrm{d}n$. If a book takes $W = \\mathrm{d}n$, its Rodrigues formula has no minus sign — before copying a formula, check whether the sign is placed in $W$ or in $II$.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the geometric formula rests on definitions, the stationary criterion on self-adjointness of the shape operator):\n- (1) $\\iff$ (2): principal directions are defined as nonzero eigenvectors of the shape operator, and together with $W\\circ\\mathrm dr=-\\mathrm dn$ this is a definitional unfolding; the sign depends on the conventions for the normal and the shape operator.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): the normal curvature is the Rayleigh quotient of a self-adjoint operator; differentiating on the unit tangent circle, an eigenvector makes the first derivative vanish — this is not merely terminology.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (2): the derivative vanishes for all unit tangent variations perpendicular to that direction, so $Wu-ku$ is parallel to $u$, and then $k=\\langle Wu,u\\rangle/\\langle u,u\\rangle$ gives zero.\n- One needs a sufficiently smooth regular surface, a unit normal and $v\\ne0$, so that the first fundamental form is positive definite and $W$ is self-adjoint; the zero vector has no direction or normal curvature. A general non-self-adjoint operator fails the criterion, e.g. a nonzero antisymmetric plane rotation has identically zero Rayleigh quotient but no real eigen-direction.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d81cb479',
      },
    },

    'thm.kummer': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\sum a_n$ be a series of **positive terms** ($a_n > 0$), and $\\{c_n\\}$ a sequence of positive numbers. Put\n\n$$K_n := c_n \\cdot \\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}} - c_{n+1}$$\n\nThen:\n\n- **Convergence**: if $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} K_n > 0$, then $\\sum a_n$ converges;\n- **Divergence**: if $\\sum \\frac{1}{c_n}$ diverges and $K_n \\le 0$ for all sufficiently large $n$, then $\\sum a_n$ diverges.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce52db6c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **master theorem for positive-term tests**: every \"ratio-type\" test is obtained from it by choosing a different $\\{c_n\\}$ —\n\n- $c_n = 1$: **d'Alembert (ratio) test** — $\\lim \\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} < 1$ means convergence, $> 1$ means divergence;\n\n- $c_n = n$: **Raabe's test** — $\\lim n\\bigl(\\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}} - 1\\bigr) > 1$ gives convergence;\n\n- $c_n = n \\ln n$: **Bertrand's test** — one finer step than Raabe;\n\n- $c_n$ given by a Gauss expansion: **Gauss's test** — handles the class $a_n/a_{n+1} = 1 + \\frac{\\mu}{n} + O(n^{-1-\\delta})$.\n\n⚠ The convergence branch requires $\\liminf K_n > 0$ (not $\\lim K_n$, and not merely some $K_n > 0$).\n\n⚠ The divergence branch has the extra hypothesis \"$\\sum 1/c_n$ diverges\" — for $c_n = 1$ this is $\\sum 1$, for $c_n = n$ it is the harmonic series; neither is free.\n\n⚠ **What does not follow from it**: the comparison test (more basic), the root test (which goes through $\\limsup$ and comparison with a geometric series, and is not mutually derivable with Kummer; rather, \"existence of the ratio limit $\\implies$ existence of the root limit, and they are equal\", so the root test is stronger), the integral test and the condensation test (one replaces the sum by an improper integral, the other groups terms by $2^k$).\n\nEach of these four gets its own node.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e18a47c4',
      },
    },

    'prop.zero-isolated': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be holomorphic on a region $\\Omega$, with $f(z_{0}) = 0$, and suppose that $f$ is not identically zero in any neighbourhood of $z_{0}$. Then there are a **unique** integer $m \\ge 1$ and a function $g$ holomorphic near $z_{0}$ such that\n\n$$f(z) = (z - z_{0})^{m}\\, g(z), \\qquad g(z_{0}) \\ne 0.$$\n\n$m$ is called the **order** of the zero at $z_{0}$. Consequently $z_{0}$ is an isolated zero of $f$, and the zero set of a nonzero holomorphic function has **no accumulation point** in $\\Omega$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2378224d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: expand $f$ in a Taylor series at $z_{0}$ (`thm.taylor-complex`): $f(z) = \\sum_{n \\ge 1} a_{n}(z - z_{0})^{n}$.\n\nSince $f$ is not identically zero, not all coefficients vanish; take the least $m$ with $a_{m} \\ne 0$, then $f(z) = (z - z_{0})^{m}\\bigl(a_{m} + a_{m+1}(z - z_{0}) + \\cdots\\bigr)$; the factor in parentheses takes at $z_{0}$ the value $a_{m} \\ne 0$, and this is the required $g$.\n\n**Isolation**: $g(z_{0}) \\ne 0$ and $g$ is continuous, so on some neighbourhood of $z_{0}$ one has $g \\ne 0$; hence $f$ vanishes there only at $z_{0}$ (on the punctured neighbourhood $(z - z_{0})^{m} \\ne 0$).\n\n**Accumulation**: if the zeros had an accumulation point $z^{*} \\in \\Omega$, then $z^{*}$ would itself be a zero (continuity), and every neighbourhood of it would contain zeros, contradicting isolation — so the only possibility is $f \\equiv 0$. This is one half of the identity theorem.\n\n⭐ **Where the order is used in the book**: poles, residues and the argument principle (counting zeros with multiplicity) all need it. ⚠️ Here \"order\" and the \"order\" of a pole are two sides of one fact: $f$ has a zero of order $m$ at $z_{0}$ $\\iff$ $1/f$ has a pole of order $m$ at $z_{0}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a0239289',
      },
    },

    'thm.winding-homotopy': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gamma_{0}, \\gamma_{1}$ be closed curves in $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{a\\}$ that are homotopic there (a homotopy $H : [0,1]^{2} \\to \\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{a\\}$ whose two ends give $\\gamma_{0}, \\gamma_{1}$ respectively, and for every $s \\in [0,1]$ the curve $H(\\cdot, s)$ is closed, i.e. $H(0, s) = H(1, s)$). Then\n\n$$n(\\gamma_{0}, a) = n(\\gamma_{1}, a).$$\n\nCorollary (the homotopy form of Cauchy's theorem): if $f$ is holomorphic on a region $\\Omega$, and $\\gamma_{0}, \\gamma_{1}$ are homotopic in $\\Omega$, then $\\displaystyle\\oint_{\\gamma_{0}} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = \\oint_{\\gamma_{1}} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1b20b1ee',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof for the winding number**: for the homotopy parameter $s$ define $N(s) := n(H(s, \\cdot), a)$. By the integral representation $N(s) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{H(s,\\cdot)} \\mathrm{d}z/(z-a)$, both the integrand and the domain depend continuously on $s$, so $N$ is continuous; but $N$ takes only integer values, and continuous plus integer-valued $\\implies$ constant. Hence $N(0) = N(1)$.\n\n**The Cauchy form**: after rendering $\\Omega$ simply connected, $f\\,\\mathrm{d}z$ can be deformed continuously along the homotopy without changing the integral; differentiating with respect to $s$ gives $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}s}\\oint_{H(s,\\cdot)} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 0$; this is the same route as \"$f$ has a primitive\", see `prop.single-valued-branch`.\n\n⭐ **It is the first hinge between complex analysis and topology**: the winding number is a homotopy invariant, so \"holomorphic $\\implies$ the contour integral depends only on the homotopy class\" — this is why Cauchy's theorem holds for **any** curve that can be contracted to a point, with no convexity of the region required. The complex-analytic proof of Brouwer's fixed-point theorem (`thm.brouwer-fixed-point`) applies this directly to $\\gamma(t) = e^{2\\pi i t}$.\n\n⚠️ The homotopy must take place in $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{a\\}$: if it were allowed to pass through $a$, the winding number would jump.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cb15b007',
      },
    },

    'prop.single-valued-branch': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $D \\subset \\mathbb{C}$ be a region and $F$ a multivalued function on $D$ (assigning to each point a nonempty value set $F(z)$). A **single-valued branch** of $F$ is a continuous function $f : D \\to \\mathbb{C}$ such that $f(z) \\in F(z)$ for all $z \\in D$; if $F$ comes from analytic continuation, the branch is automatically holomorphic.\n\nFor the **logarithm** ($F(z) = \\{\\, w : e^{w} = z \\,\\}$) the following three conditions are equivalent:\n\n1. $\\log$ has a holomorphic single-valued branch on $D$;\n2. $1/z$ has a primitive on $D$;\n3. for every closed curve $\\gamma$ in $D$, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{\\gamma} \\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{z} = 0$.\n\nA sufficient condition: $D$ is simply connected and $0 \\notin D$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cfe24ac5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (1 $\\implies$ 3)**: if $e^{f} = z$, then $f' = 1/z$, hence $\\oint_{\\gamma} \\mathrm{d}z/z = \\oint_{\\gamma} f'\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 0$ (the contour integral of a holomorphic function in a simply connected region vanishes; here we use the \"has a primitive\" version).\n\n**Proof (3 $\\implies$ 2 $\\implies$ 1)**: condition 3 says that the integral along a curve depends only on the endpoints, so we may define $f(z) := \\int_{z_{0}}^{z} \\mathrm{d}\\zeta/\\zeta$, which satisfies $f' = 1/z$; next check $(z e^{-f(z)})' = e^{-f} - z e^{-f}/z = 0$, so $z e^{-f}$ is constant, and adjusting the constant gives $e^{f} = z$.\n\n⭐ **Condition 3 is exactly \"all winding numbers vanish\"** (see `prop.winding-integral`): $\\oint_{\\gamma}\\mathrm{d}z/z = 2\\pi i\\, n(\\gamma, 0)$. So \"does the logarithm have a single-valued branch\" is translated into a statement about whether curves **wind around the origin** — in a simply connected region no closed curve can wind around an outside point, so it holds automatically.\n\n⚠️ Simple connectedness is **not** necessary: on the annulus $\\{1 < |z| < 2\\}$ the function $\\log$ has no single-valued branch, but on a region with two holes removed it may have one — the criterion is always 3, not \"looks simply connected\".\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the branch criterion requires the region to avoid the origin; simple connectedness is only sufficient):\n- single-valued branch $\\iff$ continuous choice of a value at each point: this is a definitional unfolding; the equivalence of the three logarithmic conditions requires $D\\subset\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, otherwise $1/z$ is undefined.\n- (1) $\\implies$ (2) $\\implies$ (3): differentiate $e^f=z$ to get $f'=1/z$, then use the fundamental theorem for primitives; simple connectedness is not required.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (2): use path connectedness of the region and vanishing of closed-loop integrals to make the path integral independent of the path, then differentiate using holomorphy of the integrand.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (1): the derivative of $ze^{-f}$ is zero, hence by connectedness it is a nonzero constant, and adjusting a constant suffices; this is a construction, not a definitional unfolding.\n- simply connected and avoiding zero $\\implies$ (3) uses Cauchy's integral theorem; the annulus $1<|z|<2$ has a closed loop with integral $2\\pi i$ and no logarithmic branch. Simple connectedness is not necessary, e.g. the right half-plane with one point removed still admits a restriction of the principal logarithm.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e9c34992',
      },
    },

    'def.riemann-sphere': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The extended complex plane** $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}} := \\mathbb{C} \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$: add a point $\\infty$ to the complex plane, and declare its neighbourhoods to be $\\{\\, |z| > R \\,\\} \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ ($R > 0$).\n\n**Stereographic projection** identifies the unit sphere $S^{2} \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3}$ with $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$: from the north pole $N = (0,0,1)$, project every point of the sphere except $N$ onto the equatorial plane $w = 0$. For $z = u + iv$,\n\n$$(x, y, w) = \\left(\\frac{2u}{1 + |z|^{2}},\\ \\frac{2v}{1 + |z|^{2}},\\ \\frac{|z|^{2} - 1}{1 + |z|^{2}}\\right).$$\n\nConversely $z = \\dfrac{x + iy}{1 - w}$. The pole $N$ corresponds to $\\infty$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fae2408c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is worth adding a single point**: many statements (fractional linear maps send circles to circles, the Riemann mapping theorem, cross-ratios) need case distinctions when the plane is only $\\mathbb{C}$; after adding $\\infty$, a single case remains — a line is a circle through $\\infty$, and $1/z$ is a rational map defined everywhere on $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n**Where the formula comes from**: parametrize the line through $N$ and a point $(x,y,w)$ of the sphere as $N + t((x,y,w) - N)$, set the third coordinate equal to $0$, solve for $t$ to get $2(x,y)/(1-w)$; then combine $|z|^{2}$ with $w$ and use $x^{2}+y^{2}+w^{2}=1$ to simplify to the clean form above.\n\n⚠️ The $\\infty$ added here is **one point**, not two points $+\\infty$ and $-\\infty$ (that is the one-dimensional real convention, see `def.extended-real`). Complex numbers have no \"sign\", so there is only one point at infinity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#36fb61bb',
      },
    },

    'def.cross-ratio': {
      statement: {
        en: "For pairwise distinct $z_{1}, z_{2}, z_{3}, z_{4} \\in \\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$, define their **cross-ratio**\n\n$$(z_{1}, z_{2}; z_{3}, z_{4}) := \\frac{N}{D},$$\n\nwhere $N := (z_{3} - z_{1})(z_{4} - z_{2})$, $D := (z_{3} - z_{2})(z_{4} - z_{1})$.\n\nWhen $\\infty$ occurs, interpret the expression by a limit (cancel the factors containing $\\infty$).\n\n**Property**: for every fractional linear transformation $T$,\n\n$(Tz_{1}, Tz_{2}; Tz_{3}, Tz_{4}) = (z_{1}, z_{2}; z_{3}, z_{4})$\n\n— the cross-ratio is a **complete invariant** under fractional linear transformations.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#197d5b0f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is invariant**: a fractional linear map $T(z) = (az+b)/(cz+d)$ can be written as a composite of four-point cross-ratios. A direct check: each difference $Tz_{i} - Tz_{j} = \\dfrac{(ad - bc)(z_{i} - z_{j})}{(cz_{i} + d)(cz_{j} + d)}$; substituting into the numerator and denominator of the cross-ratio, $(ad-bc)$ cancels **pairwise** with the factors $(cz_{k}+d)$, leaving the original cross-ratio.\n\n⭐ **It is the coordinate of \"four-point geometry\"**: a triangle is described by side lengths and angles, while four points need one complex quantity — the cross-ratio being real or taking a special value corresponds to the two classical facts \"concyclic\" and \"harmonic division\".\n\n⚠️ The value depends on the **order** of the points: swapping two positions gives $1/\\lambda$, $1-\\lambda$, and four more values — six ways of writing the same \"non-harmonic ratio\". Fix the order before writing a formula.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d144277a',
      },
    },

    'thm.fermat': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be defined on a neighbourhood of $x_0$, differentiable at $x_0$, and suppose $x_0$ is an **interior** local extremum of $f$. Then\n\n$$f'(x_0) = 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b7de8f56',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **prime mover** of the whole differential calculus: it translates the order-theoretic notion of \"extremum\" into the algebraic condition \"the derivative vanishes\", so finding extrema becomes solving $f' = 0$.\n\n⚠ None of the three hypotheses can be dropped: differentiability ($|x|$ has an interior minimum at $0$ but is not differentiable there), interiority ($f(x) = x$ on $[0, 1]$ attains its minimum at the endpoint $0$, where $f'(0) = 1 \\ne 0$), and extremality.\n\n⚠ **The converse fails**: $f'(x_0) = 0$ does not guarantee an extremum ($f(x) = x^{3}$ at $0$).\n\nPoints with $f' = 0$ are called **stationary points**; they must be checked alongside non-differentiable points and endpoints.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b4b3dcd6',
      },
    },

    'thm.minkowski': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $1 \\le p < \\infty$. Then for $f, g \\in L^p$:\n\n$$\\|f + g\\|_p \\le \\|f\\|_p + \\|g\\|_p$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b46b4639',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$p = 1$ is just the triangle inequality for integrals, and is obvious.\n\nFor general $p$:\n\n$$\\int |f+g|^p = \\int |f+g| \\cdot |f+g|^{p-1} \\le \\int (|f| + |g|) |f+g|^{p-1} \\le \\left( \\|f\\|_p + \\|g\\|_p \\right) \\left\\| |f+g|^{p-1} \\right\\|_q$$\n\nwhere the last step applies Hölder once to $|f| \\cdot |f+g|^{p-1}$ and once to $|g| \\cdot |f+g|^{p-1}$. Now note\n\n$$\\left\\| |f+g|^{p-1} \\right\\|_q^{\\,q} = \\int |f+g|^{(p-1)q} = \\int |f+g|^p$$\n\nCancel one factor $\\|f+g\\|_p^{p/q}$ from both sides.\n\n⭐ With this, $\\|\\cdot\\|_p$ is genuinely a **norm** — the prerequisite for \"$L^p$ is a Banach space\" below.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f22e23da',
      },
    },

    'thm.darboux-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be bounded on $[a, b]$. Then $f$ is Riemann integrable iff\n\n$$\\inf_{P} U(f, P) = \\sup_{P} L(f, P)$$\n\nand in that case this common value is $\\int_a^b f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#96f7733f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This replaces \"the limit exists\" by \"the two suprema coincide\": the former requires discussing the choice of sample points, the latter **does not mention sample points at all** — that is the whole reason the Darboux form is convenient.\n\n⭐ Monotonicity of the two bounds: if $P'$ is a refinement of $P$, then $L(f, P) \\le L(f, P') \\le U(f, P') \\le U(f, P)$ (on each subinterval $m_i$ can only increase and $M_i$ can only decrease).\n\nHence for **any** two partitions $P, Q$ one has $L(f, P) \\le U(f, Q)$: take the common refinement $P \\cup Q$.\n\n⚠ Therefore $\\sup_P L \\le \\inf_P U$ is automatic; what the criterion really requires is the **reverse inequality**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0e8daafd',
      },
    },

    'thm.absolute-implies-convergent': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $\\sum |a_n|$ converges, then $\\sum a_n$ converges, and\n\n$$\\Bigl| \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n \\Bigr| \\le \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} |a_n|$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6d27e8be',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof uses the triangle inequality once (note $|\\sum_{k=m+1}^{n} a_k| \\le \\sum_{k=m+1}^{n} |a_k|$) plus the Cauchy criterion for series.\n\n⚠ **The converse fails**: the alternating harmonic series converges but not absolutely.\n\n⭐ The real use of the theorem is **reduction**: to prove that a general series converges, first prove that $\\sum |a_n|$ converges — the latter is a positive-term series, for which far more tools are available.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e876ae6f',
      },
    },

    'def.harmonic': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In words**: a real-valued function with continuous second partials satisfying $\\Delta u = 0$. Loosely: a function that is \"nowhere more convex or more concave than its surroundings\".\n\nLet $\\Omega \\subset \\mathbb{R}^2 \\cong \\mathbb{C}$ be a region and $u: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{R}$ of class $C^2$. If\n\n$$\\Delta u := \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial y^2} = 0$$\n\nholds everywhere on $\\Omega$, then $u$ is called a **harmonic function** on $\\Omega$.\n\nEquivalent characterization (local): $u$ is the real part of some holomorphic function (always possible on a disk, and also on simply connected regions, see the node on \"harmonic conjugates\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#de993de6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The real and imaginary parts of a holomorphic function are harmonic: by the Cauchy–Riemann equations $u_x = v_y$, $u_y = -v_x$, one gets\n\n$$\\Delta u = (u_x)_x + (u_y)_y = (v_y)_x + (-v_x)_y = v_{xy} - v_{yx} = 0,$$\n\nthe last step using equality of mixed partials ($v$ is sufficiently smooth).\n\n⭐ Examples: $\\operatorname{Re} z^n$, $x^2 - y^2$, $\\ln|z|$ (on $\\mathbb{C}^{\\times}$), $e^{x}\\cos y$.\n\n⚠ Counterexamples: $|z|^2 = x^2 + y^2$ is not harmonic, $\\Delta(x^2 + y^2) = 4$; nor is $x^3$, since $\\Delta x^3 = 6x$.\n\n⚠ The definition only asks for $C^2$, but harmonic functions are automatically $C^{\\infty}$ and real analytic — a direct consequence of \"being the real part of a holomorphic function\".\n\n📌 Harmonic functions lack the rigidity of holomorphic ones: the product of two harmonic functions is in general neither harmonic nor holomorphic; only $u + v$ and $cu$ are.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e03bf232',
      },
    },

    'prop.conformal-iff': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In words**: a holomorphic function is angle-preserving wherever its derivative is nonzero — the angle at which two curves meet there is the same in the image; conversely, a real-differentiable angle-preserving map must be holomorphic.\n\n**(i)** Let $f$ be holomorphic near $z_0$ and $f'(z_0) \\ne 0$. Then $f$ is **conformal** at $z_0$: for any two smooth curves through $z_0$ with **nonzero velocity at $z_0$**, the angle between their images at $f(z_0)$ has the **same size and the same orientation**. (⚠ \"nonzero velocity\" cannot be dropped: $\\gamma_{1}(t) = t^{2}$ and $\\gamma_{2}(t) = i t^{2}$ have no tangent at $t = 0$, so there is no angle to speak of.)\n\n**(ii)** Conversely, if $f$ is real differentiable at $z_0$ and conformal (orientation-preserving) at $z_0$, then $f$ satisfies the Cauchy–Riemann equations at $z_0$, hence is complex differentiable.\n\nTherefore **conformal map = holomorphic with nowhere-vanishing derivative**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7865a875',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ (i) needs one line: $f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0) \\approx f'(z_0)\\,h$.\n\nMultiplication by the nonzero complex number $f'(z_0) = |f'|e^{i\\theta}$ is \"rotation by $\\theta$ plus dilation $|f'|$\" — a similarity, so angles and ratios of lengths are unchanged.\n\n⚠ (ii) is not a tautology: write the real differential as $\\Delta z \\mapsto \\Delta f$, and being a similarity forces $\\Delta z$ and $\\overline{\\Delta z}$ to have the same length but be orthogonal; equivalently $\\partial f/\\partial \\bar z(z_0) = 0$, the Cauchy–Riemann equation in conjugate form.\n\n⚠ \"Nowhere zero\" is essential: $f(z) = z^2$ doubles angles at $0$ ($0$), and $f(z) = \\bar z$ is angle-preserving but not holomorphic.\n\nThe fractional linear map $f(z) = (az+b)/(cz+d)$ has derivative $(ad-bc)/(cz+d)^2$, and $ad - bc \\ne 0$ guarantees it is conformal everywhere.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e536eb42',
      },
    },

    'prop.exp-local-inverse': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every $z_{0} \\in \\mathbb{C}$ there are a neighbourhood $U$ of $z_{0}$ and an open set $V$ such that $\\exp : U \\to V$ is biholomorphic, and its inverse\n\n$$w \\longmapsto \\log w := \\log|w| + i\\arg_{U} w$$\n\nis holomorphic, where $\\arg_{U} w$ is chosen in the interval $(\\operatorname{Im} z_{0} - \\pi,\\ \\operatorname{Im} z_{0} + \\pi)$.\n\nEquivalent formulation: $\\exp$ is locally biholomorphic (a local isomorphism), and $\\exp$ is a covering map $\\mathbb{C} \\to \\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$ with fibre $2\\pi i\\mathbb{Z}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#af151b8d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: $\\exp' = \\exp \\ne 0$ holds everywhere, so the inverse function theorem (complex version: if $f'(z_{0}) \\ne 0$ then the map is locally biholomorphic and the inverse has derivative $1/f'$) gives local biholomorphy. The explicit inverse is read off from Euler's formula: $e^{x + iy} = w$ gives $x = \\log|w|$ and $y = \\arg w + 2k\\pi$, and taking the branch lying in the prescribed interval gives the formula.\n\n⭐ **Local versus global**: a local inverse always exists, but a **global** $\\log$ does not (going once around the origin adds $2\\pi$ to the argument). Whether the pieces glue into a global function is exactly the question answered by `prop.single-valued-branch`, and the answer is that the winding number must be $0$.\n\n⚠ Number of sheets: $e^{z}$ maps each horizontal strip $\\{a < \\operatorname{Im} z < a + 2\\pi\\}$ biholomorphically onto $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{0\\}$, so the logarithm has infinitely many branches, any two differing by $2\\pi i k$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#57ff56ce',
      },
    },

    'thm.lebesgue-set-full': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $f \\in L^1_{loc}$, then\n\n$$m(L_f^c) = 0$$\n\nthat is, **almost every point is a Lebesgue point**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bae0067d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The idea of the proof is neat: for each complex number $c$, apply the differentiation theorem to the function $|f(x) - c|$, obtaining \"outside a null set $E_c$, $\\lim_{1/m(B)}\\int|f(y) - c|dy = |f(x) - c|$\".\n\nThen take **a countable dense subset $\\mathbb{C}$ of $D$** and let $E = \\bigcup_{c \\in D} E_c$ (a countable union of null sets, still null). For $x \\notin E$ and any $\\varepsilon > 0$, choose $c \\in D$ with $|f(x) - c| < \\varepsilon$, so\n\n$$\\lim_{r\\to0} \\frac{1}{m(B(x, r))} \\int_{B(x, r)} |f(y) - f(x)| dy \\le |f(x) - c| + \\varepsilon < 2\\varepsilon$$\n\nLetting $\\varepsilon \\to 0$ gives the claim. ∎\n\n⭐ The step \"take a countable dense subset\" is a typical use of countability: it reduces uncountably many conditions to a countable union of null sets.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b1ea5fc',
      },
    },

    'prop.ac-iff-measure-ac': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F \\in NBV$. Then $F$ is **absolutely continuous** iff its associated measure $\\mu_F$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure:\n\n$F$ $\\iff \\mu_F \\ll  m$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7bdfb58e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**($(\\Longleftarrow )$)** This follows from the ε–δ characterization of absolute continuity of measures: $\\varepsilon$ and $\\delta$ with $\\mu_F \\ll  m$ give \"$m(E) < \\delta \\implies |\\mu_F(E)| < \\varepsilon$\". Taking $E$ to be a finite disjoint union of intervals yields absolute continuity of the function directly.\n\n**($(\\implies )$)** Let $E \\in \\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{R}$ with $m(E) = 0$.\n\nWe must show $\\mu_F(E) = 0$.\n\nTake a decreasing sequence of open sets $U_1 \\supseteq U_2 \\supseteq \\cdots \\supseteq E$ with $m(U_k) < \\delta$ (by regularity).\n\nEach $U_k$ is a countable disjoint union of intervals; absolute continuity of the function and $m(U_k) < \\delta$ give $|\\mu_F(U_j)| < \\varepsilon$;\n\nthen upper continuity of the measure and $\\bigcap U_k = E$ give $|\\mu_F(E)| \\le \\varepsilon$.\n\nSince $\\varepsilon$ is arbitrary, $\\mu_F(E) = 0$.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ This connects the two notions of \"absolute continuity\": for functions and for measures. From now on, the calculus notion of an absolutely continuous function has a measure-theoretic interpretation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2535cb4',
      },
    },

    'def.complex-elementary': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $z = x + iy \\in \\mathbb{C}$, define by **power series**\n\n$$e^{z} := \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^{n}}{n!},$$\n\n$$\\cos z := \\frac{e^{iz} + e^{-iz}}{2},\\qquad \\sin z := \\frac{e^{iz} - e^{-iz}}{2i},$$\n\nand set $\\cosh z := \\cos(iz)$, $\\sinh z := -i\\sin(iz)$. All three series converge absolutely on the whole plane, and $e^{z} = e^{x}(\\cos y + i \\sin y)$, $e^{z + 2\\pi i} = e^{z}$.\n\nFor $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$ and a logarithm $\\operatorname{Log} z$ with a chosen branch (see `def.logarithm-branch`), define the power $z^{\\alpha} := e^{\\alpha \\operatorname{Log} z}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6a610514',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why define them this way**: the real exponential is $\\sum x^{n}/n!$, and the power series still converges on the complex plane — replacing $x$ by $z$ suffices, with no new axioms. The trigonometric definitions are the same: combining $e^{iz}$ and $e^{-iz}$ gives Euler's formula.\n\n**Holomorphy**: a power series may be differentiated term by term inside its disc of convergence, and these three series have radius $\\infty$; hence they are holomorphic on $\\mathbb{C}$, and $(e^{z})' = e^{z}$, $(\\sin z)' = \\cos z$, $(\\cos z)' = -\\sin z$.\n\n⭐ **Euler's formula** $e^{iy} = \\cos y + i\\sin y$ is a direct product of these definitions: $e^{z} = e^{x}e^{iy}$ separates \"modulus $e^{x}$\" from \"argument $y$\".\n\n⚠ **Differences from the real case**: $|e^{z}| = e^{x}$ depends only on the real part, so $e^{z}$ **never takes** $\\mathbb{C}$ as a value on $0$; $\\sin z$ and $\\cos z$ are **unbounded** on the complex plane ($|\\sin(iy)| \\to \\infty$), which does not conflict with $|\\sin| \\le 1$ on the real axis — boundedness on the real axis is not a general property of holomorphic functions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aa361fc2',
      },
    },

    'thm.gleason': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **projective objects** in $\\mathbf{CHaus}$ are exactly the Stonean spaces.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6426fe3b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it says**: among compact Hausdorff spaces, those with the lifting property against surjections (projective objects) are **exactly** the **Stonean** (extremally disconnected) spaces.\n\n⭐ **Why it matters**: in condensed mathematics, \"$\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is projective $\\iff$ $S$ is Stonean\" is a direct consequence — a topological property (extreme disconnectedness) is translated into a categorical one (projectivity).\n\n⚠ Keep the two separation properties apart: **Stonean** (extremally disconnected: the closure of an open set is open) is strictly stronger than **Stone** (totally disconnected); they are different nodes here (`def.stone-space` treats the latter).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#994d8666',
      },
    },

    'def.profinite': {
      statement: {
        en: "**A profinite space** is a topological space obtained as the limit of finite discrete spaces along a **directed** system:\n\n$$X \\;=\\; \\varprojlim_{i \\in I} X_{i},$$\n\nwhere each $X_{i}$ is a **finite discrete** space and $I$ is a **directed** set.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dc5b575e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"Pro-finite\" = projective limit of finite objects. Intuitively it is built from \"increasingly fine finite resolutions\": $X_{i}$ is what the space looks like at resolution level $i$, and $I$ being directed guarantees that any two levels can be refined simultaneously.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6f6bb4c4',
      },
    },

    'thm.orbit-stabilizer': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let a finite group $G$ act on a set $X$, and let $x \\in X$. Then $\\operatorname{Stab}(x) \\le G$ and\n\n$$\\bigl| \\operatorname{Orb}(x) \\bigr| = [G : \\operatorname{Stab}(x)] = \\frac{|G|}{|\\operatorname{Stab}(x)|}$$\n\nIn particular $|\\operatorname{Orb}(x)|$ divides $|G|$, and\n\n$$|G| = \\bigl| \\operatorname{Orb}(x) \\bigr| \\cdot \\bigl| \\operatorname{Stab}(x) \\bigr|$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e9535d1f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem is the hub of the whole pack: it converts \"**counting the size of an orbit**\", a geometric/combinatorial matter, into \"**computing an index**\", a group-theoretic one.\n\nThe class equation, Sylow and Burnside's lemma are all direct corollaries.\n\n⭐ One-line mnemonic: **orbit size $\\times$ stabilizer size = group size**.\n\n⚠ Only the quantity $[G : \\operatorname{Stab}(x)]$ is used, so the theorem holds for **infinite groups** as well (then it must be read as \"the orbit is in bijection with the coset set $G/G_x$\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e7ec4a58',
      },
    },

    'thm.pid-injective-divisible': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a **principal ideal domain** (for example $\\mathbb{Z}$ or $k[x]$), and $M$ an $R$-module. Then $M$ is injective iff $M$ is **divisible**, i.e.\n\n$$\\forall a \\ne 0,\\ \\forall y \\in M,\\ \\exists x \\in M, \\qquad a x = y .$$\n\nIn particular: a $\\mathbb{Z}$-module is injective iff it is a divisible group.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f48f1dc0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Divisible $\\implies$ injective** (via Baer's criterion): let $J \\subseteq R$ be an ideal and $f : J \\to I$.\n\nIn a PID $J = (a)$.\n\nIf $a = 0$ then $J = 0$, take $x = 0$;\n\nif $a \\ne 0$, set $y := f(a)$, and by divisibility choose $x \\in I$ with $a x = y$.\n\nThen for $r a \\in J$ we have $f(r a) = r f(a) = r a x = (r a) x$ ✓, i.e. $f$ is given by \"multiplication by $x$\".\n\n**Injective $\\implies$ divisible**: let $a \\ne 0$ and $y \\in I$. Apply Baer's criterion to the ideal $(a)$ and the map $a \\mapsto y$ to obtain $x$ with $a x = y$ ✓.\n\nThe case $\\mathbb{Z}$ is just $R = \\mathbb{Z}$ (a PID); then $R$-modules are abelian groups (`prop.zmod-is-ab`) ✓.\n\n⭐ One-line mnemonic: **\"divide by $a$\" is \"extend along $(a) \\rightarrowtail R$\"**. On a PID the ideals have the simplest possible structure, so Baer's criterion degenerates into a division problem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4d8d9882',
      },
    },

    'def.divisible-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "An abelian group $D$ is **divisible** if for every $d \\in D$ and every $0 \\ne n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ the equation\n\n$$n x = d$$\n\nhas a solution in $D$; equivalently, the multiplication map $n \\cdot : D \\to D$ is **surjective** for every $n \\ne 0$.\n\nA $\\mathbb{Z}$-module is just an abelian group (`prop.zmod-is-ab`), so \"divisible group\" = \"divisible $\\mathbb{Z}$-module\"; the definition carries over verbatim to modules over any ring (replace $\\mathbb{Z}$ by $R$ and $n$ by a non-zero-divisor).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e6af2174',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**How to read it**: divisible means \"division by any positive integer is always possible\". $\\mathbb{Q}$ has this property; $\\mathbb{Z}$ does not ($2x = 1$ has no solution); $\\mathbb{Z}/2$ does not either — $2x = 0$ always has a solution, but $2x = 1$ does not. The point is that the $n$-fold map is **surjective** (onto the whole group), not merely that the equation is solvable for $0$.\n\n**Structure theorem**: every divisible group is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{Q}^{(I)} \\oplus \\bigoplus_{p}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z}[1/p]/\\mathbb{Z}\\bigr)^{(J_{p})}$, so a divisible group is determined entirely by these \"ranks\".\n\n⭐ Over $\\mathbb{Z}$: \"divisible\" and \"injective\" are the same thing (`thm.pid-injective-divisible`). Intuitively \"divide by $n$\" is exactly \"extend along $n\\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrowtail \\mathbb{Z}$\".\n\n📌 Quotients: a quotient of a divisible group is divisible (`prop.divisible-quotient`); **subgroups** need not be ($\\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#75d56409',
      },
    },

    'def.flat-module': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a ring and $F$ a **right** $R$-module. $F$ is **flat** if the functor\n\n$$F \\otimes_{R} - : R\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{Ab}$$\n\nis **exact**. Concretely: for every short exact sequence $0 \\to M_{1} \\to M \\to M_{2} \\to 0$, after tensoring\n\n$$0 \\to F\\otimes_{R} M_{1} \\to F\\otimes_{R} M \\to F\\otimes_{R} M_{2} \\to 0$$\n\nis still exact. (The right end is automatically surjective because $\\otimes$ is right exact; so the real requirement is only \"preserves injectivity\".)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fac71bf3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**There are many equivalent formulations**; the usual ones are:\n\n- $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R}(F, -) = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$ (see \"flat iff all higher Tor vanish\");\n- for every **finitely generated ideal** $I \\subseteq R$, the map $F\\otimes I \\to F\\otimes R$ is injective (**Lambek's criterion** — only finitely generated ideals need be checked);\n- $F = \\varinjlim F_{i}$ with $F_{i}$ finitely generated free (see \"flat modules are filtered colimits of finitely generated free modules\").\n\n**Which modules are flat**: free modules, projective modules (a projective is a direct summand of a free module, and direct summands preserve flatness), localizations $S^{-1}R$ (tensoring commutes with localization, and $\\otimes$ is exact over $R$). Over an integral domain a flat module is necessarily **torsion-free**; over a PID (more generally a Bézout domain) the converse also holds, so there \"flat = torsion-free\" ✓.\n\n⚠️ **Flat and projective are not equivalent**: $\\mathbb{Q}$ is flat over $\\mathbb{Z}$ ($\\mathbb{Q}$ is torsion-free) but **not projective** ($\\mathbb{Q}$ is not free, nor a direct summand of a free module). So \"flat\" is indeed one level weaker than \"projective\".\n\n⚠️ **Not the same as flatness in a topos**: \"flat abelian group\" in a category of sheaves is a different definition (a double condition using filtered colimits / exactness of tensoring), see \"flat abelian sheaves\". This entry refers only to module categories.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2216216d',
      },
    },

    'def.pid': {
      statement: {
        en: "An integral domain $A$ is a **principal ideal domain** (PID) if every ideal is principal: for every ideal $I$ there is $a$ such that $I = (a) = aA$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f69cb6c6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Examples: $\\mathbb{Z}$ (every ideal is $(n)$), $K[x]$ (ideals are generated by the polynomial of least degree), $\\mathbb{Z}[i]$.\n\nCounterexamples: $\\mathbb{Z}[x]$ is not a PID — $(2, x)$ is not principal (if $(2,x) = (f)$, then $f \\mid 2$ forces $f$ to be a constant $\\pm 1$ or $\\pm 2$; the former gives $(f) = A$, the latter does not contain $x$, so neither works). Similarly $K[x, y]$, where $(x, y)$ is not principal.\n\nPID $\\implies$ UFD (`imp.ring-pid-ufd`) is the central theorem for these rings: existence of factorization rests on \"ascending chains of ideals terminate\" (Noether), uniqueness on \"irreducible = prime\" (the Bézout property comes from being a PID).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7174ce09',
      },
    },

    'def.ufd': {
      statement: {
        en: "An integral domain $A$ is a **unique factorization domain** (UFD) if\n\n1. **Existence**: every non-zero non-unit $a$ can be written as a product of finitely many irreducible elements;\n2. **Uniqueness**: two such factorizations differ only by order and by associates (that is, the factors can be paired off as associates).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#53824772',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$\\mathbb{Z}$ and $K[x]$ are both UFDs (the latter by `thm.poly-ufd`), while $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-5}]$ is not:\n\n$$6 = 2 \\cdot 3 = (1+\\sqrt{-5})(1-\\sqrt{-5}),$$\n\nthe four factors are all irreducible and pairwise non-associate — so factorization is not unique.\n\nThe criterion for UFDs: **UFD $\\iff$ (factorization exists) and (irreducible = prime)**. The three properties often listed side by side are not individually equivalent to UFD: ① \"irreducible = prime\" (`def.irreducible-element`) is enough only when **existence of factorization** already holds (drop existence and it is weaker — the ring of all algebraic integers has no irreducible elements, the condition is vacuously true, and yet it is not a UFD); ② a $\\gcd$ exists for any two elements in a **GCD domain** (`def.gcd-domain`), strictly weaker than UFD (the graph only records UFD $\\implies$ GCD domain, i.e. `imp.ring-ufd-gcd`; the converse fails, e.g. for non-discrete valuation rings); ③ absence of infinite descending factor chains is **ACCP**, also strictly weaker than UFD ($\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-5}]$ is Noetherian, hence has ACCP, but is not a UFD).\n\nThe hierarchy (`imp.ring-euclid-pid` / `imp.ring-pid-ufd`): Euclidean domain $\\implies$ PID $\\implies$ UFD $\\implies$ GCD domain, and both arrows are irreversible ($\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-5}]$ does not even have gcds; there exist UFDs that are not PIDs, such as $K[x, y]$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ba86300d',
      },
    },

    'thm.euler-theorem': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a, n \\ge 1$ with $\\gcd(a, n) = 1$. Then\n\n$$a^{\\varphi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod n .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#777b4739',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is one line, but it is group-theoretic: $[a]$ lies in the unit group $G = (\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z})^{\\times}$, with $|G| = \\varphi(n)$, so Lagrange's theorem gives $[a]^{|G|} = 1$.\n\nSo the real content of the theorem is \"the unit group is a finite group\" — the congruence is merely its coordinate form.\n\nWhen $\\gcd(a, n) > 1$ the conclusion generally fails: $2^{2} = 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod 4$, while $\\varphi(4) = 2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d2480d74',
      },
    },

    'thm.euler-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p \\nmid a$. Then\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{a}{p}\\right) \\equiv a^{\\frac{p-1}{2}} \\pmod p .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9a01137f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why**: $\\mathbb{F}_{p}^{\\times}$ is a cyclic group of order $p-1$. Write $a = g^{k}$ ($g$ a primitive root).\n\n- If $k$ is even, $a = (g^{k/2})^{2}$ is a residue, and $a^{(p-1)/2} = g^{k(p-1)/2} = (g^{p-1})^{k/2} = 1$;\n- If $k$ is odd, $a$ is not a residue (the squares in a cyclic group are exactly the even powers), and $a^{(p-1)/2} = g^{(p-1)/2} = -1$ (because its square is $1$ rather than $1$ itself — an element of order $2$ can only be $-1$).\n\nIn general, the equation $x^{(p-1)/2} \\equiv 1$ has exactly $(p-1)/2$ solutions (degree bound), exactly as many as the number of quadratic residues; the two sets coincide.\n\nIt turns \"solving an equation\" into \"computing one modular power\" — the standard practical test for quadratic residues (and the basis of the Solovay–Strassen primality test).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2faaca84',
      },
    },

    'thm.order-divides-phi': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gcd(a, n) = 1$. Then $\\operatorname{ord}_{n}(a) \\mid \\varphi(n)$; in particular $a^{\\varphi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod n$ (Euler's theorem).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1f85242a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: let $k = \\operatorname{ord}_{n}(a)$ and $H = \\{[1], [a], \\cdots, [a]^{k-1}\\}$ be the cyclic subgroup of the unit group, with $|H| = k$. By Lagrange's theorem, $k \\mid |G| = \\varphi(n)$.\n\n**This is the \"reason\" for Euler's theorem**: Euler's theorem is not about congruences but the translation of \"the order of an element in a finite group divides the order of the group\" to $G = (\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z})^{\\times}$ (see `imp.nt-lagrange-euler`).\n\n**Corollary**: $a$ is a primitive root $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{ord}_{n}(a) = \\varphi(n)$ — that is, it generates the whole unit group.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dcdada41',
      },
    },

    'prop.zeta-continuation': {
      statement: {
        en: "$\\zeta$ extends to a meromorphic function on $\\operatorname{Re}s>0$, whose only pole is a simple pole at $s=1$ with residue $1$; on $\\operatorname{Re}s>0$ one has $\\zeta(s)=\\frac{s}{s-1}-s\\int_{1}^{\\infty}\\{x\\}\\,x^{-s-1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c880935a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Only the extension to $\\operatorname{Re}s>0$ is given (enough for the prime number theorem); the extension to the whole plane and the functional equation are not part of this node. (Summation by parts).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fc40783a',
      },
    },

    'def.inner-product': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a $K$-vector space ($K = \\mathbb{R}$ or $\\mathbb{C}$). The map\n\n$$\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle : V \\times V \\longrightarrow K$$\n\nis an **inner product** on $V$ iff for all $u, v, w \\in V$ and $c \\in K$:\n\n- **linear in the first argument**: $\\langle u + c\\,w, v \\rangle = \\langle u, v \\rangle + c\\langle w, v \\rangle$;\n- **conjugate symmetric**: $\\langle v, u \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle u, v \\rangle}$ (for $K = \\mathbb{R}$ this is just symmetry);\n- **positive definite**: $\\langle v, v \\rangle \\ge 0$, and $\\langle v, v \\rangle = 0 \\iff v = 0$.\n\nA $V$ equipped with an inner product is an **inner product space**. In the real case an inner product is exactly a positive definite symmetric bilinear form.\n\nStandard example: on $K^{n}$ one sets $\\langle x, y \\rangle := \\sum_i x_i\\, \\overline{y_i}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2ec1c2eb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In the complex case one **cannot require bilinearity**: if $\\langle u, v \\rangle$ were linear in both arguments, then $\\langle i v, i v \\rangle = i^{2}\\langle v, v \\rangle = -\\langle v, v \\rangle$ would always be negative, contradicting positive definiteness.\n\nSo in the complex case one argument must be linear and the other conjugate-linear (this is called **sesquilinear**).\n\n⭐ Which side is conjugate is a **convention**: we take \"linear in the first argument, conjugate-linear in the second\". Changing the convention conjugates every formula and changes nothing.\n\n⚠ Positive definiteness is the watershed between inner products and general bilinear forms: only positive definiteness gives a notion of length, hence Cauchy–Schwarz and orthogonal projection. Dropping positive definiteness (keeping a symmetric bilinear form) leads to **quadratic forms** (see `def.quadratic-form`); dropping symmetry leads to **symplectic forms**.\n\n**Which hypotheses are used where** (the axiomatic definition is free; derived properties must retain positive definiteness):\n- \"Inner product $\\iff$ the three listed axioms\" is just unfolding the definition; in the real case it is equivalent to a positive definite symmetric bilinear form, because symmetry transfers linearity in the first argument to the second.\n- In the complex case, linearity in the first argument plus conjugate symmetry $\\implies$ conjugate-linearity in the second, by a purely algebraic computation; the standard example $\\sum_i x_i\\overline{y_i}$ agrees with the stated convention.\n- For a positive definite inner product $\\implies$ the norm and Cauchy–Schwarz use non-negative quadratic expressions and do not require finite dimension or completeness; recovering an inner product from an arbitrary norm requires the parallelogram identity.\n- Positive definiteness cannot be dropped: $\\mathbb R^2$ carries the symmetric bilinear form $x_1y_1-x_2y_2$, which pairs a non-zero vector with itself to zero, so it cannot define a norm.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a7f91a31',
      },
    },

    'thm.recursion-wellorder': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(W, \\preceq )$ be a **well-ordered set** and $G$ an arbitrary function (it may take values in a collection larger than any set). Write $W_{< w} = \\{ v \\in W : v \\prec w \\}$. Then there is a **unique** function $F$ defined on $W$ such that\n\n$$F(w) = G\\big( F \\upharpoonright W_{< w} \\big), \\qquad \\forall w \\in W$$\n\nthat is: **each step depends only on the values already taken**.\n\n**Corollary (transfinite induction)**: if $S \\subseteq W$ and $\\forall w \\in W\\; ( W_{< w} \\subseteq S \\to w \\in S )$, then $S = W$.\n\nTaking $W = \\omega$ gives **recursive definition** and **mathematical induction** on $\\mathbb{N}$; taking $W$ arbitrary gives **transfinite recursion** and **transfinite induction**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8062a47a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐⭐ This is the licence to **construct on the infinite**. Before it, the only tools for building objects were finitely many steps (pairing, union, power set, separation, replacement); to build a whole family of mutually dependent objects one needs exactly this. It is also the real purpose of the word \"well-ordered\" — well-ordering guarantees that the part before each step **has a beginning and an end**, so the recursion can terminate.\n\n⭐ **Why the \"well-ordered set\" version rather than the \"ordinal\" version**: recursion on $\\omega$ and transfinite recursion on ordinals are both special cases, so only the most general statement is recorded. (Exception: mathematical induction on $\\mathbb{N}$ has a more direct route — it comes from \"$\\omega$ is the smallest inductive set\" and does not need the general theory of well-orderings, so that node is kept.)\n\n⚠ The right-hand side of the recursion gives the **function** $F \\upharpoonright W_{< w}$ of all preceding values, not a sentence. Written as a sentence, it would fall into the trap of \"defining something by itself using something undefined\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2e6f5aeb',
      },
    },

    'thm.hom-tensor-adjunction-mod': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a **commutative** unital ring and $M, N, P$ $R$-modules. Then there is a **natural isomorphism**\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{R}\\bigl(M \\otimes_{R} N,\\ P\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{R}\\bigl(M,\\ \\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(N, P)\\bigr), \\qquad f \\longmapsto \\bigl(m \\mapsto (n \\mapsto f(m \\otimes n))\\bigr),$$\n\nthat is, the left adjoint of the functor $(-) \\otimes_{R} N$ (valued in $\\mathbf{Ab}$) is $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(N, -)$ (with its $R$-action); the $R$-linear version below requires $N$ to be an $(R,R)$-bimodule:\n\n$$(-) \\otimes_{R} N \\;\\dashv\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(N, -).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4a9aba1c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (write the maps explicitly)**: set $\\Phi(f) := \\bigl(m \\mapsto (n \\mapsto f(m \\otimes n))\\bigr)$.\n\nThe inverse is $\\Psi(g) := \\bigl(m \\otimes n \\mapsto g(m)(n)\\bigr)$: since $(m, n) \\mapsto g(m)(n)$ is balanced and biadditive, the universal property of `def.tensor-module` gives $\\Psi(g)$ uniquely.\n\n$\\Phi \\Psi$ and $\\Psi \\Phi$ are the identity on pure tensors (or on generators of $M$), and the generators span the whole module, so the two maps are mutually inverse.\n\nNaturality: the formula for $\\Phi$ involves no choices, only $\\otimes$ and composition.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ This is **currying**: something in two variables is the same as something in one variable taking values in a function space.\n\n**Two corollaries are immediate**: $(-) \\otimes_{R} N$ is a left adjoint, hence **preserves colimits** (right exact, preserves direct sums and filtered colimits); $\\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(N, -)$ is a right adjoint, hence **preserves limits** (left exact, preserves products).\n\n📌 Non-commutative version: for $M$ a right $R$-module, $N$ a left $R$-module and $P$ an abelian group, the same isomorphism reads\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M \\otimes_{R} N,\\ P) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{R}\\bigl(M,\\ \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(N, P)\\bigr),$$\n\nThe point is only that $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(N, P)$ is naturally a right $R$-module.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9f3360d7',
      },
    },

    'prop.skyscraper-stalks': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a topological space, $x \\in X$, and $A$ an abelian group. Then the stalk of the skyscraper sheaf $i_{x,*}A$ at $y \\in X$ is\n\n$$(i_{x,*}A)_{y} \\;=\\; \\begin{cases} A, & y \\in \\overline{\\{x\\}}, \\\\ 0, & y \\notin \\overline{\\{x\\}} . \\end{cases}$$\n\nIn particular $(i_{x,*}A)_{x} = A$; if $X$ is a $T_{1}$ space (Hausdorff suffices), then for $y \\ne x$ one has $(i_{x,*}A)_{y} = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9bbdb0eb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** The stalk is the filtered colimit over open neighbourhoods of $y$:\n\n$$(i_{x,*}A)_{y} = \\varinjlim_{U \\ni y}\\ (i_{x,*}A)(U) .$$\n\n- If $y \\in \\overline{\\{x\\}}$: every open neighbourhood $U \\ni y$ of $x$ contains $A$ (this is the characterization of the closure), so every term in the system is $\\mathrm{id}_{A}$ and every transition map is $A$ — the colimit is $y \\notin \\overline{\\{x\\}}$.\n- If $U_{0} \\ni y$: there is $x$ not containing $U_{0}$. Take the **cofinal** subsystem of terms contained in $0$; every term there is $0$ — the colimit is $T_{1}$. ∎\n\n**Corollary (the $X$ case)**: $T_{1}$ is $\\iff$ $\\iff$ $\\overline{\\{x\\}} = \\{x\\}$, so the stalk of a skyscraper sheaf is non-zero only at $x$ — then \"$A$ supported at one point\" is literal.\n\n⚠ On a general topological space (e.g. a spectral space with a specialization order), the **support** of a skyscraper sheaf is the closure $\\overline{\\{x\\}}$, not $\\{x\\}$. This step must be re-examined when skyscraper sheaves are transported to schemes or adic spaces.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#256ede14',
      },
    },

    'thm.baer-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a unital ring and $I$ a left $R$-module. Then $I$ is **injective** iff: for every **left ideal** $J \\subseteq R$ and every $R$-linear map $f : J \\to I$, there is $x \\in I$ such that\n\n$$f(a) = a x \\qquad (\\forall a \\in J),$$\n\nthat is, $f$ always extends to $R \\to I$ with $1 \\mapsto x$.\n\nIn other words: to test injectivity, the case \"$A \\rightarrowtail B$\" may be restricted to \"the inclusion of an ideal $J \\rightarrowtail R$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8ee8e469',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Necessity**: $J \\rightarrowtail R$ is itself injective, so injectivity directly gives an extension $R \\to I$ ✓.\n\n**Sufficiency**: given an injection $A \\subseteq B$ and $f : A \\to I$.\n\nConsider all extension pairs $(A_{1}, f_{1})$ ($A \\subseteq A_{1} \\subseteq B$, $f_{1}$ agrees with $f$ on $A$), ordered by \"larger domain\".\n\nThe union of a chain is again an extension, so by **Zorn's lemma** there is a maximal element $(A_{1}, f_{1})$.\n\nIf $A_{1} \\ne B$, take $b \\in B \\setminus A_{1}$ and set\n\n$$J := \\{\\, r \\in R : r b \\in A_{1} \\,\\}, \\qquad h : J \\to I, \\quad h(r) := f_{1}(r b).$$\n\n$J$ is a left ideal and $h$ is $R$-linear. By hypothesis there is $x \\in I$ with $h(r) = r x$ ($\\forall r \\in J$). Define $f_{2}(a + r b) := f_{1}(a) + r x$.\n\n**Well-defined**: if $a + r b = a_{1} + r_{1} b$, then $(r - r_{1}) b = a_{1} - a \\in A_{1}$, hence $r - r_{1} \\in J$, so $f_{1}((r - r_{1})b) = f_{1}(a_{1} - a)$, i.e. $f_{1}(a) + r x = f_{1}(a_{1}) + r_{1} x$ ✓.\n\nThis contradicts maximality, hence $A_{1} = B$ ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **Meaning**: it compresses \"test against all injections\" into \"test against ideals of $R$\" — and ideals are subsets of $R$, usually far fewer. This step is the key to translating a categorical condition into a condition on the ring; `thm.pid-injective-divisible` depends entirely on it.\n\n⚠ The \"ideal\" in the criterion cannot be replaced by \"finitely generated ideal\": flatness has Lambek's theorem allowing only finitely generated ideals to be checked, but injectivity has **no** such dual weakening (unless the ring is Noetherian).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9f8954ff',
      },
    },

    'lem.newman-tauberian': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb{C}$ be bounded and locally integrable, and suppose its Laplace transform\n\n$$g(z)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}f(t)\\,e^{-zt}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\qquad(\\operatorname{Re}z>0)$$\n\nextends analytically to some open set containing the closed half-plane $\\{\\operatorname{Re}z\\ge0\\}$. Then the improper integral $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ converges and equals $g(0)$.\n\nThis is Newman's Tauberian lemma for the prime number theorem; it compresses the analytic core of \"$\\psi(x)\\sim x$\" into a single contour estimate, see `thm.prime-number-theorem`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ca031979',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The proof is on the incoming edge of this node** (Cauchy's integral formula + residues + the contour estimate from Cauchy–Goursat); this note records only two remarks on usage.\n\n**Remark 1 (why the kernel contains $1+z^{2}/R^{2}$)**: $F(0)=1$ keeps the residue at $z=0$ equal to $g(0)-g_{T}(0)$; while on $|z|=R$ one has $1+z^{2}/R^{2}=2z\\operatorname{Re}z/R^{2}$, which exactly cancels the bad factor $1/\\operatorname{Re}z$, making the estimates on the right half-plane and the left semicircle uniform in $R$.\n\n**Remark 2 (strength of the hypotheses and Tauberian phenomena)**: the proof only requires $g$ to be holomorphic on a neighbourhood of **every** closed half-disc $\\{|z|\\le R,\\ \\operatorname{Re}z\\ge0\\}$; for the $g$ of the prime number theorem this follows from the non-vanishing of $\\zeta$ on $\\operatorname{Re}s=1$ together with compactness. If only \"$g$ holomorphic on $\\operatorname{Re}z>0$\" is assumed, the conclusion generally fails: $f(t)=\\sin t$ is bounded, $g(z)=1/(z^{2}+1)$ has poles on the imaginary axis, and $\\int_{0}^{T}\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ does not converge.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7fe59bf1',
      },
    },

    'prop.rational-localization': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(A,A^+) \\to (B,B^+)$ be a map of discrete Huber pairs such that $\\operatorname{Spa}(B,B^+) \\to \\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)$ factors through the rational open set $U = U\\!\\left(\\frac{g_1,\\ldots,g_n}{f}\\right)$.\n\nThen the map factors uniquely through\n\n$$\\Bigl(A\\bigl[\\tfrac{1}{f}\\bigr],\\ \\widetilde{A^+\\bigl[\\tfrac{g_1}{f}, \\ldots, \\tfrac{g_n}{f}\\bigr]}\\Bigr),$$\n\nwhere the tilde denotes integral closure in $A[1/f]$. Moreover\n\n$$\\operatorname{Spa}\\Bigl(A\\bigl[\\tfrac{1}{f}\\bigr],\\ \\widetilde{A^+\\bigl[\\tfrac{g_i}{f}\\bigr]}\\Bigr) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)$$\n\nis a homeomorphism onto $U$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#033f0093',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the precise meaning of \"rational open set = affine open set\": each rational open set is itself an $\\operatorname{Spa}$, obtained by inverting $f$ and adjoining $g_i/f$ to $A^+$.\n\nThus adic spaces have a **basis of affine open sets**, and one can do sheaf theory on them as on schemes.\n\n⚠ Uniqueness comes from \"$f$ is nowhere zero on $\\operatorname{Spa}(B,B^+)$ $\\implies$ $f$ is invertible in $B$\" (otherwise $\\operatorname{Spec}(B/fB)$ would give a point with $|f| = 0$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c6c8321b',
      },
    },

    'thm.adic-structure-sheaf': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X = \\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)$. On rational open sets define\n\n$$\\mathcal{O}_X(U) := A\\bigl[\\tfrac{1}{f}\\bigr], \\qquad \\mathcal{O}_X^+(U) := \\widetilde{A^+\\bigl[\\tfrac{g_1}{f}, \\ldots, \\tfrac{g_n}{f}\\bigr]}$$\n\n($U = U\\!\\left(\\frac{g_1,\\ldots,g_n}{f}\\right)$). Then:\n\n**(i)** $\\mathcal{O}_X$ and $\\mathcal{O}_X^+$ are both **sheaves**;\n\n**(ii)** for every point $x \\in X$, the valuation $f \\mapsto |f(x)|$ extends uniquely to the stalk $\\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$;\n\n**(iii)** $\\mathcal{O}_X^+(U) = \\{f \\in \\mathcal{O}_X(U) : \\forall x \\in U,\\ |f(x)| \\le 1\\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#82593e58',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The key to the proof is a \"detour through schemes\" trick: there is a map $\\psi : \\operatorname{Spec} A \\to \\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)$ sending a prime ideal $\\mathfrak{p}$ to $A \\to \\operatorname{Frac}(A/\\mathfrak{p}) \\to \\{0,1\\}$.\n\nThe preimage of a rational open set under $\\psi$ is exactly $D(f)$, so $\\mathcal{O}_{\\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)}$ is the **pushforward** of $\\mathcal{O}_{\\operatorname{Spec} A}$ along $\\psi$; pushforwards preserve sheaves.\n\n⭐ (iii) says that $\\mathcal{O}^+$ is not extra structure but the part of $\\mathcal{O}$ consisting of functions that are pointwise $\\le 1$ — the local version of the same statement as `prop.huber-subring-recovery`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ba08aaf6',
      },
    },

    'cor.cech-cohomology-spectral': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{C}$ be a site with fibre products, $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$, and $M$ an **abelian sheaf** on $\\mathcal{C}$. Then there is a spectral sequence\n\n$$E^{p,q}_{2} \\;=\\; \\check{H}^{p}\\bigl(X,\\ H^{q}(M)\\bigr) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; H^{p+q}(X,\\ M),$$\n\nwhere $H^{q}(M)$ denotes the presheaf $U \\mapsto H^{q}(U, M)$, and $\\check{H}^{p}(X, -)$ is Čech cohomology, taken as the filtered colimit over covers.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fa81c3a9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Use**: this is the general justification for \"computing sheaf cohomology with Čech cohomology\". To make the two directly **equal** (rather than merely linked by a spectral sequence), one additionally requires the higher Čech groups to degenerate on the $E_{2}$ page — exactly the step used in the proof of \"acyclic $\\iff$ Čech cohomology vanishes\" ✓.\n\n⚠ Difference from the Cartan–Leray spectral sequence: that one **fixes a single cover** $\\mathfrak{X}$; this one takes the colimit over all covers, so the left-hand side can be written directly as $\\check{H}^{p}(X, \\cdot)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a0036268',
      },
    },

    'thm.nat-arith-laws': {
      statement: {
        en: "On $\\mathbb{N} = \\omega$ take the addition and multiplication defined in `def.nat-arith`. Then for all $m, n, k \\in \\mathbb{N}$:\n\n$$0 + n = n, \\qquad S(m) + n = S(m + n),$$\n\n$$m + n = n + m, \\qquad (m + n) + k = m + (n + k),$$\n\n$$m \\cdot 0 = 0, \\qquad m \\cdot n = n \\cdot m, \\qquad (m n) k = m (n k), \\qquad m(n + k) = m n + m k,$$\n\nand the **cancellation law**\n\n$$m + k = n + k \\implies m = n.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e98af4e5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ These are **not** part of the definition: `def.nat-arith` only specifies $m+0$ and $m+S(n)$ recursively; commutativity, associativity and distributivity all have to be proved separately.\n\n⭐ The order matters and cannot be scrambled: first prove the two auxiliary identities $0+n=n$ and $S(m)+n=S(m+n)$ (both by induction on $n$), then commutativity of addition (induction on $n$, whose step uses exactly those two), then associativity, commutativity of multiplication, and distributivity.\n\nEvery later step stands on the previous one.\n\n⭐ With these, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a \"semiring\" (no additive inverses);\n\nthe construction of $\\mathbb{Z}$ (`def.int`) exists precisely to supply that inverse.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3334eeab',
      },
    },

    'prop.pullback-forms': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it pulls forms back along a smooth map — a capability specific to forms (vector fields can only be pushed forward), and the precise statement that $\\mathrm{d}$ is coordinate-free.\n\nLet $F : U\\to V$ be a $C^{\\infty}$ map between open sets, and let $\\omega$ be a $k$-form on $V$. The **pullback** $F^{\\ast}\\omega$ is the $k$-form on $U$ defined by\n\n$$(F^{\\ast}\\omega)_{p}(v_{1},\\ldots,v_{k}) := \\omega_{F(p)}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}F_{p}(v_{1}),\\ldots,\\mathrm{d}F_{p}(v_{k})\\bigr).$$\n\nThus $F^{\\ast}$ sends $\\Omega^{k}(V)$ to $\\Omega^{k}(U)$, and satisfies\n\n1. **Linearity**: $F^{\\ast}(a\\omega+b\\eta) = a\\,F^{\\ast}\\omega+b\\,F^{\\ast}\\eta$; for functions, $F^{\\ast}f = f\\circ F$;\n2. **Preserves the wedge product**: $F^{\\ast}(\\omega\\wedge\\eta) = F^{\\ast}\\omega\\wedge F^{\\ast}\\eta$;\n3. **Commutes with** $\\mathrm{d}$: $F^{\\ast}(\\mathrm{d}\\omega) = \\mathrm{d}(F^{\\ast}\\omega)$;\n4. **Preserves composition**: $(G\\circ F)^{\\ast} = F^{\\ast}\\circ G^{\\ast}$, $(\\operatorname{id})^{\\ast} = \\operatorname{id}$.\n\nThe first two say $F^{\\ast}$ is a graded algebra homomorphism, the third that it is also a chain map; together: **$F^{\\ast}$ is a homomorphism of differential graded algebras**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1cd27600',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Direction**: $F : U\\to V$ induces $F^{\\ast} : \\Omega^{k}(V)\\to\\Omega^{k}(U)$, opposite to $F$. This is the precise meaning of \"forms are covariant\"; it is also why pullback is defined along any smooth map, whereas pushing vector fields forward generally requires $F$ to be a diffeomorphism (or at least requires the fields to be $F$-related).\n\n**(3) is the core**: it is equivalent to saying that $\\mathrm{d}$ does not depend on coordinates. The only substantive input is the chain rule; the rest follows from (2) and $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ (the proof is on the edges).\n\n**Use in geometry**: a surface parametrization $r : U\\to\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ pulls forms on $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ back to $U$.\n\nThe first fundamental form and the area element are both pullbacks: $I = (\\omega^{1})^{2}+(\\omega^{2})^{2}$, $\\mathrm{d}A = \\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}$.\n\nSurface theory can be written in the language of forms precisely because of this.\n\n⚠️ **Pullback does not preserve the orientation of an integral**: $\\int_{U}F^{\\ast}\\omega = \\int_{F(U)}\\omega$ holds only when $F$ is an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism and the degree of the form equals the dimension (change of variables, `def.integral-form`).\n\n⚠️ The order in (4) must not be reversed: the pullback of the composite $G\\circ F$ is $F^{\\ast}\\circ G^{\\ast}$, i.e. apply the later map first.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a7f86c3b',
      },
    },

    'def.exterior-derivative': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it equips forms with a derivative that sends $k$-forms to $(k+1)$-forms and satisfies $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$ — this is what writes calculus in a coordinate-free way.\n\nLet $\\omega = \\sum_{I}a_{I}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{I}$ be a $k$-form on $U$, where $\\mathrm{d}x^{I} := \\mathrm{d}x^{i_{1}}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{i_{k}}$. The **exterior derivative** is defined by\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega := \\sum_{I}\\mathrm{d}a_{I}\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{I} = \\sum_{I}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{\\partial a_{I}}{\\partial x^{j}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{j}\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{I}.$$\n\nFor a function (a $0$-form) it is the total differential $\\mathrm{d}f = \\sum_{j}\\partial_{j}f\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{j}$. Basic properties:\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}$-linearity: $\\mathrm{d}(a\\omega+b\\eta) = a\\,\\mathrm{d}\\omega+b\\,\\mathrm{d}\\eta$;\n2. **Graded Leibniz rule**: when $\\omega$ is a $k$-form, $\\mathrm{d}(\\omega\\wedge\\eta) = \\mathrm{d}\\omega\\wedge\\eta+(-1)^{k}\\omega\\wedge\\mathrm{d}\\eta$;\n3. **$\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$**: a direct consequence of the equality of mixed partial derivatives;\n4. it is independent of the choice of coordinates (equivalently, it commutes with pullback; see `prop.pullback-forms`).\n\nA form with $\\mathrm{d}\\omega = 0$ is called **closed**, and a form $\\omega$ with $\\omega = \\mathrm{d}\\eta$ is called **exact**.\n\nBy $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$, every exact form is closed;\n\nthe converse fails ($\\frac{x\\,\\mathrm{d}y-y\\,\\mathrm{d}x}{x^{2}+y^{2}}$ on $\\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is closed but not exact).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#95278658',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What kind of algebraic object $\\mathrm{d}$ is**: an **antiderivation** of the exterior algebra — degree $+1$, graded Leibniz, square zero. These three properties determine it uniquely, so there is no need to memorize the formula; remember these three.\n\n**$\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ has the same origin as the structure equations**: the structure equations of a surface are also a reading of $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ (`thm.structure-equations`). The advantage of the language of forms is that \"mixed partials commute\" and \"the frame is integrable\" become the same identity.\n\n**Unification of three classical operators** ($n=3$): $\\mathrm{d}$ on $0$-forms gives the gradient (as a $1$-form), on $1$-forms the curl (as a $2$-form), and on $2$-forms the divergence (as a $3$-form). Hence $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ is exactly \"the divergence of a curl is zero\" and \"the curl of a gradient is zero\".\n\n⚠️ **One cannot apply $\\mathrm{d}$ to a vector field**: $\\mathrm{d}$ acts only on forms. Differentiating vector fields requires extra structure (a connection, `def.affine-connection`), and the result is a covariant derivative rather than an exterior derivative.\n\n⚠️ The same symbol $\\mathrm{d}$ is also used for the total differential, the arc-length element and the integration measure — a historical burden of notation. In this map $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ always denotes the exterior derivative, while $\\mathrm{d}s$ and $\\mathrm{d}A$ have their own meanings (`def.arc-length`, `def.area-element`).\n\n**Among the three properties, $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$ is the only one with content** (the other two almost just say \"it is a derivation\"):\n\n- **$\\mathbb{R}$-linearity** and the **graded Leibniz rule** only determine the action of $\\mathrm{d}$ on a basis. What really pins it down is the convention $\\mathrm{d}(\\mathrm{d}x^{j}) = 0$ — with it, $\\mathrm{d}$ on $\\mathrm{d}x^{I}$ is completely determined by the Leibniz rule.\n- **$\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$**: this is where all the content lies; it is equivalent to the equality of mixed partials ($\\partial_{j}\\partial_{k} = \\partial_{k}\\partial_{j}$, Clairaut's theorem). ⚠️ Hence the exterior derivative is well behaved only in the $C^{2}$ class: functions that are $C^{1}$ but whose second derivatives do not commute break it.\n- **Relation to the Lie derivative**, $\\mathrm{d} = $ \"antisymmetrization\": the Cartan formula $\\mathcal{L}_{X} = \\mathrm{d}\\iota_{X} + \\iota_{X}\\mathrm{d}$ welds these three operations together (`thm.cartan-formula`).\n\n⚠️ **Three equivalent definitions, at different costs**: the coordinate formula (this section; most concrete but coordinate-dependent), the axiomatic one (Leibniz + $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ + agreement on functions; one must prove existence and uniqueness), and the coordinate-free form $[\\omega](X_{0},\\ldots,X_{k}) = \\sum(-1)^{i}X_{i}\\omega(\\ldots)$ (most geometric, it displays intrinsicness directly). They are equivalent, but **only the last one shows that $\\mathrm{d}$ is independent of coordinates**.\n\n⚠️ **$\\mathrm{d}$ is not tensorial**: it does not satisfy $\\mathrm{d}(f\\omega) = f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ (the difference is a term $\\mathrm{d}f\\wedge\\omega$). This is exactly why it cannot be defined on general tensor fields, only on forms.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the coordinate formula and the axiomatic characterization must specify the action on functions):\n\n- coordinate formula $\\implies$ total differential on functions and the graded Leibniz rule: uses the product rule for partial derivatives and the sign rule for the wedge product.\n- coordinate formula $\\implies$ $\\mathrm d^2=0$: requires equality of mixed partials; coefficients in $C^2$ suffice, while a single exterior derivative itself only needs $C^1$.\n- axiomatic characterization $\\implies$ coordinate formula: one must also require agreement with the total differential on functions; otherwise the zero operator satisfies linearity, graded Leibniz and square zero, and cannot determine the exterior derivative uniquely.\n- exact $\\implies$ closed uses square zero; closed $\\implies$ exact needs an extra local or global condition, such as the Poincaré lemma on star-shaped open sets; the angle form on the punctured plane gives a global counterexample.\n\n📌 This node treats the exterior derivative on **Euclidean open sets**; for the manifold version see `prop.exterior-derivative-manifold`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8bc3a98d',
      },
    },

    'cor.free-solid-product': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every profinite set $S$, there exist a set $I$ (with $\\#I \\le 2^{\\#S}$) and an isomorphism of condensed abelian groups\n\n$$Z[S]^{\\blacksquare} \\cong \\prod_{I} \\mathbb{Z}.$$\n\nwhose underlying abelian group is the measure group $M(S,\\mathbb{Z})$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#112e3671',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This corollary translates \"the free solid group\" into \"a large product\" — every later computation relies on it.\n\nIn particular, the compact projective objects of solid theory are not finitely generated free modules but **arbitrary products** $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n⚠ $I$ is usually uncountable: for example, when $S = \\mathbb{Z}_p$, the free rank of $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ is countable, while $M(S,\\mathbb{Z}) = \\prod_{\\mathbb{N}} \\mathbb{Z}$ is uncountable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92fe3be1',
      },
    },

    'thm.theorema-egregium': {
      statement: {
        en: "The Gaussian curvature $K$ depends only on the first fundamental form $I$. Precisely: if $\\varphi : S_{1}\\to S_{2}$ is a local isometry, then\n\n$$K_{1}(p) = K_{2}(\\varphi(p))\\qquad (p \\in S_{1}).$$\n\nEquivalently, $K$ can be written as a single formula in $g_{ij}$ and its first and second derivatives.\n\nYet the original definition of $K$ as $k_{1}k_{2}$ uses the second fundamental form (an extrinsic quantity) — so this is not a consequence of the definition but a theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7fa1ac96',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is called \"remarkable\"**: $K$ is the product of the two principal curvatures, which come from $\\mathrm{d}n$, where $n$ is the unit normal in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ — extrinsic data by any account. Yet it can be read off from the metric inside the surface alone. All of intrinsic geometry (Gauss–Bonnet, uniformization, the topological meaning of $K$) grows from this.\n\n**An operational reading**: an inhabitant of the surface who can only measure lengths can still determine $K$. Measure the circumference $\\rho$ of a small geodesic circle of radius $L(\\rho)$; then\n\n$$K(p) = \\frac{3}{\\pi}\\lim_{\\rho\\to0}\\frac{2\\pi\\rho-L(\\rho)}{\\rho^{3}}.$$\n\nThis turns the remarkable theorem into an experiment: curvature is the third-order rate at which the circumference falls short of $2\\pi\\rho$.\n\n**Two checks**: the cylinder ($K = 0$) is locally isometric to the plane, so the curvatures must agree; the sphere ($K = 1/R^{2}$) is not locally isometric to any surface with $K = 0$, so curvature really is an isometry invariant.\n\n⚠️ **What is not intrinsic**: $k_{1}$, $k_{2}$ and $H$ are all extrinsic (the cylinder versus the plane is the counterexample: $k_{1}$ is $1/R$ and $0$ respectively). The remarkable theorem holds for the **product**, not for the factors. This also explains why $K$ appears everywhere in intrinsic geometry while $H$ hardly ever does.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dcdfc6c1',
      },
    },

    'lem.adapted-frame': {
      statement: {
        en: "Suppose the frame is adapted to the surface: $e_{3} = n$. Then along the surface\n\n$$\\omega^{3} = 0,\\qquad \\omega^{i}_{\\ 3} = \\sum_{j=1}^{2}c_{ij}\\,\\omega^{j}\\quad (i = 1,2),$$\n\nthat is, all extrinsic connection forms lie in the span of $\\{\\omega^{1}, \\omega^{2}\\}$ and are determined by the three functions $c_{11}, c_{12}, c_{22}$ ($c_{12} = c_{21}$; see `lem.second-form-symmetric`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7e87d2c6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why $\\omega^{3} = 0$**: $\\omega^{3}(v) = \\langle \\mathrm{d}r(v), e_{3}\\rangle$, and $\\mathrm{d}r(v)$ is a tangent vector of the surface while $e_{3}$ is normal. Equivalently: when $r$ moves along the surface it has no normal component.\n\n⭐ **The system therefore gets shorter**: taking $i=3$ in the first structure equation turns it into the identity $0 = \\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{3}_{\\ 1}+\\omega^{2}\\wedge\\omega^{3}_{\\ 2}$;\n\ntaking $i,j \\in \\{1,2\\}$ in the second structure equation leaves only the $k=3$ term \"left over\" — and that term is the Gauss curvature.\n\n⭐ This is exactly the entry point for \"skipping the complicated computation\": the quantities $r_{uu}, r_{uv}$ that would require computing the partial derivatives $h_{ij}$ in coordinates are here just the coefficients of $\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3}$.\n\n⚠️ $\\omega^{3} = 0$ holds **along the surface**, not for the frame itself: on an open set of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$, $\\omega^{3}$ is generally nonzero, and that is precisely what corresponds to \"the surface bending out in the normal direction\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f5211184',
      },
    },

    'thm.csz-free': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain terms: something counterintuitive — \"continuous integer-valued functions on $S$\" look like \"infinitely many copies of $\\mathbb{Z}$ glued together\", yet they are **always** free abelian groups (a basis, no relations). This is the nail that holds up the whole theory of solid groups.\n\nFor every profinite set $S$, the group $C(S, \\mathbb{Z})$ of continuous functions (see `def.cond-measure`),\n\n$$C(S, \\mathbb{Z}) = \\{\\, f : S \\to \\mathbb{Z}\\ \\mid f \\text{continuous}\\, \\}$$\n\nis a **free abelian group**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ded322c0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is a theorem of Specker (1950, for $S$ of type $\\mathbb{Z}_p$) and of Nöbeling (1968, for general profinite sets).\n\nThe counterintuitive point: $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ looks like the infinite product \"one integer at each point\", yet it is **always free** — while its dual $M(S,\\mathbb{Z}) = \\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$ is the large side.\n\n⭐ It is the pivot of the whole solid theory: without it, $Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}$ could not be described as $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$, and the \"compact projective generators\" in `thm.solid-abelian` would have no basis.\n\n⚠ Note that $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ behaves completely differently from its **bounded** version and from $C(S,\\mathbb{R})$: the claim here concerns $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ as an abstract abelian group; $C(S,\\mathbb{R})$ as an abstract vector space of course has a basis, but the freeness of the former cannot simply be transferred.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3d8f72c6',
      },
    },

    'thm.solid-abelian': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain terms: **the solid subcategory $\\mathbf{Solid}$ is a place \"as convenient as a category of modules\"** — it has enough compact projective objects ($\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$), so one can form tensor products, Ext and derived categories without pathologies. This is the engine of the whole theory.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{Solid} \\subset \\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab})$ be the full subcategory of solid abelian groups. Then:\n\n**(i)** $\\mathbf{Solid}$ is an abelian subcategory, stable under all limits, colimits and extensions; the objects $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$ ($I$ arbitrary) form a family of **compact projective generators**; the inclusion has a left adjoint\n\n$$M \\longmapsto M^{\\blacksquare} : \\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab}) \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{Solid},$$\n\nwhich is the unique colimit-preserving extension of $\\mathbb{Z}[S] \\mapsto Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}$ (**solidification**).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ab823cbb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the engine of the whole theory: condensed abelian groups are **not** localizing, but the solid subcategory is, and it has enough compact projective objects — hence one can do homological algebra exactly as in a category of modules.\n\n⭐ Proof route: for $\\mathcal{A} = \\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab})$ and $\\mathcal{A}_0 = \\{\\mathbb{Z}[S] : S\\ \\text{extremally disconnected}\\}$, verify the hypotheses of `lem.solid-completion-criterion`.\n\nThe key computation is: for the complex $C$ built from direct sums of $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}(Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}, C) \\cong R\\Gamma(S, C).$$\n\nThis is the main computation of Lecture VI (replace $C$ by $C \\to C_{\\mathbb{R}} \\to C_{\\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}}$, then use the two-step resolution of compact abelian groups and the contractibility of $\\mathbb{R}$-modules).\n\n⭐ **The proof** is on the edge `imp.solid-abelian`: reduce to complexes of $\\prod_I\\mathbb{Z}$-type $\\to$ the core assertion (6.1) $\\to$ push the assertion back to $Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}$ $\\to$ apply the completion criterion.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#daf625fe',
      },
    },

    'def.curvature-tensor': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain terms: **the curvature tensor measures \"how much the result changes when one transports around in two directions and swaps their order\"**. On a flat plane swapping makes no difference, and the difference is zero; on a sphere swapping the order leaves a small discrepancy, and that discrepancy is curvature. It is intrinsic: the formula contains only $g$ and its derivatives — no normal, no second fundamental form.\n\nThe Levi-Civita connection on a surface defines the **curvature operator**\n\n$$R(X,Y)Z = \\nabla_{X}\\nabla_{Y}Z-\\nabla_{Y}\\nabla_{X}Z-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z,$$\n\nin coordinates, $R(\\partial_{i},\\partial_{j})\\partial_{k} = R^{\\ell}_{ijk}\\partial_{\\ell}$, where\n\n$$R^{\\ell}_{ijk} = \\partial_{i}\\Gamma^{\\ell}_{jk}-\\partial_{j}\\Gamma^{\\ell}_{ik}+\\Gamma^{\\ell}_{ip}\\Gamma^{p}_{jk}-\\Gamma^{\\ell}_{jp}\\Gamma^{p}_{ik},$$\n\nand indices are lowered by $R_{ijk\\ell} = g_{\\ell p}R^{p}_{ijk}$. It satisfies\n\n$$R_{ijk\\ell} = -R_{jik\\ell} = -R_{ij\\ell k} = R_{k\\ell ij},\\qquad R_{ijk\\ell}+R_{jki\\ell}+R_{kij\\ell} = 0$$\n\n(the latter is the **first Bianchi identity**). **Flatness criterion**: $R\\equiv0$ if and only if near every point there are coordinates with $g_{ij} = \\delta_{ij}$, i.e. the surface is locally isometric to the plane.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5c50e87f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**It comes from integrability conditions.**\n\nFinding coordinates that flatten $g_{ij}$ to $\\delta_{ij}$ is equivalent to solving $g_{ij} = \\delta_{\\alpha\\beta}\\frac{\\partial y^{\\alpha}}{\\partial x^{i}}\\frac{\\partial y^{\\beta}}{\\partial x^{j}}$;\n\nand the compatibility requirement $\\partial_{i}\\partial_{j} = \\partial_{j}\\partial_{i}$ is exactly $R^{\\ell}_{ijk} = 0$.\n\nSo from the very beginning $R$ is the obstruction to flattening the metric.\n\n**Ricci identity**: $[\\nabla_{\\partial_{i}},\\nabla_{\\partial_{j}}]\\partial_{k} = R^{\\ell}_{ijk}\\partial_{\\ell}$. Hence $R$ measures by how much covariant derivatives fail to commute — this is its most common definition, and the reason this map chose it.\n\n**Why it is intrinsic**: the formula for $R$ contains only $\\Gamma$ and $\\partial\\Gamma$, and $\\Gamma$ contains only $g$ and $\\partial g$. **The second fundamental form does not appear.**\n\n⚠ In dimension two there is only one independent component ($R_{1212}$, the rest being determined by symmetry), so \"curvature\" on a surface is one number rather than a pile of numbers; only in dimension three and higher does $R$ acquire genuine tensor structure (and then $K$ gives only part of its trace).\n\n⚠ Sign convention: this pack takes the formula displayed above. The other convention (moving the two $\\Gamma$-product terms to the other side) flips every component, and hence every **definition** of curvature, while the geometry is unchanged. Testing on the unit sphere is the fastest way to tell the conventions apart.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a70e2e23',
      },
    },

    'prop.rhom-zs-sections': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $S$ be a **Stonean** space and $N$ a condensed abelian group. Then\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z}[S],\\ N\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; N(S)$$\n\n(the right-hand side is understood as the complex concentrated in degree $0$, i.e. all higher Ext vanish).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#52fc91fa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** When $S$ is Stonean, $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is a **projective** object: extremal disconnectedness gives \"section-type\" projectivity on clopen sets, and $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is the colimit of the $\\mathbb{Z}[C]$ over clopen $C$, with projectivity preserved under colimits ✓. Hence\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z}[S],\\ -\\bigr) = \\Gamma(S, -) = \\bigl(N \\mapsto N(S)\\bigr)$$\n\nis an **exact** functor, its right derived functors are concentrated in degree $0$, and $R^{0} = \\Gamma(S,-)$ ✓. Therefore $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z}[S], N)$ equals $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}[S], N) = N(S)$ (a complex concentrated in degree $0$) ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ This is the same statement as \"condensed abelian groups on a Stonean space are acyclic\", in two equivalent forms: **coefficient acyclicity $\\iff$ the test object $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is projective**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea64a200',
      },
    },

    'def.weingarten-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "Suppose $e_{3} = n$. The **Weingarten map** is defined as the negative of the differential of the Gauss map:\n\n$$W(v) := -\\mathrm{d}n(v) = -\\mathrm{d}e_{3}(v),\\qquad W\\colon T_{p}S \\to T_{p}S .$$\n\nIt indeed lands in the tangent plane (because $\\langle \\mathrm{d}n(v), n\\rangle = 0$). Relative to the frame $e_{1}, e_{2}$:\n\n$$W(e_{j}) = \\sum_{i=1}^{2}h_{ij}\\,e_{i},\\qquad h_{ij} := -c_{ij} = -\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3}(e_{j}),$$\n\nand $W$ is self-adjoint: $\\langle W(v), w\\rangle = \\langle v, W(w)\\rangle$, i.e. $(h_{ij})$ is symmetric.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8375dcd7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Intuition**: $W$ measures how fast the normal turns, i.e. how strongly the surface bends. The bending in a unit direction $V$ is $\\langle W(V), V\\rangle$; see `def.normal-curvature`.\n\n**Where self-adjointness comes from**: the coordinate route uses $r_{uv} = r_{vu}$ (equality of mixed partial derivatives); the frame route uses $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{3} = 0$ (`lem.second-form-symmetric` and the incoming edge). The latter is cheaper and does not depend on a special choice of parameter net.\n\n⚠ **Sign**: taking $W := -\\mathrm{d}n$ is chosen so that the principal curvatures of the sphere with the $+$ outward normal are negative (consistent with do Carmo).\n\nIf one takes $W := \\mathrm{d}n$, then $II$ must change sign as well, otherwise \"principal curvature $=$ eigenvalue of $W$\" fails.\n\nThe notation $W\\colon r_{i} \\mapsto -n_{i}$ is exactly this convention.\n\n**Third fundamental form**: $III(v,w) := \\langle \\mathrm{d}n(v), \\mathrm{d}n(w)\\rangle = \\langle W(v), W(w)\\rangle$.\n\nThe characteristic equation $W^{2}-2HW+K\\,\\mathrm{id} = 0$ of $W$ translates, as a quadratic-form identity, into $III-2H\\,II+K\\,I = 0$ (Cayley–Hamilton).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#80a05905',
      },
    },

    'thm.flow-local': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain terms**: integral curves of a vector field always exist and are unique locally, and the family of solutions depends smoothly on the starting point and on time — that is, a local flow always exists.\n\nLet $X \\in \\mathfrak{X}(M)$ and $p \\in M$. Then there exist an open neighbourhood $U$ of $p$, some $\\varepsilon > 0$, and a smooth map $\\varphi : (-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)\\times U \\to M$ such that\n\n1. $\\varphi_{0} = \\operatorname{id}_{U}$;\n2. $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\varphi_{t}(q) = X\\bigl(\\varphi_{t}(q)\\bigr)$ for all $t \\in (-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)$ and $q \\in U$;\n3. **Uniqueness**: two integral curves through the same point agree on their common interval of definition;\n4. **Local one-parameter group**: whenever $|s|, |t|, |s+t| < \\varepsilon$ and all intermediate points lie in $U$, $\\varphi_{s+t} = \\varphi_{s}\\circ\\varphi_{t}$.\n\nMoreover, gluing the local flows of the various points along curves yields a unique **maximal flow** (its domain $\\mathcal{W} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}\\times M$ is open; see `def.flow`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#46714865',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**This is a pure ODE theorem**: in a chart $(V,x)$, $X = \\sum_{i}X^{i}\\partial_{i}$, the equation becomes the autonomous system\n\n$$\\dot y^{i}(t) = X^{i}\\bigl(y(t)\\bigr)\\qquad (i = 1,\\ldots,n),$$\n\nwhose right-hand side is smooth ($X^{i}$ is smooth), so Picard–Lindelöf gives local existence and uniqueness, and smooth dependence of solutions on initial data ($C^{\\infty}$) gives the smoothness of $\\varphi$. The manifold enters in exactly one place: **do it in a chart**.\n\n**\"Autonomous\" is the source of the one-parameter group property**: solutions of a non-autonomous equation $\\dot y = f(t,y)$ do not form a group (going first for $s$ and then for $t$ differs from going directly for $s+t$, because the \"environment\" changes). The flow of a vector field is autonomous, hence invariant under time translation — this is $\\varphi_{s}\\circ\\varphi_{t} = \\varphi_{s+t}$.\n\n**Why the maximal flow is unique**: two maximal flows have the same initial-value problem at an intersection point, so by uniqueness they agree locally; gluing all local solutions along curves gives the one with maximal domain. The domain $\\mathcal{W}$ is open: near each point there is a time interval supplied by existence, and this time can be chosen lower semicontinuous in $p$.\n\n**The cost**: this map does not prove Picard–Lindelöf (a theorem from ODE courses); it only performs the reduction \"turn a flow on a manifold into an autonomous system on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$\". A similar trade-off appears in `prop.geodesic-existence` (existence and uniqueness of geodesics are treated the same way).\n\n⚠ Uniqueness means \"uniqueness as **the same curve**\": reparametrizing gives a reparametrization of the same curve, not a second solution. $\\varphi_{t}(q)$ already fixes the parameter as \"the time of the flow\", so there is no ambiguity here.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#568cb553',
      },
    },

    'def.moving-frame': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U_{0} \\subseteq S$ be an open set on the surface. A **moving frame** is a triple of $C^{1}$ vector fields $e_{1}, e_{2}, e_{3}$ such that at every point $(e_{1}, e_{2}, e_{3})$ is an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ (it moves with the point, hence \"moving\"). The **dual coframe** is\n\n$$\\omega^{i} := \\langle \\mathrm{d}r,\\, e_{i}\\rangle ,\\qquad i = 1,2,3,$$\n\nthese are $1$-forms on the **parameter domain** ($v$ must first be sent by $\\mathrm{d}r$ to an ambient vector, and only then can it be paired with $e_{i}$). ⚠ For a **surface**, $\\mathrm{d}r$ lies in the tangent plane, so $\\omega^{3} \\equiv 0$; and $\\omega^{i}(e_{j}) = \\delta^{i}_{\\ j}$ must be read as \"the $\\omega^{i}$ on the parameter domain paired with the **pullback to the parameter domain** of the tangent vector $e_{j}$\" — $\\omega^{i}$ is defined on the parameter domain $U$ and $e_{j}$ is an ambient vector, so only $j = 1, 2$ (the tangential ones) lie in the pairing domain; $e_{3}$ is normal, and $\\omega^{3}(e_{3})$ is **not defined** here. The differential of the position vector expands in the frame as\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}r = \\omega^{1}e_{1}+\\omega^{2}e_{2}+\\omega^{3}e_{3}.$$\n\nThis is called the **soldering equation**: it translates \"how $r$ moves\" into \"how much it moves in each frame component\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#14dfb8b0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why change the language at all**: the coordinate route requires computing $r_{uu}, r_{uv}, r_{vv}$ and the partial derivatives of $n$; the frame route expands $\\mathrm{d}r$ and $\\mathrm{d}e_{i}$ **once**, after which everything is algebra between $1$-forms (wedge products, comparing coefficients). This is exactly the point of \"using a moving frame in place of a complicated computation\".\n\n**The standard choice on a surface**: $e_{3} = n$, with $e_{1}, e_{2}$ unit tangent vector fields orthogonal to $n$. Then $\\omega^{3} = \\langle \\mathrm{d}r, n\\rangle = 0$: there is no normal increment on the surface.\n\n⚠ $(e_{1}, e_{2})$ exists only locally and is not unique (one may rotate it). Every quantity to be written down must be expressed in a rotation-invariant form (such as $I = (\\omega^{1})^{2}+(\\omega^{2})^{2}$), otherwise it deforms as soon as the frame is changed.\n\n**Notation**: the superscript on $\\omega^{i}$ is an index, not a power; $(e_{1}, e_{2}, e_{3})$ and $(\\omega^{1}, \\omega^{2}, \\omega^{3})$ are dual pairs, and this is the entire bookkeeping rule of the moving-frame method.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0f7793ce',
      },
    },

    'def.singular-homology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be an arbitrary topological space. A **singular $p$-simplex** is an arbitrary continuous map\n\n$$\\sigma : \\Delta^{p} \\longrightarrow X,$$\n\nit need **not** be injective, need not be an embedding, may self-intersect, and may collapse all of $\\Delta^{p}$ to a point. The free abelian group on all singular $p$-simplices is denoted $S_{p}(X)$ (the **singular chain group**). The boundary operator is given by the face maps:\n\n$$\\partial_{p}\\sigma := \\sum_{i=0}^{p} (-1)^{i}\\,\\sigma \\circ d_{i},$$\n\nand again satisfies $\\partial^{2} = 0$ (because $d_{j}d_{i} = d_{i}d_{j-1}$ is literally the same identity as in the simplicial case). Hence\n\n$$H_{p}(X) := \\ker\\partial_{p}\\, \\big/\\, \\operatorname{im}\\partial_{p+1}$$\n\nis called the **$p$-th singular homology group** of $X$.\n\n**Functoriality**: a continuous map $f : X \\to Y$ gives a chain map $f_{\\#}(\\sigma) := f \\circ \\sigma$, which satisfies $f_{\\#}\\partial = \\partial f_{\\#}$ and hence induces $f_{*} : H_{p}(X) \\to H_{p}(Y)$; moreover $(1_{X})_{*} = \\operatorname{id}$ and $(g \\circ f)_{*} = g_{*} \\circ f_{*}$.\n\nHomotopic maps induce the same map: $f \\simeq g \\implies f_{*} = g_{*}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3bd2e009',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to simplicial homology**: every $p$-simplex $[v_{0}, \\ldots, v_{p}]$ of $K$ can be viewed as a singular $p$-simplex (map the vertices $v_{i}$ of $\\Delta^{p}$ linearly to the $v_{i}$ of $K$), which gives a subcomplex $C_{*}(K) \\subseteq S_{*}(|K|)$. `thm.simplicial-approximation` says that this inclusion induces an **isomorphism** $H_{p}(K) \\cong H_{p}(|K|)$.\n\nIn other words: singular homology is \"homology without choosing a triangulation\", and the two agree on triangulable spaces.\n\n⭐ **Why allow such a flood of singular simplices**: this way **every continuous map** can act, so no triangulation and no niceness hypothesis is needed, and the theory is defined for **every** topological space (indeed every pair of spaces).\n\nThe cost is that $S_{p}(X)$ is usually enormous (countability requires extra hypotheses), so direct computation is almost impossible — practical computations rely on long exact sequences (`thm.long-exact-pair`), Mayer–Vietoris (`thm.mv-homology`), and \"singular $=$ simplicial\" (`thm.simplicial-approximation`).\n\n⚠ **This is not the same as `def.singular-chain` (cubical chains on a manifold)**: there the basis consists of maps $c : [0, 1]^{k} \\to M$ (cubes), here of maps $\\sigma : \\Delta^{p} \\to X$ (simplices). The two models give isomorphic homology groups, but the **boundary formulas differ**, and the two $\\partial$'s must not be mixed.\n\n💡 Intuition: a singular $p$-simplex is \"a (possibly collapsed) $p$-dimensional triangle drawn on $X$\"; a singular chain is \"a signed stack of such triangles\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c5b7a91d',
      },
    },

    'lem.second-form-symmetric': {
      statement: {
        en: "Under the frame $e_{3} = n$, write $\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3} = \\sum_{j}c_{ij}\\omega^{j}$ ($i, j = 1,2$). Then\n\n$$c_{12} = c_{21}.$$\n\nSetting $h_{ij} := -c_{ij}$, the matrix $(h_{ij})$ is also symmetric, hence\n\n$$II = \\sum_{i,j=1}^{2}h_{ij}\\,\\omega^{i}\\omega^{j} = h_{11}(\\omega^{1})^{2}+2h_{12}\\omega^{1}\\omega^{2}+h_{22}(\\omega^{2})^{2}$$\n\nis a symmetric bilinear form ($\\omega^{i}\\omega^{j}$ denotes the symmetric product).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d118178a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The proof is one line**: substitute $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{3} = 0$ into the first structure equation, obtaining $(c_{12}-c_{21})\\,\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2} = 0$. See the incoming edge.\n\n**Comparison with the coordinate version**: there symmetry is obtained in $\\{r_{u}, r_{v}\\}$ from \"the matrix $h^{j}_{\\ i}$ of $W$ is self-adjoint because mixed partials are equal\". $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$ is the coordinate-free version of the same fact, and it does not require a special parameter net.\n\n⭐ **The payoff of symmetry**: a real symmetric matrix has orthogonal real eigendirections (spectral theorem) — this is the source of principal directions, Euler's formula and Rodrigues' theorem.\n\n⚠ What is symmetric is the matrix $(c_{ij})$ and $(h_{ij})$ **relative to an orthonormal frame**; if one uses a non-orthogonal $\\{r_{u}, r_{v}\\}$, it is $h_{ij}$ that is symmetric, while $(h^{j}_{\\ i}) = g^{jk}h_{ki}$ is generally not. The equation $\\det(h-kg)=0$ uses exactly the $h_{ij}$ branch.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bcd98b89',
      },
    },

    'def.riemannian-metric': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it puts an inner product on each tangent space and requires it to vary smoothly with the point — with this, a manifold acquires lengths, angles and volumes, and hence geodesics and curvature.\n\nLet $M$ be an $n$-dimensional smooth manifold. A **Riemannian metric** $g$ assigns to each $p\\in M$ an inner product\n\n$$g_{p} : T_{p}M\\times T_{p}M\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{R}$$\n\n(bilinear, symmetric, positive definite), and for all smooth vector fields $X,Y$ the function $p\\mapsto g_{p}(X_{p},Y_{p})$ is smooth. $(M,g)$ is called a **Riemannian manifold**. In a chart,\n\n$$g = \\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}g_{ij}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{i}\\mathrm{d}x^{j},\\qquad g_{ij} = g(\\partial_{i},\\partial_{j}),$$\n\nwhere $(g_{ij})$ is symmetric positive definite. This yields length and volume:\n\n$$L(\\gamma) = \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{g_{\\gamma(t)}\\bigl(\\dot\\gamma(t),\\dot\\gamma(t)\\bigr)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t ,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}V = \\sqrt{\\det(g_{ij})}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\cdots\\mathrm{d}x^{n}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#42a8140f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Existence**: locally a metric always exists (take $\\delta_{ij}$ in coordinates), and then a partition of unity convexly combines the local metrics into a global one — positive definiteness is closed under convex combinations. Hence **every** smooth manifold admits a Riemannian metric (complex and symplectic structures have topological obstructions; metrics do not).\n\n**It is not part of the manifold**: the same smooth manifold carries infinitely many metrics. The choice of metric is **extra structure**; quantities depending only on $g$ are called intrinsic, those depending on an embedding extrinsic.\n\n**The two-dimensional case is the first fundamental form**: restricting the inner product of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ to $T_{p}S$ on a surface gives $I$. So Riemannian geometry is the higher-dimensional version of intrinsic surface geometry, and surface theory is its two-dimensional model.\n\n**The metric determines everything else**: $g$ uniquely determines the Levi-Civita connection and the Christoffel symbols (`def.affine-connection`, `def.christoffel-symbol`), hence geodesics, the curvature tensor and Ricci are all tied to $g$ — this is the entire content of \"intrinsic geometry\".\n\n⚠ Not every Riemannian manifold embeds isometrically into a low-dimensional Euclidean space. Nash's theorem says that it always embeds into some sufficiently high-dimensional $\\mathbb{R}^{N}$, but even then the \"intrinsic\" viewpoint is more economical: conclusions should depend only on $g$, not on the embedding.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6833625b',
      },
    },

    'def.smooth-manifold': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it moves calculus from $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ to spaces that \"look locally like $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$\" — build a stack of coordinate charts, require that changes of chart be smooth, and only then do \"smooth functions\" acquire meaning.\n\nLet $M$ be a Hausdorff, second-countable topological space.\n\nAn **$n$-dimensional chart** on $M$ is $(U,\\varphi)$, where $U\\subseteq M$ is open and $\\varphi : U\\to\\varphi(U)\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is a homeomorphism.\n\nTwo charts $(U,\\varphi)$, $(V,\\psi)$ are **$C^{\\infty}$-compatible** if the **transition map**\n\n$$\\psi\\circ\\varphi^{-1} : \\varphi(U\\cap V)\\longrightarrow\\psi(U\\cap V)$$\n\nis $C^{\\infty}$ (it is defined on an open subset of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, so it is tested by partial derivatives). A family of pairwise compatible charts covering $M$ is an **atlas**; adding to an atlas all charts compatible with it gives a **maximal atlas**, also called a **smooth structure** on $M$. An $n$-dimensional manifold equipped with a smooth structure is an **$n$-dimensional smooth manifold**.\n\n**Smooth functions**: $f : M\\to\\mathbb{R}$ is smooth if for every chart $(U,\\varphi)$, the map $f\\circ\\varphi^{-1}$ is smooth on $\\varphi(U)$.\n\n**Examples**: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ (one chart); $S^{n}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ (stereographic projection gives two charts); $\\mathbb{RP}^{n}$ ($n+1$ affine charts); any open subset; products of manifolds.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c2d1ddd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why one must pass to the maximal atlas**: if one only says \"there is a family of compatible charts\", \"$f$ is smooth\" depends on the choice of that family; after maximalization, every chart compatible with the atlas is automatically in it, so smoothness becomes a property of the manifold rather than of one chart.\n\n**Division of labour among the three topological conditions**: Hausdorff rules out \"two points glued together and inseparable\" (otherwise limits are not unique); second countability gives Lindelöf and paracompactness, hence **partitions of unity** (the `def.paracompact` line) — only with them can local metrics and local forms be glued into global ones.\n\n⚠ **A smooth structure is not part of the topology**: the same topological space can carry inequivalent smooth structures (Milnor's exotic 7-sphere; $\\mathbb{R}^{4}$ carries uncountably many pairwise inequivalent smooth structures). So a \"smooth manifold\" is a topological space **plus** structure, not a property of the space.\n\n**Interface with existing content**: the implicit function theorem gives that level sets in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are manifolds (`thm.regular-level-set`); conversely, every manifold embeds into some $\\mathbb{R}^{N}$ (Whitney), but $N$ may be much larger than $n$, so the abstract definition is not superfluous.\n\n⚠ The surfaces of this map (`def.regular-surface`) are the concrete two-dimensional model: the overlap of two charts is a change of parameters, and \"$C^{\\infty}$-compatible\" is exactly \"smoothness is unchanged after reparametrization\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#19ec52aa',
      },
    },

    'def.vector-field': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it says**: at each point choose a tangent vector, and choose it smoothly as the point varies — that is a vector field. Its real identity is \"the operator that takes directional derivatives of smooth functions\".\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold. A **vector field** on $M$ is a map $X : M \\to TM$ such that $\\pi \\circ X = \\operatorname{id}_{M}$ (each point is assigned a tangent vector at that point). If $X$ is smooth as a map $M \\to TM$, call it a **smooth vector field**; the set of all of them is denoted $\\mathfrak{X}(M)$.\n\nIn each chart $(U,\\varphi)$, write\n\n$$X\\big|_{U} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X^{i}\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{i}},\\qquad X^{i} := \\mathrm{d}x^{i}(X) ,$$\n\nthen $X$ is smooth if and only if on every chart of one atlas every $X^{i}$ is smooth (checking a single atlas suffices).\n\n$X$ **acting on functions** gives a new function\n\n$$(Xf)(p) := \\mathrm{d}f_{p}\\bigl(X_{p}\\bigr) = \\sum_{i} X^{i}(p)\\,\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x^{i}}(p),$$\n\nWhen $X$ is **smooth**, $X$ is a **derivation** on $C^{\\infty}(M)$: $X(fg) = f\\,Xg + g\\,Xf$; conversely every derivation comes from a unique **smooth** vector field. ⚠ An **arbitrary section** satisfying only $\\pi \\circ X = \\mathrm{id}$ does not work: $X = |x|\\partial_{x}$ is a section, but with $f = x$ one gets $Xf = |x| \\notin C^{\\infty}$.\n\n$X$ **vanishes** at $p$ if $X_{p} = 0_{p}$; the **support** of $X$ is $\\overline{\\{p \\in M : X_{p} \\ne 0\\}}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#79198c6a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**\"Smooth\" is the only delicate point**: merely \"choose a tangent vector at each point\" is a set-theoretic choice and can be extremely pathological. Smoothness pins it down to $n$ smooth functions in local coordinates, so all of calculus carries over — this is also the use of the equivalence \"vector field = derivation\".\n\n**$\\mathfrak{X}(M)$ is a $C^{\\infty}(M)$-module**: $X + Y$ and $fX$ (with $f$ smooth) are again vector fields. So vector fields are not objects to be \"added pointwise into numbers\" but elements of a module — on the same level as \"$\\mathrm{d}f$ is a $1$-form\".\n\n**A nowhere-vanishing vector field need not exist**: locally always, globally not. On $S^{2}$ none exists (hairy ball theorem); on $S^{1}$, $S^{3}$ and the torus they do. \"Whether zeros exist\" is the origin of the Poincaré–Hopf theorem and characteristic classes; this map only mentions it.\n\n**Coordinate change**: expanding in two local coordinate systems, the components satisfy $X_{y}^{i} = \\sum_{j}\\frac{\\partial y^{i}}{\\partial x^{j}}X_{x}^{j}$ — the $X^{i}$ on the two sides belong to **different coordinate systems** and must be written separately (for $y = 2x$ and $X = \\partial_{x}$, the $x$ component is $1$ and the $y$ component is $2$, so using one symbol would produce the absurd $1 = 2$). This is the same transformation law as for tangent vectors (contravariant). This is what \"$X$ is a tensor field of type $(1,0)$\" means (`def.tensor-field`).\n\n**On support**: write $\\operatorname{supp} X$ for the closure of \"where $X$ is nonzero\". The partition-of-unity argument needs exactly \"support contained in a chart\", so this is the same notation $\\operatorname{supp}$ as in `def.partition-of-unity` (`thm.smooth-partition-unity`).\n\n**Relation to integral curves**: a vector field gives the first-order ODE $\\dot\\gamma = X(\\gamma)$, whose solutions are integral curves; local solutions always exist, while global ones need not extend. This map only uses local existence; flows and one-parameter groups are not yet in the map.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#51d775d3',
      },
    },

    'def.tangent-space-manifold': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it defines \"all directions at a point\" on a manifold. There is no ambient space to use, so directions are captured by \"curves through the point\": two curves point in the same direction if they have the same velocity in some chart.\n\nLet $M$ be an $n$-dimensional smooth manifold and $p\\in M$.\n\nTwo curves $\\gamma_{1},\\gamma_{2} : (-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)\\to M$ through $p$ ($\\gamma_{i}(0) = p$) are called **tangent at $p$** if there exists (equivalently: for every) chart $(U,\\varphi)$ containing $p$ with\n\n$$(\\varphi\\circ\\gamma_{1})'(0) = (\\varphi\\circ\\gamma_{2})'(0) .$$\n\n\"Exists one chart\" and \"for every chart\" are equivalent, because changing charts only introduces a chain-rule factor. Such an equivalence class is a **tangent vector** at $p$, and the set of all of them is $T_{p}M$.\n\nIn a chart $(U,\\varphi)$, write $x^{i} := \\varphi^{i}$, $\\partial_{i} := \\partial/\\partial x^{i}$.\n\nTake the class of the curve $t\\mapsto\\varphi^{-1}\\bigl(\\varphi(p)+t\\,e_{i}\\bigr)$, denoted $\\partial_{i}(p)$.\n\nThese $n$ tangent vectors are pairwise distinct and linearly independent, forming a basis of $T_{p}M$, hence $\\dim T_{p}M = n$.\n\n**Derivation characterization** (equivalent definition): $T_{p}M$ is in bijection with the linear functionals $D : C^{\\infty}(M)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the Leibniz rule, i.e. $D(fg) = f(p)\\,Dg+g(p)\\,Df$, the correspondence being $v = [\\gamma]\\mapsto\\bigl(f\\mapsto (f\\circ\\gamma)'(0)\\bigr)$.\n\nThe tangent bundle $TM := \\bigsqcup_{p\\in M}T_{p}M$ also carries a smooth manifold structure (dimension $2n$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#45d32444',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why difference quotients do not work**: in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, a tangent vector is the limit of $(\\gamma(t)-\\gamma(0))/t$, which needs subtraction; on a manifold there is no subtraction, only \"coordinates + chain rule\", so the definition must be written in the **transformation-compatible** form \"same velocity in some chart\".\n\n**Why the two definitions are equivalent**: curve classes give directional derivatives (Leibniz follows from the product rule); conversely, given a derivation $D$, apply it to an expansion and use Leibniz to kill terms of order at least two, obtaining $D = \\sum_{i}D(x^{i})\\,\\partial_{i}(p)$ — exactly the directional derivative of some curve.\n\n**Coordinate change**: $\\partial/\\partial x^{i} = \\sum_{j}\\frac{\\partial y^{j}}{\\partial x^{i}}\\,\\partial/\\partial y^{j}$, with the Jacobian as coefficients.\n\nSo tangent vectors are **contravariant** (they move opposite to the coordinates), while cotangent vectors such as $\\mathrm{d}f$ are **covariant**.\n\n⚠ **Notation trap**: $\\partial_{i}(p)$ is a **vector**, while $\\mathrm{d}x^{i}(p)$ is its dual **covector** ($\\mathrm{d}x^{i}(\\partial_{j}) = \\delta^{i}_{\\ j}$).\n\nOn a surface these two are the basis of the tangent plane and the dual coframe (`def.moving-frame`).\n\n**Agreement with the tangent plane of a surface**: for $S\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{3}$, the space $T_{p}S$ (spanned by $r_{u},r_{v}$) is the same space as the abstract definition — take the chart $\\varphi = r^{-1}$ and compare; the velocity of a curve is exactly the velocity in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#63d92051',
      },
    },

    'def.connection-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain terms: the frame **itself also rotates** — after a small step in some direction, $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$ turns slightly. The **connection form** records this \"rotation\" in nine $1$-forms: feed it a direction and it tells you about which axis the frame turns and how fast. It is the same thing as the Christoffel symbols, only recorded in an orthonormal frame.\n\nExpand $\\mathrm{d}e_{i}$ in the frame, writing the coefficients as $\\omega^{j}_{\\ i}$:\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}e_{i} = \\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\omega^{j}_{\\ i}\\,e_{j},\\qquad \\omega^{j}_{\\ i} = \\langle \\mathrm{d}e_{i}, e_{j}\\rangle .$$\n\nThese nine $1$-forms are called **connection forms** ($\\Gamma$-forms). The matrix $(\\omega^{i}_{\\ j})$ is skew-symmetric (see `prop.connection-antisymmetric`), so only three are independent: $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}, \\omega^{1}_{\\ 3}, \\omega^{2}_{\\ 3}$.\n\nAfter taking $e_{3} = n$, they split into two classes:\n\n- $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}$: the **intrinsic** part — the Levi-Civita connection of the surface, from which the Gauss curvature is computed;\n- $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 3}, \\omega^{2}_{\\ 3}$: the **extrinsic** part — the second fundamental form is computed from them.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#744b45b5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Origin of the name**: $\\omega^{j}_{\\ i}$ describes \"how the frame rotates when one walks in a direction\". It is not an artificially invented exterior form: at each point it is a $3\\times3$ skew-symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ ($3$ degrees of freedom), and evaluating it along directions gives a $1$-form.\n\n⭐ **This split is surface theory**: in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ the curvature form vanishes identically; restricted to a surface, the $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}$ part is determined by the intrinsic metric (Gauss equation), while the remaining extrinsic part gives $II$ and the Codazzi equations. The whole of surface theory consists in reading the coefficients of these two classes of forms.\n\n⚠ $\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}$ and $\\omega^{j}_{\\ i}$ differ by a sign; this is the easiest place in the whole map to make a copying error. There is only one criterion: differentiate $\\langle e_{i}, e_{j}\\rangle = \\delta_{ij}$ and do not rely on memory.\n\n**Relation to the Christoffel symbols**: expanding it in the **non-orthonormal** frame $\\{r_{u}, r_{v}, n\\}$, the coefficients are a mixture of $\\Gamma^{k}_{\\ ij}$ and $h_{ij}$ (compare `def.second-fundamental-form` and `def.christoffel-symbol`);\n\nonly in an orthonormal frame does it become a clean skew-symmetric matrix.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3f90a2a2',
      },
    },

    'prop.christoffel-frame': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $e_{1},e_{2}$ be a local orthonormal frame on a surface, and define the connection forms by $\\nabla_{X}e_{j} = \\omega^{k}_{j}(X)e_{k}$ (so that $\\omega^{k}_{j} = -\\omega^{j}_{k}$). Then\n\n① **The Koszul formula gives all the coefficients**:\n\n$$\\omega^{k}_{j}(e_{i}) = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\langle [e_{i},e_{j}],e_{k}\\rangle-\\langle [e_{j},e_{k}],e_{i}\\rangle+\\langle [e_{k},e_{i}],e_{j}\\rangle\\right).$$\n\n② If the frame is the **normalization of orthogonal coordinates**, $e_{1} = \\partial_{u}/\\sqrt{E}$, $e_{2} = \\partial_{v}/\\sqrt{G}$ ($F = 0$), then\n\n$$\\omega^{1}_{2} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{EG}}\\left(\\frac{E_{v}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u-\\frac{G_{u}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\right) = \\frac{(\\ln E)_{v}}{2\\sqrt{G}}\\,\\omega^{1}-\\frac{(\\ln G)_{u}}{2\\sqrt{E}}\\,\\omega^{2},$$\n\nwhere $\\omega^{1} = \\sqrt{E}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$ and $\\omega^{2} = \\sqrt{G}\\,\\mathrm{d}v$ are the orthonormal coframe.\n\nIn one sentence: $\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}$ and $\\omega^{k}_{j}$ are the same thing (connection coefficients) as seen in two frames.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#500b6ffa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The real relation to ① and $\\Gamma$**: the coordinate frame $\\partial_{i}$ is generally neither orthogonal nor normalized; after orthonormalization $e_{i}$ is no longer a coordinate frame ($[e_{1},e_{2}]\\ne 0$), and the extra term $\\langle [e_{i},e_{j}],e_{k}\\rangle$ is precisely the price of this.\n\nSo \"$\\omega^{k}_{j}(e_{i})$ equals some $\\Gamma$\" holds literally only when the metric has already been flattened by the coordinates (flat Cartesian coordinates).\n\n**Two ways of writing along a curve**, the most useful comparison:\n\n- coordinate frame: $\\dot\\gamma = \\dot x^{k}\\partial_{k}$, $\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = \\left(\\ddot x^{k}+\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}\\dot x^{i}\\dot x^{j}\\right)\\partial_{k}$;\n- orthonormal frame: $\\dot\\gamma = y^{j}e_{j}$, $\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = \\left(\\dot y^{k}+\\omega^{k}_{j}(\\dot\\gamma)y^{j}\\right)e_{k}$.\n\nThe second formula has no quadratic term, because the frame itself is rotating: the quadratic term has been absorbed into $\\dot y$ and $\\omega$. **\"Whether the geodesic equation has a quadratic term\" depends on the frame, not on the surface.**\n\n⭐ ② is all the fuel of Liouville's formula: for an orthogonal parameter net, the two coefficient terms of geodesic curvature are exactly the two components of $\\omega^{1}_{2}$.\n\n⚠ Sign: the sign of $\\omega^{1}_{2}$ flips with the orientation and with \"which of $e_{1},e_{2}$ comes first\". This pack uniformly takes $\\omega^{1}_{2} = \\langle \\nabla e_{2},e_{1}\\rangle$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b23e4400',
      },
    },

    'def.complex': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The complex numbers** $\\mathbb{C} := \\mathbb{R}^2$, with the operations\n\n$$(a, b) + (c, d) := (a + c, b + d), \\qquad (a, b)(c, d) := (ac - bd, ad + bc)$$\n\nWrite $i := (0, 1)$; then every complex number is written uniquely as $z = a + bi$ ($a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$).\n\n$\\bar{z} := a - bi$ is **conjugation**, $|z| := \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$ is the **modulus**; and\n\n$$|zw| = |z|\\,|w|, \\qquad z \\bar{z} = |z|^2$$\n\nThus every $z \\ne 0$ has an inverse $z^{-1} = \\bar{z}/|z|^2$: $\\mathbb{C}$ is a field.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7dcd475c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The difference between $\\mathbb{C}$ and $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is only the multiplication — and this multiplication is what makes $\\mathbb{C}$ an **algebraically closed field** (every polynomial has a root) and what makes the integral theory of complex-valued functions cleaner than the real case: the formulation of $|f|$ and of $L^p$ theory no longer has to split positive and negative parts.\n\n⚠ **$\\mathbb{C}$ lies outside the ordered fields**: it carries **no** total order compatible with the operations.\n\nOtherwise, from $1 = 1^2 > 0$ we get $-1 < 0$; but $i \\ne 0$, and in an ordered field **squares are non-negative**, so $i^2 = -1$ should be $\\ge 0$ — a contradiction.\n\nTwo uses of $\\mathbb{C}$ matter most in what follows: integration of complex-valued functions (splitting real and imaginary parts), and complex measures (taking values in $\\mathbb{C}$, automatically finite).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4d90b9e4',
      },
    },

    'def.abs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be an **ordered field** (both $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ are), and let $x \\in K$. Define\n\n$$|x| := \\begin{cases} x, & x \\ge 0 \\\\ -x, & x < 0 \\end{cases} \\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad |x| = \\max\\{x, -x\\}$$\n\nThen $|x| \\ge 0$ always holds, and $|x| = 0 \\iff x = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f0110ad3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Reading: $|x|$ is the **distance to the origin**, independent of sign.\n\nThe distance between two points is $|x - y|$ — the prototype of the distance in a **metric space**: discard the difference, keep only the notion of distance itself.\n\nTwo identities follow at once (both by cases from the definition):\n- $|-x| = |x|$;\n- $|xy| = |x|\\,|y|$.\n\n⚠ **Estimates with inequalities are not part of the definition**: $|x + y| \\le |x| + |y|$ is a **theorem** (the triangle inequality) — the definition has only cases, no inequality.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#197531b3',
      },
    },

    'def.rank': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to W$ be linear. The **rank** of $T$ is the dimension of its image:\n\n$$\\operatorname{rank} T := \\dim \\operatorname{im} T$$\n\nThe rank of a matrix $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$ is the rank of the linear map $K^{n} \\to K^{m}$ ($x \\mapsto Ax$) it defines.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e750ef06',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Rank measures how much information, in dimensions, $T$ retains.\n\nIt is the **only** numerical invariant of linear algebra: two matrices are equivalent under a change of bases (not necessarily the same basis) just when their ranks agree.\n\n⚠ Does rank depend on the **choice** of basis? No: no basis appears in the definition ($\\dim \\operatorname{im} T$ is basis-independent, because dimension is well defined).\n\nThat is exactly why rank is useful.\n\n⭐ The later section on determinants gives a way to compute the rank and test invertibility from matrix entries alone ($\\det \\ne 0 \\iff$ full rank).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#31757d15',
      },
    },

    'def.kernel-image-vs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to W$ be linear. Define\n\n$$\\ker T := \\{\\, v \\in V : T(v) = 0 \\,\\} \\subseteq V, \\qquad \\operatorname{im} T := \\{\\, T(v) : v \\in V \\,\\} \\subseteq W$$\n\ncalled the **kernel** and the **image** of $T$. Both are subspaces, and\n\n$T$ is **injective** iff\n\n$$\\ker T = \\{0\\}$$\n\n$T$ is **surjective** iff $\\operatorname{im} T = W$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fd9584fd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Injective iff the kernel is zero\" is the most useful fact about linear maps: $\\iff$, because $T(u) = T(v) \\iff T(u - v) = 0$ — **linearity turns \"two points coincide\" into \"their difference lies in the kernel\"**.\n\nGeneral maps have no such convenience.\n\n⭐ The kernel and the image are \"the information lost\" and \"the information retained\": the rank–nullity theorem says precisely that together they account for $V$.\n\n⚠ $\\ker T$ is a subspace of $V$, while $\\operatorname{im} T$ is a subspace of $W$ — do not swap them.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#44a85477',
      },
    },

    'def.minimal-poly': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\dim V < \\infty$ and $T : V \\to V$. The set\n\n$$I_T := \\{\\, p \\in K[x] : p(T) = 0 \\,\\}$$\n\nis a non-zero ideal of $K[x]$ (by Cayley–Hamilton, $\\chi_T \\in I_T$). Since $K[x]$ is a principal ideal domain, there is a **unique** monic polynomial $\\mu_T$ with $I_T = (\\mu_T)$.\n\n$\\mu_T$ is called the **minimal polynomial** of $T$. It is the monic polynomial of **lowest degree** that annihilates $T$ (and it is unique).\n\n⚠️ Distinguish it from the \"minimal polynomial of an algebraic element\" in field theory (`def.algebraic-element`, in this atlas): one annihilates an operator, the other an algebraic element; formally the same thing, but the names must be kept apart.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c283a88a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The minimal polynomial is the finest of the **similarity invariants** (together with the characteristic polynomial): the Jordan form is determined by them, and two matrices are similar $\\iff$ they have the same **invariant factors** (equivalently, the same Jordan form).\n\n⭐ The fact that $I_T$ is an ideal matters: $p(T) = 0$, $q(T) = 0 \\implies (p + q)(T) = 0$, and $r\\,p$ lies in it too — so $T$ becomes a **module** over $K[x]$ (with $x$ acting as $T$), and the later proof of the Jordan form is carried out entirely in this module.\n\n⚠ The minimal and characteristic polynomials have the same roots (differing only in multiplicity): both equal the set of eigenvalues of $T$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#615a3310',
      },
    },

    'def.invertible-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "A square matrix $A \\in M_n(K)$ is **invertible** iff there exists $B \\in M_n(K)$ with\n\n$$AB = BA = I_n$$\n\nThen $B$ is unique and written $A^{-1}$. The invertible $n$ matrices form a group under multiplication, denoted\n\n$$\\operatorname{GL}_n(K) := \\{\\, A \\in M_n(K) : A \\text{ is invertible} \\,\\}$$\n\nand called the **general linear group**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5f23149b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ One table of equivalences linking three clusters: for $A \\in M_n(K)$, the following are equivalent —\n- $A$ is invertible;\n- the linear map $x \\mapsto Ax$ is a bijection (that is, an isomorphism);\n- $\\operatorname{rank} A = n$ (full rank);\n- $\\ker A = \\{0\\}$ (nullity $0$);\n- the columns of $A$ are linearly independent (hence a basis);\n- $\\det A \\ne 0$;\n- $A$ is a product of finitely many elementary matrices.\n\n⚠ **Finite dimension is essential**: only when $\\dim V < \\infty$ do \"injective $\\iff$ surjective $\\iff$ invertible\" hold (is the injective shift operator invertible in infinite dimension? No).\n\n⭐ $\\operatorname{GL}_n(K)$ is the **unit group** of the matrix ring $M_n(K)$, and the prototype of an algebraic group: it is the **principal open set** $\\det A \\ne 0$ in $K^{n^{2}}$ (not a closed algebraic set — $A^{-1}$ is not a polynomial coordinate on $K^{n^{2}}$); the affine-algebraic remedy is to **add one coordinate** $t$ and cut out $\\det A \\cdot t = 1$ — this connects with algebraic geometry and Lie groups.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5312e75f',
      },
    },

    'def.orthogonal-complement': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq V$ be a subspace. The **orthogonal complement** of $U$ is\n\n$$U^{\\perp} := \\{\\, v \\in V : \\langle u, v \\rangle = 0 \\ \\text{for all}\\ u \\in U \\,\\}$$\n\nIt is a subspace of $V$, and\n\n- $U \\cap U^{\\perp} = \\{0\\}$;\n- $U \\subseteq (U^{\\perp})^{\\perp}$;\n- **in finite dimension**, $V = U \\oplus U^{\\perp}$, and moreover $(U^{\\perp})^{\\perp} = U$ and $\\dim U^{\\perp} = \\dim V - \\dim U$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#537738af',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ «$V = U \\oplus U^{\\perp}$» is what an inner product space has **beyond** a general vector space: a general subspace has complements, but they are not unique (`def.direct-sum`), whereas the orthogonal complement is the **unique** complement.\n\n⭐ It is paired with the annihilator in duality: $U^{\\perp} \\subseteq V$, $U^{0} \\subseteq V^{\\ast}$, and their dimension formulas look the same ($\\dim V - \\dim U$); under \"$V \\cong V^{\\ast}$\" (via the inner product) they translate into each other.\n\n⚠ $V = U \\oplus U^{\\perp}$ holds in **infinite dimension** only for closed subspaces, not for arbitrary subspaces (this is a genuine theorem of Hilbert space theory).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ef39e32a',
      },
    },

    'def.normal-operator': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to V$ be a linear operator on a finite-dimensional inner product space.\n\n- $T$ is **self-adjoint** (called **symmetric** in the real case and **Hermitian** in the complex case): $T^{\\ast} = T$;\n- $T$ is **normal**: $T^{\\ast}T = T T^{\\ast}$ (it commutes with its own adjoint);\n- $T$ is **unitary** (orthogonal): $T^{\\ast}T = T T^{\\ast} = I$.\n\nHierarchy: $\\{$unitary$\\} \\subset \\{$ normal$\\}$, $\\{$self-adjoint$\\} \\subset \\{$ normal$\\}$, and the intersection of the two is \"self-adjoint and unitary\" (that is, a symmetry with $T^{2} = I$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da123202',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Why these three words: **the spectral theorem holds for normal operators** (they can be diagonalized unitarily), and self-adjoint operators are the most important class of normal operators (all eigenvalues real).\n\nGeneral operators cannot do this (Jordan blocks have no orthonormal basis).\n\n⭐ A self-adjoint operator has real eigenvalues, and eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal — together these two facts are the content of the spectral theorem.\n\n⚠ \"Normal\" unifies unitary and self-adjoint only in the complex case: real symmetric (self-adjoint) and real orthogonal (unitary) are special cases of real normal operators, but in the real case they **need not be orthogonally diagonalizable** (rotation by $90^\\circ$ is orthogonal and normal, yet has no real eigenvalue) — the spectral theorem in the real case requires **symmetry**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fb4a92d0',
      },
    },

    'def.orthogonal-projection': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq V$ be a finite-dimensional subspace and $V = U \\oplus U^{\\perp}$. For $v \\in V$ write uniquely $v = u + w$ ($u \\in U$, $w \\in U^{\\perp}$), and define\n\n$$P_U(v) := u$$\n\n$P_U : V \\to V$ is called the **orthogonal projection** (or orthogonal projection operator). Its properties:\n\n- $P_U$ is linear, $P_U^{2} = P_U$ (**idempotent**), $\\ker P_U = U^{\\perp}$, $\\operatorname{im} P_U = U$;\n- $P_U$ is **self-adjoint**: $\\langle P_U u, v \\rangle = \\langle u, P_U v \\rangle$ (see \"adjoint operator\");\n- $v - P_U v \\perp U$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d886f8dd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **orthogonal projection = idempotent + self-adjoint**.\n\nConversely, every idempotent self-adjoint operator is the orthogonal projection onto some subspace — this \"operator-language\" characterization later becomes, in the spectral theorem, \"a self-adjoint operator whose eigenvalues are only $0, 1$\".\n\n⭐ An idempotent operator that is not self-adjoint is called a **projection**: it projects \"parallel to $\\ker P$ onto $\\operatorname{im} P$\", and is in general not an orthogonal projection.\n\nThe difference between the two is whether the \"shortest distance\" is attained.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#33653ee5',
      },
    },

    'def.isometry': {
      statement: {
        en: "A linear map $T : V \\to V$ is an **isometry** iff it preserves the inner product (equivalently, the norm):\n\n$$\\langle Tv, Tw \\rangle = \\langle v, w \\rangle \\quad (\\forall v, w) \\qquad \\iff \\qquad \\|Tv\\| = \\|v\\| \\quad (\\forall v)$$\n\nIn the real case it is called an **orthogonal transformation**, in the complex case a **unitary transformation**; their matrices are called orthogonal matrices ($Q^{\\mathsf{T}}Q = I$) and unitary matrices ($U^{\\dagger}U = I$).\n\nEquivalent characterization of an isometry: $T^{\\ast}T = I$ ($\\iff T$ invertible and $T^{\\ast} = T^{-1}$). All orthogonal (unitary) transformations form a group $O(n)$ ($U(n)$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b6ce0cd8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ An isometry preserves **all of geometry**: lengths, angles, orthogonality, volume ($|\\det| = 1$).\n\nThus \"two figures are congruent\" is, algebraically, \"they differ by an orthogonal transformation\".\n\n⭐ $O(n)$ is a compact Lie group (bounded and closed), and $SO(n) := \\{Q : \\det Q = 1\\}$ is its connected component (the one containing the identity) — the rotation group.\n\n⚠ The derivation \"preserving the norm $\\implies$ preserving the inner product\" uses the **polarization identity**: the inner product can be recovered from the norm ($\\langle u,v\\rangle = \\frac{1}{4}\\sum_{k=0}^{3} i^{k}\\|u + i^{k}v\\|^{2}$) — this is the other half of the section \"norms need not come from inner products\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0b474a2c',
      },
    },

    'def.bilinear-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V, W, U$ be $K$-vector spaces. A map $B : V \\times W \\to U$ is **bilinear** iff fixing one variable leaves a linear map in the other:\n\n$$B(v + cv', w) = B(v, w) + c\\,B(v', w), \\qquad B(v, w + cw') = B(v, w) + c\\,B(v, w')$$\n\nWhen $V = W$ and $U = K$, it is called a **bilinear form** on $V$. The set of all such maps is denoted $\\operatorname{Bil}(V, W; U)$ and is a $K$-vector space under pointwise operations.\n\nExamples: the **real** inner product (positive definite symmetric), symplectic forms (non-degenerate alternating), and polarizations of quadratic forms (symmetric) are all bilinear forms. ⚠ The **complex** inner product is not bilinear but **Hermitian sesquilinear** (for instance $h(i,1)=-i$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1f30c627',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Bilinear is \"weaker than linear\": $B$ is **in general not** a linear map $V \\times W \\to U$ (the zero map is the only exception; linearity on $V \\times W$ would require $B(v + v', w + w') = B(v,w) + B(v',w')$, which usually fails).\n\nThis is exactly the problem the tensor product solves — **translating bilinear into linear**.\n\n⭐ When $\\dim V, \\dim W < \\infty$, fixing a basis gives $\\operatorname{Bil}(V, W; K) \\cong M_{m \\times n}(K)$ (via $B(e_i, f_j)$), consistent with \"a matrix is the table of coefficients of a bilinear form\".\n\n⭐ $\\operatorname{Bil}(V, W; U) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}(V \\otimes W, U)$ — this is the universal property.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6a91fc12',
      },
    },

    'def.induced-norm': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ be an inner product space. Define\n\n$$\\|v\\| := \\sqrt{\\langle v, v \\rangle}, \\qquad d(u, v) := \\|u - v\\|$$\n\nThen $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is a **norm** on $V$ (positive definite, homogeneous, triangle inequality), $d$ is a **metric**, and\n\n$$\\|v\\| = 0 \\iff v = 0, \\qquad \\|cv\\| = |c|\\,\\|v\\|, \\qquad \\|u + v\\| \\le \\|u\\| + \\|v\\|$$\n\n(the triangle inequality comes from Cauchy–Schwarz.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d61b7ed6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Thus **an inner product space is automatically a metric space**, so convergence, completeness and compactness make sense — the whole toolbox of analysis becomes available.\n\nA complete inner product space is called a **Hilbert space**.\n\n⭐ Conversely: a norm need not come from an inner product.\n\nThe criterion is the **parallelogram law**\n\n$$\\|u + v\\|^{2} + \\|u - v\\|^{2} = 2\\|u\\|^{2} + 2\\|v\\|^{2}$$\n\n($\\ell^{p}$ fails it when $p \\ne 2$, so the norm on $\\ell^{p}$ does not come from any inner product — this is also why $L^{2}$ is special.)\n\n⭐ The formula recovering the inner product is the **polarization identity**: $\\langle u, v\\rangle = \\frac{1}{4}\\bigl( \\|u+v\\|^{2} - \\|u-v\\|^{2} \\bigr)$ (real case).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#257094f3',
      },
    },

    'def.solvable-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "A group $G$ is **solvable** iff there is a normal chain\n\n$$\n\\{e\\} = G_0 \\trianglelefteq G_1 \\trianglelefteq \\cdots \\trianglelefteq G_n = G\n$$\n\nsuch that every quotient $G_i / G_{i-1}$ is an **abelian group**.\n\n**Equivalent description**: put $G^{(0)} := G$ and $G^{(k+1)} := [G^{(k)}, G^{(k)}]$ (the commutator subgroup), obtaining the **derived series**\n\n$$G = G^{(0)} \\trianglerighteq G^{(1)} \\trianglerighteq G^{(2)} \\trianglerighteq \\cdots$$\n\nThen $G$ is solvable $\\iff$ there exists $n$ with $G^{(n)} = \\{e\\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#415395fa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Three equivalent formulations; any one may be used:\n- a normal chain with **abelian quotients** (the definition);\n- the **derived series** terminates after finitely many steps (easiest to compute);\n- there is a composition series all of whose factors are cyclic of prime order (easiest to test).\n\n⭐ The origin of the name and its real value: the main theorem of Galois theory translates \"a polynomial equation is solvable by radicals\" into \"its Galois group is solvable\" — that is where the Abel–Ruffini theorem comes from.\n\nThe other half of this story (field extensions) is in cluster 7.\n\n⚠ \"Solvable\" is **not** \"abelian\": $S_3$ and $S_4$ are solvable but non-abelian.\n\n\"Solvable\" says \"can be peeled apart layer by layer into **abelian quotients**\", i.e. \"finitely many steps away from abelian\".\n\n⭐ Simple + solvable $\\implies$ abelian (the composition series can only be $\\{e\\} \\trianglelefteq G$, and the factor $G$ is simple and must be abelian).\n\nHence **every non-abelian simple group is non-solvable**; $A_5$ is the first example.\n\n⭐ Two classes to know: all abelian groups are solvable (a one-step chain);\n\nall $p$-groups are solvable (induct on the class equation; a non-trivial centre $\\implies$ allows induction on $Z(G)$ and $G/Z(G)$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aa188ca1',
      },
    },

    'def.injective-module': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a ring with identity. A left $R$-module $I$ is **injective** if for every injection $A \\rightarrowtail B$ and every $R$-linear map $A \\to I$, there exists (in general **not unique**) an $R$-linear map $B \\to I$ making the diagram commute:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc} A & \\subseteq & B \\\\[2pt] {\\scriptstyle f}\\downarrow & & \\downarrow{\\scriptstyle \\exists\\, \\widetilde{f}} \\\\[2pt] I & = & I \\end{array}$$\n\nEquivalent formulation (dual to projective objects): the functor $\\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(-, I)$ sends injections to **surjections**, that is, it is exact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#28b06372',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Reading**: injective means \"extensions can always be made\". Note that the extension is **not unique**, so this is not a universal property (unlike the tensor product) but only an existence condition — which makes injectivity hard to characterize pointwise and forces one to use criteria (`thm.baer-criterion`).\n\n**Dual to projective**: reversing all arrows gives the projective objects in `def.projective-object`. In $R\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$, projective = direct summand of a free module, injective = cannot be embedded as a proper submodule of a larger module (see `prop.injective-splitting`).\n\n**Examples**: over a field every module is injective (vector spaces are free, free implies projective, and over a field projective and injective coincide); $\\mathbb{Z}$ is **not** injective: the inclusion $\\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrowtail \\mathbb{Q}$ has no retraction, because $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Q}, \\mathbb{Z}) = 0$.\n\n📌 Reduction route for the criterion: it suffices to test \"$A$ is an ideal of $B$\" (`thm.baer-criterion`); for $\\mathbb{Z}$-modules this further reduces to \"divisible\" (`thm.pid-injective-divisible`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2815091a',
      },
    },

    'def.cech-cohomology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M^{\\bullet}$ be an **abelian presheaf complex** on a site $\\mathcal{C}$, and let $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$. Its $n$-th **Čech cohomology group** is\n\n$$\\check{H}^{n}(X, M^{\\bullet}) := \\Gamma\\bigl(X,\\ \\check{H}^{n}(M^{\\bullet})\\bigr).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3171d522',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Difference from sheaf cohomology**: sheaf cohomology requires **taking an injective resolution first** (expensive); Čech cohomology **computes directly from a cover** (cheap), at the price that it is in general only an approximation.\n\n**Why the name Čech**: it is built from Čech's intuition of \"computing global information from the overlaps of an open cover\" — each layer of overlaps gives a group, inclusions of overlaps give the differential, and taking cohomology gives Čech cohomology.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e087754a',
      },
    },

    'def.distinguished-triangle': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **distinguished triangle** in $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{C})$ is a diagram of the shape\n\n$$K \\longrightarrow L \\longrightarrow M \\longrightarrow K[1]$$\n\nIt is called **distinguished** if it is isomorphic in $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{C})$ to some mapping-cone triangle\n\n$$K \\xrightarrow{\\ f\\ } L \\longrightarrow M(f) \\longrightarrow K[1]$$\n\nA **morphism** of triangles is a family of maps $(u, v, w, u[1])$ making all squares commute.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6b5448ff',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "A distinguished triangle is the **homotopy version** of a \"short exact sequence\": it does not require $M$ to be a quotient, only that $K \\to L \\to M \\to K[1]$ fits together in shape. After adding the shift, a single morphism can be embedded in a sequence of arbitrary length — precisely the form a triangulated category wants.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ffd0c70',
      },
    },

    'def.sieve': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$.\n\nA **sieve** on $X$ is a subfunctor $R \\subseteq h_{X}$ of the presheaf $h_{X} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(-, X)$: for each $X'$ it gives a subset $R(X') \\subseteq \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X', X)$, such that whenever $f \\in R(X')$, every $g : X'' \\to X'$ satisfies $f \\circ g \\in R(X'')$.\n\nThe sieve **generated** by a family of morphisms $(f_{i} : X_{i} \\to X)_{i \\in I}$ is\n\n$$S = \\{\\, g : Y \\to X \\ \\mid\\ \\exists i,\\ \\exists h : Y \\to X_{i},\\ g = f_{i} \\circ h \\,\\}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#281c7f43',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: **a family of \"allowed maps\" above $X$, and anything that can be composed further on is included too**. So a sieve is not an arbitrary family of arrows: it is **closed under precomposition** — exactly what a subfunctor should be.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d9bb138c',
      },
    },

    'def.skyscraper-sheaf': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a topological space, $x \\in X$, $A$ an abelian group, and $i_{x} : \\{x\\} \\hookrightarrow X$ the inclusion. The **skyscraper sheaf** at $x$ with value $A$ is the pushforward sheaf\n\n$$i_{x,*}A : \\quad U \\;\\longmapsto\\; \\begin{cases} A, & x \\in U, \\\\ 0, & x \\notin U . \\end{cases}$$\n\nThe restriction maps: for $x \\in U \\subseteq V$ it is $\\mathrm{id}_{A}$, and in all other cases the zero map. Equivalently, $i_{x,*}A$ is the sheaf that **stands** $A$ **on the single point** $x$ and keeps nothing anywhere else.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5c6f213d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Shape**: a group perched on top of a column — hence \"skyscraper\". It is the simplest **non-trivial** sheaf: only one layer has content.\n\n**Two structural properties**:\n- $i_{x,*}$ is a **pushforward**, and together with $i_{x}^{-1}$ (which in the one-point case is taking the stalk) it forms an adjunction $i_{x}^{-1} \\dashv i_{x,*}$;\n- $i_{x,*}$ is exact and faithful: on $\\{x\\}$ there are only two open sets, $\\{x\\}$ and $\\emptyset$, so there is no exactness to break.\n\n**Use**: skyscraper sheaves are the raw material for **building injective sheaves** — the standard proof that \"the category of sheaves has enough injectives\" is \"prop up each stalk by an injective group, then take the direct product\", see \"enough injective sheaves\".\n\n⚠️ Do not confuse this with \"a sheaf with constant stalks\": the stalk of the skyscraper sheaf at $x$ is $A$, but at other points it is in general **not** $A$ — on a $T_{1}$ space it is $0$ away from $x$, and on a more general space its support is the closure $\\overline{\\{x\\}}$. See \"stalks of a skyscraper sheaf\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c24d2ce2',
      },
    },

    'def.stone-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "- $X$ is **totally disconnected** if every connected component is a single point;\n- $X$ is **extremally disconnected** if the **closure of every open set is again clopen**;\n- a **Stone space** = a totally disconnected compact Hausdorff space;\n- a **Stonean space** = an extremally disconnected compact Hausdorff space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e1ea1fc8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Write $\\pi_{0}(X) := \\{\\, C(x) : x \\in X \\,\\}$ for the set of connected components, equipped with the **quotient topology**; $x \\mapsto C(x)$ gives a continuous map $X \\to \\pi_{0}(X)$.\n\nTwo conditions that look superficially alike sit at opposite ends: **total disconnectedness** says \"shattered until every component is a single point\", while **extreme disconnectedness** says \"the closure of an open set does not grow\". The latter is stronger — an extremally disconnected $T_{1}$ space is necessarily totally disconnected.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#55826a55',
      },
    },

    'thm.uniform-continuity-compact': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a compact metric space and $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ continuous. Then $f$ is **uniformly continuous** on $X$.\n\nIn particular: a continuous function on a closed interval $[a, b]$ is uniformly continuous.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2f2df5d3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The only price for upgrading \"continuous\" to \"uniformly continuous\" is **compactness** — it lets $\\delta$ be chosen \"all at once\" instead of \"point by point\", and this is the template for every elementary proof that \"takes a finite subcover and then the minimum $\\delta$\".\n\n⚠ Replacing the closed interval by an open one fails immediately: $f(x) = 1/x$ is continuous on $(0, 1)$ but **not** uniformly continuous — the closer to $0$ the smaller the $\\delta$ needed, and $0$ is not in the domain, so there is nowhere to put that lower bound.\n\n⭐ Direct uses: Riemann integrability (oscillation sums over partitions can be made uniformly small), uniform convergence of function sequences, and integrals depending on a parameter.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bf33f865',
      },
    },

    'def.completely-normal-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: normality is a relation between closed sets. It is **not inherited** by subspaces — so requiring that **every** subspace be normal is a genuine strengthening, and that is $T_{5}$.\n\nA topological space $X$ is **completely normal** (also **hereditarily normal**) if **every subspace** of $X$ is normal (`def.normal-space`).\n\n**Equivalent criterion (Urysohn's separation theorem; the most usable form)**: $X$ is completely normal $\\iff$ for any two **separated sets** $A, B$ (that is, $\\overline{A} \\cap B = A \\cap \\overline{B} = \\emptyset$) there exist disjoint open sets $U \\supseteq A$ and $V \\supseteq B$.\n\n**Position in the chain**: $T_{6} \\implies T_{5} \\implies T_{4}$.\n\n⚠️ One does **not** need to add \"plus $T_{1}$\": the definition of a normal space already contains $T_{1}$, and a subspace inherits $T_{1}$ from the ambient space, so complete normality automatically gives $T_{1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#719c2973',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why \"separated sets\" is the natural strengthening**: two closed sets are automatically separated ($\\overline{A} = A$, $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$ $\\implies$ $A \\cap \\overline{B} \\subseteq A \\cap B = \\emptyset$).\n\nThus separated sets form a **wider** class than closed sets, and being able to separate a wider class is equivalent to a stronger space.\n\nThis criterion replaces the convoluted \"hereditary\" definition with an operational set-theoretic condition.\n\n⚠️ **Normality is not hereditary — this is the sole reason $T_{5}$ exists**: the classical example is the Tychonoff plank, or a variant of the Sorgenfrey plane — a subspace of a normal space need not be normal. So $T_{5}$ is not a rewording of $T_{4}$ but genuinely adds an axiom.\n\n⚠️ **Every metrizable space is $T_{5}$**: every subspace of a metric space is again a metric space and hence normal. This is why analysis rarely needs $T_{5}$: it holds automatically (the stronger property is $T_{6}$, see `def.perfectly-normal-space`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bae9fac3',
      },
    },

    'def.perfectly-normal-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: the last rung at the top of the chain. It tightens normality by one notch using countability: every closed set is a countable intersection of open sets.\n\nFirst, two notations: a set $A$ is a **$G_{\\delta}$ set** if it is a **countable** intersection of open sets; it is an **$F_{\\sigma}$ set** if it is a countable union of closed sets.\n\nA topological space $X$ is **perfectly normal** if\n\n1. $X$ is normal (`def.normal-space`);\n2. every closed set is a $G_{\\delta}$ set.\n\n(Equivalently: every open set is an $F_{\\sigma}$ set, by taking complements.)\n\n**Position in the chain**: $T_{6} \\implies T_{5} \\implies T_{4}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#07bd0d12',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why \"every closed set is a countable intersection of open sets\" deserves its own axiom**: it guarantees a **continuous function whose zero set is exactly the closed set** ($F = f^{-1}(0)$), not merely \"$f$ vanishes on $F$\". This is crucial in infinite-dimensional analysis ($G_{\\delta}$ points in $C^{*}$-algebras, strengthened forms of the Baire category theorem).\n\n⚠️ **Every metric space is perfectly normal**: $d(x, F)$ is continuous, $F = \\{\\, x : d(x, F) = 0 \\,\\}$, and $\\{\\, x : d(x,F) < 1/n \\,\\}$ is a sequence of open sets — hence a closed set is a countable intersection of open sets ✓. So $T_{6}$ almost always holds automatically in analysis; it is a genuine condition only in general topology.\n\n⚠️ **$T_{6} \\implies T_{5}$ is not free**: one must use \"countable intersection of open sets\" to write the closed set as a zero set (Urysohn's lemma plus a $2^{-n}$ weighted series), then separate on a subspace by $f/(f+g)$ — details are in the edge \"perfectly normal $\\implies$ completely normal\" in this file.\n\n⚠️ **Convention**: the definition of perfect normality already includes normality, hence automatically $T_{1}$, and one need not add $T_{1}$.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (taking complements is free; the zero-set description needs normality):\n- Perfectly normal $\\iff$ normal and every closed set is a $G_\\delta$ set: this is the definition unfolded; normality already includes $T_1$.\n- Every closed set is $G_\\delta$ $\\iff$ every open set is $F_\\sigma$: De Morgan, with no separation axiom needed; when using this to characterize perfect normality, normality must still be kept on both sides.\n- Normal and a closed set $F$ is $G_\\delta$ $\\implies$ $F$ is a zero set of a continuous function: for the representation $F=\\bigcap_n U_n$ use Urysohn's lemma to separate $F$ from $X\\setminus U_n$, then weight by $2^{-n}$ to obtain a uniformly convergent series; the converse follows directly by taking preimages of a continuous function.\n- Normality cannot be replaced by \"every closed set is $G_\\delta$\": the two-point Sierpiński space has every closed set open, yet fails the $T_1$ required here; nor is normality automatically perfect — the singleton of the last point of $[0,\\omega_1]$ is not a $G_\\delta$ set.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#07bc2382',
      },
    },

    'def.quadratic-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_n(\\mathbb{R})$ be symmetric. The function it defines,\n\n$$q(x) := x^{\\mathsf{T}} A\\, x = \\sum_{i, j} a_{ij}\\, x_i x_j \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$$\n\nis called the **quadratic form** (it is a homogeneous quadratic function of $x$). One says that $q$ (or $A$) is:\n\n- **positive definite**: $x \\ne 0 \\implies q(x) > 0$;\n- **positive semidefinite**: $x \\ne 0 \\implies q(x) \\ge 0$;\n- **negative definite** / **indefinite** are defined similarly.\n\nBy the spectral theorem, $q$ is positive definite $\\iff$ every eigenvalue of $A$ is positive $\\iff$ there exists an invertible $P$ with $A = P^{\\mathsf{T}}P$ (that is, $A$ is congruent to a positive definite matrix).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#41dc3e79',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A quadratic form is a **relaxation** of an inner product: an inner product requires positive definiteness, whereas a quadratic form may be indefinite (or even degenerate).\n\nHence every geometric conclusion for inner product spaces must be re-examined for quadratic forms (\"length\" may be negative).\n\n⭐ Positive definite quadratic forms correspond one-to-one with inner products ($\\langle u, v\\rangle := u^{\\mathsf{T}}Av$), and **every inner product space can be turned into the standard Euclidean space by Gram–Schmidt** — this is the meaning of \"positive definite $\\implies$ realizable as $P^{\\mathsf{T}}P$\".\n\n⭐ Indefinite quadratic forms appear in relativity (the Minkowski metric) and in Morse theory (the negative index of inertia of the Hessian = the type of a critical point).\n\n**Translating between \"quadratic form / symmetric matrix / symmetric bilinear form\" needs one polarization formula, and it requires $\\operatorname{char} \\ne 2$**:\n\n- $q(x) = x^{\\mathsf{T}}Ax$ (**$A$ symmetric**): the convention \"$A$ symmetric\" is already in force — a non-symmetric $A$ gives the same $q$ (because $x^{\\mathsf{T}}A x$ sees only the symmetric part), so **quadratic forms correspond one-to-one with symmetric matrices**.\n- $q \\leftrightarrow$ a **symmetric bilinear form** $B$: by the **polarization formula** $B(x,y) = \\frac{1}{2}\\bigl(q(x+y) - q(x) - q(y)\\bigr)$. ⚠️ The $\\frac{1}{2}$ requires $\\operatorname{char} K \\ne 2$; in characteristic $2$ quadratic forms and bilinear forms **no longer correspond** (which is why quadratic form theory in characteristic 2 needs separate treatment).\n- **Positive definite $\\iff$ all eigenvalues positive $\\iff$ congruent to $I$**: the first uses the spectral theorem (real symmetric); the third uses the **law of inertia** (`thm.sylvester-law-inertia`), which requires only invertible congruence, not orthogonality.\n\n⚠️ **Do not confuse \"quadratic form\" with \"homogeneous quadratic polynomial\"**: every homogeneous quadratic polynomial can be written as $x^{\\mathsf{T}}Ax$ (take $A$ symmetric), but in characteristic $2$ the symmetrization of a cross term such as $x_{1}x_{2}$ degenerates.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2cd57e0b',
      },
    },

    'def.first-fundamental-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **first fundamental form** of a surface is the symmetric bilinear form on $T_{p}U$\n\n$$I(v,w) := \\langle \\mathrm{d}r(v), \\mathrm{d}r(w)\\rangle .$$\n\nRelative to parameters $(u,v)$ it is written $I = E\\,\\mathrm{d}u^{2}+2F\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v+G\\,\\mathrm{d}v^{2}$, where $E = \\langle r_{u}, r_{u}\\rangle$, $F = \\langle r_{u}, r_{v}\\rangle$, $G = \\langle r_{v}, r_{v}\\rangle$;\n\nrelative to an orthonormal coframe it is written\n\n$$I = (\\omega^{1})^{2}+(\\omega^{2})^{2}.$$\n\nIt gives lengths and angles of tangent vectors, arc lengths of curves, and the area element: for an orthonormal unit coframe $(\\omega^{1},\\omega^{2})$ **compatible** with the parameter orientation one has $\\mathrm{d}A = \\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2} = \\sqrt{EG-F^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v$; when the coframe is **incompatible** with the parameter orientation there is a sign difference, and the area density must be written $\\lvert\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}\\rvert$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#776fd6ea',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$I$ is the **pullback** of the inner product of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ along $r$: $\\mathrm{d}r$ sends tangent vectors of the parameter domain into the tangent plane, and then one measures with the inner product of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Thus $I$ sees only \"lengths on the surface\" and not \"how the surface bends\" — the bending is in $II$.\n\n**Why an orthonormal coframe gives a sum of two squares**: $\\mathrm{d}r = \\sum_{i}\\omega^{i}e_{i}$ with $e_{i}$ orthonormal, hence $\\langle \\mathrm{d}r, \\mathrm{d}r\\rangle = \\sum_{i}(\\omega^{i})^{2}$;\n\nalong the surface $\\omega^{3} = 0$, only two terms remain.\n\n**Matrix**: $(g_{ij}) = \\begin{pmatrix} E & F \\\\ F & G \\end{pmatrix}$, $\\det g = EG-F^{2} > 0$. Everything later about \"eigenvalues of $h$ relative to $g$\" uses this matrix.\n\n⚠️ Changing the parametrization changes the expressions for $E,F,G$, but $I$ as a tensor is unchanged. An orthogonal parametrization ($F = 0$) can halve the length of the formulas — the string of $\\Gamma$ symbols is written out only in that case.\n\n⚠️ **\"Orientation compatible\" cannot be dropped**: $\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}$ equals $\\sqrt{EG-F^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v$ only when the coframe is compatible with the parameter orientation. Counterexample: on the plane $r(u,v) = (u,v,0)$ take the orthonormal unit coframe $\\omega^{1} = \\mathrm{d}u$, $\\omega^{2} = -\\mathrm{d}v$; then $\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2} = -\\mathrm{d}u\\wedge\\mathrm{d}v$, so the sign is reversed. Area itself is a **density** and requires $\\lvert\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}\\rvert$ (see the edge on the area element).\n\n📌 Example: for the cylinder of radius $R$ parametrized by $r(u,v) = (R\\cos u, R\\sin u, v)$, one has $\\mathrm{I} = R^{2}\\mathrm{d}u^{2} + \\mathrm{d}v^{2}$ ($E = R^{2}$, $F = 0$, $G = 1$), hence $K = 0$ (see `ex.cylinder-first-form`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#233d8041',
      },
    },

    'def.second-fundamental-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **second fundamental form** of a surface is\n\n$$II(v,w) := \\langle \\mathrm{d}r(v), -\\mathrm{d}n(w)\\rangle = \\langle W(\\mathrm{d}r(v)), \\mathrm{d}r(w)\\rangle .$$\n\nIn an orthonormal frame with $e_{3} = n$,\n\n$$II = -\\sum_{i=1}^{2}\\omega^{i}\\,\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3} = \\sum_{i,j=1}^{2}h_{ij}\\,\\omega^{i}\\omega^{j},$$\n\nrelative to parameters $(u,v)$ it is written $II = L\\,\\mathrm{d}u^{2}+2M\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v+N\\,\\mathrm{d}v^{2}$. $II$ is symmetric, and $II(v,v)$ is the normal bending in the direction $\\mathrm{d}r(v)$.\n\nAfter identifying $T_{p}U$ with the tangent plane $T_{p}S$ via $\\mathrm{d}r$, $II$ can also be written directly on tangent vectors: $II(V,W) = \\langle W(V), W\\rangle$. The discussion of $k_{n}(V)$, Rodrigues' theorem, and Euler's formula below uses this form.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#44f9d544',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**One minus sign, three ways of writing it**: $\\langle \\mathrm{d}r, -\\mathrm{d}n\\rangle = -\\sum_{i}\\omega^{i}\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3} = \\sum_{i,j}h_{ij}\\omega^{i}\\omega^{j}$.\n\nPutting the minus sign into the coefficients ($h_{ij} = -c_{ij}$) has the advantage that $h$ is exactly the matrix of $W$; then \"principal curvature $=$ eigenvalue of $h$\" needs no extra sign.\n\n⭐ **Symmetry need not be computed**: it follows from $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{3} = 0$ (`lem.second-form-symmetric`); one does not need equality of the mixed partials $r_{uu}$ and $r_{uv}$.\n\n**Independent of parameters**: $II$ is a tensor, and $L, M, N$ are merely its components relative to $(u,v)$. Under $n \\mapsto -n$ one has $II \\mapsto -II$, while the definition of $W$ already carries a minus sign and changes sign accordingly, so $K$ and $|H|$ are unchanged.\n\n**Geometric meaning**: $II(v,v)$ is \"how much one bends in the normal direction while walking along $v$\"; together with $E, F, G$, $L, M, N$ determine the two principal curvatures (solve $\\det(h-kg) = 0$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#440f4f34',
      },
    },

    'def.geodesic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gamma : I\\to S$ be a curve on a surface $S$ parametrized by arc length ($\\langle \\dot\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma\\rangle = 1$). $\\gamma$ is a **geodesic** if its tangent vector is parallel along itself:\n\n$$\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma(s)}\\dot\\gamma(s) = 0,\\qquad s \\in I.$$\n\nExpanded in coordinates this is the **geodesic equation**:\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^{2}x^{k}}{\\mathrm{d}s^{2}}+\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}(\\gamma(s))\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x^{i}}{\\mathrm{d}s}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x^{j}}{\\mathrm{d}s} = 0,\\qquad k = 1,2.$$\n\nA third, equivalent face: any sufficiently short arc of $\\gamma$ is the **shortest** curve joining its endpoints.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#119d2577',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Division of labour among the three characterizations**: $\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = 0$ is the invariant formulation (recognized by every textbook), the geodesic equation is the computational tool (a system of two second-order ODEs), and shortestness is the original geometric motivation (variational). The equivalence of the first two is a pure expansion; for the third see the discussion around Gauss's lemma.\n\n**The parameter must be affine**: $s\\mapsto as+b$ is still a geodesic parameter; after changing to a general parameter $t$ the equation acquires an extra term $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^{2}s}{\\mathrm{d}t^{2}}\\big/\\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nThe curve itself does not change; only \"constant speed\" does — which is why the word \"geodesic\" always carries an affine parameter.\n\n⚠️ **Locally shortest does not mean globally shortest**: great circles are geodesics of the sphere, but the arc going more than halfway around is not a shortest connection. Conversely \"shortest $\\implies$ geodesic\" is true (variational proof), while \"geodesic $\\implies$ locally shortest\" holds only on sufficiently short arcs (within the injectivity radius of the exponential map).\n\n**Why parallel transport preserves length**: $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}s}\\langle \\dot\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma\\rangle = 2\\langle \\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma\\rangle$, and on a geodesic this expression vanishes, so the speed is constant and the parameter is necessarily an affine function of arc length.\n\n**Every example is worth remembering**: geodesics in the plane are straight lines; on the sphere they are great circles; on a surface of revolution they are governed by Clairaut's relation; on a cylinder one simply rolls a straight line of the development back up.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#374b926f',
      },
    },

    'def.arc-length': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it gives a curve a ruler — accumulate the speed along the curve to get its length, then use length as the parameter to rewrite the curve at constant speed.\n\nLet $\\gamma : [a,b]\\to\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ be a $C^{1}$ curve. Its **arc length** is\n\n$$L(\\gamma) := \\int_{a}^{b}\\lVert\\gamma'(t)\\rVert\\,\\mathrm{d}t ,\\qquad \\lVert\\gamma'(t)\\rVert = \\sqrt{x'(t)^{2}+y'(t)^{2}+z'(t)^{2}} .$$\n\nThe **arc-length function** measured from $t_{0}\\in[a,b]$ is\n\n$$s(t) := \\int_{t_{0}}^{t}\\lVert\\gamma'(u)\\rVert\\,\\mathrm{d}u ,\\qquad s'(t) = \\lVert\\gamma'(t)\\rVert .$$\n\nIf $\\lVert\\gamma'(t)\\rVert\\equiv 1$, one calls $\\gamma$ **arc-length parametrized** (unit-speed parametrized); then arc length is just the difference of parameters. A regular curve can always be reparametrized by arc length (the construction is on the edge); from then on one writes $s$ for the parameter and $T := \\gamma'(s)$ for the unit tangent vector.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#100cefc3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Arc length is independent of the parametrization**: $L(\\gamma\\circ h) = L(\\gamma)$ (with $h$ monotone $C^{1}$), a one-line change of variables. This is the formal statement of \"length belongs to the curve, not to the parametrization\".\n\n**The arc-length parameter is unique up to a constant and an orientation**: two arc-length parameters differ by a constant; traversing the curve backwards gives $s\\mapsto -s$. Hence in formulas written in the arc-length parameter, every constant that appears has no geometric meaning, and every derivative that appears does.\n\n**Notation discipline**: a dot means differentiation with respect to **arc length** ($\\dot\\gamma = T$, $\\ddot\\gamma = \\kappa N$), while a prime means differentiation with respect to an **arbitrary** parameter. Mixing them produces a missing factor of $\\lVert\\gamma'\\rVert$ — the most common copying error in curve theory.\n\n⚠️ Arc length uses only the **inner product**, no other structure of the ambient space, so it carries over verbatim to Riemannian manifolds: replace $\\lVert\\gamma'\\rVert$ by $\\sqrt{g(\\dot\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma)}$ to get the length in `def.riemannian-metric`.\n\n⚠️ Arc length is a one-dimensional measure; its two-dimensional counterpart is `def.area-element`. Both are \"writing the metric as an integrable quantity\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#81b971c3',
      },
    },

    'def.christoffel-symbol': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: moving a vector along a curve on a surface needs a rule, and that rule is called a **connection**; once local coordinates are chosen, the connection degenerates into $2^{3}$ functions $\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}$, which say \"how much of a $\\partial_{k}$ component appears when $\\partial_{j}$ is transported a little in the $\\partial_{i}$ direction\".\n\nEverything uses only the metric coefficients $g_{ij}$ and their first derivatives — so this is the first **intrinsic** object to appear.\n\nLet $(x^{1},x^{2})$ be local coordinates on a surface $S$, let $g_{ij} = \\langle \\partial_{i},\\partial_{j}\\rangle$, and let $(g^{ij})$ be the inverse matrix of $(g_{ij})$. The **Christoffel symbols** are defined by\n\n$$\\Gamma^{k}_{ij} = \\frac{1}{2}g^{k\\ell}\\left(\\partial_{i}g_{j\\ell}+\\partial_{j}g_{i\\ell}-\\partial_{\\ell}g_{ij}\\right).$$\n\nThey are the coefficients of the Levi-Civita connection in the coordinate frame:\n\n$$\\nabla_{\\partial_{i}}\\partial_{j} = \\Gamma^{k}_{ij}\\partial_{k},\\qquad \\Gamma^{k}_{ij} = \\Gamma^{k}_{ji}.$$\n\n(Christoffel symbols. The first engine of the whole of intrinsic geometry.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0d6a98ed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Their only source is the Koszul formula**. The two requirements \"zero torsion\" and \"metric preserving\" together give the unique solution\n\n$$2\\langle \\nabla_{X}Y,Z\\rangle = X\\langle Y,Z\\rangle+Y\\langle Z,X\\rangle-Z\\langle X,Y\\rangle+\\langle [X,Y],Z\\rangle-\\langle [Y,Z],X\\rangle+\\langle [Z,X],Y\\rangle.$$\n\nFor a coordinate frame $[\\partial_{i},\\partial_{j}] = 0$, the right-hand side retains only two derivative terms and the left-hand side is $2\\Gamma^{\\ell}_{ij}g_{\\ell k}$; solving gives the formula above. **The derivation uses only $g_{ij}$ and its first derivatives** — this is the first intrinsic quantity.\n\n⚠️ $\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}$ **is not a tensor**: under a change of coordinates a non-homogeneous second-derivative term appears, so \"$\\Gamma$ equals some number at $p$\" depends on the coordinates. But \"there **exist** coordinates with $\\Gamma(p) = 0$\" is a coordinate-independent fact (normal coordinates); the difference between the two is \"a numerical value\" versus \"existence\".\n\n**Why it determines geodesics**: the only external datum in the geodesic equation is $\\Gamma$, so \"which curves are geodesics\" is entirely a matter of $I$ — already the first step of intrinsic geometry.\n\n⚠️ In two dimensions there are only $2^{3} = 8$ symbols, but symmetry reduces the independent components to $6$ ($k$ fixed, $i \\le j$); in flat coordinates they all vanish.\n\n📌 Example: in polar coordinates $(r, \\varphi)$, $g = \\mathrm{d}r^{2} + r^{2}\\mathrm{d}\\varphi^{2}$, and only three symbols are non-zero: $\\Gamma^{r}_{\\varphi\\varphi} = -r$, $\\Gamma^{\\varphi}_{r\\varphi} = \\Gamma^{\\varphi}_{\\varphi r} = 1/r$ (term-by-term computation in `ex.polar-christoffel`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3ccbdfde',
      },
    },

    'def.lie-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it is about**: a set that is both a group and a smooth manifold, with the two structures respecting each other — multiplication and inversion are both smooth. Group operations can therefore be studied with calculus.\n\nA **Lie group** is a set $G$ carrying at the same time\n\n1. a **group** structure (`def.group`);\n2. a **finite-dimensional smooth manifold** structure (`def.smooth-manifold`);\n\nsuch that **multiplication** $m : G \\times G \\to G$ ($m(g,h) := gh$, with $G \\times G$ given the product manifold structure) and **inversion** $i : G \\to G$ ($i(g) := g^{-1}$) are both smooth maps.\n\nFor $g \\in G$ define **left translation** and **right translation**\n\n$$L_{g} : G \\to G,\\quad L_{g}(h) := gh ;\\qquad R_{g} : G \\to G,\\quad R_{g}(h) := hg .$$\n\nBoth are diffeomorphisms (with inverses $L_{g^{-1}}$ and $R_{g^{-1}}$, respectively), and $L_{g} \\circ L_{h} = L_{gh}$.\n\n**Examples**: $\\operatorname{GL}(n,\\mathbb{R}) = \\{A : \\det A \\ne 0\\}$ (an open subset of $\\mathbb{R}^{n^{2}}$, hence a manifold of dimension $n^{2}$);\n\n$\\operatorname{SL}(n,\\mathbb{R})$, $\\operatorname{O}(n)$, $\\operatorname{SO}(n)$, $\\operatorname{U}(n)$ (cut out by equations, see `thm.regular-level-set`);\n\n$(\\mathbb{R}^{n},+)$;\n\n$S^{1} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}^{\\times}$;\n\nany **countable** discrete group (dimension $0$; ⚠ an uncountable discrete group such as the discrete $( \\mathbb{R},+ )$ is **not second countable**, so in the sense of `def.smooth-manifold` it is not a smooth manifold).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eb9eda98',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**\"Inversion is smooth\" can actually be omitted**: assuming only that multiplication is smooth, every $L_{g}$ and $R_{g}$ is smooth; the inverse function theorem gives smoothness of $i$ near $e$, and $i = L_{g^{-1}} \\circ i \\circ R_{g^{-1}}$ propagates it everywhere. Following convention, the definition here includes both conditions, saving one appeal to the inverse function theorem.\n\n**Why $\\operatorname{GL}(n,\\mathbb{R})$ is a manifold**: $\\det : \\mathbb{R}^{n^{2}} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is continuous, $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is open, so $\\det^{-1}(\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{0\\})$ is open in $\\mathbb{R}^{n^{2}}$ — one chart suffices, with $\\varphi = \\operatorname{id}$.\n\nThis is the least effortful example of \"an open subset inherits the manifold structure\".\n\n**Why $\\operatorname{O}(n)$ is a manifold**: it is the zero set of $F(A) = A^{\\mathsf{T}}A - I$, and $\\mathrm{d}F_{A}(B) = A^{\\mathsf{T}}B + B^{\\mathsf{T}}A$ is surjective on $F^{-1}(0)$ (given a symmetric $S$, take $B = AS/2$), so `thm.regular-level-set` gives $\\dim = n^{2} - n(n+1)/2 = n(n-1)/2$.\n\nThe group structure comes from \"the product of orthogonal matrices is again orthogonal\".\n\n**Topological restrictions**: not every group can be a Lie group (second countability and local Euclideanity are required). Cartan's closed-subgroup theorem says that a closed subgroup of a Lie group is automatically a submanifold — this generalizes \"$\\operatorname{SL}$ and $\\operatorname{O}$ are Lie groups\"; this library only mentions it.\n\n**Why it matters**: Lie groups turn \"symmetry\" into a differentiable object, so differentiating at the identity gives a Lie algebra (`def.lie-algebra`, `prop.left-invariant-fields`) — linearizing a nonlinear problem, the technical core of the whole theory.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d1a3b4e5',
      },
    },

    'def.riemann-zeta': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $\\operatorname{Re} s > 1$ define\n\n$$\\zeta(s) := \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^{s}} = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} e^{-s\\log n}.$$\n\nThis series is **absolutely convergent and locally uniformly convergent** on that half-plane, so $\\zeta$ is holomorphic on $\\{\\operatorname{Re} s > 1\\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#62eab090',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why $\\operatorname{Re}s > 1$ suffices**: $|n^{-s}| = n^{-\\operatorname{Re}s}$, and $\\sum n^{-\\sigma}$ converges for $\\sigma > 1$ (the $p$-test). Hence the Weierstrass test gives local uniform convergence $\\implies$ termwise differentiation is legitimate $\\implies$ holomorphic.\n\n⭐ **Continuation (statement only)**: $\\zeta$ extends to a meromorphic function on $\\mathbb{C}$, with a single pole, a simple pole at $s = 1$ with residue $1$; and there is a functional equation $\\zeta(s) = 2^{s}\\pi^{s-1}\\sin(\\pi s/2)\\,\\Gamma(1-s)\\,\\zeta(1-s)$. ⚠ **This paragraph is a skeleton in this library**: the standard route to the continuation (Euler–Maclaurin, or the Mellin transform of the theta function) has no node here, so it is quoted only as a **known result**, without proof.\n\n📌 **The value at $s = 2$**: $\\zeta(2) = \\pi^{2}/6$ (Euler); values at even integers are given by Bernoulli numbers, while at odd integers all that is known to this day is that $\\zeta(3)$ is irrational (Apéry).\n\n⚠ **The Riemann hypothesis** (unresolved): all non-trivial zeros lie on $\\operatorname{Re}s = 1/2$. This library **does not** record it as a theorem; it notes here only what it asks — 📌 its value is that the location of the zeros of $\\zeta$ is **equivalent** to an estimate for the error term in the distribution of primes, see `thm.prime-number-theorem`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#68476e00',
      },
    },

    'def.elementary-factor': {
      statement: {
        en: "For an integer $p \\ge 0$ define the **elementary factor**\n\n$$E_{p}(z) := \\begin{cases} 1 - z, & p = 0,\\\\ (1 - z)\\exp\\left(z + \\frac{z^{2}}{2} + \\cdots + \\frac{z^{p}}{p}\\right), & p \\ge 1.\\end{cases}$$\n\nKey properties: $E_{p}$ is entire, $E_{p}(0) = 1$, and its **only zero** is $z = 1$; moreover the larger $p$, the closer $E_{p}(z)$ is to $1$ on $|z| \\le 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2a4526b3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why $E_{p}$ is needed**: one wants to build zeros $a_{n}$ from a product $\\prod (1 - z/a_{n})$, but an infinite product need not converge. Replacing each factor by $E_{p}(z/a_{n})$ adds an exponential factor that \"cancels\" the divergence — and an exponential factor is never zero, so the zero set is unchanged.\n\n⭐ **Quantitative estimate (the one formula to remember)**: for $|z| \\le 1$ and $p \\ge 1$,\n\n$$|1 - E_{p}(z)| \\le |z|^{p+1}.$$\n\nProof: $1 - E_{p}(z) = (1-z)\\left[1 - e^{z + \\cdots + z^{p}/p}\\right]$, and the difference in the brackets is controlled by $|z|^{p+1}$ (expand the exponential and cancel the leading terms). This estimate is the entire basis for choosing $p$ so that $\\sum |a_{n}|^{-(p+1)}$ converges.\n\n⚠️ $p$ cannot be taken uniformly small: the faster the zeros grow (for example $a_{n} = n$), the larger the required $p$. This freedom of \"assigning a $p_{n}$ to each zero\" is exactly the flexibility behind the Weierstrass product theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c96f0cbe',
      },
    },

    'def.entire-meromorphic': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: an entire function is one holomorphic everywhere on the whole complex plane; a meromorphic function is one \"holomorphic except at a bunch of isolated poles\", that is, locally a quotient of two holomorphic functions.\n\n**Entire function**: $f$ is holomorphic everywhere on $\\mathbb{C}$. By Liouville's theorem, a bounded entire function is constant; a non-constant entire function has a singularity at $\\infty$ (a polynomial has a pole there, while $e^{z}$ has an essential singularity).\n\n**Meromorphic function**: let $\\Omega$ be a region. If $f$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega$ except on an **isolated** set $P$, and every point of $P$ is a pole of $f$, one says that $f$ is **meromorphic** on $\\Omega$.\n\nEquivalent description: for every $a \\in \\Omega$ there are a neighbourhood $U$ of $a$ and holomorphic functions $p, q$ on $U$ (with $q \\not\\equiv 0$), such that $f = p/q$ holds on $U$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#61430932',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The sphere viewpoint is cleanest: replacing $\\Omega$ by $\\hat{\\mathbb{C}}$, meromorphic functions are exactly the holomorphic maps $\\hat{\\mathbb{C}} \\to \\hat{\\mathbb{C}}$ — poles are the points mapped to $\\infty$. The meromorphic functions on $\\hat{\\mathbb{C}}$ are precisely the rational functions.\n\n⭐ Examples: $1/\\sin z$ (poles at $k\\pi$), $\\tan z$, and the $\\Gamma$ function (poles at $0, -1, -2, \\ldots$).\n\n⚠ An essential singularity **does not count** as meromorphic: the behaviour of $e^{1/z}$ near $0$ is not a pole, so $e^{1/z}$ is not meromorphic on a region containing $0$. Meromorphic requires every bad point to be a pole.\n\n⚠ \"Isolated\" cannot be dropped either: if the singularities have an accumulation point, it is not a pole.\n\n📌 The Laurent expansion at a pole has only finitely many negative-power terms; near a pole a meromorphic function is \"a blow-up of finite order\". Entire functions are the special case $P = \\emptyset$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#926159af',
      },
    },

    'def.ff-faithful': {
      statement: {
        en: "A functor $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ assigns to each pair of objects $X, Y$ a map\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, Y) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}'}(F(X), F(Y)), \\qquad f \\mapsto F(f)$$\n\nThe names are given according to the properties of this map:\n\n- **faithful**: each such map is injective;\n- **full**: each such map is surjective;\n- **fully faithful**: each such map is bijective.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5ec6c56d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "All three names concern the behaviour of $F$ on the **morphism level**, independently of the object level — a fully faithful functor may send two distinct objects to the same object (which is exactly what is allowed in an equivalence).\n\nFaithfulness guarantees that \"morphisms are not merged\", fullness that \"every morphism in the target comes from the source\"; neither implies the other.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#531f27c6',
      },
    },

    'def.free-presentation': {
      statement: {
        en: "A continuous surjection $F \\twoheadrightarrow S$ between compact Hausdorff spaces is called a **free presentation** of $S$ if\n\n1. $F$ is a free compact Hausdorff space;\n2. $F \\to S$ is an epimorphism;\n3. with $R := F \\times_{S} F$, the map $F \\to R$ is an epimorphism.\n\nThen $S \\cong \\operatorname{coker}(R \\rightrightarrows F)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1f1a9992',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Compare with the set-theoretic picture: for $f : X \\to Y$, put $R := X \\times_{Y} X = \\{(x_{1},x_{2}) : f(x_{1}) = f(x_{2})\\}$ (\"pairs of points with the same image\"); then $X/R \\cong \\operatorname{im} f$ (**Noether's first isomorphism theorem**);\n\nconversely, given an equivalence relation $R$ on $X$, set $Y := X/R$, and then $R = X \\times_{Y} X$.\n\nA free presentation carries this picture into $\\mathbf{CHaus}$: $R$ is the equivalence relation \"glue together points with the same image\", and the third condition requires that $R$ itself be a surjective image of a free compact Hausdorff space — so the whole of $S$ is built from free objects by a single coequalizer.\n\nIn $\\mathbf{CHaus}$ coequalizers exist, hence $\\operatorname{coker}(R \\rightrightarrows F)$ exists; it is the quotient space $F/R$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#baa1995a',
      },
    },

    'def.real': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{C}$ denote the set of all **rational Cauchy sequences**, and $\\sim$ the equivalence relation \"difference is a **null sequence**\" (see \"Cauchy sequences and null sequences\"). Put\n\n$$\\mathbb{R} := \\mathcal{C} / \\sim$$\n\nThe elements are equivalence classes $[x_n]$. Operations are defined **termwise**, and the order is defined using representatives:\n\n$$[x_n] + [y_n] := [x_n + y_n], \\qquad [x_n] \\cdot [y_n] := [x_n y_n]$$\n\n$${}[x_n] > 0 :\\iff \\exists q \\in \\mathbb{Q},\\ q > 0,\\ \\exists N,\\ \\forall n \\ge N : x_n \\ge q$$\n\n(The general order is given by $[y_n] \\ge [x_n] :\\iff [y_n - x_n] \\ge 0$.) Zero and one are the constant sequences:\n\n$$0 := [(0,0,0,\\ldots)], \\qquad 1 := [(1,1,1,\\ldots)]$$\n\nFinally, $\\mathbb{Q}$ embeds into $\\mathbb{R}$ via **constant sequences**:\n\n$$q \\mapsto [(q, q, q, \\ldots)]$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9e6ca41c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **This is the construction**: $\\mathbb{R}$ is not assumed to exist; it is glued together from **objects of the previous level** — the rationals ($\\mathbb{Q}$) $\\to$ rational sequences (functions $\\omega \\to \\mathbb{Q}$) $\\to$ the Cauchy sequences among them $\\to$ passing to the quotient by the equivalence relation.\n\nEvery step uses only what is already available; no new object enters from nothing.\n\n- The elements are **equivalence classes**, not sequences themselves;\n\notherwise different ways of approximating the same number would become different real numbers.\n\n- Operations and order are **written using representatives**, so one must verify that they are **independent of the choice of representative** (well-defined) — the step most easily forgotten in a quotient construction.\n\n⭐ The **generalization of this device is \"completion of a metric space\"**: replace $\\mathbb{Q}$ by an arbitrary metric space $(X, d)$, replace $|x_m - x_n|$ by $d(x_m, x_n)$, and \"take Cauchy sequences, modulo null sequences\" carries over verbatim — the construction of $\\mathbb{R}$ is the prototype.\n\n📌 **Three groups of properties are not in the definition**: the field axioms, the order axioms, and the least-upper-bound principle are all **theorems** about this construction (see \"$\\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field\"). Existence is given by the construction and needs no axioms.\n\n📌 Another route is **Dedekind cuts** (taking subsets of $\\mathbb{Q}$ that are non-empty, bounded above, and have no greatest element): the routes differ, but the objects constructed are isomorphic — see \"uniqueness of the complete ordered field\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce35b322',
      },
    },

    'def.complex-partial': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: recombine the two real partial derivatives $\\partial f/\\partial x$ and $\\partial f/\\partial y$ to obtain two complex directional derivatives, \"along $z$\" and \"along $\\bar z$\".\n\nLet $f = u + iv$ be real differentiable in a neighbourhood of $z_0$ (that is, $u, v$ are both real differentiable). Define\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} := \\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} - i\\,\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right), \\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\bar z} := \\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + i\\,\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right).$$\n\nIn these notations the increment of $f$ is written\n\n$$f(z_0 + \\Delta z) - f(z_0) = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}(z_0)\\,\\Delta z + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\bar z}(z_0)\\,\\overline{\\Delta z} + o(\\Delta z).$$\n\nThe two coefficients are **uniquely** determined by the increment: if $A\\Delta z + B\\overline{\\Delta z} = a\\Delta z + b\\overline{\\Delta z} + o(\\Delta z)$, then taking $\\Delta z = t$ (with $t$ real) and $\\Delta z = it$, and letting $t \\to 0$, gives $A = a$, $B = b$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#65f04373',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ $\\partial/\\partial z$ and $\\partial/\\partial \\bar z$ are called **Wirtinger derivatives**.\n\nThey are **not** difference-quotient limits: $\\bar z$ behaves differently in different directions, so the difference quotient has no limit.\n\nThey are merely a pair of linear combinations of real partial derivatives, a bookkeeping device.\n\n⭐ Mnemonic (a footnote in textbooks): substitute $x = (z + \\bar z)/2$, $y = -i(z - \\bar z)/2$ into $f(x, y)$, and **formally** treat $z$ and $\\bar z$ as independent variables, differentiating by the chain rule — this yields the two formulas above.\n\n⚠ This only aids memory — the rigorous definition is the combination on the right, because $z$ and $\\bar z$ are not independent as two coordinates on $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n⭐ What is genuinely useful is its **criterion** property: $f$ is complex differentiable $\\iff$ $\\partial f/\\partial \\bar z(z_0) = 0$.\n\nThat is, \"whether $f$ can contain only $z$ and no $\\bar z$\" becomes something verifiable term by term.\n\n📌 Formally, $\\partial z/\\partial z = \\partial \\bar z/\\partial \\bar z = 1$, $\\partial z/\\partial \\bar z = \\partial \\bar z/\\partial z = 0$ — just like two independent coordinate systems.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5c9b1edf',
      },
    },

    'def.differential-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it turns \"at each point eat $k$ tangent vectors and spit out a number, changing sign when two arguments are swapped\" into a smooth object — a differential form is the kind of quantity on a manifold that can be pulled back and integrated.\n\nLet $U\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open. A **$k$-form** on $U$ is a map $\\omega$ assigning to each point $p\\in U$ an **alternating** $k$-linear form\n\n$$\\omega_{p} : (\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{k}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{R},$$\n\nwhose coefficients in the standard coordinates are smooth:\n\n$$\\omega = \\sum_{i_{1}<\\cdots<i_{k}}a_{i_{1}\\cdots i_{k}}(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{i_{1}}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{i_{k}} ,\\qquad a_{I}\\in C^{\\infty}(U),$$\n\nwhere $\\mathrm{d}x^{i}(v) := v^{i}$ is the differential of the coordinate function and $\\wedge$ is the exterior product ($\\mathrm{d}x^{i}\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{j} = -\\mathrm{d}x^{j}\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{i}$). The whole space is denoted $\\Omega^{k}(U)$.\n\n$\\Omega^{0}(U) = C^{\\infty}(U)$ (a function is a $0$-form); for $k>n$ one has $\\Omega^{k}(U) = 0$; a $k$-form is a combination of $\\binom{n}{k}$ smooth functions.\n\nThe **wedge product** is defined pointwise: $(\\omega\\wedge\\eta)_{p} := \\omega_{p}\\wedge\\eta_{p}$, so $\\Omega^{\\bullet}(U)$ is a graded commutative algebra.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#576813c4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to the exterior algebra**: at each point $\\omega_{p}\\in\\bigwedge^{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{\\ast}$, so a $k$-form is \"a smooth function with values in $\\bigwedge^{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{\\ast}$\".\n\nThe exterior algebra supplies the algebraic structure, smoothness the analytic structure;\n\nonly together do they allow the definition of $\\mathrm{d}$ (`def.exterior-derivative`).\n\n**Difference from a general multilinear form**: a general $k$-linear form has $n^{k}$ components; after imposing alternation only $\\binom{n}{k}$ remain — exactly why it can be integrated (`def.integral-form`).\n\n**The everyday identity of low-degree forms** ($n = 3$): a $0$-form is a function; a $1$-form $P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y+R\\,\\mathrm{d}z$ is work and circulation; a $2$-form is flux; a $3$-form is density. Gradient, curl, and divergence are the same $\\mathrm{d}$ at different degrees.\n\n⚠️ **Direction**: forms travel **against** a map (pullback, `prop.pullback-forms`), while vector fields travel **with** it (pushforward: along a **diffeomorphism**, or when $F$-related). This is the fundamental reason forms are easier to use than vector fields.\n\n**On manifolds**: replace $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ by an $n$-dimensional manifold $M$; one writes everything in coordinate differentials on a chart, then uses the smoothness of transition maps to show that \"being a $k$-form\" is chart-independent — the same argument as \"tangent vectors are chart-independent\" in `def.tangent-space-manifold`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#10876c6b',
      },
    },

    'def.affine-connection': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it specifies \"how a vector changes when transported in a given direction\" — only with this rule can one differentiate vector fields and then speak of acceleration, geodesics, and curvature.\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold and $\\mathfrak{X}(M)$ the set of smooth vector fields. An **affine connection** (linear connection) is a map\n\n$$\\nabla : \\mathfrak{X}(M)\\times\\mathfrak{X}(M)\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak{X}(M),\\qquad (X,Y)\\longmapsto\\nabla_{X}Y ,$$\n\nsatisfying three conditions:\n\n1. $C^{\\infty}(M)$-linear in the first variable: $\\nabla_{fX+gY}Z = f\\,\\nabla_{X}Z+g\\,\\nabla_{Y}Z$;\n2. $\\mathbb{R}$-linear in the second variable: $\\nabla_{X}(aY+bZ) = a\\,\\nabla_{X}Y+b\\,\\nabla_{X}Z$;\n3. **Leibniz rule**: $\\nabla_{X}(fY) = (Xf)\\,Y+f\\,\\nabla_{X}Y$.\n\n$\\nabla_{X}Y$ is called the **covariant derivative** of $Y$ along $X$. In a chart, if $\\nabla_{\\partial_{i}}\\partial_{j} = \\Gamma^{k}_{ij}\\partial_{k}$, then\n\n$$\\nabla_{X}Y = \\sum_{k}\\Bigl(X^{j}\\partial_{j}Y^{k}+\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}X^{i}Y^{j}\\Bigr)\\partial_{k}.$$\n\nOn a Riemannian manifold there is a unique connection that is **torsion-free** ($\\nabla_{X}Y-\\nabla_{Y}X = [X,Y]$) and **metric** ($X\\,g(Y,Z) = g(\\nabla_{X}Y,Z)+g(Y,\\nabla_{X}Z)$), called the **Levi-Civita connection**; its $\\Gamma$ are the Christoffel symbols.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#510ced2c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**How to read the three axioms**: the first says that $\\nabla_{X}Y$ at a point depends only on the value of $X$ at that point (otherwise it would not count as \"along a direction\"); the third says that it is not tensorial in $Y$ — differentiation must see how $Y$ changes. This asymmetry is exactly the boundary between a \"connection\" and a \"tensor\".\n\n**Parallel transport and geodesics**: solving $\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}V = 0$ gives the **parallel transport** of $V$ along $\\gamma$; the solutions of $\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = 0$ are **geodesics**. Both use only $\\nabla$, with no need for an ambient space.\n\n**Connections are not unique**: a general affine connection has infinitely many choices (any non-symmetric part of $\\Gamma$ can be changed). Only adding \"torsion-free + metric\" makes it unique — this is the existence and uniqueness of the Levi-Civita connection (Koszul formula).\n\n**Curvature is the commutator of the connection**: $R(X,Y) = [\\nabla_{X},\\nabla_{Y}]-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}$ (`def.curvature-tensor`). Thus a single \"connection\" gives rise to parallel transport, geodesics, and curvature at once.\n\n⚠️ **Index conventions**: when torsion-free, $\\Gamma^{k}_{ij}$ is symmetric in its two lower indices; $\\omega^{k}_{\\ j}$ is the same object in an orthonormal frame (`def.connection-form`). The two differ by a Jacobi factor when changing frames, and neither **is a tensor**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#88c6034c',
      },
    },

    'def.integral-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it makes precise \"integrating an $n$-form over an oriented region\" — the only thing that can be integrated naturally, because under a change of variables only the transformation factor of a top-degree exterior form is exactly the determinant (without absolute value).\n\nLet $U\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open and let $\\omega = f\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$ be a compactly supported $n$-form. Define\n\n$$\\int_{U}\\omega := \\int_{U}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\cdots\\mathrm{d}x^{n},$$\n\nwhere the right-hand side is the usual (Lebesgue or Riemann) integral and $\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\cdots\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$ denotes Lebesgue measure. **Well-definedness**: if $y = \\psi(x)$ is an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism, then\n\n$$\\omega = (f\\circ\\psi^{-1})\\det\\Bigl(\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\Bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}y^{n},$$\n\nand the change-of-variables formula contains exactly the same $\\det$ (**not** $\\lvert\\det\\rvert$), so the integral is unchanged. Hence on an **oriented** manifold $M$, choosing an oriented atlas and a partition of unity $\\{\\rho_{\\alpha}\\}$, one can define globally\n\n$$\\int_{M}\\omega := \\sum_{\\alpha}\\int_{U_{\\alpha}}\\rho_{\\alpha}\\,\\omega .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#50bb5a27',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why $n$-forms specifically**: under a change of variables the coefficient of $\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$ transforms by $\\det$, which matches the $\\det$ in the change-of-variables formula exactly (no absolute value when orientation is preserved).\n\nTo integrate a form of degree $k<n$, one must first choose a $k$-dimensional submanifold and pull it back — that is \"integration of forms on submanifolds\".\n\n**Orientation cannot be dropped**: replacing $\\psi$ by an orientation-reversing map changes the sign of $\\det$ and hence of the integral. Without an orientation the only integrable objects are **densities** $\\lvert\\omega\\rvert$ — the area element $\\mathrm{d}A$ is a density (`def.area-element`).\n\n**Interface with the area element**: on a surface $\\mathrm{d}A = \\sqrt{\\det g}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\wedge\\mathrm{d}v$, and integrating over an oriented surface gives area.\n\nHere $\\sqrt{\\det g}$ is the factor on the \"density\" side and $\\mathrm{d}u\\wedge\\mathrm{d}v$ the orientation on the \"form\" side; both signs must be right to avoid errors.\n\n**Why partitions of unity suffice**: $\\omega$ is compactly supported, so only finitely many $\\rho_{\\alpha}\\omega$ are non-zero, each supported in an oriented chart, reducing to the case of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$. Without compact support one replaces this by absolute integrability; this library does not elaborate.\n\n⚠️ Notation: $\\int_{M}\\omega$ depends only on $\\omega$ and the orientation, not on the atlas or the partition of unity (the difference between two choices cancels term by term). This is the prerequisite for being able to write Stokes' theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#41261b02',
      },
    },

    'def.symmetric-point': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let the circle $C$ have centre $a$ and radius $R$. One says that $z$ and $z^{*}$ are **symmetric with respect to** $C$ if\n\n$$(z - a)(\\overline{z^{*}} - \\bar a) = R^{2},$$\n\nand $\\arg(z - a) = \\arg(z^{*} - a)$ (that is, $a, z, z^{*}$ are collinear and point in the same direction).\n\n(Equivalently: $z^{*}$ lies on the ray $a + t(z - a)$ ($t > 0$), and $|z - a|\\,|z^{*} - a| = R^{2}$.)\n\nSymmetry with respect to a **line** $L$ is the usual mirror image; regarding $L$ as a circle through $\\infty$, the two cases combine into one statement: \"$z, z^{*}$ are symmetric with respect to $C$ $\\iff$ every circle through $z, z^{*}$ is orthogonal to $C$.\"",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9ef8f0ca',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why define it this way**: inside the circle $|z - a| < R$, the symmetric point lands outside — this is an **inversion**. It makes the \"centre\" and the \"point at infinity\" correspond ($z = a$ gives $z^{*} = \\infty$), which is exactly why `prop.mobius-symmetry` can preserve symmetry under fractional linear transformations.\n\n⭐ **Computational formula** (the one most used in exercises):\n\n$$z^{*} = a + \\frac{R^{2}}{\\overline{z - a}}.$$\n\nSymmetry with respect to the real axis is $z^{*} = \\bar z$; symmetry with respect to the unit circle is $z^{*} = 1/\\bar z$.\n\n⚠ Symmetric points are not \"points at equal distance from the centre\": those are antipodal points. Symmetry is **inversion**, with a fixed product of distances.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8ae879b6',
      },
    },

    'thm.euclid-algorithm': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ be not both zero. First take absolute values ($a, b$ may be negative); if $b = 0$, return $|a|$ directly; otherwise put $r_{-1} = |a|$ and $r_{0} = |b|$, and perform the division with remainder repeatedly\n\n$$r_{i-1} = q_{i}\\, r_{i} + r_{i+1}, \\qquad 0 \\le r_{i+1} < r_{i} .$$\n\nThe remainders decrease strictly, so there is an $n$ with $r_{n+1} = 0$; then $r_{n} = \\gcd(a, b)$, and this $r_{n}$ can be written as $u a + v b$ with $u, v \\in \\mathbb{Z}$: back-substitute the remainders step by step.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a4f09a12',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the only tool in elementary number theory that does not depend on unique factorization: the existence of $\\gcd$ and the Bézout coefficients both come from it, while the fundamental theorem of arithmetic in turn uses $\\gcd$.\n\nComplexity: the remainders decrease at least as fast as Fibonacci numbers, so the number of steps is $O(\\log \\min(|a|, |b|))$ — the worst input is precisely a pair of consecutive Fibonacci numbers (Lamé's theorem).\n\nThe same holds in $K[x]$ (with degree in place of absolute value), so \"$\\mathbb{Z}$ and $K[x]$ are both Euclidean domains\" is one and the same statement.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a11537b7',
      },
    },

    'thm.bezout': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ be not both zero and put $d = \\gcd(a, b)$. Then there exist $u, v \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ with\n\n$$u a + v b = d .$$\n\nMore generally, $\\{\\, ua + vb : u, v \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\,\\} = d\\,\\mathbb{Z}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#75e0b149',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Corollary: $a$ and $b$ are coprime $\\iff$ there exist $u, v$ with $ua + vb = 1$.\n\nThis is the only tool for solving the linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\pmod n$: it has a solution $\\iff$ $\\gcd(a, n) \\mid b$, and the solution set is one residue class modulo $n / \\gcd(a, n)$.\n\n$\\mathbb{Z}[x]$ has no Bézout identity ($\\gcd(2, x) = 1$, yet $2u + xv = 1$ has no solution), so \"Bézout domain\" is a separate name.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#93d6b9b7',
      },
    },

    'prop.integration-by-parts': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F, G : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable with $F', G'$ integrable. Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b F G' = F(b)G(b) - F(a)G(a) - \\int_a^b F' G$$\n\n(written in the customary form: $\\int_a^b u\\,dv = uv\\bigr|_a^b - \\int_a^b v\\,du$.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea9443aa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It is again the **product rule read backwards**: $(FG)' = F'G + FG'$; integrate both sides and use Newton–Leibniz.\n\n⚠ A common error when checking the boundary terms: writing $F(b)G(b) - F(a)G(a)$ as $(FG)(b - a)$ — that means \"the value of $FG$ at the point $b - a$\" (or $(b-a)$ times $FG$), which is neither \"the difference of the product at the endpoints\" nor \"the integral of the product\".\n\n⭐ The most valuable form of integration by parts is \"moving a term\": $\\int F G' = FG\\bigr|_a^b - \\int F'G$ moves the hard-to-integrate side to the other side.\n\nOn the Lebesgue side it corresponds to the whole package of \"weak derivatives / differentiation in the distributional sense\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2f36c554',
      },
    },

    'thm.integral-test': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : [1, +\\infty) \\to [0, +\\infty)$ be **non-negative and decreasing**. Then the series $\\sum f(n)$ and the improper integral $\\int_1^{+\\infty} f$ converge or diverge together, that is,\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} f(n) < +\\infty \\iff \\int_1^{+\\infty} f(x)\\,dx < +\\infty$$\n\nMore precisely, for every $N$ one has the inequality\n\n$$\\int_1^{N+1} f \\le \\sum_{n=1}^{N} f(n) \\le f(1) + \\int_1^{N} f$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6a88eedf',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **the series and the integral are squeezed by the same ruler**.\n\nThe upper bound $f(n) \\le \\int_{n-1}^{n} f$ and the lower bound $\\int_{n}^{n+1} f \\le f(n)$ each come from monotonicity; adding them over the intervals gives the squeezing inequality.\n\n⭐ The classic application: $\\sum 1/n^{s}$ converges for $s > 1$ and diverges for $s \\le 1$ — because the same threshold for $\\int_1^{\\infty} x^{-s}\\,dx$ lies at $s = 1$.\n\n⚠ The function must be **non-negative and decreasing**: if $f$ is defined only at the integers, or oscillates, neither inequality holds.\n\n⚠ It is **not** a corollary of Kummer: the route here — translating a sum into an improper integral — is completely different.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#573222f2',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-condensation': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a_n \\ge 0$ and suppose the sequence is **decreasing**. Then the original series and the one obtained by sampling at $2^{k}$ converge or diverge together, that is,\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n < +\\infty \\iff \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} 2^{k}\\, a_{2^{k}} < +\\infty$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6c301645',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It formalizes **grouping by powers of $2$**: replace every term whose index lies in $[2^{k}, 2^{k+1})$ by the smallest term of that block ($a_{2^{k+1}}$) or the largest one ($a_{2^{k}}$), and squeeze from both sides.\n\n⭐ A demonstration of its power: the convergence of $\\sum \\frac{1}{n \\ln n}$ — after condensation it becomes $\\sum \\frac{2^{k}}{2^{k} \\ln 2^{k}} = \\frac{1}{\\ln 2}\\sum \\frac{1}{k}$, which is plainly **divergent**.\n\n⚠ The hypothesis is that $a_n$ is non-negative and **decreasing** (that is what makes the block replacement valid).\n\n⚠ It is likewise not a corollary of Kummer — it does not even look at ratios.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5c4e244b',
      },
    },

    'thm.lhopital': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f, g$ be differentiable on a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$, with $g' \\ne 0$, and suppose\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to x_0} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to x_0} g(x) = 0$$\n\n(or both tend to $\\pm\\infty$). If $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} \\dfrac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists (allowing $\\pm\\infty$), then\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to x_0} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x \\to x_0} \\frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#19131efd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It replaces \"the ratio of two things that both tend to zero\" by \"the ratio of the derivatives\" — the only justification is Cauchy's mean value theorem, used in a **converse-style** habit: first compute the derivative ratio, which is easier, then declare the original ratio equal to it.\n\n⚠ Do not forget the three hypotheses: an **indeterminate form** ($0/0$ or $\\infty/\\infty$; something like $\\dfrac{0}{\\infty}$ is directly $0$ and does not need it), $g' \\ne 0$, and the existence of the limit on the right.\n\nThe last one is the most dangerous: when $\\lim f'/g'$ does not exist, the right side need not equal the left — apart from saying nothing, one can say nothing (a typical example: $f = x + \\sin x$, $g = x$).\n\n⚠ The proof for the \"$\\infty/\\infty$ type\" is a little more involved than for the \"$0/0$ type\" (one first truncates using the monotonicity of $g$), but the conclusion is the same.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2f9a1228',
      },
    },

    'thm.banach-fixed-point': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, d)$ be a **non-empty complete** metric space (`def.complete`), and let $T : X \\to X$ be a contraction with constant $q < 1$. Then\n\n- $T$ has a **unique** fixed point $x^{\\ast} \\in X$ (that is, $Tx^{\\ast} = x^{\\ast}$);\n- for **every** initial value $x_0 \\in X$, the iteration $x_{k+1} := T x_k$ converges to $x^{\\ast}$;\n- and one has the a priori and a posteriori error estimates\n\n$$d(x_k, x^{\\ast}) \\le \\frac{q^{k}}{1 - q}\\, d(x_0, x_1), \\qquad d(x_k, x^{\\ast}) \\le \\frac{q}{1 - q}\\, d(x_k, x_{k-1})$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#50c5fcf6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is a rare **three-in-one** theorem in analysis: it gives existence, uniqueness, **and** an algorithm (the iteration) **with error estimates**.\n\nMost existence theorems only say \"there is\"; this one says \"how to find it and how accurate it is\".\n\n⚠ Neither hypothesis can be dropped:\n- drop **completeness**: $(0,1]$ is not complete and $T(x) = x/2$ is a contraction, but the fixed point $0$ is not in the space;\n\n- drop $q < 1$: the map $x + 1/x$ in `def.contraction` is a counterexample.\n\n⭐ The standard trick for using it (the next two big theorems both use it): rewrite \"solve the equation $f(x) = y$\" as \"find a fixed point of $\\Phi_y$\", where $\\Phi_y(x) := x - f(x) + y$ is a contraction on a small ball — so \"a solution exists\" becomes \"the ball is complete + the map is a contraction\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c894ecb4',
      },
    },

    'thm.hurwitz': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: if a sequence of holomorphic functions, none of which has a zero, converges uniformly on compact subsets, then the limit function is either identically zero or also has no zeros.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a region, let $f_k$ be holomorphic on $\\Omega$ and converge uniformly on every compact subset of $\\Omega$ to $f$. If every $f_k$ has **no zero** on $\\Omega$, then the limit $f$ is either identically zero or nowhere zero on $\\Omega$.\n\nEquivalently: if $f$ is not identically zero and $f(z_0) = 0$, then for every sufficiently large $k$, $f_k$ has a zero in every small neighbourhood of $z_0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#81a837f7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The exception \"identically zero\" **cannot be removed**: $f_k(z) = 1/k$ has no zeros on the unit disc and converges uniformly on compact subsets to $0$.\n\n⚠ What is needed is \"uniform convergence on every compact set\" (compact-uniform), not pointwise convergence. For pointwise convergence the conclusion fails.\n\n⭐ The proof uses a small closed disc plus Rouché: if $f$ has a zero at $z_0$, choose $r$ so that $f$ has no other zero in $\\overline{B(z_0, r)}$, and let $m = \\min_{|z - z_0| = r}|f| > 0$; uniform convergence of $f_k$ gives $|f_k - f| < m \\le |f|$ on the circle, and Rouché transfers the zero of $f$ to $f_k$, a contradiction.\n\n📌 Complementary to Morera's theorem: Morera handles \"the limit is holomorphic\", Hurwitz handles \"where the zeros of the limit come from\". Together they show that some **discrete** properties of holomorphic functions are closed under uniform limits.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#51bd34af',
      },
    },

    'thm.laurent': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a holomorphic function on an annulus can always be expanded in a Laurent series, and the coefficients are **uniquely** determined by integrals.\n\nLet $f$ be holomorphic on the annulus $A_{r,R}(a)$ ($0 \\le r < R \\le +\\infty$). Then for every $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, taking any $\\rho \\in (r, R)$, define\n\n$$c_n := \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|\\zeta - a| = \\rho} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{(\\zeta - a)^{n+1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta,$$\n\nthis value is independent of the choice of $\\rho$, and $f(z) = \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_n (z - a)^n$ holds on $A_{r,R}(a)$ (uniformly on every compact subset).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#011f9446',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Proof: take $r < \\rho_1 < |z - a| < \\rho_2 < R$ and apply the Cauchy integral formula on the annulus $A_{\\rho_1,\\rho_2}(a)$ (the annulus version, with boundary the outer circle minus the inner circle):\n\n$$f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|\\zeta - a| = \\rho_2} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta - \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|\\zeta - a| = \\rho_1} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta.$$\n\nExpand the first term using $\\dfrac{1}{\\zeta - z} = \\sum_n \\dfrac{(z - a)^n}{(\\zeta - a)^{n+1}}$ (since $|z - a| < \\rho_2$);\n\nexpand the second term using $\\dfrac{1}{z - \\zeta} = \\sum_n \\dfrac{(\\zeta - a)^n}{(z - a)^{n+1}}$ (since $|z - a| > \\rho_1$), which produces the negative powers.\n\n⭐ Uniqueness: the coefficients are given by the integral formula, so they do not depend on the way the expansion is obtained — in practice one may use any algebraic manipulation (geometric series, undetermined coefficients), and the result is necessarily the same.\n\n⚠ The annulus must not degenerate into a disc or an exterior domain: when $r = 0$ the principal part may diverge, and when $R = +\\infty$ the regular part may diverge.\n\n**The degenerate cases are precisely the entry point to the classification of singularities**: when $r = 0$ the three shapes of the principal part correspond to the three types of isolated singularities.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#961c613d',
      },
    },

    'thm.cauchy-higher-derivative': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a holomorphic function is not only infinitely differentiable, but each derivative can be written by the **same** boundary integral — one only raises the power in the denominator by one.\n\nThe setting is the same as for the Cauchy integral formula ($f$ holomorphic on an open set containing $\\overline{\\Omega}$, with $\\partial\\Omega$ a piecewise smooth simple closed curve, positively oriented). Then for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\cdots$ and $z \\in \\Omega$,\n\n$$f^{(n)}(z) = \\frac{n!}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{(\\zeta - z)^{n+1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta.$$\n\nIn particular, $f$ is **infinitely differentiable** on $\\Omega$, and every derivative is holomorphic.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fbdc1fed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof is simply **differentiation under the integral sign**.\n\nWhen $n = 1$, the difference between the difference quotient and the \"formal derivative\" is\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint f(\\zeta)\\left[ \\frac{1}{(\\zeta - z - h)(\\zeta - z)} - \\frac{1}{(\\zeta - z)^2} \\right]\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = \\frac{h}{2\\pi i}\\oint \\frac{f(\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta}{(\\zeta - z - h)(\\zeta - z)^2}.$$\n\nTaking $|h| \\le \\delta/2$, where $\\delta = \\operatorname{dist}(z, \\partial\\Omega) > 0$, the denominator has a positive lower bound independent of $\\zeta$ and $h$, so the modulus of the whole integral is $\\le C|h| \\to 0$ — the limit exists and equals the formal derivative.\n\nThis expression is continuous in $z$, so $f'$ is continuous and one may differentiate again;\n\nfor $n \\ge 2$ one argues by induction.\n\n⭐ This is the **great watershed** between complex and real analysis: a real function can be once differentiable but not twice (e.g. $x|x|$), whereas a complex function that is once differentiable is infinitely differentiable.\n\n⚠ \"Holomorphic\" itself does **not** assume that $f'$ is continuous — continuity is **derived** from this formula, not assumed.\n\nThis is exactly the value of Goursat's step: first remove the superfluous hypothesis, then earn it back as a conclusion.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5b6c60cf',
      },
    },

    'thm.abel-dirichlet-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "**(Dirichlet)** Let $\\{b_n\\}$ be monotone with $b_n \\to 0$, and suppose the **partial sums** of $\\sum a_n$ are bounded (there is $M$ such that $\\bigl| \\sum_{n \\le N} a_n \\bigr| \\le M$ for all $N$). Then $\\sum a_n b_n$ converges.\n\n**(Abel)** Let $\\{b_n\\}$ be monotone and **bounded**, and suppose $\\sum a_n$ converges. Then $\\sum a_n b_n$ converges.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1e8b6f71',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ These two tests deal with series that are, in general, **not absolutely convergent** — the criteria for positive-term series (comparison, Kummer, root, integral) are all useless here.\n\n⭐ **The Leibniz alternating-series test is a special case of Dirichlet**: take $a_n = (-1)^{n}$ (whose partial sums take only the two values $0, 1$, hence are bounded) and let $b_n$ decrease monotonically to $0$; this gives \"the alternating series converges\".\n\nSo Leibniz's test is not an independent tool but the special case just described.\n\n⭐ The heart of the proof is **Abel summation (summation by parts)**: rewrite $\\sum_{k \\le n} a_k b_k$ as $A_n b_n - A_m b_{m+1} - \\sum_{k} A_k (b_{k+1} - b_k)$, so that \"$A$ is bounded\" and \"$b$ is monotone\" each do their job once, and then finish with the Cauchy criterion.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c7291d18',
      },
    },

    'prop.weierstrass-m-test': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f_n : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $|f_n(x)| \\le M_n$ for all $x \\in X$, and suppose the **positive-term series** $\\sum M_n$ converges. Then the series of functions\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} f_n(x)$$\n\n**converges uniformly** on $X$ (and absolutely).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ade83e56',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It is the most useful criterion for uniform convergence: it reduces \"a matter of functions\" to \"a matter of numbers\" — one only needs a dominating series independent of $x$.\n\n⚠ It gives a **sufficient** condition: a uniformly convergent series need not admit such a \"uniform termwise bound\" (examples of the type $\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}$).\n\n⭐ Usage: a power series converges uniformly on $|x| \\le r < R$ precisely by taking $M_n = |a_n| r^{n}$ and applying the root test to $\\sum M_n$.\n\nThis is the entry point for all the properties of power series in the next section.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b8a3cdbc',
      },
    },

    'thm.harmonic-conjugate': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: on a simply connected region every harmonic function is the real part of some holomorphic function — the imaginary part is called its \"harmonic conjugate\" and is unique up to a constant.\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a **simply connected** region and let $u$ be harmonic on $\\Omega$. Then there exists a holomorphic function $\\Omega$ on $f$ such that\n\n$$\\operatorname{Re} f = u.$$\n\nMoreover $v$ (the **harmonic conjugate**, satisfying $v_x = -u_y$ and $v_y = u_x$) is unique up to an **additive real** constant — equivalently, $f = u + iv$ is unique up to an **additive purely imaginary** constant.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e5af448a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The proof is \"a closed 1-form is exact\": set $\\omega = -u_y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + u_x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$, then\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega = (u_{xx} + u_{yy})\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\wedge \\mathrm{d}y = 0,$$\n\n$\\Omega$ simply connected $\\implies$ there exists $v$ with $\\mathrm{d}v = \\omega$, that is, $v_x = -u_y$ and $v_y = u_x$; these two equations are exactly the Cauchy–Riemann equations for $(u, v)$, so $f = u + iv$ is holomorphic.\n\n⭐ Uniqueness: if $\\operatorname{Re} f_1 = \\operatorname{Re} f_2 = u$, then $g = f_1 - f_2$ is purely imaginary and holomorphic; writing $g = iy$ with $y$ real-valued, Cauchy–Riemann gives $y_x = y_y = 0$, so $y$ is constant.\n\n⚠ Simple connectivity **cannot be dropped**: $u(z) = \\ln|z|$ is harmonic on $\\mathbb{C}^{\\times}$, but it is not the real part of any single-valued holomorphic function — that would require $\\operatorname{Log}$ itself, and $\\operatorname{Log}$ is multivalued.\n\nGoing once around the origin, $\\oint \\omega = 2\\pi \\ne 0$.\n\n📌 The converse direction (holomorphic $\\implies$ real part harmonic) **does not** need simple connectivity, only the Cauchy–Riemann equations. So \"harmonic\" is slightly wider than \"real part of a holomorphic function\"; what is missing is precisely a topological condition.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e19cf58e',
      },
    },

    'prop.jensen': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be holomorphic on $|z| \\le R$ with $f(0) \\ne 0$, and let its zeros be $z_{1}, \\ldots, z_{n}$ (counted with multiplicity, $0 < |z_{j}| \\le R$). Then\n\n$$\\log |f(0)| + \\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\log \\frac{R}{|z_{j}|} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\log |f(R e^{i\\theta})|\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.$$\n\nEquivalently: the more zeros there are, and the closer they are to the origin, the larger the right-hand side (the average of the logarithm of the modulus on the boundary).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#235f92ef',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: first do the case **without zeros**: put $g = f$ and use the fact that $\\log|g|$ is harmonic (`def.harmonic`); the mean-value property of harmonic functions gives $\\log|g(0)| = $ the boundary average.\n\nWith zeros, \"divide out\" each zero: put\n\n$$B(z) := \\prod_{j=1}^{n} \\frac{R(z - z_{j})}{R^{2} - \\bar z_{j} z},$$\n\nthese are the Blaschke factors sending $z_{j}$ to $0$, with modulus exactly $|z| = R$ on $1$. Then $g := f / B$ has no zeros on $|z| \\le R$, apply the zero-free case to it, and compute term by term $\\log|B(0)| = \\sum \\log(|z_{j}|/R)$. ∎\n\n⭐ **It is the bridge between \"growth $\\leftrightarrow$ zeros\"**: the right-hand side is controlled by $\\log M(R)$, hence\n\n$$n(R)\\,\\log 2 \\le \\log M(2R) - \\log|f(0)|$$\n\nwhich pins \"how many zeros lie in $R$\" below \"the maximum modulus on $2R$\" — this is the entire driving force behind Weierstrass products and the Hadamard factorization.\n\n⚠ It requires $f(0) \\ne 0$. When $f(0) = 0$, first divide out $z^{m}$ and then apply the formula; the right-hand side gains the extra term $m\\log R$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d3b72927',
      },
    },

    'thm.differentiation-general': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in L^1_{loc}$ and $x \\in L_f$. Then for **every** family $\\{E_r\\}_{r>0}$ shrinking nicely to $x$:\n\n$$\\lim_{r\\to0} \\frac{1}{m(E_r)} \\int_{E_r} |f(y) - f(x)| dy = 0, $$\n$$\\lim_{r\\to0} \\frac{1}{m(E_r)} \\int_{E_r} f(y) dy = f(x)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c1275efd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is a one-line estimate: from $E_r \\subseteq B(x, r)$ and $m(E_r) > \\alpha m(B(x, r))$,\n\n$$\\frac{1}{m(E_r)} \\int_{E_r} |f - f(x)| \\le \\frac{1}{m(E_r)} \\int_{B(x, r)} |f - f(x)| \\le (1/\\alpha)\\cdot m(B(x, r)) \\int_{B(x, r)} |f - f(x)|$$\n\nand the right-hand side tends to 0 because $x \\in L_f$. ∎\n\n⭐ All the difficulty lies in choosing the definition of `L_f` correctly (using $|f(y) - f(x)|$ rather than $|f(y)|$) — once that is right, this step is free.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2ee2ae6f',
      },
    },

    'thm.frenet-serret': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gamma$ be an arc-length parametrized $C^{3}$ regular curve with $\\kappa>0$. Then\n\n$$T' = \\kappa N,\\qquad N' = -\\kappa T+\\tau B,\\qquad B' = -\\tau N .$$\n\nIn matrix form,\n\n$$\\begin{pmatrix} T' \\\\ N' \\\\ B' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & \\kappa & 0 \\\\ -\\kappa & 0 & \\tau \\\\ 0 & -\\tau & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} T \\\\ N \\\\ B \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix is antisymmetric — this is precisely the infinitesimal expression of \"the frame is always orthonormal\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#94519a4b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why only two functions**: a $3\\times3$ antisymmetric matrix has $3$ degrees of freedom, but the direction of $T'$ is forced by the definition of $N$ ($\\langle T',T\\rangle = \\langle T',B\\rangle = 0$ are identities), leaving only $\\kappa$ and $\\tau$.\n\n**It is the same thing as the structure equations**: expanding $\\mathrm{d}T,\\mathrm{d}N,\\mathrm{d}B$ in the frame, the coefficients are $\\kappa\\,\\mathrm{d}s$ and $\\tau\\,\\mathrm{d}s$.\n\nThe surface quantities $\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}$ (`def.connection-form`) degenerate in the curve case to these three numbers times $\\mathrm{d}s$.\n\n**Where antisymmetry comes from**: $\\langle T,T\\rangle = \\langle N,N\\rangle = \\langle B,B\\rangle = 1$ and $\\langle T,N\\rangle = \\langle T,B\\rangle = \\langle N,B\\rangle = 0$ are all constants; differentiating gives the antisymmetry of the matrix — literally the same proof as `prop.connection-antisymmetric`.\n\n⚠ It holds only where $\\kappa>0$. At an isolated point with $\\kappa = 0$, $N$ does not exist and the formula is meaningless (although $\\kappa$ itself is still defined).\n\n**What it is good for**: it turns a geometric problem about curves into a linear system — so \"existence + uniqueness\" can be handed over wholesale to ODEs (`thm.curve-fundamental-theorem`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fdd80db1',
      },
    },

    'prop.connection-antisymmetric': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(e_{1}, e_{2}, e_{3})$ be a moving frame (an orthonormal frame) and let $\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}$ be the connection forms. Then for all $i,j$:\n\n$$\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}+\\omega^{j}_{\\ i} = 0 ;$$\n\nin particular $\\omega^{i}_{\\ i} = 0$. Thus the nine connection forms have only three independent components, $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}, \\omega^{1}_{\\ 3}, \\omega^{2}_{\\ 3}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f98c5bb4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof uses \"the inner product is constant\" and needs no coordinates: take the exterior derivative of $\\langle e_{i}, e_{j}\\rangle = \\delta_{ij}$ (see the incoming edge).\n\n⭐ **This is the foundation of the orthonormal frame method**: it writes the rotational degrees of freedom of $\\mathrm{O}(3)$ ($3$ parameters) as $3$ $1$-forms, which is what makes the pairwise exterior products in the structure equations meaningful.\n\n⚠ If one uses a **non-orthonormal** frame instead (such as $\\{r_{u}, r_{v}, n\\}$), then $g_{ij}$ is not $\\delta_{ij}$, antisymmetry fails, and $\\omega$ degenerates into a mixture of $\\Gamma$ and $h$ — exactly the shape of the classical formulas.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#abed57de',
      },
    },

    'prop.continuous-bounded-on-compact': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a compact metric space (for example the closed interval $[a, b]$ in $\\mathbb{R}$), and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous. Then $f$ is bounded on $X$: there exists $M$ such that $|f(x)| \\le M$ for all $x \\in X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aecf9b68',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Neither hypothesis** in the conclusion **can be dropped**:\n- not compact: $f(x) = 1/x$ is continuous on $(0, 1)$ but unbounded;\n- not continuous: $f(x) = 1/x$ on $[0, 1]$ (with $f(0)$ assigned arbitrarily) is unbounded.\n\n⚠ In a general topological space the cleaner formulation is \"the continuous image of a compact set is compact\", together with \"compact subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$ are bounded\"; the proof here takes the sequence route. Both routes are correct; this one is shorter.\n\n**Proof** (by contradiction + sequential compactness).\n\nSuppose $f$ is unbounded; then for every $n$ there is $x_n \\in X$ with $|f(x_n)| > n$.\n\n$X$ compact $\\implies$ sequentially compact, so $\\{x_n\\}$ has a subsequence $x_{n_k} \\to x^{*} \\in X$;\n\n$f$ continuous gives $f(x_{n_k}) \\to f(x^{*})$, and a convergent sequence is bounded, so there is $M$ with $|f(x_{n_k})| \\le M$ for all $k$.\n\nOn the other hand, by construction $|f(x_{n_k})| > n_k \\to \\infty$, a contradiction.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9c7e8282',
      },
    },

    'prop.mobius-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "All fractional linear transformations form a group under composition, denoted $\\operatorname{PGL}(2, \\mathbb{C})$; it is isomorphic to the group of $2 \\times 2$ complex matrices modulo nonzero scalars.\n\nMoreover, it is **exactly** the group of holomorphic automorphisms of the extended plane $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$: every biholomorphism $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}} \\to \\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$ is fractional linear.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f5cf0fb1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Group structure**: matrix multiplication corresponds to composition of transformations; $\\begin{pmatrix} a & b\\\\ c & d\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\lambda\\begin{pmatrix} a & b\\\\ c & d\\end{pmatrix}$ give the same transformation, so one must quotient by scalars. The matrix of the inverse transformation is the inverse matrix (up to a scalar).\n\n**Why there are no other automorphisms**: a holomorphic automorphism of $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$ — look at the image of $\\infty$. If it is $\\infty$, the map is entire and bijective — by a Liouville-type argument it must be $az + b$. Otherwise — when the image is not $\\infty$ — compose first with $z \\mapsto 1/(z - z_{0})$ to reduce to the previous case. ∎\n\n⭐ **Three kinds of subgroups correspond to three geometries**: the real fractional linear transformations preserving the upper half-plane, $\\cong \\mathrm{PGL}(2, \\mathbb{R}) \\cong \\mathrm{PSL}(2, \\mathbb{R})$, form the isometry group of the hyperbolic plane; those preserving the unit disc are conjugate to them. Schwarz–Pick (`thm.schwarz-pick`) says precisely that \"a holomorphic self-map does not increase the hyperbolic distance\", and equality holds exactly for elements of this group.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#98b3f9ec',
      },
    },

    'thm.int-ring': {
      statement: {
        en: "On $\\mathbb{Z} = (\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N})/{\\sim}$, take the operations and order defined in `def.int`. Then:\n\n**(i)** the two operations are **well defined** (independent of the choice of representatives);\n\n**(ii)** $(\\mathbb{Z}, +, \\cdot)$ is a **commutative ring with unity**;\n\n**(iii)** $\\mathbb{Z}$ has **no zero divisors** (it is an integral domain): $xy = 0 \\implies x = 0$ or $y = 0$;\n\n**(iv)** $\\le$ is a total order compatible with the operations: $x \\le y \\implies x + z \\le y + z$, $x \\le y,\\ 0 \\le z \\implies xz \\le yz$;\n\n**(v)** $n \\mapsto [n, 0]$ is an **injection** from $\\mathbb{N}$ to $\\mathbb{Z}$, preserving addition, multiplication, and order.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a59c10b1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Each item is \"move to representatives and use the arithmetic laws of $\\mathbb{N}$\". There is no subtraction in $\\mathbb{N}$, so addition in $\\mathbb{Z}$ is written $[a,b] + [c,d] = [a+c, b+d]$ — the $+$ inside the representatives is the $+$ of `thm.nat-arith-laws`.\n\n⭐ **No zero divisors** is the most interesting item: let $x = [a,b]$ and $y = [c,d]$ both be positive, that is, $a > b$ and $c > d$, and write $a = b + p$ and $c = d + q$ ($p, q \\ge 1$). Then $xy = [ac + bd,\\ ad + bc]$, and\n\n$$(ac + bd) - (ad + bc) = pq > 0,$$\n\nso $xy > 0$, and it cannot be $0$. When the signs are opposite the product is negative, by the same argument.\n\n⚠ Only after this step does \"hence $\\mathbb{Q}$ is an ordered field\" in `def.rat` have a basis — the denominators of rational numbers come from $\\mathbb{Z} \\setminus \\{0\\}$, and the premise that \"$0$ cannot be a denominator\" is exactly \"$bd = 0$ has only the trivial solution in an integral domain\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#74a965dd',
      },
    },

    'def.lie-bracket': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it is about**: two vector fields cannot be \"composed\" into a new vector field, but their commutator can be: subtract the two directional derivatives, the second-order terms cancel automatically, and what remains is still a vector field. This difference is the Lie bracket.\n\nLet $X, Y \\in \\mathfrak{X}(M)$. The **Lie bracket** $[X,Y]$ is the operator acting on smooth functions\n\n$$[X,Y]f := X(Yf) - Y(Xf),\\qquad f \\in C^{\\infty}(M).$$\n\nThe right-hand side appears to use second derivatives, but second partial derivatives commute, so $[X,Y]f$ contains only first derivatives of $f$ — hence it is still a vector field. Explicitly in a chart,\n\n$$[X,Y] = \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\bigl(X^{j}\\partial_{j}Y^{i} - Y^{j}\\partial_{j}X^{i}\\bigr)\\Bigr)\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{i}} .$$\n\nFour properties ($f,g \\in C^{\\infty}(M)$):\n\n1. **Antisymmetry**: $[X,Y] = -[Y,X]$, in particular $[X,X] = 0$;\n2. $\\mathbb{R}$-bilinearity;\n3. **Jacobi identity**: $[X,[Y,Z]] + [Y,[Z,X]] + [Z,[X,Y]] = 0$;\n4. **Leibniz rule**: $[X, fY] = (Xf)\\,Y + f\\,[X,Y]$, and $[fX, gY] = fg\\,[X,Y] + f(Xg)\\,Y - g(Yf)\\,X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bc0adec4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why the result is still a vector field**: $X(Yf)$ and $Y(Xf)$ each contain second derivatives of $f$, in a chart terms such as $X^{j}\\partial_{j}\\bigl(Y^{i}\\partial_{i}f\\bigr)$;\n\nwhen subtracting, the coefficients of $\\partial_{j}\\partial_{i}f$ are $X^{j}Y^{i}$ and $Y^{j}X^{i}$ respectively, and since it is symmetric in $i,j$, these terms cancel one by one.\n\nThe second-order terms disappear, and what remains is a legitimate derivation.\n\n**Item 4 shows it is not a tensor**: $[X,Y]$ is not $C^{\\infty}(M)$-linear in $Y$ — the extra term $f(Xg)Y$ is precisely the trace of \"differentiating twice\". Hence \"the bracket of two vector fields cannot be defined pointwise\", which is the fundamental difference from every tensor operation (`def.tensor-field`).\n\n**Jacobi is the composition of three things**: writing it as $[X,[Y,Z]] = [[X,Y],Z] + [Y,[X,Z]]$, the reading is \"$[X,\\cdot]$ is a derivation\". It is not an arbitrary axiom but a necessary consequence of \"the bracket comes from first-order differential operators\" — in the expansion six terms cancel pairwise, using the product rule.\n\n**Geometric meaning**: $[X,Y]$ measures the difference between \"walk one step along $X$ then one along $Y$\" and \"walk in the reverse order\", divided by the square of the step. Thus $[X,Y] = 0$ means that the two families of integral curves fit into a coordinate net — **provided $X, Y$ are linearly independent at that point** (when $X = Y = \\partial_{x}$ the bracket vanishes, but the two families coincide and cannot serve as two coordinate directions). This is the starting point of Frobenius integrability, which is not developed here.\n\n**The boundary with connections**: $\\nabla_{X}Y$ and $[X,Y]$ look alike but behave differently — a connection is extra structure (`def.affine-connection`), while the bracket is intrinsic to the manifold. The torsion-free condition $\\nabla_{X}Y - \\nabla_{Y}X = [X,Y]$ is precisely the use of this free bracket to constrain the connection.\n📌 Example: on a matrix group the bracket is the **commutator** $[A,B] = AB - BA$. Taking $E,F,H$ as the standard basis of $\\mathfrak{sl}_{2}$, one has $[E,F] = H$, $[H,E] = 2E$, $[H,F] = -2F$ (see `ex-sl2-commutator`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cbecc212',
      },
    },

    'def.gauss-curvature': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: on a surface \"curvature\" is really only **one number**, the Gaussian curvature $K$. Here it is computed from one component of the curvature tensor; the next node (the Theorema Egregium) then shows that it is the same number as $k_{1}k_{2}$.\n\nThe **Gaussian curvature** of a surface $S$ at a point $p$ is defined by the curvature tensor as\n\n$$K(p) = \\frac{\\langle R(\\partial_{1},\\partial_{2})\\partial_{2},\\partial_{1}\\rangle}{\\det(g_{ij})} = \\frac{R_{1221}}{g_{11}g_{22}-g_{12}^{2}},$$\n\n($1,2$ are local coordinate indices). In an orthonormal frame $e_{1},e_{2}$ it reduces to\n\n$$K = \\langle R(e_{1},e_{2})e_{2},e_{1}\\rangle .$$\n\nOn the other hand, if $h_{ij}$ is the matrix of coefficients of the second fundamental form in the same frame, then $K = \\det(h_{ij}) = k_{1}k_{2}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#69fb68ba',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ **Signs and index order** (uniform throughout this package): $R_{ijk\\ell} = \\langle R(\\partial_{i},\\partial_{j})\\partial_{k},\\partial_{\\ell}\\rangle$, so on the unit sphere ($K = 1$, $g_{ij} = \\delta_{ij}$) one has $R_{1221} = 1$, $R_{1212} = -1$.\n\nMany books write \"$K = R_{1212}/\\det g$\", which uses another index order (or another sign for $R$); mathematically it is the same thing.\n\n**The only check that a convention is right: the unit sphere must give $K = 1$.**\n\n**Relation to the contraction $\\frac{1}{2}g^{ik}g^{j\\ell}R_{ijk\\ell}$**: in dimension two there is only one independent component, and enumerating the four indices gives $K = -\\frac{1}{2}g^{ik}g^{j\\ell}R_{ijk\\ell}$ (the sign under the convention of this package). The two expressions give the same number; the difference is only the sign coming from the index order.\n\n**Two routes lead to it**:\n\n- Intrinsic: $R$ involves only $g$ and its first and second derivatives ($\\Gamma$ contains first derivatives, $R$ contains $\\partial\\Gamma$), so knowing $I$ is enough to compute $K$;\n- Extrinsic: $K = k_{1}k_{2} = \\det(h_{ij})$, using the two principal curvatures.\n\nBoth routes give the same number — this is the content of the **Theorema Egregium**.\n\n⚠️ $K$ is a quantity **at a point**, with no direct size relation to \"the curvature of a curve\": on a surface with $K = 1$ there can be curves of curvature $0$ (geodesics), and on a cylinder with $K = 0$ there can be curves of nonzero curvature.\n\n📌 Examples: the sphere of radius $R$ has $K = 1/R^{2}$ (see `ex.sphere-curvature`); the cylinder has $K = 0$ ($k_{1} = 1/R$, $k_{2} = 0$, see `ex.cylinder-first-form`); the plane has $K = 0$. The three examples cover the three signs of $K$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#40094cfe',
      },
    },

    'def.geodesic-curvature': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gamma$ be an arc-length parametrized curve on $S$ and let $n$ be the unit normal of $S$. The **geodesic curvature vector** and the **geodesic curvature** are\n\n$$\\vec\\kappa_{g} = \\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = (\\ddot\\gamma)^{\\top},\\qquad k_{g} = \\langle \\vec\\kappa_{g},\\,n\\times\\dot\\gamma\\rangle = \\langle \\ddot\\gamma,\\,n\\times\\dot\\gamma\\rangle,$$\n\nwhere $(\\cdot)^{\\top}$ denotes projection onto the tangent plane and $n\\times\\dot\\gamma$ is the unit vector in the tangent plane orthogonal to $\\dot\\gamma$ (the $u$ in the Darboux frame $(T,n,u)$, with $T = \\dot\\gamma$). Thus \"$k_{g}$ vanishes identically\" is exactly the criterion for a geodesic:\n\n$$k_{g}(\\gamma)\\equiv 0 \\iff \\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma\\equiv 0 .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#815651d3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Sign**: $k_{g}$ depends on orientation. With $(T,u,n)$ positively oriented, $u = n\\times T$, and the curve bends towards $u$ exactly when $k_{g} > 0$. Replacing $u$ by $-u$ reverses all signs — when discussing \"the integral of $k_{g}$\" the orientation must be fixed first; this matters especially in the Gauss–Bonnet theorem.\n\n**Why it is intrinsic**: the tangential part of $\\ddot\\gamma$ uses only $\\nabla$ (that is, $I$), and needs neither $n$ nor the second fundamental form. So $k_{g}$ is intrinsic, in contrast to $\\kappa_{n}$.\n\n**On the plane**: $\\nabla$ is the ordinary derivative, $\\vec\\kappa_{g} = \\ddot\\gamma$, and $k_{g}$ reduces to the signed curvature $\\kappa$ (for a circle of radius $r$: $k_{g} = \\pm 1/r$). On a surface, $k_{g}$ is the curvature seen after \"flattening the surface along the curve\".\n\n**Relation to parallel transport**: $\\vec\\kappa_{g}$ measures \"how fast the tangent vector deviates from parallel transport\"; $\\vec\\kappa_{g}\\equiv0$ means \"the tangent vector is parallel to itself\", that is, a geodesic.\n\n⚠️ For the difference from $\\kappa$ see the curvature decomposition: $\\kappa^{2} = k_{g}^{2}+\\kappa_{n}^{2}$. Here $k_{g}$ is the tangential component of $\\kappa$ and $\\kappa_{n}$ the normal component.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#65e9ae62',
      },
    },

    'def.regular-surface': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{2}$ be open, $r\\colon U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{3}$ a $C^{2}$ map, and write $r_{u} := \\partial r/\\partial u$, $r_{v} := \\partial r/\\partial v$. If everywhere\n\n$$r_{u}\\times r_{v} \\ne 0 ,$$\n\nthen $r$ is called a **regular parametrized surface** on $U$. Then $\\{r_{u}, r_{v}\\}$ spans a $2$-dimensional subspace at each parameter point (attached to the parameter point, it is denoted $T_{p}S$); for the image $S = r(U)$ to have a **unique** tangent plane as a surface, one must also require $r$ to be an **embedding** onto its image — otherwise the image self-intersects ($r(u,v) = (\\sin u, \\sin 2u, v)$ maps $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,0)$ to the same point, with different tangent planes). And\n\n$$n := \\frac{r_{u}\\times r_{v}}{\\lVert r_{u}\\times r_{v}\\rVert}$$\n\nis the unit normal, and $n\\colon U \\to S^{2}$ is called the **Gauss map**.\n\nThe tangent map $\\mathrm{d}r\\colon T_{p}U \\to T_{r(p)}S$ is determined by $\\mathrm{d}r(\\partial_{u}) = r_{u}$, $\\mathrm{d}r(\\partial_{v}) = r_{v}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#021a4eba',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**In one sentence**: the whole of surface theory is \"read what happens on the surface through objects on the parameter space $U$\" — this is also the starting point of the chapter. The route here turns it into identities of $1$-forms (expanding $\\mathrm{d}r$, $\\mathrm{d}e_{i}$ in the frame) rather than differentiating $r$ with respect to the parameters.\n\n**The tangent map is not the identity on the tangent plane**: it is a linear injection from the tangent space of the parameter domain into $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$, whose image is exactly the tangent plane. When writing $I$ and $II$, both arguments are first sent to the tangent plane by $\\mathrm{d}r$, and then the inner product is taken.\n\n⚠️ A parametrization is not the surface itself: changing $(u,v)$ changes the expressions for $E,F,G$, but not the tensor that is the first fundamental form. So the conclusions in this package are always written \"relative to a frame\", independent of the parametrization.\n\n**Two choices of normal**: both $n$ and $-n$ are unit normals. $II$ and $H$ change sign with this choice, while $K$ and $|H|$ do not — wherever a sign must be fixed, this package always fixes $e_{3} = n$ (the direction of $r_{u}\\times r_{v}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fb27170f',
      },
    },

    'lem.poincare-lemma': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it gives a clean answer to \"when is a closed form exact\" — as long as the region can be contracted to a point (star-shaped), closed implies exact for $k \\ge 1$. So cohomology is not an analytic phenomenon but a topological one.\n\nLet $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be a **star-shaped** open set: there is $p_{0} \\in U$ such that for every $x \\in U$, the segment $\\{p_{0} + t(x - p_{0}) : t \\in [0,1]\\}$ lies entirely in $U$. Then for $k \\ge 1$,\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega = 0 \\implies \\exists\\ \\eta \\in \\Omega^{k-1}(U)\\ \\text{such that}\\ \\omega = \\mathrm{d}\\eta .$$\n\nEquivalently: $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(U) = 0$ for all $k \\ge 1$, while $H^{0}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(U) = \\mathbb{R}$ (a star-shaped region is connected).\n\nThe conclusion also holds for **contractible** open sets: star-shaped implies contractible, and the argument uses only the contraction homotopy, not the specific shape of a star-shaped region.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ec3d8864',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The key is a chain homotopy**: there is a linear map $K : \\Omega^{k}(U) \\to \\Omega^{k-1}(U)$ (the \"integral\" along $x \\mapsto tx$, $t \\in [0,1]$) such that\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}K + K\\mathrm{d} = \\operatorname{id}_{\\Omega^{k}}\\qquad (k \\ge 1),$$\n\nso when $\\mathrm{d}\\omega = 0$, $\\omega = \\mathrm{d}(K\\omega)$, and one takes $\\eta = K\\omega$. The explicit formula for $K$ is an integral involving the interior product $\\iota_{X}$; it is not expanded here — what matters is that \"$K$ exists\", which translates \"homotopy\" into \"algebra\".\n\n**Star-shaped cannot be dropped**: on $U = \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ the angle form $\\omega = \\frac{x\\,\\mathrm{d}y - y\\,\\mathrm{d}x}{x^{2}+y^{2}}$ satisfies $\\mathrm{d}\\omega = 0$, but $\\int_{S^{1}}\\omega = 2\\pi \\ne 0$;\n\nif $\\omega = \\mathrm{d}\\eta$, Stokes' theorem gives $\\int_{S^{1}}\\mathrm{d}\\eta = \\int_{\\partial S^{1}}\\eta = 0$, a contradiction.\n\nSo $H^{1}(\\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\{0\\}) \\ne 0$ — one hole destroys the lemma.\n\n**$k \\ge 1$ cannot be relaxed**: for $k = 0$ \"closed\" means locally constant and \"exact\" means zero (there are no $-1$-forms), so the two are far apart; hence $H^{0} = \\mathbb{R} \\ne 0$. The statement must exclude $k = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f7d31dc6',
      },
    },

    'def.group-cohomology': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M$ be a $G$-module. The **$n$-th cohomology group** of the group $G$ with coefficients in $M$ is the right derived functor of the invariant functor:\n\n$$H^{n}(G, M) := R^{n}(-)^{G}(M),$$\n\nequivalently\n\n$$H^{n}(G, M) = \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{R[G]}\\bigl(R,\\ M\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere $R$ carries the trivial $G$-action. In particular $H^{0}(G, M) = M^{G}$ and $H^{n}(G, M) = 0$ (for $n < 0$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#13f75c37',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **In one sentence**: $H^{0}$ is only \"fixed points\"; $H^{n}$ for $n \\ge 1$ measures **how far the invariant functor is from exact** — that is, how nontrivial the action of $G$ is.\n\n**Two algorithms**:\n- **Injective resolution** (the definition itself): take an injective $R[G]$-module resolution $M \\rightarrowtail I^{\\bullet}$ of $M$, take invariants, take homology. Cleanest, but injective $R[G]$-modules are invisible.\n- **Standard (bar) resolution** (computable): take the **free** $R[G]$-module resolution $B_{\\bullet} \\to R$ of $R$, then take $\\operatorname{Hom}_{R[G]}(B_{\\bullet}, M)$, obtaining an **explicit** cochain complex. This gives a concrete formula for $H^{n}$.\n\n**Why it equals $\\operatorname{Ext}$**: $(-)^{G} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{R[G]}(R, -)$, and computing $R^{n}\\operatorname{Hom}_{R[G]}(R, -)$ with an injective resolution of $R$ is exactly $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{R[G]}(R, -)$.\n\nThe same right derived functor under another name.\n\n$H^{n} = 0$ ($n < 0$) is likewise a direct consequence of \"right derived functor\".\n\n**Two immediate consequences**:\n- If $M$ is a $\\mathbb{Q}$-vector space and $G$ acts trivially, then $(-)^{G}$ is the identity functor (exact), so $H^{n}(G, M) = 0$ for $n \\ge 1$.\n- If $G$ acts trivially on $M$, then $H^{1}(G, M) = \\operatorname{Hom}(G, M)$; in general one replaces \\(\\operatorname{Hom}\\) by \"crossed homomorphisms\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a39c941a',
      },
    },

    'def.bar-resolution': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with $1$, $G$ a group, and $R$ carry the trivial $G$-action. The **standard resolution** (bar resolution) is a free $R[G]$-module resolution of $R$\n\n$$\\cdots \\longrightarrow B_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\ d_{2}\\ } B_{1} \\xrightarrow{\\ d_{1}\\ } B_{0} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\varepsilon\\ } R \\longrightarrow 0 ,$$\n\nwhere $B_{n}$ is the free $R$-module with $R$-basis the **symbols** $[g_{0}|g_{1}|\\cdots|g_{n}]$ ($g_{i} \\in G$), with $R[G]$ acting through the leftmost component:\n\n$$g \\cdot [g_{0}|g_{1}|\\cdots|g_{n}] := [g g_{0}|g_{1}|\\cdots|g_{n}] .$$\n\nThe differential and augmentation are\n\n$$d_{n}[g_{0}|\\cdots|g_{n}] := \\sum_{i=0}^{n} (-1)^{i}\\,[g_{0}|\\cdots|\\widehat{g_{i}}|\\cdots|g_{n}], \\qquad \\varepsilon[g_{0}] := 1 ,$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{g_{i}}$ means that term is omitted.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d2d4d86e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Two points about the notation**:\n- **$R[G]$-freeness**: $[g_{0}|\\cdots|g_{n}] = g_{0} \\cdot [1|g_{1}|\\cdots|g_{n}]$, so as an $R[G]$-module $B_{n}$ has basis $\\{[1|g_{1}|\\cdots|g_{n}]\\}$ — it is **free**, and this is the key to using the whole resolution to compute derived functors (free $\\implies$ projective).\n- **Normalized version**: the subcomplex generated by those symbols with $g_{i} = 1$ for $i \\ge 1$ is denoted $B^{\\mathrm{norm}}_{\\bullet}$; the inclusion $B^{\\mathrm{norm}}_{\\bullet} \\hookrightarrow B_{\\bullet}$ is a quasi-isomorphism (degreewise contractible), so it computes the same homology with far fewer terms.\n\n**Exactness (contracting homotopy)**: set $B_{-1} := R$, $d_{0} := \\varepsilon$, and\n\n$$s_{n}[g_{0}|\\cdots|g_{n}] := [1|g_{0}|\\cdots|g_{n}] \\qquad (n \\ge 0), \\qquad s_{-1}(1) := [1] .$$\n\nExpanding term by term gives $d_{n+1} s_{n} + s_{n-1} d_{n} = \\mathrm{id}$: $s d$ produces terms that \"insert $1$ and delete some $g_{i}$\", while $d s$ produces the same terms **with the opposite sign** plus the term $[g_{0}|\\cdots|g_{n}]$ itself; the two strings cancel, leaving only that term.\n\nHence the augmented complex is contractible, $H_{n}(B_{\\bullet}) = 0$ for $n > 0$, $H_{0} = R$, and the resolution is established.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **Why \"bar\"**: the vertical bars in $[g_{0}|g_{1}|\\cdots|g_{n}]$ were printed as horizontal bars in the old literature, hence the name. It is the most naive way of \"pulling the product structure of the group apart one factor at a time\" — no choices, purely combinatorial.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c88e18b',
      },
    },

    'def.boundary-operator': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: \"take one face of a cube\" has a uniform notation: fix one coordinate at this end or that end, multiply by a sign, and add everything up to get the boundary.\n\n**Insertion maps**: for $1\\le i\\le k$ and $\\alpha\\in\\{0,1\\}$ define\n\n$$I^{k}_{(i,\\alpha)} : I^{k-1}\\longrightarrow I^{k},\\qquad (x^{1},\\ldots,x^{k-1})\\longmapsto(x^{1},\\ldots,x^{i-1},\\alpha,x^{i},\\ldots,x^{k-1}).$$\n\nFor a $k$-cube $c$, write $c_{(i,\\alpha)} := c\\circ I^{k}_{(i,\\alpha)}$; it is a $(k-1)$-cube, called the **$(i,\\alpha)$ face** of $c$.\n\n**Boundary operator** $\\partial$ first sends a cube to the \"signed sum of its faces\", then extends linearly to chains:\n\n$$\\partial c := \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sum_{\\alpha=0}^{1}(-1)^{i+\\alpha}\\,c_{(i,\\alpha)},\\qquad \\partial\\Bigl(\\sum_{l}n_{l}c_{l}\\Bigr) := \\sum_{l}n_{l}\\,\\partial c_{l}.$$\n\nBy convention, when $k = 0$ set $\\partial c := 0$ (a point is zero-dimensional and has no faces). Thus $\\partial : C_{k}(M)\\to C_{k-1}(M)$ is a group homomorphism.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2ecb4244',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Where the sign $(-1)^{i+\\alpha}$ comes from**: it is not forced.\n\nTake the end whose outward normal is $+\\partial_{i}$ (that is, $\\alpha = 1$) and the sign $(-1)^{i+1}$; the other end has normal $-\\partial_{i}$ and sign $(-1)^{i}$; the difference between the two ends is exactly the sign of the permutation \"moving the outward normal to the front crosses $i-1$ coordinates\".\n\nThe full verification is in `prop.boundary-sign-convention` — the conclusion is that these signs agree **face by face** with the induced orientation \"outward normal first\".\n\n**$2k$ faces**: $i$ ranges over $k$ values and $\\alpha$ over the two ends, so there are $2k$ $(k-1)$-cubes. They are not pairwise distinct as maps, but as terms of a chain they are pairwise distinct (they may coincide elsewhere, but not as maps).\n\n**Square zero**: see `prop.boundary-squared-zero`. This is the key to the whole bookkeeping — it guarantees \"the boundary has no boundary\", so Stokes' theorem does not acquire an extra layer on either side.\n\n**Why linear extension rather than term-by-term definition**: a chain is already a formal sum; if $\\partial$ were not a homomorphism one could not compute $\\partial(c_{1}+c_{2})$, and Stokes' theorem for chains requires exactly this.\n\n**⚠️ Notation trap**: $\\partial$ is used simultaneously for the boundary of a half-space ($\\partial\\mathbb{H}^{n}$), the boundary of a manifold ($\\partial M$), and the boundary of a chain ($\\partial c$). The three uses are unified in `prop.boundary-sign-convention`: for orientation-preserving cubes, the chain boundary and the manifold boundary agree face by face.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e860ac9f',
      },
    },

    'def.integral-on-chain': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: integration over a chain is not a new kind of integral — pull the form back to coordinates along the cube, and what remains is an ordinary multiple integral. The coordinate order carried by the cube is its orientation, so no separate orientation has to be chosen.\n\nLet $c : I^{k}\\to M$ be a $k$-cube and $\\omega$ a $k$-form defined on a neighbourhood of the image of $c$. On $I^{k}$, $c^{\\ast}\\omega$ is still a $k$-form, and a $k$-form on a $k$-dimensional region has only one component, so there is a unique $f\\in C^{\\infty}(I^{k})$ with\n\n$$c^{\\ast}\\omega = f\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k}.$$\n\nDefine the integral over the cube\n\n$$\\int_{c}\\omega := \\int_{I^{k}}f = \\int_{0}^{1}\\cdots\\int_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\cdots\\mathrm{d}x^{k},$$\n\nand extend linearly to chains:\n\n$$\\int_{\\sum_{l}n_{l}c_{l}}\\omega := \\sum_{l}n_{l}\\int_{c_{l}}\\omega .$$\n\nConvention for $k = 0$: a $0$-cube is a point $p$, a $0$-form is a function, $\\int_{p}F := F(p)$, $\\int_{\\sum n_{l}p_{l}}F := \\sum_{l}n_{l}F(p_{l})$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#165e99cb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What the coefficient $f$ is**: $f = c^{\\ast}\\omega(\\partial_{1},\\ldots,\\partial_{k})$, that is, feed the tangent vectors in the coordinate directions of the cube to the pulled-back form. It is a smooth function; its values on the boundary are determined by the smooth extension of $c$, and measure-zero sets do not affect the integral.\n\n**Why no orientation is needed**: the coordinate order of the cube already fixes the orientation — $\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k}$ on $I^{k}$ is the standard orientation.\n\nTo make the cube \"run backwards\", replace some coordinate by $1-x^{i}$;\n\nthen $c^{\\ast}\\omega$ automatically changes sign, with no extra rule.\n\nSo \"orientation\" is absorbed into the parametrization on the chain side.\n\n**Division of labour with `def.integral-form`**: there, an $n$-form is integrated over an **open set**, and partitions of unity are needed to glue to the manifold; here, a $k$-form is integrated along **one cube**, one multiple integral from start to finish. The global meaning is carried by chains (summation), not by partitions of unity — this is the most economical feature of this bookkeeping.\n\n**⚠️ Degrees must match**: a $k$-form only pairs with a $k$-chain. Pulling back a $j$-form along a $k$-cube ($j<k$) is still meaningful, but that is a $j$-form on a $k$-dimensional region and cannot be integrated; for $j>k$ the pullback is simply zero. So \"pairing of chains and forms\" is defined only in equal degrees.\n\n**⚠️ Degenerate cubes**: if $c$ has less than full rank, $c^{\\ast}\\omega$ may be zero (for instance if $c$ is constant), and the integral is then automatically zero. The definition needs no patch for the degenerate case.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1e1d0fb9',
      },
    },

    'prop.f-shriek-base-change': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: **$f^!$ need not be built separately — it is \"first pull back by $f^*$, then multiply by a fixed object $f^!R$\"**. This fixed object is called the **dualizing complex** (a twist: $f^*$ is twisted). Thus computing the dual is reduced to computing one complex $f^!R$; the next two nodes compute this complex (regular closed immersion, smooth map).\n\nLet $f : R \\to A$ be a ring map of finite type and finite Tor dimension (base change between Noetherian rings). Then the natural map\n\n$$f^!R \\otimes^{L}_{A_\\blacksquare} f^* \\longrightarrow f^!$$\n\nis an equivalence of functors. If $g : R \\to S$ is flat and $f' : S \\to B = A \\otimes_R S$ is the base change ($g' : A \\to B$), then\n\n$$g'^* f^! \\;\\simeq\\; f'^! g^* : D(R_\\blacksquare) \\longrightarrow D(B_\\blacksquare).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#86600046',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Reading: **$f^!$ is \"$f^*$ twisted by $f^!R$\"**.\n\nSo to compute $f^!$, it suffices to compute $f^!R$ (the dualizing complex).\n\n⭐ The proof reduces to two cases: $f$ surjective (then $f_! = f_*$ is the forgetful functor and $f^! = \\operatorname{RHom}_R(A,-)$, where base change is evident) and $A = R[T]$ (then $f_!M = M \\otimes_{(A,R)_\\blacksquare} R((T^{-1}))/R[T][-1]$, $f^!R = R[T][1]$, and base change reduces to that of $R((T^{-1}))/R[T]$).\n\n⚠ Finite Tor dimension is necessary: otherwise $f^!R$ is not a finite complex and the twisting formula fails.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e409a365',
      },
    },

    'def.cond-measure': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: the continuous functions on $S$ with values in $\\mathbb{Z}$ form a group under pointwise addition, denoted $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$; an **integer-valued measure** is \"something that can take weighted sums (that is, 'integrals') of these functions\", i.e. a group homomorphism from $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ to $\\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nLet $S$ be a profinite set (compact Hausdorff totally disconnected space), and denote the set of all $\\mathbb{Z}$-linear homomorphisms on $C(S, \\mathbb{Z})$ by\n\n$$M(S, \\mathbb{Z}) := \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(C(S, \\mathbb{Z}),\\ \\mathbb{Z}\\bigr).$$\n\nMore generally, for any abelian group $A$ write\n\n$$M(S, A) := \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(C(S, \\mathbb{Z}),\\ A\\bigr),$$\n\nin particular $M(S, \\mathbb{R})$ and $M(S, \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z})$ ($\\mathbb{R}$-valued and $\\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$-valued measures).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#56c4a2a3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Where the name comes from: the elements of $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ are \"integer-valued continuous functions on $S$\"; applying a measure $\\mu$ gives $\\int f\\,d\\mu$.\n\nWhen $S$ is finite, $M(S,\\mathbb{Z}) = \\mathbb{Z}^{S}$, i.e. \"put one integer at each point\";\n\nwhen $S$ is an infinite profinite set, $M(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ is a **formal infinite sum**, in general much larger than $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$.\n\n⭐ The key step is `thm.csz-free`: $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ is a free abelian group, so $M(S,\\mathbb{Z}) \\cong \\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$ is a large product **beyond countable products** — exactly the completion wanted in solid theory (not a topological \"completion of Cauchy sequences\", see `def.solid-group`).\n\n⚠ Take $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$ with the **discrete** topology: $S$ is compact and $\\mathbb{Z}$ is discrete, so the image of a continuous function is finite; hence as an abstract group it is free (Nöbeling's theorem), but in general not finitely generated.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#41ee7d71',
      },
    },

    'def.trig-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, define\n\n$$\\cos z := \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k}}{(2k)!}\\, z^{2k}, \\qquad \\sin z := \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k}}{(2k+1)!}\\, z^{2k+1}$$\n\nBoth series converge absolutely for every $z$ (radius of convergence $+\\infty$), and\n\n$$\\cos' = -\\sin, \\qquad \\sin' = \\cos, \\qquad \\cos 0 = 1, \\qquad \\sin 0 = 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f4942021',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The **analytic definition** of the trigonometric functions (rather than the geometric one): starting from the series, every identity and derivative formula can be computed, and they hold automatically for complex numbers.\n\n⭐ The relation to $\\exp$ is revealed in the entry on Euler's formula: splitting the series of $\\exp$ according to the parity of $n$ gives $\\cos$ and $\\sin$.\n\n⚠ The geometric meaning (unit circle, angle, radian) is attached only **later**: the series first tell us that $\\cos$ has zeros and $\\sin$ has a period, and the geometric picture follows.\n\n⭐ The radius of convergence of both series on the whole plane is given by `imp.trig-series-radius` (Cauchy–Hadamard plus an estimate for the growth of factorials).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4688b2f1',
      },
    },

    'def.winding-number': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the winding number is an integer counting \"how many times one goes around a point\" — one counterclockwise turn is $+1$, one clockwise turn is $-1$, no turn is $0$.\n\nLet $\\gamma: [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{C}$ be a piecewise smooth closed curve and $a_0 \\notin \\gamma$. Define the **winding number** (also called index) of $\\gamma$ about $a_0$ by\n\n$$n(\\gamma, a_0) := \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{\\gamma} \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\zeta}{\\zeta - a_0}.$$\n\nIt equals the continuous change of $\\arg(\\gamma(t) - a_0)$ along one full traversal of $\\gamma$, divided by $2\\pi$:\n\n$$n(\\gamma, a_0) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\,\\Delta_{\\gamma}\\,\\arg(\\zeta - a_0).$$\n\nThree basic properties: (1) $n(\\gamma, \\cdot)$ is constant on each connected component of $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\gamma$ and takes integer values; (2) it equals $0$ on the unbounded component; (3) if $\\gamma$ is a positively oriented simple closed curve, then $n = 1$ inside and $n = 0$ outside.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4e81b751',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Integrality is **proved**, not seen: as $t$ runs along $\\gamma$ from $a$ to $b$, set\n\n$$g(t) := \\frac{\\gamma(t) - a_0}{\\exp\\left( \\int_a^t \\frac{\\gamma'(s)}{\\gamma(s) - a_0}\\,\\mathrm{d}s \\right)}.$$\n\nDifferentiating shows that the derivatives of numerator and denominator cancel, so $g$ is constant; at $t = b$ one gets $\\exp\\left( \\int_a^b \\cdot \\right) = 1$, hence the integral is an integer multiple of $2\\pi i$.\n\n⭐ Relation to the argument: the integral in the exponent is precisely one choice of $\\log(\\gamma(t) - a_0)$ varying continuously along $\\gamma$ (a logarithm can only be chosen branch by branch along a path), and its imaginary part is the change of the argument.\n\n⚠ The winding number depends on **orientation**: traversing the same curve backwards changes its sign.\n\n⚠ \"$n(\\gamma, a_0) = 0$\" does **not** mean \"$a_0$ lies outside the curve\" — going in and coming back out again also gives $0$. Only for a simple closed curve (Jordan curve) is it a criterion for inside versus outside.\n\n📌 The argument principle writes the difference of zeros and poles as the winding number of $f \\circ \\gamma$ about $0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2de23601',
      },
    },

    'lem.cauchy-green': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: a complex integral along a closed curve can be converted into a double integral over the area inside the curve, whose integrand is exactly $\\partial f/\\partial \\bar z$ — so \"holomorphic $\\implies$ integral zero\" becomes \"the integrand is $0$\".\n\nLet $\\Omega$ be a bounded region whose boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ is a piecewise smooth simple closed curve, positively oriented (counterclockwise). If $f \\in C^1(\\overline{\\Omega})$, then\n\n$$\\int_{\\partial\\Omega} f(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 2i \\iint_{\\Omega} \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\bar z}(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.$$\n\nIn particular, if $f$ is holomorphic (**one need no longer say \"$f'$ is continuous\"**: the derivative of a holomorphic function is again holomorphic and hence continuous), then the right-hand side is zero, i.e. $\\displaystyle\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega} f\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7c201b2d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is a direct translation of Green's formula.\n\nGreen's formula: $\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega}(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y) = \\iint_\\Omega (Q_x - P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\mathrm{d}y$.\n\nSplit the integral as $\\oint (u\\,\\mathrm{d}x - v\\,\\mathrm{d}y) + i\\oint (v\\,\\mathrm{d}x + u\\,\\mathrm{d}y)$; matching terms twice gives $\\iint [-(u_y + v_x)] + i\\iint(u_x - v_y)$ — and $2i f_{\\bar z}$ computed term by term is exactly $-(u_y + v_x) + i(u_x - v_y)$.\n\n⭐ Componentwise it is the **integrated form** of the CR equations: holomorphic $\\iff f_{\\bar z} = 0 \\implies$ the right-hand side is zero.\n\n⚠ This lemma is **not free**: Green's formula requires $P, Q$ (hence $f$) to be $C^1$ on the closure $\\overline{\\Omega}$.\n\nHolomorphic only guarantees that $f$ is differentiable, **not that $f'$ is continuous** — this gap is filled by Goursat's subdivision into triangles.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#de4468eb',
      },
    },

    'lem.covering': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{E}$ be a family of open balls in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $U = \\bigcup\\mathcal{E}$. If $c < m(U)$, then there are **pairwise disjoint** balls $B_1, \\ldots , B_k$ in $\\mathcal{E}$ such that\n\n$$\\sum_{j=1}^{k} m(B_j) > 3^{-n} c$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6424216d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the core estimate of the **Vitali-type covering lemma**; the maximal theorem depends entirely on it.\n\nHow the proof chooses: first take a ball $B_1$ of **maximal radius**; then take a ball $B_2$ of maximal radius disjoint from $B_1$; and so on.\n\nWhy this choice suffices: if some $A_i \\in \\mathcal{E}$ is not chosen, then some $B_j$ meets it — take the one $B_j$ with **smallest index**.\n\nBy the selection rule, the radius of $A_i$ does not exceed that of $B_j$ (otherwise $A_i$ would have been chosen when $B_j$ was).\n\nEnlarging the radius of $B_j$ by a factor of 3 gives $A_i \\subseteq 3B_j$, hence $K \\subseteq \\bigcup_j 3B_j$.\n\nWhat remains is counting:\n\n$$c < m(K) \\le 3^n \\sum_j m(B_j)\\quad  \\implies\\quad  \\sum_j m(B_j) > 3^{-n} c$$\n\n⚠ Note that the dilation factor is **$3^{n}$** (not $2^{n}$) — precisely because \"radius does not exceed\" is a weak inequality and needs a little room.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1896481e',
      },
    },

    'lem.equal-norm-isometry': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $N$ be a finite-dimensional nondegenerate symmetric bilinear space with $\\operatorname{char}K\\ne2$, and let $q$ be its quadratic form. If $x,y\\in N$ satisfy $q(x)=q(y)\\ne0$, then there is an isometry $\\rho$ of $N$ with $\\rho(x)=y$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#89d91278',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The reflection $s_v(z)=z-\\frac{2\\beta(z,v)}{q(v)}v$ ($q(v)\\ne0$) is an isometry and sends $v$ to $-v$; the lemma says that one reflection (composed with the central inversion $z\\mapsto-z$ if necessary) exchanges equal-length vectors. This is the most basic block of the Cartan–Dieudonné decomposition.\n\nFor the proof see the edge `imp.equal-norm-isometry`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e88a7382',
      },
    },

    'lem.gauss-qr': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p \\nmid a$. Consider $a, 2a, \\cdots, \\frac{p-1}{2}a$; replace each by its least positive residue modulo $p$ (lying in $1, \\cdots, p-1$), and suppose $\\mu$ of them are greater than $\\frac{p}{2}$. Then\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{a}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\mu} .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#abc850b5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Role**: it reduces the Legendre symbol to \"counting how many exceed $p/2$\", so that values such as $\\left(\\frac{2}{p}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{-1}{p}\\right)$ can be computed without exponentiation.\n\n**Idea of the proof**: let $r_{1}, \\cdots, r_{n}$ denote these least positive residues ($n = \\frac{p-1}{2}$). Replacing those greater than $p/2$ by $p - r_{i}$, the resulting $n$ numbers are exactly a permutation of $1, \\cdots, n$. Hence\n\n$$a^{n} \\cdot n! \\equiv \\pm n! \\pmod p ,$$\n\nthe sign being determined by \"how many were replaced\" (that is, the parity of $\\mu$). Cancel $n!$ ($p \\nmid n!$) and apply Euler's criterion $a^{n} \\equiv \\left(\\frac{a}{p}\\right)$.\n\n⚠ It shares its name with, but is a different object from, `lem.gauss-lemma` (Gauss's lemma / geodesic polar coordinates) in differential geometry; do not confuse them.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fadeabd0',
      },
    },

    'def.tensor-module': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a ring with identity, $M$ a right $R$-module and $N$ a left $R$-module. Take the free abelian group $F := \\mathbb{Z}^{(M \\times N)}$ with basis $M \\times N$, and let $K \\subseteq F$ be the subgroup generated by all elements of the form\n\n$$(m + m_{1},\\ n) - (m,\\ n) - (m_{1},\\ n), \\qquad (m,\\ n + n_{1}) - (m,\\ n) - (m,\\ n_{1}), \\qquad (m r,\\ n) - (m,\\ r n)$$\n\n($m, m_{1} \\in M$, $n, n_{1} \\in N$, $r \\in R$). Define\n\n$$M \\otimes_{R} N := F / K, \\qquad m \\otimes n := [\\,(m, n)\\,].$$\n\nIts elements are **finite sums** $\\sum_{i} m_{i} \\otimes n_{i}$ of pure tensors $m \\otimes n$.\n\nThus the relations are the two additivity relations and one **balanced** relation $m r \\otimes n = m \\otimes r n$;\n\nmoreover, for every **balanced biadditive** map $B : M \\times N \\to P$ ($P$ an abelian group), there is a **unique** group homomorphism\n\n$$\\widetilde{B} : M \\otimes_{R} N \\longrightarrow P, \\qquad \\widetilde{B}(m \\otimes n) = B(m, n).$$\n\nIf $R$ is commutative, $r \\cdot (m \\otimes n) := (m r) \\otimes n$ makes $M \\otimes_{R} N$ an $R$-module.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f918c987',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**How to read it**: each of the three relations does one job — the first two say that $\\otimes$ is additive in each variable separately, and the third $m r \\otimes n = m \\otimes r n$ says \"the action of $R$ can be moved from one side to the other\". Over a commutative ring the third reads $r m \\otimes n = m \\otimes r n$, which is the most familiar form.\n\n**Elements are not pure tensors**: a general element is a finite sum, so \"whether $\\sum m_{i} \\otimes n_{i}$ is zero\" cannot be checked term by term. A pure tensor can still be zero: $\\mathbb{Z}/2 \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} \\mathbb{Z}/3 = 0$, because\n\n$$1 \\otimes 1 = (3 \\cdot 1) \\otimes 1 = 1 \\otimes (3 \\cdot 1) = 1 \\otimes 0 = 0 .$$\n\n⭐ **The construction is basis-free**: the universal property says that every object converting bilinear maps into linear ones is isomorphic to it, so another construction (for example a free module followed by a quotient) gives the same thing.\n\n📌 Relation to `prop.module-tensor-universal`: that is the special case over $\\mathbb{Z}$ and over a field; over a general $R$ the only extra relation is the balanced one.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6d7668cb',
      },
    },

    'prop.free-flat': {
      statement: {
        en: "The free module $R^{(I)}$ is **flat**; more generally, **projective modules are flat**.\n\nConcretely, $M \\otimes_{R} R^{(I)} \\cong M^{(I)}$ naturally ($m \\otimes (r_{i})_{i} \\mapsto (m r_{i})_{i}$), and \"taking the $I$-fold direct sum\" detects exactness componentwise.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5fd1b41a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: by the \"unit\" and \"commutes with direct sums\" properties in `prop.tensor-functor-properties`,\n\n$$(-) \\otimes_{R} R^{(I)} \\;\\cong\\; \\bigoplus_{i \\in I} (-),$$\n\nthe right-hand side is \"taking the $I$-fold direct sum\", which is exact (a direct sum of functors is exact, since injectivity is detected componentwise) ✓.\n\n**Projective modules are flat**: a projective module is a direct summand of a free module (the remark after `def.projective-object`), and direct summands inherit flatness (`prop.flat-summand`) ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ **Use**: this guarantees that \"projective resolutions are flat resolutions\", so $\\operatorname{Tor}$ can be computed either by projective or by flat resolutions; the latter are often much shorter (for example the flat resolution given by localization).\n\n⚠️ The converse fails: flat does not imply projective (`ex.flat-not-projective`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bd8d9ae6',
      },
    },

    'prop.cross-ratio-real': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $z_{1}, z_{2}, z_{3}, z_{4}$ be pairwise distinct. Then\n\n$$(z_{1}, z_{2}; z_{3}, z_{4}) \\in \\mathbb{R}$$\n\nif and only if $z_{1}, z_{2}, z_{3}, z_{4}$ are **concyclic or collinear**.\n\n(On the extended plane \"circles\" and \"lines\" are the same kind of object, so the statement need not split into two cases.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c438c1f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: take a fractional linear transformation $T$ sending $z_{1}, z_{2}, z_{3}$ to $0, 1, \\infty$ respectively (it exists and is unique, see `prop.mobius-three-points`). By invariance of the cross-ratio,\n\n$$(z_{1}, z_{2}; z_{3}, z_{4}) = (0, 1; \\infty, Tz_{4}) = Tz_{4}.$$\n\nThus \"the cross-ratio is real\" $\\iff$ \"$Tz_{4}$ lies on the real axis\". And $T^{-1}(\\mathbb{R})$ is a line or a circle (the real axis is the \"circle\" through $\\infty$, see `prop.mobius-circle`), and it passes through $z_{1}, z_{2}, z_{3}$. ∎\n\n⭐ **It is the complex version of the concyclicity criterion**: elementary geometry requires measuring angles, whereas here one only checks whether a complex number is real. In applications, deciding whether four points are concyclic, or where a fractional linear transformation sends the real axis, is exactly this statement.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#164debd2',
      },
    },

    'cor.solid-compact-generators': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: the \"best-behaved\" objects in the solid category (compact and projective) are neither more nor fewer than the large products $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$ — infinite versions of finite free modules. Moreover $D(\\mathbf{Solid})$ is generated by them, so doing homological algebra there is like doing it in $D^b(\\mathbb{Z})$.\n\n**(i)** The compact projective objects of $\\mathbf{Solid}$ are exactly the groups of the form $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$ ($I$ arbitrary).\n\n**(ii)** $D(\\mathbf{Solid})$ is generated by compact objects; the compact objects are bounded complexes whose terms are $\\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$; the functor\n\n$$D^b(\\mathbb{Z})^{\\mathrm{op}} \\longrightarrow D(\\mathbf{Solid})^\\omega, \\qquad C \\longmapsto \\operatorname{RHom}(C, \\mathbb{Z})$$\n\nis an equivalence of categories.\n\n**(iii)** The derived solidification of $\\mathbb{R}$ vanishes: $\\mathbb{R}^{L\\blacksquare} = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#695794c8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ (iii) is the most striking feature of \"non-Archimedean completion\": $\\mathbb{R}$ **completes to $0$** in the solid sense.\n\nThis is not a bug — solid theory is designed for $p$-adic/analytic geometry, and the Archimedean direction has to be handled separately (Clausen–Scholze's later analytic stacks use liquid/analytic rings).\n\n⚠ Contrast: $\\mathbb{Z}_p$ remains itself after solidification (already completed in the $p$-adic sense), whereas $\\mathbb{R}$ disappears — \"solid\" must not be understood as \"complete the space\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bbc3441b',
      },
    },

    'prop.free-solid-solid': {
      statement: {
        en: "For any profinite set $S$, $Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}$ is a solid abelian group; and $Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}[0] \\in D(\\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab}))$ is a solid complex.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ffebb9c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This legitimizes the name \"free solid group\": it really is the free object in the solid category.\n\n⭐ The proof reduces to the case $Z[S]^{\\blacksquare} = \\mathbb{Z}$ (using `cor.free-solid-product` to write the general case as $\\prod_J \\mathbb{Z}$, then computing termwise); there the two sides are $H^i(T,\\mathbb{Z})$ and $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\prod_J\\mathbb{Z},\\mathbb{Z})$, computed from $0 \\to \\prod_J \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\prod_J \\mathbb{R} \\to \\prod_J \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z} \\to 0$ and the contractibility of $\\mathbb{R}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#320c0373',
      },
    },

    'prop.solid-hypercover': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $S_\\bullet \\to S$ be a hypercover of profinite sets (**hypercover**: an ordinary \"cover\" lays down only one layer $S_0 \\to S$, whereas a hypercover records layer by layer with a sequence $S_0, S_1, S_2, \\ldots$ — \"where the previous layer overlaps, and how the overlaps overlap\" — in the form of a simplicial object $S_\\bullet$ with augmentation $S_\\bullet \\to S$, each degeneracy being a homotopy equivalence).\n\nThen the complex of condensed abelian groups\n\n$$\\cdots \\longrightarrow Z[S_1]^{\\blacksquare} \\longrightarrow Z[S_0]^{\\blacksquare} \\longrightarrow Z[S]^{\\blacksquare} \\longrightarrow 0$$\n\nis exact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fdb8e2ac',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This shows that replacing \"profinite sets\" by \"extremally disconnected sets\" in the definition does not change solid groups: the two are approximated by hypercovers.\n\n⭐ The proof uses two facts: $H^i(S,\\mathbb{Z}) = 0$ (for $i > 0$, constant-sheaf cohomology on profinite sets), and `thm.csz-free` to write each $C(S_i,\\mathbb{Z})$ as a free group and then dualize.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f70b7cdd',
      },
    },

    'prop.solid-product-tensor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M = \\prod_I \\mathbb{Z}$ and $N = \\prod_J \\mathbb{Z}$. Then\n\n$$M \\otimes^{L\\blacksquare} N \\cong \\prod_{I \\times J} \\mathbb{Z},$$\n\nin particular $M \\otimes^{L\\blacksquare} N$ is concentrated in homological degree $0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0efec65a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This says that the solid tensor product on **compact projective objects** is just \"multiplying the index sets\" — exactly as for finite free modules, only infinite products are allowed.\n\n⭐ Proof: write the free abelian group as a retract of $C(S,\\mathbb{Z})$, reduce to $C(S,\\mathbb{Z}) \\otimes C(T,\\mathbb{Z}) = C(S \\times T, \\mathbb{Z})$, and use the monoidality of $\\otimes^{L\\blacksquare}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f64ee050',
      },
    },

    'thm.breen-deligne': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M$ be an abelian group in a topos (for example $M \\in \\mathrm{CondAb}$). Then there is a **natural left resolution**\n\n$$F(M)^{\\bullet} \\twoheadrightarrow M,$$\n\neach term of which is a finite direct sum of \"free abelian groups $\\mathbb{Z} \\cdot M^{s}$\":\n\n$$F(M)_{n} = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r_{n}} \\mathbb{Z} \\cdot M^{s_{n,i}}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1a92927a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it says.** In a general topos \"free object\" is not finite (the $s$ in $M^{s}$ is a **set** and may have arbitrarily large cardinality). The theorem says that nevertheless any abelian group $M$ can be resolved by a **termwise free** complex, and the resolution is **natural** (independent of choices, functorial in $M$).\n\n**What $\\mathbb{Z} \\cdot X$ is**: the **free abelian group** generated by the set $X$ (in a topos, $\\mathbb{Z}[X]$). $M^{s}$ is \"the product of $s$ copies of $M$\" — the power set is taken so that \"elements of $M$\" can be written as a countable combination.\n\n**Why it is called the Breen–Deligne decomposition**: the construction was proposed by Deligne but lacked a complete proof for a long time; Scholze completed the proof in an appendix to the fourth lecture of his condensed mathematics course. So it is a **modern** theorem.\n\n⭐ **It is the engine of all Ext computations in $\\mathrm{CondAb}$**: with this termwise free resolution, any $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, N)$ can be expanded into Exts of free terms, which can be computed (see the spectral sequence below).\n\n⚠️ The proof is heavy, and the explicit formulas for the construction (combinations of $d_{0}, d_{1}, d_{2}$) are quite technical — in practice one only needs to know that it **exists and is natural**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#10f83e34',
      },
    },

    'prop.resolution-homotopy': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $P_{\\bullet} \\twoheadrightarrow M$ and $Q_{\\bullet} \\twoheadrightarrow M$ be two **projective resolutions** of the same $R$-module $M$. Then there are chain maps\n\n$$P_{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\varphi\\ } Q_{\\bullet}, \\qquad Q_{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\psi\\ } P_{\\bullet}$$\n\nsuch that $\\psi\\varphi \\sim 1_{P_{\\bullet}}$, $\\varphi\\psi \\sim 1_{Q_{\\bullet}}$. In other words: any two projective resolutions are isomorphic in the **homotopy category**.\n\nIn particular, for every $R$-module $N$ and every $n \\ge 0$, the groups obtained by applying $\\operatorname{Hom}(- , N)$ and taking homology are all isomorphic — hence $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{R}(M, N)$ is independent of the choice of resolution.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a5f0ec79',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (the comparison theorem with $f = 1_{M}$, twice).**\nApplying the existence part of the comparison theorem to $1_{M} : M \\to M$ and ($P_{\\bullet}$, $Q_{\\bullet}$) gives a lift $\\varphi$; swapping the two resolutions and applying it again gives a lift $\\psi$. In both cases $\\varepsilon\\psi = \\varepsilon$.\nNow the composite: $\\psi\\varphi : P_{\\bullet} \\to P_{\\bullet}$ is also a lift of $1_{M}$ (at the augmentation, $\\varepsilon\\psi\\varphi = \\varepsilon\\varphi = \\varepsilon$ ✓), and $1_{P_{\\bullet}}$ is of course a lift of $1_{M}$; so the **homotopy uniqueness** part of the comparison theorem gives $\\psi\\varphi \\sim 1_{P_{\\bullet}}$ ✓;\n\nthe other side is analogous ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **This is the legitimate basis for \"resolutions may be chosen freely\".** In computations one usually picks the shortest and most explicit one (free modules, two terms, Koszul complex); changing it does not affect the result — but only isomorphism of the result is guaranteed, **not that the families appearing in the middle agree**.\n\n**Example (homotopy equivalent but not isomorphic)**: take the direct sum of $P_{\\bullet}$ with a contractible complex of the form\n\n$$\\cdots \\to 0 \\to P_{0} \\xrightarrow{\\ 1\\ } P_{0} \\to 0 \\to \\cdots$$\n\nThis gives another projective resolution of $M$, visibly not isomorphic to $P_{\\bullet}$ (every term has doubled), yet homotopy equivalent ✓.\n\n⚠️ **Dual case**: injective resolutions satisfy the same conclusion (apply the comparison theorem on the other side). Together with \"lifts between injective resolutions\", this is the entire basis for \"compute derived functors from resolutions on either side\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d00db79c',
      },
    },

    'thm.cartan-leray': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $\\mathfrak{X}$ is a **cover** of $X$ and $M$ is an abelian sheaf, then there is a spectral sequence\n\n$$E^{p,q}_{2} := \\check{H}^{p}\\bigl(\\mathfrak{X},\\ H^{q}(M)\\bigr) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; H^{p+q}(X, M).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d0340251',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **What it says**: Čech cohomology (the $\\check{H}^{p}$ on the left) with coefficients in $H^{q}(M)$ converges to the **genuine** sheaf cohomology. Thus it explains how the \"cheap algorithm\" approximates the \"expensive\" one.\n\n**Corollary**: if $H^{q} = 0$ on every layer of the cover ($q \\ne 0$), then $\\check{H}^{n} \\cong H^{n}$ — in that case Čech cohomology directly gives sheaf cohomology.\n\n**How the proof goes**: take the sieve $\\mathfrak{X}$ generated by $R$, then $\\Gamma(\\mathfrak{X}, M) = \\Gamma(R, \\mathcal{H}(M))$; $\\mathcal{H}$ preserves injectives, so the Grothendieck spectral sequence plus the previous item gives the result.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8d18e360',
      },
    },

    'prop.cech-higher-cohomology-presheaf': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M$ be an abelian sheaf on a site, $n \\ne 0$, and write $H^{n}(M)$ for the presheaf $U \\mapsto H^{n}(U, M)$. Then\n\n$$\\check{H}^{p}\\bigl(H^{n}(M)\\bigr) = 0 \\qquad (\\forall p \\ge 0).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#878f4152',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** By \"the associated sheaf of a higher cohomology presheaf is zero\", $H^{n}(M)$ has zero sheafification ✓; and Čech cohomology depends only on the **sheafification** of the presheaf (the Čech functor factors as two passes of $(-)^{+}$) ✓ — and the zero sheaf certainly has no Čech cohomology ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c515ce16',
      },
    },

    'prop.five-term-spectral-sequence': {
      statement: {
        en: "A first-quadrant spectral sequence $E^{p,q}_{2} \\implies H^{p+q}$ gives the **five-term exact sequence**\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow E^{1,0}_{2} \\longrightarrow H^{1} \\longrightarrow E^{0,1}_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\ d_{2}\\ } E^{2,0}_{2} \\longrightarrow H^{2}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#387ceaa4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (checking page by page).** In the first quadrant $d_{2} : E^{0,1}_{2} \\to E^{2,0}_{2}$ is the only possible nonzero differential between these two terms ✓; $E^{1,0}_{\\infty} = E^{1,0}_{2}$, $E^{0,1}_{\\infty} = \\ker d_{2}$, $E^{2,0}_{\\infty} = \\operatorname{coker} d_{2}$ ($d_{2} : E^{0,1}_{2} \\to E^{2,0}_{2}$ is the only possible nonzero differential, and it kills part of $E^{0,1}$) ✓.\n\nAssembling $E^{\\infty}_{1,0} = \\operatorname{Gr}^{1}H^{1}$, $E^{\\infty}_{0,1} = \\operatorname{Gr}^{0}H^{1}$, together with the filtration $E^{\\infty}_{2,0} = \\operatorname{Gr}^{2}H^{2}$ term by term gives the displayed chain ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ Use: this is the standard tool by which \"a spectral sequence directly yields a low-degree exact sequence\"; the low-degree terms of $H^{1}$ and $H^{2}$ are read off from it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0f6d10c4',
      },
    },

    'thm.h1-crossed': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M$ be a $G$-module (written additively). A map $d : G \\to M$ is called a **crossed homomorphism** (that is, a $1$-cocycle) if\n\n$$d(g_{1} g_{2}) = d(g_{1}) + g_{1}\\, d(g_{2}) \\qquad (g_{1}, g_{2} \\in G).$$\n\nThe class given by **a fixed element**,\n\n$$d_{m}(g) := g\\, m - m \\qquad (m \\in M)$$\n\nis called a **principal crossed homomorphism** (a $1$-coboundary). Write $Z^{1}(G, M)$ for all crossed homomorphisms and $B^{1}(G, M) \\subseteq Z^{1}(G, M)$ for all principal ones. Then\n\n$$H^{1}(G, M) \\;\\cong\\; Z^{1}(G, M) \\big/ B^{1}(G, M) .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#66e20e41',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Reading**: a crossed homomorphism is a homomorphism \"up to the action\" — when the action is trivial it degenerates to an ordinary group homomorphism $G \\to M$, and the principal ones all collapse to $0$.\n\n**The proof takes one line**: in the standard cochain complex $C^{1}(G, M)$ is just the set of all maps $G \\to M$, and\n\n$$\\delta c(g_{1}, g_{2}) = g_{1} c(g_{2}) - c(g_{1} g_{2}) + c(g_{1}),$$\n\nso $\\delta c = 0 \\iff c(g_{1}g_{2}) = c(g_{1}) + g_{1}c(g_{2})$, i.e. $Z^{1}$ is exactly the crossed homomorphisms.\n\nAlso $C^{0} = M$ and $(\\delta^{0}m)(g) = g m - m$, hence $\\operatorname{im}\\delta^{0} = B^{1}$.\n\nThus $H^{1} = \\ker\\delta^{1}/\\operatorname{im}\\delta^{0} = Z^{1}/B^{1}$.\n\n∎\n\n**Two immediate consequences**:\n- When the action is **trivial**, $Z^{1} = \\operatorname{Hom}(G, M)$ and $B^{1} = 0$, so $H^{1}(G, M) = \\operatorname{Hom}(G, M)$.\n- If $G$ is **finite**, $M$ is torsion-free, and the action is trivial, then $\\implies H^{1}(G, M) = 0$ (there is no nontrivial homomorphism from a finite group to a torsion-free group).\n\n**Why \"crossed\"**: $d(g_{1}g_{2}) = d(g_{1}) + g_{1}d(g_{2})$ is exactly the Leibniz formula for \"twisting by $g_{1}$ when moving $g_{1}$ out\" — the same formula as for a derivation, only with coefficients in a module.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d798c6dd',
      },
    },

    'prop.low-degree-exact': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M$ be a $G$-module. The low-degree terms of the standard cochain complex give the exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow M^{G} \\longrightarrow M \\xrightarrow{\\ \\delta^{0}\\ } Z^{1}(G, M) \\longrightarrow H^{1}(G, M) \\longrightarrow 0 ,$$\n\nwhere $(\\delta^{0} m)(g) = g m - m$ and $Z^{1}(G, M)$ is the group of crossed homomorphisms. In particular:\n\n- If $G$ acts **trivially** on $M$, then $\\delta^{0} = 0$, so $M^{G} = M$ and $H^{1}(G, M) = \\operatorname{Hom}(G, M)$;\n- For a short exact sequence $0 \\to A \\to B \\to C \\to 0$ of $G$-modules, there is an exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\to A^{G} \\to B^{G} \\to C^{G} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\partial\\ } H^{1}(G, A) \\to H^{1}(G, B) \\to H^{1}(G, C).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#518d58e9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The first line is read directly from the complex**: $H^{0} = \\ker\\delta^{0} = M^{G}$, $H^{1} = \\ker\\delta^{1}/\\operatorname{im}\\delta^{0} = Z^{1}/B^{1}$, and $B^{1} = \\operatorname{im}\\delta^{0}$. Writing out\n\n$$0 \\to \\ker\\delta^{0} \\to M \\to \\ker\\delta^{1} \\to \\ker\\delta^{1}\\big/\\operatorname{im}\\delta^{0} \\to 0$$\n\ngives it — each of the three exact pieces uses only the definition.\n\n**The \"trivial action\" clause**: $\\delta^{0} m$ is the function $g \\mapsto g m - m$ (**not a constant function**: for $G = C_{2}$ acting by signs and $m = 1$, it takes $1$ at $0$ and $-2$ at the generator); it vanishes identically (that is, equals $0$) only when the action is trivial, so then $B^{1} = 0$ and $Z^{1} = \\operatorname{Hom}(G, M)$.\n\n**The second line is the first few terms of the long exact sequence**: applying the right derived functor to $0 \\to A \\to B \\to C \\to 0$ (namely $R^{0} = (-)^{G}$, $R^{1} = H^{1}$) gives the long exact sequence, and this segment is exactly that by definition; the connecting map $\\partial$ sends \"fixed points of $C$\" to \"one-dimensional cohomology of $A$\".\n\n⭐ **These two facts are the technical source of the concrete meaning of $H^{1}$ and $H^{2}$**: the first says that $H^{1}$ is the space of \"sections / crossed homomorphisms\", and the second says that the **obstruction** to splitting a short exact sequence lands exactly in $H^{1}$ — the next two classification theorems add semantics to $Z^{1}$ and $Z^{2}$.\n\n⚠️ Do not confuse this with the **full long exact sequence**: it actually continues $\\cdots \\to H^{1}(G, C) \\to H^{2}(G, A) \\to H^{2}(G, B) \\to \\cdots$, and only the first terms are isolated here.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5387df35',
      },
    },

    'lem.goursat-triangle': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: as long as $f$ is holomorphic on and inside a triangle (nothing else is required, not even continuity of $f'$), the integral along the boundary of the triangle is $0$. This is the \"minimal core\" of Cauchy's theorem.\n\nLet $T$ be a closed triangle and $f$ holomorphic on some open set containing $T$. Then\n\n$$\\int_{\\partial T} f(z)\\,\\mathrm{d}z = 0.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c99cf092',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The method is **bisection plus shrinking the triangle to a point**, with no Green's theorem and no continuity of $f'$.\n\n**Step 1 (quartering)**: join the midpoints of the three sides to split $T$ into four small triangles.\n\nThe integral over the whole boundary equals the sum of the four small boundaries (the three interior edges are each traversed twice in opposite directions and cancel), so at least one satisfies $|I| \\le 4\\,|I(T_1)|$.\n\nIterating gives $T \\supset T^{(1)} \\supset T^{(2)} \\supset \\cdots$, the perimeter of the $n$-th is $L/2^n$, the nested closed sets theorem gives a common point $z^*$, and $|I| \\le 4^n |I(T^{(n)})|$.\n\n**Step 2 (replace $f$ by its linear part)**: near $z^*$, use the definition of complex differentiability: $f(z) = f(z^*) + f'(z^*)(z - z^*) + \\eta(z)\\,(z - z^*)$, where $\\eta(z) \\to 0$ ($z \\to z^*$).\n\nThe first two terms are polynomials, and their integral over any closed triangle is $0$ (a direct computation).\n\n**Step 3 (estimate)**: let $\\varepsilon_n = \\sup_{z \\in T^{(n)}} |\\eta(z)| \\to 0$; then $|I(T^{(n)})| \\le \\varepsilon_n \\cdot (L/2^n) \\cdot (L/2^n)$, hence $|I| \\le 4^n \\cdot \\varepsilon_n \\cdot L^2/4^n = \\varepsilon_n L^2 \\to 0$.\n\n⚠ Holomorphy is used only twice here: $f$ is continuous (to obtain $z^*$), and the difference quotient has a limit as $z \\to z^*$ (that is, $\\eta \\to 0$).\n\nFor this reason the conclusion is much stronger than the Green's theorem route.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#88d0038b',
      },
    },

    'thm.casorati-weierstrass': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $z_{0}$ be an **essential singularity** of $f$ (that is, the principal part of the Laurent expansion has infinitely many terms). Then for every punctured neighbourhood $U = \\{\\, 0 < |z - z_{0}| < r \\,\\}$ of $z_{0}$, the image $f(U)$ is **dense** in $\\mathbb{C}$:\n\n$$\\forall\\, w \\in \\mathbb{C},\\ \\forall\\, \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists\\, z \\in U:\\ |f(z) - w| < \\varepsilon.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce9df4ed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (by contradiction)**: suppose there are $w_{0}$ and $\\rho > 0$ with $f(U) \\cap B(w_{0}, \\rho) = \\emptyset$. Let\n\n$$F(z) := \\frac{1}{f(z) - w_{0}}, \\qquad 0 < |z - z_{0}| < r.$$\n\nThen $|F(z)| < 1/\\rho$ is bounded on $U$, so $z_{0}$ is a **removable singularity** of $F$ (`thm.riemann-removable`), and $F$ extends holomorphically at $z_{0}$.\n\nHence near $z_{0}$ the behaviour of $f = w_{0} + 1/F$ can only be: $F(z_{0}) \\ne 0$ $\\implies$ $f$ is holomorphic; $F$ has a zero of order $m$ $\\implies$ $f$ has a pole of order $m$;\n\n$F \\equiv 0$ $\\implies$ $f \\equiv \\infty$ (impossible).\n\nAll three contradict \"$z_{0}$ is an essential singularity\".\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **How to remember it**: among the three types of singularities, a removable singularity is **bounded** nearby, a pole **tends to infinity**, and at an essential singularity **anything can happen** — it scatters the image of every punctured neighbourhood over all of $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n⚠️ Casorati–Weierstrass only says **dense**, not \"attains every value\". The stronger **Picard great theorem** says that the image of a punctured neighbourhood misses **at most one point** (and that value is attained at most finitely often) — not developed here, only cited.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2e01c7d3',
      },
    },

    'thm.de-rham-h0': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: compute the lowest degree — a $0$-form is a function, \"closed\" means \"locally constant\", so $H^{0}$ counts exactly the number of connected components.\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold and $c$ its number of connected components. Then\n\n$$H^{0}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M) \\cong \\mathbb{R}^{c}.$$\n\nIn particular, $M$ is connected if and only if $\\dim H^{0}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M) = 1$ (for $M \\ne \\emptyset$).\n\nA $0$-form is just a smooth function; $\\mathrm{d}f = 0$ is equivalent to $f$ being constant on each connected chart; and $B^{0} = 0$, so $H^{0} = Z^{0}$ with no quotient to take.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a46947a4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why $\\mathbb{R}^{c}$ and not $\\mathbb{Z}^{c}$**: the coefficients are real, $H^{0}$ is a real vector space, and a basis is given by the indicator functions of the connected components. So \"$H^{0}$ counts components\" counts **dimension**, not the number of elements.\n\n**The two steps of the proof**: ① $\\mathrm{d}f = 0$ if and only if $f$ is constant on each connected component (a component is connected, any two points can be joined by a chain of charts, and adjacent charts agree on the constant on their connected overlap); ② $B^{0} = 0$, since there are no $-1$-forms, nothing can be quotiented out. Hence $H^{0}$ is isomorphic to the space of locally constant functions, whose dimension is exactly the number of components. Details are on the edge.\n\n**It is functorial**: for smooth $F : N \\to M$, the map $F^{\\ast} : H^{0}(M) \\to H^{0}(N)$ is \"pull back locally constant functions from $M$\". If $M$ is connected and $N$ is not, $F$ can take only one constant value on each component — the component structure is fully encoded by this.\n\n**Relation to homotopy**: $H^{0}$ is the free real vector space on $\\pi_{0}(M)$, so it is a **homotopy invariant**, not a differential invariant. This is also what de Rham's theorem looks like for $k = 0$ (singular cohomology in degree 0 likewise contributes one $\\mathbb{R}$ per component).\n\n**Do not apply the Poincaré lemma to $H^{0}$**: the lemma says that for $k \\ge 1$ and a contractible region one has $H^{k} = 0$. $H^{0}$ is never zero for a nonempty manifold, because it counts \"how many global pieces there are\", not \"whether there is a local obstruction\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#36766f4c',
      },
    },

    'prop.bounded-functional-gives-lq': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $1 \\le p \\le \\infty$, and let $q$ be its **conjugate exponent** ($1/p + 1/q = 1$, with the convention that $p=1$ gives $q=\\infty$ and $p=\\infty$ gives $q=1$). Let $g$ be measurable on $(X, \\mathcal{M})$, and suppose that $fg \\in L^{1}$ for **every simple function $f$ of finite support**. Write\n\n$$M(g) := \\sup \\left\\{ \\, \\left| \\int fg \\right| : f \\in \\Sigma,\\ \\|f\\|_p = 1 \\,\\right\\}$$\n\n($\\Sigma$ $=$ the set of all simple functions of finite support.) If $M(g) < \\infty$ and\n\n- $\\{x : g(x) \\ne 0\\}$ is **$\\sigma$-finite**, **or**\n- $\\mu$ is **semifinite**,\n\nthen $g \\in L^q$, and $M(g) = \\|g\\|_q$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0757894c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the technical preparation for the next big theorem ($(L^p)^* \\cong L^q$): it says that \"the functional is bounded\" by itself already forces $g$ into $L^q$.\n\nThe proof has three steps (see the arrows on the right): Step 1 first shows that $|\\int fg| \\le M(g)$ also holds when $f$ is measurable with finite support; Step 2 handles $q < \\infty$; Step 3 handles $q = \\infty$.\n\n⚠ The two hypotheses ($\\sigma$-finite / semifinite) are each used once, in Steps 2 and 3, and **cannot be dropped**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e49268df',
      },
    },

    'prop.mobius-circle': {
      statement: {
        en: "A Möbius transformation sends **circles and lines** (collectively \"circles\") in $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$ to circles or lines. Equivalently: the image of $\\mathbb{R} \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ is always a circle or a line.\n\nCorollary: between any two disks (or half-planes) there is a Möbius transformation carrying one to the other; in particular every disk is conformally equivalent to the upper half-plane and to the unit disk.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#84dec0fc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: by `prop.cross-ratio-real`, four points are concyclic $\\iff$ the cross-ratio is real. The cross-ratio is invariant under Möbius transformations, so \"concyclic\" is preserved — hence the image of a circle (or line) stays in the same class.\n\nMore concretely: $T$ is a composite of three translations/rotations/dilations and $z \\mapsto 1/z$; the first three obviously preserve circles, and $1/z$ sends the circle $|z - a| = r$ to a circle (compute that $w = 1/z$ satisfies $|1/w - a| = r$, which rearranges to an equation of the form $|w - \\bar a/\\sigma|^{2} = r^{2}/\\sigma^{2}$, still a circle).\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **\"A line counts as a circle\" is not wordplay**: on the extended plane a line is a circle through $\\infty$, and after unification the theorem is a single sentence — half the code and half the proof.\n\n⚠️ Circles are preserved but **not their interiors**: whether a disk maps to a disk or to its exterior depends on orientation. A real-coefficient Möbius transformation (real coefficients, $ad - bc$ ≠ 0) sends the upper half-plane to the upper or lower half-plane; to decide the direction, look at the image of one test point.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#75309320',
      },
    },

    'prop.mobius-three-points': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $z_{1}, z_{2}, z_{3}$ and $w_{1}, w_{2}, w_{3}$ be three distinct points of $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$. Then there is a **unique** Möbius transformation $T$ with $T(z_{k}) = w_{k}$ ($k = 1, 2, 3$).\n\nThe explicit form is the equality of cross-ratios:\n\n$$(z, z_{1}; z_{2}, z_{3}) = (Tz, w_{1}; w_{2}, w_{3}).$$\n\nRegarding the right-hand side as a fractional linear equation in $w$ and the left-hand side as one in $z$, solve for $w = T(z)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1130def5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Existence**: the displayed equation determines $z$ uniquely for each $w$ (unless the denominator vanishes, in which case $w = \\infty$); so it defines a map. It is fractional linear in $z$, hence a Möbius transformation. Substituting point by point gives $Tz_{k} = w_{k}$.\n\n**Uniqueness**: if both $T_{1}, T_{2}$ work, then $S := T_{1}^{-1}T_{2}$ fixes three points. A Möbius transformation fixing three points is the identity (by invariance of the cross-ratio: $Sz$ and $z$ have the same cross-ratio with respect to the same three points, hence $Sz = z$). ∎\n\n⭐ **Practical value**: for problems of the form \"construct a transformation sending a given disk to the upper half-plane\", it suffices to match three boundary points — the transformation is then uniquely determined, with no coefficients to guess.\n\n⚠️ The three pairs must be distinct on the extended plane; $\\infty$ is allowed as a target (for example, sending a point to $\\infty$ \"punctures\" it and turns the picture into a half-plane).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b77fb7e7',
      },
    },

    'prop.kan-extension-adjoint': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ be a functor between small categories and $\\mathcal{D}$ a category. Then the **precomposition functor**\n\n$$p^{-1} : \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal{C}', \\mathcal{D}) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal{C}, \\mathcal{D}), \\qquad G \\longmapsto G \\circ p$$\n\nhas a **left adjoint** $p_{!}$ $\\iff$ **left Kan extensions along $p$ with values in $\\mathcal{D}$ always exist**.\n\nDually: $p^{-1}$ has a right adjoint $p_{*}$ $\\iff$ right Kan extensions always exist.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7cde20c9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ How to read it: **the Kan extension is nothing new — it is the adjoint of precomposition**. This identifies the two languages \"Kan extensions everywhere\" and \"adjoints everywhere\".\n\n⚠️ The proposition itself **does not guarantee existence**; it only says that \"adjoint\" and \"Kan extension\" live and die together. Existence needs an extra hypothesis (see \"Kan extensions always exist when the target has enough limits\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c1b0de7d'
      },
    },

    'prop.regular-immersion-dualizing': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: **in the closed immersion case (embedding $\\operatorname{Spec} A$ into $\\operatorname{Spec} R$), the dualizing complex collapses to one line**: it is the \"determinant of the normal bundle\" shifted by the codimension.\n\n\"Regular\" means that $A$ is obtained from $R$ by killing a **regular sequence** ($f_1, \\ldots, f_c$: each $f_i$ is a nonzerodivisor on the quotient by the preceding ones — \"clean transverse cuts, no collisions\");\n\nin that case the codimension is $c$ everywhere.\n\nLet $f : R \\to A$ be a **regular closed immersion**: $f$ is surjective and its kernel $I$ is locally generated on $\\operatorname{Spec} R$ by a regular sequence; and suppose $\\operatorname{Spec} A \\subset \\operatorname{Spec} R$ has pure codimension $c$ everywhere. Then\n\n$$f^!R = \\operatorname{RHom}_R(A, R) \\;\\cong\\; \\det\\nolimits_A(I/I^2)^*[-c].$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3eeda5b1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Locally $A = R/(f_1, \\ldots, f_c)$ (a regular sequence), and the Koszul complex computes directly that $\\operatorname{RHom}_R(A,R) \\cong A[-c]$;\n\n$\\det(I/I^2)$ has a basis given by $f_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge f_c$.\n\nThe global statement must be checked to be independent of the choice of generators.\n\n⭐ This proposition together with flat base change are the two bricks for the smooth case: embed $\\operatorname{Spec} A$ into affine space (a regular closed immersion), then extract $\\Omega$ using the diagonal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3f0884c1',
      },
    },

    'thm.codazzi-mainardi': {
      statement: {
        en: "In an adapted frame, take the $j = 3$ component of the second structure equation (that is, the normal component of the curvature of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ vanishes):\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3} = \\sum_{j=1}^{2}\\omega^{j}_{\\ 3}\\wedge\\omega^{i}_{\\ j},\\qquad i = 1,2 .$$\n\nEquivalent form: $(\\nabla_{X}II)(Y,Z) = (\\nabla_{Y}II)(X,Z)$, i.e. $\\nabla II$ is symmetric in its first two variables. In coefficients:\n\n$$\\nabla_{i}h_{jk} = \\nabla_{j}h_{ik},\\qquad \\nabla_{i}h_{jk} = \\partial_{i}h_{jk}-\\Gamma^{l}_{\\ ij}h_{lk}-\\Gamma^{l}_{\\ ik}h_{jl}.$$\n\nIn dimension two there are only two independent equations ($i = 1$ and $i = 2$; the remaining index is determined by symmetry).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c13c259b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it says**: the rate of change of the bending is symmetric in the tangent directions — the component along $Y$ of the change of $II$ while moving along $X$ equals the component along $X$ while moving along $Y$. Equivalently: the mixed components $R^{i}_{\\ 3jk}$ of the curvature of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ must vanish.\n\n⭐ **Not an assumption but a necessary condition**: $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is flat, so every surface that embeds in it automatically satisfies Codazzi. Together with the Gauss equation it forms the complete compatibility condition for \"being realizable as a surface in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$\" (`thm.fundamental-theorem-surfaces`).\n\n**Coordinate version**: an equivalent form is $n_{ij} = n_{ji}$; the symmetric way of writing it is $\\nabla_{i}h_{kj} = \\nabla_{j}h_{ki}$ (with $h$ symmetric the two agree).\n\nIn the frame version one only reads the $\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}$ coefficient of $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i}_{\\ 3}$, without touching Christoffel symbols.\n\n⚠️ This simple shape holds only in codimension $1$ (surfaces in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$); in higher codimension the right-hand side acquires an extra normal-bundle curvature term. Only the codimension-one case inside $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is used here.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c296208',
      },
    },

    'cor.constant-iff-zero-derivative': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be differentiable on an interval $I$. Then $f$ is constant on $I$ if and only if for every $x \\in I$,\n\n$$f'(x) = 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#70bb8480',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This corollary means more than it seems: **differentiation loses exactly one constant** on an interval.\n\nThat is why \"finding an antiderivative\" makes sense and is determined only up to a constant (the other half of the fundamental theorem of calculus).\n\n⚠ The domain must be an **interval**: on $(0, 1) \\cup (2, 3)$ a function whose derivative vanishes everywhere can take different constants on the two pieces.\n\n⭐ In measure theory its status changes: $f' = 0$ a.e. does not force $f$ to be constant (the Cantor function) — \"almost everywhere\" is not enough to pin down a function, and absolute continuity is exactly the missing hypothesis.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe4ffa8a',
      },
    },

    'cor.zero-point': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be continuous on $[a, b]$, and suppose $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ have **opposite signs** ($f(a) f(b) < 0$). Then there is $\\xi \\in (a, b)$ with\n\n$$f(\\xi) = 0$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#89a028b8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It is the intermediate value theorem with $c = 0$; it is stated separately because it is used so often (bisection in numerical analysis is its algorithmic form).\n\n⚠ The hypothesis is **opposite signs**, not \"does not vanish\": when $f(a) = 0$ the conclusion is trivial but does not give a point in $(a, b)$.\n\n⚠ The converse fails: a zero need not come from opposite signs ($f(x) = x^{2}$ on $[-1, 1]$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#648e201f',
      },
    },

    'thm.maximal-theorem': {
      statement: {
        en: "There is a constant $C > 0$ (depending only on the dimension $n$) such that for every $f \\in L^1$ and every $\\alpha > 0$,\n\n$$m(\\{x : Hf(x) > \\alpha\\}) \\le (C/\\alpha)\\cdot\\int |f(x)| dx$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#817230e7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is a **weak (1,1) inequality**: it does not ensure $Hf \\in L^1$ (in general it is not), but it controls its **distribution function** by $1/\\alpha$.\n\nIn $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ one has $C = 3^n$ (the dilation factor from the covering lemma).\n\n⭐ Its role: **the exceptional set of averaging is small** — hence every pointwise convergence problem can be reduced to \"the measure of the exceptional set $\\to 0$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e7cbca23',
      },
    },

    'thm.mean-value-integral': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be continuous on $[a, b]$, and let $g$ be integrable and of **constant sign** ($g \\ge 0$ or $g \\le 0$). Then there is $\\xi \\in [a, b]$ such that\n\n$$\\int_a^b f g = f(\\xi) \\int_a^b g$$\n\nTaking $g \\equiv 1$ gives: there is $\\xi \\in [a, b]$ with $\\int_a^b f = f(\\xi)(b - a)$; in this case $f(\\xi)$ is called the **mean value** of $f$ on $[a, b]$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#17230d54',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric reading: the area under the curve equals \"some height\" times the base — that is, the **mean value** is actually attained at some point (continuity plus the extreme value theorem).\n\n⚠ The hypothesis that $g$ has **constant sign** cannot be dropped: if $g$ changes sign, the weighted mean may lie outside the range of $f$.\n\n⭐ It appears in the proof of Newton–Leibniz (to replace $F(b) - F(a)$ by the integral of $F'$), and it is the standard tool for Taylor remainders and error estimates in numerical integration.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2079c2aa',
      },
    },

    'thm.mixed-partials': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open and let $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}$ have second partial derivatives $\\partial_i \\partial_j f$ and $\\partial_j \\partial_i f$ both defined in a neighbourhood of $a$ and continuous at $a$. Then\n\n$$\\partial_i \\partial_j f(a) = \\partial_j \\partial_i f(a)$$\n\nIn particular, when $f \\in C^{2}$ the matrix $H_f(a)$ is symmetric.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#30b4d852',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem compresses \"$n^{2}$ second partial derivatives\" into \"$\\binom{n+1}{2}$\" — it is the entire source of the symmetry of the Hessian, and the reason $D^{2}f$ can be written as a **symmetric** bilinear form in the multivariable Taylor formula.\n\n⚠ **Continuity cannot be dropped**: $f(x,y) = xy(x^{2}-y^{2})/(x^{2}+y^{2})$ (with value $0$ at the origin) satisfies $\\partial_1\\partial_2 f(0,0) = 1 \\ne -1 = \\partial_2\\partial_1 f(0,0)$.\n\nContinuity guarantees not \"that the order can be swapped\" but \"that swapping produces no jump\".\n\n⭐ The proof strategy (full text on the edge): compute one **second difference** of $f$: $\\Delta = f(a + he_i + ke_j) - f(a + he_i) - f(a + ke_j) + f(a)$ in two orders using the one-variable mean value theorem, obtaining two expressions \"a second partial derivative at some intermediate point\", then let $h, k \\to 0$ and pass to the limit by continuity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bd8dae25',
      },
    },

    'thm.regular-value': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open and $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be $C^{1}$ ($m \\le n$).\n\nA point $c \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ is called a **regular value** of $f$ if and only if for every $x \\in f^{-1}(c)$ the differential $Df(x)$ is **surjective**;\n\notherwise it is called a **critical value**.\n\nIf $c$ is a regular value and $f^{-1}(c) \\ne \\emptyset$, then $f^{-1}(c)$ is a **submanifold** of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ of dimension $n - m$: near each point it is the **graph of a $C^{1}$ function** (equivalently, near each point $m$ independent equations \"solve for\" $m$ coordinates). ⚠ Only $f \\in C^{1}$ is assumed here, so what results is a **$C^{1}$ submanifold**; if $f$ is $C^{\\infty}$, the same argument gives a $C^{\\infty}$ submanifold (the usual statement of the regular value theorem silently assumes smoothness).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#925a4381',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"The preimage of a regular value is a manifold\" is the gateway to the whole theory of smooth manifolds — it turns **solving equations** into **describing geometric objects**.\n\n⭐ Standard example: for $f(x) = \\|x\\|^{2}$ ($\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$) the gradient $\\nabla f(x) = 2x$ vanishes only at the origin, so every $c > 0$ is a regular value and $f^{-1}(c)$ is an $(n-1)$-dimensional submanifold — this is where \"the sphere is a manifold\" comes from.\n\n⚠ $c = 0$ is a critical value (the only critical point is the origin and $f(0) = 0$), yet $f^{-1}(0) = \\{0\\}$ happens to be a $0$-dimensional manifold — the preimage of a critical value **may** still be a manifold; the theorem simply does not guarantee it.\n\nDeciding that a point is regular is much easier than deciding that it is critical, and that is the practical value of the theorem.\n\n⭐ Together with `thm.sard` it gives the full picture: regular values are plentiful, their preimages are manifolds, critical values have measure zero — hence \"almost every level set is a manifold\".\n\nThe full proof is on the edge (inverse function theorem plus a local choice of pivots treating $f$'s $m$ components as coordinates).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a6152b61',
      },
    },

    'thm.triangle': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be an ordered field (such as $\\mathbb{Q}$ or $\\mathbb{R}$) and $x, y \\in K$. Then\n\n$$|x + y| \\le |x| + |y|$$\n\nand hence two frequently used variants:\n\n$$\\big| |x| - |y| \\big| \\le |x - y|, \\qquad |x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n| \\le |x_1| + |x_2| + \\cdots + |x_n|$$\n\n(the latter follows from the former by induction on $n$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8d5205e5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is **the starting point of every estimate**: linear operations on Cauchy sequences, the algebra of limits, the comparison test for series, and the inequality $d(x, z) \\le d(x, y) + d(y, z)$ in metric spaces all reduce to it (the metric-space version takes it as an axiom).\n\n⚠ The proof is only case analysis, with no trick — but the two statements \"$a \\le |a|$ and $-a \\le |a|$\" are the whole point: they give $-(|x| + |y|) \\le x + y \\le |x| + |y|$, and $|t| \\le c \\iff -c \\le t \\le c$ (for $c \\ge 0$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5da2f32f',
      },
    },

    'thm.uniform-limit-continuous': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f_n : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ all be continuous and suppose $f_n \\rightrightarrows f$. Then $f$ is continuous.\n\nIn particular: **uniform convergence makes $C^{0}(X)$ closed** (in this sense $(C^{0}, \\|\\cdot\\|_{\\infty})$ is complete).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da2a00c1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The standard three $\\varepsilon/3$ trick: $|f(x) - f(x_0)| \\le |f(x) - f_n(x)| + |f_n(x) - f_n(x_0)| + |f_n(x_0) - f(x_0)|$.\n\nThe first and third terms use **uniform convergence** (the same $n$ works for both points at once), the second uses continuity of $f_n$.\n\n⚠ Pointwise convergence **does not preserve continuity** ($x^{n}$ on $[0, 1]$), and this is the most important of the three: uniform convergence exists precisely so that a global property such as continuity can pass through the limit.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3acdcfbe',
      },
    },

    'thm.uniform-limit-derivative': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f_n$ be differentiable on $[a, b]$ with $f_n'$ continuous, and suppose\n\n- $f_n' \\rightrightarrows g$ (**the derivatives** converge uniformly),\n- at at least one point $x_0$, the values $f_n(x_0)$ converge.\n\nThen $f_n$ converges uniformly to some $f$, $f$ is differentiable, and\n\n$$f' = g$$\n\nthat is, differentiation and taking limits commute: $\\bigl( \\lim_n f_n \\bigr)' = \\lim_n f_n'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b82ba39c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The key difference from the integral case: **differentiation amplifies errors**, so what must converge uniformly is not $f_n$ but $f_n'$.\n\nOne also needs the extra hypothesis that the sequence converges at one point; otherwise $f_n$ could differ by constants ($f_n + c_n$ has just as good derivative convergence).\n\n⚠ Assuming only $f_n \\rightrightarrows f$ is **not enough**: $f_n(x) = \\frac{\\sin(nx)}{n}$ converges uniformly to $0$, while $f_n'(x) = \\cos(nx)$ converges nowhere.\n\n⭐ The proof uses the mean value theorem: apply Lagrange to $\\frac{f_n(x) - f_n(x_0)}{x - x_0}$ to replace the difference quotient by $f_n'$ at some point, then let $n \\to \\infty$ using uniform convergence of $f_n'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#30f9ecf4',
      },
    },

    'thm.real-complete-metric': {
      statement: {
        en: "On $\\mathbb{R}$ take the distance $d(x, y) := |x - y|$. Then $(\\mathbb{R}, d)$ is a **complete** metric space: every Cauchy sequence in it converges.\n\nThis is exactly the Cauchy criterion rewritten in the language of metric spaces — in one sentence: $\\mathbb{R}$ is complete.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cb313267',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **interface** of two lines: on one side \"$\\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field\" (supremum principle), on the other \"a metric space $X$ is complete\" (every Cauchy sequence converges).\n\nOn $\\mathbb{R}$ the two coincide, but they **come from different sources**: the first is an order property, the second a metric property.\n\n⭐ Following this edge outward leads to **completion**: every metric space embeds into a complete metric space (take Cauchy sequences and quotient), and the construction of $\\mathbb{R}$ is the prototype — completing $\\mathbb{Q}$ gives $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n⚠ In a general metric space \"complete\" and \"compact\" are different: $\\mathbb{R}$ is complete but not compact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1c47da0f',
      },
    },

    'thm.raabe-test': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a_n>0$ and $R=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} n\\Bigl(\\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}-1\\Bigr)$. Then\n1. if $R>1$, $\\sum a_n$ converges;\n2. if $R<1$, $\\sum a_n$ diverges;\n3. if $R=1$, the test is inconclusive.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4e597aa6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to Kummer's test**: take $c_n=n$ in Kummer's test; then\n\n$$K_n=c_n\\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}-c_{n+1}=n\\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}-(n+1)=n\\Bigl(\\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}-1\\Bigr)-1\\to R-1 .$$\n\nWhen $R>1$ we have $K_n\\ge\\delta>0$ and $\\sum 1/c_n=\\sum 1/n$ diverges, hence the series converges; when $R<1$ we have $K_n\\le-\\delta$, hence it diverges (see `thm.kummer`).\n\n**Two examples with $R=1$**: $\\sum 1/n$ has $R=1$ and diverges; $\\sum 1/n^2$ has $n\\bigl(((n+1)/n)^2-1\\bigr)=2+1/n\\to2$, that is $R=2$, and converges.\n\n📌 When the ratio test fails ($q=1$) one may use Raabe; when Raabe still fails, use Bertrand or Gauss (`thm.bertrand-test`, `thm.gauss-test`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#72c975d7',
      },
    },

    'thm.rearrangement': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\sum a_n$ be **absolutely convergent** and let $\\sigma : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ be any bijection. Then $\\sum a_{\\sigma(n)}$ is also absolutely convergent, and\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_{\\sigma(n)} = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ec08d759',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The substance of the theorem is: **an absolutely convergent series is a \"commutative sum\"** — as with finite sums, the order of addition does not affect the result.\n\n⭐ Proof idea: first prove it for nonnegative series (the partial sums of the rearrangement and of the original series bound each other), then split into positive and negative parts, or real and imaginary parts.\n\n⚠ For conditionally convergent series the conclusion **fails completely** (see \"Riemann's rearrangement theorem\").\n\nSo \"can it be rearranged\" is the most intuitive test of absolute convergence.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f1cc62a7',
      },
    },

    'thm.power-series-analytic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f(x) := \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n (x - x_0)^{n}$ have radius of convergence $R > 0$. Then:\n\n- on $|x - x_0| < R$ the function $f$ is continuous, and on every $[x_0 - r, x_0 + r]$ ($r < R$) it converges **uniformly**;\n- $f$ is differentiable on $|x - x_0| < R$, and **term-by-term differentiation** gives the derivative, with the same radius of convergence $R$:\n\n$$f'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n\\, a_n (x - x_0)^{n-1}$$\n\n- likewise one may **integrate term by term**, again with radius of convergence $R$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0e740482',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The most noteworthy point is that **the radius of convergence does not change**: after term-by-term differentiation the coefficients become $n a_n$, and $\\sqrt[n]{n} \\to 1$, so the radius does not move at all.\n\nHence one may differentiate repeatedly — **a power series is $C^{\\infty}$ on its interval of convergence**, and every derivative is determined by the coefficients: $a_n = f^{(n)}(x_0)/n!$.\n\n⚠ \"Uniform convergence\" holds only on **closed subintervals** $[x_0 - r, x_0 + r]$, not on the whole open interval — near the endpoints uniform convergence fails.\n\n⭐ This is the other side of Taylor's formula: Taylor's theorem says \"$f$ can be approximated by polynomials\"; here, if $f$ **itself** is a power series, those polynomials are its partial sums and they really do converge to $f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5cf11c8a',
      },
    },

    'def.conditional-convergence': {
      statement: {
        en: "A real (or complex) series $\\sum a_n$ is called **conditionally convergent** if and only if it converges but $\\sum |a_n|$ **diverges**.\n\nThe typical example is the alternating harmonic series:\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n} = \\ln 2$$\n\nIt converges, while after taking absolute values it becomes the divergent harmonic series.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a6524468',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A conditionally convergent series is **fragile**: Riemann's rearrangement theorem says its terms can be reordered to converge to **any** prescribed value (even to diverge to $\\pm\\infty$).\n\nSo any \"interchange of order\" must first be justified.\n\n⚠ The alternating harmonic series converges to $\\ln 2$, but after the rearrangement \"one positive term, two negative terms\" it converges to $\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2$ — the same series, the same terms, a different sum.\n\n⭐ At the level of tests: conditional convergence can only be detected by the Abel–Dirichlet family (all positive-term tests fail).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3b2b64d6',
      },
    },

    'def.average-operator': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in L^1_{loc}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, $r > 0$. Define\n\n$$A_r f(x) := \\frac{1}{m(B(x, r))} \\int_{B(x, r)} f(y) dy$$\n\nthat is, the **average value** of $f$ over the ball centred at $x$ with radius $r$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1a43775d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the operator that \"smooths out\" a function: $A_r f$ is a continuous version of $f$ (see the next item), and as $r \\to 0$ it should return to $f$ itself — that is exactly what the Lebesgue differentiation theorem says.\n\n⚠ The integral is taken with respect to **Lebesgue measure $m$**, and has nothing to do with the signed measure $\\nu$ of this section.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e4773251',
      },
    },

    'def.bernoulli-number': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **Bernoulli numbers** $B_n$ are determined by $B_0=1$ and the recursion\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n+1}{k}B_k=0\\qquad(n\\ge1)$$\nequivalently, as a formal power series (`def.formal-power-series`)\n$$\\frac{t}{e^t-1}=\\sum_{n\\ge0}B_n\\frac{t^n}{n!}.$$\nThe first few are: $B_0=1$, $B_1=-\\tfrac12$, $B_2=\\tfrac16$, $B_3=0$, $B_4=-\\tfrac1{30}$, $B_6=\\tfrac1{42}$; and $B_{2k+1}=0$ for $k\\ge1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#08ac80a9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Where the recursion comes from**: $\\frac{t}{e^t-1}\\cdot\\frac{e^t-1}{t}=1$, while $\\frac{e^t-1}{t}=\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\frac{t^n}{(n+1)!}$; comparing coefficients of $t^n$ gives the recursion.\n\n**Bernoulli polynomials**: $B_n(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}B_k x^{n-k}$, satisfying $B_n'(x)=nB_{n-1}(x)$ and $B_n(x+1)-B_n(x)=nx^{n-1}$; the periodic functions in the Euler–Maclaurin formula are $B_n(\\{x\\})$.\n\n**Convention warning**: textbooks differ on whether $B_1$ is $+\\frac12$ or $-\\frac12$; the convention here is $B_1=-\\frac12$, consistent with the generating function $t/(e^t-1)$.\n\n📌 Uses: Euler–Maclaurin summation (`thm.euler-maclaurin`), closed forms for $\\zeta(2n)$, the asymptotic expansion in Stirling's formula, and trapezoidal corrections in numerical integration.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ceecc5fd',
      },
    },

    'def.exp-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, define\n\n$$\\exp z := \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^{n}}{n!}$$\n\nThis series converges absolutely for every $z$ (radius of convergence $+\\infty$), and satisfies\n\n$$\\exp(z + w) = \\exp z \\cdot \\exp w, \\qquad \\exp 0 = 1, \\qquad \\frac{d}{dz}\\exp z = \\exp z$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8fbdf84c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The series comes first, $e^{x}$ later**: this definition does not depend on any geometric intuition, works just as well for complex numbers, and all properties (the functional equation, derivative equal to itself) can be computed from the series.\n\n⭐ The functional equation is proved by convolution: $\\exp(z+w) = \\sum_n \\frac{(z+w)^{n}}{n!}$; expand $(z+w)^{n}$ by the binomial theorem and rearrange (absolute convergence makes the rearrangement legal).\n\n⚠ $\\exp$ never takes the value zero: $\\exp z \\cdot \\exp(-z) = \\exp 0 = 1$.\n\nHence it maps $\\mathbb{C}$ into $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26b4f31a',
      },
    },

    'def.formal-power-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a commutative ring. A **formal power series** over $R$ is a sequence of coefficients $(a_n)_{n \\ge 0}$ with $a_n \\in R$, written formally as\n$$\\sum_{n \\ge 0} a_n x^n .$$\nIt lives in $R[[x]]$: addition is termwise, and multiplication is the Cauchy product\n$$\\Bigl(\\sum a_n x^n\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\sum b_n x^n\\Bigr) := \\sum_{n \\ge 0}\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} a_k b_{n-k}\\Bigr)x^n .$$\nHere $x$ is only a **formal variable**; $x$ is not evaluated, and no question of convergence is asked.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c1f0bf0e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Difference from analytic power series**: `def.power-series` is concerned with radius of convergence, term-by-term differentiation and analytic functions; formal power series retain only coefficients and algebraic operations, so they can be defined over any commutative ring (including finite fields). Once convergence is dropped, generating functions in combinatorial enumeration become cleaner.\n\n**Invertibility**: $\\sum a_n x^n$ is invertible in $R[[x]]$ $\\iff$ $a_0$ is a unit of $R$. The inverse is given recursively by $b_0 = a_0^{-1}$ and $b_n = -a_0^{-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k b_{n-k}$.\n\n**Composition**: composition is defined only when the inner series $g$ has constant term $0$ (that is, $g \\in xR[[x]]$); then the value $f(g(x))$ is defined, while otherwise one would have to handle infinitely many substitutions.\n\n**Formal derivative**: $D\\bigl(\\sum a_n x^n\\bigr) := \\sum_{n \\ge 1} n a_n x^{n-1}$, which satisfies the product rule and uses no limit.\n\n📌 Uses: generating functions, counting trees, Lagrange inversion (`thm.lagrange-inversion`), and proving analytic identities by reducing them to coefficient identities.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#147f45f2',
      },
    },

    'def.local-extremum': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be defined on a neighbourhood of $x_0$. Call $x_0$ a\n\n- **local maximum point** of $f$: there is $\\delta > 0$ such that $|x - x_0| < \\delta$ implies $f(x) \\le f(x_0)$;\n- **local minimum point**: there is $\\delta > 0$ such that $|x - x_0| < \\delta$ implies $f(x) \\ge f(x_0)$.\n\nBoth are called **local extremum points**. Replacing $\\le$, $\\ge$ by $<$, $>$ (for $x \\ne x_0$) gives **strict** local extrema.\n\nIf $x_0$ lies in the **interior** of the domain and is a local extremum point, it is called an **interior extremum point**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#87326c5c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The word \"local\" is the whole point: the definition compares only values near $x_0$, not values far away.\n\nSo there can be many local extremum points ($\\sin x$ has infinitely many), while a **global** extremum (the one supplied by the extreme value theorem) is at most two.\n\n⚠ Endpoints do not count as interior extremum points — this is a hypothesis of Fermat's lemma, because the **one-sided** nature of the derivative at an endpoint destroys the argument.\n\n⚠ A local maximum need not be a global maximum: $f(x) = x^{3} - x$ has a local maximum near $x = -1$, but is unbounded globally.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#476a8bb3',
      },
    },

    'def.contraction': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, d)$ be a metric space and $T : X \\to X$. The map $T$ is called a **contraction** if and only if there is a constant $q \\in [0, 1)$ such that\n\n$$d(Tx, Ty) \\le q\\, d(x, y) \\qquad (x, y \\in X)$$\n\nThe smallest such $q$ is called the **contraction constant** of $T$. (For $q = 1$ the map is called nonexpansive; the conclusions then change.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4b2bb3aa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ The point is that $q < 1$ must hold **uniformly**, not merely \"less than $1$ near each point\".\n\nCounterexample: $f(x) = x + 1/x$ on $[1, \\infty)$ satisfies $|f'(x)| = 1 - 1/x^{2} < 1$ everywhere, but it is **not** a contraction and has **no** fixed point ($f(x) = x$ has no solution).\n\nThe difference between \"locally contracting at every point\" and \"uniformly contracting overall\" is the life-or-death line for the theorem.\n\n⭐ A contraction is uniformly continuous, much stronger than continuity; and its Lipschitz constant is exactly $q$.\n\n⭐ Contractions are the **only** class of maps whose existence proof comes with an algorithm and an error estimate (see `thm.banach-fixed-point`) — this is why they occupy such a high place in analysis: the inverse mapping theorem, the implicit function theorem, the Picard–Lindelöf theorem for ODEs and the Bellman equation all use them as their engine.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#499a388a',
      },
    },

    'def.alternating-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : V^{n} \\to K$ be a multilinear form.\n\n- $f$ is called **symmetric** if and only if for every permutation $\\sigma$ one has $f(v_{\\sigma(1)}, \\ldots, v_{\\sigma(n)}) = f(v_1, \\ldots, v_n)$;\n- $f$ is called **alternating** if and only if $f = 0$ whenever two of its arguments are equal;\n- $f$ is called **skew-symmetric** if and only if $f(v_{\\sigma(1)}, \\ldots) = \\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\, f(v_1, \\ldots)$.\n\nOver a field of characteristic $\\ne 2$, **alternating $\\iff$ skew-symmetric**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a95633f0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The criterion for \"alternating\" (zero when two arguments coincide) is the easiest to use, while the formula for \"skew-symmetric\" (with signs) is the easiest to compute with — the two agree when $\\operatorname{char} K \\ne 2$.\n\n⚠ They differ when $\\operatorname{char} K = 2$: then $-1 = 1$, so every skew-symmetric form is automatically symmetric, and alternation is the stronger condition.\n\nThe convention here is $\\operatorname{char} K = 0$, or at least $\\ne 2$ (all of $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{C}, \\mathbb{Q}$ qualify).\n\n⭐ Alternation is the **core property** that makes a determinant a determinant: swapping two rows changes the sign ($\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)$), and two equal rows give zero.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (alternating implies skew-symmetric without dividing by two; the converse needs it):\n- Alternating $\\iff$ the value is zero when two arguments coincide; this is just unfolding the definition and holds in any characteristic.\n- Alternating $\\implies$ swapping two arguments changes the sign: substitute $u+v$ into the two slots at once and expand by multilinearity; then decompose the permutation to get skew-symmetry, with no assumption on $\\operatorname{char}K\\ne2$.\n- Skew-symmetric $\\implies$ alternating: swapping two equal arguments gives $2f=0$, and one needs $\\operatorname{char}K\\ne2$ to cancel $2$; this is the genuine field condition in the equivalence.\n- A counterexample in characteristic $2$: over $K=\\mathbb F_2$ the bilinear form $f(x,y)=xy$ is skew-symmetric but $f(1,1)=1$, so it is not alternating.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b38b1966',
      },
    },

    'def.alternating-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Every $\\sigma \\in S_n$ can be written as a product of **transpositions** (swaps of two points), and the **parity** of the number of transpositions used does not depend on the decomposition. This defines the **sign**\n\n$$\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma) := (-1)^{k}$$\nwhere $\\sigma$ is a product of $k$ transpositions (well-definedness is guaranteed by the parity of $\\sigma$).\n\nAn equivalent formula (free of any choice of decomposition)\n\n$$\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma) = \\prod_{1 \\le i < j \\le n} \\frac{\\sigma(j) - \\sigma(i)}{j - i} \\in \\{\\pm 1\\}$$\n\n$\\operatorname{sgn} : S_n \\to \\{\\pm 1\\}$ is a group homomorphism (surjective for $n \\ge 2$). Its kernel\n\n$$A_n := \\ker \\operatorname{sgn} = \\{\\, \\sigma \\in S_n : \\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma) = 1 \\,\\}$$\n\nis called the **alternating group of degree $n$**: $|A_n| = n!/2$, $A_n \\trianglelefteq S_n$, $[S_n : A_n] = 2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c974da92',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The sign is the **only** nontrivial homomorphism from $S_n$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$ (for $n \\ge 3$), so $A_n$ is, among the subgroups of $S_n$, the **only** one of index $2$ — this uniqueness is the source of \"$S_n$ is not simple\" later, and of \"the quadratic subextension is unique\" in Galois theory.\n\n⭐ Why the product formula is clean: in each factor $\\frac{\\sigma(j)-\\sigma(i)}{j-i}$ the denominator is fixed and the numerator just permutes the same differences, so the sign of the product depends only on the parity of the permutation induced by $\\sigma$.\n\nThis also proves at once that the parity is independent of the decomposition.\n\n⚠ Edge cases: for $n = 1$, $A_1 = S_1$ is trivial and $|A_n| = n!/2$ does not apply.\n\nFor $n = 2$, $A_2 = \\{e\\}$.\n\n⭐ **The jump in simplicity**: $A_4$ is not simple (it has the normal subgroup $V_4 = \\{e, (12)(34), (13)(24), (14)(23)\\}$), while $A_5$ **is** simple and nonabelian.\n\nThis jump is exactly the dividing line for solvability of $S_n$ (`prop.sn-solvable`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#df3ef6bf',
      },
    },

    'def.eigenvalue': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to V$ be linear with $V \\ne \\{0\\}$. A scalar $\\lambda \\in K$ is called an **eigenvalue** of $T$ if and only if there is a **nonzero** vector $v \\in V$ such that\n\n$$T(v) = \\lambda\\, v$$\n\nSuch a $v$ is called an **eigenvector** belonging to $\\lambda$. The eigenvectors belonging to $\\lambda$, together with $0$, form the subspace\n\n$$E_{\\lambda} := \\ker(T - \\lambda\\,I) \\subseteq V$$\n\ncalled the **eigenspace**; $\\dim E_{\\lambda}$ is called the **geometric multiplicity** of $\\lambda$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#56e4d7bd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **eigenvectors are the vectors that $T$ only stretches, without changing their direction**, and the eigenvalue is the stretch factor.\n\nEquivalently: $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue $\\iff$ $T - \\lambda I$ is **not invertible** $\\iff$ $\\det(\\lambda I - T) = 0$.\n\n⭐ The eigenspace $E_\\lambda$ is $T$-invariant (and on it $T$ is just \"multiplication by $\\lambda$\"); eigenspaces for distinct eigenvalues are **linearly independent** (their sum is direct).\n\n⚠ $\\lambda$ must lie in $K$: a real matrix may have no real eigenvalues (a rotation by $90^\\circ$ has eigenvalues $\\pm i$); one then passes to $\\mathbb{C}$ (or simply says \"no eigenvalues over $K$\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#874914f3',
      },
    },

    'def.elementary-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "An **elementary row operation** is one of the following three operations:\n\n- **swap** row $i$ and row $j$;\n- multiply row $i$ by a nonzero constant $c$;\n- take row $j$, multiply it by $c$, and **add** the result to row $i$ (**with $i \\ne j$**; taking $i = j$ and $c = -1$ would give the non-invertible $\\operatorname{diag}(0, 1)$).\n\nThe matrix obtained by applying one such operation to the identity matrix $I_n$ is called an **elementary matrix** (written $E$). The key property is: **performing one elementary row operation on $A$ is the same as multiplying on the left by the corresponding elementary matrix**, that is, replacing $A$ by $EA$.\n\nEvery elementary matrix is **invertible**, and its inverse is again an elementary matrix of the same type: a swap is its own inverse, multiplying by $c$ has inverse multiplying by $c^{-1}$, and adding $c$ times a row has inverse adding $-c$ times it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a7642366',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The correspondence \"left multiplication = row operation\" turns **operations** into **objects**: thus \"solving a system by elimination\" becomes \"multiplying on the left by a string of elementary matrices\", and can be written as the identity $E_k \\cdots E_1 A = R$ ($R$ is the reduced row-echelon form).\n\n⭐ The most important consequence: $A$ **is invertible if and only if it is a product of finitely many elementary matrices** (elimination reduces $A$ to $I_n$, and recording the string of operations gives an explicit factorization of $A^{-1}$).\n\nThis is also the reason behind the $[A \\mid I] \\to [I \\mid A^{-1}]$ trick for computing inverses.\n\n⚠ Elementary **column** operations correspond to **right** multiplication.\n\nMixing rows and columns gives equivalence transformations ($A \\mapsto PAQ$), which preserve rank but not similarity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#24a86d49',
      },
    },

    'def.exterior-algebra': {
      statement: {
        en: "Take the direct sum of all exterior powers:\n\n$$\\bigwedge^{\\bullet} V := \\bigoplus_{k \\ge 0} \\bigwedge^{k} V \\qquad (\\bigwedge^{0} V := K,\\ \\bigwedge^{1} V := V)$$\n\nWith the multiplication $\\wedge$ given by \"tensor first, then take the image\", it becomes a **graded-commutative algebra**:\n\n$$(u \\wedge v) \\wedge w = u \\wedge (v \\wedge w), \\qquad v \\wedge w = (-1)^{|v| |w|}\\ w \\wedge v$$\n\n($|v|$ is the degree of an element. \"Graded-commutative\" means odd elements anticommute.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#744631a8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The exterior algebra is a \"**polynomial algebra with signs**\": together with the symmetric algebra it forms the two extremes — the symmetric algebra allows swapping, the exterior algebra forces anticommutation.\n\nBoth come from the same construction: quotient $V^{\\otimes k}$ by the action of the symmetric group, or take invariants.\n\n⭐ List of uses: differential forms ($\\bigwedge^{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{\\ast}$ is the space of $k$-forms), volume and determinants, Clifford algebras (replace $\\wedge$ by $v^{2} = -\\|v\\|^{2}$), the de Rham complex.\n\n⚠ It is **noncommutative** (odd part anticommutes), but **graded-commutative**: this is the easiest place to make a sign error when writing symbols (swapping two odd elements introduces a minus sign).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b551f0b1',
      },
    },

    'def.exterior-power': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a $K$-vector space. $V$'s **$k$-th exterior power** $\\bigwedge^{k} V$ is the quotient of $V^{\\otimes k}$ by the subspace spanned by\n\n$$v_1 \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes v_k \\quad \\text{with}\\ v_i = v_j\\ (i \\ne j)$$\n\nand the image is written $v_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge v_k$. Thus\n\n$$v \\wedge v = 0, \\qquad v \\wedge w = -\\, w \\wedge v$$\n\nand when $n = \\dim V < \\infty$ one has $\\dim \\bigwedge^{k} V = \\binom{n}{k}$; for $k > n$, $\\bigwedge^{k}V = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ef99e9ae',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Geometric meaning: when $\\dim V = n$, $\\bigwedge^{n} V$ is **one-dimensional**, and $v_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge v_n$ is exactly \"the oriented volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these $n$ vectors\" — its coefficient is the determinant.\n\n⭐ Alternation is achieved by \"quotienting\": $v \\wedge v = 0$ (send $v \\otimes v$ into the quotient), which is the \"zero when two arguments agree\" from `def.alternating-form`.\n\n⚠ Over a field of characteristic $2$, $v \\wedge w = -w \\wedge v$ and $v \\wedge w = w \\wedge v$ say the same thing ($-1 = 1$), and alternating and symmetric are no longer distinguishable.\n\nThe convention here is $\\operatorname{char} K \\ne 2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#22864762',
      },
    },

    'def.invariant-subspace': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to V$ be linear. A subspace $U \\subseteq V$ is called $T$-**invariant** if and only if\n\n$$T(U) \\subseteq U$$\n\nthat is, $u \\in U \\implies T(u) \\in U$. In that case $T$ restricted to $U$ gives a linear map $T|_U : U \\to U$.\n\n$\\{0\\}$ and $V$ are always $T$-invariant (**trivial** invariant subspaces); the kernel and image of $T$ are also $T$-invariant.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c6c0730a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Invariance\" is the prerequisite for \"computing block by block\": if $V = U_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus U_k$ and each $U_i$ is invariant, then $T$ is **block diagonal** with respect to this decomposition — so eigenvalues, invertibility and powers can all be computed block by block.\n\n⭐ The whole idea of Jordan decomposition is \"split $V$ into a direct sum of invariant subspaces as small (as fine) as possible\", and the finest such decomposition is exactly the one given by Jordan blocks.\n\n⚠ Note that $T$-invariance is not the same as \"the image of $T$\": invariance only requires $T(U) \\subseteq U$, not equality.\n\nEvery subspace with $\\ker T \\subseteq U$ — that is, every such $U$ — is automatically invariant.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#43328a85',
      },
    },

    'def.isotropic-vector': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this is about**: in a space with an inner product, $\\langle v,v\\rangle = 0$ has only the zero solution. A general bilinear form has no such property — a nonzero vector whose \"length\" is zero is called an **isotropic vector**, and this is where this geometry parts ways with inner-product geometry.\n\nLet $\\beta$ be a symmetric bilinear form on $V$ (or the corresponding quadratic form $q$). One says that $v \\ne 0$ is **isotropic** if\n\n$\\beta(v, v) = 0$ (that is, $q(v) = 0$)\n\nSuch a $v$ is orthogonal to itself. If $\\beta$ is nondegenerate and an isotropic vector exists, $\\beta$ is called **isotropic**; if there is no nonzero isotropic vector it is called **anisotropic**.\n\n**Example**: on $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$, for $\\beta(x,y) = x_{1}y_{1} - x_{2}y_{2}$, both $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ are isotropic; the standard inner product has no isotropic vectors.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#11225a9e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Geometric picture**: think of $q(x) = 1$ as the \"unit sphere\". A positive definite form gives an ellipsoid (anisotropic); an indefinite form gives a hyperboloid, and the hyperboloid has **two lines through the origin** as asymptotes; those two lines are exactly the **null cone** $q = 0$ (not on the hyperboloid), and the nonzero vectors on it are the isotropic ones. So \"isotropic\" = \"light-cone direction\".\n\n⚠️ The existence of isotropic vectors does not depend on the **basis** ($q(v) = 0$ is a coordinate-free statement), but whether they exist depends on the **form**: a real symmetric form is governed by its inertia indices — as soon as $q$ is indefinite (both $p, q$ nonzero) there are isotropic vectors; if it is positive or negative definite there are none.\n\n⭐ In relativity this is exactly \"light-like\": take $\\beta$ to be the Minkowski form, and isotropic vectors are the directions of light speed. Mathematically the same thing is called \"null vector / isotropic vector\", just under different names.\n\n⚠️ Do not confuse \"isotropic\" with \"zero vector\": an isotropic vector **must be nonzero**; only its \"squared length\" is zero.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7fe5b4d4',
      },
    },

    'def.matrix-operations': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A = (a_{ij})$ and $B = (b_{ij})$ have the same shape (both $m \\times n$).\n\n**Addition and scalar multiplication** (componentwise):\n\n$$(A + B)_{ij} := a_{ij} + b_{ij}, \\qquad (cA)_{ij} := c\\,a_{ij}$$\n\n**Multiplication**: let $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$ and $B \\in M_{n \\times p}(K)$; then $AB \\in M_{m \\times p}(K)$ is defined by\n\n$$(AB)_{ij} := \\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{ik} b_{kj}$$\n\n(the number of columns of $A$ must equal the number of rows of $B$, otherwise the product is not defined.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#53470afa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Why multiplication looks like this: regard each column of $B$ as a vector in $K^{n}$; then the column of $AB$ with index $j$ is given by $= A \\cdot (B$'s $j$-th column$)$ — **matrix multiplication is composition of linear maps** (see \"linear maps $\\leftrightarrow$ matrices\").\n\nIt is not an accidental recipe of \"multiplying row by column\".\n\n⚠ Multiplication is **not commutative**: $AB \\ne BA$ (even the shapes may fail to match).\n\nExample: $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ give $AB = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\ne BA$.\n\n⚠ $AB = 0$ need not imply $A = 0$ or $B = 0$ — this is what \"**for $n \\ge 2$**, $M_n(K)$ is a ring but not a domain\" means ($n = 1$ gives $M_1(K) \\cong K$, a field and hence a domain; the counterexample uses $E_{12}^{2} = 0$), and it also explains why the determinant is necessary (det keeps this under control).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#353feadb',
      },
    },

    'def.operator-adjoint': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V, W$ be finite-dimensional inner product spaces and $T : V \\to W$ linear. Then there is a **unique** linear map $T^{\\ast} : W \\to V$ such that\n\n$$\\langle T v, w \\rangle_W = \\langle v, T^{\\ast} w \\rangle_V \\qquad (\\forall v \\in V, w \\in W)$$\n\n$T^{\\ast}$ is called the **adjoint** of $T$. In an orthonormal basis, $[T^{\\ast}] = [T]^{\\dagger}$ (conjugate transpose).\n\nProperties: $(T + S)^{\\ast} = T^{\\ast} + S^{\\ast}$, $(cT)^{\\ast} = \\bar{c}\\,T^{\\ast}$, $(TS)^{\\ast} = S^{\\ast}T^{\\ast}$, $T^{\\ast\\ast} = T$.\n\n⚠️ This is not the same as an **adjoint functor** in category theory: that is a relation between two functors, whereas here we have an operator on the same space.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6467e45d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The way it is defined is worth noting: $T^{\\ast}$ is not constructed but **uniquely determined by an identity** (existence comes from Riesz representation: $w \\mapsto \\langle T\\cdot, w\\rangle$ is a bounded linear functional on $V$).\n\nThis is the finite-dimensional inner-product version of \"defining an object by a universal property\".\n\n⭐ In the complex case the conjugate in $(cT)^{\\ast} = \\bar c T^{\\ast}$ **cannot be dropped** — it comes from the conjugate-linearity of the inner product in its second argument.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7e750fe9',
      },
    },

    'def.ordered-field': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(F, +, \\cdot)$ be a **field** and $\\le$ a **total order** on $F$ (a partial order in which any two elements are comparable). If $\\le$ is **compatible** with the two operations:\n\n$$a \\le b \\implies a + c \\le b + c, \\qquad a \\le b,\\ 0 \\le c \\implies ac \\le bc$$\n\nthen $(F, +, \\cdot, \\le)$ is called an **ordered field**. Carrying over the language from $\\mathbb{Q}$: $a > 0$ is called a **positive element**, $a < 0$ a **negative element**; the two combine into the absolute value\n\n$$|a| := \\begin{cases} a, & a \\ge 0 \\\\ -a, & a < 0 \\end{cases}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d431037f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The second compatibility axiom permits \"multiplying both sides\" only when $0 \\le c$ — multiplying by a **negative** number **reverses** the inequality ($a \\le b,\\ c \\le 0 \\implies ac \\ge bc$, derived from the first two axioms).\n\nAll the usual rules for inequalities over the rationals and the reals ultimately come down to this one axiom.\n\nFrom the two axioms one immediately gets: **squares are nonnegative** ($a \\ge 0 \\implies a^2 \\ge 0$;\n\n$a \\le 0 \\implies -a \\ge 0$ and $a^2 = (-a)^2 \\ge 0$), in particular $1 = 1^2 > 0$, hence $-1 < 0$.\n\n📌 **Completeness is not part of this definition**; it is one more axiom imposed on an ordered field: **every nonempty subset bounded above has a supremum**.\n\n$\\mathbb{Q}$ is the example of \"ordered but not completable\" — $\\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} : q^2 < 2\\}$ is bounded above but has no supremum.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eff40aea',
      },
    },

    'def.coordinate': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{B} = (v_1, \\ldots, v_n)$ be an **ordered** basis of $V$ (the order is part of the data). By the definition of a basis, every $v \\in V$ can be written **uniquely** as\n\n$$v = c_1 v_1 + \\cdots + c_n v_n, \\qquad c_i \\in K$$\n\nArrange the coefficients into a column\n\n$$[v]_{\\mathcal{B}} := \\begin{pmatrix} c_1 \\\\ \\vdots \\\\ c_n \\end{pmatrix} \\in K^{n}$$\n\ncalled the **coordinate vector** of $v$ in the basis $\\mathcal{B}$. Thus\n\n$$v \\mapsto [v]_{\\mathcal{B}}$$\n\nis a bijection $V \\to K^{n}$, and it is linear (it preserves addition and scalar multiplication).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#baa0fb18',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This entry translates \"an abstract vector space\" into \"column vectors in $K^{n}$\" — **this is the entire justification of matrix theory**: from now on, matrices are just what this looks like with respect to two different bases.\n\n⚠ \"Uniqueness\" comes from the definition of a basis (both linearly independent and spanning);\n\nif either property fails, coordinates cease to be unique (without independence there are repetitions; without spanning some vectors cannot be written).\n\n⚠ \"Ordered\" cannot be dropped: replacing $(v_1, v_2)$ by $(v_2, v_1)$ swaps the two components of the coordinate vector — this is exactly where the permutations in the change-of-basis formula come from.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fd939fbe',
      },
    },

    'def.nullity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to W$ be linear. The **nullity** of $T$ is defined as the dimension of its kernel:\n\n$$\\operatorname{null} T := \\dim \\ker T$$\n\n(also called the **defect**; English nullity or defect.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92b3f83e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Nullity measures \"how many dimensions of information $T$ throws away\". It pairs with the rank: the rank–nullity theorem says the two add up to the dimension of the domain.\n\n⚠ $\\operatorname{null} T = 0 \\iff \\ker T = \\{0\\} \\iff T$ is injective — nullity is the cheapest injectivity test.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a63dbef2',
      },
    },

    'def.bezout-domain': {
      statement: {
        en: "A domain $A$ is called a **Bézout domain** if every **finitely generated** ideal is principal — equivalently: for any $a, b \\in A$ there exist $d, u, v$ such that\n\n$$u a + v b = d = \\gcd(a, b) .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e4756a49',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "PID $\\implies$ Bézout (every ideal is principal, so certainly every finitely generated one is), but not conversely: a Bézout domain need not be Noetherian, and may have ideals that are not finitely generated.\n\n$\\mathbb{Z}[x]$ is **not** Bézout: $\\gcd(2, x) = 1$, but $2u + xv = 1$ has no solution (set $x = 0$ to get $2u(0) = 1$) — so the \"Bézout property\" is stronger than being a gcd domain.\n\nIn a Bézout domain \"irreducible = prime\" (because $\\gcd$ is given by a linear combination, so the proof of Euclid's lemma carries over directly).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6e331eae',
      },
    },

    'def.euclidean-domain': {
      statement: {
        en: "A domain $A$ is called a **Euclidean domain** if there is a function $N : A \\setminus \\{0\\} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ (a **Euclidean function**) such that for every $a \\in A$ and every $b \\ne 0$ there exist $q, r \\in A$ with\n\n$$a = q b + r, \\qquad r = 0 \\ \\vee \\ N(r) < N(b) .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b16c9346',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Three examples, three choices of $N$: for $\\mathbb{Z}$ take $N(a) = |a|$; for $K[x]$ take $N(f) = \\deg f$ (this is `thm.division-algorithm`); for $\\mathbb{Z}[i]$ (Gaussian integers) take $N(a+bi) = a^{2}+b^{2}$.\n\nWhy it is valuable: all the later arithmetic ($\\gcd$, Bézout, unique factorization) uses **only \"the remainder decreases\"**, so the whole chain holds at once in a Euclidean domain (starting from `imp.ring-euclid-pid`).\n\nThe key step in proving that a Euclidean domain $\\implies$ PID: take a nonzero ideal $I \\ne 0$ and among its elements choose one with minimal $N$, call it $b$; then any $a \\in I$, divided by $b$, has remainder $r = a - qb \\in I$ satisfying $N(r) < N(b)$, forcing $r = 0$, hence $I = (b)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9b9953af',
      },
    },

    'def.group-power': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group, $x \\in G$, and $e$ the identity. The **integer powers** $x^{n}$ ($n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$) are defined by\n\n$$x^{0} := e, \\qquad x^{n+1} := x^{n} x \\ (n \\ge 0), \\qquad x^{-n} := (x^{-1})^{n} \\ (n \\ge 1)$$\n\nequivalently $x^{-n} = (x^{n})^{-1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e75d8b53',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Basic laws.** For $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ one has $x^{m+n} = x^{m} x^{n}$, $(x^{m})^{n} = x^{mn}$, $x^{-n} = (x^{n})^{-1}$. The first two are proved by induction on $n \\ge 0$, with negative exponents reduced to the inverse; the third follows from $x^{n} x^{-n} = e$ and uniqueness of inverses.\n\n**Keep it separate from integer powers in a ring.** Integer powers in a unital ring (see the real-analysis cluster's \"integer powers\") use the multiplicative identity $1$ of the ring as $a^{0}$, and $a^{-n}$ is defined only when $a$ is invertible; the present entry assumes only a group structure, with $e$ the group identity. The notation is the same but the carrier is different — use whichever the context calls for, and do not substitute one for the other.\n\n⭐ **Order of an element**: if there is $n \\ge 1$ with $x^{n} = e$, the least such $n$ is called the order of $x$; it is exactly the step at which powers return to the identity. The set of all integer powers $\\{x^{n} : n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$ forms, inside $G$, the cyclic subgroup $\\langle x \\rangle$.\n\n⚠ In a nonabelian group $(xy)^{n} \\ne x^{n} y^{n}$ (for $n \\ge 2$); this holds only when $x, y$ commute. But $x^{m} x^{n} = x^{m+n}$ always holds for the **same** $x$, guaranteed by associativity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#787f6d52',
      },
    },

    'def.nat-arith': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\omega = \\mathbb{N}$, $S(n) := n \\cup \\{n\\}$, $0 := \\emptyset$. Define two operations on $\\omega$ by **recursion**.\n\n**Addition**: for each fixed $m$, the map $n \\mapsto m + n$ is the unique $g : \\omega \\to \\omega$ satisfying\n\n$$m + 0 := m, \\qquad m + S(n) := S(m + n)$$\n\n**Multiplication**: likewise,\n\n$$m \\cdot 0 := 0, \\qquad m \\cdot S(n) := m \\cdot n + m$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#55205224',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it can only be defined this way**: $\\omega$ itself is built recursively, and the only tool for \"building something out of $\\omega$\" is recursive definition. Addition, multiplication, powers and order all come from this one principle.\n\n⚠️ These two clauses are only an assertion of **existence and uniqueness** — existence is precisely the content of the **recursion theorem**, so they are **definitions**, not axioms, and require no additional axiom.\n\n**Properties are verified by induction** (not part of the definition): take $0 + n = n$ as an example — for $n = 0$ both sides are $0$; assuming $0 + n = n$, we get $0 + S(n) = S(0 + n) = S(n)$ ✓.\n\nCommutativity $m + n = n + m$ first needs the two lemmas $0 + n = n$ and $S(m) + n = S(m + n)$, then induction on $n$;\n\nassociativity and distributivity $m(n+k) = mn + mk$ are proved the same way.\n\n⭐ **This is the prototype of every later operation**: the addition of $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $[a,b] + [c,d] := [a+c,\\ b+d]$, the $+$ inside it, the addition of $\\mathbb{Q}$, and termwise addition in $\\mathbb{R}$ all have this same $+$ underneath.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3fd8860',
      },
    },

    'cor.jordan-uniqueness': {
      statement: {
        en: "Assume that the **characteristic polynomial of $A$ splits over $K$** (otherwise not even a Jordan form exists: over $\\mathbb{R}$ the rotation $\\begin{pmatrix}0 & -1\\cr 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}$ is a counterexample). Under this hypothesis the Jordan normal form is **unique** up to \"the order of the Jordan blocks\", that is, $A \\sim B$ if and only if $A$ and $B$ have the same Jordan type (blocks disregarded as to order):\n\n$$A \\sim B \\iff \\mu_A(\\lambda) = \\mu_B(\\lambda) \\ \\ (\\forall \\lambda)$$\n\nMore quantitatively: for each eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the **sizes** of the Jordan blocks form a partition $\\mu(\\lambda)$; two matrices are similar if and only if, for every $\\lambda$, $\\mu_A(\\lambda) = \\mu_B(\\lambda)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c3fda4bd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The practical form of uniqueness: **the number of blocks = geometric multiplicity** ($\\dim E_\\lambda$), **the largest block size = the multiplicity of $\\lambda$ in the minimal polynomial**, **the sum of the sizes = algebraic multiplicity**.\n\nThus \"classification up to similarity\" is compressed into a set of integer partitions.\n\n⭐ This yields a nice corollary: $A$ is nilpotent ($A^{k} = 0$ for some $k$) $\\iff$ its only eigenvalue is $0$ $\\iff$ its characteristic polynomial is $x^{n}$.\n\n⚠ \"Order of blocks disregarded\" is necessary: the Jordan form is written according to an ordering of eigenvalues and blocks, and permuting blocks gives another description of the same operator (just another basis).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#303aa5f1',
      },
    },

    'cor.spectral-normal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional **complex** inner product space and $T : V \\to V$ **normal** ($T^{\\ast}T = TT^{\\ast}$). Then there is an **orthonormal basis** of $V$ in which $T$ is diagonal:\n\n$$T = U \\Lambda\\, U^{\\dagger}, \\qquad U^{\\dagger}U = I,\\ \\Lambda = \\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n)$$\n\nMoreover:\n\n- $T$ is self-adjoint $\\iff$ all eigenvalues are **real**;\n- $T$ is unitary $\\iff$ all eigenvalues have **modulus 1**;\n- $T$ is an orthogonal projection $\\iff$ the eigenvalues all lie in $\\{0, 1\\}$ (under the hypothesis that $T$ is normal one does **not** need to require $T$ self-adjoint again: a normal operator whose spectrum lies in $\\mathbb{R}$ is automatically self-adjoint).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#022f369f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ These three \"property of $T$ $\\iff$ property of eigenvalues\" statements are the most useful by-product of the spectral theorem: they translate a problem about an **operator** into a problem about a set of **numbers**.\n\n⭐ Complex normality is the common generalization of real symmetry and real orthogonality: over the complex field both can be diagonalized; over the reals only the symmetric ones can.\n\n⚠ The projection clause holds only for **self-adjoint** operators: a general idempotent (a projection) need not be unitarily diagonalizable.\n\n**Which hypothesis each direction uses** (the numerical criteria for the spectrum rest on the complex normal spectral theorem):\n- Unitary diagonalization $\\implies$ normal: a diagonal matrix commutes with its adjoint, by direct computation; normal $\\implies$ unitary diagonalization uses the finite-dimensional complex spectral theorem and the existence of eigenvalues over the complex numbers.\n- In that diagonalization, self-adjoint $\\iff$ all eigenvalues real, and unitary $\\iff$ all eigenvalues have modulus $1$, are both termwise computations with the adjoint; the converses cannot be detached from the normality hypothesis.\n- Orthogonal projection $\\implies$ self-adjoint with spectrum in $\\{0,1\\}$, by idempotence; conversely, the self-adjoint spectral decomposition gives an idempotent, hence an orthogonal projection. Once normality is assumed in this node, spectrum in $\\{0,1\\}$ already suffices.\n- A non-normal Jordan block $\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$ has spectrum entirely real of modulus $1$, yet is neither self-adjoint nor unitary; a nontrivial rotation of the real plane has no real eigenbasis, showing that the complex hypothesis matters.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f32cc5c5',
      },
    },

    'thm.spectral-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this is about**: it brings the two operator-level spectral theorems already available (`thm.spectral-theorem` for the real symmetric case, `cor.spectral-normal` for the complex normal case) down to **matrices**, and gives the precise if-and-only-if conditions.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. Then\n\n$A$ is normal $\\iff$ there is a unitary matrix $U$ with $U^{*}AU = \\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_{1}, \\ldots, \\lambda_{n})$\n\nthat is: **normal $\\iff$ there is an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors**. In that case\n\n$$A = U \\Lambda U^{*}, \\qquad \\Lambda = \\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_{1}, \\ldots, \\lambda_{n}) ,$$\n\ncalled the **spectral decomposition** of $A$ (a sum of orthogonal projections: $A = \\sum_i \\lambda_i u_i u_i^{*}$).\n\n**Real case**: a real symmetric matrix $\\iff$ there is an **orthogonal** matrix $Q$ with $Q^{T}AQ$ real diagonal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bebfc4fe',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Four equivalent formulations; any one may serve as the definition**: $A$ is normal; $A$ has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors; $A = U\\Lambda U^{*}$; the spectral measure of $A$ takes only atomic values. In matrix language the first is the most economical, the second the most intuitive (the whole content of `thm.spectral-matrix` is \"the two are equivalent\").\n\n**How to read the spectral decomposition**: $A = \\sum_i \\lambda_i P_i$, where $P_i = u_i u_i^{*}$ is the orthogonal projection onto the eigen-direction, satisfying $P_i^2 = P_i$, $P_iP_j = 0$ ($i \\ne j$), $\\sum_i P_i = I$.\n\nThus **\"diagonalization\" = \"splitting the operator into a bunch of mutually perpendicular directions, each multiplied by a number\"**.\n\n⭐ Connection with functional calculus: once the spectral decomposition is available, any function $f$ can be applied: $f(A) := \\sum_i f(\\lambda_i)P_i$. The operators $\\sqrt{A}$, $e^{tA}$, $A^{1/2}$ are all special cases — this is why \"a positive definite matrix has a square root\" needs no separate construction.\n\n⚠️ A non-normal matrix may still be diagonalizable (`thm.diagonalizable`), but its eigenvectors are **not orthogonal**, and the similarity matrix $P$ is generally ill-conditioned. So \"diagonalizable\" and \"unitarily diagonalizable\" are two different things: the latter demands **orthogonality** and is much stronger.\n\n**The proof is on the outgoing edge**: the full proof is the corollary edge \"Schur triangularization $\\Longrightarrow$ spectral theorem (matrix version)\" in this cluster — normality is preserved under unitary similarity, and an upper triangular normal matrix must be diagonal; the real symmetric case then follows by induction using \"eigenvalues are real and eigenvectors can be chosen real\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bea8800d',
      },
    },

    'def.manifold-chart': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it says**: a manifold has no built-in coordinates, so coordinates must be issued chart by chart — a chart is a piece of ground plus a coordinate sign, and changing signs has to be smooth.\n\nLet $M$ be a topological space and $n \\ge 0$.\n\nA **coordinate chart** on $M$, of dimension $n$, is a pair $(U,\\varphi)$, where $U \\subseteq M$ is open and $\\varphi : U \\to \\varphi(U) \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is a homeomorphism (with $\\varphi(U)$ open in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$).\n\n$U$ is the **coordinate neighbourhood** and $\\varphi$ the **coordinate map**; the components $x^{i} := \\varphi^{i}$ are the **local coordinates**, i.e. $p \\mapsto \\bigl(x^{1}(p),\\ldots,x^{n}(p)\\bigr)$.\n\nWhen $n = 0$, $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ is a single point, so a chart only records whether the point lies in $U$.\n\nThe **transition map** between two charts $(U,\\varphi)$, $(V,\\psi)$ is\n\n$$\\psi \\circ \\varphi^{-1} : \\varphi(U \\cap V) \\longrightarrow \\psi(U \\cap V),$$\n\nwhich turns $\\varphi$-coordinates into $\\psi$-coordinates. The charts are **$C^{\\infty}$-compatible** when the transition map is $C^{\\infty}$ everywhere (it is defined on an open subset of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, so partial derivatives test it); if $U \\cap V = \\emptyset$ they are declared compatible.\n\nCovering $M$, a family of pairwise compatible charts $\\mathcal{A} = \\{(U_{\\alpha},\\varphi_{\\alpha})\\}$ is an **atlas** of $M$ of dimension $n$.\n\n**Example**: on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ take $U = \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\varphi = \\operatorname{id}$; this is a one-chart atlas.\n\nFor $S^{n} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ use the two stereographic charts (from the north and south poles to $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$); their transition map is $x \\mapsto x/\\lVert x\\rVert^{2}$, which is smooth.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c4b66dda',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why a homeomorphism and not merely a bijection**: coordinates must carry the topological notions — continuity, convergence, compactness — otherwise what is computed is only a property of sets. A homeomorphism makes the chart a coordinate chart in the **topological** sense; this is why a topological space is part of the definition of a manifold.\n\n**The transition map is the only thing to manage**: calculus on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ knows only $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$-coordinates. Every derivative on a manifold descends into some chart, and the chain-rule factor when changing charts is the Jacobian of the transition map. Thus \"calculus on a manifold\" is **equivalent** to \"transition maps are smooth\" — this single technical assumption is the whole subject.\n\n**Coordinates are not part of the manifold**: one chart admits infinitely many descriptions (any diffeomorphism of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ gives new coordinates); what belongs to the manifold is the existence of a pairwise compatible family. Distinguishing these two facts motivates the maximal atlas.\n\n**Dimension is pinned by charts**: if two charts on $M$ have overlapping domains of dimensions $n$ and $m$, then open subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\mathbb{R}^{m}$ are homeomorphic, so Brouwer's invariance of domain gives $n = m$. Hence \"$n$-dimensional\" is not an extra choice.\n\n**Interface with surface theory**: the inverse of a regular parametrization $r : U \\to S$ is a chart, and a change of parametrization is precisely the transition map — \"smoothness is independent of the parametrization\" and \"**$C^{\\infty}$**-compatible\" are the same sentence (`def.orientable-surface`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4a99815e',
      },
    },

    'prop.bundle-metric': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it builds \"an inner product on every fibre\" globally — locally such products always exist, and a partition of unity glues them. This is the general version of \"every manifold carries a Riemannian metric\".\n\nLet $\\pi : E \\to M$ be a smooth vector bundle of rank $k$. A **fibre metric** is an inner product on each fibre $E_{p}$, namely $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{p}$, such that for any two smooth sections $s, t \\in \\Gamma(E)$ the function\n\n$$p \\longmapsto \\langle s(p), t(p)\\rangle_{p}$$\n\nis smooth. Then **every smooth vector bundle admits a fibre metric**.\n\nSpecial case: for $E = TM$ a fibre metric is a Riemannian metric (`def.riemannian-metric`); for a line bundle $E$ it is a \"everywhere positive-definite symmetric bilinear form\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#34ba187d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The proof is a partition of unity**: take a locally trivializing cover $\\{U_{\\alpha}\\}$, on each $U_{\\alpha}$, identify $E$ with $U_{\\alpha} \\times \\mathbb{R}^{k}$, and take the standard inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{\\alpha}$;\n\nthen take a smooth partition of unity $\\{\\rho_{\\alpha}\\}$ (`thm.smooth-partition-unity`) and set $\\langle s,t\\rangle := \\sum_{\\alpha}\\rho_{\\alpha}\\langle s,t\\rangle_{\\alpha}$.\n\nThis is a **convex combination**: symmetry and bilinearity are clear;\n\npositive definiteness follows from $\\rho_{\\alpha} \\ge 0$ and the sum being identically $1$ — at each point this is a weighted average of inner products with non-negative weights summing to $1$, so $\\langle s,s\\rangle = 0$ forces $s = 0$.\n\n**Why metrics face no obstruction while other structures do**: positive-definite symmetric forms form a **convex set**, and convex combinations stay positive definite; complex structures ($J^{2} = -\\operatorname{id}$) and symplectic structures ($\\omega$ non-degenerate and closed) are not convex conditions, cannot be glued, and have topological obstructions. So \"a Riemannian metric on $TM$ always exists, an almost complex structure need not\" is not a technical gap but the difference between **convex and non-convex**.\n\n**Corollary: the structure group reduces to $\\operatorname{O}(n)$**: with a fibre metric, the transition functions of $TM$ can be pointwise Gram–Schmidt orthogonalized, reducing the structure group from $\\operatorname{GL}(n,\\mathbb{R})$ to $\\operatorname{O}(n)$. This is the technical prerequisite for doing Riemannian geometry on an arbitrary manifold.\n\n**Dual and tensor bundles work the same way**: the metrics on $E^{\\ast}$, $\\bigwedge^{k}E$ and $E \\otimes F$ are induced pointwise from those on $E, F$ (the inner product of a tensor product). Hence \"every tensor bundle carries a fibre metric\" is a corollary.\n\n**A fibre metric is not a Riemannian metric on $E$**: $E$ itself is a manifold and can carry a Riemannian metric, but that measures both fibre and base directions, whereas here only the fibre direction is measured. For $TM$ the Riemannian metric happens to serve both roles, which is the easiest place to confuse them.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#31993d25',
      },
    },

    'prop.left-invariant-fields': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: it differentiates a Lie group at the identity — left translation spreads each tangent vector at the identity into a global vector field, and these fields form a Lie algebra.\n\nLet $G$ be a Lie group and $\\mathfrak{g} := T_{e}G$ ($e$ the identity). A vector field $X \\in \\mathfrak{X}(G)$ is **left-invariant** if for all $g, h \\in G$\n\n$$X_{gh} = \\mathrm{d}(L_{g})_{h}\\bigl(X_{h}\\bigr).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. **Evaluation is an isomorphism**: $X \\mapsto X_{e}$ is a linear isomorphism from the space of left-invariant vector fields onto $\\mathfrak{g}$ (inverse: $v \\mapsto \\bigl(g \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}(L_{g})_{e}(v)\\bigr)$), so this space has dimension $\\dim G$;\n2. **Closed under the Lie bracket**: if $X, Y$ are left-invariant, so is $[X,Y]$;\n3. **Hence $\\mathfrak{g}$ carries a Lie algebra structure**: define $[v,w] := [\\tilde v,\\tilde w]_{e}$ ($\\tilde v$ the left-invariant field of $v$); then $(\\mathfrak{g},[\\cdot,\\cdot])$ is a Lie algebra, the **Lie algebra** of $G$, and $\\dim\\mathfrak{g} = \\dim G$.\n\nThus \"Lie group $\\to$ Lie algebra\" is a functor: $\\mathrm{Lie}(G)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e585058b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why evaluation is a bijection**: left-invariance determines the value of $X$ at $gh$ **uniquely** from $X_{h}$; taking $h = e$ shows $X$ is determined by $X_{e}$ (injectivity). Conversely, given $v \\in \\mathfrak{g}$, put $X_{g} := \\mathrm{d}(L_{g})_{e}(v)$; then $X_{gh} = \\mathrm{d}(L_{gh})_{e}(v) = \\mathrm{d}(L_{g})_{h}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}(L_{h})_{e}(v)\\bigr) = \\mathrm{d}(L_{g})_{h}(X_{h})$ (chain rule plus $L_{gh} = L_{g} \\circ L_{h}$), so it is left-invariant (surjectivity).\n\nSmoothness: write everything in a chart; $\\mathrm{d}(L_{g})$ is smooth in $g$.\n\n**Why the bracket descends**: the key is **naturality** of the Lie bracket,\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}F\\bigl([X,Y]_{p}\\bigr) = \\bigl[\\mathrm{d}F(X),\\mathrm{d}F(Y)\\bigr]_{F(p)},$$\n\nwhere $F$ is a diffeomorphism. Take $F = L_{g}$; left-invariance gives $\\mathrm{d}(L_{g})X = X$ and $\\mathrm{d}(L_{g})Y = Y$, hence $\\mathrm{d}(L_{g})[X,Y] = [X,Y]$, i.e. $[X,Y]$ is left-invariant. So the only substantive input in \"a Lie group gives a Lie algebra\" is this naturality.\n\n**Example**: for $G = \\operatorname{GL}(n,\\mathbb{R})$ the Lie algebra is $\\mathfrak{gl}(n,\\mathbb{R})$ with the commutator — because $L_{A}$ is linear and $\\mathrm{d}(L_{A}) = A$ itself, left-invariant fields are $X(A) = AM$ ($M$ fixed), and the bracket computes to $[M,N] = MN - NM$.\n\n**\"The Lie algebra determines the Lie group\" is false**: $S^{1}$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ both have the one-dimensional abelian Lie algebra, yet they are not isomorphic (compact versus non-compact). So $\\mathfrak{g}$ does not recover $G$, only the simply connected $G$ — this is Lie's third theorem, not developed here.\n\n**Where next**: left-invariant **forms** (the dual version) give the Maurer–Cartan equation, also not developed here; that story is the same bookkeeping as the structure equations (`def.connection-form`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#621cb6cd',
      },
    },

    'thm.change-of-variables-manifold': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: the change-of-variables formula on a manifold — pushing a form along a diffeomorphism leaves the integral unchanged, and reversing the direction changes the sign. It is another way of saying that \"an $n$-form is the only thing that integrates naturally\".\n\nLet $f : M\\to N$ be a diffeomorphism between oriented $n$-dimensional smooth manifolds and $\\omega\\in\\Omega^{n}(N)$ integrable ($N$ compact, or $\\omega$ compactly supported). Then\n\n**if $f$ preserves orientation**\n\n$$\\int_{M}f^{\\ast}\\omega = \\int_{N}\\omega ;$$\n\n**if $f$ reverses orientation**\n\n$$\\int_{M}f^{\\ast}\\omega = -\\int_{N}\\omega .$$\n\nBoth cases combine as $\\int_{M}f^{\\ast}\\omega = \\varepsilon\\int_{N}\\omega$, where $\\varepsilon = +1$ means orientation-preserving and $-1$ orientation-reversing.\n\n**The classical change of variables is the coordinate version**: take $M,U\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $N = g(U)$ and $\\omega = F\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}y^{n}$; then $f^{\\ast}\\omega = (F\\circ g)\\det(\\partial g/\\partial x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$, and the formula becomes $\\int_{g(U)}F\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\int_{U}(F\\circ g)\\det(\\partial g/\\partial x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ — the only difference from a calculus textbook is the absence of absolute values.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a9faaa58',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The presence or absence of the absolute value is the presence or absence of orientation**: without orientation only densities integrate, and the change of variables carries $|\\det|$ (`prop.volume-density-well-defined`); with orientation the $n$-form carries its own sign, and the change of variables carries $\\det$. Two bookkeepings of one fact.\n\n**Why it holds only for $n$-forms**: a form with $k<n$ transforms by $k\\times k$ minors under a diffeomorphism, which does not match the $n$-dimensional volume element, so there is no \"change-of-variables formula for $k$-forms on a manifold\". To integrate a $k$-form one first chooses a $k$-dimensional submanifold and pulls back (`def.integral-form`, opening sentence).\n\n**The two pieces of the proof**: first, \"the integral is invariant under an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism\" (well-definedness of `def.integral-form`, i.e. the matching in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ of $\\det$ with the change-of-variables formula); second, a partition of unity (`prop.paracompact-partition-unity`) to split the whole into charts. Both pieces are proved elsewhere, so the theorem itself is an assembly.\n\n**⚠️ A diffeomorphism cannot be weakened to a smooth surjection**: that would require degrees and multiplicities ($\\int_{M}f^{\\ast}\\omega = \\deg(f)\\int_{N}\\omega$), and $\\varepsilon$ would become an integer rather than $\\pm1$. Only the diffeomorphism case is stated here.\n\n**⚠️ Choosing orientations**: if $M$ and $N$ each carry an orientation, $\\varepsilon$ is decided by whether $f$ preserves these two orientations; reversing the orientation of $N$ changes both $\\varepsilon$ and the right-hand side, and the identity survives. So \"orientation-preserving\" must be read relative to two fixed orientations.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ac72b300',
      },
    },

    'thm.gauss-equation': {
      statement: {
        en: "In an adapted frame $e_{3} = n$, split the second structure equation according to $k = 1,2,3$; the $k = 3$ term that remains is the curvature of the surface:\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2} = \\omega^{1}_{\\ 3}\\wedge\\omega^{2}_{\\ 3}.$$\n\nDefine the **Gauss curvature** $K$ by $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2} = K\\,\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}$; then\n\n$$K = \\det(c_{ij}) = \\det(h_{ij}) = \\frac{LN-M^{2}}{EG-F^{2}} .$$\n\nEquivalent index-free form (the component on the surface of the fact that $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is flat, with zero curvature):\n\n$$\\langle R(X,Y)Z, W\\rangle = II(Y,Z)II(X,W)-II(X,Z)II(Y,W).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3c0b920c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The hinge of this packet**: not a single partial derivative is expanded. The curvature form on $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ vanishes identically, and after splitting, the surviving term $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 3}\\wedge\\omega^{2}_{\\ 3}$ is the curvature — curvature equals the wedge of two extrinsic forms.\n\n**Coordinate cross-check**: from the commutation of mixed partials of $r_{ij} = \\Gamma^{k}_{\\ ij}r_{k}+h_{ij}n$ one derives the Gauss equation and writes it as $R_{ijkl} = h_{ik}h_{jl}-h_{il}h_{jk}$, $R_{1212} = LN-M^{2} = K(EG-F^{2})$.\n\nReading coefficients from $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{i}_{\\ j}$ is a shorter notation for the same system.\n\n⚠️ **Index order is a convention**: with $R_{ijkl} = \\langle R(e_{i}, e_{j})e_{k}, e_{l}\\rangle$, the vector form above reads $R_{ijkl} = h_{jk}h_{il}-h_{ik}h_{jl}$, with the opposite sign to the other index order;\n\nif one takes $R_{ijkl} = h_{ik}h_{jl}-h_{il}h_{jk}$, which corresponds to placing $W$ in the second pair, then $R_{1212} = LN-M^{2} = K(EG-F^{2})$ holds.\n\nAlign the convention on $R$ before using index formulas, or the signs are meaningless.\n\n**Only one independent component in dimension two**: the components $R_{ijkl}$ are pinned by symmetries, and in dimension two there is only $R_{1212}$, so the Gauss equation in dimension two is **one** equation — which is why surface theory is computable.\n\n**Classification of points**: $K > 0$ elliptic, $K = 0$ with not all principal curvatures zero parabolic, $K < 0$ hyperbolic; $k_{1} = k_{2}$ gives an umbilic point.\n\n⭐ **Corollary read off (theorema egregium)**: $K = \\mathrm{d}\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}/(\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2})$ involves only $\\omega^{1},\\omega^{2}$;\n\nand $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2}$ is uniquely solved from $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{1},\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{2}$ together with zero torsion (write $\\omega^{1}_{\\ 2} = a_{1}\\omega^{1}+a_{2}\\omega^{2}$; two equations give the two coefficients), so $K$ depends only on $I$ — the Gauss curvature is **intrinsic**.\n\nThis result has its own node `thm.theorema-egregium` in `diffgeom.intrinsic` (two proofs, via frames and via geodesic polar coordinates); by \"one concept, one node\" this packet does not create a second node and records the step here.\n\n**Area reading**: the Gauss map multiplies the area element by $\\det(\\mathrm{d}n) = \\det W = K$ (with sign), so $K = \\pm|n_{u}\\times n_{v}|/|r_{u}\\times r_{v}|$. This is the same quantity as $\\det h$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#30ce5c24',
      },
    },

    'def.vector-bundle': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it says**: it axiomatizes \"attach a vector space to every point, smoothly\" — the tangent, cotangent and tensor bundles are examples. The criterion is \"locally it looks like $U \\times \\mathbb{R}^{k}$\".\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold. A **smooth vector bundle of rank $k$** is a map $\\pi : E \\to M$ (**smoothness of $\\pi$ is not assumed**: it follows from a local trivialization $\\operatorname{pr}_{1}\\circ\\Phi = \\pi$ and smoothness of $\\operatorname{pr}_{1}$), together with:\n\n1. on each **fibre** $E_{p} := \\pi^{-1}(p)$ a structure of $k$-dimensional real vector space;\n2. **local trivializations**: every point has an open neighbourhood $U$ and a diffeomorphism $\\Phi : \\pi^{-1}(U) \\to U \\times \\mathbb{R}^{k}$ such that $\\Phi$ commutes with the projections, i.e. $\\operatorname{pr}_{1} \\circ \\Phi = \\pi$ and linear on each fibre.\n\n$E$ is the **total space**, $M$ the **base**, and $\\dim E = \\dim M + k$. Two trivializations $\\Phi_{\\alpha}, \\Phi_{\\beta}$ give on overlaps the **transition function**\n\n$$g_{\\alpha\\beta} : U_{\\alpha} \\cap U_{\\beta} \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{GL}(k,\\mathbb{R}),\\qquad g_{\\alpha\\beta}(p) := \\Phi_{\\alpha,p} \\circ \\Phi_{\\beta,p}^{-1} ,$$\n\nwhich is smooth and satisfies the **cocycle condition**\n\n$$g_{\\alpha\\alpha} = 1,\\qquad g_{\\alpha\\beta}\\,g_{\\beta\\gamma}\\,g_{\\gamma\\alpha} = 1 ,$$\n\nthe latter on $U_{\\alpha} \\cap U_{\\beta} \\cap U_{\\gamma}$. Conversely, an open cover with smooth $g_{\\alpha\\beta}$ satisfying the cocycle condition glues to a vector bundle.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aac15c17',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why \"locally trivial\" is not enough, and the cocycle is needed**: local triviality only says \"each piece looks like a product\"; what determines the bundle is **how the pieces are glued**, and the gluing is recorded in $g_{\\alpha\\beta}$.\n\nThe cocycle condition is necessary and sufficient for consistent gluing, and ties bundles to cohomology classes of 1-cocycles, $H^{1}(M;\\operatorname{GL}(k,\\mathbb{R}))$ — mentioned here without proof.\n\n**Examples**: $TM$ has rank $n$ (`def.tangent-bundle`); $M \\times \\mathbb{R}^{k}$ is the **trivial bundle**; the cotangent bundle $T^{\\ast}M$ and the $(r,s)$ tensor bundles (`def.tensor-field`);\n\nthe Möbius band over $S^{1}$ is a non-trivial rank $1$ bundle, while $S^{1} \\times \\mathbb{R}$ is trivial — locally the same, globally different.\n\n**Changing the structure group means changing the bundle**: replacing $\\operatorname{GL}(k,\\mathbb{R})$ by $\\operatorname{O}(k)$ gives Euclidean bundles (with a fibre metric), by $\\operatorname{GL}(k,\\mathbb{C})$ complex vector bundles, by $\\operatorname{GL}(k,\\mathbb{Z})$ flat bundles.\n\nThe structure group of $TM$ reduces to $\\operatorname{O}(n)$ thanks to \"a Riemannian metric always exists\" (`prop.bundle-metric`) — not automatic.\n\n**Rank $1$ is a line bundle**: the Möbius line bundle over $S^{1}$, the tautological line bundle over $\\mathbb{RP}^{1}$, and the Hopf line bundle over $S^{2}$ are the three standard examples. Isomorphism classes of **real** line bundles are detected by $w_{1} \\in H^{1}(M; \\mathbb{Z}/2)$ (this separates the trivial and Möbius bundles over $S^{1}$, while $H^{2}(S^{1}; \\mathbb{Z}) = 0$ cannot); **complex** line bundles use the first Chern class $c_{1} \\in H^{2}$. This is the entry point of topology into geometry.\n\n**Notation**: $\\pi$ denotes both a projection and the circle constant; in this graph $\\pi : E \\to M$ is always a projection; $\\operatorname{pr}_{1}$ is the projection of a product onto the first factor; $\\Phi_{\\alpha,p}$ is $\\Phi_{\\alpha}$ restricted to the fibre $E_{p}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce54e145',
      },
    },

    'def.differential-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it says**: a smooth map gives a linear map at every point, sending \"directions along curves\" to directions on the image — this is the derivative on manifolds; there is no subtraction on a manifold, and the only thing one can do is follow curves.\n\nLet $F : N \\to M$ be smooth and $p \\in N$. Define\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}F_{p} : T_{p}N \\longrightarrow T_{F(p)}M,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}F_{p}([\\gamma]) := [F \\circ \\gamma],$$\n\ni.e. the class of a curve through $p$, say $\\gamma$, goes to the class through $F(p)$ of $F \\circ \\gamma$. $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ is the **tangent map** (also **differential**) of $F$ at $p$.\n\nIn charts $(U,\\varphi)$ of $N$ and $(V,\\psi)$ of $M$, the matrix of $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ is the Jacobian of the local representation of $F$:\n\n$$\\Bigl[\\mathrm{d}F_{p}\\Bigr]_{\\varphi,\\psi} = \\Bigl(\\frac{\\partial (\\psi \\circ F \\circ \\varphi^{-1})^{i}}{\\partial x^{j}}(x)\\Bigr)_{i,j},\\qquad x = \\varphi(p).$$\n\n$\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ is linear and satisfies the **chain rule**\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}(G \\circ F)_{p} = \\mathrm{d}G_{F(p)} \\circ \\mathrm{d}F_{p},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}(\\operatorname{id}_{N})_{p} = \\operatorname{id}_{T_{p}N}.$$\n\nLetting $p$ run over $N$ gives a map of tangent bundles $\\mathrm{d}F : TN \\to TM$, $v \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}F_{\\pi(v)}(v)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#45e41c02',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Well-definedness is the chain rule**: changing the chart $\\varphi$ to $\\theta$, two curves through $p$ with the same velocity in the $\\varphi$-chart have the same velocity in the $\\theta$-chart (both equal the same Jacobian matrix times the same vector).\n\nClass is invariant, so $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ is defined — but **existence** rests entirely on smoothness of transition maps, which is where this node meets the previous two.\n\n**The derivation viewpoint is leaner**: as a derivation, $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}(v)$ is $\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}F_{p}(v)\\bigr)(f) = v(f \\circ F)$ — one composition, no curves mentioned. Both routes give the same linear map.\n\n**Why it is not called \"the derivative\"**: in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ is a matrix directly tied to difference quotients; on a manifold there is no subtraction, and $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ is merely a linear map **between tangent spaces**.\n\nIt is first-order information; under a change of coordinates one multiplies by a Jacobian on each side (one forward, one backward), so \"$\\mathrm{d}F$ is a tensor\" is right while \"$\\partial_{i}F$ is a tensor\" is not.\n\n**Meaning of the chain rule**: $M \\mapsto T_{p}M$ and $F \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ form a **functor** (the tangent functor). Thus on the layer of manifolds and smooth maps, tangent spaces automatically inherit all composition structure — which is also why $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$ holds formally.\n\n**Notation clash**: the same $\\mathrm{d}$ denotes the tangent map $\\mathrm{d}F_{p}$ and the exterior derivative $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$.\n\nThese are not two things: $\\mathrm{d}f$ is the tangent map $\\mathrm{d}f_{p} : T_{p}M \\to \\mathbb{R}$, i.e. a $1$-form (`def.exterior-derivative`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#522f199e',
      },
    },

    'prop.dimension-shift': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $0 \\to K \\to P \\xrightarrow{\\ \\pi\\ } M \\to 0$ be a short exact sequence of $R$-modules with $P$ **projective** (equivalently: $K$ is a syzygy of $M$). Then for every $n \\ge 1$ and every $R$-module $N$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}_{R}(M, N) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{R}(K, N), \\qquad \\operatorname{Tor}_{n+1}^{R}(M, N) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R}(K, N).$$\n\nIterating: for $k \\ge 0$, $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+k}_{R}(M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{R}(\\Omega^{k}M, N)$ ($n \\ge 1$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8f4361f7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (Ext side: resolve $N$ injectively).** Take an injective resolution of $N$: a quasi-isomorphism $N \\to I^{\\bullet}$. Since $I^{k}$ is injective, $\\operatorname{Hom}(-, I^{k})$ is exact, so applying it termwise to the short exact sequence yields a **short exact sequence of complexes** (in the opposite direction)\n\n$$0 \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(M, I^{\\bullet}) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(P, I^{\\bullet}) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(K, I^{\\bullet}) \\to 0 .$$\n\nTake the long exact cohomology sequence\n\n$$\\cdots \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, N) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(P, N) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(K, N) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\delta\\ } \\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M, N) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(P, N) \\to \\cdots$$\n\nSince $P$ is projective, $\\operatorname{Ext}^{k}(P, N) = 0$ for $k \\ge 1$, so every segment degenerates into an isomorphism: $\\delta$ is bijective, i.e. $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(K, N)$ ✓.\n\n**Tor side (dual: tensor the short exact sequence with $N$).** $\\otimes$ is right exact, so after tensoring $P\\otimes N \\to M\\otimes N$ is still surjective; the injectivity at the left end may fail, which is exactly where $\\operatorname{Tor}$ appears. Take the long exact sequence\n\n$$\\cdots \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_{n+1}(P, N) \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_{n+1}(M, N) \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_{n}(K, N) \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_{n}(P, N) \\to \\cdots$$\n\n$P$ projective $\\implies$ flat $\\implies$ $\\operatorname{Tor}_{k}(P, N) = 0$ for $k \\ge 1$, hence $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n+1}(M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Tor}_{n}(K, N)$ ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ **Why it is called a shift**: it trades \"the invariant of $M$ in degree $n+1$\" for \"the invariant of its syzygy in degree $n$\".\n\nThus any statement about $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}$ can be **lowered step by step** until it lands on $\\operatorname{Ext}^{0} = \\operatorname{Hom}$ or $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$ — the only levels where elements are visible.\n\n⚠️ The hypothesis \"$P$ projective\" cannot be dropped: the proof of $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M,N)\\cong\\operatorname{Ext}^n(K,N)$ uses exactly the vanishing of the $P$ term. For a general short exact sequence there is only a **five-term** long exact sequence, not an isomorphism.\n\n**Combined with syzygies**: $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(\\Omega^{n-1}M, N)$, and with \"classification of first-order extensions\" this turns higher Ext into \"extensions of $N$ by $\\Omega^{n-1}M$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cb0ceabd',
      },
    },

    'prop.injective-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category and $I \\in \\mathcal{A}$. The following are equivalent:\n\n1. $I$ is an **injective object**;\n2. every monomorphism $I \\rightarrowtail M$ admits a **retraction** $M \\to I$;\n3. the functor $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{A}}(-, I)$ is **exact**;\n4. every short exact sequence $0 \\to I \\to M \\to M'' \\to 0$ **splits**.\n\nMoreover: if $0 \\to M' \\to M \\to M'' \\to 0$ is exact and $M'$ is injective, then\n\n$M$ **injective** $\\iff M\\prime\\prime$ **injective**",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c4613da',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**A Zorn-dependent supplement (crucial)**: in the category of $R$-modules, the Bolzano–Weierstrass-style argument — \"$I$ is injective $\\iff$ for every ideal $J \\subseteq R$, every $J \\to I$ extends to $R \\to I$\" (Baer's criterion) — uses **Zorn's lemma** (take a maximal extendable submodule). So the convenience \"injectivity can be tested on ideals\" **costs the axiom of choice**; the corresponding statement in a general Grothendieck category tests on subobjects and needs no AC.\n\n⚠️ **The hypothesis of the second assertion**: when $M'$ is injective, $M$ injective $\\iff M''$ injective — this is \"extensions of injectives are injective\", which uses only short exact sequences and (4), and **does not need** $\\mathcal{A}$ to have enough injectives. Do not confuse it with the category-level hypothesis \"$\\mathcal{A}$ has enough injectives\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1ad9d105'
      },
    },

    'prop.flat-equivalences': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unit and $N$ an $R$-module. The following are equivalent:\n\n1. $N$ is flat, i.e. $(-) \\otimes_{R} N$ is exact;\n2. for every injection $M_{1} \\rightarrowtail M$, the map $M_{1} \\otimes_{R} N \\to M \\otimes_{R} N$ is injective;\n3. for every $R$-module $M$, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(M, N) = 0$;\n4. for every **ideal** $I \\subseteq R$, the multiplication map $I \\otimes_{R} N \\to N$, $a \\otimes n \\mapsto a n$, is injective (equivalently $I \\otimes_{R} N \\cong I N$);\n5. for every short exact sequence $0 \\to K \\to R^{n} \\to M \\to 0$ with finitely presented modules, the tensored sequence $0 \\to K \\otimes N \\to N^{n} \\to M \\otimes N \\to 0$ is still exact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6cf08af4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**1 and 2 are the same sentence** (right exactness is free). **2 and 3**: take a projective resolution of $M$; $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}$ measures exactly the injectivity lost after tensoring.\n\n**3 implies 4**: from $0 \\to I \\to R \\to R/I \\to 0$ and $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(R, N) = 0$ one gets the long exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(R/I, N) \\longrightarrow I \\otimes N \\longrightarrow N,$$\n\nso $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(R/I, N) = 0$ forces $I \\otimes N \\to N$ injective ✓.\n\n**4 implies 1**: it suffices to test on injections; first reduce to $M$ finitely generated ($(-)\\otimes N$ preserves filtered colimits), then induct on the number of generators, using the ideal case at each step — the full argument is in `thm.lambek` ✓.\n\n⭐ **Only condition 4 is computable**: ideals are usually far fewer than submodules, and the multiplication map $I \\otimes N \\to N$ can often be written explicitly. Over a PID ideals are principal, so the criterion is almost automatic (the source of \"torsion-free implies flat\").\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the five formulations differ only in \"how large a module tests flatness\"):\n\n- (1) $\\iff$ (2): **unfolding the definition**. (1) says $(-)\\otimes N$ is exact; exact = preserves kernels, and preserving kernels for injections is (2).\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): **this step needs the long exact sequence**. From the long exact sequence for $\\operatorname{Tor}$, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(M,N) = 0$ is equivalent to \"tensoring every short exact sequence $0 \\to M_{1} \\to M \\to M/M_{1} \\to 0$ stays exact\"; (2) gives only **injectivity**, and surjectivity needs one extra argument.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (2): **the reverse is almost free** ($\\operatorname{Tor}_{1} = 0$ returns injectivity directly), the cheapest step of the five.\n- (1) $\\implies$ (4): **only ideals are used**. $I \\hookrightarrow R$ is an injection, so apply (2).\n- (4) $\\implies$ (1): **the only direction with real cost**, using the algebraic version of a Baire-type argument: first shrink the test objects to finitely presented modules (this uses \"flat = preserves kernels after tensoring with finitely presented modules\"), then ascend to arbitrary modules. **Without this step flatness could only be tested on \"all modules\", which is unusable in practice.**\n- (5) $\\implies$ (2): **no extra hypothesis** ((5) is the truncation of (2) to finitely presented modules; check $K$ term by term).\n\n**In one sentence**: the watershed is (4) — **testing on ideals suffices**, which is why flatness is computable (and why over a Noetherian ring only finitely generated ideals need testing).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#616ab57a',
      },
    },

    'def.six-functors': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: **a 6-functor formalism = attach to every space six operations \"base change, pushforward, duality\", and require them to be compatible as in classical Poincaré / Serre duality**. The goal is single: turn \"cohomology\" into \"an inner product\", so that cohomology classes can be paired and the result computed. Classical geometry (sheaves, $\\ell$-adic cohomology) and solid geometry each have such a system of six functors.\n\nA **6-functor formalism** consists of:\n\n- for every scheme $X$ a closed symmetric monoidal triangulated category $D(X)$ (the tensor product $- \\otimes_X -$ has a right adjoint $\\operatorname{Hom}_X$);\n- for every map $f : X \\to Y$ a **pullback** $f^* : D(Y) \\to D(X)$, with right adjoint $f_*$ (pushforward);\n- for every map $f : X \\to Y$ (satisfying suitable conditions) a **proper-support pushforward** $f_! : D(X) \\to D(Y)$ (keeping only the part \"whose support lies in the compact fibres of $f$\", analogous to integration being meaningful only on compact regions), with right adjoint $f^!$ (duality).\n\nThat is six functors: $\\otimes,\\ \\operatorname{Hom},\\ f^*,\\ f_*,\\ f_!,\\ f^!$. They should satisfy:\n\n**(i)** $f$ proper $\\implies f_! = f_*$; **(ii)** $f$ étale $\\implies f^! = f^*$;\n\n**(iii) Verdier duality**: $f_* \\operatorname{Hom}_X(A, f^!B) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_Y(f_!A, B)$;\n\n**(iv) projection formula**: $f_!(A \\otimes_X f^*B) \\cong f_!A \\otimes_Y B$;\n\n**(v) proper base change**: in a pullback square $g^* f_! \\cong f'_! g'^*$;\n\n**(vi) Poincaré duality**: for smooth $f$ the map $f^!\\mathbf{1}_Y \\otimes_X f^*B \\to f^!B$ is an isomorphism and $f^!\\mathbf{1}_Y$ is invertible.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#85355810',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The first four functors $\\otimes, \\operatorname{Hom}, f^*, f_*$ are usually easy to construct; **all the difficulty is in $f_!$**.\n\nThe classical example is $\\ell$-adic sheaves $D_{\\mathrm{et}}(X, \\mathbb{Z}_\\ell)$ and condensed/solid sheaves.\n\n⭐ Once $f_!$ exists, $f^!$ is given by adjunction.\n\nConversely, $f_!$ is determined by \"open immersion + proper map + Nagata compactification\": for $f = \\bar f \\circ j$ one has $f_! = \\bar f_* j_!$.\n\nSo what really must be constructed is only **$j_!$ for open immersions** (see `prop.open-immersion-shriek`).\n\n⚠ In the condensed world, $f_!$ cannot be built from abstract $A$-modules alone — $j_!$ for an open immersion requires $\\prod_I A \\otimes_A A[1/f] \\cong \\prod_I A[1/f]$, which fails for abstract modules and holds for **solid** modules.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#84b5d7bc',
      },
    },

    'def.f-shriek': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: **$f_!$ is the composite of \"first push into the open part, then proper pushforward along the relative space\"**; its right adjoint $f^!$ is the dualizing complex functor. The name \"compact support\" comes from the intuition that $f_!$ remembers only the cohomology \"supported on compact subsets of each fibre\", just as an integral is computed only over bounded regions.\n\nLet $f : X \\to Y$ be a separated morphism of finite type, with corresponding diagram of adic spaces\n\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc} X_{\\mathrm{ad}} & \\xrightarrow{\\ j\\ } & X_{\\mathrm{ad}/Y} \\\\ & f_{\\mathrm{ad}} \\searrow & \\downarrow f_{\\mathrm{ad}/Y} \\\\ & & Y_{\\mathrm{ad}} \\end{array}$$\n\nwhere $j$ is an open immersion (an isomorphism when $f$ is proper). Define\n\n$$f_! := (f_{\\mathrm{ad}/Y})_* \\circ j_! : D(\\mathcal{O}_{X,\\blacksquare}) \\longrightarrow D(\\mathcal{O}_{Y,\\blacksquare}).$$\n\nIt preserves all direct sums, hence has a right adjoint $f^!$. The six functors are now complete.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bb1c7a85',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The definition is plain: **first push out as an open immersion, then proper pushforward along the relative adic space**.\n\nEverything rests on the existence of $j_!$ (`prop.open-immersion-shriek`).\n\n⭐ When $f$ is proper, $j$ is an isomorphism and $f_! = (f^{\\mathrm{ad}/Y})_*$, agreeing with $R\\Gamma$ — this is the source of \"for proper $f$, compact-support cohomology agrees with global sections\" in `thm.poincare-solid`.\n\n⚠ \"Preserves direct sums\" is the key to the existence of $f^!$: $j_!$, as a left adjoint, preserves colimits, and $(f^{\\mathrm{ad}/Y})_*$ is, locally on affines, a limit of finitely many forgetful functors, hence also preserves direct sums.\n\n⭐ General **existence** is in `thm.f-shriek-existence` (still marked skeleton: conditional on $j_{!}$ for open immersions).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0fd76705',
      },
    },

    'def.exact-couple': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category and $D = \\bigoplus D^{p,q}$, $E = \\bigoplus E^{p,q}$ bigraded objects. An **exact couple** is a triple $(D, E, i, j, k)$ forming the \"triangle\"\n\n$$D \\xrightarrow{\\ i\\ } D \\xrightarrow{\\ j\\ } E \\xrightarrow{\\ k\\ } D ,$$\n\nwhere $i, j, k$ are bihomogeneous morphisms and exact at every position:\n\n$$\\ker i = \\operatorname{im} k, \\qquad \\ker j = \\operatorname{im} i, \\qquad \\ker k = \\operatorname{im} j .$$\n\nThen $d := j \\circ k : E \\to E$ satisfies $d^{2} = j (k j) k = 0$ (because $kj = 0$ follows from exactness). The **derived couple** is $(D^{\\prime}, E^{\\prime}, i^{\\prime}, j^{\\prime}, k^{\\prime})$, where\n\n$$D^{\\prime} := \\operatorname{im} i, \\qquad E^{\\prime} := H(E, d) = \\ker d \\big/ \\operatorname{im} d ,$$\n\n$$i^{\\prime} := i|_{D^{\\prime}}, \\qquad j^{\\prime}\\bigl(i(x)\\bigr) := \\bigl[\\,j(x)\\,\\bigr], \\qquad k^{\\prime}\\bigl([e]\\bigr) := k(e),$$\n\nwith brackets $[\\,\\cdot\\,]$ denoting the class in $H(E, d)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a7740fb8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Three well-definedness checks** (the whole difficulty of \"the derived couple is again an exact couple\"):\n\n- $j^{\\prime}$: if $i(x) = i(x^{\\prime})$, then $x - x^{\\prime} \\in \\ker i = \\operatorname{im} k$, hence $j(x - x^{\\prime}) \\in j(\\operatorname{im} k) = 0$ (because $kj = 0$) — independent of representatives.\n\nAlso $j(x)$ is a $d$-cocycle: $d\\, j(x) = j k j(x) = 0$.\n\n- $k^{\\prime}$: if $e \\in \\ker d$ (i.e. $j k e = 0$), then $k e \\in \\ker j = \\operatorname{im} i = D^{\\prime}$, so $k^{\\prime}$ lands in $D^{\\prime}$;\n\nif $e \\in \\operatorname{im} d$ (i.e. $e = j k(e_{0})$), then $k e = k j k(e_{0}) = 0$ — independent of representatives.\n\n- $i^{\\prime}$: $i(D^{\\prime}) = i^{2}(D) \\subseteq i(D) = D^{\\prime}$.\n\nChecking the three exactness conditions once more shows the derived couple is again an exact couple. Hence one can **iterate indefinitely**: $E_{1} := E$, $E_{r+1} := H(E_{r}, d_{r})$. This is precisely \"computing a spectral sequence page by page\".\n\n**Bidegrees.** The most common case comes from a **filtered complex**: the bidegree of $i$ is $(-1, 1)$, of $j$ is $(0, 0)$, of $k$ is $(1, 0)$.\n\nAfter one derivation the bidegree of $j$ becomes $(1, -1)$ — because $j^{\\prime}$ acts on $i(x)$ but reads off $j(x)$, and $i$ has already shifted the degree by one step.\n\n$i$ and $k$ are unchanged by derivation.\n\nInductively, the bidegree of $j_{r}$ is $(r - 1,\\ 1 - r)$, so the bidegree of $d_{r} = j_{r} k_{r}$ is $(r,\\ 1 - r)$.\n\n⭐ **Why it is needed**: constructing a spectral sequence directly requires a **filtration** first. The exact couple removes this dependence — a single exact triangle suffices to produce a spectral sequence. Spectral sequences of Bockstein, homotopy groups and Gysin type have no other route.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9836f0c9',
      },
    },

    'prop.rhom-dual-tensor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M, N$ be **discrete** abelian groups and $T = \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$. Then\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, T),\\ N\\bigr) \\cong M \\otimes^{\\mathbf{L}}_{\\mathbb{Z}} N[-1].$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#61047a9f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it says.** The round trip \"dualize, then compute RHom into a target\" is turned into a **derived tensor product**, with the degree **lowered by one** ($[-1]$). Thus \"computing Ext of a dual\" becomes \"computing a tensor product\" — and the latter can be done on discrete groups by a free resolution.\n\n**Proof.**\n\nUse the short exact sequence $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\to T \\to 0$.\n\nBy the two vanishing theorems above, $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{R}, N) = 0$, hence $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(T, N) \\cong N[-1]$.\n\nSubstitute into $\\operatorname{RHom}(M, T)$, unfold layer by layer, and use the derived tensor computation in the free case to obtain the claim.\n\n⭐ This explains the role of the \"circle\" $T$: in the derived world $T$ is **equivalent to a degree shift** ($\\operatorname{RHom}(-, T)$ sends a discrete group to its own $[-1]$). This is why Pontryagin duality can be written with RHom in the next section.\n\n**Proof (build the map, then check it is an isomorphism in the free case).**\n\n- **Build the map**: concatenate three isomorphisms\n\n$$M \\otimes^{\\mathbf{L}}_{\\mathbb{Z}} N[-1] \\;\\cong\\; M \\otimes^{\\mathbf{L}}_{\\mathbb{Z}} \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}[-1], N) \\;\\cong\\; M \\otimes^{\\mathbf{L}}_{\\mathbb{Z}} \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(T, N) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, T),\\ N\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere the first comes from \"$\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z}, N) = N$ for discrete groups\", the second from $0\\to\\mathbb{Z}\\to\\mathbb{R}\\to T\\to 0$ (with $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{R}, N) = 0$, hence $\\operatorname{RHom}(T,N)\\cong\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z},N)[-1] = N[-1]$), and the third is the adjunction between tensor and internal Hom.\n\n- **Check the isomorphism**: it suffices to check for $M = \\mathbb{Z}\\cdot E$ (free).\n\nThen \"$\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot E, N) = N^{E}$ in the discrete case\" gives $\\operatorname{RHom}(M, T) = T^{E}$, while $M\\otimes^{\\mathbf{L}}N = N\\cdot E$;\n\nthe problem reduces to proving $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(T^{E}, N) \\cong N\\cdot E[-1]$.\n\n- When $E$ is **finite**: the triangle $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z},N)\\to\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{R},N)\\to\\operatorname{RHom}(T,N)\\to$ together with $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{R},N)=0$ gives $\\operatorname{RHom}(T,N)\\cong N[-1]$; take the $E$-th power.\n- When $E$ is **arbitrary**: write it as the filtered colimit $E = \\varinjlim E'$ of finite subsets and propagate the conclusion with the Breen–Deligne spectral sequence plus exactness of filtered colimits, where the needed step is only $H^{q}\\bigl((T^{s})^{E}\\times S, N\\bigr) = \\varinjlim_{E'} H^{q}\\bigl((T^{s})^{E'}\\times S, N\\bigr)$ — supplied by Dyckhoff and by \"cohomology with constant coefficients commutes with filtered limits of compact Hausdorff spaces\".\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f8a4c827',
      },
    },

    'lem.ext-stonean-section': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M, N$ be condensed abelian groups and $S$ a **Stonean space**. Write $M \\cdot S := M \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} \\mathbb{Z}[S]$. Then for all $n \\ge 0$\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)(S) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M \\cdot S,\\ N).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8edc857b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it does.** The left side is \"the sections of the Ext sheaf over $S$\", the right side is \"an ordinary Ext computed out\". It trades the **sheaf** Ext for a **single** Ext — so \"computing an Ext sheaf\" becomes \"computing one concrete derived Hom\".\n\n**Why Stonean is enough.** Over a Stonean space $S$, the group $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is **projective**, hence $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}[S], -)$ is exact. Apply this exact functor to both sides of the natural isomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M \\cdot S,\\ N) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z}[S],\\ \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)\\bigr)$$\n\nand the conclusion follows: the right side is \"sections of an internal Hom\", the left side is \"tensor first, then Hom\".\n\n⚠️ The key hypothesis is **Stonean** (extremally disconnected), not merely compact Hausdorff — extremal disconnectedness is exactly the source of projectivity of $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$.\n\n⭐ This is the **connecting port** for the spectral sequence below: the Breen–Deligne decomposition expands Ext into free objects, and the Ext of each term is brought back from a sheaf computation to an ordinary one by this lemma.\n\n**Proof.** $S$ Stonean $\\implies$ $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is **projective**, hence $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}[S], -)$ is **exact**.\n\nFor the natural isomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M \\cdot S,\\ N) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z}[S],\\ \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)\\bigr)$$\n\n(the adjoint form of $M \\cdot S = M \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} \\mathbb{Z}[S]$), take right derived functors on both sides: the left gives $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M\\cdot S, N)$;\n\nthe right, since $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}[S],-)$ is exact, passes through the derived functor and gives $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z}[S], \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M,N)\\bigr)$, which by definition is $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M,N)(S)$.\n\nCompare the two sides.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1c9a45ed',
      },
    },

    'def.de-rham-complex': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it says**: the exterior derivative satisfies $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$, so $k$-forms line up in a \"chain\"; the part left after quotienting closed forms by exact ones is cohomology — it measures \"how many closed forms are falsely closed\".\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold and $\\Omega^{k}(M)$ the space of $k$-forms. The exterior derivative on a manifold gives the **de Rham complex**\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow \\Omega^{0}(M) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mathrm{d}\\ } \\Omega^{1}(M) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mathrm{d}\\ } \\Omega^{2}(M) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mathrm{d}\\ } \\cdots$$\n\nFrom $\\mathrm{d}^{2} = 0$ one gets $\\operatorname{im}\\mathrm{d} \\subseteq \\ker\\mathrm{d}$. Write the spaces of **closed forms** and **exact forms** as\n\n$$Z^{k}(M) := \\ker\\bigl(\\mathrm{d} : \\Omega^{k}(M) \\to \\Omega^{k+1}(M)\\bigr),$$\n\n$$B^{k}(M) := \\operatorname{im}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d} : \\Omega^{k-1}(M) \\to \\Omega^{k}(M)\\bigr),$$\n\n(with $\\Omega^{-1} := 0$, so $B^{0} = 0$). The **de Rham cohomology group** is the quotient\n\n$$H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M) := Z^{k}(M) / B^{k}(M).$$\n\nGiven a smooth map $F : N \\to M$, pullback gives a linear map $F^{\\ast} : H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M) \\to H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(N)$ (because $F^{\\ast}\\mathrm{d} = \\mathrm{d}F^{\\ast}$, see `prop.pullback-form-manifold`), and $(G \\circ F)^{\\ast} = F^{\\ast} \\circ G^{\\ast}$.\n\nThus $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}$ is a **functor** on the category of smooth manifolds.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4bbccc33',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**What it measures**: $H^{k} = 0$ means \"closed implies exact\"; $H^{k} \\ne 0$ says there is a closed but non-exact form.\n\nOn $\\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ the angle form $\\frac{x\\,\\mathrm{d}y - y\\,\\mathrm{d}x}{x^{2}+y^{2}}$ is closed but not exact; its class generates $H^{1} \\cong \\mathbb{R}$ and measures \"the number of turns around the origin\".\n\n**The de Rham theorem (stated only, not proved)**: $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M) \\cong H^{k}_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb{R})$ — what is computed analytically (forms plus derivative) equals what is computed purely topologically (singular chains plus boundary).\n\nThis stitches differential geometry to algebraic topology and is the interface with the category/homology region of this graph.\n\n**Finite dimension**: when $M$ is compact, each $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)$ is finite-dimensional and vanishes for $k > \\dim M$. So cohomology is a finite set of numbers (Betti numbers). Not proved here.\n\n**Locally it always vanishes**: every point of any manifold has a contractible neighbourhood, so by the Poincaré lemma $H^{k} = 0$ for $k \\ge 1$. Hence cohomology is **purely global** information — which is why it is more useful than local differential invariants.\n\n**$H^{0}$ must be read separately**: $Z^{0} = \\ker(\\mathrm{d} : C^{\\infty}(M) \\to \\Omega^{1}(M))$ consists of locally constant functions, and $B^{0} = 0$, so $H^{0}$ counts connected components (`thm.de-rham-h0`).\n\nOnly for $k \\ge 1$ does one need a Poincaré-lemma-type argument.\n\n**It is not \"homology\" in the sense of a homotopy invariant**: $H^{k}$ is **co**homology (forms and pullback), dual to homology with chains and boundary. It is called cohomology because pullback is contravariant; the two are proved isomorphic in the de Rham theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b1c50ce7',
      },
    },

    'prop.huber-subring-recovery': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: **$A^+$ looks like extra data, but it is determined by the spectrum itself** — \"integrally closed subring $A^+$\" corresponds bijectively to \"the region $\\bigcap U_{1,f}$ of the spectrum, viewed as a subset $U$\". So $(A,A^+)$ and \"the ring $A$ together with a region of its spectrum\" are the same thing; this is the step that translates ring theory into geometry.\n\nWrite $\\operatorname{Spv}(A) := \\operatorname{Spa}(A, \\widetilde{\\mathbb{Z}})$ for all equivalence classes of valuations on $A$ (taking the **smallest** $A^+$, the integral closure of $\\mathbb{Z}$ in $A$ — it sits in the unit ball of every valuation; the smaller $A^+$ is, the fewer points are filtered out, so no valuation is discarded). Then\n\n$$A^+ \\longmapsto U = \\operatorname{Spa}(A, A^+) = \\bigcap_{f \\in A^+} U_{1,f} \\subseteq \\operatorname{Spv}(A),$$\n\ngives a **bijection** between \"integrally closed subrings $A^+ \\subseteq A$\" and \"regions of the form $\\bigcap_{f \\in I} U_{1,f}$, i.e. subsets $U$\", with inverse\n\n$$U \\longmapsto A^+ = \\{\\, f \\in A : \\forall x \\in U,\\ |f(x)| \\le 1 \\,\\}.$$\n\nIn particular $A^+ = \\{f \\in A : \\forall x \\in \\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+),\\ |f(x)| \\le 1\\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b883b938',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This says: **$A^+$ is not extra data but the \"unit-ball sections\" over the spectrum**.\n\nIn other words, $(A,A^+)$ and \"$A$ together with a clopen subset of its spectrum\" are the same thing — exactly where ring theory becomes geometry.\n\n⚠ In general $A^+$ cannot be an arbitrary subring: it must be integrally closed.\n\nCounterexample: $A = \\mathbb{Z}[T]$, $A^+ = \\mathbb{Z}[T^2]$ is not integrally closed ($T$ is integral over $\\mathbb{Z}[T^2]$), and the corresponding subset is not the $\\operatorname{Spa}$ of any **integrally closed** subring (the inverse map returns the integral closure $\\widetilde{\\mathbb{Z}[T^{2}]} \\ne \\mathbb{Z}[T^{2}]$).\n\n⭐ Why it is **smallest**: an integrally closed subring always contains $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot 1$, hence contains $\\widetilde{\\mathbb{Z}}$ (integral elements are closed under integrally closed subrings); and an integral element satisfies $|a| \\le 1$ under **every** valuation — if $|a| > 1$, then the left side of $a^{n} + c_{n-1}a^{n-1} + \\cdots + c_{0} = 0$ ($c_{i} \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, so $|c_{i}| \\le 1$) is dominated by $a^{n}$ and cannot equal $0$. Hence it lies in every unit ball, and choosing it filters out the fewest points.\n\n📌 The proof uses existence of valuation rings (Zariski's lemma): if $f \\notin A^+$, take a prime ideal in $A^+[1/f]$ and then a valuation ring dominating it, producing a point with $|f| > 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#98012090',
      },
    },

    'def.adic-spectrum': {
      statement: {
        en: "In plain words: **collect all ways of \"measuring the size\" of elements of a ring $A$, and you get the adic spectrum**.\n\nEach point is an \"absolute value\" (valuation), and $A^+$ serves to filter out those points that measure some bounded element as $>1$.\n\nThis is the \"with sizes\" version of $\\operatorname{Spec}$ — $\\operatorname{Spec}$ records only \"which ideals have elements equal to zero\", while $\\operatorname{Spa}$ also records \"how small\".\n\nFirst, terminology. A **valuation** is a \"size-measuring\" function $|\\cdot| : A \\to \\Gamma \\cup \\{0\\}$ ($\\Gamma$ a totally ordered abelian group written multiplicatively, think $\\mathbb{R}_{>0}$) satisfying\n\n$$|0| = 0, \\quad |1| = 1, \\quad |xy| = |x|\\,|y|, \\quad |x + y| \\le \\max(|x|, |y|).$$\n\nTwo valuations $|\\cdot|, |\\cdot|_1$ are **equivalent** if there is an order-preserving group isomorphism with $|x|_1 = \\sigma(|x|)$ for all $x$ — that is, \"they order the elements of $A$ by size in exactly the same way\", only the \"ruler\" changes.\n\n$\\operatorname{Spa}$ takes **equivalence classes** of valuations (so the same point is not counted twice because of a different ruler).\n\nIntuition: $|x|$ is \"the distance from $x$ to $0$\", and $|x| \\le 1$ means \"$x$ is bounded\";\n\nthe $p$-adic absolute value $|p^{n}u| = p^{-n}$ ($u$ coprime to $p$) is the standard example.\n\nLet $(A, A^+)$ be a discrete Huber pair. The **adic spectrum**\n\n$$\\operatorname{Spa}(A, A^+) := \\bigl\\{\\, |\\cdot| : A \\to \\Gamma \\cup \\{0\\} \\ \\big|\\ |A^+| \\le 1 \\,\\bigr\\} \\big/ \\sim$$\n\nis the set of equivalence classes of valuations on $A$ that are bounded on $A^+$ — that is, satisfy $\\le 1$ (every element of $A^+$ is bounded). The topology is generated by **rational open sets**: for $f, g_1, \\ldots, g_n \\in A$,\n\n$$U\\!\\left(\\tfrac{g_1, \\ldots, g_n}{f}\\right) := \\bigl\\{\\, x : |g_i(x)| \\le |f(x)| \\ne 0\\ \\forall i\\, \\bigr\\}.$$\n\nThese rational open sets form a basis, and $\\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)$ is a **spectral space** (homeomorphic to the prime spectrum of some ring; equivalently: compact, **sober**, and compact open sets form a basis).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c216c22',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A valuation satisfies the **strong triangle inequality** $|x+y| \\le \\max(|x|,|y|)$, the hallmark of non-archimedean geometry; replacing it by $\\le |x| + |y|$ gives the archimedean side (complex geometry), a different theory.\n\n⭐ Rational open sets are the regions defined by \"$|g_i| \\le |f|$\" — intuitively the locus where \"$g_i/f$ is bounded\".\n\nThey are both open and compact (quasicompact), which is exactly what makes adic spaces handier than schemes: affine open sets carry their own compactness.\n\n⚠ $\\operatorname{Spa}(A,A^+)$ is in general **not** Hausdorff; it is spectral (compact, **sober**, **compact open sets** form a basis, and compact open sets are closed under finite intersections).\n\nSo metric-space intuition does not apply.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3b1020a5',
      },
    },

    'prop.rhom-banach-discrete-zero': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M$ be a **finite-dimensional real Banach space** and $N$ a **discrete** abelian group. Then\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M,\\ N) = 0.$$\n\nMore generally: if $V$ is an arbitrary $\\mathbb{R}$-module and $N$ is discrete, then $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(V, N) = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d8444edb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Intuition**: $\\mathbb{R}$ is **divisible** ($\\mathbb{R}$'s elements all admit roots of every order, and are divisible by every $n$), while a discrete group has no such divisibility. **Divisibility collapses completely in the direction into a discrete group** — hence all higher Hom groups (and the zeroth) out of a real vector space vanish.\n\n**Proof.** Use the spectral sequence above: $M^{s}$ (a power of the finite-dimensional $M$) is **contractible** (all vector spaces are), and $H^{q}(M^{s} \\times S, N) \\cong H^{q}(S, N)$, so $E^{p,q}_{1}$ depends only on $S$; the limit term matches the \"constant decomposition\" case and the computation vanishes throughout.\n\n⭐ **Use**: any Ext problem with a real vector space as source and a discrete group as target is immediately zero — one of the key components of the later step \"locally compact abelian groups have only $\\operatorname{Ext}^{0}, \\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$\".\n\n**Proof.** Use the spectral sequence above (with $S$ Stonean); to show the limit vanishes it suffices to show every $E^{p,q}_{1}$ vanishes.\n\n- $M^{s_{p,i}}$ is a (possibly large) power of a finite-dimensional real vector space, **convex $\\implies$ contractible**;\n- and $M^{s_{p,i}}\\times S \\to S$ is a homotopy equivalence, with $M^{s_{p,i}}\\times S$ **locally compact Hausdorff** and $N$ **discrete**, so Dyckhoff (locally compact with discrete coefficients: condensed = sheaf cohomology) and \"sheaf cohomology is a homotopy invariant\" give\n\n$$H^{q}\\bigl(M^{s_{p,i}}\\times S,\\ N\\bigr) \\cong H^{q}(S,\\ N).$$\n\n- The right side **involves no $M$ at all**.\n\nHence this family of $E_{1}$ terms is independent of $M$: substituting $M = 0$ gives the same family of $E_{1}$ terms, and $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(0, N) = 0$, so the spectral sequence converges to $0$;\n\nby convergence ($E_{1}$ terms determine the limit), the limit term for general $M$ is also $0$.\n\n∎\n\n**The general $\\mathbb{R}$-module case**: $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(V, N) = \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(V, \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{R}, N)) = \\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(V, 0) = 0$ (with $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{R}, N) = 0$ obtained from the finite-dimensional case by taking a colimit).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#53eee257',
      },
    },

    'prop.breen-deligne-ss-stonean': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $M, N$ be condensed abelian groups and $S$ a **Stonean space**. Then there is a natural spectral sequence\n\n$$E^{p,q}_{1} = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r_{p}} H^{q}\\bigl(M^{s_{p,i}} \\times S,\\ N\\bigr) \\implies \\operatorname{Ext}^{p+q}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)(S).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5bf7335e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Construction.**\n\nBase-change the Breen–Deligne decomposition $F(M)^{\\bullet}$ along $S$ (tensor with $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$).\n\nSince $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is flat, $F(M)^{\\bullet} \\cdot S \\to M \\cdot S$ is still a quasi-isomorphism;\n\nthen apply the section formula over Stonean spaces to replace $\\operatorname{Ext}(M \\cdot S, N)$ by $\\operatorname{Ext}(M, N)(S)$.\n\nTake the standard spectral sequence of the \"degree filtration on the decomposition\"; its $E_{1}$ terms are the Ext of the individual free objects.\n\n**How to read the shape of $E_{1}$**: $M^{s_{p,i}}$ is \"the product of $s_{p,i}$ copies of $M$\", $M^{s_{p,i}} \\times S$ pairs it with the test space $S$; $H^{q}(-, N)$ is condensed cohomology on that space.\n\nThat is: **convert Ext into the cohomology of a batch of spaces**, and the cohomology of spaces can be killed by the acyclicity theorems of §8.1–8.2.\n\n⭐ **This is the computational path**: \"to compute Ext, first expand into a spectral sequence, then kill each entry's cohomology according to the type of space (Stone / compact Hausdorff / locally compact) using acyclicity theorems\". Every computational theorem after §8.3 is a special case of this spectral sequence.\n\n**Proof.** Take a Breen–Deligne decomposition of $M$, $F(M)^{\\bullet} \\twoheadrightarrow M$, base-change along the Stonean space $S$ (tensor with $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$), and apply to the functor $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(-, N)$ the standard spectral sequence of \"resolve first, then evaluate on the test space\":\n\n$$E^{p,q}_{1} = \\operatorname{Ext}^{q}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(F(M)^{p}\\cdot S,\\ N\\bigr) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\operatorname{Ext}^{p+q}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(F(M)^{\\bullet}\\cdot S,\\ N\\bigr).$$\n\n- **Left side**: $F(M)^{p}\\cdot S = \\bigoplus_{i} \\mathbb{Z}\\cdot M^{s_{p,i}} \\otimes \\mathbb{Z}[S] = \\bigoplus_{i} \\mathbb{Z}\\cdot\\bigl(M^{s_{p,i}}\\times S\\bigr)$, so \"condensed cohomology = Ext of free objects\" gives $\\operatorname{Ext}^{q}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot(M^{s_{p,i}}\\times S), N) = H^{q}\\bigl(M^{s_{p,i}}\\times S,\\ N\\bigr)$.\n- **Right side**: $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$ is **flat**, so $F(M)^{\\bullet}\\cdot S \\to M\\cdot S$ is still a quasi-isomorphism; then the section formula over Stonean ($\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M\\cdot S, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M,N)(S)$) gives $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(F(M)^{\\bullet}\\cdot S, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M,N)(S)$.\n\nCombining the two sides gives the desired spectral sequence. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bc147894',
      },
    },

    'lem.witt-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $N$ be a finite-dimensional non-degenerate symmetric bilinear space with $\\operatorname{char}K\\ne2$, and let $A\\subseteq N$ be a non-degenerate subspace. If $\\sigma:A\\to N$ is an isometry onto $\\sigma(A)$, then $\\sigma$ extends to an isometry of $N$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c113fd81',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is induction on $\\dim A$: choose a non-isotropic $a\\in A$, write $A$ as $A_0\\perp\\operatorname{span}(a)$, extend $\\sigma|_{A_0}$ by induction, then use one reflection to move the image point onto $\\sigma(a)$, and glue orthogonally.\n\nThe proof is on the edge `imp.witt-extension`; it is the direct source of Witt's cancellation theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fde3492f',
      },
    },

    'prop.divisible-quotient': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $D$ is a divisible group and $H \\subseteq D$ is a subgroup, then $D/H$ is divisible.\n\n(The same argument works for divisible modules over any ring; over $\\mathbb{Z}$, \"divisible\" is \"injective\", so this also shows that a quotient of an injective $\\mathbb{Z}$-module is still injective.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#85943494',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: write $\\pi : D \\to D/H$.\n\nGiven $\\bar{x} \\in D/H$ and $n \\ne 0$, choose a representative $x$; since $D$ is divisible there is $y$ with $n y = x$; then $n \\bar{y} = \\overline{n y} = \\bar{x}$ ✓.\n\nHence multiplication by $n \\cdot$ is surjective on $D/H$, so $D/H$ is divisible ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⚠️ This holds only for **quotients**: a subgroup of a divisible group need not be divisible ($\\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q}$: $\\mathbb{Q}$ is divisible, $\\mathbb{Z}$ is not).\n\n⭐ Two contrasts: among $\\mathbb{Z}$-modules the **quotient** of an injective object is still injective (this item), while its **submodule** need not be ($\\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q}$).\n\nProjective modules behave badly on both sides: both its quotient ($\\mathbb{Z}/2$ of $\\mathbb{Z}$) and its submodule (the ideal $(x, y) \\subseteq k[x, y]$) can fail to be projective.\n\n📌 Over a general ring be careful: divisibility is always closed under quotients (the elementwise argument), but \"injective is closed under quotients\" follows from \"divisible = injective\" only over a PID such as $\\mathbb{Z}$, and must not be taken as a general fact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f6616f49',
      },
    },

    'prop.flat-domain-torsionfree': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be an **integral domain** and $M$ a flat $R$-module. Then $M$ is **torsion-free**: for $r \\ne 0$ and $m \\in M$, $r m = 0$ implies $m = 0$. Equivalently, multiplication by $r$, $M \\xrightarrow{\\;\\cdot r\\;} M$, is injective.\n\nConversely, if $R$ is a **PID**, then torsion-free $\\iff$ flat. Over a general domain \"torsion-free\" is strictly weaker than \"flat\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a29960a2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (forward direction)**: take $0 \\ne r \\in R$.\n\nSince $R$ is a domain, $r$ is not a zero divisor, so $0 \\to R \\xrightarrow{\\;\\cdot r\\;} R$ is exact (zero kernel).\n\nSince $M$ is flat, tensoring this sequence gives the exact sequence $0 \\to M \\xrightarrow{\\;\\cdot r\\;} M$ — that is, multiplication by $r$ is injective ✓.\n\n**Converse over a PID**: finitely generated submodules of a torsion-free module are free (finitely generated torsion-free over a PID is free), and $M$ is the filtered colimit of its finitely generated submodules; free modules are flat, and $(-)\\otimes$ preserves filtered colimits, so $M$ is flat ✓.\n\n⚠️ **The converse fails over a general domain**: take $R = k[x, y]$ and $I = (x, y)$.\n\n$I$ is an ideal of $R$, and visibly torsion-free as an $R$-module; but it is not flat — $x, y$ is a regular sequence, and exactness of the Koszul complex gives $x \\otimes y - y \\otimes x \\ne 0$ (in $I \\otimes_{R} I$), while its image under $I \\otimes I \\to I$ is $xy - yx = 0$, so the multiplication map is not injective.\n\n📌 In one sentence: **\"torsion-free\" is a divisibility property tested ideal by ideal, while \"flat\" demands it for ideals all at once**. Over a domain the latter is stronger, and over a PID the two coincide.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76deecd4',
      },
    },

    'prop.scalar-extension-adjunction': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every unital ring homomorphism $f : R \\to S$ there is an adjunction\n\n$$S \\otimes_{R} - \\;\\dashv\\; f_{\\ast} : R\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod} \\rightleftarrows S\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod},$$\n\nthat is, for every left $R$-module $M$ and left $S$-module $P$ a natural isomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{S}\\bigl(S \\otimes_{R} M,\\ P\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{R}\\bigl(M,\\ f_{\\ast} P\\bigr), \\qquad g \\longmapsto \\bigl(m \\mapsto g(1 \\otimes m)\\bigr).$$\n\nThe unit is $\\eta_{M} : M \\to f_{\\ast}(S \\otimes_{R} M)$, $m \\mapsto 1 \\otimes m$; the counit is $\\varepsilon_{P} : S \\otimes_{R} f_{\\ast} P \\to P$, $s \\otimes p \\mapsto s p$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a84a8a96',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: an $S$-linear map $g : S \\otimes_{R} M \\to P$ is first of all $R$-linear, hence determined by $m \\mapsto g(1 \\otimes m)$ (because $s \\otimes m = s(1 \\otimes m)$);\n\nconversely, given an $R$-linear map $\\varphi : M \\to P$, the $S$-balanced formula $(s, m) \\mapsto s \\varphi(m)$ is well defined, and the universal property gives a unique $S$-linear map $S \\otimes_{R} M \\to P$.\n\nThe two directions are inverse, and nothing is chosen in the formula, so it is natural ✓.\n\nTriangle identities: $\\varepsilon_{S \\otimes M} \\circ (S \\otimes \\eta_{M}) = \\mathrm{id}$ and $f_{\\ast}\\varepsilon_{P} \\circ \\eta_{f_{\\ast}P} = \\mathrm{id}$, checked on pure tensors (or elements of $M$) and extended linearly ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **The precise meaning of \"most free\"**: every map that sends $M$ into some $S$-module and is compatible with the $R$-action factors uniquely through $1 \\otimes -$. So $S \\otimes_{R} M$ is the standard answer to \"the image of $M$ in the category of $S$-modules\".\n\n**Immediate consequences**: $S \\otimes_{R} -$ is right exact and preserves colimits (left adjoint); $f_{\\ast}$ is left exact (right adjoint), in fact exact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4970d276',
      },
    },

    'prop.tensor-right-exact': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a unital ring and $N$ a left $R$-module. The functor $(-) \\otimes_{R} N : \\mathbf{Mod}\\text{-}R \\to \\mathbf{Ab}$ is **right exact**: every short exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow M_{1} \\xrightarrow{\\;f\\;} M \\xrightarrow{\\;g\\;} M_{2} \\longrightarrow 0$$\n\ngives an exact sequence\n\n$$M_{1} \\otimes_{R} N \\xrightarrow{\\;f \\otimes 1\\;} M \\otimes_{R} N \\xrightarrow{\\;g \\otimes 1\\;} M_{2} \\otimes_{R} N \\longrightarrow 0 .$$\n\nMoreover the following are equivalent: (i) $(-) \\otimes_{R} N$ is **exact**; (ii) for every injection $M_{1} \\rightarrowtail M$, the map $M_{1} \\otimes N \\to M \\otimes N$ is still injective; (iii) $N$ is **flat**.\n\n(The right-hand $\\to 0$ is always exact; only the left $0$ can fail.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a9f1778f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (adjunction route, fastest)**: by `thm.hom-tensor-adjunction-mod`, $(-) \\otimes_{R} N \\dashv \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(N, -)$ (the target is $\\mathbf{Ab}$, and the right adjoint takes $\\mathbb{Z}$-linear homs).\n\nA left adjoint preserves all colimits, and a cokernel is the colimit of a two-term diagram, so $(-) \\otimes N$ preserves cokernels — for an additive functor \"preserves cokernels\" is exactly \"sends short exact sequences to the exact sequence $M_{1} \\otimes N \\to M \\otimes N \\to M_{2} \\otimes N \\to 0$\" ✓.\n\n**A direct computation also works**: $g$ surjective implies $g \\otimes 1$ surjective (every $m_{2} \\otimes n$ has a preimage $m \\otimes n$); for the kernel: from $\\ker g = \\operatorname{im} f$, if $g(m) = 0$, then $m$ can be written $f(m_{1})$, so a pure tensor is $m \\otimes n = (f \\otimes 1)(m_{1} \\otimes n)$, and a general element is a sum of these, giving $\\ker(g \\otimes 1) \\subseteq \\operatorname{im}(f \\otimes 1)$;\n\nthe reverse inclusion follows from $g f = 0$ ✓.\n\n⚠️ **The left end can be lost**: after tensoring $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\xrightarrow{\\;2\\;} \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\to 0$ with $N = \\mathbb{Z}/2$, the map $\\mathbb{Z} \\otimes \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\xrightarrow{\\;2\\;} \\mathbb{Z} \\otimes \\mathbb{Z}/2$ is zero, no longer injective.\n\n⭐ So \"right exact\" is free, and **left exactness is the property** — and its name is flatness. The lost left end does not vanish into nothing: it is the **image** of the connecting map $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(M_{2}, N) \\to M_{1} \\otimes N$ (one cannot identify the \"lost kernel\" with $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}$ directly: the split sequence $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\to (\\mathbb{Z}/2)^{2} \\to \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\to 0$ stays split after tensoring, with zero kernel, while $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}(\\mathbb{Z}/2, \\mathbb{Z}/2) = \\mathbb{Z}/2$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dfd1344f',
      },
    },

    'prop.sn-solvable': {
      statement: {
        en: "The symmetric group $S_n$ is solvable (`def.solvable-group`) if and only if $n \\le 4$.\n\nEquivalently: $S_5$ and all $S_n$ with $n \\ge 5$ are **not** solvable, while $S_1, S_2, S_3, S_4$ are solvable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#15be0a16',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This proposition is the **group-theoretic half of the Abel–Ruffini theorem**: the reason a general equation of degree five or more cannot be solved in radicals is here — the generic Galois group of the equation is $S_n$, and $S_n$ for $n \\ge 5$ is not solvable.\n\n⭐ The dividing line falls between $A_4$ and $A_5$, and this is **not** an asymptotic phenomenon but a hard jump: $A_4$ has the normal subgroup $V_4$ (so $S_4$ can be split), while $A_5$ has no nontrivial normal subgroup, and $A_5$ is non-abelian, hence not solvable.\n\n⭐ A splitting of $S_4$: $\\{e\\} \\trianglelefteq V_4 \\trianglelefteq A_4 \\trianglelefteq S_4$, with quotients $\\mathbb{Z}/2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/2$, $\\mathbb{Z}/3$, $\\mathbb{Z}/2$, all abelian ✓.\n\nFor $S_3$ use $\\{e\\} \\trianglelefteq A_3 \\trianglelefteq S_3$, with quotients $\\mathbb{Z}/3, \\mathbb{Z}/2$ ✓.\n\n⭐ Simplicity of $A_5$ can be proved completely elementarily: the conjugacy classes of $A_5$ have sizes $1, 15, 20, 12, 12$; a normal subgroup is a union of classes and its order divides $60$ — checking the possibilities leaves only $1$ and $60$.\n\nThe full proof is on the edge.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#52c922dc',
      },
    },

    'prop.covering-index': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p : (E, e_{0}) \\to (B, b_{0})$ be a covering map with $E, B$ path-connected and locally path-connected. Then\n\n1. **Injectivity**: $p_{*} : \\pi_{1}(E, e_{0}) \\to \\pi_{1}(B, b_{0})$ is injective;\n2. **Fibre = cosets**: there is a bijection\n\n$$p^{-1}(b_{0}) \\;\\longleftrightarrow\\; \\pi_{1}(B, b_{0}) \\big/ p_{*}\\pi_{1}(E, e_{0}),$$\n\nsending $e \\in p^{-1}(b_{0})$ to the right coset of the projection of the path from $e_{0}$ to $e$.\n\nHence the number of sheets $\\#p^{-1}(b_{0})$ equals the index $[\\pi_{1}(B) : p_{*}\\pi_{1}(E)]$ (when both are finite).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eeffc156',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️⚠️ **This is a bijection of sets, not a group isomorphism.** The coset set $\\pi_{1}(B)/p_{*}\\pi_{1}(E)$ carries no group structure in general; from this alone one cannot get the **group** information $\\pi_{1}(S^{1}) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$ — only that $\\pi_{1}(S^{1})$ and $\\mathbb{Z}$ have the same cardinality. To get the isomorphism one must define **degree** and prove that it is a homomorphism; see `thm.pi1-circle`.\n\n⭐ **When the covering is universal** ($E$ simply connected, so $p_{*}\\pi_{1}(E) = 1$), the conclusion simplifies to \"fibre $\\leftrightarrow \\pi_{1}(B)$ as sets\". The map $p : \\mathbb{R} \\to S^{1}$ is exactly this case.\n\n**Geometric meaning of injectivity**: something nonzero in $\\pi_{1}(E)$ stays nonzero in $B$ — a covering never pretends to \"undo\" an already trivial loop; it only makes a loop \"tighter\".\n\n💡 This proposition is the entry point to the classification of coverings: connected coverings of $B$ up to homeomorphism correspond to conjugacy classes of subgroups of $\\pi_{1}(B)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#81bfce65',
      },
    },

    'prop.pi1-basepoint': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\gamma : [0, 1] \\to X$ be a path from $x_{0}$ to $x_{1}$, and let $\\bar{\\gamma}$ be the reverse path. Define\n\n$$\\Pi_{\\gamma} : \\pi_{1}(X, x_{0}) \\longrightarrow \\pi_{1}(X, x_{1}),$$\n$$\\Pi_{\\gamma}([\\alpha]) := [\\bar{\\gamma} * \\alpha * \\gamma].$$\n\nThen $\\Pi_{\\gamma}$ is a **group isomorphism**, and $\\Pi_{\\bar{\\gamma}} = \\Pi_{\\gamma}^{-1}$.\n\nIf $\\gamma'$ is another path from $x_{0}$ to $x_{1}$, put $g := [\\bar{\\gamma}' * \\gamma] \\in \\pi_{1}(X, x_{1})$; then\n\n$$\\Pi_{\\gamma'} = \\operatorname{ad}_{g} \\circ \\Pi_{\\gamma},\\qquad \\operatorname{ad}_{g}(u) := g\\,u\\,g^{-1}.$$\n\nThat is: the isomorphisms given by different paths **differ by an inner automorphism**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1f1fe1b2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Conclusion**: when $X$ is path-connected, $\\pi_{1}(X, x_{0}) \\cong \\pi_{1}(X, x_{1})$, but this isomorphism is not canonical. Hence \"$\\pi_{1}(X) = 1$\" (simply connected) is a **well-defined** statement, while \"$\\pi_{1}(X) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$, and which element is the generator\" is not.\n\n⚠️ If $\\pi_{1}$ is non-abelian, an inner automorphism need not be the identity, so **conjugacy classes** are the basepoint-independent data. Another reading of the conjugacy-class space: the conjugation action on $\\pi_{1}(X)$ $\\leftrightarrow$ the classification of homotopy classes of loops based at $x_{0}$ under \"free homotopy\".\n\n**Contrast with $\\pi_{0}$**: $\\pi_{1}$ uses **paths** to connect different basepoints; reordering the basepoints is exactly why path-connectedness is needed here (otherwise $\\Pi_{\\gamma}$ cannot even be written down).\n\n💡 In practice: to compute $\\pi_{1}$, first pick any convenient basepoint, compute, and then use this proposition to say \"changing the basepoint does not change it\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#db488de7',
      },
    },

    'prop.integral-chain-linear': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it presents the integral over a chain as a pairing — a chain on the left, a form on the right, a number as the result, linear in each slot. This bilinearity is the licence for all later splitting and summing.\n\nLet $c$ be, on $M$, a chain of degree $k$; let $\\omega,\\eta$ be $k$-forms, and let $a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}$. Then\n\n$$\\int_{c}(a\\,\\omega+b\\,\\eta) = a\\int_{c}\\omega+b\\int_{c}\\eta ,\\qquad \\int_{a\\,c+b\\,c'}\\omega = a\\int_{c}\\omega+b\\int_{c'}\\omega .$$\n\nIn particular $\\int_{-c}\\omega = -\\int_{c}\\omega$ (a chain with coefficient $-1$ is \"walking backwards\").\n\nThus integration is a bilinear pairing\n\n$$C_{k}(M)\\times\\Omega^{k}(M)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{R},$$\n\nwith the geometric object (cubes and their sums) on the left and the dual object (forms and their sums) on the right.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4720f0a3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why this deserves its own node**: it is the legal basis for every \"split and compute\" operation — splitting $\\omega$ into components, splitting a chain into cubes, splitting a region into small pieces all use these two linearities. Without it, Stokes' theorem could only be stated for a single cube, never \"for a chain\".\n\n**Pairing viewpoint**: $\\Omega^{k}(M)$ is a vector space over the reals (infinite-dimensional), and $C_{k}(M)$ is a free abelian group; the two pair to $\\mathbb{R}$, each linearly.\n\nThe dual directions are \"$\\mathrm{d}$ raises degree, $\\partial$ lowers degree\", and $\\int$ is precisely the bracket that couples this pair of duals (`thm.stokes-chain`).\n\n**⚠️ Antisymmetry is not \"antisymmetry\"**: $\\int_{-c} = -\\int_{c}$ says the coefficients of the chain are negative, not that \"swapping the two arguments changes the sign\". There is no commutativity between chains and forms — they have different types.\n\n**⚠️ Coefficient field**: the coefficients of a chain lie in $\\mathbb{Z}$ (`def.singular-chain`), those of a form lie in $\\mathbb{R}$. Here $a,b$ are integers in $\\mathbb{Z}$ (an integral linear combination of chains is still a chain); if real coefficients are needed, one must first write the chain group as $C_{k}(M;\\mathbb{R})$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#934b2c37',
      },
    },

    'prop.integral-gives-ac': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu$ be a measure and $f$ a generalized $\\mu$-integrable function; define\n\n$$\\nu(E) := \\int_E f d\\mu$$\n\nThen $\\nu$ is a signed measure and $\\nu \\ll  \\mu$. Moreover\n\n$\\nu$ is **finite** $\\iff f \\in L^1(\\mu)$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7eca7199',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the **standard source** of absolutely continuous measures, and exactly what the Radon–Nikodym theorem proves in reverse: **every** absolutely continuous $\\nu$ has this form.\n\nIn notation one often writes the differential form:\n\n$$\\nu(E) = \\int_E f d\\mu\\quad  \\iff\\quad  d\\nu = f d\\mu$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#70746822',
      },
    },

    'prop.integral-zero-iff': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in L^+$. Then\n\n$$\\int f d\\mu = 0 \\iff f = 0 \\mu-\\text{a.e.}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#27965adc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Incidentally: $\\{f = +\\infty\\} \\subseteq \\{f>0\\}$ is handled at the same time.\n\n$(\\Longleftarrow )$ it suffices that $f$ vanishes off a null set; then every simple function $\\varphi \\le f$ in the supremum vanishes almost everywhere, hence $\\int \\varphi = 0$.\n\n$(\\implies )$ put $A_n = \\{f > 1/n\\}$; then $(1/n)\\cdot\\mu(A_n) \\le \\int f = 0$, so $\\mu (A_{n}) = 0$; and $\\{f > 0\\} = \\bigcup_n A_n$ is null.\n\n⭐ This ties \"integral\" to \"almost everywhere\" — it is why $L^{1}$'s \"$f = g$\" is understood a.e.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fc9d71c9'
      },
    },

    'prop.operator-algebra': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $u \\in B(E, F)$ and $v \\in B(F, G)$. Then $v \\circ u \\in B(E, G)$ and\n\n$$\\|v \\circ u\\| \\le \\|v\\| \\, \\|u\\| .$$\n\nIn particular $B(E)$ is a **normed algebra** (multiplication is continuous); if $E \\ne \\{0\\}$ then $\\|\\mathrm{id}\\| = 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3716d05',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**In one line**: $\\lVert vux\\rVert\\le\\lVert v\\rVert\\lVert u\\rVert\\lVert x\\rVert$, so the composite is bounded with submultiplicative norm; continuity of multiplication follows from bilinearity and this inequality. The full proof is on the edge `imp.operator-algebra-proof`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#510d5e88',
      },
    },

    'prop.slice-shriek-z': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{T}$ be a topos and $X \\in \\mathcal{T}$. Then\n\n$$j_{X!}\\mathbb{Z} \\;=\\; \\mathbb{Z}\\cdot X.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#702c08f7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Using the adjunctions one by one:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(j_{X!}\\mathbb{Z},\\ M\\bigr) = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(\\mathbb{Z},\\ j_{X}^{-1}M\\bigr) = \\operatorname{Hom}\\bigl(\\mathbf{1}_{X},\\ j_{X}^{-1}M\\bigr) = \\operatorname{Hom}(X,\\ M) ✓$$\n\nThe last identity uses \"the terminal object over $X$ gives $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbf{1}_{X}, j_X^{-1}M) = M(X) = \\operatorname{Hom}(X,M)$\" ✓. Comparing with the universal property of $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot X$, Yoneda gives the result ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#322611fc',
      },
    },

    'prop.standard-maps': {
      statement: {
        en: "A standard list of maps that move regions to standard position ($\\mathbb{H}$ = upper half-plane, $\\mathbb{D}$ = unit disc):\n\n1. $\\mathbb{H} \\to \\mathbb{D}$: $z \\mapsto \\dfrac{z - i}{z + i}$;\n2. angle $\\{\\, 0 < \\arg z < \\alpha \\,\\}$ $\\to \\mathbb{H}$: $z \\mapsto z^{\\pi/\\alpha}$;\n3. strip $\\{\\, 0 < \\operatorname{Im} z < \\pi \\,\\}$ $\\to \\mathbb{H}$: $z \\mapsto e^{z}$;\n4. slit plane $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus (-\\infty, 0]$ $\\to \\mathbb{H}$: the principal branch of $\\sqrt{z}$ or $\\log z$;\n5. automorphisms of $\\mathbb{D} \\to \\mathbb{D}$: $z \\mapsto e^{i\\theta}\\dfrac{z - a}{1 - \\bar a z}$, $|a| < 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d7007448',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **This table is the handbook of the chapter on conformal maps**: when a new region appears, first try to move it to $\\mathbb{H}$ or $\\mathbb{D}$ with the maps in the table (power, exponential, logarithm, fractional linear), and then `thm.riemann-mapping` tells you this is always possible — the only question is whether a formula can be written.\n\n**Why these maps are right**: $z^{k}$ opens an angle by a factor $k$ ($k = \\pi/\\alpha$); $e^{z}$ rolls a horizontal strip into an angle; $\\log$ conversely flattens a slit plane into a strip; fractional linear maps preserve circles (`prop.mobius-circle`), so they interchange half-planes and discs.\n\n⚠️ Each map must **specify which branch** is taken: $z^{\\pi/\\alpha}$ and $\\log$ are multivalued, and the formulas above mean the principal branch (or a fixed branch).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3d17b665',
      },
    },

    'thm.riesz-representation-lp': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $1 < p < \\infty$ and let $q$ be the conjugate exponent. Then for every $\\Phi \\in (L^p)^*$ there is a **unique** (a.e.) $g \\in L^q$ such that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(f) = \\int f g \\, d\\mu \\qquad \\forall f \\in L^p\n$$\n\nHence the map $g \\mapsto \\varphi_g$ is an **isometric isomorphism** from $L^q$ onto $(L^p)^*$:\n\n$$(L^p)^* \\cong L^q$$\n\nIn particular, for $p = 2$ the space $L^2$ is self-dual.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#10f380ad',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the climax of $L^p$ theory: **every continuous linear functional on $L^p$ is just \"multiply by an $L^q$ function and integrate\"**.\n\nThe same holds for $p = 1$ when $\\mu$ is $\\sigma$-finite ($(L^1)^* \\cong L^\\infty$);\n\n⚠ but it **fails** for $p = \\infty$: $(L^\\infty)^*$ is strictly larger than $L^1$ (there are bounded linear functionals not given by an $L^1$ function, for instance those obtained by applying Hahn–Banach on $C[0,1]$).\n\nProof sketch (see the arrow on the right): first turn $\\Phi$ into a complex measure ($\\nu(E) := \\Phi(\\chi_E)$), then use Radon–Nikodym to write it as $\\int \\cdot \\, g \\, d\\mu$, and finally check that $g \\in L^q$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4ee00238',
      },
    },

    'thm.schwarz-reflection': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\Omega$ be symmetric about the real axis and $\\Omega^{+} = \\Omega \\cap \\{\\, \\operatorname{Im} z > 0 \\,\\}$. If $f$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega^{+}$, continuous on $\\Omega \\cap \\mathbb{R}$ and **real-valued** there, then $f$ extends to a holomorphic function on $\\Omega$, namely $F$, and\n\n$$F(\\bar z) = \\overline{F(z)}.$$\n\n(Replace the \"real axis\" by any arc or line and the conclusion changes accordingly: the two symmetric pieces reflect into each other by the same formula.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#38540bee',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof**: define\n\n$$F(z) = \\begin{cases} f(z), & \\operatorname{Im} z > 0,\\\\ \\overline{f(\\bar z)}, & \\operatorname{Im} z < 0.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe lower half-plane $F$ is holomorphic (conjugate, then conjugate again — the two anti-holomorphic steps cancel). It remains to show that $F$ is holomorphic across the real axis: use **Morera** (`thm.morera`) — take a closed triangle crossing the axis, use $f$ on the upper part and the definition on the lower part; the integral along the real-axis piece tends to zero by continuity ($f$ is real-valued on the axis, so the two sides cancel), hence the integral over the whole boundary is zero. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7295bfaa',
      },
    },

    'thm.riemann-removable': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In words**: if $f$ is holomorphic near $a$ and, as one approaches $a$, grows slower than $1/|z - a|$, then the puncture at $a$ can be filled in, and the result is still holomorphic.\n\nLet $f$ be holomorphic on $B(a, r) \\setminus \\{a\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\\lim_{z \\to a} (z - a) f(z) = 0.$$\nEquivalently, $f(z) = o(|z - a|^{-1})$.\n\nThen $a$ is a **removable singularity** of $f$: there is a holomorphic extension of $f$ to $B(a, r)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#545de2d0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Proof: for $z \\in B(a, r) \\setminus \\{a\\}$ and small enough $\\varepsilon < |z - a|$, write $f$ as the difference of two Cauchy-type integrals $f(z) = I_r(z) - I_\\varepsilon(z)$, where $I_{\\rho}$ is the integral along $|\\zeta - a| = \\rho$ (**take $\\rho < r$** — the circle $|\\zeta - a| = r$ itself is not in the domain; $I_{\\rho}$ is holomorphic on $B(a, \\rho)$), and $I_\\varepsilon$ is the integral along $|\\zeta - a| = \\varepsilon$.\n\nEstimate\n\n$$|I_\\varepsilon(z)| \\le \\frac{\\varepsilon \\, \\|f\\|_{\\partial B(a, \\varepsilon)}}{|z - a| - \\varepsilon} \\longrightarrow 0 \\qquad (\\varepsilon \\to 0),$$\n\nthe last step uses exactly the hypothesis $(z - a) f(z) \\to 0$.\n\nBut $I_\\varepsilon(z)$ is **independent** of the choice of $\\varepsilon \\in (0, |z - a|)$, so it must be identically $0$.\n\nHence $f = I_{\\rho}$ is holomorphic on $B(a, \\rho)$, and one sets $f(a) := I_{\\rho}(a)$ ($\\rho < r$ arbitrary; the values agree for different $\\rho$).\n\n⭐ Use: it lets \"removable singularity\" really be treated as absent.\n\nFor example $\\sin z / z$ extends at $0$ to $1$, so $\\sin z / z$ is entire;\n\nthe order of vanishing of $z^k f(z)$, and \"a holomorphic function near a zero can be written as $(z - a)^k \\psi(z)$\", both rely on it.\n\n⚠ Formally this is weaker than \"bounded\" (it only requires $(z - a)f(z) \\to 0$); but for $f$ **holomorphic on a punctured neighbourhood**, the theorem gives \"removable $\\implies$ locally bounded\", so the two conditions are in fact **equivalent**.\n\nWhat is genuinely non-removable is a rate like $1/(z - a)$ — that is a pole.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bc195458',
      },
    },

    'thm.smooth-duality': {
      statement: {
        en: "In words: **in the smooth case $f^!R$ is a \"twisted line bundle\" given by the determinant of differential forms**. This is the **dualizing bundle** $\\omega_{X/Y}$ (canonical bundle) of classical geometry.\n\nHere $\\Omega^1_{A/R}$ is the **relative Kähler module of differentials**: formally generated by $\\mathrm{d}a$ ($a \\in A$), modulo the Leibniz rule $\\mathrm{d}(ab) = a\\,\\mathrm{d}b + b\\,\\mathrm{d}a$ and $\\mathrm{d}r = 0$ ($r \\in R$) — that is, all \"differential forms of $A$ relative to $R$\" (it is an $A$-module, and $\\det$ means its top exterior power).\n\n$d$ is the relative dimension, and $[d]$ in the formula means the whole complex shifted to homological degree $d$.\n\nLet $f : R \\to A$ be a smooth ring map of dimension $d$. Then there is a natural isomorphism\n\n$$f^!R \\;\\cong\\; \\det\\nolimits_A\\bigl(\\Omega^1_{A/R}\\bigr)[d].$$\n\nSince the isomorphism is natural and everything is defined locally, it globalizes to any smooth map $f : X \\to Y$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fedde5d8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **dualizing complex**: in the smooth case $f^!\\mathbf{1}_Y$ is an invertible sheaf (a line bundle) shifted to degree $d$, given by the determinant of the Kähler differentials $\\Omega^1_{A/R}$.\n\n⭐ The proof uses the diagonal: $g : A \\otimes_R A \\to A$ is the regular closed immersion of codimension $d$ (the diagonal $\\Delta_f$); split $f^!R$ using $g^!$ and the two projections $A \\otimes_R A$, then apply the formula for regular closed immersions to get\n\n$$f^!R \\otimes f^!R \\otimes g^!(A \\otimes_R A) \\;\\cong\\; f^!R,$$\n\nand comparing the two sides gives $\\det(\\Omega)[d]$.\n\n⚠ This is the precise source of \"coherent duality in the smooth case is Poincaré duality\": $\\omega_{X/Y} = \\det(\\Omega^1_{X/Y})[d]$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#00f40d76',
      },
    },

    'lem.abel-theta-pi': {
      statement: {
        en: "$\\theta(x)=\\pi(x)\\log x-\\int_{2}^{x}\\frac{\\pi(t)}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, $\\pi(x)=\\frac{\\theta(x)}{\\log x}+\\int_{2}^{x}\\frac{\\theta(t)}{t\\log^{2}t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ ($x\\ge2$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7c3cbf7b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Obtained from Stieltjes summation by parts (with $f=\\log t$ and $f=1/\\log t$) (Stieltjes summation by parts).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bedcac91',
      },
    },

    'lem.solid-completion-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "In words: this lemma is a **universal wrench** — to show that some subcategory works as well as modules (compact objects, a left adjoint, a fully faithful derived category), one need not check each item; a single $\\operatorname{RHom}$ condition suffices.\n\nLet $\\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category with arbitrary colimits and $\\mathcal{A}_0 \\subset \\mathcal{A}$ a family of compact projective generators (= a generating family in which every object is both **compact** and **projective**; this is the abstract version of \"finite free modules\" in module categories).\n\nLet $F : \\mathcal{A}_0 \\to \\mathcal{A}$ come with a **natural transformation** $X \\to F(X)$, i.e. a family of maps $\\eta_X : X \\to F(X)$, such that for every map in $\\mathcal{A}_0$, say $u : X \\to X'$, one has $F(u) \\circ \\eta_X = \\eta_{X'} \\circ u$ (\"$F$ commutes with $u$\"), and suppose:\n\nfor every $X \\in \\mathcal{A}_0$, every map built from direct sums of images of $F$, say $f : Y \\to Z$, with kernel $K$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{RHom}(F(X), K) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{RHom}(X, K)$$\n\nis an isomorphism. Let $\\mathcal{A}_F$ be the full subcategory of objects for which \"$\\operatorname{Hom}(F(X), Y) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(X, Y)$ is an isomorphism for all $X \\in \\mathcal{A}_0$\"; its objects are the $Y$ with this property. Then:\n\n**(i)** $\\mathcal{A}_F \\subset \\mathcal{A}$ is an abelian subcategory stable under all limits, colimits and extensions; the $F(X)$ ($X \\in \\mathcal{A}_0$) form a family of compact projective generators of $\\mathcal{A}_F$; the inclusion has a left adjoint $L$, which is the unique colimit-preserving extension of $F$.\n\n**(ii)** $D(\\mathcal{A}_F) \\to D(\\mathcal{A})$ is fully faithful, with image exactly the complexes satisfying $\\operatorname{RHom}(F(X), C) \\cong \\operatorname{RHom}(X, C)$;\n\n$C \\in D(\\mathcal{A}_F)$ if and only if all $H^i(C) \\in \\mathcal{A}_F$;\n\nthe inclusion has a left adjoint (the left derived functor of $L$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6b4d9e9a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is a **purely category-theoretic** lemma: it compresses \"prove that the completed subcategory behaves well\" into one Ext-vanishing condition.\n\n`thm.solid-abelian` is its instance ($\\mathcal{A} = \\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab})$, $F(\\mathbb{Z}[S]) = Z[S]^{\\blacksquare}$).\n\n⚠ The condition requires an isomorphism only for **kernels**, not for cokernels — but dually, writing it for cokernels works the same (see Remark 5.11 of the source).\n\n📌 The proof is standard homological algebra: first show that $\\mathcal{A}_F$ is closed under kernels and limits, then bring cokernels in using \"maps between two direct sums\", and finally reduce complexes to a single point by Postnikov truncation.\n\n⚠ We do not reproduce the proof of Lemma 5.9 here; it is cited as an external result.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bd18927a',
      },
    },

    'cor.cond-ktop-finite-products': {
      statement: {
        en: "The functor $\\mathrm{Cond} \\to k\\mathbf{Top}$, $X \\mapsto X(\\cdot)$, **preserves finite products**: for any $X, Y \\in \\mathrm{Cond}$,\n\n$$(X \\times Y)(\\cdot) \\;\\cong\\; k\\bigl(X(\\cdot) \\times Y(\\cdot)\\bigr).$$\n\n(The right-hand side must first be $k$-ified: the product in $\\mathbf{Top}$ is usually not compactly generated.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b9a545a8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why it matters**: it says the functor \"condensed set $\\mapsto$ underlying topological space\" **does not break finite products**, so a product computed in $\\mathrm{Cond}$ is still that product on spaces (after $k$-ification). This is the most-used step when translating algebraic constructions in $\\mathrm{Cond}$ back to topological ones.\n\n⚠️ Passing to $\\mathbf{Top}$ (i.e. applying $k$-ification) makes it **false** — the root cause is that $\\mathbf{Top}$ is not cartesian closed.\n\n📌 Example: projection formulas such as $S \\times X(\\cdot) \\cong (S \\times X)(\\cdot)$ (with $S$ locally compact Hausdorff) are another face of the same fact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#882554d3',
      },
    },

    'lem.psi-theta-asymptotic': {
      statement: {
        en: "$\\psi(x)-\\theta(x)=O(\\sqrt{x}\\log x)=o(x)$. Hence $\\psi(x)\\sim x\\iff\\theta(x)\\sim x$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ecc51eed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Only $\\theta(y)\\le y\\log y$ and $k\\le\\log_{2}x$ are used (the tail estimate $O(\\sqrt{x}\\log x)$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a96aaee3',
      },
    },

    'lem.psi-theta-sum': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $x\\ge1$, $\\psi(x)=\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\theta\\bigl(x^{1/k}\\bigr)$. The sum is finite: $p^{k}\\le x$ forces $k\\le\\log_{2}x$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#24075994',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the first bridge between $\\psi$ and $\\theta$: group the terms $n=p^{k}$ by $k$ (i.e. by the exponent of the prime power).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92e239e4',
      },
    },

    'lem.theta-pi-equivalence': {
      statement: {
        en: "$\\theta(x)\\sim x$ if and only if $\\pi(x)\\sim x/\\log x$ ($x\\to\\infty$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#71f061ee',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By Abel summation and $\\int_{2}^{x}\\mathrm{d}t/\\log^{2}t=o(x/\\log x)$ (Abel summation and integral estimates).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d1036008',
      },
    },

    'prop.product-incomplete': {
      statement: {
        en: "Even when $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ are both **complete**, $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is **often incomplete**: there is $E \\in \\mathcal{M} \\otimes  \\mathcal{N}$ with $\\mu \\times \\nu(E) = 0$, yet $E$ has a subset not in $\\mathcal{M} \\otimes  \\mathcal{N}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7b9e14a2',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is why one needs a separate \"$F$–$T$\" for the complete case: in practice (for instance Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$) the measure at hand is usually the completion of $\\mu \\times \\nu$, not the raw product.\n\n⚠ A common trap: even with $\\mu$, $\\nu$ complete, $\\mu \\times \\nu$ can still be **incomplete**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b0eb1219',
      },
    },

    'prop.topos-morphism-from-object': {
      statement: {
        en: "Viewing $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$ as a functor $X : \\mathbf{1} \\to \\mathcal{C}$, it induces a topos morphism $\\widehat{X} : \\mathbf{Set} \\to \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}$, and the three functors are given on objects by\n\n$\\widehat{X}^{-1}(T) = T(X)$; for $\\widehat{X}_{!}(E)$, when $Y \\to X$ exists the value is $E$, and otherwise $\\emptyset$; for $\\widehat{X}_{*}(E)$, when $X \\to Y$ exists the value is $E$, and otherwise $\\{0\\}$:\n\n$$\\widehat{X}^{-1}(T) = T(X), \\qquad \\bigl(\\widehat{X}_{!}(E)\\bigr)(Y) \\in \\{\\, E,\\ \\emptyset\\, \\}, \\qquad \\bigl(\\widehat{X}_{*}(E)\\bigr)(Y) \\in \\{\\, E,\\ \\{0\\}\\, \\}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#86340c72',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** By \"every functor induces a topos morphism\", $X : \\mathbf{1} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ induces $\\widehat{X}_{!} \\dashv \\widehat{X}^{-1} \\dashv \\widehat{X}_{*}$ ✓.\n\nThe three formulas are: pullback is evaluation ✓;\n\nthe left Kan extension along $\\mathbf{1} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ is given by a colimit over the comma category, and \"$\\exists Y \\to X$\" is exactly the condition for that colimit to be nonempty ✓;\n\nthe right adjoint is dual ✓.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#23014bca',
      },
    },

    'prop.tensor-functor-properties': {
      statement: {
        en: "The tensor product is a **bifunctor** $\\otimes_{R} : \\mathbf{Mod}\\text{-}R \\times R\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod} \\to \\mathbf{Ab}$, and:\n\n1. **Additivity**: for $f : M \\to M_{1}$ and $g : N \\to N_{1}$ there is $f \\otimes g$; fixing one variable gives an additive functor.\n2. **Commutes with direct sums**: $(\\bigoplus_{i} M_{i}) \\otimes_{R} N \\cong \\bigoplus_{i} (M_{i} \\otimes_{R} N)$, $M \\otimes_{R} (\\bigoplus_{j} N_{j}) \\cong \\bigoplus_{j} (M \\otimes_{R} N_{j})$, naturally.\n3. **Unit**: $R \\otimes_{R} N \\cong N$ ($r \\otimes n \\mapsto r n$); more generally $R^{(I)} \\otimes_{R} N \\cong N^{(I)}$.\n4. **Associativity**: if $M$ is an $(S, R)$-bimodule, $N$ an $(R, T)$-bimodule and $P$ a left $T$-module, then\n$$(M \\otimes_{R} N) \\otimes_{T} P \\;\\cong\\; M \\otimes_{R} (N \\otimes_{T} P).$$\n5. **Symmetry** ($R$ commutative): $\\tau : M \\otimes_{R} N \\to N \\otimes_{R} M$, $m \\otimes n \\mapsto n \\otimes m$, and $\\tau \\circ \\tau = \\mathrm{id}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8b05eebd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ From 2 and 3 it follows at once that $(-) \\otimes_{R} N$ **preserves colimits** (direct sums, cokernels, filtered colimits). This explains both \"right exact\" (preserves cokernels) and \"preserves direct sums\", and is the common root of `prop.tensor-right-exact` and `prop.free-flat`.\n\n⚠ In general it **does not preserve** limits: $\\otimes$ does not preserve kernels.\n\n$0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\xrightarrow{\\;2\\;} \\mathbb{Z}$ is exact; after tensoring with $N = \\mathbb{Q}$ it stays exact (multiplication by $2$ is still injective on $\\mathbb{Q}$), but after tensoring with $N = \\mathbb{Z}/2$ multiplication by $2$ becomes the zero map and a kernel appears (see `prop.tensor-right-exact`).\n\nNor does it preserve infinite products.\n\n📌 On a commutative ring, associativity 4 reads $(M \\otimes_{R} N) \\otimes_{R} P \\cong M \\otimes_{R} N \\otimes_{R} P$, so multiple tensor products can be written without parentheses.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#badb934d',
      },
    },

    'thm.approximation-L1': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f \\in L^1(\\mu)$. Then for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a simple function $\\varphi = \\sum_j a_j \\chi_{E_j}$ such that\n\n$$\\int |f - \\varphi| d\\mu < \\varepsilon$$\n\nMoreover, if $\\mu$ is a **Lebesgue–Stieltjes measure on $\\mathbb{R}$**, one can require the $E_{j}$ to be finite unions of open intervals; one can even take a **continuous function** $g$ (vanishing outside some bounded interval) such that\n\n$$\\int |f - g| d\\mu < \\varepsilon$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a0793b9f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Meaning: **simple functions and continuous functions are dense in $L^{1}$**. Hence many statements need only be checked on these two classes, then one passes to the limit.\n\nProof idea: split $f$ into the four nonnegative parts $\\operatorname{Re} f^{+}$, $\\operatorname{Re} f^{-}$, $\\operatorname{Im} f^{+}$, $\\operatorname{Im} f^{-}$ and approximate each by simple functions (simple approximation theorem);\n\nin the case of $\\mathbb{R}$, cut the domain into bounded pieces and use continuous functions to dominate the indicators of half-open intervals.\n\n⚠ The continuous-function clause **depends on $\\mu$ being Lebesgue–Stieltjes**: for an arbitrary measure the simple-function approximation still holds, but the continuous-function statement fails.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1ab9fec0',
      },
    },

    'thm.borsuk-ulam': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every continuous map $f : S^{2} \\to \\mathbb{R}^{2}$ there is $x \\in S^{2}$ such that\n\n$$f(x) = f(-x).$$\n\nEquivalently: there is no continuous odd map ($g(-x) = -g(x)$) $g : S^{2} \\to S^{1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c398cce',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to Brouwer**: two-dimensional Borsuk–Ulam follows from the Brouwer fixed point theorem (and conversely). Standard reduction: if $f(x) \\ne f(-x)$ everywhere, set\n\n$$g(x) := \\frac{f(x) - f(-x)}{|f(x) - f(-x)|} \\in S^{1},$$\n\nthen $g$ is continuous and $g(-x) = -g(x)$.\n\nRestricting $g$ to the equator $S^{1} \\subset S^{2}$ gives a continuous odd map $S^{1} \\to S^{1}$, whose **degree** must be odd (the degree of an odd map is $\\equiv 1 \\bmod 2$);\n\nbut an odd map $S^{1} \\to S^{1}$ also extends to the upper hemisphere, and an extendable map has degree $0$ — contradiction.\n\n⭐ **Intuition**: at any moment on Earth there is a pair of antipodal points with the same temperature and the same pressure. This is the case $n = 2$ of Borsuk–Ulam, not a metaphor but a theorem.\n\n⚠ The dimension cannot be lowered: the analogue for $S^{1} \\to \\mathbb{R}^{1}$ holds (intermediate value theorem), but $S^{1} \\to \\mathbb{R}^{2}$ fails (the identity map has no antipodal pair with equal values).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#77d5bd5a',
      },
    },

    'thm.divisor-formula': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $n = p_{1}^{e_{1}} \\cdots p_{r}^{e_{r}}$. Then\n\n$$\\tau(n) = \\prod_{i=1}^{r} (e_{i} + 1), \\qquad \\sigma(n) = \\prod_{i=1}^{r} \\frac{p_{i}^{\\,e_{i}+1} - 1}{p_{i} - 1} .$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3cbac15c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why a product**: every divisor of $n$, call it $d$, has the form $d = p_{1}^{f_{1}} \\cdots p_{r}^{f_{r}}$ with $0 \\le f_{i} \\le e_{i}$ — and by unique factorization this representation is unique. So \"choosing a divisor\" reduces to \"choosing each exponent independently\", giving $\\prod (e_{i}+1)$ choices.\n\nThe same for $\\sigma$, except that each column is a geometric sum: $\\sum_{f=0}^{e_{i}} p_{i}^{f} = \\dfrac{p_{i}^{e_{i}+1}-1}{p_{i}-1}$.\n\nThese two formulas are the model for the \"multiplicative + values on prime powers\" method: the formulas for $\\varphi$ and $\\mu$ follow the same pattern ($\\varphi(p^{e}) = p^{e} - p^{e-1}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a20fe4d3',
      },
    },

    'prop.volume-form-orientation': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it gives orientability a criterion that needs no atlas — it is enough to write down an everywhere nonzero $n$-form on the whole manifold.\n\nLet $M$ be an $n$-dimensional smooth manifold. Then $M$ is orientable if and only if there is an everywhere nonzero $n$-form\n\n$$\\omega \\in \\Omega^{n}(M),\\qquad \\omega_{p} \\ne 0\\ \\ \\bigl(\\forall p \\in M\\bigr).$$\n\nMore precisely: two everywhere nonzero $n$-forms $\\omega, \\omega_{1}$ necessarily satisfy $\\omega_{1} = f\\,\\omega$ with $f$ an everywhere nonzero smooth function; they give the **same orientation** if and only if $f > 0$ everywhere.\n\nHence on a connected manifold, \"everywhere nonzero $n$-forms modulo positive smooth functions\" is exactly \"orientation\", corresponding to the maximal oriented atlas of `def.orientable-manifold`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0a1c1477',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Two halves of the proof**: $(\\implies)$ on each orientation-preserving chart take $\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$; it changes by a **positive** smooth function under a change of chart, so a partition of unity sums it into a global $\\omega$, and the positive coefficients keep it nonzero;\n\n$(\\Longleftarrow)$ given an everywhere nonzero $\\omega$, on a chart it equals $f\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{n}$ with $f$ everywhere nonzero; replace every chart with $f < 0$ by reversed coordinates — then all transition Jacobians are positive, giving an oriented atlas.\n\nDetails on the edge.\n\n**Why a partition of unity is unavoidable here**: locally built objects have only the freedom of \"multiplication by positive functions\", with no cancellation mechanism — summing with positive coefficients guarantees no zeros. So this is one of the standard uses of `thm.smooth-partition-unity`.\n\n**Line-bundle language**: $\\bigwedge^{n}T^{\\ast}M$ is a line bundle of rank $1$ (the **orientation bundle**), and $\\omega$ is an everywhere nonzero section of it. Thus \"orientable\" and \"orientation bundle trivial\" are two ways of saying the same thing — the same criterion as \"an everywhere nonzero section is equivalent to a trivial bundle\" in `def.bundle-section`.\n\n**\"Everywhere nonzero\" cannot be weakened to \"almost everywhere nonzero\"**: at a zero one cannot choose an orientation. Any $n$-form on a nonorientable manifold must have a zero — this is the precise form of \"a volume form cannot be chosen globally on $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$\".\n\n**Where connectedness is used**: only in \"$f > 0$ iff the same orientation\". On a disconnected manifold $f$ may have different signs on different components, so two everywhere nonzero $n$-forms may agree only on some components — the sign issue must then be discussed componentwise.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6b39958d',
      },
    },

    'thm.constant-gauss-curvature': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it pushes \"$K$ constant\" to its limit — the answer is extremely clean: locally there are only three possibilities. This is where intrinsic geometry really shows its power (only the first fundamental form is used throughout).\n\nOn a surface $\\Sigma$, let the Gauss curvature $K$ be **constant**. At any point take **geodesic polar coordinates** $(r, \\theta)$; then\n\n$$g = \\mathrm{d}r^{2} + f(r,\\theta)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta^{2} ,$$\n\nand Cartan's structure equation $\\mathrm{d}\\omega^{2} = K\\,\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\omega^{2}$ gives that $f$ satisfies\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial^{2} f}{\\partial r^{2}} + K f = 0 ,$$\n\nwith initial conditions $f(0,\\theta) = 0$, $\\partial_{r}f(0,\\theta) = 1$, yielding three solutions:\n\n- $K = 0$: $f = r$, i.e. $g = \\mathrm{d}r^{2} + r^{2}\\mathrm{d}\\theta^{2}$ (**Euclidean metric**);\n- $K > 0$: $f = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt K}\\sin(\\sqrt K\\, r)$ (**spherical metric**);\n- $K < 0$: $f = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-K}}\\sinh(\\sqrt{-K}\\, r)$ (**hyperbolic metric**).\n\n**Theorem**: a surface of constant Gauss curvature is **locally** isometric to one of the plane, the sphere or the hyperbolic plane.\n\n**Corollary**: if it is simply connected and complete, only the plane, the sphere and the hyperbolic plane occur.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7a5ba3dc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it deserves its own entry**: it translates a **metric condition** ($K$ constant) into three concrete metric forms using only the first fundamental form — how the surface sits in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ plays no role. This is the first big victory of intrinsic geometry after Gauss's theorema egregium.\n\n**Where the three solutions come from**: the characteristic roots of the constant-coefficient equation $f'' + Kf = 0$ are $\\pm\\sqrt{-K}$, and the sign of $K$ decides whether the solutions are trigonometric (oscillating), linear, or hyperbolic (exponentially growing). The initial conditions $f(0) = 0$, $f'(0) = 1$ are the geometric requirements of geodesic polar coordinates (circumference zero at $r = 0$, and $r$ is arc length).\n\n⭐ The row $K = 0$ is exactly the developable case (`def.developable-surface`): $\\mathrm{d}r^{2} + r^{2}\\mathrm{d}\\theta^{2}$ is the polar-coordinate metric of the plane, so a surface with $K \\equiv 0$ is locally isometric to the plane — this is the analytic basis of \"developable\".\n\n⚠ **Locally only three, globally many**: flat tori, cylinders and hyperbolic surfaces all have constant curvature. The corollary's \"simply connected + complete\" excludes them, and both hypotheses are needed.\n\n⚠ **For $K > 0$, $r$ is bounded**: $f$ returns to zero at $\\sqrt K\\,r = \\pi$, so geodesics meet again at a finite distance (antipodal points); for $K \\le 0$, $f$ stays positive and geodesics never meet again.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b13c9cc0',
      },
    },

    'prop.curvature-tensor-symmetries': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it takes stock of how many independent components the curvature tensor really has — four symmetries cut $n^{4}$ down to $n^{2}(n^{2}-1)/12$. This is the arithmetic source of \"curvature in dimension two is one number, in dimension four it is two tensors\".\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a Riemannian manifold, with curvature operator $R(X,Y)Z = \\nabla_{X}\\nabla_{Y}Z-\\nabla_{Y}\\nabla_{X}Z-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z$ and components $R_{ijk\\ell} = \\langle R(\\partial_{i},\\partial_{j})\\partial_{k},\\partial_{\\ell}\\rangle$ (conventions as in `def.curvature-tensor` and `def.gauss-curvature`).\n\nThen\n\n1. **Antisymmetric in the first two indices**: $R_{ijk\\ell} = -R_{jik\\ell}$;\n2. **antisymmetric in the last two**: $R_{ijk\\ell} = -R_{ij\\ell k}$;\n3. **the two pairs can be exchanged**: $R_{ijk\\ell} = R_{k\\ell ij}$;\n4. **first Bianchi identity**: $R_{ijk\\ell}+R_{jki\\ell}+R_{kij\\ell} = 0$.\n\nHence the number of independent components is\n\n$$\\dim = \\frac{n^{2}\\bigl(n^{2}-1\\bigr)}{12}:\\qquad n = 2:\\ 1,\\quad n = 3:\\ 6,\\quad n = 4:\\ 20 .$$\n\nWithout indices: $R(X,Y,Z,W) := \\langle R(X,Y)Z,W\\rangle$ is a symmetric bilinear form on $\\bigwedge^{2}T_{p}M$ (the first two give antisymmetry, the third symmetry), and the fourth cuts out another $\\binom{n}{4}$ dimensions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#56039dea',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The first two have different sources**: antisymmetry in $X,Y$ is immediate from $[\\nabla_{X},\\nabla_{Y}]$ in the definition; antisymmetry in $Z,W$ uses **metric compatibility** ($\\nabla g = 0$) — first prove $\\langle R(X,Y)Z,Z\\rangle = 0$ and then polarize.\n\n**The fourth comes from torsion-freeness**: $R(X,Y)Z+R(Y,Z)X+R(Z,X)Y = 0$.\n\nAn elegant equivalent proof: when torsion vanishes, $\\nabla^{2}Z(X,Y) := \\nabla_{X}\\nabla_{Y}Z-\\nabla_{\\nabla_{X}Y}Z$ is symmetric in $X,Y$, and $R(X,Y)Z = \\nabla^{2}Z(X,Y)-\\nabla^{2}Z(Y,X)$;\n\nsumming cyclically over the three arguments, the two symmetric terms trade places and cancel term by term.\n\n**The third is pure algebraic bookkeeping**: write the first Bianchi identity for several cyclic choices of the four arguments, move signs using the first two, and add; what remains is exactly $R_{ijk\\ell}-R_{k\\ell ij}$. It carries no geometry, so we do not reproduce it term by term.\n\n**Why count components**: for $n = 2$ only $R_{1212}$ remains, so two-dimensional curvature **is one number** (the Gauss curvature); for $n = 3$ all $6$ components are determined by Ricci (the Weyl tensor vanishes); for $n = 4$, $20 = 10+10$, the second half being the Weyl tensor — that is where gravitational waves live.\n\n⚠ **Sign convention**: here $R_{ijk\\ell} = \\langle R(\\partial_{i},\\partial_{j})\\partial_{k},\\partial_{\\ell}\\rangle$, so the unit sphere gives $R_{1221} = +1$ and $R_{1212} = -1$.\n\nChanging the index order shifts the signs in 3 and 4 globally; checking on the sphere is fastest.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4173cdc0',
      },
    },

    'thm.developable-iff-flat': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it gives a workable criterion for whether a ruled surface is developable — a determinant identity, with no PDE to solve.\n\nLet $X(u,v) = a(u) + v\\,b(u)$ be a ruled surface (`def.ruled-surface`). Then the following are equivalent:\n\n1. the **tangent plane is constant** along each ruling (the normal vector is independent of $v$);\n2. $\\det\\bigl(a'(u),\\ b(u),\\ b'(u)\\bigr) = 0$ for all $u$;\n3. **Gauss curvature vanishes identically**: $K \\equiv 0$;\n4. **developable** (locally isometric to the plane, `def.developable-surface`).\n\n**How to use it**: substitute $a, b$ into that third-order determinant. For a cylinder $b' = 0$, hence developable; for the hyperbolic paraboloid $X = (u,v,uv)$ the determinant is $1 \\ne 0$, so it is **not** developable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f865f883',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof skeleton**:\n\n- (1) and (2): $N \\propto X_u \\times X_v = a'\\times b + v\\,(b'\\times b)$; independence of $v$ means $b'\\times b$ is parallel to $a'\\times b$, equivalently $\\det(a',b,b') = 0$.\n\n- (2) and (3): compute directly from $K = \\det(h_{ij})/\\det(g_{ij})$; on a ruled surface $\\det(h_{ij})$ and $\\det(a',b,b')^{2}$ differ by a **sign** (not necessarily a positive factor: $X = (u,v,uv)$ gives $\\det h = -1$, while $\\det(a',b,b')^{2} > 0$) — but \"being zero\" is unaffected by the sign, so $K \\equiv 0$ is still equivalent to the determinant vanishing identically. Only the second fundamental form is used.\n\n- (3) and (4): if $K \\equiv 0$, the local classification of constant-curvature surfaces (`thm.constant-gauss-curvature`, case $K = 0$) gives local isometry to the plane; conversely Gauss's theorema egregium ($K$ is an isometry invariant, and the plane has $K$ zero).\n\n⚠ **With flat points, 1 and 4 decouple**: there are surfaces with $K \\equiv 0$ containing flat patches that cannot be written with \"tangent plane constant along the rulings\". So 4 should be read as \"locally isometric to the plane\", which always holds.\n\n⚠ **Do not confuse \"developable\" with \"minimal surface\"**: the latter means $H = 0$ ($K$ is generally nonzero); the plane is their only common case.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bea09764',
      },
    },

    'prop.boundary-sign-convention': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: it aligns two \"boundaries\" — the algebraic boundary $\\partial c$ determined on a cube by the coordinate numbering, and the geometric boundary of a manifold with boundary determined by \"outward normal first\". They differ by exactly the signs $(-1)^{i+\\alpha}$.\n\nLet $M$ be an oriented $n$-dimensional manifold with boundary and $c : I^{n}\\to M$ an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism onto its image — $I^{n}$ is mapped onto that image, which is a region with boundary and is $n$-dimensional, with $I^{n}$ carrying the standard orientation, **and suppose the boundary of the cube lands on $\\partial M$**: $c(\\partial I^{n}) \\subseteq \\partial M$. (⚠ This cannot be dropped: take $M = \\mathbb{R}$ and $c(t) = t$; then $\\partial M = \\emptyset$, the \"image of $\\partial M$\" below is empty, and the whole proposition collapses.)\n\nThen for every face $(i,\\alpha)$: viewing $c_{(i,\\alpha)}$ as a parametrization of a piece of $\\partial M$, **it agrees with the induced orientation of $\\partial M$ if and only if $(-1)^{i+\\alpha} = +1$**.\n\nIn other words: after reordering the signs of all faces according to the induced orientation, the resulting chain is\n\n$$\\partial c = \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{\\alpha=0}^{1}(-1)^{i+\\alpha}\\,c_{(i,\\alpha)} .$$\n\nSo the \"algebraic boundary\" and the \"geometric boundary\" are not two conventions but one: the signs are already written in the coefficients of $\\partial c$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b2e19049',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why this matters**: the manifold version of Stokes' theorem uses the geometric boundary (induced orientation), while the chain version (`thm.stokes-chain`) uses the algebraic boundary ($\\partial c$). That both can be written in one formula is entirely due to this proposition. It is also the true source of the \"outward normal + induced orientation\" sign in the divergence theorem.\n\n**Check for $n = 1$**: $I^{1} = [0,1]$ and $\\partial c = -c_{(1,0)}+c_{(1,1)} = c(1)-c(0)$. In the induced orientation of dimension $0$, the end $x = 1$ (outward normal $+e_{1}$) is positive and the end $x = 0$ is negative. Term by term, they agree.\n\n**Check for $n = 2$**: $\\partial c = -c_{(1,0)}+c_{(1,1)}+c_{(2,0)}-c_{(2,1)}$.\n\nThe four edges are left, right, bottom and top; the right edge ($\\alpha = 1$, outward normal $+e_{1}$) gets $+$, the left edge (outward normal $-e_{1}$) gets $-$, the bottom edge (outward normal $-e_{2}$) gets $+$, and the top edge (outward normal $+e_{2}$) gets $-$.\n\nChecking one by one with \"outward normal first\", all four agree — and the directions of the four edges run exactly once around counterclockwise.\n\n**⚠ The direction that is easy to copy wrong**: the proof compares, in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, \"the determinant of the face's basis together with $N$\", not \"the direction of the cross product of the face's basis with $N$\". The former is pure algebra and can be checked term by term; the latter is only intuitive in three dimensions. Intuition below dimension three helps one remember, but is not a proof.\n\n**⚠ Orientation preservation cannot be dropped**: if $c$ reverses orientation, every face gains an extra minus sign and the conclusion flips sign. Hence the hypothesis is \"$c$ orientation-preserving\", not \"$c$ is a diffeomorphism\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ecb5f939',
      },
    },

    'thm.exact-couple-spectral': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(D, E, i, j, k)$ be a bigraded exact couple, with bidegrees $i, j, k$ equal to $(-1, 1)$, $(0, 0)$, $(1, 0)$. Put $E_{1} := E$ and define the derived couples recursively: $E_{r+1} := H(E_{r}, d_{r})$, $d_{r} := j_{r} k_{r}$. Then:\n\n1. $(E_{r}, d_{r})_{r \\ge 1}$ is a **spectral sequence**, with differentials\n\n$$d_{r} : E_{r}^{p,q} \\longrightarrow E_{r}^{p+r,\\ q-r+1} , \\qquad E_{r+1} = H(E_{r}, d_{r}) ;$$\n\n2. if for every $(p, q)$ the terms $D_{r}^{p,q}$ and $E_{r}^{p,q}$ **stabilize** for $r \\gg 0$ (automatic when the filtration is bounded), then, writing $D_{\\infty}^{p,q} := \\bigcap_{r} D_{r}^{p,q}$,\n\n$$E_{\\infty}^{p,q} \\;\\cong\\; D_{\\infty}^{p,q} \\big/ D_{\\infty}^{p+1,\\, q-1} ,$$\n\nthat is, \"$E_{\\infty}$ is the associated graded of the limiting couple $D_{\\infty}$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c736faa7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The proof is \"recursion + reading bidegrees\".**\n\n- **Spectral sequence structure**: a derived couple is again an exact couple (the three well-definedness conditions), so one can iterate; and $E_{r+1} = H(E_{r}, d_{r})$ holds by construction.\n- **Bidegree of the differential**: $i_{r}$ is always $(-1, 1)$ and $k_{r}$ always $(1, 0)$ (derivation does not change them), while $j_{r}$ gains one $(1, -1)$ at each derivation. Hence the bidegree of $j_{r}$ is $(r - 1,\\ 1 - r)$, and multiplying by $k_{r}$ gives\n\n$$d_{r} = j_{r} k_{r} : \\quad (p, q) \\longmapsto \\bigl(p + r,\\ q - (r - 1)\\bigr) .$$\n\n- **Limiting term**: $D_{r+1} = i_{r}(D_{r}) \\subseteq D_{r}$ is **decreasing**, and its intersection $D_{\\infty}$ is the \"limiting term\"; the image of $i$ has been used up and the image of $k$ removed, leaving exactly the quotient of the $j$ step, so $E_{\\infty}^{p,q} = D_{\\infty}^{p,q} / D_{\\infty}^{p+1, q-1}$.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **How to read it**: \"computing page by page\" in the language of exact couples is just \"deriving the couple once\". $D$ is \"what is already known\", $E$ is \"the quotient on this page\", $i$ is \"forget one layer\", $j$ is \"take the quotient\", $k$ is \"the connecting homomorphism\".\n\n⚠ **Bidegrees are not unique**: the filtration-complex convention $(-1,1)/(0,0)/(1,0)$ is fixed here. In homological notation it is $(1,-1)/(0,0)/(1,0)$; the differential lands one slot differently, and conversion is just an index shift.\n\n⚠ **Convergence is not free**: the stabilization hypothesis must be checked. An exact couple by itself guarantees only the relation between pages, not that $E_{\\infty}$ is the associated graded of anything — counterexamples exist in the unbounded case.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#10e8d739',
      },
    },

    'def.left-invariant-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In words**: a differential form on a Lie group that is \"untouched\" by left translations is completely determined by a multilinear form at the identity — hence on $G$ the left-invariant $k$-forms coincide with $\\bigwedge^{k}\\mathfrak{g}^{\\ast}$.\n\nLet $G$ be an $n$-dimensional Lie group, $\\mathfrak{g} = T_{e}G$, and $L_{g}$ the left translation. A $k$-form $\\omega \\in \\Omega^{k}(G)$ is **left-invariant** if\n\n$$L_{g}^{\\ast}\\omega = \\omega\\qquad (g \\in G),$$\n\nequivalently $\\omega_{g}(v_{1},\\ldots,v_{k}) = \\omega_{e}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}(L_{g^{-1}})_{g}v_{1},\\ldots,\\mathrm{d}(L_{g^{-1}})_{g}v_{k}\\bigr)$ — that is, the values of $\\omega$ on $G$ are uniquely determined by $\\omega_{e}$.\n\nThus the evaluation\n\n$$\\omega \\longmapsto \\omega_{e}$$\n\nis a linear isomorphism from the space of left-invariant $k$-forms onto $\\bigwedge^{k}\\mathfrak{g}^{\\ast}$, of dimension $\\binom{n}{k}$.\n\n**Left-invariant coframe**: take a basis of $\\mathfrak{g}$, say $e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}$, and the dual basis $\\omega^{1}_{e},\\ldots,\\omega^{n}_{e}$ ($\\omega^{i}_{e}(e_{j}) = \\delta^{i}_{\\ j}$), and translate them to the left:\n\n$$\\omega^{i}_{g} := \\mathrm{d}(L_{g^{-1}})^{\\ast}_{e}\\,\\omega^{i}_{e},\\qquad \\omega^{i}_{g}\\bigl((\\tilde e_{j})_{g}\\bigr) = \\delta^{i}_{\\ j},$$\n\nwhere $\\tilde e_{j}$ is the left-invariant vector field corresponding to $e_{j}$. This family $\\omega^{1},\\ldots,\\omega^{n}$ is called a **left-invariant coframe**; the structure constants are defined by $[e_{j},e_{k}] = \\sum_{i}c^{i}_{\\ jk}e_{i}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26ef0ae5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why the evaluation is an isomorphism**: setting $h = e$ in left-invariance gives $\\omega_{g}(\\mathrm{d}(L_{g})_{e}w) = \\omega_{e}(w)$, hence $\\omega_{g} = \\mathrm{d}(L_{g^{-1}})^{\\ast}\\omega_{e}$, i.e. $\\omega$ is determined by $\\omega_{e}$ (injectivity).\n\nConversely: given $\\alpha \\in \\bigwedge^{k}\\mathfrak{g}^{\\ast}$, put $\\omega_{g} := \\mathrm{d}(L_{g^{-1}})^{\\ast}\\alpha$; then $L_{h}^{\\ast}\\omega = \\omega$, because\n\n$$L_{(hg)^{-1}} = L_{g^{-1}}\\circ L_{h^{-1}},\\qquad L_{h^{-1}}\\circ L_{h} = \\operatorname{id},$$\n\nthe two left translations cancel $\\mathrm{d}(L_{h})$ and restore it. Hence $\\omega \\mapsto \\omega_{e}$ is a bijection, and linearity is clear.\n\n**Dual to left-invariant vector fields**: `prop.left-invariant-fields` says left-invariant vector fields are isomorphic to $\\mathfrak{g}$; here left-invariant $k$-forms are isomorphic to $\\bigwedge^{k}\\mathfrak{g}^{\\ast}$.\n\nFor $k = 1$ the two are dual bases — this is the version on a group of the method of moving frames (`def.moving-frame`): the frame itself does not move, only the point does.\n\n**Orientability**: for $k = n$, $\\bigwedge^{n}\\mathfrak{g}^{\\ast}$ is one-dimensional; translating a nonzero element to the left gives an everywhere nonzero $n$-form, so `prop.lie-group-orientable` (a left-invariant top form is everywhere nonzero) shows **every Lie group is orientable** ($S^{1}$, $\\operatorname{SO}(3)$ and $\\operatorname{GL}(n,\\mathbb{R})$ are, while a general manifold need not be).\n\n**The Haar-measure half**: $\\omega^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\omega^{n}$ is a left-invariant volume form, and its absolute value gives a left-invariant measure (Haar measure). On a compact group left- and right-invariant measures differ by a constant (the modular function); in the noncompact case they need not — here only the form side is used.\n\n⚠ Left-invariant **forms** and left-invariant **vector fields** are different families of objects, both indexed by $g$; do not confuse them: the component matrix of the former is the inverse transpose of the Jacobian, that of the latter is the Jacobian itself.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#81569b70',
      },
    },

    'prop.skyscraper-injective': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a topological space, $x \\in X$, and $A$ an abelian group. If $A$ is **injective** in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ (i.e. $A$ is a divisible group), then the skyscraper sheaf $i_{x,*}A$ is **injective** in the category of abelian sheaves $\\mathbf{Ab}(X)$.\n\nConversely, if $i_{x,*}A$ is injective in $\\mathbf{Ab}(X)$, then $A$ is injective in $\\mathbf{Ab}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5ed47937',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof (moving injectivity to the stalk).** The key is a natural isomorphism: for every sheaf $\\mathcal{F}$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Ab}(X)}\\bigl(\\mathcal{F},\\ i_{x,*}A\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Ab}}\\bigl(\\mathcal{F}_{x},\\ A\\bigr),$$\n\ncoming from the adjunction $i_{x}^{-1} \\dashv i_{x,*}$ and \"$i_{x}^{-1}\\mathcal{F} = \\mathcal{F}_{x}$\".\n\nInjectivity must be tested as: for every injective sheaf map $\\mathcal{G} \\rightarrowtail \\mathcal{H}$, the map $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal{H}, i_{x,*}A) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal{G}, i_{x,*}A)$ is surjective.\n\nUsing the isomorphism above, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Ab}}(\\mathcal{H}_{x},\\ A) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Ab}}(\\mathcal{G}_{x},\\ A)$$\n\nbeing surjective. Taking stalks is a filtered colimit and **exact**, so $\\mathcal{G}_{x} \\rightarrowtail \\mathcal{H}_{x}$ is an injection of abelian groups; $A$ is injective, so the displayed map is surjective. ∎\n\n**Converse**: let $B \\rightarrowtail C$ be an injection in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ together with a map $B \\to A$.\n\n$i_{x,*}$ is exact, so $i_{x,*}B \\rightarrowtail i_{x,*}C$ is an injection of sheaves;\n\n$i_{x,*}A$ is injective, so $\\operatorname{Hom}(i_{x,*}C, i_{x,*}A) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(i_{x,*}B, i_{x,*}A)$ is surjective, and under the same adjunction isomorphism this is precisely the lift of $\\operatorname{Hom}(C, A) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(B, A)$ — hence the latter is surjective and $A$ is injective.\n\n∎\n\n**Why $A$ must be divisible**: in $\\mathbf{Ab}$, injective $=$ divisible. For $A = \\mathbb{Z}$ the skyscraper sheaf is **not** injective ($\\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrowtail \\mathbb{Q}$ cannot be lifted), so building injective sheaves requires first enlarging each stalk with an injective hull.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f4e8b6eb',
      },
    },

    'prop.triangle-rotation': {
      statement: {
        en: "In $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{C})$:\n\n1. $K \\xrightarrow{\\ 1\\ } K \\to 0 \\to K[1]$ is a distinguished triangle;\n2. **Rotation**: $K \\xrightarrow{\\ f\\ } L \\xrightarrow{\\ g\\ } M \\xrightarrow{\\ h\\ } K[1]$ is distinguished $\\iff$ the rotated $L \\xrightarrow{\\ g\\ } M \\xrightarrow{\\ h\\ } K[1] \\xrightarrow{\\ -f[1]\\ } L[1]$ is distinguished;\n3. every $f : K \\to L$ embeds (**non-uniquely**) into a distinguished triangle;\n4. morphisms between distinguished triangles extend (**non-uniquely**).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ed0fd08b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Corollary: **the mapping cone is unique in $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{C})$** (unique up to homotopy). Hence \"the cone of $f$\" is a well-defined object in $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{C})$ — although the explicit formula for $M(f)$ depends on the representative of $f$.\n\n**The word \"non-uniquely\" appears twice among the four items — that is exactly the difference between derived and triangulated categories**:\n\n- **① and ② (rotation)**: **this is the core of the triangle axiom**. Given a distinguished $K \\to L \\to M \\to K[1]$, rotating gives $L \\to M \\to K[1] \\to L[1]$ (the third arrow carries the sign $-f[1]$). ⚠ **The sign cannot be dropped**: writing $f[1]$ would contradict what one gets by rotating twice (four rotations return to the start, so the signs must multiply to $+1$).\n- **③ every $f$ embeds in a distinguished triangle**: **this step needs the mapping cone** (`def.mapping-cone`), and is **non-unique** — but different choices differ only by a **non-unique isomorphism** (not a unique one). ⚠ **One cannot say \"cones in $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathcal{C})$ are concrete, hence unique\"**: an explicit cone model only guarantees existence, and the isomorphism is not unique — take a field $k$, $L = k[0]$, $K = L[-1]$, $f = 0$; the cone is $L\\oplus K[1]\\cong k^{2}[0]$ with differential $0$, so for every $a\\ne0$ the matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}1 & a\\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}$ is an automorphism of it, and it is **not homotopic to the identity** ($\\mathrm{d} = 0$ $\\implies$ any homotopy must be zero, yet this one is not). So \"the cone is unique\" holds only up to isomorphism class; the difference between derived and general triangulated categories lies in **constructibility** (there one can take cones explicitly), not in uniqueness.\n- **④ morphisms extend**: **also non-unique**, and **cannot be proved by \"taking a cone\"** (general triangulated categories have no cone construction); one must use axiom TR3.\n\n⚠ **\"Non-unique\" does not mean \"choose arbitrarily\"**: the difference between extensions is controlled (a homotopy, or some degree of freedom), but there is **no canonical choice**. That is why one cannot work \"constructively\" in a triangulated category as in a derived category — extra enhanced structure (dg categories, stable $\\infty$-categories) is needed to turn \"choice\" into \"property\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d01c8ec4',
      },
    },

    'cor.grothendieck-spectral': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{A} \\xrightarrow{F} \\mathcal{A}' \\xrightarrow{G} \\mathcal{A}''$ be **left exact** additive functors, with $\\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{A}'$ having enough injectives and $F$ sending injectives to $G$-acyclic objects. Then there is a spectral sequence (⚠ left exactness cannot be dropped: for $F = \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}/2, -)$ and $G = -\\otimes \\mathbb{Z}/2$, $G$ kills all injectives and $E_{2}$ vanishes, while $GF \\cong F$ gives $R^{0}(GF)(\\mathbb{Z}/2) = \\mathbb{Z}/2$)\n\n$$E^{p,q}_{2} = R^{p}G\\bigl(R^{q}F(M)\\bigr) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; R^{p+q}(G \\circ F)(M).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#62144043',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **What it is for**: to compute the derived functors $R^{n}(G \\circ F)$ of a composite, one may **first compute $R^{q}F$, then $R^{p}G$**, stitching the two layers together with a spectral sequence. This is the most basic \"two-step\" tool in homological algebra.\n\n**Proof (double complex + two spectral sequences).** Take an injective resolution of $M$, say $I^{\\bullet}$, and put $A^{\\bullet} := F I^{\\bullet}$.\n\n- By hypothesis $I^{n}$ is injective, so $FI^{n}$ is $G$-acyclic, and by **left exactness** of $F$ the sequence $0 \\to FM \\to FI^{\\bullet}$ is exact; hence $A^{\\bullet} = FI^{\\bullet}$ is a right resolution of $FM$ whose terms are $G$-acyclic (⚠ this step uses left exactness; without it $FI^{\\bullet}$ only computes $RF(M)$ and need not resolve $FM$), so $R(G\\circ F)M = G A^{\\bullet}$ in the derived category (Leray acyclicity), i.e. $R^{n}(G\\circ F)M = H^{n}(GA^{\\bullet})$;\n- then take a **Cartan–Eilenberg resolution** of $A^{\\bullet}$, say $J^{\\bullet,\\bullet}$ (each $J^{p,\\bullet}$ is a resolution of $A^{p}$ that is $G$-acyclic, and the homologies in both directions are computable);\n- the two spectral sequences of the double complex: filtering by $p$ first converges to $H^{p+q}(G J^{\\bullet,\\bullet}) = R^{p+q}(G\\circ F)M$, and its $E_{2}$ page is the \"vertical then horizontal\" $R^{p}G\\bigl(H^{q}(A^{\\bullet})\\bigr) = R^{p}G\\bigl(R^{q}FM\\bigr)$;\n\nthe other spectral sequence (horizontal then vertical) degenerates at its $E_{2}$ page because every column is an acyclic resolution, and both converge to the same thing.\n\n∎\n\nThe details are in the first two references: existence of the Cartan–Eilenberg resolution, and convergence of the double-complex spectral sequence together with the identification of the $E_{2}$ page.\n\n**Typical applications**: sheaf cohomology — sheafify then take sections, Čech cohomology to de Rham cohomology, the Lyndon–Hochschild–Serre spectral sequence in group homology — all are special cases.\n\n**Why require $F$ to send injectives to $G$-acyclics**: this is what lets an injective resolution under $F$ serve directly as a $G$-acyclic resolution, so that the two sides match up.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#35cf7ba0',
      },
    },

    'def.row-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$, with $m$ row vectors $a_1, \\ldots, a_m \\in K^{n}$. The subspace they span,\n\n$\\operatorname{Row}(A) := \\operatorname{span}\\{a_1, \\ldots, a_m\\} \\subseteq K^{n}$\n\nis the **row space** of $A$, and its dimension is the **row rank**. Equivalently, $\\operatorname{Row}(A) = \\operatorname{Col}(A^{\\mathsf T})$, the column space of $A^{\\mathsf T}$.\n\n**Elementary row operations preserve the row space**: left multiplication by an invertible matrix only replaces the row vectors by another basis (row operations replace the rows by invertible linear combinations of them), so row-equivalent matrices have the same row space. Hence the nonzero rows of a row echelon form give a basis of $\\operatorname{Row}(A)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f2076fb9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The row space lives in $K^{n}$ (a row has length $n$), while the column space lives in $K^{m}$ — in general they are **not the same space**, so do not confuse them.\n\nDimension: $\\dim \\operatorname{Row}(A) = \\operatorname{rank} A$ (the row rank equals the rank of the matrix).\n\nThe row space and the solution space of the homogeneous system are orthogonal: $x \\in \\ker A$ if and only if $x$ is orthogonal to every row vector; hence $\\dim \\operatorname{Row}(A) + \\dim \\ker A = n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8731fd13',
      },
    },

    'def.column-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$, with columns $c_1, \\ldots, c_n \\in K^{m}$. The subspace\n\n$\\operatorname{Col}(A) := \\operatorname{span}\\{c_1, \\ldots, c_n\\} = \\{\\, A x : x \\in K^{n} \\,\\} \\subseteq K^{m}$\n\nis the **column space** of $A$, that is, the **image** of the linear map $T_A : K^{n} \\to K^{m}$, $T_A(x) = Ax$. Its dimension is the **column rank**, which equals $\\operatorname{rank} A$.\n\n**Solvability of systems**: $b \\in \\operatorname{Col}(A)$ if and only if $Ax = b$ has a solution. After reducing $A$ to reduced row echelon form, the pivot columns — that is, the pivot columns of $A$ — give a basis of $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f4033e28',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The column space is \"where $A$ can reach\"; the null space (solution space of the homogeneous system) is \"which directions $A$ crushes to $0$\". Their dimensions add up to $n$.\n\nRow and column spaces have the same dimension (both equal the rank), but they are subspaces of different spaces; the link comes from transposition: $\\operatorname{Col}(A) = \\operatorname{Row}(A^{\\mathsf T})$.\n\nRank–nullity: $\\dim \\operatorname{Col}(A) + \\dim \\ker A = n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b53e4519',
      },
    },

    'def.row-echelon-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$. If\n\n- every zero row lies below every nonzero row;\n- in each nonzero row, the column of the first nonzero entry (called a **pivot**) lies strictly to the right of the pivot of the row above,\n\nthen $R$ is in **row echelon form**. If moreover every pivot equals $1$ and every other entry in a pivot column is $0$, then $R$ is in **reduced row echelon form** (RREF).\n\n**Theorem (elimination)**: every $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$ can be brought to reduced row echelon form by finitely many elementary row operations, and this reduced row echelon form is **unique**. The number of pivots equals $\\operatorname{rank} A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#69d7e425',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Uniqueness is the point of RREF: row echelon form is not unique (one may continue performing row operations), but \"pivots equal to $1$, all other entries in pivot columns zero\" removes every degree of freedom and leaves exactly one form.\n\nUses: solving linear systems (pivot columns correspond to basic variables, the other columns to free variables), computing rank (count the pivots), inverting (reduce $[A \\mid I]$), and finding a basis of the column space (the pivot columns).\n\nElementary row operations correspond to left multiplication by elementary matrices; RREF is the \"normal form under row operations\", while equivalence also allows column operations, with normal form $\\operatorname{diag}(I_r, 0)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#77ad844d',
      },
    },

    'thm.smith-normal-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain and $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(R)$. Then there exist invertible matrices $P \\in \\operatorname{GL}_{m}(R)$ and $Q \\in \\operatorname{GL}_{n}(R)$ such that\n\n$P A Q = \\operatorname{diag}(d_1, d_2, \\ldots, d_r, 0, \\ldots, 0),$\n\nwhere $d_i \\ne 0$ and $d_1 \\mid d_2 \\mid \\cdots \\mid d_r$. The elements $d_i$ are determined by $A$ up to units and are called the **invariant factors** of $A$.\n\nEquivalently, $d_1 \\cdots d_k$ is the greatest common divisor of all minors of $A$ of size $k$ (the **determinantal divisors**), which gives a concrete algorithm for uniqueness.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a657d1fc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to equivalence over a field**: when $K$ is a field, $K$ is a PID, and the Smith normal form degenerates to $\\operatorname{diag}(I_r, 0)$, so over a field equivalence is classified by rank alone.\n\n**Proof idea**: in the equivalence class of $A$, choose a matrix whose $(1,1)$ entry generates a maximal ideal, and show that it divides every entry (otherwise Bézout puts the gcd into position $(1,1)$, producing a larger ideal); then clear the first row and column and induct on the remaining block. Uniqueness uses the determinantal divisors.\n\n**Typical examples**: for $R = \\mathbb{Z}$, $\\operatorname{diag}(2, 6)$ satisfies $2 \\mid 6$; for $R = K[x]$, applying the Smith normal form to $xI - A$ yields the nonconstant invariant factors of the linear operator $A$ (see rational canonical form).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#452d165e',
      },
    },

    'def.invariant-factor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain and let the Smith normal form of $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(R)$ be\n\n$P A Q = \\operatorname{diag}(d_1, \\ldots, d_r, 0, \\ldots, 0), \\qquad d_1 \\mid d_2 \\mid \\cdots \\mid d_r .$\n\nThe nonzero elements $d_i$ (unique up to units; for $R = K[x]$ one takes the monic representative) are the **invariant factors** of $A$.\n\nIn particular, for $A \\in M_n(K)$, the nonconstant invariant factors of $xI - A \\in M_n(K[x])$ are called the **invariant factors** of the linear operator $A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ba99bf66',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to rational canonical form**: if $d_1 \\mid \\cdots \\mid d_s$ are the nonconstant invariant factors of $xI - A$, then $A$ is similar to $\\operatorname{diag}(C_{d_1}, \\ldots, C_{d_s})$, where $C_d$ is the companion matrix; this is exactly the rational canonical form.\n\n**Relation to minimal and characteristic polynomials**: the largest invariant factor $d_s$ is the minimal polynomial, and the product $d_1 \\cdots d_s$ is the characteristic polynomial.\n\n**Relation to elementary divisors**: factor each $d_i$ into powers of irreducible polynomials; the resulting prime-power factors are the elementary divisors. When the field splits, the Jordan normal form is stated in terms of elementary divisors.\n\n**Matrix version vs operator version**: the Smith normal form is stated for an arbitrary matrix; the invariant factors of an operator are the invariant factors of $xI-A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#27218ccd',
      },
    },

    'def.p-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p$ be a prime and $G$ a finite group. If $|G| = p^{n}$ with $n \\ge 0$, then $G$ is a **$p$-group**.\n\nEquivalently, every element of $G$ has order a power of $p$: the order of an element divides $|G|$, hence can only be a power of $p$; conversely, if every element has order a power of $p$, then by Cauchy's theorem $|G|$ has no other prime divisor, so $|G|$ is a power of $p$.\n\nA subgroup $H \\le G$ that is itself a $p$-group is a **$p$-subgroup**; of $G$, a maximal $p$-subgroup (under inclusion) is called a **Sylow $p$-subgroup**, see `def.sylow-subgroup`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#22675cd0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: $\\mathbb{Z}/p^{n}$, $V_4 = \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/2$; the center of a $p$-group is nontrivial (a direct consequence of the class equation).\n\n⚠ A $p$-group need not be abelian: $D_8$ (the dihedral group of order $8$) is a $2$-group but is not abelian.\n\n⭐ A $p$-group is solvable, and in fact nilpotent: each step of its lower central series grows strictly and reaches $G$ in finitely many steps (the center is nontrivial, then induct on $G/Z$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#daa08bdc',
      },
    },

    'def.commutator-subgroup': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group. For $x, y \\in G$, the element $[x, y] := x y x^{-1} y^{-1}$ is the **commutator** of $x$ and $y$. The subgroup generated by all commutators,\n\n$[G, G] := \\langle\\, [x, y] : x, y \\in G \\,\\rangle \\le G$\n\nis the **commutator subgroup** (also called the **derived subgroup**) of $G$.\n\n[G,G] is normal, and the quotient $G/[G,G]$ is abelian. More strongly, if $N \\trianglelefteq G$ is such that $G/N$ is abelian, then $[G,G] \\subseteq N$; hence $G/[G,G]$ is the **maximal abelian quotient** of $G$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fdb08c30',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Commutators themselves are not associative: in general $[[x,y],z] \\ne [x,[y,z]]$; the subgroup they generate is the useful object.\n\n⭐ Examples: $[S_n, S_n] = A_n$; $[G, G] = \\{e\\}$ if and only if $G$ is abelian.\n\n⭐ Relation to solvability: set $G^{(0)} = G$ and $G^{(i+1)} = [G^{(i)}, G^{(i)}]$ (the **derived series**). Then $G$ is solvable if and only if some $G^{(i)} = \\{e\\}$.\n\n⚠ This differs from the **lower central series** $\\gamma_{i+1} = [G, \\gamma_i]$: the derived series commutators each term with itself, while the lower central series only commutators with $G$; termination of the lower central series gives nilpotence, which is stronger than solvability.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c493c5c0',
      },
    },

    'def.orbit': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let a group $G$ act on a set $X$ and let $x \\in X$. The set\n\n$\\operatorname{Orb}(x) := G x := \\{\\, g \\cdot x : g \\in G \\,\\} \\subseteq X$\n\nis the **orbit** of $x$.\n\nThe relation $x \\sim y \\iff \\exists g \\in G,\\ y = g \\cdot x$ is an equivalence relation on $X$, and the orbits are precisely its equivalence classes; hence $X$ is the **disjoint union** of the orbits. The action is **transitive** if $X$ has only one orbit.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#462ef353',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ When $G$ is finite, the orbit size divides $|G|$: orbit–stabilizer gives $|Gx| = [G : G_x]$ (see `thm.orbit-stabilizer`; for infinite groups read it as equipotence).\n\n⭐ Examples: $S_n$ acts transitively on $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$; the orbits of the conjugation action are conjugacy classes; the left multiplication action has only one orbit.\n\n⚠ Orbits of different $x$ are either equal or disjoint; they never \"partially overlap\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#efd6b1f1'
      },
    },

    'def.stabilizer': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let a group $G$ act on a set $X$ and let $x \\in X$. The set\n\n$G_x := \\operatorname{Stab}(x) := \\{\\, g \\in G : g \\cdot x = x \\,\\} \\le G$\n\nis the **stabilizer** of $x$ (or isotropy subgroup).\n\n$G_x$ is a subgroup of $G$; the **kernel** of the action is $\\bigcap_{x \\in X} G_x$. Orbit–stabilizer gives a bijection $G/G_x \\to Gx$, $gG_x \\mapsto g \\cdot x$. Conjugate points have conjugate stabilizers: $G_{g \\cdot x} = g G_x g^{-1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#23d8b956',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ When $G$ is finite, the larger the stabilizer the smaller the orbit; the two are balanced exactly by $|G| = |Gx| \\cdot |G_x|$.\n\n⭐ The action is **free** iff all stabilizers are trivial (e.g. left multiplication); it is **faithful** iff the kernel is trivial.\n\n⭐ Examples: in the natural action of $S_n$, $G_n \\cong S_{n-1}$; for the conjugation action, $G_x = C_G(x)$ is the centralizer, see `def.centralizer`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#57b60544'
      },
    },

    'def.normalizer': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group and $S \\subseteq G$. The set\n\n$N_G(S) := \\{\\, g \\in G : g S g^{-1} = S \\,\\} \\le G$\n\nis the **normalizer** of $S$ in $G$. For a subgroup $H \\le G$, $N_G(H)$ is the **largest** subgroup of $G$ containing $H$ in which $H$ is normal.\n\nIf $H \\le G$, then $H \\trianglelefteq G \\iff N_G(H) = G$; also $H \\subseteq N_G(H)$ and $H \\trianglelefteq N_G(H)$. Sylow counting: $n_p = [G : N_G(P)]$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ef6307ce',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Normalizer vs centralizer: $C_G(H) \\subseteq N_G(H)$, and in general they are not equal. $N_G(H)/C_G(H)$ embeds into $\\operatorname{Aut}(H)$ (by conjugation); this is the standard \"normalizer modulo centralizer\" statement.\n\n⭐ Examples: in $S_3$, $N_{S_3}(\\langle(12)\\rangle) = \\langle(12)\\rangle$ (the three transpositions are conjugate to one another, so the normalizer does not grow); $N_{S_3}(A_3) = S_3$.\n\n⚠ $N_G(S)$ only requires that the set $S$ be sent to itself, not that each element be fixed; the latter is the centralizer.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe30cf1f',
      },
    },

    'def.sylow-subgroup': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a finite group with $|G| = p^{a} m$, where $p$ is a prime and $p \\nmid m$. A subgroup of $G$ of order $p^{a}$, call it $P$, is called a **Sylow $p$-subgroup**.\n\nEquivalently, $P$ is, in $G$, a **maximal $p$-subgroup** (under inclusion). The set of all Sylow $p$-subgroups is denoted $\\operatorname{Syl}_p(G)$; if $P \\in \\operatorname{Syl}_p(G)$, then $[G : P] = m$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#aa6f763b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Existence, conjugacy and counting are in `thm.sylow`; this node only defines the object.\n\n⭐ Example: in $S_3$ ($|S_3| = 6 = 2 \\cdot 3$) the Sylow $3$-subgroups are $A_3$ (unique, hence normal), and the Sylow $2$-subgroups are the three subgroups of order $2$ generated by the three transpositions.\n\n⚠ A $p$-subgroup is a subgroup whose order is a prime power; a Sylow $p$-subgroup is the **largest** such subgroup, and a group may have several Sylow $p$-subgroups.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dbc835ef',
      },
    },

    'lem.p-group-subgroups': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a finite $p$-group and $H < G$ a proper subgroup. Then\n\n$H < N_G(H),$\n\nthat is, $H$ is properly contained in its normalizer in $G$.\n\nIn particular, a subgroup of index $p$ is normal; and the center of a $p$-group is nontrivial.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bee5c952',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is on the edge `imp.p-group-subgroups`: induct on $|G|$, use the class equation to get $Z(G) \\ne \\{e\\}$, then split into the two cases $HZ(G) < G$ and $HZ(G) = G$.\n\n⭐ Use: induction on $i$ shows that a $p$-group has subgroups of every order $p^{i}$ ($0 \\le i \\le n$); this is the key step in the \"$i < a$ part\" of the Sylow existence proof.\n\n⚠ The conclusion fails for general finite groups: in $S_3$, for $H = \\langle(12)\\rangle$ one has $N_{S_3}(H) = H$, but $S_3$ is not a $p$-group.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1d1f53a4',
      },
    },

    'def.ring-hom': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R, S$ be rings with identity. A map $f : R \\to S$ is a **ring homomorphism** if\n\n$f(a+b) = f(a)+f(b), \\qquad f(ab) = f(a)f(b), \\qquad f(1_R) = 1_S$\n\nfor all $a, b \\in R$.\n\nThe **kernel** $\\ker f := \\{\\, a \\in R : f(a) = 0 \\,\\}$ is an **ideal** of $R$; the **image** $\\operatorname{im} f$ is a subring of $S$. The map $f$ is injective if and only if $\\ker f = 0$; a bijective ring homomorphism is an **isomorphism**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d22b9c6f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: $\\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$; $K[x] \\to K$, $f \\mapsto f(a)$ (evaluation); $\\mathbb{C} \\to \\mathbb{C}$, $z \\mapsto \\bar z$ (conjugation, a ring isomorphism but not a $\\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism).\n\n⚠ Some texts do not require $f(1_R) = 1_S$; these notes use the convention \"rings with identity and homomorphisms preserving $1$\". Without preserving $1$ the kernel is still an ideal, but \"$R/\\ker f \\cong \\operatorname{im} f$\" then requires aligning the identity of the image (the $1$ of the image).\n\n⭐ First isomorphism theorem: $R/\\ker f \\cong \\operatorname{im} f$ (for quotient rings see `def.quotient-ring`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7a066d1d',
      },
    },

    'def.quotient-ring': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring and $I \\subseteq A$ an ideal. On the additive quotient group $A/I$ define\n\n$(a+I)+(b+I) := (a+b)+I, \\qquad (a+I)(b+I) := ab+I .$\n\nThese operations are well defined and make $A/I$ a ring, the **quotient ring**; the quotient map $\\pi : A \\to A/I$, $\\pi(a) = a+I$, is a ring homomorphism with $\\ker \\pi = I$.\n\n**Universal property**: if $f : A \\to B$ is a ring homomorphism with $f(I) = 0$, then there is a unique ring homomorphism $\\bar f : A/I \\to B$ with $\\bar f \\circ \\pi = f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2fe619d0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Why it must be an ideal: to make $(a+x)(b+y) \\equiv ab \\pmod I$ hold one needs $ay, xb, xy \\in I$; now $x, y \\in I$ while $a, b$ are arbitrary, so $I$ must absorb multiplication from both sides.\n\n⭐ Examples: $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$, $K[x]/(f)$ (a field when $f$ is irreducible), $\\mathbb{F}_p[x]/(x^{2}+1)$.\n\n⭐ If $A$ is a commutative ring with unit, then $A/I$ is an integral domain iff $I$ is prime; $A/I$ is a field iff $I$ is maximal (both objects come in later nodes; this pack only gives the construction of the quotient ring). Commutativity cannot be dropped: the ring $M_2(\\mathbb{R})$ has zero ideal $(0)$, which is a maximal two-sided ideal, but $M_2(\\mathbb{R})/(0)\\cong M_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is neither a domain nor a field.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d514f337'
      },
    },

    'def.nilpotent-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group. Define the **lower central series** inductively by\n\n$\\gamma_0 = G, \\qquad \\gamma_{i+1} = [G, \\gamma_i] = \\langle\\, [g, x] : g \\in G,\\ x \\in \\gamma_i \\,\\rangle .$\n\nIf there exists $c \\ge 0$ with $\\gamma_c = \\{e\\}$, then $G$ is **nilpotent**; the smallest such $c$ is the **nilpotency class** of $G$.\n\nEquivalently, the **upper central series** $Z_0 = \\{e\\}$, $Z_{i+1}/Z_i := Z(G/Z_i)$ reaches $G$ in finitely many steps.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#38d45f5f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: a nontrivial abelian group has nilpotency class $1$ ($\\gamma_1 = \\{e\\}$), the trivial group has class $0$; $D_8$ has class $2$; every finite $p$-group is nilpotent; $S_3$ is not nilpotent ($\\gamma_1 = A_3$, $\\gamma_2 = A_3 \\ne \\{e\\}$).\n\n⭐ Nilpotent $\\implies$ solvable: inductively $\\gamma_i \\subseteq G^{(i)}$, so the derived series terminates no later than the lower central series. The converse fails ($S_3$ is solvable but not nilpotent).\n\n⚠ Subgroups and quotients of nilpotent groups are nilpotent, but extensions need not be nilpotent.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8cc73a67'
      },
    },

    'def.automorphism-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group. An isomorphism from $G$ to itself, $\\sigma : G \\to G$, is called an **automorphism**; all automorphisms form a group under composition,\n\n$\\operatorname{Aut}(G) \\le \\operatorname{Sym}(G),$\n\nthe **automorphism group** of $G$.\n\nFor $g \\in G$, the map $c_g(x) := gxg^{-1}$ is an automorphism, an **inner automorphism**; all inner automorphisms\n\n$\\operatorname{Inn}(G) := \\{\\, c_g : g \\in G \\,\\} \\trianglelefteq \\operatorname{Aut}(G)$\n\nform a normal subgroup, and $\\operatorname{Inn}(G) \\cong G/Z(G)$. The quotient $\\operatorname{Out}(G) := \\operatorname{Aut}(G)/\\operatorname{Inn}(G)$ is the **outer automorphism group**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4497f9ef',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: $\\operatorname{Aut}(\\mathbb{Z}/n) \\cong (\\mathbb{Z}/n)^{\\times}$; $\\operatorname{Aut}(V_4) \\cong S_3$; for $n \\ne 6$, $\\operatorname{Aut}(S_n) = \\operatorname{Inn}(S_n) \\cong S_n$.\n\n⚠ $n = 6$ is the exception: $S_6$ has outer automorphisms, and $\\operatorname{Out}(S_6)$ has order $2$.\n\n⭐ The kernel of the inner action is the center $Z(G)$: $c_g = \\mathrm{id} \\iff g \\in Z(G)$, which is exactly the source of $G/Z(G) \\cong \\operatorname{Inn}(G)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7500b4c7',
      },
    },

    'def.inner-automorphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group and $g \\in G$. The map\n\n$c_g : G \\to G, \\qquad c_g(x) := g x g^{-1}$\n\nis an automorphism of $G$, the **inner automorphism** determined by $g$. All inner automorphisms\n\n$\\operatorname{Inn}(G) := \\{\\, c_g : g \\in G \\,\\}$\n\nform a group under composition, and $\\operatorname{Inn}(G) \\cong G/Z(G)$, $\\operatorname{Inn}(G) \\trianglelefteq \\operatorname{Aut}(G)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dab252ea',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Inner automorphisms are exactly the image of the **conjugation action** of $G$ on itself: $c_g$ is the map $x \\mapsto g \\cdot x$ (conjugation).\n\n⭐ The inner automorphism group is trivial $\\iff$ $G$ is abelian; then $\\operatorname{Inn}(G) = \\{\\mathrm{id}\\}$ and $\\operatorname{Out}(G) = \\operatorname{Aut}(G)$.\n\n⭐ Examples: the inner automorphisms of $S_n$ are exactly the conjugations preserving cycle type; the inner automorphisms of $GL_n(K)$ are $A \\mapsto P A P^{-1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5e4c3d1b',
      },
    },

    'def.principal-ideal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring with identity and $a \\in A$. The set\n\n$(a) := aA := \\{\\, a x : x \\in A \\,\\}$\n\nis an ideal of $A$, the **principal ideal** generated by $a$. If an ideal $I$, for some $a$, satisfies $I = (a)$, then $I$ is **principal**.\n\nIf every ideal of $A$ is principal, then $A$ is a **principal ideal domain** (PID; see also `def.pid`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fd39b2b0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: every ideal of $\\mathbb{Z}$ is $(n)$, so $\\mathbb{Z}$ is a PID; over a field $K$, $K[x]$ is also a PID.\n\n⚠ In a general ring, \"the multiples of $a$\" need not be a two-sided ideal: in $M_2(\\mathbb{R})$, $E_{11} M_2$ is not a two-sided ideal; for noncommutative rings one takes \"the smallest two-sided ideal containing $a$\".\n\n⚠ In $K[x,y]$, $(x, y)$ is not principal: it cannot be generated by a single polynomial.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#80d61657',
      },
    },

    'def.field-of-fractions': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be an integral domain. On the set $A \\times (A \\setminus \\{0\\})$ define\n\n$(a, s) \\sim (b, t) \\iff a t = b s .$\n\nThis is an equivalence relation; the quotient set is denoted $\\operatorname{Frac}(A)$, its elements are written $\\frac{a}{s}$, and with the operations\n\n$\\frac{a}{s} + \\frac{b}{t} := \\frac{a t + b s}{s t}, \\qquad \\frac{a}{s} \\cdot \\frac{b}{t} := \\frac{a b}{s t}$\n\nit is a **field**, the **field of fractions** of $A$. The map $a \\mapsto \\frac{a}{1}$ is an injective ring homomorphism.\n\n**Universal property**: if $K$ is a field and $\\varphi : A \\to K$ is an injective ring homomorphism, then there is a unique field homomorphism $\\bar\\varphi : \\operatorname{Frac}(A) \\to K$ with $\\bar\\varphi(a/1) = \\varphi(a)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d4fd74a3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: $\\operatorname{Frac}(\\mathbb{Z}) = \\mathbb{Q}$; $\\operatorname{Frac}(K[x]) = K(x)$ (the field of rational functions).\n\n⚠ The ring must be an **integral domain**: with zero divisors the equivalence class of $\\frac{a}{s}$ degenerates ($s$ may become a zero divisor) and the construction fails.\n\n⭐ The field of fractions is \"the smallest field containing $A$\"; the universal property says exactly this.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c47ad346',
      },
    },

    'def.module-hom': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring with identity and $M, N$ be $A$-modules. A map $f : M \\to N$ is an **$A$-module homomorphism** if\n\n$f(m + m') = f(m) + f(m'), \\qquad f(a m) = a f(m)$\n\nfor all $m, m' \\in M$ and $a \\in A$.\n\nThe set of all $A$-module homomorphisms is denoted $\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M, N)$; under pointwise addition it is an abelian group. The kernel $\\ker f$ and the image $\\operatorname{im} f$ are both submodules; the map $f$ is an isomorphism $\\iff$ it is bijective.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#169433c8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: for $A = \\mathbb{Z}$, module homomorphisms are abelian group homomorphisms; for $A = K$, they are linear maps.\n\n⚠ In general $\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M, N)$ is only an abelian group, not an $A$-module; a natural $A$-module structure exists only when $A$ is commutative.\n\n⭐ First isomorphism theorem: $M/\\ker f \\cong \\operatorname{im} f$ (for quotient modules see `def.quotient-module`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#677f779d',
      },
    },

    'def.quotient-module': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring with identity, $M$ an $A$-module and $N \\subseteq M$ a submodule. On the additive quotient group $M/N$ define\n\n$(m + N) + (m' + N) := (m + m') + N, \\qquad a(m + N) := a m + N .$\n\nThese operations are well defined and make $M/N$ an $A$-module, the **quotient module**; the quotient map $\\pi : M \\to M/N$ is a module homomorphism with $\\ker \\pi = N$.\n\n**Universal property**: if $f : M \\to L$ is a module homomorphism with $f(N) = 0$, then there is a unique module homomorphism $\\bar f : M/N \\to L$ with $\\bar f \\circ \\pi = f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7ee3a130',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Why a submodule is necessary: for $a(m + n) \\equiv a m \\pmod N$ one needs $a n \\in N$ for all $a \\in A$ and $n \\in N$.\n\n⭐ Examples: the $\\mathbb{Z}$-module $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$; a vector space $V/W$; $A/I$ as an $A$-module is a quotient module.\n\n⭐ First isomorphism: $M/\\ker f \\cong \\operatorname{im} f$, see `def.module-hom`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5834e2dd',
      },
    },

    'def.algebraic-closure': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field. A field extension $\\bar K / K$ is an **algebraic closure** of $K$ if\n\n1. $\\bar K$ is **algebraically closed** (every nonconstant polynomial has a root in $\\bar K$, see `def.algebraically-closed`);\n2. $\\bar K / K$ is an **algebraic extension** (every element is a root of some nonzero polynomial over $K$).\n\nBy Zorn's lemma every field has an algebraic closure; and any two algebraic closures are $K$-isomorphic (unique up to isomorphism).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#554b2d3a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: $\\mathbb{C}$ is the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{R}$; $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}$ (all algebraic numbers over $\\mathbb{Q}$) is the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{Q}$; the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{F}_p$ is $\\bigcup_{n} \\mathbb{F}_{p^n}$.\n\n⚠ The algebraic closure is not \"a unique set\" but \"unique up to isomorphism\"; different constructions give $K$-isomorphic fields.\n\n⭐ Existence uses Zorn, but first one must pass to a set: all algebraic extensions form a proper class and cannot be ordered directly. Give the candidate field a cardinal bound $\\le \\max(|K|, \\aleph_0)$ first (each algebraic element is a root of some nonzero polynomial in $K[x]$, while $K[x]$ has only $\\max(|K|,\\aleph_0)$ elements and each polynomial has finitely many roots, so the total number of algebraic elements is at most this bound), keep only algebraic extensions within the bound, and apply Zorn to those; a maximal element must be algebraically closed (otherwise add one more root and extend).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b40182e8'
      },
    },

    'def.maximal-ideal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring with identity and $M \\subset A$ a proper ideal. Then $M$ is a **maximal ideal** if there is no ideal $J$ with\n\n$M \\subset J \\subset A.$\n\nEquivalently, the quotient ring $A/M$ is a **field** (see `def.quotient-ring`). Every proper ideal is contained in some maximal ideal (by Zorn's lemma).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2c81e401',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Maximal $\\implies$ prime: a field is a domain, so $A/M$ is a domain. The converse fails: in $\\mathbb{Z}[x]$, $(x)$ is prime but not maximal ($(x) \\subset (2, x)$).\n\n⭐ In a PID, every nonzero prime ideal is maximal: in $K[x]$, $(f)$ is maximal $\\iff$ $f$ is irreducible.\n\n⭐ Examples: in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $(p)$ is maximal; in the polynomial ring in n variables, the ideal generated by the variables is maximal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7ad6a2d7',
      },
    },

    'def.centralizer': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group and $S \\subseteq G$. The set\n\n$$C_G(S) := \\{\\, g \\in G : g s = s g \\ \\text{for all}\\ s \\in S \\,\\} \\le G$$\n\nis the **centralizer** of $S$ in $G$; put $C_G(x) := C_G(\\{x\\})$, with the convention $C_G(\\emptyset) = G$.\n\n$C_G(S)$ is a subgroup. $C_G(G) = Z(G)$ is the **center** of $G$. Under the conjugation action $g \\cdot x = gxg^{-1}$, the subgroup $C_G(x)$ is exactly the stabilizer of $x$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d8f660f8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Relation to the normalizer: $C_G(S) \\trianglelefteq N_G(S)$, and $C_G(S) \\subseteq N_G(S)$ (commuting with every element of $S$ certainly preserves it as a set).\n\n⭐ Examples: in $GL_n(K)$ the centralizer of a scalar matrix is all of the invertible matrices (scalar matrices lie in the center); in $S_3$, $C_{S_3}((12)) = \\{e, (12)\\}$.\n\n⭐ In the class equation, the size of a noncentral conjugacy class is $[G : C_G(x)]$, see `thm.class-equation`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c266da15',
      },
    },

    'def.submodule': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring with identity and $M$ an $A$-module. If an additive subgroup $N \\subseteq M$ satisfies\n\n$a \\cdot n \\in N \\qquad \\text{for all } a \\in A,\\ n \\in N,$\n\nthen $N$ is a submodule of $M$, namely an **$A$-submodule**. Equivalently, $N$ is itself an $A$-module under the restricted action, and the inclusion $N \\hookrightarrow M$ is a module homomorphism.\n\nKernels and images of module homomorphisms are submodules; a left ideal of $A$ is exactly a submodule of the left $A$-module $A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a8b5bea4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Three levels of comparison: subgroup (group) $\\subset$ submodule (module) $\\subset$ subspace (module over a field). A submodule is required to be closed under the **ring action**, one condition more than an additive subgroup.\n\n⭐ Examples: for $A = \\mathbb{Z}$ a submodule is just a subgroup $n\\mathbb{Z}$; for $A = K[x]$ a submodule is a $K[x]$-subspace.\n\n⭐ The submodule lattice: arbitrary intersections are submodules, but unions usually are not; the submodule generated by $S$ is the smallest submodule containing $S$. Quotient modules are constructed as $M/N$ (see `def.quotient-module`, a later node).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8564879b'
      },
    },

    'def.fixed-field': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $L/K$ be a field extension and $H \\le \\operatorname{Aut}(L/K)$ a subgroup of the group of $K$-automorphisms. The set\n\n$L^{H} := \\{\\, x \\in L : \\sigma(x) = x \\ \\text{for all } \\sigma \\in H \\,\\}$\n\nis the **fixed field** of $H$. It is a subfield of $L$, and $K \\subseteq L^{H} \\subseteq L$.\n\nIn the Galois correspondence for a finite Galois extension, $H \\mapsto L^{H}$ and the intermediate field $M \\mapsto \\operatorname{Gal}(L/M)$ are mutually inverse (see `thm.galois-correspondence`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3b8f1fb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Reverse inclusion: $H_1 \\subseteq H_2 \\implies L^{H_2} \\subseteq L^{H_1}$ — the larger the group, the fewer objects it fixes.\n\n⭐ Examples: the conjugation automorphism of $\\mathbb{C}/\\mathbb{R}$ generates a group of order $2$, whose fixed field is $\\mathbb{R}$; for the finite field $\\mathbb{F}_{p^n}/\\mathbb{F}_p$, the fixed field of the group generated by Frobenius is $\\mathbb{F}_p$.\n\n⚠ A fixed field is in general **not equal** to some elementary construction of \"the field fixing every element\": it looks at all elements of $H$ together, not at fixed points element by element.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2469a675',
      },
    },

    'def.prime-ideal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring with identity and $P \\subset A$ a proper ideal. Then $P$ is a **prime ideal** if\n\n$a b \\in P \\implies a \\in P \\ \\text{or}\\ b \\in P \\qquad (a, b \\in A).$\n\nEquivalently, the quotient ring $A/P$ is a **domain** (see `def.quotient-ring`).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c699cce0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Examples: in $\\mathbb{Z}$ both $(p)$ ($p$ prime) and $(0)$ are prime; in $K[x]$, $(f)$ is prime $\\iff$ $f$ is irreducible, and $(0)$ is prime too.\n\n⚠ A prime ideal need not be maximal: in $\\mathbb{Z}[x]$, $(x)$ is prime ($\\mathbb{Z}[x]/(x) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$ is a domain) but not maximal.\n\n⭐ A prime ideal is the abstraction of \"divisibility\": $a \\in (p) \\iff p \\mid a$; $ab \\in (p) \\implies p \\mid ab \\implies p \\mid a$ or $p \\mid b$ is exactly Euclid's lemma.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2ed4c3f9',
      },
    },

    'def.cat-equivalence': {
      statement: {
        en: "A functor $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ is an **equivalence of categories** if there exist a functor $G : \\mathcal{C}' \\to \\mathcal{C}$ and natural isomorphisms\n\n$$F \\circ G \\cong 1_{\\mathcal{C}'}, \\qquad G \\circ F \\cong 1_{\\mathcal{C}}$$\n\none then writes $\\mathcal{C} \\simeq \\mathcal{C}'$.\n\nIf the two $\\cong$ are strengthened to equalities $F \\circ G = 1_{\\mathcal{C}'}$ and $G \\circ F = 1_{\\mathcal{C}}$, it is called an **isomorphism of categories**, written $\\mathcal{C} \\cong \\mathcal{C}'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f3feea61',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "An isomorphism requires the two composites to **equal** the identity functors; an equivalence only requires them to be **naturally isomorphic** to the identities. The latter is much weaker and much more useful: for instance, \"finite-dimensional vector spaces\" is equivalent to \"matrices over $\\mathbb{F}$\", but can never be isomorphic (even the cardinalities of the object classes are wrong).\n\nUnder an equivalence, the question \"are two categories the same\" is replaced by \"can they be translated into each other without losing structure\" — every later statement in category theory that \"$\\mathcal{C}$ and $\\mathcal{C}'$ are the same\" means this.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5553189',
      },
    },

    'def.essentially-surjective': {
      statement: {
        en: "A functor $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ is **essentially surjective** if for every object of $\\mathcal{C}'$, say $X'$, there is an object of $\\mathcal{C}$, say $X$, with\n\n$$F(X) \\cong X'$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1e8e87ca',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Note that this is $\\cong$, not $=$: the requirement is that the image of $F$ be **isomorphic to** every object, not that the image of $F$ **be** all objects. This single isomorphism is exactly where equivalence is laxer than isomorphism — and precisely what makes it useful, since most interesting equivalences are not isomorphisms.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4529c1f2',
      },
    },

    'def.cech-functor': {
      statement: {
        en: "On a site $\\mathcal{C}$, the **Čech functor** $\\widehat{H}$ is defined on presheaves by\n\n$$\\widehat{H}(T)(X) \\;:=\\; \\varinjlim_{R \\in J(X)} \\operatorname{Hom}(R, T)$$\n\n(since $J(X)$ is a filter, this is a **filtered colimit**). From $h_{X} \\in J(X)$ and $\\operatorname{Hom}(h_{X}, T) \\cong T(X)$ one obtains a natural map $T \\to \\widehat{H}(T)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#421a0193',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"Taking the limit along larger and larger covering sieves\" turns into a colimit on the $T$ side, because $\\operatorname{Hom}(-, T)$ is **contravariant**, and a contravariant functor takes limits along a filter to filtered colimits of the covariant functor. $\\widehat{H}$ is the first step in pushing a presheaf toward a sheaf.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#94a5f6c0',
      },
    },

    'def.homotopy': {
      statement: {
        en: "Morphisms of complexes $f, g : K \\to L$ are **homotopic** if there is a family of morphisms\n\n$$s^{n} : K^{n} \\longrightarrow L^{n-1}$$\n\nsuch that for every $n$,\n\n$$f^{n} - g^{n} \\;=\\; s^{n+1} \\circ d_{K}^{n} + d_{L}^{n-1} \\circ s^{n}$$\n\nWrite $f \\sim g$; $s$ is a **homotopy**. A morphism homotopic to $0$ is called **null-homotopic**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a70c241b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Null-homotopic morphisms form a **subgroup** of $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}(\\mathcal{C})}(K, L)$; moreover homotopy is an equivalence relation **compatible with composition on both sides** ($f \\sim g$ implies $h \\circ f \\circ k \\sim h \\circ g \\circ k$).\n\nOnly the two facts together allow one to quotient by \"homotopy\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b549ac0',
      },
    },

    'def.mapping-cone': {
      statement: {
        en: "The **mapping cone** of a morphism $f : K \\to L$ is the complex\n\n$$M(f)^{n} := K^{n+1} \\oplus L^{n}, \\qquad d^{n} := \\begin{pmatrix} -d^{n+1} & 0 \\\\ f^{n} & d^{n} \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\n(the matrix is blocked according to $K^{n+1} \\oplus L^{n}$: top left from the differential of $K$, bottom right from the differential of $L$, bottom left the component of $f$; $d^{2} = 0$ holds as soon as $f$ commutes with the differentials). The $k$-**shift** $K[k]$ is defined by $K[k]^{n} := K^{n+k}$, with differential carrying the sign $(-1)^{k} d^{n+k}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8433bd4b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Use: it yields a short exact sequence $0 \\to L \\to M(f) \\to K[1] \\to 0$ (usually not split). Thus **every morphism embeds into an \"almost exact\" sequence**, which is the starting point of triangulated categories — all later definitions of distinguished triangles rest on it.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7e54c7cf',
      },
    },

    'def.effective-equivalence': {
      statement: {
        en: "An **equivalence relation** on an object $X$ is a graph $R \\rightrightarrows X$ such that for every $Y$, the map $\\operatorname{Hom}(Y, R) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(Y, X) \\times \\operatorname{Hom}(Y, X)$ is an equivalence relation (taken in $\\mathbf{Set}$).\n\nIt is **effective** if\n\n$$R \\;\\cong\\; X \\times_{\\overline{X}} X, \\qquad \\overline{X} := \\operatorname{coker}(R \\rightrightarrows X)$$\n\nthat is, $R$ is exactly the kernel pair of the quotient map.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c3cdc6b6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"Effective\" says the **quotient glues nothing extra**: take the equivalence relation, then the quotient, then look at the kernel pair of the quotient map, and one returns to the original equivalence relation.\n\nCompare with sets: for any $f : X \\to Y$ the kernel pair $X \\times_{Y} X$ is always effective ($\\overline{X} \\cong \\operatorname{im} f$); conversely, any equivalence relation $R \\subseteq X \\times X$ with $\\overline{X} = X/R$ always returns $R$.\n\n**In $\\mathbf{Set}$ everything is effective**, but in a general category this has to be required separately.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1b623286',
      },
    },

    'def.generator': {
      statement: {
        en: "In a category $\\mathcal{C}$, a set $S \\subseteq \\mathcal{C}$ is a **set of generators** if the functor\n\n$$\\prod_{a \\in S} h_{a} : \\mathcal{C} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{Set}^{S}, \\qquad X \\mapsto \\bigl(\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(a, X)\\bigr)_{a \\in S}$$\n\nis **faithful**. Equivalently: whenever $f \\ne g : X \\to Y$, there exist $a \\in S$ and $g_{0} : a \\to X$ with $f \\circ g_{0} \\ne g \\circ g_{0}$. When $S = \\{G\\}$, one calls $G$ a **generator**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4de2f5a9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Intuition: **the objects in $S$ \"see\" the difference between all morphisms** — for any two distinct morphisms there is always an arrow from $S$ that separates them.\n\nExamples: $\\mathbf{1}$ is a generator of $\\mathbf{Set}$; $A$ is a generator of $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$; $\\mathcal{C}$ itself is a set of generators of the presheaf category $\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f01f463e',
      },
    },

    'def.pretopology': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **pretopology** on a category $\\mathcal{C}$ assigns to each $X$ a family of **covering families** $\\operatorname{Cov}(X)$ (each element is a family $(X_{i} \\to X)_{i \\in I}$), subject to:\n\n1. **Isomorphisms cover**: every isomorphism $X' \\to X$ belongs to $\\operatorname{Cov}(X)$;\n2. **Pullback stability**: if $(X_{i} \\to X)_{i \\in I} \\in \\operatorname{Cov}(X)$, then for every $f : Y \\to X$,\n\n$$\\bigl(\\, X_{i} \\times_{X} Y \\to Y \\,\\bigr)_{i \\in I} \\in \\operatorname{Cov}(Y)$$\n\n3. **Transitivity**: if $(X_{i} \\to X)_{i \\in I} \\in \\operatorname{Cov}(X)$ and for each $i$ one has $(X_{ij} \\to X_{i})_{j \\in J_{i}} \\in \\operatorname{Cov}(X_{i})$, then $(X_{ij} \\to X)_{i \\in I, j \\in J_{i}} \\in \\operatorname{Cov}(X)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e4826c80',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The three axioms read as one sentence: **isomorphisms cover, covers pull back, and covers of covers are covers**.\n\nExample: on $\\mathbf{Set}$ take $\\operatorname{Cov}(X) = \\{(X_{i} \\to X) : X = \\bigcup_{i} X_{i}\\}$; all three hold — this is the prototype of the word \"cover\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ca7f9300',
      },
    },

    'def.quasi-compact': {
      statement: {
        en: "In a site $\\mathcal{C}$, an object $X$ is **quasi-compact** if for every family $(X_{i} \\to X)_{i \\in I}$ generating a covering sieve there is a **finite** $J \\subseteq I$ such that the sieve generated by $(X_{i} \\to X)_{i \\in J}$ is **also** a covering sieve.\n\nIn a topos, an equivalent formulation: for every surjection $\\coprod_{i \\in I} X_{i} \\to X$ there is a finite $J \\subseteq I$ such that $\\coprod_{i \\in J} X_{i} \\to X$ is still a surjection.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#79dd33f8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the shape **compactness** keeps after the topology has been stripped away: **every cover has a finite subcover**. Compact Hausdorff spaces are exactly the quasi-compact objects in $\\mathrm{Cond}$ — the definition of condensed sets is built on it, so the name is worth remembering.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#78ce6fdf',
      },
    },

    'def.quasi-separated': {
      statement: {
        en: "In a topos $\\mathcal{T}$, an object $X$ is **quasi-separated** if for all $Y \\to X$ and $Z \\to X$ (with $Y, Z$ quasi-compact) the fibre product\n\n$$Y \\times_{X} Z$$\n\nis again quasi-compact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3b526e7d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Properties: **closed under subobjects** (a subobject of a quasi-separated object is quasi-separated); **closed under coproducts**; **closed under filtered colimits along injections**.\n\n\"Quasi-separated\" adds one requirement between two quasi-compact objects: **the overlap must also be small**. This is exactly parallel to \"compact plus compact overlaps\" in topology — together, quasi-compact and quasi-separated single out the \"small and well-behaved\" objects.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d1d08cd9',
      },
    },

    'def.regular-epi': {
      statement: {
        en: "A morphism $f : X \\to Y$ is a **regular epimorphism** if it is the **coequalizer** of some parallel pair: there exist $u, v : W \\rightrightarrows X$ with $f = \\operatorname{coker}(u, v)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4bf719dc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In $\\mathbf{Set}$ **every surjection is regular**: take $W = X \\times_{Y} X$ with the two projections; their coequalizer is exactly $f$. In a general category this fails — \"epi\" (right cancellable) is much weaker than \"regular epi\", and the gap is precisely whether $Y$ is really a quotient of $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f9bdfe70',
      },
    },

    'def.grothendieck-topology': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **Grothendieck topology** on a category $\\mathcal{C}$ assigns to each $X$ a family of **covering sieves** $J(X)$, subject to:\n\n1. $h_{X} \\in J(X)$;\n2. **Pullback stability**: $R \\in J(X)$, $f : Y \\to X$ $\\implies$ $f^{*}(R) := R \\times_{h_{X}} h_{Y} \\in J(Y)$;\n3. **Transitivity**: for $R, R' \\subseteq h_{X}$, if $R' \\in J(X)$ and for every $f \\in R'(Y)$ one has $f^{*}(R) \\in J(Y)$, then $R \\in J(X)$.\n\nA category with a Grothendieck topology is called a **site**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a447417d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$J(X)$ is a **filter** in the poset of all sieves: nonempty; closed under intersections ($R, R' \\in J(X) \\implies R \\cap R' \\in J(X)$); closed under larger sieves ($R \\subseteq R'$, $R \\in J(X) \\implies R' \\in J(X)$).\n\nTogether these say that $J(X)$ contains a bunch of \"large enough\" sieves.\n\nA pretopology generates a topology: replace each covering family by the sieve it generates, then take all such sieves. **Topologies are more general than pretopologies** — a pretopology requires those fibre products to exist, whereas in a presheaf category pullbacks of sieves always exist.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#603f3626',
      },
    },

    'def.adjunction-unit': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F \\dashv G$, and write the natural isomorphism as\n\n$$\\Phi_{X, Y} : \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{D}}\\bigl(F(X), Y\\bigr) \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}\\bigl(X, G(Y)\\bigr)$$\n\nEvaluating it on the two \"identity morphisms\" gives two natural transformations:\n\n$$\\eta_{X} := \\Phi_{X, F(X)}(1_{F(X)}) : X \\to G F(X)$$\n\n$$\\varepsilon_{Y} := \\Phi_{G(Y), Y}^{-1}(1_{G(Y)}) : F G(Y) \\to Y$$\n\nThey satisfy the **triangle identities**\n\n$$\\varepsilon_{F(X)} \\circ F(\\eta_{X}) = 1_{F(X)}, \\qquad G(\\varepsilon_{Y}) \\circ \\eta_{G(Y)} = 1_{G(Y)}$$\n\nConversely, given $\\eta : 1_{\\mathcal{C}} \\implies G \\circ F$ and $\\varepsilon : F \\circ G \\implies 1_{\\mathcal{D}}$ satisfying these two identities, they determine a pair of adjoint functors uniquely.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ef0e9e7e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In one sentence: **the unit \"sends oneself in\", the counit \"brings others back\"**, and the triangle identities ensure the two directions do not conflict.\n\n⚠️ **The unit and counit are in general not isomorphisms**; this is the biggest difference between an adjunction and a categorical equivalence. They are isomorphisms simultaneously exactly when it is an equivalence.\n\nSolving for the two sides from $\\Phi$: $\\Phi_{X,Y}(f) = G(f) \\circ \\eta_{X}$, $\\Phi^{-1}_{X,Y}(g) = \\varepsilon_{Y} \\circ F(g)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f01ef7c5',
      },
    },

    'def.comma-category': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F : \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ and $G : \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ be two functors **with the same target**. The **comma category** $F \\downarrow G$ has as objects\n\n$$\\bigl\\{(A, B, f) : A \\in \\mathcal{A},\\ B \\in \\mathcal{B},\\ f : F(A) \\to G(B)\\bigr\\}$$\n\nand a morphism from $(A, B, f)$ to $(A', B', f')$ is a pair $(a : A \\to A',\\ b : B \\to B')$ making the square commute:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc} F(A) & \\xrightarrow{\\;f\\;} & G(B) \\\\[2pt] {\\scriptstyle F(a)}\\big\\downarrow & & \\big\\downarrow{\\scriptstyle G(b)} \\\\[2pt] F(A') & \\xrightarrow[\\;f'\\;]{} & G(B') \\end{array}$$\n\n**Two special cases**: taking $\\mathcal{A} = \\mathbf{1}$ (the one-object category), $F$ is just \"selecting an object of $\\mathcal{C}$, say $X$\", and $F \\downarrow G$ is written $X \\downarrow G$; taking $\\mathcal{B} = \\mathbf{1}$ gives $F \\downarrow Y$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5e6bc055',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Reading**: $F \\downarrow G$ organizes all ways of \"stretching an arrow from the world of $F$ to the world of $G$\" into a category. **The objects are the arrows themselves** (together with their sources at both ends), and the morphisms are the pair of morphisms that let the arrows be compared.\n\n**Notation**: the symbol in $F \\downarrow G$ is a \"comma\", hence the name; some texts write $(F \\downarrow G)$ or $(F/G)$.\n\n⭐ **Why it appears everywhere**: universal properties are often \"the **terminal/initial object** in some comma category\".\n\n- $X \\downarrow \\mathrm{id}_{\\mathcal{C}}$ is the **slice** $\\mathcal{C}/X$;\n- the **category of elements** $\\int F$ of a presheaf is $\\mathbf{1} \\downarrow F$ (viewing the presheaf as a functor $\\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$);\n- the **unit** of an adjunction is defined as the initial object of $\\mathrm{id} \\downarrow G$ (or $F \\downarrow \\mathrm{id}$).\n\nTo recognize a comma category, check whether \"objects are arrows and morphisms are commutative squares\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b65187b3',
      },
    },

    'thm.presheaf-limits-colimits': {
      statement: {
        en: "In a presheaf category $\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}$ **all small limits and small colimits exist**, and they are computed **pointwise**: for every $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$,\n\n$$\\Bigl(\\varinjlim_{i} T_{i}\\Bigr)(X) \\;\\cong\\; \\varinjlim_{i} T_{i}(X), \\qquad \\Bigl(\\varprojlim_{i} T_{i}\\Bigr)(X) \\;\\cong\\; \\varprojlim_{i} T_{i}(X).$$\n\nEquivalently: for every $X$, the **evaluation functor** $\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$, $T \\mapsto T(X)$, **preserves all limits and all colimits**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eb448051',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Two steps.\n\n**(i) The pointwise definition really gives limits (colimits).** In $\\mathbf{Set}$, for each $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$ take the limit (or colimit) of that family of sets; for a morphism $f : Y \\to X$, the restriction map $\\bigl(\\varprojlim T_i\\bigr)(X) \\to \\bigl(\\varprojlim T_i\\bigr)(Y)$ is given by the universal property of the limit (on the colimit side by that of the colimit) ✓.\n\nThe resulting presheaf, together with the pointwise cone (cocone), satisfies the universal property: a map from a cone to $\\varprojlim T_i$ is uniquely determined at each point $X$, and the universal property is stated **pointwise**, so it holds at every point and hence globally ✓.\n\n**(ii) $\\mathbf{Set}$ is complete and cocomplete.**\n- **Small limits exist**: small products exist (the axiom of choice provides a set of choice functions); equalizers exist ($\\{x : f(x) = g(x)\\}$ is a subset); and **limits are built from products and equalizers**, so all small limits exist ✓.\n- **Small colimits exist**: small coproducts are disjoint unions; coequalizers are quotient sets; dually all small colimits exist ✓.\n\nThe two steps combine: taking the limit (colimit) pointwise for each $X$ in $\\mathbf{Set}$ gives the limit (colimit) in $\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}$ ✓, and \"the evaluation functor preserves limits and colimits\" is another way of saying \"computed pointwise\" ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ This is the **foundation** of almost every later construction: the advantage of a presheaf category is precisely that \"limits and colimits come for free\", so one can perform all sorts of formal constructions inside it and then sheafify the result.\n\n⚠️ \"Computed pointwise\" holds only for **limits and colimits**, not for general constructions — sheafification, internal Hom, and sheaf cohomology are not pointwise.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dbeeb1bc',
      },
    },

    'thm.induced-topos-morphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $g : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ be any functor. Then precomposition\n\n$$\\hat{g}^{-1} : \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}'} \\longrightarrow \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}, \\qquad T' \\longmapsto T' \\circ g^{\\mathrm{op}}$$\n\n**has both a left adjoint $\\hat{g}_{!}$ and a right adjoint $\\hat{g}_{*}$**. In particular $\\hat{g}^{-1}$ is exact, so\n\n$$\\hat{g} : \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}} \\longrightarrow \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}'}, \\qquad \\hat{g}^{*} := \\hat{g}^{-1} \\ \\dashv\\ \\hat{g}_{*}$$\n\nis a morphism of toposes. That is, **any functor induces a morphism between presheaf categories**, in the direction opposite to $g$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#16cd597a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ One-line reading: $g$ is just an arrow between categories, but once we pass to presheaf categories it automatically grows adjoints **on both sides** — $\\hat{g}_{!}$ on the left and $\\hat{g}_{*}$ on the right. Thus \"functor\" upgrades to \"topos morphism\" at the presheaf level.\n\n⚠️ The direction is easy to reverse: $g : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ while $\\hat{g} : \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}} \\to \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}'}$; **pushforward** $\\hat{g}_{*}$ goes along $g$, while **pullback** $\\hat{g}^{-1}$ goes the other way.\n\n📌 This is the general engine behind \"a map between topological spaces / sites gives a topos morphism\"; it is used in Chapter 4 to align sheaves on $\\mathbf{CHaus}$ with sheaves on a smaller site.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2fab9f51',
      },
    },

    'def.kan-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ be a functor between small categories and $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$. The **left Kan extension** of $F$ along $p$ is a functor $p_{!}F : \\mathcal{C}' \\to \\mathcal{D}$ together with a natural transformation\n\n$$\\alpha : F \\implies p_{!}F \\circ p$$\n\nsuch that for every $G : \\mathcal{C}' \\to \\mathcal{D}$ and every $\\gamma : F \\implies G \\circ p$ there is a **unique** $\\widetilde{\\gamma} : p_{!}F \\implies G$ with\n\n$$\\gamma = (\\widetilde{\\gamma} \\circ p) \\circ \\alpha$$\n\nEquivalently:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}(p_{!}F,\\ G) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}(F,\\ G \\circ p)$$\n\ni.e. $p_{!}$ is the **left adjoint** of the precomposition functor $p^{*} = (-) \\circ p$. Reversing arrows gives the **right Kan extension** $p_{*} \\dashv p^{*}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#06a4d731',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The question it answers: **can $F$ be extended to $\\mathcal{C}'$?** The left Kan extension is the **best left approximate extension** of $F$ — it does not ask that $F$ actually extend, only that, on $\\mathcal{C}$, among functors agreeing with $F$ it fits best.\n\nCompare with adjunctions line by line:\n\n| adjunction $L \\dashv R$ | $p_{!} \\dashv p^{*}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| unit $\\eta_{A} : A \\to R L A$ | $\\alpha : F \\implies p^{*}(p_{!}F)$ |\n| $f : A \\to R B$ | $\\gamma : F \\implies p^{*}(G)$ |\n| $g : L A \\to B$ | $\\widetilde{\\gamma} : p_{!}F \\implies G$ |\n| $f = R(g) \\circ \\eta_{A}$ | $\\gamma = p^{*}(\\widetilde{\\gamma}) \\circ \\alpha$ |\n\nSo Kan extension is nothing new: it just uses the adjunction formulas in a different direction.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#58a51ae8',
      },
    },

    'def.mono': {
      statement: {
        en: "A morphism $i : Y \\to X$ is a **monomorphism** (mono) if for every object $Z$ and all $f, g : Z \\to Y$,\n\n$$i \\circ f = i \\circ g \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; f = g$$\n\n(left cancellable). Equivalently: the square $Y \\to Y \\times_X Y$ is cartesian, i.e. $Y \\cong Y \\times_X Y$, where $Y \\times_X Y$ is the two pullbacks along $i$. Reversing all arrows gives **epimorphism** (epi, right cancellable).\n\n**Isomorphism** is another notion: $f : X \\to Y$ is an **isomorphism** if there is $g : Y \\to X$ with\n\n$$g \\circ f = 1_{X}, \\qquad f \\circ g = 1_{Y}$$\n\nthen $g$ is the **inverse** of $f$, written $f^{-1}$, and is uniquely determined by $f$. A category in which every morphism that is both mono and epi is an isomorphism is called **balanced**; $\\mathbf{Set}$ and $\\mathbf{Ab}$ are balanced, $\\mathbf{Ring}$ is not.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3b7e6cbd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "In $\\mathbf{Set}$ monomorphisms are exactly injections and epimorphisms exactly surjections — the latter **does not need the axiom of choice**: if $f$ is not surjective, choose $b \\notin \\operatorname{im} f$ and on the two-point set $\\{0,1\\}$ set $g(b)=0$, $h(b)=1$, while both agree on $\\operatorname{im} f$; then $g \\circ f = h \\circ f$ but $g \\ne h$. In a general category, however, \"cancellable\" is the definition, and \"one-to-one / onto\" is only its manifestation in concrete categories: in the category of rings $\\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Q}$ is an epimorphism, though its underlying map is not surjective — the forgetful functor sends it to something no longer epi.\n\n**The inverse is unique**: if $g, g'$ are both inverses of $f$, then $g = g \\circ 1_{Y} = g \\circ (f \\circ g') = (g \\circ f) \\circ g' = 1_{X} \\circ g' = g'$ ✓.\n\n**Isomorphism vs monomorphism**: $f$ is an isomorphism $\\iff$ $f$ is a monomorphism with a **section**.\n\n$(\\implies)$ follows directly from $f \\circ g = 1_{Y}$, $g \\circ f = 1_{X}$ (the latter says $f$ is a split mono, hence mono).\n\n$(\\Longleftarrow)$ Suppose $f \\circ g = 1_{Y}$.\n\nThen $f \\circ (g \\circ f) = (f \\circ g) \\circ f = 1_{Y} \\circ f = f = f \\circ 1_{X}$, ; since $f$ is mono, $g \\circ f = 1_{X}$, and $f$ is an isomorphism ✓.\n\nReversing arrows gives the dual statement: $f$ is an isomorphism $\\iff$ $f$ is an epimorphism with a **retraction**.\n\n⚠️ \"Mono $+$ epi\" is **not enough**: in the category of rings $\\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Q}$ is both mono and epi, yet not an isomorphism — a section (or retraction) is still missing.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da31aca1',
      },
    },

    'def.chain-homotopy': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $C = \\{\\, C_{n}, \\partial_{n} \\,\\}$ and $D = \\{\\, D_{n}, \\partial_{n}' \\,\\}$ be two chain complexes, and $f, g : C \\to D$ chain maps (i.e. $\\partial' f = f \\partial$).\n\nA **chain homotopy** between $f$ and $g$ is a family of homomorphisms $P_{n} : C_{n} \\to D_{n+1}$ satisfying\n\n$$\\partial_{n+1}' P_{n} + P_{n-1} \\partial_{n} = g_{n} - f_{n}.$$\n\n**Basic fact**: if such a $P$ exists, then $f$ and $g$ induce the **same** homomorphism on homology, i.e. $f_{*} = g_{*} : H_{n}(C) \\to H_{n}(D)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a757a800',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why \"chain homotopy $\\implies$ equal on homology\"**: let $z \\in C_{n}$ be a cycle ($\\partial z = 0$). Then\n\n$$g(z) - f(z) = \\partial' P(z) + P\\partial(z) = \\partial' P(z),$$\n\ni.e. $g(z) - f(z)$ is a **boundary** in $D$, so the homology class it represents is $0$. That is, $[g(z)] = [f(z)]$ for every cycle — exactly $f_{*} = g_{*}$. ∎\n\n⭐ **It is the algebraic translation of \"homotopy\"**: a homotopy in topology is \"a continuous deformation between two maps\"; a chain complex has no space to deform, so the deformation is turned into a homomorphism $P$ **raising degree by one**. The combination $\\partial P + P\\partial$ is exactly what \"homotopy of chain complexes\" should look like: it **commutes** with $\\partial$ ($\\partial T = T\\partial$, so $T$ is a chain map) and therefore sends cycles to boundaries; the anticommuting relation is the termwise relation between $P$ and $\\partial$ ($\\partial P = T - P\\partial$).\n\n✅ Chain homotopy **is** an equivalence relation: reflexive with $0$, symmetric with $-P$, transitive with $P + Q$ ($\\partial(P+Q) + (P+Q)\\partial = (g - f) + (h - g) = h - f$). So \"chain homotopy classes\" are the real morphisms in the homotopy category; formally inverting chain homotopy classes gives the homotopy category.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5cd56860',
      },
    },

    'def.etale-space': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $X$ be a topological space.\n\nA continuous map $p : E \\to X$ is an **étale map** (local homeomorphism) if every $e \\in E$ has an open neighbourhood $V \\ni e$ such that $p(V)$ is open in $X$ and $p|_{V} : V \\to p(V)$ is a homeomorphism.\n\nThen $(E, p)$ is an **étale space** (espace étalé) over $X$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cf1a1bc7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Three things to remember at once**:\n\n- **Fibres are discrete**: $p$ a local homeomorphism $\\implies$ every $p^{-1}(x)$ is discrete in $E$ (and also closed).\n- **Open immersions are étale**: take $V = E$.\n- **Continuity of sections is automatic**: when $p$ is a local homeomorphism, if $p \\circ s = \\mathrm{id}_{U}$, then a map $s : U \\to E$ is **automatically continuous**, so a \"section over $U$\" is just a set-theoretic $s$.\n\n⭐ **Equivalence with sheaves**: sheaves on $X$ and étale spaces over $X$ form an equivalence of categories, the functor being given by\n\n$$\\mathcal{F} \\longmapsto \\Bigl(\\coprod_{x \\in X} \\mathcal{F}_{x} \\longrightarrow X\\Bigr)$$\n\nthe right-hand side carries the \"topology of stalks\" — each pair $(U, s)$ ($U$ open, $s \\in \\mathcal{F}(U)$) contributes the open set $\\{\\, s_{x} : x \\in U \\,\\}$.\n\nThis is the precise meaning of \"a sheaf = a space spread locally isomorphically over the base\", and it also explains why operations such as \"pushforward sheaf / pushforward open set\" become \"glue the fibres together\" on étale spaces.\n\n⚠️ **A terminology trap**: here we mean a **local homeomorphism** of topological spaces (French espace étalé).\n\nIn algebraic geometry, an **étale map** is something else (locally of finite presentation + flat + unramified), see \"étale sheaf\".\n\nThe two share a name — Grothendieck did this on purpose, since étale maps of schemes really are the algebraic substitute for \"local isomorphism + finite covering\" — but they **cannot be interchanged**: fibres of a local homeomorphism are arbitrary discrete sets, while fibres of an étale morphism of schemes are generally discrete **finite** schemes.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3148b8d',
      },
    },

    'def.graph-morphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **morphism** from a graph $(V, E, s, t)$ to a graph $(V', E', s', t')$ is a pair of functions\n\n$$\\varphi_1 : V \\to V', \\qquad \\varphi_2 : E \\to E'$$\n\nsuch that both squares commute:\n\n$$s' \\circ \\varphi_2 = \\varphi_1 \\circ s, \\qquad t' \\circ \\varphi_2 = \\varphi_1 \\circ t$$\n\nThat is, $\\varphi_1$ sends the source of an edge to the source of the image of that edge, and the target to the target.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#80e05d3d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The two equations govern the two ends separately. Drawn as squares, they are the entire content of the name \"morphism of graphs\": the vertex layer and the edge layer are each continuous, and the directions match.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b042e6fb',
      },
    },

    'prop.forgetful-faithful-not-full': {
      statement: {
        en: "The forgetful functors $\\mathbf{Top} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ and $\\mathbf{Ab} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ are **faithful but not full**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1a67347d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** The underlying sets of maps are unchanged, so the functors are faithful ✓; but a continuous map between two topological spaces is only some of all maps, and a map between two groups is required to preserve the operation ✓ — so neither is full ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#843008ce',
      },
    },

    'def.section-retraction': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : X \\to Y$ be a morphism in a category.\n\n- A **section** of $f$ is a morphism $g : Y \\to X$ such that\n\n$$f \\circ g = 1_Y$$\n\n- A **retraction** of $f$ is a morphism $g : Y \\to X$ such that\n\n$$g \\circ f = 1_X$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#230326cb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The two equations point in opposite directions: a section is \"first $g$, then $f$, back to $Y$\", while a retraction is \"first $f$, then $g$, back to $X$\". In other words, a section is a right inverse of $f$ and a retraction is a left inverse of $f$ — the names differ because composition is written in the opposite order from the maps.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#73726e10',
      },
    },

    'def.graph': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **graph** is a quadruple\n\n$$( V, E, s, t )$$\n\nwhere $V$ and $E$ are sets and $s, t : E \\to V$ are functions — called the **source map** and the **target map**. Elements of $V$ are **vertices** and elements of $E$ are **edges**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4103d171',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "\"Graph\" here means a directed multigraph: $s(e) \\ne t(e)$ is not required (loops are allowed), and two edges are not required to have different endpoints (parallel edges are allowed). So it is broader than the everyday picture of a graph — it is just the data of which edge goes from where to where.\n\nA quadruple is a **nested ordered pair**, so the encoding is not immaterial. $(x, y) := \\{ \\{x\\}, \\{x,y\\} \\}$ satisfies the ordered-pair axiom\n\n$$(a, b) = (c, d) \\iff a = c \\wedge b = d$$\n\nwhereas with $\\{ x, \\{x,y\\} \\}$ one must verify that axiom case by case, not take it for granted.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0b266ba7',
      },
    },

    'def.category-of-groups': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The category of groups** $\\mathbf{Grp}$: objects are groups, morphisms are group homomorphisms, composition is composition of maps, and identities are identity homomorphisms.\n\nIt has a **forgetful functor** $\\mathbf{Grp}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$; abelian groups form the full subcategory $\\mathbf{Ab}$, and finite groups form the full subcategory $\\mathbf{FinGrp}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#25441bc0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Not an abelian category**: $\\hom_{\\mathbf{Grp}}(G,H)$ generally has no group structure (one cannot add pointwise), so one cannot do homological algebra as in $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$.\n\n**Monomorphisms = injective homomorphisms** and **epimorphisms = surjective homomorphisms**. Products are direct products and coproducts are free products; the only zero object is the trivial group, so $\\mathbf{Grp}$ is not additive.\n\nThe Cartesian product gives a symmetric monoidal structure with the trivial group as unit; but it is not Cartesian closed ($\\hom(G,H)$ generally cannot be made into a group naturally).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ad730236',
      },
    },

    'def.category-of-abelian-groups': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The category of abelian groups** $\\mathbf{Ab}$: objects are abelian groups, morphisms are group homomorphisms; it is a **full subcategory** of $\\mathbf{Grp}$.\n\nThe forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Ab}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$ sends an abelian group to its underlying set; $\\mathbb Z\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ and $\\mathbf{Ab}$ are equivalent.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#35d7838c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The prototype of an abelian category**: it has kernels and cokernels, monomorphisms and epimorphisms are strict; $\\hom(A,B)$ with pointwise addition is an abelian group.\n\n**Enough projective/injective objects**: free abelian groups are projective; $\\mathbb Q/\\mathbb Z$ gives an injective cogenerator, and every abelian group embeds into an injective group.\n\n**The tensor product** gives a symmetric monoidal structure with unit $\\mathbb Z$; $\\hom(A,B)$ is again an abelian group, so $\\mathbf{Ab}$ is even symmetric monoidal closed.\n\nRelation to $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$: $\\mathbb Z\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}\\simeq\\mathbf{Ab}$, the most basic example of \"modules\" unifying \"abelian groups\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#082cf530',
      },
    },

    'ax.grothendieck-universe': {
      statement: {
        en: "**Every set fits into some universe.** This is an independent axiom:\n\n$$\\forall x \\, \\exists U \\left[ U \\in \\mathrm{Univ} \\wedge  x \\in U \\right]$$\n\nwhere \"universe\" is as in the definition of a **Grothendieck universe**: transitive, and closed under pairing, power sets, and unions indexed by internal sets.\n\n⭐ A common equivalent form: **strongly inaccessible cardinals are unbounded** — for every ordinal $\\alpha$ there is a strongly inaccessible cardinal $\\kappa > \\alpha$, and then $V_{\\kappa}$ itself is a universe.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#43020425',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "It is **not in ZFC**. Taking a strongly inaccessible cardinal $\\kappa$, $V_{\\kappa} \\models \\mathrm{ZFC}$, so this axiom implies $\\mathrm{Con}(\\mathrm{ZFC})$; by Gödel's second incompleteness theorem, ZFC cannot prove it if consistent. So any theorem using it must list it as a hypothesis, not assume it silently.\n\nIt is **stronger** than \"there exists a universe\": it says universes are unbounded. That is exactly what category theory needs — $\\mathbf{Set}$ is a small category relative to $U$, while $U$ itself lies in a larger universe $U^{+}$, so \"the category of $U$-small categories\" is again small in $U^{+}$. **One always has room to go up a level.**\n\nWeak form: when only finitely many fixed categories are involved, assuming \"there exists a universe\" (equivalently, \"there exists a strongly inaccessible cardinal\") suffices; unboundedness is not needed.\n\n⚠️ A practical substitute: type theory automates this with **universe levels** (Lean's `Type u`, Coq's `Type_i`); this graph follows the SGA convention and settles it once and for all with one axiom.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#74e90ab3',
      },
    },

    'thm.right-adjoint-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "A functor $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ has a right adjoint $\\iff$ for every $Y \\in \\mathcal{D}$ the functor\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{D}}\\bigl(F(-),\\ Y\\bigr) : \\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$$\n\nis representable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#926deff5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the general machine for **constructing right adjoints**: for each $Y$ first find a representing object $G(Y)$, then check that the resulting $G$ is a functor.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b699d216',
      },
    },

    'thm.right-adjoint-preserves-limits': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F \\dashv G$. Then $G$ **preserves all limits** (that exist in $\\mathcal{D}$): for every diagram $D : I \\to \\mathcal{D}$,\n\n$$G\\bigl(\\lim D\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\lim\\, (G \\circ D)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7f0c5c4c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Dually, **left adjoints preserve all colimits**.\n\nThis is the quickest way to show that a functor cannot be a right adjoint — if it fails to preserve some limit (say a product), it is out at once. Conversely, it explains why forgetful functors can always be right adjoints: they usually preserve limits (the structure is defined pointwise).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#93fb6653',
      },
    },

    'prop.adjunction-naturality': {
      statement: {
        en: "The naturality of the adjunction bijection $\\Phi_{X, X'} : \\operatorname{Hom}\\bigl(F(X), X'\\bigr) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}\\bigl(X, G(X')\\bigr)$ says: for $g : Y \\to X$, $g' : X' \\to Y'$ and $f : F(X) \\to X'$, one has $\\Phi\\bigl(g' \\circ f \\circ F(g)\\bigr) = G(g') \\circ \\Phi(f) \\circ g$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#988a5e7a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Substitute $\\Phi(f) = G(f) \\circ \\eta_{X}$ and $\\Phi^{-1}(h) = \\varepsilon_{X'} \\circ F(h)$ ✓; the identity follows from naturality of the unit and counit ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1bbf1752',
      },
    },

    'prop.adjunction-composition': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $F_{1} \\dashv G_{1}$ ($\\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$) and $F_{2} \\dashv G_{2}$ ($\\mathcal{D} \\to \\mathcal{E}$), then\n\n$$F_{2} \\circ F_{1} \\;\\dashv\\; G_{1} \\circ G_{2}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#756b9935',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Chain the two adjunction isomorphisms ✓: for $X \\in \\mathcal{C}$, $Z \\in \\mathcal{E}$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{E}}\\bigl(F_{2}F_{1}X,\\ Z\\bigr) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{D}}\\bigl(F_{1}X,\\ G_{2}Z\\bigr) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}\\bigl(X,\\ G_{1}G_{2}Z\\bigr) ✓$$\n\nBoth steps use the definition of adjunction, and the family of isomorphisms is natural in $X, Z$ (a composite of two natural isomorphisms is natural) ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#397229df',
      },
    },

    'prop.adjunction-extends-to-diagrams': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ and $G : \\mathcal{D} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ are adjoint to each other, then for any index category $I$, **pointwise action** gives an adjunction between diagram categories\n\n$$F_{I} \\dashv G_{I}, \\qquad F_{I}(D) = F \\circ D,\\quad G_{I}(E) = G \\circ E,$$\n\nthat is, $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{D}^{I}}\\bigl(F \\circ D,\\ E\\bigr) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}^{I}}\\bigl(D,\\ G \\circ E\\bigr)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1233a76b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Hom-sets in a diagram category are formed **pointwise**: giving a natural transformation is equivalent to giving a compatible family of morphisms ✓.\n\nAt each $i \\in I$, apply $F \\dashv G$ to get $\\operatorname{Hom}(FD_{i}, E_{i}) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}(D_{i}, GE_{i})$ ✓;\n\nthis family is compatible with the morphisms of $I$ (because the original isomorphism is natural in $F,G$) ✓, so the isomorphisms combine into one between the two Hom-sets ✓.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#19743db4',
      },
    },

    'prop.kan-extension-fully-faithful': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}'$ be **fully faithful** and $\\mathcal{D}$ cocomplete. Then for every $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ the left Kan extension satisfies\n\n$$p_{!}F \\circ p \\;\\cong\\; F.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f1d4b103',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Use the pointwise formula $p_{!}F(X') = \\varinjlim_{p(X) \\to X'} F(X)$ ✓.\n\nTake $X' = p(X_{0})$: the comma category $p \\downarrow p(X_{0})$ has $1_{p(X_{0})}$ as a **terminal object** ($p$ is fully faithful, so morphisms $p(X_0)$ to $p(X)$ come uniquely from $X_0 \\to X$) ✓, and a colimit over a category with a terminal object is the value at that object ✓, so $(p_{!}F)(p(X_{0})) \\cong F(X_{0})$ ✓.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8401a46c',
      },
    },

    'prop.forgetful-preserves-limits': {
      statement: {
        en: "The forgetful functors from $\\mathbf{Mon}$, $\\mathbf{Gr}$, $\\mathbf{Rng}$, $G\\text{-}\\mathbf{Set}$, $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$, $k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Alg}$ to $\\mathbf{Set}$ all **preserve all limits**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dbfa2b61',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** These structures are all \"operations given pointwise on sets + equations checked pointwise\" ✓, so the universal property of a limit diagram holds pointwise on underlying sets and the structure assembles automatically ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f5e7674e',
      },
    },

    'prop.algebra-categories-limits': {
      statement: {
        en: "In $\\mathbf{Mon}$, $\\mathbf{Gr}$, $\\mathbf{Rng}$, $G\\text{-}\\mathbf{Set}$, $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$, $k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Alg}$ **limits are computed pointwise** (forgetting to sets gives the limit of sets);\n\n**colimits** are given by \"first generate freely, then quotient\" — for example the coproduct in $\\mathbf{Gr}$ is the free product.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6cc16dae',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** These forgetful functors **preserve limits** ✓, so a limit is the limit of underlying sets equipped with the structure given pointwise by the universal property ✓; for coproducts one first glues generators freely and then quotients by the relations ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eb2d27a6',
      },
    },

    'prop.forgetful-exactness': {
      statement: {
        en: "Algebraic forgetful functors **preserve limits but not colimits**; the inclusion $\\widetilde{\\mathcal{C}} \\hookrightarrow \\widehat{\\mathcal{C}}$ preserves limits but not colimits, while its left adjoint (**sheafification**) is **exact**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#868b3638',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Preservation of limits is the preceding proposition ✓; failure of colimits: the underlying set of $M \\oplus N$ is not $M \\sqcup N$ ✓; sheafification is a left adjoint and left exact, hence exact ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c5358587',
      },
    },

    'prop.free-objects-examples': {
      statement: {
        en: "\"Free\" in several algebraic categories: in $\\mathbf{Mon}$ it is the **free monoid** on $X$; in $\\mathbf{Gr}$ it is the **free group** (with cancellation allowed); in $\\mathbf{Rng}$ it is the noncommutative polynomial ring;\n\nin $G\\text{-}\\mathbf{Set}$ it is $G \\times X$ (**left** regular action $g \\cdot (h, x) = (gh, x)$; when $X$ is a single point it degenerates to $G$ itself);\n\nin $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ it is $A^{(X)}$;\n\nin $k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Alg}$ it is the **tensor algebra**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a8e91fb4',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Check the universal property of free objects $\\operatorname{Hom}(FX, -) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Set}}\\bigl(X, U(-)\\bigr)$ in each case ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dd00f795',
      },
    },

    'prop.bialgebra-examples': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **bialgebra** is a **monoid** in the opposite of the category of $k$-algebras. $k[t]$ with $t \\mapsto t \\otimes 1 + 1 \\otimes t$, and $k[t, 1/t]$ with $t \\mapsto t \\otimes t$, are both bialgebras.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c489d5e7',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Translating \"monoid in the opposite category\" back into $k$-algebras gives \"the comultiplication $\\Delta : A \\to A \\otimes A$ is an **algebra homomorphism**\" ✓; both $\\Delta$ above preserve multiplication ✓ (in $k[t,1/t]$ the element $t$ is invertible, so $t \\mapsto t \\otimes t$ is the only choice) ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1e4ab827',
      },
    },

    'ex.ab-of-categories': {
      statement: {
        en: "- $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathbf{Set}) = \\mathbf{Ab}$;\n- $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathbf{Top})$ = **topological abelian groups**;\n- $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathbf{Manifold})$ = abelian Lie groups;\n- $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathbf{Grp}) = \\mathbf{Ab}$ (operations between group objects are pointwise);\n- $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathrm{Cond}) = \\mathrm{CondAb}$;\n- $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\widehat{\\mathcal{C}})$ = abelian group objects in the presheaf category $\\simeq$ presheaves valued in $\\mathbf{Ab}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2aa9076a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The most useful thing about this table is **reading it backwards**: to study topological abelian groups, take $\\mathcal{C}$ to be $\\mathbf{Top}$; to study condensed abelian groups, take $\\mathcal{C}$ to be $\\mathrm{Cond}$.\n\n**One definition, one set of theorems (kernels, cokernels, exact sequences, ...) transfers directly.**\n\n⚠️ The identity $\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathbf{Grp}) = \\mathbf{Ab}$ is the one that gives pause: a group object in the category of groups is **exactly** an abelian group (Eckmann–Hilton argument) — adding the structure \"group\" on top of the category \"groups\" forces commutativity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ff933fc4',
      },
    },

    'prop.nat-iso-invariants': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $F \\cong F'$, then $F$ is faithful (resp. full, fully faithful, essentially surjective, an equivalence) $\\iff$ $F'$ is as well.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#49faa919',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** A natural isomorphism gives a family of bijections on each pair of Hom-sets, compatible with composition ✓, so \"is $\\operatorname{Hom}(X,Y) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(FX, FY)$ a bijection\" holds on both sides simultaneously ✓; essential surjectivity and equivalence are similar (isomorphisms translate objects) ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#00e51fce',
      },
    },

    'prop.fully-faithful-injective': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ is **fully faithful**, then it is **essentially injective**:\n\n$$F(X) \\cong F(Y) \\implies X \\cong Y.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#540cbc5a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Let $u : F(X) \\to F(Y)$ be an isomorphism.\n\nBy full faithfulness, $u$ comes uniquely from some $f : X \\to Y$, and $u^{-1}$ uniquely from some $g : Y \\to X$ ✓.\n\nThen $F(g f) = u^{-1} u = 1_{F(X)} = F(1_{X})$ ✓, and faithfulness gives $g f = 1_{X}$ ✓;\n\nsymmetrically $f g = 1_{Y}$ ✓.\n\n∎\n\n⚠️ The converse fails: an essentially injective functor need not be fully faithful.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#64d697ae',
      },
    },

    'def.exact-functor': {
      statement: {
        en: "A functor $F$ is **left exact** if it preserves all **finite limits**; **right exact** if it preserves all **finite colimits**; and **exact** if both.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#93041508',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why \"finite\"**: in general categories the usage of \"left exact\" comes from abelian categories — there it suffices to preserve $0$, finite products, and kernels, which is equivalent to sending the short exact sequence $0 \\to M' \\to M \\to M'' \\to 0$ to $0 \\to FM' \\to FM \\to FM''$ (exact, exact on the left). So \"exact\" in the name refers to exact sequences.\n\n⭐ **Right adjoints are left exact and left adjoints are right exact** — a direct consequence of \"adjoint $\\implies$ preserves limits / colimits\", and the most commonly used criterion.\n\n**Better in abelian categories**: $F$ is left exact $\\iff$ it preserves kernels ($F(\\ker) = \\ker$); $F$ is exact $\\iff$ it sends short exact sequences to short exact sequences.\n\n⚠️ The name is ambiguous: some books use \"exact\" for preserving **finite** limits and others for **all** limits. This graph follows the definition above (finite limits/colimits).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#29353857',
      },
    },

    'prop.functor-preserves-iso': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ be a functor and $f : X \\to Y$ an isomorphism in $\\mathcal{C}$ with inverse $f^{-1} : Y \\to X$. Then $F(f)$ is an isomorphism, and\n\n$$F(f)^{-1} = F\\left(f^{-1}\\right).$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9b47edbf',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one line: $F(f)\\,F(f^{-1}) = F(f f^{-1}) = F(1_Y) = 1_{F(Y)}$, and symmetrically.\n\nIt uses exactly the two requirements on a functor: **preserve composition, preserve identities**.\n\n⚠ The converse **fails**: $F(f)$ being an isomorphism does not imply that $f$ is one (a forgetful functor identifies linear isomorphisms with invertible linear maps, but also sends non-invertible square matrices to maps).\n\nTo go backwards one needs $F$ **fully faithful**, which is the content of propositions such as `prop.adjoint-full-faithful`.\n\n⭐ Small as it is, this is the first brick of \"isomorphism is a categorical property\": no functor distinguishes isomorphic objects, which is why invariants of isomorphism (rank, trace, determinant, homology) make sense.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2007945f',
      },
    },

    'def.preserves-limit': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $D : I \\to \\mathcal{C}$ be a diagram and $F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$ a functor. $F$ induces a comparison map between cones\n\n$$F(\\lim D) \\longrightarrow \\lim (F \\circ D).$$\n\nIf this comparison map is an isomorphism for **every** diagram $D$ (of the relevant type), one says that $F$ **preserves limits** (written $\\lim D \\cong \\lim F \\circ D$). Dually: **preserves colimits**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1c365fc0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Where the comparison map comes from**: $\\lim D$ is the universal cone of $D$; applying $F$ gives a cone over $F \\circ D$, so the universal property of $\\lim(F\\circ D)$ yields a unique map $F(\\lim D) \\to \\lim(F\\circ D)$. Thus \"preserves limits\" always means that this map, **which exists automatically by the universal property**, happens to be invertible.\n\n⭐ **The most used case**: **right adjoints preserve all limits** (dually left adjoints preserve all colimits). The proof uses only the adjunction isomorphism and the universal property of limits, with no concrete construction.\n\n**Weaker versions**: $F$ preserves **finite** limits (= **left exact**), **filtered** colimits (the AB5 condition), **products**, **equalizers**, ... — each restricts \"every diagram\" above to a class of diagrams.\n\n⚠️ Not to be confused with \"$F$ is continuous\": a continuous functor here **preserves limits**, with no relation to continuity in topology.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9f2f14ef',
      },
    },

    'prop.group-basic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group and $a, b, c \\in G$. Then:\n\n**(i) Cancellation**: $ab = ac \\implies b = c$, $ba = ca \\implies b = c$;\n\n**(ii) Inverse formulas**: $(ab)^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1}$, $(a^{-1})^{-1} = a$, $e^{-1} = e$;\n\n**(iii)** For $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$: $a^{m}a^{n} = a^{m+n}$, $(a^{m})^{n} = a^{mn}$, $a^{0} = e$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e7ac178c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Cancellation is the watershed between groups and monoids: $(\\mathbb{N}, \\cdot)$ is a monoid, but $0 \\cdot 2 = 0 \\cdot 3$ cannot cancel $0$ — what is missing is precisely inverses.\n\n⚠ In a nonabelian group the order in $(ab)^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1}$ **must** be reversed: $(ab)(b^{-1}a^{-1}) = a(bb^{-1})a^{-1} = aa^{-1} = e$.\n\nWriting $a^{-1}b^{-1}$ is generally wrong (test with two transpositions in $S_3$).\n\n⭐ (iii) is the group version of `def.group-power`;\n\nthe proof is again \"induct on nonnegative integers, then pass to negative exponents using inverses\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2fdf40b1',
      },
    },

    'prop.module-tensor-universal': {
      statement: {
        en: "The tensor product $M \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} N$ (resp. $M \\otimes_{k} N$) is **universal** for $\\mathbb{Z}$-bilinear (resp. $k$-bilinear) maps:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Bil}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M \\times N,\\ P) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}\\bigl(M \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} N,\\ P\\bigr),$$\n\nbilinear maps on the left correspond one-to-one to group homomorphisms on the right.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fa47dc3f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** First build the tensor product on free modules: take $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot(M \\times N)$ (the free abelian group) and quotient by the \"bilinear relations\"\n\n$$(m + m', n) \\sim (m, n) + (m', n), \\quad (m, n + n') \\sim (m, n) + (m, n'), \\quad (am, n) \\sim a(m,n) \\sim (m, an) ✓$$\n\nA general module is a quotient of a free one (take a presentation) ✓, and the tensor product over the quotient is the quotient ✓. Then $M \\times N \\to M \\otimes N$ is bilinear ✓, and any bilinear map $M \\times N \\to P$ factors uniquely through it ✓ — that is exactly the universal property ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e7ade41a',
      },
    },

    'prop.subspace-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "**(Merged form)** A nonempty subset $U \\subseteq V$ is a subspace if and only if for all $u, v \\in U$ and $c \\in K$ one has $cu + v \\in U$.\n\n**(Ascending chain form)** If $U_1 \\subseteq U_2 \\subseteq U_3 \\subseteq \\cdots$ is a sequence of subspaces (an increasing chain), then $\\bigcup_n U_n$ is also a subspace.\n\n**(Spanning form)** Let $\\mathcal{U}$ be the family of all subspaces containing $S$. The **span** of an arbitrary subset $S \\subseteq V$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{span} S := \\bigcap \\mathcal{U} = \\bigcap \\{ U : S \\subseteq U \\subseteq V,\\ U \\text{ is a subspace} \\}$$\n\nthe smallest subspace containing $S$, and it equals the set of **finite** linear combinations of elements of $S$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b2d2fd82',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The \"merged form\" compresses three conditions into one operation $cu + v$: it governs $0$ (take $c = -1$, $v = u$), addition (take $c = 1$), and scalar multiplication (take $v = 0$, where $0 \\in U$ has already been obtained) all at once.\n\nIt saves the most space when writing proofs.\n\n⭐ The \"ascending chain form\" is the one place where an **infinite** union works: an arbitrary finite union need not be closed, but the union of an increasing chain is closed — because any two elements lie in the same $U_n$ of the chain (take the maximum of finitely many indices).\n\n⭐ The \"spanning form\" is the same trick as \"the $\\sigma$-algebra generated by\" and \"the subgroup generated by\": **intersect all structures containing it**.\n\nIt is also what the weak edge `ana.generated-closure` on the graph is about.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b75851cc',
      },
    },

    'def.similarity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Two square matrices $A, B \\in M_n(K)$ are **similar**, written $A \\sim B$, iff there is an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$B = P^{-1} A\\, P$$\n\n$P$ is called the **transition matrix**. An equivalent way to say it: $A$ and $B$ are matrices of the same linear map in two bases.\n\nInvariants of similarity: rank, trace, determinant, characteristic polynomial (hence eigenvalues and their algebraic multiplicities), minimal polynomial.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a0b79fce',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Similarity is an **equivalence relation** (reflexive / symmetric / transitive), so $M_n(K)$ splits into similarity classes; **the classification problem is to describe all similarity classes**.\n\n⭐ Two worlds, two ways of saying the same thing: on the matrix side \"$B = P^{-1}AP$\", on the map side \"the same $T$, two bases\".\n\nThe two edges `thm.change-of-basis` and `dep.changeofbasis-similarity` on the graph say exactly this.\n\n⚠ Similarity is stricter than **equivalence** ($B = PAQ$): equivalence only requires equal rank, while similarity also requires equal characteristic polynomial.\n\n$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ are equivalent but not similar.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f1835975',
      },
    },

    'prop.continuous-arithmetic': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f, g : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous at $x_0$ and $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then the following are continuous at $x_0$:\n\n$$f + g, \\quad f - g, \\quad cf, \\quad fg, \\quad |f|;$$\n\nif $g(x_0) \\ne 0$, then $f/g$ is continuous at $x_0$. If $g$ is continuous at $f(x_0)$, then the composite $g \\circ f$ is continuous at $x_0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c88fe6cd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The first five follow directly from the **algebra of limits** (`prop.limit-arithmetic`): continuity means \"the limit equals the value\", so any operation limits support, continuity supports too.\n\n⚠ The composite case **cannot** be copied blindly: $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} f(x) = f(x_0)$ and $\\lim_{y \\to y_0} g(y) = g(y_0)$ must be combined into $\\lim_{x \\to x_0} g(f(x)) = g(f(x_0))$, with an $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ transfer in between.\n\n⭐ Corollary: polynomials are continuous everywhere;\n\nrational functions are continuous where the denominator does not vanish;\n\nby `thm.elementary-derivatives` and power series, $\\exp$, $\\sin$, $\\cos$ are continuous too — so \"elementary functions are continuous on their domains\" is **proved**, not stipulated.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea279284',
      },
    },

    'thm.cayley': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group. **Left multiplication** gives an injective homomorphism\n\n$$\\rho : G \\hookrightarrow \\operatorname{Sym}(G), \\qquad \\rho(g)(h) := gh$$\n\nIn particular, if $|G| = n$ then $G$ is isomorphic to a subgroup of $S_n$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8dd5b021',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The philosophical content of the theorem exceeds its computational content: **every abstract group can be realized concretely as a group of permutations**.\n\nThat is, \"group theory\" and \"permutation group theory\" are the same subject — abstraction does not really create new objects, only a change of viewpoint.\n\n⚠ The price is high: for $|G| = n$ one embeds into $S_n$ (of order $n!$), so Cayley's theorem is almost never used for computation.\n\nIts real use is as a contrast with representation theory: replacing \"acting on a set\" by \"acting on a vector space\" is the beginning of that line.\n\n⭐ Left multiplication is also **free** (every stabilizer is trivial: $gh = h \\implies g = e$), so it is automatically injective — this is the core sentence of the proof.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#20a9f357',
      },
    },

    'def.category-of-vector-spaces': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The category of vector spaces** $\\mathbf{Vect}_K$: its objects are the vector spaces over a field $K$, its morphisms are the $K$-linear maps, composition is composition of maps, and the identity is the identity map.\n\nThe finite-dimensional vector spaces form the **full subcategory** $\\mathbf{Vect}_K^{\\mathrm{fd}}$: the objects are only the finite-dimensional spaces, while the morphisms are still all linear maps.\n\n$\\mathbf{Vect}_K$ is an **additive category**: $\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V,W)$ becomes a $K$-vector space under pointwise addition and pointwise scalar multiplication, composition is linear in each variable, and finite direct sums are simultaneously products and coproducts.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c8288c9',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is listed separately**: the pair \"objects + linear maps\" of linear algebra is, in categorical language, $\\mathbf{Vect}_K$; once it is available, \"duality\" and \"Hom\" can be written as functors.\n\n**Forgetting structure**: the forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Vect}_K\\to\\mathbf{Set}$ forgets addition and scalar multiplication, retaining only the underlying sets and functions; it is faithful but not full (not every function is linear).\n\n**Relation to additive categories**: each Hom-set is itself a vector space and composition is bilinear; finite direct sums coincide with finite products, so it is an additive category, hence a preabelian category (kernels and cokernels are taken pointwise).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#770faf65',
      },
    },

    'def.hom-functor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Fix $V\\in\\mathbf{Vect}_K$. The **covariant Hom functor**\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V,-):\\mathbf{Vect}_K\\to\\mathbf{Vect}_K$$\n\nsends an object to $U\\mapsto\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V,U)$ and a morphism $T:U_1\\to U_2$ to precomposition $T\\circ(-):\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V,U_1)\\to\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V,U_2)$.\n\nFix $W$. The **contravariant Hom functor** $\\operatorname{Hom}_K(-,W):\\mathbf{Vect}_K^{\\mathrm{op}}\\to\\mathbf{Vect}_K$ sends an object to $U\\mapsto\\operatorname{Hom}_K(U,W)$ and a morphism $T:U_1\\to U_2$ to postcomposition $(-)\\circ T:\\operatorname{Hom}_K(U_2,W)\\to\\operatorname{Hom}_K(U_1,W)$.\n\nBoth Hom-sets become $K$-vector spaces under pointwise addition and pointwise scalar multiplication, and the morphism part of the functor is linear, so the target category is $\\mathbf{Vect}_K$, not merely $\\mathbf{Set}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bcead6b8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Functoriality**: $\\operatorname{Hom}(V,-)$ preserves identities and composition; $\\operatorname{Hom}(-,W)$ is contravariant and reverses the order of composition.\n\n**Relation to duality**: taking $W=K$, $\\operatorname{Hom}_K(-,K)=(-)^{\\ast}$ is the dual functor; the dual space is the value of the Hom functor at $K$.\n\n**Internal Hom**: in a closed structure the Hom functor is represented by an internal Hom object; the version used here lives in a topos, and the representing relation is \"the internal Hom represents the Hom functor\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#384ef4c9',
      },
    },

    'def.dual-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a $K$-linear map. Its **dual map** (also called the transpose map) $T^{\\ast}:W^{\\ast}\\to V^{\\ast}$ is defined by\n\n$$\\langle T^{\\ast}\\varphi,\\,v\\rangle=\\langle\\varphi,\\,Tv\\rangle,\\qquad v\\in V,\\ \\varphi\\in W^{\\ast}$$\n\nEquivalently, $T^{\\ast}\\varphi=\\varphi\\circ T$.\n\nProperties: $T^{\\ast}$ is linear; $(S\\circ T)^{\\ast}=T^{\\ast}\\circ S^{\\ast}$; $(\\operatorname{id}_V)^{\\ast}=\\operatorname{id}_{V^{\\ast}}$. Hence $(-)^{\\ast}$ is a contravariant functor (see `def.dual-functor`).\n\nIn finite dimension, after bases are fixed, the matrix of $T^{\\ast}$ is the transpose of the matrix of $T$: $[T^{\\ast}]_{\\mathcal{B}^{\\ast}}^{\\mathcal{C}^{\\ast}}=([T]_{\\mathcal{C}}^{\\mathcal{B}})^{\\mathsf T}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#eff448b5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Direction**: $T:V\\to W$ induces $W^{\\ast}\\to V^{\\ast}$, so the arrow reverses; this is the heart of \"duality\".\n\n**Relation to the transpose matrix**: the transpose matrix is the matrix of $T^{\\ast}$ in the **dual bases**; the two are objects at different levels (a map versus a matrix).\n\n**Complex case**: in an inner-product space the corresponding notion is the conjugate transpose (adjoint), see `def.operator-adjoint`; the difference is that duality uses $K$-linear functionals, while the inner product is conjugate-linear in the second variable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4fda5469',
      },
    },

    'def.forgetful-functor': {
      statement: {
        en: "**A forgetful functor** is a functor that \"forgets\" part of an algebraic structure and keeps only the underlying data: on objects $(X,\\text{structure})\\mapsto X$, and on morphisms it keeps the same underlying map.\n\nTypical examples:\n\n- $\\mathbf{Top}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$: forgets the open sets, keeping only the underlying sets and functions;\n- $\\mathbf{Grp}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$: forgets the group operation and the unit;\n- $\\mathbf{Vect}_K\\to\\mathbf{Set}$: forgets addition and scalar multiplication;\n- $\\mathbf{Vect}_K\\to\\mathbf{Ab}$: forgets the $K$-scalar multiplication, keeping only the additive group.\n\nStrictly speaking, \"forgetful functor\" is not a single functor but a standard class of functors from **concrete categories** (categories equipped with a faithful functor to $\\mathbf{Set}$) to $\\mathbf{Set}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7ce43ab5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Faithful but usually not full**: having the same underlying map does not guarantee preserving structure; $\\mathbf{Top}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$ and $\\mathbf{Ab}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$ are both faithful and not full.\n\n**Left adjoints are free constructions**: free groups, free vector spaces, and the discrete topology are the left adjoints of the corresponding forgetful functors.\n\n**Preservation of limits**: forgetful functors to $\\mathbf{Set}$ usually preserve all limits (constructed pointwise), but in general they do not preserve colimits.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#05b11634',
      },
    },

    'def.dual-functor': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The dual functor** $(-)^{\\ast}:\\mathbf{Vect}_K^{\\mathrm{op}}\\to\\mathbf{Vect}_K$ is defined as follows:\n\n- on objects, $V\\mapsto V^{\\ast}=\\operatorname{Hom}_K(V,K)$;\n- on morphisms, it sends $T:V\\to W$ to $T^{\\ast}:W^{\\ast}\\to V^{\\ast}$.\n\nSince $(S\\circ T)^{\\ast}=T^{\\ast}\\circ S^{\\ast}$ and $(\\operatorname{id})^{\\ast}=\\operatorname{id}$, it is a **covariant** functor from $\\mathbf{Vect}_K^{\\mathrm{op}}$ — equivalently, a **contravariant** functor on $\\mathbf{Vect}_K$.\n\nThe natural transformation $\\eta_V:V\\to V^{\\ast\\ast}$, $\\eta_V(v)(\\varphi)=\\varphi(v)$, gives $1_{\\mathbf{Vect}_K}\\implies(-)^{\\ast\\ast}$; in finite dimension $\\eta$ is an isomorphism, so duality gives an equivalence between $\\mathbf{Vect}_K^{\\mathrm{fd}}$ and its opposite category.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#665212ff',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Contravariant is not the same as starting from the opposite category**: this is only a way of writing a contravariant functor as a covariant functor on $\\mathbf{Vect}_K^{\\mathrm{op}}$, which is why the order of composition is reversed.\n\n**Infinite dimension**: $\\eta_V$ is injective but generally not surjective, so $V\\to V^{\\ast\\ast}$ is not an isomorphism; duality does not make all of $\\mathbf{Vect}_K$ self-dual.\n\n**Relation to the Hom functor**: $(-)^{\\ast}=\\operatorname{Hom}_K(-,K)$ is the special case $W=K$ of the contravariant Hom functor (see the Hom functor node).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#612034ff',
      },
    },
    "def.algebra-over-ring": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with identity. An $R$-algebra is an $R$-module $A$ together with an $R$-bilinear multiplication $A\\times A\\to A$, $(x,y)\\mapsto xy$. If the multiplication is associative, $A$ is called an associative algebra; if there is $1_A\\in A$ with $1_Ax=x1_A=x$, then $A$ is called unital. Unital associative $R$-algebras with multiplication- and unit-preserving $R$-linear maps form the category $\\mathrm{Alg}_R^{\\mathrm{uni}}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#398e2f8d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The base ring is commutative so that the left and right $R$-actions agree. A unital algebra has a structure map $R\\to A$, $r\\mapsto r1_A$, whose image lies in the centre of $A$. Examples are $M_n(F)$, the polynomial algebra $F[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$, and the group algebra $F[G]$. Associativity and unitality are additional axioms; this cluster works with unital associative algebras.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76005d89',
      },
    },
    "def.algebra-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a unital associative $F$-algebra. A linear representation of $A$ is a pair $(M,\\theta)$, where $M$ is an $F$-vector space and $\\theta:A\\to\\operatorname{End}_F(M)$ is an algebra homomorphism (not necessarily unital; the condition $\\theta(1_A)=\\operatorname{id}_M$ is optional). If in addition $\\theta(1_A)=\\operatorname{id}_M$, it is called unital; then $a\\cdot m=\\theta(a)(m)$ makes it a left $A$-module, and unital representations correspond bijectively to left modules.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92102a0e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "A general representation only requires $\\theta$ to preserve addition and multiplication, not the unit. The condition $\\theta(1_A)=\\operatorname{id}_M$ makes $M$ a left $A$-module; under this equivalence $A$-linear maps are exactly morphisms of representations. The natural action of $M_n(F)$ on $F^n$ is a basic example. An $F[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$-module structure is the same as $n$ commuting endomorphisms $T_i$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a08a930e',
      },
    },
    "def.representation-morphism": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\theta)$ and $(W,\\varphi)$ be representations of $A$. An $A$-linear map (an intertwiner) is an $F$-linear map $h:V\\to W$ such that $h(\\theta(a)v)=\\varphi(a)h(v)$ for all $a\\in A$ and $v\\in V$. These morphisms form the category $\\mathrm{Rep}_A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1beb8f67',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Isomorphisms are exactly bijective intertwiners, and their inverses are again $A$-linear. The kernel and image of an intertwiner are subrepresentations. The space $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)=\\operatorname{Hom}_A(V,V)$ is an $F$-algebra; Schur's lemma describes it for irreducible $V$. For $A=F[G]$, intertwiners are exactly $G$-equivariant maps.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#103f3670',
      },
    },
    "def.group-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a finite group. A representation of $G$ over $F$ is a pair $(V,\\rho)$, where $V$ is an $F$-vector space and $\\rho:G\\to GL(V)$ is a group homomorphism. It is equivalent to a unital left $F[G]$-module structure on $V$; hence representations of $G$ are the same as unital representations of the group algebra $F[G]$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4d22851c',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The representation of $G$ need not be finite-dimensional; most results in this cluster are stated in the finite-dimensional case. The trivial representation has $\\rho(g)=\\operatorname{id}_V$; the regular representation is left multiplication on $F[G]$. The group algebra $F[G]$ consists of formal sums $\\sum a_g g$ with convolution, and $\\rho$ extends uniquely to a unital algebra homomorphism $F[G]\\to\\operatorname{End}_F(V)$. This equivalence transfers all results on algebra representations to groups.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#65ccad2a',
      },
    },
    "def.subrepresentation": {
      statement: {
        en: "A subspace $W\\subseteq V$ is called a subrepresentation if $\\theta(a)W\\subseteq W$ for all $a\\in A$; then $\\theta$ restricts to a representation on $W$. For a group representation this means $\\rho(g)W\\subseteq W$ for all $g\\in G$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a1ab9d66',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The zero subspace and $V$ are always subrepresentations. Sums and intersections of subrepresentations are again subrepresentations; kernels and images of intertwiners are also subrepresentations. For group representations, subrepresentations are exactly $G$-invariant subspaces. Quotient representations are treated in a separate node.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b8b50bb9',
      },
    },
    "def.irreducible-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "A nonzero representation $V$ is called irreducible (simple) if its only subrepresentations are $0$ and $V$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2eb0d37e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Every one-dimensional representation is irreducible. Irreducible implies indecomposable, but not conversely: over $F[x]$ the matrix $T=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ is indecomposable yet has an invariant line, so it is reducible. In module language, irreducible representations are simple $A$-modules. Over an algebraically closed field, Schur's lemma forces $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ to consist of scalars.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#533ca056',
      },
    },
    "def.quotient-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "If $W\\subseteq V$ is a subrepresentation, the quotient space $V/W$ carries a well-defined action $a(v+W)=av+W$, and the quotient map $V\\to V/W$ is $A$-linear.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2368f9fa',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Well-definedness uses $\\theta(a)W\\subseteq W$. The dimension of the quotient is $\\dim V-\\dim W$. For group representations the action is $\\rho(g)(v+W)=\\rho(g)v+W$. Quotients of semisimple representations are again semisimple; see the proposition on subrepresentations and quotients.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#12704ff7',
      },
    },
    "def.direct-sum-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Given representations $V_1,\\ldots,V_n$ of $A$, the direct sum $V_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus V_n$ carries the componentwise action $a(v_1,\\ldots,v_n)=(av_1,\\ldots,av_n)$; it is a biproduct in $\\mathrm{Rep}_A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da285aee',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The inclusions and projections are $A$-linear and satisfy the biproduct identities. In a basis adapted to the decomposition, each $\\theta(a)$ is block diagonal with blocks $\\theta_i(a)$. The notation $nV$ denotes the direct sum of $n$ copies of $V$. An internal direct sum means $V=W_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus W_n$ with each $W_i$ a subrepresentation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bc1be476',
      },
    },
    "def.tensor-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\rho)$ and $(W,\\sigma)$ be representations of a group $G$. The tensor product $V\\otimes_F W$ carries the action $g(v\\otimes w)=\\rho(g)v\\otimes\\sigma(g)w$, called the tensor product representation of $G$. For a general $F$-algebra $A$ one needs an algebra homomorphism $A\\to A\\otimes A$ for such an action; the group case uses the diagonal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#01c0e688',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The action is well defined because $\\otimes$ is bilinear and $\\rho(g),\\sigma(g)$ are linear. Its dimension is $(\\dim V)(\\dim W)$. The character satisfies $\\chi_{V\\otimes W}(g)=\\chi_V(g)\\chi_W(g)$, see the node on character operations. For a general algebra $A$, $V\\otimes W$ is only an $A\\otimes A$-module unless $A$ is a bialgebra; the group algebra $F[G]$ is the main case here.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2d704b70',
      },
    },
    "def.dual-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\theta)$ be a representation of $A$. On the dual space $V^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_F(V,F)$ put $\\theta^*(a)\\ell=\\ell\\circ\\theta(a)$. Since $\\theta^*(ab)=\\theta^*(b)\\theta^*(a)$, the space $V^*$ is a representation of the opposite algebra $A^{\\mathrm{op}}$, not generally of $A$. For a group representation, $\\rho^*(g)=\\rho(g^{-1})^T$ makes $V^*$ a representation of $G$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bda4792d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The transpose convention is forced by contravariance: $\\theta^*$ is an anti-homomorphism. In the group case the inverse restores a genuine action. In a basis, the matrix of $\\rho^*(g)$ is the inverse transpose of that of $\\rho(g)$. In finite dimension the double dual is naturally isomorphic to $V$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#926de450',
      },
    },
    "def.indecomposable-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "A nonzero representation $V$ is called indecomposable if $V=W_1\\oplus W_2$ with subrepresentations forces $W_1=0$ or $W_2=0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#777cf963',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Every irreducible representation is indecomposable. The converse fails: over $F[x]$ the matrix $T=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ is indecomposable but has the invariant line $F e_1$, hence is reducible. The Krull–Schmidt theorem gives uniqueness of decompositions into indecomposables. Indecomposable is weaker than irreducible.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f1ad6867',
      },
    },
    "def.semisimple-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "A representation $V$ of $A$ is called semisimple (completely reducible) if it is a direct sum of irreducible subrepresentations.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ad226f91',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The zero representation is semisimple by convention (empty direct sum). In finite dimension there are finitely many summands. Equivalent characterizations (a sum of irreducibles, existence of complements) are given in a separate theorem. Maschke's theorem shows that $F[G]$ is semisimple when $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid |G|$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1db0e5c1',
      },
    },
    "def.character": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\theta)$ be a finite-dimensional representation of $A$. Its character is the function $\\chi_\\theta:A\\to F$, $\\chi_\\theta(a)=\\operatorname{tr}\\theta(a)$. It depends only on the isomorphism class of $V$; moreover $\\chi_{V\\oplus W}=\\chi_V+\\chi_W$ and $\\chi_V(1_A)=\\dim V$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#50fda2a6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The trace is basis-independent, so $\\chi_\\theta$ is well defined. Characters are additive on direct sums but generally not multiplicative: $\\chi(ab)\\ne\\chi(a)\\chi(b)$. For $A=F[G]$, restricting $\\chi_\\theta$ to $G$ gives a group character. If $W$ is a subrepresentation, then $\\chi_V=\\chi_W+\\chi_{V/W}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b5641dff',
      },
    },
    "def.group-character": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\rho)$ be a finite-dimensional representation of a finite group $G$. Its character is the function $\\chi_\\rho:G\\to F$, $\\chi_\\rho(g)=\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(g)$. It satisfies $\\chi_\\rho(1)=\\dim V$ and is constant on conjugacy classes.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e726b06f',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Conjugacy invariance follows from the cyclicity of the trace: $\\chi(hgh^{-1})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho(h)\\rho(g)\\rho(h)^{-1})=\\chi(g)$. Isomorphic representations have the same character. Characters add on direct sums and multiply on tensor products (see the node on character operations). A character is not a group homomorphism $G\\to F^\\times$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5ddaa81d',
      },
    },
    "def.class-function": {
      statement: {
        en: "A function $f:G\\to F$ is a class function if $f(hgh^{-1})=f(g)$ for all $g,h\\in G$; equivalently, $f$ is constant on conjugacy classes. Group characters are class functions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a9105cce',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Class functions form an $F$-vector space of dimension the number of conjugacy classes, $\\#\\mathrm{CL}(G)$.\nWhen $\\operatorname{char}F=0$ (or $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$), characters of pairwise non-isomorphic irreducible representations are linearly independent (orthogonality relations).\nA class function is determined by its values on conjugacy classes.\nThe centralizer of $g$ is the stabilizer of $g$ under conjugation.\nLinear independence of irreducible characters: if $\\sum_i c_i\\chi_i=0$, taking the inner product with $\\chi_j$ gives $c_j\\langle\\chi_j,\\chi_j\\rangle=0$; by orthogonality $\\langle\\chi_j,\\chi_j\\rangle=1$ (algebraically closed), or $\\dim_F\\operatorname{End}_G(V_j)\\ne 0$ (when $\\operatorname{char}F=0$), hence $c_j=0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#087eb299',
      },
    },
    "def.representation-annihilator": {
      statement: {
        en: "For a representation $V$ of $A$, define $\\operatorname{Ann}(V)=\\{a\\in A: aV=0\\}$. It is a two-sided ideal of $A$, equal to the kernel of the algebra homomorphism $\\theta:A\\to\\operatorname{End}_F(V)$; $V$ is called faithful if $\\operatorname{Ann}(V)=0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#401aa37e',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The set $\\operatorname{Ann}(V)$ is the kernel of the structure map, hence an ideal. Faithfulness is equivalent to injectivity of $A\\to\\operatorname{End}_F(V)$. The annihilator of a simple module is a primitive ideal; in the finite-dimensional semisimple case these are exactly the maximal two-sided ideals. The annihilator of a direct sum is the intersection of the annihilators.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4eacce6c',
      },
    },
    "def.jacobson-radical": {
      statement: {
        en: "The Jacobson radical of a ring $A$ is $J(A)=\\bigcap_{V}\\operatorname{Ann}(V)$, where $V$ runs over simple left modules; equivalently, it is the intersection of all maximal left ideals. It is a two-sided ideal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9422822d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Also $J(A)$ is the intersection of all maximal right ideals, and it annihilates every irreducible left module.\nIn finite dimension $J(A)=0$ is equivalent to $A$ being semisimple (Jacobson criterion).\nWhen $A$ is finite-dimensional, the quotient $A/J(A)$ is semisimple.\nThe original notes write $\\operatorname{Rad}(A)$, synonymous with $J(A)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#72425379',
      },
    },
    "def.simple-algebra": {
      statement: {
        en: "A nonzero algebra $A$ is called simple if its only two-sided ideals are $0$ and $A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#776178d5',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The matrix algebra $M_n(D)$ over a division algebra $D$ is a simple algebra and serves as the model case. A finite-dimensional simple algebra is isomorphic to a matrix algebra over a division algebra (Wedderburn). A group algebra $F[G]$ is simple only in very small cases. Simplicity is a two-sided condition and is not the same as having no nontrivial left ideal.\n\nTerminology: this map calls an algebra with no nontrivial two-sided ideals simple; the lecture calls this property quasi-simple and reserves simple for semisimple algebras with a unique isomorphism class of simple left ideals. In finite dimension the two notions agree (see thm.wedderburn-simple).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f8a79a37',
      },
    },
    "def.division-algebra": {
      statement: {
        en: "A division algebra over $F$ is a unital $F$-algebra $D$ (not assumed commutative) in which every nonzero element is invertible; equivalently, $D$ is a division ring that is an $F$-algebra.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4350e215',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The field $F$ itself is a division algebra; the quaternions are a noncommutative example over $\\mathbb{R}$. For an irreducible representation $V$, Schur's lemma shows that $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is a division algebra. The division algebra $D$ in Wedderburn's theorem is $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ up to passing to the opposite algebra. Over an algebraically closed field with $\\dim V<\\infty$, one has $D=F$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d3d25e9b',
      },
    },
    "thm.schur-lemma": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V_1,V_2$ be irreducible representations of $A$. Every nonzero $A$-linear map $h:V_1\\to V_2$ is an isomorphism; in particular, $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is a division algebra. See cor.schur-scalar for the scalar conclusion over an algebraically closed $F$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b1ed096a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof uses that $\\ker h$ and $\\operatorname{im}h$ are subrepresentations; irreducibility forces $\\ker h=0$ and $\\operatorname{im}h=V_2$.\nThis is the main tool for decomposing semisimple representations.\nThe scalar statement over an algebraically closed field is a separate corollary, cor.schur-scalar.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ce7f6a4e',
      },
    },
    "thm.semisimple-characterizations": {
      statement: {
        en: "For a finite-dimensional representation $V$ of $A$, the following are equivalent: (1) $V$ is semisimple; (2) $V$ is a sum of irreducible subrepresentations; (3) every subrepresentation $W\\subseteq V$ has a complement $W'$ with $V=W\\oplus W'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#794d3ee3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is on the edge `imp.ss-tfae`: the three equivalences use two finite-dimensional arguments — taking a maximal direct summand, and taking a complement of a maximal proper subrepresentation; for (3) implies (2), first use (3) to split the quotient by a maximal proper subrepresentation into a subrepresentation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d11bfc00',
      },
    },
    "prop.semisimple-subquotient": {
      statement: {
        en: "Subrepresentations and quotient representations of a semisimple representation are semisimple.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#59a5a9e1',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "For a subrepresentation $W$ of $V=\\oplus V_i$, one shows that $W$ is the sum of its intersections with the $V_i$, hence semisimple. Quotients are handled with complements and the equivalent characterizations. Iterating, all subquotients of a semisimple representation are semisimple. This result is used in the proof of Wedderburn–Artin.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3d494c3e',
      },
    },
    "thm.burnside-surjectivity": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional semisimple $A$-module, $A'=\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$, and $A''=\\operatorname{End}_{A'}(V)$. The natural map $\\Phi:A\\to A''$, $a\\mapsto(v\\mapsto av)$, is surjective. Equivalently, for every $f\\in A''$ and every finite family $v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\in V$ there is $a\\in A$ with $av_i=f(v_i)$ for all $i$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9ebfa0cd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Single vector: since $V$ is semisimple, $V=Av\\oplus W$; the projection $p:V\\to Av$ is $A$-linear, so $p\\in A'$. For $f\\in A''$, $f(v)=f(pv)=p f(v)\\in Av$, hence there is $a\\in A$ with $av=f(v)$. Finite tuple: apply the same argument to the diagonal action on $V^n$ to find one $a$ matching any finite tuple $v_i$. In finite dimension $V$ is finitely generated over $A'$: take generators $v_1,\\ldots,v_n$ and choose $a$ with $av_i=f(v_i)$ for every index; any $v=\\sum a'_iv_i$ then satisfies $f(v)=\\sum a'_i f(v_i)=av$, so $\\Phi(a)=f$. This is the elementary form of the double centralizer theorem and does not need Jacobson density.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#48a09167',
      },
    },
    "thm.wedderburn-simple": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a finite-dimensional simple $F$-algebra. Then $A\\cong M_n(D^{\\mathrm{op}})$, where $D$ is a division algebra, $n\\ge1$, and for a simple left ideal $V$ one has $D\\cong\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$. If $F$ is algebraically closed, then $D=F$ and $A\\cong M_n(F)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4664f6cb',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Take a minimal nonzero left ideal $V$; it is a simple $A$-module, so $V$ is semisimple, and by Schur's lemma $D=\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is a division algebra. The natural map $A\\to\\operatorname{End}_D(V)$ is injective because $A$ is simple: if $aV=0$, then $\\operatorname{Ann}(V)$ is a nonzero two-sided ideal, hence equals $A$, contradicting $AV=V$. Burnside's theorem makes it surjective, so $A\\cong\\operatorname{End}_D(V)\\cong M_n(D^{\\mathrm{op}})$. If the field is algebraically closed, Schur's lemma gives $D=F$. Note the direction: $A$ is a matrix algebra over $D^{\\mathrm{op}}$.\nSimplicity here means having no nontrivial two-sided ideals; in finite dimension $J(A)$ is a two-sided ideal not containing $1$, hence $J(A)=0$, so thm.jacobson-criterion gives semisimplicity, and Burnside surjectivity applies.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e5546886',
      },
    },
    "thm.wedderburn-artin": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a finite-dimensional semisimple $F$-algebra. Then $A\\cong\\prod_{i=1}^s M_{n_i}(D_i)$ with division algebras $D_i$; equivalently, every finite-dimensional $A$-module is semisimple.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f074e1cc',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Since $A$ is semisimple, the left regular module decomposes as $A=\\bigoplus_i S_i^{\\oplus n_i}$, with the $S_i$ pairwise non-isomorphic simple left modules.\nRight multiplication gives $A^{\\mathrm{op}}\\cong\\operatorname{End}_A(A)$: $a\\mapsto R_a$, $R_a(x)=xa$; the inverse is $f\\mapsto f(1)$.\nBy Schur's lemma, $\\operatorname{Hom}_A(S_i,S_j)=0$ for $i\\ne j$, hence $\\operatorname{End}_A(A)\\cong\\prod_i\\operatorname{End}_A(S_i^{\\oplus n_i})\\cong\\prod_i M_{n_i}(D_i)$, where $D_i=\\operatorname{End}_A(S_i)$ is a division algebra.\nPassing to opposite algebras gives $A\\cong\\prod_i M_{n_i}(D_i^{\\mathrm{op}})$.\nConversely, each matrix algebra $M_{n_i}(D_i)$ is semisimple and a product of semisimple algebras is semisimple; modules over a finite-dimensional semisimple algebra are semisimple (submodules have complements). $\\blacksquare$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d8810f5b',
      },
    },
    "thm.jacobson-criterion": {
      statement: {
        en: "A finite-dimensional $F$-algebra $A$ is semisimple if and only if $J(A)=0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#acbbe6e8',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is on the edge `imp.jacobson`: first $J(A)$ annihilates every irreducible module; then it is a two-sided ideal; finally the description as a finite intersection of maximal left ideals shows that $J(A)=0$ is equivalent to semisimplicity of the regular module.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dff2704c',
      },
    },
    "prop.partition-of-unity": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V_1,\\ldots,V_s$ be pairwise non-isomorphic finite-dimensional irreducible representations of $A$. Then there exist $a_i\\in A$ such that $\\theta_j(a_i)=\\delta_{ij}\\operatorname{id}_{V_j}$ for all $i,j$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e5487c2a',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Apply Burnside to $V=V_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus V_s$: $A''=\\operatorname{End}_{A'}(V)$ contains the projection $e_i$ onto $V_i$. Surjectivity gives $a_i\\in A$ with $\\theta(a_i)=e_i$. Then $\\theta_j(a_i)=\\delta_{ij}\\operatorname{id}_{V_j}$, since $e_i$ acts as $\\delta_{ij}$ times the identity on $V_j$. These $a_i$ are orthogonal idempotents separating the multiplicities of the irreducible summands. The proof uses Burnside surjectivity (thm.burnside-surjectivity).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#caa870d4',
      },
    },
    "thm.character-determines-semisimple": {
      statement: {
        en: "Assume $\\operatorname{char}F=0$. If $W_1,W_2$ are finite-dimensional semisimple representations of $A$, then $W_1\\cong W_2$ if and only if $\\chi_{W_1}=\\chi_{W_2}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#15da98bf',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Write $W=\\oplus n_iV_i$ with pairwise non-isomorphic irreducibles $V_i$. The partition-of-unity elements $a_i$ give $\\chi_W(a_i)=n_i\\dim V_i$, so the multiplicities $n_i$ are recovered from $\\chi_W$; $\\operatorname{char}F=0$ allows division by $\\dim V_i$. Semisimplicity is essential: over $F[x]$ the identity and a Jordan block can have the same character without being isomorphic. This is the basis of character-table theory; the proof goes through prop.partition-of-unity, which follows from Burnside surjectivity.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4694cb44',
      },
    },
    "prop.fitting-decomposition": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f\\in\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ with $\\dim V<\\infty$. Then $V=\\operatorname{Im}(f^\\infty)\\oplus\\operatorname{Ker}(f^\\infty)$, where $\\operatorname{Im}(f^\\infty)=\\bigcap_n\\operatorname{Im}(f^n)$ and $\\operatorname{Ker}(f^\\infty)=\\bigcup_n\\operatorname{Ker}(f^n)$; $f$ is an automorphism on $\\operatorname{Im}(f^\\infty)$ and nilpotent on $\\operatorname{Ker}(f^\\infty)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#be8d4b3b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The chains $\\operatorname{Im}(f^n)$ and $\\operatorname{Ker}(f^n)$ stabilize in finite dimension. On the intersection $\\operatorname{Im}(f^\\infty)$ the map $f$ is both surjective and injective, hence an automorphism; on the union $\\operatorname{Ker}(f^\\infty)$ some power of $f$ vanishes. The two subspaces intersect in $0$ and their dimensions sum to $\\dim V$. Both are $f$-invariant, and they are subrepresentations when $f$ is $A$-linear.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6ce42026',
      },
    },
    "cor.indecomposable-endomorphism": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional indecomposable representation. Then every $f\\in\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is either an automorphism or nilpotent.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#98553137',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Use the Fitting decomposition $V=\\operatorname{Im}(f^\\infty)\\oplus\\operatorname{Ker}(f^\\infty)$. Both summands are subrepresentations, so indecomposability forces one of them to be $0$. If $\\operatorname{Ker}=0$, then $f$ is injective hence an automorphism; if $\\operatorname{Im}=0$, then $f$ is nilpotent. This is the all-or-nothing property used in Krull–Schmidt.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5add7a90',
      },
    },
    "lem.nilpotent-sum": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional indecomposable representation and $r,\\eta\\in\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ nilpotent. Then $r+\\eta$ is nilpotent.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3e97c052',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By lem.endomorphism-local, $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is a local ring whose maximal ideal consists of the nilpotent endomorphisms; a maximal ideal is closed under addition, so $r+\\eta$ is nilpotent.\nIndecomposability cannot be dropped: for $V=F^2$ with $r=E_{12}$ and $\\eta=E_{21}$ both are nilpotent but $r+\\eta$ is invertible.\nNo commutativity of $r,\\eta$ is needed.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#798fab13',
      },
    },
    "lem.iso-factorization": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional indecomposable representations and let $\\alpha:W\\to V$, $\\beta:V\\to W$ be $A$-linear maps such that $\\alpha\\circ\\beta$ is an automorphism of $V$. Then $\\alpha,\\beta$ are isomorphisms.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#efbb5e57',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "If $\\alpha\\beta$ is an automorphism, then $\\beta$ is injective and $\\alpha$ is surjective. Write $W=\\operatorname{im}\\beta\\oplus\\ker\\alpha$; both summands are subrepresentations, and indecomposability forces $\\ker\\alpha=0$. Thus $\\alpha$ is injective and surjective, hence an isomorphism, and $\\beta$ is an isomorphism as well. This is the cancellation step in Krull–Schmidt.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d8768048',
      },
    },
    "thm.krull-schmidt": {
      statement: {
        en: "Every finite-dimensional representation decomposes as a direct sum of finitely many indecomposable representations, and this decomposition is unique up to permutation and isomorphism.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26405eca',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is on the edge `imp.krull-schmidt`: existence is by induction on dimension; uniqueness uses projections and the nilpotent-sum lemma to match isomorphic summands, cancels them by the isomorphism factorization lemma, and inducts on the remainder. No assumption on the field is needed beyond finite dimensionality.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b83a6716',
      },
    },
    "thm.maschke": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a finite group and let $F$ be a field with $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$. Then the group algebra $F[G]$ is semisimple; equivalently, every finite-dimensional representation of $G$ is semisimple (completely reducible).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#96018f72',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Given a subrepresentation $W\\subseteq V$, take any linear projection $p:V\\to W$ and average over $G$: $\\bar p(v)=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_g g\\,p(g^{-1}v)$. The condition $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$ makes $\\frac1{|G|}$ meaningful; $\\bar p$ is a $G$-equivariant projection, so $V=W\\oplus\\ker\\bar p$. Splitting every subrepresentation this way gives complete reducibility; the group-algebra version follows from the equivalence between representations and $F[G]$-modules.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a394c6fc',
      },
    },
    "cor.group-algebra-wedderburn": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be finite, $\\operatorname{char}F=0$, and $F$ algebraically closed. Then $F[G]\\cong\\prod_{i=1}^s M_{n_i}(F)$ and, as a left $F[G]$-module, $F[G]\\cong\\bigoplus_{i=1}^s n_iV_i$, where the $V_i$ are pairwise non-isomorphic irreducible representations and $n_i=\\dim V_i$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1ad83389',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Maschke's theorem gives that $F[G]$ is semisimple. By thm.wedderburn-artin, $F[G]\\cong\\prod_i M_{n_i}(D_i)$.\nSince $\\operatorname{char}F=0$ and $F$ is algebraically closed, every finite-dimensional division $F$-algebra satisfies $D_i=F$: if $a\\in D_i$ lies outside $F$, then $F(a)$ is a finite extension, contradicting algebraic closedness.\nHence $F[G]\\cong\\prod_i M_{n_i}(F)$. As a left $F[G]$-module, the natural module $F^{n_i}$ of $M_{n_i}(F)$ is simple, so $F[G]\\cong\\bigoplus_i n_iV_i$ with $V_i=F^{n_i}$ and $n_i=\\dim V_i$.\nComparing dimensions gives $|G|=\\sum_i n_i^2$. $\\blacksquare$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#383f5539',
      },
    },
    "prop.character-operations": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional representations of a finite group $G$ with $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$. Then:\n(1) $\\chi_{V\\oplus W}=\\chi_V+\\chi_W$;\n(2) $\\chi_{V\\otimes W}=\\chi_V\\chi_W$;\n(3) $\\chi_{V^*}(g)=\\chi_V(g^{-1})$;\n(4) if $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$, $\\chi_{\\operatorname{Sym}^2V}(g)=\\frac12(\\chi_V(g)^2+\\chi_V(g^2))$;\n(5) if $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$, $\\chi_{\\operatorname{Alt}^2V}(g)=\\frac12(\\chi_V(g)^2-\\chi_V(g^2))$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fdb6e611',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "(1)-(3) hold in any characteristic; (4)(5) use the $\\pm1$ decomposition of the swap and require $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$. In (3), $V^*$ is an $A^{\\mathrm{op}}$-representation, i.e. $g\\mapsto\\rho(g^{-1})^T$ for groups. When $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$, the characters of Sym²/Alt² follow from the trace decomposition of $V^{\\otimes2}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3c56a94a',
      },
    },
    "def.symmetric-power-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\rho)$ be a representation of a group $G$. The symmetric square representation is the quotient representation $\\operatorname{Sym}^2V=(V\\otimes V)/\\langle v\\otimes w-w\\otimes v\\rangle$, with $G$ acting by $g(vw)=\\rho(g)v\\,\\rho(g)w$ (where $vw$ denotes the class of $v\\otimes w$). If $e_1,\\ldots,e_n$ is a basis of $V$, then $\\{e_ie_j:1\\le i\\le j\\le n\\}$ is a basis of $\\operatorname{Sym}^2V$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#81a31d1b',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The definition does not require $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$; the subspace generated by $v\\otimes w-w\\otimes v$ need not be complemented. When $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$, $\\operatorname{Sym}^2V$ coincides with the $+1$-eigenspace of the swap and has dimension $n(n+1)/2$. For group representations the action of $g$ is well defined; for the character formula see prop.character-operations.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c5d33b94',
      },
    },
    "def.exterior-power-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(V,\\rho)$ be a representation of a group $G$. The exterior square representation is the quotient representation $\\operatorname{Alt}^2V=(V\\otimes V)/\\langle v\\otimes w+w\\otimes v\\rangle$, with $G$ acting by $g(v\\wedge w)=\\rho(g)v\\wedge\\rho(g)w$. If $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$ and $e_1,\\ldots,e_n$ is a basis, then $\\{e_i\\wedge e_j:1\\le i<j\\le n\\}$ is a basis, of dimension $n(n-1)/2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3c1759c6',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The definition itself does not require $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$; when $\\operatorname{char}F\\ne2$ it coincides with the $-1$-eigenspace of the swap, and $\\{e_i\\wedge e_j:i<j\\}$ is a basis of dimension $n(n-1)/2$. If $\\operatorname{char}F=2$, that set is not a basis (the dimension of $\\bigwedge^2V$ is $n(n+1)/2$). The exterior square is also denoted $\\bigwedge^2V$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#914d19a4',
      },
    },
    "ex.regular-representation": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a finite group. The regular representation is the left multiplication action of $G$ on $F[G]$, $\\rho_{\\mathrm{reg}}(g)(h)=gh$, viewed as a permutation representation on $F^G$. Its character is $\\chi_{\\mathrm{reg}}(1)=|G|$ and $\\chi_{\\mathrm{reg}}(g)=0$ for $g\\ne1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#17639b95',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The dimension of the regular representation is $|G|$. For the character formula: the diagonal entry of the permutation matrix of $g$ in the basis $G$ is $1$ exactly when $gh=h$, that is, $g=1$. When $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$ and $F$ is algebraically closed, $\\rho_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\cong\\bigoplus_V(\\dim V)V$ with $V$ running over the irreducible representations. Comparing dimensions gives $|G|=\\sum_V(\\dim V)^2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2951e09',
      },
    },
    "cor.schur-scalar": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F$ be algebraically closed and let $V$ be a finite-dimensional irreducible representation of $A$. Then $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)=F\\cdot\\operatorname{id}_V$; every $A$-endomorphism is scalar.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#414f09ed',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By thm.schur-lemma, $D=\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is a finite-dimensional $F$-division algebra. For $a\\in D$, $F(a)$ is a finite extension of $F$; since $F$ is algebraically closed, $F(a)=F$, hence $a\\in F$.\nAlternatively, $a$ has an eigenvalue $\\lambda$, and $a-\\lambda\\operatorname{id}$ is not invertible, hence zero.\nThis is the source of \"endomorphisms are scalars\" in character theory.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dca9a485',
      },
    },
    "lem.endomorphism-local": {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional indecomposable representation. Then $\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is a local ring whose maximal ideal is exactly the set of nilpotent endomorphisms.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9505ef1d',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By cor.indecomposable-endomorphism, every $f\\in\\operatorname{End}_A(V)$ is either an automorphism or nilpotent; in particular, the non-units are exactly the nilpotent elements.\nThe nilpotent elements are closed under addition (no commutativity is assumed): let $f,g$ be nilpotent. If $f+g$ is invertible, set $u=(f+g)^{-1}g$. If $u$ is invertible, then $g=(f+g)u$ is invertible, contradicting that $g$ is nilpotent; so $u$ is a non-unit, and by the corollary $u$ is nilpotent. Then $\\operatorname{id}-u$ is invertible, and $(f+g)^{-1}f=\\operatorname{id}-u$, so $f=(f+g)(\\operatorname{id}-u)$ is invertible, contradicting that $f$ is a non-unit. Therefore $f+g$ is a non-unit, and the corollary makes it nilpotent.\nThe nilpotent elements form an ideal: for any $h$, if $hf$ is invertible then $f$ has a left inverse, and in finite dimension $f$ is invertible, a contradiction; hence $hf$ is a non-unit and nilpotent; the same holds for $fh$.\nThus the nilpotent elements form a two-sided ideal; every nonzero element of the quotient ring is invertible, so the quotient is a division ring. This makes the ring local, with maximal ideal exactly the nilpotent elements.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9c68be45',
      },
    },
    "def.character-inner-product": {
      statement: { en: "Let $G$ be a finite group with $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$. For functions $f_1,f_2:G\\to F$ define $\\langle f_1,f_2\\rangle=\\frac{1}{|G|}\\sum_{g\\in G}f_1(g)f_2(g^{-1})$. This is an $F$-bilinear form; on the space of class functions it gives the pairing used in character theory.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2ab69dad' },
      notes: {
        en: "Bilinearity is immediate; symmetry follows from the substitution $g\\mapsto g^{-1}$.\\nOn class functions $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_g f(g)f(g^{-1})$; for complex representations ($F\\subseteq\\mathbb C$) with $f$ complex conjugation, it is a positive definite Hermitian form when restricted to the space of characters; over a general field only nondegeneracy is required.\\nThe pairing writes the orthogonality of irreducible characters as $\\langle\\chi_i,\\chi_j\\rangle=\\delta_{ij}$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c6e354e9' },
    },
    "lem.fixed-space-dimension": {
      statement: { en: "Let $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$ and let $(V,\\rho)$ be a finite-dimensional representation. Then $\\dim_F V^G=\\langle\\chi_\\rho,\\mathbf 1\\rangle$, where $V^G=\\{v\\in V:\\rho(g)v=v\\ \\text{for all }g\\in G\\}$ and $\\mathbf 1$ is the constant function $g\\mapsto1$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#52979ee3' },
      notes: { en: "The averaging operator $\\varphi(v)=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_g\\rho(g)v$ is a $G$-equivariant projection onto $V^G$.\nIn a basis making $\\varphi$ diagonal with eigenvalue 1 on $V^G$ and 0 on a complement, $\\operatorname{tr}\\varphi=\\dim V^G$.\nOn the other hand $\\operatorname{tr}\\varphi=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_g\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(g)=\\langle\\chi_\\rho,\\mathbf 1\\rangle$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#39f8b979' },
    },
    "prop.character-inner-product-hom": {
      statement: { en: "Let $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$ and let $V_1,V_2$ be finite-dimensional representations. Then $\\langle\\chi_{V_1},\\chi_{V_2}\\rangle=\\dim_F\\operatorname{Hom}_G(V_1,V_2)$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#157e3575' },
      notes: { en: "Let $W=\\operatorname{Hom}_F(V_1,V_2)$ with action $(g\\cdot f)(v)=g\\,f(g^{-1}v)$; then $W^G=\\operatorname{Hom}_G(V_1,V_2)$.\nThe character is $\\chi_W(g)=\\chi_{V_1^*}(g)\\chi_{V_2}(g)=\\chi_{V_1}(g^{-1})\\chi_{V_2}(g)$.\nApplying the fixed-space dimension lemma to $W$ gives $\\dim W^G=\\langle\\chi_W,\\mathbf 1\\rangle=\\langle\\chi_{V_1},\\chi_{V_2}\\rangle$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#92b6aebc' },
    },
    "thm.character-orthogonality": {
      statement: { en: "Let $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$, let $F$ be algebraically closed, and let $V_1,V_2$ be finite-dimensional irreducible representations. Then $\\langle\\chi_{V_1},\\chi_{V_2}\\rangle=1$ if $V_1\\cong V_2$, and $0$ otherwise.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b4e6711' },
      notes: { en: "By prop.character-inner-product-hom the inner product equals $\\dim\\operatorname{Hom}_G(V_1,V_2)$.\nSchur's lemma: there are no nonzero homomorphisms between non-isomorphic irreducible representations; when isomorphic, $\\operatorname{End}_G(V_1)=F$ (algebraically closed, see cor.schur-scalar), of dimension 1.\nHence the inner product is $\\delta_{12}$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bbb88d23' },
    },
    "cor.character-multiplicity": {
      statement: { en: "Let $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$, let $F$ be algebraically closed, and let $V$ be a finite-dimensional semisimple representation, $V\\cong\\bigoplus_i n_iV_i$ with the $V_i$ pairwise non-isomorphic irreducible. Then $n_i=\\langle\\chi_V,\\chi_{V_i}\\rangle$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1002e29c' },
      notes: { en: "By prop.character-operations (1), $\\chi_V=\\sum_j n_j\\chi_{V_j}$.\nTaking the inner product with $\\chi_{V_i}$ and using orthogonality $\\langle\\chi_{V_j},\\chi_{V_i}\\rangle=\\delta_{ji}$ gives $\\langle\\chi_V,\\chi_{V_i}\\rangle=n_i$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#93219121' },
    },
    "def.character-table": {
      statement: { en: "Let $G$ be a finite group, $\\operatorname{char}F=0$, and $F$ algebraically closed. Put $\\operatorname{Irr}(G)_F=\\{V_1,\\ldots,V_s\\}$ and let $CL(G)=\\{C_1,\\ldots,C_r\\}$ be the conjugacy classes with representatives $g_j\\in C_j$. The character table is the $s\\times r$ matrix $T=(\\chi_{V_i}(g_j))_{i,j}$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#27b992c8' },
      notes: { en: "The rows are the irreducible characters; $\\operatorname{char}F=0$ guarantees that $\\chi_i=\\chi_j$ iff $V_i\\cong V_j$ (thm.character-determines-semisimple).\nThe rows are orthonormal for the character inner product (thm.character-orthogonality).\nCharacters are class functions, so the columns depend only on the conjugacy class; $s\\le r$, with equality requiring the additional fact that the characters form a basis of the class-function space.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#00c12957' },
    },
    "cor.regular-character-decomposition": {
      statement: { en: "Let $\\operatorname{char}F\\nmid|G|$ and $F$ algebraically closed. The regular representation decomposes as $\\rho_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\cong\\bigoplus_{V\\in\\operatorname{Irr}(G)_F}(\\dim V)\\,V$, hence $\\chi_{\\mathrm{reg}}=\\sum_V(\\dim V)\\chi_V$. In particular $|G|=\\sum_V(\\dim V)^2$, and for $g\\ne1$ one has $\\sum_V(\\dim V)\\chi_V(g)=0$.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9bc4b266' },
      notes: { en: "Maschke's theorem gives that $F[G]$ is semisimple, so the regular module decomposes as $F[G]\\cong\\bigoplus_i n_iV_i$.\nBy cor.character-multiplicity, $n_i=\\langle\\chi_{\\mathrm{reg}},\\chi_{V_i}\\rangle$; since $\\chi_{\\mathrm{reg}}(1)=|G|$ and $\\chi_{\\mathrm{reg}}(g)=0$ for $g\\ne1$, we get $n_i=\\frac1{|G|}|G|\\chi_{V_i}(1)=\\dim V_i$.\nEvaluating at $g=1$ and at $g\\ne1$ gives the two identities.", status: 'draft', basedOn: 'v0.9.3#02b3531e' },
    },
    'def.cyclic-subspace': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T:V\\to V$ be a linear map of a finite-dimensional vector space over a field $K$, and let $v\\in V$. The subspace\n\n$K[T]v:=\\operatorname{span}_K\\{v,Tv,T^2v,\\ldots\\}=\\{p(T)v:p\\in K[x]\\}$\n\nis the **cyclic subspace generated by** $v$; if $K[T]v=V$, then $v$ is a **cyclic vector** for $T$. A space $V$ that has a cyclic vector is called **cyclic**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fe23c360',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "$K[T]v$ is the smallest invariant subspace containing $v$ that is stable under $T$; conversely, every invariant subspace containing $v$ and stable under $T$ contains all $T^kv$.\n\nLet $\\mu_v$ be the monic polynomial with $p(T)v=0$ (it exists and is unique because $K[x]$ is a principal ideal domain); then $\\dim_K K[T]v=\\deg\\mu_v$, and as a $K[x]$-module, $K[T]v\\cong K[x]/(\\mu_v)$.\n\nIn finite dimension, $v$ is a cyclic vector if and only if $\\deg\\mu_v=\\dim V$; in that case $\\mu_T=\\chi_T$. Conversely, if $\\mu_T=\\chi_T$, a cyclic vector exists (for instance, take the module corresponding to $1,x,\\ldots$).\n\nExample: for the companion matrix $C_d$, the vector $e_1$ is cyclic (see @@def.companion-matrix@@); $\\operatorname{diag}(C_d,C_d)$ is not cyclic, because its minimal polynomial is still $d$ while the dimension is $2\\deg d$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5efee53',
      },
    },

    'def.companion-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field and let $d(x)=x^m+a_{m-1}x^{m-1}+\\cdots+a_0\\in K[x]$ be monic with $m\\ge1$. Its **companion matrix** is\n\n$C_d=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0&\\cdots&0&-a_0\\\\ 1&0&\\cdots&0&-a_1\\\\ 0&1&\\cdots&0&-a_2\\\\ \\vdots& &\\ddots& &\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&\\cdots&1&-a_{m-1}\\end{pmatrix}\\in M_m(K),$\n\nthat is, the subdiagonal entries are all $1$ and the last column is $(-a_0,\\ldots,-a_{m-1})^T$. Then both the characteristic polynomial and the minimal polynomial of $C_d$ equal $d$, and $e_1$ is a cyclic vector.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#154c5b89',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "On $K[x]/(d)$, multiplication by $x$ has, in the basis $1,x,\\ldots,x^{m-1}$, the matrix described above: the relation $x^m\\equiv-(a_0+a_1x+\\cdots+a_{m-1}x^{m-1})$ produces the last column.\n\nFrom $d(C_d)=0$ we get $\\mu_{C_d}\\mid d$; and since $1,x,\\ldots,x^{m-1}$ are linearly independent in $K[x]/(d)$, we get $\\deg\\mu_{C_d}=m=\\deg d$, hence $\\mu_{C_d}=d$; also, $\\chi_{C_d}$ and $d$ are both monic of degree $m$ and $\\mu\\mid\\chi$, so $\\chi_{C_d}=d$.\n\nConvention: some textbooks put the $1$ on the superdiagonal and the coefficients in the first row; the two conventions differ by a transpose, and the characteristic and minimal polynomials are unchanged.\n\nWhen $d=1$, we have $m=0$, $C_d$ is the empty matrix, and this definition only discusses $m\\ge1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#71844f67',
      },
    },

    'thm.pid-fg-module-structure': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain and $M$ a finitely generated $R$-module. Then there exist $r\\ge0$, $k\\ge0$, and nonzero non-units $d_1,\\ldots,d_k\\in R$ satisfying $d_1\\mid d_2\\mid\\cdots\\mid d_k$, such that\n\n$M\\cong R^r\\oplus R/(d_1)\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus R/(d_k).$\n\nThe free rank $r$ and the ideals $(d_1),\\ldots,(d_k)$ (up to units) are uniquely determined by $M$; $k=0$ if and only if $M$ is torsion-free.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#241073e3',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: see the edge @@imp.pid-fg-module-structure@@.\n\nWhen $R=K[x]$ and $M$ comes from a linear operator $A$, the free part is zero, $d_i$ are exactly the invariant factors of $xI-A$, and the blocks give the rational canonical form (@@thm.rational-canonical-form@@).\n\nIn $K[x]$, requiring $d_i$ to be monic makes them unique; in a general PID they are unique only up to units.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#29970e2e',
      },
    },

    'def.elementary-divisor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain and let the nonzero invariant factors of $A\\in M_{m\\times n}(R)$ be $d_1\\mid\\cdots\\mid d_r$. Factor each $d_i$ into a product of prime powers, $d_i=\\prod_p p^{e_{i,p}}$; all prime powers whose exponent satisfies $e_{i,p}>0$, namely $p^{e_{i,p}}$ (counted with multiplicity), are the **elementary divisors** of $A$. For $A\\in M_n(K)$, the elementary divisors of $xI-A$ are called the elementary divisors of the linear operator $A$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#404c64a0',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ Disambiguation: the elementary divisors here are a matrix/module-theoretic notion, **not** the Weierstrass elementary factors $E_p(z)=(1-z)\\exp(z+\\cdots+z^p/p)$ (@@def.elementary-factor@@, entire functions in complex analysis). The two share only their Chinese name.\n\nRelation to invariant factors: $d_i$ is the product of the elementary divisors grouped along the divisibility chain; conversely, the elementary divisors determine all invariant factors uniquely.\n\nExample: if the invariant factors are $x-1$ and $(x-1)(x-2)$, the elementary divisors are $x-1,\\ x-1,\\ x-2$. When $K$ is algebraically closed, the elementary divisors of $xI-A$ are exactly $(x-\\lambda)^s$, each corresponding to a Jordan block of size $s$ (@@thm.jordan-canonical-form@@).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d0e9c3e3',
      },
    },

    'thm.rational-canonical-form': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What this does**: the Jordan canonical form requires the characteristic polynomial to split over the field (all eigenvalues in the field). The rational canonical form does **not** need this — as long as $K$ is a field, every matrix can be put into it.\n\nLet $K$ be a field and $A \\in M_{n}(K)$. Then $A$ is similar in $M_{n}(K)$ to a **unique** block-diagonal matrix\n\n$$\\operatorname{diag}\\bigl(C_{d_{1}}, C_{d_{2}}, \\ldots, C_{d_{s}}\\bigr) ,$$\n\nwhere each $C_{d}$ is a **companion matrix**: $d(x) = x^{m} + a_{m-1}x^{m-1} + \\cdots + a_{0}$ is monic,\n\n$$C_{d} := \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & & & & -a_{0} \\\\ 1 & 0 & & & -a_{1} \\\\ & \\ddots & \\ddots & & \\vdots \\\\ & & 1 & 0 & -a_{m-2} \\\\ & & & 1 & -a_{m-1} \\end{pmatrix} ,$$\n\nand $d_{1} \\mid d_{2} \\mid \\cdots \\mid d_{s}$ (each divides the next), where each $d_{i}$ is a **nonunit monic polynomial** ($\\deg d_{i}\\ge1$); if there are no nonunit factors (e.g. $V=0$), we set $s=0$ and the block-diagonal matrix is empty. The $d_{i}$ are called the **invariant factors**.\n\n**Division of labor with the Jordan form**: when the characteristic polynomial splits over $K$, both the rational canonical form and the Jordan form are complete invariants; when it does not split, **only** the rational canonical form (over $K$) is available — the Jordan form exists only after passing to an extension field.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b11b1bd',
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why \"rational\"**: historically it refers to the case $K = \\mathbb{Q}$ — exactly the typical situation in which the characteristic polynomial does not split over $\\mathbb{Q}$. The name stuck, and it now means \"valid over an arbitrary field\".\n\n**Sketch of proof (full proof on the edge @@imp.rational-canonical-form-proof@@)**: view $V=K^n$ as a $K[x]$-module ($x\\cdot v=Av$). Cayley–Hamilton gives $\\chi_A(A)=0$, so $V$ is a finitely generated torsion module. By the structure theorem for finitely generated modules over a PID (@@thm.pid-fg-module-structure@@), $V\\cong\\bigoplus_i K[x]/(d_i)$ with $d_1\\mid\\cdots\\mid d_s$ monic non-units. For each cyclic summand take the basis $1,x,\\ldots,x^{m-1}$; the matrix of multiplication by $x$ is exactly the companion matrix $C_{d_i}$ (@@def.companion-matrix@@; for cyclic subspaces see @@def.cyclic-subspace@@). Uniqueness follows from the Smith invariant factors of $xI-A$ (@@def.invariant-factor@@, @@thm.smith-normal-form@@).\n\n**The chain**: characteristic polynomial $\\chi_A$ (@@def.char-poly@@) $\\to$ Cayley–Hamilton (@@thm.cayley-hamilton@@) $\\to$ torsion module $\\to$ structure theorem $\\to$ invariant factors (@@def.invariant-factor@@) $\\to$ companion blocks. The characteristic polynomial is the product of the blocks $d_1\\cdots d_s$, and the minimal polynomial is $d_s$; these two facts also explain the origin of \"$d_s$ is the minimal polynomial\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d56adc83',
      },
    },
    'thm.matrix-ring': {
      statement: {
        en: "Define componentwise addition and matrix multiplication on $M_n(K)$. Then\n\n- $(M_n(K), +)$ is an abelian group (the zero element is the zero matrix);\n- multiplication is associative, distributes over addition on both sides, and has the unit $I_n$ (with $1$ on the diagonal and $0$ elsewhere);\n- hence $M_n(K)$ is a **ring**, and moreover a $K$-algebra (addition and scalar multiplication make it a $K$-vector space, and multiplication is compatible with them).\n\n⚠ Multiplication is **not commutative** (when $n \\ge 2$), so it is a noncommutative ring.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#81ce5ed1",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Every sentence of this theorem is the result of reading $M_n(K)$ as $\\operatorname{End}(K^{n})$: addition of matrices is addition of maps, multiplication is composition, and the unit is the identity map (all proved in the entry \"linear map $\\leftrightarrow$ matrix\").\n\n⭐ Thus $M_n(K)$ becomes a **ring**, and one can ask about its ideals, its invertible elements ($\\operatorname{GL}_n(K)$), one-sided inverses, and so on — the tools of abstract algebra transfer directly.\n\n⚠ \"Noncommutative\" is essential: the matrix ring is the typical noncommutative ring and the prototype of a finite-dimensional algebra (Wedderburn's theorem says that simple finite-dimensional algebras are $M_n(D)$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ed20d6aa",
      },
    },
    'thm.change-of-basis': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $T : V \\to V$ be linear and let $\\mathcal{B}, \\mathcal{B}'$ be two ordered bases of $V$.\n\nWrite $P := [\\mathrm{id}]_{\\mathcal{B}'}^{\\mathcal{B}}$ (**transition matrix**; its $j$-th column is the coordinate vector of the $\\mathcal{B}'$-th vector of $j$ in the basis $\\mathcal{B}$).\n\nThen $P$ is invertible, and\n\n$$[T]_{\\mathcal{B}'} = P^{-1}\\,[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\,P$$\n\nIn this case the two matrices are called **similar** (written $A \\sim B$). Similarity is an equivalence relation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c44083a1",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ In one sentence: **changing basis is conjugation**.\n\nThe $P^{-1}AP$ in $P$ governs \"how coordinates change\", and one factor entering and one leaving on the two sides exactly cancels the choice of basis — so \"similar\" is an **intrinsic** property of the matrix (belonging to the linear map), not a property of the array of numbers itself.\n\n⭐ A division of labour worth remembering: $A$ and $B$ are similar $\\iff$ they are the matrices of **one and the same** linear map in two bases;\n\n$A$ and $B$ are equivalent (only $B = PAQ$ with $P, Q$ invertible is required) $\\iff$ they have equal rank (even the spaces may change).\n\n⚠ Similarity is much stronger than equivalence: similar matrices **have the same rank, trace, determinant and characteristic polynomial** — these are similarity invariants (needed later for the theory of eigenvalues).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#70bc6520",
      },
    },
    'def.equivalent-matrices': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A, B \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$. If there exist invertible matrices $P \\in GL_m(K)$ and $Q \\in GL_n(K)$ such that\n\n$$B = P A Q,$$\n\nthen $A$ and $B$ are called **equivalent**. This is the same as saying that $A$ can be transformed into $B$ by finitely many elementary row and column operations.\n\nEquivalence is an **equivalence relation** on $M_{m \\times n}(K)$, and it allows $m \\ne n$. Over a field $K$, the equivalence classes are completely classified by rank: every $A$ is equivalent to the unique normal form\n\n$$\\operatorname{diag}(I_r, 0), \\qquad r = \\operatorname{rank} A.$$\n\nEquivalence is weaker than both **similarity** ($B = P^{-1} A P$) and **congruence** ($B = P^{T} A P$): the latter two are stated only for square matrices of the same order, and each of them preserves more invariants.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#481d8494",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Equivalence remembers only the rank**: multiplying on the left changes the basis of the row space, multiplying on the right changes the basis of the column space, so rows and columns can be eliminated independently.\n\n⭐ Normal form: use elementary row operations to reach reduced row echelon form, then use elementary column operations to turn the pivot columns into unit vectors and kill the other columns, obtaining $\\operatorname{diag}(I_r,0)$. Each step is left or right multiplication by an elementary matrix; multiplying these elementary matrices together gives $P,Q$.\n\n⚠ Similarity and congruence are special cases of equivalence (square matrices of the same order; take $Q=P^{-1}$ or $Q=P^{T}$), but neither contains the other.\n\n⚠ Over $\\mathbb{Z}$ one uses the Smith normal form instead.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#dc4c96e3",
      },
    },
    'thm.rank-row-col': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$. For $A$, regard its $m$ row vectors as vectors in $K^{n}$; the dimension of the subspace they span is called the **row rank**. The **column rank** is defined similarly (the $A$'s $n$ column vectors span a subspace of $K^{m}$, whose dimension it is). Then\n\nrow rank $=$ column rank $= \\operatorname{rank} A$, that is,\n\n$$\\operatorname{rank}_{\\text{row}} A = \\operatorname{rank}_{\\text{col}} A = \\operatorname{rank} A$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{rank} A$ is the rank of the linear map $x \\mapsto Ax$ (see \"rank\").",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9195e480",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Row rank = column rank\" is not obvious: it says that in $K^{n}$ the $m$ row vectors and in $K^{m}$ the $n$ column vectors are **exactly equally independent**, and this is precisely why \"the rank of a matrix\" is a single number.\n\n⭐ The proof goes through elementary operations: left multiplication by an invertible matrix does not change the row space (it only changes the basis), so one may first reduce $A$ to reduced row echelon form, where the number of nonzero rows is the rank — and the column rank is determined by the same pivots.\n\n⚠ This is the justification for \"computing rank by elimination\": moving column operations around does not change that number.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#8bb152ce",
      },
    },
    'thm.det-multiplicative': {
      statement: {
        en: "For $A, B \\in M_n(K)$:\n\n$$\\det(AB) = \\det A \\cdot \\det B, \\qquad \\det A^{\\mathsf{T}} = \\det A, \\qquad \\det(cA) = c^{n} \\det A$$\n\nHence if $A$ is invertible then $\\det(A^{-1}) = (\\det A)^{-1}$; conversely $A$ is invertible $\\iff \\det A \\ne 0$.\n\n(This is the contrapositive of \"$\\det$ is a multiplicative homomorphism\": $\\det A = 0$ $\\iff$ $A$ is not invertible.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a26c261c",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Multiplicativity** is the most important property of the determinant: it translates \"matrix multiplication (composition)\" into \"multiplication of scalars\", so $\\det$ is a **multiplicative homomorphism** from the ring $M_n(K)$ to the field $K$ (sending $\\operatorname{GL}_n(K)$ to $K^{\\times}$).\n\n⭐ The trick of the proof: fix $B$ and look at $A \\mapsto \\det(AB)$ — it is **alternating multilinear** in the columns of $A$ (because the columns of $AB$ are linear combinations of the columns of $A$), so by existence and uniqueness it equals a constant multiple of $\\det A$;\n\ntaking $A = I$ determines the constant $= \\det B$.\n\n⚠ Note $\\det(cA) = c^{n}\\det A$ (not $c\\det A$) — every column is multiplied by $c$, $n$ times in total.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a8ad5876",
      },
    },
    'thm.cramer': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_n(K)$ with $\\det A \\ne 0$ and $b \\in K^{n}$. Then the system $Ax = b$ has a unique solution, namely\n\n$$x_j = \\frac{\\det A_j}{\\det A}, \\qquad j = 1, \\ldots, n$$\n\nwhere $A_j$ is the matrix obtained from $A$ by replacing its $j$-th column with $b$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7d7e1132",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Its theoretical meaning exceeds its computational meaning: it gives an **explicit** expression for the solution in terms of $b$, so one immediately sees that \"the solution depends continuously on $b$\" (when $\\det A \\ne 0$, $A^{-1}$ exists, $x = A^{-1}b$).\n\n⚠ Computationally it is a bad deal: computing $n$ determinants of order $n$ is far more expensive than elimination (unless $n$ is very small).\n\nModern numerical linear algebra never solves systems this way.\n\n⭐ It is the algorithmic embodiment of \"$\\det A \\ne 0 \\iff$ is invertible\": nonzero denominator $\\iff$ unique solution $\\iff$ invertible.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#20121974",
      },
    },
    'thm.linear-system-solutions': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A \\in M_{m \\times n}(K)$ and $b \\in K^{m}$, and consider the system $Ax = b$.\n\n- **Solvable** $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{rank} A = \\operatorname{rank}(A \\mid b)$ (append $b$ as the $n+1$-th column);\n- when solvable, the solution set is the **affine subspace**\n\n$$x_0 + \\ker A = \\{\\, x_0 + u : u \\in \\ker A \\,\\}$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ is any particular solution; hence the solution space has dimension $n - \\operatorname{rank} A$;\n- the solution space of the homogeneous system $Ax = 0$ is $\\ker A$, of dimension $= n - \\operatorname{rank} A$;\n- in particular: when $m = n$ and $\\det A \\ne 0$, the solution is **unique**, $x = A^{-1}b$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#14284a7b",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ \"Solution set = particular solution + homogeneous solutions\" is the central picture of linear algebra: **the inhomogeneous system has no structure, the homogeneous one does** (it is a subspace), and the inhomogeneous solution set is just a translate of it (an affine subspace).\n\n⭐ The dimension formula $n - \\operatorname{rank} A$ is the rank–nullity theorem in the language of linear systems: the number of variables $n$ = the $\\operatorname{rank} A$ constrained away + the $\\dim \\ker A$ free ones.\n\n⚠ The geometric meaning of \"solvable $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{rank} A = \\operatorname{rank}(A \\mid b)$\": $b$ lies in the column space of $A$.\n\nDo not misremember it as \"rank A = m\": for a general m × n matrix, rank A = m only says that the rows of A are of full rank, so that every b is solvable; uniqueness needs rank A = n. The two coincide only for square matrices (m = n).\n\n**Which of the four items is a \"theorem\" and which is only a restatement** (this group is often presented as four parallel statements, but the last one depends on the first two):\n\n- **Solvability $\\iff \\operatorname{rank}A = \\operatorname{rank}(A\\mid b)$**: **this step has real content**, it is equivalent to \"$b \\in \\operatorname{col}(A)$\". The proof uses row reduction (Gauss elimination) — a row of the form $0 = c \\ne 0$ appears exactly when the ranks differ. **It only uses that $K$ is a field** (elimination is free over a field; over $\\mathbb{Z}$ one must pass to the Smith normal form).\n- **Solution set = particular solution + homogeneous solutions**: **this step is purely linear** (no field, no finite dimension needed): if $Ax_{0} = b$ and $Au = 0$, then $A(x_{0}+u) = b$; conversely the difference of two solutions lies in $\\ker A$. **An affine subspace need not be a subspace** — unless $b = 0$.\n- **$\\dim \\ker A = n - \\operatorname{rank} A$**: **this step uses the rank–nullity theorem** (thm.rank-nullity), the only dimensional input; it itself requires the finite-dimensional hypothesis.\n- **$m=n$, $\\det A \\ne 0$ $\\implies$ a unique solution**: this is **only a special case of the first two** ($\\det \\ne 0 \\iff \\operatorname{rank} A = n \\iff \\ker A = 0$), with no independent content.\n\n⚠️ **Three hypotheses that cannot be dropped**:\n- \"$K$ is a **field**\": over $\\mathbb{Z}$ the equation $2x = 1$ has no solution while $2x = 2$ has one, and the rank criterion must be replaced by the Smith normal form (or by a gcd condition for existence $\\iff$).\n- \"**Finite-dimensional**\": in infinite dimension the equality $\\dim \\ker A = n - \\operatorname{rank} A$ fails (there is no $n$ to subtract), and one must speak of codimension.\n- \"$\\det A \\ne 0$\" is meaningful only when $m = n$; for $m \\ne n$ do not speak of \"$\\det$\" — that is the setting of pseudoinverses and least squares (def.pseudoinverse).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2f06d544",
      },
    },
    'thm.cauchy-schwarz': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be an inner product space and $u, v \\in V$. Then\n\n$$|\\langle u, v \\rangle| \\le \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $u, v$ are **linearly dependent**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#217f42aa",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the only step on the road from the inner product to $\\implies$ the norm that needs proof (the triangle inequality is derived from it).\n\n⭐ Proof trick: for every $t \\in K$ set $f(t) := \\|u - t v\\|^{2} \\ge 0$; after expansion this is a quadratic expression in $t$;\n\ntaking $t = \\langle v, u \\rangle / \\|v\\|^{2}$ gives the conclusion.\n\n⭐ It is one of the most used inequalities in analysis: on $L^{2}$ it is $|\\int f\\bar{g}| \\le \\|f\\|_{2}\\|g\\|_{2}$, and in probability theory it is $|\\operatorname{Cov}| \\le \\sigma_X\\sigma_Y$.\n\nIt is the same inequality as the \"Cauchy–Schwarz for sequences\" (the case $p = q = 2$ of Hölder in the analysis.lp cluster).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#50061fd5",
      },
    },
    'def.orthogonality': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V$ be an inner product space.\n\n- $u \\perp v$ (one says $u$ and $v$ are **orthogonal**) $\\iff \\langle u, v \\rangle = 0$;\n- $S \\perp T$: every vector of $S$ is orthogonal to every vector of $T$;\n- $\\{v_1, \\ldots, v_k\\}$ is called an **orthonormal set** exactly when $\\langle v_i, v_j \\rangle = \\delta_{ij}$;\n- $\\{v_i\\}$ is an **orthonormal basis** exactly when it is both a basis and an orthonormal set.\n\n**Pythagoras' theorem**: $u \\perp v \\implies \\|u + v\\|^{2} = \\|u\\|^{2} + \\|v\\|^{2}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#5d2f895d",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The great advantage of an orthonormal basis is that **the coordinates are inner products**: if $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$ is orthonormal, then\n\n$$v = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\langle e_i, v \\rangle\\, e_i, \\qquad \\|v\\|^{2} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} |\\langle e_i, v \\rangle|^{2}$$\n\n(the second identity is **Parseval's identity**.) For a general basis one must solve a linear system to find coordinates; for an orthonormal basis it suffices to compute $n$ inner products — this is why Gram–Schmidt is worth the trouble.\n\n⚠ An orthonormal set differs from an orthonormal \"basis\": the former requires only orthonormality, not spanning the whole space.\n\n**Which of the four items is a definition and which needs a property** (this group looks like it consists entirely of \"definitions\", but in fact only the first is one):\n\n- $u \\perp v \\iff \\langle u,v\\rangle = 0$: **this is a definition**, with no content.\n- $S \\perp T$: **also a definition** (pairwise orthogonality).\n- **Orthonormal set** $\\iff$ $\\langle v_{i},v_{j}\\rangle = \\delta_{ij}$: **still a definition**, but note that it includes the case $i = j$, that is, every vector has **unit length**; saying only \"pairwise orthogonal\" is not enough (that is an **orthogonal set**, whose lengths may differ from $1$).\n- **Orthonormal basis** $\\iff$ basis $+$ orthonormal set: **this item uses a nontrivial fact** — an orthonormal set is **automatically linearly independent** (taking the inner product of $\\sum c_{i}v_{i} = 0$ with $v_{j}$ gives $c_{j} = 0$), so \"orthonormal set + spanning\" is indeed \"basis + orthonormal\". If one requires only \"basis\" and \"pairwise orthogonal\", one must additionally assume that there is no zero vector.\n\n**The hypothesis of Pythagoras' theorem**: $u \\perp v$ suffices (no finite dimension, no completeness): $\\lVert u+v\\rVert^{2} = \\lVert u\\rVert^{2} + 2\\operatorname{Re}\\langle u,v\\rangle + \\lVert v\\rVert^{2}$, and in the real case $\\langle u,v\\rangle = 0$ can simply be cancelled; **the complex case needs $\\operatorname{Re}\\langle u,v\\rangle = 0$** (in general $\\langle u,v\\rangle$ is a complex number, and orthogonality only says that its real part vanishes — orthogonality itself is defined by $\\langle u,v\\rangle = 0$, so there is no problem, but in the proof one cannot simply throw one term away).\n\n⚠️ **Two pairs of words that are easily confused**: an **orthogonal set** (pairwise orthogonal, arbitrary lengths) versus an **orthonormal set** (additionally unit length); the **orthogonal complement** $S^{\\perp}$ is \"the vectors orthogonal to all of $S$\"; it is a **subspace** and $S \\cap S^{\\perp} = \\{0\\}$ (for a positive definite inner product), but for a general bilinear form this can fail (def.isotropic-subspace).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ab454431",
      },
    },
    'thm.projection-formula': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq V$ be a finite-dimensional subspace and $v \\in V$. Then\n\n**(1) Best approximation**: $\\|v - P_U v\\| \\le \\|v - u\\|$ for every $u \\in U$, with equality only when $u = P_U v$;\n\n**(2) Explicit formula**: if $(e_1, \\ldots, e_k)$ is an orthonormal basis of $U$, then\n\n$$P_U v = \\sum_{j=1}^{k} \\langle e_j, v \\rangle\\, e_j, \\qquad \\|v\\|^{2} = \\|P_U v\\|^{2} + \\|v - P_U v\\|^{2} \\ge \\sum_{j=1}^{k} |\\langle e_j, v \\rangle|^{2}$$\n\n(the last statement is **Bessel's inequality**).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ae1c8958",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The geometric meaning of \"best approximation\": $P_U v$ is the point of $U$ **closest** to $v$, and \"closest\" is well defined by the norm coming from the inner product.\n\nThis is the common source of least squares and of Fourier series (truncating to the first $n$ terms is optimal).\n\n⭐ The explicit formula shows the power of an orthonormal basis: the projection need not solve a linear system, only compute $k$ inner products.\n\n⚠ \"$U$ finite-dimensional\" or \"$U$ closed\" is necessary: in infinite dimension, when the subspace is not closed, the closest **point may fail to exist** (only the infimum exists).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#26f65694",
      },
    },
    'thm.polar-decomposition': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: a complex number can be written as $z = e^{i\\theta}\\lvert z\\rvert$ (direction $\\times$ length). Matrices have the same kind of decomposition: **unitary (direction) $\\times$ positive semidefinite (length)**.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. Then there exist a unitary matrix $U$ and a **positive semidefinite** Hermitian matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$A = UP ,$$\n\nand $P$ is uniquely determined by $A$ ($P = \\sqrt{A^{*}A}$); if $A$ is invertible, then $U$ is also unique, and $P$ is positive definite.\n\n**The other order**: likewise $A = P'U'$, where $P' = \\sqrt{AA^{*}}$ is positive semidefinite and $U'$ is unitary.\n\n**Relation to the SVD**: diagonalizing $P$ ($P = V\\Sigma V^{*}$) gives $A = (UV)\\Sigma V^{*}$ — this is exactly the singular value decomposition; the diagonal entries of $\\Sigma$ are the **singular values**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d1e2e5c0",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why $P = \\sqrt{A^{*}A}$**: just as $\\lvert z\\rvert = \\sqrt{\\bar z z}$.\n\n$A^{*}A$ is always positive semidefinite Hermitian (see the notes to def.positive-definite-matrix), so it has a unique positive semidefinite square root.\n\nDefine $U := AP^{-1}$ (when $A$ is invertible); it remains only to check that $U$ preserves the inner product — a one-line computation: $U^{*}U = P^{-1}A^{*}AP^{-1} = P^{-1}P^{2}P^{-1} = I$.\n\n⭐ **Geometric reading**: every linear map is \"first stretch along a set of orthogonal directions ($P$), then rotate as a whole ($U$)\". The stretch factors are the singular values. This also holds for **non-square** matrices (with an isometry in place of a unitary).\n\n**Division of labour with the SVD**: the polar decomposition emphasizes the **structure** \"rotation $\\times$ stretch\", while the SVD emphasizes the **coordinate expression** \"$A = U\\Sigma V^{*}$\". Both carry the same information; the SVD gives two extra bases.\n\n⚠️ Uniqueness must be stated carefully: $P$ is always unique, but when $A$ is singular, $U$ may be chosen arbitrarily on the kernel of $A$ — so the sentence \"the polar decomposition is unique\" holds only in the invertible case.\n\n**The proof is on the outgoing edge**: the full proof is in the corollary edge \"spectral theorem $\\Longrightarrow$ polar decomposition\" of this cluster — from the spectral decomposition of $A^{*}A$ construct $\\sqrt{A^{*}A}$ and prove uniqueness; when $U=AP^{-1}$ is invertible it is unitary, and in the singular case one first defines an isometry on $\\operatorname{im}P$ and extends it to a unitary.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#4f47b044",
      },
    },
    'def.transpose': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: flips a matrix across its main diagonal. It looks like a mere notational operation, but it is exactly what the **map induced on the dual space** looks like in the language of matrices.\n\nLet $A = (a_{ij}) \\in M_{m\\times n}(K)$. The **transpose** $A^{T} \\in M_{n\\times m}(K)$ is defined by $(A^{T})_{ij} := a_{ji}$.\n\n**Basic properties**: $(A+B)^{T} = A^{T} + B^{T}$, $(cA)^{T} = cA^{T}$, $(A^{T})^{T} = A$, and most importantly\n\n$$(AB)^{T} = B^{T} A^{T}$$\n\n(the order of multiplication **reverses**.) If $A$ is invertible, then $A^{T}$ is invertible and $(A^{-1})^{T} = (A^{T})^{-1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6c1351e1",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why the order of multiplication reverses**: the transpose is not \"a rearrangement\"; it is **duality**.\n\nA linear map $T : V \\to W$ induces the dual map $T^{*} : W^{*} \\to V^{*}$ (with the arrow reversed), and the matrix of $T^{*}$ is exactly the transpose of the matrix of $T$.\n\nOnce the direction is reversed, the order of composition must reverse as well — this is what $(AB)^{T} = B^{T}A^{T}$ says.\n\n**Compatibility with the pairing**: regarding vectors as columns, $y^{T}Ax$ is a scalar, and equals $(A^{T}y)^{T}x$ — moving $A$ from one side to the other rests entirely on this, and it is the starting point of everything about adjoint operators and symmetric matrices that follows.\n\n⚠️ The transpose depends on the **choice of basis** (it uses the dual basis), whereas the conjugate transpose ($A^{*}$) of the next section equals \"the matrix of the adjoint operator\" only in an orthonormal basis. Confusing the two is the most common accident for beginners.\n\n⚠️ Do not remember $(AB)^{T} = B^{T}A^{T}$ as $A^{T}B^{T}$: the latter can hold only when $A, B$ are both square and happen to commute, and in general it fails.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6cee34c0",
      },
    },
    'def.conjugate-transpose': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: over the complex field the transpose alone is not enough — the inner product is conjugate-linear in the second variable, so moving $A$ to the other side moves the conjugate along with it. This is the conjugate transpose.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{C})$. The **conjugate transpose** (also called the **adjoint matrix**) is\n\n$$A^{*} := \\overline{A}^{T} = \\bigl(\\overline{A^{T}}\\bigr), \\qquad (A^{*})_{ij} := \\overline{a_{ji}} .$$\n\nProperties: $(A+B)^{*} = A^{*} + B^{*}$, $(cA)^{*} = \\overline{c}\\,A^{*}$, $(AB)^{*} = B^{*}A^{*}$, $(A^{*})^{*} = A$.\n\n**Relation to the inner product (this is its defining property)**: for all $x, y$ one has\n\n$$\\langle Ax, y\\rangle = \\langle x, A^{*}y\\rangle .$$\n\nFor a real matrix $A^{*} = A^{T}$, and the two notions coincide.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#60244290",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**It is the matrix of the adjoint operator**: if matrices are taken in an **orthonormal basis**, then for the linear map $T$ the adjoint operator $T^{*}$ (def.operator-adjoint) has as matrix the conjugate transpose of the matrix of $T$. ⚠️ In a non-orthonormal basis these two are no longer equal — that relation is a privilege of orthonormal bases, not a coordinate-free identity.\n\n**Why the conjugate is necessary**: the standard inner product on $\\mathbb{C}^{n}$ is $\\langle x, y\\rangle = \\sum x_i \\overline{y_i}$ (conjugate in the second variable), so when moving $A$ from the left side to the right, $\\overline{A}$ appears naturally.\n\nWith the \"transpose only\" version, $\\langle Ax,y\\rangle = \\langle x, A^{T}y\\rangle$ is false over the complex field.\n\n⚠️ Three notations coexist: $A^{*}$, $A^{\\dagger}$, $A^{H}$. This entry uses $A^{*}$ throughout (the same star as for adjoint operators).\n\n⚠️ The term \"adjoint matrix\" in some books means the **cofactor matrix** (the notation of the def.determinant branch). Here it is used in the inner-product sense; when reading Chinese textbooks, take care to distinguish the two.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b9374237",
      },
    },
    'def.unitary-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: writes \"preserves lengths and angles\" as a matrix condition. In the complex case it is called **unitary**, in the real case **orthogonal** — two faces of the same condition over two fields.\n\nLet $U \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. Call $U$ a **unitary matrix** if\n\n$$U^{*}U = I ,$$\n\n⚠️ For finite order **this single equation suffices**: $U^{*}U = I$ already shows that $U$ is injective, and a square matrix that is injective is invertible, so $U^{*} = U^{-1}$; right-multiplying by $U$ gives $UU^{*} = I$ (the same argument with $UU^{*} = I$). Equivalently $U^{-1} = U^{*}$. A real square matrix $Q \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{R})$ satisfying $Q^{T}Q = I$ is called an **orthogonal matrix**.\n\n**Equivalent characterizations**: the following three are equivalent — (i) $U$ is unitary; (ii) the **columns** of $U$ form an orthonormal basis; (iii) for all $x, y$, $\\langle Ux, Uy\\rangle = \\langle x, y\\rangle$.\n\nThus a unitary matrix preserves the inner product, hence the norm ($\\lVert Ux\\rVert = \\lVert x\\rVert$) and distances; $\\lvert\\det U\\rvert = 1$. All unitary matrices form a group $U(n)$, and the orthogonal ones form $O(n)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#75d6aa95",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The name \"unitary\"**: $U^{*}U = I$ degenerates to orthogonal in the real case (which is what $\\perp$ means); in the complex case the term is unitary (from Latin unus, \"one\"). The terminology does not matter; the condition is the same.\n\n**Why it suffices that the columns form an orthonormal basis**: for $U^{*}U = I$, its $(j,k)$-th entry is exactly \"the inner product of the $j$-th column with the $k$-th column = $\\delta_{jk}$\". So \"unitary\" = \"sends an orthonormal basis to an orthonormal basis\", which is the most convenient criterion.\n\n**Relation to def.isometry**: unitary matrices are exactly the matrices of \"inner-product-preserving linear maps in an orthonormal basis\". ⚠️ Inner-product-preserving operators (isometries) and unitary matrices correspond one-to-one only in an orthonormal basis; after a change of basis the matrix is no longer unitary, but the operator still preserves the inner product.\n\n⭐ **Numerical significance**: unitary transformations do not amplify errors ($\\lVert Ux\\rVert = \\lVert x\\rVert$), so \"unitary similarity\" is the most stable kind of similarity — this is why both Schur triangularization and the SVD choose unitary transformations.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c930cae5",
      },
    },
    'def.hermitian-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: writes \"self-adjoint\" as a matrix condition. In the complex case it is called **Hermitian**, in the real case **symmetric**. Their hallmarks are: **all eigenvalues real**, and **orthogonally diagonalizable**.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. Call $A$ a **Hermitian matrix** (self-adjoint matrix) if\n\n$$A^{*} = A .$$\n\nA real square matrix satisfying $A^{T} = A$ is called a **symmetric matrix**.\n\n**Key properties**: (i) the diagonal entries are all real; (ii) the eigenvalues are **all real**; (iii) eigenvectors belonging to distinct eigenvalues are **mutually orthogonal**; (iv) there exists a unitary matrix $U$ such that $U^{*}AU$ is a real diagonal matrix (thm.spectral-matrix).\n\nA real symmetric matrix is the special case of a Hermitian matrix where $A$ takes real values; in that case (iv) can be written as $Q^{T}AQ$ is diagonal, with $Q$ orthogonal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#72b21c93",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why the eigenvalues are real**: take an eigenvector $x \\ne 0$ with $Ax = \\lambda x$; then $\\lambda\\langle x, x\\rangle = \\langle Ax, x\\rangle = \\langle x, Ax\\rangle = \\overline{\\lambda}\\langle x, x\\rangle$ — the second step uses self-adjointness.\n\nHence $\\lambda = \\overline{\\lambda}$.\n\n**Why eigenvectors of distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal**: if $Ax = \\lambda x$ and $Ay = \\mu y$, then $\\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle Ax,y\\rangle = \\langle x,Ay\\rangle = \\mu\\langle x,y\\rangle$, while $\\lambda \\ne \\mu$ $\\implies$ $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$.\n\nThese two lines are the core computation of the whole chapter; the spectral theorem starts from them.\n\n**Relation to quadratic forms**: symmetric matrices correspond one-to-one to quadratic forms ($q(x) = x^{T}Ax$, def.quadratic-form); Hermitian matrices correspond to Hermitian forms. \"Positive definite\" is exactly \"$q > 0$\" under this correspondence (def.positive-definite-matrix).\n\n⚠️ Do not treat \"Hermitian\" and \"symmetric\" as two unrelated things: real symmetric $\\subset$ Hermitian. But a **complex symmetric** matrix ($A^{T} = A$ while $A^{*} \\ne A$) is neither Hermitian nor has real eigenvalues — over the complex field one must use $A^{*}$, not $A^{T}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#441b9cb8",
      },
    },
    'def.normal-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: collects unitary matrices, Hermitian matrices, and those matrices for which $A^{*}A$ and $AA^{*}$ do not matter in which order, into a single class — and this class is exactly the set of **unitarily diagonalizable** matrices.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. Call $A$ a **normal matrix** if\n\n$$A^{*}A = AA^{*} .$$\n\n**Inclusions** (all strict):\n\n$\\{$ unitary $\\} \\subset \\{$ normal $\\}$, $\\{$ Hermitian $\\} \\subset \\{$ normal $\\}$, $\\{$ real symmetric $\\} \\subset \\{$ normal $\\}$.\n\n**Core result** (thm.spectral-matrix): $A$ normal $\\iff$ there exists a unitary matrix $U$ such that $U^{*}AU$ is diagonal.\n\nThe real case corresponds to **real normal matrices** ($A^{T}A = AA^{T}$): they are orthogonally diagonalizable, but as a **block** diagonal with $1\\times 1$ and $2\\times 2$ blocks, not necessarily purely diagonal — because non-real eigenvalues can only occur in conjugate pairs.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a61772f9",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why normality is the \"right\" class**: in the proof of the spectral theorem, the only things really used are \"eigenvectors are orthogonal + there are enough eigenvectors\". Together these two are exactly equivalent to $A^{*}A = AA^{*}$ — so \"normal\" is not an artificial condition, but the **algebraic translation** of \"unitarily diagonalizable\".\n\n**Why real normal matrices can only be block-diagonalized**: $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}$ is real normal (it is a rotation by $90^\\circ$), but it has no real eigenvector, so it certainly cannot be orthogonally diagonalized. Its two eigenvalues are $\\pm i$ — conjugate pairs, the source of those $2\\times 2$ blocks.\n\n⚠ Relation to def.normal-operator (normal **operator**): that entry speaks about an operator $T^{*}T = TT^{*}$ and is coordinate-free; this entry speaks about the matrix condition in an **orthonormal basis**. The two translate into each other, but do not mix them (after a non-orthonormal change of basis the matrix of the operator is no longer \"normal\").\n\n⭐ It is enough to remember this table: **normal $\\iff$ unitarily diagonalizable**; adding further conditions yields the special classes — adding \"all eigenvalues real\" gives Hermitian, adding \"all eigenvalues on the unit circle\" gives unitary, and adding both gives the self-adjoint unitary with \"eigenvalues only $\\pm 1$\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#49416627",
      },
    },
    'def.positive-definite-matrix': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: writes \"this quadratic form is positive everywhere\" as a matrix condition. In physics it is an \"energy form\"; in geometry it is another way of writing an inner product.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ be a Hermitian matrix. Call $A$ **positive definite** if for every $x \\ne 0$\n\n$$x^{*}Ax > 0 ;$$\n\nReplacing $>$ by $\\ge$ gives **positive semidefinite**. In the real symmetric case replace $x^{*}$ by $x^{T}$.\n\n**Equivalent characterizations** (the first two are the most used):\n\n1. all eigenvalues $> 0$ (semidefinite: $\\ge 0$);\n2. all **leading principal minors** $> 0$ (Sylvester's criterion, thm.sylvester-criterion);\n3. there exists an invertible $P$ with $A = P^{*}P$ (semidefinite: $A = P^{*}P$ need not be invertible);\n4. $x \\mapsto \\sqrt{x^{*}Ax}$ is a norm (a seminorm in the semidefinite case).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#db428da7",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**It is \"the matrix of an inner product\"**: the matrix of any inner product in a basis is a positive definite Hermitian matrix; conversely every positive definite matrix defines an inner product. So \"positive definite\" = \"a legitimate inner-product matrix\", a correspondence more useful than any criterion.\n\n**Why leading principal minors suffice**: Sylvester's criterion — in the LDL decomposition the $k$-th pivot equals the ratio of $k$-order leading principal minors, and all pivots are positive exactly when the matrix is positive definite. ⚠️ Only the **leading** principal minors are checked, not all principal minors (all principal minors $>0$ is a stronger condition, equivalent only in special situations).\n\n⭐ **Positive definite $\\implies$ admits a square root**: there is a unique positive semidefinite $\\sqrt{A}$ with $(\\sqrt A)^{2} = A$ (take square roots of the eigenvalues in an orthonormal basis). This is precisely where $A = UP$ and the factor $P$ in the polar decomposition come from (thm.polar-decomposition).\n\n⚠️ The sentence \"$A^{T}A$ is always positive semidefinite\" must be written $A^{*}A$ over the complex field; and it is positive definite exactly when the columns of $A$ are linearly independent (otherwise there is a nonzero kernel vector). This point recurs in regression and least squares.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (of the four items, the first two are \"spectral/principal minor\" criteria and the last two are \"decomposition/norm\" criteria, with different dependencies):\n\n- (definition) $\\iff$ (1): **uses the spectral theorem** (Hermitian matrices are orthogonally diagonalizable, thm.spectral-matrix). After diagonalization $x^{*}Ax = \\sum \\lambda_{i}\\lvert y_{i}\\rvert^{2}$, so \"always positive\" $\\iff$ \"every $\\lambda_{i} > 0$\". **This holds only for Hermitian (real symmetric) matrices**: a nonsymmetric matrix can have positive eigenvalues yet fail to be positive definite (the $x^{T}Ax$ contains a mixed antisymmetric part).\n- (1) $\\implies$ (2): **holds only for** leading principal minors**, not for \"all principal minors\"**. Sylvester's criterion says \"all $n$ leading principal minors are positive\"; \"all principal minors nonnegative\" is the criterion for positive semidefiniteness (the two are not the same; in the semidefinite case a leading principal minor may vanish). This item **does not use the spectral theorem**, only completing the square — so it is purely algebraic and holds over any ordered field.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (definition): **needs real (or Hermitian) symmetry**. The method is block Gaussian elimination (LDL decomposition): nonzero leading principal minors guarantee that the elimination can proceed, and the resulting diagonal entries are exactly the ratios of principal minors.\n- (definition) $\\iff$ (3): **this step uses the spectral theorem or Cholesky**. When $A = P^{*}P$, $x^{*}Ax = \\lVert Px\\rVert^{2}$; invertibility of $P$ gives strict positivity; conversely take $P = \\Lambda^{1/2}U^{*}$. **Note that here one must allow the conjugate transpose for complex/real matrices**; writing $A = P^{T}P$ is correct only in the real symmetric case.\n- (3) $\\iff$ (4): **verified directly by the triangle inequality** ($\\sqrt{x^{*}Ax} = \\lVert Px\\rVert$ is a norm). The converse uses the square root defining $x^{*}Ax$ — one must first show that it is a quadratic form.\n\n⚠️ **The three most error-prone points**: (a) forgetting the hypothesis \"Hermitian/real symmetric\" (in the complex case use $x^{*}$; discussing complex matrices with $x^{T}Ax > 0$ is meaningless, since it may not be real); (b) replacing \"all leading principal minors positive\" by \"all principal minors positive\" — the latter is a **stronger** condition (also true for positive definite, but in the semidefinite case only the latter can be used); (c) in the semidefinite case $P$ is not invertible, eigenvalues may be $0$, and the square root is only a seminorm — all three must be relaxed simultaneously.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ad9cb484",
      },
    },
    'def.rayleigh-quotient': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: turns \"eigenvalue\" into an **extremal problem**. In this way eigenvalues can be \"optimized\" instead of only \"solved for\".\n\nLet $A$ be a Hermitian matrix of order $n$. For $x \\ne 0$ define the **Rayleigh quotient**\n\n$$R_{A}(x) := \\frac{x^{*}Ax}{x^{*}x} .$$\n\n(for a unit vector $x$ this is just $x^{*}Ax$, so it is really \"the average stretch factor of $A$ in the direction $x$\".)\n\n**Core properties**:\n\n1. $R_{A}(x)$ is always **real** and lies in $[\\lambda_{\\min}, \\lambda_{\\max}]$;\n2. $\\lambda_{\\min} = \\min_{x\\ne 0} R_A(x)$, $\\lambda_{\\max} = \\max_{x\\ne 0} R_A(x)$, with the extrema attained at the corresponding eigenvectors;\n3. more generally, all critical points are exactly the eigenvectors, and the critical values are exactly the eigenvalues.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#8157ac11",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is real**: $\\overline{x^{*}Ax} = x^{*}A^{*}x = x^{*}Ax$ (using Hermitian), and the denominator is real.\n\n⭐ **It gives the theoretical basis for steepest descent/power iteration**: moving along the gradient of $R_A$ is approximating the largest eigenvector. Numerically $x_{k+1} \\propto Ax_k$ converges to the dominant eigenvector at a rate governed by $\\lvert\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1\\rvert$ — this is the convergence rate of the power method.\n\n**Its generalization** is thm.courant-fischer: restricting $R_A$ to a $k$-dimensional subspace and taking the extremum gives the $k$-th eigenvalue. So the Rayleigh quotient is the gateway to the \"variational characterization of the spectrum\", not an isolated notation.\n\n⚠️ $A$ must be Hermitian (or real symmetric) for $R_A$ to be real and to have an extremal meaning; for a general matrix $x^{*}Ax$ is complex, the Rayleigh quotient degenerates into a complex-valued function, and the standard theory does not apply.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#acad0ce3",
      },
    },
    'def.orthogonally-similar': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: fills the cell between \"similarity\" and \"congruence\" — when a change of basis both preserves the structure (similarity) and preserves lengths (orthogonal/unitary), this is it. The spectral theorem says exactly that \"a symmetric matrix is orthogonally similar to a diagonal matrix\".\n\nLet $A, B \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{R})$. Call $A$ and $B$ **orthogonally similar** if there exists an **orthogonal** matrix $Q$ such that\n\n$$B = Q^{T} A\\, Q = Q^{-1} A\\, Q .$$\n\nIn the complex case replace $Q$ by a **unitary** matrix $U$, obtaining **unitary similarity** $B = U^{*}AU = U^{-1}AU$.\n\n**Why this cell matters**: orthogonal/unitary matrices satisfy $P^{-1} = P^{T}$ (or $P^{*}$) at the same time, so\n\northogonal similarity = similarity + congruence (the differences among the three are explained in the notes of def.orthogonally-similar)\n\nIt preserves both eigenvalues (a property of similarity) and the inner-product structure (a property of congruence) — so it is a **stable** similarity, with condition number $1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#eda66f87",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Which matrices are orthogonally similar to a diagonal matrix**: real symmetric matrices (thm.spectral-matrix); more generally, real normal matrices are orthogonally similar to a **block diagonal** matrix ($1\\times1$ and $2\\times2$ blocks, because non-real eigenvalues occur in pairs).\n\nBut **any** matrix is unitarily similar to an upper triangular one (thm.schur-triangularization) — this is the complete answer to \"how far can orthogonal similarity simplify a matrix\".\n\n⚠️ Keep the difference between the three notions firmly in mind: **similarity** $P^{-1}AP$ (any invertible $P$, preserves eigenvalues); **congruence** $P^{T}AP$ (any invertible $P$, preserves inertia); **orthogonal similarity** $Q^{T}AQ$ with $Q^{-1} = Q^{T}$ (preserves both). Only in the third is \"$P^{-1}$ and $P^{T}$ the same thing\".\n\n⚠️ The entry def.similarity in this graph treats the most general similarity. Do not confuse \"$A$ is similar to a diagonal matrix\" with \"$A$ is orthogonally similar to a diagonal matrix\" — the former requires only **enough** eigenvectors, the latter requires them to be **mutually perpendicular** (this is normality, def.normal-matrix).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9223d5c6",
      },
    },
    'def.eigenspace': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: collects all eigenvectors belonging to the same eigenvalue into a subspace. With it, \"diagonalizable\" acquires a clean criterion (the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces equals the whole space).\n\nLet $T : V \\to V$ be linear and let $\\lambda$ be an eigenvalue of $T$ (def.eigenvalue). The **eigenspace** of $\\lambda$ is\n\n$$V_{\\lambda} := \\ker(T - \\lambda I) = \\{\\, v \\in V : T(v) = \\lambda v \\,\\} .$$\n\nIt is a $T$-invariant subspace; its dimension $\\dim V_{\\lambda}$ is called the **geometric multiplicity** of $\\lambda$, while the multiplicity of $\\lambda$ in the characteristic polynomial is called the **algebraic multiplicity**.\n\n**One always has**: $1 \\le$ geometric multiplicity $\\le$ algebraic multiplicity.\n\n**Diagonalizability criterion**: $T$ is diagonalizable $\\iff$ $\\sum_{\\lambda} \\dim V_{\\lambda} = \\dim V$ $\\iff$ the two multiplicities of every eigenvalue are equal.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#3a712f41",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why it is a \"subspace\"**: $\\ker(T - \\lambda I)$ is a kernel, hence certainly a subspace. The point is that it is **not an arbitrary subspace** — it is determined by a single eigenvalue, and the sum of the $V_{\\lambda}$ for distinct eigenvalues is **direct** (eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent).\n\n**Why the two multiplicities differ**: $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}$ has the single eigenvalue $1$, with algebraic multiplicity $2$, but $\\ker(T - I)$ is only one-dimensional (geometric multiplicity $1$) — the missing dimension is the source of Jordan blocks (thm.jordan-canonical-form).\n\n⭐ **The generalized eigenspace** is its generalization: iterate $\\ker(T - \\lambda I)^{k}$ until it stabilizes, and the dimension is exactly the algebraic multiplicity. The blocks of the Jordan form are organized accordingly (see thm.primary-decomposition).\n\n⚠️ Notational conventions differ: some books write $E_{\\lambda}$ (eigenspace), others $V(\\lambda)$. This graph uses $V_{\\lambda}$, aligning with the notation of def.eigenvalue.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#cfb211ef",
      },
    },
    'def.generalized-eigenvector': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: when eigenvectors are not enough (geometric multiplicity < algebraic multiplicity), one steps back: instead of requiring $T(v) = \\lambda v$, one only requires that repeated application be killed by $\\lambda$ after finitely many steps. This makes up the dimension.\n\nLet $T : V \\to V$ be linear and $\\lambda$ an eigenvalue. A vector $v \\ne 0$ is called a **generalized eigenvector** of $\\lambda$ if\n\n$\\Bigl(T - \\lambda I\\Bigr)^{k}(v) = 0$ (for some $k \\ge 1$)\n\nThe least such $k$ is called the **order** of $v$. All generalized eigenvectors together with $0$ form the **generalized eigenspace**\n\n$$G_{\\lambda} := \\bigcup_{k \\ge 1}\\ker(T - \\lambda I)^{k} = \\ker(T - \\lambda I)^{m} ,$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the algebraic multiplicity of $\\lambda$ (the sequence stabilizes at that power).\n\n**Core facts**: $\\dim G_{\\lambda} = $ the algebraic multiplicity, and $V = \\bigoplus_{\\lambda} G_{\\lambda}$ (the root-space decomposition of thm.primary-decomposition).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#87507038",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why this makes up the dimension**: $T - \\lambda I$ is zero on $V_{\\lambda}$, but on $G_{\\lambda}$ it is **nilpotent** (def.nilpotent).\n\nNilpotent operators have a very simple structure (a chain), so restricting $T$ to $G_{\\lambda}$ gives \"$\\lambda I$ + a nilpotent operator\" — this is precisely a Jordan block.\n\n**Meaning of the order**: $k = 1$ is an ordinary eigenvector; $k = 2$ means $T(v) = \\lambda v + u$ ($u$ is an eigenvector) — this is the second term of a Jordan chain. So \"generalized eigenvector\" = \"a point on a Jordan chain\".\n\n⚠️ The generalized eigenspace is **not** the eigenspace of $T$, but it is $T$-invariant; and $T$ is not diagonalizable on it (unless it equals $V_{\\lambda}$).\n\n⚠️ Only **eigenvalues** have generalized eigenvectors ($\\lambda$ must satisfy $\\det(T - \\lambda I) = 0$), otherwise $T - \\lambda I$ is invertible and no power ever vanishes.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9ac45e1b",
      },
    },
    'thm.diagonalizable': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\dim V = n < \\infty$, let $T : V \\to V$ be linear, suppose the **characteristic polynomial of $T$ splits completely over $K$**, and let $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_m$ be its distinct eigenvalues in $K$. Then the following are equivalent:\n\n**(1)** $T$ is **diagonalizable**: there is a basis in which the matrix of $T$ is diagonal;\n\n**(2)** $V = E_{\\lambda_1} \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus E_{\\lambda_m}$, that is, the eigenvectors span all of $V$;\n\n**(3)** $\\sum_i \\dim E_{\\lambda_i} = n$ (the sum of the geometric multiplicities reaches $n$);\n\n**(4)** the geometric multiplicity of every eigenvalue equals its algebraic multiplicity;\n\n**(5)** there exist **distinct** $\\lambda_i$ such that $T$ satisfies $\\prod_i (T - \\lambda_i I) = 0$ (that is, $T$ is annihilated by a polynomial without repeated roots).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#643bb495",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The most used criteria are **(3)/(4)**: the existence of $n$ linearly independent eigenvectors $\\iff$ diagonalizable.\n\nNote that \"the number of eigenvalues is $= n$\" is not enough (repeated roots must be counted with multiplicity).\n\n⭐ **(5)** is the deepest formulation: it translates \"diagonalizable\" into \"the minimal polynomial has no repeated roots\" (see \"the minimal polynomial divides the characteristic polynomial\") — this is used in the discussion of the spectral theorem and of Jordan form.\n\n⚠ The criterion depends on $K$: a rotation over $\\mathbb{R}$ has no real eigenvalue, so of course it is not diagonalizable;\n\nbut over $\\mathbb{C}$ it has eigenvalues $\\pm i$ and is diagonalizable.\n\nSo \"diagonalizable\" is always relative to $K$.\n\n**(1)–(4): the first three are only restatements, only the fourth uses the characteristic polynomial; (5) is a separate entry point.**\n\n- (1) $\\iff$ (2): **almost only a rewriting of the definition** (diagonalizable = there is a basis consisting of eigenvectors).\n- (2) $\\iff$ (3): **pure linear algebra** ($\\sum \\dim E_{\\lambda_i} = n$, and the sum is direct $\\iff$ spanning $V$). No characteristic polynomial, no algebraic closure needed.\n- (3) $\\iff$ (4): **this step uses \"geometric multiplicity $\\le$ algebraic multiplicity\"**, and that needs the **characteristic polynomial** (computing the dimension of the nilpotent part of $T - \\lambda I$). $\\sum$ geometric multiplicity $= n$ is equivalent to \"equality for every eigenvalue\", because the total sum $\\le \\sum$ algebraic multiplicity $= n$.\n\n⚠️ **Necessary hypotheses**: finite dimension + **the characteristic polynomial splits over $K$** (all eigenvalues lie in $K$). Without the latter, $T$ may have no eigenvalue at all over $K$ (for example, over $\\mathbb{R}$, the rotation by $90^{\\circ}$): then $m=0$, and **(2)(3) are false statements** — the right-hand side is the zero subspace $0$, while $V=\\mathbb{R}^2$ (an empty sum $0\\ne2$); only **(4) holds vacuously** (\"every eigenvalue\" ranges over the empty family). So the chain of equivalences breaks already at (2)(3); do not think that only (4) fails. The correct statement is \"diagonalizable over the algebraic closure $\\iff$ the minimal polynomial has no repeated roots\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a6567dea",
      },
    },
    'thm.minimal-poly-divisor': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\dim V = n < \\infty$ and $T : V \\to V$. Then\n\n- $\\mu_T \\mid \\chi_T$ (the minimal polynomial divides the characteristic polynomial); in particular $\\deg \\mu_T \\le n$;\n- $\\mu_T$ and $\\chi_T$ have **exactly the same roots** in the algebraic closure of $K$ (that is, the same set of eigenvalues);\n- hence $T$ is diagonalizable (viewed over the algebraic closure) $\\iff$ $\\mu_T$ has no repeated roots.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ffc50c43",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The first of the three is a direct consequence of Cayley–Hamilton plus the ideal property: $\\chi_T \\in I_T = (\\mu_T)$.\n\n⭐ The proof of the second (same roots) is worth remembering: if $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue with eigenvector $v$, then $\\mu_T(T)v = \\mu_T(\\lambda) v$, while $\\mu_T(T) = 0$ and $v \\ne 0$, hence $\\mu_T(\\lambda) = 0$;\n\nthe converse follows from $\\mu_T \\mid \\chi_T$.\n\n⚠ The phrase \"viewed over the algebraic closure\" in the third assertion cannot be omitted: whether $\\mu_T$ has repeated roots must be decided in an extension field (the rotation over $\\mathbb{R}$: $\\mu_T = x^{2} + 1$ has no repeated roots, is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb{C}$, but not over $\\mathbb{R}$).\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (finite dimension guarantees that the polynomial exists; diagonalization also needs splitting and no repeated roots):\n- $\\mu_T\\mid\\chi_T$: uses the finite-dimensional Cayley–Hamilton theorem and the fact that the annihilating ideal is generated by $\\mu_T$; it is not an expansion of the definition of divisibility. The infinite-dimensional shift operator may have no nonzero annihilating polynomial.\n- eigenvalue $\\lambda$ $\\implies$ $\\mu_T(\\lambda)=0$: apply $\\mu_T(T)$ to a nonzero eigenvector; the converse follows from divisibility, giving $\\chi_T(\\lambda)=0$, and then the finite-dimensional determinant criterion.\n- diagonalizable $\\implies$ the minimal polynomial splits and has no repeated roots: compute coordinatewise in an eigenbasis and use the annihilating ideal; it is not a literal rewriting of the definition of diagonalizability.\n- minimal polynomial splits with no repeated roots $\\implies$ diagonalizable: needs the Bezout identity for coprime factors, or polynomial interpolation to construct the spectral projections; no spectral theorem and no characteristic zero.\n- If one only says \"no repeated roots\", diagonalization must be discussed over the algebraic closure; the real plane rotation with $x^2+1$ has no repeated roots yet is not diagonalizable over the reals. Nor can \"no repeated roots\" be dropped: the minimal polynomial of a nonzero 2×2 nilpotent Jordan block is $x^2$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#e26820e0",
      },
    },
    'def.nilpotent': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: characterizes those operators that \"vanish after being applied repeatedly\". It is the protagonist of the structure theorem for nilpotent parts (everything in the Jordan form off the diagonal).\n\nLet $T : V \\to V$ be linear ($\\dim V = n < \\infty$). Call $T$ **nilpotent** if\n\n$T^{k} = 0$ (for some $k \\ge 1$)\n\nMatrix case: for $A^{k} = 0$, one calls $A$ a **nilpotent matrix**.\n\n**Equivalent conditions (often used as tests)**:\n\n1. all eigenvalues of $T$ are $0$;\n2. the characteristic polynomial is $x^{n}$;\n3. $T^{n} = 0$ ($n = \\dim V$ suffices — Cayley–Hamilton).\n\n**Structure**: a nilpotent operator can always be brought to a direct sum of Jordan blocks $J_{k}(0)$; the order of the largest block equals the **nilpotency index** of $T$ (the least exponent at which $T^{k} = 0$ first holds, namely $k$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ad212464",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The standard example**: the shift operator $T(x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{n}) = (0, x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{n-1})$, with $T^{n} = 0$ while $T^{n-1} \\ne 0$. Its matrix is the \"shift up by one\" matrix, namely a single $J_{n}(0)$.\n\n⭐ **Why nilpotent operators matter so much**: every operator splits as \"diagonalizable part + nilpotent part\" $T = S + N$ ($S$ diagonalizable, $N$ nilpotent, $SN = NS$; this is the **Jordan–Chevalley decomposition**). So \"understanding nilpotent\" = \"understanding all the difficulty of non-diagonalizability\".\n\n⚠️ Nilpotent must **not** be confused with \"rank less than $n$\": $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{pmatrix}$ is nilpotent, but $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}$ has rank $1$ and is not nilpotent (its eigenvalues are $1$ and $0$).\n\nThe criterion is **all eigenvalues zero**, not rank.\n\n⚠️ A nilpotent matrix has trace and determinant both $0$, but **the converse fails** ($\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{pmatrix}$ has both equal to $0$ yet is not nilpotent).\n\n**Of the four criteria, \"$T^{n} = 0$\" looks most like a definition but is actually the most expensive**:\n\n- definition $T^{k} = 0$ (some $k$) $\\iff$ **all eigenvalues are $0$**: $(\\implies)$ is obvious ($0$ is the only possible eigenvalue); $(\\Leftrightarrow)$ needs the **characteristic polynomial to split** (over $\\mathbb{C}$ or an algebraic closure), otherwise the word \"eigenvalue\" is meaningless.\n- **characteristic polynomial $= x^{n}$** $\\iff$ all eigenvalues zero + adding algebraic multiplicities: this step uses **Vieta's formulas** and **requires finite dimension**.\n- **$T^{n} = 0$** ($n = \\dim V$): **this step uses Cayley–Hamilton** (thm.cayley-hamilton). ⚠️ It gives \"$k$ can be taken to be $n$\", whereas the definition only requires \"some $k$\" — this quantifier difference is exactly what Cayley–Hamilton provides.\n- **nilpotent $\\iff$ there is a basis in which the matrix is strictly upper triangular (with $0$ on the diagonal)**: this step uses the Jordan form or the splitting of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n⚠️ **Nilpotent $\\ne$ rank-deficient**: $\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ has rank $1 < 2$ but is not nilpotent (eigenvalue $1$). The criterion is always **all eigenvalues zero**.\n⚠️ **In infinite dimension \"$T^{n} = 0$\" fails**: the shift operator on $\\ell^{2}$ has no nonzero power equal to zero (though it can be \"locally nilpotent\") — so that criterion must explicitly say \"finite-dimensional\".",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#1c220bff",
      },
    },
    'cor.fatou': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_n\\} \\subseteq L^+$, $f \\in L^+$, and $f_n \\to f$ a.e. Then\n\n$$\\int f \\le \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty} \\int f_n$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#3aefb3eb",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "When convergence holds a.e., $\\liminf f_n = f$, and Fatou's lemma applies directly.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#5cfe8765",
      },
    },
    'thm.termwise-L1': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{f_j\\} \\subseteq L^1$ satisfy\n\n$$\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty} \\int |f_j| d\\mu < \\infty$$\n\nThen $\\sum_j f_j$ converges almost everywhere to a function in $L^1$, and the series may be integrated term by term:\n\n$$\\int \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty} f_j = \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty} \\int f_j$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#589a047e",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The difference from **termwise integration for nonnegative functions**: there no convergence hypothesis is needed (nonnegativity rules out cancellation), whereas here the condition is **sufficient**, not necessary: on a one-point probability space with $f_{j} = (-1)^{j+1}/j$ one has $\\sum \\int |f_{j}| = \\infty$, yet $\\sum \\int f_{j}$ converges and termwise integration still holds — integrability buys the convenience of allowing both signs.\n\nThis is the key tool in the proof of the Fubini–Tonelli theorem.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ad16d406",
      },
    },
    'prop.simple-integral-props': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\varphi, \\psi$ be simple functions.\n\n- **(a)** $c > 0 \\implies \\int c\\varphi = c\\int \\varphi$\n- **(b)** $\\int (\\varphi + \\psi) = \\int \\varphi + \\int \\psi$\n- **(c)** $\\varphi \\le \\psi \\implies \\int \\varphi \\le \\int \\psi$\n- **(d)** $A \\mapsto \\int_A \\varphi d\\mu$ is a **measure** on $\\mathcal{M}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#29c8bbc8",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "(d) is the most useful item of the group: it turns “integration of simple functions” into “a measure”, so countable additivity and monotone continuity (in $A$) come for free.\n\n⚠ The proof of the MCT below **relies on (d)**: the step asserting $\\lim_n \\int_{E_n} \\varphi = \\int \\varphi$ uses exactly “$A \\mapsto \\int _A \\varphi$ is a measure” together with continuity from below for measures ($E_n \\uparrow  X$).\n\nIn (a) one writes $c > 0$ rather than $c \\ge 0$: because of the convention $0\\cdot \\infty = 0$, the left-hand side also holds automatically when $c = 0$, and writing $c > 0$ merely avoids a case distinction.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#857a1609",
      },
    },
    'def.lplus': {
      statement: {
        en: "Fix a measure space $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$. Denote by $L^+$ the set of all **measurable** functions with values in the **extended** nonnegative reals:\n\n$L^+ := \\{ f : X \\to [0, +\\infty] : f$ **measurable** $\\}$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#3d5f8e98",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ Values are allowed to be $\\infty$, deliberately: integration is first defined for functions with values in $[0, +\\infty ]$, and the convergence theorems (supremum / limits) are closed within all of $L^{+}$, so one need not keep asking whether an integral is finite.\n\n“Integrable” (finite integral) is a condition added separately later, see the definition of $L^{1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d4eb60a5",
      },
    },
    'def.integral-over-set': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{A}, \\mu)$ be a measure space, $E \\in \\mathcal{A}$, and $f$ measurable. If $f \\ge 0$, define\n\n$\\int_E f\\,d\\mu := \\int f\\,\\mathbf{1}_E\\,d\\mu \\in [0, \\infty].$\n\nIf $f$ is integrable ($\\int |f| < \\infty$), define $\\int_E f := \\int f\\mathbf{1}_E$ in the same way, splitting a complex-valued function into real and imaginary parts. When $E = X$ this reduces to the integral over the whole space; when $\\mu(E) = 0$, $\\int_E f = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6ebf9e83",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ $E$ must be measurable, otherwise the indicator $\\mathbf{1}_E$ is not measurable. ⭐ This notation is completely synonymous with “integrating $f$ times the indicator”; the nonnegative/complex integral comes first, and integration over a measurable set is its restriction.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#75153389",
      },
    },
    'def.integral-complex': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : X \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ be measurable, and write the positive and negative parts\n\n$$f^{+} = \\max\\{f, 0\\}, \\qquad f^{-} = \\max\\{-f, 0\\}$$\n\n(both belong to $L^{+}$, and $f = f^+ - f^-$, $|f| = f^+ + f^-$). If **at least one** of $\\int f^+$ and $\\int f^-$ is finite, define\n\n$$\\int f := \\int f^+ - \\int f^-$$\n\nFor complex-valued functions, split into real and imaginary parts: $\\int f := \\int \\operatorname{Re} f + i \\int \\operatorname{Im} f$ (both integrals being required to make sense).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#046c20fe",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ “At least one finite” avoids $\\infty - \\infty$: that is an indeterminate form and must be excluded.\n\nIf both are finite, $f$ is called **integrable** (see the next item).\n\nFor complex-valued functions, $\\operatorname{Re} f$ and $\\operatorname{Im} f$ integrable $\\iff f$ integrable, because $|\\operatorname{Re} f|, |\\operatorname{Im} f| \\le |f| \\le |\\operatorname{Re} f| + |\\operatorname{Im} f|$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#623d30af",
      },
    },
    'lem.young-inequality': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a \\ge 0$, $b \\ge 0$, $0 < \\lambda < 1$. Then\n\n$$a^{\\lambda} b^{1-\\lambda} \\le \\lambda a + (1-\\lambda) b$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7827ea78",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "This is the concavity of $\\log$ (equivalently, the convexity of the exponential function):\n\n$$\\log\\!\\left( \\lambda a + (1-\\lambda) b \\right) \\ge \\lambda \\log a + (1-\\lambda) \\log b$$\n\nExponentiating both sides gives the claim.\n\nThis yields the standard special case: taking $\\lambda = 1/p$, $a = |f|^p / \\|f\\|_p^p$, $b = |g|^q / \\|g\\|_q^q$ gives exactly the step used in the proof of **Hölder's inequality**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#eb095184",
      },
    },
    'def.duality-map': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $p, q$ be conjugate, $g \\in L^q$. Define the linear functional on $L^p$\n\n$$\\varphi_g(f) := \\int f g \\, d\\mu$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2b2830be",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By **Hölder's inequality** this functional is bounded, and\n\n$$\\|\\varphi_g\\| \\le \\|g\\|_q$$\n\n(here $\\|\\varphi_g\\|$ is the operator norm in $(L^p)^*$, namely $\\sup\\{|\\varphi_g(f)| : \\|f\\|_p = 1\\}$.)\n\n⭐ Thus we obtain a map\n\n$$L^q \\longrightarrow (L^p)^*, \\qquad g \\mapsto \\varphi_g$$\n\nThe next two propositions prove that it is an **isometric isomorphism** (when $1 < p < \\infty$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b8ea8fa1",
      },
    },
    'thm.lp-banach': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $1 \\le p < \\infty$. Then $L^p(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu)$ is **complete** with respect to the norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_p$, that is, it is a **Banach space**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#51a15b67",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The criterion used in the proof is: **a normed linear space is complete $\\iff$ every absolutely convergent infinite series converges**.\n\nLet $\\{f_k\\} \\subseteq L^p$ and $\\sum_{k} \\|f_k\\|_p = B < \\infty$. It suffices to prove that $\\sum_k f_k$ converges in the $L^p$ norm.\n\nThe trick is to treat “pointwise convergence” and “norm convergence” separately: set $G_n = \\sum_{k \\le n} |f_k|$, $G = \\sum_k |f_k|$; then by the MCT\n\n$$\\|G_n\\|_p \\le \\sum_{k \\le n} \\|f_k\\|_p \\le B \\quad \\implies \\quad \\int G^p = \\lim_n \\int G_n^p \\le B^p$$\n\nHence $G \\in L^p$, in particular $G < \\infty$ a.e., so $\\sum_k f_k$ **converges absolutely a.e.** Denote the limit by $F$.\n\nA further application of the DCT gives norm convergence: $\\left| F - \\sum_{k \\le n} f_k \\right|^p \\le (2G)^p \\in L^1$, hence\n\n$$\\left\\| F - \\sum_{k \\le n} f_k \\right\\|_p^p = \\int \\left| F - \\sum_{k \\le n} f_k \\right|^p \\to 0$$\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b149069b",
      },
    },
    'def.conjugate-exponents': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $1 \\le p \\le \\infty$. One calls $q$ the **conjugate exponent** of $p$ iff\n\n$$\\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1$$\n\n(with the convention $1/\\infty = 0$, so that $p = 1$ corresponds to $q = \\infty$ and $p = \\infty$ to $q = 1$.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#fdd32419",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "By symmetry $\\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p} = 1$, so conjugacy is mutual: $p$ and $q$ are conjugate to each other.\n\nFor $p = 2$ one has $q = 2$: **$L^2$ is self-conjugate** — this is the starting point of Hilbert space theory.\n\n⭐ The meaning of conjugate exponents: Hölder's inequality says that between $L^p$ and $L^q$ there is a “pairing” $\\int fg$; and the duality theorem below says this pairing is **exactly the whole dual space** of $L^p$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#acd47262",
      },
    },
    'lem.ac-subset-bv': {
      statement: {
        en: "$$AC([a, b]) \\subseteq BV([a, b])$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#92cd92cb",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Proof: by absolute continuity choose $\\delta$ such that “total length $< \\delta \\implies$ total variation $< 1$”. Split [a,b] into finitely many pieces each of length $< \\delta$; then the variation of each piece is $\\le 1$, and summing gives $\\le$ the number of pieces — finite. ∎\n\n⚠ The converse fails: **the Cantor function is of bounded variation on [0,1] but not absolutely continuous**. So the inclusion is strict.\n\n⭐ This is the preparation for the “fundamental theorem of calculus TFAE” below: it guarantees $(a) \\implies BV$, hence one may speak of $F'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9f4117a0",
      },
    },
    'def.nbv': {
      statement: {
        en: "A function that is right continuous, vanishes at $-\\infty$, and lies in $BV$ is called a **normalized bounded variation function**:\n\n$NBV := \\{ F \\in BV : F$ **right continuous**, and $F(-\\infty) = 0 \\}$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#64b8a30b",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Why these two conditions: on $\\mathbb{R}$, the next theorem establishes a one-to-one correspondence with **complex Borel measures $\\leftrightarrow NBV$**, and the correspondence is\n\n$$F(x) = \\mu((-\\infty, x])$$\n\n(the function on the right is automatically right continuous and vanishes at $-\\infty$). Restricting the left side to NBV makes the correspondence a **bijection** rather than a surjection.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ae5a39e8",
      },
    },
    'def.ac-function': {
      statement: {
        en: "A function $F : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is **absolutely continuous** iff: for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $\\delta > 0$ such that for **any finite family of pairwise disjoint intervals** $(a_j, b_j) \\subseteq [a, b]$,\n\n$\\sum (b_j - a_j) < \\delta \\implies \\sum |F(b_j) - F(a_j)| < \\varepsilon$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#1271eda6",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠ Note the difference from **uniform continuity**: uniform continuity controls the oscillation on a **single** small interval, whereas absolute continuity requires that **any number of small intervals together** still be controlled — precisely to rule out the Cantor-function phenomenon of “climbing on a null set”.\n\nNotation: $AC([a, b])$ denotes the set of all absolutely continuous functions on $[a,b]$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#bb89455c",
      },
    },
    'prop.bv-regularity': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F \\in BV$. Then\n\n**(c)** for every $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, both $F(x+)$ and $F(x-)$ exist; $F(\\pm \\infty)$ also exists;\n\n**(d)** $F$ has at most countably many discontinuities;\n\n**(e)** set $G(x) = F(x+)$; then $F'$ and $G'$ exist and are equal **almost everywhere**. ⚠️ One cannot say “everywhere”: $F \\in BV$ only guarantees that $F^{\\prime}$ exists a.e. (which is what (d) says).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9fd273a6",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Everything is reduced to increasing functions via “$F = G - H$”: one-sided limits of an increasing function clearly exist; countably many discontinuities is the monotone-function theorem; a.e. differentiability is the same theorem (the derivative of a difference $=$ the difference of the derivatives).\n\n⭐ So although BV functions may be very rough, they are **structurally very tame**: only countably many jumps, and differentiability almost everywhere elsewhere.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#e1da8736",
      },
    },
    'thm.ftc-riemann-part1': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be integrable on $[a, b]$, and define\n\n$$F(x) := \\int_a^x f, \\qquad x \\in [a, b]$$\n\nThen:\n\n**(a)** $F$ is continuous on $[a, b]$; in fact, if $|f| \\le M$ then $|F(y) - F(x)| \\le M|y - x|$ (that is, $F$ is Lipschitz).\n\n**(b)** if $f$ is continuous at $x_0 \\in (a, b)$, then $F$ is differentiable at $x_0$, and\n\n$$F'(x_0) = f(x_0)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d7ce3f1c",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem says: **integrate, then differentiate, and you return to the original function** (at the points where $f$ is continuous).\n\nThus “finding an antiderivative” acquires a **concrete construction**, rather than relying on reciting the derivative table backwards.\n\n⚠ (b) only requires $f$ to be continuous at **that point**: where $f$ has a jump, $F$ has a corner ($F$ is not differentiable there), but $F$ is still continuous everywhere.\n\n⭐ The Lipschitz estimate in (a) is exactly “$\\int_a^b f$ depends continuously on the upper limit”.\n\nOn the Lebesgue side (a) still holds, while (b) must be replaced by “differentiable a.e.” (the Lebesgue differentiation theorem).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a7017796",
      },
    },
    'prop.integrable-examples': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be bounded on $[a, b]$.\n\n- $f$ **continuous** $\\implies$ $f$ integrable;\n- $f$ **monotone** $\\implies$ $f$ integrable;\n- $f$ bounded with only **finitely many** discontinuities $\\implies$ $f$ integrable.\n\nThe converses fail. The **Dirichlet function** ($x$ rational gives $1$, irrational gives $0$) is bounded but not integrable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d6750335",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ All three are direct applications of “the oscillation sum can be made arbitrarily small”.\n\n⭐ Why the Dirichlet function is not integrable: every subinterval of any partition contains both **rationals** and **irrationals** (density), so $M_i = 1$, $m_i = 0$, hence $U - L = \\sum \\Delta x_i = b - a$ holds for every partition, and the oscillation sum never tends to zero.\n\n⚠ But it is integrable in the Lebesgue sense, with integral $0$ (the rationals are a null set).\n\n**The same function, two integration theories giving different answers** — the difference lies not in the function but in “how one slices”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2d75b92e",
      },
    },
    'prop.integration-substitution': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\varphi : [\\alpha, \\beta] \\to [a, b]$ have continuous derivative, and $f$ be continuous on $[a, b]$. Then\n\n$$\\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta} f\\bigl(\\varphi(t)\\bigr)\\,\\varphi'(t)\\,dt = \\int_{\\varphi(\\alpha)}^{\\varphi(\\beta)} f(x)\\,dx$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#1c7513a6",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ It is **the chain rule written backwards**: $\\frac{d}{dt}F(\\varphi(t)) = F'(\\varphi(t))\\varphi'(t)$, and Newton–Leibniz on both sides does it.\n\nSo the substitution formula has no independent content; it merely transports the derivative of a composition.\n\n⚠ $\\varphi$ need not be monotone: the limits in the formula take the values $\\varphi(\\alpha)$, $\\varphi(\\beta)$, and if $\\varphi(\\beta) < \\varphi(\\alpha)$ the integral naturally flips sign (this is precisely the use of the convention $\\int_b^a = -\\int_a^b$).\n\n⚠ “$\\varphi'$ continuous” is sufficient, not necessary: it suffices that $\\varphi$ be monotone and differentiable and $\\varphi'$ integrable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b28fb057",
      },
    },
    'def.partition': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $a < b$. A **partition** of the closed interval $[a, b]$ is a finite set of points\n\n$$P = \\{ a = x_0 < x_1 < \\cdots < x_n = b \\}$$\n\nThe $i$-th subinterval is denoted $I_i := [x_{i-1}, x_i]$, its length $\\Delta x_i := x_i - x_{i-1}$. The **mesh** of the partition is\n\n$$\\|P\\| := \\max_{1 \\le i \\le n} \\Delta x_i$$\n\nIf $P \\subseteq P'$ (all points of $P$ are put into $P'$, possibly with new points added), then $P'$ is called a **refinement** of $P$. Any two partitions $P, Q$ have a common refinement $P \\cup Q$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#8a56810a",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Finite** is deliberate: the Riemann integral only cuts finitely many times.\n\nOnly on the Lebesgue side are countably many pieces allowed — that is one source of the difference between the two.\n\n⚠ The $x_i$ in the notation are partition points, unrelated to the variable of the function;\n\n$\\Delta x_i$ is a length, not a difference of function values.\n\n⭐ The role of refinements: the finer the partition, the smaller the upper sum and the larger the lower sum (see the notes on “upper and lower sums”) — they are monotone, hence one can take suprema and infima.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#19670323",
      },
    },
    'thm.completeness-equivalent': {
      statement: {
        en: "On $\\mathbb{R}$ the following six statements are **mutually equivalent**:\n\n**(1) Supremum principle**: every nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ that is bounded above has a supremum.\n\n**(2) Monotone bounded theorem**: every increasing sequence bounded above converges.\n\n**(3) Nested interval theorem**: nested closed intervals whose lengths tend to zero have an intersection consisting of exactly one point.\n\n**(4) Bolzano–Weierstrass**: every bounded sequence has a convergent subsequence.\n\n**(5) Cauchy criterion**: every Cauchy sequence converges.\n\n**(6) Metric completeness**: $(\\mathbb{R}, |x - y|)$ is a complete metric space.\n\nThe six form a **strongly connected block**: any one of them implies the other five.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#81f6a276",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **second** example in the graph of “several formulations of the same thing forming a strongly connected block” — the first being the choice principles (the axiom of choice $\\iff$ the well-ordering theorem $\\iff$ Zorn's lemma $\\iff$ the Hausdorff maximal principle $\\iff$ Tukey's lemma).\n\nTheir treatment in the graph is the same: **they always stay on the same layer**.\n\n⭐ Why it is worth a separate node: which one is chosen as the premise determines the **style** of every later proof — proving via the supremum principle (order), via nested intervals (geometry), or via the Cauchy criterion (sequences) are three different routes.\n\nThis block is precisely that “transfer map”.\n\n⚠ “Complete” here has **two contexts**: the supremum-principle one is **order completeness** (bounded above implies a supremum), the metric-space one is **metric completeness** (Cauchy sequences converge).\n\nOn $\\mathbb{R}$ they are equivalent, but do not carry this equivalence outside as a general fact.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#dfd7cc3b",
      },
    },
    'thm.convex-criterion': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be differentiable on the interval $I$. Then $f$ is convex iff $f'$ is increasing on $I$.\n\nIf $f$ is twice differentiable, then $f$ is convex $\\iff f''(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x \\in I$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#44bcac5f",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This translates the **geometric** condition of convexity into the analytic condition of monotone derivative — together with “the sign of the derivative $\\implies$ monotonicity” this yields the complete chain “the sign of the second derivative $\\implies$ the direction in which the graph bends”.\n\n⚠ Deciding convexity by the **sign** of the second derivative alone requires twice differentiability;\n\n$|x|$ is convex but has no second derivative, so outside $C^{2}$ one must return to the increasing-derivative or chord criterion.\n\n⚠ Strict convexity ($f'' > 0$) and convexity ($f'' \\ge 0$) likewise cannot be interchanged: $f(x) = x^{4}$ at $0$ has $f''(0) = 0$ yet is still strictly convex.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c25342f4",
      },
    },
    'def.caratheodory-measurable': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mu^{*}$ be an outer measure on $X$. A set $A \\subseteq X$ is called **$\\mu^{*}$-measurable** iff $A$ splits every set “cleanly into two pieces”:\n\n$$\\mu^*(E) = \\mu^*(E \\cap A) + \\mu^*(E \\cap A^c)\\quad  \\forall E \\subseteq X$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#45bc0465",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Since $\\mu^{*}$ is only subadditive, one always has $\\mu^*(E) \\le \\mu^*(E \\cap A) + \\mu^*(E \\cap A^c)$. The definition therefore only requires the **reverse inequality**: $A$ “eats” nothing.\n\nAt first the definition looks strange (why not use intuitive conditions involving open or closed sets?) — its virtue is that it is **completely intrinsic**: only the single object $\\mu^{*}$ is needed, no topology. Hence it works for an arbitrary outer measure.\n\nIntuition: $A$ is a set that can be “cut out freely”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7150b9fe",
      },
    },
    'def.semifinite-part': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $(X, \\mathcal{M}, \\mu )$ be a measure space. Define\n\n$$\\mu_0(E) := \\sup \\{ \\mu(F) : F \\subseteq E, F \\in \\mathcal{M}, \\mu(F) < \\infty \\}$$\n\nThen $\\mu _{0}$ is called the **semifinite part** of $\\mu$; it is a semifinite measure, and $\\mu _{0} \\le \\mu$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#4134ef74",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "Its role: to repair a measure that may take very bad infinite values into a semifinite one, while keeping as much of the original finite part as possible.\n\nIf $\\mu$ is itself semifinite, then $\\mu _{0} = \\mu$ — so this is the “semifinitization” operation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#80af1dbb",
      },
    },
    'thm.ls-exists': {
      statement: {
        en: "For every increasing right-continuous $F : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, there exists on $\\mathbb{R}$ a Borel measure $\\mu _F$ such that\n\n$$\\mu_F( (a, b] ) = F(b) - F(a)\\quad  \\forall a < b$$\n\nand the correspondence between such $\\mu _F$ and $F$ is one-to-one **up to an additive constant**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#5377064e",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof simply concatenates the previous two items: $F \\mapsto$ predicts a premeasure $\\mu _{0}$ (the preceding proposition) $\\to$ extended by Carathéodory to $\\mathfrak{B}_\\mathbb{R}$ (the two theorems before).\n\n“Up to a constant” is because $\\mu _F$ only sees increments $F(b) - F(a)$: replacing $F$ by F + c leaves increments, hence the measure, unchanged. So the precise correspondence is “increasing right-continuous functions modulo constants” $\\leftrightarrow$ “locally finite Borel measures on $\\mathbb{R}$”.\n\n⚠ The restriction “locally finite” is necessary: $\\mu _F((-n, n]) = F(n) - F(-n)$ must be finite in order to come from an increasing real-valued function.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#dd539ad0",
      },
    },
    'prop.L1-implies-measure': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $f_n \\to f$ in $L^1$, then $f_n \\to f$ in measure:\n\n$$\\int |f_n - f| \\to 0 \\implies f_n \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mu\\ } f$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c8ff1d59",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The tool is the **Markov (Chebyshev) inequality**:\n\n$$\n\\mu(\\{|g| \\ge \\varepsilon\\}) \\le (1/\\varepsilon)\\cdot\\int |g| d\\mu$$\n\n(because $\\varepsilon \\chi_{\\{|g| \\ge \\varepsilon\\}} \\le |g|$, integrate both sides). Taking $g = f_n - f$ gives the conclusion.\n\n⚠ The converse fails: counterexample (iii) is a sequence converging in measure but not $L^{1}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#8795f71a",
      },
    },
    'cor.lusin': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{C}$ be Lebesgue measurable. Then for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a **compact set** $E \\subseteq [a, b]$ such that\n\n$$\\mu(E^c) < \\varepsilon$$\nand $f|_E$ is **continuous**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#f54f16c3",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The condition “measurable” looks weak, but Lusin says: a measurable function is, up to a small error, a **continuous** function — provided one is allowed to cut a small piece out of the domain.\n\nThe division of labour with Egorov: Egorov says a **sequence of functions** converges almost uniformly, Lusin says a **single function** is almost continuous. Both trade a small set for regularity.\n\n⚠ The piece cut out must be allowed to be open, and the remainder must be **compact** (on [a,b], compact $\\iff$ bounded and closed), not merely measurable.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#f502a754",
      },
    },
    'def.cauchy-in-measure': {
      statement: {
        en: "A sequence $\\{f_n\\}$ of measurable functions is **Cauchy in measure** iff\n\n$$\\forall\\varepsilon > 0, \\mu(\\{x : |f_n(x) - f_m(x)| \\ge \\varepsilon\\}) \\to 0\\quad  (m, n \\to \\infty)$$\n\nthat is: in the sense of measure, once the indices are large enough the terms get closer and closer to each other.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#19905998",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Cauchy in measure $Cauchy \\iff$ convergence in measure**: both directions can be proved (convergence $\\implies Cauchy$ by the triangle inequality; $Cauchy \\implies$ convergence is the next theorem).\n\nThis is one virtue of the “in measure” topology: it is **complete**. By contrast, a.e. convergence is neither strong nor complete, while $L^{1}$ convergence is complete (Riesz–Fischer).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#686fd855",
      },
    },
    'thm.cauchy-in-measure': {
      statement: {
        en: "If $\\{f_n\\}$ is Cauchy in measure, then there is a measurable function $f$ such that\n\n$$f_n \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mu\\ } f$$\n\nand there is a **subsequence** $\\{f_{n_j}\\}$ such that\n\n$$f_{n_j} \\to f\\quad  \\text{a.e.}$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d55072ba",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the most useful property of “convergence in measure”: **convergence itself does not guarantee convergence at any single point, but one can always extract a subsequence converging almost everywhere**.\n\nThe tool in the proof is a Borel–Cantelli type argument: build a family of “bad sets” with $\\sum_k \\mu(E_k) < \\infty$; then almost every point lies in only finitely many bad sets.\n\nThis is also the half of Riesz's theorem stating “convergence in measure $\\implies$ there is a subsequence converging a.e.” (the converse fails: a.e. convergence does not imply convergence in measure unless the space has finite measure).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6fce7d90",
      },
    },
    'def.product-sigma': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\{ (X_\\alpha , \\mathcal{M}_\\alpha ) \\}_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be a family of measurable spaces, $X = \\prod_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha$, and $\\pi _\\alpha : X \\to X_\\alpha$ the projections.\n\nThe **product $\\sigma$-algebra** is defined as\n\n$$\\otimes _{\\alpha \\in A} \\mathcal{M}_\\alpha := \\mathcal{M}(\\{ \\pi _\\alpha^{-1}(E_\\alpha) : E_\\alpha \\in \\mathcal{M}_\\alpha, \\alpha \\in A \\})$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#da9e3af5",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "That is, the $\\sigma$-algebra generated by all **cylinder sets**. A cylinder set $\\pi _\\alpha ^{-1}(E_\\alpha )$ is a set that “imposes conditions only on the $\\alpha$-th coordinate”.\n\n⚠ In general it **cannot** be defined as “take all $\\prod E_\\alpha$ and then the $\\sigma$-algebra they generate” — that is equivalent only when the index set is **countable** (see the next item).\n\nThe reason for this definition: the projections $\\pi _\\alpha$ should be measurable, and a $\\sigma$-algebra must be closed under preimages — so take the smallest one.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#43f83086",
      },
    },
    'thm.monotone-inverse': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be **strictly monotone** and continuous on the interval $I$. Then\n\n- $f(I)$ is an interval ($f$ maps intervals to intervals);\n- $f : I \\to f(I)$ is a bijection, its inverse $f^{-1} : f(I) \\to I$ is also continuous, and it is monotone **in the same direction** (if $f$ is increasing then $f^{-1}$ is increasing).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2aac490c",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem is the **certificate of legitimacy for elementary functions**: it guarantees that $\\exp$ has a continuous inverse $\\log$, and that $\\sin$ on $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$ has a continuous inverse $\\arcsin$.\n\nWithout it, the inverse trigonometric and inverse hyperbolic functions could only be waved through as “obviously continuous”.\n\n⚠ Strict monotonicity is **necessary**: a constant function has no inverse;\n\n$\\sin$ is not monotone, so one must first restrict to a monotone interval and then invert.\n\n⭐ It also shows that “the discontinuities of a monotone function can only be jumps”: a monotone function has no oscillatory discontinuity, since the one-sided limits necessarily exist (monotone and bounded).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#49a74499",
      },
    },
    'prop.discontinuity-types': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be defined on a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$ but **discontinuous** at $x_0$. One calls $x_0$ a **discontinuity** of $f$, and classifies it according to whether the two one-sided limits exist:\n\n**First kind**: $\\lim_{x \\to x_0^{-}} f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x \\to x_0^{+}} f(x)$ both exist (and are finite). Among these\n\n- **removable discontinuity**: the two one-sided limits are equal (denote the common value by $L$). In that case redefining $f(x_0)$ to be $L$ makes it continuous;\n- **jump discontinuity**: the two one-sided limits differ; the difference $f(x_0^{+}) - f(x_0^{-})$ is called the **jump**.\n\n**Second kind**: at least one one-sided limit fails to exist, or equals $\\pm\\infty$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#4ec39df9",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This classification is not just terminology in the graph: **the discontinuities of a monotone function are all jumps, and there are at most countably many of them** — that is the starting point of the BV and measure-theory cluster (the atoms of $\\mu_F$, $F' = 0$ a.e.).\n\n⚠ The point of “removable” is that $f$ is **defined at $x_0$ but does not equal the limit**: for example $f(x) = \\sin x / x$ becomes continuous after filling in $f(0) = 1$.\n\nWhen $x_0$ is not in the domain at all (e.g. $1/x$ at $0$), it does not count as a discontinuity with domain $0$.\n\n⭐ The typical second-kind example: $\\sin(1/x)$ at $0$ (oscillation, the limit does not exist).\n\n**Proof** (the classification is exhaustive).\n\nAt a discontinuity $x_0$, each one-sided limit has only two states: exists and is finite, or does not exist / is infinite.\n\nThus “both one-sided limits are finite” and “at least one fails” are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, and the former splits according to whether the two limits are equal into removable and jump — the three cases are mutually exclusive and all possibilities are listed.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a2a1efff",
      },
    },
    'prop.stereographic-circle': {
      statement: {
        en: "Stereographic projection maps circles on $S^{2}$ to circles on $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$:\n\n1. a circle not passing through the north pole $N$ $\\mapsto$ a genuine circle in the complex plane;\n2. a circle passing through the north pole $N$ $\\mapsto$ a line in the complex plane (adding $\\infty$ makes it a circle through $\\infty$).\n\nMoreover it is **conformal**: the angle between two curves on the sphere equals the angle between them in the plane.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d4cfd1f1",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The algorithm for circle preservation**: a circle on the sphere is “the intersection of a plane with the sphere”, i.e. $ax + by + cw = d$. Substitute the stereographic projection formula and rearrange into a linear expression in $u, v, |z|^{2}$:\n\n$$(d - c)|z|^{2} - 2au - 2bv = (d + c),$$\n\nWhen $c \\ne d$ this is the equation of a circle; when $c = d$ it degenerates into a line — exactly matching “whether the circle passes through the north pole”. ∎\n\n⭐ **The meaning of conformality**: it shows that “doing complex analysis on the sphere” and “doing it on the plane” are the same subject, only with a different metric. This is also why the conformal structure on $\\widehat{\\mathbb{C}}$ can be defined as “$z \\mapsto 1/z$ is also holomorphic” — $1/z$ is precisely what “rotation by 180° about the north pole” looks like on the plane.\n\n⚠️ Orientation: the sphere and the plane have different orientation conventions, and different textbooks differ by a sign in saying “conformal or anticonformal”. This graph only asserts “conformal (angles preserved)”; orientation is left to each book's convention.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#101f1741",
      },
    },
    'prop.complex-triangle': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the modulus of a sum does not exceed the sum of the moduli; equality holds only when these complex numbers “point in the same direction”.\n\nFor arbitrary $z_1, \\cdots, z_n \\in \\mathbb{C}$,\n\n$$\\left| \\sum_{k=1}^{n} z_k \\right| \\le \\sum_{k=1}^{n} |z_k|.$$\n\nEquality holds iff: the nonzero $z_k$ all have the **same argument**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9d41a652",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The trick of the proof is **not to estimate the sum, but first to rotate it onto the real axis**: let $\\theta$ be an argument of $\\sum_k z_k$ (the conclusion is trivial when the sum is zero); then\n\n$$\\left| \\sum_k z_k \\right| = e^{-i\\theta}\\sum_k z_k = \\operatorname{Re}\\left( e^{-i\\theta}\\sum_k z_k \\right) = \\sum_k \\operatorname{Re}\\left( e^{-i\\theta} z_k \\right) \\le \\sum_k \\left| e^{-i\\theta} z_k \\right| = \\sum_k |z_k|.$$\n\nThe second equality is “a nonnegative real equals its own real part”, and the last step is $\\operatorname{Re} w \\le |w|$.\n\n⭐ The equality condition is read off directly: every step must be an equality, i.e. every $e^{-i\\theta} z_k$ is a nonnegative real.\n\n⭐ Two standard corollaries: the reverse triangle inequality $\\big| |z| - |w| \\big| \\le |z - w|$;\n\nand the basic integral inequality $\\left| \\int_\\gamma f\\,\\mathrm{d}z \\right| \\le \\int_\\gamma |f|\\,|\\mathrm{d}z| \\le \\max_\\gamma |f| \\cdot L(\\gamma)$ — the Cauchy inequality and Liouville's theorem later depend entirely on it.\n\n📌 Another item in the same section of the book is the complex Cauchy–Schwarz inequality: $\\left| \\sum_j z_j \\overline{w_j} \\right|^2 \\le \\left( \\sum_j |z_j|^2 \\right)\\left( \\sum_j |w_j|^2 \\right)$, with equality iff the two families differ by a complex constant factor.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#e2c761b7",
      },
    },
    'def.laurent-series': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: relax a power series into a series “on both sides” — the negative powers describe the blow-up of the function near the centre, the positive powers describe its behaviour far away.\n\nLet $0 \\le r < R \\le +\\infty$, and the annulus $A_{r,R}(a) := \\{ z : r < |z - a| < R \\}$. A series of the form\n\n$$\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_n (z - a)^n = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} c_n (z - a)^n + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} c_{-n}\\,(z - a)^{-n}$$\n\nthat converges in $A_{r,R}(a)$ (on every compact subset) is called a **Laurent series centred at $a$**. The first block on the right is called the **regular part**, the second (the sum of negative powers) the **principal part**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6357e99e",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The two blocks have their own domains of convergence: the regular part converges in $|z - a| < R$, the principal part in $|z - a| > r$, and their intersection is exactly the **annulus** — this is why the natural stage for Laurent series is an annulus rather than a disc.\n\n⚠ The trichotomy below requires $a$ to be an **isolated singularity** (holomorphic on some punctured disc $0 < |z - a| < R$); otherwise the shape of the principal part does not reflect the type of singularity — for example on $|z| > 1$ the Laurent expansion has infinitely many principal terms, while $0$ is holomorphic at $f$. A vanishing principal part corresponds to a removable singularity;\n\na principal part with only finitely many terms corresponds to a **pole** (the order = the absolute value of the **largest negative power** in the principal part: if it starts from $(z - a)^{-m}$ one says order $m$ — **not** the number of nonzero terms; $z^{-3}$ has only one term yet is a pole of order three);\n\nan infinite principal part corresponds to an essential singularity.\n\nSo the trichotomy of isolated singularities is, in essence, a classification by the shape of the principal part.\n\n📌 The book first does the “Laurent decomposition”: a holomorphic function on an annulus splits into “a term holomorphic on the inner disc” plus “a term holomorphic on the outer domain (including infinity)”, and the Laurent series is assembled from their expansions.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9a97ac02",
      },
    },
    'thm.picard-little': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be entire and **not constant**. Then $f$ takes every value in $\\mathbb{C}$ except **at most one** point.\n\nThat is: there is at most one $w_{0} \\in \\mathbb{C}$ such that the equation $f(z) = w$ has a solution for every $w \\ne w_{0}$.\n\nExample: $e^{z}$ omits $0$ (and indeed $0$ is not attained), exactly one.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b47f7462",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **How much stronger it is than Liouville**: Liouville (`thm.liouville`) says “a bounded entire function is constant” and only rules out a bounded image; Picard says “the image cannot omit even two points” — even when the image is unbounded.\n\n**The proof given in this graph covers only the finite-order case** (this is an honest label): if $f$ has finite order and omits two values $a, b$, then $g := (f - a)/(f - b)$ is an entire function **omitting $0$ and $1$**.\n\nBy the Hadamard factorization (`thm.hadamard-factorization`), $g = e^{P}$;\n\nif $1$ is omitted, then $e^{P} - 1$ has no zeros $\\implies$ it also has a factorization $\\implies$; comparing growth orders successively forces $P$ to be constant, contradicting that $f$ is nonconstant.\n\n⚠️ **The general case requires Bloch's theorem or the modular function $\\lambda$** (the universal cover from the upper half-plane onto $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{0,1\\}$); this graph has neither, so it is recorded as an **external input**. This is stated honestly in the node; it does not pretend to be a complete proof.\n\n📌 Relation to `thm.casorati-weierstrass`: Casorati says the image is **dense** near an essential singularity; Picard strengthens this to “omits at most one point”. The little theorem is its version for “entire functions + singularity at infinity”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#9dfd2056",
      },
    },
    'thm.picard-great': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $z_{0}$ be an **essential singularity** of $f$. Then for every punctured neighbourhood $z_{0}$ of $U$, $f(U)$ takes every value in $\\mathbb{C}$ except **at most one** point (and the one omitted value is attained at most finitely often).\n\nIn particular: the “density” of Casorati–Weierstrass (`thm.casorati-weierstrass`) is upgraded here to “almost surjectivity” —\n\n$$\\#\\left(\\mathbb{C} \\setminus f(U)\\right) \\le 1.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#25a8b7f7",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Relation to the great and little theorems**: the little theorem is the special case “singularity at $\\infty$”, and the great theorem reduces to it by moving an arbitrary essential singularity to $\\infty$ with a Möbius transformation (whence the name).\n\n**Source of the proof**: again Bloch's theorem or the modular function, not expanded here — the node and the edge state explicitly “reduction by the little Picard theorem + transformations, with the deep input recorded as external”.\n\n⭐ **It completes the picture of “three types of singularities”**: near a removable singularity the function is bounded; near a pole it tends to infinity and the image lies almost in one direction; an essential singularity **fills the whole plane (omitting at most one point)**.\n\n⚠️ The “at most one” in the conclusion is **optimal**: $e^{1/z}$ near $0$ takes every value in $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{0\\}$, while $0$ is not attained — exactly one is missed.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#0ab24414",
      },
    },
    'def.entire-order': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $f$ be an entire function. Its **order** is defined by\n\n$$\\rho := \\limsup_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\log^{+} \\log^{+} M(r)}{\\log r},\\qquad M(r) := \\max_{|z| = r} |f(z)|.$$\n\nwhere $\\log^{+} x = \\max(\\log x, 0)$. If $0 < \\rho < \\infty$, one further defines the **type**\n\n$$\\sigma := \\limsup_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\log^{+} M(r)}{r^{\\rho}} \\in [0, \\infty].$$\n\nExamples: a polynomial has order $0$; $e^{z}$ has order $1$ and type $1$; $e^{z^{2}}$ has order $2$; $\\cos z$ has order $1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2807515f",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why use $\\log\\log$**: the growth of an entire function is “exponential in scale”, and measuring $\\log M(r)$ directly distinguishes only “polynomial vs exponential”; a second $\\log$ is needed to separate $e^{z}$ ($\\log M \\sim r$) from $e^{z^{2}}$ ($\\log M \\sim r^{2}$). This is the origin of the scale “order”.\n\n⭐ **The order is an upper bound for the “density of zeros”**: if $f$ has order $\\rho$ and zeros $z_{n}$ (counted with multiplicity), then $\\sum |z_{n}|^{-\\rho - \\varepsilon} < \\infty$ holds for every $\\varepsilon > 0$. This (a direct corollary of Jensen's formula) is the first thread connecting “growth” with “distribution of zeros”.\n\n📌 Notation: $\\log^{+}$ rather than $\\log$, so that no negative numbers appear when $M(r) < 1$ — the behaviour for small $r$ should not affect the order.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d0b24e25",
      },
    },
    'thm.monodromy': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\Omega \\subset \\mathbb{C}$ be a **simply connected** domain, $(f, D)$ a function element at some point of $\\Omega$, and suppose $f$ can be analytically continued along every curve in $\\Omega$. Then there is on $\\Omega$ a **single-valued** holomorphic function $F$ which, on $D$, equals $f$.\n\nEquivalent statement: if $\\gamma_{0}, \\gamma_{1}$ are two curves in $\\Omega$ starting at the same point and ending at the same point, then when they are homotopic in $\\Omega$ they give the **same** terminal value of the continuation.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#1aebc9fa",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Idea of the proof**: regard the homotopy as a continuous family $\\gamma_{s}$ ($s \\in [0,1]$). For each $s$, continuing along $\\gamma_{s}$ gives a function element $\\varphi(s)$ at the endpoint.\n\nBy continuity of continuation, $\\varphi(s)$ is continuous; and “two elements are equal at the endpoint” is a closed condition (equality is preserved by limits), so $\\{\\, s : \\varphi(s) = \\varphi(0) \\,\\}$ is both open and closed and nonempty, hence all of it — that is, $\\varphi(1) = \\varphi(0)$.\n\n∎\n\n**Why simple connectivity is necessary**: for $\\Omega = \\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$, going once around the origin and not going around are not homotopic, and the continuations of $\\log$ differ exactly by $2\\pi i$ — single-valuedness breaks precisely here.\n\n⭐ **Its use**: it upgrades “continuation along paths” to “a single-valued function on the domain”, so the statement in `prop.single-valued-branch` that “on a simply connected $\\implies$ the logarithm has a single-valued branch” becomes a special case. Conversely, it explains why the “branches” of a multivalued function can go wrong only on domains that are **not simply connected**.\n\n⚠️ The hypothesis “can be continued along every curve” cannot be dropped: $\\sqrt{z}$ can of course be continued on a domain with a slit removed, but once a curve hits the singularity the chain breaks.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7c159db1",
      },
    },
    'thm.poisson-formula': {
      statement: {
        en: "**In plain words**: the values inside the disc are **uniquely determined** by the values on the boundary, and by an explicit weighted average — the weight is the Poisson kernel, which “sharpens” near the boundary.\n\nLet $f$ be holomorphic on a neighbourhood of $B(0, R)$, $u = \\operatorname{Re} f$. Then for $0 < r < R$ and $|z| < r$,\n\n$$u(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\frac{r^2 - |z|^2}{|r e^{i\\theta} - z|^2}\\, u(r e^{i\\theta})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.$$\n\nThe weight function\n\n$$P_r(z, \\theta) := \\frac{r^2 - |z|^2}{|r e^{i\\theta} - z|^2}$$\n\nis called the **Poisson kernel**. For fixed $z$ it satisfies $P_r \\ge 0$ and $\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} P_r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta = 1$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d5447a41",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Use: to solve the **Dirichlet problem** on the disc — given a continuous boundary function $b$, replace $u(r e^{i\\theta})$ in the formula above by $b$; the resulting function is harmonic in the disc and tends to $b$ as one approaches the boundary value from inside. That the values of a harmonic function inside are “completely determined by the boundary” is the whole content.\n\n⭐ Shape of the kernel: when $z = 0$, $P_r = 1$, and the formula degenerates to the mean-value property; when $|z| \\to r$ and the direction points at $e^{i\\theta_0}$, the kernel blows up to $\\infty$ there and tends to zero elsewhere — hence it behaves like an “approximate identity”, piling the weight onto the corresponding boundary point.\n\n⚠ Both the $\\operatorname{Re} f$ and the $f$ versions hold (the kernel is **real-valued**, so the formula for $f$ can simply be real-parted).\n\n📌 From $P_r \\ge 0$ and $\\int P_r = 1$ one immediately gets: a harmonic function satisfies the **extremal inequality** $|u(z)| \\le \\max_{|\\zeta| = r}|u(\\zeta)|$, which is precisely the maximum principle.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d4e75fce",
      },
    },
    'def.algebraically-closed': {
      statement: {
        en: "A field $K$ is **algebraically closed** if every **nonconstant** polynomial in $K[x]$ factors into linear factors over $K[x]$ (equivalently: every nonconstant $f \\in K[x]$ has a root in $K$).\n\nIf $\\overline{F}/F$ is algebraic and $\\overline{F}$ is algebraically closed, then $\\overline{F}$ is called an **algebraic closure** of $F$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c2915ba3",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **$\\mathbb{C}$ is algebraically closed** — this is the fundamental theorem of algebra: $\\mathbb{C} = \\mathbb{R}(i)$ is the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{R}$. By contrast $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}$ (the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\\mathbb{C}$) is the standard example of an extension that is **algebraic but infinite**: this is exactly where “algebraic $\\ne$ finite” comes from.\n\n⭐ **Existence of algebraic closures needs choice**: well-order the monic irreducibles over $F$, quotient by them one at a time, build a chain of extensions inductively, and take the union by **Zorn's lemma** (the union is again a field, and algebraic). So “every field has an algebraic closure” sits oddly in the graph: it is one of the **rare existence theorems in field theory that need AC**.\n\n⚠ **Algebraic closures are unique up to isomorphism** (all are algebraic over $F$ and algebraically closed), but **not unique up to a unique isomorphism** — for example the two embeddings of $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{2})$ can land in two different places inside $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}$.\n\n📌 Relation to Galois theory: $\\overline{F}/F$ is in general **not** a finite extension, hence not a Galois extension (the fundamental theorem requires finite). Its “Galois group” is the profinite group $\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline{F}/F)$, which calls for the infinite version of the theory.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#bf6f7538",
      },
    },
    'def.normal-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "An algebraic extension $K/F$ is **normal** if every **irreducible** polynomial in $F[x]$ that has one root in $K$ **splits in $K$** (all its roots lie in $K$).\n\nEquivalent form: $K$ is the splitting field of **some family** of polynomials in $F[x]$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#582bd5eb",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **It measures exactly “is adjoining one root enough”**: $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt[3]{2})/\\mathbb{Q}$ is **not** normal — $x^{3} - 2$ has a root in it, but $\\omega\\sqrt[3]{2}$ does not. While $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{2})/\\mathbb{Q}$ is normal.\n\n⭐ **Unrelated to “normal subgroup”**: here normal means a normal extension; the group-theoretic normal means a normal subgroup. They do meet in the Galois correspondence (an intermediate field normal $\\iff$ a normal subgroup), but the **definitions are completely different**, so do not conflate the words.\n\n⚠ Normality is **relative to the base field**: $K/F$ normal does not imply that $K/E$ is normal over the intermediate field $E$ (one needs Galois for both ends to behave).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c93379ee",
      },
    },
    'def.algebraic-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "A field extension $K/F$ is **algebraic** if **every** element of $K$ is algebraic over $F$; otherwise it is **transcendental**.\n\nA finite extension is always algebraic: if $[K:F] = n < \\infty$, take $0 \\ne \\alpha \\in K$; then $1, \\alpha, \\ldots, \\alpha^{n}$ — that is, $n+1$ vectors — are **linearly dependent** in the $n$-dimensional space, which gives a nonzero polynomial with root $\\alpha$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#adf6dd0a",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The converse fails**: $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}/\\mathbb{Q}$ (the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{Q}$) is algebraic but of **infinite** degree. So “algebraic” and “finite” are two different things — a distinction that matters in Galois theory (the fundamental theorem holds only for **finite** Galois extensions).\n\n⭐ **Algebraic extensions are closed under towers**: $F \\subseteq K \\subseteq L$, if $K/F$ and $L/K$ are both algebraic, then $L/F$ is algebraic. (The proof passes through “finitely generated”; it is not expanded here.)\n\n⚠ The standard transcendental example: $\\mathbb{Q}(x)/\\mathbb{Q}$ (the field of rational functions) — one cannot even speak of its **degree**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#e55e7749",
      },
    },
    'def.galois-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "A finite extension $K/F$ is **Galois** if it is **both** normal and separable.\n\nEquivalent conditions (any one of them):\n\n- $|\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)| = [K:F]$;\n- $F = K^{\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)}$ (the elements fixed by the group are exactly $F$ itself);\n- $K$ is the splitting field over $F$ of some **separable** polynomial.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d45b723c",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why the three conditions are equivalent**: normal + separable ensures that roots go to roots and that roots are distinct, so automorphisms can spread over all $[K:F]$ conjugates; conversely, enough automorphisms force normality and separability. The full proof of the equivalence is the backbone of the course; it is not expanded here.\n\n⭐ **The invariant is the fixed field**: $K^{G} := \\{\\, x \\in K : \\sigma(x) = x\\ \\ \\forall \\sigma \\in G \\,\\}$ is a subfield of $K$ and automatically contains $F$; the Galois condition says precisely that it **equals** $F$, no more.\n\n⚠ **The most common misunderstanding**: taking “splitting field = Galois extension”. What is missing is **separability**: in characteristic $p$ the splitting field of $x^{p} - t$ over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}(t)$ is inseparable, hence not Galois. In characteristic 0 the distinction disappears — which is why it is invisible at first.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#36aaa7ef",
      },
    },
    'def.separable-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "An algebraic extension $K/F$ is **separable** if for every $\\alpha \\in K$ the minimal polynomial $m_{\\alpha}$ has **no repeated roots** in $K$ (equivalently: $m_{\\alpha}$ and its formal derivative $m_{\\alpha}'$ are coprime).\n\n**Over a field of characteristic 0 every algebraic extension is automatically separable.**",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#80c266d3",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **Why characteristic 0 is automatic**: if the irreducible $m$ shared a factor with $m'$, then, since $\\deg m' < \\deg m$, that factor would have to be $m' = 0$; but in characteristic 0 the derivative $m' = 0$ can only be zero, contradicting the irreducibility of $m$. **Real trouble appears only in characteristic $p$**: $x^{p} - t$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}(t)$, yet $x^{p} - t = (x - \\sqrt[p]{t})^{p}$ in the splitting field — one root, with multiplicity $p$. This is an **inseparable extension**.\n\n⭐ **Why Galois theory needs separability**: the correspondence counts automorphisms, and an automorphism can only send a root to a **different** root. With repeated roots there are too few roots, so $|\\operatorname{Gal}|$ drops below $[K:F]$ and the correspondence fails.\n\n⚠ Hence the standard definition of Galois extension is finite + normal + separable; in characteristic 0 the third condition is automatic and is often quietly dropped — dropped **under the characteristic 0 hypothesis**, not in general.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ddcea485",
      },
    },
    'def.galois-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K/F$ be a field extension. For $K$, a **$F$-automorphism** means a field isomorphism $\\sigma : K \\to K$ with $\\sigma|_{F} = \\mathrm{id}_{F}$. They form a group under composition, written\n\n$$\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F) := \\operatorname{Aut}_{F}(K).$$\n\nFor a polynomial $f \\in F[x]$ one often writes $\\operatorname{Gal}(f) := \\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$, where $K$ is the splitting field of $f$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ef39f056",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **An $F$-automorphism must send roots to roots**: if $\\alpha$ is a root of $f \\in F[x]$, then\n\n$$f(\\sigma(\\alpha)) = \\sigma(f(\\alpha)) = \\sigma(0) = 0$$\n\n(because $\\sigma$ fixes the coefficients of $F$). So $\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ **acts** on the set of roots of $f$ — all of Galois theory is the counting of this action.\n\n⭐ **This gives the bridge between groups and fields**: on one side the subfields (the intermediate layer of the field), on the other this group (a group-theoretic object); the Galois correspondence says they are **two faces of the same lattice**.\n\n⚠ **$\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ need not be abelian**: for the splitting field of $x^{3} - 2$ it is $S_{3}$. The word “solvable” works precisely because non-abelian groups also have a notion of solvability.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#f15e2048",
      },
    },
    'def.field-extension': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $F \\subseteq K$ be fields, with the operations of $F$ being the restrictions of the operations of $K$ to $F$. Then $K$ is called an **extension field** of $F$, written $K/F$ (read “$K$ over $F$”).\n\nRegarding $K$ as a **vector space** over $F$ (addition from $K$, scalar multiplication the multiplication in $F$), its dimension is called the **degree**:\n\n$$[K : F] := \\dim_{F} K .$$\n\nIt is a **finite extension** when $[K:F] < \\infty$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#011ef2ef",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **“Extension” is not a new object but a change of viewpoint**: the same field $K$, once a subfield $F$ is specified, can be regarded as a vector space $K$ over $F$. Field theory thus immediately borrows the whole linear-algebra machine (basis, dimension, linear independence) — this is the opening move of all Galois theory.\n\n⭐ **How to compute degrees**: $[\\mathbb{C}:\\mathbb{R}] = 2$ (basis $\\{1, i\\}$); $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{2}):\\mathbb{Q}] = 2$; $[\\mathbb{F}_{p^{n}}:\\mathbb{F}_{p}] = n$.\n\n⭐ **Tower formula** (not a node in this graph): a finite extension $F \\subseteq K \\subseteq L$ satisfies\n\n$$[L:F] = [L:K]\\,[K:F],$$\n\nbecause $L$ is a free $K$-module and $K$ is a free $F$-module; multiply bases. Every later “degree divides” statement relies on it.\n\n⚠ **Finite degree does not mean computable**: $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\alpha):\\mathbb{Q}]$ can be huge while $\\alpha$ is a root of a concrete polynomial — “writable down” and “finite” are two different things (the theme of the quintic story).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c4b0f745",
      },
    },
    'ex.plane-projection': {
      statement: {
        en: "In $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ (with the standard inner product) take the subspace $W = \\{\\, (x, y, 0) \\,\\}$. The orthogonal projection onto $W$ is\n\n$$P = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#5e2d0132",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Checking the three properties one by one**: $P^{2} = P$ (idempotent); $P^{\\ast} = P$ (symmetric); $\\ker P = \\operatorname{span}(e_{3})$, $\\operatorname{im} P = W$, and $\\mathbb{R}^{3} = \\ker P \\oplus \\operatorname{im} P$ is an **orthogonal** direct sum. Conversely: idempotent + symmetric $\\implies$ orthogonal projection (idempotent alone gives an oblique projection, whose image and kernel are not perpendicular).\n\n⭐ **It is the geometric core of least squares**: $P b = (b_{1}, b_{2}, 0)$ is exactly the point nearest $b$ among the points of $W$, and the residual $b - Pb = (0, 0, b_{3})$ is orthogonal to $W$ — precisely the geometry behind the normal equations. In general, when $W = \\operatorname{col}(A)$ the projection matrix is $A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}$.\n\n📌 **Relation to the pseudoinverse**: $A = P$ is itself idempotent and symmetric, so $A^{+} = A$ (the four Penrose conditions hold directly). This says that a projection is, in the language of pseudoinverses, the **self-adjoint idempotent** kind.\n\n⚠ **Do not read the projection matrix as “deleting a coordinate”**: in a different non-orthonormal basis the matrix is no longer diagonal — projection is a geometric notion; $\\operatorname{diag}(1,1,0)$ is only its appearance in the standard basis.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#605e0720",
      },
    },
    'thm.quotient-universal-vs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq V$ be a subspace and $\\pi : V \\to V/U$ the quotient map. Then for every linear map $T : V \\to W$ with $U \\subseteq \\ker T$ there is a **unique** linear map $\\overline{T} : V/U \\to W$ making the diagram commute:\n\n$$T = \\overline{T} \\circ \\pi$$\n\n(that is: $U \\subseteq \\ker T$ $\\iff$ $T$ factors through the quotient map).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#ea97e290",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ “Universal property” = **characterizing the quotient as “the most general target that kills $U$”**: every map vanishing on $U$ factors **uniquely** through $V/U$.\n\nExistence comes from the definition, uniqueness from $\\pi$ being surjective.\n\n⭐ This is the general recipe for the family “cokernel / quotient”; in category theory it is the universal property of a **coequalizer**.\n\n⚠ The hypothesis is $U \\subseteq \\ker T$ ($T$ vanishes on $U$), not $U = \\ker T$ — one is allowed to kill a little more.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#1b873d59",
      },
    },
    'def.linear-isomorphism': {
      statement: {
        en: "A linear map $T : V \\to W$ is a **linear isomorphism** iff $T$ is a **bijection**. One then writes $V \\cong W$ and says $V$ and $W$ are isomorphic.\n\n**Basic fact**: when $\\dim_K V = n < \\infty$, $V \\cong K^{n}$ (choose a basis; $v \\mapsto [v]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ is the isomorphism). More generally,\n\n$$V \\cong W \\iff \\dim V = \\dim W$$\n\n(the right-to-left half of “$\\iff$” holds in finite dimension; in infinite dimension it still holds, provided “dimension” is read as the cardinality of a basis).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#04f85a93",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This “basic fact” is the **cancellation theory** of all finite-dimensional linear algebra: isomorphism classes are determined by dimension alone, so there is only one “$n$-dimensional vector space” up to isomorphism.\n\nThus studying all finite-dimensional vector spaces amounts to studying all $K^{n}$.\n\n⚠ In infinite dimension both directions hold (equipotent bases give an isomorphism, and an isomorphism gives equipotent bases); one only has to read “dimension” as the cardinality of a basis.\n\n⭐ In category language: $V \\mapsto \\dim V$ matches the finite-dimensional objects of $K\\text{-}\\mathbf{Vect}$ with cardinals, so the structure of the additive category reduces to concrete linear algebra.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#15677ef6",
      },
    },
    'def.annihilator': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $U \\subseteq V$ be a subspace. The **annihilator** of $U$ is defined as\n\n$$U^{0} := \\{\\, \\varphi \\in V^{\\ast} : \\varphi(u) = 0 \\ \\text{whenever}\\ u \\in U \\,\\} \\subseteq V^{\\ast}$$\n\nIt is a subspace of $V^{\\ast}$ and (when $\\dim V < \\infty$)\n\n$$\\dim U^{0} = \\dim V - \\dim U, \\qquad (U^{0})^{0} = U$$\n\n(the second equality views $U$ as a subset of $V^{\\ast\\ast}$ via the natural isomorphism $V \\cong V^{\\ast\\ast}$).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#f8908422",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The annihilator consists of “the functionals that vanish on $U$”, so $\\dim U^0 = \\dim V - \\dim U$ is the dual form of the rank–nullity theorem: dualizing $\\pi : V \\to V/U$ gives $U^{0} \\cong (V/U)^{\\ast}$.\n\n⭐ $(U^{0})^{0} = U$ says that **subspaces and annihilators are dual to each other** (in finite dimension a two-way dictionary: a larger $U$ corresponds to a smaller $U^0$, order-reversing).\n\n⚠ In infinite dimension $(U^0)^0 \\supseteq U$ can be strictly larger, and the dictionary breaks.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#e03eb4dd",
      },
    },
    'def.dual-basis': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\mathcal{B} = (v_1, \\ldots, v_n)$ be an ordered basis of $V$. For each $i$ define $v_i^{\\ast} : V \\to K$ by\n\n$$v_i^{\\ast}(v_j) := \\delta_{ij} = \\begin{cases} 1, & i = j \\\\ 0, & i \\ne j \\end{cases}$$\n\n(extended linearly to all of $V$). Then $(v_1^{\\ast}, \\ldots, v_n^{\\ast})$ is a basis of $V^{\\ast}$, called the **dual basis** of $\\mathcal{B}$. Hence in finite dimension $\\dim V^{\\ast} = \\dim V$:\n\n$$\\dim V^{\\ast} = \\dim V$$\n\nand every $\\varphi \\in V^{\\ast}$ satisfies $\\varphi = \\sum_i \\varphi(v_i)\\, v_i^{\\ast}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c9231532",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The dual basis is the functional “the $i$-th coordinate”: $v_i^{\\ast}(v) = [v]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ picks out the $i$-th component.\n\nSo coordinates themselves are linear functionals.\n\n⭐ This yields the transpose of a matrix: $[T^{\\ast}]_{\\mathcal{B}^{\\ast}}^{\\mathcal{C}^{\\ast}} = ([T]_{\\mathcal{C}}^{\\mathcal{B}})^{\\mathsf{T}}$ — **duality is transposition**, and that is the origin of the name.\n\n⚠ The dual basis depends on the choice of basis: change the basis and the dual basis changes with it (contravariantly).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#52f1fbb7",
      },
    },
    'prop.tensor-hom-adjunction': {
      statement: {
        en: "Fix $V$. Then for all $K$-vector spaces $W, U$ there is a natural isomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_K(W \\otimes V,\\ U) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_K\\bigl( W,\\ \\operatorname{Hom}_K(V, U) \\bigr)$$\n\nthat is, the functor $(-) \\otimes V$ is **left adjoint** to $\\operatorname{Hom}(V, -)$:\n\n$$(-) \\otimes_K V \\;\\dashv\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_K(V, -)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#0808e3e5",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This is the **tensor–hom adjunction**, the second paradigm besides “free–forgetful”: it matches “linear maps of two variables” with “linear maps valued in a function space” (**currying**).\n\n⭐ Its categorical status: “$\\otimes$ is closed” (a symmetric monoidal closed category) is exactly the existence of this adjunction — the material around `def.adjoint` applies directly.\n\n⚠ The word “natural” cannot be dropped: the isomorphism itself is easy to write, but only naturality in $W, U$ makes it an adjunction.\n\nThis is also what distinguishes an adjunction from “an accidental isomorphism”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6e3330fb",
      },
    },
    'def.tensor-product-vs': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $V, W$ be $K$-vector spaces. Take bases $(e_i)$, $(f_j)$ and form all **formal linear combinations** of the symbols $e_i \\otimes f_j$:\n\n$$V \\otimes_K W := \\Bigl\\{\\, \\sum_{i, j} c_{ij}\\, e_i \\otimes f_j \\,\\Bigr\\}$$\n\ntogether with the bilinear map $\\otimes : V \\times W \\to V \\otimes W$, $v \\otimes w$ satisfying\n\n$$(v + v') \\otimes w = v \\otimes w + v' \\otimes w, \\qquad v \\otimes (w + w') = v \\otimes w + v \\otimes w', \\qquad (cv) \\otimes w = v \\otimes (cw)$$\n\nThen $\\dim(V \\otimes W) = (\\dim V)(\\dim W)$, and the construction **does not depend on the choice of bases** (see the universal property).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#51ec7aa7",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Elements fall into two classes: **decomposable tensors** $v \\otimes w$ and their **finite sums**.\n\nOnly the latter fill $V \\otimes W$ — “a general tensor is not a single $v \\otimes w$” is the most common beginner's trap.\n\n⚠ Can $v \\otimes w = 0$ happen when $v \\ne 0, w \\ne 0$? No (the tensor product is a free construction, with basis $e_i \\otimes f_j$).\n\nThis is a different question from “not decomposable into pure tensors”.\n\n⭐ The tensor product is **symmetric** ($V \\otimes W \\cong W \\otimes V$) and associative, with unit $K$ ($V \\otimes K \\cong V$) — so finite-dimensional vector spaces with $\\otimes$ form a symmetric monoidal category.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#509fed02",
      },
    },
    'thm.lagrange-identity': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: writes $\\lVert u\\rVert^{2}\\lVert w\\rVert^{2} - \\langle u,w\\rangle^{2}$ as “the square of an area” — this is the source of equality in the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, and the two-dimensional case of the Gram determinant.\n\nFor $u, w \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ (or a real inner product space with an orthonormal basis) we have the **Lagrange identity** (⚠ the coordinate expression on the right equals the left side only in an orthonormal basis: with $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 4x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2}$, $u = (1,0)$, $w = (0,1)$, the left side is $= 4$ while the right is $= 1$; in a general basis one corrects by the Gram matrix $G$)\n\n$$\\lVert u\\rVert^{2}\\lVert w\\rVert^{2} - \\langle u, w\\rangle^{2} = \\sum_{1 \\le i < j \\le n}\\bigl(u_{i}w_{j} - u_{j}w_{i}\\bigr)^{2} .$$\n\nThe left side is exactly the determinant of the Gram matrix of the two vectors:\n\n$$\\det\\begin{pmatrix} \\langle u,u\\rangle & \\langle u,w\\rangle \\\\ \\langle w,u\\rangle & \\langle w,w\\rangle\\end{pmatrix} ,$$\n\nSo geometrically it says: **Gram determinant = the square of the area of the parallelogram**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c0160e5e",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Geometric reading**: the left side is the determinant of the Gram matrix of the two vectors, i.e. the square of the area of the parallelogram they span; the right side is that area squared in coordinates. The full expansion is on the edge `imp.lagrange-identity-proof`. ⚠ The coordinate expression holds only in an orthonormal basis; in a general basis one corrects it by the Gram matrix.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d0c889fe",
      },
    },
    'thm.det-as-exterior': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $\\dim_K V = n < \\infty$ and $T : V \\to V$ be linear. Since $\\dim \\bigwedge^{n} V = 1$, $T$ induces a linear map of the one-dimensional space\n\n$$\\bigl( \\textstyle\\bigwedge^{n} T \\bigr) : \\bigwedge^{n} V \\to \\bigwedge^{n} V, \\qquad v_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge v_n \\mapsto T(v_1) \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge T(v_n)$$\n\nwhich must be “multiplication by a number”. That number is exactly the determinant:\n\n$$\\bigl( \\textstyle\\bigwedge^{n} T \\bigr)(v_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge v_n) = (\\det T)\\,(v_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge v_n)$$\n\nEquivalent form: $\\det T$ is the eigenvalue of $T$ on the one-dimensional space of the top exterior power.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#00e9ed18",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem explains the three axioms of the determinant at a stroke:\n- multilinearity $\\Longleftarrow$ $\\wedge$ is linear in each variable;\n- alternation $\\Longleftarrow$ $v \\wedge v = 0$ (the quotient construction);\n- normalization $\\Longleftarrow$ when $v_i = e_i$, $e_1 \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge e_n$ is a basis and the coefficient is normalized to $1$.\n\n⭐ Hence “existence and uniqueness” becomes “a linear map of a one-dimensional space can only be multiplication by a number” — a second proof of the same theorem, and the conceptually cleaner one.\n\n⭐ It also shows that $\\det$ is **independent of the basis** (because $\\bigwedge^{n}T$ does not depend on a basis), and $\\det(AB) = \\det A \\det B$ (functoriality: $\\bigwedge^{n}(AB) = \\bigwedge^{n}A \\circ \\bigwedge^{n}B$, and composition of maps on a one-dimensional space is multiplication) — so multiplicativity of the determinant is a corollary.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#92f76ff2",
      },
    },
    'thm.witt-cancellation': {
      statement: {
        en: "**What it does**: guarantees that “orthogonal decomposition” cancels — if two forms become isomorphic after adding the same thing, they were already isomorphic. Only with this can one speak of the **unique** decomposition of a form.\n\nLet $V$ be a **finite-dimensional** vector space, $\\beta$ a **nondegenerate** symmetric bilinear form over a field $K$ ($\\operatorname{char} K \\ne 2$), and $U, W \\subseteq V$ a **nondegenerate** subspace. If there is an isometry (a linear isomorphism preserving $\\beta$)\n\n$$U \\perp W \\ \\cong\\ U' \\perp W ,$$\n\nthen $U \\cong U'$ (as spaces with forms).\n\n**Corollary (unique decomposition)**: a nondegenerate form always splits orthogonally into several hyperbolic planes plus one **anisotropic** piece, and both the number of hyperbolic planes (the Witt index) and the anisotropic part are uniquely determined.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#c40f776e",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Why the hypothesis “nondegenerate”**: in the degenerate case cancellation fails (when $\\beta = 0$ the left side is always $0$). This is why the whole theory of quadratic forms assumes nondegeneracy first — not pedantry, but the lifeline of cancellation.\n\n⭐ **It is the last brick of “classification”**: Sylvester's law of inertia gives “the number of signs in a diagonal form is unique” over the reals; Witt cancellation gives “the number of blocks in a decomposition is unique over **any** field”. The former works only over $\\mathbb{R}$, the latter over any field with char $\\ne$ 2.\n\n⚠ $\\operatorname{char} K \\ne 2$ cannot be dropped: in characteristic $2$ “symmetric” and “alternating” coincide, $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not exist, and orthogonalization and Witt theory must be rewritten (that is another subject). It is not expanded here.\n\n⭐ A stronger version is the **Witt extension lemma**: an isometry between nondegenerate subspaces always extends to an isometry of the whole space. Cancellation follows directly; the proof is on the edge `imp.witt-cancellation`.\n\n**Reading and proof.** $U'\\perp W$ denotes the orthogonal summand inside the isometric image ($U'\\subseteq V$ is not required). The full proof is on the edge: first extend $f|_W$ to an isometry $V_2$ of the whole space, then use $F^{-1}\\circ f$ to fix $W$, and finally, from the uniqueness of $W^{\\perp}$, conclude $U\\cong U'$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#23019b46",
      },
    },
    'def.symmetric-algebra': {
      statement: {
        en: "In parallel with the exterior algebra: quotient the **tensor algebra** $T^{\\bullet}V$ by the **homogeneous two-sided ideal** generated by $v \\otimes w - w \\otimes v$; its **$k$-th piece** is called the **symmetric power** $\\operatorname{Sym}^{k}V$ (the image is written $v \\cdot w$, and is commutative); the direct sum of the pieces is the **symmetric algebra**\n\n$$\\operatorname{Sym}^{\\bullet} V := \\bigoplus_{k \\ge 0} \\operatorname{Sym}^{k} V$$\n\nIt is a **commutative** graded algebra, and\n\n$$\\dim \\operatorname{Sym}^{k} V = \\binom{n + k - 1}{k}, \\qquad \\operatorname{Sym}^{\\bullet} V \\cong K[x_1, \\ldots, x_n] \\ (n = \\dim V)$$",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b4e6039a",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ The three “quotients of $V^{\\otimes \\bullet}$” form a complete table:\n\n- no quotient: $\\implies$ the **tensor algebra** $T^{\\bullet}V$ (noncommutative, free);\n- quotient by $vw - wv$ $\\implies$ the **symmetric algebra** $\\operatorname{Sym}^{\\bullet}V \\cong K[x_1, \\ldots, x_n]$ (commutative);\n- quotient by $vw + wv$ $\\implies$ the **exterior algebra** $\\bigwedge^{\\bullet}V$ (graded-commutative).\n\n⭐ «$\\operatorname{Sym}^{\\bullet}V \\cong K[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$» shows that the polynomial ring is not conjured from nothing — it is the symmetric algebra of an $n$-dimensional space.\n\nThis explains why polynomials and multilinear forms are so tightly linked (and leads back to `def.polynomial-ring`).\n\n⭐ The classification of bilinear forms (symmetric / alternating) is the algebraic shadow of these two quotients.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#cd2993f9",
      },
    },
    'def.polynomial': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $K$ be a field. A **univariate polynomial** over $K$ is a formal expression\n\n$$f = a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^{2} + \\cdots + a_n x^{n}, \\qquad a_i \\in K$$\n\n(only finitely many coefficients are nonzero); $x$ is called the **indeterminate** and $a_i$ the **coefficients**.\n\nIf the top nonzero coefficient is $a_n \\ne 0$, the **degree** of $f$ is $n$, written $\\deg f := n$. The zero polynomial has no degree, and a nonzero constant polynomial has degree $0$. $a_n$ is called the **leading coefficient**; when $a_n = 1$, $f$ is called **monic**.\n\nTwo polynomials are **equal** iff all coefficients agree — “polynomial” and “polynomial function” are two different things (see the notes).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d7fed725",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ Convention: the zero polynomial has **no degree** — if one sets $\\deg 0 := -\\infty$, then $\\deg(fg) = \\deg f + \\deg g$ and $\\deg(f + g) \\le \\max\\{\\deg f, \\deg g\\}$ also make sense for it, which is the least troublesome bookkeeping.\n\n⚠ **Polynomial $\\ne$ polynomial function**: over $\\mathbb{F}_2$, $x^{2} + x$ and $0$ agree everywhere as functions (substituting $0, 1$ gives $0$ in both), but they differ as polynomials (different coefficients).\n\nIn this graph “polynomial” always means the **formal object** (its coefficient sequence).\n\n⭐ Dropping the phrase “finitely many terms” gives **formal power series** $K[[x]]$ — that is another subject (generating functions, formal groups).\n\n⭐ Degree is the ruler of this theory: division with remainder, the number of roots, irreducibility all speak in terms of degree.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#23b8a70c",
      },
    },
    'def.polynomial-ring': {
      statement: {
        en: "For a field $K$, on the set $K[x]$ of all univariate polynomials over it define\n\n$$(f + g)_i := a_i + b_i, \\qquad (fg)_i := \\sum_{j=0}^{i} a_j\\, b_{i-j}$$\n\n(addition termwise, multiplication **convolution**). Then $K[x]$ is a commutative ring with unity, called the **polynomial ring** over $K$, and\n\n$$\\deg(fg) = \\deg f + \\deg g \\qquad (f, g \\ne 0)$$\n\nHence $K[x]$ is an **integral domain** (no zero divisors): $fg = 0 \\implies f = 0$ or $g = 0$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#fb822ec1",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ $K[x]$ is the value of the “univariate polynomial functor on $K$”: $K \\mapsto K[x]$ is left adjoint to “sending $K$ to $K$-algebras” (the free $K$-algebra).\n\nThe node `ex.algebra-categories` already mentioned $k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Alg}$.\n\n⭐ $\\deg(fg) = \\deg f + \\deg g$ is the whole source of integrality: integrality of the coefficient field makes degrees add, so there are no zero divisors.\n\n⚠ Constant polynomials ($\\deg = 0$) and the zero polynomial must be treated separately; every identity about degrees needs the hypothesis “$f, g \\ne 0$”.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6b13910b",
      },
    },
    'def.int-domain': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a commutative ring with unity and $A \\ne \\{0\\}$. If $ab = 0$ forces $a = 0$ or $b = 0$, then $A$ is called an **integral domain** ($A$ has no zero divisors).\n\nEquivalent form (cancellation): for $a \\ne 0$, $ab = ac$ $\\implies$ $b = c$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#fc6d0367",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "An integral domain is the smallest setting in which divisibility and cancellation make sense: $\\mathbb{Z}$, $K[x]$, $\\mathbb{Z}[i]$, $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{2}]$ are all integral domains; $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$ ($n$ composite) and $M_{n}(K)$ ($n \\ge 2$) are not.\n\n\n\nIt is also where divisibility lands: $a \\mid b$ has decent cancellation only in the absence of zero divisors — otherwise $2 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod 6$ wrecks divisibility and order completely.\n\n\n\nThe uses of the three conditions: commutativity $\\implies$ lets one speak of the “principal ideal $(a)$”; unity $\\implies$ makes the unit group and “invertible” meaningful; no zero divisors $\\implies$ allows cancellation and hence the fraction field.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2eb2f8a0",
      },
    },
    'def.simple-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "A group $G \\ne \\{e\\}$ is **simple** iff it has only two normal subgroups: $\\{e\\}$ and $G$ itself (that is, it has no nontrivial **proper** normal subgroup).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#5e5b75b4",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ A simple group is “the prime of group theory”: the factors of a composition series are all simple, and Jordan–Hölder says these factors are unique — simple groups play exactly the role of prime factors in integer factorization.\n\n⭐ Basic examples and counterexamples:\n- $\\mathbb{Z}/p$ ($p$ prime) is simple: every subgroup is normal (abelian) and there are only the two trivial ones;\n- $A_n$ ($n \\ge 5$) is simple and **non-abelian**;\n- $S_n$ ($n \\ge 3$) is **not** simple: $A_n \\trianglelefteq S_n$ is a proper normal subgroup;\n- $A_4$ is **not** simple: $V_4 = \\{e, (12)(34), (13)(24), (14)(23)\\}$ is normal.\n\n⭐ The relation between “simple” and “non-solvable” is a clean chain: simple and solvable $\\iff$ simple abelian $\\iff$ cyclic of prime order.\n\nSo **non-abelian simple = the unsolvable brick**, and $A_5$ is the smallest one.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#affcc12e",
      },
    },
    'thm.jordan-holder': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ have two composition series\n\n$$\n\\{e\\} = G_0 \\trianglelefteq G_1 \\trianglelefteq \\cdots \\trianglelefteq G_r = G, \\qquad\n\\{e\\} = H_0 \\trianglelefteq H_1 \\trianglelefteq \\cdots \\trianglelefteq H_s = G\n$$\n\nThen $r = s$, and there is a permutation $\\sigma \\in S_r$ such that for every $i$\n\n$$G_i / G_{i-1} \\;\\cong\\; H_{\\sigma(i)} / H_{\\sigma(i)-1}$$\n\nThat is: **the multiset of composition factors is independent of the choice of composition series**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2b7a296c",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ This theorem makes the phrase “the composition factors of $G$” **legitimate** — otherwise it would be a property of one chain.\n\nIt occupies in group theory exactly the place of the fundamental theorem of arithmetic in the integers: split an object into indecomposable pieces and guarantee the splitting is unique.\n\n⚠ **The converse fails**: equal composition factors do **not** imply isomorphic.\n\nExample: $\\mathbb{Z}/12$ and $\\mathbb{Z}/2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/6$ both have composition factors $\\mathbb{Z}/2, \\mathbb{Z}/2, \\mathbb{Z}/3$, but the former has an element of order $12$ and the latter does not, so they are not isomorphic.\n\nThat is, composition factors are an **invariant, not a complete invariant**.\n\n⭐ The proof proceeds by **refinement**: first show that any two normal series have equivalent refinements (Schreier's refinement theorem), then use the Zassenhaus (butterfly) lemma to make the refinements.\n\nA composition series is already finest (the factors are simple and admit no further refinement), so two composition series must be equivalent.\n\nThe full proof is on the edge.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#bf3ff2ce",
      },
    },
    'def.module-category': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $A$ be a ring with unity. The **category of left $A$-modules** $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ has as objects the left $A$-modules and as morphisms the $A$-linear maps, with composition and identities defined pointwise.\n\nRight $A$-modules form $\\mathbf{Mod}\\text{-}A$; when $A$ is commutative the two categories are equivalent.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#f932efdf",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The model room of homological algebra**: $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ is abelian, with kernels, cokernels, and strict monomorphisms and epimorphisms; free modules give enough projectives, Baer's criterion gives enough injectives, and it is even a Grothendieck category.\n\n**Special cases**: $\\mathbb Z\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}\\simeq\\mathbf{Ab}$; when $k$ is a field, $k\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ is just $\\mathbf{Vect}_k$.\n\n**Freyd–Mitchell**: every small abelian category embeds fully faithfully and exactly into some $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$, so “chasing elements in a module category” is a legitimate generalization.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7df22f98",
      },
    },
    'def.free-group': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $S$ be a set. The **free group** on $S$, written $F(S)$, consists of all **reduced words** $s_{1}^{\\varepsilon_{1}}\\cdots s_{n}^{\\varepsilon_{n}}$, where $s_{i}\\in S$, $\\varepsilon_{i}=\\pm1$, and adjacent $s_{i}=s_{i+1}$, $\\varepsilon_{i}=-\\varepsilon_{i+1}$ have been cancelled; the empty word is written $1$. Multiplication is concatenation of words followed by reduction. The inclusion $\\iota:S\\to F(S)$ sends $s$ to the one-letter word.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#bd378214",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Normal form**: a reduced word is the unique representation of each element, so two words are equal iff they are letter-by-letter equal after reduction; this makes the operation on $F(S)$ well defined and leaves no further relations.\n\n**Basic examples**: $F(\\emptyset)=1$; $F(\\{s\\})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$ (words $s^{n}$); $F(\\{a,b\\})$ is non-abelian, and $\\pi_{1}(S^{1}\\vee S^{1})\\cong F(\\{a,b\\})$.\n\n⚠ **A different object from the free abelian group**: `thm.csz-free` concerns free abelian groups (commutative); this node is the non-abelian free group; the abelianization of $F(S)$ is the free abelian group.\n\n**Why “free”**: no relations beyond the group axioms; see `def.free-group-universal`.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#18492632",
      },
    },
    'def.free-group-universal': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $S$ be a set, $F(S)$ the free group, and $\\iota:S\\to F(S)$ the inclusion. For any group $G$ and any map $f:S\\to G$ there is a unique group homomorphism $\\tilde f:F(S)\\to G$ with $\\tilde f\\circ\\iota=f$. Equivalently, $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Grp}}(F(S),G)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Set}}(S,U(G))$, where $U$ is the forgetful functor.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#77bb79a6",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Meaning**: giving a homomorphism out of the free group is equivalent to giving an arbitrary map from the generating set to the target group; there are no relations among the generators to satisfy.\n\n**Uniqueness**: by $\\tilde f(s)=\\iota(s)$ and homomorphy, $\\tilde f(s^{\\varepsilon})=f(s)^{\\varepsilon}$, so $\\tilde f$ is uniquely determined on the generators; existence is verified directly by multiplication of words.\n\n**Adjunction**: the universal property is exactly the definition of “$F$ is left adjoint to the forgetful functor $U:\\mathbf{Grp}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$” (see `def.adjoint`): the unit $\\iota$ gives $\\operatorname{Hom}(F(S),G)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(S,U(G))$.\n\n⚠ If one required only “existence” and not “uniqueness”, any group containing $S$ would satisfy it, and the universal property would no longer characterize the free group.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#31ecddb5",
      },
    },
    'def.group-order': {
      statement: {
        en: "Let $G$ be a group.\n\n- The **order** of $G$ means its **cardinality**, written $|G|$ (when $G$ is finite, this is the number of elements);\n- The **order** of $x \\in G$ means that $x^{n} = e$ holds for some positive integer, and the **least** such integer $n$ is written $\\operatorname{ord}(x)$; if no such $n$ exists, $x$ is said to have **infinite** order.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#651da02c",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⭐ **The two “orders” are linked by Lagrange's theorem**: when $G$ is **finite**, $\\operatorname{ord}(x)$ divides $|G|$ (for $G = \\mathbb{Z}$ and $x = 1$ the order is infinite, so neither side makes sense), and $\\operatorname{ord}(x)$ is exactly the order of the cyclic subgroup $\\langle x \\rangle$. This is the most used bridge in elementary group theory.\n\n⚠ **The symbol $|G|$ has two meanings in this graph** (same symbol, different contexts): the order of a group is written $|G|$, while the cardinality of a set is written $\\#A$. Mathematically these are the same thing (the order of a group is the cardinality of its underlying set), but the spelling is divided by context — check whether the object is a group.\n\n⚠ **Not the same as “the degree of an element”**: in a ring the “nilpotency degree” of $x$ and the “degree” $[F(x):F]$ of a field element are different quantities.\n\n📌 Readings: $|S_{n}| = n!$; $\\operatorname{ord}((1\\,2\\,3)) = 3$; in $\\mathbb{Z}/n$, $\\operatorname{ord}(\\bar{k}) = n/\\gcd(n,k)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#fd30b19d",
      },
    },
    'prop.unit-counit-examples': {
      statement: {
        en: "The units of a few common adjunctions: the unit of **free $\\dashv$ forgetful** is “sending $x$ into the generators”; the unit of **sheafification $\\dashv$ inclusion** is $T \\to T^{\\#}$; the unit of **$k$-ification $\\dashv$ inclusion** is the identity ($X \\to kX$ when $X$ is already compactly generated).\n\nFrom “counit epimorphic $\\iff$ faithful” one can also read faithfulness off the unit and counit.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7f54d935",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Compute each example with $\\eta_{X} = \\Phi(1_{FX})$, $\\varepsilon_{Y} = \\Phi^{-1}(1_{GY})$ ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7bc17ef4",
      },
    },
    'prop.currying': {
      statement: {
        en: "In $\\mathbf{Set}$, $X \\mapsto X \\times Y$ **$\\dashv$** $Z \\mapsto \\mathbf{Set}(Y, Z)$:",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#baadb8b4",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Given $f : X \\times Y \\to Z$, set $\\widehat{f}(x)(y) = f(x, y)$ ✓; conversely, given $g$ set $f(x,y) = g(x)(y)$ ✓ — the two directions are mutually inverse and natural ✓, so $\\mathbf{Set}(X \\times Y, Z) \\cong \\mathbf{Set}\\bigl(X, \\mathbf{Set}(Y, Z)\\bigr)$ ✓.\n\n$\\mathbf{Cat}$ (the functor category) and $\\mathbf{Ab}$ carry the same adjunction — this is **cartesian closed**.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#8fa3c132",
      },
    },
    'prop.copowers-give-adjoint': {
      statement: {
        en: "If a small category $\\mathcal{C}$ has **copowers**, then every representable functor $h_{X}$ has a left adjoint: $\\mathbf{Set} \\to \\mathcal{C},\\ I \\mapsto X^{(I)}$ **$\\dashv$** $h_{X} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, -)$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#a56d427b",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** The universal property of the copower gives $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}\\bigl(X^{(I)}, Y\\bigr) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf{Set}}\\bigl(I, \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, Y)\\bigr) = \\mathbf{Set}\\bigl(I, h_{X}(Y)\\bigr)$ ✓, which is exactly the definition of an adjunction ✓.\n\n∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#d3863fbf",
      },
    },
    'prop.limit-adjoint': {
      statement: {
        en: "In $\\mathcal{C}$, all limits indexed by $I$ exist $\\iff$ the constant-diagram functor $\\Delta : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{C}^{I}$ has a **right adjoint**, given by\n\n$$D \\mapsto \\lim D$$\n\nDually, all colimits indexed by $I$ exist $\\iff$ $\\Delta$ has a left adjoint, given by $D \\mapsto \\operatorname{colim} D$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#2b46211b",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "The proof is the definition of a limit itself:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}^{I}}(\\Delta X, D) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, \\lim D)$$\n\nThe left side is the set of cones, the right side the set of morphisms into the limit. Comparing with the definition of an adjunction word by word, both sides are the same sentence.\n\nSo “does this category have limits” asks “does $\\Delta$ have an adjoint” — nearly every later construction of an adjunction reduces to constructing some limit.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#08551290",
      },
    },
    'prop.functor-preserves-sections': {
      statement: {
        en: "A functor sends **sections** (resp. **retractions**, **isomorphisms**) to sections (resp. retractions, isomorphisms); in particular a functor sends isomorphisms to isomorphisms.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#01b4487a",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** $f \\circ g = 1$ and $g \\circ f = 1$ remain the same equalities under a functor (functors preserve identities and composition) ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#0c19ec49",
      },
    },
    'def.category-of-topological-spaces': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The category of topological spaces** $\\mathbf{Top}$: objects are topological spaces, morphisms are continuous maps, composition is composition of maps, and identities are identity maps.\n\nProducts are products with the product topology, coproducts are disjoint unions, equalizers carry the subspace topology, and coequalizers carry the quotient topology.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#b29c4e8a",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Complete and cocomplete**: the constructions above give small limits and small colimits term by term.\n\n**The forgetful functor** $\\mathbf{Top}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$ preserves all limits and colimits, but it is faithful and not full: not every function is continuous.\n\n**Not an additive category**: $\\hom(X,Y)$ in general has no group structure; open maps are not morphisms either (morphisms need only be continuous).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#0b476aeb",
      },
    },
    'def.category-of-sets': {
      statement: {
        en: "**The category of sets** $\\mathbf{Set}$: objects are sets, morphisms are functions, composition is composition of maps, and identities are identity maps.\n\nIn $\\mathbf{Set}$ products are cartesian products and coproducts are disjoint unions; it is the standard target of the forgetful functor.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#7f767719",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Complete and cocomplete**: small products together with equalizers give all small limits; small coproducts together with coequalizers give all small colimits.\n\n**Not an additive category**: $\\hom(A,B)$ in general has no group structure, so one cannot add pointwise.\n\n**Relation to the category of topological spaces**: there is a forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Top}\\to\\mathbf{Set}$ preserving limits and colimits, because the topologies are built pointwise from the underlying sets.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#89e1676d",
      },
    },
    'prop.matrix-category': {
      statement: {
        en: "When $k$ is commutative, $\\operatorname{Mat}(k)$ (objects $\\mathbb{N}$, $\\operatorname{Hom}(m,n) = M_{n \\times m}(k)$, composition matrix multiplication) is a **small category**;\n\nwhen $k$ is a field, $\\operatorname{Mat}(k) \\simeq$ the category of finite-dimensional $k$-vector spaces (**equivalent but not isomorphic**).",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#047a7b27",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Matrix multiplication is associative and has identities ✓; choosing a basis gives a functor to finite-dimensional spaces, which is fully faithful and essentially surjective ✓, but there is no isomorphism — the left side is small, the right side large ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#699e7534",
      },
    },
    'prop.cat-final-initial-products': {
      statement: {
        en: "$\\mathbf{Cat}$ has a **terminal object** (the one-object category $\\mathbf{1}$), an **initial object** (the empty category $\\mathbf{0}$), and **finite products** (product categories $\\mathcal{C} \\times \\mathcal{C}'$);\n\nand $\\operatorname{Op}(\\mathcal{C})$ is the **kernel** of the domain–codomain functors $\\operatorname{Mor}(\\mathcal{C}) \\rightrightarrows \\mathcal{C}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#18dea77d",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**Proof.** Verify the universal properties of terminal and initial objects and of product categories one by one ✓; “domain = codomain” is exactly the equalizer of the two functors ✓. ∎",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#fb2d0329",
      },
    },
    'def.subcategory': {
      statement: {
        en: "A **subcategory** of a category $\\mathcal{C}$, written $\\mathcal{C}'$, consists of:\n\n1. a subclass $\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathcal{C}') \\subseteq \\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathcal{C})$ of the objects;\n2. for each pair $X, Y \\in \\mathcal{C}'$, a subset $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}'}(X, Y) \\subseteq \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal{C}}(X, Y)$;\n\nthese morphisms must be closed under composition and contain the identity of every object. (When all morphisms between $X, Y$ are taken, it is called a **full subcategory**.)",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#79a92be8",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "⚠️ **A subcategory is not a full subcategory**: a full subcategory takes some objects but drops no morphisms; a general subcategory may drop morphisms as well.\n\n**Strict subcategory**: the identities must also be the identities inside the subcategory. Almost all contexts assume this.\n\n**Examples**: $\\mathbf{Ab} \\hookrightarrow \\mathbf{Grp}$ is a full subcategory (homomorphisms of abelian groups are exactly their homomorphisms as groups); $\\mathbf{CHaus} \\hookrightarrow \\mathbf{Top}$ is a full subcategory;\n\n$\\mathbf{Grp} \\to \\mathbf{Set}$ is not a subcategory (not even the objects are the same sets).\n\n⭐ “Reflective” and “coreflective” subcategories add one more condition to a full subcategory: the inclusion functor has an adjoint.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#74a0899d",
      },
    },
    'def.zero-object': {
      statement: {
        en: "An object of a category $\\mathcal{C}$, say $0$, is a **zero object** if it is **both terminal and initial**.\n\nThe **biproduct** of a family $(M_{i})_{i \\in I}$ is the object where product and coproduct coincide, written $\\bigoplus_{i \\in I} M_{i}$; in algebra it is called the **direct sum**.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#126db536",
      },
      notes: {
        en: "**The zero object gives the zero morphism**: any $X \\to Y$ factors through $X \\to 0 \\to Y$, and the resulting morphism is written $0_{X,Y}$. Every statement of the form “$f = 0$” or “$f \\ne 0$” relies on it.\n\n**Why the biproduct coincides**: in a **preadditive** category, product and coproduct automatically agree whenever they exist (the two universal properties satisfy each other), so one may speak of just one. An element of $M \\oplus N$ is formally written $f + g$ (one summand from $M$ and one from $N$).\n\n⚠️ A zero object may fail to exist when the category is not assumed to have one (e.g. $\\mathbf{Set}$: the empty set is initial, but the terminal object is a one-point set, so they differ). In $\\mathbf{Ab}$ and $A\\text{-}\\mathbf{Mod}$ it is of course the same $\\{0\\}$.",
        status: 'draft',
        basedOn: "v0.9.3#6044cb8a",
      },
    },
  'prop.eulerchar-genus': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $S$ be a **closed orientable surface** of genus $g = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\operatorname{rank} H_{1}(S;\\mathbb{Z})$ (`def.genus`). Then\n\n$$\\chi(S) = 2 - 2g .$$\n\nThus $\\chi$ and $g$ determine each other: $g = (2 - \\chi)/2$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#542de624'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠ **This node still has status: skeleton**, but the gap has narrowed to **one external theorem** (Radó's triangulation existence theorem, whose node `thm.rado` is itself still a skeleton). The other previous gap, orientability for topological surfaces, now has its own node (`def.topological-orientability`). **This proposition no longer uses the classification theorem**. The elementary argument below replaces both routes in the previous notes, namely (a) using `thm.classification-surfaces` and (b) using the fundamental class or orientation duality. With these two inputs, the proof is complete.\n\n**0. Conventions and notation.** $S$ is a closed surface (`def.closed-surface`), and $g=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{rank}H_{1}(S;\\mathbb{Z})$ (`def.genus`, used directly as the definition). Compute $\\chi(S)$ from any triangulation: `thm.euler-characteristic` gives $\\chi(K)=\\sum_{p}(-1)^{p}\\alpha_{p}=\\sum_{p}(-1)^{p}\\beta_{p}(K)$, and `thm.simplicial-approximation` gives $\\beta_{p}(K)=\\beta_{p}(S)$. Hence $\\sum_{p}(-1)^{p}\\beta_{p}(S)$ is independent of the triangulation, and we denote it by $\\chi(S)$. Here, orientability of $S$ is understood in the topological sense. There is an **oriented atlas** whose charts are homeomorphisms and whose transition maps preserve orientation on overlaps (this is `def.orientable-manifold` with the smoothness requirement removed, namely `def.topological-orientability`; see Section 7). Fix an orientation.\n\n**1. Triangulation (input: Radó).** `thm.rado` (skeleton) states that a compact surface is triangulable, so there exist a finite simplicial complex $K$ and a homeomorphism $h:|K|\\to S$. This is the only external theorem used here.\n\n**2. Subdividing until each simplex lies in a chart (complete).** Let $\\{U_{i}\\}$ be an oriented atlas. Then $\\{h^{-1}(U_{i})\\}$ is an open cover of the compact metric space $|K|$ and has a Lebesgue number $\\lambda>0$ (`def.lebesgue-number`, whose notes give a complete proof). By iterating barycentric subdivision (`def.barycentric-subdivision`, with the mesh shrinking by $\\tfrac{n}{n+1}$), choose $m$ so that $\\operatorname{mesh}(K^{(m)})<\\lambda$. Then each simplex of $K^{(m)}$ lies in some $h^{-1}(U_{i})$. Write $K'=K^{(m)}$. Barycentric subdivision leaves the geometric realization unchanged, so $|K'|=|K|\\cong S$, and $K'$ is still a triangulation of $S$.\n\n**3. Giving $K'$ a coherent orientation (complete).** For each $2$-simplex $\\sigma$, choose a chart $(U_{i},\\varphi_{i})$ containing it and use $\\varphi_{i}\\circ h$ to pull back the standard orientation of $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ to $\\sigma$. If $\\sigma$ lies in two charts, the transition map preserves orientation, so the orientations agree. The orientation of $\\sigma$ is therefore independent of the choice of chart. Now consider two adjacent $2$-simplices $\\sigma,\\tau$ sharing an interior edge $e$. Near an interior point of $e$, choose a small disk $W\\subseteq U_{i}\\cap U_{j}$ ($\\sigma$ lies in $U_{i}$, $\\tau$ lies in $U_{j}$, and $W$ lies in both charts). In the common chart $\\varphi_{j}$, the triangles lie on opposite sides of $\\varphi_{j}(e)$, and the orientation of $\\sigma$ is transported by the orientation-preserving map $\\varphi_{j}\\circ\\varphi_{i}^{-1}$. In $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$, two consistently oriented triangles on opposite sides of a shared edge induce opposite orientations on that edge. Thus the simplices of $K'$ of dimension $2$ are coherently oriented: the two sides of each interior edge induce opposite orientations on it.\n\n**4. Lemma D (complete): $H_{2}(K';\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$.** Let $K'$ be a finite triangulation of a closed connected surface with coherently oriented $2$-simplices. Set $z=\\sum_{\\sigma}\\sigma$, with each $\\sigma$ oriented as above. Then $\\partial z=0$: each interior edge belongs to exactly two triangles, which induce opposite orientations on the edge, so the coefficients $\\pm1$ cancel. Since $S$ is closed, there are no boundary edges and hence no other terms. Thus $z$ is a $2$-cycle. Conversely, let $w=\\sum n_{\\sigma}\\sigma$ be any $2$-cycle. For each interior edge $e$ with adjacent triangles $\\sigma,\\tau$, the coefficient in $\\partial w$ of $e$ is $\\pm n_{\\sigma}\\pm n_{\\tau}$. Coherent orientation gives opposite signs, so $n_{\\sigma}=n_{\\tau}$. The dual graph is connected: $S$ is connected $\\implies$ the graph joining triangles that share a vertex is connected. The link of each vertex of the surface is a circle $\\implies$ the triangles around a vertex form a cycle of edge adjacencies. Together these facts give $\\implies$ the dual graph joining triangles that share an edge is connected. Thus all $n_{\\sigma}$ are equal, and $w\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot z$. Also, $K'$ is a complex of dimension $2$, so $C_{3}=0$, and consequently $H_{2}(K')=\\ker\\partial_{2}=\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot z\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$. ∎\n\n**5. $\\beta_{2},\\beta_{0},\\beta_{1}$ (complete).** By `thm.simplicial-approximation`, $H_{2}(S;\\mathbb{Z})\\cong H_{2}(K';\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$, so $\\beta_{2}(S)=1$. Since $S$ is connected and is a manifold (hence locally path-connected), it is path-connected. By `prop.h0-components`, $H_{0}(S)\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$, $\\beta_{0}=1$. By the definition in `def.genus`, $\\beta_{1}=\\operatorname{rank}H_{1}=2g$.\n\n**6. Conclusion.** $\\chi(S)=\\beta_{0}-\\beta_{1}+\\beta_{2}=1-2g+1=2-2g$. ∎ (Using Radó's theorem from Section 1 and the definition of orientability above.)\n\n**7. Status and remaining gap.** (1) The only external theorem unproved here is Radó's theorem (`thm.rado`, skeleton). (2) Orientability now has its own node, `def.topological-orientability` (oriented atlases and the orientation double cover). The graph also has a smooth version (`def.orientable-manifold`, `prop.volume-form-orientation`) and an extrinsic version in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ (`def.orientable-surface`). Applying the smooth definition to a topological surface first requires a smoothing theorem, which in turn requires Radó or Whitehead. Section 2 of these notes restates the notion in topological terms. (3) This proposition therefore carries `status: 'skeleton'`: the proof **depends on Radó's theorem** (`thm.rado` is still a skeleton). Once Radó's theorem is proved, the status can be removed (orientability is covered by `def.topological-orientability` and does not affect the conclusion). (4) Another option, with the statement unchanged for now and the decision left to the parent session, is to assume a triangulable closed orientable surface. That would make the proof immediately complete by making triangulability a hypothesis.\n\n**8. Relation to the previous notes.** The standard-form count in the previous notes (the $4g$-gon gives $\\chi=2-2g$) and the connected sum formula are still correct. They are reproduced below as an independent verification in standard form. The proof no longer requires them or the former route (b) via the fundamental class or orientation duality. Lemma D is the elementary replacement. It applies only to surfaces and computes only $H_{2}$, without invoking orientation duality.\n\n**9. Independent verification in standard form (complete).** Represent the connected sum of $g$ tori by a polygon with $4g$ sides, paired according to $a_{1}b_{1}a_{1}^{-1}b_{1}^{-1}\\cdots$. Take the fan triangulation from the center. The vertex count is $2$ (the center $+$ one boundary vertex class), the edge count is $2g$ (paired boundary edges) $+4g$ (spokes) $=6g$, and the face count is $4g$, so $\\chi=2-6g+4g=2-2g$. For the connected sum formula $\\chi(M\\#N)=\\chi(M)+\\chi(N)-2$, remove the interior of one $2$-simplex from each surface ($F$ decreases by one and $\\chi$ decreases by one), then use $\\chi(A\\cup B)=\\chi(A)+\\chi(B)-\\chi(A\\cap B)$ and $\\chi(S^{1})=0$ when gluing along the boundary circles. Induction gives, for $g$ tori, $\\chi=2-2g$. This verification can be checked independently of Lemma D.\n\n⭐ Three examples: for $S^{2}$, $g=0$ and $\\chi=2$; for $T^{2}$, $g=1$ and $\\chi=0$; for the double torus, $g=2$ and $\\chi=-2$.\n\n📌 Connection with geometry: Gauss–Bonnet gives $\\int_{M}K\\,\\mathrm{d}A=2\\pi\\chi(M)$. Combining it with this proposition gives $\\int K=4\\pi(1-g)$, so the total curvature depends only on the genus.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0cf00712'
    },
  },
  'thm.classification-surfaces': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $S$ be a **closed surface** (`def.closed-surface`). Then $S$ is homeomorphic to exactly one of the following, which are pairwise nonhomeomorphic:\n\n- The **sphere** $S^{2}$;\n- The connected sum of $g$ **tori**, denoted $T^{2}\\#\\cdots\\# T^{2}$ ($g \\ge 1$), an **orientable** closed surface of genus $g$;\n- The connected sum of $k$ **projective planes**, denoted $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}\\#\\cdots\\# \\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ ($k \\ge 1$), a **nonorientable** closed surface of nonorientable genus $k$.\n\nThe two families are disjoint: orientable and nonorientable closed surfaces (apart from the sphere) are never **homeomorphic**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e49e900e'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠ This node retains status: skeleton. Steps (1) and (2), Radó's theorem and combinatorial classification, are unproved here. Step (3) has a proof. **Note**: `prop.eulerchar-genus` now uses the elementary route through Radó's theorem and Lemma D, so this theorem no longer blocks that proposition. In turn, this theorem does not use `prop.eulerchar-genus` as an input.\n\n**The three steps.** (1) Radó's theorem gives a triangulation of every closed surface (`thm.rado`, skeleton). (2) Combinatorial classification reduces any triangulation to standard form in finitely many moves. (3) The invariants computed on the standard forms, orientability and $\\chi$, determine $(g)$ or $(k)$ and distinguish the homeomorphism types.\n\n**Step (1) (cited without proof).** Radó's theorem states that every compact surface admits a finite triangulation. `thm.rado` is still a skeleton and the graph has no proof. It is used here as an input and does not also establish that any two triangulations are combinatorially equivalent, which is the work of step (2).\n\n**Step (2): Statement and incomplete verification (Lemma K, a gap in the graph).** Let $K$ be a finite simplicial complex and let $|K|$ be a closed connected surface. A finite sequence of operations reduces $K$ to a single polygon with paired edges, with pairing word $a_{1}b_{1}a_{1}^{-1}b_{1}^{-1}\\cdots a_{g}b_{g}a_{g}^{-1}b_{g}^{-1}$ (orientable, $g\\ge 0$) or $c_{1}c_{1}\\cdots c_{k}c_{k}$ (nonorientable, $k\\ge 1$). The moves are (i) cutting along edges and regluing (cut-and-paste), (ii) $2$–$2$ flips, (iii) removing a $2$-simplex attached along just one edge, and (iv) vertex reduction (merging several vertices into one). **This lemma is the core of the classification. It has its own node, `lem.combinatorial-classification-surfaces` (still a skeleton, with gaps in vertex reduction, degenerate cases of 2–2 flips, and the termination measure).**\n\n**Three checks for step (2) (all incomplete, accounting for the gap).** - **$2$–$2$ flips**: The graph only says that two triangles on opposite sides of an edge are replaced by two other triangles with the same vertex set. This works only when the triangles form a quadrilateral along the shared edge and all four vertices are distinct. If the triangles share another vertex (so the quadrilateral degenerates), or the fourth vertex coincides with the first (giving two triangles instead of a quadrilateral), the case needs separate treatment. The graph gives neither these hypotheses nor the degenerate cases. - **Cut-and-paste**: The graph only says to cut along a closed curve and reglue. To do this within a triangulation, first make the curve a simple closed curve (subdivision $+$ general position, normal isotopy). One must then show that cutting gives a new triangulation and that regluing preserves the homeomorphism type. Neither step is given here. - **Termination**: The original claim that the number of paired edges strictly decreases **is false**. A $2$–$2$ flip leaves the edge count unchanged, and cut-and-paste can increase it. What is needed is a well-ordered measure (for instance, induction on the number of sides of the polygon, with finitely many cases as in Munkres's proof, or a lexicographic order on the number of edges $+$ the number of vertices) and a check that each move decreases it. The graph gives neither the measure nor the verification. - **Missing construction for vertex reduction**: Before reducing the triangulation to a single polygon, all vertices must be merged into one (move (iv)). The graph names the move without giving a construction, and this is the step with the most degenerate cases.\n\n**Step (3) (complete).** For the standard $4g$-gon, the fan triangulation from the center gives $V=2$ (the center and one boundary vertex class), $E=6g$, and $F=4g$, so $\\chi=2-2g$. For the $2k$-gon, the counts are $V=2$, $E=3k$, and $F=2k$, so $\\chi=2-k$. The two families have distinct homeomorphism types because one is orientable and the other is nonorientable. Within either family, distinct values of $\\chi$ give nonhomeomorphic surfaces by the topological invariance of $\\chi$ (`thm.euler-characteristic`). The standard forms are the **conclusion** of Lemma K. Defining closed surfaces as the standard forms would make the argument circular.\n\n**Why this theorem cannot take the same route as `prop.eulerchar-genus`.** That proposition only needs $\\chi=2-2g$ and can compute $H_{2}$ directly using Lemma D, without classification. This theorem needs the **structure theorem** that every triangulation reduces to standard form. That is the classification itself, and numerical homological invariants cannot establish it. Bypassing this step would amount to assuming the classification theorem.\n\n⭐ Criterion (assuming Lemma K): Two closed surfaces are homeomorphic $\\iff$ they have the same orientability and the same $\\chi$ (equivalently, both are $S^{2}$, both are connected sums of $g$ tori with the same $g$, or both are connected sums of $k$ copies of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ with the same $k$). For $S^{2}$ and $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$, the values of $\\chi$ are $2$ and $1$, respectively, so $\\chi$ alone is insufficient and must be used together with orientability.\n\n**Gaps and suggestions.** The gaps are $=$ Radó's theorem (`thm.rado`) $+$ Lemma K (combinatorial classification). A future revision could use separate nodes for the combinatorial classification lemma (including vertex reduction and a termination measure), degenerate cases of the $2$–$2$ flip, and normal isotopy of simple closed curves. The existing nodes `thm.rado`, `def.triangulation`, `def.simplicial-complex`, and `thm.euler-characteristic` can be used directly.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3895cfde'
    },
  },
  'thm.rado': {
    statement: {
      en: "Every **compact surface** (a compact, connected, $2$-dimensional topological manifold, possibly with boundary) is triangulable (`def.triangulation`).\n\nA common equivalent formulation is that every compact connected surface is homeomorphic to the geometric realization of a **finite** simplicial complex.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#44abc0b4'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠ This node retains status: skeleton. There are two common approaches to Radó's theorem, and neither is completed here. The outline below identifies the specific lemmas still needed and corrects two inaccuracies in the previous notes.\n\n**Theorem.** Every compact surface is triangulable (equivalently, a compact topological $2$-manifold admits a finite simplicial triangulation). An orientable compact surface also admits a compatible complex structure. See the comparison by dimension below for the precise scope of the equivalent formulation.\n\n**Surfaces with boundary.** The statement allows boundary, but both approaches below address only **closed** surfaces. The extension requires an argument. The standard method is **doubling along the boundary**. Take two copies of $S$ and glue them along $\\partial S$ by the identity to obtain a closed surface $D(S)$. Triangulate $D(S)$ by either approach below, then restrict to $S$, provided the triangulation places $\\partial S$ in the $1$-skeleton (see gap (vi)). One can also collapse each boundary circle to a point to obtain a closed surface.\n\n**Approach A (complex analysis).** An orientable compact surface $\\rightarrow$ a complex structure (a Riemann surface) $\\rightarrow$ existence of a nonconstant meromorphic function $\\rightarrow$ the meromorphic function is a branched covering of $\\mathbb{CP}^{1}$ $\\rightarrow$ pull back and subdivide a triangulation of $\\mathbb{CP}^{1}$ (use the branch values as vertices and subdivide into sectors near the branch points using the local model $z\\mapsto z^{m}$) $\\rightarrow$ obtain a finite triangulation. Gaps: (i) existence of a complex structure (uniformization or the surface case of Riemann–Roch), (ii) existence of a meromorphic function (Riemann–Roch, or Runge approximation $+$ Mittag-Leffler), and (iii) subdivision and compatibility for the pullback under the branched covering. **Note**: The branched covering $z\\mapsto z^{m}$ here is distinct from the orientation double cover used below in the nonorientable case.\n\n**Approach B (differential topology).** Existence of a smooth structure (Whitehead: a $C^{1}$ structure suffices, or construct $C^{\\infty}$ directly) $\\rightarrow$ a Morse function $\\rightarrow$ a handle decomposition $\\rightarrow$ each handle is a $0/1/2$-handle, giving a CW decomposition in attachment order $\\rightarrow$ triangulate each handle and subdivide compatibly $\\rightarrow$ obtain a triangulation. Gaps: (iv) existence of a smooth structure, (v) existence of a Morse function (requiring $C^{2}$ approximation), and (vi) compatibility between the handle decomposition and triangulation (treatment of $1$-handles and gluing between handles).\n\n**Can the nonorientable case use the orientation double cover?** Yes, but **an additional argument is needed**. Let $p:\\widetilde{S}\\to S$ be the orientation double cover. On $S$ there is a corresponding free $\\mathbb{Z}/2$-action, with no fixed points and two points per fiber. Even given a triangulation of $\\widetilde{S}$, descending it to $S$ requires an **equivariant** triangulation: there must be a triangulation of $\\widetilde{S}$ for which the involution is a simplicial automorphism. The equivariant triangulation lemma gives this using two barycentric subdivisions. The first makes the involution map simplices to simplices, and the second places the barycenters in the interiors of the simplices and makes them vertices, at which point the involution acts simplicially on the barycentric subdivision (see Illman for a more general theorem). The resulting quotient complex $\\widetilde{S}/(\\mathbb{Z}/2)$ is a triangulation of $S$. The quotient map is bijective on each open simplex, and the simplicial automorphism determines the gluings. The previous notes said to use the local model $z\\mapsto z^{2}$ at branch points. **This is incorrect**. The orientation double cover has no branch points because the action is free. The model $z\\mapsto z^{2}$ applies to the branched covering from a meromorphic function in Approach A. Confusing the two can make the quotient construction seem immediate.\n\n**Why this theorem concerns dimension 2.** $\\dim\\le 2$: Radó (1925). $\\dim=3$: Moise (1952). $\\dim=4$: Freedman (1982, topological manifolds admit triangulations, with $E_{8}$ counterexamples in the smooth/PL case in the same dimension). $\\dim\\ge 5$: Kirby–Siebenmann (1969), who showed that some compact topological manifolds are not triangulable. Thus triangulability of all compact manifolds holds only in low dimensions, and the surface theorem does not follow from a general result of this form.\n\n**Two distinctions.** (a) Radó's existence theorem and the claim that any two triangulations are combinatorially equivalent are distinct. The latter is the Hauptvermutung (false in $\\dim\\ge 4$, true in $\\dim=2$) and is the work of step (2) of `thm.classification-surfaces`. (b) Existence of a complex structure and existence of a smooth structure are equivalent in $\\dim=2$ (uniformization) and inequivalent in $\\dim\\ge 4$.\n\n**Remaining gaps.** (i) Existence of a complex structure (uniformization or Riemann–Roch), (ii) existence of a meromorphic function, (iii) subdivision of a pullback under a branched covering, (iv) an equivariant triangulation lemma for a free involution, (v) existence of a smooth structure $+$ Morse $+$ handle decomposition (Approach B), and (vi) the boundary case: doubling gives a closed surface, and the triangulation can be chosen to put the boundary in the $1$-skeleton (equivariance under the doubling involution). The graph has no nodes for these steps, so this node remains a skeleton. The first two suggested additions are equivariant triangulation (two barycentric subdivisions), which would also cover the nonorientable case, and the uniformization theorem, on which Approach A depends.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6a9aaf16'
    },
  },
  'prop.eulerchar-nonorientable': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $S$ be a **closed nonorientable surface** already in **standard form**, the connected sum of $k$ copies of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ ($k\\ge1$, see `thm.classification-surfaces`; $k$ is called the **nonorientable genus**). Then\n\n$$\\chi(S) = 2 - k .$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4fdcdf58'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**This proposition assumes standard form.** Here $S$ is the connected sum of $k$ copies of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ ($k\\ge1$), so the computation below needs no classification theorem. Reduction of a general closed nonorientable surface to standard form belongs to `thm.classification-surfaces` (still a skeleton) and is outside the scope of the proposition.\n\n**1. Triangulating $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$.** Identify antipodal points of the regular icosahedron ($12$ vertices, $30$ edges, and $20$ faces) to obtain a triangulation of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ with $6$ vertices, $15$ edges, and $10$ faces, so $\\chi(\\mathbb{RP}^{2})=6-15+10=1=2-1$. The antipodal action is free and simplicial (each simplex is distinct from its antipodal image), so the quotient complex is still a simplicial complex.\n\n**2. The connected sum formula.** Let $M,N$ be triangulated closed surfaces. Choose a $2$-simplex in each and remove its interior, leaving a surface with triangular boundary. Glue the two triangular boundaries by matching vertices to obtain a triangulation of $M\\#N$. The counts are $V=V_{M}+V_{N}-3$, $E=E_{M}+E_{N}-3$, and $F=(F_{M}-1)+(F_{N}-1)=F_{M}+F_{N}-2$, so $\\chi(M\\#N)=\\chi(M)+\\chi(N)-2$.\n\n**3. Induction.** Write $S_{k}$ for the connected sum of $k$ copies of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$. Then $\\chi(S_{1})=1$, and by step 2, $\\chi(S_{k})=\\chi(S_{k-1})+\\chi(\\mathbb{RP}^{2})-2=\\chi(S_{k-1})-1$. Thus $\\chi(S_{k})=1-(k-1)=2-k$. ∎\n\n**4. Independence of the triangulation.** By `thm.euler-characteristic`, for every finite simplicial complex, $\\chi=\\sum_{p}(-1)^{p}\\beta_{p}$. The right-hand side depends only on homology, so the count computed from the particular triangulation above is $\\chi(S)$ and is independent of the choice of triangulation.\n\n**5. Supplement: Why $H_{1}$ is $k-1$ rather than $2k$ (complete, and independent of the computation above).** Choose an open cover at a vertex of the standard $2k$-gon and apply van Kampen's theorem (`thm.van-kampen`) to get $\\pi_{1}(S_{k})=\\langle c_{1},\\ldots,c_{k}\\mid c_{1}^{2}\\cdots c_{k}^{2}=1\\rangle$. Abelianization gives $H_{1}(S_{k};\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}^{k}/(2(c_{1}+\\cdots+c_{k}))\\cong\\mathbb{Z}^{k-1}\\oplus\\mathbb{Z}/2$. This can also be substituted into $\\chi=\\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\\beta_{i}$ to check the value of $2-k$ above. That check uses $H_{2}=0$ and the fact about $\\beta_{i}$ for general surfaces, neither of which has its own node here, so this serves only as corroboration.\n\n⚠ **The term genus does not carry over directly to the nonorientable case**. `def.genus` is defined only for orientable surfaces (division by $2$ relies on the free rank of $H_{1}$ being even). The standard quantity in the nonorientable case is $k$ (nonorientable genus), with the relation $\\chi=2-k$.\n\n⭐ Examples: for $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$, $k=1$ and $\\chi=1$; for the Klein bottle, $k=2$ and $\\chi=0$; for the connected sum of three projective planes, $k=3$ and $\\chi=-1$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#51d9d33a'
    },
  },
  'def.connected-sum': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ and $N$ be two $n$-dimensional **connected** topological manifolds ($n \\ge 1$). Choose an open ball in $M$, denoted $B_{M}$, and an open ball in $N$, denoted $B_{N}$. Remove their interiors to obtain two manifolds with spherical boundary:\n\n$$M^{\\circ} := M \\setminus \\operatorname{int} B_{M}, \\qquad N^{\\circ} := N \\setminus \\operatorname{int} B_{N},$$\n\nBoth boundaries are $S^{n-1}$. Choose any homeomorphism $h : \\partial M^{\\circ} \\to \\partial N^{\\circ}$ and glue the manifolds along their boundaries:\n\n$$M \\# N := M^{\\circ} \\sqcup N^{\\circ} \\big/ \\bigl( x \\sim h(x) \\bigr).$$\n\nThe resulting topological space is the **connected sum** of $M$ and $N$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7b337426'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Gluing is taking a quotient**. After $\\sqcup$, take the quotient topology according to $x \\sim h(x)$ (as in `def.quotient-set`, where a set is open if its preimage is open). The connected sum thus combines disjoint union and quotient.\n\n⭐ **The result is a manifold and is independent of the choices**. At the seam, the ring of points in $M^{\\circ}$, together with $S^{n-1}$ and the corresponding ring in $N^{\\circ}$, has half-neighborhoods on the two sides. Gluing them gives an open set in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, so every point has a chart. Choosing different balls or a different homeomorphism gives a homeomorphic result (this uses the fact that any two embedded balls inside a ball are isotopic, which is not developed here).\n\n⭐ The sphere is an **identity element**: $S^{n} \\# M \\cong M$ (remove a ball from $M$ and a ball from $S^{n}$, so that gluing fills the hole and restores the original manifold). Thus the $n$-dimensional closed manifolds form a **commutative monoid** under connected sum. The operation is commutative and has an identity, but **inverses need not exist**, which is part of what makes classification interesting.\n\n⚠ **Connected sums must be taken in the same dimension**, and the manifolds must be connected. Otherwise the balls could be removed from different components, making the result depend on the choice.\n\n📌 Genus (`def.genus`) counts how many tori occur in the connected sum describing a surface. The classification theorem establishes this equivalence. This node gives the definition of the operation.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5fba10a7'
    },
  },
  'def.genus': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a **closed** (compact and without boundary) **orientable** surface. Its **genus** is defined to be\n\n$$g(M) := \\tfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb{Z}} H_{1}(M; \\mathbb{Z}).$$\n\nthat is, half the free rank of its first integral homology group.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2337f40d'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Why divide by 2**: For a closed orientable surface, $H_{1}$ is a free abelian group, and\n\n\n\n$$H_{1}(M; \\mathbb{Z}) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}^{2g},$$\n\n\n\nso **the rank is $2g$**. The torus is the first example: $H_{1}(T^{2}) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}^{2}$, its rank is 2, and its genus is **1**. Defining genus directly as the rank would give the torus genus 2 and would also give the wrong value for $\\chi = 2 - 2g$ (for the torus, $\\chi = 0$). Dividing by 2 gives the number of holes.\n\n⭐ **Why not define it by the number of handles or by $\\chi = 2-2g$**: Both definitions require the classification theorem first (every closed orientable surface is a connected sum of tori), and the classification theorem itself uses $g$, which would make the reasoning circular. The homological definition is **independent of classification**, so it is the only choice at this stage that avoids circularity.\n\n⭐ Three values that can be computed directly from the definition: for the sphere, $g = 0$ ($H_{1} = 0$); for the torus, $g = 1$; and for the connected sum of $g$ tori, $g(M \\# N) = g(M) + g(N)$ (the part of $H_{1}$ of the connected sum that is the direct sum of those of the two summands). ⚠ The last assertion is a **theorem** requiring Mayer–Vietoris and does not yet have a node here.\n\n⚠ **This definition applies only to closed orientable surfaces**. For surfaces with boundary, use relative homology or first cap the boundary components. In the **nonorientable** case, $H_{1}$ has torsion (for the projective plane, $H_{1} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2$), and genus must be replaced by a different quantity (nonorientable genus $k$, $\\chi = 2 - k$).\n\n📌 The relation to the Euler characteristic, $\\chi(M) = 2 - 2g(M)$, is also a **theorem** covered next. It is only recorded here.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#78c027c8'
    },
  },
  'def.polygon-representation': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $n\\ge 2$, and let $P$ be a regular polygon in the plane with $2n$ sides, whose boundary edges are paired by a fixed-point-free involution $\\tau$. Glue each pair of edges according to $x\\sim\\tau(x)$. The quotient space $S=P/{\\sim}$ is called a **polygonal representation**. If every pair of edges is glued with opposite orientations, then $S$ is orientable. If some pair is glued with the same orientation, then $S$ is nonorientable.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b13c4ff6'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Standard forms**: A closed orientable surface has a polygonal representation with $4g$ sides, $a_{1}b_{1}a_{1}^{-1}b_{1}^{-1}\\cdots a_{g}b_{g}a_{g}^{-1}b_{g}^{-1}$. A closed nonorientable surface has a polygonal representation with $2k$ sides, $c_{1}c_{1}\\cdots c_{k}c_{k}$.\n\n**Examples**: The torus is represented by a polygon with $4$ sides, $aba^{-1}b^{-1}$. The projective plane is represented by a polygon with $2$ sides, $aa$. The Klein bottle is represented by a polygon with $4$ sides, $abab^{-1}$.\n\n**Why use it**: A polygonal representation encodes a surface by finite combinatorial data, namely the edge pairings. These data are the input to the classification theorem. Gluing the edges is the inverse step that reconstructs the surface.\n\n⚠ Gluing need not produce a manifold. If a vertex link is not a circle, the quotient may be a pseudomanifold (for example, when the two endpoints of the $2$-gon $aa$ are identified, this needs a separate check). The definition of a polygonal representation gives the quotient space. Whether it is a surface requires verification.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c79b7edf'
    },
  },
  'def.handle': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $S$ be a closed surface. Remove two open disks from $S$ to obtain a surface with two boundary circles. Glue the two ends of the cylinder $S^{1}\\times[0,1]$ to the respective boundary circles. The resulting closed surface is obtained by **adding a handle** to $S$. Equivalently, adding a handle means taking the connected sum with a torus: $S\\mapsto S\\#T^{2}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4954370f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**For orientable surfaces**: Adding a handle increases the genus $g$ by $1$ and decreases the Euler characteristic by $2$. Closed orientable surfaces are exactly those obtained by adding $g$ handles to a sphere (classification theorem).\n\n**For nonorientable surfaces**: Adding a handle preserves nonorientability but increases the genus of the orientable part. The standard form for nonorientable surfaces uses crosscaps instead of handles.\n\n⚠ A handle removes two disks and attaches a cylinder, preserving orientability. A crosscap removes one disk and attaches a Möbius band, producing a nonorientable surface.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#04f9461a'
    },
  },
  'def.crosscap': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $S$ be a closed surface. Remove an open disk from $S$ and identify antipodal points on the boundary circle ($x\\sim -x$). The resulting closed surface is obtained by **adding a crosscap** to $S$. Equivalently, adding a crosscap means taking the connected sum with a projective plane: $S\\mapsto S\\#\\mathbb{R}P^{2}$. It is also equivalent to gluing a Möbius band along the boundary of the removed disk.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#374f38c4'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Effect**: Adding a crosscap decreases the Euler characteristic by $1$ and makes the surface nonorientable. Adding $k\\ge1$ crosscaps to a sphere yields all closed nonorientable surfaces (classification theorem).\n\n**Examples**: $k=1$ is the projective plane. $k=2$ is the Klein bottle.\n\n⚠ Antipodal identification changes the local structure near the boundary and gives a nonorientable surface. Gluing the disk back directly would restore the original surface.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d95ec3f4'
    },
  },
  'def.nonorientable-genus': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a closed nonorientable surface. Its **nonorientable genus** $k(M)$ is defined by requiring that $M$ be homeomorphic to the connected sum of $k$ projective planes and taking the least such integer $k\\ge 1$. Equivalently, $k(M)$ is the number of crosscaps in standard form, and the Euler characteristic satisfies $\\chi(M)=2-k(M)$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#27deb8d3'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why not use $\\chi=2-k$ directly as the definition**: That would presuppose the classification theorem (every closed nonorientable surface is homeomorphic to a standard form). Defining it as the minimum number of projective planes in a connected sum is independent of classification, following the same idea as the homological definition in `def.genus`.\n\n**Small values**: $k=1$ the projective plane. $k=2$ the Klein bottle. $k=3$ is the surface with three crosscaps.\n\n**Distinction from orientable genus**: In the orientable case, $\\chi=2-2g$. In the nonorientable case, $\\chi=2-k$. The same $\\chi$ gives different counts of holes in the two classes of surfaces, so the two notions must be distinguished.\n\n**Homology**: $H_{1}(M;\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}^{k-1}\\oplus\\mathbb{Z}/2$ ($k\\ge1$), with the torsion recording nonorientability.\n\n⚠ There is no simple conversion between $k$ and $g$. Taking the orientation double cover of a nonorientable surface gives the orientable genus $g=k-1$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#647a93b0'
    },
  },
  'def.closed-surface': {
    statement: {
      en: "A **2-dimensional topological manifold** $S$ (see `def.topological-manifold`) is called a **closed surface** if\n\n- $S$ is **compact**;\n- $S$ is **connected**;\n- $S$ **has no boundary** (each point has a neighborhood modeled on an open subset of $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$, with no points of half-plane type).\n\nEquivalently, $S$ is a compact, connected, Hausdorff, second-countable space without boundary that is locally homeomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4b02e102'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Each of the three conditions has a role, and dropping any one changes the classification theorem**:\n\n- Dropping connectedness, $\\implies$ becomes a **disjoint union** of finitely many closed surfaces, and the classification theorem applies to each component.\n\n- Dropping compactness, $\\implies$ can have infinite genus or infinitely many holes. The classification becomes much more complicated and is outside the scope of this graph.\n\n- Dropping the requirement of no boundary, $\\implies$ becomes a compact surface with boundary. Capping the boundary components with disks returns to the closed case.\n\n⭐ **Distinctions from three other notions of surface** (the reason for this node):\n\n`def.regular-surface` and `def.orientable-surface` concern subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ covered by parametrizations (**extrinsic**, with a metric and curvature). A `def.riemann-surface` is a one-dimensional **complex** manifold. This node concerns **topology alone**, with no metric, complex structure, or presumed embedding in Euclidean space.\n\n⭐ Basic examples: the sphere $S^{2}$, the torus $T^{2}$ (genus 1), the projective plane $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ (nonorientable), and the Klein bottle (nonorientable). ⚠ All four **already have nodes**: `ex.sphere`, `ex.torus`, `ex.projective-plane`, and `ex.klein-bottle`.\n\n📌 The genus in `def.genus` is defined only for **orientable** closed surfaces. The nonorientable case uses a different notion ($\\chi = 2 - k$).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5b889b98'
    },
  },
  'def.triangulation': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X$ be a topological space. A **triangulation** of $X$ is a pair $(K, h)$:\n\n- $K$ is a **simplicial complex** (`def.simplicial-complex`), and $|K|$ is its geometric realization;\n- $h : |K| \\to X$ is a **homeomorphism**.\n\nIf such a $(K, h)$ exists, then $X$ is **triangulable**. The image of each simplex under $h$ is called a **face** of $X$\n(a vertex, edge, or triangle). Thus $X$ is expressed as a union of triangles, with any two triangles that meet intersecting along a **whole edge** or at a vertex.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#896d11ff'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠️ A triangulation consists of **combinatorial data and a homeomorphism placing those data in the space**. Both are part of the triangulation.\n\nThus triangulability of $X$ is a topological property, whereas the form of $K$ is combinatorial data. The two must be distinguished.\n\n⚠️ Triangulations are highly nonunique. The same $S^{2}$ admits infinitely many genuinely different triangulations (a tetrahedron, an octahedron, arbitrary subdivisions, and so on).\n\nAny quantity defined using a triangulation therefore requires a proof of **independence from the triangulation**. That is what the result on $\\chi$ establishes (`thm.euler-characteristic` together with `thm.simplicial-approximation`).\n\n⭐ One-way implication: The realization of a simplicial complex is always triangulable (take the identity), but **some topological spaces are not triangulable**, as discussed in the next section.\n\n📌 The definition does not require $K$ to be finite. A triangulation of a compact space is automatically finite, while noncompact spaces (such as open surfaces) require **locally finite** triangulations.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2290a327'
    },
  },
  'def.topological-orientability': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a $n$-dimensional topological manifold ($n\\ge 1$). Call $M$ **orientable** if there is a collection of charts $(U_{\\alpha},\\varphi_{\\alpha})$ covering $M$ such that the transition maps between any two charts,\n\n$$\\varphi_{\\beta}\\circ\\varphi_{\\alpha}^{-1}:\\ \\varphi_{\\alpha}(U_{\\alpha}\\cap U_{\\beta})\\longrightarrow \\varphi_{\\beta}(U_{\\alpha}\\cap U_{\\beta})$$\n\nare all **orientation-preserving** (give $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ its standard orientation; see the notes for a precise definition of orientation preservation). Such an atlas is called an **oriented atlas**. If no oriented atlas exists, $M$ is **nonorientable**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#79e9eddd'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**1. What orientation preservation means.** The standard orientation of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is given by the standard generator of local homology. For $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $H_{n}(\\mathbb{R}^{n},\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\setminus\\{x\\};\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$, choose a generator as the positive orientation. Let $h:V\\to W$ be a homeomorphism between two open subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, and let $x\\in V$. We say $h$ is **orientation-preserving** at $x$ if the induced isomorphism $H_{n}(V,V\\setminus\\{x\\})\\to H_{n}(W,W\\setminus\\{h(x)\\})$ sends the standard generator to the standard generator. Otherwise it is **orientation-reversing**. If $V$ is connected, preserving orientation at one point implies preserving it everywhere (the sign on local homology varies continuously and can take only two values). For $n=1$ this is the usual notion of being increasing, and for $n=2$ it is the usual notion of an orientation-preserving homeomorphism.\n\n**2. The orientation double cover.** Let $\\widehat{M}$ be the set of all pairs $(x,\\mathfrak{o}_{x})$, where $x\\in M$ and $\\mathfrak{o}_{x}$ is a local orientation of $T_{x}M$, obtained by pulling back the standard orientation given by a chart around $x$ from $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$. Two charts give the same $\\mathfrak{o}_{x}$ if and only if the transition map preserves orientation at $x$. The charts identify $\\widehat{M}$ locally with $U\\times\\{\\pm1\\}$, giving its topology. The projection $p:\\widehat{M}\\to M$ is a two-sheeted covering called the **orientation double cover**. $M$ is orientable $\\iff$ $p$ is trivial $\\iff$ $p$ has a continuous section $\\iff$ $\\widehat{M}$ has exactly two connected components (when $M$ is connected). In the nonorientable case, $\\widehat{M}$ is connected and $\\mathbb{Z}/2$ acts freely on it, without fixed points.\n\n**3. Homological characterization.** Let $M$ be a closed connected $n$-manifold. For any $x$, $H_{n}(M,M\\setminus\\{x\\};\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$. The image of the natural map $H_{n}(M;\\mathbb{Z})\\to H_{n}(M,M\\setminus\\{x\\};\\mathbb{Z})$ can only be $0$ or $\\mathbb{Z}$ (Hatcher §3.3). $M$ is orientable $\\iff$ this image is $\\mathbb{Z}$ (equivalently, there is a **fundamental class** $[M]\\in H_{n}(M;\\mathbb{Z})$ giving a local orientation at each $x$). In the nonorientable case, $H_{n}(M;\\mathbb{Z})=0$. Proof idea: Use the transition functions of the orientation double cover to glue a constant sheaf of local homology coefficients. Orientability is precisely the triviality of this local system. The existence of a fundamental class is dual to the existence of a section.\n\n**4. The surface case (the main application here).** For a closed surface $S$, $S$ is orientable $\\iff$ $S$ contains no Möbius band as a subspace $\\iff$ $S$ admits a triangulation in which every $2$-simplex can be **coherently oriented** (the two sides of each interior edge induce opposite orientations on it, as used in Lemma D of `prop.eulerchar-genus`) $\\iff$ $H_{2}(S;\\mathbb{Z})\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$. The equivalence between a coherently oriented triangulation and an oriented atlas is the step before Lemma D: put each simplex in a chart and glue using the orientation-preserving transition maps.\n\n**5. Examples.** $S^{2}$, $T^{2}$, and all orientable closed surfaces are orientable. $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$, the Klein bottle, and the Möbius band are nonorientable. The orientation double cover of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ is $S^{2}$ (with the antipodal action), the orientation double cover of the Klein bottle is $T^{2}$, and that of the Möbius band is an annulus. These examples show that in the nonorientable case, local orientations exist but cannot be chosen consistently over the whole space.\n\n**6. Relation to the smooth definition.** Orientability for smooth manifolds is the smooth version, with transition maps of positive Jacobian determinant. Smooth orientability implies topological orientability because the smooth atlas is an oriented atlas. Conversely, for a topologically orientable smooth manifold, a partition of unity glues local $n$-forms into a nowhere-zero $n$-form (see the node on volume forms and orientability in this cluster). The definitions therefore agree on smooth manifolds. The extrinsic characterization of orientability for surfaces is the version in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$, which requires a continuous unit normal field. It applies only to surfaces that embed in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ (for example, $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ does not embed there and is outside its scope). The topological definition applies to all topological surfaces without requiring smoothness.\n\n**7. Applications.** (1) Lemma D of `prop.eulerchar-genus` uses an oriented atlas to orient the $2$-simplices of a triangulation coherently, giving $H_{2}\\cong\\mathbb{Z}$. (2) The nonorientable case of Radó's theorem uses the orientation double cover $\\widehat{S}\\to S$ (a free $\\mathbb{Z}/2$-action with no branch points). Apply the orientable case to $\\widehat{S}$, then take an equivariant refinement so that the quotient is also a triangulation (the equivariant triangulation lemma has no node here). (3) In the classification theorem for surfaces, orientability is a homeomorphism invariant and distinguishes the standard forms together with $\\chi$.\n\n⚠ **Two common points of confusion.** (1) Nonorientable surfaces can be triangulable. Radó's theorem gives triangulations of all compact surfaces, and $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ has one obtained by identifying antipodal points of a regular icosahedron. (2) The orientation double cover is a free two-sheeted covering without branch points. The local model $z\\mapsto z^{2}$ with branching belongs to the branched covering associated with a meromorphic function (the complex-analytic approach in `thm.rado`).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fef377cb'
    },
  },
  'ex.sphere': {
    statement: {
      en: "$$S^{2} := \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3} : \\lVert x \\rVert = 1 \\,\\}$$\n\nWith the subspace topology, this is a **closed surface** (`def.closed-surface`). Its genus is $g = 0$ and its Euler characteristic is $\\chi(S^{2}) = 2$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f6e799a5'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **It is the first standard form in the classification theorem** and the identity for connected sum: $S^{2} \\# M \\cong M$ (see the notes in `def.connected-sum`).\n\n⭐ **A cover by two charts**: Stereographic projection gives two charts, one omitting the north pole and the other the south pole. The transition map is of the type $x \\mapsto x/\\lVert x\\rVert^{2}$, which shows directly that $S^{2}$ is a smooth two-dimensional topological manifold.\n\n⚠ **$S^{2}$ and $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ are not homeomorphic** because only one is compact, although deleting a point gives a homeomorphism. This is one reason compactness is essential in the definition of a closed surface.\n\n📌 $H_{0} = \\mathbb{Z}$, $H_{1} = 0$, and $H_{2} = \\mathbb{Z}$, so $\\chi = 1 - 0 + 1 = 2$, in agreement with $2 - 2g$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b2b7b0f3'
    },
  },
  'ex.torus': {
    statement: {
      en: "$$T^{2} := S^{1} \\times S^{1} ,$$\n\nWith the product topology, this is a **closed orientable surface** of genus $g = 1$ and Euler characteristic $\\chi(T^{2}) = 0$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#01692568'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Three equivalent descriptions**: (1) $S^{1} \\times S^{1}$. (2) A square with opposite sides identified (the quotient of $[0,1]^{2}$ by $(0,t)\\sim(1,t)$ and $(s,0)\\sim(s,1)$). (3) $\\mathbb{R}^{2}/\\mathbb{Z}^{2}$. The third is the most convenient for computations. It is the plane modulo integer translations, with fundamental group $\\mathbb{Z}^{2}$.\n\n⭐ **Computing $\\chi = 0$**: The complex obtained by gluing the square has $V = 1$, $E = 2$, and $F = 1$, so $\\chi = 1 - 2 + 1 = 0$, in agreement with $2 - 2g = 0$.\n\n⭐ **It is the surface in the familiar comparison of a coffee cup and a doughnut**. Both are homeomorphic to $T^{2}$ (each has one hole), so they are topologically indistinguishable. Their **geometric shapes** as embedded in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ concern parametrized surfaces in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$.\n\n⚠ **$T^{2}$ cannot embed in $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$**. It is compact, two-dimensional, and without boundary, so an embedding in the plane would give a compact open subset of the plane. Only the empty set is both. A drawing of the torus on paper is therefore a **projection**.\n\n📌 $H_{0} = \\mathbb{Z}$, $H_{1} = \\mathbb{Z}^{2}$, $H_{2} = \\mathbb{Z}$, $\\chi = 1 - 2 + 1 = 0$. The rank of $H_{1}$ is **2**, and the genus is **1**, illustrating the division by 2 in `def.genus`.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d30f59f1'
    },
  },
  'ex.projective-plane': {
    statement: {
      en: "$$\\mathbb{RP}^{2} := S^{2} \\big/ (x \\sim -x) ,$$\n\nWith the quotient topology from antipodal identification, this is a **closed nonorientable surface** of nonorientable genus $k = 1$ and Euler characteristic $\\chi(\\mathbb{RP}^{2}) = 1$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4a3b9d54'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Three equivalent descriptions**: (1) A sphere with antipodal points identified. (2) A disk with antipodal boundary points identified. (3) A square with opposite sides glued in **reverse orientation** ($T^{2}$ has same-orientation gluing, so reversing orientation makes the difference).\n\n⭐ **Computing $\\chi = 1$**: Start with a disk ($\\chi = 1$). Identifying antipodal boundary points amounts to attaching a half-twisted band, which eliminates the boundary. The full circle of $V$ becomes one point, and $E$ becomes one closed curve. Thus $\\chi = 1 - 1 + 1 = 1$, in agreement with $2 - k = 1$.\n\n⭐ **Why it is nonorientable**: After going once around a line in $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$, the front becomes the back. It is a **capped Möbius band**: glue a disk to the boundary circle of a Möbius band to obtain $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$.\n\n⚠ **$\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ cannot embed in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$** (no compact nonorientable surface without boundary embeds in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$; an embedding requires going to $\\mathbb{R}^{4}$). This is a topological obstruction. The familiar crosscap picture shows an **immersion**.\n\n📌 $H_{1}(\\mathbb{RP}^{2};\\mathbb{Z}) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2$ (pure torsion, with free rank 0) and $H_{2} = 0$, so $\\chi = 1 - 0 + 0 = 1$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#37990f1a'
    },
  },
  'ex.klein-bottle': {
    statement: {
      en: "The Klein bottle $K$ is $\\mathbb{RP}^{2} \\# \\mathbb{RP}^{2}$ (the connected sum of two projective planes). It is also the quotient of a square obtained by gluing one pair of opposite sides in the same direction and the other pair in opposite directions. It is a **closed nonorientable surface** with nonorientable genus $k = 2$ and $\\chi(K) = 0$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5884c956'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Two equivalent descriptions**: (1) Glue one pair of opposite sides of a square in the **same direction** (as in $T^{2}$) and the other pair in **opposite directions**. (2) Glue two Möbius bands along their boundaries. Both constructions give the same surface (the equivalence is stated here without proof).\n\n⭐ **$\\chi = 0$**: $2 - k = 2 - 2 = 0$. The **torus** has the same value, so the information $\\chi = 0$ alone does not distinguish the Klein bottle. Orientability is also needed. The two surfaces are standard examples of the same $\\chi$ with different topological types.\n\n⚠ **It still cannot embed in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$**. No compact nonorientable surface without boundary embeds in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. The familiar glass model whose neck passes through the body is an **immersion** with self-intersection.\n\n📌 $H_{1}(K;\\mathbb{Z}) \\cong \\mathbb{Z} \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}/2$ and $H_{2} = 0$, so $\\chi = 1 - 1 + 0 = 0$.\n\n⚠ The Klein bottle is obtained by gluing two **Möbius bands** along their boundaries (`def.mobius-band`).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#21192e1f'
    },
  },
  'def.mobius-band': {
    statement: {
      en: "On the square $[0,1] \\times [0,1]$, take the equivalence relation that glues the left and right sides in **opposite directions**, namely\n\n$$(0, t) \\sim (1, 1 - t), \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 1.$$\n\nThe quotient space $M := ([0,1]\\times[0,1])/\\sim$ is called the **Möbius band**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7612fa06'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Comparison with the cylinder**: The cylinder glues the two sides in the **same direction**, $(0, t) \\sim (1, t)$. The Möbius band reverses one end before gluing ($t \\mapsto 1 - t$). This single change gives very different results.\n\n⭐ **Nonorientability**: After one circuit along the centerline $t = 1/2$, the normal vector reverses direction, so the Möbius band **admits no orientation**. It is also a standard building block for nonorientable surfaces: $M \\cup_{\\partial} M \\cong$ the Klein bottle (see `ex.klein-bottle`).\n\n**The boundary is one circle**: $\\partial M \\cong S^{1}$ (**one** circle, whereas the cylinder has two). The subspace $\\partial M$ is connected, which also gives an intuitive view of nonorientability.\n\n⚠ **It cannot embed in the plane**. There is no Möbius band in $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$. It can embed **without self-intersection** in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$, as a strip of paper given a half twist and glued. The picture that requires self-intersection is an immersion of $\\mathbb{RP}^{2}$. The realization in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is an example of a **nonorientable surface** (compare `def.orientable-surface`).\n\n📌 **Euler characteristic**: $M$ is homotopy equivalent to the cylinder (both are homotopy equivalent to $S^{1}$), so $\\chi(M) = \\chi(S^{1}) = 0$. Homotopy invariants cannot detect the twist, while orientability can.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9edaa587'
    },
  },
  'def.branched-cover': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X, Y$ be a compact Riemann surface (`def.riemann-surface`), and let $p : Y \\to X$ be a **nonconstant holomorphic** map (`def.holomorphic`). Then\n\n- There is a finite set $B \\subset X$ such that $p$ restricts to a **covering map** on $Y \\setminus p^{-1}(B) \\to X \\setminus B$ (`def.covering-space`);\n- The number of sheets $d$ (independent of the point, by the sum identity for ramification indices below) is called the **degree** of $p$;\n- $B$ is called the **branch locus**, the points of $p^{-1}(B)$ are called **ramification points**, and $p$ is called a **branched covering** of $X$ of degree $d$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4f244fa9'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Why compactness and holomorphicity are needed for this definition**: Compactness ensures a finite number of sheets and a finite $B$. Without compactness, one point can have infinitely many preimages and branch points can accumulate.\n\nHolomorphicity (the open mapping theorem and isolation of zeros) ensures that the only local model is $z \\mapsto z^{e}$. For purely topological branched coverings, finitely many sheets and a finite branch locus must be imposed as additional hypotheses.\n\n⚠️ A branched covering **becomes a covering map upon removal of** $B$. The most common example is\n\n\n\n$$p : \\mathbb{P}^{1} \\to \\mathbb{P}^{1}, \\qquad z \\mapsto z^{2}$$\n\n\n\nThis is a branched covering of degree $2$ with branch points $0$ and $\\infty$ (at these points it is $2$-to-$1$), and everywhere else it is $2$-to-$1$.\n\n📌 In brief, a branched covering is a finite-sheeted covering $+$ finitely many points where ramification is allowed.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#701f123b'
    },
  },
  'prop.abtop-to-abcond': {
    statement: {
      en: "There is a **faithful** functor that **preserves all limits**\n\n$$\\mathbf{AbTop} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{AbCond}, \\qquad M \\longmapsto \\underline{M}.$$\n\nand is **fully faithful** on **compactly generated** abelian groups.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bafe3c51'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Construction**: A topological abelian group $M$ gives the condensed abelian group\n\n\n\n$$\\underline{M} : S \\longmapsto C(S, M),$$\n\n\n\nnamely the set of continuous maps from a compact Hausdorff space $S$ to $M$, with pointwise addition. Since $M$ is a topological abelian group, $C(S, M)$ is an abelian group under pointwise addition. This defines an abelian-group-valued presheaf. The sheaf condition is gluing continuous maps, which holds.\n\n**Preservation of all limits** follows from the theorem that $\\mathbf{Top} \\to \\mathrm{Cond}$ preserves finite products. Limits of condensed objects are computed pointwise, and computing limits of continuous maps in $\\mathbf{Top}$ agrees with computing them pointwise.\n\n⭐ **Application**: Transfer a topological problem to condensed abelian groups, compute there using homological algebra (long exact sequences and derived functors), and transfer the result back. **Some information is lost**. The functor is faithful and need not be full, so isomorphism as condensed objects is weaker than topological isomorphism.\n\n**Proof (following the text, formally from the theorem on topological spaces and condensed sets).** For a topological abelian group $M$, write $\\underline{M}(S) := C(S, M)$ and use pointwise addition to obtain an object of $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ ✓ (the sheaf condition is gluing continuous maps and holds pointwise).\n- **Faithfulness**: If $f \\ne g : M \\to N$, choose a point $m$ such that $f(m)\\ne g(m)$. Then on $\\underline{M}(\\bullet)$, the maps $f,g$ already differ ✓ (the one-point test object $\\bullet$ suffices).\n- **Preservation of all limits**: Limits on the condensed side are computed objectwise, and $C\\bigl(S, \\varprojlim_{i} M_{i}\\bigr) = \\varprojlim_{i} C(S, M_{i})$. For limits of topological abelian groups (products, kernels, and equalizers), continuity of a map into the limit is determined by continuity of its components ✓.\n\nThe objectwise limit is still a sheaf, hence is the limit in $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ ✓.\n- **Full faithfulness on compactly generated groups**: Apply $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Cond}}(\\underline{X}, \\underline{Y}) = C(X, Y)$ in the compactly generated case (see the function-space characterization of internal Hom), take $X, Y$ to be compactly generated topological abelian groups, and impose the homomorphism condition. ∎\n⚠️ **No adjoint in the reverse direction**: The functor $X \\mapsto X(\\bullet)$ does not preserve products ($\\mathrm{CGWH}$ is not closed under arbitrary products), so there is no readily available adjoint to this embedding. This is also the technical reason that isomorphism as condensed objects is weaker.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2b15f25b'
    },
  },
  'prop.exact-sequence-topo-ab': {
    statement: {
      en: "Suppose\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow M' \\xrightarrow{\\;u\\;} M \\xrightarrow{\\;v\\;} M'' \\longrightarrow 0$$\n\nis an **exact sequence** of topological abelian groups, with $M''$ **weak Hausdorff**, and assume the following:\n\n**(Compact lifting condition)** For every compact Hausdorff $K'' \\subseteq M''$, there exists a compact Hausdorff $K \\subseteq M$ such that $K'' \\subseteq v(K)$.\n\nThen the sequence of condensed abelian groups\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow \\underline{M'} \\xrightarrow{\\;\\underline{u}\\;} \\underline{M} \\xrightarrow{\\;\\underline{v}\\;} \\underline{M''} \\longrightarrow 0$$\n\nis also exact.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0c0a85b1'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "Left exactness is automatic because the functor in the previous proposition preserves all limits. At the right end, $\\underline{v}$ must be an epimorphism. Epimorphisms of condensed objects are detected by pointwise surjectivity on free compact Hausdorff spaces, so this reduces to the compact lifting condition.\n\n📌 **Local compactness ensures the compact lifting condition**. Thus an entire exact sequence of locally compact abelian groups can be transferred.\n\n**Proof.**\n- **Left exactness**: The functor $M\\mapsto\\underline{M}$ preserves all limits by the previous proposition, so $0\\to\\underline{M'}\\to\\underline{M}$ is exact and $\\underline u$ is injective ✓.\n- **Image equals kernel in the middle**: $v\\circ u = 0$ holds pointwise, and the reverse inclusion $v(m) = 0 \\implies m\\in u(M')$ follows pointwise from the exact sequence of topological groups ✓.\n- **Surjectivity at the right end**: By the epimorphism criterion (pointwise surjectivity on free compact Hausdorff objects), take a free $F$ and $y : F \\to M''$.\n\n$y(F)$ is a **compact Hausdorff** subset of $M''$ ($F$ is compact and $M''$ is weak Hausdorff, so $y(F)$ is closed and Hausdorff).\n\nBy the **compact lifting condition**, choose a compact Hausdorff $K\\subseteq M$ such that $y(F)\\subseteq v(K)$.\n\nChoose a preimage at each point to obtain a map $F\\to K\\to M$.\n\nContinuity follows because $v$ is a quotient map on the compact pieces (in the exact sequence of topological groups, $v$ is a quotient map and remains one when restricted to $K$ and $y(F)$).\n\nThus $y$ lifts along the map, and $\\underline v(F)$ is surjective ✓.\n\n∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da3508d5'
    },
  },
  'thm.pontryagin-van-kampen': {
    statement: {
      en: "The functor\n\n$$M \\longmapsto \\widehat{M} := C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, \\mathbb{T}),$$\n\nis an **autoequivalence** of the category of **locally compact Hausdorff abelian groups** and is a self-adjoint equivalence.\n\nIn particular, $\\widehat{M}$ is again locally compact Hausdorff, and **taking the dual twice recovers the original group**: $\\widehat{\\widehat{M}} = M$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#403dc26a'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**What self-adjoint equivalence means**: $M \\mapsto \\widehat{M}$ is both left and right adjoint to $M \\mapsto \\widehat{M}$ (duality is adjoint to itself, with the pairing given by the evaluation map $M \\times \\widehat{M} \\to \\mathbb{T}$).\n\n**Duality carries over entire exact sequences**: If $0 \\to M' \\to M \\to M'' \\to 0$ is a (strict) exact sequence of locally compact abelian groups, then its dual $0 \\to \\widehat{M''} \\to \\widehat{M} \\to \\widehat{M'} \\to 0$ is still exact, with **all arrows reversed**.\n\nThe equivalence preserves structure, so one can solve a group-theoretic problem on the dual side and translate the result back.\n\n**Some standard correspondences**: $\\widehat{\\mathbb{R}} \\cong \\mathbb{R}$ (Fourier transform), and $\\widehat{\\mathbb{Z}} \\cong \\mathbb{T}$ and $\\widehat{\\mathbb{T}} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$ (interchanged).\n\nFor finite-dimensional real Banach spaces, the dual reduces to the usual linear dual (a real Banach space is locally compact $\\iff$ it is finite-dimensional).\n\n⭐ **This is the prototype for the theory of condensed abelian groups**. Replacing $\\mathbb{T}$ with other test objects and local compactness with other hypotheses gives various dualities (Tannaka, Gelfand, and others). Condensed mathematics moves $\\mathbb{T}$ to sheaves on CHaus.\n\n**Proof (combining three cases).**\n- **$M$ discrete**: Classical Pontryagin duality.\n\nFirst take a surjection $\\mathbb{Z}^{(I)} \\twoheadrightarrow M$. Dualizing gives a closed embedding $\\widehat M \\hookrightarrow \\mathbb{T}^{I}$, so $\\widehat M$ is compact.\n\nVerify the evaluation map $M \\to \\widehat{\\widehat M}$ by elementary computation for finite and finitely generated groups. Then use that $M$ is the filtered colimit of its finitely generated subgroups, duality turns colimits into inverse limits, and both sides are exact, to obtain the isomorphism by passing to the limit ✓.\n- **$M$ compact**: $\\widehat M$ is discrete (see the interchange of discrete and compact Hausdorff groups). For compact groups, **characters separate points** (Pontryagin's classical argument uses Peter–Weyl or finite-dimensional representation theory to give a separating family of characters of a compact abelian group). Thus the evaluation map $M \\to \\widehat{\\widehat M}$ is injective.\n\nThen use the preceding case and the fact that the dual of the compact group $M$ is discrete to complete the verification of injectivity and surjectivity ✓.\n- **$M$ locally compact in general**: By the structure theorem, $M$ contains an open subgroup $V\\oplus K$ (with $V$ a finite-dimensional real vector space and $K$ compact), and the quotient $D$ is discrete. The duality equivalence is known for $V$, $K$, and $D$ individually. The three are related by an exact sequence, and duality gives an exact sequence with the arrows reversed. The five lemma makes the middle evaluation map an isomorphism ✓.\n- **Self-adjointness**: The pairing $M\\times\\widehat M \\to \\mathbb{T}$ (evaluation) gives the adjunction $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\widehat M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}(M, \\widehat N)$. Replace $M$ with $\\widehat M$ and apply the equivalence twice ✓.\n\n∎\n**Standard reference**: Morris, *Pontryagin Duality and the Structure of Locally Compact Abelian Groups*.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d0be1d53'
    },
  },
  'def.topo-ab-group': {
    statement: {
      en: "A **topological abelian group** $(M, +, \\tau)$ is an **abelian group** $M$ equipped with a topology $\\tau$ for which the group operations are continuous:\n\n$$+ : M \\times M \\longrightarrow M, \\qquad - : M \\longrightarrow M.$$\n\nThe **continuous homomorphisms** between two topological abelian groups are denoted by\n\n$$C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N) \\;\\subseteq\\; C(M, N),$$\n\nThe right-hand side has the **compact-open topology**, and the left-hand side has the **subspace topology**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#288e7748'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **Why use the notation $C_{\\mathbb{Z}}$**: $\\mathbb{Z}$ emphasizes that these continuous maps are homomorphisms of $\\mathbb{Z}$-modules (that is, abelian groups).\n\n**Relation to internal Hom**: When $M$ is **compactly generated**, $C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N) = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)$. See the next proposition. This is another consequence of having enough test objects.\n\n⚠️ **Two common pitfalls**:\n\n\n\n- The functor in $\\underline{M} \\mapsto M$ **has no obvious adjoint**, because $X \\mapsto X(\\bullet)$ (taking the underlying topological space) **does not preserve products**;\n- Even if $M$ is a topological abelian group, $kM$ **need not be** a topological abelian group, because compactly generated spaces are not closed under products taken in topological spaces.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c30bec7b'
    },
  },
  'prop.continuous-hom-condensed': {
    statement: {
      en: "If $M, N$ are two topological abelian groups and $M$ is **compactly generated**, then there is a natural isomorphism of condensed abelian groups $C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#15322b74'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ The role of the hypothesis \"$M$ is compactly generated\": only then does $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\underline{M}, -)$ really measure continuous homomorphisms; on a general topological abelian group the condensed Hom is **larger** than the continuous homs (one can glue a map piecewise that is not continuous globally).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2abdce6f'
    },
  },
  'prop.lc-ab-preabelian': {
    statement: {
      en: "The category of locally compact Hausdorff abelian groups is **preabelian** but **not abelian**.\n\nExplicitly, the kernel is the usual kernel with the subspace topology. The cokernel is the quotient group $N/\\overline{f(M)}$ with the quotient topology (one must quotient by the **closure** to obtain a Hausdorff quotient). Morphisms **need not be strict**. For example,\n\n$$\\mathrm{id} : \\mathbb{R}^{\\mathrm{disc}} \\longrightarrow \\mathbb{R}$$\n\nis not strict, with the discrete topology on one copy of the group and the usual topology on the other.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#28251225'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ This shows that the category of topological abelian groups **lacks the structure needed** for homological algebra. This motivates passing to condensed abelian groups, which form a Grothendieck category with the requisite structure.\n\n**Why having kernels and cokernels is insufficient.** In an abelian category, every monomorphism is the kernel of some morphism. Equivalently, it is isomorphic to the kernel of its cokernel, or **strict**.\n\n$\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathrm{disc}} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a monomorphism (it is isomorphic to its image), but its cokernel is $0$. The entire image is quotiented out in the cokernel.\n\nThe kernel of the cokernel is therefore different from the original object, so strictness fails.\n\n**Proof.**\n- **Preabelian**: Finite products and finite coproducts exist (a product is the product group with the product topology, and a coproduct is the direct sum with the coproduct topology), as do kernels and cokernels. The kernel is the usual kernel with the **subspace topology**. The cokernel is $N/\\overline{f(M)}$ with the **quotient topology** ($f(M)$ need not be closed, so one must quotient by the **closure**. A quotient by a closed subgroup with the quotient topology is locally compact Hausdorff) ✓.\n- **Failure to be abelian**: Take $\\mathrm{id} : \\mathbb{R}^{\\mathrm{disc}} \\to \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nIt is continuous because the discrete topology is finer than the Euclidean topology, and it is injective, hence a monomorphism.\n\nIts **cokernel** is $\\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{R} = 0$.\n\nIn an abelian category every monomorphism is **strict**, meaning $f = \\ker(\\operatorname{coker} f)$. Here $\\ker(0) = \\mathbb{R}$, but $\\ker(0)$ carries the Euclidean topology and $\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathrm{disc}}$ does not. The two are **isomorphic but unequal** (they are not isomorphic as morphisms of topological groups) ✗.\n\n∎\n(Equivalently, $\\mathrm{id}$ is isomorphic to its image but is not the kernel of its cokernel, so the mono-epi factorization is not strict.)",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cbc0a55e'
    },
  },
  'def.pontryagin-dual': {
    statement: {
      en: "Write the **circle group** as\n\n$$\\mathbb{T} := \\{\\, z \\in \\mathbb{C} : |z| = 1 \\,\\}.$$\n\nThe **Pontryagin dual** of a topological abelian group $M$ is\n\n$$\\widehat{M} := C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, \\mathbb{T}),$$\n\nthat is, the group of all continuous homomorphisms from $M$ to $\\mathbb{T}$, equipped with the compact-open topology.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d3aa7746'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why $\\mathbb{T}$**: It is a **compact** $T_{1}$ group and the simplest group that can accommodate all characters. The dual group is therefore automatically compact (when $M$ is discrete). This is how duality reverses size.\n\n**Two basic examples** (both can be computed directly): $\\widehat{\\mathbb{Z}} \\cong \\mathbb{T}$, $\\widehat{\\mathbb{T}} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$. Duality exchanges the discrete group of integers and the compact circle group.\n\nThe third example $\\widehat{\\mathbb{R}} \\cong \\mathbb{R}$ comes from the Fourier transform: every continuous character of $y \\mapsto e^{2\\pi i xy}$ has this form.\n\n⭐ **Two independent propositions describe what duality exchanges**:\n\n\n\n- The exchange between discrete and compact Hausdorff groups concerns **topological properties**;\n- The correspondences between torsion and Stonean spaces, and between torsion-freeness and connectedness, concern **algebraic properties**.\n\n\n\nTogether these two dictionaries give the full picture of how topology encodes algebra. They also motivate the condensed mathematics approach of using compact Hausdorff spaces to probe other objects.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ad1173a9'
    },
  },
  'thm.lc-structure': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a **locally compact Hausdorff abelian group**. Then $M$ contains an **open** subgroup isomorphic to\n\n$$V \\oplus K,$$\n\nwhere $V$ is a **finite-dimensional real vector space** (viewed as an additive group) and $K$ is a **compact Hausdorff abelian group**.\n\nEquivalently, there is an exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow V \\oplus K \\longrightarrow M \\longrightarrow D \\longrightarrow 0,$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a **discrete** abelian group.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#23c132c8'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**How to read the theorem.** Locally compact Hausdorff abelian groups have three basic building blocks: **discrete groups**, **finite-dimensional real vector spaces**, and **compact Hausdorff groups**. The theorem says that every such group is an extension of $V \\oplus K$ by a discrete group.\n\n**Why $V$ must be finite-dimensional**: The additive group of a real vector space is locally compact $\\iff$ it is finite-dimensional. Infinite-dimensional real Banach spaces (such as $\\ell^{2}$) **fail to be locally compact** and fall outside the scope of the structure theorem.\n\n**What an open subgroup means**: $V \\oplus K \\subseteq M$ is an open subgroup, so the cokernel $D = M/(V\\oplus K)$ carries the quotient topology. Because $V \\oplus K$ is open, the quotient is **discrete**. Openness is what makes the quotient discrete.\n\n⭐ **This theorem drives Chapter 8.** It is used to show that higher Ext groups between locally compact abelian groups vanish. Since every object is built by extensions from three classes (discrete, finite-dimensional real Banach, and compact connected), it suffices to verify Ext vanishing for pairs from those classes. This reduces infinitely many kinds of groups to $3 \\times 3$ cases.\n\n**Proof (standard approach).**\n1. **Connected component**: $M^{0}$ (the connected component of the identity) is a closed subgroup. $M^0$ is a **connected locally compact abelian group**, and such groups are precisely those of the form $\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times K$ (with $K$ a compact connected abelian group). This is classical theory (the no-small-subgroups property and Lie theory separate the compact subgroup part of $M^{0}$ from the vector group part).\n2. **Totally disconnected quotient**: $M/M^{0}$ is a **totally disconnected** locally compact Hausdorff group. Such a group has a **compact open subgroup** (take a compact neighborhood of the identity and then the closure of the subgroup it generates).\n3. **Putting the pieces together**: Take $M_{1}$ to be the preimage of a compact open subgroup. Then $M_{1}$ is open and $M_{1}/M^{0}$ is compact. Thus $M_{1}$ is an extension of the compact group $K$ by $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$: $0 \\to K \\to M_{1} \\to \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\to 0$.\n4. **Splitting the extension**: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is a **vector group**, and an extension of a compact group by a vector group splits (average over the compact direction, or use a local section and compactness to globalize it). Hence $M_{1} \\cong \\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times K$ ✓.\n5. **Discrete quotient**: $M_{1}$ is open $\\implies$ $D := M/M_{1}$ is **discrete** in the quotient topology ✓, so $0\\to V\\oplus K\\to M\\to D\\to 0$ is exact. ∎\n**Standard reference**: Morris (as above).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3f4dd6a8'
    },
  },
  'prop.pontryagin-discrete-compact-duality': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a locally compact Hausdorff abelian group and let $\\widehat{M} = C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, \\mathbb{T})$ be its Pontryagin dual. Then\n\n- $M$ is **discrete** $\\iff$ $\\widehat{M}$ is **compact Hausdorff**;\n- $M$ is **compact Hausdorff** $\\iff$ $\\widehat{M}$ is **discrete**.\n\nThus the duality functor **exchanges** the categories of discrete abelian groups and compact Hausdorff abelian groups.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4dc89178'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why this holds.** $\\mathbb{T} = \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$ is compact: the continuous homomorphisms from the **discrete** group $M$ to the compact group $\\mathbb{T}$ form a compact space in the compact-open topology (the product $\\mathbb{T}^{M}$ is compact, and closed subsets of compact spaces are compact);\n\nconversely, when $\\widehat{M}$ is compact, $M = \\widehat{\\widehat{M}}$ is discrete.\n\n**Two examples**: $\\widehat{\\mathbb{Z}} \\cong \\mathbb{T}$ (discrete $\\to$ compact), $\\widehat{\\mathbb{T}} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$ (compact $\\to$ discrete).\n\n⭐ **This illustrates the origin of the idea of using compact Hausdorff spaces to probe other objects**: A discrete group can be turned into a compact Hausdorff space by duality. Taking quotients or subgroups of discrete groups then becomes taking closed subspaces or quotients on the compact side, turning these into topological problems.\n\n📌 This is **one half** of the Pontryagin duality dictionary (the other half concerns torsion and connectedness).\n\n**Proof.**\n- **$M$ discrete $\\implies \\widehat M$ compact**: Take a surjection $\\mathbb{Z}^{(I)} \\twoheadrightarrow M$. Duality turns the surjection into an **injection** (by precomposition), and its image is closed ($\\widehat M \\hookrightarrow \\mathbb{T}^{I}$ is a closed embedding).\n\n$\\mathbb{T}$ is compact Hausdorff, and $\\mathbb{T}^{I}$ is compact. Closed subsets of compact spaces are compact $\\implies$ $\\widehat M$ is compact ✓.\n- **$\\widehat M$ compact $\\implies M$ discrete**: By the duality theorem (a self-equivalence), $M \\cong \\widehat{\\widehat M}$. Since $\\widehat M$ is compact, the preceding direction (replacing $M$ with $\\widehat M$) gives the dual of the dual of $M$ ... A more direct attempt would be to start from the compact group $\\widehat M$ and infer compactness of its dual $\\widehat{\\widehat M} = M$ from the preceding direction. The required claim, however, is that the dual of a compact group is discrete. Take the upper half-plane $U\\subseteq\\mathbb{T}$ ($\\operatorname{Im} z > 0$), whose closure omits the identity. Then the image of any continuous homomorphism $\\widehat M \\to \\mathbb{T}$ lies in a connected component of some $\\mathbb{T}$. Since $\\widehat M$ is compact and characters separate points, $\\widehat{\\widehat M}$ has a discrete neighborhood at the origin, so $M = \\widehat{\\widehat M}$ is discrete ✓.\n- **Compact $\\implies$ discrete dual** is the same as the second direction above (duality is a self-equivalence, and its exchanges of discrete and compact groups are inverse to each other) ✓. ∎\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the forward compactness proof and the biduality theorem play different roles):\n- $M$ discrete $\\implies$ $\\widehat M$ compact Hausdorff: The characters form a closed subgroup of $\\mathbb T^M$, and the compact-open topology equals the topology of pointwise convergence. This uses Tychonoff's theorem, usually in the framework of the axiom of choice, and goes beyond unpacking the definition.\n- $M$ compact $\\implies$ $\\widehat M$ discrete: Take a small arc about the identity containing no nontrivial subgroup. Requiring a character to take values in that arc on the entire compact group leaves only the zero character. This uses the definition of the compact-open topology and the no-small-subgroups property of the circle group.\n- $\\widehat M$ compact $\\implies$ $M$ discrete and $\\widehat M$ discrete $\\implies$ $M$ compact: Apply the first two statements, respectively, and use the Pontryagin biduality isomorphism. Local compactness, the Hausdorff property, and commutativity are hypotheses of the duality theorem used here.\n- The upper semicircle used in the original note omits the identity and cannot give a neighborhood of the zero character. Use a small arc containing the identity. Both directions of the exchange between discreteness and compactness require proof. Circle-valued characters of a nonabelian group see only its abelianization and cannot recover the original group.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3f1fc2fa'
    },
  },
  'prop.pontryagin-torsion-stone': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a **discrete** abelian group and let $\\widehat{M} = C_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, \\mathbb{T})$ be its Pontryagin dual (by the other duality theorem, $\\widehat{M}$ is compact Hausdorff). Then\n\n- $M$ is **torsion** (every element has finite order) $\\iff$ $\\widehat{M}$ is a **Stonean** space (an extremally disconnected compact Hausdorff space);\n- $M$ is **torsion-free** $\\iff$ $\\widehat{M}$ is **connected**.\n\nMore generally, the torsion subgroup of $M$, namely $M_{\\mathrm{tors}}$, corresponds to the connected component of the dual, while $M/M_{\\mathrm{tors}}$ corresponds to the Stonean part of $\\widehat{M}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#995ea49c'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why torsion corresponds to extremal disconnectedness.** An element of order $n$, denoted by $x$, corresponds to an evaluation on $\\widehat{M}$ satisfying $n\\chi(x) = 0$. Its values lie in a finite subgroup $\\mathbb{Z}/n \\subseteq \\mathbb{T}$.\n\nFinite discrete spaces are **extremally disconnected**. Nondivisibility conditions split the whole dual space into clopen pieces.\n\n**Why torsion-freeness corresponds to connectedness.** Conversely, if $M$ is torsion-free (like $\\mathbb{Z}$), its dual $\\widehat{M}$ is connected. For example, $\\widehat{\\mathbb{Z}} = \\mathbb{T}$ is connected, whereas $\\widehat{\\mathbb{Z}/n} = \\mathbb{Z}/n$ is finite, discrete, and extremally disconnected.\n\n⭐ **Why this matters in condensed mathematics**: Stonean spaces are precisely the spaces whose condensed cohomology **always vanishes**, and extremal disconnectedness corresponds to torsion on the dual side. This gives a **purely algebraic** description of the starting theorem of §8.1, acyclicity on Stonean spaces.\n\n⚠️ Here $M$ is required to be **discrete**. For a general locally compact group, first apply the structure theorem to analyze the dual.\n\n**Proof.**\n- **Torsion $\\iff$ Stonean**: $M$ is torsion $\\iff$ $M = \\varinjlim_{i} M_{i}$ (with $M_{i}$ **finite**) $\\iff$ $\\widehat M = \\varprojlim_{i} \\widehat{M_{i}}$ (each $\\widehat{M_{i}}$ is again finite, and duality turns colimits into inverse limits) $\\iff$ $\\widehat M$ is a **profinite** space, that is, a Stone space (compact, Hausdorff, and totally disconnected);\n\nfor general (nontorsion) discrete groups, extremal disconnectedness corresponds to the same inverse limit structure built from finite pieces ✓.\n- **Torsion-free $\\iff$ connected**: Follow the chain of equivalences.\n$M$ fails to be torsion-free $\\iff$ there is an injection of a nontrivial **finite** subgroup $M' \\hookrightarrow M$ $\\iff$ there is a nontrivial finite quotient $\\widehat M \\twoheadrightarrow \\widehat{M'}$ (duality turns injections into surjections) $\\iff$ $\\widehat M$ is **disconnected** ✓.\n(The last step: a compact Hausdorff space is disconnected $\\iff$ it has a proper nonempty clopen subset $\\iff$ it admits a continuous surjection onto a nontrivial finite discrete space.) ∎\n⭐ Both correspondences, torsion subgroup $\\leftrightarrow$ connected component and torsion-free quotient $\\leftrightarrow$ Stonean part, can be read directly from these two chains.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (read Stonean in the original text as Stone. The two terms denote different properties):\n- Torsion $\\implies$ Stone dual: A discrete torsion abelian group is the filtered union of its finitely generated finite subgroups. Duality turns it into an inverse limit of finite groups, so it is compact Hausdorff and totally disconnected. This uses duality and inverse limit theory.\n- Stone dual $\\implies$ torsion: A compact totally disconnected group has a neighborhood basis of open subgroups. Every circle-valued character has open kernel by the no-small-subgroups property of the circle group, so its image is finite. Then use biduality to identify $M$.\n- Torsion $\\implies$ Stonean (extremally disconnected) fails: The dual of $M=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}\\mathbb Z/2$ is the Cantor group $\\prod_{n\\ge1}\\mathbb Z/2$, which is Stone and fails to be extremally disconnected. The original equivalence cannot be treated as proved by supplying additional hypotheses.\n- Torsion-free $\\iff$ connected dual: Use the connectedness criterion for compact abelian groups (no nontrivial finite continuous quotient) and the fact that duality exchanges finite quotients and finite subgroups. The criterion for continuous maps of general spaces cannot replace the criterion for group homomorphisms.\n- Both statements retain the hypothesis that $M$ is discrete. The precise correspondences are $(\\widehat M)^0\\cong\\widehat{M/M_{\\mathrm{tors}}}$ and $\\widehat M/(\\widehat M)^0\\cong\\widehat{M_{\\mathrm{tors}}}$. Torsion means that every element has finite order. Its topological characterization requires proof.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#afd032d7'
    },
  },
  'prop.abhaus-strict-exact': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $\\mathbf{AbHaus}$ be the category of **Hausdorff topological abelian groups** (with continuous homomorphisms as morphisms). Then\n\n1. For a continuous homomorphism $f : M \\to N$, the kernel is $\\ker f = f^{-1}(0)$ (with the subspace topology) and the cokernel is $\\operatorname{coker} f = N/\\overline{f(M)}$ (with the quotient topology);\n2. A continuous homomorphism is a kernel $\\iff$ it is a **homeomorphic embedding onto a closed subgroup**, and it is a cokernel $\\iff$ it is an **open surjection**;\n3. The sequence $0 \\to M' \\xrightarrow{\\varphi} M \\xrightarrow{\\psi} M'' \\to 0$ is **strictly exact** $\\iff$ it is exact as a sequence of abstract abelian groups, $\\varphi$ is a closed embedding, and $\\psi$ is an open surjection.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#683ec6e0'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ \"Strict\" is not decoration: for topological abelian groups \"exact\" and \"strictly exact\" are genuinely different — topological abelian groups are not an abelian category, and what is missing is exactly \"every morphism is determined by its kernel and cokernel\". Condensed mathematics repairs this layer by embedding topological groups into $\\mathrm{CondAb}$.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the universal property of kernel and cokernel also determines the topology):\n- Kernel $\\implies$ closed embedding: the kernel carries the subspace topology, and a Hausdorff target makes the zero point closed; conversely a closed embedding is the kernel of the quotient map $M\\to M/H$, and it must not be miscalled the equaliser of itself with zero.\n- Cokernel $\\implies$ open surjection: quotient by the closure of the image, and the quotient map of groups is open; conversely an open surjection gives a homeomorphism onto the quotient by the kernel, hence is the cokernel of its kernel — both need the universal property of the quotient topology.\n- Strictly exact $\\iff$ abstract exactness plus closed embeddings and open surjections: forward, expand the kernel–cokernel sequence and use the descriptions above; backward, recover the universal property via the two topological identifications, with no open mapping theorem.\n- Closure cannot be dropped: $\\mathbb Q\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R$ has dense but non-closed image in a Hausdorff group. Openness cannot be dropped either: the identity from $\\mathbb R$ with the discrete topology to the usual topology is a continuous surjection that is not open; the coarse-topology integer group of the original remark is not an object of this category.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9286d916'
    },
  },
  'prop.pontryagin-finite-dim-banach': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $V$ be a **finite-dimensional** real Banach space. Then its Pontryagin dual is the usual vector space dual:\n\n$$\\widehat{V} \\;\\cong\\; V^{\\ast} = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(V,\\ \\mathbb{R}).$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4a0bd0fb'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** Since $\\mathbb{T} = \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$ and $V$ is a finite-dimensional real vector space, hence **simply connected** ✓, every continuous homomorphism $V \\to \\mathbb{T}$ lifts uniquely to a continuous homomorphism $V \\to \\mathbb{R}$ ✓.\n\nNow observe that **a continuous additive map $V \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is automatically $\\mathbb{R}$-linear**. Additivity first gives $f(qv) = q f(v)$ for rational scalars ✓, and continuity then extends this to real scalars by approximation ✓.\n\nThus $\\widehat{V} \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(V, \\mathbb{R}) = V^{\\ast}$ ✓.\n\n∎\n\n📌 Finite dimensionality is essential: A real Banach space is **locally compact if and only if it is finite-dimensional**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6cece933'
    },
  },
  'def.semi-normed-ab-group': {
    statement: {
      en: "A **seminormed abelian group** is an abelian group $M$ equipped with a function $\\|\\cdot\\| : M \\to \\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}$ satisfying\n\n- $\\|0\\| = 0$;\n- $\\|s + t\\| \\le \\|s\\| + \\|t\\|$ (triangle inequality);\n- $\\|-s\\| = \\|s\\|$ (symmetry).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d1c1fbfe'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠️ **There is no requirement that $\\|s\\| = 0 \\implies s = 0$**. This is exactly what the prefix “semi” means. The elements of norm $0$ form a subgroup $M_{0} = \\{\\, s : \\|s\\| = 0 \\,\\}$.\n\n**Why start with seminorms.** Completion starts from a seminormed space. Before completing it, one first quotients out $M_{0}$. The definition of $K$-bounded exactness is most natural for **seminormed** groups, while the Banach setting gives the cleanest formulation. This is why both notions are retained.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#faa222d7'
    },
  },
  'def.banach-ab-group': {
    statement: {
      en: "A **Banach abelian group** is a seminormed abelian group $M$ satisfying\n\n- $\\|s\\| = 0 \\implies s = 0$ (**separation**, meaning that the seminorm is a norm);\n- $M$ is **complete** in the metric induced by the norm.\n\nEvery seminormed abelian group $M$ has a **Banach completion** obtained by first taking the Hausdorff quotient and then completing,\n\n$$M \\longmapsto \\widehat{M/M_{0}},\\qquad M_{0} = \\{\\, s : \\|s\\| = 0 \\,\\}.$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d46f4239'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**What this looks like in condensed mathematics.** For a Banach abelian group $M$, the sections $\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}[S], M)$ carry a supremum seminorm, so $M$ carries norm information as a condensed abelian group. All quantitative statements in §8.2 use this seminorm.\n\n⚠️ The norm here is on an **abelian group**. Every real Banach space is a Banach abelian group after forgetting scalar multiplication, and the converse fails. Many theorems in Chapter 8 use only the group structure.\n\n⭐ Taking the Hausdorff quotient and then completing is a **reflection** from the category of seminormed abelian groups onto the subcategory of Banach abelian groups. Quantitative properties such as $K$-bounded exactness pass through this reflection **with the same bounds**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c22c6f26'
    },
  },
  'def.k-bounded-exact': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M^{\\bullet}$ be a complex of **seminormed abelian groups**, and let $K \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The complex $M^{\\bullet}$ is **boundedly exact at $M^{n}$ with bound $K$** if\n\n$$\\forall s \\in M^{n},\\ \\forall \\varepsilon > 0,\\ \\exists s' \\in M^{n+1} :\\quad \\|s - d^{n+1}s'\\| \\le K\\,\\|d^{n}s\\| + \\varepsilon.$$\n\nIf $M^{\\bullet}$ is boundedly exact at every $M^{n}$ with bound $K$, it is called **$K$-bounded acyclic**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1d5a550e'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**How this differs from ordinary exactness.** Ordinary exactness says that every cycle is a boundary (an existence statement). In $K$-bounded exactness, the preimage $s'$ must exist and **satisfy a bound**. The error is at most $K\\|ds\\|$ plus an arbitrary $\\varepsilon$.\n\n**Why this stronger condition is needed.** In Banach and seminormed settings, knowing that a cycle is a boundary is insufficient because errors can accumulate when taking limits. The $K$-bounded exactness condition gives **control over countable constructions**, allowing later constructions involving geometrically decaying Cauchy sequences to converge.\n\n**What the $\\varepsilon$ term does**: The norm comparisons allow a margin so that $\\le$ still holds after taking limits. In practice, $\\varepsilon$ can be arbitrarily small.\n\n⭐ This is the **common language** of all the theorems in this section. Replace exactness with a quantitative condition involving a constant $K$, then prove that quantitative $\\implies$ qualitative.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#53dae9ae'
    },
  },
  'lem.k-bounded-completion': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M^{\\bullet}$ be a complex of seminormed abelian groups, and let $\\widehat{M}^{\\bullet}$ be its Banach completion (the completion after taking the Hausdorff quotient).\n\nThen $M^{\\bullet}$ is boundedly exact at $M^{n}$ with bound $K$ $\\iff$ $\\widehat{M}^{\\bullet}$ is boundedly exact at $\\widehat{M}^{n}$ with bound $K$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4dbfa1de'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Purpose**: This reduces the general seminormed case **to the Banach case**. Proving subsequent theorems for Banach groups therefore suffices for the general case.\n\n**Why it holds**.\n\nCompletion embeds $M^{n}$ as a **dense** subspace. The condition of $K$-bounded exactness involves only norm inequalities and an arbitrary choice of $\\varepsilon > 0$. Such conditions are **preserved in both directions** when passing to a dense subspace. Density gives sufficiently good approximations in $M^{n}$, and limits in the complete space can lie back in $\\widehat{M}^{\\bullet}$.\n\n**Proof (estimate $\\varepsilon/2$ in each direction).** First quotient out $M_{0}$ ($K$-bounded exactness is invariant under quotienting by the elements of norm zero). We may therefore assume that $M^{\\bullet} \\subseteq \\widehat{M}^{\\bullet}$ is an isometric embedding with dense image.\n- **$\\implies$**: Given $s \\in \\widehat{M}^{n}$ and $\\varepsilon>0$.\n\nBy density, choose $t \\in M^{n}$ such that $\\|s - t\\| \\le \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2(1+K\\|d^{n}\\|)}$;\n\nby the boundedness of $M^{\\bullet}$ with bound $K$, choose $s' \\in M^{n+1}$ such that $\\|t - ds'\\| \\le K\\|dt\\| + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}$.\n\nThus\n$$\\|s - ds'\\| \\le \\|s-t\\| + K\\|dt\\| + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2} = K\\|ds\\| + \\Bigl(\\|s-t\\| + K\\|ds - dt\\| + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\Bigr) \\le K\\|ds\\| + \\varepsilon .$$\n- **$\\Longleftarrow$**: Given $s \\in M^{n} \\subseteq \\widehat{M}^{n}$.\n\nBy the boundedness of $\\widehat{M}^{\\bullet}$ with bound $K$, choose $t' \\in \\widehat{M}^{n+1}$ such that $\\|s - dt'\\| \\le K\\|ds\\| + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}$;\n\nthen by density, choose $s' \\in M^{n+1}$ such that $\\|s' - t'\\| \\le \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2(1+\\|d^{n+1}\\|)}$.\n\nThus $\\|s - ds'\\| \\le K\\|ds\\| + \\varepsilon$.\n\n∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7a28b3e9'
    },
  },
  'prop.k-bounded-implies-exact': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M^{\\bullet}$ be a complex of **Banach** abelian groups. If it is boundedly exact at both $M^{n-1}$ and $M^{n}$ with bound $K$, **and $\\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$ is a closed subspace of $M^{n}$** (that is, $d^{n+1}$ has closed range), then it is **exact** at $M^{n}$ in the usual sense.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7adf267d'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**This is the central step from quantitative $\\implies$ qualitative, and it requires a closedness hypothesis.** Given a cycle $s \\in M^{n}$ (that is, $d^{n}s = 0$), we must show $s \\in \\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$.\n\n**Proof (two steps).** At $M^{n}$, bounded exactness with bound $K$ gives, for each $\\varepsilon > 0$, an element $s' \\in M^{n+1}$ such that $\\|s - d^{n+1}s'\\| \\le K\\|d^{n}s\\| + \\varepsilon = \\varepsilon$ (the last step uses $d^{n}s = 0$). Since $\\varepsilon$ is arbitrary, $s$ lies in the **closure** of $\\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$.\n\nBy **the assumption that $\\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$ is closed**, it equals its closure, so $s \\in \\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$. The reverse inclusion $\\operatorname{im} d^{n+1} \\subseteq \\ker d^{n}$ follows from $d \\circ d = 0$. Thus $\\ker d^{n} = \\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$, which is exactness. ∎\n\n⚠️ **The closed-range hypothesis is essential**: On $\\ell^{2}$, take $d(x_{j}) := (x_{j}/j)$. Then $\\operatorname{im} d$ is dense and nonclosed, and bounded exactness with bound $K$ can hold even when exactness fails. **Dense exactness $\\ne$ exactness**.\n\n⚠️ **Where the original proof goes wrong**: It tries to apply boundedness at $M^{n+1}$ with bound $K$ to $s'_{i+1} - s'_{i}$, obtaining $\\|s'_{i+1} - s'_{i}\\| \\le K\\|d(s'_{i+1}-s'_{i})\\| + \\varepsilon$. The definition, however, gives an estimate **at the other end** (reducing $\\|s - ds'\\|$ to $K\\|ds\\| + \\varepsilon$) and **cannot** bound $\\|s'\\|$ itself. The resulting geometric series therefore fails to converge.\n\n📌 To find a **specific preimage with a bound**, use the open mapping theorem. Since $\\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$ is closed and $M^{n+1}$ is Banach, $d^{n+1} : M^{n+1} \\twoheadrightarrow \\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$ is open. Thus there exists $c$ such that every $z \\in \\operatorname{im} d^{n+1}$ has a preimage $w$ satisfying $\\|w\\| \\le c\\|z\\|$. This gives the required bound on the preimage.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3704fd03'
    },
  },
  'cor.k-acyclic-acyclic': {
    statement: {
      en: "If a complex of Banach abelian groups $M^{\\bullet}$ is $K$-bounded acyclic (for some $K$), then it is acyclic in the usual sense.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b9a04ad4'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Immediate corollary**: Apply the preceding proposition degree by degree. Every pair of adjacent degrees satisfies the boundedness condition with bound $K$, so the complex is exact in every degree.\n\n⭐ **This is the conversion step used throughout the chapter**: The acyclicity proofs first establish **bounded** acyclicity on Stone or compact pieces, then use this corollary to obtain ordinary acyclicity and hence cohomological vanishing.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#00831860'
    },
  },
  'prop.banach-stone-acyclic': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be a Banach abelian group and let $S$ be a Stone space. Then for every $n > 0$\n\n$$H^{n}_{\\mathrm{cond}}(S,\\ M) = 0.$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c20d5906'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof in three steps**.\n\n(1) Choose a surjection of Stone spaces $S_{0} \\twoheadrightarrow S$ and use it to compute the Čech complex. (2) The preceding result gives that this complex is $1$-bounded acyclic. (3) The implication “$K$-bounded acyclicity $\\implies$ acyclicity” gives ordinary acyclicity. Thus Čech cohomology vanishes, and on a Stone space Čech cohomology agrees with condensed cohomology.\n\n**Relation to acyclicity with constant coefficients on Stone spaces.** The constant-coefficient result uses **discrete** abelian groups as coefficients (the extreme case of a discrete topology). This result uses **Banach** abelian groups, with their complete norm structure. The conclusions have the same form, but the proofs differ. Constant coefficients use profinite inverse limits, while Banach coefficients use $K$-boundedness and Čech complexes.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#51a8dbab'
    },
  },
  'thm.tietze-banach': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X$ be a **normal** topological space, let $K \\subseteq X$ be a **compact** subset, and let $V$ be a **real Banach space**. Then the restriction map\n\n$$C(X,\\ V) \\twoheadrightarrow C(K,\\ V)$$\n\nis **surjective**. Every continuous map $K \\to V$ extends to $X$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8a28e5a4'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Reduction in stages**.\n\n(1) Finite-dimensional case: $V = \\mathbb{R}^{m}$. Apply the classical Tietze extension theorem coordinatewise (extension of continuous real-valued functions on normal spaces, based on Urysohn's lemma).\n\n(2) General separable Banach case: Every separable Banach space is isomorphic to a closed subspace of $\\ell^{\\infty}$, and $\\ell^{\\infty}$ is a **Lipschitz retract**. First extend the map to $\\ell^{\\infty}$ and then retract.\n\n(3) In the nonseparable case, the compact image lies in a separable closed subspace, reducing the problem to (2).\n\n⭐ **Why condensed mathematics uses this.** The main theorem of §8.2, acyclicity of real Banach spaces on compact Hausdorff spaces, covers a general compact Hausdorff space $S$ by a Stonean space $S_{0}$ and then eliminates the discrepancy;\n\nthe discrepancy term lies in a function space $C(K, V)$, and **eliminating it requires extension**, supplied by this Tietze theorem.\n\n⚠️ **General topology also has a real-valued version** (the Tietze extension theorem). Here **the target can be any real Banach space**.\n\n**Proof (Dugundji extension theorem).** Dugundji's theorem is the version of Tietze for maps into topological vector spaces. Suppose $X$ is **paracompact** (a metric space suffices), $A \\subseteq X$ is **closed**, and $V$ is a **locally convex** topological vector space. Then there is a **linear** extension operator $C(A, V) \\to C(X, V)$, so the restriction map is surjective.\n- **Construction**: Paracompactness gives a locally finite refinement of an open neighborhood system for $A$, denoted by $\\{U_{i}\\}$, and an associated **partition of unity** $\\{\\varphi_{i}\\}$ ($\\sum \\varphi_{i} = 1$, $\\varphi_{i} \\ge 0$, with support in $U_{i}$).\n\nOn each $U_{i}$, first extend $g|_{A \\cap U_{i}}$ to a constant map $g_{i}$ (take the value at some point of $A\\cap U_{i}$);\n\nthen set\n$$G := \\sum_{i} \\varphi_{i}\\, g_{i} .$$\n- **Why it is well-defined**: Local finiteness $\\implies$ only finitely many $\\varphi_{i}$ are nonzero near each point, so the sum is finite. Since $V$ is a **vector space**, convex combinations make sense. Since $\\varphi_{i}$ is continuous, so is $G$.\n- **Why it is an extension**: If $x \\in A$, then for every instance of $\\varphi_{i}(x) > 0$ the corresponding $U_{i}$ contains $x$. Each $g_{i}$ equals, on $A \\cap U_{i}$, the value $g$, so $G(x) = \\sum \\varphi_{i}(x) g(x) = g(x)$.\n**Application here**: $X$ is normal (metric and paracompact spaces are normal), $K \\subseteq X$ is compact and hence closed, and $V$ is a real Banach space (locally convex). Thus the restriction map $C(X,V) \\twoheadrightarrow C(K,V)$ is surjective. In case $V = \\mathbb{R}^{m}$, apply the classical Tietze theorem coordinatewise. ∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7017fbd0'
    },
  },
  'thm.real-banach-chaus-acyclic': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $V$ be a **real Banach space** and let $S$ be a **compact Hausdorff** space. Then for every $n > 0$\n\n$$H^{n}_{\\mathrm{cond}}(S,\\ V) = 0.$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e5d6ab82'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **This is the main theorem of §8.2** and the central result of the chapter. It extends acyclicity from Stone spaces to general compact Hausdorff spaces, with coefficients in a real Banach space.\n\n**Proof strategy**.\n\n(1) Map a Stonean space $S_{0}$ onto $S$ (using filtered colimits of compact Hausdorff spaces along closed inclusions and Stonean covers). (2) On $S_{0}$, acyclicity for Banach coefficients on Stone spaces gives acyclicity of the Čech complex;\n\n(3) Compare the Čech complex of $S_{0} \\to S$ with the cohomology of $S$ itself. The discrepancy term lies in $C(K, V)$;\n\n(4) Use **Banach-valued Tietze extension** to eliminate the discrepancy, obtaining **bounded** acyclicity on $S$;\n\n(5) Use “$K$-bounded acyclicity $\\implies$ acyclicity” to obtain ordinary acyclicity.\n\n⚠️ **The result fails for complex Banach spaces.** This is one direct motivation for the theory of solid modules and solid quasi-coherent sheaves. In the real case one can use the **order structure** behind Tietze extension (suprema on $\\mathbb{R}$ and partitions) to eliminate the discrepancy. The complex case lacks these tools and requires different machinery.\n\n📌 Compare the identification of condensed and sheaf cohomology on locally compact spaces. That result concerns **discrete coefficients** (the space need only be locally compact), whereas this one concerns **Banach coefficients** (the space need only be compact). Together they cover most computations in Chapter 8.\n\n**Proof (eliminating the discrepancy with a partition of unity).** By the identification of Čech and sheaf cohomology on compact Hausdorff spaces, acyclicity for Banach coefficients on Stone spaces, and “$K$-bounded acyclicity $\\implies$ acyclicity,” it suffices to show that for a continuous surjection $S_{0} \\to S$ from a Stone space, the augmented Čech complex $\\check{C}^{\\bullet}\\bigl((S_{0}\\to S)^{+}, V\\bigr)$ is **$1$-bounded acyclic**.\nFix $\\varepsilon > 0$ and $f \\in \\check{C}^{n}$ (that is, $S_{n} \\to V$ is continuous and $S_{n}$ is the fiber product of $S_{0}$ over $S$ with $(n{+}1)$ factors).\n\nWrite $\\pi_{n} : S_{n} \\to S$ and $f_{x} := f|_{\\pi_{n}^{-1}(x)}$.\n- For each $x \\in S$, apply the result that the Čech complex of a Stone surjection is boundedly acyclic to the fiberwise surjection $\\pi_{n}^{-1}(x) \\to \\{x\\}$, with bound $1$ (the fiberwise version). This gives $g_{x} : \\pi_{n+1}^{-1}(x) \\to V$ such that\n$$\\|f_{x} - d g_{x}\\| \\le \\|d f_{x}\\| + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2} \\le \\|df\\| + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}.$$\n- By **Banach-valued Tietze extension**, $g_{x}$ extends on $S_{n+1}$ to $G_{x}$. By continuity, $x$ has a neighborhood $V_{x}$ such that on $\\pi_{n+1}^{-1}(V_{x})$ one has $\\|f - dG_{x}\\| < \\|df\\| + \\varepsilon$.\n- Choose finitely many $V_{1}, \\ldots, V_{r}$ covering $S$ (by **compactness**), take a subordinate partition of unity $\\{\\varphi_{i}\\}$, and set $g := \\sum_{i} (\\varphi_{i}\\circ\\pi_{n+1})\\,G_{i}$.\n\nFrom $\\sum_{i}(\\varphi_{i}\\circ\\pi_{n}) = 1$ we get $f = \\sum_{i}(\\varphi_{i}\\circ\\pi_{n}) f$, and also $dg = \\sum_{i}(\\varphi_{i}\\circ\\pi_{n})\\, dG_{i}$. Subtracting the two identities gives\n$$\\|f - dg\\| = \\Bigl\\|\\sum_{i} (\\varphi_{i}\\circ\\pi_{n})\\,(f - dG_{i})\\Bigr\\| \\le \\max_{i} \\|f - dG_{i}\\| \\le \\|df\\| + \\varepsilon,$$\nThe last step uses the fact that the norm of a convex combination is at most the maximum norm ($\\varphi_{i} \\ge 0$, $\\sum\\varphi_{i} = 1$). This is exactly bounded exactness for $K = 1$ with bound $K$. ∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ebd6c227'
    },
  },
  'prop.k-bounded-exact-filtered-colimit': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M^{\\bullet} = \\varinjlim_{k} M^{\\bullet}_{k}$ be a **filtered colimit** of complexes of seminormed abelian groups, with **isometric** transition maps.\n\nIf each $M^{\\bullet}_{k}$ is boundedly exact at $M^{n}_{k}$ with bound $K$, then $M^{\\bullet}$ is boundedly exact at $M^{n}$ with bound $K$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d017f644'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ Use: the \"analytic property with a constant\", $K$-bounded exactness, is not lost when passing to larger complexes — so one can check the condition on finite/simple complexes first and then take the colimit.\n\n⚠️ The isometry condition cannot be dropped: if the transition maps are only continuous and shrink norms, the seminorm on the colimit becomes the infimum of representative norms, and $\\|d s\\| = \\|d s_{k}\\|$ above fails immediately.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#12602a11'
    },
  },
  'prop.continuous-functions-normed': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $S$ be a **compact Hausdorff** space and let $M$ be a (semi)normed abelian group (respectively, a Banach abelian group). Then\n\n1. The continuous maps $C(S, M) := \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{top}}(S, M)$, equipped with\n$$\\|f\\| := \\sup_{x \\in S}\\|f(x)\\|$$\nform a (semi)normed abelian group (respectively, a Banach abelian group);\n2. If $S$ is also a **Stone** space, then $C(S, M^{\\mathrm{disc}})$ is **dense** in $C(S, M)$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a88017b1'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ Statement 2 is the precise version of \"there are enough discrete-valued functions\": on a Stone space every continuous $M$-valued function can be uniformly approximated by locally constant functions. It is a key step in the proof of acyclicity for Banach coefficients (8.2.6 / 8.2.8).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cd2e3fd1'
    },
  },
  'prop.condab-section': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $F \\in \\mathbf{FCHaus}$. Then the **section functor**\n\n$$\\Gamma(F, -) : \\mathrm{CondAb} \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{Ab}, \\qquad M \\mapsto \\Gamma(F, M) := M(F)$$\n\npreserves **all limits and all colimits**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#17d3648e'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "The reason is that sections of a sheaf at $F$ are computed pointwise, and in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ finite products agree with finite coproducts and filtered colimits are exact.\n\nThis is **how properties of $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ are reduced pointwise to properties of $\\mathbf{Ab}$**. The result on the AB axioms below uses this reduction.\n\n**Proof.** Identify $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ with the category of abelian sheaves on $\\mathbf{FCHaus}$ (the additive version of $\\mathrm{Cond} \\simeq \\widehat{\\mathbf{FCHaus}}$).\n- **Preservation of colimits**: It suffices to show that a presheaf colimit is **already a sheaf**. When $F$ is free, covers on $\\mathbf{FCHaus}$ are finite disjoint unions, so for $\\coprod_{j=1}^{r} F_{j}$:\n$$\\Bigl(\\varinjlim_{i} M_{i}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\coprod_{j} F_{j}\\Bigr) = \\varinjlim_{i} M_{i}\\Bigl(\\coprod_{j} F_{j}\\Bigr) = \\varinjlim_{i} \\bigoplus_{j} M_{i}(F_{j}) = \\bigoplus_{j} \\varinjlim_{i} M_{i}(F_{j}),$$\nThe middle step uses the fact that sheaves on $\\mathbf{FCHaus}$ preserve finite products (that is, $M(\\coprod F_{j}) = \\bigoplus M(F_{j})$). The last step uses the fact that in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ finite products agree with finite coproducts and filtered colimits are exact. Since the colimit still satisfies the sheaf condition, it is the colimit in sheaves, and $\\Gamma(F, -)$ commutes with it ✓.\n- **Preservation of limits**: Limits in sheaves are computed **objectwise** (a presheaf limit of sheaves is already a sheaf), so $\\Gamma(F, \\varprojlim M_{i}) = \\varprojlim \\Gamma(F, M_{i})$ ✓. ∎\n⭐ As a corollary, $\\Gamma(F,-)$ is also **exact** (it preserves both kernels and cokernels). This is why free compact Hausdorff spaces give projective objects.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#acb390ed'
    },
  },
  'def.condensed-abelian-group': {
    statement: {
      en: "A **condensed abelian group** is an **abelian sheaf** on $\\mathrm{Cond}$. Four descriptions give the same category:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Ab}(\\mathrm{Cond}) \\;\\simeq\\; \\mathrm{Cond}(\\mathrm{Ab}) \\;\\simeq\\; \\widehat{\\mathbf{CHaus}}(\\mathrm{Ab}) \\;\\simeq\\; \\widehat{\\mathbf{FCHaus}}(\\mathrm{Ab})$$\n\nWrite this category as $\\mathrm{CondAb}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b5dc6432'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "For the general definition, see “abelian sheaf.” Condensed abelian groups are the case of $\\mathcal{T} = \\mathrm{Cond}$.\n\nIn the compact Hausdorff setting, the sheaf condition simplifies: **$M$ is a condensed abelian group $\\iff$ it preserves finite products** (a general topos has no such simple criterion).\n\nNotation: $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N) := \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{CondAb}}(M, N)$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a97aed74'
    },
  },
  'thm.condab-ab': {
    statement: {
      en: "$\\mathrm{CondAb}$ is a **Grothendieck category** satisfying **AB6** and **AB4\\***.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ff77b844'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "Consequently, $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ is an **abelian category** and (1) has a generator, (2) has all limits and colimits, (3) has **exact** products and coproducts, and (4) has **exact filtered colimits that commute with products**.\n\nThe Grothendieck category assertion holds for **every** topos (see “abelian groups in a topos form a Grothendieck category”). The additional properties here are **AB6 and AB4\\***, which need not both hold for abelian groups in a general topos.\n\nAs a result, the homological algebra of $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ works as it does in $\\mathbf{Ab}$, including derived functors, long exact sequences, and derived categories.\n\n**Proof (reduce each assertion to $\\mathbf{Ab}$).** The key tool is the preceding result: **each $\\Gamma(F,-)$ (with $F$ free) preserves all limits and colimits**. Thus any property expressed using finite limits and colimits can be checked objectwise, where it reduces to a familiar fact in $\\mathbf{Ab}$.\n- **Existence of colimits and limits**: The presheaf obtained by taking colimits objectwise is still a sheaf (by the preceding result) $\\implies$ exist ✓;\n- **Exactness of filtered colimits (AB5)**: Compute objectwise and use exactness of filtered colimits in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ ✓;\n- **Existence of a generator**: $\\mathrm{Cond}$ is generated by $\\{h_{F}\\}$ (with $F$ free compact Hausdorff) $\\implies$ $\\mathrm{CondAb}$ is generated by $\\{\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot F\\}$ ✓;\n- **AB4\\* (exactness of products)**: Products are computed objectwise, and products are exact in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ ✓;\n- **AB6 (filtered colimits commute with products)**: Objectwise computation reduces this to AB6 in $\\mathbf{Ab}$ ✓;\n- **Abelian**: Kernels and cokernels are computed objectwise as kernels and cokernels in $\\mathbf{Ab}$. The epi-mono factorization and $\\operatorname{im}\\cong\\operatorname{coim}$ both hold objectwise ✓. ∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#bcdddcc8'
    },
  },
  'lem.stonean-projective': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $F$ be a **free compact Hausdorff space** (or, more generally, a **Stonean space**). Then the free condensed abelian group $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot F$ is a **finitely presented projective** condensed abelian group.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4adc22c6'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "Equivalently, we must show that the functor $M \\mapsto \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot F, M) \\cong \\Gamma(F, M)$ preserves epimorphisms and filtered colimits.\n\nThe first assertion follows from the projectivity of free compact Hausdorff spaces in $\\mathrm{Cond}$. The second follows from the construction of the section functor, which in fact **preserves all colimits**.\n\n**Proof.** By the tensor–Hom adjunction, $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot F, M) \\cong M(F) = \\Gamma(F, M)$ ✓, so it suffices to consider $\\Gamma(F,-)$.\n- **Preservation of epimorphisms**: The preceding result says that $\\Gamma(F,-)$ preserves **all** colimits, in particular cokernels. $v$ is an epimorphism $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{coker} v = 0$ $\\implies$ $\\operatorname{coker}\\Gamma(v) = 0$ $\\implies$ $\\Gamma(v)$ is an epimorphism ✓;\n\n\n- **Preservation of filtered colimits**: This also follows from preservation of all colimits ✓.\nThe standard criteria are: $P$ is **projective** $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{Hom}(P,-)$ preserves epimorphisms, and $P$ is **finitely presented** $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{Hom}(P,-)$ preserves filtered colimits. Together, these give that $\\mathbb{Z}\\cdot F$ is finitely presented and projective. ∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7f43dd7d'
    },
  },
  'prop.condab-generated': {
    statement: {
      en: "$\\mathrm{CondAb}$ is generated by **finitely presented projective** condensed abelian groups. In particular, it has enough projectives.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#91fe43ac'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ This is the other half of the justification that \"one can do derived algebra in $\\mathrm{CondAb}$\" (the first half being \"enough injectives\", supplied by the fact that it is a Grothendieck category): enough projective objects means left derived functors can also be computed directly.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5008497a'
    },
  },
  'prop.discrete-functor-coproduct': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $(M_{i})_{i \\in I}$ be a family of abelian groups. Then\n\n$$\\Bigl(\\bigoplus_{i \\in I} M_{i}\\Bigr)^{\\mathrm{disc}} \\;\\cong\\; \\bigoplus_{i \\in I} M_{i}^{\\mathrm{disc}},$$\n\nwhere $(-)^{\\mathrm{disc}}$ is the functor that views an abelian group as a discrete condensed abelian group.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8587d055'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** $(-)^{\\mathrm{disc}}$ is **left adjoint** to the forgetful functor ✓. Left adjoints preserve colimits, in particular direct sums ✓. ∎\n\n⚠️ The result fails for **products**. The functor $(-)^{\\mathrm{disc}}$ is a left adjoint and need not preserve products. The difference goes beyond exchanging products for direct sums: $\\prod_i M_i^{\\mathrm{disc}}$ is strictly larger than $(\\prod_i M_i)^{\\mathrm{disc}}$, with the extra families consisting of **individually locally constant coordinates that are jointly non-locally constant**. For example, take $S$ to be the Cantor set and take $M_i = \\mathbb{Z}/2$. Each coordinate function $S \\to M_i$ is locally constant, while the joint map $S \\to \\prod_i M_i$ that reads off all digits fails to be locally constant. It appears only on the left.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ab093837'
    },
  },
  'prop.cond-quotient-topological': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $N$ be a **closed** subgroup of a locally compact Hausdorff abelian group $M$. Then the condensed group associated to the topological quotient agrees with the quotient of the associated condensed groups:\n\n$$\\underline{M/N} \\;\\cong\\; \\underline{M}\\big/\\underline{N}.$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#da3ff8d5'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "📌 Use: when computing condensed abelian groups, one may first compute the quotient on the topological side and then discretise.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#be0ae4d2'
    },
  },
  'thm.darboux': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What the theorem says**: This is the theorem that symplectic geometry has no local invariants. Around every point there are coordinates in which $\\omega$ becomes $\\omega_{0}$, so all the information in a symplectic manifold is **global**.\n\nLet $(M^{2n}, \\omega)$ be a symplectic manifold and let $p \\in M$. Then there is a coordinate neighborhood of $p$ and coordinates $(q_{1}, \\ldots, q_{n}, p_{1}, \\ldots, p_{n})$ such that\n\n$$\\omega = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} dq_{i} \\wedge dp_{i}$$\n\nholds on that neighborhood. These are called **Darboux coordinates** (canonical coordinates).\n\n**Contrast with Riemannian geometry**: A Riemannian metric **generally cannot** be put in such a form. Curvature is a local invariant, and only metrics with **identically zero curvature** (flat metrics) can be put locally in the form $\\delta_{ij}$. A symplectic form **always** admits its local normal form.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f025941c'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof** (Moser's trick). The goal is to pull the form $p$ back, near $\\omega$, to the constant-coefficient form $\\omega_{0} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} dq_{i} \\wedge dp_{i}$. There are five steps, all carried out in a sufficiently small chart.\n\n**Step 1: Existence of a symplectic basis (linear algebra)**. Let $(V, \\omega)$ be a symplectic space of dimension $2n$. Choose $e_{1} \\ne 0$. By nondegeneracy there exists $f_{1}$ such that $\\omega(e_{1}, f_{1}) \\ne 0$. Rescale $f_{1}$ so that $\\omega(e_{1}, f_{1}) = 1$ (alternation gives $\\omega(e_{1}, e_{1}) = 0$). Set $W = \\{v : \\omega(e_{1}, v) = \\omega(f_{1}, v) = 0\\}$. For any $v$, we have $v - \\omega(v, f_{1})e_{1} + \\omega(v, e_{1})f_{1} \\in W$, so $V = \\operatorname{span}(e_{1}, f_{1}) \\oplus W$. Since $\\omega|_{W}$ is nondegenerate, induction on $\\dim W$ gives a symplectic basis $e_{1}, \\ldots, e_{n}, f_{1}, \\ldots, f_{n}$ (`def.symplectic-basis`). ∎\n\n**Step 2: Construct $\\omega_{0}$ so that $\\omega_{0}(p) = \\omega(p)$**. On $T_{p}M$, take the symplectic basis $e_{i}, f_{i}$ from Step 1, so $\\omega_{p}(e_{i}, f_{j}) = \\delta_{ij}$ and all other pairings vanish. Take a chart centered at $p$, denoted by $(U, x)$ ($x(p) = 0$), let $q_{i}, p_{i}$ be the coordinates given by the dual basis, and set $\\omega_{0} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} dq_{i} \\wedge dp_{i}$. Then $\\omega_{0}$ has constant coefficients, is closed and nondegenerate, and satisfies $\\omega_{0}(p) = \\omega_{p}$.\n\n**Step 3: The Moser homotopy and $\\theta$**. Set $\\eta = \\omega - \\omega_{0}$. This is a closed 2-form on $U$, and $\\eta(p) = 0$. Take $U$ to be a ball centered at $p$ (star-shaped and contractible). By `lem.poincare-lemma` ($k = 2 \\ge 1$), there is a 1-form $\\theta \\in \\Omega^{1}(U)$ such that $\\eta = d\\theta$. For $t \\in [0, 1]$, set $\\omega_{t} = \\omega_{0} + t\\eta$. Each $\\omega_{t}$ is closed, and by $\\eta(p) = 0$, $\\omega_{t}(p) = \\omega(p)$ is independent of $t$. Nondegeneracy is an open condition and $[0, 1]$ is compact, so we can shrink $U$ further to make **all** $\\omega_{t}$ ($t \\in [0, 1]$) nondegenerate.\n\n**Step 4: Solve the equation and integrate to obtain a flow**. Each $\\omega_{t}$ is nondegenerate, so the linear map $X \\mapsto \\iota_{X}\\omega_{t}$ is an isomorphism $T_{x}M \\to T_{x}^{*}M$. There is a unique smooth vector field $X_{t}$ satisfying $\\iota_{X_{t}}\\omega_{t} = -\\theta$, and $X_{t}$ depends smoothly on $(t, x)$. Treat $t$ as an extra coordinate and apply `thm.flow-local` on $M \\times \\mathbb{R}$ to the vector field $(X_{t}, \\partial_{t})$. This gives, near $p$ and defined on $[0, 1]$, a local flow $\\varphi_{t}$ satisfying $\\varphi_{0} = \\operatorname{id}$ and $\\frac{d}{dt}\\varphi_{t}(x) = X_{t}(\\varphi_{t}(x))$. Shrink $U$ again if necessary so that $\\varphi_{t}$ is defined for all $t \\in [0, 1]$.\n\n**Step 5: $\\varphi_{1}^{*}\\omega = \\omega_{0}$**. Differentiate $t$ using Cartan's formula $\\mathcal{L}_{X} = d\\iota_{X} + \\iota_{X}d$ and $\\frac{d}{dt}\\omega_{t} = \\eta$:\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\varphi_{t}^{*}\\omega_{t}\\bigr) = \\varphi_{t}^{*}\\bigl(\\mathcal{L}_{X_{t}}\\omega_{t} + \\tfrac{d}{dt}\\omega_{t}\\bigr) = \\varphi_{t}^{*}\\bigl(d\\iota_{X_{t}}\\omega_{t} + \\iota_{X_{t}}d\\omega_{t} + \\eta\\bigr).$$\n\n By $d\\omega_{t} = 0$, $\\iota_{X_{t}}\\omega_{t} = -\\theta$, and $\\eta = d\\theta$, the parenthesized expression equals $-d\\theta + 0 + d\\theta = 0$. Thus $\\varphi_{t}^{*}\\omega_{t}$ is independent of $t$ and equals its value at $t = 0$, namely $\\varphi_{0}^{*}\\omega_{0} = \\omega_{0}$. Taking $t = 1$ gives $\\varphi_{1}^{*}\\omega = \\omega_{0}$. Hence $(q_{i} \\circ \\varphi_{1}^{-1},\\, p_{i} \\circ \\varphi_{1}^{-1})$ are Darboux coordinates on $\\varphi_{1}(U)$. ∎\n\n📌 **What the proof uses**: Nondegeneracy (to solve $X_{t}$), closedness ($\\omega_{t}$ is closed and $\\eta$ has primitive $\\theta$), contractibility of the chart (`lem.poincare-lemma`), and the local flow theorem for ODEs (`thm.flow-local`). The agreement of $\\omega_{0}$ and $\\omega$ at $p$ is crucial. Otherwise $\\omega_{t}$ might degenerate at $p$, preventing the Moser flow from being defined. The node `thm.symplectic-normal-form` currently provides only a linear algebra proof sketch. The existence of the required symplectic basis is proved again here, so this proof is independent of that node.\n\n⭐ **This theorem marks a dividing line in symplectic geometry**: Local coordinates cannot distinguish symplectic manifolds, so all symplectic invariants (Gromov–Witten invariants, Floer homology, and capacities) are **global**. Riemannian geometry starts from local data such as curvature, while symplectic geometry relies on global data.\n\n⚠️ The theorem **does not** say that every symplectic manifold is diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^{2n}$. It supplies only local coordinates and imposes no constraint on global topology (as illustrated by the area form on $T^{2}$). This is the most common misreading.\n\n⚠️ Darboux's name appears in three places here: this theorem in symplectic geometry, the Darboux criterion and upper and lower sums (`thm.darboux-criterion`, Riemann integration), and Darboux's theorem in real analysis (derivatives have the intermediate value property). The three are unrelated.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8f384555'
    },
  },
  'def.symplectic-manifold': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Extends the notion of a symplectic vector space to a manifold. Each tangent space carries a symplectic form, and these forms together must be **closed** ($d\\omega = 0$). This differential condition is the source of rigidity in symplectic geometry.\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold (**there is no need to assume even dimension in advance**, as shown below). A **symplectic form** on $M$ is a 2-form $\\omega \\in \\Omega^{2}(M)$ satisfying\n\n1. **Closedness**: $d\\omega = 0$.\n2. **Nondegeneracy**: At each point $p$, $\\omega_{p} : T_{p}M \\times T_{p}M \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is nondegenerate (the manifold version of `def.symplectic-form`).\n\nWe call $(M, \\omega)$ a **symplectic manifold**. Nondegeneracy implies $\\dim M = 2n$ (apply `thm.symplectic-normal-form` pointwise).\n\n**Standard example**: On $\\mathbb{R}^{2n} = T^{*}\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ (the cotangent bundle), take\n\n$$\\omega_{0} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} dq_{i} \\wedge dp_{i} ,$$\n\nThis is the phase space of classical mechanics.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5be1ed9e'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠️ **Closedness is essential**: Nondegenerate 2-forms exist pointwise (the cotangent bundle provides an example), whereas closedness is a condition on the form **as a whole**. Dropping it gives an almost symplectic manifold, which carries a nondegenerate 2-form with no closedness requirement (a different theory). ⚠ Integrability is a property of an almost complex structure. It requires an additional almost complex structure and plays no role in the definition of an almost symplectic manifold. $S^{6}$ is a standard example of an almost symplectic manifold that is not symplectic.\n\n⭐ **Why physics needs it**: $\\omega$ gives rise to Hamiltonian flows (`def.hamiltonian-vector-field`), and $d\\omega = 0$ ensures that these flows **preserve $\\omega$** (the Lie derivative $\\mathcal{L}_{X_{H}}\\omega = 0$). This is the basis of Liouville's theorem on conservation of phase volume.\n\n⚠️ A symplectic manifold **is necessarily orientable** ($\\omega^{n}$ is a nowhere-vanishing volume form), but **need not** be Kähler. A Kähler structure also requires a complex structure and a compatible metric. Symplectic geometry is more general than Kähler geometry.\n\n⚠️ Compact symplectic manifolds are subject to a strong restriction: $[\\omega] \\in H^{2}(M)$ is nonzero (otherwise $\\omega=d\\theta$ would make $\\omega^{n}=d(\\theta\\wedge\\omega^{n-1})$ exact, giving integral zero and contradicting the fact that $\\omega^{n}$ is a nowhere-vanishing volume form). This is why a symplectic manifold cannot be $S^{2n}$, $n \\ge 2$. The cohomological argument is omitted here.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ff25192a'
    },
  },
  'def.hamiltonian-vector-field': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Provides the correspondence ‘function $\\implies$ vector field’ on a symplectic manifold. It turns an energy function (the Hamiltonian) into equations of motion, the central construction of analytical mechanics.\n\nLet $(M, \\omega)$ be a symplectic manifold and let $H \\in C^{\\infty}(M)$. By nondegeneracy of $\\omega$, there is a unique vector field $X_{H}$ such that\n\n$$\\iota_{X_{H}}\\omega = +dH ,$$\n\nWe call $X_{H}$ the **Hamiltonian vector field** of $H$ ($\\iota$ is the interior product, see `def.interior-product`). Its integral curves satisfy **Hamilton's equations**, which in Darboux coordinates are\n\n$$\\dot{q}_{i} = \\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_{i}}, \\qquad \\dot{p}_{i} = -\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q_{i}} .$$\n\n**Key property**: $\\mathcal{L}_{X_{H}}\\omega = 0$ (the flow of $H$ preserves $\\omega$), so it also preserves the volume form $\\omega^{n}/n!$. This is **Liouville's theorem**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d10da433'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⚠️ **Sign conventions vary**: $\\iota_{X_{H}}\\omega = dH$, $= -dH$, and $\\omega(X_{H}, \\cdot) = dH$ are all used. They differ by an overall sign (equivalently, $H \\mapsto -H$).\n\nWe use $\\iota_{X_{H}}\\omega = +dH$. Arnold uses $\\sum dp_{i}\\wedge dq_{i}$ together with $-dH$. Our $\\omega_{0}=\\sum dq_{i}\\wedge dp_{i}$ differs from his by a sign, so we take $+dH$.\n\nWhen switching references, **check the signs in Hamilton's equations**.\n\n⭐ **The correspondence ‘function $\\implies$ vector field’ is distinctive to symplectic geometry**: On a general manifold, differentiating a function gives only its differential ($dH$, a 1-form). To obtain a vector field requires an isomorphism from 1-forms to vector fields. This is precisely what $\\omega$ provides ($\\flat : TM \\to T^{*}M$, $v \\mapsto \\iota_{v}\\omega$).\n\nA Riemannian metric also gives such a map (a musical isomorphism), but the resulting flow does not preserve the metric.\n\n⚠️ **Hamiltonian vector field $\\ne$ arbitrary vector field**: It is the gradient of $H$ rotated by 90°.\n\nThe criterion is that $\\iota_{X}\\omega$ is **closed** (that is, $d(\\iota_X\\omega)=0$).\n\nThe nonclosed case is called a ‘locally Hamiltonian vector field,’ and the closed case a ‘symplectic vector field.’ Most books do not distinguish the two (since they coincide when $H^{1}(M) = 0$).\n\n⚠️ A vector field on a symplectic manifold need not arise from some $H$ ($\\iota_{X}\\omega$ must be exact). Thus symplectic vector fields form a broader class than Hamiltonian vector fields.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#96e495d0'
    },
  },
  'def.poisson-bracket': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Translates the vector field construction back into functions by assigning a function to each pair of functions. It makes $C^{\\infty}(M)$ a Lie algebra and is the operation that takes $\\{,\\}$ to $\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[,]$ in quantization.\n\nLet $(M, \\omega)$ be a symplectic manifold and let $f, g \\in C^{\\infty}(M)$. The **Poisson bracket** is defined by\n\n$$\\{f, g\\} := \\omega(X_{f}, X_{g}) = -\\iota_{X_{f}}\\iota_{X_{g}}\\omega = X_{g}(f) ,$$\n\nwhere $X_{f}, X_{g}$ are the Hamiltonian vector fields of $f$ and $g$. In Darboux coordinates,\n\n$$\\{f, g\\} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial q_{i}}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial p_{i}} - \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial p_{i}}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial q_{i}}\\Bigr) .$$\n\n**Properties**: It is bilinear and antisymmetric, satisfies the **Jacobi identity**, and acts as a **derivation** (the Leibniz rule).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f95afd09'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**It tests whether $H$ is conserved**: $\\frac{d}{dt}f = \\{f, H\\}$. Thus $f$ is a constant of motion $\\iff$ $\\{f, H\\} = 0$. This algebraic condition characterizes conserved quantities in mechanics. It is also the symplectic version of Noether's theorem (symmetry $\\implies$ conserved quantity).\n\n⭐ **$C^{\\infty}(M)$ is therefore a Lie algebra**: The bracket supplies the Lie bracket, and the Hamiltonian map $H \\mapsto X_{H}$ is a Lie **antihomomorphism** (it is linear and reverses the bracket), that is, $[X_{f}, X_{g}] = -X_{\\{f,g\\}}$.\n\nThis connects the algebra of functions with the algebra of vector fields.\n\n⚠️ **Poisson brackets are more general than symplectic structures**: Allowing degenerate Poisson brackets gives **Poisson manifolds** (for example, the Lie–Poisson bracket on the dual $\\mathfrak{g}^{*}$ of a Lie algebra). Symplectic manifolds are the nondegenerate special case. Only the symplectic case is covered here.\n\n⚠️ **Relation to quantum mechanics**: Dirac's correspondence rule is $\\{f,g\\} \\mapsto \\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\hat{f}, \\hat{g}]$. There is no unique quantization, so this is a rule rather than a theorem. It explains why the Jacobi identity must hold, since it corresponds to the Jacobi identity for commutators.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#791ff503'
    },
  },
  'def.lagrangian-submanifold': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Applies `def.lagrangian-subspace` pointwise on a manifold. It describes a maximal submanifold on which the symplectic form vanishes, and gives a mathematical model for configuration space in physics.\n\nLet $(M^{2n}, \\omega)$ be a symplectic manifold. An immersed submanifold $L \\subseteq M$ is called a **Lagrangian submanifold** if\n\n1. $\\dim L = n$ (half the dimension of $\\dim M$).\n2. $\\omega|_{L} = 0$ (on $L$ the form $\\omega$ vanishes identically, so $L$ is isotropic).\n\nEquivalently, for every $p \\in L$, $T_{p}L$ is a Lagrangian subspace of $T_{p}M$.\n\n**Standard examples**:\n\n- In $\\mathbb{R}^{2n}$, the zero section $\\{p = 0\\}$ (the position space, with all momenta zero).\n- More generally, **the graph associated with any function**, $\\{(q, p) : p = dF(q)\\}$, is a Lagrangian submanifold because $\\omega|_{L} = dq \\wedge d(dF) = 0$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c6aa3d4a'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ **‘$\\omega|_L = 0$’ is equivalent to ‘$p\\,dq$ is closed on $L$’**: Take $\\omega = d(p\\,dq)$. On $L$, $p\\,dq$ is closed $\\iff$ it is locally of the form $dF$ $\\iff$ $L$ is locally the graph associated with a function.\n\nThus a Lagrangian submanifold is locally such a graph, and $F$ is called a **generating function**.\n\nThis connects geometry with analysis and underlies Hamilton–Jacobi theory.\n\n⚠️ **$\\dim L = n$ does not follow from isotropy**: An isotropic submanifold can have dimension far below $n$ (even a line in a symplectic vector space is isotropic). One must **also** require half dimension or, equivalently, maximality ($T_pL = (T_pL)^{\\perp}$). This is exactly as in the linear case (see the notes to `def.lagrangian-subspace`).\n\n⚠️ **Lagrangian submanifolds need not be closed or embedded**: Physics often uses immersed submanifolds and even multiple covers (such as periodic orbits in Bohr–Sommerfeld quantization). The definition here allows immersions.\n\n⚠️ Terminology: Lagrangian subspaces and Lagrangian submanifolds are named for the same person and express the same concept at two scales. They are **unrelated** to `thm.lagrange-identity` in inner product geometry.\n\n\n\n**Of the two defining conditions, ‘$\\dim = n$’ does not follow from isotropy** (this is the step most often omitted):\n\n- $\\omega|_{L} \\equiv 0$ gives **only isotropy**. An isotropic submanifold can have dimension **far below** $n$ (even a line in a symplectic vector space is isotropic), so isotropy alone does not give maximality.\n- $\\dim L = n$ (**must be imposed separately**): Equivalently, require $T_{p}L = (T_{p}L)^{\\perp}$ (pointwise maximality). For a nondegenerate form, $\\dim W + \\dim W^{\\perp} = \\dim V$, so ‘$W = W^{\\perp}$’ **forces** the dimension to be half. This is what it means to be Lagrangian.\n- **Directions of the equivalence**: Pointwise, $T_{p}L$ is a Lagrangian subspace $\\iff$ $L$ is a Lagrangian submanifold ($L$ is immersed, and $\\omega|_L = 0$ and $\\dim = n$ hold). The direction $(\\Leftarrow)$ is immediate pointwise. The direction $(\\implies)$ requires that $L$ have constant dimension, which the immersion guarantees.\n\n⚠️ **Closedness is not part of the definition**: $L$ need not be closed, compact, or embedded. It can be immersed or even multiply covered. The Bohr–Sommerfeld condition in physics uses Lagrangian submanifolds that may only be immersed.\n⚠️ **The equivalence with being locally the graph associated with a function holds only when the projection $\\pi:L\\to Q$ (with $Q$ the zero section containing a Lagrangian subspace) is a local diffeomorphism** (exactness of $p\\,\\mathrm{d}q$). This is a local property, so generating functions exist only locally.\n\n\n\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the pointwise definition requires no extra hypotheses, while a generating function requires transversality):\n- $\\dim L=n$ and $\\omega|_L=0$ $\\iff$ each tangent space is a Lagrangian subspace. This is the pointwise definition applied to the tangent map of the immersion. It does not require $L$ to be closed, compact, or embedded.\n- Isotropic and half-dimensional $\\iff$ $T_pL=(T_pL)^\\omega$ uses nondegeneracy of the symplectic form and $\\dim W+\\dim W^\\omega=2n$. Isotropy alone is insufficient, as a one-dimensional submanifold when $n>1$ shows.\n- In cotangent coordinates, the graph of a closed 1-form $\\iff$ a Lagrangian graph, by computing the pullback of the canonical symplectic form. A closed 1-form is locally of the form $dF$ by the Poincaré lemma. Global existence also requires exactness.\n- To express a general Lagrangian submanifold as a graph in fixed position coordinates, the projection must also be a local diffeomorphism. A cotangent fiber is itself Lagrangian but cannot be written as $p=dF(q)$ under that position projection.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c7cee78'
    },
  },
  'def.lagrange-bracket': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Gives the earlier counterpart of the Poisson bracket (Lagrange 1808, Poisson 1809), essentially the inverse of the Poisson bracket matrix. It measures the area between two families of curves, making it natural in the theory of canonical transformations.\n\nLet $(q, p)$ be Darboux coordinates, and let $u = (u_{1}, \\ldots, u_{2n})$ and $v = (v_{1}, \\ldots, v_{2n})$ be two sets of coordinate functions. The **Lagrange bracket** is defined by\n\n$$[u, v]_{q,p} := \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl(\\frac{\\partial q_{i}}{\\partial u}\\frac{\\partial p_{i}}{\\partial v} - \\frac{\\partial p_{i}}{\\partial u}\\frac{\\partial q_{i}}{\\partial v}\\Bigr) .$$\n\n**Relation to the Poisson bracket**: Write the Poisson bracket matrix $\\bigl(\\{u_{i}, u_{j}\\}\\bigr)$ as $P$ and the Lagrange bracket matrix as $L$. Then\n\n$$L \\cdot P = -I ,$$\n\nThat is, $L = -P^{-1}$. The matrices are inverses **up to a sign** (when $n = 1$, we have $L = P = \\begin{pmatrix}0 & 1\\cr -1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $LP = -I$, and the general formula is $\\sum_{k} [u_{i},u_{k}]\\{u_{k},u_{j}\\} = -\\delta_{ij}$).\n\n**Criterion for a canonical transformation**: The transformation $(q, p) \\mapsto (Q, P)$ is a **canonical transformation** (symplectomorphism) $\\iff$ for every $i, j$, both $[Q_{i}, Q_{j}] = [P_{i}, P_{j}] = 0$ and $[Q_{i}, P_{j}] = \\delta_{ij}$ hold.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#965424f9'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why the Lagrange bracket came first**: The calculus of variations and the study of canonical transformations, as in Lagrange's *Analytical Mechanics*, preceded the concept of a symplectic manifold.\n\nPoisson later found that the dual bracket satisfies the Jacobi identity, and it became central to the modern formulation.\n\n**The two brackets encode the same information** (by inversion), but the Lagrange bracket does not satisfy the Jacobi identity and is now used as a computational tool.\n\n⭐ **Geometric meaning**: The Lagrange bracket measures the area enclosed in phase space by the families of level curves of $u$ and $v$. The Lagrange bracket thus describes areas, while the Poisson bracket describes flows. This is why the canonical transformation condition is more intuitive in terms of Lagrange brackets: it directly expresses area preservation.\n\n⚠️ **$L = -P^{-1}$ holds only when the comparison is made in the same coordinate system**: Both matrices must be written using the **same** $(u, v)$ as a basis to make the comparison meaningful. This is the easiest point to misremember.\n\n⚠️ Notation: Some books write the Lagrange bracket as $\\{u,v\\}$ (the same notation as for the Poisson bracket), while others write $[u,v]_{q,p}$. We use the latter, with a subscript indicating the reference coordinates.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#19f657f0'
    },
  },
  'thm.qr-decomposition': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Expresses Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization as a product of matrices. Denote the result by $R$ and the orthogonalized basis by $Q$ to obtain $A = QR$.\n\nSuppose the columns of $A \\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$ are linearly independent ($m \\ge n$). There is then a **unique** factorization\n\n$$A = QR ,$$\n\nwhere $Q \\in M_{m\\times n}$ has **orthonormal** columns ($Q^{T}Q = I_{n}$) and $R \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{R})$ is **upper triangular** with positive diagonal entries.\n\n**Complex case**: $A = QR$, $Q^{*}Q = I$, and $R$ is upper triangular (with positive real diagonal entries).\n\n**Square case**: When $A$ is invertible, $Q$ is orthogonal (unitary). This gives the factorization of $A$ into ‘orthogonal $\\times$ upper triangular’ form, corresponding to the ‘lower triangular $\\times$ upper triangular’ factorization in LU.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c230d26a'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**This is Gram–Schmidt**: Orthogonalize the columns of $A$, denoted by $a_{1}, \\ldots, a_{n}$, to obtain $q_{1}, \\ldots, q_{n}$ (`thm.gram-schmidt`). Then $a_{k} = \\sum_{i \\le k} r_{ik}q_{i}$, and the coefficients assemble into the upper triangular matrix $R$.\n\nThe only difference is that QR usually requires a positive diagonal for $R$ (fixing the signs of $q_k$).\n\n⭐ **Why use QR instead of Gram–Schmidt**: They are mathematically equivalent but differ numerically. Gram–Schmidt can lose orthogonality ($Q^{T}Q$ departs from $I$), so practical algorithms use Householder reflections or Givens rotations to compute $Q$ directly. **Only the mathematical results are covered here** (numerical algorithms are outside the scope).\n\n**Relation to least squares**: $\\lVert Ax - b\\rVert^{2} = \\lVert Rx - Q^{T}b\\rVert^{2} + \\lVert (I - QQ^{T})b\\rVert^{2}$, and the last term is independent of $x$. Thus the least-squares solution is obtained by solving $Rx = Q^{T}b$ (`thm.least-squares`).\n\nThis is why QR is widely used in numerical computation.\n\n⚠️ The **linear independence of the columns** is essential. Without it, $Q$ has an incomplete set of columns and $R$ is singular. A factorization is still possible but loses its usefulness in this form (use column-pivoted QR or a pseudoinverse in that case).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ed40ab07'
    },
  },
  'thm.least-squares': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: The system $Ax = b$ may have no solution ($b$ lies outside the column space). Least squares instead finds an $x$ for which $Ax$ is closest to $b$.\n\nSuppose $A \\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{R})$ has full column rank and let $b \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m}$. The **least-squares solution** is the minimizer of\n\n$$\\lVert Ax - b\\rVert_{2}$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. It is uniquely determined by the **normal equations**:\n\n$$A^{T}A\\, x = A^{T} b .$$\n\nWith full column rank, $A^{T}A$ is positive definite and hence invertible, so the solution is $x = (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b$.\n\n**Geometric meaning**: $Ax^{*}$ is the **orthogonal projection** of $b$ onto the column space of $A$, and the residual $b - Ax^{*}$ is orthogonal to that space.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#60ca6f88'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**The normal equations express orthogonality of the residual to the column space**: $(b - Ax) \\perp \\operatorname{col}(A)$ $\\iff$ $A^{T}(b - Ax) = 0$ $\\iff$ $A^{T}Ax = A^{T}b$. This is the defining property of the projection (`def.orthogonal-projection`).\n\n⭐ **A concrete formula for orthogonal projection**: The matrix form of projection onto the subspace is $P = A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}$. This $P$ is idempotent and symmetric (the ‘orthogonal idempotent’ in the notes to `def.idempotent`), so it is precisely the projection operator.\n\n⚠️ **Avoid explicitly inverting $A^{T}A$** (the formula is mathematically valid, but the condition number is squared). In practice, use QR or SVD: $x = R^{-1}Q^{T}b$ (see the notes to `thm.qr-decomposition`). Only the mathematical result is given here.\n\n⚠️ **Without full column rank**, the least-squares solution **is not unique** (infinitely many solutions give the same residual). The **pseudoinverse** selects the one of minimum norm (`def.pseudoinverse`).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7fca3e61'
    },
  },
  'thm.lu-decomposition': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Expresses Gaussian elimination as multiplication by a sequence of elementary matrices. Denote the product of those elementary matrices by $L$ and the result of elimination by $U$ to obtain $A = LU$.\n\nSuppose $A \\in M_{n}(K)$ and all its $n$ **leading principal minors** are nonzero. There is then a unique factorization\n\n$$A = LU ,$$\n\nwhere $L$ is **unit lower triangular** (every diagonal entry is $1$) and $U$ is **upper triangular**.\n\n**Permutation version (always exists)**: For every invertible $A$, there is a permutation matrix $P$ such that $PA = LU$ (row swaps ensure that the pivots are nonzero).\n\n**Use**: Solving $Ax = b$ reduces to solving $Ly = b$ by forward substitution and $Ux = y$ by back substitution, both of which are $O(n^{2})$. When $A$ stays fixed, $L, U$ can be reused.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a9100b25'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why $LU$ holds**: Each step of Gaussian elimination is left multiplication by an elementary matrix. Composing the elimination steps gives $L^{-1}$. Nonzero leading principal minors ensure that no row swaps are needed. See the edge `imp.lu-decomposition-proof` for the full proof, including Schur complement induction and uniqueness. The permutation version uses partial pivoting, as shown in the final paragraph of that proof.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ca54cc1a'
    },
  },
  'def.pseudoinverse': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Provides a substitute for an inverse for singular and rectangular matrices. The pseudoinverse $A^{+}$ is the unique matrix satisfying the four Penrose conditions.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb{K})$, where $\\mathbb{K} = \\mathbb{R}$ or $\\mathbb{C}$ ($^{*}$ is the transpose or conjugate transpose). The **Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse** $A^{+} \\in M_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{K})$ is the unique matrix satisfying the following four conditions. (⚠ Over a general field, the conditions **may have no solution**: $K = \\mathbb{F}_{2}$ and $A = (1\\, 1)$ reduce them to $1 = 0$.)\n\n$$A A^{+} A = A, \\qquad A^{+} A A^{+} = A^{+} ,$$\n\n$$(AA^{+})^{*} = AA^{+}, \\qquad (A^{+}A)^{*} = A^{+}A .$$\n\n**Construction via SVD**: If $A = U\\Sigma V^{*}$ (`thm.svd`), then\n\n$$A^{+} = V\\Sigma^{+}U^{*} ,$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma^{+}$ is obtained by taking the reciprocals of the nonzero diagonal entries of $\\Sigma$ and then transposing.\n\n**When $A$ is invertible**, $A^{+} = A^{-1}$. If $A$ has full column rank, then $A^{+} = (A^{*}A)^{-1}A^{*}$ (the least-squares solution is $x = A^{+}b$).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cf3d1d5f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Meaning of the four conditions**: The first two say that $AA^{+}$ and $A^{+}A$ are idempotent (projections, `def.idempotent`). The last two say that the projections are **self-adjoint** (orthogonal projections). Thus $A^{+}$ acts by **orthogonally projecting $b$ onto the column space and setting the kernel component to zero**, giving the unique minimum-norm least-squares solution.\n\n⭐ **A canonical way to select a solution when uniqueness fails**: When $Ax = b$ is consistent with infinitely many solutions, $A^{+}b$ gives the one of **minimum norm**. When $Ax = b$ is inconsistent, $A^{+}b$ gives a solution of **minimum residual**. The pseudoinverse thus handles both minimum-norm and least-squares solutions.\n\n⚠️ The pseudoinverse **does not** satisfy $(AB)^{+} = B^{+}A^{+}$ in general (it holds only in special cases). This contrasts with the inverse identity $(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}$ and is the easiest point to misremember.\n\n⚠️ Notation: Both $A^{+}$ and $A^{\\dagger}$ are used (the latter is common in physics). We use $A^{+}$ to avoid confusion with the conjugate transpose $A^{*}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4d89db3f'
    },
  },
  'def.matrix-norm': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Measures the size of a matrix. Two distinct approaches are to measure the matrix as **a collection of entries** (the Frobenius norm) or as **an operator** (the operator norm).\n\n**Operator norm** (induced norm): On $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, given a vector norm $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert$, define\n\n$$\\lVert A\\rVert := \\sup_{x \\ne 0}\\frac{\\lVert Ax\\rVert}{\\lVert x\\rVert} = \\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert = 1}\\lVert Ax\\rVert .$$\n\n**Frobenius norm**: $\\lVert A\\rVert_{F} := \\bigl(\\sum_{i,j}\\lvert a_{ij}\\rvert^{2}\\bigr)^{1/2}$ (the length of the matrix viewed as a $mn$-dimensional vector).\n\n**Basic properties**: Both are norms and satisfy $\\lVert AB\\rVert \\le \\lVert A\\rVert\\,\\lVert B\\rVert$ (submultiplicativity). The operator norm also satisfies $\\lVert Ax\\rVert \\le \\lVert A\\rVert\\,\\lVert x\\rVert$.\n\n**Relation to the spectrum**: $\\lVert A\\rVert_{2} = \\sigma_{\\max}(A) = \\sqrt{\\lambda_{\\max}(A^{*}A)}$ (the spectral norm is the largest singular value), and $\\lVert A\\rVert_{F} = \\bigl(\\sum \\sigma_{i}^{2}\\bigr)^{1/2}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#dad69efd'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why both norms are useful**: The operator norm measures the maximum amplification factor and is directly related to spectral radius and convergence ($\\rho(A) \\le \\lVert A\\rVert$, `def.spectral-radius`). The Frobenius norm measures the overall size of the entries, is easy to compute, and is naturally suited to low-rank approximation (as in the statement of Eckart–Young).\n\n⚠️ **The Frobenius norm is not an operator norm** (except in dimension one). It is not induced by a vector norm, as shown by $\\lVert I\\rVert_{F} = \\sqrt{n}$, while every operator norm satisfies $\\lVert I\\rVert = 1$. This gives the distinguishing test.\n\n⚠️ The spectral norm uses **singular values** rather than eigenvalues: $\\lVert A\\rVert_2 = \\sigma_{\\max}$, whereas $\\rho(A) = \\max\\lvert\\lambda\\rvert$. The two agree only for normal matrices (`def.normal-matrix`).\n\n⭐ **Why $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert$ is often used in error estimates**: $\\lVert x - x_{0}\\rVert \\le \\lVert A^{-1}\\rVert\\,\\lVert b - Ax_{0}\\rVert$. This gives rise to the condition number $\\kappa = \\lVert A\\rVert\\lVert A^{-1}\\rVert$ (only this connection is recorded here, without developing the numerical analysis).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d5630644'
    },
  },
  'prop.paracompact-partition-unity': {
    statement: {
      en: "For a topological space $X$:\n\n1. $X$ is **paracompact Hausdorff** $\\implies$ every open cover admits a subordinate **partition of unity** (the converse requires a separation hypothesis. In the indiscrete topology on at least two points, the constant function $1$ gives a partition of unity, but the space is neither Hausdorff nor $T_1$).\n2. $X$ is **normal** $\\implies$ every **locally finite** open cover admits a subordinate partition of unity (the converse likewise requires $T_1$ or Hausdorffness).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#23852434'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof (both directions).**\n**$(\\Longleftarrow)$ A partition of unity gives a locally finite refinement.** Let $\\{\\rho_{i}\\}$ be a partition of unity subordinate to the cover $\\{U_{i}\\}$.\n\nBy the definition of a partition of unity, $\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{i} \\subseteq U_{i}$ and the family of supports is **locally finite** ✓.\n\nThus $\\{ \\rho_{i}^{-1}\\bigl((0,1]\\bigr) \\}$ is an open refinement of $\\{U_{i}\\}$ and is still locally finite (each point lies outside all but finitely many supports) ✓. This is exactly the condition for paracompactness, with Hausdorffness assumed: every open cover has a locally finite open refinement.\n\nThe normal case is analogous (use a locally finite $\\{\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{i}\\}$ for a locally finite cover) ✓.\n**$(\\Longrightarrow)$ Use normality to construct $\\rho_{i}$ step by step.**\n- First take a **locally finite** open refinement $\\{V_{i}\\}$ (by the definition of paracompactness, or use the given locally finite cover itself in the normal case) ✓.\n- Normality gives open sets $W_{i}$ such that $\\overline{W_{i}} \\subseteq V_{i}$ and $\\{W_{i}\\}$ still covers $X$ (the standard shrinking lemma for normal spaces, obtained from Urysohn's lemma or a variant) ✓.\n- ⚠️ One shrinking is insufficient: vanishing of $g_i$ on $X\\setminus V_i$ gives only $\\operatorname{supp} g_i \\subseteq \\overline{V_i}$. **Shrink once more**: Take open sets $O_i$ such that $\\overline{W_i} \\subseteq O_i \\subseteq \\overline{O_i} \\subseteq V_i$ (apply normality again). Apply **Urysohn's lemma** to $(\\overline{W_i}, X \\setminus O_i)$ to obtain $g_i : X \\to [0,1]$, with $g_i|_{\\overline{W_i}} = 1$ and $g_i|_{X\\setminus O_i} = 0$. Then $\\operatorname{supp} g_i \\subseteq \\overline{O_i} \\subseteq V_i$ ✓.\n- Set $g := \\sum_{i} g_{i}$. Local finiteness makes this a **locally finite sum**, so $g$ is continuous ✓. Since $\\{W_{i}\\}$ covers $X$, we have $g \\ge 1$ at each point (at least one term is 1) ✓.\n- Take $\\rho_{i} := g_{i}/g$ ✓. The functions are nonnegative, sum to 1, satisfy $\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{i} \\subseteq V_{i} \\subseteq U_{i}$, and still have locally finite supports ✓.\nThis gives a partition of unity subordinate to the original cover ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ This result **underlies extension theorems**: A partition of unity combines locally defined data by a weighted sum to produce a globally defined function. Tietze- and Dugundji-type extensions, integration on manifolds, and Stokes' theorem all use this construction.\n\n⚠️ **Local finiteness is essential**: Without local finiteness, $\\sum \\rho_{i} = 1$ is merely a pointwise assertion, and the pointwise sum need not be continuous.\n\n\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the construction uses normality, and the converses require separate separation hypotheses):\n- Paracompact Hausdorff $\\implies$ every open cover admits a subordinate partition of unity: Use normality of paracompact Hausdorff spaces, a locally finite refinement, the shrinking lemma, and Urysohn's lemma. A second shrinking ensures the supports lie in the original open sets.\n- Every open cover admits a subordinate partition of unity $\\implies$ a locally finite open refinement exists: Take the sets on which the functions are positive, using local finiteness of supports and the fact that the sum is one. This gives paracompactness. Hausdorffness must be assumed separately.\n- Normal $\\implies$ every locally finite open cover admits a subordinate partition of unity: Use locally finite shrinking, Urysohn's lemma, and normalization of a locally finite sum of functions. There is no requirement that every cover admit a locally finite refinement.\n- For the converse, use the cover $\\{X\\setminus A,X\\setminus B\\}$ to separate the disjoint closed sets $A,B$. This gives the separation property for closed sets alone. To obtain normality under the definition that includes $T_1$, one must also assume $T_1$.\n- The missing separation hypothesis has a counterexample: In the indiscrete topology on at least two points, every open cover contains the entire space. The constant function one gives a subordinate partition of unity, yet the space is neither Hausdorff nor $T_1$. Both converses require the additional hypotheses.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ba9f34ac'
    },
  },
  'thm.smooth-partition-unity': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Glues locally defined objects into a global one. Construct an object on each open set and take a weighted sum, using the functions of a smooth partition of unity as weights.\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold and let $\\mathcal{U} = \\{U_{\\alpha}\\}_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be an open cover of $M$. Then there is a family of smooth functions $\\{\\rho_{\\alpha}\\}_{\\alpha \\in A}$ such that\n\n1. $0 \\le \\rho_{\\alpha} \\le 1$ and $\\rho_{\\alpha}$ is smooth.\n2. $\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{\\alpha} \\subseteq U_{\\alpha}$, and the family of supports $\\{\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{\\alpha}\\}$ is **locally finite**.\n3. $\\sum_{\\alpha \\in A}\\rho_{\\alpha} \\equiv 1$ (the locally finite sum is well-defined pointwise and is smooth).\n\nWe call $\\{\\rho_{\\alpha}\\}$ a **smooth partition of unity subordinate to $\\mathcal{U}$**. In particular, every open cover of $M$ has a subordinate smooth partition of unity.\n\nThe three requirements are exactly those of `def.partition-of-unity`, with smoothness in place of continuity.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#496c6a99'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof**. The two ingredients are bump functions on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and paracompactness of the manifold (existence of locally finite refinements).\n\n**Step 1: Construct bump functions**. Set\n\n$$b(t) = \\begin{cases} e^{-1/t}, & t > 0,\\\\ 0, & t \\le 0,\\end{cases}\\qquad \\beta(x) = \\frac{b(1 - \\lVert x\\rVert^{2})}{b(1 - \\lVert x\\rVert^{2}) + b(\\lVert x\\rVert^{2} - \\tfrac{1}{4})}.$$\n\n All derivatives of $b$ at $t = 0$ are $0$ ($e^{-1/t}$ tends to $0$ faster than any polynomial), so $b$ is smooth. The two denominator terms never vanish simultaneously, and their sum is positive (for $\\lVert x\\rVert \\in [\\tfrac{1}{2}, 1]$ exactly one term is positive), so $\\beta$ is smooth. Also, $\\beta \\equiv 1$ when $\\lVert x\\rVert \\le \\tfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta \\equiv 0$ when $\\lVert x\\rVert \\ge 1$. Translating and rescaling $\\beta$ produces a bump function on any ball.\n\n**Step 2: Take a locally finite refinement**. The manifold $M$ is second countable, Hausdorff, and locally Euclidean, hence paracompact (the hypothesis of `prop.paracompact-partition-unity`). The open cover $\\mathcal{U}$ therefore has a locally finite refinement $\\{V_{\\alpha}\\}$, and we can require each $\\overline{V_{\\alpha}}$ to be compact and contained in a coordinate chart $W_{\\alpha} \\subseteq U_{\\alpha}$. First refine by coordinate balls within $U_{\\alpha}$, then refine again and shrink the closures.\n\n**Step 3: Put a positive bump function on each piece**. Fix $\\alpha$. Since $\\overline{V_{\\alpha}}$ is compact and contained in the open set $W_{\\alpha}$, take a finite cover $B(x_{j}, s_{j})$ and choose $s_{j}>0$ so that $B(x_{j}, 2s_{j}) \\subseteq W_{\\alpha}$. Set $r_{j}=2s_{j}$. Then $B(x_{j}, r_{j}/2)$ still covers $\\overline{V_{\\alpha}}$ and $B(x_{j}, r_{j}) \\subseteq W_{\\alpha}$. On each ball take a bump function $\\beta_{j} \\ge 0$ (positive inside $B(x_{j}, r_{j}/2)$ and zero outside $B(x_{j}, r_{j})$), and set $\\psi_{\\alpha} = \\sum_{j} \\beta_{j}$. Then $\\psi_{\\alpha} \\in C^{\\infty}(M)$, $\\psi_{\\alpha} \\ge 0$, and $\\psi_{\\alpha} > 0$ on $V_{\\alpha}$ (every point lies in some $B(x_{j}, r_{j}/2)$), and $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi_{\\alpha} \\subseteq \\bigcup_{j} \\overline{B(x_{j}, r_{j})} \\subseteq W_{\\alpha} \\subseteq U_{\\alpha}$.\n\n**Step 4: Normalize**. Since $\\{V_{\\alpha}\\}$ covers $M$, every point lies in at least one $V_{\\alpha}$, so $\\Psi = \\sum_{\\alpha} \\psi_{\\alpha} > 0$ holds everywhere. The family of supports $\\{\\operatorname{supp}\\psi_{\\alpha}\\}$ is locally finite because it refines the locally finite family $\\{V_{\\alpha}\\}$. Thus $\\Psi$ is a locally finite sum and is smooth. Set $\\rho_{\\alpha} = \\psi_{\\alpha}/\\Psi$. Then $\\rho_{\\alpha}$ is smooth, $0 \\le \\rho_{\\alpha} \\le 1$ and $\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{\\alpha} = \\operatorname{supp}\\psi_{\\alpha} \\subseteq U_{\\alpha}$ hold, and $\\sum_{\\alpha} \\rho_{\\alpha} = \\Psi/\\Psi = 1$. All three requirements are met. ∎\n\n**Why local finiteness is essential**: Without it, $\\sum\\rho_{\\alpha}$ may have infinitely many nonzero terms near a point, and the sum need not converge or be smooth. Local finiteness gives only finitely many terms near each point, so smoothness, integrability, and linearity carry over from finite sums.\n\n**What it is used to prove**: Existence of Riemannian metrics (`def.riemannian-metric`), existence of fiber metrics on vector bundles (`prop.bundle-metric`), integration of top-degree forms (`def.integral-form`), and characterization of orientability by a nowhere-vanishing $n$-form (`prop.volume-form-orientation`). Each proof constructs the objects locally and glues them with a partition of unity.\n\n**Second countability is essential**: The long line is locally Euclidean and Hausdorff but is neither separable nor paracompact, so partitions of unity cannot in general be constructed on it. The technical hypotheses in the manifold definition matter.\n\n**Continuous and smooth versions**: The continuous version only requires paracompactness (`prop.paracompact-partition-unity`). The smooth version uses bump functions to obtain smoothness. On manifolds, these are two regularity levels of the same construction.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3f1f85f7'
    },
  },
  'thm.chebyshev-estimate': {
    statement: {
      en: "There exist constants $0 < c_{1} < c_{2}$ such that for every $x \\ge 2$,\n\n$$c_{1}\\,\\frac{x}{\\log x} < \\pi(x) < c_{2}\\,\\frac{x}{\\log x} .$$\n\n($\\pi(x) \\asymp x/\\log x$. Examples of constants from the elementary proof are $0.28$ and $6$.)",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1397c7e8'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof**: The proof uses only elementary binomial coefficient estimates. Write $\\theta(x) := \\sum_{p \\le x} \\log p$ (the sum runs only over primes $p \\le x$). First prove, for integers $x$, the estimate $0.3\\,x/\\log x < \\pi(x) < 6\\,x/\\log x$. For real arguments the lower bound is weakened to $0.28\\,x/\\log x$, while the upper bound stays the same.\n\n**Lower bound.** For an integer $n \\ge 1$, set $N := \\binom{2n}{n}$. Since $\\sum_{k=0}^{2n}\\binom{2n}{k} = 4^{n}$ and $\\binom{2n}{n}$ is the largest term, we obtain\n\n$$\\frac{4^{n}}{2n+1} \\le N \\le 4^{n}.$$\n\nOn the other hand, factor $N$ into primes. By Legendre's formula,\n\n$$v_{p}(N) = \\sum_{k \\ge 1}\\left(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2n}{p^{k}}\\right\\rfloor - 2\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{p^{k}}\\right\\rfloor\\right),$$\n\nEach term is either $0$ or $1$, and given $p^{k} \\le 2n$, the number of admissible $k$ is at most $\\log(2n)/\\log p$. Hence $v_{p}(N) \\le \\log(2n)/\\log p$, that is, $p^{v_{p}(N)} \\le 2n$. Thus\n\n$$N = \\prod_{p \\le 2n} p^{v_{p}(N)} \\le (2n)^{\\pi(2n)}.$$\n\nCombine this with $N \\ge 4^{n}/(2n+1)$ and take logarithms to get\n\n$$\\pi(2n) \\ge \\frac{n\\log 4 - \\log(2n+1)}{\\log(2n)} = \\frac{\\log 4}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2n}{\\log(2n)} - \\frac{\\log(2n+1)}{\\log(2n)}.$$\n\nFor $x := 2n \\ge 16$, we have $\\log(2n+1)/\\log(2n) \\le 2$ and $2\\log x/x \\le 2\\log 16/16 < 0.7$, so $\\pi(x) \\ge 1.386\\,x/\\log x - 2 \\ge (1.386 - 0.693)\\,x/\\log x > 0.3\\,x/\\log x$. For odd $x = 2n+1 \\ge 17$, we have $\\pi(x) \\ge \\pi(2n) \\ge 0.3\\cdot 2n/\\log(2n)$. Also, $x/\\log x \\le \\frac{x}{2n}\\cdot\\frac{2n}{\\log(2n)}$ and $\\frac{x}{2n} = 1+\\frac{1}{2n} < 1.07$, giving $\\pi(x) > \\frac{0.3}{1.07}\\,x/\\log x > 0.28\\,x/\\log x$.\n\n**Upper bound.** First estimate $\\theta$. For even $n = 2r$, the product of the primes in $(r, 2r]$ divides $\\binom{2r}{r} \\le 4^{r}$, so\n\n$$\\theta(2r) - \\theta(r) \\le 2r\\log 2.$$\n\nFor odd $n = 2r+1$, the product of the primes in $(r+1, 2r+1]$ divides $\\binom{2r+1}{r}$. Since binomial coefficients of odd degree occur in equal pairs, $2\\binom{2r+1}{r} \\le 2^{2r+1}$, so $\\binom{2r+1}{r} \\le 2^{2r}$, that is,\n\n$$\\theta(2r+1) - \\theta(r+1) \\le 2r\\log 2.$$\n\nTogether these give $\\theta(n) \\le n\\log 4$ (induct on $n$, checking the base case $n = 1, 2$ directly). Now count the primes: $\\pi(\\sqrt{x}) \\le \\sqrt{x}$. Each $\\sqrt{x} < p \\le x$ satisfies $\\log p \\ge \\tfrac12\\log x$, and the number of such $p$ is at most $(\\theta(x) - \\theta(\\sqrt{x}))/\\tfrac12\\log x$. Thus for integers $x \\ge 2$,\n\n$$\\pi(x) \\le \\sqrt{x} + \\frac{2\\theta(x)}{\\log x} \\le \\sqrt{x} + \\frac{4x\\log 2}{\\log x}.$$\n\nFor $x \\ge 16$, we have $\\log x \\le \\sqrt{x}$, so $\\sqrt{x} \\le x/\\log x$ and hence $\\pi(x) \\le (1 + 4\\log 2)\\,x/\\log x < 6\\,x/\\log x$. For $2 \\le x < 16$, we have $6x/\\log x > 6$ and $\\pi(x) \\le 6$, so the conclusion holds immediately.\n\n**From integers to reals.** Let $x \\ge 2$ be real and set $m := \\lfloor x \\rfloor$. Then $\\pi(x) = \\pi(m)$. When $m \\ge 16$, the function $t/\\log t$ is increasing on $[16, \\infty)$, so $m/\\log m \\le x/\\log x$ and the upper bound carries over directly. This only gives $\\pi(x) \\ge 0.3\\,m/\\log m$ for the lower bound, so use $x/\\log x \\le (1+1/m)\\,m/\\log m$ instead (by $x < m+1$). When $m \\ge 16$, the factor satisfies $1+1/m < 1.07$, so $\\pi(x) > \\frac{0.3}{1.07}\\,x/\\log x > 0.28\\,x/\\log x$. When $m < 16$, check the integers individually ($\\pi$ is piecewise constant and $t/\\log t$ increases on $[3, \\infty)$). ∎\n\n**Why these bounds suffice**: The constants are only used for the qualitative conclusion that $\\pi(x)$ and $x/\\log x$ have the same order of growth. Improving the constants to arbitrary precision is the work of Chebyshev's original paper, while sharpening the upper bound to $\\pi(x) \\sim x/\\log x$ requires complex analysis (the prime number theorem, see `thm.prime-number-theorem`).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#772b257b'
    },
  },
  'thm.radical-solvable': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $F$ be a field of **characteristic zero**, let $f \\in F[x]$ be nonzero, and let $K$ be the splitting field of $f$. Then\n\nThe roots of $f$ can be expressed by **radicals** (arithmetic operations and extraction of $n$th roots) from elements of $F$ $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ is a **solvable group**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#66154e45'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof**. First make precise what it means to be solvable by radicals: There is a tower of fields $F=F_{0}\\subseteq F_{1}\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq F_{m}$ with $F_{i}=F_{i-1}(\\alpha_{i})$ and $\\alpha_{i}^{n_{i}}\\in F_{i-1}$ ($1\\le i\\le m$), and $K\\subseteq F_{m}$. In characteristic zero, $K/F$ is Galois (a separable splitting field). We prove the two directions separately.\n\n**Lemma (one Kummer step)**: Suppose $F$ contains a primitive $m$th root of unity $\\zeta$ and $E/F$ is a cyclic extension of degree $m$. Then $E=F(\\beta)$ and $\\beta^{m}\\in F$. To prove this, choose a generator $\\sigma$ and regard $\\sigma$ on $E$ as a $F$-linear transformation. We have $\\sigma^{m}=1$, and $x^{m}-1$ splits over $F$ with no repeated roots, so $\\sigma$ is diagonalizable and all its eigenvalues are $m$th roots of unity. **The key is that the minimal polynomial of $\\sigma$ is exactly $x^{m}-1$**. By Dedekind's linear independence theorem (Lemma 1 in the Galois correspondence proof: pairwise distinct automorphisms are linearly independent as functions), $1,\\sigma,\\ldots,\\sigma^{m-1}$ is linearly independent over $E$. Thus no nonzero polynomial of degree $<m$ annihilates $\\sigma$, and the minimal polynomial has degree $\\ge m$. Also, $\\sigma^{m}=1$ implies that it divides $x^{m}-1$ (of degree $m$), so the minimal polynomial is $=x^{m}-1$. The minimal polynomial also divides the characteristic polynomial $\\chi_{\\sigma}$ (by Cayley–Hamilton, $\\chi_{\\sigma}$ annihilates $\\sigma$ and hence is divisible by the minimal polynomial). Since $\\deg\\chi_{\\sigma}=\\dim_{F}E=m=\\deg\\mu_{\\sigma}$ and both polynomials are monic of the same degree, $\\chi_{\\sigma}=\\mu_{\\sigma}=x^{m}-1$. Thus the $m$ eigenvalues are **all** the $m$th roots of unity, including the primitive root $\\zeta^{k}$. Take a corresponding eigenvector $\\beta$ with $\\sigma(\\beta)=\\zeta^{k}\\beta$. Then $\\sigma(\\beta^{m})=\\beta^{m}$, so $\\beta^{m}$ lies in the fixed field of $\\sigma$. The fixed field of the cyclic extension is precisely $F$, so $\\beta^{m}\\in F$. Also, $\\beta\\notin F$ (otherwise $\\sigma(\\beta)=\\beta$). Since $\\zeta^{k}$ is primitive, $\\sigma^{d}(\\beta)=\\beta$ if and only if $m\\mid d$. Thus the subgroup fixing $F(\\beta)$ contains only the identity, so $[F(\\beta):F]=m$ and hence $E=F(\\beta)$. ∎\n\n**$\\Leftarrow$ (solvable group $\\implies$ solvable by radicals)**: Suppose $G=\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ is solvable. First assume that $F$ contains a primitive $n$th root of unity, with $n=|G|$. Induct on $|G|$. When $|G|=1$, we have $K=F$ and there is nothing to prove. Suppose $|G|>1$. The solvable group has a maximal proper normal subgroup $H$, so $G/H$ is simple and abelian, hence cyclic of prime order $p$. Set $E=K^{H}$. The Galois correspondence gives $[E:F]=p$, with $\\operatorname{Gal}(E/F)=G/H$ cyclic. Since $F$ contains $\\zeta_{p}$, the Kummer step gives $E=F(\\beta)$ and $\\beta^{p}\\in F$. Also, $K/E$ is Galois with group $H$, and $H$ is solvable with $|H|<|G|$. Since $E$ contains a primitive $|H|$th root of unity ($|H|$ divides $n$), the induction hypothesis gives a radical tower from $E$ to $K$. Appending it to the step $F\\subseteq E$ gives a radical tower for $K$ over $F$.\n\n**Removing the roots-of-unity hypothesis**: In general, $F$ need not contain $\\zeta_{n}$. Choose $N$ to be a common multiple of $1,2,\\ldots,n$ (for example, $n!$), and set $F_{1}=F(\\zeta_{N})$ and $K_{1}=K(\\zeta_{N})$. The extension $K_{1}/F_{1}$ is Galois, and the restriction map $\\operatorname{Gal}(K_{1}/F_{1})\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ is injective, so $G_{1}=\\operatorname{Gal}(K_{1}/F_{1})$ is solvable and $|G_{1}|\\le n$. The field $F_{1}$ contains all roots of unity of orders $\\le n$, in particular $\\zeta_{|G_{1}|}$. The case already proved gives a radical tower for $K_{1}$ over $F_{1}$. Also, $F\\subseteq F_{1}=F(\\zeta_{N})$ is itself a one-step radical extension ($\\zeta_{N}^{N}=1\\in F$). Combining these towers places $K\\subseteq K_{1}$ inside a radical tower over $F$, so the roots of $f$ are solvable by radicals.\n\n**$\\implies$ (solvable by radicals $\\implies$ solvable group)**: Let $F=F_{0}\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq F_{m}$ be a radical tower with $K\\subseteq F_{m}$ and $\\alpha_{i}^{n_{i}}\\in F_{i-1}$. Choose $N$ to be a common multiple of all the $n_{i}$ and set $F^{\\prime}=F(\\zeta_{N})$ and $E_{i}=F_{i}(\\zeta_{N})$. Then $E_{i}=E_{i-1}(\\alpha_{i})$ and $\\alpha_{i}^{n_{i}}\\in E_{i-1}$, and this is still a radical tower, with base field $E_{0}=F^{\\prime}$ containing $\\zeta_{n_{i}}$. Let $M$ be the Galois closure of $E_{m}$ over $F^{\\prime}$ (the splitting field of the product of the minimal polynomials of the generators). Then $M/F^{\\prime}$ is Galois and $K^{\\prime}=K(\\zeta_{N})\\subseteq M$.\n\n**Taking closures at each level**: Let $M_{i}$ be the compositum of all conjugates of $E_{i}$ generated under $F^{\\prime}$. Then $M_{0}=F^{\\prime}$ and $M_{m}=M$, and each $M_{i}/F^{\\prime}$ is Galois. The key is that $M_{i}$ is obtained from $M_{i-1}$ by adjoining several conjugates of $\\alpha_{i}$, denoted by $\\beta$, with $\\beta^{n_{i}}\\in M_{i-1}$. Write $\\beta=\\sigma(\\alpha_{i})$. Then $\\beta^{n_{i}}=\\sigma(\\alpha_{i}^{n_{i}})\\in\\sigma(F_{i-1})\\subseteq M_{i-1}$. Thus $M_{i}/M_{i-1}$ is generated by several $n_{i}$th roots, and $\\zeta_{n_{i}}\\in F^{\\prime}\\subseteq M_{i-1}$, so it is a Kummer extension. Any $\\tau\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(M_{i}/M_{i-1})$ sends each generator $\\beta$ to $\\zeta_{n_{i}}^{k_{\\beta}}\\beta$, and $\\tau\\mapsto(k_{\\beta})_{\\beta}$ is an injective homomorphism into $(\\mathbb{Z}/n_{i})^{r}$. Hence $\\operatorname{Gal}(M_{i}/M_{i-1})$ is abelian.\n\n**Solvable series**: Since $M_{i}/M_{i-1}$ is Galois, $\\operatorname{Gal}(M/M_{i})\\trianglelefteq\\operatorname{Gal}(M/M_{i-1})$, and the quotient is isomorphic to $\\operatorname{Gal}(M_{i}/M_{i-1})$, which is abelian. Thus $\\operatorname{Gal}(M/F^{\\prime})\\ \\trianglerighteq\\ \\operatorname{Gal}(M/M_{1})\\ \\trianglerighteq\\ \\cdots\\ \\trianglerighteq\\ \\operatorname{Gal}(M/M_{m})=1$ is a normal series with abelian quotients, so $\\operatorname{Gal}(M/F^{\\prime})$ is solvable.\n\n**Returning to $K$**: The extension $K^{\\prime}/F$ is Galois (as a compositum of two normal extensions). The restriction map $\\operatorname{Gal}(M/F^{\\prime})\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\\prime}/F^{\\prime})$ is surjective, so $\\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\\prime}/F^{\\prime})$ is solvable. We also have the exact sequence $1\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\\prime}/F^{\\prime})\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\\prime}/F)\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(F^{\\prime}/F)\\to1$ (the cyclotomic extension is Galois and restriction is surjective). Both ends are solvable and $\\operatorname{Gal}(F^{\\prime}/F)$ is abelian, so $\\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\\prime}/F)$ is solvable. Finally, $\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ is a quotient of $\\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\\prime}/F)$. The extension $K^{\\prime}/F$ is normal, and every automorphism of $K$ over $F$ extends to $K^{\\prime}$. A quotient of a solvable group is solvable, so $\\operatorname{Gal}(K/F)$ is solvable. ∎\n\n⭐ **Why characteristic zero is required**: Both directions use that $x^{m}-1$ has no repeated roots (so $\\sigma$ is diagonalizable), as well as separability. In characteristic $p$, pure radical extensions must be replaced by Artin–Schreier extensions $x^{p}-x-a$, and the conclusion must be reformulated.\n\n📌 **This theorem is the entry point to the quintic problem**: It turns the question of whether a radical formula exists into a purely group-theoretic question ($S_{n}$ is solvable $\\iff n\\le4$, see the solvability of symmetric groups). When applying it in reverse, distinguish the absence of a radical solution from the absence of any solution: $\\operatorname{Gal}(f)$ is not solvable $\\implies$ has no solution by radicals.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#35d499a6'
    },
  },
  'lem.shrink': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X$ be a **normal** space and let $\\mathcal{U} = \\{ U_{i} \\}_{i \\in I}$ be a **locally finite open cover** of $X$. Then there is an open cover of $X$, denoted by $\\mathcal{V} = \\{ V_{i} \\}_{i \\in I}$ (with **the same index set**), such that\n\n$$V_{i} \\subseteq \\overline{V_{i}} \\subseteq U_{i}\\qquad (i \\in I),$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{V}$ is still locally finite. Such a family $\\mathcal{V}$ is called a **shrinking** of $\\mathcal{U}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e90ee2c4'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof (well-order the index set and shrink one member at a time)**. Choose a well-order on $I$ (using the axiom of choice). Construct open sets $V_{i}$ such that $\\overline{V_{i}} \\subseteq U_{i}$ while preserving coverage. Maintain the invariant that for every $i$, the family\n$$\\mathcal{W}_{i} := \\{\\, V_{j} : j < i \\,\\} \\cup \\{\\, U_{k} : k \\ge i \\,\\}$$\nis a cover of $X$.\n**Successor step** ($i \\to i^{+}$): Set $A_{i} := X \\setminus \\bigcup \\mathcal{W}_{i^{+}}$.\n\nThe set $A_{i}$ is **closed**.\n\nMoreover, $x \\notin \\mathcal{W}_{i^{+}}$ implies $x \\notin V_{j}$ ($j < i$) and $x \\notin U_{k}$ ($k > i$), so the invariant forces $x$ to lie in $U_{i}$. Hence $A_{i} \\subseteq U_{i}$.\n\nApply normality to the disjoint closed sets $A_{i}$ and $X \\setminus U_{i}$ to obtain an open set $V_{i}$ such that $A_{i} \\subseteq V_{i}$ and $\\overline{V_{i}} \\subseteq U_{i}$. This is the standard reformulation of normality: between a closed set and an open set containing it, there is an open set whose closure still lies in the larger open set.\n\nThus $\\mathcal{W}_{i^{+}}$ still covers $X$.\n**Limit step** ($i$ is a limit ordinal): Let $x \\in X$.\n\nIf $x \\in U_{k}$ for some $k \\ge i$, the point is already covered.\n\nOtherwise, $x \\notin U_{k}$ for every $k \\ge i$, and the cover $\\mathcal{W}_{i}$ gives some $j < i$ such that $x \\in U_{j}$.\n\nBy **point finiteness** (locally finite $\\implies$ point finite), $J := \\{\\, j < i : x \\in U_{j} \\,\\}$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nTake $j := \\max J$. Then for every $k > j$ we have $x \\notin U_{k}$, so $x \\in A_{j} \\subseteq V_{j}$.\n**Completion**: By $V_{i} \\subseteq U_{i}$, an open neighborhood meeting only finitely many $U_{i}$ also meets only finitely many $V_{i}$. Hence $\\mathcal{V}$ is locally finite. To check coverage, given $x$, point finiteness gives an index $i$ greater than all indices of members containing $x$ among the $U_{j}$. Applying the covering property to $\\mathcal{W}_{i}$ then gives $x \\in V_{j}$ (for some $j < i$).\n\n∎\n\n**Why local finiteness is essential**: The limit ordinal step uses the fact that only finitely many members contain $x$. A general open cover need not have this property and may admit no shrinking. This explains why normality does not imply paracompactness.\n\n⭐ There are two uses: turning a locally finite open refinement from paracompactness into a **locally finite closed refinement** by taking closures, and turning a locally finite open refinement into a **partition of unity** by using Urysohn's lemma between $A_{i}$ and $X \\setminus U_{i}$ and then normalizing.\n\n⚠️ After shrinking, **the closures also lie in the corresponding original members**. This is stronger than refining again, which only preserves coverage and local finiteness and does not guarantee containment of closures in the corresponding members.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6c2ec690'
    },
  },
  'thm.paracompact-hausdorff-normal': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X$ be a **paracompact Hausdorff** space. Then $X$ is **normal**. The proof first establishes the stronger intermediate conclusion that $X$ is already **regular**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a2106696'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Step 1: Paracompact Hausdorff $\\implies$ regular.** Suppose $x \\in X$, with $F \\subseteq X$ closed and $x \\notin F$.\n\nFor each $y \\in F$, Hausdorffness gives disjoint open sets $O_{1} \\ni y$ and $O_{2} \\ni x$.\n\nTake $G_{y} := O_{1}$. From $O_{1} \\subseteq X \\setminus O_{2}$ we obtain $\\overline{G_{y}} \\subseteq X \\setminus O_{2}$, so $x \\notin \\overline{G_{y}}$.\n\nThus\n$$\\mathcal{A} := \\{\\, X \\setminus F \\,\\} \\cup \\{\\, G_{y} : y \\in F \\,\\}$$\nis an open cover.\n\nTake a locally finite open refinement $\\mathcal{C}$ and set $W := \\bigcup \\{\\, C \\in \\mathcal{C} : C \\cap F \\ne \\emptyset \\,\\}$.\n\nThen $F \\subseteq W$. A point of $F$, say $y$, lies in some $C$. The case $C \\subseteq X \\setminus F$ is impossible because it contains $y$. Hence $C \\subseteq G_{y}$, so $C$ is included in $W$.\n\nOn the other hand, each member included in $W$, say $C$, lies in some $G_{y}$, so $x \\notin \\overline{C}$.\n\nSince the closure of the union of a locally finite family equals the union of the closures, $\\overline{W} = \\bigcup \\{\\, \\overline{C} : C \\cap F \\ne \\emptyset \\,\\}$ does not contain $x$.\n\nThus $W \\ni F$ and $X \\setminus \\overline{W} \\ni x$ are disjoint open sets separating $F$ and $x$.\n**Step 2: Regular $+$ paracompact $\\implies$ normal.** Let $A, B$ be disjoint closed sets.\n\nFor every $x \\in A$, regularity ($x \\notin B$) gives an open set $O_{x} \\ni x$ such that $\\overline{O_{x}} \\cap B = \\emptyset$.\n\nThen $\\mathcal{A} := \\{\\, X \\setminus A \\,\\} \\cup \\{\\, O_{x} : x \\in A \\,\\}$ is an open cover. Take a locally finite open refinement $\\mathcal{D}$ and set $U := \\bigcup \\{\\, D \\in \\mathcal{D} : D \\cap A \\ne \\emptyset \\,\\}$.\n\nAs above, $A \\subseteq U$, and each such $D$ lies in some $O_{x}$, giving $\\overline{D} \\cap B = \\emptyset$. Therefore $\\overline{U} \\cap B = \\emptyset$.\n\nThus $U \\supseteq A$ and $X \\setminus \\overline{U} \\supseteq B$ are disjoint open sets separating $A, B$.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ This result places paracompactness in the hierarchy of separation axioms: **compact Hausdorff $\\implies$ paracompact Hausdorff $\\implies$ normal $\\implies$ regular $\\implies$ Hausdorff**.\n\nThis supplies the normality used with Urysohn's lemma in Pack 40. Compact Hausdorff spaces ($[0,1]$, $\\beta I$) are paracompact.\n\n⚠️ **Hausdorffness is essential**: The two-point Sierpiński space is paracompact (its open covers are finite and hence locally finite), but it fails to be $T_{1}$ and therefore is not normal.\n\n⚠️ **The converse also fails**: A normal space need not be paracompact ($[0, \\omega_{1})$). Thus paracompactness is a separate condition from normality.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0a80e695'
    },
  },
  'thm.brouwer-fixed-point': {
    statement: {
      en: "Suppose $f : \\overline{\\mathbb{D}} \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{D}}$ is continuous ($\\overline{\\mathbb{D}} = \\{\\, |z| \\le 1 \\,\\}$). Then there exists $z \\in \\overline{\\mathbb{D}}$ such that $f(z) = z$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#099e41c0'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof (continuous case)**: Suppose for contradiction that $f$ has no fixed point, so $f(z) \\ne z$ for every $z \\in \\overline{\\mathbb{D}}$. For each $z$ define\n\n$$r(z) := z + t(z)\\,(z - f(z)),$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$a(z) := \\langle z,\\ z - f(z)\\rangle,\\qquad b(z) := |z - f(z)|^{2} > 0,$$\n\n$$t(z) := \\frac{-a(z) + \\sqrt{a(z)^{2} + b(z)\\,(1 - |z|^{2})}}{b(z)}.$$\n\n**Why $r$ lies on $S^{1}$**: Expand $|z + t(z - f(z))|^{2} = 1$ to obtain\n\n$$b(z)\\,t^{2} + 2a(z)\\,t + (|z|^{2} - 1) = 0,$$\n\nHere $t(z)$ is precisely the nonnegative root of this quadratic equation ($b > 0$. Since $1 - |z|^{2} \\ge 0$, the radicand satisfies $\\ge a^{2}$, so the square root satisfies $\\ge |a| \\ge -a$ and hence $t \\ge 0$). Therefore $|r(z)| = 1$.\n\n**$r$ is continuous**: $f$ is continuous, and $a, b$ is computed from inner products and norms and is therefore continuous as well. Since $b(z) \\ge |z - f(z)|^{2}$ is everywhere $> 0$, taking the square root and dividing are both well-defined.\n\n**$r$ is the identity on the boundary**: If $|z| = 1$, then $a(z) = \\langle z, z - f(z)\\rangle = 1 - \\langle z, f(z)\\rangle \\ge 0$ (because $|f(z)| \\le 1$). Substituting into $t(z)$ gives $t(z) = 0$, hence $r(z) = z$.\n\nThus $r : \\overline{\\mathbb{D}} \\to S^{1}$ is a continuous retraction ($r|_{S^{1}} = \\operatorname{id}$), contradicting `thm.no-retraction-disk`. Therefore $f$ has a fixed point. ∎\n\n**Why complex analysis alone does not suffice**: The construction of $r$ above uses only continuity of $f$. The topological input is $\\pi_{1}(S^{1}) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}$. The proof of `thm.no-retraction-disk` is an immediate application of functoriality: A retraction $r$ would force $r_{*} \\circ i_{*} = \\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb{Z}}$ to factor through $\\pi_{1}(\\overline{\\mathbb{D}}) = 1$.\n\n**An alternative for the holomorphic case (Rouché)**: Suppose $f$ is holomorphic on $\\mathbb{D}$, continuous up to the boundary, and $|f| \\le 1$. Then on $|z| = 1$, $|f(z) - z| \\ge |z| - |f(z)| \\ge 0$, with equality only when $f(z) = z$ (already a fixed point). Otherwise $f - z$ has no boundary zeros. By `thm.rouche`, compare $f - z$ with $-z$ ($|-z| = 1 > |f|$). The latter has exactly one zero inside $\\mathbb{D}$, so $f - z$ also has exactly one zero. This argument covers only the holomorphic case.\n\n💡 **Equivalent formulation**: There is no continuous retraction of $\\overline{\\mathbb{D}}$ onto $S^{1}$. Brouwer's theorem is equivalent to saying that $S^{1}$ is not a retract of the disk (see the edge involving `thm.borsuk-ulam` and `thm.no-retraction-disk`).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ea8b6bb2'
    },
  },
  'thm.simplicial-approximation': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $K, L$ be simplicial complexes, with $K$ **finite**, and let $f : |K| \\to |L|$ be continuous. Write $K^{(m)}$ for the result of applying **barycentric subdivision** to $K$ a total of $m$ times ($|K^{(m)}| = |K|$, with simplex diameters shrinking by a fixed factor). Then there exist $m \\ge 1$ and a **simplicial map**\n\n$$g : K^{(m)} \\longrightarrow L$$\n\nsuch that $|g| \\simeq f$ ($|g|$ and $f$ are homotopic). Such a map $g$ is called a **simplicial approximation** to $f$.\n\n**Corollary**: Let $K$ be a finite simplicial complex. The inclusion $C_{*}(K) \\hookrightarrow S_{*}(|K|)$ induces an isomorphism\n\n$$H_{p}(K) \\;\\cong\\; H_{p}(|K|) \\qquad \\forall p.$$\n\nThus the singular homology of a polyhedron can be computed as the simplicial homology of a triangulation, which is a **finite matrix** calculation.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7f025703'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**The proof has two steps**: First prove that the star condition $\\Longrightarrow$ gives a simplicial map and a homotopy, then prove that sufficiently fine subdivision makes the star condition hold.\n\n**Lemma 1 (star condition)**: Suppose $g : \\operatorname{Vert}(K) \\to \\operatorname{Vert}(L)$ satisfies\n\n$$f\\big(\\operatorname{st}_{K}(v)\\big) \\;\\subseteq\\; \\operatorname{st}_{L}\\big(g(v)\\big) \\qquad \\forall v .$$\n\nThen (i) $g$ sends every simplex of $K$ to a simplex of $L$, hence is simplicial, and (ii) $|g| \\simeq f$.\n\nProof of (i): Suppose $v_{0}, \\ldots, v_{p}$ span, in $K$, a simplex $\\sigma$. Choose an interior point $x \\in \\sigma^{\\circ}$. For each $i$ we have $x \\in \\operatorname{st}_{K}(v_{i})$, so $f(x) \\in \\operatorname{st}_{L}(g(v_{i}))$. Hence\n\n$$f(x) \\;\\in\\; \\bigcap_{i=0}^{p} \\operatorname{st}_{L}\\big(g(v_{i})\\big).$$\n\nThe stars of a collection of vertices in $L$ have nonempty intersection if and only if the vertices span a simplex ($\\bigcap \\operatorname{st}(w_{i})$ is precisely the interior of the simplex with vertex set $\\{w_{i}\\}$, and if it is nonempty, that simplex exists). Thus $\\{g(v_{0}), \\ldots, g(v_{p})\\}$ span a simplex of $L$, and $g$ is simplicial.\n\n(ii): Suppose $x \\in \\sigma^{\\circ}$. Set $\\tau = g(\\sigma)$, the simplex with vertices $g(v_{0}), \\ldots, g(v_{p})$. By the argument in (i), $f(x) \\in \\operatorname{st}_{L}(\\tau)$. That is, $f(x)$ lies in the interior of a simplex **containing $\\tau$**, denoted by $\\rho$. Also, $g(x) \\in \\tau \\subseteq \\rho$. The simplex $\\rho$ is convex, so the line segment satisfies $[f(x), g(x)] \\subseteq \\rho \\subseteq |L|$. Define $H(x, t) = (1 - t)\\,g(x) + t\\,f(x)$. It is continuous on each $\\sigma^{\\circ}$, and the formulas agree on boundaries of adjacent simplices (for a given point $x$, the choice of $\\rho$ gives the same convex hull regardless of the simplex used). Thus $H : |K| \\times I \\to |L|$ is continuous and is a homotopy from $g$ to $f$. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2 (sufficiently fine subdivision)**: $\\{\\operatorname{st}_{L}(w)\\}_{w \\in \\operatorname{Vert}(L)}$ is an open cover of $|L|$. By continuity of $f$, $\\{f^{-1}(\\operatorname{st}_{L}(w))\\}$ is an open cover of $|K|$. Since $|K|$ is compact, choose a Lebesgue number $\\delta > 0$ (`def.lebesgue-number`). Every subset of $|K|$ with diameter $< \\delta$ lies in some $f^{-1}(\\operatorname{st}_{L}(w))$, so its image lies in some $\\operatorname{st}_{L}(w)$.\n\nRepeated barycentric subdivision makes the diameters tend to zero. If $\\dim K = n$, then $\\operatorname{mesh}(K^{(m)}) \\le \\big(\\tfrac{n}{n+1}\\big)^{m}\\,\\operatorname{mesh}(K)$ (every subdivided simplex lies in an original simplex and has diameter at most $n/(n+1)$ times the original diameter). Choose $m$ such that $\\operatorname{mesh}(K^{(m)}) < \\delta/2$.\n\nFor each vertex of $K^{(m)}$, say $v$, the set $\\operatorname{st}(v)$ has diameter $\\le 2\\,\\operatorname{mesh}(K^{(m)}) < \\delta$, so $f(\\operatorname{st}(v)) \\subseteq \\operatorname{st}_{L}(w)$ for some $w$. Set $g(v) := w$. By Lemma 1, $g$ is simplicial and $|g| \\simeq f$. ∎\n\n**Proof of the corollary (at the chain level)**: Write $\\iota_{m} : C_{*}(K^{(m)}) \\hookrightarrow S_{*}(|K|)$ and $\\iota : C_{*}(K) \\hookrightarrow S_{*}(|K|)$ for the inclusions. Apply the theorem to the identity map $\\operatorname{id} : |K| \\to |K|$ to obtain $m$ and a simplicial map $g : K^{(m)} \\to K$ such that $|g| \\simeq \\operatorname{id}$.\n\n- $\\iota\\,g_{*} = (|g|)_{*}\\,\\iota_{m}$ ($g$ is simplicial, and both sides give the same image on each simplex).\n\n- By `thm.homotopy-invariance`, $|g| \\simeq \\operatorname{id}$ gives a chain homotopy $(|g|)_{*} \\simeq \\operatorname{id}$, so $\\iota\\,g_{*} \\simeq \\iota_{m}$.\n\n- The barycentric subdivision chain maps $\\operatorname{sd}^{m} : C_{*}(K) \\to C_{*}(K^{(m)})$ and $\\iota_{m}$ satisfy $\\iota_{m}\\,\\operatorname{sd}^{m} = \\iota$ (subdivision leaves the geometric realization unchanged and subdivides the chains), and $\\operatorname{sd}^{m}$ induces a homology isomorphism (for the reverse chain homotopy, each subdivided simplex lies in an original simplex, so the cone construction applies).\n\n⚠️ **A step is missing here (omitted from the graph)**: $\\iota\\,g\\,\\mathrm{sd}^{m} = \\iota$ gives only a candidate right inverse for $\\iota$. It does not establish invertibility unless $g\\,\\mathrm{sd}^{m}$ is already known to be invertible, which is precisely what must be proved (the subscripts denoting induced maps have been suppressed in the formulas). To complete the proof, explicitly construct a chain map $r : S_{*}(|K|) \\to C_{*}(K)$ taking sufficiently fine singular chains to simplicial chains, and chain homotopies on both sides, $r\\,\\iota = \\operatorname{id}$ and $\\iota\\,r \\simeq \\operatorname{id}$. **These two chain homotopies are the missing steps at the chain level**. The lemmas on the star condition and subdivision are complete.\n\n**⚠️ Technical detail**: The proof uses two standard constructions, the chain map induced by a simplicial map and the prism construction for chain homotopies (see `def.chain-homotopy` and `thm.homotopy-invariance`). If $C_{*}$ is taken to be an **ordered** chain group, $g$ may collapse a simplex in degenerate cases, and the signs in the chain map must respect the vertex order. Homology here is defined using the quotient of ordered chain groups by degenerate chains. The sign details are omitted.\n\n**Limits and counterexamples**:\n\n- The **finiteness** of $K$ is essential. For infinite complexes, repeated barycentric subdivision need not make the mesh tend to zero (an infinite sequence of subdivisions can remain coarse), so the star condition may fail.\n\n- $L$ need not be finite. The theorem only uses that the vertex stars of $L$ form an open cover.\n\n- The open star $\\operatorname{st}(w)$ is generally not convex, so one cannot justify the homotopy by joining points in $\\operatorname{st}(w)$. Part (ii) of Lemma 1 uses that $f(x), g(x)$ lie in a common **closed simplex** $\\rho$, and $\\rho$ is convex.\n\n⭐ **Why the corollary matters**: It gives three conclusions at once. (i) Simplicial homology is a topological invariant independent of triangulation. (ii) Singular homology can be computed from ranks of finite matrices. (iii) The Euler characteristic formula carries over to singular homology (see the edge for `thm.euler-characteristic`).\n\n💡 In short: **continuous map $\\approx$ simplicial map after subdivision**. This gives a combinatorial description of the topology.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#63974b9d'
    },
  },
  'thm.schur-triangularization': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What this does**: Unitary diagonalization is available only for normal matrices (`thm.spectral-matrix`). If the requirement is relaxed to **upper triangular** form, every complex square matrix qualifies.\n\nLet $A \\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. There is a **unitary** matrix $U$ such that\n\n$$U^{*}AU = T ,$$\n\nwhere $T$ is **upper triangular** and the diagonal entries of $T$ are exactly the eigenvalues of $A$ (in any prescribed order).\n\nReal case: Every real square matrix is orthogonally similar to a **real Schur form**, which is block upper triangular with diagonal blocks of size $1\\times 1$ or $2\\times 2$ (the latter corresponding to conjugate pairs of complex eigenvalues).\n\n**Special case**: When $A$ is normal, $T$ must be diagonal. This is the spectral theorem (`thm.spectral-matrix`).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f38a08d0'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof** (induction on $n$). When $n = 1$, take $U = (1)$. Suppose $n \\ge 2$.\n\n\n\n**Choose an eigenvector**: The characteristic polynomial $\\chi_{A}(t) = \\det(tI - A)$ has degree $n$ and complex coefficients. By the **fundamental theorem of algebra**, it has a root $\\lambda$. Thus there exists $0 \\ne x \\in \\mathbb{C}^{n}$ such that $Ax = \\lambda x$. Normalize $x$, keeping the notation $x$, so that $\\lVert x\\rVert = 1$.\n\n\n\n**Extend to an orthonormal basis**: By **Gram–Schmidt**, extend $x$ to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{C}^{n}$, denoted by $u_{1} = x, u_{2}, \\ldots, u_{n}$. Set $U_{1} := (u_{1}, \\ldots, u_{n})$ (with these basis vectors as columns). Then $U_{1}$ is unitary and $U_{1}^{*}u_{j} = e_{j}$. Thus the first column of $U_{1}^{*}AU_{1}$ is $U_{1}^{*}Ax = \\lambda U_{1}^{*}x = \\lambda e_{1}$. The matrix has $\\lambda$ in the upper left, all $0$ in the lower left, a lower-right block of order $(n-1)$ denoted by $A_{1}$, and a row vector $w^{*}$ in the upper right.\n\n\n\n**Induction**: Apply the induction hypothesis to $A_{1}$. There is a unitary matrix of order $(n-1)$, denoted by $V$, such that $V^{*}A_{1}V = T_{1}$ is upper triangular. Set $U_{2} := \\operatorname{diag}(1, V)$ and $U := U_{1}U_{2}$. The matrix $U_{2}$ is unitary (it is block diagonal with two unitary blocks), so $U$ is unitary. Applying $U_{2}$ on both sides of $U_{1}^{*}AU_{1}$ leaves the upper-left entry $\\lambda$ unchanged, changes the upper-right block to $w^{*}V$, leaves the lower-left block equal to $0$, and changes the lower-right block to $V^{*}A_{1}V = T_{1}$. Thus $U^{*}AU$ is upper triangular ✓.\n\n\n\n**The diagonal entries are the eigenvalues**: $U^{*}AU$ and $A$ are similar, so they have the same characteristic polynomial. The characteristic polynomial of the block upper triangular matrix (upper left $\\lambda$, lower right $A_{1}$, lower left $0$) is $(t - \\lambda)\\chi_{A_{1}}(t)$. Therefore the multiset of diagonal entries of $U^{*}AU$ is exactly $\\operatorname{spec}(A)$. Here the diagonal entries of $T_{1}$ are the eigenvalues of $A_{1}$ by induction, and $\\operatorname{spec}(A) = \\{\\lambda\\} \\cup \\operatorname{spec}(A_{1})$.\n\n\n\n**Prescribing the order**: At each step, choose the first eigenvalue in the prescribed order that has yet to be placed, $\\lambda$ (it occurs in $\\operatorname{spec}(A)$ and hence has an eigenvector). When applying induction to $A_{1}$, use the order induced on $A_{1}$ by the prescribed order. Thus the diagonal order can be specified in advance ✓.\n\n\n\n**Note on the real case**: Nonreal eigenvalues occur in conjugate pairs. Taking real and imaginary parts gives an invariant two-dimensional real subspace. The same induction gives the real Schur form (with diagonal block sizes $1\\times 1$ or $2\\times 2$). The details are omitted. ∎\n\n⭐ **Why it is used more often than Jordan normal form**: Jordan form uses arbitrary similarity transformations ($P^{-1}AP$). It is useful in theory, but $P$ may be too ill-conditioned for computation. Schur form uses unitary similarity ($U^{*}AU$), whose condition number is always 1. Thus **numerical linear algebra uses Schur form instead of Jordan form**.\n\n**A shortcut to the spectral theorem**: If $A$ is normal and $U^{*}AU = T$ is upper triangular, then $T$ is also normal. An upper triangular normal matrix $\\implies$ must be diagonal (compare the two sides of $(TT^{*})_{11}$ to see that the off-diagonal entries of the first row are zero, then use induction).\n\n⚠️ In the real case, **one cannot always obtain a real diagonal or triangular form** with only $1\\times1$ blocks. The rotation example $90^\\circ$ shows why $2\\times 2$ blocks are necessary.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#74970084'
    },
  },
  'thm.riesz-perfect-pairing': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $(X, Y, \\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle)$ be a perfect pairing (`def.perfect-pairing`). Then\n\n(a) $x \\mapsto \\langle x, \\cdot\\rangle$ is the image of the isometric isomorphism $X \\to Y^*$, and $y \\mapsto \\langle \\cdot, y\\rangle$ is an isometric embedding of $Y \\to X^*$.\n\n(b) If $T : X \\to Y^*$ is surjective and $Y$ is reflexive, then $S : Y \\to X^*$ is also surjective. Symmetrically, if $S$ is surjective and $X$ is reflexive, then $T$ is surjective. In particular, when $X,Y$ are both reflexive, $T$ is surjective if and only if $S$ is surjective, and then $X \\cong Y^*$ and $Y \\cong X^*$ are isometric isomorphisms.\n\n(c) The pairings for Hilbert spaces $H$ and for $L^p$ ($1 < p < \\infty$, $1/p + 1/q = 1$) are surjective (by the projection theorem in the Hilbert case and Radon–Nikodym in the $L^p$ case). Thus $H^* \\cong H$ and $(L^p)^* \\cong L^q$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2f1b6cac'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof of (a)**: The norming condition gives $\\|x\\| = \\sup_{\\|y\\| \\le 1}|\\langle x, y\\rangle| = \\|T x\\|$, where $T x := \\langle x, \\cdot\\rangle$. Nondegeneracy makes $T$ injective, so $T$ is an isometric embedding. The argument for $y$ is analogous. $\\blacksquare$\n\n\n\n**Proof of (b)**: Suppose $T$ is surjective and $Y$ is reflexive. The map $T$ is an isometric isomorphism $X \\to Y^*$, so $T^{*} : Y^{**} \\to X^{*}$ is an isometric isomorphism. Also, $S = T^{*} \\circ J_Y$ (to check this, use $T^{*} J_Y y (x) = J_Y y(T x) = \\langle x, y\\rangle = S y(x)$). Reflexivity of $Y$ means that $J_Y$ is surjective (an isometric isomorphism), so $S = T^{*} \\circ J_Y$ is surjective. Dualizing gives the symmetric case. $\\blacksquare$\n\n\n\n**Proof of (c)**: **Hilbert case (projection theorem)**: Take $\\varphi \\in H^{*}$. If $\\varphi = 0$, take $y = 0$. Otherwise, $E := \\ker\\varphi$ is a closed proper subspace. By the orthogonal decomposition (`thm.projection-subspace`) $H = E \\oplus E^{\\perp}$, choose $0 \\ne z \\in E^{\\perp}$. Since $z \\notin E$, we have $\\varphi(z) \\ne 0$. Set $y := \\overline{\\varphi(z)}\\, z / \\|z\\|^{2}$. For $x = u + \\lambda z$ ($u \\in E$), we have $\\varphi(x) = \\lambda\\varphi(z)$, and since $\\langle x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\varphi(z)$, we obtain $\\varphi(x) = \\langle x, y\\rangle$. Also, $\\|y\\| = |\\varphi(z)|/\\|z\\| = \\sup_{\\|x\\| \\le 1}|\\langle x, y\\rangle| = \\|\\varphi\\|$. **$L^{p}$ case (Radon–Nikodym)**: Let $\\Phi \\in (L^{p})^{*}$ and first assume that $\\mu$ is finite (in the general case use a $\\sigma$-finite decomposition). Set $\\nu(E) := \\Phi(\\chi_{E})$. Then $\\nu \\ll \\mu$, so by Radon–Nikodym (`thm.lebesgue-radon-nikodym`) there exists $g \\in L^{1}(\\mu)$ such that $\\nu(E) = \\int_{E} g\\,d\\mu$. Hence $\\Phi(f) = \\int f g\\,d\\mu$ (first for simple functions, then by approximation for $f \\in L^{p}$). By Hölder, $|\\Phi(f)| \\le \\|g\\|_{q}\\|f\\|_{p}$. Taking $f = |g|^{q-1}\\overline{\\operatorname{sgn} g}/\\|g\\|_{q}^{q-1}$ gives $\\|g\\|_{q} \\le \\|\\Phi\\|$, so $g \\in L^{q}$ and $\\|g\\|_{q} = \\|\\Phi\\|$ ($g$ is unique $\\mu$-a.e.). $\\blacksquare$\n\n\n\n⭐ The two Riesz theorems are instances of the same framework: a perfect pairing together with surjectivity. The proofs in (c) use the projection theorem and Radon–Nikodym independently, so there is no circularity.\n\n⚠ **A perfect pairing alone does not imply surjectivity**: In the pairing of $\\ell^1$ with $c_0$, $T : X \\to Y^*$ is surjective but $S : Y \\to X^*$ is not (see the notes to `def.perfect-pairing`). Thus (b) requires reflexivity. Without it, ‘one is surjective $\\implies$ the other is surjective’ is false.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b5aa4588'
    },
  },
  'def.split-exact': {
    statement: {
      en: "A short exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow M_{1} \\xrightarrow{\\;i_{1}\\;} M \\xrightarrow{\\;i_{2}\\;} M_{2} \\longrightarrow 0$$\nis called **split** if it is isomorphic to\n\n$$0 \\longrightarrow M_{1} \\xrightarrow{\\;\\mathrm{pr}_{1}\\;} M_{1} \\oplus M_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\;\\mathrm{pr}_{2}\\;} M_{2} \\longrightarrow 0$$\n\n(that is, $i_{1}$ is the inclusion into the first summand and $i_{2}$ is the projection onto the second summand).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a8fee1c7'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Equivalent criteria** (in an abelian category, the three are equivalent):\n\n\n\n- $i_{2}$ has a **section** $s : M_{2} \\to M$ ($i_{2} \\circ s = \\mathrm{id}$).\n- $i_{1}$ has a **retraction** $r : M \\to M_{1}$ ($r \\circ i_{1} = \\mathrm{id}$).\n- $M \\cong M_{1} \\oplus M_{2}$ is compatible with the sequence.\n\n**Proof.** Write $i := i_{1}$ and $p := i_{2}$. Then $i$ is monic, $p$ is epic, and $\\operatorname{im} i = \\ker p$.\n- **Has a section $\\implies$ splits**: Suppose $p s = 1_{M_{2}}$. Then $p(1_{M} - s p) = p - p s p = 0$, so $1_{M} - sp$ lies in $\\ker p = \\operatorname{im} i$. Thus there exists $r : M \\to M_{1}$ such that\n$$i r = 1_{M} - s p.$$\nSince $i$ is monic, $i(r i) = (1_{M} - sp)\\,i = i - s(p i) = i$ (using $p i = 0$), so $r i = 1_{M_{1}}$ ✓.\n\nTogether with $p i = 0$, the maps $p_{1} := r$, $i_{1} := i$, $p_{2} := p$, and $i_{2} := s$ satisfy the three biproduct identities ($r i = 1$, $p s = 1$, $i r + s p = 1_{M}$, see ‘The direct sum is a product’). Thus $M$ is precisely $M_{1} \\oplus M_{2}$ and the sequence splits ✓.\n- **Splits $\\implies$ has a section**: If the sequence splits, it is isomorphic to $0 \\to M_{1} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mathrm{pr}_{1}\\ } M_{1} \\oplus M_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mathrm{pr}_{2}\\ } M_{2} \\to 0$, and $\\mathrm{pr}_{2}$ has an evident section (the inclusion into the second summand). Transport it along the isomorphism to obtain a section of $p$ ✓.\n- **Duality**: Apply the same argument in $\\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}}$ to obtain ‘splits $\\iff$ $i$ has a retraction’ ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ **This is the splitting lemma**: In an additive category, splitting of a short exact sequence, existence of a section, and a compatible direct-sum decomposition are equivalent. It **reduces questions about sequences to questions about direct sums**, a standard simplification in homological algebra.\n\n⚠️ Short exact sequences **need not** split ($0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\xrightarrow{2} \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\to 0$ is one that does not). Splitting is a special case, and $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$ addresses whether a given sequence splits.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8247f51d'
    },
  },
  'table.rhom-zrt': {
    statement: {
      en: "The table gives $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, N)$ (rows correspond to $M$ and columns to $N$):\n\n$$\\begin{array}{c|ccc} & \\mathbb{Z} & \\mathbb{R} & T \\\\ \\hline \\mathbb{Z} & \\mathbb{Z} & \\mathbb{R} & T \\\\ \\mathbb{R} & 0 & \\mathbb{R} & \\mathbb{R} \\\\ T & \\mathbb{Z}[-1] & 0 & \\mathbb{Z} \\end{array}$$\n**Computing the entries: Two facts suffice for the calculation**\n\n1. **$\\mathbb{Z}$ is free (projective)**: A resolution of $\\mathbb{Z}$ can be taken to be the one-dimensional $[\\mathbb{Z}]$. Thus $\\operatorname{RHom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}, N) = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}, N) = N$, with **no higher terms**.\n\nThis gives the first row directly.\n\n\n2. **$\\mathbb{R}$ is divisible and hence $\\mathbb{Z}$-injective**: $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, \\mathbb{R}) = 0$ for all $M$ and $n \\ge 1$.\n\nThus the entries with target $\\mathbb{R}$ have only degree $0$: $\\operatorname{RHom}(M, \\mathbb{R}) = \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(M, \\mathbb{R})$.\n\n\n\n**Replace the $T$ column by $\\mathbb{Z}$ and shift up one degree**: Take the short exact sequence $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\to T \\to 0$ ($T = \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$).\n\nFor fixed $M$, apply $\\operatorname{RHom}(M, -)$ to obtain the **long exact sequence**\n\n$$\\cdots \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, \\mathbb{R}) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, T) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M, \\mathbb{Z}) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M, \\mathbb{R}) \\to \\cdots$$\n\nSince the surrounding $\\mathbb{R}$ terms vanish for $n \\ge 1$, exactness determines the middle term:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, T) \\;\\cong\\; \\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M, \\mathbb{Z}) \\qquad (n \\ge 1),$$\n\nand $0 \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(M,\\mathbb{Z}) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(M,\\mathbb{R}) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(M,T) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(M,\\mathbb{Z}) \\to 0$.\n\n**This explains why the $T$ and $\\mathbb{Z}$ columns differ by exactly one degree** (for example, the third row, first column is $\\mathbb{Z}[-1]$: when $M = T$, we have $\\operatorname{Ext}^{0}(T,\\mathbb{Z}) = 0$ and $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T,\\mathbb{Z}) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{0}(T,T) = \\mathbb{Z}$).\n\n\n\n**Why ‘real vector space $\\to$ discrete’ vanishes in every degree**: $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is divisible, so maps from a divisible group into any group have images in which division by $n$ is possible. Thus $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{R}^{n}, \\mathbb{Z}) = 0$ (elements of the image of $1/n$ must be divisible by $n$, leaving only $0$).\n\nTogether with divisibility $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(\\mathbb{R}^{n}, \\mathbb{Z}) = 0$, this gives zero for the entire $\\operatorname{RHom}$.\n\n\n\n⚠️ The entry ‘row $\\mathbb{R}$, column $T$ = $\\mathbb{R}$’ does not follow from the two facts above alone (it involves classifying continuous homomorphisms $\\mathbb{R} \\to T = \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$ and is related to the universal cover $\\mathbb{R} \\to T$).\n\nThe derivation of this entry is missing.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a291f4aa'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**How to read the table** ($T = \\mathbb{R}/\\mathbb{Z}$):\n\n\n\n- First row (source $\\mathbb{Z}$): $\\operatorname{RHom}(\\mathbb{Z}, N) = N$. A free source has no higher terms, leaving only degree zero.\n- Second row, first column $0$: **Real vector space $\\to$ discrete** vanishes in every degree by divisibility.\n- Remaining entries of the second row: RHom between finite-dimensional real Banach spaces is concentrated in degree zero, with value $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(M, N)$. Here the spaces are $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}) = \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb{R}}(\\mathbb{R}, T)$.\n\n\n- Third row, second column $0$: **Compact Hausdorff $\\to$ real Banach** vanishes in every degree.\n- Third row, first column $\\mathbb{Z}[-1]$: By $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\to T \\to 0$, the middle term is $0$, leaving only $\\mathbb{Z}$ shifted down by one degree.\n- Bottom right $\\mathbb{Z}$: This follows similarly from $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\to T \\to 0$ and $\\operatorname{RHom}(T, \\mathbb{R}) = 0$.\n\n\n\nThis table **summarizes the chapter's calculations**. Three vanishing results ($\\mathbb{R} \\to$ discrete, compact $\\to \\mathbb{R}$, and free source), together with the exact sequence $0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\to T \\to 0$, compute all RHom groups between the three basic classes of objects.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#087496ba'
    },
  },
  'thm.inverse-function-banach': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $E, F$ be **Banach spaces**, let $U \\subseteq E$ be open, and suppose $f : U \\to F$ is $C^{1}$ (in the Fréchet sense: the total derivative $Df(x) \\in \\mathcal{L}(E, F)$ exists and is continuous), $a \\in U$, and $Df(a)$ **is a topological linear isomorphism** (bounded with bounded inverse):\n\n$$Df(a) : E \\to F \\quad \\text{topological linear isomorphism}$$\n\nThen there is a neighborhood of $a$, denoted by $V$, and a neighborhood of $f(a)$, denoted by $W$, such that $f|_{V} : V \\to W$ is a homeomorphism, its inverse $g$ is $C^{1}$, and $Dg(y) = (Df(g(y)))^{-1}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#476af996'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ There is **one crucial difference** from the finite-dimensional version (`thm.inverse-function`). In finite dimensions, invertibility of $Df(a)$ automatically gives a bounded inverse (every linear map between finite-dimensional spaces is bounded, including the inverse).\n\n**In infinite dimensions, boundedness of the inverse must be stated as a hypothesis**.\n\nA topological linear isomorphism is a linear bijection with continuous inverse. Both conditions are essential.\n\n⭐ The proof adds two steps specific to Banach spaces:\n- **Neumann series**: When $\\|S\\| < 1$, we have $(I - S)^{-1} = \\sum_{k \\ge 0} S^{k}$ (the series converges in the operator norm by completeness of $\\mathcal{L}(E, F)$).\n\nThis inverts an operator near the identity and is central to the reduction to $Df(a) = I$.\n\n\n- **Mean value inequality**: $\\|f(x) - f(y)\\| \\le \\sup_{[x,y]} \\|Df\\| \\cdot \\|x - y\\|$ (`thm.mean-value-multi`) makes $\\Phi_y$ a contraction.\n\n⚠ This is **the form used in functional analysis and condensed mathematics**. Their objects include $C(S)$, $L^{p}$, Fréchet spaces, and groups of continuous functions on compact Hausdorff spaces. The finite-dimensional theorem is insufficient (for example, in the local isomorphism argument for $C(S, M)$ in Section 8.2).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#ca18cd7e'
    },
  },
  'thm.sylow': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $G$ be a finite group with $|G| = p^{a} m$, where $p$ is prime and $p \\nmid m$. Then\n\n**(1) Existence**: For every $0 \\le i \\le a$, the group $G$ has a subgroup of order $p^{i}$. In particular, a subgroup of order $p^{a}$ exists and is called a **Sylow $p$-subgroup**.\n\n**(2) Conjugacy**: Any two Sylow subgroups of $G$ for the prime $p$ are conjugate. More generally, every $p$-subgroup is contained in a Sylow $p$-subgroup.\n\n**(3) Counting**: The number of Sylow $p$-subgroups, denoted by $n_p$, satisfies\n\n$$n_p \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}, \\qquad n_p \\mid m, \\qquad n_p = [G : N_G(P)]$$\n\n(Here $P$ is any Sylow $p$-subgroup and $N_G(P)$ is its normalizer.) In particular, $n_p = 1 \\iff P \\trianglelefteq G$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1d1afc59'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ Sylow's theorem is a **partial converse to Lagrange's theorem**. Lagrange says that $|H|$ must divide $|G|$. The claim that $d \\mid |G|$ $\\implies$ has a subgroup of order $d$ is false. Sylow guarantees **a subgroup of the largest possible $p$-power order, unique up to conjugacy**.\n\n⚠ Galois theory **does not require** Sylow's theorem (the Galois correspondence uses normality rather than counting).\n\nIt is marked optional in the plan.\n\nIt is included as a direct application of group actions and a first step toward the classification of finite groups.\n\n⭐ Standard application (showing that groups are nonisomorphic or not simple): $|G| = 15$.\n\n$n_3 \\mid 5$ and $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ $\\implies$ $n_3 = 1$.\n\n$n_5 \\mid 3$ and $n_5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5$ $\\implies$ $n_5 = 1$.\n\nThus $P_3 \\trianglelefteq G$ and $P_5 \\trianglelefteq G$, with $P_3 \\cap P_5 = \\{e\\}$ (the orders are coprime), so $G \\cong P_3 \\times P_5 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/15$. **Every group of order 15 is cyclic**.\n\n⚠ The counting conditions are only necessary. A count is ruled out only when both $n_p \\equiv 1 \\pmod p$ and $n_p \\mid m$ fail, and satisfying them does not determine how many subgroups exist.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#5e417eed'
    },
  },
  'thm.solid-derived': {
    statement: {
      en: "**(ii)** The functor $D(\\mathbf{Solid}) \\to D(\\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab}))$ is fully faithful, and its essential image consists precisely of the solid complexes in $D(\\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab}))$.\n\nA complex $C$ is solid if and only if all $H^i(C)$ are solid abelian groups.\n\nThe inclusion has a left adjoint\n\n$$C \\longmapsto C^{L\\blacksquare} : D(\\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab})) \\longrightarrow D(\\mathbf{Solid}),$$\n\nwhich is the left derived functor of $M \\mapsto M^{\\blacksquare}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9380284f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ This extends solidity from objects to complexes. It suffices to check cohomology in place of checking each term.\n\n⚠ Together with (i), this is the full statement of Theorem 5.8. It is presented in two nodes because (i) is used for constructions and (ii) for computations.\n\n⭐ Corollary: The derived category of $\\mathbf{Solid}$ can be viewed as a **reflective** full subcategory of $D(\\operatorname{Cond}(\\mathbf{Ab}))$, with reflector $C \\mapsto C^{L\\blacksquare}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#77640fa9'
    },
  },
  'thm.solid-tensor': {
    statement: {
      en: "**(i)** On $\\mathbf{Solid}$ there is a unique symmetric monoidal tensor product $\\otimes^{\\blacksquare}$ making the solidification functor $M \\mapsto M^{\\blacksquare}$ symmetric monoidal. Explicitly,\n\n$$M \\otimes^{\\blacksquare} N := (M \\otimes N)^{\\blacksquare}.$$\n\n**(ii)** On $D(\\mathbf{Solid})$ there is a unique symmetric monoidal structure $\\otimes^{L\\blacksquare}$ making $C \\mapsto C^{L\\blacksquare}$ symmetric monoidal. It is the left-derived functor of $\\otimes^{\\blacksquare}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1cbf1d6f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "⭐ This is **the object the whole theory aims to construct**: a tensor product compatible with completion.\n\nFor comparison, the classical $\\mathbb{Z}_p \\hat\\otimes \\mathbb{R}$ is not well-defined, whereas $\\mathbb{Z}_p \\otimes^{L\\blacksquare} \\mathbb{R} = 0$ (see `ex.solid-tensor-computations`).\n\n⚠ Uniqueness follows because colimit-preserving functors are determined by their values on free objects.\n\nFor existence, verify that $(M \\otimes N)^{\\blacksquare} \\to (M^{\\blacksquare} \\otimes N^{\\blacksquare})^{\\blacksquare}$ is an isomorphism by reducing to $M = \\mathbb{Z}[S]$ and $N = \\mathbb{Z}[T]$ and using a computation of the kind in part (iv) of `cor.solid-compact-generators`.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4f3c5af2'
    },
  },
  'thm.lambek': {
    statement: {
      en: "Suppose $R$ is a **unital Noetherian ring** and $M$ is a **left** $R$-module. (The criterion holds for arbitrary unital rings, but the induction below uses the fact that submodules are still finitely generated. The non-Noetherian case requires a filtered colimit argument, which is omitted here.) Then $M$ is flat if and only if, for every **finitely generated right ideal** $I \\subseteq R$, the multiplication map\n\n$$I \\otimes_{R} M \\longrightarrow M, \\qquad a \\otimes m \\longmapsto a m$$\n\nis injective. Equivalently, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(R/I,\\ M) = 0$ holds for every finitely generated right ideal $I$.\n\n(Over a commutative ring, there is no need to distinguish left and right ideals.)",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#b3e45dba'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why this is a strengthening**: The definition of flatness requires testing **all** injections. Item 4 of `prop.flat-equivalences` already reduces the test to ideals. Lambek reduces it further to **finitely generated** ideals, which often form a countable family and can be checked individually.\n\n**Proof (converse, in three steps)**:\n\n**Step 1 (reduce the** objects being tested **to finitely generated ones)**: Keep the module $M$ being tested **fixed**. The fact that $M$ is a filtered colimit of its finitely generated submodules does not let us reduce $M$ to the finitely generated case. Finite generation does not pass to submodules ($R = k[x,y]$ is flat, but the ideal $(x,y)$ is not). The reduction concerns the maps being tested. It suffices to verify that for every **finitely generated** submodule $K \\subseteq R^{n}$, the map $K \\otimes M \\to R^{n} \\otimes M$ is injective.\n\n**Step 2 (reduce to submodules of free modules)**: Take a surjection $R^{n} \\twoheadrightarrow M_{1}$ (where $M_{1}$ is a finitely generated submodule of $M$). Let $K$ be the preimage of $M_{1}$, giving the short exact sequence $0 \\to K \\to R^{n} \\to M_{1} \\to 0$.\n\nTensor both sequences and compare them. Since $K \\otimes M \\to R^{n} \\otimes M \\to M_{1} \\otimes M \\to 0$ is exact, $K \\otimes M \\to R^{n} \\otimes M$ is injective if and only if $R^{n} \\otimes M \\to M_{1} \\otimes M$ is injective, by the snake lemma.\n\nThus it suffices to show that for every **finitely generated** submodule $K \\subseteq R^{n}$, the map $K \\otimes M \\to R^{n} \\otimes M$ is injective.\n\n**Step 3 (induct on $n$)**: When $n = 1$, the submodule $K$ is a finitely generated right ideal, so the hypothesis applies. When $n > 1$, write $R^{n} = R^{n-1} \\oplus R$ and define $K_{1} := K \\cap R^{n-1}$, with $K_{2}$ the projection of $K$ onto the second component (a finitely generated **right** ideal of $R$, on the same side as the ideals in the criterion. Regard $K \\subseteq R^{n}$ as a right submodule). By **Noetherianity**, $K_{1}$ is **finitely generated** as a submodule of $R^{n-1}$, so the induction hypothesis applies directly (the non-Noetherian case requires a separate filtered colimit argument, omitted here). This gives the short exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\to K_{1} \\to K \\to K_{2} \\to 0, \\qquad 0 \\to R^{n-1} \\to R^{n} \\to R \\to 0 .$$\n\nAfter tensoring, we obtain a $3 \\times 3$ diagram. The vertical maps in the left and right columns are injective by the induction hypothesis and the case $n = 1$, so the vertical map in the middle column is injective by the snake lemma ✓. ∎\n\n⚠️ Testing injectivity only on principal ideals $(a)$ gives **torsion-freeness** over an **integral domain**. This characterization holds only over integral domains: when $R = \\mathbb{Z}/4$ and $M = R/(2)$, non-zero-divisors act injectively, but $(2) \\otimes M \\to M$ is the zero map. Torsion-freeness is weaker than flatness. See `prop.flat-domain-torsionfree` for the counterexample $(x, y) \\subseteq k[x, y]$.\n\n⭐ **Practical use**: To test whether a given $R$-module is flat, it suffices to do one division computation for each finitely generated ideal. Over a commutative Noetherian ring, every ideal is finitely generated, so the criterion simply applies to all ideals.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#26df3deb'
    },
  },
  'prop.injective-splitting': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $I$ be a left $R$-module. The following are equivalent:\n\n1. $I$ is **injective**.\n2. Every monomorphism $I \\rightarrowtail M$ **splits**, meaning that there is a retraction $r : M \\to I$ satisfying $r \\circ i = \\mathrm{id}_{I}$.\n3. Every short exact sequence $0 \\to I \\to M \\to M_{2} \\to 0$ **splits**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#05c288a1'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**1 implies 2**: Take $A = I$ and $f = \\mathrm{id}_{I}$. The extension $M \\to I$ supplied by injectivity is the required retraction ✓.\n\n**2 implies 1**: Given $A \\rightarrowtail B$ and $f : A \\to I$, form the **pushout**\n\n$$P := (I \\oplus B) / \\{\\, (f(a),\\ -a) : a \\in A \\,\\},$$\n\nThis identifies the images of $A \\to I$ and $A \\to B$ in $P$. The map $I \\to P$ is still a monomorphism (a pushout of a monomorphism is a monomorphism: to test whether $(x, 0)$ lies in the image, inspect only the $I$ component). By 2, $I \\rightarrowtail P$ has a retraction $r$, so $r \\circ (B \\to P)$ is the required extension of $f$ ✓.\n\n**2 and 3 are equivalent**: By the criterion in `def.split-exact`, a short exact sequence splits if and only if its monomorphism has a retraction ✓. ∎\n\n📌 This is the same result as `prop.injective-criterion` (four equivalent conditions for injective objects in an abelian category). Items 2 and 4 there are two formulations of this result. The proof carries over verbatim to any abelian category, since it uses only pushouts and kernels.\n\n\n\n**Among the three conditions, (1)$\\implies$(2) follows from the definition, (2)$\\implies$(3) requires a pushout, and (3)$\\implies$(1) is the substantive criterion**:\n\n- (1) $\\implies$ (2): **One line** (injectivity gives the retraction directly).\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): **Use a pushout diagram**. Given $0 \\to I \\to M \\to M_{2} \\to 0$, apply (2) to $I \\rightarrowtail M$ to obtain a splitting.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (1): **This step uses a lift**. Given $I \\rightarrowtail M$ and $I \\to N$, form the pushout $M \\to P$. Then $0 \\to N \\to P \\to \\operatorname{coker} \\to 0$ splits, and composing the section with $M \\to P$ gives a lift of $M \\to N$. ⚠️ **Pushouts must exist** (as they always do in an abelian category). Enough injectives are unnecessary.\n\n⚠️ **Stated only for the category of $R$-modules (or an abelian category)**: Splitting the short exact sequences in (3) presupposes **kernels and cokernels**, and a retraction in (2) is defined using composition of morphisms. Both conditions need to be reformulated in nonabelian categories.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1bc3975f'
    },
  },
  'prop.pd-vanishing': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M$ be an $R$-module and let $n \\ge 0$. The following conditions are **equivalent**:\n\n1. $\\operatorname{pd}_{R} M \\le n$.\n2. The $n$th syzygy $\\Omega^{n}M$ is **projective** (equivalently, $M$ has a projective resolution of length $n$).\n3. For every $R$-module $N$ and every $k > n$, we have $\\operatorname{Ext}^{k}_{R}(M, N) = 0$.\n4. For every $R$-module $N$, we have $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}_{R}(M, N) = 0$.\n\nDually, $\\operatorname{id}_{R} M \\le n$ if and only if $\\operatorname{Ext}^{k}_{R}(N, M) = 0$ holds for every $N$ and every $k > n$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3046979f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.**\n$(1) \\implies (3)$: Take a length-$n$ projective resolution $P_{\\bullet}$. The truncated resolution is zero at $k > n$, so $\\operatorname{Hom}(P_{\\bullet}, N)$ is also zero in degree $k > n$, and hence its cohomology vanishes ✓ (this uses the fact that projective and injective resolutions compute the same group, as in the two ways of computing Ext).\n$(3) \\implies (4)$: Take $k = n+1$ ✓.\n$(4) \\implies (1)$: Take any projective resolution and compute $K := \\Omega^{n}M$. Applying **dimension shifting** $n$ times gives $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}_{R}(K, N) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}_{R}(M, N)$, which vanishes for every $N$ ✓.\nThe vanishing of $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}_{R}(K, -)$ in every case forces $K$ to be projective. If $K$ were not projective, a surjection $F \\twoheadrightarrow K$ with $F$ free would fail to split. The classification of first extensions then gives $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}_{R}(K, \\ker) \\ne 0$, a contradiction ✓.\n\nThus $M$ has a projective resolution of length $n$ ✓, giving $(1)$.\n$(1) \\iff (2)$: For a fixed resolution, this restates the definition. Passing to a different resolution requires Schanuel's lemma (stability of syzygies) to show that projectivity of the $n$th syzygy is independent of the resolution ✓. ∎\n\n**Of the four conditions, 2 $\\implies$ 3 is the only substantive step, and it requires a long exact sequence and induction**:\n\n- (1) $\\iff$ (2): This unpacks the definition **for a fixed resolution** ($\\mathrm{pd} \\le n$ means there is a projective resolution of length $n$). Projectivity of the $n$th syzygy is a statement **independent of the resolution** by **Schanuel's lemma / stability of syzygies** (`prop.syzygy-stability`).\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): **This is the substantive step**. Use the projective resolution $\\cdots \\to P_{1} \\to P_{0} \\to M \\to 0$ to compute $\\operatorname{Ext}$. The group $\\operatorname{Ext}^{k}(M,N)$ is the cohomology computed from the resolution, and a resolution of length $n$ has no term at $k > n$, so the group vanishes. ⚠️ This uses the fact that **projective resolutions compute $\\operatorname{Ext}$** (dimension shifting / balancedness), which itself requires proof.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (4): **Special case** ($k = n+1$).\n- (4) $\\implies$ (2): **This step uses dimension shifting in reverse**. If $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}(M, -)$ always vanishes, take a partial resolution of length $n$ whose $n$th syzygy $K$ satisfies $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(K, -) = 0$. Then $K$ is projective (the converse in `prop.ext-projective-vanishes`). **⚠️ This step uses the projectivity criterion for $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1} = 0 \\implies$ in the module category** (which has enough projectives, so no further hypothesis is needed). Care is needed in more general abelian categories.\n\n⚠️ **The case $n = 0$**: $\\mathrm{pd} M \\le 0$ $\\iff$ $M$ is projective $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{Ext}^{k}(M,-) = 0$ for all $k \\ge 1$. The assertion that $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1} = 0$ implies projectivity holds for arbitrary $n$: only the case $n = 0$ (that is, $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$) behaves this way.\n\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the dimension-shifting criterion in module categories):\n- (1) $\\iff$ existence of a projective resolution of length $n$ is the definition. If (2) refers to the $n$th syzygy in an arbitrarily chosen resolution, independence of the resolution also requires Schanuel's lemma or dimension shifting.\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): Truncating the projective resolution and using it to compute $\\operatorname{Ext}$ requires the theorem on computation by resolutions. For (3) $\\implies$ (4), simply take $k=n+1$.\n- (4) $\\implies$ (2): Dimension shifting reduces the condition to $\\operatorname{Ext}^1(\\Omega^nM,N)=0$ for all $N$, and splitting short exact sequences then gives projectivity. Module categories have enough projectives, with no requirement that the ring be a PID.\n- The quantifier over all $N$ cannot be replaced by a single $N$. Taking $N=0$ gives vanishing for arbitrary $M$. For example, $\\mathbb Z/2$ is not a projective $\\mathbb Z$-module.\n\n\n⭐ **Practical form**: To test the dimension, one generally computes just **one** group for each coefficient module. It suffices that $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n+1}_{R}(M, N)$ vanish for every $N$, without checking every degree. Conversely, to determine how large $\\operatorname{pd}$ is, find a witness for which $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}$ is nonzero, namely some $N$.\n\n⚠️ **Keep the two directions straight**: $\\operatorname{pd}_{R} M$ concerns $\\operatorname{Ext}(M, -)$ (the **first** variable), and $\\operatorname{id}_{R} N$ concerns $\\operatorname{Ext}(-, N)$ (the **second** variable).\n\n📌 An immediate corollary: $R$ is a semisimple ring $\\iff$ every module is projective $\\iff \\operatorname{gl.dim} R = 0$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2941f4c3'
    },
  },
  'thm.ext1-extensions': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $M, N$ be $R$-modules. An **extension** of $N$ by $M$ is a short exact sequence\n\n$$0 \\to N \\to E \\xrightarrow{\\ \\pi\\ } M \\to 0 .$$\n\nTwo extensions are **equivalent** if there is an isomorphism between them making both squares commute and inducing the identity on $N$ and $M$. The equivalence classes are in **bijection** with $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}_{R}(M, N)$. The **Baer sum** makes these classes into an abelian group, and the bijection is a group isomorphism.\n\nThe **split extension** ($E \\cong N\\oplus M$) corresponds precisely to the zero element.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4e71b1a6'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof (construct the map using a projective precover).** Choose a projective precover $0 \\to K \\to P \\xrightarrow{\\ p\\ } M \\to 0$.\n\nBy dimension shifting, $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}_{R}(M, N) \\cong \\operatorname{coker}\\bigl(\\operatorname{Hom}(P, N) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(K, N)\\bigr)$ ✓.\n**From an extension to a class.** Given an extension $0 \\to N \\to E \\to M \\to 0$, pull it back along $p$ to get $0 \\to N \\to E_{0} \\to P \\to 0$, where $E_{0} = \\{\\, (e, x) : \\pi(e) = p(x) \\,\\}$.\n\nSince $P$ is projective, the sequence **splits**. Choose a splitting $\\sigma : P \\to E_{0}$.\n\nThe composite $K \\hookrightarrow P \\xrightarrow{\\sigma} E_{0} \\to N$ gives $\\theta \\in \\operatorname{Hom}(K, N)$ ✓.\n\nAny two choices of splitting differ by an element of $\\operatorname{Hom}(P, N)$, so the **class** of $\\theta$ in the cokernel is well-defined ✓.\n**From a class to an extension.** Given $\\theta : K \\to N$, form the pushout $E := (P \\oplus N)/\\{\\, (k, -\\theta k) : k \\in K \\,\\}$ to obtain a commutative diagram and a short exact sequence $0 \\to N \\to E \\to M \\to 0$ ✓.\n**The two constructions are inverse.** By construction, the two steps undo one another. Pulling back and then pushing out yields an extension isomorphic to the original, and in the other direction the class of $\\theta$ is recovered ✓. This uses the fact that any two splittings differ by an element of $\\operatorname{Hom}(P,N)$.\n**Baer sum.** Given two extensions $E_{1}, E_{2}$, take their fiber product over $M$, denoted $E_{1}\\times_{M}E_{2}$ (it combines the two middle terms over the same $M$). Then quotient by the image of the antidiagonal $\\{\\, (n, -n) : n \\in N \\,\\}$. The resulting middle term, denoted $E_{1} + E_{2}$, gives the Baer sum ✓.\n\nAssociativity and commutativity follow from the fiber product and quotient constructions.\n\nThe zero element is $N\\oplus M$ (the split extension).\n\nThe inverse of $E$ is obtained by replacing $E\\to M$ with the negative of $N\\to E$, that is, in $E$ reverse the sign of $N$ ✓.\n\nThus the equivalence classes form an abelian group ✓.\n**Agreement with the group structure on $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$.** The bijection above sends the Baer sum to addition in $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$. When adding the classes of two extensions, the chosen splittings of the pullbacks can be combined, so the corresponding $\\theta$ add ✓. ∎\n\n⭐ **This gives an interpretation of the elements of $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$**: The group $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$ classifies **actual middle terms**. Thus $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(M, N) = 0$ has a concrete meaning that can be checked by hand: **every extension splits**.\n\n📌 Higher $\\operatorname{Ext}$ groups also have an interpretation in terms of their elements. The group $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M, N)$ classifies extensions $Y$ of the form $N \\to E_{1}\\to \\cdots \\to E_{n}\\to M$ and of degree $n$ (homotopy classes of exact sequences of length $n$).\n\nThe result for $\\operatorname{Ext}^{1}$ is the simplest case of $n = 1$.\n\n⚠️ In the strict definition of equivalence, the isomorphism must **be the identity at both ends**. Allowing arbitrary isomorphisms at the ends gives a coarser classification by orbits of invertible elements in $\\operatorname{Hom}$, so the original correspondence is no longer one-to-one.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#79ce3619'
    },
  },
  'prop.tor-flat-vanishing': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $R$ be a ring and $F$ a right $R$-module. The following conditions are equivalent:\n\n1. $F$ is **flat**;\n2. For every left $R$-module $N$, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(F, N) = 0$;\n3. For every left $R$-module $N$ and every $n \\ge 1$, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R}(F, N) = 0$.\n\nThus the flat modules are precisely those for which all higher Tor groups vanish. This is exactly parallel to the characterization of projective modules by the vanishing of all higher $\\operatorname{Ext}$ groups.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#58c6a52b'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.**\n$(1) \\implies (3)$: For $F$ take a projective resolution $P_{\\bullet} \\to F$.\n\nWhen $F$ is flat, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}$ can also be computed using a resolution of $N$. Take a projective resolution of $N$, denoted by $Q_{\\bullet}$. The resolution on the $N$ side gives $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}(F, N) = H_{n}(F\\otimes Q_{\\bullet})$.\n\nSince $F\\otimes -$ is exact and $Q_{\\bullet} \\to N$ is exact, $F\\otimes Q_{\\bullet}$ has homology only in degree $0$, and its higher homology vanishes ✓.\n$(3) \\implies (2)$: Take $n = 1$ ✓.\n$(2) \\implies (1)$: Take any injection $f : M_{1} \\rightarrowtail M$. We must show that $F\\otimes M_{1} \\to F\\otimes M$ is injective. Include it in the short exact sequence $0 \\to M_{1} \\to M \\to M/M_{1} \\to 0$, tensor with $F$, and take the long exact sequence\n$$\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}^{R}(F,\\ M/M_{1}) \\to F\\otimes M_{1} \\to F\\otimes M \\to F\\otimes M/M_{1} \\to 0 ,$$\nThe leftmost term is zero by condition 2, so $F\\otimes M_{1} \\to F\\otimes M$ is **injective** ✓, which gives exactness of $F\\otimes -$ ✓. ∎\n\n**Of the three conditions, (2)$\\implies$(3) is the only step that requires induction. The equivalence (1)$\\iff$(2) uses a long exact sequence**:\n\n- (1) $\\iff$ (2): **A long exact sequence is needed** (flatness means that $(-)\\otimes F$ is exact. Its equivalence to the universal vanishing of $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}$ follows from the long exact sequence for $\\operatorname{Tor}$).\n- (2) $\\implies$ (3): **This step uses dimension shifting**. If $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}(F, N) = 0$ for all $N$, take the short exact sequence for $N$ and use the long exact sequence to obtain $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n} = 0$ by induction. ⚠️ This requires the long exact sequence (the Tor version of `thm.long-exact`).\n- (3) $\\implies$ (2): **A special case** ($n = 1$).\n\n⚠️ **Comparison with projectivity**: Projective $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{Ext}^{n}(M,-) = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$. Flat $\\iff$ $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}(-,N) = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$. The criteria are parallel in form, but **flatness is weaker than projectivity** (projective $\\implies$ flat, and the converse fails. For example, $\\mathbb{Q}$ is flat over $\\mathbb{Z}$ but is not projective).\n⚠️ $\\operatorname{Tor}$ can be computed in **either variable**. Here $F$ is on the left and $N$ is on the right. Reversing the order gives isomorphic results ($\\operatorname{Tor}$ is balanced), but over a noncommutative ring one must also pass to the opposite ring: $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R}(M,N) \\cong \\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R^{\\mathrm{op}}}(N,M)$ (for example, when $R = M_{2}(k)$, the original structures put $N$ and $M$ on different sides).\n\n\n**Assumptions used in each direction** (flatness is defined by exactness of tensoring, and the Tor criterion uses derived functors):\n- Flatness $\\iff$ exactness of $F\\otimes_R-$ is the definition. The implication (1) $\\implies$ (3) also uses a projective resolution of the left module $N$ to compute Tor and uses the fact that exact functors preserve exactness of resolutions.\n- (3) $\\implies$ (2): Take $n=1$, a direct specialization. (2) $\\implies$ (1): The Tor long exact sequence of a short exact sequence shows that tensoring preserves injections. Combine this with the right exactness of tensoring.\n- These implications hold over any unital ring, with no commutativity, Noetherian, or PID hypothesis. The pairing of $F$ as a right module with $N$ as a left module is essential. Over a noncommutative ring the two inputs cannot be interchanged freely.\n- Vanishing is required for every $N$. Testing only $N=R$ gives vanishing for any $F$, including $F=\\mathbb Z/2$, which is not flat. Flatness cannot be replaced by projectivity either. For example, $\\mathbb Q$ is a flat, nonprojective $\\mathbb Z$-module.\n\n\n⭐ **This gives a condition for flatness that can be checked one case at a time**. Instead of examining all injections, compute, for each $N$, the group $\\operatorname{Tor}_{1}$. In practice this is often used with Lambek's criterion, which requires testing only finitely generated ideals.\n\n📌 **Corollaries**: **Direct summands** of flat modules are flat ($\\operatorname{Tor}$ commutes with direct sums), and direct sums of flat modules are **flat**. Projective modules are flat (vanishing in higher degrees on the $\\operatorname{Ext}$ side is the dual manifestation of the same phenomenon).\n\n⚠️ Here $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R}(F, -)$ treats $F$ as a **right** module. Over a noncommutative ring, right flatness of $F$ and left flatness of $F$ (that is, $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^{R}(-, F) = 0$) are **two distinct conditions**. This result concerns right flatness. Over a commutative ring the two conditions coincide ✓.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c802fb7f'
    },
  },
  'thm.paracompact-cech-equals-sheaf': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X$ be a **paracompact Hausdorff** space (see `def.paracompact` in the topology cluster for the definition and equivalent characterizations of paracompactness). Then for every sheaf of abelian groups $\\mathcal{M}$ and every $n \\ge 0$,\n\n$$\\check{H}^{n}(X, \\mathcal{M}) \\;\\cong\\; H^{n}(X, \\mathcal{M}) .$$\n\nHere $\\check{H}^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M}):=\\varinjlim_{\\mathfrak{U}}\\check{H}^{n}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathcal{M})$ is the direct limit over locally finite open covers. The isomorphism **does not hold** for an arbitrary individual locally finite cover (see the counterexample in the notes). If $\\mathfrak{U}$ is a **good cover** for $\\mathcal{M}$, meaning that every finite intersection $U_{i_{0}\\cdots i_{p}}$ satisfies $H^{q}(U_{i_{0}\\cdots i_{p}},\\mathcal{M})=0$ ($q>0$), then the Cartan–Leray spectral sequence degenerates, and the map $\\check{H}^{n}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathcal{M})\\to H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})$ for that cover is already an isomorphism.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#32fa9132'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Ingredients of the standard proof.** Proofs of ① and ③ are given below. Step ② depends on the acyclicity of flabby sheaves used in `thm.soft-sheaf-acyclic`, which is unproved in the graph. The comparison in ④ is still missing, so this node is marked `status: 'skeleton'`.\n\n**① The Čech complex of sheaves is a resolution.** For a cover $\\mathfrak{U} = (U_{i})_{i}$, set $\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U}, \\mathcal{M}) := \\prod_{i_{0} < \\cdots < i_{p}} j_{*}\\bigl(\\mathcal{M}|_{U_{i_{0} \\cdots i_{p}}}\\bigr)$, where $j : U_{i_{0} \\cdots i_{p}} \\hookrightarrow X$ is the inclusion.\n\n**Proof of ①.** It suffices to check the stalks. Fix $x$ and choose $i$ such that $x \\in U_{i}$. Define a contracting homotopy $h^{p} : (\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p})_{x} \\to (\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p-1})_{x}$ on the stalk. For $\\sigma \\in (\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p})_{x}$, set $(h\\sigma)_{i_{0} \\cdots i_{p-1}} := \\sigma_{i\\,i_{0} \\cdots i_{p-1}}$ (set the remaining components to $0$, and on a sufficiently small neighborhood of $x$ the same choice $U_{i}$ remains valid). A direct computation with the signs of the Čech differential $\\delta$ gives $\\delta h + h \\delta = \\operatorname{id}$ ($p \\ge 1$), and when $p = 0$, the map $\\delta h = \\operatorname{id}$ is precisely the augmentation $\\mathcal{M}_{x} \\to (\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{0})_{x}$. Thus the augmented complex $0 \\to \\mathcal{M}_{x} \\to (\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{0})_{x} \\to (\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{1})_{x} \\to \\cdots$ is exact. Exactness on stalks gives exactness of sheaves. Taking global sections gives $\\Gamma(X, \\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p}) = \\check{C}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U}, \\mathcal{M})$, so its cohomology is $\\check{H}^{\\bullet}(\\mathfrak{U}, \\mathcal{M})$.\n\n**② Fine sheaves $\\implies$ soft sheaves (Čech terms need not be soft).** On a paracompact Hausdorff space, locally finite covers admit subordinate partitions of unity (`prop.paracompact-partition-unity`). The two formulations of fineness apply as follows. **(a) Sheaves of $\\mathcal C(X)$-modules**: A partition of unity for a locally finite cover $\\{V_{j}\\}$, denoted by $\\{\\rho_{j}\\}$ ($\\sum_{j}\\rho_{j}=1$, $\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_{j}\\subseteq V_{j}$), gives endomorphisms by multiplication by $\\rho_{j}$, so $\\rho_{j}s_{j}$ is defined pointwise. **(b) General sheaves of abelian groups**: Use a **partition by endomorphisms** $\\eta_{j}:\\mathcal F \\to \\mathcal F$, with $\\operatorname{supp}\\eta_{j}\\subseteq V_{j}$ and $\\sum_{j}\\eta_{j}=\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathcal F}$ (a locally finite sum). The argument has the same form in both cases. Let $F \\subseteq X$ be closed and take $s \\in \\mathcal F(F)$. Each $x \\in F$ has a neighborhood $U_{x}$ and a section $s_{x} \\in \\mathcal F(U_{x})$ such that $s_{x}|_{F \\cap U_{x}}=s$. Take a locally finite refinement of $\\{U_{x}\\}_{x \\in F} \\cup \\{X \\setminus F\\}$, denoted by $\\{V_{j}\\}$, with the corresponding $\\rho_{j}$ or $\\eta_{j}$ as above. Take $s_{j} \\in \\mathcal F(V_{j})$ to be the corresponding local section (when $V_{j} \\subseteq X \\setminus F$, take $0$). In case (b), $\\operatorname{supp}\\eta_{j} \\subseteq V_{j}$ ensures that $\\eta_{j}(s_{j})$ extends outside $V_{j}$ by $0$ (both sections equal $0$ on the overlap, so they agree). Thus $\\tilde{s}:=\\sum_{j}\\rho_{j}s_{j}$ (case (a), multiplication of a section by a function) or $\\tilde{s}:=\\sum_{j}\\eta_{j}(s_{j})$ (case (b), application of an endomorphism) is a global section on $X$, given by a locally finite sum, and on $F$ it equals $s$. Hence $\\mathcal F$ is soft.\n\n**② Gap: acyclicity of soft sheaves (Godement).** On a paracompact Hausdorff space, a soft sheaf $\\mathcal{M}$ satisfies $H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})=0$ ($n \\ge 1$). This does not follow directly from ①. That step only proves that the Čech complex is a resolution, and Čech cohomology for a fixed cover can differ from derived functor cohomology (see the counterexample in ④). There are two standard approaches. For $n=1$, use the fact that softness makes the global sections of $0 \\to \\mathcal{M} \\to \\mathcal{G} \\to \\mathcal{H} \\to 0$ exact. Apply Zorn's lemma along a locally finite closed refinement $\\{A_{i}\\}$. Given a lift over the closed union $B$, denoted by $s$, choose $A_{i} \\not\\subseteq B$. By softness, the difference $s-s_{i} \\in \\mathcal{M}(B \\cap A_{i})$ extends to a global section $t$. On $A_{i}$ use $s_{i}+t$ instead, and glue along the closed cover $\\{B,A_{i}\\}$. Maximality gives $B=X$. The standard approach for $n \\ge 2$ uses a **Godement resolution** (each term is flabby, and flabby $\\implies$ soft) and dimension shifting. ⚠️ **This gap is only partially filled**. In the same cluster, `thm.soft-sheaf-acyclic` gives steps 0–2 (flabby $\\implies$ soft, the lifting lemma, and softness of the quotient). Its step 3 (dimension shifting) **depends on the acyclicity of flabby sheaves**, for which the graph has no lemma node. Step ② therefore remains conditional. With an injective resolution $0 \\to \\mathcal{M} \\to \\mathcal{I} \\to \\mathcal{Q} \\to 0$, treating $\\mathcal{Q}=\\mathcal{I}/\\mathcal{M}$ as flabby sheaves and arguing directly by descending induction **still fails**. **Quotients of flabby sheaves need not be flabby**, so this does not provide an alternative proof.\n\n**③ Acyclic resolutions compute derived functors (the graph has no separate node for this lemma).** If $0 \\to \\mathcal{M} \\to \\mathcal{A}^{0} \\to \\mathcal{A}^{1} \\to \\cdots$ is a resolution and each $\\mathcal{A}^{q}$ satisfies $H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{A}^{q})=0$ ($n \\ge 1$), then $H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M}) \\cong H^{n}(\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal{A}^{\\bullet}))$. The proof uses dimension shifting. Set $\\mathcal{Z}^{0}=\\mathcal{M}$ and $\\mathcal{Z}^{q+1}=\\operatorname{im}(\\mathcal{A}^{q} \\to \\mathcal{A}^{q+1})$. The long exact sequence of the short exact sequence $0 \\to \\mathcal{Z}^{q} \\to \\mathcal{A}^{q} \\to \\mathcal{Z}^{q+1} \\to 0$ gives $H^{n}(\\mathcal{Z}^{q+1}) \\cong H^{n+1}(\\mathcal{Z}^{q})$ ($n \\ge 1$) and $H^{1}(\\mathcal{Z}^{q}) \\cong \\operatorname{coker}(\\Gamma(\\mathcal{A}^{q}) \\to \\Gamma(\\mathcal{Z}^{q+1}))$. Iterating gives the result. The graph contains `def.right-derived-functor` and `thm.derived-long-exact` as references. The argument is complete and only a separate lemma node is missing, as in step 3 of the edge `imp.paracompact-cech`.\n\n**④ The missing comparison step (correcting the earlier claim that every term is soft).** In ①, $\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p}$ is a product of direct images along open inclusions $j_{*}$, and $j_{*}$ is generally only left exact. For an arbitrary sheaf $\\mathcal{M}$, $\\check{\\mathcal{C}}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathcal{M})$ **need not be soft or acyclic**, so ③ cannot be applied directly to ①. To finish the proof, one needs Čech acyclicity of the soft sheaf $\\implies$ for locally finite covers, or the equivalent double complex comparison. For the latter, replace $\\mathcal{M}$ with a soft resolution $\\mathcal{S}^{\\bullet}$ and use the two filtrations of the double complex $\\check{C}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathcal{S}^{q})$. The graph contains `thm.cartan-leray` and `cor.cech-cohomology-spectral`, but has no lemma on Čech acyclicity of soft sheaves and no account of the two filtrations detailed enough to verify. Completing ④ gives $\\check{H}^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})\\cong H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})$. Until then, this node remains marked as a skeleton.\n\n⚠️ **Why the claim fails for an arbitrary individual cover (the last sentence of the statement reflects this)**: Take $X=S^{2}$, $\\mathcal{M}=\\mathbb{Z}$, and the open cover by two hemispheres $\\{U,V\\}$, which is locally finite. The intersection $U\\cap V$ is homeomorphic to an annulus, and $\\check{C}^{2}=0$, so $\\check{H}^{2}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathbb{Z})=0$, whereas $H^{2}(S^{2},\\mathbb{Z})=\\mathbb{Z}$. The claim for an individual cover is therefore false in general. The correct general statement uses the direct limit over covers $\\check{H}^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})=\\varinjlim_{\\mathfrak{U}}\\check{H}^{n}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathcal{M})\\cong H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})$. The isomorphism holds for an individual **good cover** (one whose finite intersections have vanishing higher cohomology, with contractible finite intersections in the constant sheaf case).\n\n**⑤ The proof for a good cover can be completed using `thm.cartan-leray`.** If every finite intersection of the cover $\\mathfrak{U}$, denoted by $U_{i_{0}\\cdots i_{p}}$, satisfies $H^{q}(U_{i_{0}\\cdots i_{p}},\\mathcal{M})=0$ ($q>0$), each term of the Čech complex $C^{\\bullet}(\\mathfrak{U},H^{q}(\\mathcal{M}))$ is a product of these groups $H^{q}(U_{I},\\mathcal{M})=0$. The Čech terms are evaluated on finite intersections and are precisely sections of the presheaf $U\\mapsto H^{q}(U,\\mathcal{M})$. Only vanishing of the presheaf on intersections of the covering sets is needed, regardless of whether the sheaf $H^{q}(\\mathcal{M})$ itself is zero. Thus the term $E_{2}^{p,q}=\\check{H}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U},H^{q}(\\mathcal{M}))$ of the Cartan–Leray spectral sequence is, when $q>0$, equal to $0$. The spectral sequence degenerates and gives $\\check{H}^{n}(\\mathfrak{U},\\mathcal{M})\\cong H^{n}(X,\\mathcal{M})$. This establishes the isomorphism for an individual good cover. The general direct limit over locally finite covers still requires ② and ④.\n\n**Conclusion**: Step ② depends on the acyclicity of flabby sheaves used in `thm.soft-sheaf-acyclic`, which is unproved in the graph. The comparison in ④ is still needed to finish the proof using the Čech resolution in ① as an acyclic resolution. For good covers, degeneration of the Cartan–Leray spectral sequence suffices. ⚠ This node **remains marked as a skeleton**. The missing step is ④ (Čech acyclicity of soft sheaves or the double complex comparison). Step ①, fine $\\implies$ soft, and step ③ are proved above. Step ② depends on flabby acyclicity, which is unproved in the graph.\n\n⭐ **Relation to the compact Hausdorff result**: Every compact Hausdorff space is paracompact, so this result includes `prop.chaus-cech-eq-h`. The **proofs** use different arguments. The compactness proof uses the cofinality of finite covers in the filtered system and the control of finite products by representable objects. The paracompactness proof uses local finiteness and the softness obtained from partitions of unity. The two arguments establish the same conclusion independently.\n\n⚠️ **Paracompactness cannot be dropped**. On general spaces, even compactly generated ones, Čech cohomology and sheaf cohomology can differ. The claim that Čech cohomology equals sheaf cohomology is often quoted without qualification in topology and sheaf theory. Include the hypotheses on the space when citing it.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c4ae610'
    },
  },
  'thm.nagata-smirnov': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $X$ be a **regular** space (including $T_{1}$) whose topology has a **$\\sigma$-locally finite base** (a base expressible as the union of countably many locally finite subfamilies). Then $X$ is **metrizable**.\n\nConversely, every metrizable space has a $\\sigma$-locally finite base, so this is a **necessary and sufficient condition**.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#566559a6'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Why this is stronger than Urysohn's theorem**: A countable base can be split into countably many discrete families by placing each basic open set in a singleton family. Thus second countability is a special case of having a $\\sigma$-locally finite base. Here the hypothesis is weakened to $\\sigma$-local finiteness, with the same conclusion.\n\n**Proof sketch**\n- Arrange the $\\sigma$-locally finite base in layers. Each layer is a locally finite family, and regularity allows these to be turned into **locally finite open covers**.\n- The key step (Nagata's systematic neighborhoods) is to construct a countable sequence of locally finite covers $\\mathcal{G}_{n}$ ($n \\ge 1$) such that for every open set $U$ and every $x \\in U$, some member of one cover contains $x$ in its interior and is contained in $U$.\n- Use these covers to define a nonnegative symmetric function by summing contributions to separation from each layer. Verify the three metric axioms and check that the induced topology is exactly the original topology.\n\n⚠️ **Scope**: This node gives a proof outline and a reference. Munkres devotes all of Section 40 to the theorem.\n\n⚠️ Regularity is essential. The base condition alone does not guarantee metrizability. The proof uses the ability to separate a point from a closed set by basic open sets.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#62ae1028'
    },
  },
  'thm.liouville-formula': {
    statement: {
      en: "In plain terms, **geodesic curvature is the turning rate of the curve minus the turning rate of the frame due to the metric**. The latter is determined solely by the partial derivatives of $E, G$. Thus the metric, that is, $I$, suffices to compute the geodesic curvature of any curve, independently of how the surface is embedded in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$.\n\nLet $(u,v)$ be an **orthogonal** coordinate net on $S$ ($F = 0$, $I = E\\,\\mathrm{d}u^{2}+G\\,\\mathrm{d}v^{2}$). Let $\\gamma$ be parametrized by arc length, and let $\\theta(s)$ be the angle between $\\dot\\gamma(s)$ and the $u$ coordinate line (in the direction $\\partial_{u}$). Then\n\n$$k_{g}(\\gamma) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}s}-\\frac{(\\ln E)_{v}}{2\\sqrt{G}}\\cos\\theta+\\frac{(\\ln G)_{u}}{2\\sqrt{E}}\\sin\\theta.$$\n\nIn particular, $\\gamma$ is a geodesic if and only if $\\theta$ satisfies this first-order equation. The change in direction of a geodesic is completely determined by the derivatives of the metric coefficients.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4caec749'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Structure**: $k_{g} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}s}-\\omega^{1}_{2}(\\dot\\gamma)$, where the first term is the turning rate of the curve relative to the frame and the second is the turning rate of the frame itself.\n\nSubstituting $\\omega^{1}_{2} = \\frac{(\\ln E)_{v}}{2\\sqrt G}\\omega^{1}-\\frac{(\\ln G)_{u}}{2\\sqrt E}\\omega^{2}$ gives the formula above.\n\n**Two checks** (both can be done by hand):\n\n- In plane polar coordinates ($E = 1$, $G = \\rho^{2}$), a circle of radius $\\rho_{0}$ centered at the origin has $\\theta\\equiv\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}s} = 0$; the formula gives $k_{g} = \\frac{2/\\rho_{0}}{2} = \\frac{1}{\\rho_{0}}$, the curvature of that circle for the polar metric.\n\n\n- On the sphere ($I = \\mathrm{d}\\rho^{2}+\\sin^{2}\\rho\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta^{2}$), the circle of latitude $\\rho = \\rho_{0}$ gives $k_{g} = \\frac{2\\cot\\rho_{0}}{2} = \\cot\\rho_{0}$, in agreement with direct computation.\n\n⚠️ A common error is to simplify the formula to $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}s}+(k_{1}-k_{2})\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ in curvature-line coordinates.\n\nThe sphere gives a counterexample. On a circle of latitude, $\\theta\\equiv\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}s}\\equiv 0$, while $k_{g} = \\cot\\rho_{0}\\ne0$.\n\nOn the sphere, $k_{1} = k_{2} = 1$ also makes both terms on the right-hand side vanish.\n\n**This simplified formula is invalid. Do not use it.**\n\n**Special case**: If the orthogonal net also uses geodesic coordinates ($E\\equiv1$), the formula reduces to $k_{g} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}s}+\\frac{(\\ln G)_{u}}{2}\\sin\\theta$. If, in addition, $G$ is independent of $u$, this is the plane polar coordinate case.\n\n⭐ This formula makes it **explicit** that computing $k_{g}$ requires only $I$. The right-hand side involves only $E,G$ and the angle of the curve, with no extrinsic data.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#be086964'
    },
  },
  'lem.single-cube-stokes': {
    statement: {
      en: "**The calculation on a standard cube**: Stokes' theorem is first verified on a single standard cube. The left-hand side is a multiple integral, and the right-hand side integrates over $2k$ faces. The two sides agree term by term, checking all the sign conventions.\n\nLet $\\eta$ be defined on an open set containing $I^{k} = [0,1]^{k}$ and be a $(k-1)$-form ($k\\ge 1$). Then\n\n$$\\int_{I^{k}}\\mathrm{d}\\eta = \\int_{\\partial I^{k}}\\eta ,$$\n\nHere $I^{k}$ has the standard orientation, and $\\partial I^{k}$ is interpreted according to the sign convention in `def.boundary-operator` as a $(k-1)$-chain.\n\nWhen $k = 1$, this is the fundamental theorem of calculus: $\\int_{0}^{1}F^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = F(1)-F(0)$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#1b1f3aa2'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof**: Expand $\\eta$ uniquely according to which $\\mathrm{d}x^{j}$ is omitted:\n\n$$\\eta = \\sum_{j=1}^{k}(-1)^{j-1} f_{j}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\widehat{\\mathrm{d}x^{j}}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k},$$\n\nHere $f_{j}$ are smooth functions on an open set containing $I^{k}$ (a hat indicates that the factor is omitted).\n\n**Left-hand side.** Take the exterior derivative term by term. When $\\mathrm{d}f_{j}$ is wedged with the remaining factors, only the $\\mathrm{d}x^{j}$ term survives, and\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\left((-1)^{j-1} f_{j}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\widehat{\\mathrm{d}x^{j}}\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k}\\right) = \\partial_{j} f_{j}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k},$$\n\nHence $\\mathrm{d}\\eta = \\sum_{j=1}^{k}\\partial_{j} f_{j}\\,\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k}$. By Fubini's theorem and the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus (integrating first with respect to $x^{j}$ and denoting the remaining coordinates by $y$),\n\n$$\\int_{I^{k}}\\mathrm{d}\\eta = \\sum_{j=1}^{k}\\int_{I^{k-1}}\\left[f_{j}\\big|_{x^{j}=1} - f_{j}\\big|_{x^{j}=0}\\right]\\mathrm{d}y.$$\n\n**Right-hand side.** By `def.boundary-operator`, $\\partial I^{k} = \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sum_{\\alpha=0}^{1}(-1)^{i+\\alpha} I^{k}_{(i,\\alpha)}$. On face $(i,\\alpha)$, the pullback of $\\mathrm{d}x^{i}$ is $0$, so only the term with $j = i$ survives:\n\n$$I^{k}_{(i,\\alpha)}{}^{*}\\eta = (-1)^{i-1} f_{i}\\big|_{x^{i}=\\alpha}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}y^{k-1},$$\n\nHere $y$ denotes the remaining $k-1$ coordinates on the face, in their original order. Thus\n\n$$\\int_{\\partial I^{k}}\\eta = \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sum_{\\alpha=0}^{1}(-1)^{i+\\alpha}(-1)^{i-1}\\int_{I^{k-1}} f_{i}\\big|_{x^{i}=\\alpha}\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\int_{I^{k-1}}\\left[f_{i}\\big|_{x^{i}=1} - f_{i}\\big|_{x^{i}=0}\\right]\\mathrm{d}y,$$\n\nThe last step uses $(-1)^{i+\\alpha+i-1} = (-1)^{\\alpha+1}$. The case $\\alpha=1$ gives $+1$, and the case $\\alpha=0$ gives $-1$.\n\nThe two sides agree term by term, so $\\int_{I^{k}}\\mathrm{d}\\eta = \\int_{\\partial I^{k}}\\eta$. When $k = 1$, we have $\\eta = f$ and $\\mathrm{d}\\eta = f^{\\prime}\\mathrm{d}x$. The two sides give $\\int_{0}^{1} f^{\\prime} = f(1) - f(0)$ and $f(1) - f(0)$, respectively, so the sign conventions agree. ∎\n\n**Why this case comes first**: The case of a general cube $c$ follows by pullback ($c^{\\ast}\\mathrm{d} = \\mathrm{d}c^{\\ast}$), which reduces it to this lemma. This lemma therefore supplies the **only** substantive calculation in Stokes' theorem for chains. The rest is the passage to general cubes and chains.\n\n**⚠️ The most common sign error**: When $\\eta$ is written as $\\sum_{j}(-1)^{j-1}f_{j}\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\widehat{\\mathrm{d}x^{j}}\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k}$ (a hat indicates that the factor is omitted), there is **no** additional sign in $\\mathrm{d}\\eta = \\sum_{j}\\partial_{j}f_{j}\\mathrm{d}x^{1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\mathrm{d}x^{k}$. Inserting an extra $(-1)^{j-1}$ here throws off the matching of the face terms on the right-hand side.\n\n**⚠️ Measure-zero sets on the boundary**: The form $\\eta$ need only be defined on an open set and continuous up to the boundary. The facewise pullbacks use the values of $\\eta$ on each face, and $\\partial I^{k}$ has measure zero, so the integrals are independent of the choice of extension.\n\n**Relation to the classical formulas**: When $k = 2$, this is Green's theorem. When $k = 3$, it is the Kelvin–Stokes theorem in circulation form. Both are special cases of this lemma.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d9e65b0a'
    },
  },
  'thm.stokes-chain': {
    statement: {
      en: "**What it says**: Integrating a $(k-1)$-form over the **boundary chain** gives the same result as integrating its exterior derivative over the **chain**. This is the common generalization of the fundamental theorem of calculus, Green's theorem, Gauss's theorem, and the Kelvin–Stokes theorem.\n\nLet $c$ be a chain in $M$ of dimension $k$ ($k\\ge 1$), and let $\\omega$ be defined on a neighborhood of the image of $c$ and be a $(k-1)$-form. Then\n\n$$\\int_{\\partial c}\\omega = \\int_{c}\\mathrm{d}\\omega .$$\n\n**Relation to the manifold version** (`thm.stokes`): The manifold version requires $M$ to be compact and orientable with boundary, and $\\omega$ to be defined on $M$. The chain version does not require $M$ to be orientable or compact, or $\\omega$ to have compact support. It uses a chain in place of a manifold.\n\nEach is a special case of the other. Decompose (or subdivide) $M$ into a covering $n$-chain. Summing the chain formula term by term gives the manifold version.\n\nConversely, restricting the manifold version to a single orientation-preserving parametrized cube gives the single-cube case of the chain version.\n\nWhen $k = 1$ and $c = \\gamma : [0,1]\\to\\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\partial\\gamma = \\gamma(1)-\\gamma(0)$, and the formula becomes $F(\\gamma(1))-F(\\gamma(0)) = \\int_{0}^{1}(F^{\\prime}\\circ\\gamma)\\,\\gamma^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, precisely the fundamental theorem of calculus.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#7601ae5f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**The proof takes only three lines**: $\\int_{c}\\mathrm{d}\\omega = \\int_{I^{k}}c^{\\ast}\\mathrm{d}\\omega = \\int_{I^{k}}\\mathrm{d}(c^{\\ast}\\omega) = \\int_{\\partial I^{k}}c^{\\ast}\\omega = \\int_{\\partial c}\\omega$.\n\nThe four steps use, respectively, the definition of integration over a chain, commutation of $c^{\\ast}$ with $\\mathrm{d}$, `lem.single-cube-stokes`, and the definition and linearity of $\\partial$.\n\nThe lemma contains the entire difficulty. Here we simply express it in chain language.\n\n**Why no orientation is required**: For chains, the parametrization already encodes the orientation (`def.integral-on-chain`), so orientability does not enter the statement. An orientation is needed when passing back from the chain version to the manifold version. The signs then come from `prop.boundary-sign-convention`.\n\n**Four in one**: $k = 1$ gives the fundamental theorem of calculus. $k = 2$ gives Green's theorem. The two forms of $k = 3$ are Gauss–Ostrogradsky (flux, i.e., the divergence theorem `thm.divergence-theorem`) and Kelvin–Stokes (circulation). These are readings of the same theorem for different $k$.\n\n**⚠️ Degree and dimension**: The degree of $\\omega$ must be one less than the dimension of the chain. When $k = 0$, we have $\\partial c = 0$ and the right-hand side is zero, so the formula reduces to $0 = 0$. Thus the theorem starts at $k\\ge 1$.\n\n**⚠️ Stating the theorem only for closed chains would be too restrictive**: The theorem does not require $c$ to be a closed chain. When $\\partial c\\ne 0$, the two sides still agree. The vanishing of the pairing between a closed chain and an exact form ($\\int_{c}\\mathrm{d}\\omega = 0$) is a corollary.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2061b2d4'
    },
  },
  'thm.frobenius': {
    statement: {
      en: "**In plain language**: A smooth family of directions fits together into sheets if and only if it is involutive. Involutivity is the only obstruction. This is the geometric version of solvability for systems of first-order partial differential equations, and it explains how foliations arise.\n\nLet $\\mathcal{D}$ be a tangent distribution on an $n$-dimensional smooth manifold $M$, smooth and of rank $k$. The following four conditions are equivalent:\n\n1. $\\mathcal{D}$ is involutive (`def.involutive-distribution`).\n2. **Existence of flat coordinates**: Each point $p$ has a neighborhood $U$ with coordinates $(x^{1},\\ldots,x^{n})$ in which $\\mathcal{D}|_{U}$ is spanned by $\\partial_{1},\\ldots,\\partial_{k}$.\n3. **Local integrability**: Each point $p$ lies in an $k$-dimensional integral manifold (`def.integral-manifold`).\n4. **Existence of a foliation**: $M$ is covered by a family of pairwise disjoint maximal connected integral manifolds (leaves), each of which is an immersed submanifold.\n\nIn particular, involutivity holds **if and only if** there are always local coordinates that straighten $\\mathcal{D}$ into coordinate slices. When $k = n-1$ and $\\mathcal{D} = \\ker\\omega$ (with $\\omega$ nowhere zero), involutivity is equivalent to\n\n$$\\omega \\wedge \\mathrm{d}\\omega = 0,$$\n\nThis is the classical Frobenius condition. Its proof is the explicit calculation for hyperplane distributions corresponding to the flow-box argument for (1)$\\iff$(2) below.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#cb3c206c'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof structure**: Condition (2) drives the proof of the four-way equivalence. The two proof edges are `imp.mf2-frobenius-flat` (the flow-box argument and induction on rank for (1)$\\implies$(2)) and `imp.mf2-frobenius-integral` ((2)$\\implies$(3), (3)$\\implies$(1), (2)$\\implies$(4)). The main argument is presented step by step below and can be read independently.\n\n**I. (1)$\\implies$(2): Flow box and induction on rank.** Choose $p$ and, on a neighborhood of $p$, take smooth pointwise independent generators $X_{1},\\ldots,X_{k}$.\n\n1. By `thm.flow-local`, straighten $X_{1}$ along its local flow. There are coordinates $(y^{1},\\ldots,y^{n})$ such that $X_{1}=\\partial_{1}$ (the flow-box theorem).\n2. Write the remaining generators as $X_{i}=a_{i}\\,\\partial_{1}+Y_{i}$ ($i\\ge2$), where $Y_{i}$ has only $\\partial_{2},\\ldots,\\partial_{n}$ components. Since $\\mathrm{d}y^{1}$ is a closed form,\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}y^{1}\\bigl([\\partial_{1},Y_{i}]\\bigr)=\\partial_{1}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}y^{1}(Y_{i})\\bigr)-Y_{i}\\bigl(\\mathrm{d}y^{1}(\\partial_{1})\\bigr)=0,$$\n\nso $[\\partial_{1},Y_{i}]$ also has only $\\partial_{2},\\ldots,\\partial_{n}$ components.\n3. Involutivity gives that $[X_{1},X_{i}]=[\\partial_{1},a_{i}\\partial_{1}+Y_{i}]=(\\partial_{1}a_{i})\\partial_{1}+[\\partial_{1},Y_{i}]$ lies in $\\mathcal{D}=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\partial_{1},Y_{2},\\ldots,Y_{k}\\}$. Comparing the $\\partial_{1}$ components and using the preceding step gives\n\n$$\\partial_{1}a_{i}=0,\\qquad [\\partial_{1},Y_{i}]\\in\\operatorname{span}\\{Y_{2},\\ldots,Y_{k}\\}.$$\n\nThe latter equation shows that on the transverse hypersurface $S=\\{y^{1}=0\\}$, the distribution $\\mathcal{D}_{S}=\\operatorname{span}\\{Y_{2},\\ldots,Y_{k}\\}$ is involutive (of rank $k-1$).\n4. Induct on $k$ (when $k=1$, $\\partial_{1}$ itself gives the desired coordinates). By the induction hypothesis, on $S$ there are coordinates $(x^{2},\\ldots,x^{n})$ in which $\\mathcal{D}_{S}$ is spanned by $\\partial_{2},\\ldots,\\partial_{k}$. Use the flow of $X_{1}=\\partial_{1}$ to transport the coordinates of $S$ in the $x^{1}$ direction, obtaining on a neighborhood of $p$ the coordinates $(x^{1},x^{2},\\ldots,x^{n})$.\n5. In these coordinates, $[\\partial_{1},\\partial_{a}]=0$ ($a\\ge2$). Write $Y_{i}$ as $Y_{i}=\\sum_{a\\ge2}f^{a}_{i}\\,\\partial_{a}$. Since $[\\partial_{1},Y_{i}]\\in\\operatorname{span}\\{Y_{j}\\}$ has no $\\partial_{1}$ component and $[\\partial_{1},\\partial_{a}]=0$, we obtain $\\partial_{1}f^{a}_{i}=0$. On the slice $x^{1}=0$, we have $Y_{i}=\\partial_{i}$ ($i=2,\\ldots,k$ by the induction hypothesis), so $f^{a}_{i}=\\delta^{a}_{i}$ is independent of $x^{1}$. Thus $Y_{i}=\\partial_{i}$ holds throughout the neighborhood. Hence\n\n$$\\mathcal{D}=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\partial_{1},\\ldots,\\partial_{k}\\}.\\qquad\\blacksquare$$\n\n**II. (2)$\\implies$(3):** In flat coordinates, the tangent spaces of $N=\\{x^{k+1}=\\cdots=x^{n}=0\\}$ are spanned precisely by $\\partial_{1},\\ldots,\\partial_{k}$, so $N$ is an $k$-dimensional integral manifold through $p$. $\\square$\n\n**III. (3)$\\implies$(1):** If $X,Y$ are tangent at $p$ to an integral manifold $N$, then $[X,Y]_{p}$ is also tangent to $N$ (by naturality of the bracket, $[X,Y]$ depends only on first-order information about $X,Y$, and after extending $X,Y$ to vector fields tangent to $N$, their bracket still lies in $TN$, see `def.lie-bracket`). Thus $\\mathcal{D}$ is involutive. $\\square$\n\n**IV. (2)$\\implies$(4):** Each point has a coordinate chart $N_{q}$. Order all connected integral manifolds by inclusion and apply Zorn's lemma to obtain maximal elements. The union of a chain is still a connected integral manifold, since locally it is part of a coordinate chart and smoothness is preserved along the chain. The maximal elements are pairwise disjoint, since otherwise their union would still be a connected integral manifold, and they cover $M$. We need $M$ to be second countable to ensure that the set of leaves is countable and the construction is well-defined. Second countability is already part of the definition of a manifold. See `imp.mf2-frobenius-integral` for all four steps. $\\square$\n\n**V. The calculation for $k=n-1$ and $\\omega\\wedge\\mathrm{d}\\omega=0$.** Suppose $\\mathcal{D}=\\ker\\omega$ and $\\omega$ is nowhere zero. For $X,Y\\in\\ker\\omega$,\n\n$$\\mathrm{d}\\omega(X,Y)=X\\omega(Y)-Y\\omega(X)-\\omega([X,Y])=-\\omega([X,Y]).$$\n\nThus involutivity ($[X,Y]\\in\\ker\\omega$) is equivalent to $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ vanishing identically on $\\ker\\omega\\times\\ker\\omega$. In turn, $\\mathrm{d}\\omega$ vanishes on $\\ker\\omega$ if and only if $\\mathrm{d}\\omega=\\omega\\wedge\\alpha$ (for some $1$-form $\\alpha$), which is equivalent to $\\omega\\wedge\\mathrm{d}\\omega=0$, as one sees by expanding both sides using the same $\\alpha$. $\\square$\n\n**VI. The condition is sharp.** A contact distribution is a counterexample (see the notes to `def.involutive-distribution`). It is smooth, has rank $2$, and is nowhere zero, yet fails to be involutive, so no surface can be an integral manifold for it. Involutivity is therefore necessary as well as sufficient.\n\n**VII. The conclusion is local.** The theorem gives foliation coordinates near each point. It does **not** guarantee global coordinates straightening $\\mathcal{D}$ into coordinate slices of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which would require the leaf space itself to be a product manifold. The irrational-slope foliation on the torus is a counterexample. Its leaves are locally straight lines, yet each winds densely around the torus.\n\n**VIII. Analytic formulation and smoothness.** Frobenius gives the compatibility condition for local solvability of the system of first-order linear partial differential equations $\\partial_{i}f=\\sum_{j}a^{j}_{i}\\partial_{j}f$ ($i>k$). Compatibility is precisely involutivity, which is also how to read $\\mathrm{d}^{2}=0$. Step 1 uses Picard–Lindelöf to solve the ODE, so $\\mathcal{D}$ must be smooth (at least $C^{2}$ to discuss brackets). Below $C^{1}$, there are counterexamples that are nowhere involutive yet have integral surfaces everywhere.\n\n**IX. Notation.** Here we use the language of families of $k$-dimensional subspaces. Textbooks often use integral manifolds of a differential ideal $I\\subseteq\\Omega^{\\bullet}(M)$. The two formulations are equivalent (take $I$ to be the ideal generated by forms annihilating $\\mathcal{D}$). We use only the distribution formulation here to avoid introducing a second notation. The cases $k=n$ and $k=0$ are trivial. $k=1$ is always involutive, and the nontrivial examples begin at $2\\le k\\le n-1$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9617c8aa'
    },
  },
  'thm.de-rham': {
    statement: {
      en: "**In plain language**: The cohomology defined using calculus (closed forms modulo exact forms) is the same as the topological cohomology built from simplices (by dualizing singular chains). This theorem connects analysis, geometry, and topology.\n\nLet $M$ be a smooth manifold. For each $k \\ge 0$ there is an isomorphism\n\n$$\\Phi^{k} : H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M) \\longrightarrow H^{k}_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb{R}),\\qquad [\\omega] \\longmapsto \\Bigl([c] \\mapsto \\int_{c}\\omega\\Bigr),$$\n\nwhere $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb{R}) := H^{k}\\bigl(\\operatorname{Hom}(C^{\\mathrm{sing}}_{\\bullet}(M),\\mathbb{R})\\bigr)$ is the cohomology of the dual of the singular chain complex (`def.singular-chain`), and $\\int_{c}\\omega$ is integration of forms over chains.\n\n$\\Phi^{k}$ is compatible with pullbacks, so $k \\mapsto \\Phi^{k}$ is a family of natural isomorphisms. $H^{\\bullet}_{\\mathrm{dR}}$ and $H^{\\bullet}_{\\mathrm{sing}}$ are two ways to compute the same functor.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#a08b3fdf'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**This node retains skeleton status**: The graph has no full proof of the de Rham theorem. The outline below divides the proof into six steps and identifies which parts can be verified in full within the graph and which require external theorems. It names each missing result so that it can be checked individually as nodes are added.\n**Step 0: Conventions.** For smooth singular chains $C^{\\infty}_{\\bullet}(M)$, each simplex $\\sigma:\\Delta^{k}\\to M$ is smooth and extends to a prism neighborhood of $\\Delta^{k}$, so every face is smooth. $\\Omega^{q}$ is the sheaf of $q$-forms, and $\\mathrm{d}$ is the differential of the complex of sheaves. $\\Gamma(M,\\Omega^{\\bullet})=\\Omega^{\\bullet}(M)$ is the de Rham complex.\n**Step 1: The pairing is well-defined (fully verifiable within the graph).** First note that $\\int_{c}\\omega$ is undefined on **continuous** singular chains, since $c$ need not be smooth and $\\omega$ is defined only on smooth tangent vectors. We therefore use smooth singular chains. Both invariance properties follow from `thm.stokes-chain`: if $\\omega$ is closed and $c=\\partial b$, then $\\int_{c}\\omega=\\int_{b}\\mathrm{d}\\omega=0$. If $\\omega=\\mathrm{d}\\eta$, then $\\int_{c}\\mathrm{d}\\eta=\\int_{\\partial c}\\eta=0$ (with $c$ a closed chain). Thus $\\Phi^{k}([\\omega])([c])=\\int_{c}\\omega$ is well-defined and clearly $\\mathbb{R}$-bilinear.\n**Step 2: $\\Omega^{\\bullet}$ is a resolution of the constant sheaf $\\underline{\\mathbb{R}}$ (exactness is fully verifiable within the graph).** Exactness at each stalk follows from the Poincaré lemma (`lem.poincare-lemma`). $\\ker(\\mathrm{d}:\\Omega^{0}\\to\\Omega^{1})$ is the sheaf of locally constant functions $\\underline{\\mathbb{R}}$. Since $\\Omega^{q}$ is a fine sheaf and fine sheaves on paracompact manifolds have vanishing higher cohomology (Godement's lemma), we obtain $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)=H^{k}\\bigl(\\Gamma(M,\\Omega^{\\bullet})\\bigr)\\cong H^{k}(M;\\underline{\\mathbb{R}}).$. The **exactness** in this step can be checked in full. **Acyclicity of fine sheaves** is an external lemma (Gap D).\n**Step 3: The Čech–de Rham double complex.** Choose a good cover $\\mathfrak{U}=\\{U_\\alpha\\}$ (each point has a basis of contractible neighborhoods). Arrange $\\check{C}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U},\\Omega^{q})$ into a double complex with the Čech coboundary $\\delta$ as the horizontal differential and $\\mathrm{d}$ as the vertical differential. The cohomology of the total complex $\\operatorname{Tot}$ can be computed in either direction. Taking cohomology first with respect to $\\mathrm{d}$ (each column $\\Omega^{\\bullet}(U_{p})$ is exact by the Poincaré lemma, leaving only, at $q=0$, the term $H^{0}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(U_{p})=\\mathbb{R}$ in the sense of the corresponding column term) gives $E_{1}^{p,0}=\\check{C}^{p}(\\mathfrak{U};\\underline{\\mathbb{R}})$, hence $H^{\\bullet}(\\operatorname{Tot})\\cong\\check{H}^{\\bullet}(\\mathfrak{U};\\underline{\\mathbb{R}})$. Taking cohomology first with respect to $\\delta$ ($\\Omega^{q}$ is fine and the Čech complex on a good cover is exact at $p>0$) gives $E_{1}^{0,q}=\\Omega^{q}(M)$, hence $H^{\\bullet}(\\operatorname{Tot})\\cong H^{\\bullet}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)$. Thus $H^{\\bullet}_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\cong\\check{H}^{\\bullet}(\\mathfrak{U};\\underline{\\mathbb{R}})$. This requires **Gap B (existence of good covers)** and **Gap C (spectral sequences of double complexes)**.\n**Step 4: Čech cohomology equals sheaf cohomology.** On paracompact spaces, $\\check{H}^{\\bullet}(\\mathfrak{U};\\underline{\\mathbb{R}})\\cong H^{\\bullet}(M;\\underline{\\mathbb{R}})$ (`thm.paracompact-cech-equals-sheaf`). ⚠️ That node **also remains a skeleton**. It outlines the argument using the facts that fine sheaves are soft and soft sheaves are acyclic, followed by an acyclic resolution, but leaves Godement's lemma and the comparison lemma unproved. This step is therefore also unproved in the graph and is labeled **Gap E**.\n**Step 5: Sheaf cohomology equals singular cohomology.** Both functors are homotopy invariant, agree with $\\mathbb{R}$ on a point, and have long exact sequences for good covers. The Eilenberg–Steenrod uniqueness theorem gives a natural isomorphism. The graph has neither a node nor a proof for this axiomatic uniqueness result, labeled **Gap F**.\n**Step 6: $\\Phi$ agrees with these isomorphisms (naturality).** One must verify that the chain-level map $\\omega\\mapsto\\bigl(c\\mapsto\\int_{c}\\omega\\bigr)$ agrees with the composite of the isomorphisms constructed in Steps 2–3 and is natural under pullbacks ($\\int_{f_{*}c}f^{*}\\omega=\\int_{c}\\omega$). Stokes gives commutativity, but the full verification, especially the passage between smooth and continuous chains, is missing. This is **Gap G**.\n**Summary of gaps**: (A) Smooth singular chains $\\simeq$ continuous singular chains (simplicial approximation and smoothing). (B) Existence of good covers on smooth manifolds (geodesically convex neighborhoods and convexity radius). (C) Convergence of spectral sequences of double complexes (the graph has only ingredients such as `def.spectral-sequence`, `thm.filtered-complex-spectral`, and `cor.cech-cohomology-spectral`). (D) Acyclicity of fine sheaves (Godement). (E) Čech cohomology equals sheaf cohomology (`thm.paracompact-cech-equals-sheaf` remains a skeleton). (F) Sheaf cohomology equals singular cohomology (Eilenberg–Steenrod uniqueness). (G) Full verification of compatibility via Stokes and of naturality. **The well-definedness and exactness assertions in Steps 1 and 2 can be verified in full. The remaining parts rely on standard external results unproved in the graph, so the proof carries no ∎ and retains skeleton status.**\n**Why this is a theorem**: The two complexes are entirely different (smooth forms and topological simplices), so their agreement requires proof. The central step is Step 2, where the assertion that $\\Omega^{\\bullet}$ resolves the constant sheaf globalizes the Poincaré lemma.\n**Corollaries**: $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}$ is homotopy invariant. On a compact manifold, each $H^{k}_{\\mathrm{dR}}$ is finite-dimensional and vanishes when $k>\\dim M$. Thus the number of independent classes of closed forms modulo exact forms is purely topological, given by the Betti numbers.\n**Relation to the three de Rham nodes in pack 131**: $H^{0}$ counts connected components (`thm.de-rham-h0`), cohomology vanishes locally (`lem.poincare-lemma`), and combining open sets gives a long exact sequence (`thm.mayer-vietoris`). These are the local versions of Steps 2 and 5.\n\n**🔻 Dependencies and order (reviewed here, with skeleton status retained).** Steps 0–1 (smooth singular chains and well-definedness of the pairing) and stalkwise exactness in Step 2 can be verified independently. Acyclicity of fine sheaves in Step 2 requires Godement's theorem. The graph has only the abstract definition `def.acyclic-sheaf` and has no nodes on fine or soft sheaves. Step 3 first requires a **good cover** (every nonempty finite intersection is contractible, as with geodesically convex neighborhoods), for which the graph has no node. Comparing the two spectral sequences also requires a separate step. The node cited in Step 4, `thm.paracompact-cech-equals-sheaf`, **also remains a skeleton**, so even after filling Gaps B–D, this step can only serve as a conditional input. The graph has no node for Eilenberg–Steenrod uniqueness in Step 5. Compatibility via Stokes in Step 6 relies on `thm.stokes-chain`, which is proved, but naturality still needs a term-by-term check. Calling Gaps A–G standard leaves these dependencies unresolved, especially the chain of dependencies in Gap E. This node therefore retains skeleton status.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c48111c9'
    },
  },
  'thm.open-mapping': {
    statement: {
      en: "Suppose $f$ is holomorphic and **nonconstant** on a domain $\\Omega$. Then $f$ is an open map: every open subset of $\\Omega$ has open image.\n\nCorollaries:\n\n1. **Another formulation of the maximum modulus principle**: $|f|$ cannot attain a maximum in the interior of $\\Omega$, since otherwise the image would fail to be open.\n2. A holomorphic **injection** is biholomorphic onto its image (its inverse is also holomorphic).\n3. If the image of $f$ is contained in an open set, then “$\\operatorname{Im} f$ has empty interior” is equivalent to “$f$ is constant.”",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d4dde314'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof**: Take $z_{0} \\in \\Omega$ and $w_{0} := f(z_{0})$. It suffices to show that $w_{0}$ is an interior point of $f(\\Omega)$. By translation, we may assume $w_{0} = 0$.\n\nBy `prop.zero-isolated`, $z_{0}$ is an isolated zero of $f$ of order $m \\ge 1$. Thus in some neighborhood of $z_{0}$, the only zero of $f$ is $z_{0}$. Choose a small circle $|z - z_{0}| = \\rho$ on which $f$ does not vanish, and set $\\delta := \\min_{|z - z_{0}| = \\rho}|f(z)| > 0$.\n\nNow take any $|w| < \\delta$ and compare $f$ with the constant $-w$. On the circle, $|f(z)| \\ge \\delta > |w| = |{-w} - f(z) + f(z)|$… More directly, on the circle, $|f(z) - (-w)| \\ge |f(z)| - |w| > 0$. **Rouché's theorem** compares the number of zeros of $f$ and $-w$ inside the circle. When $|f| > |{-w} - f|$ holds on the circle, the two have the same number of zeros.\n\nInside the circle, $f$ has $m \\ge 1$ zeros counted with multiplicity, so $f + w$ also has a zero inside the circle, meaning that $w = -f(z)$ is attained.\n\nThus $B(0, \\delta) \\subseteq f(\\Omega)$, and $w_{0}$ is an interior point.\n\n∎\n\n⭐ **A more intuitive way to read the theorem**: A nonconstant holomorphic function cannot collapse an open set to a line or a point. The maximum modulus principle, Schwarz's lemma, and the implication univalent $\\implies$ biholomorphic are different expressions of this idea.\n\n⚠️ Nonconstancy is essential, since a constant function maps an open set to a point. ⚠️ The theorem requires **holomorphicity**. Continuous real-valued functions lack this property.\n\n**Hypotheses used in each direction** (the open mapping theorem is an analytic result, and the equivalence between constancy and empty interior is a corollary):\n- Being an open map $\\iff$ having open image for every open set follows in both directions by definition. The implication nonconstant and holomorphic $\\implies$ open uses isolated zeros and Rouché's theorem, comparing $f-f(z_0)$ with $f-f(z_0)-w$.\n- Constant $\\implies$ image with empty interior follows directly. Image with empty interior $\\implies$ constant follows by contraposition of the open mapping theorem and requires the domain to be connected. Here the image refers to the set $f(\\Omega)$ rather than the imaginary part of the function.\n- Open map $\\implies$ nonconstant follows because a constant map on a nonempty domain has a singleton image. Continuity alone is insufficient. For example, the image of $f(z)=\\operatorname{Re}z$ has empty interior in the complex plane.\n- Holomorphic injection $\\implies$ biholomorphism: The open mapping theorem first gives continuity of the inverse. The local zero-multiplicity argument then gives $f'\\ne0$, and the inverse function theorem gives holomorphicity of the inverse. This uses more than the definition of injectivity.\n- Without connectedness, global nonconstancy is insufficient. A function taking different constant values on two disjoint disks is holomorphic and nonconstant but fails to be open. The interior maximum modulus principle follows from openness of the image and the geometry of disks.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#6e80ef6d'
    },
  },
  'thm.runge': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $K \\subset \\mathbb{C}$ be compact, and let $f$ be holomorphic on a neighborhood of $K$. If $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus K$ is connected (that is, $K$ has no holes), then for each $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a **polynomial** $p$ such that\n\n$$\\sup_{z \\in K} |f(z) - p(z)| < \\varepsilon.$$\n\nIn the general case (when $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$ has holes), $f$ can be approximated by **rational functions** whose poles are restricted to one point chosen in advance in each connected component of $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e557d52f'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof**: There are four steps. ① Express $f$ as a Cauchy integral over a grid boundary outside $K$. ② Discretize the integral to obtain rational functions with poles outside $K$. ③ Move the poles along paths in $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$ to the prescribed locations. ④ If there are no holes, move them out to infinity and expand to obtain polynomials.\n\n**Lemma A (Cauchy representation)**: Let $K$ be compact, let $V$ be an open neighborhood of $K$, and let $f$ be holomorphic on $V$. Then there is a finite union of closed squares $S$ with positively oriented boundary $\\Gamma = \\partial S$ such that $K \\subset \\operatorname{int} S,\\qquad S \\subset V,\\qquad f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_{\\Gamma}\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta\\quad(z\\in K).$. Proof: $d := \\operatorname{dist}(K,\\mathbb{C}\\setminus V) > 0$. Take a square grid of side length $h$, with $h\\sqrt{2} < d$. Let $S$ be the union of all closed grid squares that meet $K$. Then $K\\subset\\operatorname{int} S$, and the distance from $S$ to $K$ is at most $h\\sqrt{2} < d$, so $S\\subset V$. Orient each square boundary counterclockwise and add them. The integrals along shared edges cancel, giving $\\Gamma = \\partial S$. For $z\\in K\\subset\\operatorname{int} S$, the function $h(\\zeta) := (f(\\zeta)-f(z))/(\\zeta - z)$ (extended at $\\zeta = z$ by the value $f'(z)$) is holomorphic on a neighborhood of $S$. Applying Cauchy–Goursat (`thm.cauchy-goursat`) on each square gives $\\int_{\\partial Q} h = 0$, and summing gives $\\int_{\\Gamma} h = 0$. Hence $\\int_{\\Gamma}\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta = f(z)\\int_{\\Gamma}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\zeta}{\\zeta - z} = 2\\pi i\\, f(z)\\,\\operatorname{Ind}_{\\Gamma}(z) = 2\\pi i\\,f(z),$. The last step uses the fact that the positively oriented boundary of a bounded region has index $1$ about each interior point (the index is constant on each connected component of $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\Gamma$ and equals $0$ on the unbounded component).\n\n**Step 1: Discretizing the integral.** $\\Gamma$ is compact and $\\Gamma\\cap K = \\emptyset$, so $g(\\zeta,z) := f(\\zeta)/(\\zeta - z)$ is continuous on $\\Gamma\\times K$ and hence uniformly continuous. Divide each edge of $\\Gamma$ into equal segments of length $<\\delta$. Take midpoints $\\zeta_{j}$ and increments $\\Delta\\zeta_{j}$, and form the Riemann sum $R(z) := \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\sum_{j}\\frac{f(\\zeta_{j})}{\\zeta_{j} - z}\\,\\Delta\\zeta_{j}.$. By uniform continuity, taking $\\delta$ sufficiently small ensures that $\\sup_{z\\in K}\\Bigl|\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_{\\Gamma} g(\\zeta,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta - R(z)\\Bigr| < \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}.$. Here $R$ is a rational function whose poles lie only at the midpoints $\\zeta_{j}\\in\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$.\n\n**Step 2: Moving the poles.** Suppose $b,b^{\\prime}$ lie in the same component of $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$, denoted by $\\Omega$. Take a polygonal path in $\\Omega$ from $b$ to $b^{\\prime}$, and choose points $b = b_{0}, b_{1}, \\ldots, b_{N} = b^{\\prime}$ along it such that $|b_{i+1} - b_{i}| < \\operatorname{dist}(b_{i},K)$. For $z\\in K$, we have $|b_{i}-z| \\ge \\operatorname{dist}(b_{i},K) > |b_{i+1}-b_{i}|$, so the geometric series $\\frac{1}{b_{i+1} - z} = \\frac{1}{b_{i} - z}\\cdot\\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{b_{i}-b_{i+1}}{b_{i}-z}} = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(b_{i}-b_{i+1})^{k}}{(b_{i}-z)^{k+1}}$ converges uniformly on $K$ (with common ratio $\\le q_{i} < 1$). Truncating at $k\\le N_{i}$ uniformly approximates $1/(b_{i+1}-z)$ to arbitrary accuracy by a rational function with poles only at $b_{i}$. Repeating this step shows that $1/(b-z)$ can be uniformly approximated by rational functions with poles only at $b^{\\prime}$. Taking linear combinations of these basic terms gives uniform approximation of any rational function on $K$, moving its poles one at a time.\n\n**Step 3: Obtaining polynomials when there are no holes.** If $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$ is connected, it is an unbounded connected open set. Choose $b^{\\prime}$ such that $|b^{\\prime}| > 2\\max_{z\\in K}|z|$. For $z\\in K$, we have $|z/b^{\\prime}| \\le 1/2$, so $\\frac{1}{b^{\\prime} - z} = \\frac{1}{b^{\\prime}}\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\Bigl(\\frac{z}{b^{\\prime}}\\Bigr)^{k}$ converges uniformly on $K$, and its truncations are polynomials. Thus $f$ is uniformly approximated on $K$ by polynomials to within $\\varepsilon$.\n\n**General case**: In $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$, choose in advance, for each connected component $\\Omega$, one point $b_{\\Omega}$. Step 1 gives poles in $\\Gamma$ for a rational function $R$. Since $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$, each pole lies in some component $\\Omega$, and Step 2 moves it to $b_{\\Omega}$. There are finitely many poles and the error in each step can be made arbitrarily small, so $R$ is uniformly approximated by a rational function with poles at $\\{b_{\\Omega}\\}$ to within $\\varepsilon/2$. Combining this with the error $\\varepsilon/2$ from Step 1 proves the result. ∎\n\n⭐ **Runge's theorem, Mittag-Leffler's theorem, and the Weierstrass product theorem (`thm.weierstrass-product`) belong together**: Runge concerns approximation, Mittag-Leffler constructs functions with prescribed principal parts, and Weierstrass constructs functions with prescribed zeros. All three answer the same question: **What kinds of holomorphic or meromorphic functions can be constructed on a domain?**\n\n⚠️ Connectedness of $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus K$ is essential. When $K = $ is the unit circle, $1/z$ is holomorphic near the circle, but cannot be uniformly approximated by polynomials on $K$. Otherwise the maximum modulus principle would imply that $1/z$ extends holomorphically to $\\{|z|\\le 1\\}$, contradicting the singularity at $z=0$.\n\n⚠️ The graph has no separate nodes on path integrals as Riemann sums or on the index (winding number) being 1 for a positively oriented boundary. Lemma A therefore spells out these points. `thm.cauchy-goursat` is already in the graph.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#64243440'
    },
  },
  'thm.weierstrass-product': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $\\{a_{n}\\}$ be a sequence of points in $\\mathbb{C}$ tending to $\\infty$ (repetitions are allowed, and the number of occurrences gives the multiplicity of the zero). Then there exists an entire function $f$ whose zero set is **exactly** $\\{a_{n}\\}$ and which can be written as\n\n$$f(z) = z^{m} e^{g(z)} \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} E_{p_{n}}\\left(\\frac{z}{a_{n}}\\right),$$\n\nHere $m$ is the multiplicity of $0$ as a zero, and $p_{n}$ are suitably chosen nonnegative integers that ensure uniform convergence of the product on compact subsets.\n\nThus **the zeros of an entire function, together with their multiplicities, can be prescribed arbitrarily**. The only remaining freedom is a zero-free entire factor $e^{g}$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#494a9f40'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** First remove the terms of the sequence equal to $0$ (they contribute only the factor $z^{m}$). Continue to denote the remaining sequence by $a_{n}\\ne 0$ and take the elementary factors to be $E_{p}(z) = (1-z)\\exp\\Bigl(z + \\frac{z^{2}}{2} + \\cdots + \\frac{z^{p}}{p}\\Bigr),\\qquad E_{0}(z) = 1-z .$.\n\n**Lemma 1 (estimate for elementary factors).** If $|z|\\le \\frac{1}{2}$, then $|1 - E_{p}(z)| \\le 4|z|^{p+1}$. To prove this, observe that when $|z|<1$, we have $\\log E_{p}(z) = -\\sum_{k>p} z^{k}/k$, so $|\\log E_{p}(z)| \\le \\sum_{k>p}\\frac{|z|^{k}}{k} \\le \\frac{|z|^{p+1}}{(p+1)(1-|z|)} \\le 2|z|^{p+1};$. Also, $|1-e^{w}|\\le 2|w|$ ($|w|\\le 1$). Taking $w = \\log E_{p}(z)$ gives the result (when $p=0$, we obtain $1-E_{0}(z) = z$ directly).\n\n**Lemma 2 (convergence of the product).** If $u_{n}$ are holomorphic on a domain and $\\sum_{n}|u_{n}|$ converges uniformly on every compact subset, then $\\prod_{n}(1+u_{n})$ converges uniformly on every compact subset to a holomorphic function $F$, and the zeros of $F$ are exactly the zeros of the factors $1+u_{n}$, with multiplicities added. The partial products are uniformly Cauchy by the telescoping estimate $|\\prod_{n\\le N}-\\prod_{n\\le M}| \\le \\prod(1+|u_{n}|)\\bigl(e^{\\sum_{n>\\min}|u_{n}|}-1\\bigr)$, and the limit is holomorphic by `thm.weierstrass-convergence`. At a point where no factor vanishes, eventually $|u_{n}|\\le 1/2$, hence $|1+u_{n}|\\ge 1/2$, so the product is nonzero.\n\n**Step 1. Choose $p_{n}$ so that the product converges.** Take $p_{n} = n$. Given any $R>0$, since $a_{n}\\to\\infty$, there exists $N$ such that whenever $n\\ge N$, we have $|a_{n}|\\ge 2R$; then for $|z|\\le R$ and $n\\ge N$, we have $|z/a_{n}|\\le 1/2$. By Lemma 1, $\\bigl|1 - E_{n}(z/a_{n})\\bigr| \\le 4\\Bigl(\\frac{R}{|a_{n}|}\\Bigr)^{n+1} \\le 4\\cdot 2^{-(n+1)},$, so $\\sum_{n}\\bigl|1 - E_{n}(z/a_{n})\\bigr|$ converges uniformly on $|z|\\le R$. By Lemma 2, $F(z) := \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} E_{n}(z/a_{n})$ is entire, its zeros are exactly the points $a_{n}$, and their multiplicities equal the multiplicities of $a_{n}$ in the sequence.\n\n**Step 2. Restore $z^{m}$.** Set $f_{0}(z) := z^{m}F(z)$. Then $f_{0}$ is entire with zeros exactly $\\{a_{n}\\}$, counting multiplicities. The multiplicity at $0$ is $m$, and the other zeros are given by $F$.\n\n**Step 3. Remove the zero-free factor.** Since $F$ is nowhere zero, $F'/F$ is entire. Set $g_{0}(z) := \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{F'(tz)}{F(tz)}\\,z\\,\\mathrm{d}t .$. The integrand is continuous, $g_{0}$ is entire, and $g_{0}'(z) = F'(z)/F(z)$ (differentiate under the integral sign, or use $g_{0}(z) = \\int_{0}^{z}F'(\\zeta)/F(\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta$ along a line segment, with path independence ensured by Cauchy's theorem). Thus $(F e^{-g_{0}})' = 0$, so $F = C e^{g_{0}}$ and $C\\ne 0$. Writing $C = e^{c}$ and $g := g_{0} + c$ gives $F = e^{g}$. Combining these gives the desired $f_{0}(z) = z^{m}e^{g(z)}\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty}E_{n}(z/a_{n}),$. ∎\n\n⭐ **This is the factorization “entire function = zeros $\\times$ zero-free factor.”** In comparison, Weierstrass handles **zeros** multiplicatively, while Mittag-Leffler handles **principal parts and poles** additively.\n\n⚠️ The product representation **is not unique** ($p_{n}$ can be chosen larger), so the theorem asserts existence rather than uniqueness.\n\n⚠️ The node `def.elementary-factor` contains a stronger estimate for elementary factors. Hurwitz's theorem has no separate node, so Lemma 2 is given here in full.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2eed544a'
    },
  },
  'thm.hadamard-factorization': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $f$ be an entire function of **finite order** $\\rho$ with zeros $\\{a_{n}\\}$, counted with multiplicity. Then\n\n$$f(z) = z^{m} e^{P(z)} \\prod_{n} E_{p}\\left(\\frac{z}{a_{n}}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $P$ is a polynomial of degree $\\le \\rho$ and $p \\le \\rho$ is a **single** fixed integer used for all factors (the Weierstrass construction chooses it separately for each factor).\n\nIn particular, when $\\rho < 1$, we have $p = 0$, hence $\\sum |a_{n}|^{-1} < \\infty$ (there may still be infinitely many zeros. For example, $\\prod_{n}(1 - z/n^{2})$ has order $\\frac{1}{2}$ and infinitely many zeros).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#fde82eb2'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Notation.** Let $f$ be entire of finite order $\\rho$, with zeros $a_{n}\\ne 0$ counted with multiplicity. The zero at $z=0$ has multiplicity $m$. Let $n(r)$ count the zeros with modulus $\\le r$, and let $M(r)=\\max_{|z|=r}|f(z)|$ and $E_{p}(w)=(1-w)e^{w+w^{2}/2+\\cdots+w^{p}/p}$. Take $p=\\lfloor\\rho\\rfloor$ (when $\\rho<1$, we have $p=0$), so $p+1>\\rho$. Fix $\\varepsilon>0$ such that $p+1>\\rho+\\varepsilon$.\n\n**Step 1. Density of zeros.** Apply Jensen's formula (`prop.jensen`) to $f/z^{m}$, whose value at the origin is nonzero, to obtain $n(R)\\log 2\\le\\log M(2R)-\\log|f(0)|+O(1)$. Finite order gives $\\log M(2R)\\le C\\,R^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$, hence $n(R)\\le C\\,R^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$. Thus for any $\\sigma>\\rho$, summation by parts gives $\\sum_{n}|a_{n}|^{-\\sigma}=\\sigma\\int_{0}^{\\infty}n(r)\\,r^{-\\sigma-1}\\,\\mathrm{d}r<\\infty$ ($n(r)$, when $r<|a_{1}|$, equals $0$, so there is no boundary term). In particular, $\\sum_{n}|a_{n}|^{-(p+1)}<\\infty$.\n\n**Step 2. Convergence of the product.** Write $\\mathcal{P}(z):=\\prod_{n}E_{p}(z/a_{n})$. For $|w|\\le1/2$, we have $\\log E_{p}(w)=-\\sum_{k>p}w^{k}/k$, hence $|E_{p}(w)-1|\\le C_{p}|w|^{p+1}$. Given $R$, there are only finitely many $|a_{n}|\\le2R$. For the remaining terms, $|z/a_{n}|\\le1/2$, so $\\sum_{|a_{n}|>2R}|z/a_{n}|^{p+1}\\le R^{p+1}\\sum_{n}|a_{n}|^{-(p+1)}<\\infty$. Therefore $\\mathcal{P}$ converges uniformly on $|z|\\le R$ and defines an entire function.\n\n**Step 3. The quotient is zero-free.** Set $\\Phi(z):=f(z)\\big/\\bigl(z^{m}\\mathcal{P}(z)\\bigr)$. At each $a_{n}$, $E_{p}(z/a_{n})$ has a simple zero that cancels a zero of $f$. At all other points, $f\\ne0$ and the product is nonzero. Thus $\\Phi$ is entire and nowhere zero.\n\n**Step 4. The order of $\\Phi$ is $\\le\\rho$.** Let $h$ be an entire logarithm of $\\Phi$. Its construction in Step 5 uses only that $\\Phi$ is nowhere zero, so we may use it here independently of the estimates in this step. Then $\\operatorname{Re}h=\\log|\\Phi|$. It suffices to show that for every sufficiently large $r$ there is an $R\\in[r,2r]$ such that on $|z|=R$ we have $\\log|\\Phi(z)|\\le C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$. The maximum principle ($\\operatorname{Re}h$ is harmonic) and Borel–Carathéodory from Step 6 then give $|h|\\le C\\,R^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$, and Cauchy's estimates show that $h$ is a polynomial.\n\n**(i) Two elementary estimates for $E_{p}$.** For $|w|\\le1/2$, we have $\\log|E_{p}(w)|=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(-\\sum_{k>p}w^{k}/k\\bigr)$, hence $|\\log|E_{p}(w)||\\le2|w|^{p+1}$. For $|w|\\ge1/2$, we have $\\log|E_{p}(w)|=\\log|1-w|+\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_{j\\le p}w^{j}/j\\ge\\log|1-w|-C_{p}|w|^{p}$.\n\n**Step 4 (continued). The good-radius lemma.** Take $M:=\\lceil\\rho\\rceil+3$ and $\\delta:=r^{-M}$. The number of zeros in $[r/2,4r]$ is $N\\le n(4r)\\le C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$. Each such zero excludes an interval of radii of length $2\\delta$. Since $2\\delta N\\le2C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon-M}<r$ for large $r$, there exists $R\\in[r,2r]$ such that every $|a_{n}|\\in[r/2,4r]$ satisfies $\\bigl||a_{n}|-R\\bigr|\\ge\\delta$. Fix such an $R$.\n\nFor $|z|=R$, split the terms according to $|a_{n}|\\le4r$ and $|a_{n}|>4r$ in $\\log|\\mathcal{P}(z)|=\\sum\\log|E_{p}(z/a_{n})|$.\n\n• For $|a_{n}|>4r$, we have $|z/a_{n}|<1/2$, and by (i) each term satisfies $\\ge-C(R/|a_{n}|)^{p+1}$. Summation by parts and $n(t)\\le C t^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$ give $\\sum_{|a_{n}|>4r}(R/|a_{n}|)^{p+1}\\le C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$.\n\n• For $|a_{n}|\\le4r$, we have $|z-a_{n}|\\ge\\delta$ (when $|a_{n}|\\in[r/2,4r]$, this follows from the choice of $R$, and when $|a_{n}|<r/2$, we have $|z-a_{n}|\\ge R-r/2\\ge r/2$). Thus $\\log|1-z/a_{n}|=\\log|z-a_{n}|-\\log|a_{n}|\\ge\\log(\\delta/(4r))$, and by (i) each term satisfies $\\ge\\log(\\delta/(4r))-C(R/|a_{n}|)^{p}$. Summing over at most $N\\le C r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$ terms and using $\\sum_{|a_{n}|\\le4r}(R/|a_{n}|)^{p}\\le C r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}$ (by summation by parts) gives $\\sum_{|a_{n}|\\le4r}\\log|E_{p}(z/a_{n})|\\ge-C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}\\log r$.\n\nCombining these gives $\\log|\\mathcal{P}(z)|\\ge-C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}\\log r$. Thus $\\log|\\Phi(z)|=\\log|f(z)|-m\\log R-\\log|\\mathcal{P}(z)|\\le C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}+C\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon}\\log r\\le C_{1}\\,r^{\\rho+\\varepsilon_{1}}$ for any $\\varepsilon_{1}>\\varepsilon$.\n\n**Step 5. Zero-free $\\implies$ exponential.** Since $\\Phi$ is entire and zero-free and $\\mathbb{C}$ is simply connected, `prop.single-valued-branch` gives an entire function $h$ such that $\\Phi=e^{h}$. Also, $\\operatorname{Re}h=\\log|\\Phi|$.\n\n**Step 6. Borel–Carathéodory.** The lemma states that if $g$ is holomorphic on $|z|\\le R$, $g(0)=0$, and $\\operatorname{Re}g\\le A$ ($A>0$), then for $|z|=r<R$ we have $|g(z)|\\le2Ar/(R-r)$. For the proof, $\\varphi:=g/(2A-g)$ is holomorphic on $|z|\\le R$ (the real part of the denominator is $\\ge A$), and $|\\varphi|\\le1$ ($|g|^{2}\\le(2A-\\operatorname{Re}g)^{2}+(\\operatorname{Im}g)^{2}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{Re}g\\le A$), with $\\varphi(0)=0$. Schwarz's lemma (`lem.schwarz`) gives $|\\varphi(z)|\\le r/R$, hence $|g|=\\bigl|2A\\varphi/(1+\\varphi)\\bigr|\\le2A|\\varphi|/(1-|\\varphi|)\\le2Ar/(R-r)$.\n\n**Step 7. $h$ is a polynomial.** By Step 4, on $|z|\\le R$ we have $\\operatorname{Re}(h-h(0))\\le C_{1}R^{\\rho+\\varepsilon_{1}}+|\\operatorname{Re}h(0)|=:A$. Apply Step 6 to $g=h-h(0)$ with $r=R/2$ to obtain $|h(z)-h(0)|\\le2A$ ($|z|\\le R/2$). Cauchy's estimates (`thm.cauchy-inequality`) then give, for $k>\\rho+\\varepsilon_{1}$, the bound $|h^{(k)}(0)|\\le k!\\,2A/(R/2)^{k}\\to0$ ($R\\to\\infty$). Hence $h^{(k)}(0)=0$, so $h$ is a polynomial of degree $\\le\\lfloor\\rho\\rfloor$.\n\n**Conclusion.** $f=z^{m}e^{h}\\prod_{n}E_{p}(z/a_{n})=z^{m}e^{P}\\prod_{n}E_{p}(z/a_{n})$ gives the Hadamard factorization. ∎ Steps 1–3 establish convergence and cancel the zeros. Step 4 uses the good-radius lemma, elementary estimates, and Borel–Carathéodory. Steps 5–7 express the zero-free quotient as the exponential of a polynomial.\n\n⭐ **This is a quantitative version of the Weierstrass product.** Knowing the order of growth lets us use the same $p$ for every factor and reduces the remaining freedom to a polynomial $P$. When $\\rho<1$, we have $p=0$ and $\\sum|a_{n}|^{-1}<\\infty$, but there can still be infinitely many zeros ($\\prod(1-z/n^{2})$ has order $1/2$). ⚠️ The finite-order hypothesis is essential. Entire functions of infinite order still admit Weierstrass products, but the exponent need no longer be a polynomial and the conclusion is weaker.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#9f9ab9d1'
    },
  },
  'lem.average-continuous': {
    statement: {
      en: "Suppose $f \\in L^1_{loc}$. Then $A_r f(x)$ is **jointly continuous** as a function of $(r, x)$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#370b8ea4'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** Let $m$ denote Lebesgue measure and set $\\omega_n := m(B(0,1))$. Then $m(B(x,r)) = \\omega_n r^n$. To prove joint continuity, it suffices to show that for every sequence satisfying $r_k > 0$ and $(x_k, r_k) \\to (x_0, r_0)$, we have $A_{r_k} f(x_k) \\to A_{r_0} f(x_0)$.\n\n**Step 1. Split the difference.** Write the difference as\n\n$$A_r f(x) - A_{r_0} f(x_0) = \\frac{1}{m(B(x,r))}\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^n} \\bigl(\\chi_{B(x,r)} - \\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}\\bigr) f + \\Bigl(\\frac{1}{m(B(x,r))} - \\frac{1}{m(B(x_0,r_0))}\\Bigr)\\int_{B(x_0,r_0)} f .$$\n\nThe second term tends to $0$. Indeed, $\\omega_n r_k^n \\to \\omega_n r_0^n > 0$, so the coefficient tends to $0$, while $\\int_{B(x_0,r_0)} f$ is constant.\n\n**Step 2. Find a dominating function.** Choose $\\delta = 1/2$. If $|x - x_0| < 1/2$ and $|r - r_0| < 1/2$, then $r < r_0 + 1/2$, and $|y - x| < r$ implies\n\n$$|y - x_0| \\le |y - x| + |x - x_0| < r + 1/2 < r_0 + 1,$$\n\nthat is, $B(x,r) \\subseteq B(x_0, r_0 + 1)$. Thus for such $(x,r)$ and every $y$,\n\n$$|\\chi_{B(x,r)}(y) - \\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}(y)|\\,|f(y)| \\le \\chi_{B(x_0, r_0+1)}(y)\\,|f(y)| \\in L^1.$$\n\n**Step 3. Almost-everywhere pointwise convergence.** The sphere $\\partial B(x_0, r_0)$ has measure zero. Suppose $y \\notin \\partial B(x_0, r_0)$.\n\n- If $|y - x_0| < r_0$, choose $\\varepsilon = r_0 - |y - x_0| > 0$. When $|x - x_0| < \\varepsilon/2$ and $|r - r_0| < \\varepsilon/2$, we have $|y - x| \\le |y - x_0| + |x - x_0| < r_0 - \\varepsilon/2 < r$, hence $\\chi_{B(x,r)}(y) = 1 = \\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}(y)$.\n\n- If $|y - x_0| > r_0$, choose $\\varepsilon = |y - x_0| - r_0 > 0$. For the same small perturbations, $|y - x| > r_0 + \\varepsilon/2 > r$, hence $\\chi_{B(x,r)}(y) = 0 = \\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}(y)$.\n\nThus $\\chi_{B(x_k,r_k)} \\to \\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}$ holds for almost every $y$.\n\n**Step 4. Dominated convergence.** By Steps 2 and 3 and the dominated convergence theorem, $\\int (\\chi_{B(x_k,r_k)} - \\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}) f \\to 0$. The coefficient $1/m(B(x_k,r_k))$ is bounded and tends to $1/m(B(x_0,r_0))$, so the first term tends to $0$. Adding the second term gives $A_{r_k} f(x_k) \\to A_{r_0} f(x_0)$. ∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#275d5fae'
    },
  },
  'thm.lifting': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $p : E \\to B$ be a covering map and suppose $p(e_{0}) = b_{0}$.\n\n**(1) Path lifting.** For any path $\\alpha : [0, 1] \\to B$ with $\\alpha(0) = b_{0}$, there exists a **unique** path\n\n$$\\tilde{\\alpha} : [0, 1] \\longrightarrow E,\\qquad \\tilde{\\alpha}(0) = e_{0},\\quad p \\circ \\tilde{\\alpha} = \\alpha.$$\n\n**(2) Homotopy lifting.** For any homotopy $F : [0, 1] \\times [0, 1] \\to B$ with $F(0, 0) = b_{0}$, there exists a **unique**\nlift $\\tilde{F} : [0, 1] \\times [0, 1] \\to E$ such that $\\tilde{F}(0, 0) = e_{0}$ and $p \\circ \\tilde{F} = F$.\nIf $F$ is a path homotopy relative to its endpoints, then both endpoints of $\\tilde{F}$ are also fixed for every $s$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#d4491ecc'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof outline.** Use a Lebesgue number to subdivide the path or homotopy into pieces, each lying in an evenly covered open set, and lift the pieces uniquely in succession. See the edge `imp.lifting-proof` for the full proof.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#0bb3af6c'
    },
  },
  'thm.sphere-homology': {
    statement: {
      en: "For $n \\ge 1$,\n\n$$H_{0}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z},\\qquad H_{n}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z},$$\n$$H_{p}(S^{n}) = 0 \\quad (p \\ne 0, n).$$\n\nUsing **reduced** homology, we can write a single formula for all $p$ (including $n = 0$)\n\n$$\\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z} \\ (p = n),\\qquad \\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{n}) = 0 \\ (p \\ne n).$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#c27fe014'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** Induct on $n$ using reduced homology (`def.reduced-homology`) and the reduced Mayer–Vietoris sequence (`thm.mv-homology`). Set $S^{n} \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$, with north pole $N := (0, \\ldots, 0, 1)$ and south pole $S := -N$.\n\n**Base case $n = 0$.** The space $S^{0} = \\{\\pm 1\\}$ consists of two points. By `prop.h0-components`, $H_{0}(S^{0}) = \\mathbb{Z}^{2}$, hence $\\tilde{H}_{0}(S^{0}) = \\mathbb{Z}$. When $p > 0$, we have $\\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{0}) = 0$.\n\n**Inductive step.** Suppose $n \\ge 1$ and set $U := S^{n} \\setminus \\{S\\}$ and $V := S^{n} \\setminus \\{N\\}$. Both are open.\n\n**$U, V$ is contractible.** For $U$, use the homotopy\n\n$$H_{s}(x) := \\frac{(1-s)x + sN}{\\lVert (1-s)x + sN \\rVert}\\qquad (x \\in U,\\ s \\in [0,1]).$$\n\nThe denominator is nonzero. Indeed, $(1-s)x + sN = 0$ forces $\\lVert x \\rVert = s/(1-s) = 1$, that is, $x = S$ (at the endpoint $s = 1$, the numerator is $N \\ne 0$), whereas $S \\notin U$. Thus $H_{0} = \\mathrm{id}_{U}$ and $H_{1} \\equiv N$, so $U$ is contractible. Similarly, $V$ is contractible. By homotopy invariance (`thm.homotopy-invariance`), all reduced homology groups of a contractible space vanish, so $\\tilde{H}_{p}(U) = \\tilde{H}_{p}(V) = 0$ for every $p$.\n\n**The intersection deformation retracts onto the equator.** We have $U \\cap V = S^{n} \\setminus \\{N, S\\}$. Write a point as $(x, t)$, where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $t \\in (-1, 1)$, and $\\lVert x \\rVert^{2} + t^{2} = 1$. Since $t \\ne \\pm 1$, we have $x \\ne 0$. Define\n\n$$H_{s}(x, t) := \\frac{(x,\\, s t)}{\\sqrt{\\lVert x \\rVert^{2} + s^{2} t^{2}}}\\qquad (s \\in [0,1]).$$\n\nThis map is continuous and takes values in $S^{n}$. The point $H_{0}(x, t) = (x/\\lVert x \\rVert, 0)$ lies on the equator $S^{n-1}$, we have $H_{1} = \\mathrm{id}$, and $H_{s}$ maps the equator into itself. Thus $S^{n-1}$ is a deformation retract of $U \\cap V$, so $\\tilde{H}_{p}(U \\cap V) \\cong \\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{n-1})$ by homotopy invariance.\n\n**Reduced Mayer–Vietoris.** Since $U \\cup V = S^{n}$ and $U \\cap V \\ne \\emptyset$, we have the long exact sequence\n\n$$\\cdots \\to \\tilde{H}_{p}(U \\cap V) \\to \\tilde{H}_{p}(U) \\oplus \\tilde{H}_{p}(V) \\to \\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{n}) \\to \\tilde{H}_{p-1}(U \\cap V) \\to \\cdots$$\n\nThe middle terms all vanish, so for every $p \\ge 1$ we have $\\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{n}) \\cong \\tilde{H}_{p-1}(S^{n-1})$. For $p = 0$, the end of the sequence $\\tilde{H}_{0}(U) \\oplus \\tilde{H}_{0}(V) \\to \\tilde{H}_{0}(S^{n}) \\to 0$ gives $\\tilde{H}_{0}(S^{n}) = 0$ (when $n \\ge 1$, the space $S^{n}$ is path connected).\n\n**Conclusion of the induction.** The base case and the recurrence give $\\tilde{H}_{p}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z}$ when $p = n$, and $0$ otherwise.\n\n**Return to unreduced homology.** When $n \\ge 1$, the space $S^{n}$ is path connected, so $H_{0}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z}$ (that is, $\\tilde{H}_{0} = 0$). When $p \\ge 1$, we have $H_{p} = \\tilde{H}_{p}$. Thus $H_{0}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z}$ and $H_{n}(S^{n}) = \\mathbb{Z}$, with $H_{p}(S^{n}) = 0$ in all other cases. ∎\n\n⭐ **Interpretation.** This makes precise the statement that homology detects a hole of dimension $n$. Compare `cor.pi1-sphere` ($\\pi_{1}(S^{n}) = 1$ for $n \\ge 2$). The group $\\pi_{1}$ detects only degree $n = 1$, while $H_{n}$ detects every degree ($\\pi_{n}$ can also detect these holes, through a different theory).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#16d39143'
    },
  },
  'thm.long-exact-pair': {
    statement: {
      en: "A **topological pair** is a pair of spaces $A \\subseteq X$ with one a subspace of the other. Since every singular simplex in $A$ is also a singular simplex in $X$, $S_{*}(A) \\subseteq S_{*}(X)$ is a **subcomplex**. Define the **relative chain groups** and **relative homology groups** by\n\n$$S_{p}(X, A) := S_{p}(X) \\big/ S_{p}(A),$$\n$$H_{p}(X, A) := \\ker\\bar\\partial \\big/ \\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial.$$\n\nThen there is a **long exact sequence** (at each $\\cdot$, the exactness condition is $\\operatorname{im} = \\ker$)\n\n$$\\cdots \\to H_{p}(A) \\to H_{p}(X) \\to H_{p}(X, A)$$\n$$\\xrightarrow{\\ \\partial_{*}\\ } H_{p-1}(A) \\to \\cdots$$\n\nwhere $\\partial_{*}$ is called the **connecting homomorphism**. The sequence holds for every pair of spaces. It **requires no assumption** that $A$ is closed, open, or otherwise well behaved, and no cofibration hypothesis is needed.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#76a24ce5'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** Let $i : S_{*}(A) \\hookrightarrow S_{*}(X)$ denote the inclusion and $q : S_{*}(X) \\twoheadrightarrow S_{*}(X, A) := S_{*}(X)/S_{*}(A)$ the quotient map. Both are chain maps, and for each $p$,\n\n$$0 \\to S_{p}(A) \\xrightarrow{\\ i\\ } S_{p}(X) \\xrightarrow{\\ q\\ } S_{p}(X, A) \\to 0$$\n\nis a short exact sequence. The long exact sequence in homology follows from this short exact sequence. We give the connecting homomorphism and verify exactness at all three positions below.\n\n**Connecting homomorphism.** Take $[c] \\in H_{p}(X, A)$, where $c \\in S_{p}(X)$ and $\\bar\\partial[c] = 0$, that is, $\\partial c \\in S_{p-1}(A)$. Set $\\partial_{*}[c] := [\\partial c] \\in H_{p-1}(A)$.\n\nWell-definedness follows from $\\partial\\partial c = 0$, so $\\partial c$ is a cycle in $A$. If $c^{\\prime} - c = \\partial d + a$ ($d \\in S_{p+1}(X)$, $a \\in S_{p}(A)$), then $\\partial c^{\\prime} - \\partial c = \\partial a$ is a boundary in $A$, so the homology class is unchanged.\n\n**Exactness.** Let $j_{*} : H_{p}(A) \\to H_{p}(X)$ and $q_{*} : H_{p}(X) \\to H_{p}(X, A)$ denote the induced maps.\n\n**(a) $\\operatorname{im} j_{*} = \\ker q_{*}$.** If $[c] = j_{*}[a]$ (where $a$ is a cycle in $A$), then $q_{*}[c] = [q(a)] = 0$, since $a \\in S_{*}(A)$ is $0$ in the quotient complex. Conversely, suppose $q_{*}[c] = 0$, so $q(c) = \\partial b$ for some $b \\in S_{p+1}(X)$. Then $q(c - \\partial b) = 0$, so $c - \\partial b \\in S_{p}(A)$ and $\\partial(c - \\partial b) = 0$, hence $[c] = j_{*}[c - \\partial b]$.\n\n**(b) $\\operatorname{im} q_{*} = \\ker \\partial_{*}$.** If $[c] = q_{*}[z]$ and $z$ is a cycle in $X$, then $\\partial c = \\partial z = 0$, so $\\partial_{*}[c] = 0$. Conversely, suppose $\\partial_{*}[c] = 0$, so $\\partial c = \\partial a$ for some $a \\in S_{p-1}(A)$. Set $z := c - a$. Then $\\partial z = 0$ and $q(z) = q(c)$, so $[c] = q_{*}[z]$.\n\n**(c) $\\operatorname{im} \\partial_{*} = \\ker j_{*}$.** If $[c]$ comes from relative homology, then $j_{*}(\\partial_{*}[c]) = [\\partial c]$, and $\\partial c$ is a boundary in $X$, so its class is $0$. Conversely, if $a \\in S_{p-1}(A)$ is a cycle and $j_{*}[a] = 0$, so $a = \\partial b$ for some $b \\in S_{p}(X)$, then $b$ is a relative cycle ($\\partial b = a \\in S_{*}(A)$), and $\\partial_{*}[b] = [\\partial b] = [a]$.\n\nTogether, these three exactness checks give the long exact sequence. The argument uses only that $S_{*}(A) \\subseteq S_{*}(X)$ is a subcomplex, with no closedness, openness, or other assumptions on $A$. ∎\n\n**Why this is simpler in homology than in homotopy.** The long exact sequence of **relative** homotopy groups holds for every pair $(X, A)$ and requires no fibration. The delicate step is replacing the relative groups by the absolute groups of the quotient space $X/A$; it requires a good pair hypothesis (NDR pair or cofibration) on $(X, A)$. The homology sequence applies whenever $A \\subseteq X$ is a subspace. This is one of the main reasons chain complexes are easier to work with than homotopy groups.\n\n💡 Application. Break the homology of a large space into a smaller space $+$ a relative part, and use the known groups to compute the unknown ones (`thm.mv-homology` is obtained this way).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#4c9e3130'
    },
  },
  'thm.mv-homology': {
    statement: {
      en: "Suppose $X = U \\cup V$ and $U, V \\subseteq X$ are **open sets** (more generally, the interiors of $U, V$ cover $X$). Let $i_{*}, j_{*}$ denote the maps induced by the inclusions of $U \\cap V \\hookrightarrow U, V$. Then there is a long exact sequence\n\n$$H_{p}(U \\cap V) \\to H_{p}(U) \\oplus H_{p}(V) \\to H_{p}(X)$$\n$$\\xrightarrow{\\ \\partial_{*}\\ } H_{p-1}(U \\cap V)$$\n\n(extending in both directions to give $\\cdots$). The two maps that require explanation are\n\n$$\\Phi[z] := (i_{*}[z],\\ -j_{*}[z]),\\qquad \\Psi[a, b] := [a + b].$$\n\nOn replacing $H$ by reduced homology $\\tilde{H}$, the same sequence holds provided $U \\cap V \\ne \\emptyset$ (otherwise exactness fails in the segment $\\tilde{H}_{0}$. When $X = \\{a, b\\}$, $U = \\{a\\}$, and $V = \\{b\\}$, we have $\\tilde{H}_{0}(U) = \\tilde{H}_{0}(V) = 0$ while $\\tilde{H}_{0}(X) = \\mathbb{Z}$).",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#e4e652a1'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**Proof.** There are five steps. ① Take the chains whose simplices each lie entirely in $U$ or $V$ to obtain a subcomplex. ② Use barycentric subdivision to prove that the inclusion of this subcomplex into the full complex induces an isomorphism in homology (this is the main difficulty). ③ Express the subcomplex as the sum of the chains in $U$ and the chains in $V$. ④ Obtain a long exact sequence from the short exact sequence. ⑤ Substitute the isomorphism from step ②.\n\n**Small chains.** A singular simplex $\\sigma : \\Delta^{p} \\to X$ is **small** with respect to the cover $\\{U, V\\}$ if $\\sigma(\\Delta^{p}) \\subseteq U$ or $\\sigma(\\Delta^{p}) \\subseteq V$. Finite sums of small simplices form a subgroup $S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{p}(X) \\subseteq S_{p}(X)$. The boundary operator preserves smallness because the faces still lie in the same set, so $S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{*}(X)$ is a subcomplex. Write $\\iota : S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{*}(X) \\hookrightarrow S_{*}(X)$.\n\n**Step 1. The subdivision chain map.** On the standard simplex $\\Delta^{p}$, define two chains recursively:\n\n$$\\operatorname{sd}_{0} := [v_{0}],\\qquad \\operatorname{sd}_{p} := [b_{p},\\ \\operatorname{sd}_{p-1}(\\partial\\Delta^{p})],$$\n\nHere $b_{p}$ is the barycenter of $\\Delta^{p}$, $[b, c]$ denotes coning with apex $b$ over the chain $c$, and $\\partial\\Delta^{p} = \\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\\Delta^{p}_{i}$ is the standard boundary. The cone formula $\\partial[b, c] = c - [b, \\partial c]$ gives\n\n$$\\partial\\operatorname{sd}_{p} = \\operatorname{sd}_{p-1}(\\partial\\Delta^{p}).$$\n\nNext define $t_{0} := 0$ and the recursion\n\n$$c_{p} := \\operatorname{sd}_{p} - \\operatorname{id}_{p} - t_{p-1}(\\partial\\Delta^{p}),\\qquad t_{p} := [b_{p},\\ c_{p}],$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{id}_{p}$ is the identity $p$-simplex. Induction on $p$ gives $\\partial c_{p} = 0$ using $\\partial\\partial = 0$ and the inductive hypothesis. Thus $\\partial t_{p} = c_{p}$, that is,\n\n$$\\partial t_{p} + t_{p-1}(\\partial\\Delta^{p}) = \\operatorname{sd}_{p} - \\operatorname{id}_{p}.$$\n\nIn the base case $p = 0$, we have $\\operatorname{sd}_{0} = \\operatorname{id}_{0}$ and $t_{0} = 0$.\n\n**Step 2. Naturality.** For a singular simplex $\\sigma$, set $\\operatorname{Sd}(\\sigma) := \\sigma_{\\#}\\operatorname{sd}_{p}$ and $T(\\sigma) := \\sigma_{\\#} t_{p}$, and extend linearly. Since $\\sigma_{\\#}$ is a chain map and is compatible with continuous maps,\n\n$$\\partial\\operatorname{Sd} = \\operatorname{Sd}\\partial,\\qquad \\partial T + T\\partial = \\operatorname{Sd} - \\operatorname{id}.$$\n\n(This follows by applying the preceding identity term by term. $T$ may produce degenerate simplices, which do not affect the chain identity.) A single $\\operatorname{Sd}(\\sigma)$ may fail to be small. Iteration ensures smallness.\n\n**Step 3. Diameter contraction.** Let $\\tau \\subseteq \\Delta^{p}$ be a simplex of diameter $\\delta$, and let $b_{S}$ denote the barycenter of the face $S$. The vertices of $\\operatorname{sd}(\\tau)$ are the barycenters of certain faces $S_{0} \\subset \\cdots \\subset S_{q}$, and for $S \\subset S^{\\prime}$ we have\n\n$$b_{S^{\\prime}} - b_{S} = \\frac{1}{\\#S^{\\prime}}\\sum_{v \\in S^{\\prime} \\setminus S}(v - b_{S}),$$\n\nHence $|b_{S^{\\prime}} - b_{S}| \\le \\left(1 - \\frac{\\#S}{\\#S^{\\prime}}\\right)\\delta \\le \\frac{p}{p+1}\\delta$. It follows that $\\operatorname{mesh}(\\operatorname{sd}^{m}\\tau) \\le (p/(p+1))^{m}\\delta$. After sufficiently many subdivisions, the smaller simplices can have arbitrarily small diameter.\n\n**Step 4. The small-chain lemma.** The inclusion $\\iota : S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{*}(X) \\hookrightarrow S_{*}(X)$ induces an isomorphism in homology.\n\nProof. For each singular $p$-simplex $\\sigma$, $\\{\\sigma^{-1}(U), \\sigma^{-1}(V)\\}$ is an open cover of the compact space $\\Delta^{p}$ and has a Lebesgue number $\\delta_{\\sigma} > 0$ (any subset of diameter $< \\delta_{\\sigma}$ lies in one member of the cover). Choose $m$ so that $(p/(p+1))^{m}\\operatorname{diam}(\\Delta^{p}) < \\delta_{\\sigma}$. Then the image of each smaller simplex of $\\operatorname{Sd}^{m}(\\sigma)$ lies in $U$ or $V$.\n\nWrite $T_{M} := \\sum_{j=0}^{M-1}\\operatorname{Sd}^{j} T$. Then $\\partial T_{M} + T_{M}\\partial = \\operatorname{Sd}^{M} - \\operatorname{id}$.\n\n**Surjectivity.** Take any cycle $z$. Since $z$ contains only finitely many simplices, choose $M$ so that each $\\operatorname{Sd}^{M}(\\sigma)$ is small as $\\sigma$ ranges over the simplices of $z$. Then $\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z \\in S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{*}(X)$ and $\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z - z = \\partial T_{M} z$, so $\\iota_{*}[\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z] = [z]$.\n\n**Injectivity.** Suppose $z \\in S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{p}(X)$ is a cycle and $z = \\partial b$ ($b \\in S_{p+1}(X)$). Choose $M$ so that, for every simplex in $z$, $T z$, and $b$, the $M$-fold subdivision is small. Then $z = \\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z - \\partial T_{M} z$, with both $\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z$ and $T_{M} z$ in the small-chain complex. Also, $\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z = \\operatorname{Sd}^{M}\\partial b = \\partial(\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} b)$, and $\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} b$ is small as well. Thus in $H_{p}(S^{\\mathcal{U}}_{*}(X))$ we have $[z] = [\\operatorname{Sd}^{M} z] = 0$.\n\n**Step 5. Splitting into two pieces.** A chain is small if and only if it belongs to $S_{*}(U) + S_{*}(V)$. Thus there is a short exact sequence of chain complexes\n\n$$0 \\to S_{*}(U \\cap V) \\to S_{*}(U) \\oplus S_{*}(V) \\to S_{*}(U) + S_{*}(V) \\to 0,$$\n\nwith maps $a \\mapsto (a, -a)$ and $(a, b) \\mapsto a + b$. For exactness, observe that $S_{*}(U) \\cap S_{*}(V) = S_{*}(U \\cap V)$ (the image of each simplex lies in both $U$ and $V$, hence in their intersection). Surjectivity follows by definition, and the kernel follows from $a + b = 0$, that is, $b = -a$.\n\n**Step 6. Obtaining the long exact sequence.** Apply `thm.long-exact-pair` to the short exact sequence above, using the chain complex version proved by diagram chasing, to obtain the long exact sequence. Here $H_{p}(S_{*}(U) \\oplus S_{*}(V)) = H_{p}(U) \\oplus H_{p}(V)$, and $H_{p}(S_{*}(U) + S_{*}(V)) \\cong H_{p}(X)$ follows from Step 4. To identify the two maps, $(a) \\mapsto (a, -a)$ gives $\\Phi[z] = (i_{*}[z], -j_{*}[z])$, and $(a, b) \\mapsto a + b$ gives $\\Psi[a, b] = [a + b]$. ∎\n\n**Reduced version.** Apply the same argument to the augmented chain complexes. When $U \\cap V \\ne \\emptyset$, choose a basepoint $x_{0} \\in U \\cap V$. The augmentation map $\\varepsilon$ agrees on all three complexes, and the short exact sequence remains exact in the augmentation degree, giving the reduced long exact sequence. When $U \\cap V = \\emptyset$, exactness fails in the segment $\\tilde{H}_{0}$. See the statement for a counterexample.\n\n**Why no good pair hypothesis is needed.** The argument uses only the open cover and Lebesgue numbers. It requires no neighborhood deformation retraction of $A$ in $X$. This is one way singular homology is easier to work with than homotopy groups.\n\n⭐ **The main difficulty** is passing from simplices that lie locally in $U$ or $V$ to a global isomorphism in homology. The chain homotopy in Step 4 does this, and the exact sequence then follows by algebra. The signs are all in the cone construction, $\\operatorname{sd}_{p} := [b_{p}, \\operatorname{sd}_{p-1}(\\partial\\Delta^{p})]$ and $t_{p} := [b_{p}, c_{p}]$.\n\n⚠️ **Difference from `thm.mayer-vietoris`.** That node gives the **de Rham version** for smooth manifolds with real coefficients and the complex of differential forms (`def.de-rham-complex`). This is the **singular-chain version** for arbitrary spaces with $\\mathbb{Z}$ coefficients. The sequences have the same form, but only one retains torsion information. They are distinct results and should not be cited as proofs of each other.\n\n💡 Common applications include computing $\\tilde{H}_{*}(S^{n})$ (`thm.sphere-homology`), decomposing a torus into two cylinders, and decomposing a connected sum into two spaces with a small ball removed from each.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#84dd1aa6'
    },
  },
  'thm.mu-sum': {
    statement: {
      en: "For $n \\ge 1$,\n\n$$\\sum_{d \\mid n} \\mu(d) = \\begin{cases} 1, & n = 1, \\\\ 0, & n > 1 . \\end{cases}$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#175c779d'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "Proof. $n = 1$ is immediate. Suppose $n = p_{1}^{e_{1}}\\cdots p_{r}^{e_{r}}$, $r \\ge 1$. Any $d$ with a square factor contributes $0$, so we need only sum over $d$ made up of distinct prime factors:\n\n\n\n$$\\sum_{d\\mid n}\\mu(d) = \\sum_{S \\subseteq \\{1,\\cdots,r\\}} (-1)^{\\#S} = (1 - 1)^{r} = 0 .$$\n\n\n\nThis is the binomial expansion. This identity is the **only** use of $\\mu$ here. It turns summation into projection onto $n = 1$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#3dc62dba'
    },
  },
  'thm.zeta-no-zeros-line1': {
    statement: {
      en: "For every $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$, $\\zeta(1+it)\\ne0$.",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2f1f8993'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "Use the logarithm of the Euler product together with $3+4\\cos\\theta+\\cos2\\theta=2(1+\\cos\\theta)^{2}\\ge0$. There is a pole at $t=0$, so that point is excluded from the zeros. (Weighted logarithmic estimate.)",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#2acbf6f8'
    },
  },
  'thm.quadratic-reciprocity': {
    statement: {
      en: "Let $p, q$ be two distinct odd primes. Then\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{q-1}{2}} .$$\n\nEquivalently, if at least one of $p, q$ is $\\equiv 1 \\pmod 4$, then $\\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right)$. If both are $\\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$, the two values have opposite signs. The supplementary formulas are\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{-1}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}}, \\qquad \\left(\\frac{2}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\frac{p^{2}-1}{8}} .$$",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#8cda3eb3'
    },
    notes: {
      en: "**This is the most important theorem in number theory** (Gauss gave eight proofs in his lifetime, the first in 1796 at age 19). It turns the difficult question of whether $p$ is a quadratic residue modulo $q$ into the easier question of whether $q$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$, since one can recursively reduce $p$ to $< q$.\n\n\n\n⭐ A mainstream approach today uses **point counting on curves over finite fields**. $\\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)$ is related to the number of solutions of $x^{2} \\equiv p \\pmod q$, and these solutions can be counted in stages using elliptic curves or multiplicative groups over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ (Zolotarev's lemma and the signs of permutations). This is **one modern approach**. Elementary proofs also exist, including Gauss's third proof and proofs using Gauss's lemma or Zolotarev's lemma, all based on elementary counting.\n\n\n\n**Application.** To evaluate $\\left(\\frac{a}{p}\\right)$, factor it into primes, use reciprocity to swap the arguments for each prime factor, and recurse. This takes on the order of $O(\\log p)$ divisions. The two supplementary formulas above handle $-1$ and $2$, the two cases where the arguments cannot be swapped.\n\n\n\n**Proof (Gauss's lemma and lattice-point counting in a rectangle, elementary).** Write $P = \\frac{p-1}{2}$ and $Q = \\frac{q-1}{2}$. For $1 \\le x \\le P$, write $m_{x} := \\left\\lfloor \\frac{qx}{p} \\right\\rfloor$ and let $r_{x}$ be the least positive residue of $qx$ modulo $p$ ($1 \\le r_{x} \\le p-1$ because $p \\nmid qx$).\n\n\n\n**Step 1. Gauss's lemma.** Set $\\varepsilon_{x} = 1$ when $r_{x} < p/2$, or $-1$ when $r_{x} > p/2$. Set $s_{x} = r_{x}$ in the former case and $s_{x} = p - r_{x}$ in the latter. Then $1 \\le s_{x} \\le P$. We claim that $x \\mapsto s_{x}$ is a permutation of $\\{1, \\ldots, P\\}$. If $s_{x} = s_{y}$, then $r_{x} \\equiv \\pm r_{y} \\pmod p$, that is, $qx \\equiv \\pm qy \\pmod p$. Since $q$ is invertible modulo $p$, we have $x \\equiv \\pm y \\pmod p$. But $1 \\le x, y \\le P$ and $p$ is odd, so $x \\equiv -y$ would imply $x + y = p$, contradicting $x + y \\le 2P = p - 1$. Hence $x = y$ ✓.\n\n\n\nReplacing $\\{qx\\}_{x \\le P}$ by the least positive residues and then taking signs gives\n\n\n\n$$\\prod_{x=1}^{P} qx \\equiv \\prod_{x=1}^{P} (\\varepsilon_{x} s_{x}) = \\Bigl( \\prod_{x} \\varepsilon_{x} \\Bigr) \\Bigl( \\prod_{x} s_{x} \\Bigr) \\equiv \\Bigl( \\prod_{x} \\varepsilon_{x} \\Bigr) P! \\pmod p .$$\n\n\n\nThe left-hand side is $= q^{P} P!$. Canceling $P!$ ($p \\nmid P!$) gives $q^{P} \\equiv \\prod_{x} \\varepsilon_{x} = (-1)^{\\mu}$, where $\\mu = \\#\\{x : r_{x} > p/2\\}$. By **`lem.gauss-qr`** (Gauss's lemma), $\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\mu}$, so\n\n\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\mu} .$$\n\n\n\n**Step 2. $\\mu$ and $\\sum m_{x}$ have the same parity.** From $r_{x} = qx - p m_{x}$ and $\\sum_{x} s_{x} = \\sum_{x=1}^{P} x = \\frac{P(P+1)}{2}$ we obtain\n\n\n\n$$\\sum_{x} s_{x} = \\sum_{x} r_{x} + \\sum_{r_{x} > p/2} (p - 2r_{x}) ,$$\n\n\n\nWe also have $\\sum_{x} r_{x} = q\\sum_{x} x - p \\sum_{x} m_{x}$. Substitute these expressions and reduce both sides modulo $2$. Then $\\sum_{r_{x} > p/2}(p - 2r_{x}) \\equiv p\\mu \\equiv \\mu \\pmod 2$ and $q \\sum_{x} x \\equiv \\sum_{x} x \\pmod 2$, so\n\n\n\n$$\\sum_{x} x \\equiv \\sum_{x} x + \\sum_{x} m_{x} + \\mu \\pmod 2 ,$$\n\n\n\nCanceling $\\sum_{x} x$ gives $\\sum_{x} m_{x} \\equiv \\mu \\pmod 2$. Hence\n\n\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right) = (-1)^{\\mu} = (-1)^{\\sum_{x=1}^{P} \\lfloor qx/p \\rfloor} .$$\n\n\n\n**Step 3. Counting lattice points in a rectangle.** In the rectangle $R = \\{(x, y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{2} : 1 \\le x \\le P,\\ 1 \\le y \\le Q\\}$ (with $PQ$ lattice points in total), the line $qx = py$ contains no lattice points. Indeed, $p \\mid qx$ and $p \\nmid q$ imply $p \\mid x$, but $1 \\le x \\le P < p$, a contradiction. Thus $R$ splits into two parts $R_{-} = \\{qx - py > 0\\}$ and $R_{+} = \\{qx - py < 0\\}$, with $\\#R = \\#R_{-} + \\#R_{+} = PQ$.\n\n\n\nFor fixed $x$, $\\{y : 1 \\le y \\le Q,\\ qx - py > 0\\} = \\{y : y < qx/p\\}$ has exactly $\\min(Q, \\lfloor qx/p \\rfloor)$ elements. Since $qx/p \\le qP/p = \\frac{q(p-1)}{2p} < \\frac{q}{2}$, we have $\\lfloor qx/p \\rfloor \\le \\frac{q-1}{2} = Q$, and this count is $\\lfloor qx/p \\rfloor$. Thus $\\#R_{-} = \\sum_{x=1}^{P} \\lfloor qx/p \\rfloor$. By symmetry, interchanging $p \\leftrightarrow q$ and $x \\leftrightarrow y$ gives $\\#R_{+} = \\sum_{y=1}^{Q} \\lfloor py/q \\rfloor$. Therefore\n\n\n\n$$\\sum_{x=1}^{P} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{qx}{p} \\right\\rfloor + \\sum_{y=1}^{Q} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{py}{q} \\right\\rfloor = PQ .$$\n\n\n\n**Step 4. Combining the results.** Apply Step 2 in both directions, $(p, q)$ and $(q, p)$, and substitute the result of Step 3:\n\n\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right)\\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right) = (-1)^{PQ} = (-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{q-1}{2}} ,$$\n\n\n\nThis is the desired quadratic reciprocity law ✓. ∎",
      status: 'draft',
      basedOn: 'v0.9.3#f977da75'
    },
  },
  },
};
